window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"id": "l1a", "title": "Lecture #1a: Basics of Diagnosis: Innominates and Pubic Bone", "module": "OMM", "content": "id l1a module OMM l1a Lecture 1a Basics of Diagnosis Innominates and Pubic Bone\\n\\n Lecturer To Shan Li D.O.\\n Session Lecture 1a\\n\\n Introduction and Objectives Slide 2 \\nThis lecture introduces the structural examination of the pelvis focusing on the diagnosis of somatic dysfunctions of the innominates and pubic bone.\\n Key Goals \\n Understand pelvic anatomy and biomechanics.\\n Perform and interpret Standing Flexion Test StFT ASIS Compression Test and Seated Flexion Test SeFT .\\n Differentiate between Iliosacral Pelvic and Sacroiliac Sacral dysfunctions.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Anatomy of the Pelvis Slide 5-6 \\n Bones\\n Innominate Composed of three parts that fuse at the acetabulum \\n Ilium \\n Ischium \\n Pubis \\n Sacrum Articulates with the ilia.\\n\\n Joints\\n Sacroiliac Joint SIJ \\n L-shaped or C-shaped.\\n Allows for mobility though limited Slide 6 .\\n Mobility decreases with age. \\n Pubic Symphysis Fibrocartilaginous joint.\\n\\n Ligaments Slide 7 15 \\n Ligaments e.g. sacrotuberous sacrospinous provide stability and create foramina for neurovascular structures.\\n Clinical Relevance They guide motion and can become tender/restrictive in somatic dysfunction.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Biomechanics and Function\\n\\n Gait and Motion Slide 17 \\n Physiologic motion of the sacrum and ilia occurs during walking.\\n Somatic Dysfunction Affects gait efficiency and increases energy expenditure.\\n\\n correlate\\n Pregnancy-Related Changes Slide 18 \\n Pathophysiology Horizontalization of sacrum increased laxity due to Relaxin .\\n Presentation Anterior pelvic tilt increased lumbar lordosis potential for pain low back pelvic .\\n Lecturer s Point \\ Center of gravity shifts anteriorly... Sacroiliac joint and pubic symphysis become more lax.\\ \\n \\n\\n Clinical Implications Slide 20 \\n Pelvic dysfunction often presents as \\n Low back pain hip/groin pain.\\n Sciatic neuralgia piriformis syndrome .\\n Visceral issues GI GU via autonomic relationships.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Structural Examination Screening Tests\\n\\n 1. Standing Flexion Test StFT Slide 23-28 \\n Purpose Diagnoses Iliosacral Dysfunction Ilio-sacral = Ilium moving on Sacrum = Pelvic SD .\\n Execution \\n 1. Patient stands feet 6-8 inches apart.\\n 2. Physician monitors PSIS bilaterally eyes at level .\\n 3. Patient bends forward slowly.\\n Positive Result One PSIS moves \\ First and Farthest\\ superiorly.\\n Interpretation The side that moves superiorly is the Dysfunctional Side restricted side . The ilium is \\ locked\\ to the sacrum on that side and moves with it.\\n\\n pearl\\n StFT Mnemonic Standing = S ide of I lium Iliosacral .\\n \\n\\n 2. Seated Flexion Test SeFT Slide 32-35 \\n Purpose Diagnoses Sacroiliac Dysfunction Sacro-iliac = Sacrum moving on Ilium = Sacral SD .\\n Execution \\n 1. Patient seated feet flat stabilizing the ilia .\\n 2. Physician monitors PSIS bilaterally.\\n 3. Patient bends forward.\\n Positive Result One PSIS moves \\ First and Farthest\\ .\\n Interpretation Indicates the side of sacral dysfunction.\\n Note The \\ standing\\ vs \\ seated\\ distinction isolates the ilia standing = mobile vs sacroiliac mechanics seated = ilia locked by tuberosities .\\n\\n highyield\\n StFT vs SeFT Summary Slide 36 \\n StFT + Iliosacral Dysfunction → Look for Innominate/Pubic shears.\\n Axis Inferior Transverse Axis.\\n SeFT + Sacroiliac Dysfunction → Look for Sacral Torsions/Shears.\\n Axis Middle Transverse Axis.\\n \\n\\n 3. ASIS Compression Test Pelvic Rock Slide 30-31 \\n Purpose Lateralization Determines which side is dysfunctional .\\n Helps confirm StFT/SeFT findings or distinguish when tests are equivocal.\\n Execution \\n 1. Patient supine.\\n 2. Physician applies posterior-medial pressure on ASIS towards the SI joint.\\n 3. Assess for \\ spring\\ or resistance.\\n Positive Result The side with Restriction less spring board-like feel is the positive side.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Static Landmarks Examination Slide 40-42 \\nAfter lateralizing finding the side check landmarks to classify the dysfunction e.g. Anterior/Posterior Rotation Upslip/Downslip .\\n\\n Anterior Landmarks\\n ASIS Heights Compare superior/inferior.\\n Pubic Tubercles Check for superior/inferior shears.\\n Medial Malleoli Assess functional leg length.\\n\\n Posterior Landmarks\\n PSIS Heights Compare.\\n Iliac Crest Heights Compare.\\n Ischial Tuberosities Compare heights.\\n Sacral Sulci Depth.\\n\\n pearl\\n Diagnosis Flow \\n1. Screen StFT Pelvic vs SeFT Sacral .\\n2. Lateralize ASIS Compression confirms side .\\n3. Diagnose Check landmarks ASIS PSIS etc. on the dysfunctional side relative to the other.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Review Questions Slide 44-52 \\n\\nSee the Questions tab for interactive board-style practice. Lecture 1a Basics of Diagnosis Innominates and Pubic Bone lecturer To Shan Li D.O. OMM session Lecture 1a prev next l1b pdf pdfs/Lecture 1a. T. Li D.O. PPT .pdf mindmap Basics of Pelvic Diagnosis\\n Anatomy\\n Innominate Bones Ilium Ischium Pubis\\n Joints Sacroiliac SIJ Pubic Symphysis\\n Ligaments Sacrotuberous Sacrospinous Stability \\n Biomechanics\\n Motion Occurs during gait\\n Pregnancy Relaxin → Increased laxity → Anterior Tilt\\n Screening Tests\\n Standing Flexion StFT \\n Diagnoses Iliosacral Innominate Dysfunction\\n Axis Inferior Transverse\\n Positive restricted side moves Superior\\n Seated Flexion SeFT \\n Diagnoses Sacroiliac Sacral Dysfunction\\n Axis Middle Transverse\\n Mechanism Ischial tuberosities lock ilia\\n ASIS Compression \\n Purpose Lateralization confirms side \\n Positive Resistance/Rigidity\\n Landmarks Static Exam \\n Anterior ASIS Pubic Tubercles Medial Malleoli\\n Posterior PSIS Iliac Crests Ischial Tuberosities Sacral Sulci questions 1. A 32-year-old female presents with right-sided low back pain. Structural examination reveals a positive Standing Flexion Test on the right and a negative Seated Flexion Test. The ASIS Compression Test is positive on the right. What is the most likely category of somatic dysfunction? Sacroiliac Dysfunction Sacral SD Iliosacral Dysfunction Innominate SD Lumbar Somatic Dysfunction Psoas Syndrome Pubic Shear 1 A positive Standing Flexion Test StFT indicates Iliosacral Innominate dysfunction. The Seated Flexion Test is negative ruling out primary Sacroiliac Sacral dysfunction. The ASIS compression confirms the right side is involved. Therefore the diagnosis lies within the iliosacral/innominate category B . 2. While performing the Standing Flexion Test the physician notes the right PSIS moves more superiorly than the left. Which biomechanical principle explains this finding? The right iliosacral joint is hypermobile The right iliosacral joint is restricted hypomobile The left sacrum is torsioned The right hamstrings are tight The left quadratus lumborum is tight 1 In the flexion tests the PSIS on the dysfunctional side moves first and farthest superiorly. This is because the joint is restricted hypomobile and locks early carrying the ilium up with the sacrum as the patient bends forward. The normal side maintains motion longer and doesn t rise as high initially. 3. A pregnant patient in her third trimester presents with anterior pelvic tilt and increased lumbar lordosis. Which hormone is primarily responsible for the increased laxity of her sacroiliac joints and pubic symphysis? Estrogen Progesterone Relaxin Oxytocin Prolactin 2 Relaxin is the hormone secreted during pregnancy that increases ligamentous laxity particularly in the pelvis SI joints pubic symphysis to accommodate childbirth. This laxity contributes to the structural changes described Slide 18 . 4. The Seated Flexion Test is designed to isolate the motion of the sacrum by eliminating the influence of which structures? The lumbar spine The lower extremities/Ilia The thoracic cage The cervical spine The abdominal muscles 1 By sitting the weight is borne by the ischial tuberosities. This effectively locks the ilia and minimizes the influence of the lower extremities and by extension the iliosacral mechanics on the ilium side allowing the physician to assess sacroiliac sacral motion more specifically Slide 23 . 5. Which axis of motion is primarily associated with Iliosacral Pelvic motion as tested by the Standing Flexion Test? Superior Transverse Axis Middle Transverse Axis Inferior Transverse Axis Oblique Axis Vertical Axis 2 Iliosacral motion ilium moving on sacrum occurs primarily around the Inferior Transverse Axis Slide 36 . Sacroiliac motion sacrum moving on ilium occurs around the Middle Transverse Axis . Standing Flexion Test assesses which dysfunction? Iliosacral Dysfunction Innominate/Pelvic Concept Seated Flexion Test assesses which dysfunction? Sacroiliac Dysfunction Sacral Concept What does a positive StFT indicate biomechanically? The PSIS on the positive side moves first and farthest because the Ilio-sacral joint is restricted hypomobile . Concept What is the purpose of the ASIS Compression Test? Lateralization identifying which side involved . Clinical Which hormone causes pelvic ligament laxity in pregnancy? Relaxin Glossary Axis of motion for Iliosacral Dysfunction? Inferior Transverse Axis Concept Axis of motion for Sacroiliac Dysfunction? Middle Transverse Axis Concept Anatomy What 3 bones fuse to form the Innominate? Ilium Ischium Pubis Glossary In StFT if Right PSIS moves higher which side is dysfunctional? Right side Clinical Normal result for ASIS Compression Test? Springy/Resilient feel Restrictions feel rigid/board-like . Clinical anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l1b", "title": "Lecture #1b: Diagnosis of the Pelvis (Lab Prep)", "module": "OMM", "content": "id l1b module OMM l1b Lecture 1b Diagnosis of the Pelvis Lab Prep \\n\\n Lecturer Michael J. Terzella D.O.\\n Session Lab 15 Prep\\n\\n Introduction\\nThis lecture prepares students for the Pelvis Lab 15 . It reinforces the diagnostic concepts from Lecture 1a and introduces specific Innominate and Pubic Somatic Dysfunctions.\\n Goal Efficiently examine name and record somatic dysfunctions of the pelvis.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Review of Diagnostic Tests Slide 4-6 8-13 \\n 1. Standing Flexion Test StFT \\n Significance Lateralizes Innominate Iliosacral dysfunction.\\n Positive PSIS moves first and farthest superiorly.\\n Rule Detailed diagnosis is named for the side of the positive StFT .\\n\\n 2. ASIS Compression Test Pelvic Rock \\n Significance Lateralizes dysfunction; useful screening but less specific can be Iliosacral or Sacroiliac .\\n Positive Resistance to posterior-medial compression \\ Hard end-feel\\ .\\n\\n 3. Static Landmarks\\n Anterior ASIS Pubic Tubercles Medial Malleoli.\\n Posterior PSIS Iliac Crests Ischial Tuberosities Sacral Sulci.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Innominate Somatic Dysfunctions Slide 26-36 \\n\\n Innominate Rotations\\nMotion of the innominate on the sacrum around the Inferior Transverse Axis .\\n\\n Anterior Rotation Slide 28-29 \\n Mechanism Innominate rotates anteriorly.\\n Landmarks on dysfunctional side \\n ASIS Inferior\\n PSIS Superior\\n Medial Malleolus Inferior Long Leg \\n Symptoms Hamstring tightness Sciatica Piriformis .\\n\\n Posterior Rotation Slide 30-31 \\n Mechanism Innominate rotates posteriorly.\\n Landmarks on dysfunctional side \\n ASIS Superior\\n PSIS Inferior\\n Medial Malleolus Superior Short Leg \\n Symptoms Inguinal/Groin pain Rectus Femoris Medial Knee pain Sartorius .\\n\\n Innominate Shears\\nTranslatory motion of the entire innominate usually traumatic .\\n\\n Superior Shear Upslip Slide 33-34 \\n Mechanism Entire innominate shifts superiorly e.g. landing on one leg .\\n Landmarks \\n ASIS Superior\\n PSIS Superior\\n Pubic Tubercle Superior\\n Medial Malleolus Superior Short Leg \\n Motion Laxity in superior translation.\\n\\n Inferior Shear Downslip Slide 35-36 \\n Mechanism Entire innominate shifts inferiorly e.g. getting leg caught in stirrup .\\n Landmarks \\n ASIS Inferior\\n PSIS Inferior\\n Pubic Tubercle Inferior\\n Medial Malleolus Inferior Long Leg \\n\\n pearl\\n Shear Tip In a shear ALL landmarks move in the same direction e.g. Superior Shear = ASIS High PSIS High Pubes High . In rotations ASIS and PSIS move in opposites .\\n \\n\\n Innominate Flares Slide 37 \\n Outflare ASIS further from midline associated with Posterior Rotation .\\n Inflare ASIS closer to midline associated with Anterior Rotation .\\n\\n---\\n\\n Pubic Somatic Dysfunctions Slide 38-42 \\n\\n Pubic Shears Slide 38 \\n Superior Shear Pubic tubercle high on dysfunctional side.\\n Inferior Shear Pubic tubercle low on dysfunctional side.\\n Clinical Trauma childbirth.\\n\\n Pubic Symphysis Dysfunctions\\nDysfunction at the joint itself without asymmetry of height.\\n\\n correlate\\n Pubic Compression Adducted Pubis Slide 39-40 \\n Pathophysiology Pubic bones forced together Trauma .\\n Presentation \\n Bilateral tenderness.\\n Bulging symphyseal cartilage.\\n Restricted Pelvic Ring motion Bilaterally restricted ASIS Compression .\\n Urinary symptoms frequency/urgency .\\n Diagnosis No height asymmetry but restricted motion and tenderness.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Pubic Gapping Abducted Pubis Slide 41-42 \\n Pathophysiology Pubic bones pulled apart Childbirth Fractures .\\n Presentation \\n Deep Sulcus at symphysis.\\n Bilateral tenderness.\\n Urinary symptoms.\\n Often very painful.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Case Study Slide 45 \\n Patient 20F Equestrian fell from horse foot caught in stirrup.\\n Findings Right Side \\n StFT + Right\\n ASIS Inferior\\n PSIS Inferior\\n Pubic Tubercle Inferior\\n Medial Malleolus Inferior\\n Diagnosis Right Inferior Innominate Shear .\\n Reasoning All landmarks are inferior on the right consistent with the mechanism of injury traction on the leg .\\n\\n Lab Prep Pearls\\n Order of Exam \\n 1. Screen StFT/SeFT .\\n 2. Lateralize ASIS Compression .\\n 3. Reseat the Pelvis! Bridge .\\n 4. Static Landmarks Anterior then Posterior .\\n 5. Diagnose. Lecture 1b Diagnosis of the Pelvis Lab Prep lecturer Michael J. Terzella D.O. OMM session Lab Prep Lecture prev l1a next l2 pdf pdfs/Lecture 1b. M. Terzella D.O. PPT .pdf mindmap Pelvic Somatic Dysfunctions\\n Innominate Rotations\\n Anterior Rotation \\n ASIS Inferior\\n PSIS Superior\\n Leg Long\\n Association Inflare Hamstring tightness\\n Posterior Rotation \\n ASIS Superior\\n PSIS Inferior\\n Leg Short\\n Association Outflare Groin pain Rectus \\n Innominate Shears\\n Superior Shear Upslip \\n All Landmarks Superior ASIS PSIS Pubes \\n Cause Vertical impact landing on leg \\n Inferior Shear Downslip \\n All Landmarks Inferior\\n Cause Traction stuck in stirrup \\n Pubic Dysfunctions\\n Shears Sup/Inf asymmetry named for StFT side \\n Compression Adduction \\n Sign Bulging rigidity\\n Cause Trauma\\n Gapping Abduction \\n Sign Deep Sulcus\\n Cause Childbirth questions 1. A patient presents with right-sided pelvic pain. Examination reveals Right ASIS is Superior Right PSIS is Inferior and Right Medial Malleolus is Superior. What is the most likely diagnosis? Right Anterior Innominate Rotation Right Posterior Innominate Rotation Right Superior Innominate Shear Left Anterior Innominate Rotation Left Superior Innominate Shear 1 In a Posterior Rotation the ASIS moves up Superior and the PSIS moves down Inferior . This also pulls the leg up causing a short leg Superior Medial Malleolus . This matches the findings exactly. 2. Which of the following findings is most consistent with a Superior Innominate Shear Upslip ? ASIS High PSIS Low ASIS Low PSIS High ASIS High PSIS High Pubic Tubercle High ASIS Low PSIS Low Pubic Tubercle Low ASIS High PSIS High Pubic Tubercle Low 2 In a Shear Upslip the entire bone translates superiorly. Therefore ALL landmarks on that bone ASIS PSIS Pubic Tubercle Ischial Tuberosity will be found Superior compared to the healthy side. 3. A postpartum female presents with pubic pain and urinary urgency. Examination reveals bilateral pubic tenderness and a deep palpable sulcus at the pubic symphysis. ASIS and PSIS heights are symmetric. What is the diagnosis? Superior Pubic Shear Inferior Pubic Shear Pubic Compression Adducted Pubic Gapping Abducted Bilateral Innominate Outflare 3 The key finding is the deep sulcus at the symphysis and history of childbirth which suggests Pubic Gapping Abduction . Compression would present with a bulging cartilage. Shears would present with height asymmetry. 4. In an Anterior Innominate Rotation which muscle group is most likely to be tight/hypertonic? Hamstrings Quadriceps/Rectus Femoris Gluteus Maximus Biceps Femoris External Obliques 1 This is a bit distinct check Slide 29 vs 31 . Actually Hamstrings are tight in Anterior rotation Lecturer slide 28 notes Subjective complaints Hamstring tightness for Anterior . Wait biomechanically Rectus pulls it Anterior. Let s check slide 29 Anteriorly Rotated... Hamstring tightness . Why? Because the ischial tuberosity moves superiorly/posteriorly putting tension on the hamstrings. Conversely Quadriceps Rectus dysfunction is associated with Posterior rotation Slide 31 because it attaches to AIIS/ASIS and pulls it down. 5. What is the critical first step before performing the static landmark examination? Treat the lumbar spine Reseat the pelvis Bridging Measure True leg length Perform the Seated Flexion Test Check cranial rhythmic impulse 1 Reseating the pelvis asking patient to bend knees lift hips and set them down is crucial to ensure the patient is straight and soft tissues are relaxed preventing False positives in landmark asymmetry Slide 15 . Landmarks for Anterior Innominate Rotation? ASIS Low PSIS High Leg Long Clinical Landmarks for Posterior Innominate Rotation? ASIS High PSIS Low Leg Short Clinical Landmarks for Superior Innominate Shear? ASIS High PSIS High Pubes High All Up Clinical Landmarks for Inferior Innominate Shear? ASIS Low PSIS Low Pubes Low All Down Clinical Key finding for Pubic Compression? Bulging cartilage restricted pelvic ring bilateral tenderness. Clinical Key finding for Pubic Gapping? Deep sulcus history of delivery/trauma. Clinical Muscle associated with Anterior Innominate pain? Hamstrings tightness Concept Muscle associated with Posterior Innominate pain? Rectus Femoris / Sartorius Groin/Knee pain Concept Innominate Inflare is associated with which rotation? Anterior Rotation Concept Innominate Outflare is associated with which rotation? Posterior Rotation Concept anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l2", "title": "Lecture #2: Overview of Neuroembryology", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l2 module Neuro l2 Lecture 2 Overview of Neuroembryology\\n\\n Lecturer K. Poole Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 2\\n Topic Embryonic development of the Nervous System\\n\\n 1. Gastrulation and Neurulation Slides 3-8 \\n\\n Gastrulation Week 3 \\nThe process begins with Gastrulation establishing the three germ layers \\n Ectoderm Gives rise to the Nervous System and skin epidermis .\\n Mesoderm Forms muscle bone connective tissue and the Notochord .\\n Endoderm Forms the gut tube and internal organs.\\n\\n Notochord Induction\\nThe Notochord a rod-like mesodermal structure is the key inducer. It secretes signaling molecules including Sonic Hedgehog SHH and BMPs that signal the overlying ectoderm to thicken and differentiate into the Neural Plate Slide 4 17 .\\n\\n Neurulation\\nThe transformation of the neural plate into the neural tube is the fundamental event of the nervous system.\\n1. Neural Plate Thickened ectoderm Day 19 .\\n2. Neural Groove The midline invaginates Day 20 .\\n3. Neural Folds The lateral edges elevate.\\n4. Neural Tube The folds fuse in the midline creating a hollow tube. This fusion begins in the cervical region and \\ zips\\ rostrally and caudally.\\n\\n Neuropore Closure \\nThe tube is initially open at both ends. Timing is critical \\n Rostral Anterior Neuropore Closes around Day 25 .\\n Caudal Posterior Neuropore Closes around Day 27-28 .\\n\\n 2. Neural Crest Cells Slides 13-15 \\nAs the neural tube closes cells at the crest of the neural folds detach and migrate away. These are the Neural Crest Cells often termed the \\ 4th Germ Layer\\ due to their wide variation.\\n PNS Derivatives Dorsal Root Ganglia Sensory Autonomic Ganglia Schwann Cells.\\n Other Derivatives Melanocytes Adrenal Medulla Craniofacial bones/cartilage face Odontoblasts teeth .\\n\\n 3. Neural Tube Differentiation Slides 16-19 \\nThe wall of the neural tube organizes into distinct layers \\n Ventricular Zone Inner layer neuroepithelium becomes Ependymal cells lining ventricles.\\n Mantle Layer Middle layer becomes Gray Matter cell bodies .\\n Marginal Layer Outer layer becomes White Matter axons .\\n\\n Dorsal-Ventral Patterning Slide 17 28 \\n Alar Plate Dorsal Induced by BMPs/TGF-\\u03b2 . Becomes Sensory regions Dorsal Horn .\\n Basal Plate Ventral Induced by SHH from Notochord . Becomes Motor regions Ventral Horn .\\n Sulcus Limitans The groove separating Alar sensory and Basal motor plates.\\n\\n 4. Brain Development Vesicles Slides 21-25 \\nThe rostral neural tube expands into vesicles.\\n 3 Primary Vesicles Week 4 \\n1. Prosencephalon Forebrain \\n2. Mesencephalon Midbrain \\n3. Rhombencephalon Hindbrain \\n\\n 5 Secondary Vesicles Week 5 \\n Prosencephalon divides into \\n Telencephalon Cerebral Hemispheres Lateral Ventricles.\\n Diencephalon Thalamus Hypothalamus Retina Third Ventricle.\\n Mesencephalon Remains Mesencephalon Midbrain Cerebral Aqueduct .\\n Rhombencephalon divides into \\n Metencephalon Pons Cerebellum Upper 4th Ventricle.\\n Myelencephalon Medulla Lower 4th Ventricle.\\n\\n 5. Clinical Correlates Congenital Disorders\\n\\n correlate\\n Neural Tube Defects NTDs \\nCaused by failure of neuropore closure. Strongly associated with Folate Vitamin B9 deficiency.\\n Anencephaly Meroencephaly Failure of Rostral neuropore closure. Forebrain and skull vault fail to form. \\ Frog-like\\ appearance. Incompatible with life.\\n Spina Bifida Failure of Caudal neuropore closure.\\n Occulta Defect in vertebral arch only. Tuft of hair/dimple. Asymptomatic.\\n Meningocele Meninges herniate through defect. Fluid-filled sac.\\n Myelomeningocele Meninges + Spinal Cord herniate. Severe neurologic deficit paralysis sensory loss .\\n Encephalocele Herniation of brain tissue through a skull defect often occipital .\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Holoprosencephaly HPE Slide 26-27 \\n Pathology Failure of the Prosencephalon Forebrain to cleavage/divide into two hemispheres.\\n Mechanism Defect in midline induction often SHH pathway .\\n Presentation Spectrum of midline facial defects Cleft lip/palate hypotelorism Cyclopia single eye and proboscis tube nose in severe cases.\\n Associations Patau Syndrome Trisomy 13 Fetal Alcohol Syndrome.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Dandy-Walker Syndrome Slide 32 \\n Pathology Agenesis or hypoplasia of the Cerebellar Vermis .\\n Mechanism Failure of the Rhombic Lip to develop Metencephalon .\\n Imaging Large posterior fossa cyst dilated 4th ventricle + small/absent cerebellum.\\n Symptoms Hydrocephalus Ataxia Delayed motor development.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Microcephaly Slide 38 \\n Pathology Small brain → Small skull Calvaria grows in response to brain growth .\\n Causes \\n Genetics Autosomal recessive.\\n Infections Zika Virus Cytomegalovirus CMV Rubella Toxoplasmosis.\\n Environment Fetal Alcohol Sulfate Radiation.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Hydrocephalus Slide 44 \\n Pathology Accumulation of CSF in ventricles due to obstruction or impaired absorption.\\n Congenital Cause Aqueductal Stenosis narrowing of Cerebral Aqueduct of Sylvius is the most common cause. Blocks flow from 3rd to 4th ventricle.\\n Signs Rapidly enlarging head circumference bulging fontanelles \\ Sunset eyes\\ .\\n Lecture 2 Overview of Neuroembryology lecturer K. Poole Ph.D. Neuro session Lecture 2 prev l1b next l3 pdf pdfs/Lecture 2. K. Poole Ph.D. PPT -1.pdf questions 1. Which of the following cells are derived from the Neural Crest? Motor neurons of the ventral horn Astrocytes of the CNS Dorsal Root Ganglion DRG cells Oligodendrocytes Microglia 2 Neural Crest cells migrate to form the DRGs autonomic ganglia Schwann cells and adrenal medulla. CNS glial cells astrocytes oligos come from the neural tube neuroectoderm . Microglia come from mesoderm. 2. A newborn has a tuft of hair over the lumbar spine but no neurologic deficits. What is the most likely diagnosis? Spina Bifida Occulta Meningocele Myelomeningocele Anencephaly Rachischisis 0 Spina Bifida Occulta is a defect in the vertebral arch mesoderm fusion often marked by a tuft of hair or dimple. The meninges and cord remain in place. 3. Which vitamin deficiency is strongly associated with Neural Tube Defects? Vitamin B12 Vitamin C Folic Acid B9 Vitamin D Vitamin A 2 Folic Acid supplementation prior to conception and during early pregnancy significantly reduces the risk of NTDs. 4. The Metencephalon gives rise to which adult structures? Medulla Midbrain Thalamus and Hypothalamus Pons and Cerebellum Cerebral Hemispheres 3 The Metencephalon develops into the Pons and Cerebellum . The Myelencephalon becomes the Medulla. 5. Which structure induces the formation of the Neural Plate from the ectoderm? Primitive Streak Notochord Yolk Sac Intermediate Mesoderm Neural Crest 1 The Notochord derived from mesoderm secretes signaling molecules e.g. Sonic Hedgehog that induce the overlying ectoderm to differentiate into the Neural Plate . Tissue origin of CNS? Neuroectoderm Neural Tube Embryology Tissue origin of PNS neurons/glia? Neural Crest Embryology Structure inducing neural plate? Notochord Embryology Adult derivative of Telencephalon? Cerebrum Cerebral Hemispheres Embryology Adult derivative of Diencephalon? Thalamus Hypothalamus Retina Embryology Failure of rostral neuropore closure? Anencephaly Pathology Failure of caudal neuropore closure? Spina Bifida Pathology Vitamin to prevent NTDs? Folic Acid B9 Prevention Closure day of Rostral Neuropore? Day 25 Embryology Closure day of Caudal Neuropore? Day 27-28 Embryology BandB Neurology > Embryology > Nervous System Development FirstAid Neurology > Embryology Pathoma CNS > Congenital Malformations Physeo Anatomy > Neuroanatomy > Embryology Germ Layer Rule Ectoderm = Skin + Brain.\\n Mesoderm = Muscle + Bone + Notochord.\\n Endoderm = Gut. Neural Crest Mnemonic MOTEL PASS M elanocytes O dontoblasts T racheal cartilage E nterochromaffin cells L aryngeal cartilage P arafollicular cells A drenal medulla S chwann cells S piral septum. Neuropore Dates Rostral closes Day 25 . Caudal closes Day 27-28 . Folate is critical before this window. Alar vs Basal Plate Alar = Dorsal = Sensory Top .\\n Basal = Ventral = Motor Bottom . Holoprosencephaly Failed forebrain cleavage. Spectrum Cleft lip → Cyclopia. Associated with Trisomy 13 Patau and SHH mutations. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l3", "title": "Lecture #3: Neuroanatomy: Meninges, BBB, CSF, Ventricles", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l3 module Neuro l3 Lecture 3 Neuroanatomy Meninges BBB CSF Ventricles\\n\\n Lecturers Vladimir Grubisic M.D. Ph.D. & Vincy Mathew\\n Session Neuroanatomy\\n\\n 1. Central Nervous System Barriers Slides 6-9 \\nThe CNS is an immunologically privileged site protected from the fluctuating chemical environment of the blood by three major barriers \\n\\n Blood-Brain Barrier BBB \\n Location Capillaries within the brain parenchyma.\\n Structure \\n Endothelial Cells The physical barrier connected by Tight Junctions Zona Occludens .\\n Pericytes Contractile cells that wrap around endothelial cells.\\n Astrocyte End-feet Induce and maintain the barrier properties.\\n Function Highly selective.\\n Permeable Lipid-soluble substances O2 CO2 Ethanol Steroid hormones .\\n Impermeable Polar/Large molecules Glucose Amino acids Dopamine require specific transporters.\\n\\n correlate\\n Parkinson s Disease & Levodopa Slide 12 \\n Problem Parkinson s is due to dopamine depletion. Dopamine is polar and cannot cross the BBB .\\n Solution Levodopa L-DOPA is a precursor that can cross the BBB via amino acid transporters. Once inside neurons it is converted to dopamine by DOPA decarboxylase.\\n \\n\\n Other Barriers\\n Blood-CSF Barrier Formed by Tight Junctions between the Epithelial Cells of the Choroid Plexus .\\n Arachnoid Barrier Avascular arachnoid epithelium seals the CSF from the extracellular fluid of the dura.\\n\\n Circumventricular Organs CVOs Slide 14-16 \\nSpecific brain regions that lack a BBB . Their capillaries are fenestrated to allow communication between the blood and brain for sensory or secretory functions.\\n Sensory CVOs \\n Area Postrema Located in the caudal medulla floor of 4th ventricle . Functions as the Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone CTZ to detect toxins and induce vomiting .\\n Subfornical Organ SFO & OVLT Detect plasma osmolarity and Angiotensin II.\\n Secretory CVOs \\n Posterior Pituitary Neurohypophysis Releases Oxytocin and ADH.\\n Pineal Gland Secretes Melatonin.\\n Median Eminence Releases hypothalamic hormones.\\n\\n correlate\\n Levodopa-Induced Nausea Slide 16 \\nBecause the Area Postrema lacks a BBB circulating Levodopa can stimulate dopamine receptors there before entering the brain causing severe nausea/vomiting.\\n Prevention Co-administration of Carbidopa a peripheral DOPA decarboxylase inhibitor that does not cross the BBB.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. The Meninges Slides 20-25 \\nThe brain is encased in three layers \\ PAD\\ \\n1. Dura Mater \\ Hard Mother\\ Tough outer layer.\\n Periosteal Layer Attached to the skull.\\n Meningeal Layer Inner layer. Separates from periosteal layer to form Dural Venous Sinuses and Dural Folds Falx Cerebri Tentorium Cerebelli .\\n2. Arachnoid Mater \\ Spider Mother\\ Wispy intermediate layer.\\n Arachnoid Granulations One-way valves that drain CSF into venous sinuses.\\n3. Pia Mater \\ Soft Mother\\ Delicate inner layer adherent to the brain surface follows gyri/sulci .\\n\\n Meningeal Spaces & Hemorrhages\\n Epidural Space Potential space between Skull and Dura.\\n Vessel Middle Meningeal Artery MMA .\\n Pathology Epidural Hematoma Trauma to Pterion → Lens-shaped bleed .\\n Subdural Space Potential space between Dura and Arachnoid.\\n Vessel Bridging Veins .\\n Pathology Subdural Hematoma Shearing forces/Shaken Baby → Crescent-shaped bleed .\\n Subarachnoid Space Real space between Arachnoid and Pia.\\n Contents CSF and major cerebral arteries/veins.\\n Pathology Subarachnoid Hemorrhage Ruptured Aneurysm → \\ Thunderclap Headache\\ .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. Brain Herniations Slides 26-29 \\nIncreased Intracranial Pressure ICP can force brain tissue through compartments.\\n\\n correlate\\n A. Subfalcine Cingulate Herniation \\n Displacement Cingulate gyrus herniates under the Falx Cerebri .\\n Sequelae Can compress the Anterior Cerebral Artery ACA → Contralateral leg weakness.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n B. Transtentorial Uncal Herniation \\n Displacement Medial Temporal Lobe Uncus herniates past the Tentorium Cerebelli .\\n Sequelae \\n Compresses CN III Oculomotor → Ipsilateral \\ Blown\\ Pupil Fixed & Dilated \\ Down and Out\\ eye.\\n Compresses Cerebral Peduncle → Contralateral Hemiparesis.\\n Compresses PCA → Cortical blindness.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n C. Transforaminal Tonsillar Herniation \\n Displacement Cerebellar Tonsils herniate through the Foramen Magnum .\\n Sequelae Compresses the Medulla Cardiorespiratory centers → Respiratory Arrest and death.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 4. Ventricular System & CSF Slides 35-43 \\n CSF Flow Pathway\\n1. Lateral Ventricles via Foramen of Monro →\\n2. Third Ventricle via Cerebral Aqueduct of Sylvius →\\n3. Fourth Ventricle via Foramina of Luschka Lateral and Magendie Medial →\\n4. Subarachnoid Space →\\n5. Arachnoid Granulations →\\n6. Superior Sagittal Sinus Venous blood .\\n\\n Hydrocephalus\\nAccumulation of CSF resulting in ventricular enlargement.\\n Non-Communicating Obstructive Blockage within the ventricles.\\n Common Cause Aqueductal Stenosis Slide 44 . Lateral & 3rd ventricles dilate; 4th remains normal.\\n Communicating Blockage of absorption Arachnoid Granulations or flow in subarachnoid space. All ventricles dilate.\\n Causes Meningitis Subarachnoid Hemorrhage scarring .\\n Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus NPH Chronic communicating hydrocephalus in elderly.\\n Triad \\ Wet Wobbly Wacky\\ Urinary Incontinence Ataxia/Magnetic Gait Dementia .\\n Hydrocephalus Ex Vacuo Apparent enlargement of ventricles due to brain atrophy e.g. Alzheimer s Huntington s . ICP is normal .\\n\\n Cerebral Venous Thrombosis CVT Slide 26 \\n Thrombosis in dural sinuses e.g. Superior Sagittal Sinus .\\n Risk Factors Hypercoagulable states Pregnancy OCPs Infection Mastoiditis .\\n Signs Headache Papilledema Seizures due to venous congestion/infarction . Lecture 3 Neuroanatomy Meninges BBB CSF Ventricles lecturer Vladimir Grubisic M.D. Ph.D. & Vincy Mathew Neuroanatomy session Lecture 3 prev l2 next l4 pdf pdfs/Lecture 3. V. Grubisic M.D. Ph.D. PPT -1.pdf mindmap Neuroanatomy BBB & Meninges\\n Barriers\\n BBB \\n Tight Junctions Zona Occludens \\n Permeable Lipids O2\\n Blocked Polar molecules Dopamine \\n CVOs No BBB \\n Area Postrema Vomiting \\n Posterior Pituitary\\n Meninges\\n Dura Outer \\n Epidural Space MMA \\n Subdural Space Bridging Veins \\n Arachnoid Middle \\n Subarachnoid Space CSF Arteries \\n Pia Inner \\n Herniations\\n Subfalcine Cingulate gyrus Leg weak \\n Uncal Temporal lobe Blown Pupil \\n Tonsillar Cerebellum Resp Arrest \\n Ventricles\\n Flow Lat → 3rd → 4th\\n Hydrocephalus NPH Wet Wobbly Wacky questions 1. A patient presents with neurological symptoms secondary to increased blood-brain barrier BBB permeability after a traumatic brain injury. Damage to which of the following proteins essential in tight junctions would most likely likely disrupt BBB integrity? Connexin-43 Laminin Zona Occludens ZO-1 Integrin Fibronectin 2 From Slide 11 Zona Occludens ZO proteins along with Claudins and Occludins form the tight junctions between endothelial cells. Damage to these compromises the barrier. 2. Levodopa is treated for Parkinson s disease because unlike Dopamine it can cross the Blood-Brain Barrier. However it frequently causes nausea. This is due to its interaction with which brainstem structure that lacks a BBB? Nucleus Tractus Solitarius Pineal Gland Area Postrema Subfornical Organ Median Eminence 2 From Slide 18 The Area Postrema is a Circumventricular Organ CVO in the medulla vomiting center that lacks a BBB. It detects emetic agents like circulating Levodopa/Dopamine in the blood before they enter the protected CNS. 3. A 26-year-old man presents with a dilated unresponsive right pupil blown pupil after a head injury. CT shows a subdural hematoma with displacement of the medial temporal lobe. What is the specific type of herniation? Subfalcine Cingulate Herniation Transforaminal Tonsillar Herniation Transtentorial Uncal Herniation Central Herniation Upward Cerebellar Herniation 2 From Slide 32 The Uncas medial temporal lobe herniating past the tentorium compresses CN III causing an ipsilateral blown pupil. This is a classic sign of Uncal Herniation. 4. Which layer of the meninges is highly vascular and contains the Middle Meningeal Artery rupture of which leads to an Epidural Hematoma? Pia Mater Arachnoid Mater Meningeal Dura Periosteal Dura / Epidural Space interface Subarachnoid Space 3 The Middle Meningeal Artery runs in the Epidural space between the skull and the periosteal layer of the dura . Trauma to the pterion often ruptures this artery. 5. In the ventricular system CSF flows from the Third Ventricle to the Fourth Ventricle via which structure? Foramen of Monro Foramen of Magendie Foramen of Luschka Cerebral Aqueduct of Sylvius Central Canal 3 CSF flows Lateral → Monro → 3rd → Aqueduct of Sylvius → 4th → Luschka/Magendie. Which proteins form Tight Junctions in the BBB? Occludin Claudin Zona Occludens ZO Concept Which brain area lacks BBB and triggers vomiting? Area Postrema Anatomy Why is Levodopa used instead of Dopamine? Dopamine is polar blocked by BBB ; Levodopa crosses and converts. Pharm Structure separating cerebral hemispheres? Falx Cerebri Anatomy Structure separating cerebrum and cerebellum? Tentorium Cerebelli Anatomy Contents of Epidural Space? Middle Meningeal Artery MMA Clinical Contents of Subdural Space? Bridging Veins Clinical Sign of Uncal Herniation? Ipsilateral Blown Pupil CN III compression Pathology Sign of Subfalcine Herniation? Contralateral leg weakness ACA compression Pathology Triad for Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus NPH ? Wet Incontinence Wobbly Ataxia Wacky Dementia Clinical BandB Neurology > Meninges & CSF BBB Rule Lipid-soluble substances O2 Alcohol cross freely. Polar substances Dopamine Glucose need transport. Tight Junctions are key. Parkinson s Rx Give Levodopa not Dopamine. Levodopa crosses BBB via AA transporter and converts to Dopamine inside. Median Eminence vs Area Postrema Both lack BBB CVOs . Area Postrema = Vomiting CTZ . Median Eminence = Hormones. Epidural vs Subdural Epidural = Lens shaped MMA Trauma .\\n Subdural = Crescent shaped Bridging Veins Elderly . Uncal Herniation Medial temporal lobe pushes on CN III . First sign = Ipsilateral Blown Pupil . NPH Triad Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus \\n1. Wet Incontinence \\n2. Wobbly Ataxia \\n3. Wacky Dementia anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l4", "title": "Lecture #4: Blood Supply of the CNS", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l4 module Neuro l4 Lecture 4 Blood Supply of the CNS\\n\\n Lecturer J. Xie Ph.D. & Vincy Mathew\\n Session Neuroanatomy\\n\\n 1. Overview of Cerebral Circulation Slides 3-6 \\nThe brain represents only 2% of body weight but consumes 20% of the body s oxygen and glucose .\\nBlood enters the cranium via two major systems \\n1. Anterior Circulation Internal Carotid Arteries Supplies ~80% of the brain Telencephalon Diencephalon .\\n2. Posterior Circulation Vertebral Arteries Supplies ~20% of the brain Brainstem Cerebellum Occipital lobes .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. Anterior Circulation ICA Slides 8-14 \\nThe Internal Carotid Artery ICA enters the skull via the Carotid Canal passes through the Cavernous Sinus and gives off 4 key Intradural branches OPAM \\n\\n 1. Ophthalmic Artery Slide 14 \\n First branch. Supplies the eye/retina.\\n Clinical Occlusion causes Amaurosis Fugax transient monocular blindness described as a \\ curtain coming down\\ .\\n\\n 2. Posterior Communicating Artery PCom Slide 15 \\n Connects the Anterior ICA and Posterior PCA circulations.\\n Clinical Common site for Berry Aneurysms . Aneurysm here can compress CN III causing an ipsilateral \\ Down and Out\\ eye with blown pupil.\\n\\n 3. Anterior Cerebral Artery ACA Slides 16-17 20 \\n Supplies the Medial surface of the Frontal and Parietal lobes.\\n Function Motor/Sensory to the Lower Limb Leg/Foot and trunk.\\n Stroke Symptoms Contralateral Leg weakness and sensory loss.\\n\\n 4. Middle Cerebral Artery MCA Slides 18-19 20 \\n Largest branch. Runs laterally through the Sylvian Fissure.\\n Supplies the Lateral surface of Frontal Temporal and Parietal lobes.\\n Function Motor/Sensory to the Face and Upper Limb Arm/Hand . Also supplies language centers in the dominant hemisphere.\\n Stroke Symptoms Contralateral Face/Arm weakness Face/Arm sensory loss.\\n Left MCA Dominant Aphasia Broca s or Wernicke s .\\n Right MCA Non-dominant Hemineglect.\\n\\n correlate\\n Lenticulostriate Arteries Slide 19 \\n Small deep perforating branches of the MCA supplying the Basal Ganglia and Internal Capsule.\\n Pathology High pressure Hypertension causes lipohyalinosis and microaneurysms Charcot-Bouchard Aneurysms .\\n Result Rupture leads to Intraparenchymal Hemorrhage . Occlusion leads to Lacunar Infarcts pure motor strokes .\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. Posterior Circulation Vertebrobasilar Slides 23-26 \\n Vertebral Arteries VA \\n Ascend through transverse foramina of C6-C1. Enter skull via Foramen Magnum.\\n Branches \\n Anterior Spinal Artery ASA Supplies anterior 2/3 of spinal cord.\\n PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Supplies Lateral Medulla and Inferior Cerebellum. Occlusion → Wallenberg Syndrome .\\n\\n Basilar Artery BA \\n Formed by fusion of VAs. Runs along the Pons.\\n Branches \\n AICA Anterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Supplies Lateral Pons.\\n Pontine Arteries Supply the Pons. Occlusion → Locked-in Syndrome .\\n SCA Superior Cerebellar Artery Supplies Superior Cerebellum.\\n\\n Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA \\n Terminal bifurcation of the Basilar Artery.\\n Supplies Occipital Lobe Visual Cortex and Inferior Temporal lobe.\\n Stroke Symptoms Contralateral Homonymous Hemianopsia Visual field loss often with Macular Sparing .\\n\\n pearl\\n CN III Rule The Oculomotor Nerve CN III acts as a sandwich filling between the SCA Superior Cerebellar and PCA Posterior Cerebral arteries. Aneurysms here compress CN III.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 4. Collateral & Venous Circulation\\n\\n Circle of Willis Slides 29-30 \\n Anastomotic ring at the base of the brain providing collateral flow.\\n Formed by ACom ACA ICA PCom PCA.\\n Note The circle is incomplete in ~50% of people.\\n Watershed Zones Border areas between ACA/MCA/PCA territories. Susceptible to ischemia during Global Hypoperfusion e.g. Cardiac Arrest or severe hypotension → \\ Man in a Barrel\\ syndrome proximal weakness .\\n\\n Venous Drainage Slides 32-42 \\nVeins drain into Valveless Dural Venous Sinuses \\n Superior Sagittal Sinus SSS Drains superficial veins + CSF via arachnoid granulations .\\n Cavernous Sinus A venous plexus surrounding the Sella Turcica.\\n Contents ICA CN VI freely inside and CN III IV V1 V2 in the wall .\\n Pathology Cavernous Sinus Thrombosis or Fistula can cause multiple cranial nerve palsies.\\n\\n Presentation \\ Thunderclap Headache\\ Worst headache of life meningeal signs.\\n Lecture 4 Blood Supply of the CNS lecturer J. Xie Ph.D. Neuroanatomy session Lecture 4 prev l3 next l5 pdf pdfs/Lecture 4. J. Xie Ph.D. PPT .pdf mindmap CNS Blood Supply\\n Anterior Circ ICA \\n Ophthalmic Eye Amaurosis Fugax \\n ACA Medial Brain Legs \\n MCA Lateral Brain Face/Arms + Speech \\n Lenticulostriate Lacunar stroke \\n PCom Connects to Posterior\\n Posterior Circ Vert-Basilar \\n Vertebral A. → PICA \\n Basilar A. → AICA SCA PCA \\n PCA Occipital Vision \\n Circle of Willis\\n ACom Connects ACAs \\n PCom Connects ICA-PCA \\n Venous\\n Sup. Sagittal Sinus\\n Cavernous Sinus CN III IV V VI \\n Transverse/Sigmoid → IJV questions 1. A 65-year-old man presents with sudden difficulty understanding language. He speaks fluently but uses nonsensical words Word Salad . MRI shows an ischemic stroke. Which artery is most likely involved and which area is affected? ACA - Broca s Area MCA - Broca s Area MCA - Wernicke s Area PCA - Occipital Lobe PICA - Lateral Medulla 2 From Slide 46 Wernicke s Area comprehension is in the Superior Temporal Gyrus supplied by the MCA . Damage leads to Receptive Aphasia Fluent but meaningless . Broca s Expressive is also MCA but frontal. 2. A patient experiences a curtain coming down over their right eye which resolves after a few minutes Amaurosis Fugax . This symptom is a warning sign for a blockage in which artery? Ophthalmic Artery Branch of ICA Middle Cerebral Artery Posterior Cerebral Artery Basilar Artery External Carotid Artery 0 From Slide 14 The Ophthalmic Artery is the first branch of the Intradural ICA. Transient occlusion causes temporary monocular blindness Amaurosis Fugax a TIA warning sign. 3. A patient with chronic hypertension presents with a pure motor stroke. MRI reveals a small lacunar infarct in the internal capsule. Which small arteries are likely responsible? Callosomarginal Arteries Lenticulostriate Arteries Anterior Choroidal Arteries Posterior Communicating Arteries Pontine Arteries 1 From Slide 19 Lenticulostriate Arteries deep branches of MCA supply the Basal Ganglia and Internal Capsule. They are prone to lipohyalinosis and Charcot-Bouchard aneurysms in hypertension. 4. In the Circle of Willis which artery connects the Anterior Circulation ICA system to the Posterior Circulation Vertebrobasilar system ? Anterior Communicating Artery ACom Posterior Communicating Artery PCom Anterior Choroidal Artery Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Basilar Artery 1 The Posterior Communicating Artery PCom connects the ICA to the PCA linking the two major circulatory systems. 5. Contralateral leg weakness and sensory loss is most characteristic of a stroke in which vascular territory? MCA ACA PCA PICA AICA 1 From Slide 20 The Anterior Cerebral Artery ACA supplies the medial surface of the cortex where the Leg/Foot area of the motor/sensory homunculus is located. Branches of Intradural ICA? OPAM Ophthalmic PCom ACA MCA Anatomy Which artery supplies the visual cortex? Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA Anatomy Clinical sign of ACA stroke? Contralateral Leg Weakness Clinical Clinical sign of MCA stroke? Contralateral Face/Arm Weakness + Aphasia Clinical Artery passing through Cavernous Sinus? Internal Carotid Artery ICA Anatomy Transient monocular vision loss Curtain ? Amaurosis Fugax Ophthalmic Artery ischemia Clinical Common site for Berry Aneurysm causing CN III palsy? Posterior Communicating Artery PCom Pathology Vessels prone to rupture in hypertension Basal Ganglia ? Lenticulostriate Arteries Charcot-Bouchard Pathology What nerve passes between SCA and PCA? CN III Oculomotor Anatomy Risk factors for Cerebral Venous Thrombosis CVT ? Pregnancy OCPs Hypercoagulability Clinical BandB Neurology > Spinal Cord Anatomy CNS Blood Flow Rule The brain is 2% of body weight but consumes 20% of oxygen/glucose. It has zero reserve. Stroke Homunculus Rule ACA = Medial aspect = Legs/Feet .\\n MCA = Lateral aspect = Face/Arms/Hands + Speech. Amaurosis Fugax Transient monocular blindness Curtain coming down is a TIA of the Ophthalmic Artery warning of impending ICA stroke. CN III Compression Aneurysms of the PCom or between SCA and PCA often compress CN III causing a Down and Out eye with a blown pupil . Lenticulostriate Arteries These small vessels supply the Basal Ganglia. They are the primary site of hypertensive lipohyalinosis and Lacunar Infarcts . Aphasia Localization Aphasia is usually Left MCA.\\n Broca s Frontal = Broken speech.\\n Wernicke s Temporal = Word salad. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l5", "title": "Lecture #5: Clinical Applications of Neuroimaging", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l5 module Neuro l5 Lecture 5 Clinical Applications of Neuroimaging\\n\\n Lecturer S. Zakhary D.O.\\n Session Neuroanatomy\\n\\n 1. Computed Tomography CT Slides 5-18 \\n Mechanism\\n Uses Helical X-ray beams to measure the attenuation density of tissues.\\n Hounsfield Units HU The scale of density.\\n Hyperdense White Bone +1000 HU Acute Hemorrhage Calcifications.\\n Isodense Gray Brain parenchyma.\\n Hypodense Black Air -1000 HU Fat CSF Water.\\n Windows Different software settings to highlight specific tissues e.g. Bone Window for fractures Brain Window for strokes .\\n\\n Clinical Indications\\n Neurologic Emergencies The \\ workhorse\\ of the ER.\\n Acute Hemorrhage Stroke/Trauma First-line test. Fast <30s .\\n Trauma Skull fractures.\\n Initial Stroke To rule out hemorrhage before giving tPA.\\n\\n CT Contrast Iodinated \\n Uses Infection Abscess Tumors Vascular pathology CTA .\\n Contraindications/Risks \\n 1. Contrast-Induced Nephropathy CIN Check Creatinine/GFR. Hydrate.\\n 2. Allergy Mild hives to Anaphylaxis. Pre-medicate with Steroids/Benadryl.\\n 3. Metformin Risk of Lactic Acidosis . Discontinue for 48 hours.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. Magnetic Resonance Imaging MRI Slides 19-27 \\n Mechanism\\n Uses strong magnetic fields to align protons. No Ionizing Radiation. \\n Sequences \\n T1 Fat is bright Anatomy . Good for structure.\\n T2 Water/CSF is bright Pathology . Good for Edema.\\n Diffusion Weighted Imaging DWI Most sensitive for Acute Ischemic Stroke shows cytotoxic edema within minutes .\\n\\n Clinical Indications\\n Soft Tissue Pathology Superior resolution for brain parenchyma.\\n Tumors Multiple Sclerosis Plaques Infections. \\n Posterior Fossa/Brainstem CT has bone artifact here; MRI does not.\\n\\n MRI Contrast Gadolinium \\n Risk Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis NSF .\\n Rare but serious fibrosis of skin/organs.\\n Contraindicated if GFR < 30 Severe Renal Failure .\\n\\n Summary CT vs. MRI\\n\\n| Feature | CT | MRI |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Speed | Fast < 1 min | Slow 30-45 mins |\\n| Cost | Cheaper | Expensive |\\n| Radiation | Yes X-ray | No |\\n| Bone | Excellent | Poor |\\n| Soft Tissue | Fair | Excellent |\\n| Contraindications | Pregnancy relative | Metal Implants Pacemakers |\\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. Vascular Imaging Slides 28-38 \\n Carotid Duplex Ultrasound\\n Indication Screening for Carotid Artery Stenosis bruit TIA .\\n Pros Non-invasive No radiation Portable.\\n Cons Operator dependent.\\n\\n Conventional Angiography Digital Subtraction Angiography - DSA \\n Gold Standard for vascular pathology.\\n Procedure Catheter inserted femoral/radial → dye injected directly into vessels.\\n Usage \\n Diagnostic Aneurysms AVMs Vasculitis.\\n Therapeutic Coiling aneurysms Thrombectomy clot removal .\\n Risks Invasive stroke risk 1% access site bleeding.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 4. Clinical Correlates Intracranial Hemorrhages Slides 47-53 \\n\\n correlate\\n Epidural Hematoma \\n Vessel Rupture of Middle Meningeal Artery MMA branch of Maxillary .\\n Cause Trauma to Pterion Temple . Rarely crosses sutures.\\n CT Appearance Lens-shaped Biconvex hyperdensity.\\n Clinical \\ Lucid Interval\\ Knocked out → Fine → Rapid Decline/Death .\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Subdural Hematoma \\n Vessel Rupture of Bridging Veins .\\n Cause Shearing forces falls in elderly alcoholics shaken baby .\\n CT Appearance Crescent-shaped hyperdensity. Crosses sutures.\\n Clinical Gradual onset Chronic or Acute.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Subarachnoid Hemorrhage SAH \\n Vessel Ruptured Berry Aneurysm Circle of Willis or Trauma.\\n CT Appearance Hyperdensity in Sulci and Cisterns Starburst .\\n Clinical \\ Thunderclap Headache\\ \\ Worst headache of my life\\ Meningismus.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 5. Enhancement Patterns Slide 15 \\n Ring-Enhancing Lesion Central necrosis with peripheral enhancement.\\n Differential M.A.G.I.C. D.R. \\n R adiation necrosis Lecture 5 Clinical Applications of Neuroimaging lecturer S. Zakhary D.O. Neuroanatomy session Lecture 5 prev l4 next l6 pdf pdfs/Lecture 5. S. Zakhary D.O. PPT .pdf mindmap Neuroimaging\\n CT Scan\\n Physics X-ray Attenuation \\n Uses Acute Hemorrhage Bone Trauma\\n Contrast Iodine Risk Allergy Kidney \\n MRI\\n Physics Magnets T1/T2 \\n Uses Ischemia DWI Tumors MS\\n Contrast Gadolinium Risk NSF \\n Contra Metal Pacemaker \\n Angiography\\n DSA Gold Standard Vascular \\n Therapeutic Coiling \\n Ultrasound Carotid Stenosis screening\\n Pathology Signs\\n Ring Enhancement Abscess GBM\\n Hyperdense CT Acute Blood questions 1. A 65-year-old male presents to the ER with sudden onset right-sided weakness and aphasia starting 30 minutes ago. What is the most appropriate INITIAL imaging modality? Non-contrast CT Head MRI Brain with Gadolinium Carotid Ultrasound Conventional Angiography Lumbar Puncture 0 From Slide 48 Non-contrast CT is the first-line study for suspected stroke Code Stroke . It is fast <30s and rules out Hemorrhage . If no blood is seen the patient may be a candidate for tPA. MRI takes too long for the initial decision. 2. A diabetic patient taking Metformin is scheduled for a CT scan with IV contrast. What is the recommended management regarding their medication? Take Metformin as usual Discontinue Metformin for 48 hours BEFORE the scan Discontinue Metformin for 48 hours AFTER the scan Double the dose of Metformin Replace Metformin with Insulin permanently 2 From Slide 46/Slide 134 Metformin must be discontinued for 48 hours after contrast administration or before in elective cases to prevent the risk of Lactic Acidosis if contrast-induced nephropathy occurs. 3. Which of the following is an absolute contraindication for an MRI of the brain? Pregnancy 1st trimester Shellfish Allergy Cardiac Pacemaker Non-compatible Renal Failure GFR < 60 Claustrophobia 2 From Slide 23/Slide 57 Metallic Implants like older Pacemakers are an absolute contraindication due to the strong magnetic field. Allergy is an issue for Contrast not the MRI itself. Renal failure is an issue for Gadolinium not the MRI itself. 4. A patient with severe renal failure GFR < 30 requires neuroimaging. You specifically avoid Gadolinium-based contrast due to the risk of which condition? Anaphylaxis Lactic Acidosis Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis NSF Acute Tubular Necrosis Stevens-Johnson Syndrome 2 From Slide 27 Gadolinium in patients with GFR < 30 is associated with Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis NSF a rare but serious scleroderma-like fibrosing disease. 5. Ring- Enhancing Lesion on a contrast CT or MRI is classically associated with which of the following pathologies? Multiple Sclerosis Brain Abscess Ischemic Stroke Acute Meningioma Subdural Hematoma 1 From Slide 15 The classic differential for a Ring-Enhancing Lesion is Abscess or Glioblastoma/Metastasis . Meningiomas typically show diffuse homogeneous enhancement dural tail . First-line imaging for acute stroke? Non-contrast CT Head Radiology What does hyperdense white material on CT usually represent? Bone or Acute Blood Radiology Risk of Gadolinium in renal failure? Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis NSF Pathology Classic appearance of Abscess on contrast MRI? Ring-Enhancing Lesion Radiology Contraindication for MRI? Metal Implants Pacemaker Safety Best modality for Acute Ischemic Stroke most sensitive ? Diffusion Weighted MRI DWI Radiology Metformin management with Contrast? Hold for 48 hours Risk of Lactic Acidosis Pharm Appearance of Meningioma with contrast? Diffuse enhancement + Dural Tail Radiology Gold standard for Aneurysm/AVM? Conventional Angiography DSA Radiology Mechanism of CT? X-ray attenuation Hounsfield Units Physics FirstAid Neurology > Radiology Code Stroke Imaging Non-contrast CT is the first-line radiologic intervention for suspected stroke to rule out hemorrhage. CT Density Rule Hyperdense White = Bone Acute Blood Calcification.\\n Hypodense Black = Air Fat Water CSF. Metformin & Contrast Metformin must be withheld for 48 hours after iodinated contrast administration to prevent Lactic Acidosis . MRI Safety Strong magnetic fields can dislodge ferromagnetic objects. Pacemakers and metallic implants are absolute contraindications. Gadolinium & Renal Failure Avoid Gadolinium in patients with GFR < 30 due to the risk of Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis NSF . Ring-Enhancing Lesion Classic Differential MAGIC DR Metastasis Abscess Glioblastoma Infarct Contusion Demyelination Radiation. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l6", "title": "Lecture #6: Anatomy of the Spinal Cord", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l6 module Neuro l6 Lecture 6 Anatomy of the Spinal Cord\\n\\n Lecturer J. Xie Ph.D.\\n Session Neuroanatomy\\n\\n 1. Gross Anatomy of the Spinal Cord Slides 5-8 \\nThe spinal cord is a cylindrical structure that serves as the main information superhighway between the brain and the body. It is flattened dorsoventrally and resides within the protective vertebral column.\\nCrucially the spinal cord is shorter than the vertebral column. In adults it ends at the L1-L2 vertebral level forming a tapered tip called the Conus Medullaris . Below this level the spinal canal contains a collection of nerve roots called the Cauda Equina horse s tail floating in the Lumbar Cistern of CSF.\\n\\n 2. Meninges and Vascular Supply Slides 10-11 40-41 \\nThe spinal cord is wrapped in three meningeal layers \\n1. Dura Mater The tough outer layer. Unlike the cranial dura which is double-layered the spinal dura is single-layered . Between the dura and the vertebrae lies the Epidural Space a real space containing fat and veins Venous Plexus of Batson .\\n2. Arachnoid Mater The middle spider-web-like layer.\\n3. Pia Mater The delicate inner layer attached directly to the cord. It forms lateral projections called Denticulate Ligaments that anchor the cord to the dura.\\n\\n Blood Supply \\nThe cord is supplied by one Anterior Spinal Artery ASA and two Posterior Spinal Arteries PSA .\\n ASA Supplies the anterior 2/3 of the cord including the Ventral Horns/Motor and STT/Pain .\\n PSA Supplies the posterior 1/3 Dorsal Columns/Sensory .\\n\\n correlate\\n Anterior Spinal Artery ASA Syndrome \\n- Pathophysiology Infarction of the ASA often from aortic surgery or occlusion .\\n- Presentation Sudden onset of complete motor paralysis Corticospinal tract and loss of pain/temperature sensation Spinothalamic tract below the lesion.\\n- Key Distinction Proprioception and Vibration are SPARED because the Dorsal Columns are supplied by the Posterior Spinal Arteries.\\n \\n\\n 3. Internal Topography and Tracts Slides 29-39 \\nThe internal structure consists of a central \\ H-shaped\\ butterfly of Gray Matter cell bodies surrounded by White Matter axons .\\n\\n The Dorsal Column-Medial Lemniscus DCML Pathway\\nThis ascending pathway conveys fine touch vibration and proprioception .\\nPrimary afferents enter the cord and ascend ipsilaterally in the Dorsal Columns Fasciculus Gracilis for legs Cuneatus for arms . They do not synapse until they reach the Medulla Nucleus Gracilis/Cuneatus . There they decussate Internal Arcuate Fibers and ascend to the Thalamus.\\n\\n The Spinothalamic Tract STT - Anterolateral System\\nThis pathway conveys pain temperature and crude touch .\\nPrimary afferents enter via Lissauer s Tract and synapse immediately in the Dorsal Horn Substantia Gelatinosa . The second-order neurons decussate immediately via the Anterior White Commissure and ascend contralaterally.\\n\\n The Corticospinal Tract CST \\nThis is the primary descending motor pathway for voluntary movement.\\nFibers originate in the Motor Cortex descend through the brainstem and decussate in the Medulla Pyramids . They descend in the spinal cord as the Lateral Corticospinal Tract and synapse on Anterior Horn Cells Lower Motor Neurons .\\n\\n 4. Spinal Reflexes Slides 23-27 \\nReflexes are involuntary motor responses to stimuli mediated by local spinal circuits.\\n\\n Stretch Reflex Myotatic \\nThis is the simplest reflex e.g. Knee Jerk . It is Monosynaptic .\\n- Mechanism Muscle stretch stimulates specific sensory receptors Muscle Spindles → Ia afferent fires → Direct synapse on Alpha Motor Neuron → Muscle contracts.\\n- Function Maintains muscle tone and posture.\\n\\n Flexor Withdrawal & Crossed-Extensor Reflexes\\nThese are Polysynaptic .\\n- Flexor Withdrawal A painful stimulus invokes pulling the limb away Flexion .\\n- Crossed-Extensor Simultaneously the opposite limb extends to support body weight preventing falling .\\n\\n correlate\\n Tetanus \\n- Pathophysiology Clostridium tetani toxin Tetanospasmin travels retrogradely from a wound to the spinal cord. It cleaves SNARE proteins in Renshaw Cells inhibitory interneurons .\\n- Mechanism Prevents release of inhibitory neurotransmitters GABA and Glycine .\\n- Presentation Unchecked excitation of motor neurons leads to spastic paralysis lockjaw trismus and opisthotonos arching back .\\n \\n\\n 5. Clinical Correlates Additional \\n\\n correlate\\n Shingles Herpes Zoster \\n- Pathophysiology Reactivation of Varicella Zoster Virus VZV which lies dormant in the Dorsal Root Ganglia DRG .\\n- Presentation Painful vesicular rash in a specific dermatomal distribution does not cross midline .\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Disc Herniation Slides 14-15 \\n- Pathophysiology The soft Nucleus Pulposus herniates through a tear in the tough Annulus Fibrosus .\\n- Location Posterolateral herniation is most common and typically compresses the spinal nerve root below the level of the disc e.g. L4/L5 disc compresses L5 root .\\n- Presentation Radiculopathy shooting pain sensory loss and weakness in the affected nerve distribution.\\n Lecture 6 Anatomy of the Spinal Cord lecturer J. Xie Ph.D. Neuroanatomy session Lecture 6 prev l5 next l7 pdf pdfs/Lecture 6. J. Xie Ph.D. PPT .pdf mindmap Spinal Cord\\n Tracts\\n Ascending Sensory \\n DCML Touch/Prop. Ipsilateral in cord → Decussates Medulla\\n STT Pain/Temp. Contralateral in cord → Decussates Cord\\n Descending Motor \\n CST Motor Ipsilateral in cord → Decussates Medulla\\n Reflexes\\n Stretch Monosynaptic DTRs \\n Withdrawal Polysynaptic Pain \\n Pathology\\n ASA Syndrome Spares Dorsal Columns\\n Brown-Sequard Hemi-section\\n Ipsi Motor Proprioception\\n Contra Pain/Temp\\n Tetanus Blocks Inhibition questions 1. A patient presents with loss of pain and temperature sensation in the right leg and spastic paralysis and loss of vibration sense in the left leg. This Dissociated Sensory Loss pattern Brown-S\\u00e9quard Syndrome is caused by a hemisection lesion on which side of the spinal cord? Right side Ipsilateral to pain loss Left side Ipsilateral to motor/vibration loss Bilateral central lesion Dorsal Columns only Anterior Horn only 1 Brown-S\\u00e9quard Hemisection <br>- Ipsilateral signs Motor CST decussated above + Vibration DCML decussates above .<br>- Contralateral signs Pain/Temp STT decussates immediately . <br>Since motor/vibration loss is on the Left the lesion is on the Left . 2. Tetanus toxin causes spastic paralysis by blocking the release of inhibitory neurotransmitters GABA and Glycine from which specific spinal cord cells? Alpha Motor Neurons Gamma Motor Neurons Renshaw Cells Dorsal Root Ganglion Cells Clarke s Nucleus Cells 2 From Slide 22 Tetanospasmin blocks the release of GABA/Glycine from Renshaw Cells inhibitory interneurons leading to unchecked excitation tetany . 3. An infarction of the Anterior Spinal Artery ASA would result in all of the following deficits below the lesion level EXCEPT Loss of pain sensation STT Loss of temperature sensation STT Spastic Paralysis CST Loss of vibration and proprioception DCML Loss of crude touch 3 From Slide 41 The ASA supplies the anterior 2/3 of the cord STT CST . The Dorsal Columns DCML are supplied by the Posterior Spinal Arteries and are SPARED in ASA syndrome. 4. Which spinal tract carries fine touch and proprioception and ascends ipsilaterally within the spinal cord? Lateral Corticospinal Tract Spinothalamic Tract Dorsal Column Medial Lemniscus DCML Rubrospinal Tract Vestibulospinal Tract 2 The DCML enters the cord and ascends in the posterior columns on the SAME side ipsilateral until it reaches the medulla where it decussates. 5. In the stretch reflex e.g. Knee Jerk the primary afferent neuron synapses directly onto the alpha-motor neuron. This type of reflex is classified as Polysynaptic Disynaptic Monosynaptic Intersegmental Contralateral 2 From Slide 24 The stretch reflex is the simplest reflex and is Monosynaptic one synapse between sensory and motor neuron . Function of Dorsal Column Medial Lemniscus DCML ? Fine Touch Vibration Proprioception Neuro Function of Spinothalamic Tract STT ? Pain and Temperature Neuro Function of Corticospinal Tract CST ? Voluntary Motor Control Neuro Where does DCML decussate? Medulla Internal Arcuate Fibers Neuro Where does STT decussate? Spinal Cord Anterior White Commissure Neuro Where does CST decussate? Medulla Pyramids Neuro What is spard in Anterior Spinal Artery Syndrome? Dorsal Columns Proprioception/Vibration Pathology Virus dormant in DRG causing Shingles? Varicella Zoster Virus VZV Micro Toxin blocking Renshaw Cells GABA/Glycine ? Tetanus Toxin Micro Reflex level for Patellar Knee Jerk? L3-L4 Clinical BandB Neurology > Somatosensory Systems Lumbar Puncture LP Because the spinal cord ends at L1-L2 a Lumbar Puncture is safely performed at the L3-L4 or L4-L5 interspace to avoid damaging the cord. Vascular Watershed The mid-thoracic region T4-T8 is a vascular watershed area and is most susceptible to infarction during hypotension. DCML Rule Lesions in the spinal cord affect the DCML Ipsilaterally because it doesn t cross until the medulla . STT Rule Lesions in the spinal cord affect the STT Contralaterally typically 1-2 segments below the lesion due to Lissauer s tract ascent . CST Rule Lesions in the spinal cord cause Ipsilateral Spastic Paralysis UMN signs below the lesion because it already crossed in the medulla . Renshaw Cells Renshaw cells are the \\ brakes\\ of the motor system. Without them e.g. Tetanus toxin blockade motor neurons fire uncontrollably. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l7", "title": "Lecture #7: Internal Anatomy of Brain Stem I", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l7 module Neuro l7 Lecture 7 Internal Anatomy of Brain Stem I\\n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D.\\n Session Neuroanatomy\\n\\n 1. Overview of Brainstem Divisions Slides 4-5 \\nThe brainstem is divided into three major regions Rostral to Caudal \\n1. Midbrain Mesencephalon The most superior portion.\\n2. Pons Metencephalon The middle bulbous portion.\\n3. Medulla Myelencephalon The most inferior portion continuous with the spinal cord.\\n\\n 2. External Anatomy Ventral View Slide 6 \\n Midbrain Characterized by the Cerebral Peduncles Crus Cerebri massive stalks containing descending motor fibers from the cortex.\\n Pons Characterized by the Basilar Pons a bulbous structure with transverse fibers.\\n Medulla \\n Pyramids Two medial columns containing the Corticospinal Tract .\\n Pyramidal Decussation The crossing point of motor fibers at the caudal medulla border with spinal cord .\\n Olive A lateral bump produced by the Inferior Olivary Nucleus motor coordination .\\n\\n 3. External Anatomy Dorsal View Slide 7-8 \\n Midbrain Tectum \\n Superior Colliculi Visual reflexes/saccades.\\n Inferior Colliculi Auditory pathway.\\n Pons \\n Cerebellar Peduncles \\n Superior SCP Main output from cerebellum.\\n Middle MCP Connects Pons to Cerebellum massive input .\\n Inferior ICP Connects Medulla to Cerebellum.\\n Floor of 4th Ventricle The open posterior aspect.\\n Medulla \\n Gracile Tubercle Medial bump Leg sensory .\\n Cuneate Tubercle Lateral bump Arm sensory .\\n Obex The caudal point where the 4th ventricle closes.\\n\\n 4. Internal Anatomy Cross-Sectional Zones Slide 10 \\nIn cross-section the brainstem is divided anterior-to-posterior \\n1. Basilar Region Ventral Contains descending motor tracts Corticospinal Corticobulbar .\\n2. Tegmentum Middle The \\ core\\ containing Cranial Nerve Nuclei Reticular Formation and sensory tracts.\\n3. Tectum Dorsal Found only in the Midbrain Colliculi .\\n\\n 5. Key Nuclei by Level Slides 15-26 \\n\\n Medulla\\n Gracile & Cuneate Nuclei Dorsal column nuclei Touch/Proprioception .\\n Inferior Olivary Nucleus Large crumpled sac appearance. Motor learning Climbing fibers to cerebellum .\\n Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus Pain/Temp for the face extends down to C2 .\\n\\n Pons\\n Pontine Nuclei Scattered in the basilar pons. Relay cortex information to the cerebellum via the MCP.\\n Facial Nucleus CN VII Motor to face.\\n Abducens Nucleus CN VI Lateral eye movement.\\n\\n Midbrain\\n Substantia Nigra Dark pigmented line Dopamine . Degenerates in Parkinson s.\\n Red Nucleus Motor coordination Rubrospinal tract .\\n Periaqueductal Gray PAG Pain modulation Opioid receptors .\\n Superior & Inferior Colliculi. \\n\\n correlate\\n Cranial Nerve Exit Points Rule of 4s \\n Midbrain CN 3 4 Oculomotor interpeduncular fossa Trochlear dorsal exit! .\\n Pons CN 5 6 7 8 Trigeminal lateral pons Abducens/Facial/Vestibulocochlear pontomedullary junction .\\n Medulla CN 9 10 11 12 Glossopharyngeal/Vagus/Accessory retro-olivary Hypoglossal pre-olivary sulcus .\\n Lecture 7 Internal Anatomy of Brain Stem I lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D. Neuroanatomy session Lecture 7 prev l6 next l8 pdf pdfs/Lecture 7. I. Kurtzer Ph.D. PPT .pdf mindmap Brainstem Anatomy I\\n Midbrain\\n Ventral Cerebral Peduncles Motor \\n Dorsal Tectum Sup/Inf Colliculi \\n Internal Aqueduct Substantia Nigra Red Nucleus\\n CN III IV\\n Pons\\n Ventral Basilar Pons Transverse fibers \\n Dorsal 4th Ventricle Floor\\n Internal Pontine Nuclei MCP\\n CN V VI VII VIII\\n Medulla\\n Ventral Pyramids Olive\\n Dorsal Gracile/Cuneate Tubercles\\n Internal Inf. Olive DC Nuclei\\n CN IX X XI XII questions 1. Which structure is a hallmark feature of the dorsal midbrain? Pyramids Inferior Olive Superior Colliculus Fourth Ventricle Gracile Tubercle 2 The Superior Colliculus is part of the Tectum found on the dorsal aspect of the midbrain. The Pyramids and Olive are ventral medulla structures. 2. The pyramidal decussation marks the caudal boundary of which brainstem division? Midbrain Pons Medulla Diencephalon Spinal Cord 2 The Pyramidal Decussation is found at the junction of the Medulla and the spinal cord. 3. Which cranial nerve is the only one to exit from the dorsal aspect of the brainstem? CN III Oculomotor CN IV Trochlear CN V Trigeminal CN VI Abducens CN VII Facial 1 CN IV Trochlear exits dorsally from the midbrain tectum. All other cranial nerves exit ventrally or laterally. 4. Damage to the Substantia Nigra in the midbrain is most commonly associated with which disorder? Huntington s Disease Alzheimer s Disease Parkinson s Disease Multiple Sclerosis Myasthenia Gravis 2 The Substantia Nigra contains dopaminergic neurons. Their degeneration leads to the motor symptoms of Parkinson s Disease . 5. Which ventricular space is located between the Pons/Medulla and the Cerebellum? Lateral Ventricle Third Ventricle Cerebral Aqueduct Fourth Ventricle Central Canal 3 The Fourth Ventricle is the open space bounded anteriorly by the Pons/Medulla and posteriorly by the Cerebellum. Three divisions of Brainstem Rostral→Caudal ? Midbrain → Pons → Medulla Anatomy Hallmark dorsal structure of Midbrain? Tectum Corpora Quadrigemina / Colliculi Anatomy Ventral columns of Medulla contain? Corticospinal Tract Pyramids Anatomy Only CN to exit dorsally? CN IV Trochlear Anatomy What marks the Medulla-Spinal Cord border? Pyramidal Decussation Anatomy Function of Inferior Colliculus? Auditory Processing Physiology Function of Superior Colliculus? Visual/Saccadic Eye Movements Physiology Function of Substantia Nigra? Dopamine production Motor Control Physiology Nucleus responsible for Olive bump? Inferior Olivary Nucleus Anatomy Ventricular space of the Midbrain? Cerebral Aqueduct Anatomy Tectum Location The Tectum Roof exists ONLY in the Midbrain . It consists of the Superior and Inferior Colliculi. Cerebral Aqueduct The Cerebral Aqueduct is a hallmark of the Midbrain . The 4th Ventricle is associated with the Pons and Medulla. Decussation Rule The Pyramidal Decussation crossing of motor fibers marks the transition from Medulla to Spinal Cord. CN IV Uniqueness The Trochlear Nerve CN IV is the ONLY cranial nerve to exit from the Dorsal aspect of the brainstem. Inferior Olive Function The Inferior Olive Medulla is a MAJOR source of input to the cerebellum Climbing Fibers for motor learning. Substantia Nigra Located in the Midbrain cerebral peduncles. Contains Dopaminergic neurons. Loss = Parkinson s Disease. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l8", "title": "Lecture 8: Internal Anatomy of Brain Stem II - Major Tracts and Cranial Nerve Nuclei", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l8 module Neuro l8 Lecture 8 Internal Anatomy of Brain Stem II - Major Tracts and Cranial Nerve Nuclei\\n\\n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 8\\n\\n 1. Major Tracts of the Brainstem Slide 3-17 \\n\\nThe brainstem serves as a major conduit for ascending and descending pathways connecting the spinal cord cerebellum and cerebrum. Understanding the relative positions of these tracts at different levels Midbrain Pons Medulla is crucial for localizing lesions.\\n\\n Descending Tracts\\n Corticospinal Tract \\n Function Voluntary motor control.\\n Pathway Descends from the cerebral cortex.\\n Trajectory It is always the most ventral structure throughout the brainstem.\\n Decussation Most fibers decussate at the caudal medulla pyramidal decussation to form the lateral corticospinal tract in the spinal cord.\\n\\n Ascending Tracts\\n Dorsal Column-Medial Lemniscus DCML \\n Function Fine touch vibration and proprioception for the body.\\n Pathway \\n Spinal Cord Ascends centrally as the Dorsal Columns Gracile and Cuneate fasciculi .\\n Medulla Synapses in the Dorsal Column Nuclei Gracile and Cuneate nuclei .\\n Decussation Axons form Internal Arcuate Fibers which decussate in the caudal medulla Slide 16 .\\n Brainstem Ascent Ascends as the Medial Lemniscus .\\n Trajectory It is always medial to the spinothalamic tract. Its orientation changes from vertical medulla to horizontal midbrain as it ascends.\\n Spinothalamic Tract Anterolateral System \\n Function Pain and temperature sensation.\\n Pathway Decussates immediately in the spinal cord.\\n Trajectory Ascends through the brainstem in a lateral position. It remains lateral to the DCML at all levels Slide 17 .\\n\\n 2. Cerebellar Peduncles Slide 18-20 \\n\\nThe cerebellum communicates with the brainstem via three paired peduncles located at specific levels \\n\\n Inferior Cerebellar Peduncle ICP \\n Level Medulla and lower Pons.\\n Function Primarily afferent input to the cerebellum from the spinal cord e.g. dorsal spinocerebellar tract and medulla.\\n Middle Cerebellar Peduncle MCP \\n Level Pons.\\n Function Entirely afferent input from the pontine nuclei corticopontine pathway . It is the largest peduncle.\\n Superior Cerebellar Peduncle SCP \\n Level Upper Pons and lower Midbrain.\\n Function Primarily efferent output from the deep cerebellar nuclei to the red nucleus and thalamus.\\n\\n 3. Cranial Nerve Nuclei Organization Slide 22-26 \\n\\nThe organization of cranial nerve nuclei follows a rational developmental logic based on the neural tube plates alar = sensory basal = motor .\\n\\n Functional Columns\\nNuclei are arranged in longitudinal columns. A general rule for the brainstem is Motor is Medial Sensory is Lateral .\\n\\n Motor Columns Medial to Lateral \\n 1. Somatic Motor GSE Most medial near the midline. Innervates myotome-derived muscles tongue eye .\\n Nuclei Oculomotor III Trochlear IV Abducens VI Hypoglossal XII .\\n 2. Branchial Motor SVE Ventrolateral. Innervates pharyngeal arch muscles.\\n Nuclei Trigeminal Motor V Facial Motor VII Nucleus Ambiguus IX X Spinal Accessory XI .\\n 3. Visceral Motor GVE Parasympathetic.\\n Nuclei Edinger-Westphal III Superior Salivatory VII Inferior Salivatory IX Dorsal Motor Nucleus of Vagus X .\\n\\n Sensory Columns Medial to Lateral \\n 1. Visceral Sensory GVA/SVA \\n Nuclei Nucleus Solitarius VII IX X - taste and cardiorespiratory .\\n 2. Somatic Sensory GSA General sensation from face.\\n Nuclei Trigeminal Nuclei Mesencephalic Pontine Spinal .\\n 3. Special Sensory SSA Most lateral.\\n Nuclei Vestibular and Cochlear Nuclei VIII .\\n\\n mnemonic\\n Cranial Nerves Sensory/Motor/Both \\n \\ Some Say Marry Money But My Brother Says Big Brains Matter More\\ \\n\\n S ome I - Sensory\\n S ay II - Sensory\\n M arry III - Motor\\n M oney IV - Motor\\n B ut V - Both\\n M y VI - Motor\\n B rother VII - Both\\n S ays VIII - Sensory\\n B ig IX - Both\\n B rains X - Both\\n M atter XI - Motor\\n M ore XII - Motor\\n Lecture 8 Internal Anatomy of Brain Stem II - Major Tracts and Cranial Nerve Nuclei Neuroscience questions A patient presents with loss of vibration and proprioception on the left side of the body. A lesion is located in the medulla. Which structure is most likely affected? Left Nucleus Gracilis/Cuneate Right Medial Lemniscus Left Medial Lemniscus Right Spinothalamic Tract Left Lateral Corticospinal Tract 1 The DCML decussates in the caudal medulla as internal arcuate fibers. After decussation the fibers ascend as the Medial Lemniscus on the contralateral side. Therefore a lesion in the medulla affecting the Right Medial Lemniscus would cause loss of vibration/proprioception on the Left side. Which cranial nerve nucleus column is located most laterally in the brainstem? Somatic Motor Branchial Motor Visceral Motor Somatic Sensory Special Sensory 4 Following the Motor Medial Sensory Lateral rule the Special Sensory column hearing and balance CN VIII is the most lateral column. The Middle Cerebellar Peduncle conveys fibers primarily from which location? Spinal Cord Medulla Pons Midbrain Deep Cerebellar Nuclei 2 The Middle Cerebellar Peduncle MCP connects the Pons to the Cerebellum and conveys afferent fibers from pontine nuclei. Where does the Corticospinal tract decussate? Midbrain Upper Pons Lower Pons Upper Medulla Caudal Medulla 4 The Corticospinal tract descends ventrally and decussates at the Pyramids in the Caudal lower Medulla. Which nucleus provides parasympathetic innervation to the pupil constriction ? Oculomotor Nucleus Edinger-Westphal Nucleus Trochlear Nucleus Abducens Nucleus Superior Salivatory Nucleus 1 The Edinger-Westphal nucleus is the Visceral Motor parasympathetic component of CN III Oculomotor responsible for pupil constriction. Decussation level of Corticospinal Tract Caudal Medulla pyramids Neuroanatomy Decussation level of DCML Caudal Medulla internal arcuate fibers Neuroanatomy Decussation level of Spinothalamic Tract Spinal Cord anterior white commissure Neuroanatomy Relative position of DCML vs Spinothalamic in brainstem DCML is always MEDIAL to Spinothalamic Neuroanatomy Brainstem level of Inferior Cerebellar Peduncle Medulla and Lower Pons Neuroanatomy Brainstem level of Middle Cerebellar Peduncle Pons Neuroanatomy Brainstem level of Superior Cerebellar Peduncle Upper Pons and Lower Midbrain Neuroanatomy General rule for CN Nuclei location Medial-Lateral Motor is Medial Sensory is Lateral Neuroanatomy Nucleus responsible for taste CN VII IX X Nucleus Solitarius Gustatory nucleus Neuroanatomy Nucleus supplying pharyngeal muscles swallowing Nucleus Ambiguus CN IX X XI Neuroanatomy BandB Neurology > Brainstem > Brainstem Anatomy FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Brainstem Pathoma CNS Tract Trajectories Rule The Corticospinal tract is always the most ventral structure. The DCML is always medial to the Spinothalamic tract. The Spinothalamic tract is always lateral . Medial vs. Lateral Rule In the brainstem cranial nerve nuclei organization Motor is Medial Sensory is Lateral . Decussation Levels Corticospinal Caudal Medulla Pyramids .\\\\n DCML Caudal Medulla Internal Arcuate Fibers .\\\\n Spinothalamic Spinal Cord immediately on entry . Peduncle Levels Inferior Medulla/Lower Pons Afferent .\\\\n Middle Pons Afferent .\\\\n Superior Upper Pons/Midbrain Efferent . Nucleus Ambiguus The Nucleus Ambiguus supplies branchial motor innervation to the pharynx and larynx swallowing gag reflex via CN IX and X. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l9", "title": "Lecture 9: Reticular Formation", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l9 module Neuro l9 Lecture 9 Reticular Formation\\n\\n Lecturer J. Xie Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 9\\n\\n 1. Reticular Formation Overview Slide 6-7 \\n\\nThe Reticular Formation RF is a phylogenetically old set of nuclei located throughout the brainstem core extending from the caudal medulla to the lateral hypothalamus. It is not a single anatomical structure but a diffuse network responsible for \\ life-sustaining\\ functions.\\n\\n Structure Composed of short interneurons reflexes and long projection neurons neuromodulation .\\n Key Concept A single neuron can synapse with 25 000+ others allowing diffuse modulation of the entire CNS.\\n\\n 2. Neurochemistry of the Reticular Formation Slide 8-13 \\n\\nThe RF houses the nuclei for major neuromodulatory systems. You MUST know the location and neurotransmitter for each \\n\\n Serotonin 5-HT \\n Nuclei Raphe Nuclei midline of entire brainstem .\\n Functions Mood Arousal Pain modulation Raphe Magnus Sleep.\\n Norepinephrine NE \\n Nuclei Locus Coeruleus pons/midbrain junction and Lateral Tegmental Area.\\n Functions Arousal RAS Attention \\ Fight or Flight\\ Pain modulation.\\n Dopamine DA \\n Nuclei Ventral Tegmental Area VTA and Substantia Nigra Midbrain .\\n Pathways \\n Nigrostriatal Coordination of movement degenerate in Parkinson s .\\n Mesolimbic Reward/Addiction Schizophrenia positive symptoms .\\n Mesocortical Cognition Schizophrenia negative symptoms .\\n Acetylcholine ACh \\n Nuclei Pedunculopontine Tegmental Nucleus PPT Brainstem and Nucleus Basalis of Meynert Forebrain .\\n Functions Arousal REM sleep generation Memory degenerate in Alzheimer s .\\n Histamine \\n Nuclei Tuberomammillary Nucleus Posterior Hypothalamus .\\n Function Arousal/Wakefulness Antihistamines cause drowsiness .\\n\\n 3. Reticular Activating System ARAS & Consciousness Slide 16-24 \\n\\nThe Ascending Reticular Activating System ARAS projects to the cortex to maintain Wakefulness and Alertness .\\n\\n Disorders of Consciousness\\n Coma Low arousal no awareness. Dysfunction of ARAS or bilateral cortex.\\n Persistent Vegetative State \\ Unresponsive Wakefulness\\ . Arousal is present eyes open sleep-wake cycles but NO awareness of environment.\\n Minimally Conscious State Intermittent inconsistent awareness.\\n\\n correlate\\n Locked-in Syndrome \\n Pathophysiology Ischemic stroke of the Ventral Pons Basilar Artery .\\n Presentation Quadriplegia corticospinal disruption and anarthria corticobulbar disruption .\\n Key Feature The ARAS is spared tegmentum so the patient is Fully Awake and Aware but cannot move.\\n Communication Vertical eye movements controlled by midbrain and blinking are usually preserved.\\n \\n\\n 4. Sleep Physiology Slide 25-33 \\n\\n Stages \\n N1-N2 Light sleep Sleep Spindles K-Complexes .\\n N3 Slow Wave Sleep Delta waves - Deep restorative sleep.\\n REM Rapid Eye Movement dreaming atonia. EEG looks like \\ Awake\\ Beta/Sawtooth .\\n Neurochemistry \\n Wakefulness Promoters NE 5-HT Histamine ACh Hypocretin Orexin .\\n Sleep Promoters GABA VLPO nucleus and Melatonin.\\n\\n correlate\\n Narcolepsy \\n Pathophysiology Loss of Hypocretin Orexin neurons in the lateral hypothalamus.\\n Presentation \\n Excessive Daytime Sleepiness. \\n Cataplexy Sudden loss of muscle tone triggered by emotion.\\n Sleep Paralysis and Hypnagogic Hallucinations .\\n Treatment Stimulants Amphetamines to increase NE/DA.\\n \\n\\n 5. Descending Motor & Pain Control Slide 37-39 \\n\\n Motor Rigidity Posturing \\n Decorticate Posturing Flexion of upper limbs. Lesion Above Red Nucleus midbrain . Rubrospinal tract intact.\\n Decerebrate Posturing Extension of all limbs. Lesion Below Red Nucleus . Vestibulospinal tracts dominate. Worse prognosis.\\n\\n Descending Pain Modulation\\nThe brainstem can inhibit incoming pain signals Pain Inhibits Pain .\\n Pathway Periaqueductal Gray PAG → Rostral Ventromedial Medulla RVM Raphe Magnus → Dorsal Horn block pain transmission via Enkephalins/Serotonin/NE .\\n Clinical Antidepressants like SnRIs Duloxetine or TCAs Amitriptyline treat chronic pain by boosting NE/5-HT in this inhibitory pathway. Lecture 9 Reticular Formation Neuroscience questions A patient presents with locked-in syndrome following a stroke. They are fully aware but cannot move their limbs or speak. Which structure is most likely spared preserving their consciousness? Basis Pontis Corticospinal Tracts Reticular Activating System Tegmentum Medullary Pyramids Internal Capsule 2 Locked-in syndrome is caused by a lesion to the ventral pons basis pontis destroying corticospinal and corticobulbar tracts. Consciousness is preserved because the Reticular Activating System ARAS located in the dorsal tegmentum is spared. Which neurotransmitter is synthesized primarily in the Locus Coeruleus? Serotonin Dopamine Acetylcholine Norepinephrine Histamine 3 The Locus Coeruleus is the primary site of Norepinephrine NE synthesis in the brainstem involved in arousal and attention. A comatose patient exhibits abnormal posturing with extension of both upper and lower extremities to painful stimuli. This Decerebrate rigidity suggests a lesion at which level? Cortex Above the Red Nucleus At or Below the Red Nucleus Spinal Cord Thalamus 2 Decerebrate rigidity extension of all limbs implies a lesion at or below the Red Nucleus midbrain/upper pons removing rubrospinal influence and leaving vestibulospinal extension unchecked. A 25-year-old male complains of excessive daytime sleepiness and episodes of sudden muscle weakness when laughing cataplexy . A deficiency in which neuropeptide is likely? Serotonin GABA Hypocretin Orexin Dopamine Melatonin 2 Narcolepsy with cataplexy is classically caused by a loss of Hypocretin Orexin neurons in the lateral hypothalamus. Which drug class treats chronic neuropathic pain by enhancing the descending pain inhibitory pathway from the brainstem? SSRIs Fluoxetine Benzodiazepines SNRIs Duloxetine Dopamine Agonists Antihistamines 2 SNRIs like Duloxetine and TCAs are effective for neuropathic pain because they inhibit reuptake of Norepinephrine and Serotonin enhancing the descending inhibitory signals from the RVM/PAG to the spinal cord. Location of Serotonin 5-HT cell bodies Raphe Nuclei Neurochemistry Location of Norepinephrine NE cell bodies Locus Coeruleus Neurochemistry Location of Dopamine DA cell bodies Midbrain Substantia Nigra & Ventral Tegmental Area VTA Neurochemistry Location of Histamine cell bodies Tuberomammillary Nucleus Hypothalamus Neurochemistry Lesion location for Decorticate Rigidity Above the Red Nucleus Midbrain Neurology Lesion location for Decerebrate Rigidity At or Below the Red Nucleus Neurology Which is worse Decorticate or Decerebrate? Decerebrate indicates deeper brainstem damage Neurology Neurotransmitter deficiency in Narcolepsy Hypocretin Orexin Neurology Area spared in Locked-in Syndrome Reticular Activating System Tegmentum & Vertical Eye Movements Neurology Structure responsible for Descending Pain Modulation Periaqueductal Gray PAG Neurology BandB Neurology > Brainstem > Brainstem Anatomy FirstAid Neurology > Pathology > Coma & Sleep Pathoma CNS Decorticate vs. Decerebrate Decorticate Flexor posturing Hands to C ore . Lesion Above Red Nucleus.\\\\n Decerebrate Extensor posturing worse . Lesion Below Red Nucleus. Locating Neurotransmitters Raphe Nuclei = Serotonin 5-HT .\\\\n Locus Coeruleus = Norepinephrine NE .\\\\n Substantia Nigra = Dopamine DA .\\\\n Tuberomammillary Nucleus = Histamine. Locked-in Syndrome Caused by a Ventral Pontine stroke Basilar artery . The ARAS is spared Tegmentum so the patient is fully awake/aware but quadriplegic. Can only blink or move eyes vertically. Narcolepsy Mechanism Caused by a deficiency of Hypocretin Orexin producing neurons in the Lateral Hypothalamus . Pain Modulation Pathway PAG Midbrain → RVM Medulla → Spinal Cord Dorsal Horn. Mediated by Opioids Serotonin and NE. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l10", "title": "Lecture 10: Physiology of the Autonomic Nervous System", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l10 module Neuro l10 Lecture 10 Physiology of the Autonomic Nervous System\\n\\n Lecturer V. Grubisic M.D. Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 10\\n\\n 1. ANS Organization Slide 2-16 \\n\\nThe Autonomic Nervous System ANS maintains homeostasis through two opposing divisions Sympathetic \\ Fight or Flight\\ and Parasympathetic \\ Rest and Digest\\ . Both systems utilize a two-neuron chain preganglionic and postganglionic .\\n\\n Sympathetic Division\\n Origin Thoracolumbar T1-L2 lateral horn.\\n Neuron Lengths Short Preganglionic Long Postganglionic.\\n Neurotransmitters \\n Preganglionic Acetylcholine Nicotinic receptors .\\n Postganglionic Norepinephrine Adrenergic receptors mostly.\\n Exceptions Sweat glands ACh/Muscarinic and Adrenal Medulla Epi/NE directly into blood .\\n\\n Parasympathetic Division\\n Origin Craniosacral CN III VII IX X and S2-S4 .\\n Neuron Lengths Long Preganglionic Short Postganglionic ganglia near target .\\n Neurotransmitters \\n Preganglionic Acetylcholine Nicotinic .\\n Postganglionic Acetylcholine Muscarinic .\\n\\n 2. Clinical Correlates of ANS Dysfunction\\n\\n Horner s Syndrome Slide 14 \\nLesion of the sympathetic pathway to the head/neck.\\n Triad Ptosis droopy eyelid Miosis constricted pupil Anhidrosis lack of sweating .\\n\\n Autonomic Dysreflexia Slide 28 \\n Cause Spinal Cord Injury above T6 .\\n Mechanism A noxious stimulus e.g. full bladder triggers a massive sympathetic surge below the lesion.\\n Consequence Widespread vasoconstriction causes life-threatening Hypertension .\\n Reflex Baroreceptors detect high BP → Vagus nerve slows heart → Bradycardia .\\n Result Hypertension + Bradycardia.\\n\\n Bladder Dysfunction Slide 31-32 \\n Spastic UMN Bladder Lesion above Pontine Micturition Center PMC but below cortex or between PMC and cord. Reflex is hyperactive. Detrusor contracts against closed sphincter.\\n Atonic LMN Bladder Lesion of Sacral cord or Cauda Equina. Reflex arc destroyed. Bladder fills but cannot contract overflow incontinence .\\n\\n 3. Enteric Nervous System ENS Slide 33-37 \\n\\nThe \\ Brain of the Gut\\ capable of functioning independently.\\n Plexuses Myenteric Auerbach s - Motility and Submucosal Meissner s - Secretion .\\n\\n correlate\\n Hirschsprung s Disease \\n Pathophysiology Congenital failure of Neural Crest Cells to migrate to the distal colon.\\n Presentation Newborns fail to pass meconium.\\n Result Aganglionic Megacolon . The affected segment lacks ENS plexuses and remains constantly constricted cannot relax causing obstruction and dilation upstream.\\n Treatment Surgical resection of the aganglionic segment.\\n \\n\\n 4. Cardiovascular Reflexes Slide 27 \\n\\n Carotid Sinus Massage Mechanically stimulates baroreceptors → mimics high BP → increases Vagal tone → slows Heart Rate. Used to terminate SVT.\\n highyield\\n Contraindication Do not perform Carotid Massage in patients with carotid stenosis or recent stroke as it may dislodge an embolus.\\n Lecture 10 Physiology of the Autonomic Nervous System Neuroscience questions A patient with a T4 spinal cord injury develops a pounding headache and profuse sweating. Their blood pressure is 210/110 mmHg and heart rate is 50 bpm. What is the most likely physiological mechanism? Hypovolemic Shock Autonomic Dysreflexia Septic Shock Orthostatic Hypotension Vasovagal Syncope 1 This is classic Autonomic Dysreflexia a noxious stimulus triggers a sympathetic surge below the lesion Hypertension which triggers a baroreceptor reflex to slow the heart Bradycardia . Which neurotransmitter is released by sympathetic postganglionic neurons innervating sweat glands? Norepinephrine Epinephrine Acetylcholine Dopamine Serotonin 2 Sweat glands are the exception to the rule. Sympathetic postganglionic neurons innervating them release Acetylcholine acting on Muscarinic receptors. A newborn fails to pass meconium within 48 hours. Imaging reveals a dilated colon proximal to a narrowed distal segment. Biopsy of the narrowed segment would likely show Hypertrophy of the muscularis externa Absence of Myenteric and Submucosal ganglia Inflammation of the mucosa Excessive Neural Crest Cell migration Presence of Lewy Bodies 1 Hirschsprung s Disease is characterized by the absence of ganglia aganglionosis in the distal colon due to failed neural crest migration. Horner s Syndrome is characterized by which constellation of signs? Mydriasis Ptosis Anhidrosis Miosis Exophthalmos Hyperhidrosis Miosis Ptosis Anhidrosis Mydriasis Exophthalmos Anhidrosis Ptosis Miosis Hyperhidrosis 2 Horner s Syndrome presents with Miosis constriction Ptosis drooping and Anhidrosis dryness on the ipsilateral side. Where are the cell bodies of preganglionic sympathetic neurons located? Brainstem Nuclei Sacral Spinal Cord S2-S4 Thoracolumbar Lateral Horn T1-L2 Dorsal Root Ganglia Paravertebral Ganglia 2 The sympathetic division is Thoracolumbar meaning preganglionic neurons originate in the lateral horn of the spinal cord from T1 to L2. Location of Sympathetic Preganglionic Neurons Thoracolumbar Lateral Horn T1-L2 Anatomy Location of Parasympathetic Preganglionic Neurons Craniosacral Brainstem + S2-S4 Anatomy Neurotransmitter of ALL Preganglionic Neurons Acetylcholine Nicotinic Receptor Physiology Neurotransmitter of Sympathetic Postganglionic Neurons Target Norepinephrine Adrenergic Receptor Physiology Neurotransmitter of Parasympathetic Postganglionic Neurons Target Acetylcholine Muscarinic Receptor Physiology Triad of Horner s Syndrome Ptosis Miosis Anhidrosis Pathology Condition causing Aganglionic Megacolon Hirschsprung s Disease Pathology Signs of Autonomic Dysreflexia Hypertension + Bradycardia + Headache/Sweating Pathology Spinal cord level risk for Autonomic Dysreflexia Above T6 Pathology Bladder type in Lower Motor Neuron Sacral lesion Atonic Flaccid Bladder Pathology BandB Neurology > Autonomic Nervous System > ANS Pharmacology FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Autonomic Nervous System Sympathetic vs. Parasympathetic Lengths Sympathetic Short Pre Long Post.\\\\n Parasympathetic Long Pre Short Post. Horner s Triad Ptosis Muller s muscle paralysis Miosis Dilator pupillae paralysis Anhidrosis Sudomotor loss . Autonomic Dysreflexia Triad In SCI > T6 Hypertension Sympathetic surge + Bradycardia Baroreceptor reflex + Headache . Hirschsprung s Mechanism Failed migration of Neural Crest Cells . Result Aganglionic segment that cannot relax constricted causing upstream dilation megacolon . Sympathetic Exceptions Most sympathetic postganglionics release NE. Exceptions Sweat glands ACh and Adrenal Medulla Epi/NE directly . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l11", "title": "Lecture 11: Hypothalamus", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l11 module Neuro l11 Lecture 11 Hypothalamus\\n\\n Lecturer V. Grubisic M.D. Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 11\\n\\n 1. Hypothalamus Overview Slide 6-8 \\n\\nThe Hypothalamus is the master control center for Homeostasis regulating the Autonomic Nervous System Endocrine System and Limbic System.\\nMnemonic for Functions TAN HATS \\n T hirst and Water Balance\\n A denohypophysis Control\\n N eurohypophysis Control\\n H unger\\n A utonomic Regulation\\n T emperature Regulation\\n S exual urges / S leep\\n\\n 2. Major Nuclei and Functions Slide 9-16 \\n\\n Paraventricular Nucleus PVN Produces Oxytocin ADH TRH CRH. Projects to autonomic centers.\\n Supraoptic Nucleus SON Produces mainly ADH Vasopressin and Oxytocin.\\n Suprachiasmatic Nucleus SCN Master Circadian Clock . Regulated by light input from retina.\\n Anterior Hypothalamus Cooling Parasympathetic . Lesion → Hyperthermia. \\ A/C = Anterior Cooling\\ .\\n Posterior Hypothalamus Heating Sympathetic . Lesion → Hypothermia Poikilothermia .\\n Mammillary Bodies Episodic Memory. Input from Hippocampus Fornix .\\n\\n correlate\\n Wernicke-Korsakoff Syndrome \\n Cause Thiamine Vitamin B1 deficiency often due to chronic alcoholism.\\n Pathology Degeneration of the Mammillary Bodies and Medial Dorsal Thalamus.\\n Triad Confusion Ataxia Ophthalmoplegia Wernicke s Encephalopathy .\\n Sequelae Korsakoff s Psychosis Anterograde/Retrograde Amnesia + Confabulation if untreated.\\n \\n\\n 3. Feeding Regulation Slide 19-22 \\n\\n Lateral Hypothalamus Hunger Center . Lesion → Anorexia \\ Lateral makes you Lean\\ .\\n Ventromedial Nucleus Satiety Center . Lesion → Hyperphagia and Obesity \\ Ventromedial makes you Very Massive\\ .\\n Hormones \\n Leptin From adipose tissue. Signals satiety inhibits Lateral stimulates Ventromedial .\\n Ghrelin From stomach. Signals hunger.\\n\\n 4. Water Balance Disorders Slide 12 \\n\\n Diabetes Insipidus DI \\n Neurogenic Lack of ADH secretion Hypothalamic/Posterior Pituitary damage .\\n Sxs Polyuria Polydipsia Dilute Urine.\\n SIADH Syndrome of Inappropriate ADH \\n Cause Ectopic production Small Cell Lung Cancer CNS disorders Drugs.\\n Sxs Water retention Hyponatremia Cerebral Edema.\\n\\n highyield\\n Mnemonic Feeding Centers \\n L ateral = L ean Hunger Center; lesion causes starvation .\\n V entromedial = V ery M assive Satiety Center; lesion causes obesity .\\n Lecture 11 Hypothalamus Neuroscience questions A chronic alcoholic presents with confusion ataxia and nystagmus. MRI reveals atrophy of the Mammillary Bodies. What is the most likely diagnosis? Alzheimer s Disease Parkinson s Disease Wernicke s Encephalopathy Huntington s Disease Vascular Dementia 2 This is the classic presentation of Wernicke s Encephalopathy due to Thiamine deficiency which damages the mammillary bodies. A patient develops hyperphagia and significant obesity after a brain tumor resection. Which hypothalamic nucleus was most likely damaged? Lateral Hypothalamus Supraoptic Nucleus Ventromedial Nucleus Suprachiasmatic Nucleus Anterior Hypothalamus 2 The Ventromedial Nucleus is the Satiety Center. Damage leads to unchecked hunger and obesity Very Massive . Which hypothalamic nucleus is responsible for circadian rhythms? Arcuate Nucleus Suprachiasmatic Nucleus Paraventricular Nucleus Posterior Hypothalamus Mammillary Nucleus 1 The Suprachiasmatic Nucleus SCN regulates sleep-wake cycles and circadian rhythms. Damage to the Anterior Hypothalamus would result in Hypothermia Hyperthermia Diabetes Insipidus Amnesia Insomnia 1 The Anterior Hypothalamus regulates Cooling . Damage prevents cooling resulting in Hyperthermia . In neurogenic Diabetes Insipidus there is a lack of which hormone? Oxytocin Prolactin ADH Vasopressin TSH GH 2 Neurogenic Diabetes Insipidus causes polyuria due to a failure to secrete ADH Vasopressin from the posterior pituitary/hypothalamus. Function of Lateral Hypothalamus Hunger Feeding Center Physiology Function of Ventromedial Hypothalamus Satiety Physiology Function of Anterior Hypothalamus Cooling Parasympathetic Physiology Function of Posterior Hypothalamus Heating Sympathetic/Shivering Physiology Function of Suprachiasmatic Nucleus SCN Circadian Rhythms Physiology Function of Mammillary Bodies Episodic Memory Papez Circuit Physiology Lesion of Mammillary Bodies causes Wernicke-Korsakoff Syndrome Amnesia/Confabulation Pathology Lesion of Lateral Hypothalamus causes Anorexia Failure to eat Pathology Lesion of Ventromedial Hypothalamus causes Hyperphagia Obesity Pathology Major hormones of Posterior Pituitary ADH Vasopressin and Oxytocin Physiology BandB Endermo > Hypothalamus FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Hypothalamus Pathoma CNS Hypothalamic Feeding Centers Lateral Hypothalamus LH Hunger Center. Lesion = Starvation Lateral makes you Lean .\\\\n Ventromedial Nucleus VMN Satiety Center. Lesion = Obesity VMN makes you Very Massive . Temperature Regulation Anterior Hypothalamus Cooling Parasympathetic . Lesion = Hyperthermia.\\\\n Posterior Hypothalamus Heating Sympathetic . Lesion = Hypothermia. Wernicke-Korsakoff Anatomy Caused by Thiamine B1 deficiency leading to necrosis of the Mammillary Bodies . Circadian Rhythm Controlled by the Suprachiasmatic Nucleus SCN which receives direct input from the retina. ADH Production ADH is synthesized in the Supraoptic and Paraventricular nuclei and released from the Posterior Pituitary . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l12", "title": "Lecture 12: Anatomy and Physiology of the Thalamus", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l12 module Neuro l12 Lecture 12 Anatomy and Physiology of the Thalamus\\n\\n Lecturer J. Xie Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 12\\n\\n 1. Thalamic Organization Slide 12-18 \\n\\nThe Thalamus is the \\ Gateway to the Cortex\\ . Most sensory information except olfaction must synapse here before reaching the cortex.\\n\\n Relay Nuclei Specific \\n VPL Ventral Posterolateral \\n Input DCML and Spinothalamic Tract Body .\\n Output Somatosensory Cortex Postcentral Gyrus .\\n Function Vibration Proprioception Pain Temp from Body .\\n VPM Ventral Posteromedial \\n Input Trigeminal Face and Gustatory Solitary .\\n Output Somatosensory Cortex.\\n Function Sensation from Face + Taste .\\n LGN Lateral Geniculate Nucleus \\n Input Optic Tract.\\n Output Visual Cortex V1 .\\n Function Vision \\ L ight\\ .\\n MGN Medial Geniculate Nucleus \\n Input Inferior Colliculus.\\n Output Auditory Cortex.\\n Function Hearing \\ M usic\\ .\\n VA/VL Ventral Anterior/Lateral \\n Input Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum.\\n Output Motor Cortex.\\n Function Motor planning and coordination.\\n Anterior Nucleus \\n Input Mammillothalamic Tract.\\n Output Cingulate Gyrus.\\n Function Memory and Emotion Papez Circuit .\\n\\n 2. Thalamocortical States Slide 23-26 \\n Tonic Mode Linear transmission. High fidelity. Awake state depolarized by ARAS NE ACh 5-HT Histamine .\\n Burst Mode Oscillatory. Low fidelity. Sleep state hyperpolarized . Blocks sensory input preventing waking. Absence Seizures occur in this mode.\\n\\n 3. Internal Capsule Slide 35-39 \\nWhite matter highway carrying fibers to/from cortex.\\n Anterior Limb Thalamocortical fibers Anterior/DM .\\n Genu Corticobulbar Tract Motor to face/brainstem .\\n Posterior Limb \\n Corticospinal Tract Motor to body .\\n Somatosensory Radiations VPL/VPM to Cortex .\\n Visual/Auditory Radiations Retrolenticular/Sublenticular .\\n Blood Supply Primarily MCA Lenticulostriate and Anterior Choroidal . Thalamus is PCA .\\n\\n 4. Clinical Syndromes Slide 29-32 \\n\\n Dejerine-Roussy Syndrome Thalamic Pain Syndrome \\n Cause Stroke PCA damaging VPL/VPM.\\n Presentation Contralateral hemianesthesia followed by agonizing burning pain Allodynia induced by touch or even emotion.\\n\\n Artery of Percheron Infarct\\n Variant Single artery arising from PCA supplying Bilateral paramedian thalamus + midbrain.\\n Stroke Bilateral thalamic infarct.\\n Presentation Coma RAS damage Memory loss Vertical gaze palsy. Lecture 12 Anatomy and Physiology of the Thalamus Neuroscience questions A patient suffers a stroke and develops severe burning pain on the right side of their body that is exacerbated by light touch. Which nucleus was most likely infarcted? Right VPL Left VPL Right VPM Left MGN Left Anterior Nucleus 1 Thalamic Pain Syndrome Dejerine-Roussy is contralateral. A Left VPL infarct affects the Right side of the body. VPL handles body sensation. Visual information from the optic tract synapses in which thalamic nucleus? Medial Geniculate Nucleus Lateral Geniculate Nucleus Ventral Posterolateral Nucleus Pulvinar Ventral Anterior Nucleus 1 LGN = L ight Vision . MGN = Music Hearing . Which limb of the internal capsule contains the corticospinal tract? Anterior Limb Genu Posterior Limb Retrolenticular Part Sublenticular Part 2 The Posterior Limb of the Internal Capsule carries the Corticospinal tract Motor to body and Somatosensory radiations. Which thalamic nucleus is part of the Papez circuit involved in memory? Ventral Lateral Anterior Nucleus Pulvinar Lateral Posterior Centromedian 1 The Anterior Nucleus receives input from the Mammillary Bodies and projects to the Cingulate Gyrus forming part of the Papez circuit for memory/emotion. The Burst Mode of thalamocortical firing is associated with Focused attention REM Sleep Slow Wave Sleep Panic attacks Active learning 2 Burst Mode is an oscillatory state that disconnects the cortex from sensory input characteristic of Slow Wave Sleep and Absence Seizures . Input to VPL Nucleus Spinothalamic & DCML Body Sensation Neuroanatomy Input to VPM Nucleus Trigeminal & Solitary Tract Face & Taste Neuroanatomy Input to LGN Optic Tract Vision Neuroanatomy Input to MGN Inferior Colliculus Hearing Neuroanatomy Input to VA/VL Nuclei Basal Ganglia & Cerebellum Motor Neuroanatomy Contents of Internal Capsule Genu Corticobulbar Tract Neuroanatomy Contents of Internal Capsule Posterior Limb Corticospinal Tract & Sensory Radiations Neuroanatomy Artery supplying the Thalamus Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA Neuroanatomy Cause of Thalamic Pain Syndrome Stroke in VPL/VPM Dejerine-Roussy Pathology Thalamic mode during Deep Sleep Burst Mode Physiology BandB Neurology > Brainstem > Thalamus FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Thalamus Pathoma CNS VPM vs VPL Mnemonic VPM = M akeup on the Face Face sensation + Taste .\\\\n VPL = L egs/Body Body sensation . Geniculate Nuclei Mnemonic L GN = L ight Visual .\\\\n M GN = M usic Auditory . Dejerine-Roussy Syndrome Central Post-Stroke Pain. Thalamic lesion causes initial numbness followed by hypersensitivity and severe burning pain on the contralateral side. Internal Capsule Genu The Genu knee contains the Corticobulbar tract Face motor . A lesion here affects CN motor nuclei e.g. UMN facial droop . Thalamic Blood Supply Principally the Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA . The Artery of Percheron is a rare variant supplying bilateral thalami. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l13", "title": "Lecture 13: Clinical Decision Making", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l13 module Neuro l13 Lecture 13 Clinical Decision Making\\n\\n Lecturer R. Morgan D.O.\\n Session Lecture 13\\n\\n 1. Dual Process Theory Slide 4-5 \\n\\nClinical reasoning involves two cognitive systems \\n System 1 Intuitive Fast automatic reflexive pattern recognition. Used by experts for common cases. Prone to Heuristic Biases .\\n System 2 Hypothetico-Deductive Slow analytical deliberate. Used for complex or unclear cases. Less prone to bias but resource-intensive.\\n\\n 2. Cognitive Biases Heuristics Slide 5 26-28 \\n\\n Anchoring Locking onto salient features in the patient s initial presentation too early and failing to adjust.\\n Availability Bias Judging a diagnosis as more likely because it is easily recalled e.g. you just saw 3 cases of Appendicitis so the next belly pain must be Appendicitis .\\n Confirmation Bias Seeking evidence to support your hypothesis while ignoring evidence that refutes it.\\n Premature Closure Stopping the diagnostic process before verifying the diagnosis or considering other possibilities. \\ When the diagnosis is made the thinking stops.\\ \\n\\n 3. The Diagnostic Framework\\n\\n VINDICATE Mnemonic Slide 15 \\nA checklist to generate a broad Differential Diagnosis DDx .\\n V ascular\\n I nfectious\\n N eoplastic\\n D rug / Degenerative\\n I nflammatory / Idiopathic\\n C ongenital\\n A utoimmune / Allergic\\n T rauma / Toxic\\n E ndocrine / Metabolic\\n\\n Illness Scripts Slide 18 22 \\nAn organized mental summary of a disease including \\n Epidemiology/Pathophysiology Who gets it? Why?\\n Time Course Acute Subacute Chronic?\\n Salient Symptoms/Signs Key features Prototypical presentation .\\n Diagnostics/Treatment Labs Imaging Meds.\\n\\n 4. Problem Representation Slide 12 35 \\nA one-sentence summary of the case used to communicate and reason.\\n Structure Patient Age/Sex/Risk Factors presents with Time Course Chief Complaint characterized by Key Features .\\n Example \\ A 3yo male with no PMH presents with 2 days of fever and ear pulling found to have a bulging erythematous TM.\\ \\n\\n correlate\\n Case Study Acute Otitis Media AOM \\n Presentation Fever Ear Pain Otalgia Bulging Tympanic Membrane TM Loss of benchmarks.\\n Pathogens Strep pneumoniae Haemophilus influenzae Moraxella catarrhalis .\\n DDx \\n Serous Otitis Media Fluid effusion but no active infection/bulging.\\n Otitis Externa Swimmer s ear. Canal inflammation. Pain with tragal motion. flesh-colored canal makes this unlikely.\\n Lecture 13 Clinical Decision Making Neuroscience questions A resident diagnoses a patient with Influenza because everyone coming into the ER today has the flu despite the patient having neck stiffness and a rash. Which cognitive bias is at play? Anchoring Availability Bias Confirmation Bias Premature Closure Sunk Cost Fallacy 1 Availability Bias is judging frequency or probability by how easily examples come to mind e.g. a recent run of flu cases . When generating a differential diagnosis for a 60-year-old male with back pain you use the mnemonic VINDICATE. The N stands for Neurologic Nutritional Neoplastic Nociceptive Neonatal 2 N in VINDICATE stands for Neoplastic Cancer a critical category to consider in older adults with new back pain. Which of the following best describes System 2 thinking? Fast and reflexive Pattern recognition Slow and analytical Prone to emotional influence Automatic association 2 System 2 is the slow deliberate analytical mode of thinking used for complex problems. A 3-year-old presents with ear pain. Exam reveals a bulging erythematous tympanic membrane with loss of bony landmarks. The most likely diagnosis is Otitis Externa Serous Otitis Media Mastoiditis Acute Otitis Media Suppurative Foreign Body 3 Bulging and erythema of the TM are hallmark signs of Acute Suppurative Otitis Media. You suspect a patient has a Pulmonary Embolism. You order a D-dimer which is negative but you dismiss the result as a lab error because you are so sure of your diagnosis. This is an example of Confirmation Bias Anchoring Availability Bias Visceral Bias Gambler s Fallacy 1 Confirmation Bias involves interpreting evidence to support your existing beliefs the PE diagnosis while dismissing contradictory evidence the negative D-dimer . System 1 Thinking Fast Intuitive Automatic Clinical Reasoning System 2 Thinking Slow Analytical Deliberate Clinical Reasoning Availability Bias Diagnosing based on what is readily recalled recent cases Clinical Reasoning Anchoring Bias Sticking to initial impressions despite new data Clinical Reasoning Premature Closure Stopping the diagnostic process too early Clinical Reasoning VINDICATE V Vascular Clinical Reasoning VINDICATE I first Infectious Clinical Reasoning VINDICATE N Neoplastic Clinical Reasoning Illness Script Components Pathophys Epidemiology Time Course Symptoms Diagnostics Treatment Clinical Reasoning Key sign of Acute Otitis Media Bulging Tympanic Membrane Clinical Reasoning BandB Infectious Disease > HEENT FirstAid Public Health > Epidemiology > Bias Pathoma General Principles System 1 vs System 2 System 1 Fast Intuitive Gut feeling . Prone to bias.\\\\n System 2 Slow Analytical Math problem . More accurate. VINDICATE Mnemonic Vascular Infectious Neoplastic Drug/Degenerative Inflammatory/Idiopathic Congenital Autoimmune Trauma/Toxic Endocrine/Metabolic. Confirmation Bias The tendency to look only for evidence that confirms your hunch and ignore evidence that contradicts it. Availability Bias Diagnosing something because you recently saw it or it s top of mind e.g. flu during flu season . Premature Closure The most common fatal error Accepting a diagnosis before it is fully verified and stopping the search for alternatives. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l15", "title": "Lecture 15: General Sensory Systems", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l15 module Neuro l15 Lecture 15 General Sensory Systems\\n\\n Lecturer J. Xie Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 15\\n\\n 1. Sensory Receptors & Fibers Slide 5-13 \\n\\n Fiber Types\\n A-beta A\\u03b2 Large Myelinated. Fast 30-70 m/s . Carries Touch Vibration Proprioception .\\n A-delta A\\u03b4 Small Myelinated. Medium speed. Carries Sharp Pain First Pain Cold .\\n C fibers Unmyelinated. Slow 0.5-2 m/s . Carries Dull/Burning Pain Second Pain Warmth Itch .\\n\\n Mechanoreceptors Touch \\n Meissner s Corpuscle Fast adapting Small field. Light Touch/Grip . Glabrous skin.\\n Merkel Disc Slow adapting Small field. Static Pressure/Texture Braille .\\n Pacinian Corpuscle Fast adapting Large field. Vibration .\\n Ruffini Ending Slow adapting Large field. Skin Stretch Grip shape .\\n\\n 2. Ascending Pathways Slide 26-30 \\n\\n Dorsal Column-Medial Lemniscus DCML \\nCarries Fine Touch Vibration Proprioception from the body.\\n1. 1st Order DRG. Ascend in Dorsal Columns Gracilis=Lower Cuneatus=Upper .\\n2. 2nd Order Nucleus Gracilis/Cuneatus Medulla . Decussate as Internal Arcuate Fibers to form Medial Lemniscus .\\n3. 3rd Order VPL Nucleus Thalamus → Primary Somatosensory Cortex S1 .\\n\\n Spinothalamic Tract Anterolateral System \\nCarries Pain Temperature Crude Touch .\\n1. 1st Order DRG. Synapse in Dorsal Horn Substantia Gelatinosa .\\n2. 2nd Order Dorsal Horn. Decussate immediately in Anterior White Commissure. Ascend in anterolateral funiculus.\\n3. 3rd Order VPL Nucleus Thalamus → S1 Cortex.\\n\\n 3. Trigeminal System Face Slide 31-33 \\n Touch/Proprioception Trigeminal Ganglion → Principal Sensory Nucleus Pons → Decussate → VPM.\\n Pain/Temp Trigeminal Ganglion → Descend to Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus Medulla → Decussate → VPM.\\n Proprioception Jaw Mesencephalic Nucleus Midbrain - UNIQUE Cell bodies inside CNS .\\n\\n 4. Clinical Correlates Slide 38-43 \\n\\n correlate\\n Brown-S\\u00e9quard Syndrome \\n Pathology Hemisection of the spinal cord e.g. stabbing/trauma .\\n Presentation \\n Ipsilateral Loss of Motor Corticospinal and Touch/Vibration/Proprioception DCML below lesion.\\n Contralateral Loss of Pain/Temperature Spinothalamic 1-2 segments below lesion due to Lissanuer s tract ascent before crossing .\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Phantom Limb Pain \\n Pathology Cortical reorganization after amputation.\\n Mechanism The cortical area for the amputated limb e.g. arm loses input and is invaded by adjacent areas e.g. Face . Touching the face is perceived as touching the missing arm.\\n Nature Often burning/painful resistant to meds.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Thalamic Lesion Dejerine-Roussy \\n Lesion VPL/VPM.\\n Presentation Contralateral Hemianesthesia loss of all sensation . Later develops into severe burning pain Thalamic Pain Syndrome .\\n \\n\\n 5. Pain Modulation Slide 18-20 \\n Gate Control Theory A\\u03b2 fibers rubbing activate inhibitory interneurons in the dorsal horn blocking C fiber pain transmission.\\n Descending Pathway PAG → RVM → Dorsal Horn Inhibits pain via Serotonin/NE . Lecture 15 General Sensory Systems Neuroscience questions A patient cannot feel temperature or sharp pain in their right leg but vibration sense is intact. Where is the lesion most likely located? Right Fasciculus Gracilis Left Spinothalamic Tract Right Spinothalamic Tract Left Medial Lemniscus Right Dorsal Horn 1 Pain/Temp from the Right leg travels to the left side of the spinal cord immediately. A lesion in the Left Spinothalamic Tract causes loss of pain/temp on the Right side. Which receptor is responsible for sensing vibration? Merkel Disc Meissner Corpuscle Pacinian Corpuscle Ruffini Ending Free Nerve Ending 2 Pacinian Corpuscles are rapidly adapting and specialized for sensing Vibration . A hemisection of the spinal cord at T10 on the left side Brown-S\\u00e9quard would result in Loss of pain/temp on the left leg Loss of vibration/proprioception on the right leg Loss of motor function on the right leg Loss of pain/temp on the right leg Bilateral loss of all sensation 3 Hemisection on the Left causes Contralateral Right loss of Pain/Temp STT crosses immediately and Ipsilateral Left loss of Touch/Motor. The cell bodies of the primary afferent neurons for the face Trigeminal are located in the Dorsal Root Ganglion Trigeminal Ganglion Principal Sensory Nucleus Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus VPM Nucleus 1 Primary sensory neurons for the face are in the Trigeminal Gasserian Ganglion . Which fibers carry slow burning pain? A-alpha A-beta A-delta B fibers C fibers 4 C fibers are unmyelinated and slow conducting responsible for the delayed dull burning second pain . Pathway for Fine Touch and Proprioception Dorsal Column-Medial Lemniscus DCML Neuroanatomy Pathway for Pain and Temperature Spinothalamic Tract Anterolateral System Neuroanatomy Where does DCML decussate? Medulla Internal Arcuate Fibers Neuroanatomy Where does Spinothalamic Tract decussate? Spinal Cord Anterior White Commissure Neuroanatomy Function of Pacinian Corpuscle Vibration Physiology Function of A-delta fibers Fast Sharp Pain Cold Physiology Function of C fibers Slow Dull Pain Warmth/Itch Physiology Brown-S\\u00e9quard Contralateral Deficit Pain and Temperature STT Pathology Brown-S\\u00e9quard Ipsilateral Deficit Motor CS & Vibration/Proprioception DCML Pathology Thalamic Nucleus for Body Sensation VPL Neuroanatomy BandB Neurology > Spinal Cord > Spinal Cord Tracts FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Spinal Cord Pathoma CNS DCML vs STT Decussation DCML crosses in the Medulla Internal Arcuate Fibers .\\\\n Spinothalamic crosses in the Spinal Cord Anterior White Commissure . VPL vs VPM VPL = L egs/Body Body sensation .\\\\n VPM = M akeup/Face Face sensation . Fiber Speed Rule A-beta Touch > A-delta Fast Pain > C Slow Pain . Myelination and diameter determine speed. Brown-S\\u00e9quard Deficits Ipsilateral Motor & Touch. Contralateral Pain & Temp. Phantom Limb Mechanism Maladaptive Cortical Reorganization Neuroplasticity where face area invades hand area. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l16", "title": "Lecture 16: Visual System", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l16 module Neuro l16 Lecture 16 Visual System\\n\\n Lecturer G. Otazu Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 16\\n\\n 1. Eye Functional Anatomy Slide 4-10 \\n\\nThe eye focuses light onto the retina to transduce it into neural signals.\\n Refraction Most focusing power comes from the Cornea air-water interface not the lens.\\n Accommodation The Lens changes shape to focus near objects.\\n Near Vision Reflex Ciliary muscle Contracts → Zonules Relax → Lens becomes Rounder . + Pupil constriction + Convergence .\\n Far Vision Ciliary muscle Relaxes → Zonules Tighten → Lens flattens.\\n Aqueous Humor Produced by Ciliary Body. Drains via Canal of Schlemm . Blockage = Glaucoma .\\n\\n Retina Organization\\n Fovea Center of Macula. All Cones . Highest acuity. No blood vessels/layers in light path.\\n Periphery Mostly Rods . High sensitivity low light low acuity.\\n Blind Spot Optic Disc Where optic nerve exits. No photoreceptors.\\n\\n 2. Visual Pathway Slide 26-30 \\n\\n1. Retina Light hits Photoreceptors → Bipolar Cells → Ganglion Cells.\\n2. Optic Nerve CN II Ganglion cell axons.\\n3. Optic Chiasm Nasal fibers temporal field CROSS . Temporal fibers nasal field stay ipsilateral.\\n4. Optic Tract Contains ipsilateral Temporal fibers + contralateral Nasal fibers. Projects to LGN .\\n5. Lateral Geniculate Nucleus LGN Thalamic relay.\\n Magno M layers Motion/Depth.\\n Parvo P layers Color/Form.\\n6. Geniculocalcarine Tract Optic Radiations \\n Meyer s Loop Temporal Lobe Loops around lateral ventricle. Carries Superior Visual Field \\ Pie in the Sky\\ .\\n Dorsal/Parietal Radiations Carries Inferior Visual Field \\ Pie on the Floor\\ .\\n7. Primary Visual Cortex V1 Occipital Lobe. Upper bank Cuneus = Inferior Field. Lower bank Lingual = Superior Field.\\n\\n 3. Visual Field Lesions Slide 26-29 32 \\n Monocular Blindness Lesion of Optic Nerve .\\n Bitemporal Hemianopsia Lesion of Optic Chiasm .\\n Cause Pituitary Adenoma compressing chiasm centrally.\\n Effect Loss of peripheral vision \\ Tunnel Vision\\ .\\n Homonymous Hemianopsia Lesion of Optic Tract or PCA Stroke .\\n Effect Loss of same field in both eyes e.g. Left field perception lost in both eyes .\\n Macular Sparing PCA strokes often spare the fovea supplied by MCA collaterals .\\n Quadrantanopsia \\n Meyer s Loop Temporal \\ Pie in the Sky\\ Contralateral Superior Quadrant .\\n Parietal Radiations \\ Pie on the Floor\\ Contralateral Inferior Quadrant .\\n\\n correlate\\n Papilledema Slide 34 \\n Pathophysiology Increased Intracranial Pressure ICP transmitted through the subarachnoid space surrounding the Optic Nerve.\\n Sign Swelling of the Optic Disc visible on fundoscopy. Blurred margins.\\n Context Brain tumor Meningitis Pseudotumor Cerebri.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Hemispatial Neglect Slide 43-44 \\n Lesion Right Parietal Lobe Non-dominant hemisphere .\\n Presentation Patient ignores the Left side of the world/body.\\n Starts eating from only right side of plate.\\n Shaves only right face.\\n Draws only right half of clock.\\n Note This is an Attention deficit not a visual one.\\n \\n\\n mnemonic\\n Visual Field Defects \\n Tem poral Loop = Pie in the Sky Temporal lobe lesion → Superior field defect .\\n Parietal Path = Pie on the Floor Parietal lobe lesion → Inferior field defect .\\n Lecture 16 Visual System Neuroscience questions A patient presents with tunnel vision bumping into objects on their periphery. MRI reveals a mass compressing the optic chiasm. What is the most likely diagnosis? Right Optic Nerve Compression Pituitary Adenoma Temporal Lobe Tumor Parietal Lobe Tumor Occipital Lobe Stroke 1 A Pituitary Adenoma compresses the specialized crossing fibers in the Optic Chiasm causing Bitemporal Hemianopsia Tunnel Vision . A patient cannot see the upper left quadrant of their visual field Pie in the Sky . Where is the lesion located? Right Temporal Lobe Meyer s Loop Left Temporal Lobe Meyer s Loop Right Parietal Lobe Left Parietal Lobe Right Occipital Cortex 0 A Left upper field defect maps to the Right brain Contralateral . In the Sky Superior implies the inferior fibers Meyer s Loop in Temporal Lobe . So Right Temporal Lobe . Accommodation for near vision involves which action of the ciliary muscle? Relaxation Contraction Hyperpolarization Atrophy No change 1 The ciliary muscle Contracts loosening the zonules allowing the lens to spring into a rounder shape for near focus. Papilledema is a clinical sign indicative of Retinal Detachment Glaucoma Increased Intracranial Pressure Cataracts Macular Degeneration 2 Papilledema swollen optic disc is caused by transmitted Increased Intracranial Pressure ICP . A patient with a Right Parietal Lobe stroke only eats food on the right side of their plate and ignores the left side. This is called Homonymous Hemianopsia Agnosia Hemispatial Neglect Apraxia Prosopagnosia 2 Hemispatial Neglect is an attention deficit classically due to a non-dominant Right Parietal Lobe lesion. Visual Deficit Optic Chiasm Lesion Bitemporal Hemianopsia Neurology Visual Deficit Right Optic Tract Lesion Left Homonymous Hemianopsia Neurology Visual Deficit Meyer s Loop Temporal Lesion Contralateral Superior Quadrantanopia Pie in the Sky Neurology Visual Deficit Parietal Radiation Lesion Contralateral Inferior Quadrantanopia Pie on the Floor Neurology Lesion causing Left Hemispatial Neglect Right Parietal Lobe Neurology Sign of Increased ICP on Eye Exam Papilledema Pathology Function of Ciliary Muscle Contraction Accommodation Near Vision Physiology Area of Retina with highest acuity Fovea Macula Physiology Blood Supply to Visual Cortex Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA Neuroanatomy What is spared in PCA stroke visual field defect? Macula Macular Sparing Pathology BandB Neurology > The Eye > Visual Fields FirstAid Neurology > Ophthalmology Pathoma CNS Pituitary Adenoma Deficit Compresses Optic Chiasm → Bitemporal Hemianopsia Tunnel Vision . Meyer s Loop Deficit Temporal Lobe lesion → Superior Homonymous Quadrantanopia Pie in the Sky . Parietal Radiation Deficit Parietal Lobe lesion → Inferior Homonymous Quadrantanopia Pie on the Floor . Macular Sparing Occipital Cortex PCA infarcts often spare the Macula because it has dual blood supply MCA collaterals . Right Parietal Lobe Lesion Causes Left Hemispatial Neglect . The Right parietal lobe attends to both sides while the Left only attends to the Right. Loss of Right = Loss of Left attention. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l17", "title": "Lecture 17: Anatomy and Physiology of the Auditory System", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l17 module Neuro l17 Lecture 17 Anatomy and Physiology of the Auditory System\\n\\n Lecturer G. Otazu Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 17\\n\\n 1. Auditory Anatomy Slide 4-6 10-14 \\n\\n Outer Ear Funnels sound.\\n Middle Ear Ossicles Malleus Incus Stapes amplify sound to transfer it from air tympanic membrane to fluid oval window .\\n Impedance Matching Necessary because fluid differs from air. Stapes pushes on Oval Window.\\n Protective Reflex Stapedius CN VII and Tensor Tympani CN V3 contract to dampen loud sounds.\\n Inner Ear Cochlea \\n Tonotopy Base = High Frequencies Narrow/Stiff . Apex = Low Frequencies Wide/Floppy .\\n Ion Gradients Endolymph Scala Media is high in K+ . Perilymph is low in K+.\\n\\n 2. Mechanotransduction Organ of Corti Slide 15-18 \\nThe Organ of Corti sits on the basilar membrane.\\n Inner Hair Cells IHC The primary sensory receptors 95% of afferents .\\n Outer Hair Cells OHC Cochlear amplifiers. Damage = Tinnitus .\\n Transduction Vibration bends stereocilia → Tip links open K+ channels → K+ influx depolarization → Ca2+ influx → Glutamate release.\\n\\n 3. Auditory Pathway Slide 20-21 \\n\\ E. C. O. L. I. M.\\ mnemonic.\\n1. E ighth Nerve Spiral Ganglion .\\n2. C ochlear Nuclei Medulla .\\n3. S uperior O live Pons - Sound Localization Time/Intensity diffs . First bilateral processing.\\n4. L ateral Lemniscus.\\n5. I nferior Colliculus Midbrain .\\n6. M edial Geniculate Nucleus M GN Thalamus .\\n7. Auditory Cortex Temporal Lobe/Heschl s Gyrus .\\n\\n 4. Hearing Loss Types Slide 27-29 \\n Conductive Middle/Outer ear issue Wax Otitis Media Otosclerosis .\\n Rinne Bone > Air.\\n Weber Localizes to Affected ear.\\n Sensorineural Inner ear/Nerve issue Presbycusis Noise Drugs .\\n Rinne Air > Bone Normal pattern but reduced .\\n Weber Localizes to Unaffected ear.\\n\\n 5. Clinical Correlates Slide 33-37 \\n\\n correlate\\n Acoustic Neuroma Vestibular Schwannoma \\n Pathology Benign tumor of Schwann cells on CN VIII Vestibulocochlear .\\n CPA Angle Compression of Cerebellopontine Angle structures.\\n Symptoms Unilateral Hearing Loss Tinnitus Vertigo . Can compress CN VII Facial palsy if large.\\n Diagnosis MRI.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Usher Syndrome \\n Genetics Autosomal Recessive.\\n Pathology Mutation in stereocilia tip-link proteins e.g. Cadherin 23 .\\n Presentation Congenital Deafness + Retinitis Pigmentosa Progressive Blindness . Major cause of deaf-blindness.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n EAST Syndrome \\n Acronym E pilepsy A taxia S ensorineural deafness T ubulopathy.\\n Pathology Mutation in KCNJ10 Kir4.1 K+ channel .\\n Mechanism Failure to maintain High K+ in Endolymph prevents hair cell depolarization.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Presbycusis \\n Definition Age-related sensorineural hearing loss.\\n Pattern Loss of High Frequencies first Base of cochlea wears out .\\n Mechanism Hair cell loss.\\n Lecture 17 Anatomy and Physiology of the Auditory System Neuroscience questions A 65-year-old male complains of difficulty hearing women s voices and birds chirping high frequencies in noisy environments. Otoscopy is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis? Otosclerosis Presbycusis Meniere s Disease Cholesteatoma Otitis Media 1 Presbycusis is age-related sensorineural hearing loss affecting High Frequencies first due to hair cell loss at the cochlear base. A patient presents with unilateral hearing loss tinnitus and facial weakness. MRI reveals a mass at the Cerebellopontine Angle. What is the likely diagnosis? Pituitary Adenoma Glioblastoma Multiforme Vestibular Schwannoma Acoustic Neuroma Meningioma Craniopharyngioma 2 Vestibular Schwannoma arises on CN VIII causing hearing loss/tinnitus and can compress the adjacent CN VII Facial nerve at the CPA. In the auditory pathway where does sound localization computing time/intensity differences first occur? Cochlear Nucleus Superior Olivary Complex Inferior Colliculus Medial Geniculate Nucleus Auditory Cortex 1 The Superior Olivary Complex is the first site receiving Bilateral input allowing it to compare timing and intensity data for localization. EAST Syndrome is caused by a mutation affecting the transport of which ion in the inner ear? Sodium Calcium Chloride Potassium Magnesium 3 EAST Syndrome is a K+ channelopathy Kir4.1 preventing the maintenance of high Potassium in the endolymph. During a Weber test the sound lateralizes to the patient s Left ear. Bone conduction is greater than Air conduction in the Left ear. This suggests Right Sensorineural Hearing Loss Left Sensorineural Hearing Loss Right Conductive Hearing Loss Left Conductive Hearing Loss Normal Hearing 3 Weber lateralizing to the affected ear + Rinne showing Bone > Air is the classic pattern for Conductive Hearing Loss in that ear Left . Auditory Pathway Mnemonic E. COLIM Eighth nerve Cochlear nuc Olive Lat Lemniscus Inf Colliculus MGN Neuroanatomy Frequency map of Cochlea Base = High. Apex = Low. Physiology Ion concentrated in Endolymph Potassium K+ Physiology Function of Superior Olivary Complex Sound Localization Neuroanatomy Muscle innervated by CN VII in ear Stapedius Anatomy Muscle innervated by CN V3 in ear Tensor Tympani Anatomy Usher Syndrome Presentation Deafness + Blindness Retinitis Pigmentosa Pathology Acoustic Neuroma Symptoms Unilateral Hearing Loss Tinnitus Vertigo Pathology Weber Test Conductive Loss Lateralizes to the Bad ear Clinical Skills Rinne Test Sensorineural Loss Air > Bone Normal pattern Clinical Skills BandB Neurology > The Ear > Hearing FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Auditory Pathoma Head & Neck Pathway Mnemonic E. COLIM E ighth Nerve → C ochlear Nucleus → O live Superior → L ateral Lemniscus → I nferior Colliculus → M GN. Cochlear Tonotopy Base = High Frequency Stiff/Narrow .\\\\n Apex = Low Frequency Floppy/Wide . Rinne & Weber Rules Conductive Weber to Bad Ear. Rinne Bone > Air.\\\\n Sensorineural Weber to Good Ear. Rinne Air > Bone. Acoustic Reflex Mediated by CN VII Stapedius and CN V3 Tensor Tympani to dampen loud sounds. EAST Syndrome Mechanism K+ Channel mutation Kir4.1 . No K+ gradient in Endolymph = No Hearing. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l18", "title": "Lecture 18: Anatomy & Physiology of the Vestibular System", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l18 module Neuro l18 Lecture 18 Anatomy & Physiology of the Vestibular System\\n\\n Lecturer R. Ramos Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 18\\n\\n 1. Peripheral Anatomy Slide 4-15 \\nThe Vestibular Labyrinth detects head motion via hair cells mechanotransduction .\\n Otolith Organs Utricle & Saccule \\n Function Detect Linear Acceleration e.g. gravity elevator car acceleration .\\n Mechanism Hair cells embedded in Macula covered by Otoconia crystals . Gravity pulls crystals → bends hairs.\\n Mnemonic U tricle = U pright Horizontal plane . S accule = S ideways Vertical plane .\\n Semicircular Canals Anterior Posterior Horizontal \\n Function Detect Angular Rotational Acceleration head turning .\\n Mechanism Crista Ampullaris covered by Cupula gelatin .\\n Fluid Dynamics When head turns Left Endolymph lags moves Right → bends Cupula.\\n\\n 2. Vestibular Pathways & Reflexes Slide 16-20 \\n Vestibulo-Ocular Reflex VOR Stabilizes gaze during head movement.\\n Path Head Left → Left SCC active → Vestibular Nuclei → Right CN VI Abd & Left CN III MR → Eyes move Right .\\n Vestibulo-Spinal Tracts \\n Lateral Extensor muscles Anti-gravity/Balance .\\n Medial Neck muscles Head stabilization .\\n\\n 3. Peripheral Vertigo Disorders Slide 26-30 \\n\\n correlate\\n Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo BPPV \\n Mechanism Otoconia crystals dislodge from Utricle and float into a Semicircular Canal usually Posterior .\\n Presentation Brief <1 min episodes of severe vertigo triggered by Head Position changes rolling over in bed .\\n Diagnosis Dix-Hallpike Maneuver elicits nystagmus.\\n Treatment Epley Maneuver repositions crystals .\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Meniere s Disease \\n Mechanism Endolymphatic Hydrops Excess fluid pressure .\\n Presentation Triad of Episodic Vertigo mins-hours + Tinnitus + Sensorineural Hearing Loss .\\n Treatment Low salt diet Diuretics.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Labyrinthitis \\n Mechanism Inflammation/Infection of the inner ear.\\n Presentation Continuous severe vertigo + Hearing Loss + Nausea. Often follows a viral infection.\\n \\n\\n 4. Central Vertigo Disorders Slide 31-34 \\n\\n correlate\\n Wallenberg Syndrome Lateral Medullary Syndrome \\n Pathology Infarct of PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery .\\n Signs \\n Vestibular Nuclei Vertigo Nystagmus.\\n Nucleus Ambiguus dysphagia hoarseness.\\n Spinothalamic Tract Contralateral loss of Pain/Temp Body .\\n Spinal Trigeminal Ipsilateral loss of Pain/Temp Face .\\n Sympathetic Ipsilateral Horner s Syndrome Ptosis Miosis Anhidrosis .\\n \\n\\n mnemonic\\n Peripheral vs Central Vertigo \\n Peripheral Severe vertigo Hearing Loss common Fatigable nystagmus. BPPV Meniere s .\\n Central Mild vertigo No Hearing Loss usually Non-fatigable nystagmus CNS signs Ataxia Dysphagia . Stroke MS .\\n Lecture 18 Anatomy & Physiology of the Vestibular System Neuroscience questions A patient presents with episodes of room-spinning vertigo lasting 30 seconds triggered only when rolling over in bed. Hearing is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis? Meniere s Disease Vestibular Neuritis Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo BPPV Acoustic Neuroma Wallenberg Syndrome 2 BPPV is characterized by brief <1 min position-dependent vertigo with normal hearing caused by dislodged otoconia. Which structure detects linear acceleration and head tilt relative to gravity? Crista Ampullaris Semicircular Canals Otolith Organs Utricle/Saccule Cochlea Organ of Corti 2 The Otolith Organs Utricle and Saccule contain the macula and otoconia specifically designing them to detect Linear Acceleration and gravity. A patient presents with vertigo dysphagia hoarseness ipsilateral facial numbness and contralateral body numbness. Examination reveals an ipsilateral Horner s syndrome. Which artery is occluded? Anterior Spinal Artery Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery PICA Anterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery AICA Middle Cerebral Artery MCA 2 This is Wallenberg Lateral Medullary Syndrome caused by occlusion of the PICA . The constellation of Vertigo Vestibular Dysphagia Ambiguus and Crossed Sensory loss is classic. What is the primary mechanism of Meniere s Disease? Otoconia dislodgement Endolymphatic Hydrops Excess fluid Demyelination Bacterial Infection Tumor Compression 1 Meniere s Disease is caused by Endolymphatic Hydrops swelling/excess pressure of the endolymphatic compartment . The Vestibulo-Ocular Reflex VOR allows for Hearing high frequencies Maintaining upright posture Stabilizing gaze during head movement Localizing sound in space Detecting gravity 2 The VOR generates eye movements equal and opposite to head movements to stabilize gaze on a fixed target. Function of Semicircular Canals Detect Angular Rotational Acceleration Physiology Function of Otolith Organs Utricle/Saccule Detect Linear Acceleration & Gravity Physiology Diagnosis Brief positional vertigo no hearing loss BPPV Pathology Diagnosis Vertigo + Tinnitus + Hearing Loss Meniere s Disease Pathology Maneuver to treat BPPV Epley Maneuver Clinical Skills Artery involved in Lateral Medullary Syndrome PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Pathology Key signs of Wallenberg Syndrome Dysphagia Vertigo Horner s Crossed Pain/Temp loss Pathology Where are Otoconia normally located? Utricle and Saccule Macula Anatomy Symptoms of Acoustic Neuroma Unilateral Hearing Loss Tinnitus Vertigo CPA angle Pathology Fluid with high Potassium in Inner Ear Endolymph Physiology BandB Neurology > The Ear > Vestibular System FirstAid Neurology > Ophthalmology Pathoma CNS Otoliths vs Canals Otoliths Utricle/Saccule = Linear/Gravity.\\\\n Canals SCC = Angular/Rotation. BPPV Pathophysiology Dislodged Otoconia Otoliths floating in the Semicircular Canals. Meniere s Triad 1. Vertigo\\\\n2. Tinnitus\\\\n3. Hearing Loss Fluctuating . Wallenberg Syndrome Artery PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery . Causes Lateral Medullary Syndrome. Dix-Hallpike Diagnostic maneuver for BPPV . Induces nystagmus if positive. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l19", "title": "Lecture 19: Pharmacology of Direct-Acting Cholinomimetics", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l19 module Neuro l19 Lecture 19 Pharmacology of Direct-Acting Cholinomimetics\\n\\n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D.\\n Session Lecture 19\\n\\n 1. Cholinergic Receptors Slide 9-11 \\n Muscarinic GPCRs Located on effector organs Heart Smooth Muscle Glands .\\n M1 M3 M5 Gq Increase Ca2+ Contraction/Secretion .\\n M2 M4 Gi Decrease cAMP Inhibition/Bradycardia .\\n Nicotinic Ion Channels Located at Ganglia Nn and Neuromuscular Junction Nm .\\n Ligand-gated Na+/K+ channels.\\n\\n 2. Muscarinic Agonists Slide 30-33 \\n\\ Direct-Acting\\ means they bind directly to the receptor unlike AChE inhibitors .\\n\\n Choline Esters Quaternary Amines \\n Poorly absorbed do not cross BBB. \\n Acetylcholine ACh Rapidly hydrolyzed. Used only for Ophthalmic Surgery Miosis .\\n Methacholine \\n use Bronchoprovocation Test to diagnose Asthma. Induces bronchospasm in hyperreactive airways.\\n Bethanechol \\n Use Urinary Retention Post-op Post-partum Neurogenic bladder .\\n Mnemonic \\ Bethany call chol me if you want to pee.\\ \\n Carbachol Glaucoma rarely used . Mixed M and N activity.\\n\\n Alkaloids Tertiary Amines \\n Well absorbed Cross BBB. \\n Pilocarpine \\n Use Glaucoma Opens meshwork via miosis Xerostomia Sjogren s Radiation .\\n Mnemonic \\ Pile on the sweat and tears.\\ \\n Cevimeline Synthetic M3 agonist. Used for Sjogren s Syndrome Dry mouth .\\n\\n 3. Nicotinic Agonists Slide 36-39 \\n Nicotine \\n Action Biphasic. Low dose = Stimulation. High dose = Depolarizing Blockade Paralysis .\\n Use Smoking Cessation NRT .\\n Varenicline Chantix \\n Action Partial Agonist at $\\\\\\\\alpha 4\\\\\\\\beta 2$ neuronal receptors.\\n Benefit Reduces cravings agonist effect AND blocks nicotine from bindings antagonist effect reducing reward.\\n\\n 4. Adverse Effects & Contraindications Slide 34 \\n Muscarinic Toxicity SLUDGE \\n S alivation L acrimation U rination D efecation G I Upset E mesis.\\n DANGER Bradycardia and Bronchoconstriction .\\n Contraindications \\n Asthma/COPD Bronchoconstriction .\\n Peptic Ulcer Disease Increased Acid .\\n GI/Urinary Obstruction Risk of rupture .\\n\\n 5. Clinical Correlates Slide 33 \\n\\n correlate\\n Sj\\u00f6gren s Syndrome \\n Pathology Autoimmune destruction of exocrine glands Salivary Lacrimal .\\n Presentation Xerostomia Dry mouth Keratoconjunctivitis Sicca Dry eyes .\\n Treatment Pilocarpine or Cevimeline Muscarinic Agonists to stimulate secretion.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Asthma Diagnosis \\n Drug Methacholine .\\n Test Inhaled methacholine challenge.\\n Result A patient with asthma will bronchoconstrict at a much lower dose than a healthy person.\\n Lecture 19 Pharmacology of Direct-Acting Cholinomimetics Neuroscience questions A 55-year-old male with post-operative urinary retention needs medication to help void. Examination confirms no obstruction. Which drug is most appropriate? Methacholine Atropine Bethanechol Pilocarpine Nicotine 2 Bethanechol is a direct muscarinic agonist with selectivity for the bladder/GI used to treat non-obstructive urinary retention. A patient being treated for Sjogren s syndrome complains of excessive sweating and diarrhea. This is likely due to which medication? Pilocarpine Atropine Scopolamine Ipratropium Varenicline 0 Pilocarpine is used to increase salivation in Sjogren s but is non-selective causing systemic muscarinic side effects like sweating diaphoresis and diarrhea. Which drug acts as a partial agonist at nicotinic receptors to aid in smoking cessation? Bupropion Varenicline Nicotine Patch Methacholine Carbachol 1 Varenicline is a partial agonist at $\\\\alpha 4\\\\beta 2$ nicotinic receptors reducing cravings and withdrawal symptoms. Methacholine is primarily used clinically for Treating Glaucoma Diagnosing Asthma Treating Urinary Retention Smoking Cessation Treating Alzheimer s 1 Methacholine is used in the bronchoprovocation challenge test to diagnose asthma . Direct-acting cholinomimetics should be avoided in patients with which condition? Glaucoma Xerostomia Peptic Ulcer Disease Constipation Hypertension 2 Muscarinic agonists increase gastric acid secretion which can exacerbate Peptic Ulcer Disease . Drug for Post-op Urinary Retention Bethanechol Pharmacology Drug for Diagnosis of Asthma Methacholine Pharmacology Drug for Sjogren s Syndrome Pilocarpine or Cevimeline Pharmacology Mechanism of Varenicline Partial Agonist at Nicotinic Receptors Pharmacology Contraindication for Muscarinic Agonists Lung Asthma/COPD Pharmacology Contraindication for Muscarinic Agonists GI Peptic Ulcer Disease or Obstruction Pharmacology SLUDGE Mnemonic Salivation Lacrimation Urination Defecation GI upset Emesis Toxicology Pilocarpine Class Muscarinic Agonist Tertiary Amine Pharmacology Bethanechol Class Muscarinic Agonist Quaternary Amine - No CNS Pharmacology Receptors on Sweat Glands Muscarinic Sympathetic Cholinergic Physiology SketchyPharm Autonomic Drugs > Parasympathetics > Cholinomimetics FirstAid Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs BandB Pharmacology > ANS > Cholinergic Agonists Bethanechol Use Non-obstructive Urinary Retention . Bethany call me to pee . Pilocarpine Use Open Angle Glaucoma and Sjogren s Syndrome Sweat/Saliva . Methacholine Challenge Used to diagnose Asthma via induced bronchoconstriction. Varenicline Mechanism Partial Agonist at $\\\\alpha 4\\\\beta 2$ Nicotinic receptors. Reduces withdrawal + Blocks reward. Cholinomimetic Contraindications Asthma causes bronchospasm Peptic Ulcers causes acid Obstruction causes rupture . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l20", "title": "Lecture 20: Indirect-Acting Cholinomimetics (Cholinesterase Inhibitors)", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l20 module Neuro l20 Lecture 20 Indirect-Acting Cholinomimetics Cholinesterase Inhibitors \\n\\n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D.\\n Session Lecture 20\\n\\n 1. Mechanism & Classification Slide 9 16 \\nIndirect-acting agents inhibit Acetylcholinesterase AChE increasing the concentration of endogenous Acetylcholine in the synapse. This stimulates both Muscarinic and Nicotinic receptors.\\n Quaternary Amines No CNS entry Neostigmine Pyridostigmine.\\n Tertiary Amines Enter CNS Physostigmine Donepezil Organophosphates.\\n\\n 2. Clinical Uses Slide 20 36 \\n\\n Neuromuscular Junction Disorders\\n Myasthenia Gravis MG \\n Pathology Autoantibodies against Nicotinic receptors Nm .\\n Treatment Pyridostigmine Oral long-acting or Neostigmine .\\n Diagnosis Edrophonium Tensilon test. Rapid short-acting. Improvement = +MG. Historical .\\n Reversal of NMJ Blockade \\n Neostigmine is used to reverse non-depolarizing neuromuscular blockers e.g. Rocuronium after surgery.\\n\\n CNS Disorders\\n Alzheimer s Disease \\n Drugs Donepezil Rivastigmine Galantamine .\\n Mechanism Increase CNS ACh to improve cognition/memory.\\n Anticholinergic Toxicity Atropine Overdose \\n Drug Physostigmine .\\n Why? It crosses the BBB to reverse central delirium unlike Neostigmine .\\n\\n Glaucoma\\n Echothiophate Organophosphate Long acting .\\n\\n 3. Toxicity & Poisoning Slide 24-28 \\n\\n Organophosphates Insecticides Nerve Gas \\n Mechanism Irreversible inhibition of AChE via phosphorylation . \\ Aging\\ makes the bond permanent.\\n Muscarinic Signs DUMBELS D iarrhea U rination M iosis B radycardia/Bronchospasm E mesis L acrimation S alivation.\\n Nicotinic Signs Fasciculations → Paralysis Depolarizing blockade .\\n Treatment \\n 1. Atropine Blocks Muscarinic effects life-saving for lungs/heart .\\n 2. Pralidoxime 2-PAM Regenerates AChE IF given before aging. Relieves neuromuscular paralysis Atropine does not .\\n\\n 4. Clinical Correlates Slide 22 \\n\\n correlate\\n Myasthenic vs. Cholinergic Crisis \\n Myasthenic Crisis Low ACh. Respiratory failure due to undertreatment of MG. Action Give more Pyridostigmine.\\n Cholinergic Crisis Excess ACh. Depolarizing blockade due to overtreatment. Action Stop drug give Atropine.\\n differentiation Edrophonium test improves Myasthenic crisis but worsens Cholinergic crisis.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Ogilvie Syndrome \\n Condition Acute Colonic Pseudo-obstruction massive dilation without physical blockage .\\n Treatment Neostigmine stimulates GI motility .\\n Lecture 20 Indirect-Acting Cholinomimetics Cholinesterase Inhibitors Neuroscience questions A farmer is brought to the ER with pinpoint pupils excessive salivation difficulty breathing and muscle fasciculations. Exposure to an insecticide is suspected. Which drug should be administered immediately to treat the respiratory secretions and bradycardia? Physostigmine Pralidoxime Atropine Neostigmine Pilocarpine 2 Atropine is the first-line antidote for the life-threatening muscarinic effects bronchorrhea bronchospasm bradycardia of organophosphate poisoning. Which drug is an appropriate treatment for Alzheimer s disease? Neostigmine Pyridostigmine Donepezil Edrophonium Pilocarpine 2 Donepezil is a centrally acting AChE inhibitor used for Alzheimer s. Neostigmine/Pyridostigmine do not cross the BBB . A child ingests his grandmother s eye drops Atropine and develops hot red skin dilated pupils and delirium. Which antidote can reverse the CNS delirium? Neostigmine Physostigmine Pralidoxime Donepezil Edrophonium 1 The patient has Anticholinergic toxicity Mad as a hatter . Physostigmine is a tertiary amine that crosses the BBB to reverse CNS toxicity. What is the mechanism of action of Pralidoxime 2-PAM ? Blocks muscarinic receptors Blocks nicotinic receptors Regenerates Acetylcholinesterase Inhibits Acetylcholine release Directly activates GABA receptors 2 Pralidoxime is a cholinesterase regenerator. It breaks the bond between the organophosphate and the enzyme if given before aging . Which drug is preferred for long-term oral management of Myasthenia Gravis? Edrophonium Physostigmine Pyridostigmine Echothiophate Acetylcholine 2 Pyridostigmine has a longer duration of action 4-6h and is oral making it ideal for maintenance therapy in Myasthenia Gravis . Antidote for Organophosphate Poisoning Muscarinic Atropine Toxicology Antidote for Organophosphate Poisoning Enzyme Regenerator Pralidoxime 2-PAM Toxicology Antidote for Atropine Overdose Physostigmine Toxicology Drug for Myasthenia Gravis Maintenance Pyridostigmine Pharmacology Drug for Alzheimer s Disease AChE Inhibitor Donepezil Pharmacology Why doesn t Neostigmine treat Alzheimer s? It is a Quaternary amine Charged → Doesn t cross BBB Pharmacology Symptoms of Muscarinic Excess DUMBELS Diarrhea Urination Miosis Bradycardia Emesis Lacrimation Salivation Toxicology Drug used to diagnose Myasthenia Gravis Historical Edrophonium Pharmacology Mechanism of Organophosphates Irreversible inhibition of Acetylcholinesterase Toxicology Treatment for Ogilvie Syndrome Neostigmine Pharmacology SketchyPharm Autonomic Drugs > Parasympathetics > Acetylcholinesterase Inhibitors FirstAid Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs BandB Pharmacology > ANS > Cholinergic Agonists Physostigmine vs. Neostigmine Physostigmine crosses BBB Treats Atropine OD .\\\\n Neostigmine does NOT cross BBB Treats MG urinary retention . DUMBELS Mnemonic Diarrhea Urination Miosis Bradycardia Emesis Lacrimation Salivation Muscarinic excess . Organophosphate Antidotes 1. Atropine Muscarinic symptoms .\\\\n2. Pralidoxime Nicotinic/Muscle symptoms - regenerates enzyme . Myasthenia Gravis Treatment Pyridostigmine Long-acting oral . Increases ACh at NMJ. Alzheimer s Drugs Donepezil Rivastigmine Galantamine . Increase CNS ACh. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l21", "title": "Lecture 21: Muscarinic Antagonists (Anticholinergics)", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l21 module Neuro l21 Lecture 21 Muscarinic Antagonists Anticholinergics \\n\\n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D.\\n Session Lecture 21\\n\\n 1. Class Overview Slide 7-8 \\n Mechanism Competitive antagonists or inverse agonists at Muscarinic receptors M1-M5 .\\n Structure \\n Tertiary Amines Cross BBB Atropine Scopolamine Benztropine.\\n Quaternary Ammonium No CNS Ipratropium Tiotropium Glycopyrrolate.\\n\\n 2. Clinical Uses Slide 21-23 \\n Atropine \\n Bradycardia Blocks vagal tone M2 → Increases HR.\\n Antidote For Organophosphate poisoning.\\n Pre-op Reduces metabolic secretions.\\n Note Low doses can cause Paradoxical Bradycardia blocks presynaptic M2 autoreceptors .\\n Scopolamine \\n Motion Sickness Transdermal patch bypasses first-pass .\\n Respiratory COPD/Asthma \\n Ipratropium Short & Tiotropium Long . Block bronchoconstriction + decrease secretions.\\n Urinary Incontinence Overactive Bladder \\n Oxybutynin . Relaxes detrusor muscle.\\n Parkinson s Disease \\n Benztropine . Treats tremor restores ACh/Dopamine balance .\\n Ophthalmology \\n Tropicamide/Cyclopentolate . Induce Mydriasis Dilation & Cycloplegia Paralysis of accommodation for exams.\\n\\n 3. Toxicity Anticholinergic Toxidrome Slide 26-28 \\n Red as a Beet Cutaneous flushing vasodilation to dissipate heat .\\n Dry as a Bone No sweating Anhidrosis dry mouth.\\n Hot as Hades Hyperthermia cant sweat .\\n Blind as a Bat Mydriasis dilated pupils + Cycloplegia blurry near vision .\\n Mad as a Hatter Delirium Hallucinations Central toxicity .\\n Full as a Flask Urinary Retention.\\n Treatment Physostigmine crosses BBB - Use with caution! \\n\\n 4. Contraindications Slide 25 \\n Narrow-Angle Glaucoma Dilation blocks outflow → Acute crisis.\\n BPH Prostatic Hyperplasia Worsens urinary retention.\\n Elderly Risk of falls confusion delirium BEERS criteria .\\n GI Obstruction/Ileus Worsens constipation.\\n\\n 5. Clinical Correlates Slide 31 \\n\\n correlate\\n Drug Interactions \\n Oxybutynin + Macrolides Erythromycin Macrolides inhibit CYP3A4 . Oxybutynin is metabolized by CYP3A4. Result → Anticholinergic Toxicity .\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Use in Special Populations \\n Children High risk of Hyperthermia \\ Hot as Hades\\ .\\n Elderly High risk of Delirium \\ Mad as a Hatter\\ and Falls. Avoid if possible.\\n Lecture 21 Muscarinic Antagonists Anticholinergics Neuroscience questions A 75-year-old male with benign prostatic hyperplasia BPH takes an over-the-counter cold medication and develops acute urinary retention. Which ingredient likely caused this? Guaifenesin Acetaminophen Diphenhydramine Anticholinergic Ibuprofen Pseudoephedrine 2 Diphenhydramine has strong anticholinergic properties which can precipitate urinary retention in patients with BPH. Why is Atropine contraindicated in patients with Narrow-Angle Glaucoma? It causes miosis It increases aqueous humor production It causes mydriasis which blocks the outflow angle It causes cataract formation It lowers intraocular pressure too much 2 Atropine causes Mydriasis dilation . In narrow-angle glaucoma the bunched-up iris blocks the trabecular meshwork dangerously increasing pressure. To prevent motion sickness a patient applies a Transderm Scop patch. What is the active drug and its mechanism? Scopolamine; Muscarinic Antagonist Scopolamine; Nicotinic Antagonist Meclizine; H1 Antagonist Ondansetron; 5-HT3 Antagonist Metoclopramide; D2 Antagonist 0 Scopolamine is a Muscarinic Antagonist used transdermally for motion sickness prevention. Which drug is used to treat tremors in Parkinson s disease by restoring the balance between Acetylcholine and Dopamine? Atropine Ipratropium Benztropine Oxybutynin Succinylcholine 2 Benztropine is a centrally acting antimuscarinic used to treat Parkinsonian tremors Parkinson s = Low Dopamine + High ACh . A child presents with flushed skin dry mouth dilated pupils and tachycardia after eating berries from a garden plant. The most likely plant is Foxglove Digitalis Jimson Weed Datura/Atropine Oleander Poison Ivy Mushrooms Muscarine 1 Jimson Weed Datura stramonium contains belladonna alkaloids Atropine/Scopolamine causing the classic anticholinergic toxidrome. Anticholinergic Toxidrome Mad as a Hatter Delirium / Hallucinations Toxicology Anticholinergic Toxidrome Blind as a Bat Mydriasis + Cycloplegia Blurred vision Toxicology Drug for Overactive Bladder Oxybutynin Pharmacology Inhaled Muscarinic Antagonist for COPD Tiotropium Long or Ipratropium Short Pharmacology Why Atropine causes Tachycardia Blocks M2 receptors on SA node Removes vagal brake Physiology Mechanism of Paradoxical Bradycardia Blockade of Presynaptic Autoreceptors M2 → Increased ACh release Physiology Contraindication Narrow Angle Glaucoma Muscarinic Antagonists Atropine Pharmacology Contraindication BPH Muscarinic Antagonists Causes Retention Pharmacology Drug for Motion Sickness Patch Scopolamine Pharmacology Treats Parkinson s Tremor Benztropine Pharmacology SketchyPharm Autonomic Drugs > Parasympathetics > Muscarinic Antagonists FirstAid Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs BandB Pharmacology > ANS > Muscarinic Antagonists Anticholinergic Toxidrome Red Flush Dry No sweat Hot Hyperthermia Blind Mydriasis Mad Delirium Full Retention . Paradoxical Bradycardia Low dose Atropine blocks Presynaptic M2 autoreceptors → Increases ACh releases → Slows Heart. Reversal Agents Atropine reverses AChE Inhibitors.\\\\n Physostigmine reverses Atropine. Contraindications Glaucoma Angle Closure BPH Retention Elderly Delirium . Ipratropium/Tiotropium Quaternary amines No CNS . Inhaled for COPD/Asthma . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l22", "title": "Lecture 22: Ganglionic and Neuromuscular Blockers", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l22 module Neuro l22 Lecture 22 Ganglionic and Neuromuscular Blockers\\n\\n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D.\\n Session Lecture 22\\n\\n 1. Ganglionic Blockers Slide 7-9 \\n Target Neuronal Nicotinic Receptors Nn at autonomic ganglia.\\n Mecamylamine \\n Mechanism Blocks output of BOTH Sympathetic and Parasympathetic systems.\\n Effect Predictable based on Dominant Tone \\n Arterioles Symp dominant Vasodilation → Hypotension Orthostatic .\\n Heart Para dominant Tachycardia.\\n Gut/Bladder Para dominant Constipation Urinary Retention.\\n Eye Para dominant Mydriasis Cycloplegia.\\n\\n 2. Neuromuscular Blockers NMBAs Slide 19-23 \\nTarget Nicotinic Nm receptors at the Neuromuscular Junction NMJ .\\n\\n Depolarizing NMBA\\n Succinylcholine SCh \\n Structure Two ACh molecules.\\n Mechanism Agonist. Causes sustained depolarization → Fasciculations → Flaccid paralysis Phase I . Desensitization Phase II .\\n Metabolism Plasma Cholinesterase Pseudocholinesterase .\\n Duration Very short <8 min . Prolonged in patients with genetic deficiency. \\n Adverse Effects \\n Malignant Hyperthermia MH Triggered by SCh + Inhaled Anesthetics e.g. Halothane . Muscle rigidity hyperthermia. Treat with Dantrolene .\\n Hyperkalemia Life-threatening in Burn/Trauma patients due to receptor upregulation/rhabdomyolysis .\\n\\n Non-Depolarizing NMBAs Competitive Antagonists \\n Isoquinolines \\n Atracurium Degraded by Hofmann Elimination spontaneous no organ weakness . Side Effect Histamine release Hypotension + Laudanosine accumulation Seizures .\\n Cisatracurium Hofmann elimination. No histamine. Safe for organ failure.\\n Aminosteroids \\n Rocuronium Fast onset can replace SCh for intubation . Biliary excretion.\\n Vecuronium Renal/Biliary excretion.\\n\\n 3. Reversal Slide 27 \\n Cholinesterase Inhibitors Neostigmine Increase ACh to compete with NMBAs. Always give with Atropine to prevent muscarinic bradycardia.\\n Sugammadex Specialized chelator. Encapsulates Rocuronium/Vecuronium for rapid reversal. No muscarinic side effects.\\n\\n 4. Clinical Correlates\\n\\n correlate\\n Malignant Hyperthermia \\n Trigger Succinylcholine or Volatile Anesthetics.\\n Genetic Mutation in Ryanodine Receptor RyR1 .\\n Pathology Uncontrolled Ca2+ release from SR → Rigidity → Heat/Acidosis.\\n Cure Dantrolene Blocks RyR1 .\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Hofmann Elimination \\n Drug Atracurium / Cisatracurium.\\n Concept Breaks down spontaneously in plasma based on pH and temperature.\\n Pearl Ideal for patients with multisystem organ failure Kidney/Liver don t matter .\\n Lecture 22 Ganglionic and Neuromuscular Blockers Neuroscience questions A patient undergoes surgery and develops muscle rigidity tachycardia and a temperature of 105\\u00b0F shortly after induction. Which drug is the most likely trigger? Rocuronium Succinylcholine Cisatracurium Propofol Fentanyl 1 Succinylcholine is a classic trigger for Malignant Hyperthermia along with volatile anesthetics . Which neuromuscular blocker undergoes spontaneous Hofmann elimination and is therefore safe in patients with renal and hepatic failure? Pancuronium Rocuronium Vecuronium Cisatracurium Succinylcholine 3 Cisatracurium and Atracurium degrades spontaneously in plasma Hofmann elimination independent of organ function. What is the mechanism of action of Sugammadex? Inhibits Acetylcholinesterase Blocks Ryanodine Receptors Chelates/Encapsulates Aminosteroid NMBAs Stimulates Nicotinic Receptors Blocks Muscarinic Receptors 2 Sugammadex forms a complex with aminosteroids Rocuronium/Vecuronium to reverse blockade rapidly. A patient with severe burns requires intubation. Which neuromuscular blocker is CONTRAINDICATED due to the risk of hyperkalemia? Rocuronium Cisatracurium Vecuronium Atracurium Succinylcholine 4 Succinylcholine causes depolarization and K+ efflux. In burn patients upregulated receptors this can cause massive hyperkalemia and cardiac arrest. The use of Neostigmine to reverse neuromuscular blockade can cause bradycardia. Which drug is co-administered to prevent this? Pralidoxime Physostigmine Atropine Epinephrine Dantrolene 2 Neostigmine increases ACh everywhere Muscarinic + Nicotinic . Atropine is given to block the Muscarinic effects Bradycardia while allowing Nicotinic effects Muscle strength . Contraindication for Succinylcholine Burns Trauma Hyperkalemia Hx of Malignant Hyperthermia Pharmacology Treatment for Malignant Hyperthermia Dantrolene Pharmacology NMBA metabolized by Plasma Cholinesterase Succinylcholine Pharmacology NMBA eliminated by Hofmann Elimination Cisatracurium / Atracurium Pharmacology Depolarizing Neuromuscular Blocker Succinylcholine Pharmacology Dominant Autonomic Tone Heart Parasympathetic Vagal Physiology Dominant Autonomic Tone Blood Vessels Sympathetic Physiology Sugammadex reverses which drugs? Rocuronium and Vecuronium Pharmacology Why give Atropine with Neostigmine reversal? To prevent Muscarinic side effects Bradycardia Pharmacology Ganglion Blocker Drug Name Mecamylamine Pharmacology SketchyPharm Autonomic Drugs > Parasympathetics > Neuromuscular Blockers FirstAid Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs BandB Pharmacology > ANS > NMBAs Succinylcholine & Potassium Causes K+ efflux. Contraindicated in Burns Trauma Crush injuries and Denervation risk of Cardiac Arrest . Dantrolene Mechanism Blocks Ryanodine Receptors RyR1 on Sarcoplasmic Reticulum. Treats Malignant Hyperthermia. Hofmann Elimination Atracurium/Cisatracurium . Spontaneous degradation. Safe in renal/hepatic failure. Dominant Tone Concept Ganglionic blockers remove the dominant tone. Vessels = Symp Block → Hypotension . Heart = Para Block → Tachycardia . Sugammadex Encapsulates Rocuronium/Vecuronium . Reversal without cholinergic side effects. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l23", "title": "Lecture 23: Pathology of Auditory and Vestibular System", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l23 module Neuro l23 Lecture 23 Pathology of Auditory and Vestibular System\\n\\n Lecturer A. Reno D.O.\\n Session Lecture 23\\n\\n 1. Approach to Dizziness Slide 6-27 \\n Vertigo Illusion of movement \\ Spinning\\ .\\n Peripheral Inner Ear \\n Nystagmus Unidirectional usually horizontal/rotary .\\n Fixation Extinguishes nystagmus.\\n Walking Patient can usually walk.\\n Hearing Often affected Tinnitus/Loss .\\n Central Brainstem/Cerebellum - Stroke/MS \\n Nystagmus Bidirectional or Vertical .\\n Fixation Does NOT extinguish .\\n Walking Patient often cannot stand/walk Ataxia .\\n Hearing Usually normal.\\n \\ Red Flags\\ Comparison to Stroke HINTS Exam .\\n\\n 2. Key Vestibular Pathologies\\n Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo BPPV \\n Duration Seconds < 1 minute .\\n Trigger Head movement Rolling over in bed .\\n Mechanism Dislodged Otoconia Calcium Carbonate crystals in posterior semicircular canal.\\n Diagnosis Dix-Hallpike maneuver Rotary nystagmus .\\n Treatment Epley Maneuver .\\n\\n Meniere s Disease Endolymphatic Hydrops \\n Duration Hours 20 min - 24 hrs .\\n Classic Triad \\n 1. Episodic Vertigo .\\n 2. Tinnitus \\ Roaring\\ .\\n 3. Sensorineural Hearing Loss Fluctuating low frequency .\\n Mechanism Excess Endolymph fluid.\\n Treatment Low salt diet Diuretics HCTZ/Triamterene .\\n\\n Vestibulo-Cochlear Infections\\n Vestibular Neuritis Vertigo only No hearing loss . Duration Days . Usually viral. Rx Corticosteroids.\\n Labyrinthitis Vertigo + Hearing Loss . Viral or Bacterial.\\n\\n 3. Hearing Loss Evaluation Slide 40-47 \\n Conductive CHL \\n Anatomy Outer/Middle ear Wax Otitis Media Otosclerosis .\\n Weber Lateralizes to BAD ear.\\n Rinne BC > AC Bone Conduction > Air Conduction .\\n\\n Sensorineural SNHL \\n Anatomy Cochlea/Nerve Presbycusis Noise Meniere s Acoustic Neuroma .\\n Weber Lateralizes to GOOD ear.\\n Rinne AC > BC Normal pattern but reduced .\\n\\n 4. Clinical Correlates\\n correlate\\n The HINTS Exam \\nUsed to distinguish Central Stroke from Peripheral vertigo in acute vestibular syndrome.\\n1. H ead I mpulse Normal impulse = Central bad . Peripheral has corrective saccade .\\n2. N ystagmus Direction changing = Central .\\n3. T est of S kew Vertical Skew = Central .\\n Pearl \\ INFARCT\\ Impulse Normal Fast-phase Alternating Refixation on Cover Test .\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Acoustic Neuroma Vestibular Schwannoma \\n Unilateral Sensorineural Hearing Loss + Tinnitus.\\n Slow growing.\\n Diagnose with MRI .\\n Lecture 23 Pathology of Auditory and Vestibular System Neuroscience questions A 45-year-old woman complains of room-spinning dizziness that lasts for 30 seconds when she rolls over in bed. It is associated with nausea. What is the most likely diagnosis? Meniere s Disease Vestibular Neuritis Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo BPPV Acoustic Neuroma Multiple Sclerosis 2 BPPV is characterized by brief <1 min episodes of vertigo triggered by head position changes. A patient presents with vertigo tinnitus and hearing loss in the right ear. The episodes last for about 4 hours. Which treatment is most appropriate for long-term management? Epley Maneuver Antibiotics Low salt diet and Diuretics Carbamazepine Chemotherapy 2 The patient has Meniere s Disease Triad + Duration . Treatment involves reducing endolymph fluid with salt restriction and diuretics . Weber test lateralizes to the LEFT ear. Rinne test shows Bone Conduction > Air Conduction on the LEFT. What is the diagnosis? Right Sensorineural Hearing Loss Right Conductive Hearing Loss Left Sensorineural Hearing Loss Left Conductive Hearing Loss Normal Hearing 3 Weber to the Left + Rinne BC > AC on Left = Left Conductive Hearing Loss . In CHL bone conduction is better than air and it lateralizes to the affected ear . Which finding on physical exam strongly suggests a CENTRAL cause of vertigo e.g. Stroke ? Nystagmus that suppresses with fixation Unidirectional horizontal nystagmus Positive Head Impulse Test Corrective Saccade Vertical Nystagmus Hearing Loss 3 Vertical nystagmus or direction-changing nystagmus is a red flag for Central pathology. Peripheral nystagmus is usually horizontal/rotary and suppresses with fixation. A patient has sudden onset of severe vertigo lasting 3 days with nausea and vomiting but NO hearing loss. Findings include horizontal nystagmus. What is the most likely diagnosis? Labyrinthitis Vestibular Neuritis Meniere s Disease BPPV Otosclerosis 1 Vestibular Neuritis presents with continuous vertigo for days often post-viral with preserved hearing . Labyrinthitis would include hearing loss . Diagnosis Vertigo < 1 minute triggered by movement BPPV Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo ENT Diagnosis Vertigo Hours + Tinnitus + Hearing Loss Meniere s Disease ENT Diagnosis Vertigo Days + No Hearing Loss Vestibular Neuritis ENT Diagnosis Vertigo Days + Hearing Loss Labyrinthitis ENT Weber Test Lateralization Conductive Loss Lateralizes to the BAD affected ear Diagnosis Weber Test Lateralization Sensorineural Loss Lateralizes to the GOOD unaffected ear Diagnosis Maneuver to Diagnose BPPV Dix-Hallpike Diagnosis Maneuver to Treat BPPV Epley Maneuver Treatment Mechanism of Meniere s Disease Endolymphatic Hydrops Excess fluid in inner ear Pathology Central vs Peripheral Nystagmus Fixation Peripheral suppresses with fixation. Central does NOT. Diagnosis AMBOSS Neurology > Vertigo and Dizziness FirstAid Neurology > Otology BandB Neurology > The Ear Vertigo Duration Clues < 1 min BPPV.\\\\n Hours Meniere s Disease.\\\\n Days Vestibular Neuritis / Labyrinthitis. Peripheral vs. Central Nystagmus Peripheral Extinguishes with fixation. Horizontal/Rotary.\\\\n Central Does NOT extinguish. Vertical or Direction-Changing. Meniere s Triad 1. Episodic Vertigo\\\\n2. Tinnitus Roaring \\\\n3. Sensorineural Hearing Loss. Weber/Rinne Rules Conductive Bad ear hears Weber louder. BC > AC.\\\\n Sensorineural Good ear hears Weber louder. AC > BC. Treatment of BPPV Epley Maneuver Repositioning of otoconia . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l24", "title": "Lecture 24: Olfaction and Taste", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l24 module Neuro l24 Lecture 24 Olfaction and Taste\\n\\n Lecturer G. Otazu Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 24\\n\\n 1. Olfaction Smell \\n Receptors Bipolar neurons in olfactory epithelium.\\n Turnover Every 7 days stem cells .\\n Receptors GPCRs.\\n Pathway \\n 1. Olfactory Nerves CN I pass through Cribriform Plate of Ethmoid bone.\\n 2. Synapse in Glomeruli of Olfactory Bulb.\\n 3. Mitral/Tufted Cells Second order form Olfactory Tract.\\n 4. Project directly to Primary Olfactory Cortex Piriform Cortex Amygdala Entorhinal Cortex.\\n Note Olfaction is the ONLY sense that bypasses the Thalamus before reaching the cortex.\\n\\n 2. Taste Gustation \\n Modalities \\n Salty Na+ influx Amiloride-sensitive channels .\\n Sour H+ ions Block K+ channels .\\n Sweet/Bitter/Umami GPCRs G-protein coupled .\\n Innervation \\n Anterior 2/3 CN VII Facial - Chorda Tympani .\\n Posterior 1/3 CN IX Glossopharyngeal .\\n Epiglottis/Pharynx CN X Vagus .\\n Pathway \\n 1. Nerves project to Nucleus Tractus Solitarius NTS in Medulla.\\n 2. Project to VPM of Thalamus.\\n 3. Project to Gustatory Cortex Insula/Frontal Operculum .\\n\\n 3. Clinical Correlates\\n correlate\\n Kallmann Syndrome \\n Triad Anosmia + Delayed Puberty Hypogonadism .\\n Mechanism Failure of GnRH neurons to migrate from olfactory placode to hypothalamus.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Foster-Kennedy Syndrome \\n Cause Meningioma of the Olfactory Groove.\\n Triad \\n 1. Ipsilateral Anosmia Tumor compresses olfactory tract .\\n 2. Ipsilateral Optic Atrophy Tumor compresses Optic Nerve .\\n 3. Contralateral Papilledema Increased intracranial pressure .\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Uncinate Fits Temporal Lobe Epilepsy \\n Seizures originating in the Uncus Medial Temporal Lobe .\\n Aura Hallucination of foul smells Cacosmia .\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Neurodegenerative Diseases \\n Parkinson s / Alzheimer s Hyposmia decreased smell is often the earliest clinical sign appearing years before motor or cognitive symptoms.\\n Lecture 24 Olfaction and Taste Neuroscience questions A 16-year-old male presents with delayed puberty. Physical exam reveals small testes and a complete inability to smell since birth. What is the most likely diagnosis? Klinefelter Syndrome Kallmann Syndrome Foster-Kennedy Syndrome Turner Syndrome Uncinate Epilepsy 1 Kallmann Syndrome is characterized by hypogonadotropic hypogonadism delayed puberty and anosmia due to migration failure of GnRH neurons. A patient presents with seizures preceded by a hallucination of a foul smell burning rubber . Where is the seizure focus likely located? Postcentral Gyrus Occipital Lobe Uncus Temporal Lobe Frontal Lobe Cerebellum 2 Uncinate fits are temporal lobe seizures involving the Uncus primary olfactory cortex area resulting in olfactory hallucinations Cacosmia . Which cranial nerve is responsible for taste sensation on the anterior two-thirds of the tongue? Trigeminal V Facial VII Glossopharyngeal IX Vagus X Hypoglossal XII 1 The Facial Nerve CN VII carries taste from the anterior 2/3 of the tongue via the Chorda Tympani . A 55-year-old woman presents with loss of smell in her right ear and vision loss in her right eye. Fundoscopy reveals right optic atrophy and left papilledema. What is the most likely diagnosis? Pituitary Adenoma Foster-Kennedy Syndrome Multiple Sclerosis Occipital Stroke Glioblastoma Multiforme 1 Foster-Kennedy Syndrome Olfactory Groove Meningioma causes ipsilateral anosmia and optic atrophy direct compression and contralateral papilledema ICP . Loss of olfaction is often an early prodromal sign of which neurodegenerative disorder? Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis ALS Parkinson s Disease Huntington s Disease Multiple Sclerosis Myasthenia Gravis 1 Hyposmia loss of smell frequently precedes motor symptoms in Parkinson s Disease and Alzheimer s by several years. Diagnosis Anosmia + No Puberty Kallmann Syndrome Neurology Diagnosis Ipsilateral Anosmia/Optic Atrophy + Contralateral Papilledema Foster-Kennedy Syndrome Neurology CN for Taste Anterior 2/3 Tongue CN VII Facial Anatomy CN for Taste Posterior 1/3 Tongue CN IX Glossopharyngeal Anatomy Taste Modalities using Ion Channels Salty Na+ and Sour H+ Physiology Taste Modalities using GPCRs Sweet Bitter Umami Physiology Seizure aura with foul smell Cacosmia Uncinate Fit Temporal Lobe/Uncus Neurology Unique feature of Olfactory Pathway Does not relay in Thalamus before Cortex Anatomy Primary Olfactory Cortex location Piriform Cortex Temporal Lobe Anatomy Thalamic Nucleus for Taste VPM Ventral Posteromedial Anatomy AMBOSS Neurology > Cranial Nerves FirstAid Neurology > Otology/Ophthalmology BandB Neurology > The Chemical Senses Olfactory Pathway Uniqueness Olfaction is the only sensory modality that projects directly to the cortex without relaying through the Thalamus first. Kallmann Syndrome Teenager with No Smell + No Puberty . GnRH neuron migration failure . Foster-Kennedy Syndrome Olfactory Groove Meningioma Ipsilateral Anosmia/Optic Atrophy + Contralateral Papilledema . Taste Innervation Anterior 2/3 = CN VII .\\\\nPosterior 1/3 = CN IX .\\\\nThroat = CN X . Taste Transduction Salty/Sour = Ion Channels .\\\\nSweet/Bitter/Umami = GPCRs . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l25", "title": "Lecture 25: Limbic System - Neuroanatomy, Regional Function and Limbic Pathology", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l25 module Neuro l25 Lecture 25 Limbic System - Neuroanatomy Regional Function and Limbic Pathology lecturer Jennifer Xie Ph.D. session Lecture 25 Neuroscience prev l24 next l26 pdf pdfs/Lecture 25. J. Xie Ph.D. PPT -1.pdf transcript transcripts/Lecture 25 Limbic System - Neuroanatomy Regional Function and Limbic Pathology Faculty J. Xie Ph.D..srt Lecture 25 Limbic System - Neuroanatomy Regional Function and Limbic Pathology Lecturer Jennifer Xie Ph.D. Session Lecture 25 1. Introduction to the Limbic System Slide 5-7 The Limbic System Latin limbus = border is a complex network of cortical and diencephalic structures located on the medial border of the cerebral hemisphere. It serves as a bridge linking the conscious intellectual functions of the cerebral cortex with the unconscious autonomic regulation of the brainstem and hypothalamus . Primary Function Regulates primitive emotional drives memory storage/retrieval and homeostasis. The 5 F s Mnemonic mnemonic Functions of the Limbic System The 5 F s F eeding Satiety/Hunger F leeing Fear response F ighting Aggression F eeling Emotion F ornicating Sexual behavior Key Structures While the exact composition is debated the Big 3 universally agreed-upon components are 1. Cingulate Gyrus Emotional processing of sensory input. 2. Hippocampal Formation Memory consolidation and spatial navigation. 3. Amygdala Fear anger and emotional association. Other components include the Mammillary Bodies Hypothalamus Anterior Thalamic Nuclei Septal Area Reward and Orbitofrontal Cortex Social behavior . 2. Cingulate Gyrus Slide 9-10 The Cingulate Gyrus wraps around the corpus callosum and acts as a gateway between the cortex and limbic system. Function It adds emotional coloring to sensory perceptions e.g. how pain feels emotionally . Clinical Relevance Cingulotomy A surgical lesioning of the anterior cingulate cortex. Historically used to treat intractable pain or severe depression/anxiety. Patients report they still feel the pain but it doesn t bother them anymore loss of the affective/suffering component . 3. Hippocampal Formation & Memory Slide 13-17 The Hippocampus Latin for Seahorse is critical for learning memory consolidation and spatial navigation. Structure Composed of the Dentate Gyrus Hippocampus Proper Cornu Ammonis CA1-CA4 and Subiculum . Vulnerability The CA1 pyramidal cells are exceptionally sensitive to hypoxia/ischemia . They will die within minutes of oxygen deprivation Sommer s Sector . Lateralization Right Hippocampus Spatial Orientation and navigation e.g. London Taxi Drivers have enlarged right posterior hippocampi . Left Hippocampus Verbal memory. The Fornix Slide 16-17 The major output tract of the hippocampus. Pathway Hippocampus → Fimbria → Crus → Body → Columns of Fornix → Mammillary Bodies Hypothalamus . correlate Temporal Lobe Epilepsy Pathophysiology The medial temporal lobe Hippocampus/Amygdala has the lowest seizure threshold in the brain. Presentation Complex Partial Psychomotor Seizures . Preceded by an Aura e.g. burning rubber smell/uncinate fits hallucinations deja vu . Automatisms Lip smacking chewing swallowing or fumbling of hands. Altered consciousness dreamy state . correlate Herpes Simplex Encephalitis Pathophysiology HSV-1 has a specific tropism for the limbic cortex Medial Temporal Lobes/Orbitofrontal cortex . Presentation Acute onset of fever headache seizures and bizarre behavior. Sequelae Bilateral necrosis of the medial temporal lobes leads to profound Anterograde Amnesia inability to form new memories and Kluver-Bucy-like symptoms. Imaging Hyperintensity in temporal lobes on MRI Slide 12 . 4. The Papez Circuit Slide 30-31 Discovered by James Papez 1937 this is the fundamental circuit for Episodic Memory Consolidation and emotional expression. highyield The Papez Circuit Pathway 1. Hippocampus Subiculum 2. $\\\\xrightarrow \\\\text Fornix $ Mammillary Bodies 3. $\\\\xrightarrow \\\\text Mammillothalamic Tract $ Anterior Thalamic Nucleus 4. $\\\\xrightarrow \\\\text Internal Capsule Ant. Limb $ Cingulate Gyrus 5. $\\\\xrightarrow \\\\text Cingulum bundle $ Entorhinal Cortex / Hippocampus The circuit closes 5. Amygdala & Emotion Slide 19-23 The Amygdala Almond is located deep in the uncus of the temporal lobe anterior to the hippocampus. Function Mediates Fear Anger Aggression and the emotional significance of memories e.g. fear conditioning . Connections Hypothalamus Triggers autonomic responses to emotion tachycardia sweating . Olfactory System Why smells trigger strong emotional memories Proustian memory . correlate Kluver-Bucy Syndrome Pathophysiology Bilateral lesion of the Amygdala . associated with HSV encephalitis trauma or lobectomies. Presentation 1. Placidity Loss of fear and aggression docile . 2. Hyperorality Putting inappropriate objects in mouth. 3. Hypersexuality Indiscriminate sexual behavior. 4. Hypermetamorphosis Compulsive visual exploration of objects. 5. Visual Agnosia Psychic blindness inability to recognize emotional significance of objects . 6. Memory Disorders Slide 36-39 Classification of Memory 1. Explicit Declarative Facts Semantic and Events Episodic . Requires Medial Temporal Lobe Hippocampus & Diencephalon. 2. Implicit Procedural Skills habits conditioning. Skills e.g. riding a bike Basal Ganglia Cerebellum Motor Cortex. Emotional Conditioning Amygdala. Skeletal Conditioning Cerebellum. Clinical Cases correlate Patient H.M. History Bilateral medial temporal lobectomy for intractable epilepsy. Deficit Severe Anterograde Amnesia cannot form new explicit memories . Retrograde memory pre-surgery was largely intact. Key Finding His Procedural Memory was intact he could learn to solve the Tower of Hanoi puzzle but couldn t remember practicing it . Implication Proved that hippocampus is essential for consolidation of new declarative memories but not for procedural skills. correlate Wernicke-Korsakoff Syndrome Pathophysiology Thiamine Vitamin B1 Deficiency intimately associated with chronic Alcoholism . Lesion Sites Mammillary Bodies hemorrhagic necrosis Dorsomedial Thalamus Periaqueductal gray. Wernicke s Encephalopathy Acute/Reversible Triad C onfusion A taxia N ystagmus/Ophthalmoplegia \\ CAN of beer\\ . Korsakoff s Psychosis Chronic/Irreversible Severe Anterograde Amnesia . Confabulation Making up stories to fill memory gaps lack of insight . Treatment IV Thiamine BEFORE Glucose Glucose first precipitates the encephalopathy . correlate Alzheimer s Disease Pathophysiology Widespread cortical atrophy specifically Hippocampal Atrophy Early memory loss. Nucleus Basalis of Meynert Degeneration of Cholinergic neurons. Neurochemistry Deficiency of Acetylcholine ACh . Treatment AChE Inhibitors Aricept to increase synaptic ACh Slide 38 . 7. Septal Area & Reward Slide 25 Located near the anterior commissure. Contains the Nucleus Accumbens . Function The Pleasure Center . Mediates reward and reinforcement. Neurotransmitter Dopamine Mesolimbic pathway . Clinical Implicated in Addiction . The 5 F s of the Limbic System Feeding Fleeing Fighting Feeling and Sex. Sommer s Sector Vulnerability CA1 Pyramidal cells in the hippocampus are the most sensitive neurons to hypoxia . First to die in ischemic stroke or cardiorespiratory arrest. Papez Circuit Sequence Hippocampus → Fornix → Mammillary Bodies → Ant. Thalamic Nucleus → Cingulate Gyrus → Hippocampus. Kluver-Bucy Syndrome Bilateral Amygdala lesion. Hyper sexuality Hyper orality Placidity No fear . Wernicke s Triad Confusion Ataxia Nystagmus/Ophthalmoplegia CAN of Beer . Caused by B1 Thiamine deficiency harming Mammillary Bodies . Procedural vs. Declarative Memory Hippocampal damage Patient H.M. destroys Declarative New facts/events but spares Procedural Skills/Habits - Basal Ganglia/Cerebellum . HSV Encephalitis Target HSV-1 loves the Medial Temporal Lobe . Causes hemorrhagic necrosis fever seizures and bizarre behavior. questions A chronic alcoholic is brought to the ER with confusion ataxia and ophthalmoplegia. MRI reveals degeneration of the mammillary bodies. Which nutrient deficiency is responsible? Vitamin B12 Cobalamin Vitamin B1 Thiamine Vitamin B3 Niacin Vitamin B6 Pyridoxine Vitamin C Ascorbic Acid 1 Wernicke-Korsakoff Syndrome is caused by Thiamine B1 deficiency common in alcoholics. It damages the Mammillary Bodies and medial thalamus. The triad is Confusion Ataxia Nystagmus. A patient undergoes bilateral temporal lobectomy for intractable epilepsy. Post-operatively he interacts with objects by putting them in his mouth displays inappropriate sexual behavior toward staff and appears completely fearless. Which structure s absence explains these symptoms? Hippocampus Cingulate Gyrus Amygdala Mammillary Bodies Septal Nuclei 2 This is Kluver-Bucy Syndrome Hyperorality Hypersexuality Placidity . It is caused by bilateral lesions to the Amygdala . A 65-year-old male with a history of cardiac arrest 2 weeks ago now presents with severe short-term memory loss. He cannot remember what he ate for breakfast or the names of his new doctors. Which specific neuronal population was likely damaged by hypoxia? Purkinje cells of the Cerebellum Anterior Horn cells of Spinal Cord CA1 Pyramidal cells of the Hippocampus Dopaminergic neurons of Substantia Nigra Cholinergic neurons of Nucleus Basalis 2 The CA1 Pyramidal neurons of the Hippocampus Sommer s sector are the most sensitive cells in the brain to hypoxic-ischemic injury . Damage here causes anterograde amnesia. In the Papez circuit fibers travel from the mammillary bodies to the anterior nucleus of the thalamus via which tract? Fornix Cingulum Stria Terminalis Mammillothalamic Tract Medial Forebrain Bundle 3 The Mammillothalamic Tract connects the Mammillary Bodies to the Anterior Nucleus of the Thalamus . Patient H.M. had his medial temporal lobes removed. He could no longer form new memories of events Episodic but he could learn to solve puzzles Procedural . Which structure is primarily responsible for the preserved procedural memory? Hippocampus Basal Ganglia Amygdala Septal Nuclei Mammillary Bodies 1 Procedural Memory skills/habits is processed by the Basal Ganglia Cerebellum and Motor Cortex which were intact in H.M. The Hippocampus removed drives Declarative/Episodic memory. Which limbic structure is responsible for spatial navigation? Right Hippocampus Physiology What is the classic triad of Wernicke s Encephalopathy? Confusion Ataxia Nystagmus/Ophthalmoplegia CAN of Beer Pathology Bilateral lesions of the Amygdala cause what syndrome? Kluver-Bucy Syndrome Placidity Hyperorality Hypersexuality Pathology What is the major output tract of the Hippocampus? The Fornix Anatomy Which neurons are most sensitive to cerebral hypoxia? CA1 Pyramidal neurons of the Hippocampus Pathology What is the primary neurotransmitter deficiency in Alzheimer s Disease? Acetylcholine ACh due to loss of Nucleus Basalis of Meynert Pathology HSV-1 Encephalitis typically affects which brain region? Medial Temporal Lobes Limbic Cortex Pathology Damage to the Mammillary Bodies is seen in whom? Alcoholics Thiamine/B1 deficiency → Wernicke-Korsakoff Syndrome Pathology What connects the Mammillary Bodies to the Anterior Thalamus? Mammillothalamic Tract Anatomy What defines Anterograde Amnesia? Inability to form NEW memories Old memories intact Pathology BandB Neurology > Limbic System FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Limbic System Pathoma CNS > Degenerative Diseases mindmap Limbic System Components The Big 3 Cingulate Gyrus Emotional processing Pain affect Hippocampus Memory Spatial nav Right Amygdala Fear Anger Kluver-Bucy Papez Circuit Hippocampus → Fornix Mammillary Bodies → Mammillothalamic Tract Ant. Thalamus → Internal Capsule Cingulate → Cingulum Pathology Kluver-Bucy Bilateral Amygdala. Hypersex/oral. Wernicke-Korsakoff B1 Def. Mammillary Bodies. Confabulation. HSV Encephalitis Medial Temporal Lobe. Bizarre behavior. Alzheimer s Hippocampal atrophy. Low ACh. Patient H.M. Medial Temporal removal. Anterograde Amnesia. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l26", "title": "Lecture 26: Neurologic Exam Part 1 - Cranial Nerves & Mental Status", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l26 module Neuro l26 Lecture 26 Neurologic Exam Part 1 - Cranial Nerves & Mental Status\\n\\n Lecturer L. Mark D.O.\\n Session Lecture 26\\n\\n 1. Mental Status & History\\n Mental Status Exam Appearance Behavior Speech Mood Cognition.\\n Glasgow Coma Scale GCS \\n Score 3 Coma to 15 Normal .\\n Components E ye opening V erbal response M otor response. \\ EVM-456\\ .\\n\\n 2. Cranial Nerve Examination\\n CN I Olfactory \\n Test Smell Coffee Cloves one nostril at a time.\\n Deficit Anosmia. DDx COVID-19 Kallmann Syndrome Trauma .\\n\\n CN II Optic \\n Visual Acuity Snellen Chart 20/20 .\\n Visual Fields Confrontation test Static finger wiggle . detects Hemianopsia.\\n Fundoscopy \\n Red Reflex Orange glow.\\n Optic Disc Check for Papilledema blurred margins or Cupping Glaucoma .\\n\\n CN III IV VI Oculomotor Trochlear Abducens \\n Pupils CN III \\n Direct Constriction in illuminated eye.\\n Consensual Constriction in opposite eye.\\n Near Triad Convergence Constriction Accommodation.\\n EOM H-Pattern.\\n CN VI Deficit Eye cannot look out Lateral deviation failure .\\n CN III Deficit Eye is \\ Down and Out\\ + Ptosis + Dilated Pupil.\\n\\n CN V Trigeminal \\n Sensory V1 Opthalmic V2 Maxillary V3 Mandibular - Pinprick/Light touch.\\n Motor Muscles of Mastication Temporalis/Masseter clench .\\n Corneal Reflex Afferent V1 Senses touch → Efferent VII Blink .\\n\\n CN VII Facial \\n Motor Facial expression Smile Frown Puff cheeks Close eyes tight .\\n Central Lesion Stroke Spares forehead.\\n Peripheral Lesion Bell s Palsy Affects entire half of face Forehead + Lower .\\n Sensory Taste Anterior 2/3.\\n\\n CN VIII Vestibulocochlear \\n Gross Hearing Whisper test / Finger rub.\\n Weber Test Tuning fork on vertex.\\n Lateralizes to BAD ear Conductive Loss.\\n Lateralizes to GOOD ear Sensorineural Loss.\\n Rinne Test Mastoid BC vs. Air AC .\\n AC > BC Normal or Sensorineural.\\n BC > AC Conductive Loss.\\n\\n CN IX X Glossopharyngeal Vagus \\n Palate Say \\ Ah\\ . Uvula deviates AWAY from lesion.\\n Gag Reflex Sensory IX Motor X.\\n\\n CN XI Accessory \\n Motor Shrug shoulders Trapezius Turn head SCM .\\n\\n CN XII Hypoglossal \\n Motor Protrude tongue. Tongue deviates TOWARDS lesion \\ Licks the lesion\\ . Lecture 26 Neurologic Exam Part 1 - Cranial Nerves & Mental Status Neuroscience questions A patient presents with right-sided hearing loss. Weber test lateralizes to the RIGHT ear. Rinne test shows Bone Conduction > Air Conduction on the right. What is the diagnosis? Right Sensorineural Hearing Loss Right Conductive Hearing Loss Left Sensorineural Hearing Loss Left Conductive Hearing Loss Normal Hearing 1 Weber lateralizing to the affected bad ear + Rinne BC > AC indicates a Conductive Hearing Loss in that ear. On physical exam a patient s tongue deviates to the LEFT when protruded. Which cranial nerve is lesioned? Right CN XII Left CN XII Right CN X Left CN X Left CN IX 1 The tongue deviates towards the side of the CN XII lesion weak side . Left deviation = Left CN XII lesion. When asking a patient to say Ah the uvula deviates to the RIGHT. This suggests a lesion of which cranial nerve? Right CN X Left CN X Right CN IX Left CN IX Right CN XII 1 The uvula deviates away from the CN X lesion pulled by the intact strong side . Right deviation = Left CN X lesion. Which component of the corneal reflex is carried by the Facial Nerve CN VII ? Sensory Afferent Pain Sensory Afferent Touch Motor Efferent Blink Lacrimation only None 2 CN V1 provides the sensory Afferent limb. CN VII provides the Motor Efferent limb to close the eyelid Orbicularis Oculi . A patient has a dilated right pupil that is unresponsive to light and the right eye is positioned down and out . Which cranial nerve is affected? CN II CN III CN IV CN VI CN VII 1 These are classic signs of a CN III Oculomotor Palsy Ptosis Mydriasis dilation and Down-and-Out deviation. Tongue Deviation Direction Towards the Lesion CN XII Neurology Uvula Deviation Direction Away from the Lesion CN X Neurology Corneal Reflex Afferent Limb CN V1 Ophthalmic Neurology Corneal Reflex Efferent Limb CN VII Facial Neurology Rinne Test Conductive Loss Result BC > AC Bone Conduction > Air Conduction Ent CN III Palsy Signs Down and Out eye Ptosis Dilated Pupil Neurology Glasgow Coma Scale Components Eyes 4 Verbal 5 Motor 6 Emergency Test for CN XI Shoulder Shrug Trapezius & Head Turn SCM Neurology CN VII Test Facial Expressions Smile Frown Puff Cheeks Neurology Weber Test Lateralization Sensorineural To the GOOD ear Ent AMBOSS Neurology > Cranial Nerves FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Cranial Nerves BandB Neurology > The Brainstem > Cranial Nerves Tongue Deviation CN XII Lesion Tongue deviates TOWARDS the side of the lesion Licks the lesion . Uvula Deviation CN X Lesion Uvula deviates AWAY from the side of the lesion pulled by the strong side . Corneal Reflex Afferent CN V1 Sensation .\\\\n Efferent CN VII Blink . Rinne Interpretation BC > AC = Conductive Hearing Loss Abnormal .\\\\n AC > BC = Normal or Sensorineural . GCS Scoring EVM-456 .\\\\nEyes 4 Verbal 5 Motor 6 .\\\\nMax 15 Min 3. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l27", "title": "Lecture 27: OMM Lab 17 Prep - Pelvic Counterstrain & HVLA", "module": "OMM", "content": "id l27 module OMM l27 Lecture 27 OMM Lab 17 Prep - Pelvic Counterstrain & HVLA\\n\\n Lecturer T. Li D.O.\\n Session Lecture 27\\n\\n 1. Counterstrain CS Overview\\n Definition Indirect Passive technique. \\ Fold and Hold\\ .\\n Mechanism Shortens muscle to silence proprioceptive feedback Muscle Spindles/Gamma Gain .\\n Safety Very gentle. Good for acute pain.\\n Reaction 30-50% experience soreness for 1-2 days Warn patient! .\\n\\n 2. Steps of Counterstrain\\n1. Find Point Locate tenderpoint.\\n2. Scale Call it \\ 10/10\\ .\\n3. Position Move to position of ease reduce to 0/10 or $\\\\\\\\le$ 3/10 .\\n4. Hold 90 Seconds .\\n5. Return Passive return to neutral Slowly! .\\n6. Reassess Check tenderness again.\\n\\n 3. Pelvic Counterstrain Points\\n Iliacus Deep in iliac fossa.\\n Tx Flexion + External Rotation Frog Leg .\\n Psoas Medial to ASIS.\\n Tx F-S T -R A Flex Sidebend Toward Rotate Away .\\n Inguinal Lateral to pubic tubercle.\\n Tx F-ADD-IR Flex Adduct Internal Rotate .\\n Low Ilium Superior pubic ramus.\\n Tx Flexion.\\n\\n 4. Innominate HVLA\\n Diagnosis Reminder \\n Anterior Rotation ASIS Low PSIS High.\\n Posterior Rotation ASIS High PSIS Low.\\n Superior Shear All landmarks high.\\n HVLA Setup \\n Post. Rotation Extend the hip to rotate anteriorly .\\n Ant. Rotation Flex the hip to rotate posteriorly .\\n Superior Shear Traction leg Abduct + IR + Pull .\\n\\n 5. Clinical Correlates\\n correlate\\n Counterstrain Contraindications \\n Absolute No Somatic Dysfunction No Consent Lack of Cooperation.\\n Relative Severe Osteoporosis Acute Illness Instability.\\n Note Recent trauma or fracture would depend on location but Osteoporosis is the classic relative contraindication.\\n Lecture 27 OMM Lab 17 Prep - Pelvic Counterstrain & HVLA Neuroscience questions A patient feels soreness the day after a counterstrain treatment. How should this be managed? Immediate ER referral Repeat the treatment immediately Reassurance and hydration Normal reaction Prescribe Opioids Apply Heat only 2 Post-treatment soreness occurs in 30-50% of patients and lasts 1-2 days. It is a normal reaction due to metabolic release. Reassurance and water are key. What is an ABSOLUTE contraindication to Counterstrain? Osteoporosis Acute Pain Pregnancy Refusal of treatment No Consent Pediatric patient 3 Lack of consent and absence of somatic dysfunction are the main Absolute contraindications. Osteoporosis is a Relative contraindication. For a Psoas tenderpoint medial to ASIS what is the correct treatment position? Extension Sidebend Away Rotate Toward Flexion Sidebend Away Rotate Toward Flexion Sidebend Toward Rotate Away Extension only Flexion only 2 Psoas = F-ST-RA . Flexion Sidebend Toward the point Rotate Away from the point. Which finding suggests a Posterior Innominate Rotation? ASIS Low PSIS High ASIS High PSIS Low ASIS Low PSIS Low ASIS High PSIS High Pubic Shear only 1 Posterior rotation pulls the ASIS up High and pushes the PSIS down Low . What is the goal pain score reduction during Counterstrain positioning? Reduce by 10% Reduce to 5/10 Reduce to 7/10 Reduce to 0/10 or at least <= 3/10 Increase pain to provoke release 3 The goal is 100% reduction 0/10 but any reduction to 3/10 or less is considered acceptable to proceed with the hold. Psoas Counterstrain Setup F-ST-RA Flex SB Toward Rot Away OMM Iliacus Counterstrain Setup Frog Leg Flex + External Rotation OMM Inguinal Counterstrain Setup F-ADD-IR Flex Adduct Int Rotate OMM Counterstrain Hold Time 90 Seconds OMM Counterstrain Mechanism Silent Muscle Spindles Gamma Gain OMM Anterior Innominate Diagnosis ASIS Low PSIS High OMM Posterior Innominate Diagnosis ASIS High PSIS Low OMM Absolute Contraindication to CS No Consent / No Dysfunction OMM Relative Contraindication to CS Osteoporosis / Severe Illness OMM Post-CS Soreness Duration 1-2 Days Normal OMM BandB Musculoskeletal > Spine FirstAid Musculoskeletal > Anatomy Psoas Counterstrain Position F-S T -R A \\\\n F lexed S idebent T oward R otated A way. Counterstrain Hold Time 90 Seconds is the standard hold time for Counterstrain. Inguinal Counterstrain F-ADD-IR \\\\n F lexed ADD ucted I nternally R otated. Common crossing legs position . Anterior Innominate Findings ASIS Lower PSIS Higher on the affected side. Posterior Innominate Findings ASIS Higher PSIS Lower on the affected side. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l28", "title": "Lecture 28: Anatomy & Physiology of the Neocortex", "module": "DPR", "content": "id l28 module DPR l28 Lecture 28 Anatomy & Physiology of the Neocortex\\n\\n Lecturer R. Ramos Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 28\\n\\n 1. Frontal Lobe & Executive Function\\n Functions Planning Inhibition Personality \\ Filter\\ Social norms .\\n Pathology \\n Frontotemporal Dementia Pick s Disease \\n Gene MAPT mutation Tau protein .\\n Symptoms Disinhibition Apathy Personality changes e.g. gambling rude comments early onset 45-65y .\\n Classic Case Phineas Gage Iron rod through frontal lobe .\\n Frontal Eye Fields Area 8 Controls biological/voluntary saccades rapid eye movements to contralateral side.\\n\\n 2. Parietal Lobe & Attention\\n Right Parietal Lobe Non-Dominant \\n Function Spatial attention to BOTH sides of the world.\\n Lesion Right MCA Stroke Hemispatial Neglect . Patient interacts only with the Right side eats half plate draws half clock .\\n Left Parietal Lobe Dominant - Angular Gyrus \\n Lesion Gerstmann Syndrome .\\n Tetrad \\n 1. Agraphia Can t write .\\n 2. Acalculia Can t do math .\\n 3. Finger Agnosia Can t ID fingers .\\n 4. Left-Right Disorientation .\\n\\n 3. Temporal Lobe & Recognition\\n Fusiform Face Area FFA Inferior temporal gyrus.\\n Function Facial recognition.\\n Lesion Prosopagnosia Face Blindness .\\n Medial Temporal Lobe MTL Hippocampus/Memory.\\n Alzheimer s Tau tangles Amyloid plaques. Atrophy of MTL.\\n\\n 4. Insula & Cingulate\\n Insula Buried deep in lateral sulcus.\\n Functions Gustatory Taste Interoception feeling inside body Disgust Addiction/Craving Urge to smoke .\\n Stimulation Elicits taste sensations or disgust .\\n Cingulate Gyrus Part of Limbic System.\\n Cingulotomy Surgical lesioning used for intractable Chronic Pain or severe OCD .\\n\\n 5. Histology Highlights\\n Betz Cells Giant pyramidal cells in Layer 5 of Primary Motor Cortex Output to Corticospinal tract .\\n Cytoarchitecture Neocortex has 6 layers. Layer 4 = Input Sensory Layer 5 = Output Motor . Lecture 28 Anatomy & Physiology of the Neocortex Neuroscience questions A 55-year-old man presents with significant personality changes. He has become sexually inappropriate with strangers and has lost his life savings gambling. He has no memory loss. Genetic testing reveals a MAPT mutation. What is the diagnosis? Alzheimer s Disease Frontotemporal Dementia Behavioral Variant Huntington s Disease Vascular Dementia Parkinson s Disease 1 The combination of Disinhibition gambling inappropriate behavior + Personality change + MAPT mutation + Early onset 55y points to Frontotemporal Dementia . A patient cannot distinguish their left hand from their right hand and has lost the ability to perform simple addition. Where is the lesion most likely located? Right Parietal Lobe Left Inferior Parietal Lobe Angular Gyrus Right Temporal Lobe Left Frontal Lobe Occipital Lobe 1 This is Gerstmann Syndrome Acalculia L-R confusion . The lesion is in the dominant usually Left Angular Gyrus Inferior Parietal Lobule . Electrical stimulation of which brain region is most likely to elicit a sensation of bad taste or disgust? Primary Motor Cortex Fusiform Gyrus Insular Cortex Broca s Area Wernicke s Area 2 The Insula processes Gustatory taste and visceral information including feelings of disgust . A patient recovering from a stroke combs only the right side of their hair and eats only food on the right side of their tray. Which artery was likely occluded? Left MCA Right MCA Left ACA Right ACA Basilar Artery 1 This is Hemispatial Neglect ignoring the Left side . The lesion is in the Right Parietal Lobe supplied by the Right MCA . Damage to Frontal Area 8 would most likely result in a deficit of Language comprehension Face recognition Voluntary saccadic eye movements Hearing Fine motor control of the hand 2 Frontal Area 8 corresponds to the Frontal Eye Fields which control voluntary rapid eye movements saccades . Role of Insula Taste Gustatory & Disgust / Interoception Cortex Lesion causing Hemispatial Neglect Right Posterior Parietal Lobe Right MCA Neurology Lesion causing Gerstmann Syndrome Left Angular Gyrus Dominant Parietal Neurology Symptoms of Gerstmann Syndrome Agraphia Acalculia Finger Agnosia L-R Disorientation Neurology Gene associated with Frontotemporal Dementia MAPT Tau protein Pathology Symptoms of Frontotemporal Dementia Disinhibition Apathy Personality Change Pathology Function of Fusiform Face Area Facial Recognition Lesion = Prosopagnosia Cortex Function of Frontal Eye Fields Area 8 Voluntary Saccades Physiology Layer of Neocortex with Betz Cells Layer 5 Output to Spinal Cord Histology Cingulotomy is a treatment for... Chronic Pain or OCD Psychiatry AMBOSS Neurology > Neuroanatomy > Cerebral Cortex FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Lobes BandB Neurology > Cerebrum Gerstmann Syndrome Tetrad Lesion of Left Angular Gyrus .\\\\n1. Agraphia\\\\n2. Acalculia\\\\n3. Finger Agnosia\\\\n4. L-R Disorientation Hemispatial Neglect Lesion of Right Parietal Lobe Non-dominant .\\\\nPatient neglects the Left side of the world. Frontotemporal Dementia FTD Behavioral variant. Disinhibition Apathy Personality change.\\\\n MAPT gene Tau . Insula Functions Taste Disgust Interoception Addiction Cravings Smoking . Prosopagnosia Face blindness. Lesion of Fusiform Face Area Temporal Lobe . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l29", "title": "Lecture 29: Neocortical Development", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l29 module Neuro l29 Lecture 29 Neocortical Development\\n\\n Lecturer R. Ramos Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 29\\n\\n 1. The 3 P s of Brain Development\\n1. Production Proliferation Making neurons.\\n2. Placement Migration Moving neurons to correct layers.\\n3. Performance Synaptic function.\\n\\n 2. Production Proliferation \\n Location Ventricular Zone VZ .\\n Mechanism Radial Glial cells divide to produce neurons.\\n Pathology \\n Primary Microcephaly \\ Small brain\\ . Reduced proliferation.\\n Genes ASPM Abnormal Spindle-like Microcephaly Citron Kinase .\\n Hemi-megalencephaly One hemisphere is too large. excess proliferation. Risk of Epilepsy .\\n\\n 3. Placement Migration \\n Pattern Inside-Out . Oldest neurons are deep Layer 6 Youngest are superficial Layer 2 .\\n Guidance Radial Glia fibers guide neurons. Reelin secreted by Cajal-Retzius cells in Layer 1 tells them when to stop.\\n Pathology neuronal migration disorders \\n Periventricular Nodular Heterotopia PVH Neurons fail to leave VZ. Nodules lining ventricles.\\n Gene Filamin A .\\n Subcortical Band Heterotopia Double Cortex Neurons stuck in white matter Intermediate Zone .\\n Genes DCX LIS1 .\\n Lissencephaly \\ Smooth Brain\\ No gyri Agyria .\\n Gene Reelin mutations.\\n Molecular Layer Heterotopia Neurons migrate too far past Layer 2 into Layer 1 . Associated with Dyslexia and Epilepsy .\\n\\n 4. Gyrification\\n Timeline Large fissures Sylvian form first 14w . Frontal gyri form last. Lecture 29 Neocortical Development Neuroscience questions A newborn MRI shows a smooth brain surface with no gyri or sulci. What is the most likely diagnosis? Hemi-megalencephaly Lissencephaly Periventricular Nodular Heterotopia Double Cortex Syndrome Porencephaly 1 Lissencephaly literally means Smooth Brain . It is a severe migration disorder where gyri fail to form Agyria . Which cell type serves as the scaffold for neuronal migration in the developing neocortex? Astrocytes Oligodendrocytes Radial Glia Microglia Schwann Cells 2 Radial Glia extend processes from the ventricle to the pial surface acting as train tracks for migrating neurons. An MRI reveals nodules of gray matter lining the lateral ventricles. Which gene mutation is most strongly associated with this finding? Reelin Filamin A DCX ASPM MAPT 1 This describes Periventricular Nodular Heterotopia PVH . The classic association is Filamin A an actin-binding protein needed for migration initiation . In the Inside-Out pattern of cortical development which layer is formed LAST? Layer 6 Layer 5 Layer 4 Layer 2 Subplate 3 Deep layers 6 form first. Superficial layers 2 form last. Layer 1 is the marginal zone and exists early but the cortical plate neurons fill in 6→2 . Molecular Layer Heterotopia neurons migrating too far is often found in the brains of patients with Parkinson s Disease Dyslexia ALS Huntington s Disease Multiple Sclerosis 1 Small clusters of neurons in Layer 1 Molecular Layer Heterotopia are seen in Dyslexia and Epilepsy . Inside-Out Migration meaning Deep layers 6 formed first Superficial 2 formed last Embryology Radial Glia function Progenitor cells + Scaffold for migration Embryology Gene for Periventricular Nodular Heterotopia Filamin A Pathology Gene for Double Cortex Syndrome DCX / LIS1 Pathology Gene for Microcephaly ASPM / Citron Kinase Pathology Cells that secrete Reelin Cajal-Retzius Cells Layer 1 Embryology Lissencephaly means... Smooth Brain Agyria Pathology Molecular Layer Heterotopia association Dyslexia / Epilepsy Pathology Ventricular Zone function Site of neuronal proliferation Embryology 3 P s of Brain Dev Production Placement Performance Embryology AMBOSS Pediatrics > Neurology > Congenital anomalies FirstAid Neurology > Embryology Inside-Out Migration Neocortex forms Inside-Out . Layer 6 is born first. Layer 2 is born last. Double Cortex Syndrome Also called Subcortical Band Heterotopia . A band of gray matter is stuck in the white matter between the cortex and ventricle. Reelin Function Secreted by Cajal-Retzius cells in the Marginal Zone Layer 1 . Acts as a Stop signal for migrating neurons. Primary Microcephaly Genes ASPM and Citron Kinase . Defects in mitosis/cytokinesis. Filamin A Mutation Causes Periventricular Nodular Heterotopia Neurons stuck at the starting line . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l30", "title": "Lecture 30: Supraspinal Motor Systems (Cortex & Brainstem)", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l30 module Neuro l30 Lecture 30 Supraspinal Motor Systems Cortex & Brainstem \\n\\n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 30\\n\\n 1. Pyramidal System Upper Motor Neurons \\n Definition UMNs originate in the Cortex M1 Premotor SMA and project to LMNs in the Brainstem or Spinal Cord.\\n Corticospinal Tract CST \\n Lateral CST 85% Decussates at Medullary Pyramids. Controls Distal Limbs Fine motor .\\n Ventral CST 15% Descends ipsilaterally decussates in spinal cord. Controls Axial/Proximal muscles Posture .\\n Corticobulbar Tract \\n Controls Cranial Nerves Head/Neck .\\n Innervation Most CN nuclei receive Bilateral input.\\n Exceptions \\n CN VII Face Lower Face receives only Contralateral input. Upper Face is Bilateral .\\n CN XII Tongue Tongue deviates AWAY from the UMN lesion towards the weak side .\\n\\n 2. UMN vs. LMN Lesions\\n\\n| Feature | UMN Lesion Cortex/Stem | LMN Lesion Nerve/Root |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Tone | Spastic Increased | Flaccid Decreased |\\n| Reflexes | Hyperreflexia | Hyporeflexia |\\n| Babinski | Present + Toe up | Absent Toe down |\\n| Atrophy | Mild Disuse | Severe Denervation |\\n| Fasciculations | No | Yes |\\n\\n 3. Brainstem Tracts & Posturing\\n Rubrospinal Tract Originates in Red Nucleus Midbrain . Flexor tones of upper limbs.\\n Vestibulospinal Tract Extensor tone Antigravity .\\n Decorticate Posture \\ Mummy\\ \\n Lesion ABOVE Red Nucleus e.g. Cerebral Hemispheres .\\n Presentation Arms Flexed Red nucleus intact Legs Extended.\\n Decerebrate Posture \\n Lesion BELOW Red Nucleus e.g. Pons/Midbrain .\\n Presentation Arms Extended Legs Extended Rubrospinal lost Vestibulospinal unopposed .\\n\\n 4. Specific Clinical Syndromes\\n Locked-In Syndrome \\n Lesion Pons Basilar Artery .\\n Effect Bilateral CST/Corticobulbar damage. Quadriplegia + Anarthria can t speak .\\n Spared Vertical eye movements/Blinking Midbrain/CN III spared . Consciousness intact.\\n Bell s Palsy LMN CN VII Paralysis of Entire Hemi-face Forehead + Chin .\\n Stroke UMN CN VII Paralysis of Lower Face Only Forehead spared . Lecture 30 Supraspinal Motor Systems Cortex & Brainstem Neurology questions A patient presents with right-sided facial weakness involving the forehead and chin. What is the most likely location of the lesion? Left Motor Cortex Face area Left Internal Capsule Right Facial Nerve CN VII Left Facial Nucleus Right Trigeminal Nerve 2 Weakness of the entire hemiface forehead + chin indicates an LMN lesion Bell s Palsy or CN VII injury on the same side . A comatose patient exhibits extension of both the upper and lower extremities when stimulated. This posturing suggests a lesion located Above the Red Nucleus Below the Red Nucleus e.g. Pons In the Spinal Cord In the Thalamus In the Cerebellum 1 Extension of all limbs is Decerebrate posturing which occurs with lesions below the Red Nucleus rubrospinal tract lost . Which of the following is a classic sign of an Upper Motor Neuron UMN lesion? Fasciculations Severe Atrophy Hyporeflexia Babinski Sign Flaccidity 3 Babinski sign extensor plantar response is a hallmark of UMN dysfunction. The others are LMN signs. A patient suffers a stroke affecting the Left Primary Motor Cortex. The tongue deviates to which side upon protrusion? Left Right Does not deviate Upward Downward 1 With a UMN lesion Left Cortex the tongue deviates AWAY from the lesion towards the weak side . So it points to the Right .\\\\n Wait let me double check my pearl... LMN = Toward. UMN = Away. Correct. So Left Cortex lesion → Right Genioglossus weak → Tongue points Right. Correct Answer is Right. Options Left/Right . Which tract is primarily responsible for voluntary fine motor control of the distal upper extremities? Ventral Corticospinal Tract Lateral Corticospinal Tract Vestibulospinal Tract Reticulospinal Tract Tectospinal Tract 1 The Lateral Corticospinal Tract controls distal muscles hands/fingers . The Ventral tract controls axial muscles. UMN Lesion Signs Spasticity Hyperreflexia Babinski + Neurology LMN Lesion Signs Flaccidity Atrophy Fasciculations Neurology Decorticate Posture Arms Flexed Mummy . Lesion Above Red Nucleus. Neurology Decerebrate Posture Arms Extended. Lesion Below Red Nucleus. Neurology Bell s Palsy vs Stroke Face Bell s LMN = Whole Face. Stoke UMN = Lower Face. Neurology Lateral Corticospinal Tract Decussation Medullary Pyramids Neuroanatomy Rubrospinal Tract Origin Red Nucleus Midbrain Neuroanatomy Locked-In Syndrome Lesion Pons Basilar Artery Neurology AMBOSS Neurology > Neuroanatomy > Pyramidal & Extrapyramidal FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Spinal Tracts UMN vs LMN Face UMN Stroke Spares forehead Look for wrinkles! .\\\\n LMN Bell s Affects whole face Forehead smooth . Decorticate vs Decerebrate De-COR-ticate Arms like C s Flexed to Cor e . Lesion Above Red Nucleus.\\\\n De-cere-brate Arms like E s Extended . Lesion Below Red Nucleus Worse prognosis . Tongue Deviation Lick your lesions. \\\\n LMN Tongue deviates TOWARD lesion.\\\\n UMN Tongue deviates AWAY from lesion. Locked-In Syndrome Pontine Stroke. Quadriplegia. Only eyes move Vertical . Consciousness preserved. Pyramidal Decussation Occurs at the Cervicomedullary Junction Lower Medulla . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l31", "title": "Lecture 31: Basal Ganglia", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l31 module Neuro l31 Lecture 31 Basal Ganglia\\n\\n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 31\\n\\n 1. Basal Ganglia Anatomy\\n Striatum Input Caudate + Putamen. Releases GABA .\\n Pallidum Output GPi Internal & GPe External . Releases GABA .\\n Subthalamic Nucleus STN Releases Glutamate Excitatory .\\n Substantia Nigra pars Compacta SNc Releases Dopamine .\\n\\n 2. The Pathways Albin-DeLong Model \\n1. Direct Pathway GO \\n Promotes movement.\\n Striatum inhibits GPi → Disinhibition of Thalamus.\\n D1 Receptors Dopamine excites this pathway.\\n2. Indirect Pathway NO-GO \\n Inhibits movement.\\n Striatum inhibits GPe → STN active → GPi active → Thalamus inhibited.\\n D2 Receptors Dopamine inhibits this pathway Releases the brake .\\n3. Hyperdirect Pathway Fast suppression Freeze! . Cortex → STN → GPi.\\n\\n 3. Parkinson s Disease Hypokinetic \\n Pathology Loss of Dopaminergic neurons in SNc . Lewy Bodies $\\\\alpha$-synuclein .\\n Symptoms TRAP \\n T remor Resting Pill-rolling .\\n R igidity Cogwheel .\\n A kinesia / Bradykinesia Slow movement Masked face .\\n P ostural Instability.\\n History MPTP toxin caused acute Parkinsonism in drug users \\ Frozen Addicts\\ .\\n\\n 4. Huntington s Disease Hyperkinetic \\n Pathology Atrophy of Caudate Nucleus Boxy ventricles on MRI . loss of GABA/ACh neurons.\\n Genetics CAG trinucleotide repeat >40 . Autosomal Dominant . Anticipation worse in next gen .\\n Symptoms Chorea Dance-like Dementia Depression. Signs appear age 30-50.\\n\\n 5. Other Disorders\\n Hemiballismus \\n Lesion Subthalamic Nucleus STN lacunar stroke .\\n Symptoms Wild ballistic flailing of contralateral arm/leg.\\n Tardive Dyskinesia \\n Cause Chronic use of Antipsychotics D2 blockers .\\n Symptoms Repetitive lip smacking tongue rolling. Late onset. Lecture 31 Basal Ganglia Neurology questions A patient presents with wild uncontrollable flailing movements of the right arm and leg. An MRI would most likely show a lacunar stroke in which structure? Left Substantia Nigra Left Subthalamic Nucleus Right Subthalamic Nucleus Left Caudate Right Putamen 1 Hemiballismus contralateral flailing is caused by a lesion in the Subthalamic Nucleus STN . Right movements = Left STN. Which neurotransmitter is primarily released by the neurons of the Subthalamic Nucleus? GABA Dopamine Glutamate Acetylcholine Serotonin 2 The STN is the only intrinsic BG nucleus that is excitatory releasing Glutamate . All others Striatum GPe GPi SNr release GABA. In Huntington s Disease which brain structure undergoes the most significant atrophy leading to ventriculomegaly? Substantia Nigra Caudate Nucleus Thalamus Hippocampus Cerebellum 1 Huntington s causes atrophy of the Striatum specifically the Caudate Nucleus leading to boxy dilated ventricles. A 65-year-old male presents with a resting tremor masked facies and a shuffling gait. Histology would likely reveal Neurofibrillary Tangles Lewy Bodies Amyloid Plaques Negri Bodies Pick Bodies 1 This is Parkinson s Disease. The hallmark histology is Lewy Bodies alpha-synuclein in the Substantia Nigra. Dopamine binding to D2 receptors in the striatum results in Excitation of the Indirect Pathway Inhibition of the Indirect Pathway Excitation of the Direct Pathway Inhibition of the Direct Pathway Inhibition of the Thalamus 1 D2 receptors are inhibitory . They are on the Indirect No-Go pathway. So Dopamine inhibits the Indirect pathway essentially turning off the stop signal which promotes movement. Hemiballismus Lesion Contralateral Subthalamic Nucleus STN Neurology Parkinson s Pathology Loss of Dopamine in SNc + Lewy Bodies Pathology Huntington s Genetic Defect CAG Repeat >40 on Ch 4 Genetics Direct Pathway Function Promotes Movement Disinhibits Thalamus Physiology Neurotransmitter of Striatum GABA Neuroscience Neurotransmitter of STN Glutamate Neuroscience Tardive Dyskinesia Cause Chronic D2 Blockers Antipsychotics Pharmacology MPTP causes... Parkinsonism Destroys SNc Pathology AMBOSS Neurology > Pathology > Degenerative diseases FirstAid Neurology > Pathology > Movement Disorders Parkinson s TRAP T remor Resting \\\\n R igidity\\\\n A kinesia\\\\n P ostural Instability Huntington s CAG Mnemonic C audate lost\\\\n A Ch lost\\\\n G ABA lost\\\\n Also CAG Repeats Hemiballismus Lesion Damage to the Subthalamic Nucleus STN causes contralateral flailing. Dopamine Receptors D1 = Direct Go .\\\\n D2 = Indirect No-Go .\\\\nDopamine stimulates D1 and inhibits D2 both resulting in Movement . MPTP Toxin Destroys SNc neurons causing rapid permanent Parkinsonism. Synthetic heroin case . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l32", "title": "Lecture 32: Cerebellum", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l32 module Neuro l32 Lecture 32 Cerebellum\\n\\n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 32\\n\\n 1. Neuroanatomy\\n Organization Cortex 3 layers covering Deep Nuclei.\\n Deep Nuclei Lateral to Medial \\ D on t E at G reasy F ood\\ .\\n D entate.\\n E mboliform.\\n G lobose.\\n F astigial.\\n Circuits \\n Inputs \\n Mossy Fibers From Spinal cord/Brainstem. Excitatory Glutamate .\\n Climbing Fibers From Inferior Olive . Excitatory Glutamate .\\n Processing \\n Purkinje Cells The sole output of the cerebellar cortex. Releases GABA Inhibitory onto Deep Nuclei.\\n Output \\n Deep Nuclei send final signal to Thalamus/Brainstem.\\n\\n 2. Functional Divisions & Pathology\\n\\n| Division | Anatomy | Function | Lesion Symptoms |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Vestibulo-cerebellum | Flocculonodular Lobe | Balance Eye movements | Vertigo Nystagmus Wide-based gait Truncal Ataxia . |\\n| Spino-cerebellum | Vermis & Paravermis | Posture Trunk/Limb Gait | Ataxic Gait \\ Drunk walk\\ . Alcoholism damages Anterior Lobe Vermis . |\\n| Cerebro-cerebellum | Lateral Hemispheres | Fine motor planning | Intention Tremor Dysmetria Dysdiadochokinesia . |\\n\\n 3. Clinical Syndromes\\n Essential Tremor \\n Most common movement disorder. Familial Autosomal Dominant .\\n Postural/Action Tremor worse when holding a cup .\\n Note Improves with Alcohol . Often self-medicated .\\n Intention Tremor \\n Cerebellar lesion. Tremor worsens as you approach target .\\n Resting Tremor \\n Basal Ganglia Parkinson s .\\n\\n 4. DANISH Mnemonic Cerebellar Signs \\n D ysdiadochokinesia Can t do rapid alternating movements .\\n A taxia.\\n N ystagmus.\\n I ntention Tremor.\\n S peech Slurred/Scanning .\\n H ypotonia. Lecture 32 Cerebellum Neurology questions A chronic alcoholic presents with a wide-based staggering gait but normal arm coordination. Which region of the cerebellum is most likely degenerated? Flocculonodular Lobe Anterior Lobe Vermis Lateral Hemisphere Dentate Nucleus Inferior Olive 1 Chronic alcoholism typically causes atrophy of the Anterior Lobe Vermis leading to Truncal Ataxia and gait issues while sparing the limbs Lateral Hemispheres . Which of the following describes the output of Purkinje cells? Excitatory Glutamate to Deep Nuclei Inhibitory GABA to Deep Nuclei Excitatory Glutamate to Thalamus Inhibitory GABA to Thalamus Excitatory Acetylcholine to Spinal Cord 1 Purkinje Cells provide Inhibitory GABA input to the Deep Cerebellar Nuclei modulating the final output. A patient cannot rapidly supinate and pronate their hand Dysdiadochokinesia on the left side. Where is the lesion? Right Cerebellar Hemisphere Left Cerebellar Hemisphere Vermis Right Motor Cortex Right Basal Ganglia 1 Cerebellar symptoms are Ipsilateral . Dysdiadochokinesia limb coordination maps to the Lateral Hemisphere . So Left hand issue = Left Cerebellar Hemisphere . A child presents with frequent falls nystagmus and vomiting. An MRI reveals a tumor in the roof of the 4th ventricle compressing the flocculonodular lobe. What is the most likely diagnosis? Glioblastoma Multiforme Meningioma Medulloblastoma Schwannoma Pituitary Adenoma 2 Medulloblastoma is a common pediatric tumor that often arises in the Vermis/Flocculonodular lobe causing truncal ataxia and hydrocephalus vomiting . Climbing fibers which provide powerful excitatory input to Purkinje cells originate from which structure? Spinal Cord Pontine Nuclei Inferior Olive Red Nucleus Vestibular Nuclei 2 Climbing Fibers originate exclusively from the Inferior Olive Medulla . Deep Cerebellar Nuclei Lat to Med Dentate Emboliform Globose Fastigial Neuroanatomy Purkinje Cell Neurotransmitter GABA Inhibitory Neuroscience Climbing Fibers Origin Inferior Olive Neuroanatomy Lesion causing Dysdiadochokinesia Ipsilateral Lateral Cerebellar Hemisphere Neurology Classic sign of Cerebrocerebellar lesion Intention Tremor Neurology Sign of Vermis Lesion Truncal Ataxia Drunk Walk Neurology Essential Tremor characteristics Action/Postural. Improves w/ Alcohol. Neurology DANISH meaning Dysdiadochokinesia Ataxia Nystagmus Intention Tremor Slurred Speech Hypotonia Neurology AMBOSS Neurology > Neuroanatomy > Cerebellum FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Cerebellum Deep Nuclei Mnemonic D on t E at G reasy F ood Lateral to Medial \\\\n D entate E mboliform G lobose F astigial. Cerebellar Hemisphere vs Vermis Lateral lesions affect Limbs fall Toward side of lesion .\\\\n Medial Vermis lesions affect Trunk Truncal Ataxia fall anywhere . Alcoholic Cerebellar Degeneration Chronic alcohol use specifically damages the Anterior Lobe Vermis causing a wide-based ataxic gait. Essential Tremor Action tremor eating/writing . Improves with Alcohol. Tx Beta-blockers. Output of Cerebellar Cortex Purkinje Cells are the ONLY output of the cortex. They are Inhibitory GABA . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l33", "title": "Lecture 33: Sympathomimetics I - Direct Agonists", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l33 module Neuro l33 Lecture 33 Sympathomimetics I - Direct Agonists\\n\\n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D.\\n Session Lecture 33\\n\\n 1. Receptors & Physiology\\n $\\\\\\\\alpha 1$ Gq Vasoconstriction Skin/Splanchnic Mydriasis Pupil Dilation Urinary Sphincter Contraction.\\n $\\\\\\\\alpha 2$ Gi Presynaptic Inhibition Decreases NE release .\\n $\\\\\\\\beta 1$ Gs Heart $\\\\\\\\uparrow$ HR $\\\\\\\\uparrow$ Contractility Kidney $\\\\\\\\uparrow$ Renin .\\n $\\\\\\\\beta 2$ Gs Bronchodilation Vasodilation Skeletal Muscle Uterine Relaxation Tocolysis .\\n $\\\\\\\\beta 3$ Gs Bladder Detrusor Relaxation.\\n\\n 2. Direct-Acting Sympathomimetics\\n\\n| Drug | Receptors | Action | Clinical Use |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Epinephrine | $\\\\\\\\alpha \\\\\\\\beta$ | Low Dose $\\\\\\\\beta 2$ Hypotension . High Dose $\\\\\\\\alpha 1$ Hyperstension . | Anaphylaxis DOC Cardiac Arrest. |\\n| Norepinephrine | $\\\\\\\\alpha 1 = \\\\\\\\alpha 2 > \\\\\\\\beta 1$ | Potent Vasoconstriction. Reflex Bradycardia. | Septic Shock DOC . |\\n| Dopamine | D1 > $\\\\\\\\beta 1$ > $\\\\\\\\alpha 1$ | Low Renal Vasodilation. Med Inotropy. High Vasoconstriction. | Shock Renal sparing theoretically Heart Failure. |\\n| Dobutamine | $\\\\\\\\beta 1 > \\\\\\\\beta 2$ | $\\\\\\\\uparrow$ Inotropy > Chronotropy. | Heart Failure Decompensated Stress Testing. |\\n| Phenylephrine | $\\\\\\\\alpha 1$ | Vasoconstriction. | Nasal Decongestant Hypotension Mydriasis. |\\n| Isoproterenol | $\\\\\\\\beta 1 = \\\\\\\\beta 2$ | $\\\\\\\\uparrow$ HR Vasodilation $\\\\\\\\downarrow$ DBP . | Bradycardia Heart block Rarely used . |\\n\\n 3. Hemodynamics Key Concepts \\n Mean Arterial Pressure MAP $\\\\\\\\text CO \\\\\\\\times \\\\\\\\text TPR $.\\n Pulse Pressure $\\\\\\\\text Systolic - \\\\\\\\text Diastolic $.\\n Epinephrine Reversal Giving an $\\\\\\\\alpha$-blocker before Epinephrine converts its pressor effect $\\\\\\\\alpha 1$ to a depressor effect $\\\\\\\\beta 2$ unmasked → Hypotension.\\n Reflex Bradycardia Pure $\\\\\\\\alpha 1$ agonists Phenylephrine/Norepinephrine cause massive vasoconstriction → $\\\\\\\\uparrow$ BP → Baroreceptors fire → Reflex $\\\\\\\\downarrow$ HR.\\n\\n 4. Clinical Pearls\\n Anaphylaxis Epinephrine is the ONLY first-line drug. Prevents shock $\\\\\\\\alpha 1$ opens airway $\\\\\\\\beta 2$ stops mast cells.\\n Septic Shock Norepinephrine is first-line to restore MAP.\\n Tachyphylaxis Rapidly diminishing response to successive doses seen with Indirect agonists like Tyramine/Ephedrine less so with direct . Lecture 33 Sympathomimetics I - Direct Agonists Neurology questions A patient in septic shock requires a vasopressor to maintain Mean Arterial Pressure MAP . Which agent is the first-line choice due to its potent alpha-1 and beta-1 activity with minimal beta-2 effects? Epinephrine Dopamine Norepinephrine Phenylephrine Isoproterenol 2 Norepinephrine is the Drug of Choice DOC for Septic Shock. It provides strong vasoconstriction alpha-1 and some inotropy beta-1 without the hypotension associated with beta-2 stimulation. A patient is given a drug that increases Systolic BP decreases Diastolic BP and increases Heart Rate. Which drug fits this profile? Phenylephrine Norepinephrine Isoproterenol Clonidine Propranolol 2 Isoproterenol is a non-selective Beta agonist Beta-1 and Beta-2 . Beta-1 increases SBP and HR. Beta-2 causes vasodilation dropping DBP. Phenylephrine is administered intravenously. What represents the expected cardiovascular response? Increased BP Increased HR Increased BP Decreased HR Decreased BP Increased HR Decreased BP Decreased HR No change in BP Increased HR 1 Phenylephrine is a pure Alpha-1 agonist. It increases TPR and BP. The body responds with Reflex Bradycardia Decreased HR . Which receptor subtype is primarily responsible for the bronchodilatory effects of Epinephrine used in anaphylaxis? Alpha-1 Alpha-2 Beta-1 Beta-2 Dopamine-1 3 Beta-2 receptors mediate bronchodilation and skeletal muscle vasodilation . Alpha-1 mediates the vasoconstriction that supports BP. Dobutamine is primarily used in the setting of Anaphylactic Shock Septic Shock Decompensated Heart Failure Hypertension Asthma 2 Dobutamine Beta-1 > Beta-2 is an inotrope used to increase cardiac output in Decompensated Heart Failure or cardiogenic shock. Epinephrine Receptor Profile Alpha-1 Alpha-2 Beta-1 Beta-2 Pharmacology Norepinephrine Receptor Profile Alpha-1 > Alpha-2 > Beta-1 NO Beta-2 Pharmacology Phenylephrine Receptor Profile Pure Alpha-1 Agonist Pharmacology Isoproterenol Receptor Profile Beta-1 = Beta-2 Non-selective Beta Pharmacology Dobutamine Use Heart Failure Inotropy Pharmacology Drug of Choice for Anaphylaxis Epinephrine Pharmacology Drug of Choice for Septic Shock Norepinephrine Pharmacology Reflex response to Phenylephrine Bradycardia Decreased HR Pharmacology AMBOSS Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs > Sympathomimetics FirstAid Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs Epinephrine Dosage Effects Low Dose = Beta-2 Dominant Vasodilation/Hypotension .\\\\n High Dose = Alpha-1 Dominant Vasoconstriction/Hypertension . Norepinephrine vs Epinephrine Norepinephrine has NO Beta-2 activity. It causes pure vasoconstriction and reflex bradycardia. Epinephrine has Beta-2 activity Bronchodilation . Dopamine Dosing Low Renal D1.\\\\n Medium Cardiac Beta-1.\\\\n High Pressor Alpha-1. Phenylephrine Reflex A pure Alpha-1 agonist Phenylephrine increases BP but causes Reflex Bradycardia lowers HR . Beta-1 Selectivity Dobutamine is a Beta-1 agonist used for Heart Failure to increase contractility without massively increasing Heart Rate Chronotropy . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l34", "title": "Lecture 34: Sympathomimetics II - Indirect & Mixed Acting", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l34 module Neuro l34 Lecture 34 Sympathomimetics II - Indirect & Mixed Acting\\n\\n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D.\\n Session Lecture 34\\n\\n 1. Beta-2 Agonists Lungs & Uterus \\n SABA Short-Acting Albuterol Levalbuterol.\\n Use Acute Asthma/COPD exacerbation \\ Rescue\\ .\\n LABA Long-Acting Salmeterol Formoterol.\\n Use Chronic maintenance. Always combine with Steroids ICS in asthma to prevent mortality.\\n Tocolytics Terbutaline .\\n Use Relaxes uterine smooth muscle to delay preterm labor allows time for corticosteroids to mature fetal lungs .\\n\\n 2. Beta-3 Agonists Bladder \\n Mirabegron \\n Action Relaxes Detrusor muscle.\\n Use Overactive Bladder OAB . Alternative to Anticholinergics .\\n\\n 3. Alpha-2 Agonists Central Sympatholytics \\nReduces sympathetic outflow from the brainstem.\\n Clonidine \\n Uses Hypertension resistant Opioid Withdrawal ADHD.\\n Adverse Rebound Hypertension if stopped abruptly. dry mouth sedation.\\n Methyldopa \\n Use Hypertension in Pregnancy Safe for fetus .\\n Adverse Coombs+ Hemolytic Anemia Autoimmune Drug-induced Lupus.\\n\\n 4. Indirect & Mixed Acting\\n Amphetamines Adderall \\n Mechanism Displaces NE/DA from vesicles into synapse Reverse transport .\\n Use ADHD Narcolepsy.\\n Cocaine \\n Mechanism Blocks Reuptake NET/DAT .\\n Effect Vasoconstriction Local Anesthetic + Pressor .\\n Ephedrine \\n Mechanism Mixed Direct Agonist + Indirect Release .\\n Use Hypotension during Anesthesia.\\n Pseudoephedrine \\n Use Nasal Decongestant. Precursor for Meth hence restricted .\\n\\n 5. MAO Inhibitors The Cheese Effect \\n Mechanism Inhibits Monoamine Oxidase enzyme that breaks down NE/DA/Serotonin .\\n Tyramine Interaction Tyramine found in Aged Cheese Wine Cured Meats releases stored NE. Commonly degraded by GI MAO.\\n Crisis If on MAO-I Tyramine is NOT degraded → Massive NE release → Hypertensive Crisis . Lecture 34 Sympathomimetics II - Indirect & Mixed Acting Neurology questions A pregnant woman presents with chronic hypertension. Which centrally acting alpha-2 agonist is considered safe and appropriate for management? Clonidine Methyldopa Lisinopril Losartan Propranolol 1 Methyldopa is the classic safe antihypertensive for pregnancy. ACE inhibitors Lisinopril and ARBs are teratogenic. A patient on a Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitor MAOI for depression consumes a charcuterie board with aged cheese and wine. They present to the ER with a severe headache and BP of 220/120. mechanism is Direct Alpha-1 agonism by Tyramine Displacement of stored Norepinephrine by Tyramine Blockade of Norepinephrine reuptake Allergic reaction to wine Serotonin Syndrome 1 Tyramine acts as an indirect sympathomimetic by displacing stored NE from vesicles. Usually broken down by MAO but persists in MAO-I patients. Which drug works by relaxing the detrusor muscle via Beta-3 agonism to treat overactive bladder? Oxybutynin Mirabegron Bethanecol Tamsulosin Finasteride 1 Mirabegron is a Beta-3 agonist. Oxybutynin is an Anticholinergic which also works but via M3 blockade . Amphetamines exert their stimulant effect primarily by Directly activating Alpha-1 receptors Blocking the degradation of Acetylcholine Displacing Norepinephrine and Dopamine from vesicles into the synapse Blocking the reuptake of Serotonin only Directly activating Beta-2 receptors 2 Amphetamines enter the nerve terminal via uptake and then displace NE/DA from vesicles reversing the transporter to pump NE/DA OUT into the synapse. Salmeterol is classified as a Short-Acting Beta Agonist SABA Long-Acting Beta Agonist LABA Alpha-1 Antagonist Muscarinic Antagonist Corticosteroid 1 Salmeterol is a LABA used for asthma maintenance. Albuterol is a SABA. Methyldopa Indication Hypertension in Pregnancy Pharmacology Methyldopa Adverse Effect Coombs + Hemolytic Anemia Pharmacology Clonidine Mechanism Central Alpha-2 Agonist Decreases Sympathetic Outflow Pharmacology Mirabegron Target Beta-3 Receptor Bladder Relaxation Pharmacology Terbutaline Use Tocolysis Delay Labor Pharmacology Tyramine Mechanism Displaces Stored NE Indirect Agonist Pharmacology Cocaine Mechanism Blocks Reuptake NET/DAT Pharmacology Albuterol vs Salmeterol Albuterol = SABA Acute . Salmeterol = LABA Maintenance . Pharmacology AMBOSS Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs > Sympathomimetics FirstAid Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs Methyldopa in Pregnancy Methyldopa is the classic choice for Hypertension in Pregnancy Preeclampsia prophylaxis . Watch for Hemolytic Anemia . Clonidine Rebound Never stop Clonidine abruptly. Causes massive Rebound Hypertension due to upregulated receptors. Cocaine vs Amphetamine Cocaine BLOCKS reuptake NET blocker .\\\\n Amphetamine REVERSES transporter Displaces NE . Both increase synaptic NE . Cheese Reaction MAO Inhibitors + Tyramine Wine/Cheese = Hypertensive Crisis. Tyramine displaces NE . Terbutaline Tocolysis Beta-2 agonist used to Stop Labor Relax Uterus . Turbulence delays the flight birth . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l35", "title": "Lecture 35: Cranial Nerves I, II, III, IV, VI (Eye Movements)", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l35 module Neuro l35 Lecture 35 Cranial Nerves I II III IV VI Eye Movements \\n\\n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 35\\n\\n 1. CN I Olfactory & CN II Optic \\n CN I Visceral Sensory. Bypasses Thalamus.\\n Pathology Anosmia Early sign of Parkinson s - Lewy bodies in Olfactory Bulb .\\n CN II Special Sensory.\\n Reflex Pupillary Light Reflex Afferent II → Pretectal Area → Bilateral Edinger-Westphal → Efferent III .\\n\\n 2. CN III Oculomotor \\n Motor Medial Superior Inferior Recti; Inferior Oblique; Levator Palpebrae.\\n Parasympathetic Edinger-Westphal Nucleus → Pupil Constriction Miosis & Lens Accommodation.\\n Lesion CN III Palsy \\n \\ Down and Out\\ eye Unopposed SO4/LR6 .\\n Ptosis Droopy eyelid .\\n Mydriasis Blown pupil - loss of parasympathetics .\\n Note The Superior Rectus and Levator are innervated by the Contralateral nucleus but the nerve fibers cross inside the complex so a NERVE lesion affects the ipsilateral eye.\\n\\n 3. CN IV Trochlear \\n Motor Superior Oblique Depresses/Intorts eye .\\n Unique Features \\n 1. Only CN to exit Dorsally .\\n 2. Only CN to Decussate Completely Right Nucleus → Left Superior Oblique .\\n Lesion \\n Vertical Diplopia Worse when looking down e.g. stairs/reading .\\n Head Tilt Patient tilts head AWAY from the affected side to compensate/align eyes.\\n\\n 4. CN VI Abducens & Gaze Control\\n Motor Lateral Rectus Abducts eye .\\n Horizontal Gaze Circuit \\n To look RIGHT Right PPRF → Right Abducens Nucleus .\\n Right Abducens Nucleus does TWO things \\n 1. Contracts Ring Lateral Rectus Abducts Right Eye .\\n 2. Sends signal via MLF Medial Longitudinal Fasciculus to Left Oculomotor Nucleus → Contracts Left Medial Rectus Adducts Left Eye .\\n Pathology \\n CN VI Palsy Esotropia Eye turns in .\\n INO Internuclear Ophthalmoplegia \\n Lesion of MLF .\\n Ipsilateral eye cannot Adduct Medial Rectus palsy .\\n Contralateral eye has Nystagmus.\\n Convergence is SPARED because CN III is intact .\\n Classic Cause Multiple Sclerosis Bilateral INO .\\n\\n 5. Summary Table\\n\\n| Nerve | Muscles | Lesion Sign |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| CN III | All others + Levator + Pupil | Down & Out Ptosis Blown Pupil |\\n| CN IV | Superior Oblique | Head tilt AWAY Vertical Diplopia stairs |\\n| CN VI | Lateral Rectus | Esotropia Eye turns In | Lecture 35 Cranial Nerves I II III IV VI Eye Movements Neurology questions A patient presents with vertical diplopia that worsens when walking down stairs. They are observed tilting their head to the left. Which cranial nerve is likely injured? Left CN IV Right CN IV Left CN III Right CN VI Left CN VI 2 Vertical diplopia stairs problem suggests CN IV Trochlear palsy. The head tilts AWAY from the lesion. Left tilt = Right CN IV lesion. A patient cannot look to the right with the right eye. When attempting to look right the left eye also fails to adduct. Convergence is intact. Where is the lesion? Right Abducens Nerve Right Abducens Nucleus Right MLF Left MLF Right PPRF 2 This is a Right Abducens Nucleus lesion. It knocks out the Right LR Can t look right AND the signal to the Left MR via MLF causing a gaze palsy to that side. A simple nerve lesion would only affect the Right LR . In Internuclear Ophthalmoplegia INO the lesion is located in the Abducens Nerve Oculomotor Nerve Medial Longitudinal Fasciculus MLF Superior Colliculus Lateral Geniculate Nucleus 2 INO is caused by damage to the MLF disconnecting the Abducens nucleus from the contralateral Oculomotor nucleus. Which cranial nerve contains the parasympathetic fibers responsible for pupillary constriction? Optic II Oculomotor III Trochlear IV Trigeminal V Abducens VI 1 CN III Oculomotor carries parasympathetics from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus to the ciliary ganglion for miosis and accommodation. Anosmia is an early and often overlooked clinical sign of which neurodegenerative disorder? Alzheimer s Disease Parkinson s Disease Huntington s Disease ALS Multiple Sclerosis 1 Parkinson s Disease is strongly associated with early Anosmia due to Lewy Body deposition in the olfactory bulb. Eye Muscles Innervation Mnemonic SO4 LR6 All Others 3 Neuroanatomy CN IV Exit Point Dorsal Brainstem Midbrain Neuroanatomy CN III Palsy Signs Down & Out Ptosis Mydriasis Neurology INO Lesion Location Medial Longitudinal Fasciculus MLF Neurology Head Tilt in CN IV Palsy Away from Lesion Neurology Vertical Diplopia Cause CN IV Palsy Neurology Parasympathetic Nucleus for Eye Edinger-Westphal Nucleus Neuroanatomy Abducens Nucleus Function Abducts Ipsilateral Eye + Adducts Contralateral Eye via MLF Neurophysiology AMBOSS Neurology > Neuroanatomy > Cranial Nerves FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Cranial Nerves SO4 LR6 All The Rest 3 S uperior O blique = CN 4 .\\\\n L ateral R ectus = CN 6 .\\\\nAll other eye muscles = CN 3 . CN IV Trochlear Trivia The only cranial nerve to exit from the BACK Dorsal of the brainstem. Also the only one to cross completely! INO Internuclear Ophthalmoplegia Lesion of MLF . Eye lags when looking towards nose Adduction deficit . The other eye shakes Nystagmus . Seen in MS . Parkinson s Early Sign Anosmia Loss of smell is often the FIRST sign of Parkinson s appearing years before motor symptoms. Head Tilt in CN IV Palsy Head tilts AWAY from the lesion. Right CN IV palsy = Left Head Tilt . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l36", "title": "Lecture 36: Cranial Nerves V, VII-XII (Brainstem Nuclei)", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l36 module Neuro l36 Lecture 36 Cranial Nerves V VII-XII Brainstem Nuclei \\n\\n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 36\\n\\n 1. CN V Trigeminal \\n Sensory Face \\n Mesencephalic Nucleus Proprioception Jaw jerk .\\n Principal Pontine Nucleus Discriminative Touch Face .\\n Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus Pain & Temperature Face . Extends down to C-spine .\\n Motor V3 Muscles of Mastication Temporalis Masseter Pterygoids .\\n Clinical \\n Trigeminal Neuralgia \\ Suicide disease\\ . Sharp shooting pain.\\n Corneal Reflex Afferent V1 Ophthalmic → Efferent VII Facial .\\n\\n 2. CN VII Facial \\n Motor Muscles of Facial Expression Stapedius dampens sound Stylohyoid Digastric Post .\\n Parasympathetic Superior Salivatory Nucleus → Lacrimal Tears Submandibular/Sublingual Saliva .\\n Sensory Taste Anterior 2/3 of Tongue.\\n Clinical \\n Bell s Palsy LMN Ipsilateral paralysis of ENTIRE face Forehead involved . Hyperacusis Loss of taste.\\n UMN Lesion Stroke Contralateral paralysis of LOWER face only Forehead Spared due to bilateral innervation .\\n\\n 3. CN IX Glossopharyngeal & CN X Vagus \\n CN IX \\n Sensory Post 1/3 Tongue Taste/Feel Carotid Body/Sinus.\\n Motor Stylopharyngeus.\\n Reflex Gag Reflex Afferent .\\n CN X \\n Motor Nucleus Ambiguus → Pharynx/Larynx/Palate.\\n Visceral Dorsal Motor Nucleus → GI/Heart Parasymp .\\n Reflex Gag Reflex Efferent .\\n Lesion Uvula deviates AWAY from lesion. Hoarseness.\\n\\n 4. CN XI Accessory & CN XII Hypoglossal \\n CN XI Trapezius Shrug SCM Turn head . Lesion Shoulder droop cannot turn head away from lesion.\\n CN XII Tongue muscles. Lesion Tongue deviates TOWARD lesion \\ Lick your wounds\\ .\\n\\n 5. Brainstem Syndromes\\n Lateral Medullary Wallenberg Syndrome \\n Artery PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery .\\n Nuclei \\n Ambiguus Dysphagia Hoarseness.\\n Vestibular Vertigo Nystagmus.\\n Spinal V Ipsilateral Facial Pain/Temp loss.\\n Spinothalamic Contralateral Body Pain/Temp loss.\\n Sympathetic Ipsilateral Horner s Syndrome.\\n Medial Medullary Syndrome \\n Artery ASA Anterior Spinal Artery .\\n Nuclei \\n Hypoglossal XII Tongue deviates to lesion.\\n Corticospinal Tract Contralateral Hemiparesis.\\n Medial Lemniscus Contralateral Proprioception/Vibration loss. Lecture 36 Cranial Nerves V VII-XII Brainstem Nuclei Neurology questions A patient presents with sudden onset of right-sided facial drooping involving the forehead and the lower face. They also complain that sounds seem abnormally loud in the right ear. The most likely location of the lesion is Left Motor Cortex Right Internal Capsule Right Facial Nerve Left Facial Nerve Right Trigeminal Nerve 2 Right Facial Nerve LMN lesion causes Bell s Palsy Whole hemiface paralysis and Hyperacusis loss of Stapedius . A patient sticks out their tongue and it deviates to the LEFT. There is atrophy of the left side of the tongue. Which nerve is injured? Right CN XII Left CN XII Right CN X Left CN X Left CN VII 1 The tongue deviates TOWARD the side of the lesion Lick your wounds . Left deviation + Atrophy = Left CN XII LMN lesion. In Lateral Medullary Syndrome Wallenberg which nucleus is responsible for the characteristic dysphagia and hoarseness? Solitary Nucleus Nucleus Ambiguus Dorsal Motor Nucleus of Vagus Hypoglossal Nucleus Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus 1 Nucleus Ambiguus provides motor innervation to the pharynx and larynx via IX and X . Damage causes dysphagia and hoarseness. Loss of the afferent limb of the corneal reflex would result from damage to which cranial nerve? CN II CN III CN V1 CN VII CN VIII 2 The afferent sensing limb of the corneal reflex is CN V1 Ophthalmic division of Trigeminal . The efferent blinking is CN VII. A patient has loss of pain and temperature sensation on the RIGHT side of the face and the LEFT side of the body. This crossed sensory deficit suggests a lesion in the Right Lateral Medulla Left Lateral Medulla Right Medial Medulla Left Medial Medulla Right Thalamus 0 Crossed findings Ipsi Face Contra Body are a hallmark of brainstem lesions. Right Lateral Medulla hits Right Spinal V Face and Right Spinothalamic carrying Left Body info . Corneal Reflex Limbs In V1. Out VII. Neurology Gag Reflex Limbs In IX. Out X. Neurology Tongue Deviation Rule Deviates TOWARD the lesion Lick your wounds Neurology Uvula Deviation Rule Deviates AWAY from the lesion Neurology Nucleus Ambiguus Function Motor to Pharynx/Larynx Swallowing/Speech - CN IX X XI Neuroanatomy Wallenberg Syndrome Artery PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Neurology Bell s Palsy vs Stroke Face Bell s = Whole Face. Stroke = Lower Face only Forehead spared . Neurology Hyperacusis Nerve CN VII Stapedius muscle paralysis Neurology AMBOSS Neurology > Neuroanatomy > Cranial Nerves FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Cranial Nerves Bell s Palsy vs Stroke Bell s LMN Can NOT wrinkle forehead Whole face paralyzed .\\\\n Stroke UMN CAN wrinkle forehead Forehead spared . Uvula vs Tongue Uvula CN X Deviates AWAY from lesion.\\\\n Tongue CN XII Deviates TOWARD lesion. Corneal Reflex In CN V1 Feels the cotton .\\\\n Out CN VII Blinks the eye . Lateral Medullary Syndrome Wallenberg PICA stroke. Specific sign Dysphagia/Hoarseness Nucleus Ambiguus + Crossed Pain/Temp Face same side Body opposite side . Gag Reflex In CN IX Glossopharyngeal .\\\\n Out CN X Vagus . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l37", "title": "Lecture 37: Neurologic Exam II - Motor & Reflexes", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l37 module Neuro l37 Lecture 37 Neurologic Exam II - Motor & Reflexes\\n\\n Lecturer R. Morgan D.O.\\n Session Lecture 37\\n\\n 1. Motor System\\n Grading 0-5 Scale \\n 0 No contraction.\\n 1 Trace / Flicker.\\n 2 Active movement with gravity eliminated horizontal .\\n 3 Active movement against gravity only .\\n 4 Active movement against resistance but weak .\\n 5 Normal strength.\\n Key Roots \\n C5/6 Biceps Deltoid Brachioradialis.\\n C7 Triceps Wrist Extension.\\n C8/T1 Hand intrinsics Grip .\\n L2/3/4 Iliopsoas Hip Flex Quads Knee Ext .\\n L5 Tibialis Ant Dorsiflex/Heel walk .\\n S1 Gastroc Plantarflex/Toe walk .\\n\\n 2. Reflexes DTRs \\n Grading 0-4 Scale \\n 0 Absent.\\n 2 Normal.\\n 4 Hyperactive with Clonus UMN .\\n Roots \\n Biceps C5 C6.\\n Brachioradialis C6.\\n Triceps C7.\\n Patellar L4.\\n Achilles S1.\\n Reinforcement Jendrassik Maneuver hook fingers and pull distracts patient to elicit hard-to-find reflexes.\\n\\n 3. UMN vs LMN Findings\\n\\n| Feature | UMN Upper Motor Neuron | LMN Lower Motor Neuron |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Tone | Spastic Increased | Flaccid Decreased |\\n| Reflexes | Hyperreflexia +Clonus | Hyporeflexia / Absent |\\n| Babinski | Positive Upgoing toe | Negative Downgoing |\\n| Atrophy | Mild Disuse | Severe Denervation |\\n| Fasciculations | Absent | Present |\\n\\n 4. Coordination & Gait Cerebellum \\n Dysdiadochokinesis Inability to perform Rapid Alternating Movements RAM .\\n Dysmetria Overshooting/Undershooting Finger-to-Nose .\\n Ataxia Unsteady gait. Tandem Walking Heel-to-toe brings out subtle ataxia.\\n Romberg Test \\n Patient stands feet together eyes CLOSED .\\n Positive Patient sways/falls.\\n Meaning Loss of Proprioception Dorsal Columns . Note Cerebellar ataxia occurs with eyes OPEN too. \\n\\n 5. Other Signs\\n Pronator Drift Eyes closed arms out palms up. If one arm pronates and drops → Contralateral UMN Corticospinal weakness.\\n Primitive Reflexes Grasp Rooting Snout. Seen in Frontal Lobe lesions or babies . Lecture 37 Neurologic Exam II - Motor & Reflexes Neurology questions A patient presents with weakness in the right arm. On examination they have increased tone spasticity and hyperactive reflexes 3+ in the right biceps and triceps. There is no atrophy. This pattern suggests a lesion in the Lower Motor Neuron e.g. Brachial Plexus Upper Motor Neuron e.g. Corticospinal Tract Neuromuscular Junction Cerebellum Muscle fiber directly 1 UMN signs include Spasticity Hyperreflexia and preserved muscle bulk no atrophy . LMN signs would be flaccidity and atrophy. Which of the following describes muscle strength Grade 3/5? Active movement against gravity and resistance Active movement against gravity only Active movement with gravity eliminated Flicker of contraction only Normal strength 1 Grade 3 is movement against gravity but NOT resistance. Grade 2 is gravity eliminated. Grade 4 is against resistance. A positive Romberg test swaying significantly only when eyes are closed primarily indicates dysfunction of The Cerebellum The Motor Cortex Proprioception Dorsal Columns The Basal Ganglia The Thalamus 2 Romberg tests Proprioception . If vision eyes open compensates but balance fails when eyes close it s a sensory ataxia Dorsal Columns . The Achilles tendon reflex is mediated primarily by which spinal nerve root? L3 L4 L5 S1 S2 3 Achilles reflex is S1 . Patellar is L4. Dysdiadochokinesis inability to perform rapid alternating movements is a hallmark sign of damage to the Cerebellum Frontal Lobe Spinal Cord Basal Ganglia Cranial Nerves 0 Incoordination of rapid movements RAM is a classic Cerebellar sign. S1 Reflex Achilles Ankle Jerk Neurology L4 Reflex Patellar Knee Jerk Neurology C7 Reflex Triceps Neurology C5/C6 Reflex Biceps / Brachioradialis Neurology Grade 3 Strength Against Gravity Only Neurology Grade 2 Strength Gravity Eliminated Neurology Jendrassik Maneuver Use Reinforce/Distract for Reflexes Neurology Positive Romberg Meaning Sensory Ataxia Dorsal Column Proprioception Loss Neurology AMBOSS Neurology > Clinical Neurology > Neurological Examination FirstAid Neurology Reflex Roots Mnemonic S1 2 Buckle my shoe - Ankle .\\\\n L3 4 Kick the door - Knee .\\\\n C5 6 Pick up sticks - Biceps .\\\\n C7 8 Lay them straight - Triceps . Romberg is Sensory A positive Romberg falling only when eyes CLOSED indicates a Sensory Ataxia Dorsal Columns/Proprioception NOT Cerebellum. Pronator Drift Highly specific sign for subtle UMN Weakness . The supinator muscles are weaker than pronators in UMN lesions. Babinski Sign Stroke sole of foot. Normal = Toes curl DOWN. UMN Lesion = Big toe goes UP Dorsiflex + fanning . Jendrassik Maneuver Can t get a knee jerk? Have them hook hands and PULL. Distraction unmasks the reflex. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l38", "title": "Lecture 38: OMM Lab 18 - Sacral Diagnosis", "module": "OMM", "content": "id l38 module OMM l38 Lecture 38 OMM Lab 18 - Sacral Diagnosis\\n\\n Lecturer P. Noto D.O.\\n Session Lecture 38\\n\\n 1. Axes of Motion\\n Oblique Axes Named for the superior pole.\\n Right Oblique Axis Runs from Right Superior to Left Inferior pole.\\n Left Oblique Axis Runs from Left Superior to Right Inferior pole.\\n Usage Walking cycle Torsions .\\n Transverse Axes \\n Superior S2 Respiratory / Craniosacral motion.\\n Middle S2 Postural / Flexion-Extension.\\n Inferior S3 Innominate rotation Ilial shear .\\n\\n 2. Diagnosing Torsions vs Shears\\n L5 Rules \\n 1. L5 rotates OPPOSITE to the sacrum.\\n 2. L5 sidebends to the SAME side as the Oblique Axis.\\n Example If L5 is Rotated Right Sacrum is Rotated Left.\\n Seated Flexion Test SFT \\n Positive on the side of Dysfunction Blocked motion .\\n The axis is on the Opposite side of the positive SFT.\\n Physiologic Forward vs Non-Physiologic Backward \\n Physiologic Neutral mechanics Type I . \\ Forward\\ torsions L on L R on R .\\n Spring Test Negative It springs / Good motion .\\n Sphinx Test Negative Symmetry improves with extension .\\n Non-Physiologic Non-neutral Type II . \\ Backward\\ torsions L on R R on L .\\n Spring Test Positive Hard end feel / No spring .\\n Sphinx Test Positive Asymmetry gets worse / Pain .\\n\\n 3. The 4-Step Diagnosis\\n1. Seated Flexion Test Determines the side of dysfunction and thus the axis is on the opposite side .\\n2. Spring/Sphinx Determines if it is Forward Negative or Backward Positive .\\n3. L5 Confirms rotation.\\n4. Deep Sulcus / Post ILA \\n Forward Torsion Deep Sulcus and Posterior/Inf ILA are on Opposite sides.\\n Backward Torsion Deep Sulcus and Posterior/Inf ILA are on Opposite sides.\\n Wait simplify \\n Deep Sulcus = Side of Forward Rotation Sacral Base Anterior .\\n Posterior ILA = Side of Backward Rotation Sacral Base Posterior .\\n\\n 4. Specific Diagnoses\\n Left on Left Forward Torsion \\n Facing Left.\\n Axis Left.\\n L5 Rotated Right.\\n Spring Negative Good .\\n Right on Left Backward Torsion \\n Facing Right.\\n Axis Left.\\n L5 Rotated Left.\\n Spring Positive Bad .\\n Unilateral Shears \\n Not an axis dysfunction. Whole side is stuck forward or backward.\\n SFT + on same side as deep sulcus/low ILA if flexion or shallow sulcus/high ILA if extension . Lecture 38 OMM Lab 18 - Sacral Diagnosis Neurology questions A patient has a positive Seated Flexion Test on the RIGHT. The Spring Test is NEGATIVE. L5 is Rotated Right. What is the diagnosis? Right on Right Sacral Torsion Left on Left Sacral Torsion Right on Left Sacral Torsion Left on Right Sacral Torsion Right Unilateral Sacral Flexion 1 Step 1 SFT Right + → Axis is on the Left . So it s ? on Left .\\\\n Step 2 Spring Negative → Forward Torsion L on L .\\\\n Check Forward torsions have SAME letters. L on L fits. Which of the following findings is consistent with a Non-Physiologic Backward Sacral Torsion? Negative Sphinx Test Negative Spring Test Positive Spring Test L5 Rotated opposite to Sacrum Symmetrical Sacral Sulci 2 Positive Spring Test Hard/Stiff indicates the sacral base is stuck backward Posterior which is a non-physiologic backward torsion. In a Right on Right sacral torsion L5 will be Rotated Left Sidebent Left Rotated Left Sidebent Right Rotated Right Sidebent Right Rotated Right Sidebent Left Neutral 1 R on R Axis is Right → L5 Sidebends Right . Rotation is Right → L5 Rotates Left Opposite . Pattern Rot Left SB Right Type I neutral mechanics . The Superior Transverse Axis S2 is associated with which motion? Postural Flexion/Extension Innominate Rotation Respiratory / Craniosacral Motion Walking Cycle Disc Herniation 2 Superior Axis Respiratory/Craniosacral.\\\\n Middle Axis Postural.\\\\n Inferior Axis Innominate Ilial . If the Seated Flexion Test is positive on the LEFT which oblique axis is engaged? Left Oblique Axis Right Oblique Axis Middle Transverse Axis Inferior Transverse Axis Vertical Axis 1 SFT is positive on the side of dysfunction. The functioning axis is on the OPPOSITE side. Positive Left → Axis is Right . SFT Positive Side Meaning Side of Dysfunction Axis is Opposite OMM Spring Test Positive Meaning Stuck Backward Non-physiologic / Backward Torsion OMM L5 Rotation Rule Rotates OPPOSITE to Sacrum OMM L5 Sidebending Rule Sidebends SAME side as Axis OMM Same Letters e.g. L on L Forward Torsion Physiologic OMM Different Letters e.g. R on L Backward Torsion Non-Physiologic OMM AMBOSS OMM > Sacrum FirstAid OMM > Sacrum L5 Rule Mnemonic L5 acts like a Bully . If L5 rotates Right the Sacrum turns away Left . Sidebending side = Axis side. Spring Test Rules If it Springs it s a Functional Physiologic/Forward torsion.\\\\nIf it s Stiff Positive it s a Stuck Non-physiologic/Backward torsion. Naming Torsions Rotation on Axis .\\\\n R on R Forward Physiologic .\\\\n L on L Forward Physiologic .\\\\n R on L Backward Non-Physiologic .\\\\n L on R Backward Non-Physiologic .\\\\n Hint Same letters = Forward. Different letters = Backward. Seated Flexion Test Finds the side of ILIOSACRAL dysfunction. The axis is on the OPPOSITE side of the positive test. Sphinx Test Logic Extension Sphinx makes a forward sacrum happy more symmetrical . It makes a backward sacrum sad more asymmetrical . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l39", "title": "Lecture 39: Pharmacology - Adrenergic Antagonists", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l39 module Neuro l39 Lecture 39 Pharmacology - Adrenergic Antagonists\\n\\n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D.\\n Session Lecture 39\\n\\n 1. Alpha-Adrenergic Antagonists Alpha Blockers \\n Non-Selective Alpha-1 & Alpha-2 \\n Phenoxybenzamine Irreversible Covalent bond . Long duration. Used pre-op for Pheochromocytoma prevents hypertensive crisis .\\n Phentolamine Competitive Reversible . Used for Epinephrine Reversal local anesthetic overdose and Pheochromocytoma diagnosis/surgery.\\n Side Effects Severe Orthostatic Hypotension Reflex Tachycardia due to Alpha-2 block increasing NE release .\\n Selective Alpha-1 Blockers -zosins \\n Prazosin Terazosin Doxazosin Used for HTN add-on and BPH.\\n Tamsulosin Flomax Alpha-1A selective Prostate specific . Used for BPH less hypotension .\\n Adverse Effects First-Dose Effect Syncope/Severe Orthostatis → Take at bedtime! Floppy Iris Syndrome Cataract surgery risk .\\n Selective Alpha-2 Antagonist \\n Yohimbine Increases NE release blocks feedback . \\ Aphrodisiac\\ . Causes anxiety/tremor.\\n\\n 2. Beta-Adrenergic Antagonists Beta Blockers \\n Non-Selective Beta-1 & Beta-2 \\n Propranolol Lipophilic CNS effects nightmares/sedation . Used for Migraine prophylaxis Performance Anxiety Stage fright Thyroid Storm.\\n Contraindication Asthma/COPD Bronchoconstriction via B2 .\\n Beta-1 Selective Cardioselective \\ A-M\\ - Atenolol Metoprolol Esmolol .\\n Safer in lung disease.\\n Combined Alpha/Beta Blockers \\n Labetalol Carvedilol Block Alpha-1 Vasodilation + Beta-1/2.\\n Use CHF Carvedilol decreases mortality Hypertensive Emergencies Labetalol .\\n\\n 3. Key Pharmacology Concepts\\n Epinephrine Reversal \\n Normal Epi Increases BP Alpha-1 vasoconstriction > Beta-2 dilation .\\n Epi + Alpha Blocker Decreases BP Alpha blocked unmasks Beta-2 vasodilation .\\n Diabetes & Beta Blockers \\n Beta blockers MASK symptoms of hypoglycemia tachycardia tremors .\\n Non-selectives delay recovery from hypoglycemia block liver gluconeogenesis .\\n Withdrawal \\n NEVER stop abruptly. Chronic use UPREGULATES receptors.\\n Sudden stop → Rebound HTN Angina MI Sudden Death.\\n\\n 4. Therapeutic Uses Summary\\n\\n| Indication | Preferred Agents | Mechanism |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Hypertension | Metoprolol Atenolol | Decrease CO Renin inhibition. |\\n| Angina/MI | Metoprolol Carvedilol | Decrease O2 demand HR/Contractility . |\\n| CHF | Carvedilol Metoprolol Succinate Bisoprolol | Block sympathetic remodeling. Increase survival. |\\n| BPH | Tamsulosin Doxazosin | Relax prostate smooth muscle Alpha-1 . |\\n| Pheochromocytoma | Phenoxybenzamine | Irreversible Alpha block before surgery. |\\n| Glaucoma | Timolol | Decrease aqueous production. | Lecture 39 Pharmacology - Adrenergic Antagonists Neurology questions A patient with Pheochromocytoma is scheduled for surgery. Which drug is most appropriate for preoperative management to prevent hypertensive crisis? Propranolol Phenoxybenzamine Atenolol Prazosin Yohimbine 1 Phenoxybenzamine is an Irreversible alpha blocker. It is the gold standard for pheochromocytoma pre-op to prevent catecholamine surges. A 65-year-old male is started on Terazosin for BPH. What important counseling point must be provided? Take on an empty stomach because food reduces absorption. Take with grapefruit juice to enhance effect. Take the first dose at bedtime to avoid syncope. Discontinue immediately if nasal congestion occurs. Expect heart rate to decrease significantly. 2 First-Dose Effect Alpha-1 blockers cause significant orthostatic hypotension/syncope initially. Taking it at bedtime mitigates the risk of falling. Which of the following beta blockers also possesses Alpha-1 blocking activity providing additional vasodilation? Metoprolol Atenolol Carvedilol Timolol Esmolol 2 Carvedilol and Labetalol trigger Combined blockade Beta-1 Beta-2 and Alpha-1 . This provides vasodilation + heart rate control. A diabetic patient taking insulin is prescribed a beta blocker. They should be warned that the drug might mask which symptom of hypoglycemia? Sweating Hunger Tachycardia Confusion Fatigue 2 Tachycardia and tremors are mediated by the sympathetic response to hypoglycemia. Beta blockers mask these. Sweating cholinergic is usually preserved. The phenomenon known as Epinephrine Reversal describes a drop in blood pressure when Epinephrine is administered in the presence of A Beta Blocker An Alpha Blocker A Muscarinic Agonist A Ganglionic Blocker A Calcium Channel Blocker 1 An Alpha Blocker like Phentolamine blocks the vasoconstriction Alpha-1 of Epi leaving the vasodilation Beta-2 unopposed → BP drop. Phenoxybenzamine Mechanism Irreversible Alpha Blocker Pharmacology First Dose Effect Drug Class Alpha-1 Blockers -zosins Pharmacology A-M Beta Blockers Atenolol Metoprolol Beta-1 Selective Cardioselective Pharmacology Carvedilol / Labetalol Mechanism Mixed Alpha-1 and Beta Blockers Pharmacology Epinephrine Reversal Cause Epi + Alpha Blocker Unmasks Beta-2 Vasodilation Pharmacology Hypoglycemia Signs Masked by Beta Blockers Tachycardia Tremors Anxiety Pharmacology Hypoglycemia Sign SPARED by Beta Blockers Sweating Cholinergic Pharmacology Pheochromocytoma Pre-op Drug Phenoxybenzamine Pharmacology primarySource SketchyPharm chapter Autonomic Drugs > Adrenergic Antagonists alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs resource AMBOSS chapter Pharmacology > Autonomic nervous system Epinephrine Reversal Giving an Alpha Blocker Phentolamine before Epinephrine turns Epi s Pressor effect into a Depressor effect BP drops due to unopposed Beta-2 vasodilation . First Dose Effect Alpha-1 blockers Prazosin/Terazosin cause severe orthostatic hypotension on the first dose. Counseling Take the first dose at Bedtime . Beta Blocker Withdrawal Chronic beta blockade causes receptor Upregulation . Abrupt withdrawal leads to supersensitivity = Rebound Hypertension / MI . Always TAPER. Diabetes & Beta Blockers Beta blockers mask the warning signs of hypoglycemia palpitations tremors anxiety . Sweating Cholinergic is the only sign that remains. Pheochromocytoma Rule Always give Alpha blockers BEFORE Beta blockers. A before B . Giving Beta first leaves Alpha unopposed → Hypertensive Crisis. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l40", "title": "Lecture 40: Spinal Cord Syndromes & Mononeuropathies", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l40 module Neuro l40 Lecture 40 Spinal Cord Syndromes & Mononeuropathies\\n\\n Lecturer A. Leder D.O.\\n Session Lecture 40\\n\\n 1. Spinal Cord Anatomy Review\\n Dorsal Columns Vibration Proprioception Fine Touch. Cross in Brainstem .\\n Spinothalamic Tract Pain Temperature Crude Touch. Cross immediately/1-2 levels up .\\n Corticospinal Tract Motor. Cross in Medulla .\\n\\n 2. Spinal Cord Syndromes\\n Central Cord Syndrome Syringomyelia \\n Lesion Center of cord crossing spinothalamic fibers .\\n Presentation \\ Cape-like\\ loss of pain/temperature across shoulders/arms. Upper extremities > Lower.\\n Causes Syringomyelia Chiari I Trauma Hyperextension Tumor.\\n Posterior Column Syndrome Tabes Dorsalis \\n Lesion Dorsal Columns.\\n Presentation Loss of Proprioception/Vibration → Sensory Ataxia Stomping Gait +Romberg . Lhermitte Sign Shock down back .\\n Causes Neurosyphilis Tabes Dorsalis - Triad Lightning pains Ataxia Argyll Robertson Pupil B12 Deficiency.\\n Posterolateral Column Syndrome Subacute Combined Degeneration \\n Lesion Dorsal Columns + Corticospinal Tract.\\n Presentation Ataxia/Numbness + Spastic Paresis.\\n Causes Vitamin B12 Deficiency Pernicious anemia Gastric bypass Vegans Nitrous Oxide abuse \\ Whippets\\ .\\n Anterior Cord Syndrome \\n Lesion Anterior 2/3 of cord Spares Dorsal Columns .\\n Presentation Complete Motor Paralysis + Loss of Pain/Temp. Proprioception/Vibration SPARED .\\n Causes Anterior Spinal Artery ASA infarct Aortic aneurysm repair/surgery .\\n Brown-S\\u00e9quard Syndrome Hemicord \\n Lesion Half of the cord Hemisection .\\n Presentation \\n Ipsilateral Loss of Motor CST + Proprioception/Vibration DC .\\n Contralateral Loss of Pain/Temp Spinothalamic starting 1-2 levels below .\\n Causes Stab wound MS Tumor.\\n Cauda Equina Syndrome \\n Lesion Nerve roots L2-S5 Horse s tail .\\n Presentation Saddle Anesthesia Bowel/Bladder dysfunction Retention/Incontinence Radicular pain.\\n Management Surgical Emergency Decompression within 48hrs .\\n\\n 3. Common Mononeuropathies\\n Median Nerve Carpal Tunnel . Numbness 1st 3.5 digits. Thenar atrophy.\\n Ulnar Nerve Cubital Tunnel . Numbness 4th/5th digits. \\ Claw Hand\\ weak interossei .\\n Radial Nerve Saturday Night Palsy . Spiral groove compression. Wrist Drop .\\n Common Peroneal Fibular Nerve Unilateral Foot Drop Steppage gait . Compression at Fibular Head.\\n Lateral Femoral Cutaneous Nerve Meralgia Paresthetica . Numbness of lateral thigh. Caused by tight belts obesity pregnancy. Lecture 40 Spinal Cord Syndromes & Mononeuropathies Neurology questions A patient presents with loss of pain and temperature sensation in a cape-like distribution over the shoulders and arms. Vibration and proprioception are intact. This pattern is consistent with Anterior Cord Syndrome Central Cord Syndrome Brown-S\\u00e9quard Syndrome Posterior Column Syndrome Cauda Equina Syndrome 1 Central Cord Syndrome Syringomyelia expands from the central canal disrupting the crossing Spinothalamic fibers first causing the cape-like dissociation. Following a stab wound to the right side of the spinal cord Hemisection at T10 which finding would be expected? Loss of pain and temperature on the Right leg Loss of vibration and proprioception on the Left leg Paralysis of the Left leg Loss of pain and temperature on the Left leg Bilateral loss of all sensation 3 Brown-S\\u00e9quard Contralateral loss of Pain/Temp. Right lesion → Left leg Pain/Temp loss. Right leg would lose Motor/Vibe . A patient recovering from abdominal aortic aneurysm repair develops bilateral leg paralysis and loss of pain sensation but preserves vibration sense. The most likely diagnosis is Posterior Spinal Artery Infarct Anterior Spinal Artery Infarct Vitamin B12 Deficiency Tabes Dorsalis Guillain-Barr\\u00e9 Syndrome 1 Anterior Spinal Artery supplies the anterior 2/3 Motor + Spinothalamic . Infarct causes Anterior Cord Syndrome Paralysis + Pain loss with Dorsal Column sparing Vibe intact . Which of the following creates a Saturday Night Palsy causing wrist drop? Median Nerve compression at carpal tunnel Ulnar Nerve compression at cubital tunnel Radial Nerve compression at spiral groove Common Peroneal compression at fibular head Sciatic Nerve compression 2 Radial Nerve compression at the spiral groove humerus often from falling asleep with arm over a chair Saturday Night causes Wrist Drop . Subacute Combined Degeneration of the spinal cord involves demyelination of the Posterior Columns and Corticospinal Tracts due to deficiency of Vitamin B1 Thiamine Vitamin B3 Niacin Vitamin B12 Cobalamin Vitamin E Copper 2 Vitamin B12 deficiency or Nitrous Oxide toxicity causes Subacute Combined Degeneration. Central Cord Syndrome Pattern Cape-like Pain/Temp Loss Neurology Tabes Dorsalis Cause Neurosyphilis Tertiary Neurology Brown-Sequard Pain/Temp loss side Contralateral Opposite side Neurology Brown-Sequard Motor/Vibe loss side Ipsilateral Same side Neurology Wrist Drop Nerve Radial Nerve Neurology Foot Drop Nerve Common Peroneal Fibular Nerve Neurology Saddle Anesthesia + Incontinence Cauda Equina Syndrome Emergency Neurology Subacute Combined Degeneration Cause B12 Deficiency / Nitrous Oxide Neurology primarySource AMBOSS chapter Neurology > Spinal Cord Disorders alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Neurology > Spinal Cord Lesions resource Pathoma chapter Central Nervous System > Spinal Cord resource SketchyPath chapter Neurology > Spinal Cord Brown-S\\u00e9quard Rules You lose Motor & Vibe on the SAME side Ipsi .\\\\nYou lose Pain & Temp on the OPPOSITE side Contra . Anterior Cord Spare Anterior Spinal Artery stroke kills everything EXCEPT the Dorsal Columns . So they can still feel Vibration/Proprioception but can t move or feel pain. Syringomyelia Sign Cape-like distribution of numbness Pain/Temp loss across the shoulders and back. Often associated with Chiari Malformation . Whippets & B12 Nitrous Oxide irreversibly oxidizes B12 leading to Subacute Combined Degeneration Posterolateral cord syndrome . Look for ataxia + numbness in a party-goer. Cauda Equina Flags New onset Incontinence or retention + Saddle Anesthesia . This is an MRI + Surgery EMERGENCY. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l41", "title": "Lecture 41: From Myelopathy to Myopathy", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l41 module Neuro l41 Lecture 41 From Myelopathy to Myopathy\\n\\n Lecturer A. Leder D.O.\\n Session Lecture 41\\n\\n 1. Localization Approach\\n Myelopathy Spinal Cord UMN signs below Sensory level Bowel/Bladder.\\n Anterior Horn Cell LMN signs Weakness Atrophy Fasciculations . Sensory SPARED .\\n Radiculopathy Root Radiating pain Dermatomal sensory loss.\\n Plexopathy Multiple nerves/roots involved. Painful e.g. Parsonage-Turner .\\n Peripheral Neuropathy Stocking-glove sensory loss Distal weakness.\\n NMJ Fatigable weakness Diplopia/Ptosis. Sensory SPARED .\\n Myopathy Proximal muscle weakness. Sensory SPARED .\\n\\n 2. Anterior Horn Cell Disorders\\n ALS Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis \\n UMN + LMN Signs in same segment.\\n Asymmetric limb weakness Bulbar signs Dysarthria/Dysphagia .\\n Sensory Spared .\\n Polio & West Nile Virus \\n Acute Asymmetric flaccid paralysis LMN .\\n Fever/Meningeal signs.\\n Spinal Muscular Atrophy SMA \\n Genetic SMN1 . Symmetric LMN weakness.\\n\\n 3. Peripheral Nerve Disorders\\n Guillain-Barr\\u00e9 Syndrome AIDP \\n Acute Ascending Paralysis. Post-infectious Campylobacter Zika .\\n LP Albuminocytologic Dissociation High Protein Normal Cells .\\n Tx IVIG or Plasma Exchange. NO Steroids .\\n CIDP Chronic Inflammatory Demyelinating Polyneuropathy \\n Chronic >8 weeks . \\ Chronic GBS\\ .\\n Tx Steroids IVIG.\\n Leprosy Hansen s Disease. Mutilating neuropathy Armadillos .\\n\\n 4. Neuromuscular Junction NMJ \\n Myasthenia Gravis MG \\n Post-synaptic ACh Receptor Antibodies.\\n Clinical Ptosis Diplopia Bulbar weakness. Fatigue Worse at end of day .\\n Association Thymoma .\\n Lambert-Eaton Myasthenic Syndrome LEMS \\n Pre-synaptic Voltage-Gated Calcium Channel Antibodies.\\n Clinical Proximal leg weakness. Facilitation Strength IMPROVES with use . Dry mouth.\\n Association Small Cell Lung Cancer .\\n Botulism \\n Toxin blocks ACh release SNARE proteins .\\n Descending Paralysis. Pupils dilated/fixed. Honey infants .\\n\\n 5. Myopathies\\n Polymyositis/Dermatomyositis \\n Proximal symmetric weakness difficulty rising from chair .\\n Pain is uncommon .\\n Elevated CK. Lecture 41 From Myelopathy to Myopathy Neurology questions A 35-year-old male presents with ascending weakness starting in his legs and moving up. Reflexes are absent. Two weeks ago he had a diarrheal illness. CSF shows protein of 150 High and WBC of 2 Normal . What is the diagnosis? Multiple Sclerosis Guillain-Barr\\u00e9 Syndrome Myasthenia Gravis ALS Polio 1 GBS presents with acute ascending paralysis and areflexia after an infection Campylobacter . CSF shows Albuminocytologic Dissociation . Which of the following findings is classic for Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis ALS ? Pure sensory loss in a stocking-glove distribution Symmetric proximal muscle weakness with pain Combination of UMN and LMN signs with sensory sparing Diplopia and ptosis worsening at night Ascending paralysis with bladder dysfunction 2 ALS affects Anterior Horn Cells LMN and Corticospinal Tracts UMN . The hallmark is mixed UMN/LMN signs. Sensation is typically preserved. A patient with suspected Myasthenia Gravis is tested. Which antibody is most specific for this condition? Anti-Voltage Gated Calcium Channel Anti-Acetylcholine Receptor AChR Anti-Jo-1 Anti-GM1 Anti-Hu 1 AChR antibodies are pathogenic in Myasthenia Gravis Post-synaptic . Calcium channel antibodies are for Lambert-Eaton. Lambert-Eaton Myasthenic Syndrome is most commonly associated with which underlying malignancy? Thymoma Small Cell Lung Cancer Renal Cell Carcinoma Lymphoma Melanoma 1 Small Cell Lung Cancer is found in ~50% of patients with LEMS. Thymoma is associated with Myasthenia Gravis . Which feature distinguishes Polio from ALS? Polio causes LMN signs only; ALS causes UMN & LMN signs. Polio is progressive; ALS resolves spontaneously. Polio affects sensation; ALS spares sensation. Polio causes spasticity; ALS causes flaccidity. Polio is autoimmune; ALS is viral. 0 Polio destroys Anterior Horn Cells LMN only → Flaccid paralysis. ALS destroys AHCs LMN + CSTs UMN → Mixed Spastic/Flaccid. ALS Finding Mixed UMN + LMN Signs Neurology GBS CSF Finding Albuminocytologic Dissociation High Protein Normal Cells Neurology Myasthenia Gravis Mechanism Antibodies to Post-synaptic ACh Receptors Neurology Lambert-Eaton Mechanism Antibodies to Pre-synaptic Voltage-Gated Calcium Channels Neurology Lambert-Eaton Association Small Cell Lung Cancer Neurology Myasthenia Gravis Association Thymoma Neurology Botulism Presentation Descending Paralysis + Dilated Pupils Neurology primarySource AMBOSS chapter Neurology > Neuromuscular Junction Disorders alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Neurology > Neuromuscular resource Pathoma chapter Musculoskeletal > Neuromuscular Junction resource SketchyPath chapter Neurology > Neuromuscular & Peripheral Nerve UMN vs LMN Signs UMN Spasticity Hyperreflexia Babinski + No atrophy.\\\\n LMN Flaccidity Hyporeflexia Fasciculations Atrophy. ALS Hallmark Presence of UMN and LMN signs in the same limb/segment. e.g. Atrophied muscle has a brisk reflex . Sensory is Intact . GBS vs CIDP Treatment Both get IVIG/PLEX.\\\\n GBS Steroids do NOT work.\\\\n CIDP Steroids DO work. MG vs LEMS MG Eyes Post-synaptic. Worse with use. Thymoma.\\\\n LEMS Legs Pre-synaptic Ca channels . BETTER with use. Small Cell Ca. Albuminocytologic Dissociation High Protein but Normal WBCs in CSF. Classic finding in Guillain-Barr\\u00e9 Syndrome . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l42", "title": "Lecture 42: Diseases of Myelin", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l42 module Neuro l42 Lecture 42 Diseases of Myelin\\n\\n Lecturer M. Plummer M.D.\\n Session Lecture 42\\n\\n 1. Demyelinating Diseases Myelin Destruction \\n Multiple Sclerosis MS \\n Autoimmune destruction of CNS myelin Oligodendrocytes .\\n Pathology Periventricular Plaques \\ Dawson s Fingers\\ . Reactive Gliosis. Preservation of Axons early .\\n CSF Oligoclonal IgG Bands Intrathecal synthesis .\\n Clinical Relapsing-Remitting course. Optic Neuritis Painful vision loss INO MLF lesion Lhermitte Sign Electric shock with neck flexion Uhthoff Phenomenon Heat worsens symptoms .\\n Epidemiology Women > Men. Far from equator Vitamin D .\\n Guillain-Barr\\u00e9 Syndrome GBS \\n Autoimmune destruction of PNS myelin Schwann Cells .\\n Pathology Segmental demyelination + Lymphocytic infiltration.\\n Clinical Acute Ascending Paralysis + Areflexia.\\n Trigger Campylobacter jejuni CMV EBV Molecular Mimicry .\\n CSF Albuminocytologic Dissociation High Protein Normal Cells .\\n Central Pontine Myelinolysis Osmotic Demyelination \\n Cause Rapid correction of Hyponatremia . \\ From Low to High your Pons will Die\\ .\\n Clinical Locked-In Syndrome Quadriplegia + Preserved Consciousness/Eye movements .\\n\\n 2. Dysmyelinating Diseases Leukodystrophies \\n Definition Genetic defects in myelin formation or turnover .\\n Metachromatic Leukodystrophy MLD \\n Deficiency Arylsulfatase A .\\n Accumulation Sulfatides.\\n Path Metachromasia change in dye color .\\n Krabbe Disease Globoid Cell Leukodystrophy \\n Deficiency Galactocerebrosidase .\\n Accumulation Galactosylsphingosine Psychosine - Toxic.\\n Path Globoid Cells Engorged Macrophages .\\n Adrenoleukodystrophy ALD \\n Genetics X-Linked Recessive ABCD1 gene .\\n Defect Peroxisomal transporter.\\n Accumulation Very Long Chain Fatty Acids VLCFA .\\n Clinical Adrenal Insufficiency + CNS deterioration in young boys.\\n\\n 3. Infectious Demyelination\\n Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy PML \\n Pathogen JC Virus Reactivation .\\n Host Immunosuppressed AIDS CD4 < 200 .\\n Path Lytic infection of Oligodendrocytes. Enlarged nuclei with \\ Ground Glass\\ viral inclusions.\\n Clinical Rapidly progressive multifocal deficits. Death. Lecture 42 Diseases of Myelin Neurology questions A 28-year-old female presents with sudden loss of vision in her right eye associated with pain on eye movement. MRI shows periventricular white matter plaques. Analysis of her CSF would most likely reveal Albuminocytologic Dissociation Oligoclonal IgG Bands Neutrophilic Pleocytosis Decreased Glucose Positive India Ink Stain 1 Oligoclonal Bands found in the CSF but not serum indicate intrathecal IgG production a hallmark of Multiple Sclerosis . A 45-year-old alcoholic is brought to the ER with severe hyponatremia Na = 102 . The residents rapidly correct his sodium to 135 over 6 hours. Two days later he is quadriplegic but can blink and move his eyes vertically. What happened? Stroke in the MCA territory Subarachnoid Hemorrhage Central Pontine Myelinolysis Wernicke s Encephalopathy Herniation 2 Central Pontine Myelinolysis Osmotic Demyelination Syndrome is caused by rapid correction of hyponatremia . It destroys the Pons causing Locked-In Syndrome . Which pattern of weakness is characteristic of Guillain-Barr\\u00e9 Syndrome? Descending paralysis with pupillary dilation Hemiparesis sparing the face Ascending flaccid paralysis with areflexia Proximal muscle weakness with preserved sensation Waxing and waning weakness improving with rest 2 GBS causes an acute Ascending paralysis toes to nose with loss of reflexes Areflexia . Adrenoleukodystrophy involves the accumulation of which substance due to a peroxisomal defect? Sphingomyelin Sulfatides Very Long Chain Fatty Acids VLCFA Galactocerebroside Copper 2 ALD is a defect in the ABCD1 transporter preventing the breakdown of Very Long Chain Fatty Acids in peroxisomes. Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy PML is caused by the reactivation of which virus in immunocompromised patients? Herpes Simplex Virus 1 HSV-1 Cytomegalovirus CMV JC Virus Epstein-Barr Virus EBV Varicella Zoster Virus VZV 2 JC Virus causes PML. Mnemonic J ohn C unningham virus destroys oligodendoctyes in AIDS . Multiple Sclerosis CSF Finding Oligoclonal IgG Bands Neurology Lhermitte Sign Electric shock down back with neck flexion MS/Spinal Cord Neurology Uhthoff Phenomenon Heat worsens MS symptoms Neurology Central Pontine Myelinolysis Cause Rapid correction of Hyponatremia Neurology Metachromatic Leukodystrophy Enzyme Arylsulfatase A Neurology Krabbe Disease Hallmark Globoid Cells Neurology Adrenoleukodystrophy Accumulation Very Long Chain Fatty Acids VLCFA Neurology PML Virus JC Virus Neurology primarySource Pathoma chapter Central Nervous System > Demyelinating Disorders alternatives resource AMBOSS chapter Neurology > Demyelinating Disorders resource FirstAid chapter Neurology > Demyelinating resource SketchyPath chapter Neurology > Demyelinating MS Triad Charcot 1. Scanning Speech Dysarthria \\\\n2. Intention Tremor Cerebellar \\\\n3. Nystagmus INO/MLF Uhthoff s Phenomenon MS symptoms get WORSE with HEAT Hot bath exercise fever . Heat slows conduction in demyelinated axons. CPM Rule From Low to High your Pons will Die Hyponatremia → Rapid Correction → CPM/Locked-in .\\\\n From High to Low your Brain will Blow Hypernatremia → Rapid Correction → Cerebral Edema . Krabbe Findings Look for Globoid Cells Multinucleated Giant Cells and optic atrophy. Adrenoleukodystrophy Think Adrenal glands + Leuko White matter + Dystrophy Bad growth . Young boy with bronzing skin Addison s and behavioral changes. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l43", "title": "Lecture 43: Pharmacology of Multiple Sclerosis", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l43 module Neuro l43 Lecture 43 Pharmacology of Multiple Sclerosis\\n\\n Lecturer M. Pino Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 43\\n\\n 1. Acute Exacerbations Flare-ups \\n Glucocorticoids High Dose IV Methylprednisolone \\n Mechanism Induces Lipocortin → Inhibits Phospholipase A2 → Decreased Prostaglandins/Leukotrienes.\\n Use Shortens duration of acute attacks e.g. Optic Neuritis . Does NOT change long-term prognosis.\\n Adverse Effects HPA axis suppression Taper needed Osteoporosis Hyperglycemia Insomnia.\\n\\n 2. Disease Modifying Therapies DMTs \\n Interferon Beta 1a & 1b \\n Mechanism Cytokine JAK/STAT ; Inhibits T-cell activation/migration across BBB.\\n Adverse Effects Flu-like symptoms Fever chills aches Depression Liver toxicity.\\n Glatiramer Acetate \\n Mechanism Synthetic polypeptide Myelin Basic Protein decoy . Shifts immune response from Th1 Pro-inflammatory to Th2 Anti-inflammatory .\\n Adverse Effects Injection site reactions Post-injection systemic reaction Flushing Chest chest tightness - benign but scary .\\n Note Safest in Pregnancy .\\n Natalizumab \\n Mechanism Monoclonal antibody against $\\\\\\\\alpha$4-integrin . Blocks leukocytes from binding VCAM-1 and entering the CNS.\\n Adverse Effects PML Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy due to JC Virus reactivation.\\n Fingolimod \\n Mechanism S1P Receptor Modulator . Sequesters lymphocytes in lymph nodes can t exit .\\n Adverse Effects Bradycardia Monitor EKG at first dose Macular Edema.\\n Dimethyl Fumarate \\n Mechanism Activates Nrf2 Antioxidant pathway .\\n Adverse Effects Flushing GI upset.\\n Teriflunomide \\n Mechanism Inhibits Pyrimidine synthesis Dihydroorotate dehydrogenase .\\n Adverse Effects Hepatotoxicity Teratogenicity Contraindicated in pregnancy .\\n Mitoxantrone \\n Mechanism Anthracycline analog. Intercalates DNA / Inhibits Topoisomerase II.\\n Use Secondary Progressive MS.\\n Adverse Effects Cardiotoxicity Dilated Cardiomyopathy - monitor EF Acute Myeloid Leukemia AML . \\ Blue\\ discoloration of urine/sclera.\\n Anti-CD20 Ocrelizumab Rituximab \\n Mechanism B-cell depletion.\\n Adverse Effects Infusion reactions Hepatitis B reactivation.\\n\\n 3. Symptomatic Treatment\\n Spasticity \\n Baclofen GABA-B agonist .\\n Tizanidine $\\\\\\\\alpha$2-adrenergic agonist .\\n Walking Speed \\n Dalfampridine K+ Channel Blocker - Enhances conduction.\\n Urinary Incontinence \\n Oxybutynin Muscarinic Antagonist .\\n Botulinum Toxin Cleaves SNARE proteins . Lecture 43 Pharmacology of Multiple Sclerosis Neurology questions A 32-year-old female with Multiple Sclerosis is started on a new medication. Since starting she complains of flushed skin and profound gastrointestinal distress. Which medication mechanism is most likely responsible? Inhibition of Dihydroorotate Dehydrogenase Activation of Nrf2 pathway Sequestration of lymphocytes in lymph nodes Blockade of alpha-4 integrin Depletion of CD20 B cells 1 Dimethyl Fumarate activates the Nrf2 antioxidant pathway. Its most common side effects are Flushing and GI upset. A patient being treated for MS develops Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy PML . Which drug mechanism is associated with this complication? Interferon beta-1a Glatiramer Acetate Natalizumab Baclofen Methylprednisolone 2 Natalizumab blocks alpha-4 integrin preventing immune surveillance in the CNS allowing JC Virus reactivation PML . Which medication helps improve walking speed in MS patients by blocking potassium channels? Tizanidine Dalfampridine Oxybutynin Modafinil Amantadine 1 Dalfampridine blocks voltage-gated Potassium channels increasing action potential duration and improving conduction. Before starting a patient on Fingolimod which baseline test is most critical? Liver Function Tests Echocardiogram Electrocardiogram EKG Pulmonary Function Tests Thyroid Stimulating Hormone 2 Fingolimod can cause significant Bradycardia and heart block after the first dose. Observation and EKG monitoring are required. Which agent treats spasticity in MS by acting as an agonist at GABA-B receptors? Diazepam Dantrolene Baclofen Tizanidine Cyclobenzaprine 2 Baclofen is a GABA-B agonist used centrally for spasticity. Tizanidine is an alpha-2 agonist; Dantrolene works on muscle Ryanodine receptors . Natalizumab Mechanism Blocks Alpha-4 Integrin prevents CNS entry Pharmacology Natalizumab Serious AE PML JC Virus Pharmacology Fingolimod Mechanism S1P Receptor Modulator Sequesters lymphocytes Pharmacology Fingolimod AE Bradycardia First dose Pharmacology Teriflunomide Contraindication Pregnancy Teratogenic Pharmacology Glatiramer Acetate Mechanism Myelin Basic Protein Decoy Th1 → Th2 Pharmacology Interferon Beta AE Flu-like symptoms Depression Pharmacology Dalfampridine Mechanism Potassium Channel Blocker Improves walking Pharmacology primarySource SketchyPharm chapter Smooth Muscle & CNS Drugs > Multiple Sclerosis Drugs alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Pharmacology > Neurologic resource AMBOSS chapter Neurology > Multiple Sclerosis > Management PML Risk Natalizumab is highly effective but carries a risk of PML Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy if the patient is JC Virus positive. Natalizumab says NO to leukocytes entering the brain . Fingolimod & The Heart Fingolimod causes Bradycardia . You must monitor the patient s heart rate/EKG for 6 hours after the First Dose . Mitoxantrone Toxicity Like Doxorubicin Mitoxantrone is Cardiotoxic Dilated Cardiomyopathy . Must monitor Ejection Fraction. Pregnancy & MS Teriflunomide is Teratogenic . Glatiramer Acetate is generally considered the safest during pregnancy. Walking Drug Dalfampridine is the Walking Drug . It blocks Potassium Channels to prolong the action potential and improve conduction in demyelinated nerves. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l44", "title": "Lecture 44: Introduction to Ophthalmology", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l44 module Neuro l44 Lecture 44 Introduction to Ophthalmology\\n\\n Lecturer A. Nattis D.O.\\n Session Lecture 44\\n\\n 1. The Red Eye\\n Conjunctivitis \\n Viral Pink Eye Watery discharge. Adenovirus. Highly contagious.\\n Bacterial Purulent pus discharge. \\ Glued shut\\ in AM.\\n Allergic Itching Pruritus . Bilateral.\\n Acute Angle Closure Glaucoma \\n Presentation Severe eye pain Headache Nausea/Vomiting Haloes around lights .\\n Exam Mid-dilated fixed pupil . Cloudy cornea. High IOP. Emergency .\\n Uveitis Iritis \\n Presentation Deep pain Photophobia Light sensitivity Redness.\\n Exam Cells and Flare in anterior chamber. Irregular pupil Synechiae .\\n Association Autoimmune HLA-B27 Sarcoidosis .\\n Scleritis \\n Presentation Deep \\ boring\\ pain. Wakes patient at night.\\n Association Rheumatoid Arthritis.\\n\\n 2. Neuro-Ophthalmology Pupils \\n Horner s Syndrome \\n Triad Ptosis Droopy lid Miosis Small pupil Anhidrosis No sweat .\\n Cause Sympathetic pathway lesion Pancoast tumor Carotid dissection .\\n CN III Palsy \\n Presentation Eye is \\ Down and Out\\ . Ptosis.\\n Pupil If Dilated \\ Blown\\ it s a PCom Aneurysm Surgical Emergency . If pupil spared it s Ischemic Diabetes .\\n Relative Afferent Pupillary Defect RAPD / Marcus Gunn Pupil \\n Test Swinging Flashlight Test.\\n Result Pupil appears to Dilate when light is swung to the bad eye because it senses less light than the good eye .\\n Cause Optic Nerve lesion Optic Neuritis .\\n Argyll Robertson Pupil \\n Sign Accommodates but does not react to light \\ Prostitute s Pupil\\ .\\n Cause Neurosyphilis.\\n\\n 3. Retina & Optic Nerve\\n Retinal Detachment \\n Symptoms Flashes and Floaters . \\ Curtain coming down\\ over vision.\\n Tx Surgical Emergency.\\n Central Retinal Artery Occlusion CRAO \\n Sign Cherry Red Spot on macula. Pale retina. Sudden painless vision loss.\\n Cause Embolus Stroke of the eye .\\n Central Retinal Vein Occlusion CRVO \\n Sign \\ Blood and Thunder\\ Diffuse hemorrhages .\\n Optic Neuritis \\n Sign Painful vision loss. RAPD + . Associated with MS .\\n Giant Cell Arteritis Temporal Arteritis \\n Sign Ischemic Optic Neuropathy AION . Jaw claudication. Scalp tenderness. high ESR.\\n Tx High Dose Steroids immediately to save the other eye. Lecture 44 Introduction to Ophthalmology Neurology questions A 65-year-old male presents with sudden painless loss of vision in his right eye. Fundoscopy reveals a pale retina with a cherry-red spot at the fovea. What is the most likely diagnosis? Central Retinal Vein Occlusion Retinal Detachment Central Retinal Artery Occlusion Acute Angle Closure Glaucoma Optic Neuritis 2 Central Retinal Artery Occlusion CRAO presents with a pale retina edema and a Cherry Red Spot thin fovea showing choroid . It is an embolic event. A patient presents with a painful red eye nausea and halos around lights. The pupil is mid-dilated and non-reactive. What is the immediate concern? Viral Conjunctivitis Acute Angle Closure Glaucoma Uveitis Scleritis Corneal Abrasion 1 Acute Angle Closure Glaucoma is an ocular emergency characterized by high pressure pain halos and a fixed mid-dilated pupil. Which finding is part of the classic triad of Horner s Syndrome? Mydriasis Dilated Pupil Exophthalmos Bulging Eye Anhidrosis Lack of sweating Diplopia Double vision Hyperhidrosis Excess sweating 2 Horner s Triad Ptosis Miosis Anhidrosis on the ipsilateral face . Does the pupil in a CN III palsy due to a PCom aneurysm typically present as dilated or constricted? Constricted Miotic Dilated Mydriatic Normal Irregular Pear-shaped 1 Parasympathetic fibers run on the outside of CN III. Compressive lesions like an PCom Aneurysm compress these fibers first causing a Dilated pupil. A patient with MS presents with pain on eye movement and decreased color vision. Swinging flashlight test shows a Relative Afferent Pupillary Defect RAPD . Diagnosis? Papilledema Optic Neuritis Glaucoma Cataract Macular Degeneration 1 Optic Neuritis is inflammation of the optic nerve common in MS. It causes painful vision loss and an RAPD . Horner s Syndrome Triad Ptosis Miosis Anhidrosis Ophthalmology Acute Angle Closure Glaucoma Symptoms Pain Haloes Nausea Fixed Mid-dilated Pupil Ophthalmology Cherry Red Spot Diagnosis Central Retinal Artery Occlusion CRAO Ophthalmology Blood and Thunder Fundus Central Retinal Vein Occlusion CRVO Ophthalmology Retinal Detachment Symptoms Flashes Floaters Curtain falling Ophthalmology CN3 Palsy Appearance Down and Out Ophthalmology Painful Vision Loss + MS Optic Neuritis Ophthalmology Painless Vision Loss + Jaw Claudication Giant Cell Arteritis AION Ophthalmology primarySource AMBOSS chapter Ophthalmology > Red Eye / Retina alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Ophthalmology resource Pathoma chapter Eye Pathology resource BandB chapter Neurology > The Eye > Ophthalmology Basics Horner s vs CN3 Palsy Horner s Pupil is Small Miosis . Ptosis is mild.\\\\n CN3 Palsy Pupil is Large Dilated . Ptosis is severe Eye shut . The Painful Red Eye Sudden pain + Haloes + Hard eye = Acute Glaucoma .\\\\nPhotophobia + Autoimmune Hx = Uveitis .\\\\nContact Lens + Sleeping in them = Corneal Ulcer Pseudomonas . Retinal Detachment Warning New onset Flashes and Floaters is a Retinal Detachment until proven otherwise. Papilledema Bilateral Optic Disc Swelling due to increased Intracranial Pressure. In an obese young female think Pseudotumor Cerebri IIH . Hutchinson s Sign Shingles vesicle on the tip of the nose predicts ocular involvement Herpes Zoster Ophthalmicus . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l45", "title": "Lecture 45: Structure-Function Integration to Identify Lesions", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l45 module Neuro l45 Lecture 45 Structure-Function Integration to Identify Lesions\\n\\n Lecturers J. Xie Ph.D. & K. Poole Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 45\\n\\n 1. Brainstem Vascular Syndromes\\n Lateral Medullary Syndrome Wallenberg \\n Artery PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery or Vertebral Artery.\\n Key Structures & Symptoms \\n Nucleus Ambiguus CN 9 10 Dysphagia Hoarseness Decreased Gang Reflex. Specific to Lateral .\\n Vestibular Nuclei Vertigo Nystagmus Vomiting.\\n Sympathetic Tract Horner s Syndrome Ipsilateral .\\n Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus Loss of Pain/Temp on Ipsilateral Face .\\n Spinothalamic Tract Loss of Pain/Temp on Contralateral Body .\\n Inferior Cerebellar Peduncle Ataxia Fall toward lesion .\\n Mnemonic \\ Don t PICA horse hoarseness that can t eat dysphagia .\\ \\n Medial Medullary Syndrome \\n Artery Anterior Spinal Artery ASA .\\n Key Structures & Symptoms \\n Corticospinal Tract Contralateral Hemiparesis Upper & Lower limbs .\\n Medial Lemniscus Contralateral loss of Proprioception/Vibration.\\n CN XII Hypoglossal Tongue deviates Toward the lesion \\ Lick your wounds\\ .\\n Medial Pontine Syndrome \\n Artery Paramedian branches of Basilar Artery.\\n Key Contralateral Hemiparesis + CN VI Abducens palsy Eye can t look out .\\n\\n 2. Cranial Nerve & Vessel Associations\\n CN III Oculomotor \\n Runs between Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA and Superior Cerebellar Artery SCA .\\n Susceptible to PCom Aneurysms → Dilated Pupil + Down & Out Eye.\\n\\n 3. Cerebellar Lesions\\n Lateral Cerebellum Hemispheres \\n Affects Limbs .\\n Signs Dysdiadochokinesia Intention Tremor Dysmetria.\\n Side Ipsilateral fall/ataxia.\\n Medial Cerebellum Vermis/Flocculonodular \\n Affects Trunk .\\n Signs Truncal Ataxia Wide-based gait Nystagmus.\\n Alcohol Specific damage to Vermis .\\n\\n 4. Spinal Cord Tracts Review\\n Dorsal Column-Medial Lemniscus DCML \\n Function Touch Vibration Proprioception.\\n Crossing Medulla Internal Arcuate Fibers .\\n Spinothalamic Tract STT \\n Function Pain Temperature.\\n Crossing Spinal Cord Anterior White Commissure 1-2 levels up .\\n Corticospinal Tract CST \\n Function Voluntary Motor.\\n Crossing Medulla Pyramids . Lecture 45 Structure-Function Integration to Identify Lesions Neurology questions A 65-year-old male presents with sudden onset vertigo vomiting and difficulty swallowing dysphagia . Exam shows a droopy left eyelid small left pupil loss of pain sensation on the left face and loss of pain sensation on the right body. Where is the lesion? Right Lateral Medulla Left Lateral Medulla Left Medial Medulla Right Medial Pons Left Lateral Pons 1 Left Lateral Medullary Syndrome Wallenberg Ipsilateral Horner s Ipsilateral Face sensory loss Contralateral Body sensory loss Dysphagia Nucleus Ambiguus . Lesion is on the Left . A patient presents with right-sided hemiparesis arm and leg and the tongue deviates to the left upon protrusion. Sensation of pain and temperature is intact. Which artery is likely occluded? Left PICA Right PICA Left Anterior Spinal Artery Right Anterior Spinal Artery Basilar Artery 2 Medial Medullary Syndrome Tongue deviates Toward lesion Left CN XII . Hemiparesis is Contralateral Right side . Artery is the Anterior Spinal Artery . Alcoholic cerebellar degeneration primarily affects which structure resulting in a wide-based ataxic gait? Flocculus Lateral Hemispheres Vermis Dentate Nucleus Inferior Olive 2 Chronic alcohol use causes atrophy of the Anterior Vermis leading to truncal ataxia and gait instability legs/trunk often sparing the arms. A cavernous sinus thrombosis would most likely affect which cranial nerve traveling through the sinus? Optic Nerve CN II Abducens Nerve CN VI Facial Nerve CN VII Vestibulocochlear Nerve CN VIII Hypoglossal Nerve CN XII 1 CN VI Abducens travels inside the cavernous sinus along with the Carotid Artery . CN III IV V1 V2 are in the lateral wall. CN VI is often the first affected. In Brown-S\\u00e9quard syndrome spinal cord hemisection pain and temperature sensation are lost Ipsilateral to the lesion at the level of the lesion Contralateral to the lesion 1-2 segments below Ipsilateral to the lesion 1-2 segments below Bilaterally at the level of the lesion only Completely preserved 1 Spinothalamic tract crosses almost immediately. A hemisection causes Contralateral loss of Pain/Temp starting 1-2 segments below the lesion. Wallenberg Syndrome Artery PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Neurology Wallenberg Syndrome Key Symptom Dysphagia / Hoarseness Nucleus Ambiguus Neurology Medial Medullary Artery Anterior Spinal Artery ASA Neurology Medial Medullary Key Sign Tongue deviation to lesion side + Contralateral Hemiparesis Neurology Cerebellar Vermis Lesion Truncal Ataxia Drunk walk Neurology Cerebellar Hemisphere Lesion Limb Ataxia Finger-to-Nose Neurology CN 3 Palsy + Dilated Pupil PCom Aneurysm Neurology primarySource AMBOSS chapter Neurology > Brainstem Syndromes alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Neurology > Brainstem resource Pathoma chapter Central Nervous System > Brainstem resource SketchyPath chapter Neurology > Brainstem Rule of 4s Medial vs Lateral Medial structures Motor pathway CST Medial Lemniscus MLF Motor CNs 3 4 6 12 .\\\\n Lateral structures Spinothalamic Spinocerebellar Spinal Trigeminal Sympathetic Sensory CNs 8 . Wallenberg Hallmark The ONLY brainstem stroke with Dysphagia/Hoarseness Nucleus Ambiguus is Lateral Medullary PICA . Tongue Deviation CN XII lesion Tongue deviates TOWARD the side of the lesion Lick your wounds . This is the hallmark of Medial Medullary Syndrome . PCom Aneurysm A Blown Pupil Dilated with a CN III palsy is a PCom Aneurysm until proven otherwise. Ischemic CN III palsy Diabetes spares the pupil. Cerebellar Hemisphere vs Vermis Lateral = L imbs Arm/Leg ataxia .\\\\n Medial Ver M is = Midline Trunk/Gait ataxia . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l46", "title": "Lecture 46: Sensory System & Special Techniques", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l46 module Neuro l46 Lecture 46 Sensory System & Special Techniques\\n\\n Lecturer R. Morgan D.O.\\n Session Lecture 46\\n\\n 1. Sensory Pathways Review\\n Spinothalamic Tract \\n Modality Pain Temperature Crude Touch.\\n Fibers Small Unmyelinated/Thinly myelinated.\\n Posterior Columns DCML \\n Modality Vibration Proprioception Fine Touch.\\n Fibers Large Heavily myelinated.\\n Testing Always test Distal first Toes/Fingers . If normal distally proximal is likely normal.\\n\\n 2. Cortical Sensation Discriminative \\n Requires Intact primary sensation DCML/STT + Intact Parietal Lobe Sensory Cortex .\\n Stereognosis Identify common object key coin in hand with eyes closed.\\n Graphesthesia Identify number drawn on palm.\\n Two-Point Discrimination Distinguish two points vs one.\\n Extinction Touch both sides simultaneously. If patient only feels one side suspects contralateral parietal lesion Neglect .\\n\\n 3. Meningeal Signs Meningitis/SAH \\n Brudzinski Sign \\n Maneuver Flex patient s neck chin to chest .\\n Positive Involuntary flexion of Hips and Knees .\\n Kernig Sign \\n Maneuver Flex hip/knee to 90 degrees then slowly Extend the knee.\\n Positive Pain/Resistance in hamstring.\\n\\n 4. Special Musculoskeletal Tests\\n Cervical Spine Radiculopathy \\n Spurling Test Extend and Rotate head to affected side + Axial Compression. Positive = Radiation of pain down arm.\\n Distraction Test Lift head Traction . Positive = Relief of pain.\\n Lumbar Spine Sciatica/Disc Herniation \\n Straight Leg Raise SLR Passive leg raise. Positive = Radicular pain between 30-70 degrees.\\n Bragard s Test SLR + Dorsiflexion of foot. Increases nerve stretch.\\n Crossed Well SLR Raise the Unaffected leg. Positive = Pain in the Affected leg. Highly specific for Disc Herniation .\\n Malingering / Functional \\n Hoover Test Cup both heels. Ask patient to lift the \\ paralyzed\\ leg.\\n Positive for Malingering You do NOT feel downward pressure from the \\ normal\\ heel. Normal physiology requires pushing down with the opposite leg to lift one leg . Lecture 46 Sensory System & Special Techniques Neurology questions You are performing a neurological exam on a patient claiming to have a paralyzed right leg. You cup your hands under both heels and ask him to lift his right leg. He strains but his leg doesn t move. You feel no pressure in your left hand under his left heel. What is the interpretation? True Right Leg Paralysis Left Leg Paralysis Positive Hoover Sign Lack of Effort Sciatic Nerve Impingement Cauda Equina Syndrome 2 Positive Hoover Sign Lack of downward pressure in the contralateral normal heel indicates the patient is not making a genuine effort to lift the affected leg. Which physical exam finding is most specific for a lumbar disc herniation? Positive Straight Leg Raise Ipsilateral Positive Crossed Well Straight Leg Raise Paraspinal muscle tenderness Decreased range of motion Negative Brudzinski sign 1 While the Ipsilateral SLR is sensitive the Crossed Well Straight Leg Raise pain in the affected leg when lifting the healthy leg is highly Specific for disc herniation. A patient presents with fever and severe headache. When you flex the patient s neck their hips and knees spontaneously flex. This is a positive Kernig Sign Brudzinski Sign Babinski Sign Lhermitte Sign Hoffmann Sign 1 Brudzinski Sign Neck flexion causes involuntary hip/knee flexion. It suggests meningeal irritation Meningitis . A patient complains of shooting pain down their right arm. You extend and rotate their head to the right and apply downward pressure. This maneuver reproduces the pain. What is the test? Distraction Test Spurling Test Tinel Sign Phalen Test Lhermitte Sign 1 Spurling Test Cervical compression with extension/rotation narrows the neural foramen. Reproduction of radicular pain indicates Cervical Radiculopathy. Which sensory modality is carried by the Dorsal Columns Posterior Columns ? Pain Temperature Crude Touch Vibration and Proprioception Itch 3 Dorsal Columns Vibration Proprioception Fine Touch. Spinothalamic = Pain Temp Crude Touch . Brudzinski Sign Neck flexion → Hip/Knee flexion Meningitis Neurology Kernig Sign Knee extension → Pain Meningitis Neurology Spurling Test Cervical Compression → Radicular Pain Radiculopathy Neurology Hoover Test Test for Malingering Lack of contralateral heel pressure Neurology Crossed Straight Leg Raise Lift Good Leg → Pain in Bad Leg Specific for Disc Herniation Neurology Stereognosis Identify object in hand Parietal Cortex Neurology Graphesthesia Identify number drawn on palm Parietal Cortex Neurology primarySource AMBOSS chapter Neurology > Neurological Examination alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Neurology > Exam resource BandB chapter Neurology > Clinical Neurology > Sensory Exam Hoover Test Logic In a True effort to lift one leg the other leg MUST push down. If you don t feel downward pressure from the good leg when they try to lift the bad leg they aren t really trying. BrudZinski vs Kernig B rudzinski = B rain Neck flexion causes knees to bend .\\\\n K ernig = K nee Knee extension causes pain . Spurling Test think Spurling crushes the Spine . Compression reproduces radicular pain. Distraction relieves it. Crossed SLR The Crossed Straight Leg Raise lifting the good leg causes pain in the bad leg is very Specific for a Herniated Disc. Stocking-Glove The classic pattern of Diabetic Polyneuropathy. Starts in toes/feet longest nerves and ascends. Sensory loss is symmetrical. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l48", "title": "Lecture 48: Pharmacology: Sedative Hypnotics", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l48 module Neuro l48 Lecture 48 Pharmacology Sedative Hypnotics Neurology Lecture 48 Pharmacology Sedative Hypnotics\\n\\n Lecturer M. Pino Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 48\\n\\n 1. Mechanisms of CNS Depression\\n Story Mode \\nTo induce sedation or sleep we typically need to \\ calm down\\ the brain s excitatory pathways. The most effective way to do this is by enhancing the activity of GABA Gamma-Aminobutyric Acid the primary inhibitory neurotransmitter in the CNS. When GABA binds to its receptor it opens a chloride channel allowing negatively charged chloride ions $Cl^-$ to flow into the neuron. This causes hyperpolarization making the neuron less likelytor complex but interact with it in distinct ways that determine their safety profile.\\n\\n 1.1 GABA-A Receptor Interactions\\n GABA Dependent vs. Independent \\n Benzodiazepines & Z-Drugs \\n Bind to a specific site between $\\\\alpha$ and $\\\\gamma$ subunits .\\n Require GABA to be present to open the channel.\\n Effect Increase the frequency of channel opening.\\n Safety Exhibit a \\ Ceiling Effect\\ — once receptors are saturated they can t open more channels without GABA. Less risk of fatal respiratory depression alone.\\n Barbiturates & Ethanol \\n Bind to a distinct site Barbiturates between $\\\\alpha$ and $\\\\beta$ .\\n Do NOT require GABA at high doses can open the channel independently .\\n Effect Increase the duration of channel opening.\\n Safety No Ceiling Effect . Can cause profound CNS depression coma and fatal respiratory depression as dose increases.\\n\\n 1.2 Dose-Response Relationship\\n Linear Slope Drug A - Barbiturates/Alcohol \\n Steep linear increase in CNS depression with dose.\\n Sedation $\\\\rightarrow$ Hypnosis $\\\\rightarrow$ Anesthesia $\\\\rightarrow$ Coma $\\\\rightarrow$ Death.\\n Plateau Effect Drug B - Benzodiazepines \\n Curve flattens out.\\n Significant sedation but reduced risk of lethal respiratory depression unless combined with other depressants .\\n\\n correlate\\n Drug-Drug Interactions & Respiratory Depression \\n The Lethal Combo \\nWhile Benzodiazepines rarely cause fatal respiratory depression on their own due to the ceiling effect combining them with other CNS depressants removes this safety net.\\n Mechanism Synergistic effects on GABA/respiratory centers.\\n Combinations to Avoid \\n Benzodiazepines + Alcohol \\n Benzodiazepines + Opioids Fentanyl Heroin Oxycodone \\n Benzodiazepines + Barbiturates \\n Clinical Consequence A patient taking Xanax Alprazolam who drinks alcohol at a party runs a significantly higher risk of stopping breathing.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. Barbiturates\\n Story Mode \\nOnce the mainstay of sedation Barbiturates \\ -barbital\\ have largely been replaced by Benzodiazepines due to their narrow therapeutic index and high abuse potential. However they retain niche uses in neurology epilepsy and anesthesia. Their ability to induce liver enzymes also makes them practically troublesome for drug interactions.\\n\\n 2.1 Key Agents & Uses\\n Phenobarbital \\n Use Seizure disorders Epilepsy . First-line for neonatal seizures .\\n Thiopental \\n Use Induction of anesthesia rapid onset .\\n Mechanism Highly lipid-soluble $\\\\rightarrow$ crosses Blood-Brain Barrier BBB instantly.\\n Primidone Metabolized to Phenobarbital.\\n\\n 2.2 Pharmacokinetics\\n Metabolism \\n Hepatic metabolism.\\n CYP Inducers especially CYP3A4 Increases metabolism of other drugs e.g. Oral Contraceptives $\\\\rightarrow$ unwanted pregnancy; Warfarin $\\\\rightarrow$ clotting risk .\\n Redistribution Thiopental \\n Rapid onset due to high blood flow to the brain.\\n Short duration not because of metabolism but because the drug redistributes to less vascular tissues muscle fat .\\n\\n 2.3 Adverse Effects\\n Respiratory Depression Fatal in overdose supported ventilation required; alkalize urine to trap and excrete .\\n Sedation/Hangover Especially in elderly.\\n Tolerance & Dependence \\n Metabolic Tolerance Enzyme induction leads to faster breakdown.\\n Pharmacodynamic Tolerance Receptor downregulation.\\n Contraindication Porphyria induces ALA synthase $\\\\rightarrow$ heme synthesis $\\\\rightarrow$ toxic porphyrin accumulation .\\n\\n correlate\\n Porphyria Exacerbation \\n The Mechanism \\nBarbiturates induce Cytochrome P450 enzymes. The synthesis of these enzymes requires heme . The body responds by ramping up heme production via ALA synthase.\\n In Porphyria Patients They have a genetic block in the heme pathway. Ramping up synthesis upstream leads to a toxic buildup of porphyrin intermediates.\\n Symptoms Abdominal pain psychiatric symptoms \\ madness\\ red/brown urine.\\n Rule Never give Barbiturates to a patient with acute intermittent porphyria.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. Benzodiazepines\\n Story Mode \\nBenzodiazepines \\ -zolam\\ \\ -zepam\\ are the modern workhorses for anxiety acute seizure control and alcohol withdrawal. By requiring GABA to work they offer a safer profile. Choice of agent often depends on pharmacokinetics half-life and metabolic pathway especially in patients with \\ fragile\\ livers like the elderly.\\n\\n 3.1 Agents & Classification\\n Long-Acting \\n Diazepam Valium Chlordiazepoxide \\n Use Alcohol withdrawal smoother taper seizures muscle spasm.\\n Metabolism Converted to active metabolites e.g. desmethyldiazepam with very long half-lives 40-100 hours .\\n Intermediate/Short-Acting \\n Lorazepam Ativan Status epilepticus anxiety.\\n Alprazolam Xanax Panic disorder rapid onset high abuse potential.\\n Midazolam Versed Procedural sedation/anesthesia colonoscopy . Causes Anterograde Amnesia .\\n\\n 3.2 Metabolism & The Elderly\\n The Problem Elderly patients have reduced Phase I oxidative metabolism and renal function. Long-acting benzos Diazepam accumulate $\\\\rightarrow$ falls confusion pseudodementia .\\n The Solution \\ OTL\\ Use agents that undergo only Phase II Conjugation/Gluronidation .\\n O xazepam\\n T emazepam\\n L orazepam\\n\\n 3.3 Antidote Flumazenil\\n Mechanism Competitive antagonist at the Benzodiazepine binding site.\\n Use Reversal of Benzodiazepine overdose e.g. post-surgery sedation .\\n Warning Can precipitate acute withdrawal seizures in chronic benzodiazepine users. Administer with extreme caution.\\n\\n correlate\\n \\ Floppy Baby\\ Syndrome \\n Benzos in Pregnancy \\nBenzodiazepines cross the placenta.\\n Effect Neonatal CNS depression.\\n Presentation Hypotonia \\ rag doll\\ appearance lethargy difficulty sucking/feeding respiratory depression.\\n Contraindication Avoid benzos in pregnancy and breastfeeding if possible.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 4. Z-Drugs & Insomnia\\n Story Mode \\nFor patients who just need sleep without the anxiolytic muscle relaxant or anticonvulsant effects of benzos we use \\ Z-Drugs\\ . They are structurally different but bind to a specific subtype of the benzodiazepine receptor BZ1/omega-1 that mediates sedation.\\n\\n 4.1 Agents\\n Zolpidem Ambien \\n Zaleplon Sonata \\n Eszopiclone Lunesta \\n\\n 4.2 Key Features\\n Mechanism Agonist at BZ1 receptor subtype.\\n Effect Induces sleep with minimal effect on sleep architecture REM/Slow Wave Sleep preserved better than benzos .\\n Pharmacokinetics Short half-life $\\\\rightarrow$ less daytime \\ hangover\\ .\\n Adverse Effects Sleep-walking/driving/eating behaviors.\\n Reversal Can be reversed by Flumazenil .\\n\\n 4.3 Melatonin Agonists\\n Ramelteon Tasimelteon \\n Mechanism Agonists at MT1/MT2 receptors in Suprachiasmatic Nucleus.\\n Use Sleep-onset insomnia Non-24 blind patients .\\n Benefit No abuse potential non-controlled no withdrawal.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 5. Ethanol Alcohol \\n Story Mode \\nEthanol is a \\ dirty drug\\ that hits multiple targets. It potentiates GABA sedation and inhibits NMDA/Glutamate loss of memory/excitation . Chronic use leads to a compensatory upregulation of NMDA receptors which explains the dangerous hyperexcitability seen in withdrawal.\\n\\n 5.1 Metabolism\\n1. Ethanol $\\\\xrightarrow Alcohol\\\\ Dehydrogenase $ Acetaldehyde Toxic flushing nausea \\n2. Acetaldehyde $\\\\xrightarrow Aldehyde\\\\ Dehydrogenase $ Acetate Excreted \\n Zero-Order Kinetics Enzymatic pathways saturate easily. Rate of elimination is constant regardless of concentration.\\n\\n 5.2 Alcohol Withdrawal\\n Mechanism Chronic suppression of NMDA + upregulation of receptors. Abrupt stop $\\\\rightarrow$ unopposed glutamate excitation.\\n Symptoms \\n 6-12h Tremors anxiety insomnia.\\n 12-48h Seizures hallucinosis.\\n 48-96h Delirium Tremens DTs - Autonomic instability BP/HR spikes delirium fatal if untreated.\\n Treatment \\n Benzodiazepines Long-acting Chlordiazepoxide/Diazepam to taper Lorazepam if liver failure .\\n\\n 5.3 Alcohol Use Disorder Treatments\\n Disulfiram Inhibits Aldehyde Dehydrogenase $\\\\rightarrow$ Acetaldehyde buildup upon drinking $\\\\rightarrow$ sickness negative reinforcement .\\n Naltrexone Opioid antagonist $\\\\rightarrow$ reduces \\ reward\\ /craving properties of alcohol dopamine link .\\n Acamprosate NMDA antagonist/GABA modulator $\\\\rightarrow$ reduces craving/withdrawal dysphoria.\\n\\n correlate\\n Fetal Alcohol Syndrome FAS \\n Mechanism Maternal alcohol assumption creates teratogenic accumulation of acetaldehyde and ROS.\\n Presentation \\n Growth Retardation IUGR .\\n Facial Dysmorphia Smooth philtrum thin upper lip small eye openings microphthalmia .\\n CNS Microcephaly intellectual disability behavioral issues.\\n Safe Limit There is no known safe amount of alcohol in pregnancy.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 6. Narcolepsy & Stimulants\\n Story Mode \\nIf the problem is too much sleep Narcolepsy or inability to focus ADHD we need to ramp up Norepinephrine and Dopamine. Note that one unique treatment Sodium Oxybate is actually a profound depressant used to force deep sleep at night to improve daytime wakefulness.\\n\\n 6.1 Stimulants\\n Amphetamines Adderall / Methylphenidate Ritalin \\n Mechanism Block reuptake AND increase release of NE/DA.\\n Use ADHD Narcolepsy.\\n SE Tachycardia hypertension anxiety appetite suppression weight loss growth stunting in kids.\\n Modafinil \\n Mechanism Unclear likely increases DA release; effects on Orexin/Histamine.\\n Use Narcolepsy Shift-Work Sleep Disorder.\\n Benefit \\ Wakefulness promoting\\ with less sympathomimetic jittery side effects than amphetamines.\\n\\n 6.2 Sodium Oxybate GHB \\n Mechanism GABA-B agonist.\\n Use Narcolepsy with Cataplexy sudden loss of muscle tone .\\n Effect Consolidates deep sleep at night $\\\\rightarrow$ improved daytime alertness.\\n Safety \\ Date Drug\\ potential. highly restricted REMS program . CNS depression risk.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 7. Transcript-Slide Gap Analysis\\n| Topic | Transcript Emphasis Included | Slide Only Excluded |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Elderly & Metabolism | Strong emphasis on why Lorazepam is preferred conjugation only and Diazepam is avoided active metabolites . | Simply listing metabolic pathways without the clinical \\ why\\ . |\\n| Redistribution | Detailed explanation of Thiopental kinetics Brain $\\\\rightarrow$ Muscle/Fat is why you wake up NOT metabolism. | Charts of distribution kinetics. |\\n| Situational Anxiety | Specific mention of Propranolol for stage fright violinist example to block peripheral symptoms without sedation. | General list of beta-blocker uses. |\\n| Fomepizole | Mentioned as antidote for Methanol/Ethylene Glycol antifreeze poisoning primarily distinct from Ethanol treatment. | Chemical structures of alcohols. |\\n| Date Drugs | Context on Flunitrazepam Rohypnol and GHB causing anterograde amnesia as a mechanism for assault. | Generic list of illicit substances. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n 8. AnKing & Resources\\n Primary \\n Zanki Pharmacology CNS Pharmacology Sedative-Hypnotics \\n Alternatives \\n Boards and Beyond Pharmacology > CNS > Anxiolytics & Hypnotics\\n Sketchy Pharm CNS Pharmacology > Sedative-Hypnotics The \\ Relaxing Barbershop\\ for Barbs \\ Ben s Diner\\ for Benzos .\\n Sketchy Pharm CNS Pharmacology > Alcohol Drugs \\ Al-Kohol s Bar\\ .\\n First Aid CNS Pharmacology > Sedative-Hypnotics & Alcohol.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 9. High Yield Slides\\n Mechanism of Action Diagrams Showing the GABA-A receptor and binding sites for Benzodiazepines vs. Barbiturates.\\n Dose-Response Curves The linear line Barbs/Coma vs. the plateau line Benzos/Safety .\\n Metabolic Pathways Diagram showing Phase I vs Phase II highlighting the \\ OTL\\ benzos skipping Phase I.\\n Alcohol Withdrawal Timeline Timeline showing Tremors $\\\\rightarrow$ Seizures $\\\\rightarrow$ DTs.\\n Narcolepsy Treatment Algorithm Flowchart involving Modafinil/Stimulants/Sodium Oxybate. questions A 75-year-old female requires a benzodiazepine for anxiety. Which agent is most appropriate to minimize the risk of accumulation falls? Diazepam Chlordiazepoxide Lorazepam Clorazepate Flurazepam 2 Lorazepam is an OTL benzo that only undergoes Phase II metabolism conjugation which is preserved in aging. Others have long-acting metabolites. Which sedative-hypnotic drug overdose can be reversed with Flumazenil? Phenobarbital Ethanol Zolpidem Thiopental Sodium Oxybate 2 Zolpidem a Z-drug acts at the Benzodiazepine binding site and CAN be antagonized by Flumazenil. Barbiturates Alcohol and GHB work at different sites/mechanisms. Which Benzodiazepines are safe in liver failure/elderly? OTL Oxazepam Temazepam Lorazepam Pharmacology Mechanism of Barbiturates Potentiate GABA-A Increase Duration of opening Pharmacology Mechanism of Benzodiazepines Potentiate GABA-A Increase Frequency of opening Pharmacology Zanki Pharmacology CNS Pharmacology > Sedative-Hypnotics BandB Pharmacology > CNS > Anxiolytics & Hypnotics Sketchy CNS Pharmacology > Sedative-Hypnotics OTL for Elderly The OTL benzos O xazepam T emazepam L orazepam bypass Phase I oxidation and only undergo Phase II conjugation. This makes them safer for the elderly. Barbs vs Benzos BarbiDURATes increase DURATion .\\\\n Frenzodiazepines increase FREQquency . Redistribution You wake up from Thiopental NOT because it s metabolized but because it leaves the brain to go into fat/muscle Redistribution . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l49", "title": "Lecture 49: Sleep and Sleep Disturbances", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l49 module Neuro l49 Lecture 49 Sleep and Sleep Disturbances Neurology Lecture 49 Sleep and Sleep Disturbances\\n\\n 1. Sleep Physiology\\n Story Mode \\nSleep isn t just a passive \\ off\\ switch; it s a highly active metabolic state essential for clearing neurotoxins like amyloid-beta and tau and consolidating memory. It follows a circadian rhythm 24-hour cycle driven by internal biological clocks. Interestingly without external cues sunlight our natural rhythm drifts to about 25 hours. This cycle orchestrates a shift from the catabolic/feeding state of wakefulness to the anabolic/fasting state of sleep prioritizing growth hormone secretion and oxidative repair.\\n\\n 1.1 Sleep Stages & EEG Architecture Slide 97 \\n Wakefulness \\n EEG Alpha waves 8-13 Hz when awake at rest; Beta waves >13 Hz when active.\\n Tone High muscle tone.\\n N1 Light Sleep \\n EEG Theta waves 4-7 Hz . Slowing of brain activity.\\n Signs Eyelids droop pupils constrict slow roving eye movements.\\n N2 Intermediate Sleep \\n EEG Sleep Spindles bursts of fast activity and K-complexes on a background of Theta.\\n Significance Most of total sleep time.\\n N3 Deep/Slow Wave Sleep \\n EEG Delta waves <4 Hz slow and high amplitude .\\n Function Physical restoration clearance of neurotoxins. Hardest to wake from.\\n REM Rapid Eye Movement \\n EEG Sawtooth/Erratic waves looks like wakefulness - \\ Paradoxical Sleep\\ .\\n Physiology Slide 154 \\n Brain Vivid illogical dreaming.\\n Body Atonia near-complete paralysis to prevent acting out dreams.\\n Autonomic Instability irregular HR/BP loss of thermoregulation.\\n Extraocular Muscles The only muscles that move rapid bursts .\\n\\n 1.2 Sleep Architecture & Latency\\n Cycles We do NOT cycle 1->2->3->REM->3->2->1 sequentially. Cycles vary; REM predominates later in the night early morning .\\n Sleep Latency Time from \\ lights out\\ to N1.\\n REM Latency Time from Sleep Onset to first REM period.\\n Clinical Relevance Short REM latency is a hallmark of Narcolepsy and Depression .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. Dyssomnias Disorders of Timing/Quality \\n Story Mode \\nDyssomnias are problems with getting to sleep staying asleep or waking up. They can be intrinsic primary or extrinsic secondary to environment/illness . The most common offender is Insomnia which is often a symptom of poor habits sleep hygiene rather than a primary disease.\\n\\n 2.1 Insomnia Slide 192 \\n Definition Difficulty initiating or maintaining sleep resulting in daytime fatigue.\\n Acute Common 95% of adults often stress-related.\\n Chronic >3 months affects 10% of adults.\\n Secondary Causes 90% \\n Psychiatric Depression Anxiety PTSD.\\n Medical COPD Heart Failure PND GERD Pain.\\n Substances Caffeine Alcohol disrupts deep sleep Steroids Diuretics nocturia .\\n Treatment Hierarchy \\n 1. Treat Underlying Cause e.g. control pain stop caffeine .\\n 2. Sleep Hygiene Gold Standard \\n Same wake/sleep time daily.\\n Bedroom = Sleep & Sex ONLY no screens/stress .\\n Avoid late meals/alcohol.\\n 3. Pharmacology \\n First Line \\ Z-Drugs\\ Zolpidem Zaleplon Eszopiclone or Melatonin agonists Ramelteon .\\n Second Line Benzodiazepines Avoid in elderly due to falls; decreases N3 sleep .\\n Last Resort Antipsychotics/Antidepressants High side effects .\\n\\n 2.2 Narcolepsy Slide 315 \\n Story Mode \\nImagine REM sleep—with its paralysis and vivid dreams—suddenly intruding into your waking life. That is Narcolepsy. You might collapse while laughing Cataplexy because the REM paralysis switch got flipped while you were awake.\\n Pathophysiology Loss of Orexin Hypocretin producing neurons in the lateral hypothalamus.\\n Genetic Link Strong association with HLA-DQB1 0602 .\\n Tetrad of Symptoms \\n 1. Excessive Daytime Sleepiness \\ Sleep Attacks\\ .\\n 2. Cataplexy Sudden loss of muscle tone triggered by strong emotion laughter surprise .\\n 3. Sleep Paralysis Inability to move upon waking/falling asleep.\\n 4. Hallucinations \\n Hypnagogic Go-ing to sleep.\\n Hypnopompic Pop-ping out of bed waking .\\n Diagnosis Polysomnogram showing Short REM Latency Sleep Onset REM periods .\\n Treatment Stimulants Modafinil Methylphenidate + Venlafaxine/SSRI for Cataplexy .\\n\\n 2.3 Obstructive Sleep Apnea OSA Slide 349 \\n Mechanism Soft tissue tongue/palate collapses during sleep $\\\\rightarrow$ Airway obstruction $\\\\rightarrow$ Hypoxia $\\\\rightarrow$ Arousal. Prevents deep sleep.\\n Risk Factors Obesity Large Neck Circumference Male Gender Retrognathia small jaw .\\n Presentation Snoring witness apnea partner usually reports it morning headaches daytime somnolence.\\n Diagnosis Polysomnogram Sleep Study .\\n Treatment \\n CPAP Continuous Positive Airway Pressure Pneumatic splint to keep airway open.\\n Weight loss.\\n Surgery rarely .\\n\\n 2.4 Restless Leg Syndrome RLS Slide 268 \\n Symptom Uncomfortable sensation creeping/crawling in legs at rest relieved by movement .\\n Timing Worse at night/evening.\\n Secondary Causes Iron Deficiency Pregnancy Renal Failure.\\n Treatment \\n Check Iron Ferritin levels first!\\n Dopamine Agonists Pramipexole Ropinirole Gabapentin/Pregabalin.\\n\\n correlate\\n Circadian Rhythm Disorders \\n Jet Lag \\n Mismatch between internal clock and external time.\\n Treatment Melatonin; adapt to destination light/dark cycle immediately.\\n\\n Delayed Sleep Phase \\n \\ Night Owls\\ . Sleep 2am - 10am. Common in adolescents.\\n Treatment Light therapy in morning.\\n\\n Advanced Sleep Phase \\n \\ Early Birds\\ . Sleep 8pm - 4am. Common in elderly.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. Parasomnias Disorders of Behavior \\n Story Mode \\nParasomnias are unwanted behaviors occurring during sleep. The key distinction is WHEN they occur Non-REM N3 vs. REM. N3 parasomnias are \\ disorders of arousal\\ where the brain is half-awake but memory is off. REM parasomnias are \\ disorders of disinhibition\\ where the paralysis that usually protects us fails.\\n\\n 3.1 Non-REM Parasomnias Stage N3 \\n Sleep Terrors Pavor Nocturnus \\n Who Children 1% .\\n Event Child screams looks terrified autonomic markers high HR sweating . Cannot be consoled .\\n Outcome Goes back to sleep. Amnesia for the event in the morning.\\n Tx Reassurance grow out of it .\\n Sleepwalking Somnambulism \\n Who Children up to 30% rarely adults.\\n Event Motor activity walking eating driving while asleep.\\n Risk Injury/Safety.\\n Tx Safety measures locks/alarms . Benzodiazepines if severe/refractory.\\n\\n 3.2 REM Parasomnias\\n REM Sleep Behavior Disorder RBD \\n Mechanism Loss of normal REM atonia paralysis .\\n Event Patient acts out vivid/violent dreams punching kicking yelling .\\n Risk Injury to self or bed partner.\\n Clinical Pearl Highly predictive of future Synucleinopathies Parkinson s Disease Lewy Body Dementia .\\n Tx Clonazepam Melatonin.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 4. Sleep Deprivation & Safety\\n Story Mode \\nA tired doctor is a dangerous doctor. Sleep deprivation specifically impairs the Prefrontal Cortex the center for judgment and executive function. This leads to rigid thinking inability to adapt to new info and increased risk-taking—a recipe for medical errors.\\n\\n 4.1 Consequences\\n1. Cognitive 4-5 hours sleep/night causes cumulative impairment equal to intoxication. Caffeine does NOT fix judgment errors.\\n2. Immune <7 hours sleep = 3x risk of Common Cold. Reduced vaccine response.\\n3. Fatal Familial Insomnia Prion disease. Progressive insomnia $\\\\rightarrow$ Death. Proves sleep is vital for life .\\n\\n highyield\\n The Intern Studies \\n House Staff & Driving \\nResidents working frequent 24+ hour shifts are a high-risk population.\\n 49% reported falling asleep at the wheel.\\n 90% of these events occurred post-call .\\n Result Increased MVAs and Near-Misses.\\n Takeaway You cannot \\ train\\ yourself to not need sleep. It is a biological imperative for safety.\\n questions A 68-year-old male presents with a complaint from his wife that he \\ fights\\ in his sleep. She reports he punches and yells and recently fell out of bed. When awakened he vividly recalls a dream where he was fighting off an attacker. What is the most likely diagnosis? Sleep Terrors Somnambulism REM Sleep Behavior Disorder Obstructive Sleep Apnea Nocturnal Seizures 2 The patient acts out dreams due to a loss of REM atonia. Key features are the violent nature occurring in an older male and recall of the dream . This is distinct from Sleep Terrors children no recall and is a risk factor for Parkinson s. A 22-year-old medical student reports difficulty staying awake in class. She reports falling asleep instantly when she lies down. She also describes episodes of sudden weakness in her knees when she laughs at a joke. Which of the following is likely deficient in her CNS? Dopamine Serotonin Acetylcholine Orexin Hypocretin GABA 3 This is Narcolepsy . The presentation of Excessive Daytime Sleepiness + Cataplexy muscle weakness with emotion is classic. It is caused by the autoimmune destruction of Orexin-producing neurons in the hypothalamus. Which of the following sleep stages is correctly matched with its characteristic EEG waveform? Stage N1 - Delta Waves Stage N2 - Sleep Spindles and K-Complexes Stage N3 - Alpha Waves REM Sleep - Delta Waves Wakefulness Active - Theta Waves 1 Stage N2 is characterized by Sleep Spindles and K-Complexes on a Theta background. N1 has Theta. N3 has Delta Slow waves . REM has \\ Sawtooth\\ or Beta-like waves. Wakefulness has Alpha rest or Beta active . A 45-year-old female complains of an uncomfortable \\ creeping\\ sensation in her legs every evening that makes it hard to relax. Walking around the room relieves the feeling. What is the most appropriate initial diagnostic step? Polysomnogram Nerve Conduction Study Serum Ferritin level MRI of Lumbar Spine Trial of Benzodiazepines 2 This is Restless Leg Syndrome RLS . It is strongly associated with Iron Deficiency . Checking iron stores Ferritin is mandatory before starting symptomatic treatment with dopamine agonists or gabapentin. What is the effect of chronic sleep deprivation on the immune system? Increased antibody response to vaccines Decreased risk of upper respiratory infections Increased susceptibility to common cold Rhinovirus Enhanced T-cell proliferation No significant effect 2 Study data shows that <7 hours of sleep significantly increases the risk of contracting the common cold Rhinovirus and lowers antibody responses to vaccinations. What sleep stage do Sleep Terrors and Sleepwalking occur in? Stage N3 Deep/Slow Wave Sleep Neurology What sleep stage do Nightmares occur in? REM Sleep Patient remembers them Neurology Mechanism of Narcolepsy Loss of Orexin Hypocretin neurons in lateral hypothalamus Neurology What genetic allele is associated with Narcolepsy? HLA-DQB1 0602 Neurology Clinical Tetrad of Narcolepsy EDS Cataplexy Sleep Paralysis Hypnagogic/pompic Hallucinations Neurology REM Sleep Behavior Disorder is a precursor to which diseases? Synucleinopathies Parkinson s Lewy Body Dementia Neurology First-line pharmacotherapy for Insomnia after hygiene Z-Drugs Zolpidem etc. or Melatonin agonists Pharmacology Secondary causes of Restless Leg Syndrome Iron Deficiency Check Ferritin! Pregnancy Renal Failure Neurology Effect of Benzodiazepines on Sleep Architecture Decreases N3 Deep Sleep Pharmacology Diagnosis of frequent snoring + daytime sleepiness in obese male Obstructive Sleep Apnea Diagnose with Polysomnogram Neurology Zanki Pharmacology CNS Pharmacology > Sedative-Hypnotics BandB Neurology > Sleep > Sleep Disorders Sketchy Path Neurology > Sleep First Aid Psychiatry > Sleep Disorders REM vs N3 Parasomnias N3 Deep Sleep Terrors Walking. NO Memory. Children.\\\\n REM Behavior Disorder Acting out dreams . Vivid Memory. Elderly Parkinson s risk . Cataplexy Trigger Cataplexy sudden loss of tone in Narcolepsy is specifically triggered by strong emotion most commonly laughter . Ferritin & RLS Always check Iron/Ferritin in clear-cut Restless Leg Syndrome. Iron deficiency is a reversible cause. Sleep Latency Definitions Sleep Latency Time to fall asleep High in Insomnia Low in Sleep Deprivation .\\\\n REM Latency Time to first dream Low in Narcolepsy & Depression . Z-Drugs Zolpidem Zaleplon Eszopiclone. They bind the BZ1 receptor subtype to induce sleep without the muscle relaxant/anticonvulsant effects of Benzos. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l50", "title": "Lecture 50: Seizures and Epilepsy", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l50 module Neuro l50 Lecture 50 Seizures and Epilepsy Neurology Lecture 50 Seizures and Epilepsy\\n\\n 1. Definitions & Pathophysiology\\n Story Mode \\nClassically we think of a seizure as a \\ storm\\ in the brain. Physiologically this is an imbalance between Excitation Glutamate and Inhibition GABA . Too much gas or not enough brakes leads to hypersynchronous aberrant neuronal firing.\\n Seizure Transient occurrence of signs/symptoms due to abnormal excessive or synchronous neuronal activity.\\n Epilepsy A disease of the brain defined by \\n 1. At least two unprovoked seizures >24 hours apart.\\n 2. One unprovoked seizure with a high probability >60% of recurrence e.g. structural lesion on MRI .\\n 3. Diagnosis of an Epilepsy Syndrome.\\n\\n 1.1 The \\ VITAMINS\\ of Etiology\\nCommon causes of seizures to remember \\n V ascular Stroke especially in elderly \\n I nfection Meningitis Encephalitis \\n T rauma TBI \\n A utoimmune Anti-NMDA \\n M etabolic Hypoglycemia Hyponatremia \\n I ngestion/Withdrawal Alcohol withdrawal Benzos \\n N eoplasm Tumors \\n S ychogenic Pseudo-seizures / PNES \\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. Seizure Semiology & Classification\\n Story Mode \\nThe old terms \\ Grand Mal\\ and \\ Petit Mal\\ are out. We now classify based on Onset Focal vs. Generalized and Awareness .\\n\\n 2.1 Focal Seizures Start in one hemisphere \\n Focal Aware Old Simple Partial \\n Neuron storm is limited to one spot. Patient is Awake/Aware .\\n Jacksonian March Activity \\ marches\\ along the motor strip Thumb $\\\\rightarrow$ Hand $\\\\rightarrow$ Arm .\\n Aura A focal aware seizure manifesting as a subjective sensation e.g. chemical smell rising epigastric sensation fear before spreading.\\n Focal Impaired Awareness Old Complex Partial \\n Seizure affects consciousness/mentation.\\n Automatisms Repetitive non-purposeful movements Lip smacking picking at clothes .\\n Localization Most common in Temporal Lobe Hippocampus/Amygdala .\\n\\n 2.2 Generalized Seizures Both hemispheres involved \\n Generalized Tonic-Clonic GTC \\n Tonic Phase Stiffening \\ Epileptic Cry\\ air forced through closed cords Cyanosis.\\n Clonic Phase Rhythmic jerking.\\n Postictal Limp unresponsive loud snoring airway risk! .\\n Absence Seizures Old Petit Mal \\n Clinically Sudden behavioral arrest \\ blank stare\\ <15 seconds. No postictal confusion.\\n Triggers Hyperventilation .\\n Mechanism Hyperpolarization of Thalamocortical Relay neurons leads to de-inactivation of T-Type Calcium Channels $\\\\rightarrow$ Burst firing.\\n Gene CACNA1H .\\n EEG 3 Hz Spike and Wave Pathognomonic .\\n Treatment Ethosuximide .\\n\\n correlate\\n Status Epilepticus \\n Definition \\n Seizure lasting > 5 minutes convulsive or > 10 minutes non-convulsive .\\n OR Two seizures without return to baseline in between.\\n Management \\n Medical Emergency! \\ Time is Brain\\ Excitotoxicity .\\n 1st Line Benzodiazepines IV Lorazepam /Ativan .\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. Epilepsy Syndromes Genetics & Structural \\n Story Mode \\nWe are moving away from \\ Idiopathic\\ epilepsy effectively. Advanced genetics now link specific channelopathies to specific syndromes.\\n\\n 3.1 Ion Channelopathies\\n Dravet Syndrome \\n Gene SCN1A Nav1.1 Sodium Channel loss of function in inhibitory interneurons .\\n Presentation Febrile seizures in infancy $\\\\rightarrow$ Refractory epilepsy developmental regression. High mortality SUDEP .\\n EAST Syndrome \\n Acronym E pilepsy A taxia S ensorineural deafness T ubulopathy Kidney .\\n Gene KCNJ10 Kir4.1 Potassium channel in Astrocytes .\\n Mechanism Astrocytes can t buffer extracellular K+ $\\\\rightarrow$ Neurons depolarize.\\n\\n 3.2 Structural & Neurocutaneous Syndromes\\n Tuberous Sclerosis Complex TSC \\n Genes TSC1 Hamartin or TSC2 Tuberin .\\n Pathway Loss of inhibition of mTOR pathway $\\\\rightarrow$ Uncontrolled cell growth Hamartomas .\\n Clinical \\n Brain Cortical Tubers cause epilepsy Subependymal Nodules.\\n Skin Ash Leaf Spots Hypopigmented Shagreen Patches Facial Angiofibromas.\\n Heart Rhabdomyoma.\\n Kidney Angiomyolipoma.\\n Sturge-Weber Syndrome \\n Gene GNAQ Somatic Mosaicism NOT hereditary .\\n Clinical Port-Wine Stain V1/V2 distribution Leptomeningeal Angioma Glaucoma Seizures.\\n Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome LGS \\n Presentation Childhood <8yo . Intellectual disability.\\n Seizures Multiple types Atonic/Drop Attacks Tonic .\\n EEG Slow Spike & Wave < 2.5 Hz .\\n Gene GABRB3 GABA receptor subunit .\\n\\n 3.3 Autoimmune & Encephalitis\\n Anti-NMDA Receptor Encephalitis \\n Antibody IgG against NR1 subunit of NMDA receptor.\\n Demographics Young women. Associated with Ovarian Teratoma .\\n Course Viral prodrome $\\\\rightarrow$ Psychosis/Hallucinations $\\\\rightarrow$ Catatonia/Mute $\\\\rightarrow$ Hyperkinetic Orofacial Dyskinesia .\\n Rasmussen Encephalitis \\n Pathology T-Cell mediated chronic encephalitis affecting one hemisphere .\\n Outcome Progressive atrophy and hemiparesis.\\n Treatment Hemispherectomy Functional hemispherectomy usually in children due to neuroplasticity .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 4. Workup & Treatment Modalities\\n 4.1 Diagnostics\\n EEG Gold standard. Can use Hyperventilation or Photic Stimulation to provoke.\\n Imaged \\n CT Emergency Rule out bleed/mass . Quick.\\n MRI Elective/Detailed. Looks for Mesial Temporal Sclerosis Cortical Dysplasia Lissencephaly Heterotopia .\\n\\n 4.2 Treatments\\n Pharmacology Goal is seizure freedom with minimal side effects.\\n Ketogenic Diet High Fat Low Carb. Mechanism specific unclear but effective in peds.\\n Surgical \\n Resection Lesionectomy or Lobectomy e.g. for Temporal Lobe Epilepsy .\\n Corpus Callosotomy Palliative. Prevents spread generalization of seizures protecting against drop attacks. Spares the Splenium to preserve some function.\\n Neuromodulation \\n Vagal Nerve Stimulator VNS Pacemaker for the vagus nerve.\\n RNS Responsive Neurostimulation Closed-loop system. Detects seizure pattern $\\\\rightarrow$ Zaps it to abort.\\n DBS Deep Brain Stimulation Anterior Nucleus of Thalamus.\\n\\n highyield\\n The \\ Do Not Miss\\ Associations \\n| Syndrome | Gene/Antibody | Key Feature |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Dravet | SCN1A | Febrile Szs $\\\\rightarrow$ Regression |\\n| Absence | CACNA1H | 3Hz Spike/Wave Ethosuximide |\\n| Lennox-Gastaut | GABRB3 | Atonic Drop Szs Slow Spike/Wave |\\n| Tuberous Sclerosis | TSC1/TSC2 | Cortical Tubers Ash Leaf Spots mTOR |\\n| Anti-NMDA | Anti-NR1 | Ovarian Teratoma Psychiatric onset |\\n questions A 6-year-old boy is brought to the pediatrician by his teacher who reports he \\ zones out\\ multiple times a day for 10 seconds. He does not respond to his name during these episodes but immediately resumes activity afterward. Hyperventilation in the office reproduces the event. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of this disorder? Loss of GABA-A receptor function SCN1A sodium channel mutation T-type calcium channel hypersynchrony Glutamate excitotoxicity via NMDA receptors Potassium channel accumulation in astrocytes 2 This is Absence Epilepsy . The hallmark is a brief <15s stare triggered by hyperventilation with no postictal state. The pathophysiology involves hyperpolarization of thalamocortical relay neurons leading to rebound burst firing via T-type Calcium Channels . Treatment is Ethosuximide. A 24-year-old female presents with new-onset seizures and acute psychosis. She has been acting strangely with fluctuating catatonia and agitation. Physical exam reveals rhythmic tongue-smacking movements. An MRI of the pelvis is ordered. What is the physician looking for? Renal Angiomyolipoma Ovarian Teratoma Uterine Fibroids Polycystic Ovaries Bladder transitional cell carcinoma 1 This clinical picture Psychosis + Seizures + Orofacial Dyskinesia in a young woman is classic for Anti-NMDA Receptor Encephalitis . It is a paraneoplastic syndrome strongly associated with Ovarian Teratomas . A 4-month-old infant presents with a prolonged febrile seizure. Over the next year he develops multiple seizure types including myoclonic and atonic seizures and shows signs of developmental regression. Genetic testing reveals a mutation in SCN1A. What is the most likely diagnosis? West Syndrome Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome Dravet Syndrome Juvenile Myoclonic Epilepsy Benign Rolandic Epilepsy 2 Dravet Syndrome is a severe myoclonic epilepsy of infancy caused by an SCN1A mutation. It classically begins with prolonged febrile seizures in the first year of life and progresses to intractable epilepsy and intellectual disability. A patient with intractable epilepsy undergoes a corpus callosotomy. Which of the following structures is typically spared during this procedure to preserve inter-hemispheric transfer of visual information? Rostrum Genu Body Splenium Anterior Commissure 3 The Splenium posterior portion of the corpus callosum is often spared anterior 2/3rds callosotomy to preserve the transfer of visual information between the occipital lobes maintaining some functional connectivity while preventing the generalization of seizures. Which of the following EEG findings is paired with the correct Epilepsy Syndrome? 3 Hz Spike and Wave - Lennox Gastaut Syndrome Hypsarrhythmia - Childhood Absence Epilepsy Slow <2.5 Hz Spike and Wave - Lennox Gastaut Syndrome Centrotemporal Spikes - Dravet Syndrome Generalized Polyspike - Focal Temporal Lobe Epilepsy 2 Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome is characterized by a Slow Spike and Wave pattern <2.5 Hz on EEG. Note 3 Hz Spike/Wave is Absence. Hypsarrhythmia is West Syndrome. Centrotemporal spikes are Rolandic Epilepsy . What is the pathognomonic EEG finding in Absence Seizures? 3 Hz Spike and Wave complexes Neurology What gene mutation is associated with Dravet Syndrome? SCN1A Sodium Channel Nav1.1 Neurology A young woman presents with psychosis oro-facial dyskinesia and seizures. What tumor should be screened for? Ovarian Teratoma Anti-NMDA Receptor Encephalitis Neurology What cutaneous finding is pathognomonic for Tuberous Sclerosis? Ash Leaf Spots Hypopigmented macules Neurology What is the mechanism of action of Ethosuximide? Blocks T-Type Calcium Channels in the Thalamus Pharmacology First-line treatment for Status Epilepticus? IV Benzodiazepines Lorazepam Pharmacology What epilepsy syndrome is associated with GABRB3 mutation and slow <2.5Hz spike-wave EEG? Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome Neurology Gene mutation associated with Sturge-Weber Syndrome? GNAQ Somatic mosaicism Neurology Which antiepileptic drug is contraindicated in pregnancy highly teratogenic ? Valproate Neural Tube Defects Pharmacology What is the Jacksonian March ? Progressive spread of focal seizure activity along the motor homunculus Neurology Zanki Step 2 Neurology > Epilepsy BandB Neurology > Seizures > Epilepsy Syndromes Sketchy Path Neurology > Seizures First Aid Neurology > Pathology > Seizures Absence vs Focal Impaired Absence <15s immediate recovery provoked by hyperventilation.\\\\n Focal Impaired Awareness >30s postictal confusion automatisms common. Sturge-Weber Triad 1. Port-Wine Stain V1/V2 \\\\n2. Leptomeningeal Angioma Seizures \\\\n3. Glaucoma Increased IOP Tuberous Sclerosis Mnemonic ASH LEAF A sh leaf spots S hagreen patches H eart rhabdomyoma L ung hamartomas E pilepsy Cortical Tubers A ngiomyolipoma Renal F acial angiofibroma. Status Epilepticus Tx 1st Line Benzodiazepines Lorazepam . 2nd Line Fosphenytoin/Valproate/Levetiracetam. 3rd Line Pentobarbital/Propofol. Jacksonian March Focal motor seizure activity that spreads along the motor homunculus e.g. finger to hand to arm to face reflecting electrical spread in the precentral gyrus. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l51", "title": "Lecture 51: Differential Diagnosis of Memory Disorders", "module": "DPR", "content": "id l51 module DPR l51 Lecture 51 Differential Diagnosis of Memory Disorders Neurology Lecture 51 Differential Diagnosis of Memory Disorders\\n\\n 1. Evaluation of Memory Loss\\n Story Mode \\nNot all memory loss is dementia. The first step is to distinguish between Delirium acute fluctuating inattention and Dementia chronic progressive . We must also rule out MCI Mild Cognitive Impairment where function is preserved despite memory complaints.\\n\\n 1.1 Delirium vs. Dementia\\n Delirium \\n Time Acute onset hours to days .\\n Course Fluctuating.\\n Key Feature Inattention and altered level of consciousness.\\n Cause Often underlying medical illness infection metabolic on top of a vulnerable brain dementia .\\n Dementia \\n Time Chronic months to years .\\n Course Progressive decline.\\n Key Feature Memory/Cognitive loss without altered consciousness until late stages .\\n\\n 1.2 Reversible Causes\\nBefore condemning a patient to a neurodegenerative diagnosis rule out the fixable stuff \\n Metabolic Hypothyroidism B12 deficiency.\\n Structural Subdural hematoma Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus NPH .\\n Infectious Syphilis HIV.\\n Toxic Alcohol Wernicke s Medications Benzos Anticholinergics .\\n Psychiatric Pseudodementia Depression .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. Major Neurodegenerative Dementias\\n Story Mode \\nDifferent dementias attack different parts of the brain leading to distinct \\ signatures.\\ Alzheimer’s hits memory Hippocampus . FTD hits personality Frontal lobes . LBD hits the visual cortex and motor system.\\n\\n 2.1 Alzheimer s Disease AD \\n Epidemiology Most common cause. Age is the 1 risk factor.\\n Genetics \\n Late Onset APOE e4 allele Risk factor not causative .\\n Early Onset APP Presenilin-1 Presenilin-2 Autosomal Dominant .\\n Presentation \\n Short-term memory loss Hippocampus .\\n Language deficits Word finding .\\n Visuospatial issues Getting lost .\\n Pathology/Imaging \\n MRI Hippocampal Atrophy Mesial Temporal Lobes .\\n PET Temporoparietal Hypometabolism .\\n Treatment \\n AChE Inhibitors Donepezil Rivastigmine GI side effects .\\n NMDA Antagonist Memantine Moderate-Severe .\\n Anti-Amyloid mAbs Lecanemab/Donanemab Risk of ARIA Amyloid Related Imaging Abnormalities - bleed/edema .\\n\\n 2.2 Vascular Dementia\\n Mechanism \\ Strokes\\ accumulating over time.\\n Subcortical Slow processing speed lethargy small vessel disease .\\n Multi-Infarct Step-wise decline.\\n Cerebral Amyloid Angiopathy CAA \\n Buildup of amyloid in vessel walls.\\n Presentation \\ Amyloid Spells\\ Transient focal deficits mimicking TIA and lobar hemorrhages .\\n\\n 2.3 Lewy Body Dementia LBD \\n Core Features \\n Parkinsonism Rigidity bradykinesia.\\n Visual Hallucinations detailed often people/animals e.g. Robin Williams case .\\n Fluctuations Good days/bad days.\\n REM Sleep Behavior Disorder RBD Acting out dreams loss of atonia .\\n Treatment Caution EXTREME sensitivity to Antipsychotics can cause severe rigidity/death . Avoid Benzos.\\n\\n 2.4 Frontotemporal Dementia FTD \\n Presentation \\n Behavioral Variant Onset in 50s-60s. Disinhibition apathy loss of empathy hyperorality craving sweets compulsive behaviors Bruce Willis case . Memory is spared early on.\\n Primary Progressive Aphasia Language loss first.\\n Genetics MAPT GRN C9ORF72.\\n Management SSRIs for behavior. AChE inhibitors do NOT help.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. Other Specific Etiologies\\n\\n 3.1 Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus NPH \\n Triad \\ Wet Wobbly Wacky\\ \\n Urinary Incontinence Wet .\\n Gait Apraxia / Magnetic Gait Wobbly .\\n Dementia Wacky .\\n Pathophysiology Enlarged ventricles comprising white matter tracts legs/bladder fibers run medially .\\n Diagnosis/Rx High Volume Lumbar Puncture drain 30cc . If gait improves $\\\\rightarrow$ VP Shunt .\\n\\n 3.2 Prion Diseases\\n Mechanism Conversion of normal PrPc helical to abnormal PrPsc Beta-pleated sheet $\\\\rightarrow$ rapid spongiform degeneration.\\n Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease CJD \\n Presentation Rapidly progressive dementia + Startle Myoclonus + Ataxia. Death < 1 year.\\n Diagnosis MRI Cortical ribboning CSF RT-QuIC is gold standard; 14-3-3 is old/nonspecific .\\n Fatal Familial Insomnia FFI - The Mystery Case \\n Alfredo s Case 53yo male with profound insomnia dysautonomia sweating pinpoint pupils tachycardia and dream-enactment.\\n Course Thalamic degeneration prevents sleep $\\\\rightarrow$ Death.\\n\\n 3.3 Chronic Traumatic Encephalopathy CTE \\n Cause Repetitive mild TBI Football Boxing .\\n Timeline Symptoms start years/decades after sports career.\\n Presentation \\n Mood/Behavior Explosivity aggression suicide Aaron Hernandez .\\n Cognitive Dementia later.\\n Motor Parkinsonism/ALS-like features.\\n Pathology Tauopathy.\\n\\n 3.4 Wernicke s Encephalopathy\\n Cause Thiamine B1 deficiency Alcoholics .\\n Triad Confusion Ataxia Ophthalmoplegia Nystagmus/Lateral Rectus palsy .\\n Rx IV Thiamine BEFORE Glucose to prevent precipitating Korsakoff s .\\n Korsakoff s Psychosis Chronic irreversible amnesia + Confabulation damage to mammillary bodies/thalamus .\\n\\n highyield\\n Dementia Buzzwords \\n| Diagnosis | Buzzword / Key Feature |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Alzheimer s | Hippocampal Atrophy Amyloid Plaques/Tau Tangles |\\n| LBD | Visual Hallucinations Parkingonism Neuroleptic Sensitivity |\\n| Frontotemporal | Disinhibition Hyperorality Young onset 50s |\\n| NPH | Wet Wobbly Wacky Magnetic Gait |\\n| CJD | Startle Myoclonus Rapid progression |\\n| Wernicke s | Ataxia Ophthalmoplegia Thiamine |\\n questions A 72-year-old male is brought in by his family for evaluated of \\ acting strange.\\ They report he has been talking to people who aren t there and sometimes thinks his wife is an imposter. On exam he has mild rigidity in the upper extremities. Which medication should be AVOIDED in this patient? Donepezil Carbidopa-Levodopa Haloperidol Memantine Melatonin 2 The patient has features of Lewy Body Dementia Visual Hallucinations Capgras delusion/imposter Parkinsonism . LBD patients have severe neuroleptic sensitivity . Typical antipsychotics like Haloperidol can precipitate severe rigidity catatonia or even death. A 55-year-old woman is brought by her husband for personality changes. She has been making inappropriate sexual comments to strangers eating only candy bars and spending thousands of dollars on ceramic figurines. Her memory for recent events is relatively preserved. What is the most likely diagnosis? Alzheimer s Disease Frontotemporal Dementia Behavioral Variant Bipolar Disorder Vascular Dementia Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease 1 This is the classic presentation of Behavioral Variant FTD Early onset <60 Disinhibition Hyperorality dietary changes compulsive behaviors and spared memory early in the course. A 68-year-old male presents with a \\ magnetic gait \\ where his feet appear stuck to the floor. He has also had several episodes of urinary incontinence and mild memory decline. A high-volume lumbar puncture is performed and his walking improves significantly. What is the next best step in management? Start Carbidopa-Levodopa Start Donepezil Refer for Ventriculoperitoneal Shunt Repeat Lumbar Puncture in 1 month Start Finasteride 2 This patient has Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus Wet Wobbly Wacky . The positive response to the large volume LP diagnostic tap predicts a good response to permanent CSF diversion via a VP Shunt . A 45-year-old female presents with rapid cognitive decline over the last 3 months. She has developed severe ataxia and startle myoclonus. An EEG shows generalized periodic sharp wave complexes. What is the gold standard diagnostic test for this condition? CSF 14-3-3 protein CSF RT-QuIC assay Brain Biopsy Serum Ceruloplasmin MRI with contrast 1 This is Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease CJD Rapid dementia myoclonus ataxia . While 14-3-3 was used historically RT-QuIC Real-Time Quaking-Induced Conversion is now the specific gold standard test on CSF to detect the abnormal prion protein. Which of the following findings is most strongly associated with Cerebral Amyloid Angiopathy? Subcortical lacunar infarcts Lobar intracerebral hemorrhages Tremor at rest Early gait instability Choroid Plexus Cyst 1 Cerebral Amyloid Angiopathy CAA involves amyloid deposition in the vessel walls of the cortex making them friable. This leads to recurrent Lobar Hemorrhages in the hemispheres sparing deep structures and \\ Amyloid Spells.\\ What is the classic triad of Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus? Urinary Incontinence Gait disturbance Dementia Wet Wobbly Wacky Neurology Which dementia is characterized by visual hallucinations and parkinsonism? Lewy Body Dementia Neurology What is the gold standard CSF test for CJD? RT-QuIC Real-time Quaking-Induced Conversion Neurology What MRI finding is associated with Alzheimer s Disease? Hippocampal Atrophy Neurology A patient with dementia develops acute rigidity and obtundation after receiving Haloperidol. What is the diagnosis? Severe Neuroleptic Sensitivity in Lewy Body Dementia Pharmacology What vitamin must be replenished before glucose in an alcoholic patient? Thiamine B1 - to prevent Wernicke s precipitation Neurology What specific sleep disorder is a prodrome for synucleinopathies LBD/Parkinson s ? REM Sleep Behavior Disorder RBD Neurology What protein accumulates in Chronic Traumatic Encephalopathy CTE ? Tau It is a Tauopathy Neurology Side effect concern for new Anti-Amyloid monoclonal antibodies Lecanemab ? ARIA Amyloid Related Imaging Abnormalities - Edema/Hemorrhage Pharmacology Genetic risk factor allele for Late-Onset Alzheimer s? APOE e4 Neurology Zanki Step 2 Neurology > Dementia BandB Neurology > Dementia Sketchy Path Neurology > Neurodegenerative Delirium vs Dementia Delirium is acute fluctuating and affects ATTENTION. Dementia is chronic progressive and affects MEMORY attention preserved until late . NPH Triad Wet Incontinence Wobbly Magnetic Gait Wacky Dementia . Treat with Shunt. LBD Neuroleptic Sensitivity Avoid typical antipsychotics Haldol in Lewy Body Dementia. They can cause severe parkinsonism and death. Wernicke s Tx Give Thiamine BEFORE Glucose. Glucose first depletes remaining thiamine and precipitates Wernicke s. FTD Features Disinhibition Apathy Hyperorality sweets Compulsive behaviors. Memory often spared early. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l52", "title": "Lecture 52: Pharmacology: Antiseizure Drugs 1", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l52 module Neuro l52 Lecture 52 Pharmacology Antiseizure Drugs 1 Pharmacology Lecture 52 Pharmacology Antiseizure Drugs 1\\n\\n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D.\\n Session Lecture 52\\n\\n 1. Introduction to Seizures & Epilepsy\\n Story Mode \\nA seizure is essentially an electrical storm in the brain defined as a paroxysmal event due to abnormal excessive or synchronous neuronal activity. When a patient has recurrent seizures due to a chronic underlying process we diagnose Epilepsy . The goal of pharmacotherapy is to stabilize these neurons either by putting the brakes on excitation blocking Sodium/Calcium channels inhibiting Glutamate or stepping on the clutch enhancing GABA inhibition .\\n\\n 1.1 Classification Slide 6 8 \\n Focal Onset Seizures \\n Arise from a specific discrete local population of neurons in one hemisphere.\\n Mechanism Hyperexcitability due to rapidly firing Voltage-Gated Sodium Channels .\\n Evolution Can evolve into bilateral tonic-clonic seizures.\\n Primary Generalized Onset Seizures \\n Arise in both hemispheres simultaneously.\\n Involve deep structures Thalamus/Brainstem .\\n Status Epilepticus \\n Definition >5 minutes of continuous seizure activity OR >2 seizures without recovery of consciousness in between.\\n\\n 1.2 Pharmacokinetics Overview Slide 15-22 \\n Absorption/Distribution All ASDs Anti-Seizure Drugs must cross the BBB. Most are highly protein bound potential for displacement interactions .\\n Metabolism Most undergo hepatic metabolism.\\n Inducers Carbamazepine Phenytoin Phenobarbital.\\n Inhibitor Valproate.\\n Withdrawal Gradual taper is mandatory. Abrupt cessation can precipitate seizures or status epilepticus.\\n\\n correlate\\nSuicidality in ASDs\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Mechanism unclear; controversial FDA warning.\\n- Presentation New onset suicidal ideation or behavior.\\n- Lecturer s Point All ASDs carry a warning for increased risk of suicidality. Patients especially young ones should be monitored for mood changes.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. Voltage-Gated Sodium Channel Blockers\\n Story Mode \\nThe \\ fast\\ sodium current is responsible for the upstroke of the action potential. In a seizure neurons fire repetitively at high frequencies. Sodium channel blockers work by binding to the channel in its Inactivated State preventing it from resetting to the active state. This prolongs the refractory period. Importantly these drugs exhibit Use-Dependence they bind more strongly to neurons that are firing rapidly seizing while sparing normal neurons firing at normal rates.\\n\\n 2.1 Mechanism of Action Slide 30-35 \\n The Cycle Resting Closed $\\\\rightarrow$ Depolarization $\\\\rightarrow$ Open Na+ in $\\\\rightarrow$ Inactivated Gate closed $\\\\rightarrow$ Repolarization $\\\\rightarrow$ Resting.\\n Fast Inactivation Enhancers \\n Drugs Phenytoin Carbamazepine Lamotrigine Oxcarbazepine .\\n Bind to the channel pore during the Open/Inactivated state.\\n Stabilize the channel in the inactivated state refractory .\\n Slow Inactivation Enhancer \\n Drug Lacosamide .\\n Stabilizes the \\ Slow Inactivation\\ state a conformational change occurring over seconds during prolonged depolarization .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. Phenytoin PHT & Fosphenytoin\\n Story Mode \\nPhenytoin is the \\ classic\\ dirty drug of neurology. It’s effective but it follows Zero-Order Kinetics saturation kinetics meaning small dose increases can lead to exponential spikes in blood levels. It also has a laundry list of cosmetic and teratogenic side effects \\ P-H-E-N-Y-T-O-I-N\\ . Fosphenytoin was developed as a water-soluble prodrug to solve the infusion issues of Phenytoin Phlebitis/Purple Glove .\\n\\n 3.1 Pharmacokinetics Slide 40-55 \\n Absorption \\n Phenytoin Base Chewable/Suspension Erratic absorption.\\n Phenytoin Sodium Capsules Better absorption.\\n Distribution Highly protein bound Albumin .\\n Hypoalbuminemia Liver failure Nephrotic syndrome Pregnancy Leads to INCREASED Free Phenytoin levels $\\\\rightarrow$ Toxicity.\\n Displacement Uremia kidney failure displaces PHT from albumin.\\n Metabolism CRITICAL \\n Zero-Order Kinetics Enzymes CYP2C9/2C19 become saturated at therapeutic levels.\\n Elimination is constant amount per time not constant fraction .\\n Consequence Small dose increase $\\\\rightarrow$ Disproportionately LARGE increase in serum concentration.\\n Induction Potent INDUCER of CYP3A4 UGT etc. Eats up other drugs .\\n\\n 3.2 Formulations Phenytoin vs. Fosphenytoin Slide 43-45 \\n| Feature | Phenytoin IV | Fosphenytoin IV/IM |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Solubility | Poor Lipophilic | High Water soluble Prodrug |\\n| Solvent | Propylene Glycol + Ethanol pH 12 | Water based |\\n| Adverse Effects | Hypotension/Arrhythmias due to Propylene Glycol Phlebitis | Less Hypotension Minimal irritation |\\n| Infusion Rate | Must be SLOW risk of chemical burn | Can be faster |\\n| IM Use | CONTRAINDICATED Tissue necrosis | Safe for IM |\\n\\n 3.3 Adverse Effects Slide 61-68 \\n mnemonic\\n PHENYTOIN Side Effects \\n PHENYTOIN \\n\\n- P P -450 Inducer / P eripheral Neuropathy / P urple Glove\\n- H H irsutism excess hair \\n- E E nlarged Gums Gingival Hyperplasia / E rythema Multiforme SJS \\n- N N ystagmus / Neurotoxicity Ataxia Slurred speech \\n- Y Y ellowing Hepatotoxicity - rare \\n- T T eratogenicity Fetal Hydantoin Syndrome \\n- O O steomalacia Vit D deficiency from enzyme induction \\n- I I nterference with Folate Megaloblastic Anemia \\n- N N europathy\\n \\n\\n correlate\\nPurple Glove Syndrome\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Extravasation of IV Phenytoin highly alkaline + propylene glycol .\\n- Presentation Distal limb edema discoloration purple/blue pain.\\n- Complications Skin necrosis compartment syndrome amputation.\\n- Prevention Use Fosphenytoin instead.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\nFetal Hydantoin Syndrome\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Teratogenic effect of Phenytoin and Carbamazepine/Valproate .\\n- Presentation Cleft lip/palate Microcephaly Hypoplastic fingernails IUGR.\\n- Risk 2-3x risk of major malformations.\\n- Management Folic acid supplementation though may not fully prevent .\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 4. Carbamazepine CBZ & Pharmacogenetics\\n Story Mode \\nCarbamazepine is chemically related to Tricyclic Antidepressants. It is a potent sodium channel blocker used for focal seizures. Its claim to fame is Auto-Induction it induces its own metabolism so half-life decreases over the first few weeks and severe genetically determined skin reactions.\\n\\n 4.1 Pharmacogenetics Slide 23-28 \\nScreening is MANDATORY in at-risk populations before starting Carbamazepine and chemically related Oxcarbazepine/Phneytoin/Lamotrigine .\\n\\n HLA-B 1502 \\n Population Asian ancestry Han Chinese South Asian .\\n Risk SJS / TEN Stevens-Johnson Syndrome / Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis .\\n Action SCREEN all Asian patients. If positive $\\\\rightarrow$ AVOID Carbamazepine.\\n HLA-A 3101 \\n Population European Japanese Korean.\\n Risk DRESS Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms .\\n\\n highyield\\n Auto-Induction \\nCarbamazepine induces CYP3A4 the very enzyme that metabolizes it.\\n Effect Serum levels DROP over the first few weeks of therapy despite constant dosing.\\n Management Dose may need to be increased after 3-4 weeks once auto-induction is maximal.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 5. Lamotrigine LTG \\n Story Mode \\nLamotrigine is the \\ Clean\\ broad-spectrum agent. It blocks Sodium channels AND inhibits Glutamate release. It is effective for almost everything Focal Generalized Absence Bipolar . Its major Achilles heel is the Life-Threatening Rash SJS which is why we must titrate it painfully slowly \\ Start low go slow\\ .\\n\\n 5.1 Pharmacokinetics & Dosing Slide 69-75 \\n Interaction with Valproate \\n Valproate inhibits UGT glucuronidation .\\n Effect DOUBLES Lamotrigine half-life 60 hours vs 24 hours .\\n Risk Massive increase in SJS/Rash risk .\\n Management reduce dose by 50% also titrate even SLOWER every other day dosing initially .\\n Interaction with Inducers Phenytoin/Carbamazepine \\n Decreases Lamotrigine half-life.\\n Management Higher loading/maintenance doses needed.\\n\\n 5.2 Clinical Uses\\n Broad Spectrum Focal Generalized Tonic-Clonic Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome Absence 2nd line .\\n Bipolar Disorder Maintenance treatment prevents Depressive episodes ; NOT for acute Mania.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 6. Lacosamide LCM \\n Story Mode \\nLacosamide is the newer cleaner cousin. Instead of fast inactivation it enhances Slow Inactivation . It has excellent bioavailability 100% minimal protein binding and renal excretion fewer drug interactions . The one specific catch is the heart.\\n\\n 6.1 Mechanism & Side Effects Slide 80-87 \\n Mechanism Enhances Slow Inactivation of Voltage-Gated Na+ Channels.\\n Key Toxicity PR Interval Prolongation .\\n Risk Bradycardia AV Block.\\n Action Get a baseline ECG in patients with known cardiac conduction issues.\\n Pharmacokinetics \\n Renally excreted 40% unchanged .\\n No major CYP interactions unlike Phenytoin/Carbamazepine .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 7. Summary Tables\\n\\n 7.1 Sodium Channel Blockers Comparison\\n| Drug | Mechanism | Key PK Feature | Key Toxicity | Clinical Pearl |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Phenytoin | Fast Inactivation | Zero-Order Kinetics ; Potent Inducer | Gingival Hyperplasia Hirsutism Purple Glove Osteomalacia | Fosphenytoin is safer for IV. |\\n| Carbamazepine | Fast Inactivation | Auto-Inducer | SJS HLA-B 1502 DRESS Hyponatremia SIADH | Screen Asians for HLA-B 1502. |\\n| Lamotrigine | Fast Inactivation + Glutamate Inhibit | Valproate Doubles Half-life | SJS Rash | \\ Start Low Go Slow\\ titration. |\\n| Lacosamide | Slow Inactivation | Renal Elimination | PR Prolongation | Get ECG aimed at cardiac patients. |\\n\\n highyield\\n Teratogenicity Hierarchy \\n1. Valproate HIGHEST Risk Neural Tube Defects/Spina Bifida - AVOID.\\n2. Phenytoin/Carbamazepine High Risk Cleft palate cardiac defects .\\n3. Lamotrigine/Levetiracetam Safest options for pregnancy.\\n questions A 35-year-old female with a history of epilepsy is well-controlled on Valproate. She recently started a new medication as add-on therapy for breakthrough seizures. Four weeks later she presents to the ER with a severe peeling rash affecting her face and trunk. Which medication was most likely added? Levetiracetam Gabapentin Lamotrigine Lacosamide Topiramate 2 This case describes Stevens-Johnson Syndrome/Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis. Lamotrigine carries a Black Box Warning for serious skin rashes. Valproate inhibits the glucuronidation of Lamotrigine increasing its half-life and serum levels which drastically increases the risk of this rash if the dose isn t significantly reduced and titrated slowly. A patient is started on an anti-seizure medication that follows zero-order kinetics at therapeutic concentrations. Which of the following best describes the pharmacokinetic consequence of this property? Half-life is constant regardless of plasma concentration A small increase in dose can lead to a disproportionately large increase in serum concentration The drug is eliminated by the kidneys unchanged The drug induces its own metabolism A constant fraction of the drug is eliminated per unit time 1 Phenytoin follows Zero-Order Saturation Kinetics at therapeutic levels. This means metabolic enzymes are saturated and a constant amount not fraction of drug is eliminated per unit time. Consequently once saturation is reached even small dose increases can cause massive spikes in toxicity. Which of the following patients should be screened for the HLA-B 1502 allele prior to starting Carbamazepine? A 50-year-old Caucasian male with kidney failure A 25-year-old African American female with hypertension A 30-year-old Han Chinese male with no other medical history A 70-year-old Hispanic female with liver disease A 40-year-old of Northern European descent 2 The HLA-B 1502 allele is strongly associated with Stevens-Johnson Syndrome SJS and Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis TEN induced by Carbamazepine and Phenytoin/Lamotrigine in patients of Asian ancestry particularly Han Chinese Thai Malaysian and South Asian . Screening is mandatory in these populations. A 6-year-old child presents with gingival hyperplasia overgrown gums and hirsutism. Which anti-seizure medication is the child most likely taking? Ethosuximide Phenytoin Valproic Acid Gabapentin Levetiracetam 1 Gingival Hyperplasia and Hirsutism are classic cosmetic side effects of Phenytoin . They are not typically seen with the other agents listed. A patient requiring IV phenytoin for status epilepticus is mistakenly given the drug via a rapid IV push into a small hand vein. The hand subsequently becomes swollen painful and turns purple. What is the mechanism of this reaction? Allergic reaction to the sulfa moiety Precipitation of phenytoin crystals in the tissue due to high pH and propylene glycol Vasoconstriction caused by sodium channel blockade Clotting factor inhibition Autoimmune vasculitis 1 This is Purple Glove Syndrome . Phenytoin is poorly soluble and requires Propylene Glycol and a high pH 12 solvent. Rapid infusion or extravasation causes severe focal toxicity crystal precipitation and vasoconstriction/thrombosis leading to necrosis. Fosphenytoin avoids this risk. What is the specific HLA allele associated with SJS/TEN in Asian patients taking Carbamazepine? HLA-B 1502 Pharmacogenetics Which anti-seizure drug follows Zero-Order kinetics at therapeutic levels? Phenytoin Pharmacology What effect does Valproate have on Lamotrigine metabolism? Inhibits glucuronidation -> Doubles Lamotrigine half-life Risk of Rash Pharmacokinetics Name two cosmetic side effects specific to Phenytoin. Gingival Hyperplasia Hirsutism Adverse Effects What is the mechanism of action of Lacosamide? Enhances Slow Inactivation of Na+ channels Mechanism Why is Fosphenytoin preferred over Phenytoin for IV loads? Water soluble less risk of hypotension/bradycardia/Purple Glove Syndrome Clinical What is the classic adverse effect of Carbamazepine that requires blood monitoring besides agranulocytosis ? Hyponatremia SIADH Adverse Effects Which ASD is an Auto-Inducer? Carbamazepine Pharmacokinetics What cardiac adverse effect is associated with Lacosamide? PR Interval Prolongation Bradycardia/Heart Block Adverse Effects Which vitamin deficiency is caused by Phenytoin induction? Folate Megaloblastic anemia and Vitamin D Osteomalacia Nutrition Physeo Pharmacology > Neurology > Antiepileptics BandB Neurology > Pharmacology > Antiepileptic drugs Sketchy Pharm Neuro/Psych > Antiepileptics HLA-B 1502 Warning Always screen Asian patients for HLA-B 1502 before starting Carbamazepine. Positive = High Risk for SJS/TEN. Zero-Order Kinetics Phenytoin metabolism saturates. Small dose increase = Huge blood level spike. Lamotrigine + Valproate Valproate DOUBLES Lamotrigine levels. You must cut the Lamotrigine dose in half and titrate very slowly to avoid THE RASH. Purple Glove Syndrome Caused by Phenytoin extravasation propylene glycol/pH 12 . Use Fosphenytoin to avoid it. Gingival Hyperplasia Classic side effect of Phenytoin along with Hirsutism . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l53", "title": "Lecture 53: Pharmacology: Antiseizure Drugs, Part 2", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l53 module Neuro l53 Lecture 53 Pharmacology Antiseizure Drugs Part 2 Pharmacology Lecture 53 Pharmacology Antiseizure Drugs Part 2\\n\\n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D.\\n Session Lecture 53\\n\\n 1. Broad Spectrum Agents\\n Story Mode \\nBroad-spectrum anti-seizure drugs ASDs are the \\ utility players\\ of epilepsy treatment. Because they target multiple mechanisms\\u2014often hitting sodium channels to stop firing and enhancing GABA to increase inhibition\\u2014they are effective against a wide variety of seizure types including focal generalized tonic-clonic and absence seizures. This makes them an excellent choice when the exact seizure type is unclear.\\n\\n 1.1 Valproate Divalproex \\n Mechanisms The definition of \\ Broad Spectrum.\\ \\n Blocks voltage-dependent Sodium $Na^+$ channels .\\n Blocks T-type Calcium $Ca^ 2+ $ channels Treats Absence .\\n Enhances GABA activity inhibitory .\\n Therapeutic Uses \\n Seizure First-line for many types Focal Generalized Absence Myoclonic. Used for Status Epilepticus off-label .\\n Non-Seizure Bipolar Disorder Acute Mania and Migraine Prophylaxis .\\n Pharmacokinetics \\n Mitochondrial metabolism Beta-oxidation .\\n Inhibitor of CYP enzymes increases levels of other drugs like Lamotrigine .\\n Adverse Effects \\n GI Nausea/Vomiting common limit by taking with food or ER formulations .\\n Cosmetic Weight Gain Hair Loss Alopecia Tremor.\\n Serious Hepatotoxicity Monitor LFTs Pancreatitis Thrombocytopenia .\\n Teratogenicity Neural Tube Defects Spina Bifida . Avoid in pregnancy if possible.\\n\\n correlate\\n Hyperammonemic Encephalopathy \\n The Mechanism \\nValproate is metabolized in the mitochondria and can interfere with the Urea Cycle.\\n Result Accumulation of Ammonia $NH 3$ in the blood.\\n Presentation Acute change in mental status confusion lethargy in a patient taking Valproate even with normal liver enzymes.\\n Risk Factor Concurrent use with Topiramate synergistic risk .\\n \\n\\n 1.2 Topiramate Topamax \\n Mechanisms \\n Blocks $Na^+$ channels.\\n Enhances GABA-A activity.\\n Antagonizes AMPA/Kainate Glutamate receptors.\\n Weak Carbonic Anhydrase Inhibitor .\\n Therapeutic Uses \\n Seizure Focal Generalized Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome.\\n Non-Seizure Migraine Prophylaxis Weight Loss in combination with Phentermine .\\n Adverse Effects \\n CNS \\ Dope-a-max\\ \\u2013 Cognitive slowing word-finding difficulties sedation paresthesias tingling .\\n Weight Loss contrast with Valproate .\\n Renal Kidney Stones Calcium Phosphate stones due to alkaline urine from carbonic anhydrase inhibition .\\n Ocular Acute Myopia/Angle Closure Glaucoma.\\n\\n correlate\\n Hypohidrosis & Hyperthermia \\n The Mechanism \\nTopiramate s carbonic anhydrase inhibition can impair sweat gland function.\\n Target Children are most vulnerable.\\n Risk Reduced sweating $\\\\downarrow$ heat dissipation leads to dangerous overheating Hyperthermia during play or hot weather.\\n Management Counsel parents to monitor for lack of sweating during exertion.\\n \\n\\n 1.3 Levetiracetam Keppra \\n Mechanism Unique. Binds to SV2A Synaptic Vesicle Protein 2A .\\n Prevents vesicle docking and neurotransmitter release.\\n Pharmacokinetics \\n Excellent Profile 100% Bioavailability Renal excretion unchanged No drug-drug interactions .\\n Adverse Effects \\n Generally well-tolerated sedation dizziness .\\n Neuropsychiatric \\ Keppra Rage\\ \\u2013 Irritability agitation aggression or psychosis rare .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. Narrow Spectrum Agents\\n Story Mode \\nUnlike the broad-spectrum agents these drugs have a specific lane. Ethosuximide is a sniper it hits one target T-type channels to fix one problem Absence seizures . Gabapentinoids are \\ structural mimics\\ of GABA that don t actually touch GABA receptors but block calcium channels to stop neuropathic pain and focal seizures.\\n\\n 2.1 Ethosuximide\\n Mechanism Blocks T-Type Calcium Channels in the Thalamus.\\n disrupts the 3Hz spike-and-wave rhythm of absence seizures.\\n Therapeutic Use Absence Seizures ONLY Childhood Absence Epilepsy .\\n Note Ineffective for tonic-clonic seizures.\\n Adverse Effects \\n GI Nausea/Vomiting/Pain very common .\\n CNS Fatigue Dizziness.\\n Rare SJS/TEN Aplastic Anemia.\\n\\n 2.2 Gabapentinoids Gabapentin & Pregabalin \\n Mechanism Bind to the $\\\\alpha 2\\\\delta$ subunit of voltage-gated Calcium channels presynaptic .\\n Reduces calcium influx $\\\\rightarrow$ reduces glutamate release.\\n Note Do NOT bind GABA receptors despite the name.\\n Therapeutic Uses \\n Neuropathic Pain Diabetic neuropathy Post-herpetic neuralgia Fibromyalgia.\\n Seizure Focal Seizures Adjunct . Exacerbates Generalized seizures. \\n Adverse Effects Sedation Peripheral Edema Weight gain.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. GABA Modulators Barbiturates & Benzos \\n Story Mode \\nThese drugs work directly on the GABA-A receptor the brain s primary \\ off switch.\\ By opening the chloride channel they hyperpolarize neurons. The distinction is in how they open it Benzos increase Frequency Benzos = Frenz while Barbiturates increase Duration Barbi-durates .\\n\\n 3.1 Phenobarbital\\n Mechanism Barbiturate. Binds allosteric site on GABA-A.\\n Increases Duration of $Cl^-$ channel opening.\\n Danger At high doses can open the channel without GABA direct agonist $\\\\rightarrow$ Coma/Death.\\n Pharmacokinetics \\n Potent Inducer of CYP450 enzymes eats up other drugs .\\n Long half-life days .\\n Therapeutic Uses \\n Neonatal Seizures First line .\\n Status Epilepticus Refractory .\\n Adverse Effects Profound sedation Respiratory Depression Hyperactivity in children paradoxical .\\n\\n 3.2 Benzodiazepines Diazepam Lorazepam Midazolam \\n Mechanism Binds allosteric site on GABA-A.\\n Increases Frequency of $Cl^-$ channel opening.\\n Safety Require GABA to work Ceiling effect .\\n Therapeutic Uses \\n Acute Status Epilepticus First Line - \\ Abortive therapy\\ .\\n Chronic Rarely used due to Tolerance .\\n Specific Agents \\n Lorazepam IV Preferred for Status stays in brain longer .\\n Diazepam IV/Rectal Highly lipid soluble $\\\\rightarrow$ rapid entry $\\\\rightarrow$ rapid Redistribution to fat/muscle short duration of action .\\n Midazolam IM/Buccal/Nasal Used when IV access is not available e.g. EMS .\\n\\n correlate\\n \\ Floppy Baby\\ Syndrome \\n Benzos in Pregnancy \\nChronic use of benzodiazepines near delivery can cause neonatal withdrawal and hypotonia.\\n Presentation Neonate with severe hypotonia \\ rag doll\\ difficulty suckling apnea and hypothermia.\\n Management Supportive care; avoid benzos in pregnancy if possible.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n 4. Other Agents\\n\\n 4.1 Cannabidiol Epidiolex \\n Mechanism Unknown does not act on CB1/CB2 receptors .\\n Uses Severe childhood epilepsy syndromes \\n Dravet Syndrome \\n Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome \\n Tuberous Sclerosis Complex \\n Adverse Effects Somnolence elevated liver enzymes LFTs .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 5. Summary Tables\\n\\n highyield\\n Drug Selection by Seizure Type \\n| Seizure Type | First Line / Effective | Contraindicated / Ineffective |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Focal | Carbamazepine Phenytoin Lamotrigine Levetiracetam | -- |\\n| Generalized Tonic-Clonic | Valproate Levetiracetam Lamotrigine Topiramate | Carbamazepine Phenytoin can worsen some generalized types |\\n| Absence | Ethosuximide Valproate | Phenytoin Carbamazepine Gabapentin |\\n| Status Epilepticus | Benzodiazepines Lorazepam $\\\\rightarrow$ Fosphenytoin/Valproate | -- |\\n \\n\\n highyield\\n Key Side Effects & Buzzwords \\n| Drug | Buzzword / Key Side Effect |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| Valproate | Neural Tube Defects Hepatotoxicity Pancreatitis Hyperammonemia |\\n| Topiramate | Weight Loss Kidney Stones Mental dulling \\ Dopeamax\\ |\\n| Levetiracetam | Agitation/Aggression \\ Keppra Rage\\ |\\n| Ethosuximide | GI Distress Pain/Nausea |\\n| Phenytoin | Gingival Hyperplasia Hirsutism Zero-order kinetics |\\n| Lamotrigine | Stevens-Johnson Syndrome Rash |\\n questions A 7-year-old boy is brought to the clinic for frequent staring spells where he is unresponsive for 5-10 seconds. An EEG shows a 3-Hz spike-and-wave pattern. You prescribe the first-line finding for this condition. Which of the following is the mechanism of action of this drug? Binds to SV2A vesicle protein Blocks voltage-gated Sodium channels Blocks T-Type Calcium Channels Enhances GABA-A receptor frequency Blocks Alpha-2-Delta Calcium subunit 2 The clinical presentation staring spells and EEG 3Hz spike-and-wave are classic for Absence Seizures . The drug of choice is Ethosuximide which works by blocking T-Type Calcium Channels in the Thalamus. A 25-year-old male with a history of obesity and migraines presents for management of his focal seizures. He is interested in a medication that might also help him lose weight. Which agent would be most appropriate? Valproate Gabapentin Topiramate Phenytoin Carbamazepine 2 Topiramate is associated with weight loss and is also used for migraine prophylaxis making it an excellent choice for this patient. In contrast Valproate and Gabapentin are associated with weight gain. A neonate is born with severe hypotonia difficulty feeding and respiratory depression. The mother reports taking an anti-seizure medication for anxiety during her pregnancy. Which mechanism best explains the infant s presentation? Sodium channel blockade T-type calcium channel blockade Increased frequency of GABA-A channel opening Folate antagonism SV2A binding 2 This is \\ Floppy Baby\\ Syndrome caused by benzodiazepine withdrawal/effect in the neonate. Benzodiazepines work by increasing the frequency of GABA-A chloride channel opening. Phenytoin Folate antagonist causes Fetal Hydantoin Syndrome cleft lip/palate not acute hypotonia. A patient with refractory seizures is started on Valproate. Two weeks later he presents with confusion and lethargy. LFTs are normal but ammonia levels are elevated. Prior to this he was taking another ASD. Which co-medication most likely increased the risk of this complication? Levetiracetam Topiramate Lamotrigine Gabapentin Ethosuximide 1 Topiramate and Valproate both can cause hyperammonemia and their concurrent use significantly increases the risk of Hyperammonemic Encephalopathy . Which of the following anti-seizure medications functions by binding to the SV2A synaptic vesicle protein? Phenobarbital Lamotrigine Levetiracetam Tiagabine Vigabatrin 2 Levetiracetam has a unique mechanism of action it binds to SV2A Synaptic Vesicle Glycoprotein 2A to prevent neurotransmitter release. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l54", "title": "Lecture 54: Aphasia and Common Strokes", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l54 module Neuro l54 Lecture 54 Aphasia and Common Strokes Neurology Lecture 54 Aphasia and Common Strokes\\n\\n Lecturer A. Leder D.O.\\n Session Lecture 54\\n\\n 1. Aphasia Syndromes\\n Story Mode \\nAphasia is a disorder of language not speech interruption of the \\ mind-to-mouth\\ connection . To diagnose the type you simply need to check three boxes Fluency Is speech smooth or broken? Comprehension Do they understand you? and Repetition Can they say \\ No ifs ands or buts\\ ? .\\n\\n 1.1 The \\ Perisylvian\\ Aphasias Repetition is Impaired \\nThese occur around the Sylvian fissure where the main language arc resides.\\n Broca s Aphasia Expressive \\n Lesion Left Inferior Frontal Gyrus . usually superior division of Left MCA .\\n Features \\n Non-Fluent Speech is broken effortful telegraphic \\ Car... go... store\\ .\\n Comprehension Intact They know what they want to say and get frustrated .\\n Repetition Impaired.\\n Associated Contralateral Right face/arm weakness.\\n Wernicke s Aphasia Receptive \\n Lesion Left Superior Temporal Gyrus . Inferior division of Left MCA .\\n Features \\n Fluent Speech is voluminous but meaningless \\ Word Salad\\ .\\n Comprehension Impaired They don t realize they aren t making sense .\\n Repetition Impaired.\\n Associated Right Superior Quadrantanopia \\ Pie in the sky\\ .\\n Global Aphasia \\n Lesion Large Left MCA infarct knocking out both Broca s and Wernicke s areas.\\n Features All Imapired Non-fluent No comprehension No repetition .\\n Conduction Aphasia \\n Lesion Arcuate Fasciculus The bridge between Broca and Wernicke .\\n Features \\n Fluent speech Intact comprehension.\\n Repetition IMPAIRED The connection is broken . They act like Wernicke s but know they are making mistakes and try to correct them.\\n\\n 1.2 The \\ Transcortical\\ Aphasias Repetition is SPARED \\nThese lesions are \\ around\\ the language centers watershed zones but spare the core Arcuate Fasciculus loop.\\n Transcortical Motor Like Broca s Non-fluent Good Comp but Repetition is Intact . ACA/MCA Watershed .\\n Transcortical Sensory Like Wernicke s Fluent Poor Comp but Repetition is Intact . MCA/PCA Watershed . They may exhibit Echolalia parroting back words .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. Ischemic Stroke Syndromes by Territory\\n\\n 2.1 Anterior Cerebral Artery ACA \\n Territory Medial surface of the brain Leg homunculus area .\\n Presentation \\n Contralateral Lower Extremity Weakness Leg > Arm .\\n Abulia / Akinetic Mutism Lack of will to move or speak Frontal lobe damage .\\n Urinary Incontinence. \\n\\n 2.2 Middle Cerebral Artery MCA \\n Territory Lateral surface of the brain Face/Arm homunculus area .\\n Presentation \\n Contralateral Face and Arm Weakness Face/Arm > Leg .\\n Gaze Preference Eyes look Toward the lesion away from the weakness .\\n Dominant Left Hemi Aphasia.\\n Non-Dominant Right Hemi Hemineflect ignoring the left side of the world .\\n\\n 2.3 Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA \\n Territory Occipital lobe and Inferior Temporal lobe.\\n Presentation \\n Contralateral Homonymous Hemianopsia Visual field cut with Macular Sparing .\\n Alexia without Agraphia Left PCA + Splenium of Corpus Callosum . Patient can write but cannot read even what they just wrote .\\n Gerstmann Syndrome Dominant Angular Gyrus .\\n 1. Agraphia Can t write .\\n 2. Acalculia Can t do math .\\n 3. Finger Agnosia Can t identify fingers .\\n 4. Left-Right Disorientation.\\n\\n 2.4 Internal Carotid Artery ICA \\n Mechanism Occlusion of the source pipe for both ACA and MCA.\\n Presentation Combination of ACA + MCA symptoms Dense hemiplegia face/arm/leg .\\n Clinical Pearl Amaurosis Fugax \\ Curtain coming down\\ . Transient monocular blindness due to embolus to the Ophthalmic artery first branch of ICA .\\n\\n 2.5 Watershed Infarcts\\n Mechanism Systemic hypoperfusion e.g. Cardiac Arrest or severe hypotension . The areas between vascular territories ACA-MCA or MCA-PCA borders die first.\\n Presentation \\ Man-in-a-Barrel\\ Syndrome .\\n Bilateral proximal arm and weakness Shoulders/Thighs .\\n spares the hands and feet and face.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. Lacunar Strokes\\n Story Mode \\nThese are small vessel strokes lenticulostriate arteries caused by Hypertension lipohyalinosis . They affect deep structures Internal Capsule Thalamus Pons and have very specific \\ Pure\\ presentations.\\n Pure Motor Posterior Limb of Internal Capsule. Contralateral weakness only .\\n Pure Sensory VPL of Thalamus. Contralateral numbness only .\\n Dysarthria-Clumsy Hand Genu of Internal Capsule or Pons.\\n Ataxic Hemiparesis Pons or Internal Capsule.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 4. Brainstem Syndromes\\n Story Mode \\nThe brainstem is high-value real estate. A small stroke here causes massive problems. The rule of thumb Crossed Signs Ipsilateral Cranial Nerve deficit + Contralateral Body deficit .\\n\\n 4.1 Lateral Medullary Wallenberg Syndrome\\n Vessel PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Remember \\ Don t Pica a horse hoarseness \\ .\\n Key Features \\n Dysphagia / Hoarseness Nucleus Ambiguus CN IX X . Loss of Gag reflex.\\n Vertigo / Nystagmus Vestibular Nuclei.\\n Ipsilateral Horner s Syndrome Sympathetic fibers.\\n Ipsilateral Facial Numbness / Contralateral Body Numbness Pain/Temp .\\n Ipsilateral Ataxia. \\n\\n 4.2 Locked-In Syndrome\\n Vessel Basilar Artery thrombosis.\\n Lesion Bilateral Ventral Pons.\\n Presentation \\n Quadriplegia all motor tracts cut .\\n Anarthria cannot speak .\\n Consciousness is Preserved Reticular formation is spared .\\n Communication Vertical Eye Movements and Blinking spared midbrain centers .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 5. Hemorrhagic Strokes\\n\\n| Type | Etiology | CT Appearance | Clinical Pearl |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Epidural Hematoma | Trauma Temporal bone fx $\\\\rightarrow$ Middle Meningeal Artery rupture. | Lens-shaped Biconvex . Does NOT cross suture lines. | \\ Talk and Die\\ Lucid interval followed by rapid deterioration. |\\n| Subdural Hematoma | Trauma Falls/Elderly/Alcoholics $\\\\rightarrow$ Bridging Veins rupture. | Crescent-shaped . Crosses suture lines. | Slow onset. associated with cerebral atrophy. |\\n| Subarachnoid Hemorrhage | Aneurysm Rupture Berry Aneurysm or Trauma. | Blood in cisterns/sulci \\ Starfish\\ pattern . | \\ Worst Headache of my Life\\ Thunderclap . Risk of vasospasm days later. |\\n\\n correlate\\n Cerebral Venous Sinus Thrombosis CVST \\n The Story \\nA stroke that doesn t follow arterial rules. Think of this in a Hypercoagulable patient Pregnancy OCP use Cancer Factor V Leiden .\\n Mechanism Clot in the Dural Venous Sinuses e.g. Sagittal Sinus prevents blood drainage $\\\\rightarrow$ Congestion $\\\\rightarrow$ Hemorrhage.\\n Presentation Headache High ICP Seizures Papilledema.\\n Diagnosis MR Venogram MRV or CT Venogram.\\n Treatment Anticoagulation even if there is some hemorrhage you must clear the clot .\\n \\n\\n highyield\\n Arterial Dissection \\n Patient Young patient with neck trauma chiropractic manipulation or spontaneous.\\n Presentation \\n Neck Pain + Headache.\\n Horner s Syndrome Ipsilateral The sympathetic fibers run along the carotid artery. Enlargement of the artery compresses them.\\n TIA/Stroke symptoms from emboli.\\n \\n questions A 72-year-old male presents with right-sided face and arm weakness but with relative sparing of the leg. He is also found to have gaze deviation to the left. Which vascular territory is most likely affected? Left Anterior Cerebral Artery ACA Left Middle Cerebral Artery MCA Right Middle Cerebral Artery MCA Left Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA Basilar Artery 1 Left MCA Stroke . The MCA supplies the lateral surface of the cortex Face/Arm area . Weakness is contralateral Right . Gaze deviation is toward the lesion Left away from the hemiparesis. A patient presents with word salad speech. They speak fluently and with normal rhythm but the content is meaningless. They cannot repeat phrases or follow commands. Where is the most likely lesion? Inferior Frontal Gyrus Superior Temporal Gyrus Arcuate Fasciculus Angular Gyrus Occipital Lobe 1 This is Wernicke s Aphasia Fluent Impaired Comprehension Impaired Repetition . The lesion is in the Superior Temporal Gyrus Wernicke s Area of the dominant hemisphere. A 25-year-old female presents with severe headache and specialized imaging reveals a thrombosis of the superior sagittal sinus. Which of the following is a key risk factor for this condition? Hypertension Atrial Fibrillation Tobacco Use Oral Contraceptive Use Diabetes Mellitus 3 Cerebral Venous Sinus Thrombosis CVST is associated with hypercoagulable states. Oral Contraceptive Use is a major risk factor especially in young women. HTN/DM/Smoking are risks for arterial strokes. A patient is able to speak fluently and understand commands perfectly but cannot repeat the phrase No ifs ands or buts . They are aware of their errors and try to correct them. What is the diagnosis? Broca s Aphasia Wernicke s Aphasia Conduction Aphasia Global Aphasia Transcortical Motor Aphasia 2 Conduction Aphasia is characterized by Fluent speech + Intact Comprehension + Impaired Repetition . It is caused by a lesion to the Arcuate Fasciculus . A patient presents with sudden onset of the worst headache of my life . A non-contrast CT shows a starfish pattern of hyperdensity in the basal cisterns. What represents the most likely etiology? Rupture of Middle Meningeal Artery Rupture of Bridging Veins Rupture of Berry Aneurysm Hypertensive lipohyalinosis Carotid Dissection 2 This is a Subarachnoid Hemorrhage Thunderclap headache + Starfish pattern . The most common non-traumatic cause is the rupture of a Saccular Berry Aneurysm . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l55", "title": "Lecture 55: Cerebrovascular Diseases", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l55 module Neuro l55 Lecture 55 Cerebrovascular Diseases Neurology Lecture 55 Cerebrovascular Diseases\\n\\n Lecturer W. Zaloga D.O.\\n Session Lecture 55\\n\\n 1. Ischemic Stroke Pathology\\n Story Mode \\nIschemia is a lack of blood flow which means lack of Oxygen Hypoxia and lack of Glucose Hypoglycemia . The brain is incredibly hungry and has zero reserves.\\n\\n 1.1 The Ischemic Cascade Excitotoxicity \\nWhen neurons run out of energy ATP their ion pumps fail.\\n1. Depolarization Sodium/Calcium flow IN Potassium flows OUT.\\n2. Glutamate Dump The dying neuron releases a massive amount of Glutamate excitatory neurotransmitter .\\n3. Calcium Influx Glutamate opens NMDA channels on neighbors $\\\\rightarrow$ Massive Calcium influx.\\n4. Enzyme Activation Calcium activates \\ executioner\\ enzymes proteases lipases that chew up the cell from the inside.\\n5. Result Liquefactive Necrosis .\\n\\n 1.2 Timeline of Ischemic Changes\\n| Time | Microscopic Change | Macroscopic Change |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| 0-12 Hours | \\ Red Neurons\\ Eosinophilic cytoplasm pyknotic nuclei . First visible sign. | Normal or slight blurring of gray-white junction. |\\n| 12-24 Hours | Neutrophils infiltrate Acute inflammation . | Tissue becomes pale and soft. |\\n| 1-3 Days | Macrophages Microglia arrive to clean up the mess. | Swelling/Edema Risk of Herniation . |\\n| 1-2 Weeks | Reactive Gliosis Astrocytes proliferate + Vascular proliferation. | Liquefactive Necrosis Mushy cavity . |\\n| > 2 Weeks | Glial Scar formation Astrocytic border . | Cystic cavity hole in the brain . |\\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. Stroke Classifications\\n 2.1 Thrombotic Stroke\\n Mechanism Clot forms locally at the site of infarction.\\n Cause Ruptured Atherosclerotic plaque.\\n Key Locations Carotid bifurcation MCA origin Basilar artery.\\n Presentation \\ Stuttering\\ onset symptoms wax and wane .\\n\\n 2.2 Embolic Stroke\\n Mechanism Clot travels from elsewhere .\\n Causes \\n Cardiogenic Atrial Fibrillation Left Atrial Appendage Post-MI thrombus Vegetation Endocarditis .\\n Fat Emboli Long bone fractures femur . Triad Hypoxemia + Petechial Rash + Confusion .\\n Key Features Sudden maximal deficit at onset. Affects multiple vascular territories Embolic Shower .\\n\\n 2.3 Global Ischemia Watershed Infarcts \\n Mechanism Systemic Hypotension Shock Cardiac Arrest .\\n Location Border zones between ACA/MCA or MCA/PCA.\\n Histology Laminar Necrosis Pyramidal neurons of Hippocampus and Cortex layers 3 5 & 6 are most vulnerable .\\n\\n 2.4 Lacunar Strokes\\n Mechanism Lipohyalinosis Hyaline Arteriolosclerosis of small penetrating vessels due to Chronic Hypertension .\\n Vessels Lenticulostriate arteries.\\n Location Basal Ganglia Thalamus Internal Capsule Pons.\\n Clinical Pure Motor or Pure Sensory deficits. No cortical signs No aphasia no neglect .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. Hemorrhagic Strokes & Vascular Malformations\\n\\n| Condition | Etiology | Key Features |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Intracerebral Hemorrhage ICH | Hypertension Deep bleeds Basal Ganglia/Thalamus via Charcot-Bouchard Microaneurysms . <br> Amyloid Angiopathy Lobar/Cortical bleeds in elderly . | Rapid onset focal deficit + Headache + Nausea/Vomiting High ICP . |\\n| Subarachnoid Hemorrhage SAH | Ruptured Berry Aneurysm ACA-Comm is 1 site . Associated with PCKD Ehlers-Danlos. | \\ Thunderclap Headache\\ . Nuchal rigidity. Lumbar Puncture Xanthochromia yellow CSF . |\\n| A-V Malformation AVM | Congenital tangle of arteries-to-veins no capillaries . High flow shunting. | present in Young Adults 10-30yo with Seizures or Hemorrhage. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n 4. Traumatic Brain Injury TBI \\n\\n 4.1 Skull Fractures\\n Linear Simple crack.\\n Depressed Bone fragment pushed inward requires surgery .\\n Diastatic Separation of cranial sutures.\\n Basilar Fracture of the skull base.\\n Signs Raccoon Eyes Periorbital ecchymosis Battle Sign Mastoid ecchymosis CSF Rhinorrhea/Otorrhea Halo sign .\\n\\n 4.2 Parenchymal Injuries\\n Concussion Reversible altered mental status amnesia/confusion with NO structural damage on CT/MRI.\\n Contusion Bruising of brain tissue Hemorrhage + Edema .\\n Coup Injury at site of impact Head hits wall .\\n Contrecoup Injury opposite to impact Brain sloshes back . Common in frontal/temporal poles.\\n Diffuse Axonal Injury DAI \\n Mechanism Shearing/Rotational forces e.g. High-speed car crash .\\n Pathology Stretching/tearing of axons in deep white matter Corpus Callosum/Brainstem .\\n Imaging Multiple punctate hemorrhages on MRI SWI sequence . CT often normal.\\n Clinical Instant coma. Poor prognosis.\\n Chronic Traumatic Encephalopathy CTE Accumulation of Tau protein neurofibrillary tangles after repetitive mild TBI Boxers Football . Dementia aggression parkinsonism.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 5. Herniation Syndromes\\n The Story \\nThe skull is a closed box. If you add volume Blood Tumor Edema the brain has nowhere to go but... squished against rigid membranes or out the holes.\\n\\n 5.1 Subfalcine Cingulate Herniation\\n Movement Cingulate gyrus pushed under the Falx Cerebri.\\n Compression Anterior Cerebral Artery ACA .\\n Clinical Contralateral Leg Weakness .\\n\\n 5.2 Transtentorial Uncal Herniation\\n Movement Uncus Medial Temporal Lobe pushed under the Tentorium Cerebelli.\\n Compression \\n 1. CN III Ipsilateral \\ Blown Pupil\\ Dilated/Fixed + \\ Down and Out\\ eye. First sign .\\n 2. Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA Contralateral Homonymous Hemianopsia Cortical Blindness .\\n 3. Cerebral Peduncle Contralateral Hemiparesis or Ipsilateral if Kernohan s Notch phenomenon .\\n 4. Duret Hemorrhages Tearing of paramedian basilar arteries $\\\\rightarrow$ Brainstem bleed $\\\\rightarrow$ Death.\\n\\n 5.3 Tonsillar Herniation\\n Movement Cerebellar Tonsils pushed through the Foramen Magnum.\\n Compression Brainstem Medulla .\\n Clinical Cardiorespiratory Arrest \\\\\\ Coning\\\\\\ .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 6. High Yield Renders\\n\\n highyield\\n Stroke Syndromes Cheat Sheet \\n| Vessel | Territory | Key Deficit | Buzzword |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| ACA | Medial Surface | Leg > Arm Weakness | \\ Abulia\\ Lack of will |\\n| MCA | Lateral Surface | Face/Arm > Leg Weakness | Aphasia Left Neglect Right |\\n| PCA | Occipital Lobe | Homonymous Hemianopsia | \\ Macular Sparing\\ |\\n| Lacunar | Deep Structures | Pure Motor or Pure Sensory | Hypertension No Cortical Signs |\\n \\n\\n highyield\\n Herniation Syndromes \\n Subfalcine Cingulate gyrus under Falx $\\\\rightarrow$ ACA compression $\\\\rightarrow$ Leg weakness.\\n Uncal Medial temporal lobe under Tentorium $\\\\rightarrow$ CN III compression $\\\\rightarrow$ \\ Down & Out\\ Pupil.\\n Tonsillar Cerebellar tonsils into Foramen Magnum $\\\\rightarrow$ Brainstem compression $\\\\rightarrow$ Death.\\n \\n questions A patient suffers a severe ischemic stroke and dies 6 hours later. An autopsy is performed. What is the most likely microscopic finding in the affected brain tissue? Neutrophilic infiltration Macrophage infiltration Red Neurons Reactive Gliosis Liquefactive Necrosis 2 Red Neurons eosinophilic cytoplasm pyknotic nuclei are the earliest visible microscopic change appearing within 12-24 hours sometimes as early as 6 hours . Neutrophils arrive at 24 hours. A 65-year-old male with a history of poorly controlled hypertension presents with sudden onset pure motor hemiparesis. MRI reveals a small fluid-filled cavity in the internal capsule. What is the underlying pathophysiology? Ruptured Berry Aneurysm Amyloid Angiopathy Lipohyalinosis Cardiogenic Embolism Global Ischemia 2 This is a Lacunar Stroke . The mechanism is Lipohyalinosis thickening/destabilization of the small penetrating lenticulostriate arteries due to chronic Hypertension . A patient involved in a high-speed motor vehicle accident is comatose on arrival. CT scan is unremarkable but MRI shows multiple punctate hemorrhages in the corpus callosum and brainstem. What is the diagnosis? Subdural Hematoma Epidural Hematoma Diffuse Axonal Injury Coup-Contrecoup Injury Chronic Traumatic Encephalopathy 2 Diffuse Axonal Injury DAI is caused by shearing forces in high-speed trauma. It affects deep white matter tracts. It is often invisible on CT but shows as microhemorrhages on MRI SWI . During a brain herniation event a patient develops a dilated fixed pupil on the right side. Which structure is being compressed? Optic Nerve Oculomotor Nerve CN III Trochlear Nerve CN IV Abducens Nerve CN VI Sympathetic chain 1 This is Uncal Herniation . The uncus of the temporal lobe herniates under the tentorium compressing the ipsilateral Oculomotor Nerve CN III causing a blown pupil . What is the primary neurotransmitter responsible for excitotoxicity and subsequent neuronal death in ischemic stroke? GABA Dopamine Serotonin Glutamate Acetylcholine 3 Glutamate is the major excitatory neurotransmitter. In ischemia it is dumped into the synapse overstimulating NMDA receptors and causing massive Calcium influx which kills the cell. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l56", "title": "Lecture 56: Neurodegenerative Disease and Selective Neuropathy", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l56 module Neuro l56 Lecture 56 Neurodegenerative Disease and Selective Neuropathy Neurology Lecture 56 Neurodegenerative Disease and Selective Neuropathy\\n\\n Lecturer M. Plummer M.D.\\n Session Lecture 56\\n\\n 1. General Principles\\n Common Theme Accumulation of protein aggregates inclusions resistant to degradation.\\n Toxicity Aggregates elicit stress responses damaging neurons.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. Information on Specific Diseases\\n\\n 2.1 Alzheimer s Disease AD \\n Epidemiology Most common cause of dementia in elderly.\\n Clinical Slow onset memory loss $\\\\rightarrow$ disorientation $\\\\rightarrow$ severe variability/immobility.\\n Pathology \\n Gross Global Atrophy Widened sulci Narrowed gyri . Hydrocephalus ex vacuo ventricles dilate to fill space .\\n Microscopic \\n 1. Senile Neuritic Plaques Extracellular. Core of Beta-Amyloid A$\\\\beta$ .\\n Derived from Amyloid Precursor Protein APP cleavage by $\\\\beta$-secretase and $\\\\gamma$-secretase.\\n A$\\\\beta$42 is the toxic aggregation-prone form.\\n 2. Neurofibrillary Tangles NFTs Intracellular. Hyperphosphorylated Tau protein microtubule-associated .\\n Correlation Number of Tangles correlates better with degree of dementia than plaques.\\n Genetics \\n APP Chr 21 Down Syndrome patients get AD by age 40 Trisomy 21 = extra APP gene .\\n Presenilin 1 Chr 14 & 2 Chr 1 Early-onset familial AD increase A$\\\\beta$42 .\\n ApoE4 Chr 19 Increased risk for sporadic late-onset AD.\\n\\n 2.2 Frontotemporal Lobar Degeneration Pick Disease \\n Clinical Signification behavioral/personality changes disinhibition precede memory loss. Aphasia.\\n Gross \\ Knife-Edge\\ Atrophy of Frontal and Temporal lobes. Spares Parietal/Occipital.\\n Microscopic Tau inclusions Pick Bodies in some variants FTLD-Tau . Spheroidal inclusions.\\n Note FTLD-TDP43 is another variant Ubiquitin/TDP-43 inclusions associated with ALS.\\n\\n 2.3 Parkinson s Disease PD \\n Pathophysiology Loss of Dopaminergic neurons in the Substantia Nigra Pars Compacta .\\n Clinical TRAP \\n T remor Resting \\ Pill-rolling\\ .\\n R igidity Cogwheel .\\n A kinesia / Bradykinesia Slow movement shuffling gait masked facies .\\n P ostural Instability.\\n Microscopic Lewy Bodies Intracellular eosinophilic inclusions with halo .\\n Composed of $\\\\alpha$-Synuclein .\\n MPTP Contaminant in illicit opioids $\\\\rightarrow$ Acute Parkinsonism destroys Substantia Nigra .\\n\\n 2.4 Huntington s Disease HD \\n Genetics Autosomal Dominant. CAG Trinucleotide Repeat expansion in HTT gene Chr 4 .\\n Anticipation Repeats strictly increase during spermatogenesis Paternal transmission $\\\\rightarrow$ earlier onset in next gen .\\n Pathology Atrophy of the Caudate Nucleus and Putamen . Loss of GABAergic neurons.\\n Clinical Chorea jerky purposeless movements Dementia Depression/Suicide. Presents in 30s-40s.\\n\\n 2.5 Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis ALS \\n Definition degeneration of Upper Motor Neurons UMN in cortex and Lower Motor Neurons LMN in spinal cord/brainstem.\\n Clinical \\n LMN signs Weakness Atrophy Fasciculations.\\n UMN signs Hyperreflexia Spasticity Babinski sign.\\n Sensation is intact. \\n Genetics Familial cases often linked to SOD1 mutation Superoxide Dismutase 1 .\\n\\n 2.6 Prion Disease Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease - CJD \\n Mechanism Conversion of normal Prion protein PrP$^ c $ $\\\\alpha$-helix to pathogenic form PrP$^ sc $ $\\\\beta$-sheet .\\n PrP$^ sc $ induces conformational change in normal PrP$^ c $ chain reaction .\\n Pathology Spongiform Encephalopathy vacuoles in gray matter . No inflammation.\\n Clinical Rapidly Progressive Dementia Startle Myoclonus. Death < 1 year.\\n Types Sporadic most common Variant Mad Cow/BSE Familial Iatrogenic.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. Peripheral Neuropathies\\n\\n 3.1 Charcot-Marie-Tooth CMT Disease\\n Etiology Hereditary Motor and Sensory Neuropathy HMSN . Auto Dominant PMP22 duplication is common .\\n Clinical Distal muscle atrophy \\ Stork leg\\ deformity / Inverted/Champagne bottle legs Pes Cavus High arch Hammer toes.\\n Microscopic Repetitive Demyelination/Remyelination $\\\\rightarrow$ \\ Onion Bulb\\ formation. Palpable nerves.\\n\\n 3.2 Diabetic Neuropathy\\n Patterns \\n 1. Distal Symmetric Sensorimotor \\ Stocking and Glove\\ sensory loss/paresthesia. Ulcers.\\n 2. Autonomic Orthostatic hypotension erectile dysfunction urinary retention neurogenic bladder .\\n Pathogenesis High glucose $\\\\rightarrow$ AGEs Advanced Glycation End-products $\\\\rightarrow$ Microvascular damage vasa nervorum + Sorbitol accumulation osmotic damage .\\n\\n 3.3 Traumatic/Compression Neuropathies\\n Traumatic Neuroma Tangled mass of axons/Schwann cells after nerve transection failed regeneration . Painful.\\n Carpal Tunnel Syndrome Compression of Median Nerve at wrist. Numbness in thumb/index/middle fingers. Thenar atrophy.\\n Morton s Neuroma Compression of interdigital nerve usually 3rd/4th toe interspace . Related to high heels.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 4. High Yield Renders\\n\\n highyield\\n Neurodegenerative Diseases The \\ Protein & Location\\ Match \\n| Disease | Protein Aggregate | Location of Atrophy | Key Clinical Feature |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Alzheimer s | Beta-Amyloid Plaques Tau Tangles | Temporoparietal / Global | Memory Loss |\\n| Pick s FTD | Tau Pick Bodies | Frontal / Temporal \\ Knife-edge\\ | Personality Change > Memory |\\n| Parkinson s | Alpha-Synuclein Lewy Bodies | Substantia Nigra | TRAP Tremor Rigidity Akinesia |\\n| Huntington s | Huntingtin CAG Repeats | Caudate Nucleus | Chorea + Dementia |\\n| ALS | TDP-43 / SOD1 | UMN Cortex + LMN spinal cord | Weakness + Spasticity No sensory loss |\\n| CJD | Prion $PrP^ sc $ | Gray Matter Spongiform | Rapid Dementia + Myoclonus |\\n \\n questions A 55-year-old male presents with changes in personality and inappropriate social behavior. His family reports he has become disinhibited and apathetic. Memory is relatively preserved. MRI shows significant atrophy of the frontal and temporal lobes. What is the most likely diagnosis? Alzheimer s Disease Parkinson s Disease Pick Disease Frontotemporal Dementia Huntington s Disease Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease 2 This is Pick Disease Frontotemporal Dementia . The key features are early behavioral/personality changes disinhibition sparing memory initially and Knife-edge atrophy of frontal/temporal lobes. Histology often shows Tau inclusions Pick bodies . A 40-year-old male presents with choreiform movements and progressive dementia. His father had similar symptoms starting at age 50. Genetic testing reveals an expanded CAG repeat sequence on Chromosome 4. Which brain structure is most likely atophied? Substantia Nigra Hippocampus Caudate Nucleus Cerebellum Motor Cortex 2 This is Huntington s Disease CAG repeats Autosomal Dominant Anticipation . The characteristic pathology is atrophy of the Caudate Nucleus and putamen leading to chorea. A 70-year-old male presents with a resting tremor in his right hand rigidity and a shuffling gait. Histological examination of his brain post-mortem would most likely reveal which finding in the Substantia Nigra? Neurofibrillary Tangles Senile Plaques Negri Bodies Lewy Bodies Spongiform change 3 This is Parkinson s Disease TRAP symptoms . The hallmark histology is Lewy Bodies alpha-synuclein inclusions in the Substantia Nigra . A 60-year-old female presents with rapidly progressive dementia and myoclonus. She dies 4 months later. Autopsy reveals spongiform changes in the gray matter with no inflammation. What is the underlying pathogenic mechanism? Accumulation of Beta-Amyloid Loss of Dopaminergic neurons Conversion of PrPc to PrPsc CAG repeat expansion SOD1 mutation 2 This is Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease CJD a Prion disease. The mechanism is the conversion of normal alpha-helical PrPc to the pathological beta-sheet rich PrPsc which aggregates and causes spongiform encephalopathy. A patient with long-standing diabetes mellitus develops numbness and tingling in both feet that has progressed up to the ankles. This stocking-and-glove distribution is characteristic of which type of neuropathy? Mononeuritis Multiplex Distal Symmetric Polyneuropathy Autonomic Neuropathy Radiculopathy Traumatic Neuroma 1 Distal Symmetric Polyneuropathy is the most common form of diabetic neuropathy. It presents as a sensory loss/paresthesia starting in the toes and moving proximally Stocking distribution and later affecting hands Glove . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l57", "title": "Lecture 57: Parkinson", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l57 module Neuro l57 Lecture 57 Parkinson s Disease and its Variants Neurology Lecture 57 Parkinson s Disease and its Variants\\n\\n Lecturer A. Leder D.O.\\n Session Lecture 57\\n\\n 1. Parkinson s Disease PD \\n Story Mode \\nParkinson s is a disease of \\ Too Little Dopamine\\ movement is stuck . It s a synucleinopathy meaning the garbage protein dumping into the cells is Alpha-Synuclein. The classic patient is elderly slow stiff and has a tremor.\\n\\n 1.1 Clinical Features TRAP \\n T remor Resting tremor 4-6 Hz . \\ Pill-rolling\\ . Usually Asymmetric at onset.\\n R igidity \\ Cogwheel\\ rigidity ratchet-like resistance to passive movement .\\n A kinesia / Bradykinesia Slowness of movement.\\n Masked Facies Hypomimia .\\n Micrographia Small handwriting .\\n Shuffling Gait Festinating gait with reduced arm swing.\\n Freezing Feet stuck to floor especially in doorways .\\n P ostural Instability Loss of balance frequent falls usually a Late finding .\\n\\n 1.2 Non-Motor Symptoms\\nOften precede motor symptoms by years.\\n Anosmia Loss of smell.\\n Constipation. \\n REM Sleep Behavior Disorder RBD Acting out dreams punching/kicking due to loss of REM atonia.\\n Depression/Anxiety. \\n\\n 1.3 Pathology & Genetics\\n Pathology \\n Loss of Dopaminergic Neurons in the Substantia Nigra Pars Compacta .\\n Lewy Bodies Intracellular eosinophilic inclusions composed of Alpha-Synuclein .\\n Genetics \\n GBA Glucocerebrosidase Risk factor for PD and Lewy Body Dementia. Linked to Gaucher s Disease Ashkenazi Jews .\\n LRRK2 Common cause of familial PD Ashkenazi Jews North African Berbers . Slower progression.\\n Parkin Early-onset PD <40 yo . Autosomal Recessive.\\n Environmental \\n Risks Pesticides Paraquat Rotenone Well water Head trauma.\\n Protective Smoking Caffeine.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. Atypical Parkinsonism Parkinson-Plus Syndromes \\n Story Mode \\nThese look like Parkinson s but \\ Something is Wrong.\\ They don t respond well to Levodopa progress faster and have unique \\ Red Flags.\\ \\n\\n 2.1 Multiple System Atrophy MSA \\n Pathology Alpha-Synucleinopathy Glial Cytoplasmic Inclusions .\\n Key Features Parkinsonism +\\n 1. Autonomic Dysfunction Dysautonomia Severe Orthostatic Hypotension fainting when standing Urinary Incontinence/Retention Erectile Dysfunction.\\n 2. Cerebellar Signs Ataxia.\\n Clinical Pearl Inspiratory Stridor.\\n\\n 2.2 Progressive Supranuclear Palsy PSP \\n Pathology Tauopathy Tau protein accumulation .\\n Key Features \\n Vertical Gaze Palsy Cannot look Down trouble reading going down stairs .\\n Early Falls Often falling Backwards .\\n Pseudobulbar Palsy Dysphagia dysarthria \\ growling speech\\ .\\n\\n 2.3 Corticobasal Degeneration CBD \\n Pathology Tauopathy.\\n Key Features \\n Markedly Asymmetric symptoms.\\n Alien Limb Phenomenon One hand acts on its own e.g. unbuttoning shirt while other buttons it .\\n Apraxia Inability to perform complex motor tasks despite motor strength.\\n\\n 2.4 Dementia with Lewy Bodies DLB \\n Pathology Alpha-Synucleinopathy Cortical Lewy Bodies .\\n Key Features \\n Dementia within 1 year of motor symptoms or before .\\n Visual Hallucinations Early vivid often children or animals.\\n Fluctuating Cognition \\ Good days and bad days.\\ \\n Severe Neuroleptic Sensitivity Antipsychotics cause severe worsening of parkinsonism/death.\\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. Pharmacology of Parkinson s\\n Strategy Increase Dopamine Levodopa Agonists MAO/COMT inhibitors or Decrease Acetylcholine Anticholinergics .\\n\\n 3.1 Levodopa / Carbidopa\\n Mechanism \\n Levodopa Precursor to Dopamine. Crosses Blood-Brain Barrier Dopamine cannot .\\n Carbidopa DOPA Decarboxylase Inhibitor . Does NOT cross BBB. Prevents peripheral conversion of L-DOPA $\\\\rightarrow$ Dopamine reduces systemic side effects like nausea/arrhythmias .\\n Use Gold Standard. Best for motor symptoms bradykinesia/rigidity .\\n Issues \\n \\ Wearing Off\\ Effects last shorter over time.\\n Dyskinesias Chorea-like movements at peak dose.\\n On-Off Phenomenon Unpredictable fluctuations.\\n\\n 3.2 Dopamine Agonists Pramipexole Ropinirole Rotigotine \\n Mechanism Directly stimulate Dopamine receptors D2/D3 .\\n Side Effects \\n Impulse Control Disorders Pathological Gambling Hypersexuality Excessive Spending.\\n Sleep Attacks Sudden onset of sleep danger while driving .\\n Hallucinations.\\n\\n 3.3 Enzyme Inhibitors\\n MAO-B Inhibitors Selegiline Rasagiline \\n Inhibit breakdown of Dopamine in the brain.\\n Selegiline Metabolized to Amphetamine $\\\\rightarrow$ Insomnia don t take at night .\\n COMT Inhibitors Entacapone Opicapone \\n Inhibit peripheral breakdown of Levodopa methyl-transferase .\\n Must be given with Levodopa .\\n Side Effect Orange/Dark Urine .\\n\\n 3.4 Amantadine\\n Mechanism Antiviral. NMDA Antagonist + Increases Dopamine release.\\n Use Treating Levodopa-induced Dyskinesias .\\n Side Effect Livedo Reticularis Lacy purplish discoloration of skin on legs .\\n\\n 3.5 Anticholinergics Trihexyphenidyl Benztropine \\n Mechanism Restores Dopamine/ACh balance.\\n Use Predominantly for Tremor in younger patients \\ Shake & Bake\\ .\\n Side Effects \\ Anti-SLUDGE\\ Dry mouth urinary retention constipation confusion . Avoid in Elderly .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 4. High Yield Renders\\n\\n highyield\\n Parkinsonism Differential Diagnosis \\n| Condition | Key Clinical Feature The \\ Red Flag\\ | Pathology |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Parkinson s Disease | Asymmetric Resting Tremor Good L-DOPA response | Synuclein Lewy Body |\\n| MSA | Autonomic Failure Orthostasis ED + Cerebellar signs | Synuclein Glial Inclusion |\\n| PSP | Vertical Gaze Palsy Can t look down + Early Falls | Tau |\\n| Lewy Body Dementia | Visual Hallucinations + Dementia early <1 yr | Synuclein Cortical Lewy Body |\\n| Corticobasal CBD | Alien Limb + Apraxia + Severe Asymmetry | Tau |\\n \\n\\n highyield\\n Parkinson s Pharmacology Buzzwords \\n| Drug Class | Examples | Buzzword Side Effect |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Dopamine Agonists | Pramipexole Ropinirole | Gambling / Hypersexuality Sleep Attacks |\\n| Amantadine | -- | Livedo Reticularis Lacy skin rash |\\n| COMT Inhibitors | Entacapone | Orange Urine |\\n| MAO-B Inhibitors | Selegiline | Metabolized to Amphetamine Insomnia |\\n| Levodopa | -- | Dyskinesia Wriggling movements |\\n \\n questions A 68-year-old male presents with a resting tremor in his right hand that improves with movement. He also has a shuffling gait and decreased facial expression. Which of the following pathological findings is most likely present in his brain? Neurofibrillary tangles in the Hippocampus Caudate Nucleus atrophy Loss of pigmented neurons in the Substantia Nigra Spongiform change in the Cortex Demyelination of the Periventricular White Matter 2 This characteristic presentation of Parkinson s Disease TRAP Tremor Rigidity Akinesia Postural Instability is caused by the loss of Dopaminergic neurons in the Substantia Nigra Pars Compacta . A 72-year-old male with Parkinson s disease is started on a new medication. Two months later his wife reports that he has gambled away their life savings and has become hypersexual. Which class of medication is most likely responsible for these behaviors? MAO-B Inhibitor Selegiline Dopamine Agonist Pramipexole COMT Inhibitor Entacapone Anticholinergic Benztropine Amantadine 1 Dopamine Agonists like Pramipexole and Ropinirole are known to cause Impulse Control Disorders such as pathological gambling hypersexuality and excessive shopping due to their stimulation of the mesolimbic reward pathway. A 65-year-old female presents with frequent falls often falling backwards. On exam she has difficulty looking down vertical gaze palsy and her speech is slurred. She has not responded well to Levodopa. What is the most likely diagnosis? Idiopathic Parkinson s Disease Multiple System Atrophy MSA Progressive Supranuclear Palsy PSP Corticobasal Degeneration CBD Dementia with Lewy Bodies DLB 2 This is Progressive Supranuclear Palsy PSP . The classic triad is Vertical Gaze Palsy trouble looking down early Falls especially backwards and Pseudobulbar palsy. It is a Tauopathy. Levodopa is almost always administered with Carbidopa. What is the primary function of Carbidopa? It increases the breakdown of Levodopa in the brain It facilitates transport of Levodopa across the Blood-Brain Barrier It inhibits DOPA decarboxylase in the periphery It directly stimulates dopamine receptors It prevents the reuptake of dopamine 2 Carbidopa is a peripheral DOPA decarboxylase inhibitor . It cannot cross the BBB so it inhibits the conversion of Levodopa to Dopamine in the periphery increasing the amount of Levodopa that reaches the brain and reducing systemic side effects. A 45-year-old Ashkenazi Jewish male presents with symptoms of Parkinson s disease. He mentions that his cousin had Gaucher s disease. Which genetic mutation is most likely involved? SNCA Alpha-Synuclein LRRK2 GBA Glucocerebrosidase Parkin PINK1 2 Mutations in the GBA gene which encodes Glucocerebrosidase are the most common genetic risk factor for Parkinson s disease especially in the Ashkenazi Jewish population and are linked to Gaucher s Disease . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l58", "title": "Lecture 58: Pharmacology: Parkinson and Alzheimer", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l58 module Neuro l58 Lecture 58 Pharmacology Parkinson and Alzheimer Neurology Lecture 58 Pharmacology Parkinson and Alzheimer\\n\\n Lecturer M. Pino Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 58\\n\\n 1. Parkinson s Disease PD Pharmacology\\n Goal Restore Dopamine DA levels or reduce Acetylcholine ACh activity to fix the DA/ACh imbalance.\\n\\n 1.1 Levodopa / Carbidopa Sinemet \\n Mechanism \\n Levodopa L-Dopa Precursor to Dopamine. Crosses the Blood-Brain Barrier BBB via neutral amino acid transporter. Converted to DA by Dopa Decarboxylase DDC .\\n Carbidopa Inhibits Peripheral DDC . Does NOT cross BBB.\\n Why Combine? Without Carbidopa only 1-3% of L-Dopa enters the brain most converted peripherally $\\\\rightarrow$ side effects . With Carbidopa 10% enters brain.\\n Adverse Effects \\n Peripheral Nausea/Vomiting CTZ stimulation Orthostatic Hypotension Cardiac Arrhythmias.\\n Central Dyskinesias writhing movements after long-term use Psychosis Hallucinations/Delusions .\\n Drug Interactions \\n High Protein Meals Amino acids compete for transporter $\\\\rightarrow$ Decrease efficacy.\\n Vitamin B6 Pyridoxine Increases peripheral breakdown of L-Dopa Avoid high doses .\\n\\n 1.2 COMT Inhibitors \\ -capone\\ \\n Mechanism Inhibit Catechol-O-Methyltransferase COMT blocking conversion of L-Dopa to 3-O-Methyldopa 3-OMD . Prolongs L-Dopa half-life \\ On\\ time .\\n Agents \\n Entacapone Peripheral inhibition only.\\n Tolcapone Central & Peripheral. Black Box Warning Hepatotoxicity Monitor LFTs .\\n Opicapone Long-acting Once daily .\\n Side Effect Orange/Brown discoloration of urine .\\n\\n 1.3 MAO-B Inhibitors \\ -giline\\ \\n Mechanism Inhibit Monoamine Oxidase B MAO-B preventing central breakdown of Dopamine.\\n Agents \\n Selegiline Metabolized to Amphetamine/Methamphetamine $\\\\rightarrow$ Insomnia Do not take late at night .\\n Rasagiline Irreversible inhibitor.\\n Safinamide Reversible MAO-B inhibitor + Glutamate modulation.\\n Neuroprotection May prevent MPTP conversion to toxic MPP+ preventing mitochondrial damage .\\n Interactions Risk of Serotonin Syndrome if combined with SSRIs/TCAs though risk is lower with selective MAO-B vs MAO-A .\\n\\n 1.4 Dopamine Agonists\\n Mechanism Directly stimulate Post-synaptic Dopamine Receptors D2/D3 . No conversion needed.\\n Agents \\n Pramipexole Mirapex D3 > D2. Antioxidant properties. Also for Restless Leg Syndrome RLS .\\n Ropinirole Requip D2/D3. Also for RLS.\\n Rotigotine Transdermal Patch stable levels .\\n Adverse Effects \\n Impulse Control Disorders Pathological Gambling Hypersexuality Shopping.\\n Sleep Attacks Sudden onset of sleep without warning.\\n\\n 1.5 Rescue Therapy Apomorphine\\n Use Acute subcutaneous injection for severe \\ Off\\ episodes Freezing .\\n Adverse Effect Severe Nausea/Vomiting.\\n Management Pre-treat with Trimethobenzamide .\\n Contraindication Do NOT use 5-HT3 Antagonists Ondansetron/Zofran $\\\\rightarrow$ Causes profound Hypotension and Syncope.\\n\\n 1.6 Amantadine\\n Mechanism Antiviral Flu A - M2 channel blocker . In PD NMDA Receptor Antagonist + increases DA release.\\n Use Treating Levodopa-induced Dyskinesias .\\n Side Effect Livedo Reticularis Purplish net-like skin rash .\\n\\n 1.7 Adenosine Antagonist Istradefylline\\n Mechanism Adenosine A2A receptor antagonist in Basal Ganglia. Reduces \\ Off\\ time.\\n Side Effects Insomnia Structurally related to Caffeine .\\n\\n 1.8 Anticholinergics Benztropine Trihexyphenidyl \\n Use Tremor predominant PD.\\n Adverse Effect Antimuscarinic Dry mouth urinary retention confusion . Avoid in Geriatrics Beers List .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 2. Alzheimer s Disease AD Pharmacology\\n Goal Increase Acetylcholine ACh or reduce Glutamate Excitotoxicity.\\n\\n 2.1 Acetylcholinesterase Inhibitors AChEIs \\n Mechanism Inhibit breakdown of ACh in the synaptic cleft.\\n Agents \\n Donepezil Aricept . \\n Rivastigmine Inhibits AChE and Butyrylcholinesterase. Patch available.\\n Galantamine Also acts as a nicotinic receptor agonist.\\n Adverse Effects Cholinergic Excess SLUDGE .\\n Nausea Diarrhea Vomiting.\\n Bradycardia Risk of syncope Caution with Beta-blockers .\\n Bronchoconstriction Caution in asthma/COPD .\\n\\n 2.2 NMDA Receptor Antagonist\\n Agent Memantine .\\n Mechanism Uncompetitive NMDA Antagonist . Blocks tonic/excessive glutamate stimulation excitotoxicity while allowing physiologic signals.\\n Use Moderate to Severe AD. Can be combined with AChEIs.\\n\\n 2.3 Anti-Amyloid Monoclonal Antibodies\\n Mechanism Bind and clear Amyloid Beta plaques.\\n Agents \\n Lecanemab Targets Protofibrils.\\n Donanemab. \\n Adverse Effects ARIA Amyloid-Related Imaging Abnormalities .\\n ARIA-E Edema/Effusion.\\n ARIA-H Micro-Hemorrhage/Hemosiderin.\\n Monitoring Requires serial MRIs .\\n\\n---\\n\\n 3. High Yield Renders\\n\\n highyield\\n Parkinson s Drug Toxicities & Pearls \\n| Drug | Key Mechanism/Fact | The \\ Board Exam\\ Side Effect |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Carbidopa | Peripheral DDC Inhibitor | Reduces Peripheral side effects Nausea/Ortho |\\n| Tolcapone | COMT Inhibitor Central/Periph | Hepatotoxicity Monitor LFTs |\\n| Entacapone | COMT Inhibitor Peripheral | Orange Urine |\\n| Selegiline | MAO-B Inhibitor | Metabolized to Amphetamine Insomnia |\\n| Dopamine Agonists | Pramipexole Ropinirole | Impulse Control Gambling Sleep Attacks |\\n| Apomorphine | \\ Rescue\\ Injection | Severe Nausea NO Zofran! $\\\\rightarrow$ Hypotension |\\n| Amantadine | NMDA Antagonist | Livedo Reticularis Lacy rash |\\n \\n\\n highyield\\n Alzheimer s Drug Toxicities \\n| Class | Agents | Key Side Effects |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| AChE Inhibitors | Donepezil Rivastigmine Galantamine | Bradycardia Falls/Syncope Diarrhea Nausea |\\n| NMDA Antagonist | Memantine | Dizziness Confusion Use in Mod-Severe AD |\\n| Monoclonals | Lecanemab Donanemab | ARIA Edema/Hemorrhage on MRI |\\n \\n questions A 75-year-old male with Parkinson s disease is started on Selegiline as an adjunct to his regimen. He complains of difficulty falling asleep. This side effect is most likely due to the metabolism of the drug into which substance? Dopamine Norepinephrine Amphetamine Serotonin Histamine 2 Selegiline an MAO-B inhibitor is metabolized into l-methamphetamine and l-amphetamine which have stimulant properties and can cause insomnia . It should be taken earlier in the day. A patient with Parkinson s disease experiences severe \\ off\\ episodes where he freezes and cannot move. His physician prescribes a subcutaneous rescue medication. Which antiemetic is CONTRAINDICATED in this patient due to the risk of profound hypotension? Trimethobenzamide Ondansetron Promethazine Prochlorperazine Metoclopramide 1 The rescue medication is likely Apomorphine . It causes severe nausea but it is CONTRAINDICATED to use 5-HT3 antagonists like Ondansetron Zofran because the combination causes severe hypotension and loss of consciousness. Trimethobenzamide is the preferred antiemetic. A 68-year-old female is prescribed Entacapone to help manage her motor fluctuations. She calls the clinic in a panic because her urine has changed color. What color is her urine likely to be? Blue Red Orange/Brown Green Black 2 Entacapone a COMT inhibitor is known to cause a harmless orange or brownish discoloration of the urine . A patient with Alzheimer s disease is started on Donepezil. During a follow-up visit his heart rate is noted to be 48 bpm. This bradycardia is a result of Blockade of Nicotinic receptors Increased Acetylcholine activity at the SA node Inhibition of Calcium channels Stimulation of Beta-1 receptors Blockade of Muscarinic receptors 1 Donepezil is an Acetylcholinesterase Inhibitor which increases levels of Acetylcholine. Excess ACh acts on M2 muscarinic receptors in the heart parasympathetic activation leading to Bradycardia which can increase the risk of syncope and falls. A patient with early Alzheimer s disease is started on Lecanemab. Two months later a surveillance MRI is performed. It shows areas of edema ARIA-E . What is the mechanism of action of this drug? Uncompetitive NMDA receptor antagonist Acetylcholinesterase inhibitor Monoclonal antibody against Amyloid Beta Muscarinic receptor agonist Inhibitor of Tau phosphorylation 2 Lecanemab is a monoclonal antibody that targets Amyloid Beta protofibrils. A known adverse effect of this class is Amyloid Related Imaging Abnormalities ARIA which can present as edema ARIA-E or hemorrhage ARIA-H . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l59", "title": "Lecture #59: History and Physical Exam of the Aging Patient", "module": "Clinical Medicine", "content": "id l59 title Lecture 59 History and Physical Exam of the Aging Patient lecturer P. Ryo D.O. module Clinical Medicine session Lecture 59 prev l58 next pdf pdfs/Lecture 59. P. Ryo D.O. PPT - with notes .pdf transcript transcripts/Lecture 59 DPR History and Physical Exam of the Aging Patient Faculty P. Ryo D.O..srt summary Lecture 59 History and Physical Exam of the Aging Patient Lecturer P. Ryo D.O. Session Lecture 59 The Aging Population and Demographics Slide 2-5 The global population is undergoing a significant demographic shift with the number of older adults increasing rapidly. By 2050 the U.S. population over 65 is expected to nearly double. This epidemiologic transition means that non-communicable chronic diseases are replacing infectious diseases as the primary burden of illness. Because of this shift there is a concomitant change in the dependency ratio —the ratio of those in the workforce to those depending on it children retirees . As the population ages the demand for medical care social services and pension systems increases often straining available resources. General Principles of Aging Slide 6 Geriatric medicine focuses on care for those >65 years old with Oldest Old being >80 . There are four fundamental principles that govern the physiology and clinical presentation of the aging patient 1. Homeostatic Decline Organ reserves decrease making patients less able to tolerate stress illness injury . 2. Multifactorial Comorbidities Patients rarely have just one problem; conditions interact. 3. Polypharmacy Multiple medications increase the risk of adverse drug events and interactions. 4. Atypical Presentation Disease often presents non-specifically e.g. confusion instead of fever/pain . Physiological Changes of Aging Slide 7-12 Aging affects every organ system leading to a progressive decline in functional reserve. This homeostenosis means that while resting function may be preserved the body s ability to respond to maximal demand is reduced. Cardiovascular System - Vascular Stiffening The aorta and arteries become rigid arteriosclerosis leading to increased systolic blood pressure and widened pulse pressure. - Cardiac Function Maximal heart rate declines due to loss of pacemaker cells. There is a decreased response to beta-adrenergic stimulation. - Structural Changes Left ventricular hypertrophy mild can occur to compensate for increased afterload. - Clinical Finding An S4 heart sound is a common and often normal finding in healthy elderly adults due to decreased ventricular compliance atrial kick pushing against a stiff ventricle . Respiratory System - Mechanical Changes Chest wall stiffening and kyphosis reduce compliance. - Lung Parenchyma Loss of elastic recoil causes air trapping and increased residual volume. Alveolar surface area decreases reducing gas exchange capacity. - Protective Mechanisms Cough reflex is blunted increasing the risk of aspiration and pneumonia. correlate Atelectasis & Pneumonia - Pathophysiology Decreased lung elasticity weaker respiratory muscles and kyphosis lead to poor aeration. Blunted cough reflex enables aspiration. - Presentation Fever may be absent. Tachypnea or confusion may be the only signs. - Risk Factors Immobility hospital admission dysphagia. - Lecturer s Point Because physiologic reserves are low a respiratory infection can rapidly lead to failure. Gastrointestinal System - Sensation Blunted peritoneal signs. A patient with an acute abdomen e.g. appendicitis diverticulitis may have minimal guarding or rebound tenderness. - Motility generally slows leading to constipation. - Oral Health Gingival recession and decreased saliva xerostomia increase the risk of caries and periodontal disease affecting nutrition. Renal and Genitourinary System - Renal Function GFR declines with age. Drug clearance is reduced necessitating dose adjustments. - Bladder Capacity decreases and residual volume increases. Involuntary bladder contractions become more common. - Men Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia BPH is very common causing obstruction. - Women Atrophic vaginitis due to estrogen loss causes dryness dyspareunia and increased urinary tract infection risk. correlate Urinary Incontinence - Pathophysiology Multifactorial—bladder modification detrusor instability pelvic floor weakness BPH men or functional inability to reach the toilet. - Presentation Involuntary leakage Stress Urge Overflow or Functional . - Lecturer s Point Incontinence is NOT a normal part of aging; it is a common but pathologic condition that should be treated to prevent social isolation. Nervous System - Sensory Decreased vibratory sense and proprioception increasing fall risk . - Reflexes Deep tendon reflexes may be diminished especially at the ankles. - Thermoregulation Susceptibility to hypothermia increases. Fever response to infection is blunted. correlate Presbycusis - Pathophysiology Sensorineural hearing loss due to loss of hair cells in the cochlea. - Presentation Bilateral symmetric high-frequency hearing loss. Difficulty hearing speech in noisy environments. - Social Impact Can lead to depression and social withdrawal if untreated. Approach to the Geriatric Patient Slide 13-17 The Office Environment The physical setting should be adapted to minimize barriers. - Lighting Bright glare-free lighting accommodates presbyopia and cataracts. - Access Remove clutter fall hazards . Use chairs with arms to assist in standing. - Temperature Keep rooms warm as elderly patients are sensitive to cold. Communication Strategies - Pace Slow down. Allow time for the patient to process questions and respond. - Sensory Aids Ensure the patient is wearing their glasses and hearing aids. - Position Sit at eye level facing the patient directly to allow for lip-reading. - Health Literacy Use large font for printed instructions. Avoid medical jargon. Functional Assessment Slide 18-20 Functional status is the vital sign of geriatrics. It is a stronger predictor of mortality and quality of life than specific disease diagnoses. Activities of Daily Living ADLs These are basic self-care tasks required for survival. DEATH is a common mnemonic Dressing Eating Ambulating/Transfer Toileting Hygiene/Bathing . Transcript items Bathing Dressing Toileting Transferring Continence Feeding. Instrumental Activities of Daily Living IADLs These are more complex skills needed for independent living in the community. SHAFT is a mnemonic Shopping Housekeeping Accounting Food prep Telephone . Transcript items Telephone Shopping Food preparation Housekeeping Laundry Transportation Medications Finances. Significance IADL impairment often precedes ADL impairment and is an early sign of cognitive decline e.g. trouble managing finances . highyield The 10-Minute Screener A rapid validated tool to assess key geriatric domains 1. Vision Ask about difficulty; objective test Snellen . 2. Hearing Whisper test or audiometry. 3. Mobility Timed Up and Go TUG test. 4. Urinary Incontinence In the last year have you lost your urine and gotten wet? 5. Nutrition Weight loss >10lbs in 6 months? 6. Memory Three-item recall. 7. Depression Do you often feel sad or depressed? Medication Management Slide 21-22 The Beers Criteria A list of Potentially Inappropriate Medications PIMs in older adults published by the American Geriatrics Society AGS . - Goal Reduce adverse drug events falls delirium GI bleeds . - Common Culprits Benzodiazepines falls/confusion Anticholinergics confusion/constipation NSAIDs GI bleed/renal injury PPIs C. diff/osteoporosis . - Nuance It is a guideline not a law. Sometimes a medication is necessary despite being on the list but it requires careful documentation and monitoring. Polypharmacy Defined as using multiple drugs typically >5 . - Adverse Risk Increases exponentially with the number of drugs. - Review Brown Bag review bring all pill bottles to every visit is the gold standard for reconciliation. Cognitive Screening Slide 23-26 Cognitive decline is common but often goes unreported. Early detection allows for planning and safety interventions. | Test | Description | Pros | Cons | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | MMSE Folstein | 30-point scale. Tests orientation registration attention recall language. | Widely known. Score <24 suggests impairment. | Copyrighted. Education/Language bias. Less sensitive for Mild Cognitive Impairment MCI . | | MoCA Montreal | 30-point scale. Tests executive function visuospatial naming memory. | Excellent sensitivity for Mild Cognitive Impairment. | Takes longer ~10-15 min . Requires training. | | Mini-Cog | 1. Three-word registration<br>2. Clock Drawing Test<br>3. Three-word recall | Very fast <3 min . High sensitivity for dementia. | Less detailed than MoCA. | correlate Dementia vs. Delirium - Dementia Chronic progressive decline in cognition months to years . Attention is usually intact until late stages. - Delirium Acute onset hours to days fluctuating course inattention hallmark . Usually reversible if underlying cause UTI meds electrolyte imbalance is treated. - Lecturer s Point The Mini-Cog is a great quick screen for baseline deficits but always assess for acute changes delirium first. Fall Risk and Mobility Slide 27 Falls are the leading cause of fatal and non-fatal injuries in older adults. - Risk Factors Polypharmacy >4 meds environmental hazards rugs poor lighting lower extremity weakness sensory impairment. - Assessment Tool Timed Up and Go TUG . - Patient starts sitting stands walks 3 meters 10 ft turns walks back and sits. - Normal < 10 seconds. - Abnormal High Risk > 20 seconds Transcript references >20s as abnormal/cutoff for high risk . Health Maintenance and Screening Slide 28-35 Decisions for screening should be individualized based on life expectancy >10 years rule and functional status rather than chronological age alone. Cancer Screening - Breast Mammography every 1-2 years until age 75. Discuss pros/cons after 75. - Colorectal Colonoscopy/FIT testing until age 75. Decision 76-85 is individual. Stop >85. - Prostate PSA screening is controversial; shared decision making based on risk/benefit. - Lung Low-dose CT for high-risk smokers 50-80 yo 20 pak-year history current or quit <15y ago . Immunizations - Influenza Annual High-dose preferred for >65 . - Pneumococcal PCV15/20 followed by PPSV23 if 15 used . - Zoster Shingles Shingrix 2 doses . Recommended for all >50. - Tdap Tetanus booster every 10 years. - COVID-19 Follow current CDC guidelines. Osteoporosis Screening - Tool DEXA Scan Dual-Energy X-ray Absorptiometry . - Indication - All Women ≥ 65 years . - Men ≥ 70 years . - Younger postmenopausal women or men 50-69 with risk factors fragility fracture steroids RA smoking alcohol . - Diagnosis T-Score - Normal ≥ -1.0 - Osteopenia -1.0 to -2.5 - Osteoporosis ≤ -2.5 Treatment indicated - FRAX Score Calculates 10-year fracture risk. Use to decide treatment in patients with Osteopenia Treat if Hip Fracture risk ≥3% or Major Osteoporotic Fracture risk ≥20% . highyield Elder Abuse & Neglect - Types Physical Sexual Emotional Financial Neglect including Self-Neglect . - Perpetrators 90% are family members. - Screening No single tool but ask direct questions Do you feel safe at home? Is anyone hurting you? - Action Mandatory reporting to Adult Protective Services APS if there is suspicion . You do not need proof. questions question 1. A 75-year-old male presents with a history of falls. The Timed Up and Go TUG test is performed and the patient takes 16 seconds to complete it. What is the clinical interpretation? options Normal mobility Low risk Indicates high risk of falls > 12 seconds Indicates severe dementia Indicates need for immediate hospitalization Indicates orthostatic hypotension correctAnswer 1 rationale A TUG score > 12 seconds indicates an increased risk of falling. Scores > 20 seconds indicate high risk. Under 10 seconds is considered normal. question 2. Which of the following is an example of an Instrumental Activity of Daily Living IADL ? options Bathing Dressing Managing Finances Toileting Feeding correctAnswer 2 rationale Managing finances is an IADL requires higher cognitive function . Bathing Dressing Toileting and Feeding are basic ADLs DEATH mnemonic . question 3. According to the Beers Criteria which class of medications should be avoided in the elderly due to the risk of confusion urinary retention and constipation? options ACE Inhibitors Anticholinergics e.g. Diphenhydramine Statins Calcium Channel Blockers SSRIs correctAnswer 1 rationale Anticholinergics like Benadryl/Diphenhydramine have strong side effects in the elderly confusion retention constipation dry mouth and are on the Beers Criteria Do Not Use list. question 4. What is the primary difference between Dementia and Delirium? options Dementia is acute; Delirium is chronic Dementia affects attention first; Delirium affects memory first Delirium has an acute onset and fluctuating course; Dementia is chronic and progressive Delirium is irreversible; Dementia is reversible There is no difference correctAnswer 2 rationale Delirium is acute fluctuating and affects attention/consciousness often reversible . Dementia is chronic progressive and initially affects memory consciousness is intact until late stages . question 5. A 67-year-old female has a DEXA scan T-score of -2.7. What is the diagnosis? options Normal Bone Density Osteopenia Osteoporosis Severe Osteoporosis with fracture Osteomalacia correctAnswer 2 rationale A T-score < -2.5 defines Osteoporosis . Osteopenia is between -1.0 and -2.5. Normal is > -1.0. flashcards What is the cutoff score for a positive Mini-Cog? < 3/5 Recall < 3 or Abnormal Clock Diagnosis Which screening tool is best for Mild Cognitive Impairment MCI ? MoCA Montreal Cognitive Assessment Diagnosis Which vaccines are recommended specifically for adults > 65? High-dose Flu Pneumococcal PCV20 or PCV15+PPSV23 Zoster Shingrix Prevention What condition presents with high-frequency hearing loss in the elderly? Presbycusis Clinical What is the FRAX score used for? Calculating 10-year fracture risk to guide treatment in Osteopenia Management What is the most common cause of elder abuse? Family members 90% Clinical ankingResource BandB Geriatrics > General FirstAid Public Health Sciences > Geriatrics SketchyPath Bone > Osteoporosis pearls S4 in Elderly An S4 heart sound is often a normal finding in the elderly due to decreased ventricular compliance stiff ventricle . Atypical Presentation Geriatric patients often present with confusion or falls for serious conditions like pneumonia UTI or MI rather than typical fever or chest pain. IADLs vs ADLs IADL impairment Managing finances driving often precedes ADL impairment Bathing Eating . IADLs require higher-level executive function. Osteoporosis Cutoffs Normal > -1.0\\nOsteopenia -1.0 to -2.5\\nOsteoporosis < -2.5 Fall Risk Cutoff A Timed Up and Go TUG test > 12 seconds indicates fall risk >20s is high risk . mindmap The Aging Patient Physiology of Aging - Cardiovascular Arterial stiffness -> Increased SBP S4 heart sound - Respiratory Decreased compliance blunted cough reflex Pneumonia risk - Renal Decreased GFR decreased drug clearance - Sensory Presbycusis High freq loss Presbyopia Cataracts Functional Assessment - ADLs DEATH Dressing Eating Ambulating Toileting Hygiene - IADLs SHAFT Shopping Housekeeping Accounting Food prep Telephone - Fall Risk TUG Test >12s abnormal Screening & Prevention - Cognition MMSE Mini-Cog MoCA Best for MCI - Osteoporosis DEXA Women >65 T-score < -2.5 - Vaccines Flu Pneumococcal Shingles >50 Tdap - Meds Beers Criteria Avoid Anticholinergics Benzos anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l60", "title": "Lecture #60: Sacral MET (Part 2)", "module": "OMM", "content": "id l60 title Lecture 60 Sacral MET Part 2 lecturer Deb Whitson D.O. module OMM session Lecture 60 prev l59 next l61 pdf pdfs/Lecture 60. D. Whitson D.O. PPT .pdf transcript summary Lecture 60 Sacral MET Part 2 Lecturer Deb Whitson D.O. Session Lecture 60 Introduction and Objectives This lecture serves as a preparation for the OMM Lab on Sacral Muscle Energy Treatment MET specifically focusing on Unilateral and Bilateral sacral dysfunctions. It builds upon previous sacral mechanics concepts. The core goal is to differentiate between Physiologic Flexion and Non-Physiologic Extension dysfunctions using static landmarks Sulci ILAs and dynamic testing Spring Sphinx . --- Sacral Diagnosis Review Slide 2-3 Physiologic Motion Nutated Sacral Flexion The sacral base moves anteriorly. Respiratory Occurs during Inhalation . Associated Dysfunctions Unilateral Flexion Bilateral Flexion Forward Torsions. Non-Physiologic Motion Counternutated Sacral Extension The sacral base moves posteriorly. Respiratory Occurs during Exhalation . Associated Dysfunctions Unilateral Extension Bilateral Extension Backward Torsions. --- Unilateral Sacral Dysfunctions Slide 4-6 These dysfunctions occur when one side of the sacrum moves more than the other but without a defined oblique axis like torsions. They are often termed Shears . 1. Unilateral Sacral Flexion Inferior Shear Mechanism One side of the sacral base moves anteriorly and gets stuck deep. Findings e.g. Left Unilateral Flexion Seated Flexion Test Positive on the Dysfunctional Side Left . Sacral Sulcus Deep on the dysfunctional side Left . ILA Posterior/Inferior on the dysfunctional side Left . L5 Rotated Toward the dysfunctional side Deep sulcus attracts L5 . Provocation Tests Spring Test Negative Good spring / Good Dog . Sphinx Test Negative Symmetry improves or stays same . 2. Unilateral Sacral Extension Superior Shear Mechanism One side of the sacral base moves posteriorly and gets stuck shallow. Findings e.g. Left Unilateral Extension Seated Flexion Test Positive on the Dysfunctional Side Left . Sacral Sulcus Shallow on the dysfunctional side Left . ILA Anterior/Superior on the dysfunctional side Left . L5 Rotated Away from the dysfunctional side Shallow sulcus pushes L5 away . Provocation Tests Spring Test Positive Resistance / Bad Dog . Sphinx Test Positive Asymmetry worsens . pearl Shear Rule In a Unilateral Dysfunction Shear the Deep Sulcus and Posterior ILA are on the SAME SIDE . Contrast with Torsions where they are on OPPOSITE sides . --- Bilateral Sacral Dysfunctions Slide 8-11 These involve the entire sacral base moving together Symmetric . 1. Bilateral Sacral Flexion Mechanism Both sacral bases move anteriorly Nutated and get stuck. common in post-partum or after heavy lifting. Findings Seated Flexion Test False Negative Symmetric . Sacral Sulci Deep Bilaterally. ILAs Posterior/Inferior Bilaterally. Provocation Tests Spring Test Negative . Sphinx Test Negative . 2. Bilateral Sacral Extension Mechanism Both sacral bases move posteriorly Counternutated . Extension = Exhalation = Excavation shallow . Findings Seated Flexion Test False Negative Symmetric . Sacral Sulci Shallow Bilaterally. ILAs Anterior/Superior Bilaterally. Provocation Tests Spring Test Positive . Sphinx Test Positive . --- Summary of Diagnosis Slide 13 | Dysfunction | Sulcus | ILA | Spring Test | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | Unilateral Flexion | Deep Same side | Posterior Same side | Negative - | | Unilateral Extension | Shallow Same side | Anterior Same side | Positive + | | Bilateral Flexion | Deep Bilateral | Posterior Bilateral | Negative - | | Bilateral Extension | Shallow Bilateral | Anterior Bilateral | Positive + | highyield Spring & Sphinx Rules Spring Test Tests for Extension Resistance . Positive = Stuck in Extension Bad Dog - bites/hard . Negative = Flexion allowed Good Dog - soft/springy . Sphinx Test Tests for Flexion Ability to move forward . Positive Worsens = Cannot flex Stuck in Extension . Negative Improves = Can flex Stuck in Flexion or Normal . correlate L5 Rules Fryette s Rotation L5 rotates Opposite to the Sacrum. Sacrum facing Left -> L5 rotates Right. Sidebending L5 sidebends Same side as the Oblique Axis for torsions . For shears Unilateral L5 simply follows the distinct Deep Sulcus attracts Shallow Repels rule. questions question 1. A patient presents with low back pain. Structural exam reveals a positive Seated Flexion Test on the Right. The Right Sacral Sulcus is deep and the Right ILA is posterior/inferior. The Spring test is negative. What is the diagnosis? options Right Unilateral Sacral Extension Left Unilateral Sacral Extension Right Unilateral Sacral Flexion Left Unilateral Sacral Flexion Right on Right Sacral Torsion correctAnswer 2 rationale The positive Seated Flexion Test lateralizes to the Right . Deep Sulcus + Posterior ILA on the SAME side indicates a Unilateral Shear dysfunction. Since the landmarks Deep/Posterior indicate the sacral base has moved anteriorly Flexion and the Spring test is Negative confirms Flexion it is a Right Unilateral Sacral Flexion. question 2. In a patient with a Left Unilateral Sacral Extension what are the expected findings for the L5 vertebra? options Rotated Left Rotated Right Neutral Flexed Sidebent Right correctAnswer 1 rationale In Left Unilateral Extension the Left sacral base is Shallow Posterior and the Right is relatively Deep Anterior . L5 rotates toward the Deep Sulcus Right . Therefore L5 is Rotated Right. question 3. Which of the following findings is consistent with a Bilateral Sacral Flexion? options Positive Spring Test Bilateral Shallow Sulci Bilateral Posterior/Inferior ILAs Positive Sphinx Test Bilateral Anterior/Superior ILAs correctAnswer 2 rationale Bilateral Flexion = Nutated Base Forward Apex Back . Apex Back = Posterior ILAs. Base Forward = Deep Sulci. Spring Test should be Negative. question 4. The Spring Test applies a force in which direction? options Anteriorly on the Sacral Base Posteriorly on the Sacral Apex Anteriorly on the L5 Spinous Process Superiorly on the Ischial Tuberosities Medially on the PSIS correctAnswer 0 rationale The Spring test involves applying a PA Posterior-to-Anterior force on the lumbosacral junction/sacral base to assess for resistance. question 5. Which axis is associated with Unilateral Sacral Dysfunctions Shears ? options Superior Transverse Middle Transverse Inferior Transverse Oblique Axis No Axis Non-physiologic shift correctAnswer 4 rationale Unilateral dysfunctions are often called Shears because they do not occur around a specific axis unlike torsions Oblique or physiologic flexion/extension Middle Transverse . flashcards Positive Spring Test indicates what? Sacral Extension Resistance / Bad Dog Diagnosis Negative Spring Test indicates what? Sacral Flexion Good Spring / Good Dog Diagnosis Landmarks for Unilateral Sacral Flexion Same Side ? Deep Sulcus + Posterior ILA Same Side Clinical Landmarks for Unilateral Sacral Extension Same Side ? Shallow Sulcus + Anterior ILA Same Side Clinical Seated Flexion Test lateralizes what? Sacroiliac Dysfunction Sacral side Concept Bilateral Sacral Flexion findings? Deep Sulci Bilaterally Posterior ILAs Bilaterally Negative Spring Diagnosis L5 Rotates toward which sacral landmark? The Deep Sulcus Concept ankingResource BandB Osteopathic Medicine > Sacrum FirstAid OMM > Sacrum Savarese Sacrum pearls Spring Test Mnemonic Springing is fun Flexion -> Negative is Flexion. Resistance is hard -> Positive is Extension. Shear Identification If the Deep Sulcus and Posterior ILA are on the SAME side it s a Shear Unilateral . If they are on OPPOSITE sides it s a Torsion. mindmap Sacral Dysfunctions Part 2 Unilateral Shears Unilateral Flexion Inferior Sulcus Deep Same side ILA Posterior Same side Tests Spring - Sphinx - Unilateral Extension Superior Sulcus Shallow Same side ILA Anterior Same side Tests Spring + Sphinx + Bilateral Symmetric Bilateral Flexion Sulci Deep Bilaterally ILAs Posterior Bilaterally Tests Spring - Bilateral Extension Sulci Shallow Bilaterally ILAs Anterior Bilaterally Tests Spring + anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l61", "title": "Lecture #61: Gait and Movement", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l61 module Neuro l61 Lecture 61 Gait and Movement lecturer Adena Leder D.O. Neurology session Lecture 61 prev l60 next l62 pdf pdfs/Lecture 61. A. Leder D.O. PPT - updated 1.29.26 .pdf highYieldPdf content/L61 HighYield Render.pdf transcript Lecture 61 Gait and Movement\\n \\n Lecturer Adena Leder D.O.\\n Session Lecture 61\\n\\n Introduction to Movement Disorders Slide 2 \\n\\nMovement disorders represent a complex interplay between the basal ganglia cerebellum and motor cortex. The approach to a patient with a suspected movement disorder such as Parkinson s Disease PD requires a systematic evaluation of their history observation \\ Look\\ passive tone \\ Feel\\ and active strength/gait \\ Move\\ . Understanding the distinction between hyperkinetic and hypokinetic states is fundamental to narrowing the differential diagnosis.\\n\\n Core Assessement Objectives\\n- History Establish onset progression and risk factors.\\n- Observation \\ Look\\ for tremors posture and involuntary movements.\\n- Tone \\ Feel\\ for rigidity lead-pipe vs. cogwheel .\\n- Function \\ Assess\\ strength and observed gait mechanics.\\n\\n History Taking The Foundations Slide 3-8 \\n\\nA detailed history is the cornerstone of diagnosis as many movement disorders are clinical diagnoses without definitive biomarkers.\\n\\n Demographics and Onset Slide 3 \\n- Age Early vs. Late onset changes the differential.\\n- Handedness Asymmetry is a hallmark of idiopathic PD symptoms often start on the dominant side .\\n- Timeline Establishing the \\ Year 0\\ of symptom onset.\\n- Prodromal Phase Non-motor symptoms often precede motor symptoms by years e.g. loss of smell constipation .\\n\\n Medical and Medication History Slide 4-5 \\n- Secondary Parkinsonism Rule out drug-induced causes.\\n - Anti-emetics Metoclopramide Reglan used long-term.\\n - Anti-psychotics Dopamine blockers Haloperidol Risperidone .\\n - Other Offenders Lithium and Valproic Acid Depakote can cause tremor.\\n- Comorbidities Cardiovascular disease Diabetes Cancer history.\\n\\n correlate\\nDrug-Induced Parkinsonism\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Blockade of dopamine D2 receptors in the nigrostriatal pathway.\\n- Presentation Symmetric parkinsonism unlike asymmetric idiopathic PD often without resting tremor.\\n- Diagnosis Temporal relationship with offending agent; lack of response to Levodopa.\\n- Treatment Stop the offending agent.\\n- Risk Factors Elderly high potency antipsychotics anti-emetics.\\n \\n\\n Environmental Exposures Slide 8 \\nCertain toxins are selectively toxic to dopaminergic neurons in the Substantia Nigra.\\n\\n| Exposure | Context | Mechanism/Notes |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Pesticides | Farm/Vineyard work | Rotenone Paraquat Glyphosate Roundup |\\n| Well Water | Rural living | Heavy metals/Pesticide runoff |\\n| Agent Orange | Vietnam Veterans | Increased risk of PD |\\n| MPTP | Synthetic Heroin | Acute permanent destruction of Substantia Nigra |\\n| Head Trauma | Boxers/Football | Chronic Traumatic Encephalopathy CTE overlap |\\n\\n Genetics Slide 6-7 \\nWhile most PD is sporadic idiopathic genetics play a role especially in young-onset cases.\\n- Prevalence 15% of patients have a family history.\\n- Risk Group Ashkenazi Jewish descent Higher carrier rate .\\n- Major Genes \\n - LRRK2 Common autosomal dominant.\\n - GBA1 Associated with Gaucher s carrier state.\\n - PRKN Parkin Early onset recessive.\\n\\n Review of Systems The \\ Hidden\\ Signs Slide 11-12 \\n\\nParkinson s Disease is a whole-body synucleinopathy not just a brain disease. Autonomic and sensory dysfunction often constitute the \\ Prodrome.\\ \\n\\n highyield\\n The Parkinson s Prodrome \\nSymptoms that can precede motor signs by 10-20 years \\n1. Anosmia Loss of smell.\\n2. REM Sleep Behavior Disorder RBD Acting out vivid dreams injuring bed partner due to loss of REM atonia.\\n3. Constipation Slow colonic transit.\\n4. Autonomic Dysfunction Orthostatic hypotension urinary frequency/urgency.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\nSeborrheic Dermatitis\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Dysregulation of autonomic control of sebaceous glands sebum production .\\n- Presentation \\ Greasy \\ scaly erythematous rash typically on the face brow nasolabial folds and scalp.\\n- Lecturer s Point A common dermatologic finding in PD patients.\\n \\n\\n The Physical Examination Slide 14-17 \\n\\n Cranial Nerves and face\\n- Hypomimia \\ Masked Facies\\ \\u2013 reduction in facial expressivity and blinking.\\n- Vertical Gaze Limitation suggests Progressive Supranuclear Palsy PSP an atypical parkinsonism.\\n- Glabellar Tap Persistent blinking when tapping the forehead Myerson s sign .\\n\\n Motor Tone Assessment\\nExamination of tone differentiates spasticity corticospinal from rigidity extrapyramidal .\\n- Lead-Pipe Rigidity Uniform constant resistance throughout range of motion.\\n- Cogwheel Rigidity Ratchet-like resistance Rigidity + Tremor .\\n- Asymmetry Idiopathic PD typically starts unilaterally .\\n\\n Tremor Analysis\\n- Resting Tremor \\ Pill-rolling\\ 4-6 Hz . Suppressed with movement. Classic for PD.\\n- Postural/Action Tremor Occurs when holding a pose against gravity. Classic for Essential Tremor .\\n\\n correlate\\nEssential Tremor\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Cerebellar-thalamic circuit oscillation.\\n- Presentation Bilateral symmetric action tremor hands voice head . improves with alcohol.\\n- Diagnosis Clinical. Spiral drawing test shows shaky axis.\\n- Treatment Propranolol Beta-blocker or Primidone.\\n- Differentiation PD is Resting; ET is Action.\\n \\n\\n Gait Assessment Slide 18 \\nThe \\ Get Up and Go\\ or observable gait provides high-yield diagnostic clues.\\n- Shuffling Short steps marche \\u00e0 petits pas .\\n- Fenestration Accelerating steps to keep balance.\\n- Freezing Feet feel \\ stuck to the floor \\ especially in doorways/turning.\\n- En Bloc Turning Turning like a statue/log rather than pivoting segments.\\n- Reduced Arm Swing Often the first motor sign early asymmetric .\\n\\n Diagnosis and Management Slide 19 \\n\\nDiagnosis remains primarily clinical requiring Bradykinesia plus either Rigidity or Resting Tremor.\\n\\n Diagnostic Tests\\n- DaTscan Dopamine transporter SPECT scan. Shows loss of uptake in striatum comma shape becomes a period .\\n- Levodopa Challenge Significant improvement in symptoms confirms Dopaminergic deficiency responsive in PD non-responsive in Parkinson-Plus syndromes .\\n- Skin Biopsy For Alpha-synuclein deposits.\\n\\n mnemonic\\n The \\ S\\ Signs of Parkinson s Disease \\n S-WORDS Slide 20 \\n\\n- S hake Resting Tremor \\n- S tiffness Rigidity \\n- S low Bradykinesia \\n- S tate Masked Facies \\n- S huffle Gait \\n- S mall writing Micrographia \\n- S mell loss Anosmia \\n- S leep REM behavior disorder \\n- S oft speech Hypophonia \\n- S eborrhea Dermatitis \\n- S adness Depression \\n \\n questions 1. A 62-year-old male presents with a resting tremor in his right hand and general slowness of movement. His wife notes that he has been acting out violent dreams and shouting in his sleep for the past 5 years. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? Essential Tremor Parkinson s Disease Drug-Induced Parkinsonism Huntington s Disease Vascular Dementia 1 Parkinson s Disease is characterized by the triad of resting tremor bradykinesia and rigidity. REM Sleep Behavior Disorder RBD is a strong prodromal sign that can precede motor symptoms by years. 2. Which of the following clinical findings is REQUIRED for a diagnosis of Parkinson s Disease? Resting Tremor Bradykinesia Postural Instability Cogwheel Rigidity Anosmia 1 Bradykinesia slowness of movement is the central and required feature for the diagnosis of parkinsonism. While tremor is common some patients present with the akinetic-rigid variant without tremor. 3. A 25-year-old male presents with new-onset resting tremor and rigidity. He has a history of Schizophrenia and recently started a new medication. Which mechanism explains his motor symptoms? Loss of dopaminergic neurons in the Substantia Nigra Blockade of Dopamine D2 receptors Depletion of Acetylcholine in the Striatum Cerebellar atrophy Autoimmune attack on the neuromuscular junction 1 Blockade of D2 receptors by antipsychotic medications e.g. Haloperidol causes Drug-Induced Parkinsonism . This is a functional blockade not the structural cell death seen in idiopathic PD. 4. During a gait examination a patient takes multiple small shuffling steps and turns his body as a single rigid unit. This turning pattern is best described as Ataxic turning En bloc turning Magnetic gait Scissoring gait Steppage gait 1 En bloc turning is the classic description of a PD patient turning like a statue or log requiring multiple small steps to reorient due to axial rigidity and bradykinesia. 5. Which environmental exposure is historically linked to the rapid destruction of the Substantia Nigra and acute parkinsonism? Lead paint MPTP Asbestos Silica dust Radon 1 MPTP is a neurotoxin found in synthetic heroin contaminants that selectively destroys dopaminergic neurons in the Substantia Nigra serving as a model for MPTP-induced parkinsonism. What is the cardinal motor feature required for a diagnosis of Parkinson s Disease? Bradykinesia plus Rigidity OR Tremor Diagnosis What type of tremor is characteristic of Parkinson s Disease? Resting Tremor 4-6 Hz Pill-rolling Clinical What dermatologic condition is commonly associated with Parkinson s Disease? Seborrheic Dermatitis Clinical What sleep disorder is a strong prodromal sign of synucleinopathies like PD? REM Sleep Behavior Disorder RBD Clinical What is the mechanism of Drug-Induced Parkinsonism? Dopamine D2 receptor blockade e.g. Antipsychotics Metoclopramide Pathology What genetic mutation is most common in Ashkenazi Jewish patients with PD? LRRK2 or GBA1 Genetics What physical exam finding suggests Progressive Supranuclear Palsy PSP rather than PD? Vertical Gaze Palsy Inability to look up/down Diagnosis What is En bloc turning? Turning the body as a single rigid unit/log requiring multiple steps Clinical What effect does Cigarette Smoking have on Parkinson s risk? It is associated with a DECREASED risk protective Epidemiology What differentiates Essential Tremor from Parkinson s Tremor? Essential Tremor is Action/Postural and symmetric; PD is Resting and asymmetric Diagnosis BandB Neurology > Movement Disorders Pathoma CNS > Degenerative Disorders > Parkinson Disease SketchyPath Neuro > Degenerative > Parkinson FirstAid Neurology > Pathology > Parkinson Disease The Parkinson s Prodrome Non-motor symptoms like Anosmia Constipation and REM Sleep Behavior Disorder can appear 10-20 years before the first tremor. Asymmetry is Key Idiopathic PD almost always starts asymmetrically one hand/side . Symmetric onset should raise suspicion for Drug-Induced Parkinsonism or Atypical syndromes. Tremor vs. Bradykinesia Bradykinesia is the disability-causing symptom. Tremor is the cosmetic symptom. Some patients are Akinetic-Rigid and never develop a tremor. Arm Swing Reduced arm swing on one side is often the earliest detectable motor sign of PD during gait analysis. mindmap Gait and Movement Disorders\\n \\n Parkinson s Disease TRAP \\n- T remor Resting Pill-rolling Asymmetric\\n- R igidity Cogwheel vs Lead-pipe\\n- A kinesia/Bradykinesia Slow movement Masked facies Decreased blink\\n- P ostural Instability Late finding Pull test\\n\\n Etiology\\n- Idiopathic Most common Age > 60\\n- Genetic LRRK2 GBA1 Ashkenazi Jewish \\n- Environmental Pesticides MPTP Agent Orange\\n- Drug-Induced Antipsychotics Metoclopramide D2 Blockers \\n\\n Prodromal Signs\\n- Sensory Anosmia Smell loss \\n- Sleep REM Sleep Behavior Disorder\\n- Autonomic Constipation Orthostasis\\n\\n Differential Diagnosis\\n- Essential Tremor Action tremor Improves w/ Alcohol Bilateral\\n- PSP Vertical Gaze Palsy Early Falls\\n- MSA Severe Autonomic Dysfunction anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l62", "title": "Lecture #62: Brachial Plexus Lesions and Neuropathies", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l62 module Neuro l62 Lecture 62 Brachial Plexus Lesions and Neuropathies lecturer Matthew Mihlbachler Ph.D. Neurology session Lecture 62 prev l61 next l63 pdf pdfs/Lecture 62. M. Mihlbachler Ph.D. PPT .pdf highYieldPdf content/L62 HighYield Render.pdf transcript content/L62 FullTranscript.txt Lecture 62 Brachial Plexus Lesions and Neuropathies\\n\\n Lecturer Matthew Mihlbachler Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 62\\n\\n Introduction to Peripheral Nerve Injuries Slide 2-3 \\n\\nUnderstanding peripheral nerve injuries requires a solid grasp of the brachial plexus architecture and the relationship between spinal nerves dermatomes and myotomes. A \\ nerve\\ is a bundle of axons and injury can be partial or complete. The clinical presentation\\u2014whether it s a motor deficit weakness/paralysis or sensory deficit numbness/tingling \\u2014depends entirely on the location of the lesion. We differentiate between injuries at the \\ Root/Trunk\\ level affecting multiple nerves and \\ Peripheral Nerve\\ level affecting specific muscles/skin areas .\\n\\n Key Principles\\n- Roots vs. Peripheral Nerves Lesions at the root level affect myotomes groups of muscles and dermatomes skin strip . Peripheral nerve lesions affect the specific muscles and skin supplied by that nerve.\\n- Variation Anatomical variations exist; not every patient presents with textbook symptoms.\\n- Scope This lecture focuses on classic well-documented neuropathies.\\n\\n Brachial Plexus Trunk Lesions\\n\\nInjuries to the trunks of the brachial plexus cause widespread deficits because they affect fibers destined for multiple peripheral nerves. These are often associated with traumatic traction injuries during birth or severe trauma in adults.\\n\\n Superior Trunk Lesion C5-C6 Erb-Duchenne Palsy Slide 35-40 \\n\\nExcessive separation of the neck and shoulder e.g. shoulder dystocia during birth motorcycle accident tears the C5 and C6 roots of the Superior Trunk. This devastates the muscles of the shoulder and arm supplied by these roots.\\n\\n- Nerves Affected \\n - Musculocutaneous n. Loss of Biceps Brachii Flexion Supination .\\n - Axillary n. Loss of Deltoid Abduction and Teres Minor Lateral Rotation .\\n - Suprascapular n. Loss of Supraspinatus Abduction initiation and Infraspinatus Lateral Rotation .\\n- Resulting Posture The \\ Waiter s Tip\\ position. The arm is adducted loss of deltoid/supraspinatus medially rotated loss of lateral rotators and extended/pronated loss of biceps .\\n\\n correlate\\nErb-Duchenne Palsy Erb s Palsy \\n\\n- Pathophysiology Traction injury rupturing Superior Trunk C5-C6 .\\n- Presentation \\ Waiter s Tip\\ position \\n - Adducted shoulder \\n - Medially Rotated shoulder \\n - Extended elbow \\n - Pronated forearm \\n- Sensory Loss Lateral aspect of arm and forearm C5-C6 dermatomes .\\n- Risk Factors Shoulder dystocia macrosomia motorcycle accidents.\\n \\n\\n mnemonic\\n Erb-Duchenne Palsy \\n \\ Waiter s Tip\\ \\n\\n- Imagine a waiter subtly accepting a tip behind their back Arm hangs by side rotated internally palm facing back.\\n \\n\\n Inferior Trunk Lesion C8-T1 Klumpke s Palsy Slide 43-49 \\n\\nHyper-abduction of the arm e.g. catching a tree branch while falling pulling an infant s arm during birth tears the C8 and T1 roots. This primarily affects the intrinsic muscles of the hand.\\n\\n- Nerves Affected \\n - Ulnar n. C8-T1 All intrinsic hand muscles it supplies.\\n - Median n. C8-T1 component Intrinsic hand muscles Thumbnail lateral lumbricals .\\n- Key Deficit Loss of ALL intrinsic hand muscles Lumbricals and Interossei .\\n - Function of Lumbricals Flex MCP Extend PIP/DIP.\\n - Loss Extension of MCP by unopposed extensor digitorum Flexion of PIP/DIP by unopposed flexors .\\n- Result Total Claw Hand .\\n\\n correlate\\nKlumpke s Palsy\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Traction injury rupturing Inferior Trunk C8-T1 .\\n- Presentation \\ Total Claw Hand\\ Slide 49 .\\n - Clawing Hyperextension of MCP + Flexion of PIP/DIP in ALL digits 2-5 .\\n - Atrophy Wasting of intrinsic hand muscles interossei guttering .\\n- Sensory Loss Medial aspect of arm forearm and hand C8-T1 dermatomes .\\n- Lecturer s Point Distinguish from isolated Ulnar Claw which spares digits 2-3 . Klumpke affects ALL digits because C8-T1 feeds both Ulnar and Median intrinsic muscles.\\n \\n\\n Peripheral Nerve Injuries The Scapula and Shoulder\\n\\nMoving distally isolated nerve injuries present with more specific functional losses.\\n\\n Long Thoracic Nerve C5-C7 Slide 52 \\nLesion causes paralysis of the Serratus Anterior which anchors the scapula to the thoracic wall.\\n\\n correlate\\nWinged Scapula\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Damage to Long Thoracic Nerve Saltatory/Stab wound Radical Mastectomy .\\n- Presentation Medial border of scapula protrudes posteriorly \\ wings\\ when patient pushes against a wall.\\n- Functional Deficit Inability to abduct arm above horizontal loss of scapular rotation .\\n \\n\\n Axillary Nerve C5-C6 Slide 54-55 \\nCommonly injured in Surgical Neck Fractures of the humerus or Anterior-Inferior Dislocation of the shoulder.\\n\\n highyield\\n Axillary Nerve Injury \\n- Motor Loss of Abduction >15 degrees due to Deltoid paralysis Slide 54 .\\n- Sensory Loss of sensation over the \\ Regimental Badge\\ area Lateral shoulder .\\n \\n\\n Radial Nerve Injuries Posterior Cord \\n\\nThe Radial nerve supplies the Extensors of the arm forearm and hand. Presentation depends heavily on the level of injury.\\n\\n Axilla Level \\ Saturday Night Palsy\\ Slide 57-58 \\nCompression in the axilla e.g. drunk arm over chair crutches .\\n- Deficits \\n - Triceps Loss of elbow extension often spared if gravity assists .\\n - Wrist/Fingers Loss of extension -> Wrist Drop .\\n - Sensory Posterior arm forearm and dorsum of hand.\\n\\n correlate\\nSaturday Night Palsy Radial Neuropathy at Axilla \\n\\n- Pathophysiology Compression of Radial Nerve in the axilla.\\n- Presentation Wrist Drop inability to extend wrist/fingers .\\n- Sensory Numbness on posterior arm/forearm/hand.\\n- Differentiation Triceps reflex may be diminished unlike distal lesions .\\n \\n\\n Mid-Shaft Humerus Fracture Radial Groove Slide 60-61 \\nThe radial nerve spirals around the humerus in the radial groove.\\n- Deficits \\n - Triceps SPARED Branches to triceps arise proximal to the groove. Elbow extension is intact.\\n - Wrist/Fingers Wrist Drop persists extensors of forearm are distal .\\n - Sensory Posterior Arm sensory SPARED . Numbness is limited to the dorsum of the hand.\\n\\n highyield\\n Radial Nerve Axilla vs. Spiral Groove \\n- Axilla Saturday Night Triceps weak/paralyzed + Wrist Drop + Posterior Arm Numbness.\\n- Spiral Groove Humerus Fracture Triceps SPARED + Wrist Drop + NO Posterior Arm Numbness.\\n \\n\\n Median Nerve Injuries Lateral/Medial Cords \\n\\nThe Median nerve supplies the Flexors of the forearm except FCU and ulnar half of FDP and the Thenar muscles OAF Oppose Abduct Flex thumb and Lateral 2 Lumbricals .\\n\\n Lesion at Elbow Supracondylar Fracture Slide 65-68 \\n- Motor Loss \\n - Forearm Flexors Loss of Pronation Wrist Flexion weak Digit Flexion Digits 1-3 .\\n - Hand Loss of Thenar muscles No opposition .\\n- Presentation \\n - Hand of Benediction occurs when asked to MAKE A FIST . Digits 1 2 3 cannot flex. Digits 4 5 flex Ulnar nerve intact .\\n - Ape Hand Atrophy of Thenar eminence thumb lies flat .\\n\\n correlate\\nHand of Benediction Benediction Sign \\n\\n- Pathophysiology High Median Nerve Injury at Elbow/Proximal .\\n- Presentation When asked to MAKE A FIST index and middle fingers remain extended; thumb remains extended. Ring and little fingers flex.\\n- Mechanism Loss of FDS all and FDP lateral half . FDP medial half supplied by Ulnar n. remains intact.\\n- Lecturer s Point Do not confuse with Ulnar Claw! Benediction is an ACTIVE sign asking to make a fist .\\n \\n\\n Lesion at Wrist Carpal Tunnel Syndrome / Slash Slide 69-70 \\n- Motor Loss LOAF muscles Lateral 2 Lumbricals Opponens Abductor pollicis brevis Flexor pollicis brevis .\\n - Forearm Flexors SPARED Patient CAN flex fingers can make a fist . NO Hand of Benediction.\\n- Sensory Loss Lateral 3.5 digits.\\n - Carpal Tunnel Palmar cutaneous branch SPARED branches proximal to tunnel . Palm sensation intact.\\n - Suicide Slash Palmar cutaneous branch cut. Palm numb.\\n\\n correlate\\nCarpal Tunnel Syndrome\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Compression of Median Nerve in carpal tunnel.\\n- Presentation Paresthesia in lateral 3.5 digits. Thenar atrophy late .\\n- Spare Palmar Cutaneous Branch sensation over thenar eminence is INTACT .\\n- Risk Factors Repetitive use pregnancy hypothyroidism.\\n \\n\\n Ulnar Nerve Injuries Medial Cord \\n\\nThe Ulnar nerve supplies FCU Medial half of FDP and most Intrinsic Hand Muscles Hypothenar Interossei Medial 2 Lumbricals Adductor Pollicis .\\n\\n Lesion at Elbow Cubital Tunnel Syndrome Slide 72 \\n- Motor Loss \\n - FCU & Medial FDP Weak wrist flexion Loss of DIP flexion in digits 4-5.\\n - Intrinsics Loss of abduction/adduction Interossei Loss of lumbricals 3-4.\\n- Presentation Ulnar Claw when opening hand .\\n- Ulnar Paradox Clawing is LESS severe at the elbow than at the wrist. Why? Because the FDP flexor to digits 4-5 is ALSO paralyzed so the fingers aren t pulled into as much flexion.\\n\\n correlate\\nCubital Tunnel Syndrome\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Compression of Ulnar Nerve at medial epicondyle funny bone .\\n- Presentation Numbness in medial 1.5 digits. Weak grasp. Mild Clawing.\\n- Ulnar Paradox High lesion = Less Clawing. Low lesion = More Clawing.\\n \\n\\n Lesion at Wrist Guyon s Canal Syndrome Slide 76-78 \\n- Motor Loss Intrinsic muscles ONLY. Forearm flexors FDP are SPARED.\\n- Presentation Severe Ulnar Claw .\\n - Since FDP is working it pulls the DIP joints of digits 4-5 into flexion against the paralyzed lumbricals/interossei.\\n\\n correlate\\nGuyon s Canal Syndrome Ulnar Tunnel \\n\\n- Pathophysiology Compression at wrist e.g. cyclist handlebars .\\n- Presentation Ulnar Claw Severe .\\n - Claw Hyperextension of MCP + Flexion of PIP/DIP Digits 4-5 .\\n- Sensory Medial 1.5 digits. Dorsal cutaneous branch SPARED branches proximal to wrist -> Dorsal hand sensation intact.\\n \\n\\n highyield\\n The \\ Hand\\ Signs Summary Slide 80 \\n\\n| Sign | Nerve | Level | Action/Context | Feature |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Wrist Drop | Radial | Axilla/Humerus | Resting/Extension | Cannot extend wrist |\\n| Hand of Benediction | Median | Elbow High | Making a Fist | Digits 1-3 won t flex |\\n| Ape Hand | Median | Any | Resting | Thenar atrophy |\\n| Ulnar Claw | Ulnar | Wrist Low | Extending Fingers | Digits 4-5 clawed |\\n| Total Claw | C8-T1 Klumpke | Trunk | Resting | All digits clawed |\\n \\n questions 1. A 21-year-old wrestler complains of weakness in his hand and tingling in his 5th digit. Examination reveals weakness in finger adduction and abduction. He has tenderness at the medial epicondyle. Which diagnosis is most likely? Carpal Tunnel Syndrome Cubital Tunnel Syndrome Guyon s Canal Syndrome Erb-Duchenne Palsy Pronator Teres Syndrome 1 Cubital Tunnel Syndrome is compression of the ulnar nerve at the medial epicondyle. It causes paresthesia in the ulnar distribution and weakness of intrinsic hand muscles interossei leading to difficulty holding a piece of paper adduction . 2. A newborn presents with the right arm adducted medially rotated elbow extended and forearm pronated Waiter s Tip position . Where is the lesion? C8-T1 Roots Inferior Trunk C5-C6 Roots Superior Trunk Posterior Cord Axillary Nerve Long Thoracic Nerve 1 Erb-Duchenne Palsy involves the Superior Trunk C5-C6 . Loss of abductors C5 lateral rotators C5 and flexors/supinators C5-C6 leads to the classic posture. 3. A patient with a mid-shaft humeral fracture presents with wrist drop. Detailed sensation testing reveals numbness on the dorsum of the hand but sensation on the posterior arm is INT. Which nerve structure is spared? Deep branch of Radial Nerve Posterior Brachial Cutaneous Nerve Superficial branch of Radial Nerve Axillary Nerve Musculocutaneous Nerve 1 In a Spiral Groove lesion mid-shaft fracture the Posterior Brachial Cutaneous Nerve supplying posterior arm skin is spared because it branches off the Radial nerve proximal to the groove. 4. When asked to make a fist a patient s index and middle fingers remain extended while the ring and little fingers flex normally. The thumb also fails to flex. This presentation is best described as Ulnar Claw Total Claw Hand Hand of Benediction Wrist Drop Dupuytren s Contracture 2 Hand of Benediction is a sign of High Median Nerve Injury at the elbow . It is an active sign seen when attempting to make a fist due to paralysis of FDS and lateral FDP. 5. A patient presents with winging of the right scapula when pushing against a wall. The injured nerve originates from which spinal levels? C3 C4 C5 C5 C6 C7 C8 T1 C5 C6 C6 C7 C8 1 The Long Thoracic Nerve which innervates the Serratus Anterior originates from roots C5 C6 C7 C5-6-7 wings your heaven . What is the classic posture of Erb-Duchenne Palsy? Waiter s Tip Adducted Medially Rotated Extended Pronated Clinical Which nerve roots are damaged in Klumpke s Palsy? C8-T1 Inferior Trunk Anatomy What physical exam finding is characteristic of Long Thoracic Nerve injury? Winged Scapula medial border protrudes Diagnosis What area of sensation is lost in Axillary Nerve injury? Lateral shoulder Regimental Badge area Clinical Distinguish Radial Nerve injury at Axilla vs. Mid-Humerus regarding Triceps function. Axilla Triceps weak/paralyzed. Mid-Humerus Triceps SPARED. Diagnosis What nerve injury causes Hand of Benediction ? High Median Nerve injury at elbow Clinical Why is sensation over the thenar eminence spared in Carpal Tunnel Syndrome? The Palmar Cutaneous branch of the Median nerves branches PROXIMAL to the carpal tunnel. Anatomy What is the Ulnar Paradox ? High ulnar lesions cause LESS clawing than low lesions because FDP to digits 4-5 is also paralyzed . Concept Which deformity results from combined Combined Median and Ulnar intrinsic paralysis e.g. Klumpke s ? Total Claw Hand Clawing of all digits Clinical What is the common cause of Saturday Night Palsy ? Compression of the Radial Nerve in the axilla e.g. arm over chair . Etiology BandB Neurology > Peripheral Nervous System > Brachial Plexus FirstAid Neurology > Pathology > Brachial Plexus Lesions Pathoma N/A Benediction vs. Claw Benediction is an ACTIVE sign High Median injury asked to make fist . Ulnar Claw is a PASSIVE resting deformity Low Ulnar injury . C5-6-7 Wings Back to Heaven Mnemonic for Long Thoracic Nerve C5-C6-C7 -> Serratus Anterior -> Winged Scapula. The Palmar Cutaneous Exception Both Median and Ulnar nerves have palmar cutaneous branches that arise PROXIMAL to the wrist tunnels. If the PALM is numb the lesion is NOT in the tunnel Carpal/Guyon . mindmap Brachial Plexus Injuries\\n\\n Trunk Lesions\\n- Superior Trunk C5-C6 Erb-Duchenne Waiter s Tip \\n- Inferior Trunk C8-T1 Klumpke s Total Claw \\n\\n Peripheral Nerves\\n- Long Thoracic Winged Scapula C5-7 \\n- Axillary Deltoid paralysis Surgical neck \\n- Radial Wrist Drop Saturday Night Palsy \\n- Median \\n - High Hand of Benediction\\n - Low Ape Hand / Carpal Tunnel\\n- Ulnar \\n - High Cubital Tunnel\\n - Low Ulnar Claw Guyon s Canal anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l63", "title": "Lecture #63: Headache", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l63 module Neuro l63 Lecture 63 Headache lecturer Adam Reno D.O. Neurology session Lecture 63 prev l62 next l64 pdf pdfs/Lecture 63. A. Reno DO MPH PPT .pdf highYieldPdf content/L63 HighYield Render.pdf transcript content/L63 FullTranscript.txt Lecture 63 Headache\\n\\n Lecturer Adam Reno D.O.\\n Session Lecture 63\\n\\n Introduction and Assessment Slide 2-16 \\n\\nHeadaches are a massive burden on patients leading to lost work social isolation and suffering. The primary goal of assessment is to exclude urgent/emergent causes Secondary Headaches and accurately diagnose benign recurrent ones Primary Headaches . Diagnosis relies heavily on History \\u2014imaging is often unnecessary unless \\ Red Flags\\ are present.\\n\\n Primary vs. Secondary\\n- Primary Benign recurrent the headache is the disease Migraine Tension Cluster .\\n- Secondary Symptom of underlying pathology Tumor Bleed Infection Giant Cell Arteritis .\\n\\n Red Flags \\ SNOOP\\ Equivalents Slide 55-59 \\nThe lecturer emphasizes several \\ Red Flags\\ that demand immediate imaging/workup \\n- Sudden Onset \\ Thunderclap\\ or \\ Worst headache of my life\\ Subarachnoid Hemorrhage .\\n- New Onset > 50 Giant Cell Arteritis or Tumor.\\n- Systemic Signs Fever Weight Loss HIV Cancer history.\\n- Neurological Deficits Altered mental status focal signs.\\n- Positional Worse when standing Low pressure or laying/bending High pressure .\\n\\n Primary Headaches\\n\\n Migraine Headache Slide 23-35 \\nMigraine is a neuronal dysfunction not just vascular . It involves Cortical Spreading Depression and activation of the Trigeminal-Vascular system.\\n\\n Phases\\n1. Prodrome Irritability food cravings frequent urination hours/days before .\\n2. Aura 20-30% Transient neurological deficits Visual > Sensory > Motor . Lasts 5-60 mins.\\n3. Headache Unilateral throbbing nausea photophobia.\\n4. Postdrome \\ Migraine Hangover\\ tired washed out .\\n\\n correlate\\nMigraine Headache\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Cortical Spreading Depression + Trigeminal activation + CGRP release.\\n- Presentation \\n - Unilateral 60-70% \\n - Throbbing/Pulsating \\n - Aggravated by activity \\n - Nausea/Vomiting \\n - Photophobia/Phonophobia \\n- Diagnosis Clinical 5 attacks lasting 4-72h .\\n- Triggers Stress Estrogen Menses/OCPs Chocolate Cheese Sleep changes.\\n- Acute Treatment NSAIDs Triptans 5-HT agonists Antiemetics Metoclopramide .\\n- Prophylaxis Beta-blockers Propranolol Topiramate Amitriptyline CGRP antagonists.\\n \\n\\n highyield\\n Migraine Diagnostic Criteria Visual Mnemonic \\n \\ POUND\\ \\n- P ulsating\\n- O ne-day duration 4-72 hours \\n- U nilateral\\n- N ausea/Vomiting\\n- D isabling intensity\\n \\n\\n Tension-Type Headache Slide 37-41 \\nThe most common headache type. Often associated with stress anxiety and depression.\\n\\n correlate\\nTension-Type Headache\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Pericranial muscle tenderness/sensitization.\\n- Presentation \\n - Bilateral \\ Band-like\\ squeezing .\\n - Non-pulsating Pressing/Tightening .\\n - Mild-Moderate intensity.\\n - NO Nausea/Vomiting.\\n - NO aggravation by activity.\\n- Treatment \\n - Episodic NSAIDs Acetaminophen OMT.\\n - Chronic >15 days/mo Amitriptyline TCA Stress management.\\n \\n\\n Cluster Headache Slide 42 \\nA Trigeminal Autonomic Cephalalgia. Severe suicide-inducing pain.\\n\\n correlate\\nCluster Headache\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Hypothalamic activation.\\n- Presentation \\n - Male > Female 20-50s .\\n - Strictly Unilateral Peri-orbital \\ Ice pick in eye\\ .\\n - Autonomic Signs Ipsilateral Tearing Rhinorrhea Ptosis Miosis Conjunctival injection.\\n - Behavior Agitated pacing unlike Migraineurs who lie still .\\n - Timing Attacks occur in \\ clusters\\ daily for weeks often at night.\\n- Acute Treatment 100% Oxygen Non-rebreather Sumatriptan Subcutaneous .\\n- Prophylaxis Verapamil.\\n \\n\\n Secondary Headaches Slide 55-59 \\n\\nThese encompass life-threatening conditions requiring urgent intervention.\\n\\n Subarachnoid Hemorrhage SAH \\n- Cause Rupture of Saccular Berry Aneurysm Circle of Willis .\\n- Presentation \\ Thunderclap Headache\\ Maximum intensity in seconds \\ Worst headache of my life\\ .\\n- Workup Non-contrast CT Head first line . If negative but suspicion high -> Lumbar Puncture Xanthochromia .\\n\\n Meningitis / Encephalitis\\n- Triad Headache + Fever + Nuchal Rigidity Stiff Neck .\\n- Encephalitis Add Altered Mental Status and Seizures.\\n\\n Giant Cell Temporal Arteritis\\n- Pathophysiology Vasculitis of medium/large arteries branches of External Carotid .\\n- Demographics age > 50 Female > Male.\\n- Presentation Unilateral temporal headache Jaw Claudication Scalp tenderness.\\n- Complication Blindness Ophthalmic artery occlusion . This is a medical emergency.\\n- Workup/Tx Elevated ESR/CRP . Start High-Dose Steroids immediately before biopsy confirms .\\n\\n correlate\\nGiant Cell Arteritis Temporal Arteritis \\n\\n- Presentation Elderly patient >50 with new unilateral headache jaw pain when chewing and tender temporal artery.\\n- Risk Irreversible Blindness .\\n- Management Start Steroids Prednisone IMMEDIATELY . Do not wait for temporal artery biopsy.\\n \\n\\n Intracranial Mass Tumor \\n- Red Flag Headache that is worse in the morning due to hypoventilation/increased CO2/vasodilation during sleep or worse with Valsalva coughing bending .\\n\\n Post-Dural Puncture Headache Lumbar Puncture Headache \\n- Cause CSF leak after LP or Epidural e.g. C-section .\\n- Presentation Positional Headache Worse when standing/sitting RELIEVED by lying flat .\\n- Treatment Blood Patch Caffeine.\\n\\n highyield\\n Headache Comparison Table \\n\\n| Type | Location | Quality | Duration | Associated Sx |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Migraine | Unilateral | Throbbing | 4-72 hrs | Nausea Photo/Phonophobia Aura |\\n| Tension | Bilateral | Tight Band | 30m - 7d | Muscle tenderness No Nausea |\\n| Cluster | Unilateral Eye | Boring/Stabbing | 15-180 min | Tearing Rhinorrhea Ptosis Autonomic |\\n \\n\\n questions 1. A 30-year-old male presents with severe lancinating pain behind his right eye. He is pacing around the room and appears agitated. Exam reveals right-sided ptosis miosis and rhinorrhea. Treatment with 100% Oxygen provides relief. What is the diagnosis? Migraine with Aura Tension-Type Headache Cluster Headache Trigeminal Neuralgia Giant Cell Arteritis 2 Cluster Headache presents in males with severe unilateral periorbital pain and autonomic symptoms tearing rhinorrhea Horner s syndrome . Patients are agitated pacing . 100% Oxygen is the first-line abortive therapy. 2. A 72-year-old female presents with a new onset right-sided headache and pain in her jaw when chewing steak. She reports some blurring of vision in her right eye. What is the most appropriate immediate next step? Temporal Artery Biopsy Start High-Dose Corticosteroids Non-contrast CT Head Prescribe Sumatriptan Lumbar Puncture 1 This patient has symptoms of Giant Cell Temporal Arteritis Age >50 Headache Jaw Claudication Visual changes . The risk of permanent blindness is high. You must Start Corticosteroids immediately to prevent vision loss; do not delay treatment for the biopsy. 3. A 25-year-old female complains of a tight band squeezing her head bilaterally. The pain is mild-moderate and does not worsen with routine physical activity. She denies nausea or vomiting. What is the most likely diagnosis? Migraine without Aura Tension-Type Headache Cluster Headache Pseudotumor Cerebri Sinusitis 1 Tension-Type Headache is bilateral non-pulsating band-like and notably lacks the nausea/vomiting and aggravation by activity seen in migraines. 4. A 45-year-old male presents to the ER screaming This is the worst headache of my life! It started instantly like a thunderclap while he was lifting weights. He has no fever but has some nuchal rigidity. The most urgent diagnostic test is MRI Brain Non-contrast CT Head Lumbar Puncture EEG Erythrocyte Sedimentation Rate ESR 1 Subarachnoid Hemorrhage presents as a Thunderclap headache. The initial test of choice is a Non-contrast CT Head to look for blood in the subarachnoid space. 5. A patient who recently underwent a C-section with epidural anesthesia complains of a severe headache that is relieved when lying flat but returns immediately upon standing. What is the mechanism? Corebral Venous Thrombosis CSF Leak Low Pressure Meningitis Preeclampsia Postpartum Migraine 1 Post-Dural Puncture Headache is caused by a persistent CSF leak lowering intracranial pressure. The hallmark is the postural nature worse standing better lying down . What are the POUND criteria for Migraine? Pulsating One-day 4-72h Unilateral Nausea/Vomiting Disabling. Diagnosis What are the autonomic features of Cluster Headache? Ptosis Miosis Lacrimation Rhinorrhea Conjunctival Injection all ipsilateral . Clinical What is the first-line ABORTIVE treatment for Cluster Headache? 100% Oxygen and Sumatriptan . Pharmacology How do you distinguish Tension Headache from Migraine? Tension is Bilateral Non-pulsating and has NO Nausea/Vomiting or Aura. Diagnosis What is the most feared complication of Giant Cell Arteritis? Permanent Blindness need immediate steroids . Clinical Describe the headache of a Brain Tumor. Worse in the morning awakening or with Valsalva coughing/bending . Clinical What is a Thunderclap headache suggestive of? Subarachnoid Hemorrhage Ruptured Aneurysm . Clinical What characteristic defines a Post-Dural Puncture Headache? Positional Worse standing Improved lying flat. Diagnosis Which population is most affected by Cluster Headaches? Males 20-50s often smokers. Epidemiology What is the classic presentation of Temporal Arteritis? Elderly >50 Unilateral Temporal Headache Jaw Claudication Elevated ESR. Clinical BandB Neurology > Headache FirstAid Neurology > Pathology > Headache Pathoma N/A Migraine vs. Cluster Behavior A Migraine patient wants to lie still in a dark quiet room. A Cluster Headache patient is agitated restless and paces around the room. Treat First Biopsy Later If you suspect Giant Cell Arteritis start Steroids immediately to save vision. Do not wait for the temporal artery biopsy results. SNOOP for Red Flags Systemic Ssx Fever/Weight Loss Neurologic Ssx Onset Sudden/Thunderclap Older age >50 new onset Pattern change Positional/Valsalva . mindmap Headaches\\n\\n Primary Benign Recurrent \\n- Migraine Unilateral Pulsating Nausea Aura. Tx Triptans \\n- Tension Bilateral Band-like No Nausea. Tx NSAIDs \\n- Cluster Unilateral Eye Autonomic Ssx Restless. Tx 100% O2 \\n\\n Secondary Red Flags \\n- Vascular \\n - SAH Thunderclap \\n - Temporal Arteritis Jaw Claudication Blindness risk \\n- Infection Meningitis Fever Stiff Neck \\n- Structural \\n - Tumor Morning headache \\n - Low Pressure Post-LP Positional anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l64", "title": "Lecture #64: Introduction to Neuropharmacology", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l64 module Neuro l64 Lecture 64 Introduction to Neuropharmacology lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. Neuropharmacology session Lecture 64 prev l63 next l65 pdf pdfs/Lecture 64. L. Goldstein Pharm.D. PPT .pdf highYieldPdf content/L64 HighYield Render.pdf transcript content/L64 FullTranscript.txt Lecture 64 Introduction to Neuropharmacology General Anesthetics \\n\\n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D.\\n Session Lecture 64\\n\\n Inhaled Anesthetics Pharmacokinetics\\n\\nThe goal of inhaled anesthesia is to achieve equilibrium between the partial pressure of the gas in the alveoli FA and the brain FCNS . The speed of induction depends on the rate at which FA approaches the inspired concentration FI .\\n\\n Key Factors Affecting Uptake Slide 10 28-36 \\n\\n1. Solubility Blood Gas Partition Coefficient \\n - Low Solubility e.g. Nitrous Oxide Desflurane The gas does not want to dissolve in blood. It quickly saturates the blood and raises partial pressure -> Rapid Induction and Rapid Recovery .\\n - High Solubility e.g. Halothane Isoflurane The gas dissolves readily in blood like sugar in tea . It takes a lot of gas to saturate the blood before partial pressure rises -> Slow Induction and Slow Recovery .\\n\\n2. Cardiac Output \\n - Increased CO Removes gas from alveoli faster -> Slows the rise of FA -> Slower Induction . Counter-intuitive but high yield .\\n - Decreased CO Speeds induction especially for soluble agents .\\n\\n3. Alveolar Ventilation Hyperventilation speeds induction.\\n\\n highyield\\n Solubility & Induction Speed \\n- Low Blood Gas Coefficient N2O Desflurane = FAST Onset.\\n- High Blood Gas Coefficient Halothane = SLOW Onset.\\n \\n\\n MAC Minimum Alveolar Concentration Slide 52 \\n- Definition The alveolar concentration that prevents movement in 50% of patients in response to surgical incision Standardized Stimulus .\\n- Potency MAC is inversely related to potency. Low MAC = High Potency .\\n - Halothane MAC 0.75% Very Potent.\\n - Nitrous Oxide MAC >100% Weak anesthetic cannot achieve surgical anesthesia alone .\\n- Additivity MAC values are additive 0.5 MAC N2O + 0.5 MAC Isoflurane = 1 MAC .\\n\\n Inhaled Anesthetics Agents & Toxicity\\n\\n Nitrous Oxide N2O \\n- Properties Gas at room temp. Low solubility Fast onset . Weak potency. Good Analgesia.\\n- Side Effects \\n - Diffusion Hypoxia On discontinuation N2O rushes into alveoli displacing O2. Prevention Administer 100% O2 for minutes after stopping N2O.\\n - Expansion of Air-Filled Spaces Contraindicated in Pneumothorax Bowel Obstruction Middle Ear surgery.\\n - Vitamin B12 Inhibition Inhibits Methionine Synthase -> Megaloblastic Anemia Neuropathy.\\n\\n Halogenated Agents Isoflurane Desflurane Sevoflurane Halothane \\n- Mechanism GABA-A agonism NMDA antagonism.\\n- Organ Effects \\n - CV Vasodilation -> Hypotension. Desflurane causes transient Tachycardia Sympathetic stim .\\n - Resp Respiratory Depression Bronchodilation Sevoflurane is best for asthmatics . Desflurane is Pungent airway irritation .\\n - CNS Increased Cerebral Blood Flow -> Increased Intracranial Pressure ICP .\\n- Toxicities \\n - Malignant Hyperthermia Genetic disorder Ryanodine receptor . Triggered by Halogenated agents + Succinylcholine. Tx Dantrolene .\\n - Halothane Hepatitis Immune-mediated liver necrosis Rare .\\n - Nephrotoxicity Sevoflurane -> \\ Compound A\\ but rare in humans .\\n\\n correlate\\nMalignant Hyperthermia\\n\\n- Trigger Volatile Anesthetics Isoflurane Sevoflurane etc. + Succinylcholine.\\n- Pathophysiology Uncontrolled Ca2+ release from Sarcoplasmic Reticulum.\\n- Signs Muscle Rigidity Masseter spasm Hyperthermia Hypercapnia High CO2 Tachycardia Acidosis.\\n- Treatment Stop agent Give Dantrolene .\\n \\n\\n Intravenous Anesthetics Slide 62-70 \\n\\nUsed for Induction Rapid onset due to high lipophilicity . Action is terminated by Redistribution Blood -> Brain -> Muscle/Fat NOT metabolism.\\n\\n Propofol\\n- Mechanism GABA-A agonist.\\n- Clinical Use Induction Maintenance TIVA ICU Sedation. \\ Milk of Amnesia\\ .\\n- Pros Anti-emetic prevents nausea Rapid recovery.\\n- Cons Hypotension Vasodilation Respiratory Depression Pain on Injection described as burning.\\n- Notes Formulated in egg/soybean emulsion bacterial growth risk allergy .\\n\\n Etomidate\\n- Mechanism GABA-A agonist.\\n- Clinical Use Induction in patients with Cardiovascular Instability .\\n- Pros Hemodynamic Stability Minimal change in BP/HR .\\n- Cons Adrenocortical Suppression inhibits 11-beta-hydroxylase Nausea/Vomiting.\\n\\n Ketamine\\n- Mechanism NMDA Receptor Antagonist .\\n- Clinical Use Dissociative Anesthesia eyes open but unresponsive Trauma/Shock.\\n- Pros Sympathomimetic Increases BP HR Cardiac Output Bronchodilation Good for Asthmatics .\\n- Cons Emergence Delirium Hallucinations Nightmares .\\n\\n Dexmedetomidine\\n- Mechanism Alpha-2 Agonist central .\\n- Clinical Use ICU Sedation Adjunct.\\n- Effects Sedation usually without respiratory depression. Causes Bradycardia/Hypotension.\\n\\n highyield\\n IV Anesthetic Choice High Yields \\n- Patient with Hypotension/Trauma Use Ketamine Boosts BP or Etomidate Stable BP . Avoid Propofol.\\n- Patient with Asthma Use Ketamine or Sevoflurane Bronchodilators . Avoid Desflurane Pungent .\\n- Patient with Head Injury High ICP Avoid Ketamine increases ICP and Volatile agents increase ICP . Propofol/Barbiturates are preferred decrease metabolic rate .\\n- Post-Op Nausea Use Propofol Anti-emetic . Avoid N2O and Opioids.\\n \\n questions 1. An anesthesiologist wishes to speed up the induction of anesthesia with a highly soluble volatile agent. Which maneuver would be most effective? Decreasing Alveolar Ventilation Increasing Cardiac Output Increasing Alveolar Ventilation Using an agent with a higher Blood Gas partition coefficient Administering a beta-blocker 2 Increasing Alveolar Ventilation delivers more gas to the alveoli speeding the rise of partial pressure FA and thus speeding induction. Conversely increasing Cardiac Output slows induction by removing gas from the alveoli too quickly. 2. A trauma patient with severe hypotension requires emergency intubation. Which induction agent is preferred due to its ability to increase blood pressure and heart rate? Propofol Etomidate Ketamine Thiopental Sevoflurane 2 Ketamine is a sympathomimetic that increases BP HR and cardiac output making it ideal for hemodynamically unstable patients shock . Propofol causes hypotension. 3. Following a surgery using Nitrous Oxide the patient is extubated and allowed to breathe room air. Minutes later she becomes hypoxic. What is the cause? Malignant Hyperthermia Diffusion Hypoxia Laryngospasm Pneumothorax expansion Opioid overdose 1 Diffusion Hypoxia occurs when N2O is stopped abruptly; the gas rushes from the blood into the alveoli displacing oxygen. It is prevented by administering 100% O2 for several minutes post-anesthesia. 4. Which anesthetic agent is associated with adrenocortical suppression via inhibition of 11-beta-hydroxylase? Ketamine Propofol Midazolam Etomidate Dexmedetomidine 3 Etomidate inhibits 11-beta-hydroxylase preventing cortisol synthesis. It is generally safe for single-dose induction but typically avoided for long-term infusions in septic patients. 5. Comparing two inhaled anesthetics Drug A has a Blood Gas partition coefficient of 0.42. Drug B has a coefficient of 2.30. Which statement is true? Drug A has a slower onset of action than Drug B Drug B has a faster recovery time than Drug A Drug A is more soluble in blood than Drug B Drug A has a faster onset of action than Drug B Drug B is less potent than Drug A 3 Typically Low Solubility Low Blood Gas coefficient correlates with Fast Onset . Drug A 0.42 is less soluble than Drug B 2.30 so Drug A will have a faster onset . What does a LOW Blood Gas partition coefficient indicate about induction speed? Fast Induction and Fast Recovery . Pharmacokinetics What is the measure of Potency for inhaled anesthetics? MAC Minimum Alveolar Concentration . Low MAC = High Potency. Pharmacokinetics Which induction agent causes Adrenocortical Suppression? Etomidate. Pharmacology Which induction agent is Dissociative and increases Cardiac Output? Ketamine. Pharmacology What terminates the action of a single bolus of Propofol? Redistribution not metabolism . Pharmacokinetics What is the trigger for Malignant Hyperthermia? Halogenated Anesthetics + Succinylcholine. Pathology What is the antidote for Malignant Hyperthermia? Dantrolene. Pharmacology Why is Nitrous Oxide contraindicated in Pneumothorax? It expands air-filled spaces. Safety Which volatile anesthetic is associated with Nephrotoxicity Compound A ? Sevoflurane. Pharmacology Which IV anesthetic is known as an Anti-emetic? Propofol. Pharmacology Physeo Pharmacology > Neuro > General Anesthetics FirstAid Pharmacology > Anesthetics Solubility and Speed Think of Solubility like a sink . If the blood sink is huge high solubility it takes forever to fill up rise in partial pressure before it spills over into the brain. Low solubility = tiny sink = fills fast = fast anesthesia. Ketamine & Asthma Ketamine is a bronchodilator. It s an excellent choice for induction in an asthmatic patient who is actively wheezing. Propofol Burn Propofol hurts on injection vascular irritation . Lidocaine is often mixed with it to blunt the pain. mindmap General Anesthetics\\n\\n Inhaled\\n- Pharmacokinetics \\n - Low Solubility -> Fast Onset Desflurane N2O \\n - High Solubility -> Slow Onset Halothane Isoflurane \\n - Increased CO -> Slower Onset\\n- Agents \\n - N2O Weak Fast Diffusion Hypoxia.\\n - Halogenated Potent MH risk Hypotension.\\n - Sevoflurane Bronchodilation.\\n - Desflurane Pungent Tachycardia.\\n\\n Intravenous\\n- Propofol Anti-emetic Hypotension Standard induction.\\n- Etomidate Cardiac Stability Adrenal suppression.\\n- Ketamine Sympathomimetic High BP Dissociative Bronchodilator.\\n- Dexmedetomidine Alpha-2 agonist Sedation w/o Resp Depression .\\n- Thiopental Barbiturate High ICP control . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l65", "title": "Lecture #65: Pharmacology of General Anesthetics Part 2", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l65 module Neuro l65 Lecture 65 Pharmacology of General Anesthetics Part 2 lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. Neuropharmacology session Lecture 65 prev l64 next l66 pdf pdfs/Lecture 65. L. Goldstein Pharm.D. PPT .pdf highYieldPdf content/L65 HighYield Render.pdf transcript content/L65 FullTranscript.txt Lecture 65 Pharmacology of General Anesthetics Part 2\\n\\n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D.\\n Session Lecture 65\\n\\n---\\n\\n Effect of Inhaled Anesthetics on Organ Systems \\n\\n Cardiovascular Effects \\nMost inhaled anesthetics cause a dose-dependent decrease in blood pressure but the mechanisms differ.\\n- Isoflurane Desflurane Sevoflurane Primarily decrease blood pressure by decreasing systemic vascular resistance vasodilation while preserving cardiac output.\\n - Note This preservation of cardiac output makes them preferable for patients with impaired myocardial function compared to halothane.\\n- Halothane & Enflurane Decrease blood pressure primarily by depressing myocardial contractility leading to decreased cardiac output.\\n- Nitrous Oxide N2O Has negligible cardiovascular effects overall because its mild sympathetic stimulation offsets any direct myocardial depression.\\n- Baroreflex Generally depressed by volatile anesthetics.\\n - Desflurane Exception Causes transient sympathetic stimulation tachycardia hypertension when the concentration is increased rapidly.\\n - correlate\\n Coronary Artery Disease CAD \\n Limit the use of Desflurane or increase concentration slowly in patients with CAD as the resulting tachycardia and hypertension can increase myocardial oxygen demand and induce ischemia or infarction .\\n \\n\\n Respiratory Effects \\nVolatile anesthetics are respiratory depressants.\\n- Ventilation Decrease tidal volume and increase respiratory rate rapid shallow breathing . The overall effect is a decrease in minute ventilation.\\n- CO2 Response They blunt the ventilatory response to hypercapnia increased PaCO2 .\\n- Airway Irritation Desflurane and Isoflurane are pungent and can cause coughing breath-holding or laryngospasm poor for mask induction .\\n- Bronchodilation All volatiles cause bronchodilation. Sevoflurane is non-pungent and a potent bronchodilator making it the drug of choice for inhalational induction especially in patients with reactive airways e.g. asthma .\\n\\n Cerebral Effects \\n- Uncoupling Volatile anesthetics increase cerebral blood flow CBF vasodilation and intracranial pressure ICP while simultaneously decreasing cerebral metabolic rate of oxygen CMRO2 .\\n - Note Nitrous oxide increases both CBF and CMRO2.\\n - Note Sevoflurane increases ICP less than others.\\n- correlate\\n Increased Intracranial Pressure ICP \\n Inhaled anesthetics should be used with caution in patients with space-occupying lesions tumors or head trauma as the increase in CBF can dangerously elevate intracranial pressure . Hyperventilation to lower PaCO2 can help counteract this vasodilation.\\n \\n\\n Neuromuscular & Renal/Hepatic \\n- Muscle Volatiles relax skeletal muscle and potentiate the effects of neuromuscular blockers. N2O does not .\\n- Kidney/Liver Decrease renal and hepatic blood flow dose-dependent .\\n- Uterus Volatiles relax uterine smooth muscle can lead to uterine atony/hemorrhage . N2O does not.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Toxicities of Inhaled Anesthetics \\n\\n Malignant Hyperthermia MH \\nA life-threatening hypermetabolic state triggered by all halogenated volatile anesthetics and succinylcholine in genetically susceptible individuals Ryanodine receptor mutations .\\n- Pathophysiology Uncontrolled calcium release causing sustained muscle contraction.\\n- Signs Hypercapnia early sign tachycardia muscle rigidity hyperthermia late sign metabolic acidosis hyperkalemia.\\n- Treatment Stop agent hyperventilate give Dantrolene .\\n- Important Nitrous Oxide does NOT trigger MH. \\n\\n Hepatotoxicity \\n- Halothane Hepatitis A specific immune-mediated necrosis caused by the formation of trifluoroacetyl chloride from CYP2E1 metabolism which haptenates liver proteins.\\n - Risk factors Female obesity prior exposure.\\n - Note Desflurane Isoflurane and Sevoflurane have minimal metabolism and very low risk.\\n\\n Nephrotoxicity \\n- Compound A Sevoflurane interacts with soda lime CO2 absorbers to form Compound A which is nephrotoxic in rats. No proven renal injury in humans but valid theoretical concern use adequate fresh gas flow .\\n\\n Carbon Monoxide CO Production \\n Desflurane most > Isoflurane > Sevoflurane can produce CO when passed through desiccated dry CO2 absorbers .\\n- Prevent by replacing absorbents regularly.\\n\\n Nitrous Oxide N2O Specific Toxicities \\n1. Expansion of Air-Filled Spaces N2O is 35x more soluble than N2. It diffuses into air cavities faster than N2 leaves increasing pressure/volume.\\n - correlate\\n N2O Contraindications \\n Avoid N2O in patients with pneumothorax bowel obstruction air embolism or during middle ear surgery as expansion of these spaces can cause catastrophic rupture or pressure damage.\\n \\n2. Diffusion Hypoxia Upon discontinuation N2O rushes from blood into alveoli diluting O2. Reversed by giving 100% O2 for several minutes after stopping N2O.\\n3. Vitamin B12 Inhibition N2O oxidizes the cobalt in B12 inhibiting methionine synthase .\\n - correlate\\n Megaloblastic Anemia & Neuropathy \\n Prolonged exposure or use in patients with preexisting deficiency can lead to megaloblastic anemia and subacute combined degeneration of the cord peripheral neuropathy . Avoid in patients with B12 deficiency/malnutrition.\\n \\n4. Second Gas Effect High uptake of N2O concentrates the remaining gases in the alveoli speeding up induction of co-administered agents.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Intravenous Anesthetics \\n\\n Propofol \\nThe most common induction agent.\\n- Mechanism Potentiates chloride current through the GABA-A receptor.\\n- Effects Rapid induction and recovery \\ clear headed\\ . Antiemetic Anti-pruritic Bronchodilation . Decreases CBF ICP and CMRO2.\\n- Adverse Effects \\n - Pain on injection mitigate with lidocaine .\\n - Hypotension Causes profound vasodilation and myocardial depression.\\n - Respiratory Depression Significant apnea.\\n- correlate\\n Propofol Infusion Syndrome PRIS \\n Rare but fatal reaction to high-dose/long-term infusion. Signs include metabolic acidosis rhabdomyolysis hyperkalemia hepatomegaly and cardiac failure.\\n \\n- Formulation Egg lecithin/soybean oil emulsion risk of bacterial growth careful in egg/soy allergy—though most reactions are to egg white protein not lecithin .\\n\\n Etomidate \\n- Mechanism GABA-A agonist.\\n- Key Feature Hemodynamic Stability . Minimal change in HR BP or CO.\\n- Indication Induction agent of choice for patients with cardiovascular instability or shock .\\n- Adverse Effects \\n - Adrenal Suppression Inhibits 11-beta-hydroxylase preventing cortisol synthesis. Avoid in septic shock patients who need their adrenal reserve.\\n - Myoclonus Involuntary muscle movements.\\n - PONV High incidence of postoperative nausea and vomiting.\\n\\n Ketamine \\n- Mechanism NMDA receptor antagonist blocks glutamate .\\n- Effects Dissociative anesthesia catatonia amnesia analgesia .\\n - Cardiovascular Causes Sympathetic Stimulation increase HR BP CO via catecholamine release. Good for hypovolemic shock.\\n - Respiratory Maintains airway reflexes and respiratory drive. Potent bronchodilator good for severe asthma induction .\\n - Cerebral Increases CMRO2 CBF and ICP.\\n- Adverse Effects \\n - Emergence Delirium Hallucinations vivid dreams disorientation. Prevent with benzodiazepines.\\n - correlate\\n Contraindications for Ketamine \\n Avoid in patients with severe hypertension angina or active ischemia due to increased myocardial oxygen demand. Also historically avoided in head trauma due to ICP though this is debated.\\n \\n\\n Dexmedetomidine Precedex \\n- Mechanism Highly selective Alpha-2 adrenergic agonist central action .\\n- Effects Sedation mimics natural sleep easily rousable Anxiolysis Analgesia . NO respiratory depression.\\n- Adverse Effects Bradycardia and Hypotension .\\n\\n Benzodiazepines Midazolam \\n- Mechanism GABA-A agonist.\\n- Use Preoperative anxiolysis amnesia anterograde .\\n- Reversal Flumazenil .\\n\\n---\\n\\n Adjuncts \\n- Opioids Fentanyl etc. Provide analgesia. Reversal Naloxone .\\n- Lidocaine IV bolus blunts airway reflexes coughing during intubation.\\n\\n highyield\\n Quick Drug Selection Guide \\n Asthma/Bronchospasm Sevoflurane inhaled Ketamine IV Propofol IV .\\n Hemodynamic Instability/Shock Etomidate IV Ketamine IV .\\n Head Injury High ICP Propofol Barbiturates Etomidate . Avoid Ketamine Volatiles unless hyperventilated .\\n Reactive Airway/Smoking Sevoflurane. Avoid Desflurane.\\n History of PONV Propofol TIVA . Avoid Etomidate N2O Opioids.\\n Bowel Obstruction/Pneumothorax Avoid Nitrous Oxide.\\n CAD/Ischemia Avoid Ketamine Desflurane tachycardia .\\n \\n questions 1. A 55-year-old male with a history of severe coronary artery disease presents for coronary artery bypass grafting. During induction the anesthesiologist chooses an agent known for maintaining hemodynamic stability but causing adrenal suppression. Which drug was likely used? Propofol Ketamine Etomidate Midazolam Sevoflurane 2 Etomidate is known for its excellent hemodynamic stability minimal effect on HR/BP but has the unique side effect of inhibiting 11-beta-hydroxylase leading to adrenal suppression. 2. A 24-year-old male undergoes surgery for a bowel obstruction. Upon induction with a gas anesthetic the bowel rapidly distends complicating the surgery. Which agent is the most likely cause? Sevoflurane Isoflurane Desflurane Nitrous Oxide Propofol 3 Nitrous Oxide N2O is much more soluble than nitrogen. It diffuses into air-filled spaces like an obstructed bowel faster than nitrogen can leave causing rapid expansion of the space. It is contraindicated in bowel obstruction pneumothorax and middle ear surgery. 3. A child undergoing a brief procedure with sevoflurane anesthesia develops rapid breathing tachycardia muscle rigidity and a rapidly rising body temperature. What is the mechanism of the antidote for this condition? Blockade of NMDA receptors Inhibition of calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum Activation of GABA-A receptors Competitive antagonism of acetylcholine receptors Agonism of alpha-2 adrenergic receptors 1 The presentation is Malignant Hyperthermia . The antidote is Dantrolene which works by inhibiting calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum via ryanodine receptors stopping the uncontrolled contraction. 4. A trauma patient with significant blood loss and hypotension requires emergency intubation. Which induction agent would best support this patient s blood pressure via sympathetic stimulation? Propofol Etomidate Ketamine Thiopental Midazolam 2 Ketamine stimulates the sympathetic nervous system increasing HR and BP making it an excellent induction agent for patients in hypovolemic shock who need hemodynamic support. Propofol causes hypotension. 5. A patient with a known deficiency of Vitamin B12 undergoes a lengthy procedure using a specific inhaled anesthetic. Postoperatively they develop signs of megaloblastic anemia and neuropathy. This effect is due to inhibition of which enzyme? 11-beta-hydroxylase Methionine synthase Ala-synthase Monoamine oxidase Pseudocholinesterase 1 Nitrous Oxide irreversibly oxidizes the cobalt atom of Vitamin B12 which inhibits Methionine Synthase . This can lead to megaloblastic anemia and neurologic sequelae subacute combined degeneration especially in B12-deficient patients. Which IV anesthetic is known for its hemodynamic stability and minimal respiratory depression? Etomidate Clinical What is the primary mechanism of action of Ketamine? NMDA receptor antagonist Pharmacology Which inhaled anesthetic should be avoided in patients with pneumothorax or bowel obstruction? Nitrous Oxide N2O Contraindications What drug is the specific treatment for Malignant Hyperthermia? Dantrolene Antidote Which IV anesthetic causes adrenal suppression by inhibiting 11-beta-hydroxylase? Etomidate Adverse Effects What is a unique adverse effect of Ketamine during recovery? Emergence delirium hallucinations vivid dreams Adverse Effects Which inhaled anesthetic causes the most airway irritation and coughing? Desflurane Isoflurane is also pungent Adverse Effects Which IV anesthetic has antiemetic properties? Propofol Clinical What potentially nephrotoxic substance is formed when Sevoflurane degrades in soda lime? Compound A Toxicology Which induction agent increases intracranial pressure and cerebral blood flow? Ketamine Physiology BandB Neurology > Pharmacology > Anesthetics FirstAid Pharmacology > Anesthetics Desflurane & CAD Desflurane can cause transient sympathetic stimulation tachycardia/HTN making it risky for patients with CAD. Sevoflurane Induction Sevoflurane is the best agent for inhalational induction due to its lack of pungency and potent bronchodilation. N2O in Pneumothorax Nitrous Oxide is contraindicated in pneumothorax and bowel obstruction because it diffuses into air-filled spaces 35x faster than nitrogen leaves causing expansion. Etomidate & Shock Etomidate is the drug of choice for induction in hemodynamically unstable patients but causes adrenal suppression. Ketamine & ICP Ketamine is a dissociative anesthetic that increases sympathetic tone good for shock but increases ICP bad for head trauma . Propofol & PONV Propofol has antiemetic properties making it ideal for TIVA to reduce PONV. MH Trigger Malignant Hyperthermia is triggered by halogenated volatiles and succinylcholine; treat with Dantrolene. mindmap \\n- Inhaled Anesthetics \\n - Mechanism Unknown globally but likely GABA potentiators / NMDA inhibitors.\\n - PK MAC potency Solubility speed .\\n - Organ Effects \\n - CV Vasodilation Iso/Des/Sevo Myocardial Depression Halothane .\\n - Resp Depression Bronchodilation Sevo ++ .\\n - Brain Increased CBF/ICP Decreased CMRO2.\\n - Toxicities \\n - Malignant Hyperthermia All volatiles .\\n - Halothane Hepatitis Immune .\\n - Nephrotoxicity Sevo -> Cmpd A .\\n - N2O Specifics Expansion of air spaces B12 inhibition Diffusion Hypoxia.\\n- Intravenous Anesthetics \\n - Propofol GABA Standard induction. Hypotension/Apnea. Antiemetic.\\n - Etomidate GABA CV Stability. Adrenal suppression. Myoclonus.\\n - Ketamine NMDA Dissociative. Sympathetic boost Shock . Hallucinations.\\n - Dexmedetomidine Alpha-2 Sedation without resp depression.\\n anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l66", "title": "Lecture #66: Pharmacology: Local Anesthetics", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l66 module Neuro l66 Lecture 66 Pharmacology Local Anesthetics lecturer L. Goldstein Neuropharmacology session Lecture 66 prev l65 next l67 pdf pdfs/Lecture 66. L. Goldstein.pdf highYieldPdf content/L66 HighYield Render.pdf transcript content/L66 FullTranscript.txt Lecture 66 Pharmacology Local Anesthetics\\n\\n Lecturer L. Goldstein\\n Session Lecture 66\\n\\n---\\n\\n Introduction & Mechanism of Action\\n\\nLocal anesthetics LAs are diverse agents used to block sensation in a specific area of the body without loss of consciousness. Their primary utility lies in preventing pain transmission during surgical or dental procedures. To understand how they work we must first appreciate the physiology of the voltage-gated sodium channel. Nerves transmit signals via action potentials which are propagated by the rapid influx of sodium ions through these channels.\\n\\nLocal anesthetics function by diffusing through the nerve membrane into the axoplasm where they bind to the intracellular portion of voltage-gated sodium channels. Crucially they bind preferentially to channels in the open and inactivated states—a phenomenon known as state-dependent block . By stabilizing the inactivated state they prevent the channel from resetting to the resting state thereby halting the propagation of action potentials. This blockade is also use-dependent meaning nerves that are firing rapidly such as pain fibers are blocked more effectively than resting nerves.\\n\\n highyield\\n Mechanism of Action \\n- Target Intracellular portion of Voltage-Gated Na+ Channels .\\n- Binding State High affinity for Open and Inactivated states State-Dependent .\\n- Effect Stabilizes the inactivated state $\\\\\\\\rightarrow$ prevents Na+ influx $\\\\\\\\rightarrow$ blocks action potential propagation.\\n- Use-Dependence Higher firing frequency = Stronger block Pain fibers > Motor fibers .\\n \\n\\n Susceptibility to Blockade\\nNot all nerve fibers are blocked equally. The sensitivity of a nerve fiber to LA blockade is determined by its diameter myelination and firing frequency. In general smaller diameter and higher firing frequency increase sensitivity.\\n\\n- Order of Blockade \\n 1. Small myelinated Sympathetic fibers - Blocked First \\n 2. Small unmyelinated Pain/Temp - C fibers & Small myelinated Pain/Temp - A$\\\\\\\\delta$ fibers \\n 3. Large myelinated Touch Pressure Motor - Blocked Last \\n\\n mnemonic\\n Fiber Blockade Order \\n \\ Silly Puns Make Me Laugh\\ \\n- S iopathetic Sympathetic \\n- P ain Pain/Temp \\n- T ouch Touch/Pressure \\n- M otor Motor \\n \\n\\n Structure & Pharmacokinetics\\n\\nLocal anesthetics consist of a lipophilic aromatic ring connected to a hydrophilic amine by an intermediate chain. This intermediate chain defines the two major classes Esters and Amides . This distinction is critical for determining metabolism and allergic potential.\\n\\n Esters vs. Amides\\n- Esters Metabolized by plasma pseudocholinesterase to PABA para-aminobenzoic acid . PABA is a common allergen making hypersensitivity reactions more common in this class.\\n- Amides Metabolized in the liver by CYP450 enzymes. Hypersensitivity is extremely rare.\\n\\n mnemonic\\n Amides have Two I s \\n- Esters One i Procaine Cocaine Tetracaine Benzocaine\\n- Amides Two i s L i doca i ne Mep i vaca i ne Bup i vaca i ne Rop i vaca i ne Pr i loca i ne\\n \\n\\n pH and pKa The Onset of Action\\nLocal anesthetics are weak bases pKa 7.5–9.0 . They exist in equilibrium between a non-ionized lipid-soluble form and an ionized water-soluble charged form.\\n- Non-Ionized Form Crosses the nerve membrane Lipid soluble .\\n- Ionized Form Binds to the receptor inside the channel Active form .\\n\\n The Infection Concept \\nInfected tissue is acidic low pH . In an acidic environment the equilibrium shifts towards the ionized form $LA + H^+ \\\\\\\\rightarrow LAH^+$ . Since the ionized form cannot cross the membrane the drug cannot reach its target site. Therefore local anesthetics are less effective in infected/acidic tissue.\\n\\n Potency Duration and Absorption\\n- Potency Defined by Lipid Solubility . Highly lipophilic drugs e.g. Bupivacaine penetrate membranes easily $\\\\\\\\rightarrow$ High Potency.\\n- Duration Defined by Protein Binding and Vascularity .\\n - High Protein Binding = Long Duration Drug stays at receptor .\\n - High Vascularity = Short Duration Drug washed away .\\n- Vasoconstrictors Epinephrine is often added to LAs to cause vasoconstriction. This decreases systemic absorption reduces toxicity and prolongs the duration of action by keeping the drug at the site.\\n\\n correlate\\n Vasoconstrictor Contraindication \\n Epinephrine should NOT be used in tissues with end-arterial supply Fingers Toes Penis Nose due to risk of ischemia and necrosis.\\n Obstetrics Epinephrine is avoided in labor epidurals as vasoconstriction can compromise uteroplacental blood flow and cause maternal hypertension.\\n \\n\\n Clinical Pharmacology of Agents\\n\\n Ester Local Anesthetics\\n1. Procaine Prototype short duration. Metabolized to PABA. Rarely used now.\\n2. Chloroprocaine Rapid onset short duration. Used in epidurals for C-sections.\\n3. Tetracaine Long duration high potency. Used for spinal anesthesia and ophthalmology. Note CSF has little pseudocholinesterase prolonged action Slide 36 .\\n4. Benzocaine Topical only low pKa very lipophilic . Used for mucous membranes e.g. teething endoscopy .\\n5. Cocaine Only LA with intrinsic vasoconstrictive properties blocks NE reuptake . Used Topically for ENT procedures nose/throat to shrink mucosa and reduce bleeding.\\n\\n correlate\\n Methemoglobinemia Benzocaine \\n Benzocaine spray/gel can cause oxidation of iron in hemoglobin $Fe^ 2+ \\\\\\\\rightarrow Fe^ 3+ $ leading to Methemoglobinemia .\\n- Presentation \\ Chocolate brown\\ blood cyanosis shortness of breath.\\n- Treatment Methylene Blue.\\n \\n\\n Amide Local Anesthetics\\n1. Lidocaine The \\ Standard\\ . Rapid onset medium duration. Broad utility local spinal epidural IV antiarrhythmic .\\n2. Mepivacaine Similar to Lidocaine but less vasodilation. Used in dentistry.\\n3. Bupivacaine High potency long duration. Preferred for epidurals post-op pain. High Cardiotoxicity. \\n4. Ropivacaine Similar to Bupivacaine but less cardiotoxic and less motor block good for labor—\\ walking epidural\\ .\\n5. Prilocaine Medium duration. Risk of methemoglobinemia.\\n\\n Toxicity Systemic & Neural \\n\\nLocal Anesthetic Systemic Toxicity LAST occurs when drug levels rise excessively in the blood usually due to accidental IV injection or excessive dose. The toxicity targets the CNS and Cardiovascular systems.\\n\\n Local Anesthetic Systemic Toxicity LAST \\n- Phase 1 CNS Excitation Perioral numbness metallic taste tinnitus agitation seizures.\\n- Phase 2 CNS Depression Coma respiratory arrest.\\n- Phase 3 CV Collapse Hypotension arrhythmias cardiac arrest.\\n\\n highyield\\n LAST progression \\nNumbness/Tinnitus $\\\\\\\\rightarrow$ Seizures $\\\\\\\\rightarrow$ Coma $\\\\\\\\rightarrow$ Cardiac Arrest.\\n Key Concept CNS signs usually precede CV signs Warning sign! EXCEPT with Bupivacaine where they can happen simultaneously.\\n \\n\\n correlate\\n Bupivacaine Cardiotoxicity \\n Bupivacaine has high affinity for cardiac Na+ channels and dissociates slowly \\ Fast In Slow Out\\ .\\n- Risk Severe refractory arrhythmias and cardiovascular collapse.\\n- Avoid Do not use for IV regional anesthesia Bier Block .\\n- Rescue Intralipid Emulsion Lipid Rescue acts as a lipid sink to soak up the lipophilic drug.\\n \\n\\n Specific Neural Toxicities\\n1. Transient Neurologic Symptoms TNS \\n - Associated with Lidocaine Spinal .\\n - Presentation Pain/dysesthesia in buttocks/legs resolving in days.\\n - Prognosis Full recovery no permanent damage.\\n2. Cauda Equina Syndrome \\n - Associated with Lidocaine Spinal Continuous Microcatheters - historical .\\n - Presentation Bowel/bladder dysfunction saddle anesthesia paralysis.\\n - Prognosis Potential for permanent damage.\\n\\n Hematologic Toxicity\\n- Methemoglobinemia Caused by Prilocaine metabolite o-toluidine and Benzocaine .\\n\\n Allergies\\n- Esters High risk due to PABA . Cross-reactivity exists among esters.\\n- Amides Very rare. No cross-reactivity with esters. If allergic to one amide may try another preservative-free .\\n questions 1. A 25-year-old woman in labor requests an epidural. The anesthesiologist selects an agent that provides adequate sensory analgesia but minimizes motor blockade to allow her to participate in labor. Which agent was most likely chosen? Tetracaine Ropivacaine Procaine Cocaine Benzocaine 1 Ropivacaine is a long-acting amide local anesthetic similar to bupivacaine but with less motor blockade at analgesic doses. 2. A patient undergoing a dental procedure receives a potentially toxic dose of a local anesthetic. Which early sign would most likely alert the dentist to impending systemic toxicity? Cardiovascular collapse Respiratory depression Perioral numbness and metallic taste Profound hypotension Bradycardia 2 CNS toxicity typically precedes CV toxicity. Early symptoms include circumoral numbness tinnitus and metallic taste. 3. Which local anesthetic is an ester that is metabolized to a product known to cause hypersensitivity reactions? Lidocaine Bupivacaine Mepivacaine Tetracaine Ropivacaine 3 Tetracaine is an ester one i . Esters are metabolized to PABA which is immunogenic. 4. A child is brought to the ER with cyanosis and shortness of breath after his parents applied a generous amount of a teething gel. Blood appears chocolate-brown. Which agent is implicated? Lidocaine Benzocaine Bupivacaine Ropivacaine Procaine 1 Benzocaine can cause Methemoglobinemia oxidizes Fe2+ to Fe3+ presenting with cyanosis and chocolate-brown blood. 5. Why is epinephrine often co-administered with lidocaine for infiltration anesthesia? To increase the pH of the solution To preventing oxidation of lidocaine To reduce the pain of injection To prolong the duration of action via vasoconstriction To block potassium channels 3 Epinephrine is a vasoconstrictor; it keeps the drug at the site longer prolonging duration and reducing systemic absorption. Which class of local anesthetics has a higher risk of allergic reactions? Esters due to PABA metabolite Concept What is the mechanism of action of local anesthetics? Block intracellular voltage-gated Na+ channels Open/Inactivated states Concept Which nerve fibers are blocked first? Small myelinated fibers Sympathetic Concept What is the treatment for Local Anesthetic Systemic Toxicity LAST ? IV Lipid Emulsion Intralipid Clinical Which local anesthetic causes methemoglobinemia? Prilocaine and Benzocaine Clinical Which local anesthetic is the most cardiotoxic? Bupivacaine Clinical Why is epinephrine added to local anesthetics? To cause vasoconstriction prolong duration and decrease systemic absorption Concept What is the primary metabolism of Amide anesthetics? Hepatic CYP450 Concept What is the primary metabolism of Ester anesthetics? Plasma pseudocholinesterase Concept Why are local anesthetics less effective in infected tissue? Acidic pH causes ionization; ionized drug cannot cross membrane Concept What is a specific side effect of spinal Lidocaine? Transient Neurologic Symptoms TNS Clinical Which ion channel state do LAs bind to most strongly? Inactivated state and Open Concept BandB Neurology > Pharmacology > Local Anesthetics FirstAid Pharmacology > Anesthetics > Local Anesthetics SketchyPharm Autonomic Drugs > Local Anesthetics Pathoma Systemic > Pharmacology Esters vs Amides Rule If the drug name has two i s it s an Amide Lidocaine Bupivacaine . If it has one i it s an Ester Procaine Cocaine . Infection and pH Local anesthetics are ineffective in infected tissue because the acidic environment ionizes the drug preventing it from crossing the nerve membrane. Bupivacaine Toxicity Bupivacaine is severely cardiotoxic. Resuscitation is difficult; use Lipid Rescue Intralipid . Order of Block Small myelinated > Small unmyelinated > Large myelinated > Large unmyelinated. Pain is lost before Touch which is lost before Motor. Cocaine s Unique Feature Cocaine is the only LA that causes vasoconstriction blocks NE reuptake . All others are vasodilators often require Epinephrine . mindmap \\n- Mechanism \\n - Block Na+ channels Intracellular .\\n - Prefer Open/Inactivated states.\\n - Use-Dependent High frequency = more block .\\n- Structure \\n - Esters 1 i PABA metabolite Allergy . Pseudocholinesterase.\\n - Amides 2 i s Hepatic metabolism.\\n- Pharmacokinetics \\n - pH Acidic tissue infection = Ionized drug = No effect.\\n - Potency = Lipid Solubility.\\n - Duration = Protein Binding + Vasoconstrictors Epi .\\n- Toxicity LAST \\n - CNS Numbness -> Seizures.\\n - CV Hypotension -> Arrest.\\n - Bupivacaine Cardiotoxic Lipid Rescue .\\n - Benzocaine/Prilocaine Methemoglobinemia.\\n anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l67", "title": "Lecture #67: Pharmacology: Opioid Analgesics", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l67 module Neuro l67 Lecture 67 Pharmacology Opioid Analgesics lecturer M. Pino Ph.D. Neuropharmacology session Lecture 67 prev l66 next l68 pdf pdfs/Lecture 67. M. Pino Ph.D. PPT .pdf highYieldPdf content/L67 HighYield.pdf transcript transcripts/Lecture 67 Pharmacology Opioid Analgesics Faculty M. Pino Ph.D..srt Lecture 67 Pharmacology Opioid Analgesics\\n\\n Lecturer M. Pino Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 67\\n\\n Download High Yield PDF ./L67 HighYield.pdf \\n\\n Introduction & Mechanism Slide 2-3 \\n\\nOpioids are the cornerstone of management for moderate to severe pain. Derived initially from the opium poppy Papaver somniferum these agents work by mimicking endogenous peptides endorphins enkephalins dynorphins at specific receptors in the central and peripheral nervous systems. To understand their pharmacology think of the \\ Mu\\ $\\\\mu$ receptor as the primary switch for analgesia—but also for the major side effects like respiratory depression and dependence.\\n\\nWhen an opioid binds to a G-protein coupled receptor GPCR on a neuron two things happen to stop the pain signal \\n1. Presynaptically It closes voltage-gated $Ca^ 2+ $ channels preventing the release of excitatory neurotransmitters glutamate substance P .\\n2. Postsynaptically It opens $K^+$ channels causing potassium efflux and hyperpolarization making the neuron harder to excite.\\n\\nThis dual mechanism effectively \\ silences\\ the pain pathway. However because these receptors are ubiquitous the \\ silence\\ extends to the gut constipation the brainstem respiratory centers apnea and the inhibitory pathways in the midbrain euphoria/reward .\\n\\n highyield\\n Mechanism of Action \\n- Receptors Mu $\\\\mu$ Delta $\\\\delta$ Kappa $\\\\kappa$ . All are Gi/Go-coupled GPCRs .\\n- Presynaptic Blocks $Ca^ 2+ $ channels $\\\\rightarrow$ $\\\\downarrow$ Neurotransmitter release.\\n- Postsynaptic Opens $K^+$ channels $\\\\rightarrow$ Hyperpolarization $\\\\rightarrow$ $\\\\downarrow$ Neuronal firing.\\n- Result Inhibits pain transmission in the Dorsal Horn and stimulates descending inhibitory pathways.\\n \\n\\n Opioid Receptors Slide 5-6 \\n\\n| Receptor | Key Effects | Endogenous Ligand |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Mu $\\\\mu$ | Analgesia supraspinal/spinal Respiratory Depression Euphoria Sedation Constipation Miosis Dependence | Endorphins |\\n| Kappa $\\\\kappa$ | Analgesia spinal Sedation Dysphoria Hallucinations Psychotomimetic | Dynorphins |\\n| Delta $\\\\delta$ | Analgesia antidepressants effects | Enkephalins |\\n\\n Classification of Opioids Slide 7-12 \\n\\nOpioids are classified by their origin Natural vs. Synthetic and their intrinsic activity at the receptor Full Agonist vs. Partial Agonist vs. Antagonist .\\n\\n 1. Strong Agonists Full Mu Agonists \\nThese drugs have high affinity and high intrinsic activity at the $\\\\mu$ receptor. They are used for severe pain.\\n\\n- Morphine The prototype. Used for severe pain MI cancer trauma . Metabolized to Morphine-6-Glucuronide M6G active potent and Morphine-3-Glucuronide M3G inactive/neuroexcitatory .\\n- Methadone Full agonist with Unique Properties \\n - Long half-life variable 15-40+ hours allowing for once-daily dosing.\\n - NMDA antagonist activity helps with neuropathic pain/tolerance .\\n - Use Opioid detoxification prevents withdrawal and maintenance; chronic pain.\\n - Toxicity Can cause QT prolongation Torsades de Pointes .\\n- Fentanyl Highly lipophilic 100x more potent than morphine. Rapid onset short duration redistribution . Used in anesthesia chronic pain patches and breakthrough pain lollipop/lozenge . No histamine release cardiac stable .\\n- Meperidine Antimuscarinic effects tachycardia dry mouth . Metabolite Normeperidine accumulates in renal failure $\\\\rightarrow$ Seizures . Contraindicated with MAOIs Serotonin Syndrome . Lecturer Note Rarely used now due to toxicity. \\n- Hydromorphone / Oxymorphone Potent semi-synthetics.\\n\\n 2. Moderate/Weak Agonists\\nUsed for mild-to-moderate pain or cough. often combined with NSAIDs/Acetaminophen.\\n\\n- Codeine Prodrug. Metabolized to Morphine by CYP2D6 .\\n - Pharmacogenetics Ultra-rapid metabolizers high CYP2D6 can have fatal overdose respiratory depression . Poor metabolizers get no analgesia.\\n - Use Antitussive cough suppression and mild pain.\\n- Oxycodone / Hydrocodone Oral agents. Oxycodone is metabolized by CYP2D6/3A4. often formulated with acetaminophen Percocet/Vicodin .\\n- Tramadol Dual Mechanism Weak $\\\\mu$ agonist + Inhibits Serotonin/NE reuptake.\\n - Risk Seizures lowers threshold and Serotonin Syndrome .\\n\\n 3. Mixed Agonist-Antagonists\\nDesigned to provide analgesia with a \\ ceiling effect\\ on respiratory depression and lower abuse potential. However they can precipitate withdrawal in patients dependent on full agonists.\\n\\n- Buprenorphine Partial $\\\\mu$ agonist $\\\\kappa$ antagonist.\\n - High Affinity Slow Dissociation Hard to reverse with Naloxone.\\n - Use Opioid Use Disorder Suboxone = Buprenorphine + Naloxone and pain.\\n - Ceiling Effect Safer therapeutic index for respiratory depression.\\n- Nalbuphine / Pentazocine $\\\\kappa$ agonist $\\\\mu$ antagonist.\\n - Pentazocine Causes Dysphoria/Psychosis via $\\\\kappa$ activation.\\n\\n correlate\\n Precipitated Withdrawal \\nAdministering a partial agonist Buprenorphine or mixed agonist-antagonist Pentazocine to a patient physically dependent on a full agonist Morphine/Heroin will displace the full agonist from the receptor.\\n- Result Immediate severe withdrawal symptoms pain agitation diarrhea .\\n \\n\\n 4. Antagonists\\n- Naloxone Short-acting IV/Intranasal. Used for Acute Overdose .\\n - Clinical Pearl Duration 1-2 hrs is shorter than most opioids; monitor for re-narcotization .\\n- Naltrexone Long-acting Oral/IM. Used for Maintenance relapse prevention in Opioid and Alcohol Use Disorder.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Pharmacokinetics & Adverse Effects Slide 15-20 \\n\\n Metabolism\\n- Hepatic glucuronidation is the main pathway.\\n- Renal Excretion Metabolites M6G M3G Normeperidine accumulate in kidney failure.\\n - Caution Avoid Morphine and Meperidine in renal failure. Use Fentanyl clean metabolism or Hydromorphone.\\n\\n Acute Adverse Effects \\ MORPHINES\\ \\n- M iosis Pinpoint pupils - Tolerance does NOT develop. \\n- O rthostatic Hypotension Histamine release .\\n- R espiratory Depression Decreased response to $CO 2$ - Cause of death in overdose. \\n- P hysical Dependence/Pain control.\\n- H istamine Release Itching/Pruritus hypotension - Morphine/Codeine/Meperidine. \\n- I ncreased ICP Head injury contraindication - secondary to increased $pCO 2$ causing vasodilation .\\n- N ausea/Vomiting Stimulates CTZ .\\n- E uphoria.\\n- S edation.\\n\\n Chronic Adverse Effects\\n- Constipation Receptors in GI tract decrease motility. Tolerance does NOT develop. Regimen needed Senna/Stool Softener .\\n- Tolerance Needing higher doses for same effect. Develops to Analgesia Euphoria Sedation Respiratory Depression.\\n - NO Tolerance Miosis Constipation.\\n\\n Opioid Toxidrome The \\ Triad\\ \\n1. Coma CNS Depression \\n2. Miosis Pinpoint Pupils \\n3. Respiratory Depression Cyanosis Apnea \\n\\n correlate\\n Serotonin Syndrome \\nCombining Meperidine Tramadol or Dextromethorphan with MAOIs/SSRIs/Linezolid can trigger Serotonin Syndrome.\\n- Presentation Hyperthermia Autonomic Instability Hyperreflexia/Clonus Agitation.\\n- Libby Zion Case Caused by Meperidine + MAOI.\\n \\n\\n Clinical Uses beyond Pain\\n\\n1. Cough Suppression Antitussive \\n - Dextromethorphan NMDA antagonist isomer of codeine analog. No analgesic/addictive properties at varying doses.\\n - Codeine Effective at sub-analgesic doses.\\n2. Diarrhea \\n - Loperamide Imodium $\\\\mu$ agonist that does not cross BBB. Acts locally on gut.\\n - Diphenoxylate Combined with Atropine to discourage abuse.\\n questions 1. A 65-year-old male with end-stage renal disease requires opioid analgesia for a fracture. Which agent is the safest choice to avoid metabolite accumulation and toxicity? Morphine Meperidine Fentanyl Codeine Hydrocodone 2 Fentanyl is metabolized by the liver to inactive metabolites that are nontoxic making it safer for patients with renal failure. Morphine and Meperidine have toxic metabolites that accumulate. 2. A patient chronically taking high-dose morphine for cancer pain arrives with respiratory depression and pinpoint pupils. Naloxone is administered stabilizing breathing. Which of the following effects of morphine will persist despite chronic use i.e. minimal tolerance develops ? Euphoria Sedation Constipation Nausea Analgesia 2 Tolerance develops to most opioid effects analgesia sedation respiratory depression euphoria . Little to no tolerance develops to Constipation and Miosis . 3. A heroin addict is admitted for surgery. To control pain the resident administers an analgesic that unexpectedly precipitates severe agitation abdominal cramps and dilated pupils. Which drug was likely given? Methadone Fentanyl Buprenorphine Hydromorphone Oxycodone 2 Buprenorphine is a partial mu agonist . In a patient tolerant to a full agonist heroin a partial agonist acts as a net antagonist precipitating acute withdrawal. 4. Which opioid is associated with serotonin syndrome when combined with SSRIs and can lower the seizure threshold? Morphine Fentanyl Tramadol Codeine Methadone 2 Tramadol has a dual mechanism weak mu agonism + inhibition of Serotonin/NE reuptake. This affects seizure threshold and serotonin syndrome risk. 5. Which agent works as an anti-diarrheal by stimulating mu receptors in the GI tract without crossing the blood-brain barrier? Dextromethorphan Loperamide Diphenoxylate Naloxone Methylnaltrexone 1 Loperamide is a peripheral mu agonist that does not cross the BBB. It slows gut motility to treat diarrhea without causing CNS effects. What is the primary mechanism of action of Opioids? Presynaptic Close Ca2+ channels decrease neurotransmitter release ; Postsynaptic Open K+ channels hyperpolarization Concept Which opioid adverse effects do NOT develop tolerance? Constipation and Miosis Pinpoint pupils Clinical What is the specific toxicity of Meperidine? Seizures due to toxic metabolite Normeperidine Clinical Which opioid can prolong the QT interval? Methadone Clinical Why is Buprenorphine used in opioid maintenance? Partial agonist with long duration and ceiling effect on respiratory depression Concept What drug is used to reverse acute opioid overdose? Naloxone Mu antagonist Clinical Which opioid is safe to use in renal failure? Fentanyl hepatic metabolism no active metabolites Clinical What is the classical triad of Opioid Overdose? Coma Miosis Pinpoint pupils Respiratory Depression Clinical Which opioid has dual action as a Mu agonist and Serotonin/NE reuptake inhibitor? Tramadol Concept What happens if you give a partial agonist Buprenorphine to a heroin addict? Precipitated Withdrawal Clinical Which opioid is used primarily as an antitussive cough suppressant ? Dextromethorphan or Codeine Clinical What is the mechanism of Loperamide? Peripheral Mu agonist does not cross BBB ; slows gut motility Concept SketchyPharm Smooth Muscle & CNS > Opioids > Opioid Agonists BandB Neurology > Pharmacology > Opioids FirstAid Pharmacology > Toxicities and Side Effects > Opioid Withdrawal Pathoma N/A Tolerance Exceptions Tolerance develops to analgesia euphoria and respiratory depression but NOT to Constipation or Miosis. Renal Failure Safety Avoid Morphine M6G/M3G and Meperidine Normeperidine in renal failure. Use Fentanyl. Meperidine Risks Causes Seizures Normeperidine and Serotonin Syndrome with MAOIs . Mydriasis antimuscarinic instead of miosis. Tramadol s Dual Danger Tramadol lowers seizure threshold and can cause Serotonin Syndrome. Precipitated Withdrawal Buprenorphine is a partial agonist with high affinity. It kicks full agonists off the receptor causing immediate withdrawal in dependent patients. mindmap \\n- Mechanism \\n - Block Na+ channels Intracellular .\\n - Prefer Open/Inactivated states.\\n - Use-Dependent High frequency = more block .\\n- Structure \\n - Esters 1 i PABA metabolite Allergy . Pseudocholinesterase.\\n - Amides 2 i s Hepatic metabolism.\\n- Pharmacokinetics \\n - pH Acidic tissue infection = Ionized drug = No effect.\\n - Potency = Lipid Solubility.\\n - Duration = Protein Binding + Vasoconstrictors Epi .\\n- Toxicity LAST \\n - CNS Numbness -> Seizures.\\n - CV Hypotension -> Arrest.\\n - Bupivacaine Cardiotoxic Lipid Rescue .\\n - Benzocaine/Prilocaine Methemoglobinemia.\\n anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l68", "title": "Lecture #68: Physiology: Drug Addiction", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l68 module Neuro l68 Lecture 68 Physiology Drug Addiction lecturer G. Otazu Ph.D. Neuropharmacology session Lecture 68 prev l67 next l69 pdf pdfs/Lecture 68. G. Otazu Ph.D. PPT .pdf highYieldPdf content/L68 HighYield Render.pdf transcript content/L68 FullTranscript.txt Lecture 68 Physiology Drug Addiction\\n\\n Lecturer G. Otazu Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 68\\n\\n---\\n\\n Introduction to Drug Addiction Slide 2-3 \\n\\nDrug addiction remains a critical public health crisis with over 64 000 overdose deaths reported in 2015 alone. It is a leading cause of accidental death often initiating in young adulthood around age 20 with gateway substances like marijuana. Beyond the staggering human toll the economic burden—including healthcare lost productivity and crime—is estimated at $740 billion annually.\\n\\nTo understand addiction we must move beyond the stigma and view it through a physiological lens. Addiction is defined as a chronic relapsing disease characterized by compulsive drug seeking and use despite harmful consequences. It is not merely a failure of willpower but a fundamental hijacking of the brain s reward circuitry. The process begins with recreational use which in susceptible individuals progresses to compulsive use. The cycle of addiction involves three stages binge/intoxication driven by the reward pathway withdrawal/negative affect driven by the stress pathway and preoccupation/anticipation driven by executive dysfunction and craving .\\n\\n The Mesolimbic Reward Pathway Slide 6-8 \\n\\n The core of addiction pathology lies in the Mesolimbic Dopamine Pathway . This system evolved to reinforce behaviors essential for survival such as eating drinking and reproduction. When we engage in these activities the brain releases dopamine the \\ learning signal\\ that says \\ This was good do it again.\\ \\n\\nAnatomy is destiny here. The pathway originates in the Ventral Tegmental Area VTA of the midbrain. Dopaminergic neurons from the VTA project to the Nucleus Accumbens NAc in the limbic system. This VTA-NAc connection is the primary highway of reward. However the system is more complex involving \\n- Amygdala Processes emotions and stress conditioned learning .\\n- Hippocampus Encodes context and memory e.g. remembering where drugs were used .\\n- Prefrontal Cortex Governing executive function planning and decision-making.\\n\\n highyield\\n The Mesolimbic Pathway \\n- Origin Ventral Tegmental Area VTA in the midbrain.\\n- Target Nucleus Accumbens NAc in the limbic system.\\n- Neurotransmitter Dopamine DA .\\n- Function Reinforcement Learning Reward Prediction Error .\\n \\n\\n Reward Prediction Error Slide 10-12 \\nWait isn t dopamine just \\ pleasure\\ ? Not exactly. Seminal experiments by Wolfram Schultz revealed that dopamine neurons signal Reward Prediction Error .\\n1. Unexpected Reward Dopamine fires strongly Positive error . The brain learns \\ Surprise reward! Learn this context.\\ \\n2. Predicted Reward Dopamine firing shifts to the cue stimulus not the reward itself. The brain expects the reward.\\n3. Omitted Reward Dopamine firing drops below baseline Negative error . The brain learns \\ Prediction failed. Update behavior.\\ \\n\\nDrugs of abuse bypass this natural calibration. They cause a massive pharmacological release of dopamine that never habituates . The brain receives a constant \\ Better than expected!\\ signal cementing the drug-taking behavior as a survival priority outweighing food or safety.\\n\\n Mechanisms of Drug Classes Slide 14 \\n\\nDrugs of abuse can be categorized by their specific molecular targets in the reward pathway. While they all ultimately increase dopamine in the Nucleus Accumbens they get there by different routes.\\n\\n Class 1 Psychostimulants Direct Sympathomimetics \\nThese agents directly increase monoamine concentration in the synaptic cleft.\\n\\n Amphetamines Slide 16-17 \\nAmphetamines are molecular trojan horses. They resemble dopamine and enter the presynaptic neuron via the Dopamine Transporter DAT . Once inside they invade synaptic vesicles via VMAT2 displacing endogenous dopamine into the cytoplasm.\\n- Mechanism \\n 1. Enters via DAT.\\n 2. Displaces DA from vesicles.\\n 3. Reverses DAT The high intracellular DA concentration causes DAT to pump DA out into the synapse reverse transport .\\n 4. Result Massive non-impulse-dependent DA release.\\n- Intoxication Euphoria psychomotor agitation insomnia tachycardia mydriasis dilated pupils .\\n- Withdrawal \\ The Crash\\ —depression lethargy increased appetite sleep disturbance.\\n\\n Cocaine Slide 18-20 \\nCocaine is a blockade artist. unlike amphetamine it does not enter the neuron to work.\\n- Mechanism Binds to and blocks DAT and NET/SERT . This prevents reuptake trapping dopamine in the synapse.\\n- Unique Feature Cocaine is a locally anesthetic Na+ channel blocker and a Vasoconstrictor blocks NE reuptake .\\n- Clinical Consequence Cocaine chest pain is due to coronary artery vasospasm .\\n- Complication Ischemia myocardial infarction intracranial hemorrhage.\\n- Micro-aneurysms Chronic use can cause \\ crack lung\\ or cerebral micro-aneurysms.\\n\\n correlate\\n Cocaine-Induced Chest Pain \\n- Pathophysiology Inhibition of biochemical reuptake of Norepinephrine NE $\\\\rightarrow$ Excess NE stimulation of Alpha-1 receptors $\\\\rightarrow$ Vasoconstriction.\\n- Presentation Chest pain hypertension agitation.\\n- Treatment Benzodiazepines first-line Nitroglycerin Phentolamine.\\n- CONTRAINDICATION Beta-Blockers are contraindicated!\\n- Why? Blocking Beta-2 vasodilatory receptors leaves Alpha-1 vasoconstrictive receptors unopposed $\\\\rightarrow$ Worsening hypertension and ischemia \\ Unopposed Alpha Stimulation\\ .\\n \\n\\n Nicotine Slide 22 \\n- Mechanism Agonist at Nicotinic Acetylcholine Receptors nAChRs on VTA dopamine neurons.\\n- Effect Direct depolarization of VTA neurons $\\\\rightarrow$ increased firing $\\\\rightarrow$ DA release in NAc.\\n- Intoxication Restlessness anxiety.\\n- Withdrawal Irritability anxiety craving increased appetite.\\n\\n Class 2 Depressants GABA-ergic Agents \\nThese drugs act like the brakes of the brain primarily targeting the GABA-A receptor.\\n\\n Alcohol Ethanol Slide 24-27 \\n- Mechanism Enhances GABA-A receptor activity inhibitory and inhibits NMDA glutamate receptors excitatory .\\n- Intoxication Disinhibition ataxia slurred speech blackouts.\\n- Chronic Sequelae Cirrhosis Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome peripheral neuropathy.\\n- Withdrawal Tremors anxiety seizures \\ Rum fits\\ and the life-threatening Delirium Tremens .\\n\\n correlate\\n Delirium Tremens DTs \\n- Pathophysiology Chronic alcohol use downregulates GABA receptors and upregulates NMDA receptors to maintain homeostasis . Abrupt cessation leads to massive overexcitation.\\n- Timing 2-4 days 48-96 hours after last drink.\\n- Presentation Altered mental status autonomic instability fever tachycardia hypertension hallucinations.\\n- Treatment Benzodiazepines Chlordiazepoxide Diazepam Lorazepam .\\n- Lecturer s Point This is a medical emergency with high mortality if untreated.\\n \\n\\n Benzodiazepines & Barbiturates Slide 29-32 \\nBoth bind allosterically to the GABA-A receptor chloride channel but at different sites.\\n- Benzodiazepines Increase the Frequency of channel opening. \\ Frenzodiazepines\\ \\n- Barbiturates Increase the Duration of channel opening. \\ Barbi-durates\\ \\n- Addiction Mechanism How do inhibitors cause dopamine release? via Disinhibition .\\n - VTA dopamine neurons are normally kept in check by local GABAergic interneurons.\\n - Benzos/Barbs bind to GABA-A receptors on these interneurons .\\n - Inhibiting the inhibitor Interneuron $\\\\rightarrow$ Releases the brake on the DA neuron $\\\\rightarrow$ DA firing increases.\\n- Receptor Nuance Slide 31 The GABA interneurons in the VTA have Alpha-1 containing GABA-A receptors. VTA dopamine neurons have Alpha-3. Benzos preferentially target Alpha-1 explaining why they silence the interneuron but not the DA neuron itself.\\n\\n Class 3 Opioids Slide 34-36 \\n- Agents Heroin Morphine Fentanyl Oxycodone.\\n- Mechanism Agonists at the Mu-Opioid Receptor Gi-coupled .\\n- Pathway Similar to repressants they work via Disinhibition . They bind Mu receptors on GABA interneurons in the VTA $\\\\rightarrow$ hyperpolarization decrease GABA release $\\\\rightarrow$ VTA DA neurons fire freely.\\n- Intoxication \\ Pinpoint pupils\\ Miosis respiratory depression CNS depression constipation euphoria.\\n\\n correlate\\n Opioid Withdrawal \\n- Pathophysiology Upregulation of cAMP/NE pathways to counteract chronic inhibition. Removal of drug leads to noradrenergic storm.\\n- Presentation Dilated pupils mydriasis piloerection \\ cold turkey\\ rhinorrhea lacrimation yawning nausea/vomiting diarrhea.\\n- Lecturer s Point While alcohol withdrawal can kill you seizures opioid withdrawal makes you want to die misery but is rarely fatal.\\n- Neonatal Abstinence Syndrome Newborns of dependent mothers presenting with high-pitched cry tremors and irritability.\\n \\n\\n Class 4 Hallucinogens & Others\\n\\n Phencyclidine PCP Slide 38 \\n- Mechanism Non-competitive antagonist at NMDA Glutamate receptors .\\n- Presentation Violent behavior nystagmus vertical/horizontal impulsivity analgesia psychosis.\\n- Complication Trauma due to analgesia/aggression rhabdomyolysis.\\n\\n MDMA Ecstasy Slide 43 \\n- Mechanism Functions like amphetamine reverses transporters but with high affinity for SERT Serotonin Transporter .\\n- Effect Massive release of Serotonin 5-HT > Dopamine/NE.\\n- Intoxication Euphoria emotional closeness empathogen hyperthermia hyponatremia due to excess water intake + SIADH-like effect .\\n- Complication Serotonin Syndrome especially if combined with SSRIs .\\n\\n Marijuana THC Slide 41 \\n- Mechanism Partial agonist at CB1 Cannabinoid receptors .\\n- Action CB1 receptors are presynaptic and Gi-coupled. They inhibit GABA release Disinhibition in the VTA.\\n- Intoxication Euphoria increased appetite \\ munchies\\ conjunctival injection red eyes dry mouth slowed reaction time.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Summary of Mechanisms Table\\n\\n| Drug Class | Primary Target | Mechanism on Reward Pathway | Key Intoxication Sign |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Amphetamine | DAT / VMAT2 | Reverses DAT; Displaces DA from vesicles | Agitation Pupil Dilation |\\n| Cocaine | DAT Transporter | Blocks Reuptake of DA/NE/5-HT | Chest Pain Pupil Dilation |\\n| Opioids | Mu-Receptor | Disinhibition inhibits GABA interneuron | Pinpoint Pupils Resp Depression |\\n| Benzos | GABA-A Alpha-1 | Disinhibition inhibits GABA interneuron | Sedation Resp Depression |\\n| Nicotine | nAChR | Direct depolarization of VTA neuron | Alertness Anxiety |\\n| PCP | NMDA Receptor | Antagonist Blocks Glutamate | Nystagmus Violence |\\n| MDMA | SERT > DAT | Reverses SERT Massive 5-HT release | Hyperthermia Hyponatremia |\\n| Marijuana | CB1 Receptor | Disinhibition Presynaptic inhibition of GABA | Red Eyes Munchies |\\n\\n mnemonic\\n Pupil Changes \\n- Miosis Constriction Opioids Cholinergics. \\ Pinpoint requires opioids\\ \\n- Mydriasis Dilation Amphetamines Cocaine LSD Withdrawal from Opioids. \\ Wide eyed excitement\\ \\n \\n questions 1. A 22-year-old male is brought to the ED by friends after a rave. He is agitated hypertensive and hyperthermic 104°F . Sodium is 120 mEq/L. He reports feeling \\ connected to everyone\\ earlier. Which neurotransmitter mechanism is primarily responsible for his condition? Direct agonism of GABA-A receptors Reversal of Serotonin SERT transporters Antagonism of NMDA receptors Inhibition of Voltage-gated Na+ channels Agonism of Mu-opioid receptors 1 MDMA Ecstasy causes massive release of Serotonin by reversing SERT. It presents with hyperthermia hyponatremia excess water intake and empathy. 2. A 35-year-old man presents with severe chest pain and agitation. ECG shows ST depression. Blood pressure is 180/110 mmHg. He admits to using cocaine. Which medication is CONTRAINDICATED? Lorazepam Nitroglycerin Phentolamine Metoprolol Verapamil 3 Beta-blockers Metoprolol are contraindicated in cocaine toxicity due to \\ Unopposed Alpha Stimulation \\ which can worsen vasoconstriction and hypertension. 3. Which drug of abuse works by acting as a non-competitive antagonist at the NMDA glutamate receptor? LSD Heroin PCP Phencyclidine Cocaine Methamphetamine 2 PCP acts as an antagonist at NMDA receptors . Key signs include nystagmus vertical/horizontal and violent behavior. 4. A patient in the ED presents with rhinorrhea lacrimation dilated pupils and severe abdominal cramping. He is yawning frequently. Withdrawal from which substance is most likely? Alcohol Cocaine Heroin Benzodiazepines Marijuana 2 Opioid Heroin withdrawal presents with flu-like symptoms rhinorrhea lacrimation yawning dilated pupils and GI distress. 5. Benzodiazepines increase dopamine release in the Nucleus Accumbens via which mechanism? Direct stimulation of D2 receptors Inhibition of GABAergic interneurons in the VTA Disinhibition Blocking the Dopamine Transporter DAT Stimulating Nicotinic receptors on VTA neurons Reversing VMAT2 transport 1 Benzodiazepines inhibit GABAergic interneurons via Alpha-1 GABA-A receptors in the VTA. This removes inhibition on the DA neurons Disinhibition allowing them to fire. What is the primary neurotransmitter of the reward pathway? Dopamine Concept Where do the dopaminergic neurons of the reward pathway originate? Ventral Tegmental Area VTA Concept What is the mechanism of action of Cocaine? Blocks reuptake of Dopamine NE and Serotonin Blocks DAT/NET/SERT Concept What is the mechanism of action of Amphetamines? Enters neuron via DAT and reverses it pumps DA out ; displaces DA from vesicles Concept Why is Methadone used for opioid withdrawal? It is a long-acting Mu-opioid agonist prevents withdrawal without high Clinical What are the classic trio of signs for Opioid overdose? Pinpoint pupils Miosis Respiratory Depression Coma CNS depression Clinical What drug causes vertical/horizontal nystagmus and violent behavior? PCP Phencyclidine Clinical Which receptors does Alcohol primarily affect? Enhances GABA-A inhibitory and inhibits NMDA excitatory Concept What is the specific danger of combining Tyramine-rich foods with MAOIs? Hypertensive Crisis Clinical How do Benzodiazepines affect the GABA-A receptor? Increase Frequency of channel opening Concept How do Barbiturates affect the GABA-A receptor? Increase Duration of channel opening Concept What is the treatment for Delirium Tremens? Benzodiazepines Clinical What is the mechanism of MDMA Ecstasy ? Reverses SERT flods synapse with Serotonin Concept Why are Beta-blockers contraindicated in Cocaine toxicity? Unopposed Alpha-1 vasoconstriction worsens hypertension Clinical B&B Psychiatry > Substance Abuse > Substance Abuse FirstAid Psychiatry > Pharmacology > Substance Use SketchyPharm Psych > Substance Abuse Schreber Addiction Module Disinhibition Concept Depressants Opioids Benzos Alcohol increase dopamine by inhibiting the GABAergic interneurons that normally braking the VTA dopamine neurons. Cocaine vs Amphetamine Cocaine BLOCKS the door DAT blocker . Amphetamine BREAKS IN and pushes dopamine out DAT reversal/Vesicle displacement . Beta-Blocker Trap Never give Beta-blockers for Cocaine chest pain. It leaves Alpha receptors unopposed causing severe vasoconstriction. Opioid Withdrawal vs Alcohol Withdrawal Alcohol withdrawal is fatal seizures/DTs . Opioid withdrawal makes you wish you were dead flu-like misery but is rarely fatal. Nystagmus Vertical/Horizontal nystagmus in an agitated patient is pathognomonic for PCP intoxication. mindmap \\n- Mesolimbic Pathway \\n - VTA -> Nucleus Accumbens DA release .\\n - Mechanism Reward Prediction Error.\\n- Stimulants \\n - Cocaine Blocks DAT. Chest pain Vasospasm . No Beta-blockers.\\n - Amphetamine Reverses DAT. Displaces vesicles.\\n - Nicotine Agonist at nAChR VTA .\\n- Depressants Disinhibition \\n - Alcohol GABA-A agonist / NMDA antagonist. DTs Fatal .\\n - Benzos GABA-A Frequency Alpha-1 subunit in VTA .\\n - Opioids Mu-agonist. Pinpoint pupils Resp Depression. Withdrawal = Flu-like.\\n- Others \\n - PCP NMDA Antagonist. Violence Nystagmus.\\n - MDMA SERT Reversal. Serotonin Syndrome Hyponatremia.\\n - Marijuana CB1 Agonist Presynaptic inhibition .\\n anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l69", "title": "Lecture #69: Substance Use Disorders", "module": "DPR", "content": "id l69 module DPR l69 Lecture 69 Substance Use Disorders lecturer A. Coladner D.O. Neuropharmacology session Lecture 69 prev l68 next l70 pdf pdfs/Lecture 69. A. Coladner D.O. PPT .pdf highYieldPdf content/L69 HighYield Render.pdf transcript content/L69 FullTranscript.txt Lecture 69 Substance Use Disorders Lecturer A. Coladner D.O. Session Lecture 69 Substance Use Disorders SUD Overview Substance Use Discovery involves a complex interaction between brain circuitry genetics and environmental factors where an individual continues to use a substance despite significant harm. It is a chronic relapsing brain disease characterized by compulsive drug seeking and use. The DSM-5 defines SUD based on a pathological pattern of behaviors related to the use of the substance. Diagnosis requires meeting specific criteria within a 12-month period and severity is graded based on the count of these criteria met Mild 2-3 Moderate 4-5 and Severe 6+ . The 11 Criteria Grouped Slide 4 Impaired Control Taking the substance in larger amounts or longer than intended. Persistent desire or unsuccessful efforts to cut down. Spending a great deal of time obtaining using or recovering from the substance. Craving A strong desire or urge to use. Social Impairment Recurrent use resulting in failure to fulfill major role obligations work school home . Continued use despite persistent social or interpersonal problems. Important social occupational or recreational activities are given up. Risky Use Recurrent use in situations where it is physically hazardous e.g. driving . Continued use despite knowledge of having a persistent physical or psychological problem caused by the substance. Pharmacological Criteria Tolerance Need for increased amounts to achieve intoxication or diminished effect with the same amount. Withdrawal The characteristic withdrawal syndrome for the substance or taking the substance to relieve withdrawal. Note Tolerance and withdrawal do NOT count as criteria if the medication is taken as prescribed under medical supervision e.g. opioids for chronic pain . highyield Medical Use Exception Tolerance and Withdrawal generally do NOT count toward the diagnosis of a Substance Use Disorder if the substance is being used solely under appropriate medical supervision e.g. a patient on chronic opioids for cancer pain . --- Alcohol Use Disorder Alcohol affects the central nervous system as a depressant. It has profound public health implications contributing significantly to emergency room visits overdoses and deaths annually. Screening and history taking are critical as patients may minimize their use. The disorder is characterized by the inability to stop or control alcohol use despite adverse social occupational or health consequences. Alcohol Intoxication Slide 8-9 Presentation Slurred speech Incoordination / Ataxia Unsteady gait Nystagmus Involuntary eye movement Impaired attention or memory Stupor or Coma Signs Flushed face Conjunctival Injection also in Cannabis decreased level of consciousness. Alcohol Poisoning Slide 10-14 Mechanism Alcohol level overwhelms the liver s metabolic capacity. Signs Respiratory Depression Breathing slows/stops Hypothermia Incontinence Seizures Coma. Loss of gag reflex risk of aspiration . Management Airway protection intubation if necessary . IV Fluids and Glucose Check fingerstick first! . Thiamine Vitamin B1 Administer BEFORE or with glucose to prevent Wernicke s Encephalopathy . Haloperidol Preferred for severe agitation. Alcohol Withdrawal Slide 16-17 Timeline Minor Withdrawal 6-36 hours Tremors The Shakes Anxiety Headache Diaphoresis Palpitations GI upset. Seizures 6-48 hours Generalized tonic-clonic. Alcoholic Hallucinosis 12-48 hours Visual/Tactile hallucinations with intact orientation. Delirium Tremens DTs 48-96 hours MEDICAL EMERGENCY . Delirium Tremens DTs Slide 17 Triad Delirium Confusion/Disorientation + Hallucinations often visual/tactile bugs + Autonomic Instability Fever Tachycardia Hypertension . Mortality High if untreated. correlate Delirium Tremens DTs - Pathophysiology Chronic alcohol use downregulates GABA receptors and upregulates NMDA Glutamate receptors. Abrupt cessation leads to uninhibited excitation Glutamate storm . - Presentation Delirium Confusion Agitation Fever Tachycardia >100 bpm Hypertension Diaphoresis. - Diagnosis Clinical diagnosis based on history of cessation + hyperadrenergic state. - Treatment Benzodiazepines Diazepam Chlordiazepoxide Lorazepam . IV Fluids. - Lecturer s Point Fifteen percent will develop DTs... Treatment is Diazepam. Treatment of Alcohol Use Disorder Detoxification Benzodiazepines Gold Standard . Maintenance Naltrexone Opioid antagonist; reduces cravings and the high from drinking. Acamprosate NMDA modulator. Disulfiram Aldehyde dehydrogenase inhibitor Causes sickness if drinking . --- Opioid Use Disorder Slide 26-36 Opioids bind to mu-opioid receptors to produce analgesia and euphoria. The epidemic of opioid misuse involves natural Morphine Codeine semi-synthetic Heroin Oxycodone and synthetic Fentanyl Methadone varieties. Fentanyl is particularly dangerous due to its extreme potency 50-100x morphine and its clandestine addition to other street drugs. Opioid Intoxication Classic Triad 1. Coma / CNS Depression 2. Respiratory Depression Lethal mechanism 3. Miosis Pinpoint Pupils Other Signs Constipation Slurred speech Impairment in attention. Opioid Withdrawal Mechanism Loss of inhibitory opioid tone leads to Noradrenergic NE storm. Presentation Flu-like illness. Not life-threatening unlike Alcohol/Benzo withdrawal but extremely uncomfortable. Symptoms Dysphoria & Craving. Rhinorrhea Runny nose & Lacrimation Tearing . Yawning . Piloerection Gooseflesh -> term Cold Turkey . Mydriasis Dilated pupils . Muscle aches Diarrhea Nausea/Vomiting. mnemonic Opioid Withdrawal SLUDGE stops flight begins Symptoms are the opposite of intoxication - Intoxication Constipation Miosis Dry. - Withdrawal Diarrhea Mydriasis Rhinorrhea Lacrimation Yawning. Treatment of Opioid Use Disorder Slide 37 Overdose Naloxone Mu-antagonist . Intranasal formulation is widely available. Maintenance Therapy MAT 1. Methadone Full Mu-Agonist . Long half-life. Reduces cravings and withdrawal. Requires daily clinic visits. 2. Buprenorphine Partial Mu-Agonist . High affinity low intrinsic activity. Ceiling effect creates safety profile. Can be prescribed in office Suboxone = Buprenorphine + Naloxone . 3. Naltrexone Mu-Antagonist . Blocks opioid effects. Patient must be fully detoxed first to avoid precipitated withdrawal. --- Stimulant Use Disorder Cocaine Amphetamines Slide 44-53 Stimulants increase synaptic levels of Dopamine Norepinephrine and Serotonin. Cocaine blocks reuptake; Amphetamines increase release. The clinical picture is one of sympathetic overdrive. Stimulant Intoxication Psychological Euphoria Agitation Hypervigilance Grandiosity Paranoia. Physical Mydriasis Dilated pupils . Tachycardia / Hypertension. Chest Pain Coronary vasospasm . Weight loss Chills Sweating. Complications Cardiac Arrest Seizures Stroke. Stimulant Withdrawal The Crash Symptoms Dysphoria / Severe Depression Suicide risk . Fatigue / Psychomotor retardation. Hypersomnia with Vivid unpleasant dreams . Increased Appetite . --- Cannabis Use Disorder Slide 22-24 Cannabis acts on CB1 and CB2 receptors. It is the most commonly used illicit drug. Modern strains have very high THC content leading to higher risks of acute psychosis and anxiety. Cannabis Intoxication Psychological Euphoria High Anxiety Sensation of slowed time Impaired judgment and motor coordination. Physical Signs Conjunctival Injection Red Eyes - Hallmark . Increased Appetite The Munchies . Dry Mouth Cotton mouth . Tachycardia. Cannabis Withdrawal Timeline Starts within ~1 week of cessation. Symptoms Irritability Anger Aggression. Anxiety / Restlessness. Insomnia / Disturbing dreams. Decreased appetite / Weight loss. Depressed mood. --- Nicotine Use Disorder Slide 19-21 Nicotine stimulates nicotinic acetylcholine receptors releasing Dopamine reward . It is highly addictive with relapse rates similar to heroin. Nicotine Withdrawal Symptoms Irritability Frustration Anger. Anxiety . Difficulty Concentrating . Increased Appetite / Weight Gain. Restlessness Insomnia Depressed mood. Treatment Smoking Cessation Varenicline Chantix Slide 20 Mechanism Partial Agonist at alpha-4-beta-2 nicotinic receptors. Effect Reduces cravings agonist activity AND blocks reward from smoking antagonist activity . Efficacy Most effective monotherapy. Bupropion Zyban Slide 20 Mechanism NE/DA Reuptake Inhibitor NDRI . Use Reduces cravings. Contraindicated in seizure disorders. Nicotine Replacement Therapy NRT Patch Gum Lozenge Agonists . --- Sedative-Hypnotic Use Disorder Benzodiazepines Barbiturates Slide 38-42 Mechanism involves potentiation of GABA-A receptors inhibitory . The clinical profile of intoxication and withdrawal mirrors alcohol almost exactly. Intoxication Slurred speech Incoordination Unsteady gait Nystagmus Stupor/Coma. Danger Respiratory depression especially when combined with alcohol Synergistic effect . Withdrawal Warning Life-Threatening Like alcohol . Symptoms Autonomic hyperactivity Sweating Pulse >100 Hand tremor Insomnia Nausea. Severe Grand Mal Seizures Delirium. --- Hallucinogen Use Disorder PCP LSD Slide 24 Phencyclidine PCP Intoxication Behavior Belligerence excessive impulsivity Violence unpredictability. Physical Signs Nystagmus Vertical or Horizontal - Key Differentiator . Analgesia Diminished response to pain -> Superhuman strength . Hypertension Tachycardia Seizures. LSD Lysergic Acid Diethylamide Signs Perceptual changes depersonalization derealization illusions Mydriasis Tachycardia. Not usually violent like PCP. --- Substance Intoxication & Withdrawal Comparison Table | Substance | Intoxication Signs | Withdrawal Signs | Key Life Risks | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | Alcohol | Slurred speech Ataxia Nystagmus | Tremor DTs Autonomic instability Seizures | Withdrawal is Fatal | | Opioids | Miosis Resp Depression Coma | Mydriasis Rhinorrhea Flu-like Yawning | Overdose is Fatal | | Stimulants | Mydriasis Tachycardia Chest Pain | Depression Crash Hunger Sleep | Cardiac Arrest Intox | | Cannabis | Red Eyes Munchies Dry Mouth | Irritability Insomnia No Appetite | Impaired Driving | | Benzos | Same as Alcohol | Same as Alcohol Seizures | Withdrawal is Fatal | | PCP | Nystagmus Vert/Horiz Violence | N/A | Trauma/Violence | questions 1. A 24-year-old male is brought to the ED by police after being found acting aggressively. He is agitated shouting and requires 4-point restraints. Physical exam is remarkable for vertical nystagmus and profound insensitivity to pain. Which substance is most likely responsible? Cocaine Heroin Lysergic acid diethylamide LSD Phencyclidine PCP Methamphetamine 3 PCP intoxication is classically associated with violent functionality/belligerence vertical nystagmus and analgesia high pain tolerance . 2. A 45-year-old female with a history of alcohol use disorder is admitted for surgery. 72 hours post-admission she becomes confused agitated and claims to see insects crawling on the walls. Her vitals are HR 115 BP 160/95 Temp 101.5°F. What is the most appropriate pharmacologic treatment? Haloperidol Diazepam Thiamine only Propranolol Fomepizole 1 The patient is experiencing Delirium Tremens DTs Confusion + Hallucinations + Autonomic Instability . Benzodiazepines e.g. Diazepam Lorazepam are the treatment of choice to substitute for the withdrawal of alcohol s GABAergic effect. 3. A 30-year-old male presents requesting help with smoking cessation. He is concerned about weight gain and craving. Which medication acts as a partial agonist at the alpha-4-beta-2 nicotinic receptor? Nicotine Patch Bupropion Varenicline Nortriptyline Clonidine 2 Varenicline is a partial agonist at the alpha-4-beta-2 nicotinic receptor. It reduces cravings and withdrawal agonist effect while blocking the rewarding effects of nicotine antagonist effect . 4. A patient presents with severe rhinorrhea lacrimation yawning and dilated pupils. They report feeling like they have the \\ worst flu of their life\\ . Which substance withdrawal is this clinical picture most consistent with? Alcohol Cocaine Heroin Cannabis Tobacco 2 Opioid Heroin withdrawal typically presents as a severe flu-like illness with Rhinorrhea Lacrimation Yawning Piloerection and Mydriasis . 5. Which physical exam finding is most specific for Cannabis intoxication? Miosis Vertical Nystagmus Horizontal Nystagmus Conjunctival Injection Bradypnea 3 Conjunctival Injection Red eyes is the most consistent physical sign of acute cannabis intoxication often accompanied by tachycardia and dry mouth. What is the classic triad of Delirium Tremens? Delta Mental Status Delirium Hallucinations Visual/Tactile Autonomic Instability Fever HR^ . Alcohol What is the classic triad of Opioid Overdose? Coma Respiratory Depression Miosis Pinpoint pupils . Opioids Which withdrawal syndromes can be fatal? Alcohol and Sedative-Hypnotics Benzodiazepines/Barbiturates . Safety What is the mechanism of Varenicline? Partial Agonist at alpha-4-beta-2 nicotinic receptors. Pharmacology What is the key physical exam differentiator for PCP intoxication? Nystagmus Vertical or Horizontal . Diagnosis What physical sign distinguishes Opioid Withdrawal from Intoxication? Pupil Size Intoxication = Miosis Pinpoint ; Withdrawal = Mydriasis Dilated . Diagnosis What is the treatment for Alcohol Withdrawal? Benzodiazepines e.g. Diazepam Chlordiazepoxide Lorazepam . Treatment What signs characterize Stimulant Withdrawal? Dysphoria \\ The Crash\\ Hypersomnia Increased Appetite. Stimulants Which sign is hallmark for Cannabis intoxication? Conjunctival Injection Red Eyes . Cannabis Why is Thiamine given before glucose in alcoholics? To prevent precipitation of Wernicke s Encephalopathy. Safety BandB Behavioral Science > Substance Use Disorder > Alcohol Pathoma Fundamentals > Neoplasia Not a direct match check Psych FirstAid Psychiatry > Substance Use SketchyPharm Autonomic Drugs > Parasympathetic Physeo Psychiatry > Substance Use Disorder Pupils The Window to the Drug Pinpoint Miosis Opioids Intoxication .\\n Dilated Mydriasis Stimulants Cocaine/Amphetamines Opioids Withdrawal Hallucinogens LSD . Deadly Withdrawal Remember You can die from withdrawal of Booze Alcohol and Benzos . You will just wish you were dead from Opioid withdrawal. PCP Presentation Violent Super-strength Vertical Nystagmus. Treat with Benzodiazepines and antipsychotics. mindmap Substance Use Disorders\\n\\n Alcohol\\n- Intoxication Slurred speech Ataxia.\\n- Withdrawal Tremors -> Seizures -> DTs Fatal .\\n- Tx Benzos.\\n\\n Opioids\\n- Intoxication Miosis Resp Depression.\\n- Withdrawal Flu-like Mydriasis Yawning.\\n- Tx Naloxone Rescue Methadone/Suboxone Maint .\\n\\n Stimulants\\n- Intoxication Mydriasis Chest Pain Tachycardia.\\n- Withdrawal Sleepy Hungry Depressed.\\n\\n Hallucinogens\\n- PCP Nystagmus Violence.\\n- Cannabis Red Eyes Munchies. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l70", "title": "Lecture 70: Pediatric H&P I (Birth to 5 Years)", "module": "Pediatrics", "content": "id l70 title Lecture 70 Pediatric H&P I Birth to 5 Years lecturer A. Patel D.O. module Pediatrics session Lecture 70 prev l69 next l71 pdf pdfs/Lecture 70. A. Patel D.O. PPT .pdf summary Lecture 70 Pediatric H&P I Birth to 5 Years Story Mode The First Checkup Imagine you are a new pediatric resident. You walk into the room to see a 2-week-old neonate Baby Liam brought in by his exhausted first-time parents. They have a list of questions Is his breathing too fast? What is this rash on his face? Why are his eyes crossed sometimes? Is he eating enough? This lecture is your guide to navigating these visits from the very first newborn exam to the preschool years. You aren t just checking boxes; you are monitoring the incredible trajectory of human development catching silent killers like biliary atresia or congenital heart defects and reassuring parents that yes that rash erythema toxicum is completely normal. The Newborn Exam Head to Toe The newborn exam is likely the most thorough physical exam you will perform. Why? Because babies can t tell you what s wrong. You must be the detective. 1. Vitals & Measurements The Fifth Vital Sign Heart Rate fast! Normal is 120-160 bpm . Respiratory Rate Fast! Normal is 30-60 breaths/min . Growth Charts These are crucial. WHO Growth Charts Use for 0-2 years . CDC Growth Charts Use for 2+ years . Fenton Charts Specifically for preterm infants . We track Length Weight and Head Circumference . Dropping percentiles is a red flag. 2. HEENT Head Fontanelles The soft spots . Anterior closes by 12-15 months ; Posterior closes by 1 month . Clinical Correlate Sunken = Dehydration. Bulging = Increased ICP meningitis hydrocephalus . Shape Caput Succedaneum Edema that crosses suture lines benign resolves in days . Cephalohematoma Blood under periosteum that does NOT cross suture lines resolves in weeks risk of jaundice . Subgaleal Hemorrhage Crosses suture lines massive blood loss possible medical emergency . Craniosynostosis Premature fusion of sutures requiring surgery e.g. Sagittal = Scaphocephaly/Boat head . Plagiocephaly Flat spot from positioning tummy time helps . Eyes Red Reflex Must be present bilaterally. Red Flag Leukocoria White reflex = Retinoblastoma or Congenital Cataracts. Alignment Intermittent strabismus is normal up to 2-4 months . Conjunctivitis Chemical <24 hrs silver nitrate . Gonococcal 2-5 days. Purulent severe. Prevent w/ Erythromycin ointment. Treat w/ IM Ceftriaxone. Chlamydia 5-14 days. Watery/mucoid. Treat w/ Oral Erythromycin/Azithromycin. HSV Keratitis dendrites . Ears Check for pits/tags renal association if other anomalies present and position Low set = Down Syndrome . Nose Obligate nose breathers. Choanal Atresia bony blockage cyanosis with feeding prevents breathing. Mouth Palpate hard/soft palate for clefts. Epstein Pearls benign white cysts are normal. 3. Chest & Lungs Observation Look for retractions or nasal flaring distress . Pectus Excavatum indented chest or Carinatum pigeon chest . Breasts Witch s Milk discharge or hypertrophy is normal due to maternal hormones. Clavicles Palpate for crepitus/step-offs. Fractures are common from birth trauma; treat by pinning sleeve to chest immobilization . 4. Cardiovascular Murmurs Very common. Innocent Systolic soft <3/6 position-dependent louder supine asymptomatic. Pathologic Diastolic loud holosystolic clicks fixed split S2 symptomatic cyanosis failure to thrive . Pulses Palpate Femoral and Brachial . Clinical Correlate Weak/absent femoral pulses = Coarctation of the Aorta . 5. Abdomen & Cord Umbilical Cord Should have 2 Arteries 1 Vein AVA . Single umbilical artery implies renal/genetic anomalies. Falls off in 10-14 days. Delayed separation >2 weeks = Leukocyte Adhesion Deficiency . Omphalitis Erythema around stump = IV antibiotics widely. Granuloma Pink tissue after cord falls off = Treat w/ Silver Nitrate. Hernia Umbilical hernias usually resolve by age 3-5. Masses Wilms Tumor does not cross midline Neuroblastoma crosses midline . 6. Genitourinary GU Male Check for Cryptorchidism undescended testes and urethral opening. Hypospadias Ventral bottom . Do NOT circumcise foreskin used for repair . Epispadias Dorsal top - associated with bladder exstrophy. Hydrocele Fluid in transilluminates . Female Vaginal discharge/bleeding is normal maternal estrogen withdrawal . Fused may need cream. Ambiguous Genitalia Medical emergency! Rule out Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia CAH salt-wasting crisis . 7. Musculoskeletal The Hips Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip DDH . Risk factors Female Breech Family History . Barlow Adduct & Push posterior --> Dislocates Clunk . Ortolani Abduct & Pull anterior --> Relocates Clunk . Galeazzi Knee height discrepancy. Imaging Ultrasound < 4 months; X-ray > 4 months. 8. Skin The Scary but Benign Erythema Toxicum Neonatorum Flea-bitten appearance pustules on red base . Eosinophils. Benign. Transient Pustular Melanosis Pustules that rupture leaving hyperpigmented macules. Benign. Milia Tiny white sebaceous cysts on nose. Benign. Nevus Simplex Stork bite nape or Angel kiss glabella . Fades. Mongolian Spots Congenital Dermal Melanocytosis Blue-grey macules on back/buttocks. Benign document to differentiate from abuse . Hemangioma Vascular tumor. Grows then involutes. Treat with Propranolol if obstructing vision/airway. Seborrheic Dermatitis Cradle Cap . Greasy scales. Treat with oil/brushing. 9. Neuro Primitive Reflexes These should appear at birth and disappear to allow voluntary movement. Persistence often indicates CP or neuro deficit. | Reflex | Description | Disappears By | | --- | --- | --- | | Moro Startle | Drop head back -> Arms extend/abduct then adduct | 4 months | | Rooting | Stroke cheek -> Turns head to side | 4 months | | Palmar Grasp | Finger in palm -> Hand grasps | 3 months | | Plantar Grasp | Pressure on sole -> Toes curl | 8-15 months | | ATNR Fencing | Turn head -> Face arm extends occiput arm flexes | 6 months | | Galant | Stroke spine -> Hips swing to side | 4-6 months | Developmental Milestones High Yield This is the highest yield section for boards! | Age | Gross Motor | Fine Motor | Language | Social | | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | | 2 Mo | Lifts head/chest prone | Tracks past midline | Coos | Social smile | | 4 Mo | Rolls front to back | Grasps rattle | Laughs | Looks at hand | | 6 Mo | Sits tripod Rolls back to front | Transfers objects hand to hand Raking grasp | Babbles | Stranger anxiety begins | | 9 Mo | Crawls Pulls to stand | Pincer grasp thumb-finger | Mama/Dada nonspecific | Wave bye-bye Object permanence | | 12 Mo | Walks first steps | Puts block in cup | Mama/Dada specific + 1 word | Drinks from cup | | 15 Mo | Walks well creeps stairs | Stack 2 blocks | 3-6 words | Uses spoon/fork | | 18 Mo | Runs stairs handheld | Stack 4 blocks | 10-25 words | Proto-play | | 2 Yr | Jumps stairs 2 feet/step | Stack 6 blocks | 2-word sentences 50+ words | Parallel play No! | | 3 Yr | Tricycle stairs alternating | Circle Stack 9 blocks | 3-word sentences 75% intelligible | Toilet training? | | 4 Yr | Hops on 1 foot | Cross + | Tells stories 100% intelligible | Cooperative play | | 5 Yr | Skips | Square | Fluent speech | Dress self | Health Maintenance & Guidance Nutrition Breastfeeding Gold standard. Contraindications Galactosemia HIV in US Active TB Chemo/Radiation Ebola Street drugs . Breastfed babies need Vitamin D supplements. Solids Introduce 4-6 months . Note Early introduction of peanuts 4-6mo decreases allergy risk. Cow s Milk Start at 1 year . Max 24 oz/day prevents Iron Deficiency Anemia . Screening Schedule Newborn Metabolic screen PKU Hypothyroidism Sickle Cell etc Hearing CCHD Critical Congenital Heart Disease . Anemia & Lead 12 months and 24mo if high risk . Autism M-CHAT 18 months and 24 months . Vision & Hearing Objective testing starts at 3-4 years . Blood Pressure Starts at 3 years . Vaccinations Snapshot Birth Hep B . 2 4 6 Months Dr. Hip D TaP R otavirus H ib I PV P CV . 12-15 Months M ad H elicopter V M MR H ep A V aricella . 4-6 Years Boosters DTaP IPV MMR Varicella . 11-12 Years M en T weens H ave H PV M eningococcal T dap H PV . analysis content/L70 HighYield Render Analysis.md ankiTags primary BandB Pediatrics Newborns Newborn Nursery secondary OME Pediatrics Newborn FirstAid Public Health Pediatrics questions id 1 question A 2-week-old male infant is brought to the clinic for a well-child check. On physical examination you note a swelling on the right side of the head that does not cross the suture lines. The parents are concerned. What is the most likely diagnosis? options Caput Succedaneum Cephalohematoma Subgaleal Hemorrhage Craniosynostosis Hydrocephalus correctAnswer 1 rationale Cephalohematoma is a collection of blood under the periosteum that does not cross suture lines. Caput Succedaneum is edema that crosses suture lines. Subgaleal hemorrhage is a dangerous accumulation of blood that crosses suture lines and can cause hemodynamic instability. id 2 question A 9-month-old female is brought in for a routine checkup. Which of the following developmental milestones would you expect her to have recently achieved or be performing? options Walking independently Speaking in 2-word sentences Pincer grasp thumb-finger Copying a circle Hopping on one foot correctAnswer 2 rationale By 9 months infants typically develop the pincer grasp fine motor crawl pull to stand and have object permanence. Walking independently is a 12-month milestone. 2-word sentences are 2 years. Copying a circle is 3 years. id 3 question During a newborn exam you perform the Ortolani maneuver. You feel a clunk as you abduct the hip. What is the next best step in management for this 2-day-old female? options Reassurance and follow-up in 2 weeks Immediate surgical consult Triple diapers Referral for Pavlik harness fitting Orthopedics Ordering a CT scan of the hips correctAnswer 3 rationale A positive Ortolani sign indicates hip instability Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip . The standard of care is referral to Orthopedics typically for a Pavlik harness. Triple diapers are not effective treatment. id 4 question A 4-day-old neonate presents with purulent bilious eye discharge and eyelid swelling. Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment? options Oral Erythromycin Topical Erythromycin ointment only IM Ceftriaxone Warm compresses Observation correctAnswer 2 rationale Purulent conjunctivitis within the first 2-5 days is highly suspicious for Gonococcal ophthalmia neonatorum which requires systemic treatment with IM Ceftriaxone to prevent corneal perforation and blindness. Chlamydia presents later 5-14 days and is watery/mucoid. id 5 question Which of the following reflexes is expected to disappear by 4 months of age? options Moro Reflex Babinski Reflex Parachute Reflex Patellar Reflex Achilles Reflex correctAnswer 0 rationale The Moro startle reflex typically disappears by 4 months. The Babinski reflex can persist normally until 12-24 months. The Parachute reflex appears around 6-9 months and persists. DTRs Patellar/Achilles persist. flashcards front Normal Newborn Heart Rate range back 120 - 160 bpm front Normal Newborn Respiratory Rate range back 30 - 60 breaths/min front Swelling on newborn head that crosses suture lines back Caput Succedaneum front Swelling on newborn head that does NOT cross suture lines back Cephalohematoma front White pupillary reflex Leukocoria indicates... back Retinoblastoma or Congenital Cataracts front Umbilical cord vessel anatomy back 2 Arteries 1 Vein AVA front Milestone Sits without support back 6 months front Milestone Pincer grasp back 9 months front Milestone Walks alone back 12 months front Milestone 2-word sentences back 2 years front Disappearance of Moro Reflex back 4 months front Screening age for Autism M-CHAT back 18 months and 24 months front Treatment for Hemangioma obstructing vision back Propranolol front Barlow Maneuver action back Adduct and push Posterior Dislocates front Ortolani Maneuver action back Abduct and pull Anterior Relocates pearls Head Control 2 months = 45 degrees 4 months = 90 degrees no head lag . Weight Doubles by 6 months Triples by 1 year. Foreign Body Aspiration Sudden onset wheezing/choking in a toddler --> Rigid Bronchoscopy. Epiglottitis Thumbprint sign on X-ray Drooling Tripoding. Medical Emergency. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l71", "title": "Lecture #71: Basics of Diagnosis: AC, SC, and GH Joints", "module": "OMM", "content": "id l71 title Lecture 71 Basics of Diagnosis AC SC and GH Joints lecturer T. S. Li D.O. module OMM session Lecture 71 prev l70 next l72 pdf pdfs/Lecture 71a. T. Li D.O. PPT .pdf summary Lecture 71 Basics of Diagnosis Acromioclavicular AC Sternoclavicular SC and Glenohumeral GH Joints Lecturer T. S. Li D.O. Session Lecture 71 Structural Overview and Clinical Approach Slide 2-5 The shoulder is a complex region formed by multiple articulations including the sternoclavicular SC acromioclavicular AC and glenohumeral GH joints as well as the scapulothoracic articulation. Evaluating shoulder pain requires carefully determining whether the symptoms truly stem from the shoulder joint itself or if they are referred from other regions such as the cervical spine thoracic spine or ribcage. A comprehensive clinical assessment—incorporating history taking orthopedic examination and neurological tests reflexes strength dermatomal sensation —must be combined with osteopathic evaluation to pinpoint the origin of somatic dysfunctions accurately. Clinical Considerations Pain referred from the neck upper back or ribcage can present as shoulder pain. Assess for involvement of nerve structures vasculature and lymphatics traversing the shoulder. Previous injuries surgeries or immobility can alter normal motion presenting as restriction or excessive mobility independent of pure somatic dysfunction. Sternoclavicular SC Joint Diagnosis Slide 6-12 Conceptually treating the sternum and manubrium as the torso and the SC joint as the shoulder joint helps in understanding clavicular motion. During shoulder motion the medial clavicle at the SC joint moves reciprocally to the distal clavicle. Testing is typically performed with the physician behind or in front of the patient using palpation and observation to assess active or passive glides. SC Joint Adduction/Abduction dysfunctions Abduction Dysfunction When shrugging the shoulders the medial clavicle glides inferiorly. If it glides inferiorly well but fails to rise superiorly upon relaxation it is an SC abduction somatic dysfunction. Adduction Dysfunction When depressing the shoulder the medial clavicle glides superiorly. If it glides superiorly freely but stays superior and does not move inferiorly upon relaxation it is an SC adduction somatic dysfunction. SC Joint Flexion/Extension dysfunctions Flexion Dysfunction When protracting the shoulder the medial clavicle glides posteriorly. If it remains posterior and resists moving anteriorly upon relaxation returning to neutral it is an SC flexion somatic dysfunction. Extension Dysfunction When retracting the shoulder the medial clavicle glides anteriorly. If it stays stuck anteriorly and does not move back posteriorly upon returning to neutral it is an SC extension somatic dysfunction. Prevalence Extension and Adduction are the most common dysfunctions at the SC joint. Combination Dysfunctions A patient can have a combined dysfunction e.g. an extended and adducted clavicle . Always diagnose and treat the more prominent restriction first then reassess to see if a secondary dysfunction remains. highyield SC Joint Reciprocal Motion Because the clavicle acts like a lever system motion at the distal clavicle creates opposite motion at the medial SC clavicle - Shoulder Elevation shrug -> Distal clavicle goes UP -> Medial clavicle glides INFERIORLY. - Shoulder Depression -> Distal clavicle goes DOWN -> Medial clavicle glides SUPERIORLY. - Shoulder Protraction -> Distal clavicle goes ANTERIOR -> Medial clavicle glides POSTERIORLY. - Shoulder Retraction -> Distal clavicle goes POSTERIOR -> Medial clavicle glides ANTERIORLY. Acromioclavicular AC Joint Diagnosis Slide 13-20 The AC joint is tested using the distal clavicle s motion relative to the acromion. Motion testing is primarily passive and utilizes gross movements of the upper extremity to induce gapping or closing at the AC joint rather than trying to move the joint directly with the physician s fingers. To palpate properly place the middle finger over the joint space with the index and ring fingers on the acromion and distal clavicle respectively. AC Joint Superior/Inferior Glides Inferior Glide Step-up The distal clavicle glides inferiorly explicitly but does not move superiorly. The palpating finger feels a step up moving from the clavicle to the higher acromion. Superior Glide Step-down The distal clavicle resists springing inferiorly—it favors a superior position. Moving from the distal clavicle to the acromion feels like a step down. AC Joint Adduction/Abduction dysfunctions Adduction Dysfunction Induced by adducting the arm pulling it across the chest ; this physiologically gaps the AC joint. If the joint remains gapped and does not close appropriately during abduction it is an AC adduction dysfunction. Abduction Dysfunction Induced by abducting the arm; this physically closes the AC joint space. If it closes well but fails to gap upon adduction it is an AC abduction dysfunction. Joint Motion Couples Abduction somatic dysfunction frequently couples with superior glide and internal rotation of the distal clavicle. Adduction somatic dysfunction frequently couples with inferior glide and external rotation of the distal clavicle. highyield AC Joint Diagnosis Rules When diagnosing AC joint adduction/abduction use gross shoulder motions to move the joint. Adducting the patient s arm across the chest will physiologically gap the AC joint. Abducting their arm laterally will close the AC joint space. The dysfunction is named for the position in which the joint is free or prefers to rest. Glenohumeral GH Joint Diagnosis Slide 21-25 The GH joint is critical for overall upper extremity function. Osteopathic evaluation here involves establishing both major gross range of motion and minor gliding motions to ensure proper seating of the humeral head within the glenoid fossa. Analogous to gently wiggling a stuck dresser drawer restoring minor glides often frees up major motion barriers without requiring excessive force. Major Motions Flexion Extension Abduction Adduction Internal and External Rotation. Minor Motions Glides Evaluated by stabilizing the top of the shoulder acromion/scapula and using the active hand to manipulate the proximal humerus. Specific tests check for Anterior vs. Posterior glides and Superior vs. Inferior glides. Coupled Mechanics of the GH Joint Extension / Abduction / External Rotation These major motions mechanically pair with an anterior glide of the humeral head. Flexion / Adduction / Internal Rotation These major motions structurally pair with a posterior glide of the humeral head. Clinical Relevance Abnormal structural motion at the GH joint can generate pain and contribute to impingement syndromes involving rotator cuff tendons nerves and adjacent vasculature. analysis content/L71 HighYield Render Analysis.md ankingResource primarySource BandB chapter Musculoskeletal > Anatomy > Shoulder alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Musculoskeletal > Anatomy > Shoulder Joint resource Physeo chapter Anatomy > Musculoskeletal > Upper Extremity resource Bootcamp chapter Anatomy > Upper Limb > Shoulder flashcards front What is the reciprocal motion of the medial clavicle when the shoulder is elevated/shrugged? back It glides inferiorly. tag Concept front What is an SC joint abduction somatic dysfunction? back The medial clavicle glides inferiorly during shoulder shrugging but fails to glide superiorly when relaxed. tag Concept front What is an SC joint extension somatic dysfunction? back The medial clavicle glides anteriorly during retraction but fails to return posteriorly. tag Concept front What are the two most common dysfunctions at the SC joint? back Extension and Adduction dysfunctions. tag Concept front In AC joint diagnosis what does a step-up palpation from the distal clavicle to the acromion indicate? back Inferior glide of the AC joint. tag Concept front What motions typically couple with an AC joint abduction dysfunction? back Superior glide and internal rotation. tag Concept front What motions typically couple with an AC joint adduction dysfunction? back Inferior glide and external rotation. tag Concept front How do you induce physiological gapping to test for AC joint adduction dysfunction? back By adducting the arm across the chest. tag Concept front During major motion testing of the Glenohumeral joint what glide of the humeral head pairs with extension abduction and external rotation? back Anterior glide. tag Concept front During major motion testing of the Glenohumeral joint what glide of the humeral head pairs with flexion adduction and internal rotation? back Posterior glide. tag Concept pearls title Treating Combination Dysfunctions content If an SC joint presents with combination dysfunctions e.g. Extended and Adducted prioritize and treat the more prominent restriction first then reassess to determine if the other dysfunction resolved or requires separate treatment. title Minor Glides Restore Major Motion content In the glenohumeral joint treating the minor gliding dysfunctions e.g. anterior/posterior or superior/inferior glides often frees up the major gross range of motion operating similarly to slightly shifting a stuck dresser drawer to unbind it. questions id 1 question A 45-year-old male presents with right shoulder pain after a fall. You perform an osteopathic examination to assess the sternoclavicular SC joint. You ask the patient to actively shrug his shoulders and find that the medial part of the right clavicle moves inferiorly freely but when he relaxes his shoulders it fails to glide back superiorly. What is the correct osteopathic diagnosis? options Right SC joint adduction somatic dysfunction Right SC joint abduction somatic dysfunction Right SC joint flexion somatic dysfunction Right SC joint extension somatic dysfunction Right AC joint inferior glide dysfunction correctAnswer 1 rationale During a shoulder shrug the distal clavicle elevates causing the medial clavicle to reciprocally glide inferiorly. If the medial clavicle glides inferiorly but does not return superiorly to neutral upon relaxation the restriction is in the superior glide meaning the freedom of motion is in the inferior glide. According to the naming convention a distally elevated abducted clavicle position describes an SC abduction somatic dysfunction. id 2 question A 32-year-old tennis player complains of localizing pain to the top of his shoulder. Passive motion testing of the left acromioclavicular AC joint reveals that the distal clavicle glides superiorly without resistance but resists inferior springing. When palpating from the distal clavicle onto the acromion you feel a step down . What associated motions are most likely present with this functional state? options Adduction and External Rotation Adduction and Internal Rotation Abduction and Internal Rotation Abduction and External Rotation Flexion and Extension correctAnswer 2 rationale The step down presentation and free superior glide indicate an AC joint superior glide dysfunction. Biomechanically at the AC joint an abduction somatic dysfunction frequently couples with a superior glide and internal rotation of the distal clavicle. id 3 question You are examining a patient with limited glenohumeral range of motion. To assess the minor glides of the glenohumeral joint you stabilize the shoulder and manipulate the proximal humerus. If a patient has a severe restriction in glenohumeral extension abduction and external rotation which of the following minor glides of the humeral head is most likely functionally restricted? options Posterior glide Superior glide Anterior glide Inferior glide Medial glide correctAnswer 2 rationale Major motions like extension abduction and external rotation are coupled with an anterior glide of the humeral head. Thus restriction in these major gross motions often directly correlates with a restriction in the specific associated minor anterior glide. id 4 question A patient undergoes passive range of motion testing of the acromioclavicular AC joint. You bring their right arm across their chest adduction and note that the AC joint space gaps open perfectly. However when you return the arm laterally into abduction the AC joint fails to comfortably close. What is the most appropriate osteopathic diagnosis? options AC joint abduction somatic dysfunction GH joint anterior glide dysfunction SC joint adduction somatic dysfunction AC joint adduction somatic dysfunction GH joint posterior glide dysfunction correctAnswer 3 rationale The AC joint normally gaps when the humerus is adducted and closes when abducted. If it gaps freely in adduction but resists closing during abduction it implies a freedom in adduction. The somatic dysfunction is named for the freedom making this an AC joint adduction somatic dysfunction. id 5 question A 28-year-old female presents with neck and shoulder discomfort. While evaluating her left sternoclavicular SC joint you ask her to protract her shoulders by moving them forward. You feel her left medial clavicle glide posteriorly. As she relaxes back to neutral the left medial clavicle fails to return anteriorly. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? options Left SC joint flexion somatic dysfunction Left SC joint extension somatic dysfunction Left SC joint adduction somatic dysfunction Left SC joint superior glide Left AC joint internal rotation dysfunction correctAnswer 0 rationale During shoulder protraction the distal clavicle moves anteriorly which causes the medial clavicle at the SC joint to move reciprocally posterior. If it remains stuck posteriorly and won t return anteriorly upon relaxation into neutral it is named for the freedom of motion remaining protracted/posterior . This biomechanical positioning corresponds to an SC joint flexion somatic dysfunction. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l72", "title": "Lecture #72: Gliomas", "module": "Neurology", "content": "id l72 title Lecture 72 Gliomas lecturer M. Plummer M.D. module Neurology session Lecture 72 prev l71 next l73 pdf pdfs/Lecture 72. M. Plummer M.D..pdf summary Lecture 72 Gliomas Lecturer M. Plummer M.D. Session Lecture 72 Introduction to Gliomas Slide 1 Gliomas represent a diverse group of tumors originating from the glial cells of the central nervous system which include astrocytes oligodendrocytes and ependymal cells. Because glial cells are the supporting tissue of the brain their neoplastic transformation creates space-occupying lesions that compress surrounding structures and interrupt normal neural function. Gliomas comprise roughly 30% of all brain tumors and are categorized by the World Health Organization WHO into four distinct grades. The highest-grade area within any single tumor mass fundamentally dictates its overall grade and prognosis. WHO Classification Overview Grade I Pilocytic astrocytoma benign non-infiltrating Grade II Infiltrating low-grade astrocytoma diffuse Grade III Anaplastic astrocytoma Grade IV Glioblastoma highly malignant Pilocytic Astrocytoma Grade I Slide 2-4 Pilocytic astrocytomas are the most common benign central nervous system neoplasm seen in children though they can also occur in young adults. Unlike higher-grade gliomas these tumors tend to be well-circumscribed and non-infiltrating allowing for a much better prognosis following surgical resection. They are classically located in the cerebellum of children leading to distinctive cerebellar signs upon presentation. Epidemiology & Location Peak occurrence 8-13 years of age. Most common location in children Cerebellum. Most common location in adults Supratentorial region. Lecturer Emphasis There is an association with neurofibromatosis type 1 NF1 particularly when the tumor arises in less common locations such as the optic chiasm. Histology Biphasic architecture featuring tightly packed bipolar cells mixed with loosely packed multipolar cells and microcysts. Rosenthal Fibers Intracytoplasmic brightly eosinophilic extensions with a characteristic corkscrew-like appearance. Classically described as a cystic lesion with a mural nodule when located in the cerebellum. Staining Positive for Glial Fibrillary Acidic Protein GFAP a marker for astrocytes. Prognosis Excellent compared to other gliomas. Children have a 90-95% 5-year progression-free survival rate after surgical resection. Adult prognosis is slightly less favorable but still robust 85% 5-year survival . correlate Pilocytic Astrocytoma - Pathophysiology Benign slow-growing neoplastic proliferation of astrocytes Grade I . - Presentation Depends on location. Cerebellar tumors common in children present with gait difficulty ataxia dizziness nystagmus and new-onset clumsiness. - Diagnosis MRI showing a cystic mass with an enhancing mural nodule. Pathologically GFAP positive with Rosenthal fibers. - Treatment Surgical resection. - Risk Factors Neurofibromatosis type 1 particularly for optic pathway gliomas . - Lecturer s Point Though Rosenthal fibers are characteristic and high yield for this pathology they are not exclusively limited to pilocytic astrocytomas. mnemonic Pilocytic Astrocytoma PILO - P Pediatric most common benign CNS tumor - I I Grade I - L Location in cerebellum cystic with mural nodule - O Osmiophilic / Rosenthal fibers corkscrew eosinophilic Infiltrating Low-Grade Astrocytoma Grade II Slide 5-6 Infiltrating low-grade astrocytomas often referred to as diffuse astrocytomas mark the transition from benign circumscribed masses to tumors that actively invade surrounding native brain tissue. This infiltrative nature means that while they replicate slowly achieving a true surgical cure is virtually impossible. Epidemiology & Location Median age at diagnosis 30-40 years. Predilection Frontal and temporal lobes. Histology Hypercellular glial lesions with nuclear atypia. Low mitotic activity low degree of cell division . Staining Positive for GFAP and Vimentin an intermediate filament . Prognosis Median survival is highly variable 2-12 years . Patients are never considered truly cured; recurrence and progression are common. correlate Infiltrating Low-Grade Astrocytoma Diffuse Astrocytoma - Pathophysiology Grade II infiltrative neoplasm of astrocytes. - Presentation Often debuts with new-onset seizures in a previously healthy young adult. - Diagnosis MRI showing a diffuse infiltrative mass; histology shows hypercellularity and atypia but low mitoses. - Treatment Maximum safe surgical debulking combined with chemoradiation. - Lecturer s Point Recurrence typically occurs within 2 cm of the original tumor location due to its infiltrative nature. Anaplastic Astrocytoma Grade III Slide 7-8 Anaplastic astrocytomas represent a more malignant progression of astrocytic tumors. The term anaplastic describes the poorly differentiated nature of the cells. As these tumors grow and increase intracranial pressure they produce significant neurological compromise. Epidemiology & Location Mean age 45 years. Predilection Frontal and temporal lobes. Histology Greater cellular atypia and more frequent mitotic figures compared to Grade II. Key Distinction Plentiful mitoses but intentionally lacks microvascular proliferation and necrosis which are the defining features of Glioblastoma . Prognosis Median survival is roughly 2-3 years. Treated with surgical debulking and radiation chemotherapy added in adults . correlate Anaplastic Astrocytoma - Pathophysiology Grade III malignant proliferation of astrocytes. - Presentation Seizures headaches focal deficits altered mental status. Frontal lobe involvement may cause memory issues personality changes and contralateral paralysis. Temporal lobe involvement causes seizures speech and memory problems. - Diagnosis Brain imaging and biopsy showing atypia and frequent mitoses but NO necrosis or microvascular proliferation. - Treatment Surgical debulking radiation and chemotherapy. Glioblastoma Grade IV Slide 9-10 Glioblastoma historically known as glioblastoma multiforme GBM is the most lethal invasive and aggressive of all glial tumors. It spreads rapidly along white matter tracts devastating normal brain architecture and leading to a remarkably short survival even with maximal therapy. Epidemiology & Location Accounts for over half 52% of all malignant primary brain tumors. Typical location Frontal and temporal lobes arising from hemispheric white matter. Butterfly Glioma It frequently crosses the midline via the corpus callosum or anterior commissure invading the contralateral hemisphere. Histology Marked nuclear atypia and extraordinarily high mitotic activity. Pseudopalisading Necrosis Tumor cells line up in a picket-fence formation surrounding a central necrotic core. Microvascular proliferation is prominently seen. Prognosis Extremely poor. Median survival is 15 months with a 5-year survival rate of <5%. correlate Glioblastoma GBM - Pathophysiology Grade IV highly malignant astrocytoma. - Presentation Rapid onset of seizures severe focal deficits paresis and headaches. - Diagnosis Ring-enhancing lesion crossing the corpus callosum on MRI. Histology shows pseudopalisading necrosis microvascular proliferation and GFAP positivity. - Treatment Maximum safe debulking followed by radiation and chemotherapy. - Lecturer s Point The headaches caused by GBM do not always fit the classic brain tumor headache pattern and may be clinically indistinguishable from tension headaches. highyield Differentiating High-Grade Astrocytomas The key diagnostic difference between an Anaplastic Astrocytoma Grade III and a Glioblastoma Grade IV is the presence of vascular proliferation and necrosis. If a high-grade astrocytic tumor has pseudopalisading necrosis or microvascular proliferation it is automatically a Grade IV Glioblastoma. Oligodendroglioma Slide 11-12 Oligodendrogliomas originate from oligodendrocytes the cells responsible for myelinating axons within the central nervous system. These tumors have distinct molecular signatures that make them uniquely responsive to certain therapies offering a somewhat more favorable long-term prognosis than high-grade astrocytomas. Epidemiology & Location Peak incidence 35-44 years adults . Predilection Frontal and temporal lobes. Histology Fried-Egg Appearance Cells with round uniform nuclei and clear cytoplasmic halos. Chicken-Wire Capillary Pattern A delicate network of branching intertwining blood vessels. Frequent mucoid and cystic degeneration with hemorrhage and focal calcifications. Molecular Features Strong association with co-deletion of 1p and 19q chromosomal arms which predicts a more favorable prognosis and improved response to chemotherapy. correlate Oligodendroglioma - Pathophysiology Neoplasm of myelin-producing oligodendrocytes. - Presentation Seizures are the most common initial presentation 66% . Frontal lobe tumors may cause unilateral muscle weakness and personality changes. - Diagnosis Imaging often shows a calcified tumor in the frontal lobe. Histology shows fried-egg cells and chicken-wire capillaries. - Treatment Surgical resection radiation and chemotherapy. - Prognosis Better than astrocytomas. 10-year survival is >60% though anaplastic variants Grade III fare worse. Ependymoma Slide 13-14 Ependymomas arise from ependymal cells which line the ventricles and the central canal of the spinal cord playing a crucial role in cerebrospinal fluid CSF production and regulation. The anatomical location of these tumors largely dictates their distinct clinical presentations in children versus adults. Epidemiology & Location Third most common child brain/spine tumor 30% diagnosed under age 3 . Children Arise from the floor of the fourth ventricle . Adults More commonly arise in the spinal cord . Histology Perivascular Pseudorosettes Spiral clusters of tumor cells arranging their cellular processes around a central blood vessel leaving an anuclear zone immediately adjacent to the vessel. Ependymal Rosettes Cells arranged circumferentially around an empty central lumen mimicking a ventricle . Rod-shaped basal bodies modified centrioles are present near the nucleus. Prognosis Spinal cord tumors adults have a better prognosis because they are far easier to resect than ventricular tumors. The extent of surgical resection is the single greatest prognostic predictor. correlate Ependymoma - Pathophysiology Tumor of the ependymal lining. - Presentation Children 4th ventricle present with obstructive hydrocephalus headache nausea vomiting ataxia macrocephaly lethargy . Adults spinal cord present with compressive sensory/motor deficits dysesthesias spastic paresis . - Diagnosis MRI of the brain/spine. Histology shows perivascular pseudorosettes. - Treatment Maximal safe surgical resection followed by targeted radiation. - Complications Obstructive hydrocephalus and drop metastases . - Lecturer s Point Because these tumors can spread via CSF seeding drop metastases any patient diagnosed with an ependymoma MUST have complete imaging of the entire neuraxis brain and full spine . analysis content/L72 HighYield Render Analysis.md ankingResource primarySource BandB chapter Neurology > Neuro-Oncology > Brain Tumors alternatives resource Pathoma chapter Central Nervous System > CNS Tumors resource FirstAid chapter Neurology > Pathology > Brain Tumors resource SketchyPath chapter Neuro > CNS Tumors flashcards front What is the most common benign central nervous system neoplasm seen in children? back Pilocytic Astrocytoma WHO Grade I . tag Concept front Which glioma is characterized by a biphasic architecture with eosinophilic corkscrew-like Rosenthal fibers? back Pilocytic Astrocytoma. tag Concept front What is the key histological difference separating Grade III Anaplastic Astrocytoma from Grade IV Glioblastoma? back Glioblastoma features microvascular proliferation and pseudopalisading necrosis; Anaplastic Astrocytoma does not. tag Concept front What brain tumor classically crosses the midline via the corpus callosum producing a butterfly appearance? back Glioblastoma GBM . tag Clinical front Which neuro-oncological marker is associated with a favorable prognosis and chemotherapy response in Oligodendroglioma? back 1p/19q chromosomal co-deletion. tag Concept front Histologically which glioma presents with fried-egg cells and a chicken-wire capillary pattern? back Oligodendroglioma. tag Concept front Where do Ependymomas most commonly arise in children versus adults? back Children Floor of the 4th ventricle. Adults Spinal cord. tag Clinical front What specific risk necessitates complete neuraxis imaging in patients diagnosed with an Ependymoma? back Cerebrospinal fluid CSF seeding which leads to drop metastases . tag Clinical front What classic presentation is seen when an Ependymoma arises in the 4th ventricle of a child? back Obstructive hydrocephalus headache nausea vomiting lethargy . tag Clinical front What intermediate filament uniquely stains positive alongside GFAP in Infiltrating Low-Grade Astrocytomas? back Vimentin. tag Concept pearls title Glioblastoma Headache Characteristics content The headaches caused by glioblastomas do not necessarily follow the classic brain tumor headache pattern worse in the morning with vomiting and may be clinically indistinguishable from standard tension headaches in the early stages. title Imaging the Neuraxis content Because ependymomas routinely seed the cerebrospinal fluid causing drop metastases imaging exclusively the primary lesion is insufficient. The entire neuraxis full brain and spine MRI must be evaluated. questions id 1 question A 9-year-old boy is brought to the pediatrician by his parents due to new-onset clumsiness frequent falls and dizzy spells over the past three months. Physical examination reveals an ataxic gait and horizontal nystagmus. Magnetic resonance imaging MRI of the brain demonstrates a well-circumscribed cystic lesion with an enhancing mural nodule located in the cerebellum. Biopsy of the mass reveals GFAP-positive bipolar cells and corkscrew-shaped eosinophilic structures. What is the most likely diagnosis? options Ependymoma Oligodendroglioma Glioblastoma Pilocytic astrocytoma Anaplastic astrocytoma correctAnswer 3 rationale The patient s age 9 years location of the tumor cerebellum imaging findings cystic with mural nodule and histology Rosenthal fibers which are eosinophilic corkscrew structures and GFAP positivity all point definitively to Pilocytic Astrocytoma WHO Grade I . id 2 question A 55-year-old male is rushed to the emergency department after suffering a generalized tonic-clonic seizure at work. He reports having progressively worsening headaches over the past two months and weakness in his right arm. An MRI of his brain reveals a massive contrast-enhancing irregular lesion in the left frontal lobe that crosses the midline through the corpus callosum into the right hemisphere. A biopsy would most likely reveal which of the following histological features? options Round cells with clear cytoplasm and a branching capillary network Perivascular pseudorosettes and rod-shaped basal bodies GFAP-positive cells with nuclear atypia but lacking necrosis Pseudopalisading tumor cells surrounding central areas of necrosis Biphasic tissue with loosely packed microcysts and bipolar cells correctAnswer 3 rationale The description of a massive tumor crossing the corpus callosum butterfly glioma in an older adult is highly characteristic of Glioblastoma Grade IV . Histologically Glioblastoma is distinguished by marked atypia high mitotic activity microvascular proliferation and specifically pseudopalisading necrosis where tumor cells line up around a central necrotic core. id 3 question A 38-year-old previously healthy female presents with a single episode of a seizure. MRI demonstrates a partially calcified mass in her left frontal lobe. Post-surgical biopsy reveals uniform cells with central round nuclei and clear peripheral cytoplasm forming a fried-egg appearance. Which of the following molecular findings is most likely associated with this patient s tumor and portends a favorable response to chemotherapy? options GFAP over-expression 1p/19q co-deletion Neurofibromatosis type 1 mutation NF2 microdeletion Vimentin positivity correctAnswer 1 rationale The patient has an Oligodendroglioma indicated by her age bracket location frontal lobe calcification and the classic fried-egg histology. The defining and highly tested molecular feature of Oligodendroglioma that confers a favorable diagnostic prognosis and better response to treatment is the 1p and 19q chromosomal co-deletion. id 4 question A 4-year-old girl is evaluated for a two-week history of morning vomiting lethargy and an enlarging head circumference. Imaging reveals a solid mass occupying the floor of the fourth ventricle. The mass is surgically resected and histological analysis shows tumor cells arranged radially around an empty central lumen as well as spirals of cells around blood vessels. What is the most critical next step in the workup of this patient s condition? options Genetic testing for 1p/19q deletions MRI of the complete spinal neuraxis Screening for optic nerve gliomas Positron emission tomography PET of the chest and abdomen Immediate administration of high-dose chemotherapy correctAnswer 1 rationale The tumor in the 4th ventricle displaying ependymal rosettes cells around an empty lumen and perivascular pseudorosettes spirals around blood vessels is an Ependymoma. Because ependymomas can readily shed cells into the cerebrospinal fluid causing drop metastases along the spinal cord comprehensive MRI imaging of the entire neuraxis full brain and spine must be performed immediately to evaluate for spread. id 5 question A 42-year-old male undergoes a brain biopsy for a rapidly growing temporal lobe mass that has caused progressive memory loss and seizures. Pathology demonstrates a highly cellular glial neoplasm with significant nuclear atypia and abundant mitotic figures. However the pathologist reports an absolute lack of both microvascular proliferation and necrosis. What is the World Health Organization WHO grading for this patient s tumor? options Grade I Grade II Grade III Grade IV Grade V correctAnswer 2 rationale The tumor described is an Anaplastic Astrocytoma. The key to differentiating it from lower grade infiltrative astrocytomas Grade II is the presence of significant nuclear atypia and high/frequent mitotic activity. The key distinguishing it from a Glioblastoma Grade IV is the deliberate absence of microvascular proliferation and necrosis. Therefore it is a WHO Grade III tumor. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l73", "title": "Lecture #73: Primary Brain and Peripheral Nerve Sheath Tumors", "module": "Neurology", "content": "id l73 title Lecture 73 Primary Brain and Peripheral Nerve Sheath Tumors lecturer M. Plummer M.D. module Neurology session Lecture 73 prev l72 next l74 pdf pdfs/Lecture 73. M. Plummer M.D..pdf summary Lecture 73 Primary Brain and Peripheral Nerve Sheath Tumors Lecturer M. Plummer M.D. Session Lecture 73 Meningiomas Slide 1-3 Primary brain tumors rarely spread to distant organs unlike metastatic lesions. Within the central nervous system meningiomas are considered the most common primary benign brain tumor representing 20-30% of primary brain tumors . They originate not from brain parenchyma but from the arachnoid cells of the meninges. Because they are slow-growing and external to the brain tissue itself extra-axial the brain has time to adapt to the pressure often allowing these tumors to become quite large before causing symptomatic compression. Their prognosis is generally excellent if complete surgical resection is achievable. Epidemiology & Location Most common primary brain tumor predominantly benign . Peak incidence in adults with a 2 1 female-to-male predominance. Common locations Parasagittal falcine and cerebral convexities. Pathology & Associations Hormonal Influence Contain progesterone receptors and may rapidly increase in size during pregnancy. Genetics High association with neurofibromatosis type 2 NF2 . Up to 80% have NF2 gene mutations. Imaging Characteristically present with a dural tail on MRI indicating their dural-based extra-axial origin. Histology Spindle cells arranged in a classic whorled pattern. Presence of lamellated calcifications known as psammoma bodies . Clinical Picture Symptoms depend on location but can include headaches seizures vision problems and focal neurological deficits. correlate Meningioma - Pathophysiology Benign proliferation of arachnoid cells causing extra-axial compression of brain tissue. - Presentation Often asymptomatic until large. Presents with headaches seizures and focal deficits. - Diagnosis MRI showing a dural-based mass with a dural tail . Histology shows psammoma bodies and whorled spindle cells. - Treatment Surgical resection. Radiation therapy is second-line if unresectable. - Risk Factors Neurofibromatosis type 2 NF2 and prior radiation. - Lecturer s Point These tumors have progesterone receptors and may dramatically enlarge during pregnancy. Medulloblastomas Slide 4-5 Medulloblastoma is the most common malignant pediatric brain tumor. It arises from undifferentiated embryonic tissue embryonal tumor that remains in the brain after birth and undergoes malignant transformation. Because it classically originates in the cerebellum and often extends into the fourth ventricle it aggressively disrupts motor coordination and obstructs cerebrospinal fluid flow. Epidemiology Most common malignant brain tumor in children. 70% occur in children under 10 years old. Clinical Presentation Typically located in the cerebellum. Presents with headache cerebellar ataxia cranial nerve VI palsies. Can rapidly cause hydrocephalus by invading the fourth ventricle. Histology Small round blue cells of undifferentiated embryonal lineage. Homer-Wright Rosettes Tumor cells surrounding a central neuropil a tangle of nerve fibers synapses and glial cells . Prognosis & Treatment High-grade malignancy demanding aggressive treatment resection radiation and chemotherapy. Highly prone to metastasis drop metastases necessitating complete whole-neuraxis imaging. Children with average risk have a 70-80% 5-year survival rate though those under 3 years old have poorer outcomes. correlate Medulloblastoma - Pathophysiology Highly malignant embryonal neoplasm of the cerebellum. - Presentation Headache poor coordination/ataxia CN VI palsy and signs of increased intracranial pressure hydrocephalus . - Diagnosis MRI of the full neuraxis to check for CSF seeding. Histology shows Homer-Wright rosettes and small round cells. - Treatment Surgical resection whole-brain/spinal radiation and chemotherapy. - Complications Drop metastases to the spinal cord. - Lecturer s Point Because these tumors seed the CSF aggressively complete whole-neuraxis imaging is strictly required. Neuronal Tumors and Gangliogliomas Slide 6-7 Neuronal tumors are rare comprising only 1% of brain neoplasms and consist of either pure neuronal cells or a mix of both abnormal glial and neuronal elements. The most notable of these is the ganglioglioma. Because they typically arise directly within the brain parenchyma and disrupt normal neuronal networks patients classically present with unprovoked new-onset seizures. Ganglioglioma A mixed tumor comprising neoplastic ganglion cells neurons and glial supporting cells. Typically slow-growing and low-grade WHO Grade I . Most common location is the temporal lobe. Histology Dysmorphic often binucleated neurons with peripheral Nissl bodies rough endoplasmic reticulum . The glial component may show Rosenthal fibers if astrocytic and frequent calcifications. Prognosis Generally benign with an excellent prognosis 10-year survival of 84-93% . Often cured via surgical resection alone. correlate Ganglioglioma - Pathophysiology Benign mixed tumor of dysplastic neurons and neoplastic glial cells. - Presentation Typically presents as new-onset seizures in a child or young adult. - Diagnosis MRI showing a temporal lobe mass. Histology reveals binucleated neurons with Nissl bodies and occasionally Rosenthal fibers. - Treatment Surgical resection is usually curative. - Complications Up to 10% may undergo malignant transformation. Peripheral Nerve Sheath Tumors Schwannoma Slide 8-11 Schwann cells provide crucial myelination for the peripheral nervous system. When these cells undergo neoplastic transformation they form Schwannomas—the most common nerve sheath tumors in the human body. Because these tumors are encapsulated and grow outside the nerve fascicles they compress rather than invade the nerve allowing for relatively straightforward surgical removal without destroying the underlying nerve. Pathophysiology & Genetics Caused by the loss of function of the tumor suppressor protein merlin encoded by NF2 on Chromosome 22 . Merlin naturally dictates contact inhibition of proliferation via E-cadherin; its absence allows unchecked growth. Histology Demonstrate dense areas Antoni A and loose areas Antoni B . Diagnostic feature within Antoni A Verocay bodies nuclear palisading with alternating nuclear and anuclear zones . Vestibular Schwannoma Acoustic Neuroma Affects the vestibular branch of CN VIII. Arises near the brainstem at the cerebellopontine angle. Presents with unilateral hearing loss tinnitus and vestibular dizziness. As it grows it frequently compresses CN V trigeminal causing facial numbness and occasionally CN VII or the cerebellum ipsilateral ataxia . correlate Vestibular Schwannoma - Pathophysiology Benign encapsulated proliferation of Schwann cells on CN VIII at the cerebellopontine angle. - Presentation Unilateral sensorineural hearing loss tinnitus vertigo. Expanding tumors compress CN V causing facial numbness. - Diagnosis MRI Brain with contrast. Histology shows Antoni A/B regions and Verocay bodies. - Treatment Observation surgical excision or stereotactic radiosurgery. - Risk Factors Bilateral vestibular schwannomas are intimately linked with and pathognomonic for Neurofibromatosis type 2. - Lecturer s Point Despite the classic cerebellopontine angle syndrome name implying CN VII involvement CN V is more readily compressed than CN VII because CN VII is physically more resilient. Neurofibromas and MPNSTs Slide 12-16 Unlike Schwannomas Neurofibromas are unencapsulated masses comprising a chaotic mixture of Schwann cells mast cells and fibroblasts. While the vast majority are benign they infiltrate extensively into the nerve architecture itself making surgical enucleation impossible without sacrificing the parent nerve. Pathophysiology & Genetics Driven by loss of function of neurofibromin NF1 gene on Chromosome 17 . Neurofibromin normally inhibits the Ras oncoprotein; its loss causes Ras hyperactivity and cellular proliferation. Histology Unencapsulated spindle cells resembling shredded carrots. Subtypes Localized cutaneous Circumscribed nodular tumors on the skin. Plexiform Diffuse disfiguring tumors tracking along nerve plexuses. Pathognomonic for NF1 and carry a high risk of malignant transformation. Diffuse Extensive infiltration into the dermis/subcutis. Malignant Peripheral Nerve Sheath Tumors MPNSTs Aggressive mitotically active spindle cell sarcomas. Often arise from malignant transition of plexiform neurofibromas in NF1 patients. Present as rapidly enlarging firm necrotic masses in the trunk or extremities. highyield Schwannoma vs. Neurofibroma A key distinction for board questions and surgical planning is encapsulation. Schwannomas are encapsulated and displace the nerve bundle allowing for clean surgical excision. Neurofibromas are unencapsulated infiltrate the native nerve fascicles shredded carrots and cannot be easily resected without sacrificing the nerve entirely. Neurofibromatosis Types 1 and 2 Slide 17-21 Neurofibromatosis encompasses two distinct autosomal dominant tumor-suppressor syndromes that profoundly increase the risk of developing nervous system neoplasms. | Condition | Gene/Protein | Chromosome | Classic Tumors | Key Clinical Findings | Prognosis | | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | | Neurofibromatosis Type 1 NF1 | Neurofibromin | 17 | Neurofibromas Optic Gliomas MPNSTs Pheochromocytomas | Café-au-lait spots axillary/inguinal freckling Lisch nodules iris hamartomas | 15-year shortened lifespan. Risk of malignant transformation MPNSTs . | | Neurofibromatosis Type 2 NF2 | Merlin | 22 | Vestibular Schwannomas Meningiomas Ependymomas | Bilateral vestibular schwannomas cataracts | High morbidity; early death often in 30s due to massive intracranial tumor burden. | correlate Neurofibromatosis Type 1 von Recklinghausen disease - Pathophysiology Autosomal dominant mutation of NF1 tumor suppressor gene neurofibromin driving unregulated Ras signaling. - Presentation Multiple café-au-lait macules axillary/inguinal freckling vision issues optic gliomas in children <6 and disfiguring plexiform neurofibromas. - Diagnosis Clinical criteria + genetic testing. Presence of Lisch nodules is highly specific. - Treatment Regular symptom surveillance. MPNSTs require aggressive multidisciplinary treatment. - Complications Malignant transformation of neurofibromas; associated with pheochromocytomas and seizures. correlate Neurofibromatosis Type 2 - Pathophysiology Autosomal dominant NF2 mutation merlin on chromosome 22 disrupting contact inhibition E-cadherin . - Presentation Hearing loss/tinnitus in a young adult bilateral symptoms pathognomonic . - Diagnosis MRI reveals bilateral vestibular schwannomas. - Treatment Surgical excision radiation and monoclonal antibodies bevacizumab . - Lecturer s Point Avoid excessive radiation when possible for NF2-associated tumors as it can trigger secondary tumor formation. mnemonic NF1 vs NF2 Chromosomes NF1 is on chromosome 17 von Recklinghausen has 17 letters . NF2 is on chromosome 22 type 2 -> 22 . analysis content/L73 HighYield Render Analysis.md ankingResource primarySource BandB chapter Neurology > Neuro-Oncology > Brain Tumors alternatives resource Pathoma chapter Central Nervous System > CNS Tumors resource FirstAid chapter Neurology > Pathology > Brain Tumors resource SketchyPath chapter Neuro > CNS Tumors flashcards front What is the most common primary brain tumor? back Meningioma benign . tag Concept front Which primary brain tumor characteristically features a dural tail on MRI? back Meningioma. tag Clinical front What are the classic histological findings of a meningioma? back Spindle cells arranged in a whorled pattern and psammoma bodies. tag Concept front What is the most common malignant brain tumor in children? back Medulloblastoma. tag Concept front What specific histological structure is pathognomonic for medulloblastomas? back Homer-Wright rosettes tumor cells surrounding a central neuropil . tag Concept front Why is complete whole-neuraxis imaging required when diagnosing a medulloblastoma? back Because they are highly prone to seeding the CSF via drop metastases . tag Clinical front What type of brain tumor typically presents with unprovoked new-onset seizures originating from the temporal lobe in children or young adults? back Ganglioglioma. tag Clinical front What histological features are diagnostic for Schwannomas? back Antoni A areas Antoni B areas and Verocay bodies nuclear palisading . tag Concept front Bilateral vestibular schwannomas are pathognomonic for what genetic condition? back Neurofibromatosis Type 2 NF2 . tag Clinical front What is the key structural difference between a Schwannoma and a Neurofibroma? back Schwannomas are encapsulated can be cleanly resected from the nerve . Neurofibromas are unencapsulated and directly infiltrate the nerve. tag Concept pearls title Meningiomas and Hormones content Meningiomas inherently possess progesterone receptors meaning these naturally slow-growing tumors may precipitously enlarge and become symptomatic during pregnancy. title Cerebellopontine Angle Compression content While vestibular schwannomas arise near CN VII and CN VIII at the cerebellopontine angle they more frequently compress the trigeminal nerve CN V rather than the facial nerve CN VII because CN VII is structurally more resilient. questions id 1 question A 45-year-old female presents to her primary care physician with a two-month history of a persistent headache that is worse when lying down along with a recent focal seizure affecting her right arm. An MRI of the brain with contrast reveals a well-circumscribed homogenously enhancing 3 cm mass attached to the parasagittal dura. The mass demonstrates a dural tail . Pathologic evaluation of the resected tumor would most likely reveal which of the following? options Small round blue cells forming Homer-Wright rosettes Spindle cells arranged in a whorled pattern with lamellated calcifications Dysmorphic binucleated neurons with peripheral Nissl bodies Unencapsulated spindle cells resembling shredded carrots Regions of dense nuclear palisading alternating with loose cellular areas correctAnswer 1 rationale The patient is presenting with a Meningioma evidenced by her age female sex location parasagittal extra-axial and classic MRI appearance dural tail . The histology of a meningioma consists of whorled spindle cells and psammoma bodies lamellated calcifications . id 2 question A 6-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department because he has become increasingly clumsy falling frequently. His parents also note he complains of headaches every morning and occasionally vomits before school. Truncal ataxia and a right-sided abducens nerve palsy are noted on exam. MRI brain displays a large mass in the cerebellum compressing the fourth ventricle. Which of the following is true regarding this patient s condition? options The primary treatment is observation as this is a slow-growing benign tumor The patient must undergo full imaging of the spinal cord due to risk of drop metastases The tumor cells characteristically arrange around empty central lumens The tumor is derived from neoplastic ganglion cells and glia It is associated with an autosomal dominant mutation on chromosome 17 correctAnswer 1 rationale This clinical picture describes Medulloblastoma the most common malignant pediatric brain tumor. It classically arises in the cerebellum causing ataxia and can obstruct the 4th ventricle causing hydrocephalus morning vomiting headache CN VI palsy . Because it readily seeds the cerebrospinal fluid full neuraxis spinal imaging is strictly necessary to rule out drop metastases. id 3 question A 22-year-old male with no significant past medical history presents to the neurology clinic for evaluation of ringing in his right ear and progressive right-sided hearing loss over the past year. He also reports recent sensations of dizziness. Physical examination uncovers decreased hearing in the right ear and decreased pinprick sensation over his right cheek. MRI demonstrates a mass at the right cerebellopontine angle. If this patient s tumor is removed what is the most likely finding on light microscopy? options Psammoma bodies Rosenthal fibers and loose microcysts Verocay bodies Fried-egg cells and chicken-wire capillaries Pseudopalisading necrosis correctAnswer 2 rationale The patient has a Vestibular Schwannoma causing right-sided deafness tinnitus and vertigo CN VIII involvement as well as facial numbness CN V compression . Pathologically Schwannomas are defined by dense Antoni A areas and loose Antoni B areas with Verocay bodies regions of nuclear palisading present within the Antoni A regions. id 4 question A 28-year-old female presents to the dermatologist requesting removal of several bumpy skin tags that have been developing over her chest and back during the past five years. She states she has a history of learning troubles in school. Physical examination reveals multiple soft flush-colored nodular lesions extensive axillary freckling and six irregular flat light-brown macules on her torso. Which of the following neoplasms is this patient heavily predisposed to developing? options Malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumor MPNST Pilocytic astrocytoma Ependymoma Vestibular schwannoma Meningioma correctAnswer 0 rationale This patient possesses multiple stigmata of Neurofibromatosis Type 1 NF1 neurofibromas axillary freckling café-au-lait spots and learning disabilities. Patients with NF1 have a significant risk up to 10% of their neurofibromas particularly the plexiform subtype undergoing malignant transformation into Malignant Peripheral Nerve Sheath Tumors MPNSTs . id 5 question During a surgical resection of a peripheral nerve tumor from a patient s median nerve the surgeon notes that the mass is entirely non-encapsulated. It appears to infiltrate the native nerve fascicles diffusely making removing the mass without sacrificing the native nerve impossible. The genetic mutation most likely responsible for this specific tumor presentation inhibits which of the following cellular pathways? options E-cadherin contact inhibition Ras oncoprotein activity P53 tumor suppression VEGF-mediated angiogenesis Apoptosis via Bcl-2 over-expression correctAnswer 1 rationale The tumor described—unencapsulated and infiltrating the nerve fascicles—is a Neurofibroma unlike a Schwannoma which is encapsulated and can be shelled out . Neurofibromas occur due to the loss of neurofibromin the NF1 gene product which normally acts as a negative regulator of the Ras pathway. Loss of neurofibromin therefore disinhibits Ras leading to unchecked cellular proliferation. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l74", "title": "Lecture #74: Neuro Birth Defects", "module": "Neurology", "content": "id l74 title Lecture 74 Neuro Birth Defects lecturer W. Zaloga D.O. module Neurology session Lecture 74 prev l73 next l75 pdf pdfs/Lecture 74. W. Zaloga D.O. PPT .pdf summary Lecture 74 Neuro Birth Defects Lecturer W. Zaloga D.O. Session Lecture 74 1. Neuroembryology Review Neurological birth defects often originate from problems during embryogenesis. The nervous system develops from the ectoderm induced by the notochord whose remnants become the nucleus pulposus . The process of neurulation forms the neural plate which folds into the neural tube . The tips of the folds are neural crest cells which migrate to form the peripheral nervous system and other tissues. The neural tube itself forms the central nervous system brain and spinal cord and its central cavity becomes the ventricular system . Failure of proper closure or neuronal migration during these embryonic stages leads to various congenital anomalies. highyield - Notochord Induces overlying ectoderm to form the neural plate. Remnant = nucleus pulposus. - Neural Crest Cells Migrate to form PNS and melanocytes among other tissues. - Neural Tube Forms the CNS neurons and glial cells and ventricular system. 2. Neural Tube Defects NTDs Neural tube defects occur due to failure of the neural tube or its neuropores to close typically around weeks 3-4 . This leaves a persistent connection between the amniotic cavity and the spinal canal. Risk Factors Maternal malnutrition alcoholism obesity diabetes significant risk low folate levels and certain drugs e.g. valproic acid carbamazepine phenytoin . Prevention Folic acid supplementation $\\ge$ 0.4 mg/day . Markers Elevated maternal serum and amniotic alpha-fetoprotein AFP and amniotic acetylcholinesterase except in spina bifida occulta where AFP is normal . Spina Bifida Occulta - Pathology Defect in the bony vertebral arch but the neural tube is closed. The spinal cord and meninges are in their normal location. - Presentation Usually asymptomatic. Often marked by a skin signpost like a tuft of hair hypertrichosis lipoma or capillary malformation in the lower back midline. Meningocele & Myelomeningocele - Meningocele Dilated meninges protrude through a bony defect. Skin is typically present but thin. The spinal cord remains in its correct location. - Myelomeningocele Open Defect The spinal cord and meninges herniate through the defect. The skin is usually absent or very thin. - Clinical Almost always associated with a Chiari II malformation and consequent hydrocephalus. The leading cause of death in these patients is hydrocephalus. - Myeloschisis/Rachischisis The most severe form with exposed and unfused neural tissue without skin or meningeal covering. Encephalocele & Anencephaly - Encephalocele Cranium Bifidum Protrusion of a CSF-filled meningeal sac cranial meningocele or sac containing brain tissue encephalocele through a midline cranial defect. Associated with Chiari malformations and Dandy-Walker anomaly. - Anencephaly Failure of the rostral cephalad neuropore to close. Results in absence of the forebrain and an open cranial vault. - Clinical Causes polyhydramnios because the fetus cannot swallow amniotic fluid. Incompatible with life. correlate Maternal Diabetes & NTDs Maternal diabetes is a significant risk factor for neural tube defects. It s crucial for diabetic mothers to have good glycemic control and take sufficient folic acid prior to conception to minimize this risk. 3. Disorders of Neuronal Migration Neuronal migration issues affect the movement of glia and neurons to form the brain s layered structure e.g. the 6-layered cerebral cortex . This leads to disorganized ectopic or poorly formed gyri/sulci typically resulting in intellectual disability and intractable seizures . Lissencephaly Agyria - Pathology Smooth brain syndrome . Failure of gyri and sulci development. - Presentation Markedly delayed development intellectual disability severe seizures microcephaly. Can have hydrocephalus due to CSF flow issues. Schizencephaly - Pathology Split brain . Clefts in the cerebral hemispheres that extend from the cortex to the ventricles. Frequently bordered by microgyria. - Presentation Severe intellectual disability intractable seizures microcephaly hydrocephalus. May require surgical removal of epileptogenic foci. Neuronal Heterotopias - Pathology Ectopic neurons in the white matter or periventricular space. They didn t migrate to the surface properly. - Subtypes - Subcortical Band Heterotopia Thin layer of gray matter inside white matter. - Periventricular Nodular Heterotopia Nodules of gray matter along the ventricles. - Presentation Intractable seizures. Polymicrogyria - Pathology Abnormally small and numerous gyri commonly along the Sylvian fissure. - Causes Genetic mutations prenatal infections or abnormal twinning. - Presentation Drug-resistant epilepsy cognitive impairment. 4. Agenesis/Dysgenesis Disorders & Forebrain Anomalies These are developmental disorders involving defective formation of the prosencephalon forebrain . Corpus Callosum Agenesis - Pathology Partial or complete failure of corpus callosum formation. - Presentation If isolated the patient may be asymptomatic. If part of a syndrome e.g. with polymicrogyria it can cause significant neurological abnormalities seizures cognitive issues vision/hearing problems . Holoprosencephaly - Pathology Failure of the embryonic forebrain prosencephalon to divide into two cerebral hemispheres. Often due to mutations in the Sonic Hedgehog SHH signaling pathway. - Severity - Lobar Mildest nearly complete separation. - Semilobar Incomplete separation with a mono-ventricle. - Alobar Severe a single large cerebrum. - Presentation Often associated with craniofacial anomalies e.g. cyclopia a single nostril cleft palate . Seen in Patau syndrome Trisomy 13 . mnemonic Holo -prosencephaly Brain is a Holo whole single lobe. Associated with Sonic Hedgehog . 5. Size Anomalies Microcephaly & Megalencephaly - Microcephaly Head circumference $>$ 3 SDs below the mean. Caused by genetics infections teratogens alcohol radiation malnutrition. Generally poor prognosis with developmental delays and intellectual disability. - Megalencephaly / Macrocephaly - Megalencephaly Increased brain mass due to increased cell number/size e.g. Benign Familial Megalencephaly . Often linked to neuropsychiatric disorders particularly autism . - Macrocephaly Large head circumference which can be due to hydrocephalus increased fluid not brain tissue . 6. Posterior Fossa Anomalies These malformations typically involve the cerebellum brainstem and fourth ventricle often affecting CSF flow. Dandy-Walker Malformation - Pathology Partial or complete agenesis of the cerebellar vermis . Results in cystic dilation of the 4th ventricle large posterior fossa cyst . - Presentation Usually presents in the first year with hydrocephalus macrocephaly . Can include delayed milestones respiratory failure or be associated with spina bifida. Chiari Malformations Hindbrain abnormalities with varying degrees of cerebellar tonsillar herniation through the foramen magnum. - Chiari I - Pathology Herniation of cerebellar tonsils $>$ 5mm through the foramen magnum. - Presentation Usually asymptomatic in childhood presenting in adulthood with occipital headaches that worsen with Valsalva maneuvers coughing laughing . Often associated with syringomyelia . - Chiari II - Pathology Herniation of the cerebellar vermis brainstem and 4th ventricle. - Presentation Causes non-communicating hydrocephalus aqueductal stenosis . Strongly associated with myelomeningocele present in $>$ 95% . Often a neurosurgical emergency. 7. Syringomyelia & Hydrocephalus Syringomyelia - Pathology A fluid-filled cyst syrinx developing within the spinal cord tissue most commonly cervicothoracic . Often associated with Chiari I malformation trauma or tumors modifying CSF flow. - Clinical Presentation Causes a bilateral cape-like loss of pain and temperature sensation in the upper extremities. This is because the syrinx expands and compresses the decussating fibers of the spinothalamic tract in the anterior white commissure. Hydrocephalus - Pathology Condition characterized by impaired circulation or absorption of CSF leading to enlarged ventricles and increased intracranial pressure. - Causes - Congenital Aqueductal stenosis Dandy-Walker Chiari II holoprosencephaly. - Acquired Intraventricular hemorrhage tumors adhesions post-surgery or CNS infection. - Presentation Rapidly increasing head circumference across percentiles in infants. Vomiting ataxia and papilledema due to Increased ICP. - Treatment Often requires surgical intervention e.g. VP shunt . 8. Cerebral Palsy - Pathology A non-progressive disorder of movement posture and tone caused by a developmental disturbance or insult to the immature brain prenatal perinatal or postnatal . - Causes The most common cause is perinatal asphyxia hypoxic-ischemic event . Other causes include premature birth leading risk factor infantile meningitis/encephalitis and head trauma. - Presentation Various patterns depending on the brain lesion e.g. spastic athetoid ataxic . It is fundamentally a motor disorder; intellect and cognition may be entirely intact. The patient might exhibit hyperreflexia spasticity and abnormal postures e.g. scissoring of legs . analysis content/L74 HighYield Render Analysis.md ankingResource primarySource BandB chapter Neurology > Congenital > Neural Tube Defects alternatives resource Pathoma chapter Central Nervous System > Congenital Malformations resource FirstAid chapter Neurology > Embryology flashcards front Which neural tube defect is characterized by a failure of the rostral cephalad neuropore to close? back Anencephaly. tag Concept front What is the primary embryonic structure that induces the overlying ectoderm to form the neural plate? back The notochord. tag Concept front What are the common maternal serum and amniotic fluid markers for an open neural tube defect? back Elevated alpha-fetoprotein AFP and elevated acetylcholinesterase. tag Clinical front A tuft of hair hypertrichosis on the lower back is a classic sign of which neural tube defect? back Spina bifida occulta. tag Clinical front Which malformation is almost universally associated with a myelomeningocele? back Chiari II malformation. tag Clinical front What disorder is characterized by a smooth brain lacking normal gyri and sulci due to defective neuronal migration? back Lissencephaly Agyria . tag Concept front Holoprosencephaly is most closely associated with a mutation in which signaling pathway? back Sonic Hedgehog SHH pathway. tag Pathology front What genetic syndrome is commonly associated with holoprosencephaly and cyclopia? back Patau syndrome Trisomy 13 . tag Pathology front What posterior fossa anomaly is defined by the absence of the cerebellar vermis leading to cystic dilation of the 4th ventricle? back Dandy-Walker malformation. tag Concept front A patient with cape-like loss of pain and temperature sensation in the upper extremities likely has what condition? back Syringomyelia. tag Clinical front Which Chiari malformation is characterized by the herniation of cerebellar tonsils >5mm through the foramen magnum often presenting with occipital headaches exacerbated by Valsalva? back Chiari I malformation. tag Clinical front What non-progressive disorder of movement and posture is most commonly caused by perinatal asphyxia or premature birth? back Cerebral Palsy. tag Clinical pearls title Neuroembryology content The notochord induces overlying ectoderm to form the neural plate. Notochord remnants become the nucleus pulposus. title Maternal Diabetes & NTDs content Maternal diabetes is a significant risk factor for neural tube defects. It s crucial for diabetic mothers to have good glycemic control and take sufficient folic acid prior to conception to minimize this risk. title Holo-prosencephaly content Brain is a Holo whole single lobe. Associated with Sonic Hedgehog mutations. questions id 1 question A mother gives birth to a neonate who initially feeds poorly and exhibits severe seizures. MRI reveals a brain with an almost completely smooth surface lacking normal gyri and sulci. This condition is a result of an abnormality in which of the following processes? options Neural tube closure Neuronal migration Ascending colon rotation Notochord induction Pharyngeal arch development correctAnswer 1 rationale The condition described is lissencephaly agyria which presents as a smooth brain lacking normal gyri and sulci. It is caused by defective neuronal migration during embryogenesis. Affected infants often have severe developmental delays intellectual disability and intractable seizures. id 2 question A 28-year-old woman is evaluated for occipital headaches that she says are triggered whenever she laughs or coughs. An MRI of her brain reveals cerebellar tonsils extending 8 mm below the level of the foramen magnum. Which of the following conditions is most strongly associated with her diagnosis? options Myelomeningocele Syringomyelia Holoprosencephaly Dandy-Walker malformation Encephalocele correctAnswer 1 rationale This patient has symptoms and MRI findings consistent with a Chiari I malformation herniation of cerebellar tonsils >5 mm through the foramen magnum . Chiari I often presents in adulthood with headaches exacerbated by Valsalva maneuvers and is heavily associated with the development of syringomyelia a fluid-filled cyst in the spinal cord . id 3 question A newborn is noted to have a large midline head defect through which a sac of meninges and brain tissue is protruding. Examination of prenatal records shows polyhydramnios. Failure of which of the following embryonic structures to close is the most likely cause of this patient s condition? options Caudal neuropore Rostral neuropore Notochord Prosencephalon Neural crest correctAnswer 1 rationale The presence of polyhydramnios and a severe midline cranial defect with protruding brain tissue describes anencephaly or an encephalocele variant. Anencephaly often indicated by polyhydramnios as the fetus is unable to swallow amniotic fluid results from the failure of the rostral cephalad neuropore to close. id 4 question A newborn presenting with a cleft palate cyclopia and a single central nostril has an MRI demonstrating a single large mono-ventricle with fusion of the cerebral hemispheres. Which of the following signaling pathways is most likely mutated in this condition? options FGF Wnt Sonic Hedgehog Notch TGF-beta correctAnswer 2 rationale The infant s presentation is classic for alobar holoprosencephaly characterized by failure of the prosencephalon to separate into two hemispheres presenting with midline facial defects like cyclopia . Mutations in the Sonic Hedgehog SHH signaling pathway are a known primary cause of holoprosencephaly. It is also strongly associated with Patau syndrome Trisomy 13 . id 5 question A 32-year-old male presents with bilateral loss of pain and temperature sensation across his shoulders and arms but fine touch and proprioception are preserved. He states he recently burned his hand on the stove and didn t initially feel it. An MRI of the cervical spine is most likely to show which of the following? options Compression of the posterior columns Dilated central cavity within the spinal cord Demyelination of the lateral corticospinal tract Anterior horn cell degeneration Herniation of the cerebellar vermis correctAnswer 1 rationale The patient s cape-like distribution of pain and temperature sensory loss is the hallmark of syringomyelia. It occurs due to a syrinx a fluid-filled cyst or dilated central cavity in the cervicothoracic spinal cord that expands and compresses the decussating spinothalamic nerve fibers in the anterior white commissure while sparing the posterior columns fine touch/proprioception . id 6 question A pregnant woman undergoes prenatal screening revealing elevated levels of alpha-fetoprotein AFP and acetylcholinesterase in the amniotic fluid. Which of the following conditions in the fetus is the most likely cause of these findings? options Spina bifida occulta Dandy-Walker malformation Lissencephaly Myelomeningocele Down syndrome correctAnswer 3 rationale Elevated amniotic AFP and acetylcholinesterase indicates an open neural tube defect where the spinal canal communicates with the amniotic fluid. Myelomeningocele is an open neural tube defect. Of note spina bifida occulta is a closed defect covered by skin so AFP remains normal. id 7 question A neonate is found to have a severe tuft of hair over the lumbosacral region. Imaging shows a defect in the vertebral arch but the spinal cord and meninges are in their typical position. What is the most likely diagnosis? options Meningocele Myeloschisis Syringomyelia Spina bifida occulta Encephalocele correctAnswer 3 rationale Spina bifida occulta is the mildest neural tube defect involving a failure of the bony vertebral arch to fuse. The spinal cord and meninges are typically in their normal position. It is often asymptomatic but can be marked by a dimple lipoma or a tuft of hair hypertrichosis over the defect. id 8 question An infant comes to the clinic for a well-child check. The pediatrician measures the infant s head circumference and finds it has drastically jumped from the 50th percentile to the 95th percentile over the last two months. Additionally an MRI shows cystic dilation of the fourth ventricle and absence of the cerebellar vermis. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? options Chiari II malformation Dandy-Walker malformation Holoprosencephaly Subcortical band heterotopia Benign familial megalencephaly correctAnswer 1 rationale Dandy-Walker malformation is characterized by agenesis/hypoplasia of the cerebellar vermis leading to a massive cystic dilation of the 4th ventricle taking up the posterior fossa. This leads to non-communicating hydrocephalus resulting in rapidly expanding head circumference macrocephaly in infants. id 9 question A healthy infant born prematurely at 28 weeks gestation is evaluated in the NICU. By their first year the parents notice their child s motor milestones are severely delayed. Examination reveals hypertonia hyperreflexia and scissoring of the legs when held upright. Intelligence seems appropriately interactive. What is the most likely cause and diagnosis for this patient? options Intractable seizures causing neuronal heterotopia Perinatal asphyxia or ischemia causing cerebral palsy Maternal valproic acid use causing myelomeningocele Genetic mutation causing Chiari I malformation Deficient folic acid causing anencephaly correctAnswer 1 rationale The presentation of non-progressive motor-dominant delays with hypertonia/spasticity in a child with a history of premature birth strongly points to cerebral palsy. The most common underlying cause for cerebral palsy is an insult to the developing brain such as perinatal asphyxia ischemia or hemorrhage occurring before or during birth. id 10 question A patient with a myelomeningocele begins experiencing sudden respiratory distress and rapid deterioration. Imaging confirms herniation of the cerebellar vermis brainstem and fourth ventricle through the foramen magnum. This life-threatening complication is known as options Chiari I malformation Chiari II malformation Dandy-Walker malformation Syringomyelia Meningocele correctAnswer 1 rationale A Chiari II malformation involves the down-ward herniation of the cerebellar vermis brainstem and the 4th ventricle through the foramen magnum. It is extremely heavily associated with myelomeningoceles >95% of patients with a myelomeningocele have a Chiari II . It often presents as an emergency due to hydrocephalus or brainstem compression causing respiratory failure. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l75", "title": "Lecture #75: Pediatric Neurology 1", "module": "Neurology", "content": "id l75 title Lecture 75 Pediatric Neurology 1 lecturer T. Chan D.O. module Neurology session Lecture 75 prev l74 next l76 pdf pdfs/Lecture 75. T. Chan D.O. PPT .pdf summary Lecturer T. Chan D.O. Session Lecture 75 Introduction to Pediatric Seizures Slide 2 Seizures in pediatrics often lack the classic full shaking presentation seen in adults; they can be incredibly subtle. Infants and young children may exhibit blank stares random falling atonic seizures lip smacking or repetitive movements that only a keen observer or a home video might catch. Because the immature brain of an infant naturally produces assorted odd movements establishing whether an event is a true seizure or a benign phenomenon requires careful history-taking and observation. - Identifying Seizures in Infants - Subtle Presentations Can manifest as lip smacking startles jerks rhythmic eye blinking or rolling. - Diagnostic Tools Home videos are highly valuable since providers rarely witness the event. - Action Steps Always time the seizure turn the patient on their side to prevent aspiration and avoid putting anything in their mouth. correlate Neonatal Seizures - Pathophysiology Immature brain susceptibility combined with insults like hypoxia hemorrhage or metabolic derangements. - Presentation Subtle movements lip smacking jitteriness. - Diagnosis EEG and thorough history timing postictal state . - Treatment Correct underlying cause e.g. hypoglycemia electrolytes sepsis . - Lecturer s Point Neonatal seizures almost always imply an underlying insult rather than primary epilepsy. Febrile Seizures Slide 8 9 11 Febrile seizures are the most common neurologic disorder of infants and young children. They occur when the immature brain is subjected to a rapid rise in body temperature which lowers the seizure threshold. Reassuring parents is a primary goal as these events are typically benign and self-resolving requiring no further workup if all criteria for a simple febrile seizure are met. - Criteria for Simple Febrile Seizures Slide 8 - Age Range 6 months to 5 years. - Duration Less than 15 minutes. - Characteristics Generalized tonic-clonic. - Frequency Occurs only once in a 24-hour period. - Postictal State Full recovery afterward often sleep or quiet period . - Prerequisites No prior neurological issues or afebrile seizures. - Complex Febrile Seizures Slide 9 - Focal features lasting >15 minutes occurring outside the typical age range or presenting with >1 episode in 24 hours. Warrant further workup e.g. EEG neurology referral . highyield Simple Febrile Seizures do not require extensive workup like a lumbar puncture or EEG if the child returns to baseline and has a clear source of infection like otitis media . correlate Febrile Seizures - Pathophysiology Rate of rise in body temperature often independent of the absolute peak temperature lowers the seizure threshold. - Presentation Generalized tonic-clonic movements in the setting of fever followed by a postictal phase. - Diagnosis Clinical; must meet strict age and duration criteria. - Treatment Reassurance for simple febrile seizures. Rectal diazepam if prolonged >5 mins. - Risk Factors High fever strong genetic susceptibility 10-20% of parents/siblings have a history . - Lecturer s Point Parents feel immense guilt but cannot prevent these by aggressively treating fevers; emphasize that natural brain development eventually resolves the susceptibility. Epileptic Syndromes of Childhood Slide 12-27 Childhood epilepsy encompasses a wide spectrum from benign variants that are outgrown to severe encephalopathies that permanently halt development. It is essential to differentiate between devastating syndromes like Infantile Spasms and benign entities like Benign Myoclonus of Infancy since their prognoses and treatments differ astronomically. | Syndrome | Age of Onset | Key Clinical Feature | EEG Finding | Treatment/Notes | |---|---|---|---|---| | Infantile Spasms West Syndrome | 3-7 months | Clustered sit-up flexor spasms | Hypsarrhythmia chaotic | ACTH or Vigabatrin; High risk of cognitive impairment. Associated with Tuberous Sclerosis. | | Benign Myoclonus of Infancy | 3-8 months | Sporadic brief shuddering jerks | Normal | Reassurance; remits spontaneously. | | Absence Seizures | 4-10 years | Sudden blank stares/daydreaming provoked by hyperventilation | 3 Hz spike and wave | Ethosuximide 1st line . | | BECTS Rolandic Epilepsy | 7-9 years | Simple partial seizures during sleep/awakening | Centrotemporal sharp waves | Often no AED needed; excellent prognosis. | | Juvenile Myoclonic Epilepsy JME | Adolescence | Myoclonic jerks specifically upon awakening; triad also includes generalized & absence. Provoked by sleep deprivation or flickering lights | 4-6 Hz polyspike and wave | Valproic acid lifelong treatment needed . | | Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome | < 7 years | Multiple seizure types with cognitive dysfunction | Slow <2.5 Hz spike-wave | Multi-drug therapy; poor prognosis. | correlate Infantile Spasms West Syndrome - Pathophysiology Often secondary to insults like hypoxia brain injury or genetic syndromes Tuberous Sclerosis . - Presentation Clusters of flexor spasms jackknife seizures often misidentified as colic. - Diagnosis Hypsarrhythmia on EEG. - Treatment Hormonal therapy ACTH or Vigabatrin. - Complications Developmental regression loyal of milestones and lifelong cognitive impairment. - Lecturer s Point Look for ash leaf spots with a Wood s lamp to rule in Tuberous Sclerosis. correlate Absence Seizures - Pathophysiology Sudden profound impairment of consciousness without loss of body tone. - Presentation Sudden blanking out that mimics daydreaming; provoked by hyperventilation. - Diagnosis 3 Hz spike and wave on EEG. - Treatment Ethosuximide or Valproic Acid. Valproic Acid covers more broad spectrum. - Lecturer s Point You can elicit these in the clinic by having the child hyperventilate e.g. using a pinwheel . correlate Juvenile Myoclonic Epilepsy JME - Pathophysiology Genetic generalized epilepsy syndrome. - Presentation Myoclonic jerks typically occurring right after waking up. - Diagnosis 4-6 Hz polyspike and wave on EEG. - Treatment Broad-spectrum AEDs like Valproic acid. - Risk Factors Sleep deprivation and photosensitivity flickering lights/video games . - Lecturer s Point This condition requires lifelong medication contrary to childhood epilepsies like Rolandic which are outgrown. Noncompliance in teenagers is a major trigger. Non-Seizure Abnormal Movements Slide 46 Not every dramatic spelling or jerking is a seizure. Toddlers and infants present with bizarre reactions to emotional distress reflux or typical medications. Recognizing these conditions prevents unnecessary and invasive epilepsy workups allowing the clinician to calm worried parents. - Chorea Sydenham s Chorea - Often seen post-Group A Strep infection as part of Rheumatic Fever. - Presents as continuous non-rhythmic writing movements and poor handwriting. - Acute Dystonic Reactions - Caused by medications antiemetics with phenothiazines . - Rapidly reversed with IV diphenhydramine Benadryl . - Sandifer Syndrome - Severe gastroesophageal reflux causing infant to arch their back bizarrely mimicking a seizure. - Self-Stimulation - Toddlers may accidentally self-stimulate causing shuddering and eye-rolling. Managed by distraction and changing the environment. correlate Breath-Holding Spells - Pathophysiology An extreme vagal response or temper tantrum causing momentary cerebral hypoxia. - Presentation Toddler cries heavily during a tantrum turns blue and passes out occasionally with brief twitching. - Diagnosis Clinical history. - Treatment Reassurance. It is benign. - Risk Factors Associated with iron deficiency anemia lower oxygen carrying capacity leads to faster hypoxia . - Lecturer s Point The history of an inciting emotional trigger like taking away a candy bar is key to the diagnosis. Ataxia in Children Slide 38 39 46 When a child suddenly cannot walk straight the differential spans from benign post-viral syndromes to catastrophic tumors. The timeline of onset—whether acute or progressive—is the most telling feature. | Condition | Age of Onset | Characteristics | Course/Prognosis | |---|---|---|---| | Acute Cerebellar Ataxia | 2-5 years | Post-infectious sudden onset unsteadiness | Self-limited weeks . Diagnosis of exclusion. | | Benign Positional Vertigo | Young child | Sudden attacks of vertigo; child sits down confused | Self-limited; often a migraine precursor. | | Ataxia Telangiectasia | Early childhood | Ataxia telangiectasias recurrent sinopulmonary infections | Progressive; high risk of cancer. | | Friedrich s Ataxia | >10 years | Distal weakness loss of reflexes hypertrophic cardiomyopathy | Progressive; monitor cardiac status. | highyield Always obtain a cardiology consult for patients suspected of having Friedrich s Ataxia to evaluate for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. Simple Febrile Seizure Criteria Must be 6 months to 5 years old lasting less than 15 minutes generalized non-focal occurring only once in a 24-hour period with a full return to baseline. Hypsarrhythmia The classic chaotic EEG pattern seen in Infantile Spasms West Syndrome correlating with an incredibly poor developmental prognosis. Absence Seizures & Hyperventilation Having a child hyperventilate such as blowing on a pinwheel can provoke an absence seizure in the clinic allowing for direct observation. Rolandic Epilepsy Course Rolandic epilepsy involves central temporal spikes and seizures occurring near sleep but carries an excellent prognosis and is almost always outgrown without lifelong medication. Dystonic Reaction Reversal A sudden painful dystonic reaction often drug-induced by antiemetics can be rapidly reversed with IV diphenhydramine Benadryl . Breath-Holding Spells association Children who suffer from breath-holding spells should be evaluated for iron deficiency anemia as diminished oxygen-carrying capacity exacerbates the spells. questions A 15-month-old girl is brought to the emergency department after a 3-minute episode of generalized shaking. Her parents report that she had a runny nose yesterday and her rectal temperature in the ED is 103.1°F. She is currently asleep but awakens easily and acts at her neurologic baseline. She has no prior history of seizures. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management? A. Perform a lumbar puncture to rule out meningitis. B. Discharge with reassurance and antipyretics. C. Prescribe daily valproic acid. D. Order a stat electroencephalogram EEG . E. Administer IV lorazepam. B This patient meets all the criteria for a simple febrile seizure age between 6 months and 5 years generalized tonic-clonic activity duration less than 15 minutes single occurrence in 24 hours and no prior neurologic history. Because she has returned to her baseline and has a clear source of fever URI no further workup like LP or EEG is indicated and she can be managed with reassurance and antipyretics. A 5-month-old male is brought to the clinic by his parents because he has been jerking multiple times a day. The episodes consist of sudden flexion of his head and torso resembling a sit-up and occur in clusters. His parents note he is no longer rolling over. An EEG shows hypsarrhythmia. Which of the following conditions is most strongly associated with this patient s diagnosis? A. Rheumatic fever B. Friedrich s Ataxia C. Tuberous Sclerosis D. Iron deficiency anemia E. Group A Streptococcus infection C This infant is presenting with infantile spasms West syndrome characterized by flexor spasms developmental regression and hypsarrhythmia on EEG. Infantile spasms are strongly associated with Tuberous Sclerosis which can present early on with hypopigmented macules ash leaf spots . A 6-year-old boy is brought to the pediatrician by his mother due to frequent episodes of daydreaming at school. His teacher reports that he will suddenly stare blankly for 5-10 seconds drop his pencil and then resume his work as if nothing happened. During the clinic visit the physician has the boy blow heavily on a pinwheel for one minute which precipitates an episode. Which of the following is the first-line medication for this condition? A. Ethosuximide B. Carbamazepine C. Vigabatrin D. Phenytoin E. Diphenhydramine A The patient s presentation and provocation by hyperventilation are classic for absence seizures. The EEG would typically show a 3 Hz spike-and-wave pattern. The first-line treatment for absence seizures is ethosuximide. Note that drugs like carbamazepine and phenytoin can actually exacerbate absence seizures. A 16-year-old male is brought to the clinic after experiencing a generalized tonic-clonic seizure while cramming for his final exams on 3 hours of sleep. He mentions that for the past year he occasionally drops his toothbrush in the morning due to sudden jerks in his arms. An EEG demonstrates 4-6 Hz polyspike and wave discharges. Which of the following best describes the long-term prognosis for his condition? A. He will likely outgrow the condition by his early twenties. B. Spontaneous remission is expected if he normalizes his sleep schedule. C. Lifelong antiepileptic therapy is generally required. D. He is at high risk of developing severe cognitive regression. E. The condition typically evolves into Lennox-Gastaut syndrome. C This patient has Juvenile Myoclonic Epilepsy JME characterized by the triad of myoclonic jerks often in the morning generalized tonic-clonic seizures and absence seizures. It is famously provoked by sleep deprivation. Unlike childhood epilepsy syndromes e.g. Rolandic epilepsy JME typically requires lifelong treatment with a broad-spectrum antiepileptic drug like valproic acid. A 12-year-old girl is evaluated for a progressive decline in her ability to walk. She has noted weakness in her distal extremities and on physical exam she has absent deep tendon reflexes and high-arched feet. Which of the following complications is most critical to monitor for in this patient? A. Hepatocellular carcinoma B. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy C. Severe immunodeficiency D. Tuberous sclerosis E. Gastric ulcers B This patient s progressive ataxia distal weakness absent deep tendon reflexes and foot deformities pes cavus are highly suggestive of Friedrich s Ataxia. A critical associative complication of Friedrich s Ataxia is hypertrophic cardiomyopathy which requires evaluation and monitoring by a cardiologist. What are the criteria for a simple febrile seizure? Age 6mo-5yr <15 min duration generalized tonic-clonic 1 episode in 24 hours returning to baseline. Clinical What classic EEG finding is associated with Infantile Spasms? Hypsarrhythmia a chaotic background pattern . Concept Infantile Spasms are strongly associated with which genetic neurocutaneous syndrome? Tuberous Sclerosis look for ash leaf spots . Clinical What is the first-line treatment for Absence Seizures? Ethosuximide. Concept What simple maneuver can elicit an absence seizure in a clinical setting? Hyperventilation e.g. blowing on a pinwheel . Concept What is the key difference in prognosis between Rolandic Epilepsy and Juvenile Myoclonic Epilepsy JME ? Rolandic Epilepsy is almost always outgrown while JME requires lifelong antiepileptic treatment. Concept What medication should be used to rapidly reverse an acute dystonic reaction? IV diphenhydramine Benadryl . Clinical What is Sandifer Syndrome? Severe gastroesophageal reflux in an infant that causes sudden back-arching and abnormal posturing mimicking a seizure. Glossary Breath-holding spells are often exacerbated by what nutritional deficiency? Iron deficiency anemia. Clinical What cardiac condition is heavily associated with Friedrich s Ataxia? Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. Clinical BandB Pediatrics > Neurology > Seizures Pathoma Nervous System > Degenerative Disorders FirstAid Neurology > Pathology > Epilepsy SketchyPharm Neuro > Anticonvulsants anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l76", "title": "Lecture #76: Pediatric Neurology II", "module": "Neurology", "content": "id l76 title Lecture 76 Pediatric Neurology II lecturer T. Chan D.O. module Neurology session Lecture 76 prev l75 next l77 pdf pdfs/Lecture 76. T. Chan D.O. PPT -1.pdf summary Lecture 76 Pediatric Neurology II Lecturer T. Chan D.O. Session Lecture 76 1. Infant Hypotonia Slide 2 When evaluating an infant neurological tone is a critical component of the examination. Healthy infants should not lie flat like they re on a beach with completely splayed limbs; they should exhibit a natural flexed posture arms and legs slightly bent demonstrating an ability to fight gravity. Hypotonia a floppy baby is identified through maneuvers like assessing head lag when pulled to sit ventral suspension checking if they flop like a noodle and vertical suspension checking if they slip through the examiner s hands . Various genetic metabolic and muscular disorders can impair this critical baseline tone. Spinal Muscular Atrophy Type 1 Werdnig-Hoffmann Disease - Pathology Degeneration of the anterior horn cells in the spinal cord. - Presentation The classic floppy baby with profound generalized hypotonia and muscle weakness observed shortly after birth. - Details Babies have absent deep tendon reflexes areflexia and characteristic tongue fasciculations . These infants usually have normal to bright intellect bright eyes but are trapped in failing bodies quickly becoming wheelchair-bound and handicapped. - Complications Severe muscle weakness leads to difficulty swallowing and choking as well as significant respiratory compromise. correlate Infantile Spinal Muscular Atrophy Werdnig-Hoffmann - Pathology Degeneration of anterior horn cells. - Presentation Profound hypotonia floppy baby absent deep tendon reflexes and tongue fasciculations. Intellect remains intact. - Complications Swallowing difficulties respiratory failure wheelchair dependence at a very early age. - Lecturer s Point These kids have bodies that do not work right but their brains are working fine and they will look at you with bright observant eyes. Down Syndrome - Pathology Trisomy 21. - Presentation Infants often exhibit generalized hypotonia falling through your hands and significant joint hyper-laxity. This is often felt during the exam rather than appearing as a completely paralyzed floppy state like Werdnig-Hoffmann. correlate Down Syndrome Trisomy 21 - Presentation Generalized hypotonia and significant joint laxity in infancy. The baby might feel heavy or slip through your hands and head lag may be more pronounced. - Lecturer s Point You feel the hypotonia in these infants more than simply seeing it; they are noticeably weaker than normal though not fully paralyzed. Prader-Willi Syndrome - Pathology Deletion of the paternal chromosome 15 15q11-q13 . - Presentation These infants are born profoundly hypotonic and feed very poorly. However in later childhood a switch goes off leading to intense insatiable hyperphagia and subsequent severe obesity. - Physical Features Developmental delays hypogonadism and characteristically small hands and feet. correlate Prader-Willi Syndrome - Pathology Deletion of paternal chromosome 15. - Presentation Infant presents with severe hypotonia and failure to thrive/poor feeding. Later transitions to extreme hyperphagia developmental delay and severe obesity. - Treatment Extreme environmental controls to prevent overeating e.g. locking cabinets and refrigerators . - Lecturer s Point A lot of people forget that Prader-Willi infants start out incredibly floppy and have trouble eating before they switch into the extreme over-eating phase. Dermatomyositis - Pathology An autoimmune inflammatory myopathy that involves the skin and muscle. - Presentation Causes progressive proximal muscle weakness shoulders and hips . Patients have difficulty completing tasks like brushing hair reaching for high cupboards or climbing stairs. - Physical Signs Classically associated with a heliotrope rash violet-colored rash around eyelids and Gottron papules scaly patches over the joints . Duchenne and Becker Muscular Dystrophy - Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy DMD - Pathology An X-linked recessive mutation leading to absent or severely truncated dystrophin protein. - Presentation Progressive muscle weakness starting in early childhood. Patients exhibit Gowers sign using hands to walk up the legs when rising from a seated position and pseudohypertrophy of the calves replacement of muscle with fat and collagen . - Diagnosis Markedly elevated serum creatine kinase CK levels. - Complications Frequently causes a dilated cardiomyopathy leading to heart failure or arrhythmias. Death typically results from cardiovascular causes or respiratory failure due to weak diaphragm/chest wall muscles. - Becker Muscular Dystrophy BMD - Pathology Mutation resulting in partially functioning reduced or mutated dystrophin. - Presentation Similar to DMD but presents later in life and progresses more slowly. correlate Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy - Pathology Complete absence of functional dystrophin protein. - Presentation Proximal muscle weakness Gowers sign pseudohypertrophy of the calves. - Diagnosis Elevated serum Creatine Kinase CK . - Complications Dilated cardiomyopathy respiratory failure wheelchair dependence resulting in a shortened lifespan though advancing medical care has improved survival into the 30s-40s . - Lecturer s Point Keep them out of wheelchairs as long as possible using physical therapy because wheelchair dependence rapidly accelerates scoliosis and respiratory compromise. highyield Hypotonia Summary Table | Condition | Mechanism/Pathology | Key Feature | Complications | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | Werdnig-Hoffmann | Anterior horn cell degeneration | Floppy baby + tongue fasciculations | Respiratory/swallowing failure | | Prader-Willi | Paternal Chromosome 15 deletion | Infant hypotonia $\\\\rightarrow$ later hyperphagia | Severe obesity hypogonadism | | DMD | Absent dystrophin protein | Proximal weakness calf pseudohypertrophy | Cardiomyopathy respiratory failure | | Dermatomyositis | Autoimmune myopathy | Proximal weakness + Heliotrope rash | Trouble climbing stairs/brushing hair | 2. Head Shapes and Size Slide 17 The size and shape of an infant s head are critical indicators of underlying brain growth and possible congenital fusion issues. The skull initially consists of separate bony plates connected by flexible sutures and fontanels to allow passage through the birth canal molding and accommodate rapid brain growth. Measuring head circumference tracks brain expansion; falling off the curve may indicate poor brain growth or congenital infections while rapidly increasing percentiles could suggest macrocephaly or hydrocephalus. Fontanel Closure Timeline - Anterior Fontanel The larger diamond-shaped opening. Tends to close between 6 to 18 months of age average around 12 months . - Posterior Fontanel Smaller palpable as a slight depression. Closes very early usually around 1.5 to 2 months . Superficial Cranial Traumas - Caput Succedaneum A superficial boggy swelling of the scalp. It crosses suture lines as it is an edema above the periosteum. It is generally harmless and resolves spontaneously. - Cephalohematoma A subperiosteal hemorrhage blood accumulation under the periosteum often related to a difficult delivery e.g. vacuum extraction . It forms a distinct firm goose egg that does not cross suture lines . It generally reabsorbs but can increase the risk of neonatal jaundice bilirubin load as blood breaks down . mnemonic CAPUT Succedaneum C rosses A ll P arts U p T op It crosses the suture lines across the whole head like wearing a CAP . Cranial Shapes & Sutures - Positional Plagiocephaly Flattening of one side of the head extremely common due to the Back to Sleep campaign to prevent SIDS. Often cosmetic; treated with repositioning and tummy time when awake. Rarely requires a molding helmet. - Craniosynostosis Abnormal premature fusion of one or more cranial sutures. The brain continues to grow pushing perpendicularly away from the fused suture leading to a deformed skull and significant facial asymmetry. On exam a fused suture may feel like a raised mountain range rather than a flat plane. - Important Syndromes - Crouzon Syndrome Associated with craniosynostosis but often with normal intellect. - Apert Syndrome Associated with craniosynostosis but also features significant limb defects such as syndactyly fusion of digits . correlate Craniosynostosis - Pathology Premature fusion of cranial sutures restricting natural brain expansion. - Presentation Asymmetric head shape bossing of the forehead facial asymmetry unequal eyes ears nose creases . Palpable ridging along the fused suture. - Diagnosis 3D CT scan. - Treatment Surgical carpentry to release the fused bony plates to allow normal growth. - Lecturer s Point You must distinguish this from benign positional plagiocephaly by specifically looking for significant facial/anterior asymmetry. 3. Infections with Neurological Consequences Slide 24 Pediatric infections can severely impact the rapidly developing central and peripheral nervous systems. TORCH Infections - Include T oxoplasmosis O ther Parvovirus Varicella Syphilis R ubella C MV and H IV/HSV. - Presentation Often diagnosed when there are signs of restricted brain growth microcephaly hepatosplenomegaly hearing loss or abnormal skin rashes. Confirmed via TORCH titers. Meningitis in Infants - Presentation Infant meningitis can be subtle. Babies may exhibit lethargy extreme irritability a fever or dangerously low temp vomiting or seizures. A key physical finding in an infant with an open fontanel is a bulging tense anterior fontanel indicative of increased intracranial pressure. - Signs Severe cases might exhibit Cushing s Triad irregular respirations hypertension bradycardia . Kernig s and Brudzinski s signs are generally unreliable in young infants. Infant Botulism - Pathophysiology Ingestion of Clostridium botulinum spores classically from honey which germinate in the infant gut and release a neurotoxin blocking acetylcholine release. - Presentation A previously healthy normal baby suddenly develops progressive hypotonia lethargy poor gag reflex choking/drooling and descending weakness. - Complications Can progress to respiratory arrest. correlate Infant Botulism - Pathology Botulinum toxin prevents presynaptic acetylcholine release. - Presentation Acute-onset progressive hypotonia lethargy poor feeding weak cry descending paralysis. - Risk Factors Ingestion of honey spores in infants < 1 year old or improperly canned goods. - Treatment Supportive care; human botulism immune globulin. Febrile Seizures & Specific Pathogens - Roseola A viral exanthem causing high fever. As the fever breaks a benign rash appears. The rapid rise in extreme temperature can occasionally trigger a simple febrile seizure . - Shigella A gastrointestinal pathogen that excretes Shiga toxin . This acts as a neurotoxin effectively lowering the seizure threshold . Shigella infections complicated by fever are particularly notorious for causing seizures in children. Bell s Palsy - Presentation Acute unilateral lower motor neuron facial paralysis often viral/idiopathic . - Treatment Note In pediatrics patience is key. The condition typically resolves spontaneously over weeks. The most critical intervention is ensuring the affected eye does not dry out; use an eye patch or frequent lubricating eye drops because the child cannot close the eyelid fully. Corticosteroids Prednisone are used less aggressively in kids than in adults. 4. Trauma & Injury Slide 29 Traumatic injuries whether accidental birth-related or non-accidental can have long-lasting neurological repercussions. Brachial Plexus Injuries - Generally arise from a difficult delivery and severe traction on the shoulder/neck. - Erb s Palsy Upper Trunk The classic waiter s tip posture. Characterized by an asymmetric Moro reflex only the healthy arm splays outward . Must differentiate from a simple clavicular fracture which also presents with an asymmetric Moro. - Klumpke s Palsy Lower Trunk A worse prognosis. Leads to intrinsic hand muscle weakness poor grip . correlate Brachial Plexus Birth Palsy - Pathology Traction injury during delivery. - Presentation Asymmetric Moro reflex. - If upper Erb s Waiter s tip good hand grip. - If lower Klumpke s Weak hand grip claw hand. - Lecturer s Point Always put your finger in the baby s hand. If they have a good grip you can breathe easier because it s Erb s and not Klumpke s. Shaken Baby Syndrome Abusive Head Trauma - Mechanism The infant head is large and the neck muscles are too weak to stabilize against violent shaking forces. This shears bridging veins and delicate vessels. - Presentation Lethargy vomiting seizures or coma. - Diagnosis Characterized by subdural hematomas crescent-shaped on CT and retinal hemorrhages visible on dilated ophthalmologic exam . Concussions - Presentation Highly variable. Patients can be physically uncoordinated balance nausea headaches cognitively impaired foggy memory difficulties or emotionally labile. Sleep disturbances either insomnia or sleeping constantly are an incredibly common often overlooked symptom. - Management - Strict physical and cognitive rest initially no screens no texting dark room . - Progress slowly back into low-intensity activities. - Return to play must purely be driven by complete clinical resolution. The neurological exam must be 100% normal—including complex tasks like tandem gait heel-to-toe walking . highyield Return-to-Play Concussion Protocol A patient cannot be cleared to return to sports until they are 100% asymptomatic and pass a rigorous neurological exam. Pay special attention to cerebellar tests such as tandem gait walking heel-to-toe . Even if an athlete feels fine failure to confidently perform a tandem gait indicates incomplete recovery. analysis content/L76 HighYield Render Analysis.md ankingResource primarySource BandB chapter Neurology > Muscle Disorders > Muscular Dystrophy alternatives resource Pathoma chapter Central Nervous System > Neuropathology resource FirstAid chapter Neurology > Neuroanatomy resource BandB chapter Neurology > Congenital > Neural Tube Defects flashcards front What is the classic clinical presentation of an infant with Werdnig-Hoffmann disease SMA Type 1 ? back Profound hypotonia floppy baby absent deep tendon reflexes and tongue fasciculations with preserved intellect. tag Clinical front Which congenital syndrome usually presents with an infant who slips through your hands due to hypotonia and profound joint laxity? back Down Syndrome Trisomy 21 . tag Clinical front What syndrome presents with severe hypotonia in infancy eventually transitioning to extreme hyperphagia and obesity in childhood? back Prader-Willi Syndrome Paternal Chromosome 15 deletion . tag Clinical front What clinical sign involves a child using their hands and arms to walk up their own body from a squatting position due to proximal muscle weakness? back Gowers sign classically seen in Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy . tag Clinical front What is the most common cause of morbidity/mortality in patients with Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy? back Dilated cardiomyopathy leading to heart failure and respiratory failure. tag Pathology front When do the anterior and posterior fontanels typically close in a healthy infant? back Anterior 6 to 18 months average 12 mo . Posterior 1.5 to 2 months. tag Concept front What neonatal head swelling crosses the cranial suture lines? back Caput Succedaneum It s like a CAP on the head . tag Clinical front What neonatal head swelling is confined and does NOT cross the suture lines? back Cephalohematoma subperiosteal hemorrhage . tag Clinical front What condition is characterized by premature fusion of the cranial sutures causing skull deformities and facial asymmetry? back Craniosynostosis. tag Concept front Why are honey and improperly canned foods dangerous for infants under 1 year of age? back Risk of infant botulism spores germinate in gut toxin causes descending flaccid paralysis . tag Concept front What specific finding on ophthalmologic examination is highly suggestive of Shaken Baby Syndrome? back Retinal hemorrhages along with subdural hematomas . tag Clinical front What specific neurological exam finding would prevent a pediatrician from clearing a concussed teen for sports even if they report feeling 100% fine ? back An inability to smoothly perform the tandem gait heel-to-toe walking during a cerebellar assessment. tag Clinical pearls title Duchenne vs. Becker content DMD results from an absent or severely truncated dystrophin protein presenting earlier and more severely. Becker results from a partially functioning/mutated dystrophin presenting later with slower progression. title Meningeal Signs in Infants content Classic neck stiffness Kernig s sign and Brudzinski s sign are unreliable in young infants. You must rely on signs like extreme irritability lethargy fever and a tense bulging anterior fontanel. title Bell s Palsy Management content While systemic steroids are sometimes given the most critical pediatric management of Bell s palsy is to protect the cornea with eye patches or frequent lubricating drops as the patient cannot forcefully close the affected eye. questions id 1 question A mother brings her 7-month-old son into the clinic concerned that he seems much weaker than usual. Previously hitting his milestones over the last week he has developed difficulty feeding drooling and a weak cry. On exam the infant is lethargic with generalized hypotonia and poor head control. The parents recently began introducing solid foods including homemade purees and teas sweetened with local raw honey. What is the precise mechanism of action contributing to this patient s current condition? options Degeneration of anterior horn cells Absence of the dystrophin protein Inhibition of presynaptic acetylcholine release Demyelination of peripheral nerves Antibodies against postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors correctAnswer 2 rationale The patient s acute-onset descending progressive hypotonia drooling and history of honey ingestion is classic for Infant Botulism. Clostridium botulinum spores in the honey germinate in the infant s GI tract and release botulinum toxin which inhibits the presynaptic release of acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction leading to flaccid paralysis. id 2 question A newborn is evaluated in the nursery after a difficult vacuum-assisted vaginal delivery. The physician notes a distinct firm swollen mass over the right parietal bone that does not cross the sagittal or coronal suture lines. Which of the following is the most appropriate management and expected complication for this finding? options Aspiration; high risk of meningitis Reassurance; high risk of unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia Surgical release; high risk of permanent facial asymmetry Helmet therapy; high risk of positional plagiocephaly Reassurance; high risk of future seizures correctAnswer 1 rationale A firm cranial swelling that does not cross suture lines following a vacuum-assisted delivery describes a cephalohematoma a subperiosteal hemorrhage . It requires no acute intervention but can increase the risk of neonatal jaundice hyperbilirubinemia as the significant blood load is broken down and reabsorbed. id 3 question A 4-year-old boy is brought to the pediatrician due to clumsiness and delays in walking. When placed on the exam room floor the boy is asked to stand up. He rolls onto his stomach and uses his hands to walk up his thighs to achieve an upright posture. Examination of his lower extremities reveals significantly enlarged firm calves. Laboratory testing would most likely reveal extreme elevation of which of the following? options Acetylcholinesterase Alpha-fetoprotein Creatine kinase Alkaline phosphatase Lactate dehydrogenase correctAnswer 2 rationale The patient demonstrates Gowers sign and calf pseudohypertrophy which are hallmark findings of Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy DMD . DMD involves severe ongoing muscle degeneration. This massive muscle breakdown releases high volumes of intracellular enzymes into the blood resulting in a markedly elevated serum Creatine Kinase CK . id 4 question During a newborn exam a physician notes a severely asymmetrical facial structure with significant bossing of the forehead a misaligned nose crease and unevenly set ears. Palpation of the skull reveals a hard raised ridge along the left coronal suture. Which of the following conditions is most likely responsible for these findings? options Positional plagiocephaly Caput succedaneum Craniosynostosis Cephalohematoma Meningocele correctAnswer 2 rationale An asymmetric facial structure frontal bossing and a palpable mountain range ridge along a cranial suture strongly point to craniosynostosis the premature fusion of cranial sutures restricting natural brain expansion. Caput and Cephalohematoma cause localized soft-tissue swelling but do not cause sutural ridging or facial bone deformities. Positional plagiocephaly usually presents as a flat occiput but preserves anterior facial symmetry. id 5 question A neonate is found to have significant joint laxity and profound generalized hypotonia shortly after birth. When lifted the physician notes he falls right through your hands. A genetic workup confirms a chromosomal abnormality. If this patient also exhibits a single palmar crease upslanting palpebral fissures and an endocardial cushion defect what is the most likely diagnosis? options Prader-Willi syndrome Down syndrome Werdnig-Hoffmann disease Apert syndrome Duchenne muscular dystrophy correctAnswer 1 rationale Down syndrome Trisomy 21 frequently presents with recognizable generalized hypotonia significant joint hyper-laxity upslanting palpebral fissures and congenital heart defects e.g. endocardial cushion defects . Prader-Willi also presents with infantile hypotonia but is associated with hypogonadism and a future switch to hyperphagia. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l77", "title": "Lecture #77: Neurodevelopmental and Behavioral Problems in Children and School Dysfunction", "module": "DPR", "content": "id l77 module DPR l77 Lecture 77 Neurodevelopmental and Behavioral Problems in Children and School Dysfunction lecturer Dr. Adam Hurst Pediatrics session Lecture 77 prev l76 next l78 pdf pdfs/Lecture 77. A. Hurst M.D. PPT -1.pdf Lecture 77 Neurodevelopmental and Behavioral Problems in Children and School Dysfunction Lecturer Dr. Adam Hurst Session Lecture 77 Developmental Delays and General Concepts Slide 2 Normal child development has a wide range of normal variation. Developmental milestones provide a framework to monitor a child s progress over time. A developmental delay occurs when a child falls outside this range of normal variation in any domain motor speech cognitive or social . A crucial distinction for a provider is recognizing when an area of concern is merely a delay versus a disorder the latter of which features a significant impairment in day-to-day functioning. - Key Principles of Delay - Can be isolated one domain or global two or more domains . - Delay does not inherently mean disorder; disorder implies functional limitation. - Loss of milestones is ALWAYS a red flag and warrants immediate workup often correlating with genetic conditions or underlying neurologic insults. - Early intervention speech therapy physical therapy occupational therapy shows great progress and is the cornerstone of management. Communication and Language Disorders Slide 13 Language development serves as an early marker for cognitive development. When a child fails to develop typical language abilities the primary objective is to differentiate a simple delay from a structural sensory or pervasive neurodevelopmental issue. A critical step in any child with a speech or language delay regardless of a normal newborn screening is to undergo formal audiology testing to rule out hearing loss. - Approach to Speech/Language Delay - History & Physical Assess prenatal/birth history family history and perform a full physical exam to look for dysmorphic features suggesting genetic etiologies. - Hearing Assessment MANDATORY All children presenting with speech/language concerns at any age must be referred for a formal hearing assessment by an audiologist. - Treatment Early referral to speech-language pathology Speech Therapy should not be delayed while awaiting further evaluations. correlate Language Disorder - Pathophysiology Deficit in comprehension receptive and/or production expressive of language. - Presentation Reduced vocabulary limited sentence structure and limited conversation skills. Often presents as a delay in the onset of words up to 3 years of age. Child may compensate using gestures but often becomes frustrated leading to tantrums. - Diagnosis Clinical diagnosis established via formal speech-language evaluation. Must rule out hearing impairment environmental deprivation or other medical conditions. - Treatment Speech Therapy. - Lecturer s Point Despite compensation strategies deficits lead to functional limitations. Early therapy is crucial. correlate Speech Sound Disorder - Pathophysiology Persistent difficulty with speech and sound articulation/production. - Presentation Inability to articulate sounds interfering with speech intelligibility. Expected intelligibility 50% by age 2 75% by age 3 and 100% by age 4. A 5-year-old with only 50% intelligibility is highly concerning for this disorder. - Diagnosis Clinical evaluation. - Treatment Speech Therapy. correlate Child-Onset Fluency Disorder Stuttering - Pathophysiology Impairment in speech fluency and timing. - Presentation Stuttering. Occurs in 1% of school-aged children 3x more common in boys. - Diagnosis Clinical. - Treatment Speech Therapy. correlate Social Pragmatic Communication Disorder - Pathophysiology Impairment in the use of language for social exchanges and in adapting communication styles to context. - Presentation Difficulty interpreting ambiguous language humor or sarcasm. Does not follow conventional conversation norms. These children are actively seeking social interaction but have trouble achieving it properly leading to being bullied or ostracized. - Diagnosis Clinical. Must be explicitly differentiated from Autism Spectrum Disorder ASD . - Treatment Speech Therapy and Social Skills Therapy. - Lecturer s Point Unlike children with ASD children with social pragmatic communication disorder possess a social drive and want to interact but lack the ability to adapt their communication style. Autism Spectrum Disorder ASD Slide 18 Autism Spectrum Disorder is a lifelong neurodevelopmental disorder characterized by two core dimensions deficits in social communication/interaction and the presence of restrictive or repetitive behaviors. The prevalence of ASD is increasing now estimated at 1 in 36 children 2020 data with males affected four times more frequently than females. The umbrella term ASD now encompasses previous diagnoses like pervasive developmental delay and Asperger s syndrome. Notably most individuals with ASD have normal cognitive intelligence IQ > 70 . - Risk Factors - Genetic Chromosomal disorders Fragile X is the most common Down syndrome Rett syndrome Tuberous sclerosis. High sibling recurrence rate up to 25% . - Biological Older parental age prenatal exposures alcohol heavy metals TORCH infections prematurity low birth weight. - Lecturer s Nuance Vaccines do NOT cause autism. - Diagnostic Criteria DSM-5 Based structure - Social Deficits Needs at least 1 - Lack of social-emotional reciprocity no back-and-forth conversation limited joint attention . - Deficits in nonverbal communication poor eye contact lack of facial expression absence of social smile or pointing . - Difficulty developing maintaining or understanding relationships poor imaginative play lack of interest in peers . - Restrictive/Repetitive Behaviors Needs at least 2 of 4 - Stereotyped or repetitive movements/speech echolalia idiosyncratic phrases lining up toys . - Insistence on sameness inflexible adherence to routines extreme distress at small changes. - Highly restricted fixated interests. - Hyper- or hypo-reactivity to sensory input indifference to pain visual fascination with lights excessive smelling/touching . highyield Autism Spectrum Disorder Diagnosis and Management - Screening The M-CHAT-R is commonly performed at 18 and 24 months of age. - Management Principle Early diagnosis and intervention are optimal. Do not delay management steps speech therapy occupational therapy Applied Behavioral Analysis ABA while awaiting a formal in-depth evaluation by a developmental pediatrician or multidisciplinary team. Intellectual Disability Slide 28 Intellectual disability formerly mental retardation involves a combination of impairments in both cognitive intellectual and adaptive development. The severity of the impairment normally dictates the age of diagnosis; severe cases are recognized early while mild intellectual disability may only be recognized when academic struggles arise during the school years. - Key Components - Cognitive Development Reduced IQ typically < 70 . - Adaptive Functioning Deficits in conceptual skills language reading writing social skills and practical skills activities of daily living personal care . - Etiology - Most common preventable cause Fetal Alcohol Spectrum Disorder. - Most common genetic causes Fragile X Trisomy 21 Down syndrome . - Common underlying finding Normal physical exam does not rule out genetic cause. Up to 14% of children with normal exams have an underlying genetic etiology. - Workup & Management - History & Physical Screen for dysmorphia growth parameters and refer for vision/hearing testing. - Assessment Psychoeducational testing e.g. WPPSI WISC WAIS for cognition; VABS ABAS for adaptive functioning usually performed by a psychologist. - Management Multidisciplinary team. Under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act IDEA states must provide a free and appropriate public education in the least restrictive environment. Children are given an Individualized Education Program IEP . Motor Disorders Slide 30 Motor disorders manifest as physical movement abnormalities that can interfere with daily life though they generally lack concurrent cognitive delays. These can range from coordination issues to the involuntary presence of tics. correlate Developmental Coordination Disorder - Presentation Deficits in executing coordinated motor skills. Child is extremely clumsy slow or inaccurate with tasks like using scissors or riding a bike. Avoids sports. - Diagnosis Clinical. correlate Stereotypical Movement Disorder - Presentation Repetitive purposeless motor behaviors e.g. body rocking hand shaking head banging . May or may not be self-injurious. correlate Tourette Syndrome vs. Chronic Tic Disorder - Tourette Syndrome Defined by the presence of multiple motor tics AND one or more vocal tics present for at least one year. - Persistent/Chronic Tic Disorder Defined by the presence of either motor OR vocal tics but not both present for at least one year. - Symptoms - Motor Tics Eye blinking facial grimacing head jerking. - Vocal Tics Grunting clearing throat repetition of words. - Key Feature Tics are involuntary but can sometimes be voluntarily suppressed temporarily. Learning Disorders Slide 35 Learning disorders are a specific category of school dysfunction where a child exhibits a discrepancy between their intellectual ability and their academic performance. Unlike Intellectual Disability children with varying forms of Learning Disorders have a normal overall IQ but experience isolated academic skill deficits that result in achievement scores substantially below their expected age or intelligence level. - Presentation - Poor academic performance. Often not detected until older grades as the child compensates by memorizing early on. Eventually leads to failing grades behavioral acting out due to frustration or spending excessive amounts of time on homework. - Symptoms must be present for at least 6 months despite interventions. - Risk Factors Prematurity family history and preschool developmental problems strong association with prior speech/language delayed children . - Categories - Reading Learning Disability Dyslexia Most common 80% of learning disorders . Issues with word recognition decoding or comprehension. - Writing Learning Disability. - Math Learning Disability. - Workup - Ensure difficulties are NOT due to hearing/vision issues intellectual disability motor disability or inadequate educational instruction. - Detailed school history grades teacher concerns IEP presence homework length. - Psychoeducational testing accomplished by schools or psychologists combines cognitive assessment and achievement testing. Behavior Problems ODD and Conduct Disorder Slide 40 Behavior disorders often present as significant school problems and frequently co-occur with underlying learning disorders or psychosocial stressors. Diagnosing these requires obtaining a comprehensive multi-source history parents teachers child and assessing if the behaviors are disproportionate for the child s developmental stage. correlate Oppositional Defiant Disorder ODD - Presentation A pattern of behavior characterized by trouble with authority breaking rules argumentativeness and non-compliance. Typically presents before age 8 and no later than early adolescence. - Duration Symptoms must be present for at least 6 months. - Lecturer s Point Primarily defiance and rule-breaking lacking the physical aggression and norm-violating destruction seen in Conduct Disorder. correlate Conduct Disorder CD - Presentation Severe behavior that violates the rights of others or major societal norms. Characterized by aggression towards others/animals destruction of property setting fires and breaking laws . - Duration/Onset Can be child-onset <10 years or adolescent-onset >10 but <16 . Symptoms usually present for at least 6 months with 3 or more incidents in the past year. - Risk Factors Male sex higher prevalence neglect psychological mistreatment witnessing domestic violence or experiencing physical discipline. - Lecturer s Point Conduct disorder is distinguished from ODD by the presence of true aggression and destruction of property. - Management of Behavior Problems - Parent Behavior Management Teaches techniques and strategies to reinforce good behaviors and use appropriate consequences. Consistency is key. - Cognitive Behavior Therapy Problem-solving skills. - Medications Used as an adjunct to behavioral therapies for severely aggressive behaviors or to treat comorbid conditions e.g. using stimulants if the underlying trigger is untreated ADHD . What defines a global developmental delay? A delay that involves at least two or more developmental domains motor speech cognitive social . Concept What historical finding in a child s development is ALWAYS considered a red flag requiring immediate evaluation? Loss of developmental milestones. Concept What is the mandatory first step in the workup of any child presenting with a speech or language delay? Formal audiology testing to assess for hearing loss. Clinical How much of a 3-year-old s speech should be intelligible to a stranger? 75%. 50% by age 2 and 100% by age 4 . Concept What distinguishes Social Pragmatic Communication Disorder from Autism Spectrum Disorder ASD ? Children with Social Pragmatic Communication Disorder have a social drive and desire interaction but lack the communication skills whereas ASD is characterized by a lack of social interest/drive. Clinical What is the most common known genetic cause of Autism Spectrum Disorder and Intellectual Disability? Fragile X syndrome. Glossary What are the two core dimensions of deficits required for a diagnosis of Autism Spectrum Disorder? 1 Deficits in social communication/interaction and 2 restrictive or repetitive behaviors/interests. Clinical What is the key difference between an Intellectual Disability and a Learning Disorder regarding IQ? Intellectual Disability features a reduced overall IQ <70 whereas Learning Disorders occur in children with a normal range of intelligence. Concept How is Tourette Syndrome definitively differentiated from Persistent/Chronic Tic Disorder? Tourette Syndrome requires BOTH multiple motor tics AND at least one vocal tic. Chronic Tic Disorder is either motor OR vocal but not both. Clinical What is the most common type of learning disability? Reading Learning Disability Dyslexia accounting for 80% of specific learning disorders. Concept What is the primary difference in presentation between Oppositional Defiant Disorder ODD and Conduct Disorder? ODD involves defiance non-compliance and argumentativeness. Conduct Disorder involves severe violations of societal norms including aggression towards people/animals setting fires and destruction of property. Clinical questions A 3-year-old boy is brought to the clinic by his parents because he cannot be understood when speaking to unfamiliar relatives at family gatherings. The parents state the child follows complex instructions at home and eagerly points to toys to share them with his parents. On exam the child interacts playfully with the physician but his speech is only 50% intelligible. Formal audiological testing is normal. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? A. Autism Spectrum Disorder B. Social Pragmatic Communication Disorder C. Intellectual Disability D. Speech Sound Disorder E. Language Disorder D. Speech Sound Disorder Speech Sound Disorder is characterized by persistent difficulty with speech and sound articulation/production that interferes with speech intelligibility. A 3-year-old should be 75% intelligible to strangers; being only 50% intelligible indicates an articulation disorder. His normal receptive language joint attention pointing/sharing and sociability rule out Autism Intellectual Disability and Language Disorder. A 24-month-old boy is evaluated for delayed speech. His mother notes he only says the word no . He also displays poor eye contact does not respond to a name and throws severe tantrums when his daily routine of lining up cars is interrupted. He has no dysmorphic features. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management? A. Await the results of a formal multidisciplinary autism evaluation before initiating therapy B. Reassure the mother that delayed speech is common in boys C. Refer for formal audiological testing and initiate speech/behavioral therapies immediately D. Obtain a chromosomal microarray to rule out Fragile X syndrome E. Prescribe a trial of an SSRI for his restrictive behaviors C. Refer for formal audiological testing and initiate speech/behavioral therapies immediately This child exhibits core features of Autism Spectrum Disorder ASD including social deficits and restrictive/repetitive behaviors along with a speech delay. Crucially any child with a speech delay must undergo formal audiological testing to rule out hearing loss. Furthermore management steps such as early intervention speech therapy or ABA should be initiated as soon as possible and never delayed while awaiting a formal diagnosis from a specialist. An 8-year-old girl is brought to the pediatrician due to difficulties in school over the past 8 months. Her teacher reports she is failing reading and often becomes irritable and disruptive during designated quiet reading time. She has no physical difficulties copying from the board or participating in gym class. Her overall IQ was recently tested at 105. Audiology and vision screens are normal. What is the most likely underlying mechanism for her school dysfunction? A. Deficit in general cognitive ability and adaptive functioning B. Isolated deficit in an academic skill despite normal intelligence C. Impairment in adapting communication to specific social contexts D. An underlying sensory deficit limiting educational input E. A genetic syndrome affecting overall neurodevelopment B. Isolated deficit in an academic skill despite normal intelligence This presentation is consistent with a Learning Disorder specifically reading learning disability or dyslexia . Learning disorders are isolated academic skill deficits that yield poor achievement despite a normal intelligence quotient IQ which she has 105 . They are not due to sensory deficits or intellectual disability which requires low IQ and adaptive deficits choice A . A 12-year-old boy is brought to the clinic by his mother because he has been repeatedly suspended from school. Over the past year he has been involved in multiple physical fights with peers was caught torturing neighborhood stray cats and was recently arrested for intentionally setting a trash can on fire at a local park. Which of the following is the most accurate diagnosis? A. Oppositional defiant disorder B. Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder C. Tourette syndrome D. Conduct disorder E. Bipolar disorder D. Conduct disorder Conduct disorder is characterized by a severe pattern of behavior that violates the basic rights of others or major societal norms specifically highlighted by aggression toward people or animals destruction of property setting fires and serious rule violations laws . Oppositional defiant disorder involves defiance and non-compliance but lacks physical aggression and extreme destruction. A 9-year-old boy is evaluated for recurrent sudden brief intermittent movements. He has a history of facial grimacing and shoulder shrugging that began at age 7 and has periodically changed over the years. Over the past 14 months he has also developed recurrent episodes of loud involuntary grunting in class. He is able to suppress these sounds for brief periods when prompted by the teacher but ultimately must release them. What is the most likely diagnosis? A. Chronic tic disorder B. Stereotypical movement disorder C. Tourette syndrome D. Developmental coordination disorder E. Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder C. Tourette syndrome Tourette Syndrome is defined by the presence of BOTH multiple motor tics facial grimacing shoulder shrugging AND at least one vocal tic grunting present for at least 1 year. The tics are semi-voluntary in that they feel like an urge that can be temporarily suppressed. Chronic tic disorder requires the presence of EITHER motor OR vocal tics but not both. BandB Psychiatry > Child Psychiatry > Autism Spectrum Disorder BandB Psychiatry > Child Psychiatry > ADHD & Conduct Disorder BandB Psychiatry > Child Psychiatry > Learning Disorders FirstAid Psychiatry > Childhood Disorders Red Flag Milestone Loss A loss of previously acquired developmental milestones is ALWAYS a red flag that requires immediate neurologic and genetic workup. ASD Core Concept Unlike most neurodevelopmental or communicative disorders Social Pragmatic Communication Disorder presents with a desire for social interaction whereas Autism Spectrum Disorder ASD fundamentally lacks an intrinsic social drive. Universal Hearing Assessment Any child presenting with a speech or language delay at any age MUST undergo formal audiological testing regardless of whether they passed their newborn hearing screen. Normal vs Decreased IQ Learning disorders feature isolated academic skill deficits with a NORMAL IQ and normal adaptive functioning. Intellectual Disability requires a DECREASED IQ <70 and deficits in adaptive functional skills. ODD vs CD Oppositional Defiant Disorder ODD is characterized by defiance and argumentativeness without severe aggression. Conduct Disorder CD is defined by severe aggression toward people/animals and overt destruction of property or laws. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l78", "title": "Lecture #78: Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder & Adolescent Development", "module": "Pediatrics & Adolescent Medicine", "content": "id l78 title Lecture 78 Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder & Adolescent Development lecturer A. Hurst M.D. module Pediatrics & Adolescent Medicine session Lecture 78 prev l77 next l79 pdf pdfs/Lecture 78. A. Hurst M.D. PPT -1.pdf summary Lecture 78 Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder & Adolescent Development Lecturer A. Hurst M.D. Session Lecture 78 Introduction to ADHD Slide 2-7 Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder ADHD is one of the most common neurodevelopmental disorders of childhood. Proper understanding of ADHD s epidemiology and biological foundations provides crucial context for its clinical management. ADHD alters cognitive development making sustaining attention or curbing impulsivity significantly more difficult than for neurotypical peers. It is imperative to remember that while boys are diagnosed more frequently symptoms often persist into adulthood interfering markedly with a patient s capacity for daily living over their lifetime. Epidemiology Affects approximately 9% of children in the United States. Boys are twice as likely to be diagnosed as girls. Lecturer Emphasis There is no specific association based on race or ethnicity. Increased incidence is seen in patients with other neurologic developmental or psychiatric anomalies e.g. tuberous sclerosis autism spectrum disorder fragile X syndrome anxiety . Approximately 60% of adults who experienced ADHD symptoms in childhood will continue to have symptoms that interfere with daily functioning. Risk Factors ADHD pathogenesis is multi-factorial stemming from genetic biological and environmental influences. Environmental Prenatal smoke exposure prenatal alcohol exposure and lead exposure. Biological Low birth weight and prematurity. Definition & Types A heterogeneous neurodevelopmental disorder characterized by symptoms of inattention motor hyperactivity and impulsivity that are developmentally inappropriate. | ADHD Type | Key Features | Diagnostic Requirement | | --- | --- | --- | | Inattentive | Difficulty sustaining attention cannot organize tasks easily distracted. | $\\\\ge$ 6 of 9 inattentive symptoms. | | Hyperactive-Impulsive | Fidgety always on the go blurts out answers cannot wait turn. | $\\\\ge$ 6 of 9 hyperactive/impulsive symptoms. | | Combined | Features of both forms of the disorder. | $\\\\ge$ 6 of 9 from BOTH categories. | correlate Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder ADHD - Pathophysiology Neurodevelopmental disorder linked to multifaceted genetic biological prematurity low birth weight and environmental factors lead prenatal smoke/alcohol . - Presentation Highly variable. Encompasses inattention disorganized distractible hyperactivity fidgeting cannot sit still and impulsivity blurting answers . - Diagnosis DSM-5 criteria $\\\\ge$ 6 symptoms if <17 years present for $\\\\ge$ 6 months starting prior to age 12 occurring in 2 or more settings causing functional impairment. - Treatment <6 years old Parent training in behavior management first-line . If meds needed methylphenidate over amphetamines. $\\\\ge$ 6 years old Stimulants are first-line supplemented with behavioral therapy. - Complications Increased risk for oppositional defiant disorder ODD conduct disorder anxiety depression and substance use. - Lecturer s Point A diagnosis does not mandate that symptoms were recognized prior to age 12 merely that the patient can recall symptom onset before turning 12. Diagnosis and Evaluation of ADHD Slide 8-15 Accurately diagnosing ADHD requires pulling information from multiple overlapping spheres of a child’s life as symptoms must be present in more than one setting to meet clinical criteria. Because cardiovascular anomalies or sensory deficits can masquerade as behavioral issues or cause complications with stimulant treatment a comprehensive physical examination is just as critical as the psychosocial history. Gathering collateral information from teachers and coaches bridges the gap between the child s home behavior and their scholastic functioning. History & Physical Examination Patient History The cornerstone of diagnosis. Evaluate behaviors at home and at school. Physical Exam Must obtain baseline vitals height weight BP pulse because first-line stimulant medications commonly increase heart rate/blood pressure and decrease appetite leading to weight loss. Evaluate language development hearing and vision to rule out other causes of inattentiveness e.g. a child who cannot hear the teacher may appear distracted . DSM-5 Criteria Breakdown Must exhibit $\\\\ge$ 6 symptoms if <17 years old or $\\\\ge$ 5 symptoms if $\\\\ge$ 17 years old . Symptoms must be present for at least 6 months. Onset of symptoms must be prior to 12 years of age. Symptoms must be present in 2 or more settings e.g. home and school . Must cause clinically significant functional impairment . ADHD Rating Scales Tools like the Vanderbilt Rating Scale parent and teacher forms are highly utilized. Lecturer Emphasis Rating scales are NOT diagnostic on their own; they document the frequency of symptoms across multiple settings and gauge the level of functional impairment. Comorbidities Oppositional Defiant Disorder ODD is seen in ~50% of children with the combined type of ADHD. Other associations include conduct disorder depression anxiety substance use and specific learning or language disorders. highyield ADHD Treatment by Age The choice of initial therapy for ADHD strictly depends on the patient s age. For children under 6 years old Parent Training in Behavior Management PTBM is the definitive first-line therapy and if medication becomes necessary methylphenidate is preferred over amphetamine. For children 6 years and older stimulant medication is the first-line therapy ideally combined with behavioral interventions. Adolescent Development Slide 16-25 Adolescence is a uniquely turbulent transitional period bridging childhood dependence and adult autonomy. The process is fraught with asynchronous development where physical maturation frequently outpaces cognitive and emotional maturity leading to perilous risk-taking behaviors. Recognizing how adolescents systematically evolve from concrete thinkers to abstract hypothetical reasoners helps clinicians tailor their anticipatory guidance to fit the patient s exact cognitive stage. Key Stages of Adolescence Early 11-14 years. Middle 15-17 years. Late 18-21 years. Four Pillars of Development Biological Puberty . Cognitive. Psychosocial. Emotional. Cognitive and Psychosocial Transitions Cognitive Development Progression Concrete Thinking Early adolescence. The patient only grasps real tangible objects and direct knowledge; they cannot process hypothetical outcomes or link long-term cause-and-effect. Abstract Thinking The ability to imagine unseen possibilities and explore consequences through logical thought processes. Formal Operational Thinking Involves metacognition thinking about thinking . Late adolescents consider how others perceive them. Imaginary Audience A detrimental egocentric thought pattern where the adolescent believes everyone is constantly scrutinizing them. Psychosocial & Emotional Development Characterized by building autonomy emotional and economic independence and an individual identity self-concept and self-esteem . Self-Concept The patient s perception of themselves e.g. talents ethnic/religious identity . Self-Esteem The patient s assessment of their own self-worth. Asynchrony Physical development often drastically outpaces emotional development. When emotionally immature teenagers physically look like young adults they are treated as such precipitating a dangerous surge in risk-taking behaviors substance abuse unprotected sex . highyield Adolescent Mental Health Statistics Adolescence is highly vulnerable over 20% have a mental health disorder and half of all lifetime mental illness cases begin by age 14. Extraordinarily suicide constitutes the leading cause of death in patients aged 10-14. Therefore fostering a safe trusted medical home is a critical intervention. The Adolescent Well Visit Slide 26-30 The primary goal of the adolescent office visit is to establish a bridge of trust and deliver targeted anticipatory guidance. Physicians must meticulously screen for risky behaviors using structured psychosocial assessments while navigating the nuanced legal and ethical boundaries of patient confidentiality. Cultivating this safe space encourages the adolescent to disclose sensitive information that profoundly impacts their health trajectory. HEEADSSS Psychosocial Interview Provides a standardized approach for evaluating risk and protective factors. mnemonic Adolescent Psychosocial Screening HEADS - H Home environment situation and safety - E Education and Employment school performance - A Activities peer groups exercise eating habits - D Drugs substance use - S Sexuality Suicide/Depression Safety Interview Process Routinely ask the parent or guardian to step out of the room. This promotes autonomy and allows the teenager to answer truthfully regarding sensitive topics. Confidentiality Criteria & Exceptions Physicians can deliver confidential services to consenting minors primarily regarding reproductive health sexuality gender identity substance use and mental health. Emancipated Minors May consent for themselves in all aspects of medical treatment. Exceptions Mandatory Reporting Providers MUST break confidentiality if the patient is in imminent danger suicide/homicide if there is suspicion of physical/sexual abuse or upon diagnosis of certain state-mandated communicable diseases. analysis content/L78 HighYield Render Analysis.md ankingResource primarySource BandB chapter Psychiatry > Pathology > ADHD & Autism alternatives resource Bootcamp chapter Psychiatry > Psychology > ADHD resource SketchyPsych chapter Child & Adolescent Psychiatry > Disruptive Conduct & Impulse Control Disorders > Oppositional Defiant Intermittent Explosive & Conduct Disorders resource FirstAid chapter Psychiatry > Childhood & Early Onset Disorders > ADHD flashcards front What is the recommended first-line treatment for a 5-year-old child recently diagnosed with ADHD? back Parent training in behavior management PTBM . tag Clinical front If medication is required for a child under the age of 6 with ADHD which pharmacological agent is preferred? back Methylphenidate preferred over amphetamines . tag Clinical front According to DSM-5 criteria before what age must ADHD symptoms present for a diagnosis to be made? back Symptoms must be present prior to age 12. tag Concept front Why is it critical to obtain baseline heart rate blood pressure and weight before initiating stimulant therapy for ADHD? back Stimulants commonly cause tachycardia hypertension decreased appetite and weight loss. tag Clinical front What is the most frequently co-occurring behavioral comorbidity in children diagnosed with combined type ADHD? back Oppositional Defiant Disorder ODD – seen in approximately 50% of cases. tag Concept front In adolescent cognitive development what is the term for the egocentric belief that everyone is constantly watching and judging them? back The Imaginary Audience . tag Concept front What developmental asynchrony is highly correlated with a surge in risk-taking behaviors among adolescents? back Physical development often outpaces emotional development looking like an adult but thinking like a child . tag Clinical front What are the three absolute exceptions where a physician must legally break adolescent confidentiality? back 1. Imminent danger to self or others suicide/homicide \\\\n2. Suspicion of physical or sexual abuse\\\\n3. Diagnosis of reportable communicable diseases. tag Clinical front What is the leading cause of death for patients between the ages of 10 and 14 years old? back Suicide. tag Clinical front According to DSM-5 criteria how many symptoms and across how many settings must a 10-year-old demonstrate to be diagnosed with ADHD? back At least 6 symptoms present in 2 or more settings e.g. home and school . tag Concept pearls title ADHD Age of Diagnosis vs Symptom Onset content While DSM-5 criteria mandate that symptoms must have begun prior to 12 years of age a patient does not actually have to be formally diagnosed before turning 12. Adult diagnoses are common as long as early childhood symptom onset can be recalled. title The Danger of Developmental Asynchrony content Because an adolescent s emotional maturity typically lags significantly behind their biological/physical maturation society often treats them older than they psychologically are. This dynamic is a primary driver for increased risk-taking behaviors such as substance abuse and unprotected sex. title Baseline Vitals in ADHD content Never start a child on a stimulant without a robust baseline height weight pulse and blood pressure. Stimulants strongly suppress appetite and increase sympathetic tone requiring close monitoring during follow-up visits. questions id 1 question A 5-year-old boy is brought to the clinic by his mother because his kindergarten teacher reports he is highly disruptive cannot wait his turn and frequently runs around the classroom when he is supposed to be seated. The mother notes he behaves similarly at home and has been this way for the past 8 months. After a comprehensive evaluation and rating scales from both the parent and teacher he is diagnosed with Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder ADHD combined type. What is the most appropriate initial management for this patient? options Prescribe low-dose methylphenidate Prescribe an amphetamine-based stimulant Initiate parent training in behavior management PTBM Refer for cognitive behavioral therapy specifically targeting imaginary audience Reassure the mother and re-evaluate when he turns 6 correctAnswer 2 rationale For children under the age of 6 diagnosed with ADHD the standard of care and definitive first-line therapy is Parent Training in Behavior Management PTBM . Medication specifically methylphenidate not amphetamines is only considered if initial behavioral interventions fail. id 2 question A 15-year-old female presents to the clinic for a routine well-child check. During the HEADS assessment she asks her mother to step out of the room. Speaking privately with the physician she confides that she has recently become sexually active and requests screening for sexually transmitted infections STIs . She explicitly asks that you do not tell her parents. Under which of the following circumstances would the physician be legally required to break this patient s confidentiality? options If she tests positive for Chlamydia If she admits to binge drinking on the weekends If she discloses that her older brother has been physically hitting her If she requests a prescription for oral contraceptives If she admits to experimenting with marijuana correctAnswer 2 rationale Physicians can maintain confidentiality with minors regarding reproductive health including STI testing and contraception mental health and substance use. However mandatory reporting laws dictate that confidentiality MUST be broken if there is suspicion or disclosure of physical or sexual abuse or if the patient poses an imminent danger to themselves or others. id 3 question A 20-year-old college student presents to the student health clinic reporting extreme difficulty focusing during lectures and chronic procrastination that is severely impacting his grades. He states I have always been easily distracted and disorganized even back in elementary school but my parents never believed in doctors. He meets all adult criteria for the inattentive subtype of a neurodevelopmental disorder. Which of the following DSM-5 criteria must be satisfied to officially diagnose him with ADHD? options He must exhibit at least 6 symptoms of inattention presently He must provide medical records confirming a diagnosis before age 12 His symptoms must be currently present predominantly in a single setting the university He must recall the onset of his inattentive symptoms occurring prior to age 12 He must undergo an MRI to rule out prefrontal cortex abnormalities correctAnswer 3 rationale To diagnose ADHD in a patient 17 years or older they only need to exhibit 5 symptoms not 6 . They do not require an established childhood diagnosis of ADHD but they must have a reliable history indicating that the symptom onset occurred prior to 12 years of age. Symptoms must also still cause functional impairment across 2 or more settings. id 4 question A 13-year-old boy is brought to the clinic for a sports physical. While discussing his future he demonstrates an inability to anticipate how playing multiple contact sports might impact his long-term joint health. He struggles to answer hypothetical questions and focuses entirely on the fact that he wants to play football right now. Based on models of adolescent cognitive development which stage of thinking is this patient most likely exhibiting? options Abstract thinking Formal operational thinking Metacognition Concrete thinking Imaginary audience behavior correctAnswer 3 rationale Concrete thinking is characteristic of early adolescence. Patients in this stage only comprehend direct facts and tangible events; they struggle to link cause-and-effect or to process hypothetical long-term consequences of their current behaviors. This contrasts with abstract and formal operational thinking which develop later in adolescence. id 5 question A 9-year-old boy is diagnosed with the combined type of ADHD. His physician is preparing to initiate first-line pharmacological therapy with a stimulant medication. During patient education the physician should counsel the parents to monitor for which of the following common side effects associated with this medication class? options Bradycardia and hypotension Increased appetite and rapid weight gain Decreased appetite and weight loss Peripheral edema and joint pain Excessive daytime somnolence correctAnswer 2 rationale Stimulant medications like methylphenidate and amphetamines are sympathomimetic agents. Their most common side effects include decreased appetite weight loss difficulty sleeping insomnia tachycardia and elevated blood pressure. This is why obtaining baseline vitals height weight pulse BP prior to starting therapy is critical. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l79", "title": "Lecture #79: The Pharmacologic Management of ADHD", "module": "Pharmacology", "content": "id l79 title Lecture 79 The Pharmacologic Management of ADHD lecturer D. Cummins Pharm.D. module Pharmacology session Lecture 79 prev l78 next l80 pdf pdfs/Lecture 79. D. Cummins Pharm.D. BCPS PPT .pdf summary Lecture 79 The Pharmacologic Management of ADHD Lecturer D. Cummins Pharm.D. Session Lecture 79 Overview of ADHD Management Slides 2-5 The management of Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder ADHD is multimodal; pharmacotherapy is typically just one component of a broader treatment plan. Before initiating drug therapy it is critical to identify specific individualized behavioral academic and social goals. This enables proper evaluation of the medication s true clinical benefit. When medications work they are not causing a paradoxical effect . Instead they restore baseline inhibitory tone. In a normal state the brain s default mode network is active during rest and strictly inhibited during focused tasks. Patients with ADHD have a failure of this inhibitory system; they cannot turn off their background thoughts leading to inattention and impulsivity. Medications theoretically increase dopamine and norepinephrine to reestablish this crucial inhibition. Multimodal Treatment Includes behavioral therapy training adults/caregivers training interventions targeting patient skills via feedback and pharmacotherapy. Default Mode Network DMN Normally active during rest inhibited during a task. ADHD patients fail to inhibit the DMN. Neurochemistry Dopamine provides overall inhibitory effect; Norepinephrine validates/strengthens signals between brain networks. The two transmitters differ by only one hydroxyl group allowing cross-reactivity at transporter sites. Stimulants First-Line Therapy Slides 6-13 Stimulants—specifically the amphetamine family and methylphenidate—are the clear first-line pharmacologic agents for ADHD unless specific comorbidities exist. They act centrally to increase dopamine and norepinephrine in the synaptic cleft. Despite the name stimulant their therapeutic effect is to increase baseline inhibitory control over impulsivity and distractibility. Because patients may respond favorably to one class but not the other clinical management often requires a trial-and-error approach. Response Rates Approximately 40% of patients respond to both amphetamines and methylphenidate. Another 40% respond to only one or the other. Mechanism of Action Methylphenidate A non-selective reuptake inhibitor. It blocks the NET Norepinephrine Transporter and DAT Dopamine Transporter keeping more neurotransmitters in the synapse. Amphetamines Possess a multi-modal pseudosubstrate mechanism. 1 They block reuptake transporters. 2 They are taken up into the presynaptic neuron and enter storage vesicles displacing norepinephrine into the cytosol. 3 The excess cytosolic norepinephrine flows out into the synapse via reverse transport . Adverse Effects Insomnia Dose early in the day or use immediate-release forms. Growth Suppression/Weight Loss Primarily due to appetite suppression not altered metabolism. Administer medications alongside or after high-calorie meals. Children on methylphenidate may lose ~3kg in the first year which tapers off. Cardiovascular Mild increases in heart rate 1-2 bpm and blood pressure 1-4 mmHg . Baseline EKG is only required if specific cardiac risk factors exist. Tics May precipitate brief recurrent motor contractions. If the tic impairs function more than the ADHD switch to a non-stimulant. Priapism Prolonged painful erections >4 hours have been reported primarily with methylphenidate. Psychiatric Black box warning for potential abuse/addiction/misuse Schedule II . May also precipitate psychosis mood disturbances or severe anxiety attacks. highyield Amphetamine Mechanism of Action Amphetamines increase synaptic dopamine and norepinephrine via a unique three-step process they block reuptake transporters they displace neurotransmitters from presynaptic storage vesicles and they induce reverse transport of the neurotransmitter back into the synaptic cleft. Non-Stimulant Medications Slides 14-20 Non-stimulants are primarily utilized as alternatives when patients suffer from intolerable adverse effects on stimulants possess significant cardiovascular abnormalities or have a comorbid substance use disorder preventing the use of Schedule II drugs . While they do not carry the same risk of abuse they are generally less robust in efficacy and possess a much slower onset of action 2-4 weeks compared to the 1-2 hours of stimulants . Atomoxetine Strattera & Viloxazine Mechanism Selective norepinephrine reuptake inhibitors. Adverse Effects Unlike stimulants they commonly cause somnolence sedation . They share side effects of decreased appetite and mild cardiovascular increases. Warnings Carry a black box warning for suicidal ideation similar to standard antidepressants due to identical mechanism . Rare liver injury has been reported with atomoxetine. Alpha-2 Adrenergic Agonists Guanfacine Clonidine Mechanism While traditionally viewed as centrally acting presynaptic antihypertensives in ADHD they are believed to act on postsynaptic alpha-2A receptors in the prefrontal cortex to increase blood flow and enhance executive functioning. Adverse Effects Sedation bradycardia and hypotension. Warning Rebound hypertension can occur if stopped abruptly. Must be tapered. Bupropion Wellbutrin Mechanism Weak norepinephrine and dopamine reuptake inhibitor. Off-label use Used in adults/adolescents. Adverse Effects Insomnia tachycardia. May uniquely lower the seizure threshold . correlate Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder ADHD - Pathophysiology Failure of central inhibitory systems to suppress the default mode network during active tasks. Due to insufficient dopamine and norepinephrine. - Presentation Inattentiveness lack of impulse control hyperactivity resulting in functional impairment. - Treatment First Line Stimulants Amphetamines Methylphenidate combined with behavioral therapies. Second Line Non-stimulants Atomoxetine Alpha-2 Agonists . - Lecturer s Point Stimulant therapy does not produce a paradoxical effect. The stimulation of dopamine and norepinephrine pathways reestablishes the lost inhibitory control. highyield Drug Holidays A drug holiday involves taking a patient off their medication for a set period e.g. weekends summertime to allow for catch-up growth and to reassess the necessity of continuing therapy. This is ONLY appropriate for Stimulants. It is entirely inappropriate and not recommended for Non-stimulants like Atomoxetine or Guanfacine due to their delayed 2-4 week onset of action and for alpha-a agonists the risk of rebound hypertension. analysis content/L79 HighYield Render.pdf ankingResource primarySource BandB chapter Psychiatry > Pharm > ADHD Drugs alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Psychiatry > Pharmacology > CNS Stimulants resource SketchyPharm chapter Neuro/Psych > Stimulants > ADHD resource Bootcamp chapter Psychiatry > Pharmacology > Stimulants flashcards front What is the general mechanism of action by which stimulants alleviate ADHD symptoms? back They increase synaptic Dopamine and Norepinephrine which reestablishes inhibitory control over the default mode network. tag Concept front By what three mechanisms do amphetamines increase synaptic neurotransmitter levels? back 1 Blockade of NET/DAT reuptake transporters. 2 Displacement of neurotransmitters from storage vesicles. 3 Promotion of reverse transport into the synapse. tag Concept front Why do stimulants cause weight loss in pediatric patients? back Due to profound appetite suppression reduced food intake NOT an alteration in metabolic rate. tag Clinical front Are baseline EKGs recommended for all patients prior to initiating stimulant therapy? back No. Baseline EKGs are only required if the patient has a structural cardiac abnormality or a family history of sudden cardiac death. tag Clinical front What non-stimulant ADHD medication carries a black box warning for increased suicidal ideation? back Atomoxetine and Viloxazine because their mechanism as selective norepinephrine reuptake inhibitors mimics standard antidepressants. tag Clinical front Why are drug holidays not recommended for patients taking non-stimulant ADHD medications? back Because non-stimulants have a slow onset of action 2-4 weeks . Stopping them requires a complete retitration process and stopping alpha-2 agonists can cause rebound hypertension. tag Clinical front If a patient on an alpha-2 agonist clonidine or guanfacine for ADHD abruptly stops their medication what cardiovascular consequence may occur? back Rebound hypertension. tag Clinical front What is the mechanism of action of Atomoxetine? back Selective norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor. tag Concept front Which off-label ADHD medication uniquely carries the risk of lowering the seizure threshold? back Bupropion. tag Clinical pearls title Not a Paradoxical Effect content The calming of a hyperactive child with a stimulant is routinely misunderstood as a paradoxical effect. In reality the drug is stimulating the specific neurotransmitter pathways dopamine/norepinephrine responsible for central inhibition granting the child the capacity to self-regulate. title Insomnia vs Somnolence content When choosing drugs remember the side effect split Stimulants distinctly cause insomnia whereas Atomoxetine a non-stimulant possesses the unique adverse effect of somnolence sedation . title DEA Scheduling of Stimulants content All stimulant medications amphetamines and methylphenidate are DEA Schedule II controlled substances due to their potential for abuse misuse and addiction. Consequently these prescriptions biologically cannot have automatic refills. questions id 1 question A 9-year-old boy is brought to the pediatrician by his mother. For the past year his teacher has reported that he is easily distracted rarely completes assignments and frequently blurts out answers in class. Behavioral interventions have failed to improve his symptoms. He is diagnosed with ADHD and started on a medication that works primarily by displacing neurotransmitters from presynaptic vesicles and inducing their reverse transport into the synapse. Which of the following drugs was most likely prescribed? options Methylphenidate Atomoxetine Guanfacine Dextroamphetamine Clonidine correctAnswer 3 rationale The drug described utilizes the unique mechanism of Amphetamines—displacing dopamine and norepinephrine from presynaptic storage vesicles and forcing them into the synaptic cleft via reverse transport. Dextroamphetamine belongs to this class. Methylphenidate only blocks the reuptake transporters. Atomoxetine is a selective norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor. Guanfacine and clonidine are alpha-2 agonists. id 2 question A 7-year-old boy recently diagnosed with ADHD is started on a trial of methylphenidate. Two months later at a follow-up appointment his father expresses concern that the child has lost roughly 1 kilogram of weight and asks if the medication is speeding up the child s metabolism dangerously. How should the physician best respond? options The weight loss is primarily due to an increased basal metabolic rate which usually stabilizes after six months. The medication causes severe nausea and vomiting which accounts for the weight loss. The medication suppresses the feeding center in the hypothalamus resulting in reduced food intake rather than altered metabolism. The weight loss is a recognized sign of methylphenidate toxicity and the dose must be halved immediately. Weight loss is unexpected; the child should be urgently evaluated for an underlying malignancy. correctAnswer 2 rationale Growth suppression and weight loss are very common expected side effects of stimulant medications particularly in the first year of therapy. This is caused directly by drug-induced appetite suppression anorexia meaning the child simply consumes fewer calories. It is not caused by an increase in basal metabolic rate or persistent vomiting. id 3 question A 14-year-old male with ADHD has been successfully maintained on extended-release clonidine for the past twelve months. He leaves for a two-week summer camp but forgets to pack his medication thereby abruptly missing 4 days of his doses. Upon presenting to the camp nurse with a severe headache what finding is most likely to be present on physical examination? options Profound bradycardia Significantly elevated blood pressure Sustained priapism Somnolence and lethargy Bilateral conjunctival injection correctAnswer 1 rationale Clonidine is a centrally acting alpha-2 adrenergic agonist. A classic and dangerous complication of abruptly discontinuing alpha-2 agonists is rebound hypertension and potentially rebound tachycardia . The correct management is to slowly taper the dose if discontinuation is desired. id 4 question An 11-year-old girl is diagnosed with ADHD. During the history taking the mother mentions the child’s older brother passed away suddenly at age 14 from an undiagnosed heart defect while playing basketball. The physician wishes to avoid the stimulant class entirely due to cardiovascular risk and decides to prescribe atomoxetine. The mother should be counseled to monitor her daughter closely for which of the following severe adverse effects? options New-onset motor tics Severe insomnia at bedtime Suicidal ideations Rebound hypertension if a dose is missed Lowered seizure threshold correctAnswer 2 rationale Atomoxetine is a selective norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor SNRI-like mechanism . Because its mechanism closely mirrors that of classic antidepressants the FDA requires it to carry a black box warning regarding an increased risk of suicidal ideations in children and adolescents. Stimulants are more associated with tics and insomnia. Bupropion lowers the seizure threshold. Clonidine/Guanfacine cause rebound hypertension. id 5 question A 10-year-old boy has been taking an amphetamine-based stimulant for ADHD. A drug holiday during the summer months from his medication is proposed to assess his baseline behavior and allow for catch-up growth. This drug holiday would be CONTRAINDICATED if the patient had instead been prescribed which of the following medications? options Methylphenidate immediate release Lisdexamfetamine Methylphenidate extended release Guanfacine Dextroamphetamine/Amphetamine salts correctAnswer 3 rationale Drug holidays are only appropriate for stimulant medications because they wash out quickly and work quickly when restarted . They are not recommended for non-stimulants because non-stimulants take 2-4 weeks to reach clinical efficacy. Furthermore abruptly stopping Guanfacine an alpha-2 agonist can trigger dangerous rebound hypertension. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l80", "title": "Lecture #80: Mood Disorders, Depressive Disorders and Bipolar Mood Disorders", "module": "Psychiatry", "content": "id l80 title Lecture 80 Mood Disorders Depressive Disorders and Bipolar Mood Disorders lecturer C. Dyson M.D. module Psychiatry session Lecture 80 prev l79 next null pdf pdfs/Lecture 80. C. Dyson M.D. PPT .pdf summary Lecture 80 Mood Disorders Depressive Disorders and Bipolar Mood Disorders Lecturer C. Dyson M.D. Session Lecture 80 Overview of Mood Disorders Slides 2-5 Mood disorders represent one of the most clinically significant categories of psychiatric illness affecting millions of patients and serving as a leading cause of disability worldwide. Understanding how to properly categorize screen for and differentiate the spectrum of depressive and manic conditions is essential to clinical practice. The critical first step in evaluating any patient presenting with depressive symptoms is to rule out bipolar disorder because initiating an antidepressant as monotherapy in an undiagnosed bipolar patient can precipitate a devastating manic episode. This lecture provides a systematic framework for distinguishing normal sadness from clinical depression unipolar from bipolar illness and primary psychiatric disorders from those caused by medical conditions or substances. The Mood Spectrum Mood disorders are broadly divided into depressive disorders unipolar and bipolar disorders each with specific diagnostic thresholds time requirements and treatment algorithms. Lecturer Emphasis Before diagnosing major depressive disorder you MUST screen for bipolar disorder first. Prescribing SSRIs as monotherapy to a bipolar patient can trigger or worsen manic episodes. This is a board-favorite concept. Depressive Disorders Covered Major Depressive Disorder MDD Persistent Depressive Disorder Dysthymia MDD with Seasonal Pattern formerly Seasonal Affective Disorder Depression with Atypical Features Substance/Medication-Induced Depressive Disorder Depressive Disorder Due to Another Medical Condition Peripartum Mood Disturbances Postpartum Blues MDD with Peripartum Onset Premenstrual Dysphoric Disorder PMDD Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder DMDD highyield Rule Out Bipolar Before Treating Depression Most antidepressants especially SSRIs prescribed as monotherapy can worsen manic or hypomanic episodes in patients with undiagnosed bipolar disorder. Always screen for a history of mania/hypomania using tools like the Mood Disorder Questionnaire MDQ before initiating antidepressant therapy. If bipolar is identified a mood stabilizer or second-generation antipsychotic must be the foundation of treatment. Major Depressive Disorder Slides 6-21 Major Depressive Disorder is characterized by discrete episodes of persistently depressed mood or anhedonia lasting at least two weeks with enough accompanying vegetative and cognitive symptoms to cause significant functional impairment. The pathophysiology centers on decreased levels of three key monoamine neurotransmitters norepinephrine serotonin and dopamine. Neuroimaging studies consistently reveal decreased frontal lobe and hippocampal volumes in patients with untreated depression emphasizing that this is a biological disease with tangible structural consequences not merely a psychological state. Pathophysiology Slide 11 Decreased norepinephrine serotonin and dopamine neurotransmitter levels. Brain imaging decreased frontal lobe and hippocampal volumes in untreated depression. DSM-5 Diagnostic Criteria Slides 12-14 Requires 5 or more of 9 symptoms present most days over the past 2 weeks. Must impair functioning if not impairing it is not a disorder . Not better accounted for by another illness or substance. mnemonic MDD Diagnostic Symptoms SIG E CAPS - S Sleep changes insomnia or hypersomnia - I Interest loss anhedonia - G Guilt or worthlessness - E Energy loss or fatigue - C Concentration difficulty or indecisiveness - A Appetite changes increased or decreased - P Psychomotor agitation or retardation - S Suicidal ideation or recurrent thoughts of death Epidemiology Slide 13 US lifetime prevalence of MDD 17%. Peak age of onset for unipolar depression 20-40 years old. Peak age of onset for psychotic depression 50-70 years old. Screening Tools Slides 16-17 PHQ-2 Quick 2-question screening tool for all patients. Can be part of a review of symptoms. PHQ-9 Used when PHQ-2 is positive. Provides severity grading and monitors treatment response. correlate Major Depressive Disorder MDD - Pathophysiology Decreased norepinephrine serotonin and dopamine. Decreased frontal lobe and hippocampal volumes on brain imaging in untreated patients. - Presentation Depressed mood or anhedonia must have at least one plus additional SIG E CAPS symptoms for at least 2 weeks. - Diagnosis DSM-5 5 or more of 9 symptoms present most days for at least 2 weeks with functional impairment. Screen with PHQ-2 then PHQ-9 if positive. - Treatment First-line SSRIs citalopram escitalopram sertraline paroxetine fluoxetine plus CBT or other psychotherapy. SNRIs duloxetine venlafaxine and bupropion NRI are alternatives. ECT for treatment-resistant or acutely suicidal patients. - Complications 21% of patients with recurrent depressive disorder attempt suicide. Chronic depression leads to structural brain changes. - Lecturer s Point Always screen for bipolar disorder before initiating antidepressant therapy. SSRIs as monotherapy in bipolar patients can precipitate mania. Treatment Slides 18-21 Pharmacotherapy first-line SSRIs Citalopram escitalopram Lexapro sertraline Zoloft paroxetine fluoxetine. Side effects GI symptoms tremor sedation insomnia headache sexual dysfunction. SNRIs Duloxetine Cymbalta venlafaxine. NRI Bupropion Wellbutrin - contraindicated in patients with eating disorders and seizure disorders. Psychotherapy Cognitive Behavioral Therapy CBT interpersonal therapy psychodynamic therapy Acceptance and Commitment Therapy ACT . CBT is based on the principle that psychological problems stem from faulty thinking patterns and learned unhelpful behaviors. Electroconvulsive Therapy ECT Slide 20 Rapid-acting treatment for refractory depression depression with psychotic features catatonia and acute suicidality. Induces tonic-clonic seizure under anesthesia with neuromuscular blockade. Adverse effects disorientation headache partial anterograde/retrograde amnesia usually resolves within 6 months . No absolute contraindications . Safe in pregnant patients and older adults. Transcranial Magnetic Stimulation TMS Slide 21 Uses magnetic fields to stimulate underactive brain areas that regulate mood. Non-invasive; daily 30-40 minute sessions over several weeks. Patients can drive themselves to and from treatments unlike ECT . Contraindicated in patients with history of seizures/epilepsy or intracranial metallic/magnetic implants. correlate Electroconvulsive Therapy ECT - Pathophysiology Induces a generalized tonic-clonic seizure under general anesthesia and neuromuscular blockade to reset dysfunctional neural circuits. - Presentation Used for treatment-resistant depression depression with psychotic features catatonia acute suicidality and refractory schizoaffective disorder. - Diagnosis Clinical decision based on failure of pharmacotherapy or need for rapid treatment response. - Treatment Administered as a series of sessions; most common adverse effects are disorientation headache and temporary anterograde/retrograde amnesia. - Lecturer s Point ECT has NO absolute contraindications. It is very safe and effective even in pregnant patients and older adults. This is a commonly tested fact. Suicide Risk Assessment Slides 27-29 Suicide is an ever-present concern in patients with mood disorders. One of the most dangerous myths in clinical medicine is the belief that asking about suicidal ideation will plant the idea in a patient s mind. Evidence conclusively demonstrates this is false. Clinicians must proactively and directly assess suicidal risk in every patient presenting with depressive symptoms as 21% of patients with recurrent depressive disorder will attempt suicide during their lifetime. Key Fact Asking about suicide does NOT increase suicidal thoughts in patients. Statistic 21% of patients with recurrent depressive disorder attempt suicide. mnemonic Suicide Risk Factors SAD PERSONS - S Sex male - A Age young adult or older adult - D Depression - P Previous attempt HIGHEST risk factor - E Ethanol or drug use - R Rational thinking loss psychosis - S Sickness medical illness - O Organized plan - N No spouse or other social support - S Stated future intent highyield Previous Suicide Attempt is the Strongest Risk Factor Among the SAD PERSONS risk factors for suicide a previous suicide attempt is the single strongest predictor of future completed suicide. Male sex older age social isolation and having an organized plan are also critical risk factors. Always ask directly about suicidal ideation; it does not plant the idea. Depressive Disorder Variants Slides 30-39 51-53 Beyond classic MDD several variants and related conditions exist that present with depressive features but have unique diagnostic criteria time courses or symptom profiles. Understanding these distinctions is crucial for both accurate diagnosis and targeted treatment. Persistent Depressive Disorder Dysthymia Slide 30 Often milder than MDD. Requires 2 or more depressive symptoms lasting at least 2 years at least 1 year in children . Any remission must last 2 months or less. MDD with Seasonal Pattern Slide 31 Formerly called Seasonal Affective Disorder. Major depressive episodes occurring only during a particular season usually winter . Must occur in at least 2 consecutive years and most years across a lifetime. Depression with Atypical Features Slide 34 Most common subtype of depression Lecturer Emphasis . Key features mood reactivity transient improvement with positive events hypersomnia hyperphagia leaden paralysis heavy feeling in arms/legs long-standing interpersonal rejection sensitivity. Substance/Medication-Induced Depressive Disorder Slide 35 Common substances alcohol opiates cocaine amphetamines corticosteroids. Symptoms develop soon after intoxication withdrawal or medication exposure. Depressive Disorder Due to Another Medical Condition Slide 36 Possible causes hypothyroidism adrenal insufficiency systemic lupus erythematosus vitamin B12 deficiency. Peripartum Mood Disturbances Slides 37-39 | Condition | Onset/Timing | Duration | Key Features | Treatment | | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | | Postpartum Blues | 2-3 days after delivery | Usually resolves within 2 weeks | Depressed affect tearfulness fatigue | Supportive care | | MDD with Peripartum Onset | During pregnancy or within 4 weeks of delivery | Meets MDD criteria | Full depressive episode | CBT + SSRIs first-line | Premenstrual Dysphoric Disorder PMDD Slide 53 Severe cyclic mood disorder with emotional/physical symptoms in the luteal phase of the menstrual cycle. Symptoms resolve after menses and cause significant impairment. Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder DMDD Slide 53 Childhood disorder marked by chronic irritability and frequent severe temper outbursts disproportionate to the situation. Must persist for at least 1 year. correlate Depression with Atypical Features - Pathophysiology May involve different monoamine pathway dysregulation compared to classic melancholic depression. - Presentation Mood reactivity mood brightens with positive events hypersomnia hyperphagia/weight gain leaden paralysis interpersonal rejection sensitivity. - Diagnosis Clinical based on presence of mood reactivity plus 2 or more additional atypical features. - Treatment MAOIs historically most effective; SSRIs commonly used. CBT as adjunct. - Lecturer s Point This is the most common subtype of depression. The key distinguishing feature is mood reactivity meaning the patient s mood can temporarily improve in response to positive events unlike melancholic depression. Bipolar Disorders Slides 40-50 Bipolar disorder represents the other major pole of mood pathology. In contrast to unipolar depression bipolar illness is defined by the presence of manic or hypomanic episodes often alternating with depressive episodes. The distinction between Bipolar I and Bipolar II hinges on whether full manic episodes occur. A manic episode involves a persistently elevated expansive or irritable mood with increased activity/energy lasting at least one week accompanied by at least three additional symptoms. The clinical significance is profound if manic symptoms result in hospitalization it is classified as mania regardless of duration. Manic Episode Criteria Slide 43 Persistently elevated expansive or irritable mood with increased activity/energy lasting at least 1 week. 3 or more or 4 if irritable mood of the following symptoms mnemonic Manic Episode Symptoms DIG FAST - D Distractibility - I Indiscretion/Impulsivity reckless behaviors gambling spending sexual indiscretion - G Grandiosity inflated self-esteem may progress to delusions - F Flight of ideas or racing thoughts - A Activity increase goal-directed behavior psychomotor agitation - S Sleep deficit decreased need for sleep - T Talkativeness pressured speech Mania vs. Hypomania Slide 45 | Feature | Mania | Hypomania | | --- | --- | --- | | Duration | At least 1 week or any duration if hospitalized | At least 4 days but less than 7 | | Severity | Causes marked impairment | Patient often still functional | | Hospitalization | May require hospitalization | Does NOT require hospitalization | | Psychosis | May include psychotic features | No psychotic features | Bipolar Subtypes Slide 46 | Subtype | Key Requirement | | --- | --- | | Bipolar I | At least 1 manic episode with or without hypomanic or depressive episodes | | Bipolar II | A hypomanic AND a depressive episode NO history of manic episodes | | Cyclothymic Disorder | Milder form; fluctuating mild depressive and hypomanic symptoms for at least 2 years symptoms present at least half the time remission lasting 2 months or less | correlate Bipolar I Disorder - Pathophysiology Complex interplay of genetic neurochemical monoamine dysregulation and environmental factors. Strong genetic component. - Presentation Characterized by at least one manic episode. Patients alternate between manic highs DIG FAST symptoms and depressive lows SIG E CAPS symptoms . - Diagnosis DSM-5 At least 1 manic episode lasting at least 1 week or any duration if hospitalized . No requirement for depressive episodes. - Treatment Acute Mania Second-generation antipsychotics SGAs and/or mood stabilizers lithium valproic acid carbamazepine . Acute Bipolar Depression SGA plus SSRI or other antidepressant NEVER antidepressant monotherapy . Psychotherapy for insight and compliance. - Complications High suicide risk substance use functional impairment psychotic features. - Lecturer s Point If manic symptoms result in hospitalization it is classified as MANIA regardless of the timing of symptoms. This is a board-favorite distinction. Bipolar Treatment Slide 50 Acute Mania Second-generation antipsychotics SGAs and/or mood stabilizers valproic acid lithium carbamazepine as mono or dual therapy. Acute Bipolar Depression SGA plus SSRI or other antidepressant monotherapy or adjunctive to mood stabilizer like lithium . Critical Warning Antidepressants used as monotherapy can trigger manic episodes. Must be combined with SGAs or mood stabilizers. Psychotherapy to improve insight medication compliance and help patients identify mood changes. analysis content/L80 HighYield Render.pdf ankingResource primarySource BandB chapter Psychiatry > Pathology > Mood Disorders alternatives resource BandB chapter Psychiatry > Pathology > Psychosis resource BandB chapter Psychiatry > Pharm > Antidepressants resource BandB chapter Psychiatry > Pharm > Lithium resource Bootcamp chapter Psychiatry > Psychotic and Mood Disorders > Major Depressive Disorder resource Bootcamp chapter Psychiatry > Psychotic and Mood Disorders > Bipolar Disorder resource Bootcamp chapter Psychiatry > Psychotic and Mood Disorders > Suicide resource Bootcamp chapter Psychiatry > Pharmacology > SSRIs resource Bootcamp chapter Psychiatry > Pharmacology > Lithium resource FirstAid chapter Psychiatry > Pathology > Major Depressive Disorder resource FirstAid chapter Psychiatry > Pathology > Bipolar Disorder resource FirstAid chapter Psychiatry > Pathology > Manic Episode resource FirstAid chapter Psychiatry > Pathology > Mood Disorder resource Physeo chapter Psychiatry > Pathology > Mood Disorders flashcards front What is the first step before initiating antidepressant therapy in a patient with depressive symptoms? back Screen for bipolar disorder e.g. using the Mood Disorder Questionnaire . SSRIs as monotherapy can precipitate mania in undiagnosed bipolar patients. tag Clinical front What mnemonic is used to remember the 9 diagnostic symptoms of Major Depressive Disorder? back SIG E CAPS Sleep changes Interest loss anhedonia Guilt/worthlessness Energy loss Concentration difficulty Appetite changes Psychomotor agitation/retardation Suicidal ideation. tag Concept front How many symptoms and for how long must they be present to diagnose MDD per DSM-5? back 5 or more of 9 SIG E CAPS symptoms present most days for at least 2 weeks causing functional impairment. tag Concept front What is the lifetime prevalence of MDD in the United States? back 17%. tag Concept front What neurotransmitters are classically decreased in Major Depressive Disorder? back Norepinephrine serotonin and dopamine. tag Concept front What structural brain changes are seen in untreated MDD on neuroimaging? back Decreased frontal lobe and hippocampal volumes. tag Concept front What screening tool is used first for depression and what tool follows a positive result? back PHQ-2 is the initial screening tool. If positive PHQ-9 is administered for severity grading and monitoring. tag Clinical front What are the absolute contraindications to Electroconvulsive Therapy ECT ? back There are NO absolute contraindications to ECT. It is safe in pregnant patients and older adults. tag Clinical front What is the single strongest risk factor for completed suicide? back A previous suicide attempt. tag Clinical front What mnemonic summarizes suicide risk factors? back SAD PERSONS Sex male Age Depression Previous attempt Ethanol/drug use Rational thinking loss Sickness Organized plan No social support Stated intent. tag Concept front What is the key distinguishing feature of depression with atypical features? back Mood reactivity transient improvement in mood in response to positive events . Also hypersomnia hyperphagia leaden paralysis interpersonal rejection sensitivity. tag Clinical front What mnemonic is used to remember manic episode symptoms? back DIG FAST Distractibility Indiscretion/Impulsivity Grandiosity Flight of ideas Activity increase Sleep deficit Talkativeness. tag Concept front What distinguishes a manic episode from a hypomanic episode? back Mania lasts at least 1 week or any duration if hospitalized causes marked impairment and may include psychosis. Hypomania lasts at least 4 days but less than 7 patient remains functional and there is no psychosis. tag Concept front What defines Bipolar I vs. Bipolar II disorder? back Bipolar I At least 1 manic episode with or without depressive episodes . Bipolar II Hypomanic plus depressive episode with NO history of full manic episodes. tag Concept front Why is bupropion Wellbutrin contraindicated in patients with eating disorders or seizure disorders? back Bupropion lowers the seizure threshold. Patients with eating disorders especially bulimia and seizure disorders are at increased seizure risk. tag Clinical pearls title Always Rule Out Bipolar Before Treating Depression content Most antidepressants especially SSRIs given as monotherapy can worsen manic/hypomanic episodes in bipolar patients. Always use the Mood Disorder Questionnaire MDQ or a thorough history to screen for mania/hypomania before starting antidepressants. If bipolar is confirmed a mood stabilizer or SGA must anchor the treatment regimen. title ECT Has No Absolute Contraindications content Despite appearing aggressive ECT is remarkably safe and effective. It is the treatment of choice for severe treatment-resistant depression acute suicidality catatonia and depression with psychotic features. It can safely be used in pregnant patients and older adults. title Asking About Suicide Does Not Plant the Idea content A pervasive myth is that directly asking patients about suicidal thoughts will put ideas in their head. Evidence conclusively shows this is false. Direct empathic questioning about suicide is a clinical imperative and does not increase risk. title If Hospitalized It Is Mania content Regardless of symptom duration if manic-type symptoms are severe enough to require hospitalization they are classified as mania not hypomania . This distinction determines whether the diagnosis is Bipolar I vs. Bipolar II. title Atypical Depression Is Actually the Most Common Subtype content Despite its name depression with atypical features mood reactivity hypersomnia hyperphagia leaden paralysis rejection sensitivity is the most common subtype of depression encountered clinically. questions id 1 question A 32-year-old woman presents to her primary care physician with a 3-week history of persistent sadness inability to enjoy her hobbies difficulty sleeping decreased appetite with a 5-pound weight loss fatigue and difficulty concentrating at work. She denies any prior episodes of elevated mood excessive energy or reckless behavior. Her PHQ-2 is positive and PHQ-9 score is 18 moderately severe . Labs including TSH CBC and CMP are within normal limits. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial treatment? options Start lithium Start an SSRI such as sertraline and refer for CBT Immediately refer for electroconvulsive therapy Start bupropion monotherapy Prescribe a benzodiazepine for sleep correctAnswer 1 rationale For a patient meeting DSM-5 criteria for MDD without features of bipolar disorder screened with MDQ and history negative for mania/hypomania the first-line treatment is an SSRI plus cognitive behavioral therapy CBT . ECT is reserved for treatment-resistant or acutely suicidal cases. Lithium is primarily a mood stabilizer for bipolar disorder. Bupropion is an alternative but not first-line for classic MDD. Benzodiazepines do not treat the underlying depression. id 2 question A 28-year-old man presents to the emergency department after his roommate found him with a plan to overdose on medications. He has a history of recurrent major depressive disorder and a prior suicide attempt 2 years ago. He reports hopelessness insomnia anhedonia and has been drinking heavily. Using the SAD PERSONS mnemonic which of the following is considered the STRONGEST individual risk factor for completed suicide? options Male sex Depression Ethanol use Previous suicide attempt Organized plan correctAnswer 3 rationale Among the SAD PERSONS risk factors a previous suicide attempt is the strongest predictor of future completed suicide. While all listed options are valid risk factors this patient has multiple previous attempt consistently ranks as the most powerful individual predictor in the literature. id 3 question A 45-year-old woman with a known history of bipolar I disorder presents to her psychiatrist during a depressive episode. She is currently on lithium for maintenance therapy but still reports persistent low mood hypersomnia and guilt. Her psychiatrist considers adding pharmacotherapy for her depressive symptoms. Which of the following treatment strategies is most appropriate? options Discontinue lithium and start fluoxetine monotherapy Add fluoxetine to her current lithium regimen Switch to bupropion monotherapy Discontinue all medications and start CBT alone Add a first-generation antipsychotic as monotherapy correctAnswer 1 rationale In bipolar depression antidepressants like SSRIs should NEVER be used as monotherapy because they can precipitate a manic episode. The correct approach is to add an antidepressant like fluoxetine to her existing mood stabilizer lithium . This provides antidepressant coverage while the mood stabilizer prevents manic switching. Discontinuing lithium to start SSRI monotherapy is the most dangerous option. id 4 question A 22-year-old graduate student is brought to the emergency department by her friends after a week of unusually elevated mood sleeping only 2 hours per night while feeling fully rested pressured speech spending over $5 000 on impulsive online purchases and believing she has been chosen to write a bestselling novel despite having no writing background. She requires admission for stabilization. Which of the following best characterizes this patient s episode? options Hypomanic episode Major depressive episode with psychotic features Manic episode Cyclothymic disorder Persistent depressive disorder correctAnswer 2 rationale This patient displays classic DIG FAST manic symptoms decreased sleep grandiosity pressured speech talkativeness indiscretion impulsive spending and increased activity. Critically her symptoms are severe enough to require hospitalization which automatically classifies this as a manic episode regardless of duration. Hypomania does not require hospitalization and does not include psychotic features like grandiose delusions. id 5 question A 35-year-old woman presents complaining of chronic low mood for the past 3 years. She reports poor appetite low energy and difficulty making decisions but states that when something good happens at work her mood temporarily brightens. She also endorses a constant feeling of heaviness in her arms and legs sleeping 10-12 hours per night and extreme sensitivity to interpersonal rejection. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? options Persistent depressive disorder dysthymia Major depressive disorder with seasonal pattern Bipolar II disorder Depression with atypical features Premenstrual dysphoric disorder correctAnswer 3 rationale This patient demonstrates the hallmark features of depression with atypical features mood reactivity mood temporarily brightens with positive events hypersomnia 10-12 hours/night leaden paralysis heaviness in arms and legs and interpersonal rejection sensitivity. Despite the name atypical this is actually the most common subtype of depression. Dysthymia is milder and lacks these specific atypical features. Seasonal pattern would require symptom correlation with specific seasons. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l81", "title": "Lecture #81: Anxiety Disorders: Generalized Anxiety Disorder, Panic Disorder, Social Anxiety Disorder", "module": "Psychiatry", "content": "id l81 title Lecture 81 Anxiety Disorders Generalized Anxiety Disorder Panic Disorder Social Anxiety Disorder lecturer A. Raj Psy.D. module Psychiatry session Lecture 81 prev l80 next null pdf pdfs/Lecture 81. A. Raj Psy.D. PPT .pdf summary Lecture 81 Anxiety Disorders Generalized Anxiety Disorder Panic Disorder Social Anxiety Disorder Lecturer A. Raj Psy.D. Session Lecture 81 Introduction to Anxiety Disorders Slide 5-7 Anxiety is a universal human experience that becomes pathological when it is excessive intense chronic and causes functional impairment. The amygdala which processes emotion and fear plays a central role. Pathological anxiety is driven by a combination of genetics environmental life events including Adverse Childhood Experiences ACEs and neurochemical imbalances specifically involving the dysregulation and low levels of GABA and Serotonin. Domains of Anxiety Manifests in physical autonomic response affective fear/panic cognitive worry/catastrophizing and behavioral avoidance domains. Cultural Competence Providers must be aware of their own blind spots and microaggressions. It is crucial to frame mental illness as a biological disorder to build trust especially with underrepresented populations. Generalized Anxiety Disorder GAD Slide 15-21 Generalized Anxiety Disorder is characterized by persistent excessive and difficult-to-control worry about various daily events. Because this chronic state of hyperarousal takes a significant toll on the body and mind patients frequently experience muscle tension fatigue and sleep disturbances alongside their cognitive symptoms. GAD frequently co-occurs with major depression up to 80% of patients . Epidemiology 2 1 Female-to-Male ratio. Higher risk in unmarried individuals minority populations and lower socioeconomic statuses. Onset is typically in the late teens to early 20s. Clinical Presentation Diagnosed if symptoms persist for at least 6 months. WATCHERS Mnemonic W orry A nxiety T ension in muscles C oncentration difficulty H yperarousal irritability E nergy loss R estlessness S leep disturbance correlate Generalized Anxiety Disorder GAD - Pathophysiology Dysregulation of GABA and Serotonin leading to chronic hyperarousal in the amygdala. - Presentation Excessive difficult-to-control worry and tension lasting >6 months. Includes restlessness fatigue muscle tension and sleep disturbance. - Diagnosis Clinical utilizing DSM-5 criteria WATCHERS symptoms . Rule out medical/substance causes. - Treatment First-line CBT + SSRIs or SNRIs citalopram sertraline etc. . Buspirone or pregabalin can be used for augmentation. Maintain treatment for at least 12 months. - Risk Factors Unmarried low socioeconomic status adverse childhood experiences ACEs . - Complications Up to 80% have another psychiatric disorder commonly depression. Panic Disorder Slide 22-28 Panic disorder is marked by the presence of recurrent unexpected panic attacks—abrupt intense bursts of severe anxiety that peak within minutes. Because these attacks trigger massive autonomic surges patients often feel as though they are dying having a heart attack or losing control. Following an attack patients develop a profound persistent fear of experiencing another one leading to maladaptive changes in behavior avoidance . Epidemiology 2 1 Female-to-Male ratio. Onset late adolescence to early 20s. Highly comorbid with depression; poor outcomes are seen when comorbidities are present. Clinical Presentation Palpitations sweating trembling shortness of breath chest pain nausea dizziness paresthesias derealization and fear of dying. Diagnosis At least one attack followed by 1 month of persistent worry about additional attacks or maladaptive behavior changes. correlate Panic Disorder - Pathophysiology Dysregulation of fear circuits leading to recurrent unprovoked sympathetic nervous system surges. - Presentation Abrupt bursts of intense fear peaking in minutes accompanied by palpitations sweating shortness of breath chest pain and fear of dying. - Diagnosis Recurrent unexpected attacks followed by >1 month of worry about future attacks or behavioral avoidance. - Treatment SSRIs paired with CBT is the best long-term option Long term CBT improvement is 40% sustainable vs 18% for meds alone . SNRIs Venlafaxine are second line. - Lecturer s Point Avoid TCAs MAOIs and Benzodiazepines as first-line due to side effects abuse potential and overdose risks. Social Anxiety Disorder SAD Slide 30-34 Social Anxiety Disorder involves an intense fear of social situations where the individual feels exposed to potential scrutiny or judgment by others. Because this fear is often deeply ingrained patients will systematically avoid social interactions significantly disrupting their occupational academic and interpersonal functioning. Epidemiology 3 2 Female-to-Male ratio though males and females present for care equally. When it affects men it often leads to poorer outcomes compared to female patients. Onset is in late adolescence to early 20s. Performance-Only Subtype A specific variant where anxiety is restricted exclusively to speaking or performing in public. correlate Social Anxiety Disorder SAD - Pathophysiology Hyper-reactivity of the amygdala in response to social scrutiny. - Presentation Extreme fear of negative evaluation in social situations leading to avoidance and functional impairment. - Diagnosis Clinical diagnosis based on disproportionate fear of social interactions. - Treatment First line CBT and SSRIs/SNRIs. - Treatment Performance Only CBT + exposure therapy first line. For rare/single performances Beta-blockers Propranolol or low-dose clonazepam can be used. - Complications Up to 80% comorbidity with depression. highyield Treatment Principles Across Anxiety Disorders For Generalized Anxiety Disorder Panic Disorder and generalized Social Anxiety Disorder first-line psychopharmacological treatment is SSRIs or SNRIs ideally paired with Cognitive Behavioral Therapy CBT . Benzodiazepines should generally be avoided due to the high risk of substance abuse and dependence especially in populations with comorbid conditions. mnemonic WATCHERS Symptoms of GAD - W Worry - A Anxiety - T Tension muscle - C Concentration difficulty - H Hyperarousal / Irritability - E Energy loss - R Restlessness - S Sleep disturbance analysis content/L81 HighYield Render Analysis.md ankingResource primarySource BandB chapter Psychiatry > Anxiety Disorders alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Psychiatry > Pathology > Anxiety Disorders resource Bootcamp chapter Psychiatry > Anxiety Obsessive-Compulsive and Trauma-and-Stressor-Related Disorders flashcards front What are the two most commonly dysregulated neurotransmitters implicated in anxiety disorders? back GABA low and Serotonin low . tag Concept front In order to meet diagnostic criteria for Generalized Anxiety Disorder how long must the symptoms of excessive worry persist? back At least 6 months. tag Clinical front Which anxiety disorder typically requires tracking WATCHERS symptoms Worry Anxiety Tension etc. ? back Generalized Anxiety Disorder GAD . tag Clinical front What are the first-line pharmacologic options for maintaining treatment in Generalized Anxiety Disorder? back SSRIs or SNRIs. tag Clinical front In addition to recurring unexpected panic attacks what timeframe of persistent worry about future attacks is required to diagnose Panic Disorder? back 1 month or more. tag Clinical front Why are TCAs MAOIs and Benzodiazepines NOT first-line for Panic Disorder? back High risk of side effects abuse potential and potential for fatal overdose. tag Concept front What is the recommended first-line pharmacological treatment for the Performance Only subtype of Social Anxiety Disorder? back Beta-blockers Propranolol . tag Clinical front What is the most effective long-term treatment strategy for Panic Disorder according to sustained improvement rates? back A combination of Cognitive Behavioral Therapy CBT and SSRIs. tag Clinical front What psychiatric comorbidity is found in up to 80% of patients with Generalized Anxiety Disorder or Social Anxiety Disorder? back Depression. tag Clinical pearls title Long-Term Panic Disorder Treatment content While SSRIs are effective for Panic Disorder patients treated with Cognitive Behavioral Therapy CBT show a 40% long-term improvement rate after discontinuation compared to only 18% for those treated with medication alone. Pairing the two yields the best outcomes. title Performance-Only Social Anxiety content While generalized social anxiety disorder is treated with daily SSRIs/SNRIs the performance-only subtype like a musician or public speaker is uniquely treated on an as-needed basis with Beta-blockers like propranolol to blunt the autonomic surge without sedating the patient. questions id 1 question A 24-year-old female presents to her primary care physician stating she can t stop worrying. She reports that for the last 8 months she has been immensely stressed about her finances her performance at her job and her family s health even when there is no objective crisis. She frequently feels on edge is easily fatigued suffers from muscle tension in her neck and shoulders and has trouble falling asleep. Medical workup and thyroid function are normal. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial pharmacological treatment for this patient? options Lorazepam Sertraline Propranolol Quetiapine Buspirone monotherapy correctAnswer 1 rationale This patient meets criteria for Generalized Anxiety Disorder GAD because she has excessive worry about multiple things lasting longer than 6 months alongside muscle tension fatigue and sleep disturbances WATCHERS criteria . The first-line maintenance pharmacological therapies for GAD are SSRIs like sertraline or SNRIs. id 2 question A 21-year-old male is brought to the emergency department after experiencing a sudden overwhelming episode of fear while studying in the library. He felt his heart racing began sweating profusely felt a choking sensation and was convinced he was having a heart attack and was going to die. His EKG and troponins are entirely normal. He mentions he had a similar episode two months ago and has since completely avoided the library because he is terrified of having another attack. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? options Generalized Anxiety Disorder Specific Phobia Panic Disorder Social Anxiety Disorder Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder correctAnswer 2 rationale The patient is experiencing Panic Disorder defined by recurrent unexpected panic attacks abrupt surges of intense fear with autonomic symptoms followed by at least 1 month of persistent worry about experiencing another attack or altering his behavior avoiding the library because of them. id 3 question A medical student is preparing for a high-stakes clinical skills presentation. She reports that while she is generally comfortable interacting with patients and peers in everyday settings the thought of standing in front of the grading faculty and presenting formally causes her hands to tremble and her voice to shake uncontrollably. She is seeking medication to help exclusively with these presentations. Which of the following is the best therapeutic option for her specific symptoms? options Daily Venlafaxine As-needed Propranolol Daily Citalopram As-needed Quetiapine As-needed Lorazepam correctAnswer 1 rationale This patient describes Social Anxiety Disorder - Performance Only subtype. Because her anxiety is specifically limited to public speaking/performing she does not require daily SSRIs. The standard first-line medication for a performance-only episode is an as-needed beta-blocker such as propranolol to reduce autonomic symptoms tremors tachycardia without sedating the patient. id 4 question Which of the following neurochemical imbalances is classically theorized to contribute most directly to the pathophysiology of Generalized Anxiety and Panic Disorders? options High levels of Dopamine and low levels of Norepinephrine Low levels of GABA and low levels of Serotonin High levels of Serotonin and high levels of Glutamate Low levels of Acetylcholine and high levels of Dopamine High levels of GABA and high levels of Serotonin correctAnswer 1 rationale Dysregulation and low levels of gamma-aminobutyric acid GABA the primary inhibitory neurotransmitter and Serotonin are the primary neurochemical abnormalities implicated in Anxiety Disorders. This is why SSRIs and GABA-modulating drugs like benzodiazepines though second line are effective. id 5 question A primary care physician is diagnosing a 32-year-old unmarried woman from a low socioeconomic background with Generalized Anxiety Disorder. According to epidemiological data what is the most likely psychiatric comorbidity this patient either currently has or is at the greatest risk of developing? options Bipolar I Disorder Schizophrenia Major Depressive Disorder Borderline Personality Disorder Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder correctAnswer 2 rationale Up to 80% of patients with Generalized Anxiety Disorder as well as Panic Disorder and Social Anxiety Disorder have another psychiatric comorbidity and the most common of these is Depression. The presence of depression paired with anxiety often implies a more difficult treatment course. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l82", "title": "Lecture #82: Schizophrenia and Other Psychotic Disorders", "module": "Psychiatry", "content": "id l82 title Lecture 82 Schizophrenia and Other Psychotic Disorders lecturer Giovanni Fardella OMS-3 and C. Dyson M.D. module Psychiatry summary Lecture 82 Schizophrenia and Other Psychotic Disorders This lecture explores the diagnostic criteria clinical presentation and treatment strategies for schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorders. Differentiating between primary psychiatric definitions and alternative systemic causes is emphasized as a foundational step when evaluating psychotic patients. Defining Psychosis and Its Characteristics Psychosis encompasses severe mental disorders marked by a disconnection from reality. Clinically it frequently presents with positive symptoms such as delusions fixed false beliefs strongly held despite contrary evidence hallucinations perceptions lacking external stimuli mostly auditory or visual in primary psychotic disorders and disorganized speech derailment word salad or loose associations . Before declaring a psychiatric diagnosis a clinician must rule out non-psychiatric causes. highyield Keep in mind Always rule out potentially life-threatening or reversible medical causes of psychosis first! Differentials encompass neurocognitive issues e.g. dementias brain lesions delirium epilepsy and other organic triggers infections endocrine disorders like thyroid storm substance intoxication or withdrawal . Diagnosing Schizophrenia According to the DSM-5 diagnosing schizophrenia requires at least two of the following symptoms for a significant portion of a 1-month period and at least one must be a core psychotic symptom marked with an asterisk 1. Delusions 2. Hallucinations 3. Disorganized speech 4. Grossly disorganized or catatonic behavior 5. Negative symptoms e.g. flat affect avolition alogia anhedonia asociality These signs must persist for at least 6 months including prodromal or residual phases with marked social or occupational dysfunction. Typical onset is the early-to-mid 20s for males and late 20s for females affecting ~1% of the global population. Positive vs. Negative Symptoms - Positive symptoms refer to added aberrant behaviors or perceptions delusions hallucinations and disorganized thinking. - Negative symptoms mean something is effectively missing emotional flatness blunted affect poverty of speech alogia lack of initiative avolition and social withdrawal. These are harder to treat and severely impact daily functioning. Spectrum of Psychotic Duration The timeline configuration is the most critical key to differentiating schizophrenia from similar psychotic breakpoints - Brief Psychotic Disorder Delusions hallucinations or disorganized speech lasting less than 1 month often triggered by significant stress followed by a full return to baseline. - Schizophreniform Disorder Identical symptoms to schizophrenia but the duration spans 1 to 6 months . Functional impairment is not required for diagnosis. ~1/3 fully recover; the rest tend to progress to schizophrenia. - Schizophrenia Must persist for greater than 6 months with functional impairment. Special Considerations Catatonia Catatonia presents as a marked psychomotor disturbance and can accompany schizophrenia bipolar disorder or medical illnesses. A clinical diagnosis requires 3 or more of 12 defining features including - Stupor No psychomotor activity or interaction. - Catalepsy Muscular rigidity or fixed posture. - Waxy Flexibility Maintaining a posture into which the patient was placed. - Mutism Little to no verbal response. - Echolalia/Echopraxia Mimicking speech or movements of others. Schizoaffective Disorder A fascinating but tricky diagnostic profile is schizoaffective disorder blending the elements of a mood disorder with schizophrenia. - Requires meeting criteria for both a major mood episode Depressive or Bipolar type AND schizophrenia. - The distinguishing hallmark Psychotic symptoms must occur for at least 2 weeks without any major mood episode . This proves that the psychosis is independent of the mood swings unlike major depressive disorder with psychotic features where psychosis only happens during the deep depression . Other Psychotic and Personality Disorders - Delusional Disorder At least 1 month of fixed non-bizarre or bizarre delusions without fulfilling criteria for schizophrenia e.g. no overt hallucinations except tactile or olfactory ones closely tied to the delusion . Patients often function well otherwise. - Postpartum Psychosis A severe post-delivery psychiatric emergency characterized by mood-congruent delusions hallucinations and risks of harm to the baby or mother. Treat promptly with hospitalization and antipsychotics. - Schizoid Personality Disorder Type A Persistent detachment from social relationships and restricted emotional expression. The individual prefers isolation e.g. the aloof night-shift worker . - Schizotypal Personality Disorder Type A Part of the schizophrenia spectrum typified by eccentric appearances peculiar behavior magical thinking and social anxiety e.g. Professor Trelawney archetype . Treatment Modalities Dosing with Second-Generation Antipsychotics SGAs e.g. aripiprazole ziprasidone olanzapine is the standard treatment path due to a lower risk profile compared to First-Generation counterparts. Watch out for side effects like Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome NMS —a life-threatening reaction comprising lead-pipe rigidity high fever altered mental status and autonomic instability. Another chronic risk is Tardive Dyskinesia marked by involuntary repetitive movements like lip-smacking which can be permanent. takeaways Before a primary psychiatric diagnosis of a psychotic disorder can be made a comprehensive medical workup CBC CMP Tox Screen must exclude an underlying substance or physiological imbalance. Schizophrenia diagnosis requires ≥2 symptoms one must be delusion hallucination or disorganized speech for ≥1 month with total disturbance lasting ≥6 months. Brief psychotic disorder lasts <1 month schizophreniform lasts 1-6 months and schizophrenia lasts >6 months. Negative symptoms alogia avolition anhedonia flat affect reflect the absence of normal behaviors and severely impede functioning often responding poorly to medication compared to positive symptoms. Schizoaffective disorder requires ≥2 weeks of psychotic symptoms in the total absence of mood symptoms to differentiate it from a mood disorder with psychotic features. Delusional disorder purely encompasses delusions ≥1 month without other psychotic symptoms like overt auditory/visual hallucinations or disorganization. Second-generation antipsychotics are first-line to limit risks but one must monitor carefully for Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome and tardive dyskinesia. clinicalCorrelates title Auditory Hallucinations content The most common hallucination format in schizophrenia is auditory e.g. hearing critical voices . Olfactory hallucinations suggest temporal lobe epilepsy whereas tactile hallucinations strongly point towards substance intoxication or withdrawal e.g. alcohol amphetamines . title Schizoid vs. Schizotypal vs. Schizophrenia content These conditions sit on a spectrum. Schizoid loner indifferent apathetic and schizotypal magical odd quirky are personality disorders devoid of fully manifesting primary psychosis. In contrast schizophrenia contains marked breaks from reality altering overall functional capacity. title Erotomanic Delusions content Also known as de Clérambault s syndrome this delusion makes a patient falsely believe that someone often of higher status like a doctor or celebrity is profoundly in love with them despite the two parties having never spoken. It can precipitate stalking behaviors. questions question A 24-year-old male is brought to the ED by his roommate. The patient has been constantly barricading his door for the past three weeks claiming the government is sending microwaves through the walls to read his thoughts. He has no prior medical history and his urine drug screen is negative. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? type multiple choice options Schizophrenia Schizophreniform disorder Brief psychotic disorder Schizoaffective disorder Delusional disorder answer Brief psychotic disorder explanation The patient is experiencing delusions government microwaves and changed behavior for exactly 3 weeks. Symptoms lasting between 1 day and 1 month define a brief psychotic disorder. If it persists past the 1-month mark up to 6 months it would be classified as schizophreniform disorder. topic Schizophrenia Spectrum question A 28-year-old female presents with a 2-month history of hearing voices narrating her life believing her food is poisoned and maintaining poor personal hygiene. Which of the following signs or symptoms is considered a negative symptom of her presumed condition? type multiple choice options Auditory hallucinations Persecutory delusions Disorganized speech Avolition Paranoia answer Avolition explanation Avolition a lack of initiative or goal-directed behavior is a negative symptom representing a loss of regular capability. Hallucinations delusions and disorganized speech are positive symptoms signifying an addition of aberrant behaviors. topic Positive vs Negative Symptoms question Which of the following temporal criteria must be met to establish a diagnosis of schizophrenia according to DSM-5? type multiple choice options Symptoms for at least 1 month with total disturbance lasting >1 month Symptoms for at least 2 weeks with total disturbance lasting >3 months Symptoms for at least 1 month with total disturbance lasting >6 months Symptoms for at least 6 months with total disturbance lasting >1 year Symptoms for at least 2 weeks without a mood episode answer Symptoms for at least 1 month with total disturbance lasting >6 months explanation Schizophrenia requires ≥2 core psychotic symptoms to be actively present for at least 1 month. Crucially the continuous overall disturbance including prodromal or residual periods must persist for at least 6 months. topic Diagnostic Criteria question A 35-year-old male with a history of schizophrenia is found by police standing completely motionless in the middle of a park. When EMS arrives they move his arm to take his blood pressure and his arm remains suspended exactly in that position after they let go. What is the correct term for this physical finding? type multiple choice options Waxy flexibility Catalepsy Mutism Echopraxia Stupor answer Waxy flexibility explanation Waxy flexibility is a core sign of catatonia where a patient s limbs will remain rigidly fixed in whatever unnatural posture an examiner places them in akin to molding hot wax. topic Catatonia question A 42-year-old female experiences immense grief after her father passes away. Over the next week she is found pacing incessantly stating that she hears God telling her to repent and is entirely inconsolable. Three weeks later her behavior fully resolves and she returns to her baseline job as a banker. Which of the following is her most likely diagnosis? type multiple choice options Major Depressive Disorder with psychotic features Schizophreniform disorder Postpartum psychosis Schizoaffective disorder Brief psychotic disorder answer Brief psychotic disorder explanation A rapid stress-induced breakdown characterized by auditory hallucinations and pacing that fully resolves within 1 month dictates a brief psychotic disorder. There is a definitive break from reality a very concise temporal frame < 1 month and an eventual full return to baseline. topic Psychotic Disorders question How does one clinically differentiate schizoaffective disorder from a primary mood disorder with psychotic features? type multiple choice options Schizoaffective disorder features solely negative symptoms during the mood episode. A mood disorder with psychotic features strictly presents with delusions no hallucinations. Schizoaffective disorder requires at least 2 weeks of notable psychotic symptoms in the absence of a major mood episode. Schizoaffective strictly requires 6 months of active manic phases alongside hallucinations. Psychosis in schizoaffective disorder only happens within the timeframe of a major depressive episode. answer Schizoaffective disorder requires at least 2 weeks of notable psychotic symptoms in the absence of a major mood episode. explanation To distinguish schizoaffective disorder from major depression with psychotic features or bipolar disorder with psychotic features the patient must have experienced delusions or hallucinations independently for ≥2 weeks while their mood profile was ostensibly unperturbed. topic Schizoaffective vs Mood Disorder question A 20-year-old male in medical school is known by his peers for wearing extremely odd clothing carrying a crystal strictly for aura cleansing and believing profoundly in clairvoyance. While socially awkward he does not hallucinate holds no fixed paranoid delusions and manages his coursework appropriately. Which personality disorder does this pattern best fit? type multiple choice options Schizoid personality disorder Avoidant personality disorder Schizophreniform disorder Schizotypal personality disorder Paranoid personality disorder answer Schizotypal personality disorder explanation Schizotypal personality disorder encapsulates an eccentric appearance magical thinking/odd beliefs and interpersonal awkwardness without cresting into full primary psychosis and marked functional disruption. topic Personality Disorders question Which of the following medication side effects corresponds to a potentially lethal triad of muscle rigidity autonomic instability and high fever in a patient being treated for schizophrenia? type multiple choice options Tardive dyskinesia Agranulocytosis Neuroleptic malignant syndrome Serotonin syndrome Acute dystonia answer Neuroleptic malignant syndrome explanation Neuroleptic malignant syndrome NMS is an acute life-threatening response to antipsychotics characterized by lead-pipe rigidity altered mental stature hyperthermia and autonomic dysfunction. topic Pharmacology question A 52-year-old male has held a steadfast unwavering belief for the past 4 years that the CIA is tracking his movements via a microchip in his dental filling. He does not hallucinate continues to maintain his job as an accountant and his behavior is otherwise not odd. What is this patient s most likely diagnosis? type multiple choice options Delusional disorder Schizophrenia Brief psychotic disorder Schizoaffective disorder Schizotypal personality disorder answer Delusional disorder explanation The presence of a solitary encapsulated delusion surviving for >1 month with strictly no other bizarre behavior or psychotic symptoms like disorganized speech or hallucinations is the cornerstone of a delusional disorder. topic Delusional Disorder question While interviewing a 30-year-old female patient with known schizophrenia she continually repeats the question How are you today? exactly identically every time you ask her a new question. Which manifestation of catatonia is this patient exhibiting? type multiple choice options Echolalia Echopraxia Grimacing Mutism Mannerisms answer Echolalia explanation Echolalia involves thoughtlessly mimicking or repeating the verbalizations of another individual while echopraxia dictates mimicking their physical movements. topic Catatonia flashcards front What is the time criterion required to diagnose a Brief Psychotic Disorder? back Symptoms must last from at least 1 day up to 1 month with a full return to premorbid functioning. front What timeline distinguishes Schizophreniform Disorder from Schizophrenia? back Schizophreniform Disorder symptoms persist between 1 month and 6 months whereas Schizophrenia entails symptoms spanning greater than 6 months. front What is the key chronological condition required to diagnose Schizoaffective Disorder? back The patient must experience psychotic symptoms for at least 2 continuous weeks in the absence of a major mood depressive or manic episode. front What are the common positive symptoms of schizophrenia? back Delusions hallucinations most commonly auditory and disorganized speech/behavior. front Name four common negative symptoms often remembered as the A s . back Alogia poverty of speech Avolition lack of drive Anhedonia inability to feel pleasure and Asociality social withdrawal —alongside flat affect. front What is Delusional Disorder? back A disorder defined by the presence of ≥1 fixed delusion s lasting ≥1 month with no other accompanying psychotic traits no prominent hallucinations or disorganized speech . front How do tactile hallucinations alter the main differential diagnosis approach? back Tactile hallucinations point intensely towards an underlying non-psychiatric cause frequently substance intoxication/withdrawal e.g. alcohol withdrawal or amphetamine toxicity . front What characterizes Waxy Flexibility in catatonia? back A physical state where a patient’s limbs retain any position they are mechanically placed into by an examiner. front How do Schizoid and Schizotypal personality disorders significantly differ? back Schizoid Type A is marked by extreme detachment and indifference to social interaction. Schizotypal Type A centers around bizarre or magical thinking eccentric dress and paranoia residing mildly on the schizophrenia spectrum. front What are the hallmark characteristics of Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome NMS ? back Triggered by antipsychotics it features severe hyperthermia lead-pipe muscle rigidity altered mental status and autonomic instability/dysfunction. ankingRef Anki/Board Resources - Boards & Beyond Psychiatry > Psychotic Disorders > Schizophrenia - First Aid for the USMLE Step 1 Psychiatry chapter - Schizophrenia spectrum section and antipsychotic pharmacology details focus strictly on timelines <1 mo = Brief 1-6 mo = Schizophreniform >6 mo = Schizophrenia . - Sketchy Medical Sketchy Pharm - Antipsychotics focus heavily on Atypical Antipsychotics The Ghost Whisperer setting to understand receptor targets and specific adverse effects . - Pathoma Not exceptionally relevant for psychiatric clinical disorders outside of some neuroanatomical correlations. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l83", "title": "Lecture #83: Unusual Neurological Diseases, Somatoform Disorders and Factitious Disorders", "module": "Neurology", "content": "id l83 title Lecture 83 Unusual Neurological Diseases Somatoform Disorders and Factitious Disorders lecturer A. Leder D.O. module Neurology summary Lecture 83 Unusual Neurological Diseases Somatoform Disorders and Factitious Disorders This lecture bridges the gap between purely neurological presentations and primary psychiatric manifestations highlighting a variety of unusual neurological syndromes somatoform disorders and factitious presentations. The primary goal is to learn how to distinguish structural and measurable neurological disorders from those with psychiatric origins or intended deception. Unusual Neurological Syndromes Several rare but fascinating neurological syndromes present with highly specific deficits that can easily be misdiagnosed if the clinician is unaware of them - Alien Limb Phenomenon Seen in 30-50% of patients with corticobasal degeneration . A limb acts seemingly on its own outside voluntary control performing complex actions like unbuttoning a shirt while the patient remains largely unaware of the limb s movement. - Anton Syndrome Anton-Babinski Syndrome Visual anosognosia due to bilateral occipital lobe damage resulting in cortical blindness. The patient strongly denies their vision loss and will confabulate to mask their deficit e.g. complimenting glasses you aren t wearing and then walking into a wall . - Split Brain Syndrome Callosal Disconnection Syndrome Results from damage or surgical transection of the corpus callosum . If a blindfolded patient holds an object in their left hand the sensory information travels to the right hemisphere which handles spatial tasks . However because the connection to the left hemisphere which handles speech and naming is severed the patient cannot name the object until it is placed in the right hand. - Congenital Prosopagnosia An inability to recognize familiar faces even family members or famous people despite an intact visual cortex. To recognize individuals the patient often relies on voice. It can also be acquired via bilateral lesions of the temporo-occipital area. - Alice in Wonderland Syndrome A perceptual disorder involving brief transient visual distortions where objects or body parts appear abnormally large or small. It is most commonly associated with viral infections esp. Epstein-Barr virus but can also be caused by migraines illicit substances or psychiatric disorders. No permanent brain abnormalities are seen. - Phantom Limb Syndrome Experiencing painful sensory feelings referred to a missing limb or breast/rectum post-amputation. The classic non-pharmacological treatment involves mirror therapy . Autoimmune Movement and Seizure Disorders - NMDAR Encephalitis An autoimmune encephalitis occasionally presenting as a paraneoplastic syndrome secondary to an ovarian teratoma . It initially presents with neuropsychiatric symptoms agitation paranoia followed by medication-resistant seizures and movement disorders. A pelvic ultrasound is critical for female patients presenting with these exact symptoms. - Dopa-Responsive Dystonia Segawa Disease A lower extremity dystonia in childhood presenting as a severe gait abnormality. Notably it has a diurnal fluctuation symptoms are absent in the morning post-sleep and severely worsen by the evening. It responds remarkably to low-dose levodopa . - Paroxysmal Kinesigenic Dyskinesia Brief dystonic posturing triggered by sudden voluntary movements stress or sleep deprivation. It can occur up to 100 times a day and responds well to anti-seizure medications like carbamazepine . - Transient Global Amnesia Acute onset of anterograde amnesia inability to form new memories preventing the patient from knowing the date or where they are resulting in repetitive questioning. Unlike dementia the episode resolves within 24 hours. - Focal Seizure with Dyscognitive Features Formerly called complex partial seizures. Involves abnormal electrical impulses causing an alteration of consciousness blank stare unresponsiveness accompanied by automatisms lip smacking chewing picking . It typically lasts 1-3 minutes followed by a period of confusion. - Non-Epileptic Seizures Dissociative seizures Psychogenic events that visually mimic epileptic seizures but have no abnormal brain electrical activity . They are triggered by physical or mental stress. Treatment centers around psychotherapy rather than antiepileptics. Somatoform Factitious and Related Psychiatric Disorders Clinicians frequently encounter patients whose physical symptoms span the psychiatric spectrum—ranging from unconscious manifestation of distress to outright intentional deception. - Functional Neurological Disorder Neurologic symptoms weakness paralysis non-epileptic seizures abnormal gait that completely lack underlying structural disease and whose systemic findings are incompatible with recognized neurologic pathways. The symptoms are unconscious but cause genuine clinical distress. - Factitious Disorder Munchausen s Syndrome The patient intentionally falsifies symptoms induces injury or infects themselves e.g. injecting feces into an IV line to achieve the patient/sick role . Crucially there is no external secondary reward like money or drugs . Munchausen s represents a severe variant marked by pseudologia fantastica exaggerated lying and geographic wandering from hospital to hospital. - Factitious Disorder Imposed on Another Munchausen by Proxy A dangerous form of child or elder abuse where the caregiver intentionally causes clinical sickness in the dependent to garner medical attention and sympathy. - Malingering The purposeful faking or exaggerating of physical or psychological symptoms intending to attain a secondary gain e.g. avoiding jail securing an insurance settlement or workers comp acquiring opioids . This is not considered a psychiatric illness. - Illness Anxiety Disorder Formerly known as hypochondriasis. An excessive intense preoccupation and anxiety about having or developing a severe undiagnosed medical condition despite minimal or absent somatic symptoms and repeated medical reassurance. - Body Dysmorphic Disorder BDD An intense preoccupation with a practically invisible or highly exaggerated perceived physical flaw. This triggers repetitive distressing behavioral checks and may lead patients to excessively seek out unnecessary plastic surgeries. - Pseudocyesis A fascinating false yet resolute belief of being pregnant. The body actually physically mirrors pregnancy abdominal distention cessation of menses morning sickness despite definitively negative pregnancy tests and an empty uterus on sonogram. highyield Differentiating Intent When faced with a patient exhibiting unexplainable symptoms focus on their intent and the incentive. - No conscious intent + distress = Functional Neurological Disorder or Illness Anxiety Disorder . - Conscious intent + seeking the sick role internal primary gain = Factitious Disorder . - Conscious intent + seeking measurable external reward secondary gain = Malingering . takeaways Alien limb phenomenon involves involuntary complex movements of a limb and is classically associated with corticobasal degeneration. Anton Syndrome arises from bilateral occipital damage resulting in cortical blindness paired with visual anosognosia and confabulation. Split brain callosal disconnection prevents interhemispheric transfer. A patient holding an object in the left hand processing in the right hemisphere cannot verbally name it because speech relies on the disconnected left hemisphere. NMDAR encephalitis must be strongly suspected in young women presenting with new-onset severe psychiatric symptoms followed by abnormal movements and seizures. Always screen for an ovarian teratoma. Dopa-Responsive Dystonia causes childhood gait issues that worsen significantly throughout the day diurnal fluctuation and resolves with levodopa. Functional Neurological Disorder involves genuine symptoms lacking structural etiology Factitious Disorder involves deliberate fabrication to assume the sick role and Malingering involves explicit fabrication for an external reward. clinicalCorrelates title NMDAR Encephalitis vs Psychosis content NMDAR encephalitis frequently mimics an acute psychotic break early in its course. Suspect neuro-immunological involvement when acute treatment-resistant psychiatric symptoms are rapidly followed by organic neurological signs seizures dyskinesias choreoathetosis . title Functional vs. Organic Weakness content Functional Neurological Disorder can present as paralysis. Hoover s sign lack of downward pressure from the paralyzed leg when the patient attempts to lift their healthy leg against resistance is a classic clinical maneuver to distinguish functional weakness from true organic paresis. title Alice in Wonderland Syndrome Associations content When a pediatric patient rapidly reports visual distortions where objects seem incredibly shrunken micropsia or massive macropsia immediately consider a recent viral infection Epstein-Barr Virus or the onset of atypical migraines. questions question A 19-year-old female presents with recent rapidly progressive agitation paranoia and severe memory deficits. A week later she developed choreoathetoid movements and had a generalized tonic-clonic seizure. Her parents note she had a mild flu-like illness a month prior. Given the likely diagnosis what diagnostic imaging is most urgently indicated to look for an underlying etiology? type multiple choice options Abdominal CT to evaluate for appendicitis Pelvic ultrasound to evaluate for an ovarian teratoma Chest X-ray to evaluate for small cell lung cancer Renal ultrasound to evaluate for Wilms tumor Thyroid ultrasound to evaluate for medullary thyroid cancer answer Pelvic ultrasound to evaluate for an ovarian teratoma explanation The patient s presentation sequence viral prodrome -> rapid neuropsychiatric decline -> seizures/movement disorder is the classic hallmark of anti-NMDAR encephalitis. This autoimmune condition occurs very frequently as a paraneoplastic syndrome secondary to an ovarian teratoma. Thus a pelvic sonogram is a critical work-up step. topic Neurological Syndromes question A 62-year-old male suffered bilateral posterior cerebral artery strokes. During his neurological examination he walks directly into a door frame. When asked how many fingers the physician is holding up he confidently states four despite the physician holding up none. When told he is incorrect he insists the lighting in the room is simply poor. What is this syndrome called? type multiple choice options Alien Limb Phenomenon Prosopagnosia Anton-Babinski Syndrome Callosal Disconnection Syndrome Phantom Limb Syndrome answer Anton-Babinski Syndrome explanation Anton-Babinski syndrome is a form of visual anosognosia caused by bilateral occipital lobe damage cortical blindness . The defining feature is the patient s absolute denial of their blindness and their generation of confabulations to excuse their visual failures. topic Cortical Blindness/Anosognosia question A 10-year-old girl is brought to the clinic for a severe gait abnormality. Her parents complain she walks completely normally when waking up for school but by dinner time she requires a wheelchair due to extreme stiffness and twisting in her legs. A trial of carbidopa/levodopa entirely resolves her symptoms. What is the diagnosis? type multiple choice options Corticobasal degeneration Functional Neurological Disorder Paroxysmal Kinesigenic Dyskinesia Dopa-Responsive Dystonia Segawa Disease Munchausen by Proxy answer Dopa-Responsive Dystonia Segawa Disease explanation Dopa-responsive dystonia strongly features a classic diurnal fluctuation fine in the morning debilitating dystonia by evening and an incredibly robust life-changing response to levodopa dosing. topic Movement Disorders question During an assessment a patient is blindfolded. The physician places a set of keys in the patient s left hand and asks him to name the object. The patient is unable to do so. However when the keys are transferred to the right hand he immediately names them. What neurological structure has most likely been severely damaged or surgically severed? type multiple choice options Left optic tract Corpus callosum Anterior commissure Bilateral occipital lobes Right parietal lobe answer Corpus callosum explanation This is Split Brain Syndrome. Sensory input from the left hand goes to the right cerebral hemisphere. Without a corpus callosum to transfer this information over to the left hemisphere where the speech and language centers reside the patient cannot linguistically name the object he is feeling. topic Neuroanatomy question A 34-year-old nurse is admitted for severe recurrent hypoglycemia. While investigating her C-peptide levels are found to be pathologically low while exogenous insulin levels in her blood are extraordinarily high. Upon room search syringes and insulin vials are found hidden in her bag. She admits to injecting the insulin stating she merely wanted the doctors to care for her. She was not seeking money or time off work. This best describes type multiple choice options Functional Neurological Disorder Malingering Illness Anxiety Disorder Factitious Disorder Somatic Symptom Disorder answer Factitious Disorder explanation Factitious disorder Munchausen s involves the intentional creation or induction of disease strictly to assume the sick or patient role . The defining criteria that separates this from malingering is the acute lack of an external secondary gain like avoiding work or getting a lawsuit settlement . topic Psychiatric Disorders question A 23-year-old male was involved in a minor fender bender in a parking lot. His medical evaluation shows no structural or nerve injuries. However he arrives at the clinic in a neck brace loudly complaining of excruciating pain and complete paralysis of his right arm. You find out he has an active $500 000 lawsuit against the other driver’s insurance. This presentation is most consistent with type multiple choice options Illness Anxiety Disorder Malingering Factitious Disorder Functional Neurological Disorder Munchausen s Syndrome answer Malingering explanation Malingering is the deliberate fabrication or extreme exaggeration of symptoms motivated purely by secondary external rewards such as a large financial insurance settlement. topic Psychiatric Disorders / Deception question A 55-year-old male is noted sitting at dinner when he suddenly freezes stares blankly and begins repeatedly smacking his lips and picking at his shirt collar. He is entirely unresponsive to his wife calling his name. Three minutes later the activity stops but he remains deeply confused for another ten minutes. What kind of seizure did he most likely experience? type multiple choice options Absence seizure Non-epileptic Psychogenic seizure Focal seizure with dyscognitive features Generalized tonic-clonic seizure Myoclonic seizure answer Focal seizure with dyscognitive features explanation Formerly known as complex partial seizures focal seizures with dyscognitive features are identified by an alteration of consciousness blank stare lack of responsiveness paired with classic automatisms lip smacking picking followed by a post-ictal period of confusion. Absence seizures do not generally have a long post-ictal confusional state. topic Seizure Disorders question A 9-year-old complains that his bedroom furniture looks exceedingly small and far away claiming his bed resembles a dollhouse bed. His pediatrician notes he was just recovering from Infectious Mononucleosis. What syndrome is he most likely experiencing? type multiple choice options Anton-Babinski Syndrome Alice in Wonderland Syndrome Prosopagnosia Charles Bonnet Syndrome Capgras Delusion answer Alice in Wonderland Syndrome explanation Alice in Wonderland Syndrome involves brief sensory distortions where items seem macropsic or micropsic. It is highly associated with viral infections like Epstein-Barr virus in pediatric populations. topic Perceptual Disorders question A 24-year-old female presents deeply concerned about a massive hideous bump on her nose that she believes makes her unlovable. The examining physician notes her nose is completely straight and normal. The patient admits she checks mirrors 40 times a day and is requesting a referral to a plastic surgeon. What is the diagnosis? type multiple choice options Illness Anxiety Disorder Body Dysmorphic Disorder Factitious Disorder Somatic Symptom Disorder Functional Neurological Disorder answer Body Dysmorphic Disorder explanation Body Dysmorphic Disorder is characterized by an obsessive fixation on an exaggerated or fully imagined physical flaw resulting in extreme distress repetitive checking behaviors and repeated attempts to surgically correct the perceived defect. topic Somatoform Disorders question A 65-year-old male is playing golf when he suddenly asks his friend Where are we? Why are we playing golf? His friend explains the situation but three minutes later the man asks the exact same question again. Aside from his repetitive questioning and inability to form new memories of the afternoon his neurological exam is perfectly normal. The next morning he is fully recovered. What is the diagnosis? type multiple choice options Focal seizure with dyscognitive features Transient Global Amnesia Alzheimer s Dementia Wernicke Encephalopathy Dissociative fugue answer Transient Global Amnesia explanation Transient global amnesia is a temporary < 24 hrs acute bout of anterograde amnesia. Patients are unable to lay down new memories and thus predictably ask the exact same questions repeatedly as they continually lose their immediate situational context. topic Amnestic Disorders flashcards front What is the classic clinical hallmark of Alien Limb Phenomenon and what neurodegenerative disease is it most associated with? back Involuntary complex unconscious goal-directed movements of a single limb. It is most associated with Corticobasal Degeneration. front Describe Anton-Babinski Syndrome and its anatomical etiology. back Visual anosognosia severe denial of clinical blindness and confabulation to cover up visual mistakes. It is caused by bilateral occipital lobe damage cortical blindness . front Anti-NMDAR encephalitis frequently presents with neuro-psychiatric distress and seizures. What underlying neoplasm must you absolutely screen for particularly in young females? back Ovarian teratoma. A pelvic ultrasound is mandatory. front What dictates the clinical diagnosis of Dopa-Responsive Dystonia Segawa Disease ? back Childhood lower extremity dystonia exhibiting intense diurnal fluctuation symptoms worsen greatly by afternoon/evening that completely resolves with levodopa administration. front How do you distinguish Factitious Disorder Munchausen from Malingering based strictly on intent? back Factitious Disorder is the intentional falsification of symptoms to assume the sick role an internal psychological gain . Malingering is intentional falsification strictly to achieve an external secondary gain money drugs avoiding jail . front What separates Illness Anxiety Disorder from Somatic Symptom Disorder? back Illness Anxiety Disorder involves an overwhelming anxiety of acquiring or having a disease despite minimal or completely absent physical somatic symptoms. Somatic Symptom Disorder heavily relies on the presence of severe distressing physical symptoms. front What is Prosopagnosia? back The specific inability to recognize familiar human faces despite having entirely intact primary vision. It can be congenital or acquired via bilateral temporo-occipital lesions. front What common viral infection is heavily linked to the temporary visual/size distortions characterizing Alice in Wonderland Syndrome? back Epstein-Barr Virus EBV . front In Split Brain Syndrome taking an object in the left hand prevents the patient from physically naming it. Why? back The sensory input goes to the right hemisphere. A severed corpus callosum prevents the right hemisphere from sending that information to the left hemisphere where the brain s language and speech centers are located. front What is Pseudocyesis? back The false but resolute belief that a woman is pregnant leading to physical somatic manifestations mirroring pregnancy amenorrhea abdominal distention despite a definitively non-gravid status. ankingRef Anki/Board Resources - Boards & Beyond Psychiatry > Somatoform & Factitious Disorders. - First Aid for the USMLE Step 1 Psychiatry chapter - Review the chart comparing Factitious Disorder Malingering and Somatic Symptom/Illness Anxiety Disorders. - First Aid for the USMLE Step 1 Neurology chapter - Focal seizures vs Generalized seizures and visual agnosias. - Pathoma Female Reproductive System for Ovarian Teratomas / paraneoplastic anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis correlations . anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l84", "title": "Lecture #84: DPR - History and Physical Exam: Pediatrics Part 2 (6yo - 10yo)", "module": "Pediatrics", "content": "id l84 title Lecture 84 DPR - History and Physical Exam Pediatrics Part 2 6yo - 10yo lecturer A. Patel D.O. module Pediatrics session Lecture 84 prev l83 next l86 pdf pdfs/Lecture 84. A. Patel D.O. PPT .pdf summary Lecture 84 DPR - History and Physical Exam Pediatrics Part 2 6yo - 10yo Lecturer A. Patel D.O. Session Lecture 84 1. Well-Child Visit The School-Age Interview Slides 1-3 The school-age well-child visit ages 6-10 shifts its focus from purely developmental milestones to a broader assessment of academic performance social integration and emerging behavioral health concerns. As children enter more rigorous academic environments previously unnoticed issues like learning disabilities and ADHD often surface for the first time. The clinician s role at this stage is to screen for barriers to academic and social success while promoting healthy lifestyle habits. - School & Academics - Focus on school performance grades and social dynamics - This is the age when learning disabilities and ADHD commonly present as academic demands increase Slide 2 - Always ask about screen time -- AAP recommends limiting to 2 hours/day Lecturer Emphasis - Diet & Exercise - Ensure adequate calcium intake 2-3 servings/day milk yogurt cheese - Encourage sports participation -- associated with improved team-building skills and reduced future risk-taking behaviors Lecturer Emphasis - Establish good sleep habits at least 9 hours per night is crucial for development and learning - Dental Health & Vision - Brushing twice daily dentist visits every 6 months - Visual screening is critical -- children may not realize their vision is declining; refer to optometrist/ophthalmologist if abnormal - Lipid & Cardiac Screening - AAP recommends one random non-fasting lipid screening between ages 9-11 Slide 3 - Cardiac screening questions syncope during exercise chest pain during exercise family history of early cardiac death highyield Screen time should be limited to 2 hours/day per AAP guidelines. Children ages 6-10 need at least 9 hours of sleep and 2-3 servings of calcium daily. The AAP recommends a random non-fasting lipid screening between ages 9-11. 2. Growth Assessment BMI Blood Pressure and Short Stature Slides 4-8 Pediatric Obesity Childhood obesity is a significant public health concern affecting approximately 1 in 5 U.S. children and adolescents. Unlike adults who use absolute BMI cutoffs pediatric obesity is defined by BMI percentiles based on age and sex. - BMI Percentile Classifications Slide 4 - Obese at or above the 95th percentile - Overweight 85th to 95th percentile - Underweight less than 5th percentile correlate Pediatric Obesity - Pathophysiology Caloric excess relative to expenditure with genetic behavioral and environmental contributors. - Presentation BMI at or above 95th percentile for age and sex. Associated with insulin resistance and dyslipidemia. - Diagnosis BMI percentile charting at each well-child visit. - Treatment Dietary modification eliminate sugary juices replace with water increased physical activity family-based behavioral counseling. - Lecturer s Point 1 in 5 US children suffer from obesity. Practical interventions like cutting out juices are achievable goals. Blood Pressure in Children - For children ages 1-12 BP is interpreted using percentile-based tables for age sex and height Slide 5 - Elevated BP at or above the 90th percentile - Stage 1 HTN at or above the 95th percentile - Stage 2 HTN at or above the 95th percentile + 12 mmHg - For children 13 and older use adult designations Normal less than 120/80 - Heart Rate by Age 1-2 yrs 110-120 bpm; 2-6 yrs 100-110 bpm; 6-10 yrs 75-90 bpm approaches adult range Short Stature - Etiologies - Familial Calculate mid-parental height Girls paternal ht cm - 13 + maternal ht / 2; Boys add 13 instead - Constitutional growth delay Late bloomers. Bone age shows younger than chronological age; they will eventually catch up - Pathologic GH deficiency malnutrition celiac disease Lecturer Emphasis - Idiopathic All tests normal - Key Diagnostic Tool Bone age X-ray -- if delayed suggests constitutional delay correlate Constitutional Growth Delay - Pathophysiology Normal variant where pubertal development occurs later. Skeletal maturation bone age lags behind chronological age. - Presentation Short stature in an otherwise healthy child growing along a lower percentile with delayed bone age. - Diagnosis Bone age X-ray shows skeletal maturity younger than chronological age. Labs GH thyroid are normal. - Treatment Reassurance -- these children will reach their predicted mid-parental height. - Lecturer s Point Parents often realize they are also short once you calculate mid-parental height explaining the child s stature. 3. AAP Recommended Screenings Slide 9 - Every visit History physical exam height/weight - Starting at age 3 BMI and blood pressure measured annually - Annual Vision hearing developmental behavioral/social/emotional screening - Immunizations Assess at every visit; catch up as needed - Oral health Discuss fluoride supplementation if local water is not fluoridated 4. Adverse Childhood Experiences ACEs Slides 10-12 ACEs represent a framework for understanding how childhood trauma translates into lifelong health consequences. The CDC-Kaiser ACE Study demonstrated a dose-response relationship the higher the ACE score the greater the risk for chronic disease and behavioral health problems. - ACEs Questionnaire 10-point tool assessing domestic violence substance abuse incarceration homelessness abuse and neglect - Health Outcomes Higher scores strongly correlate with obesity diabetes cardiovascular disease depression substance abuse - Mechanism Chronic childhood stress can alter epigenetics leading to maladaptive health responses - Affects all communities -- not limited to low-income or specific ethnic groups Lecturer Emphasis - Recommended Reading The Deepest Well by Dr. Nadine Burke Harris Lecturer Emphasis highyield ACEs are scored on a 10-point questionnaire. Higher scores dose-dependently correlate with obesity diabetes cardiovascular disease depression substance abuse and shortened lifespan. These stressors can alter epigenetics leading to chronic disease. 5. Depression Screening PHQ-2 and PHQ-9 Slides 13-15 - PHQ-2 Initial Screen begins at age 10 1. Little interest or pleasure in doing things anhedonia 2. Feeling down depressed or hopeless - Score of 3 or more = positive prompts full PHQ-9 - PHQ-9 Deep Screen Additional questions on sleep fatigue appetite concentration psychomotor changes and suicidal ideation - Classifies depression as minor moderate or severe - If SI or HI identified urgent intervention required Lecturer Emphasis correlate Major Depressive Disorder in Adolescents - Pathophysiology Multifactorial -- genetic predisposition combined with environmental stressors. Serotonin NE and dopamine dysregulation. - Presentation Anhedonia sadness sleep/appetite changes fatigue poor concentration. Irritability may be more prominent than sadness in adolescents. - Diagnosis PHQ-2 screen score 3 or more triggers PHQ-9 . PHQ-9 classifies severity. - Treatment Psychotherapy CBT +/- SSRIs fluoxetine is first-line in pediatrics . - Lecturer s Point Depression screening begins at age 10. If SI/HI is endorsed the patient needs help sooner rather than later. 6. ADHD Slides 16-22 ADHD is a chronic pervasive neurodevelopmental disorder characterized by inappropriate hyperactivity impulsivity and inattention that adversely affect daily functioning. The Vanderbilt Assessment Scale is the cornerstone of objective diagnosis requiring impairment across multiple settings. | Subtype | Predominant Symptoms | More Common In | | --- | --- | --- | | Inattentive | Poor focus distractibility | Females | | Hyperactive-Impulsive | Fidgeting disruptive behavior | Males | | Combined most common | Both inattention and hyperactivity | Males | - Symptoms must present before age 12 in 2+ settings home + school - Vanderbilt Assessment Scale Separate forms for parent and teacher - Symptoms begin around age 4 peak at 7-8 as academic demands increase - DDx Age-appropriate behavior anxiety depression learning disabilities sleep disorders poor sleep causes hyperactivity in kids bipolar disorder - Treatment Medication + Behavioral therapy is best Lecturer Emphasis - First-line Stimulants Adderall Concerta/Ritalin - Second-line Non-stimulants atomoxetine clonidine guanfacine - IEP for academic support - Watch for appetite suppression and weight loss from stimulants correlate Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder ADHD - Pathophysiology Neurodevelopmental disorder involving prefrontal cortex dopamine/NE dysfunction. Strong genetic component multifactorial. - Presentation Hyperactivity impulsivity and/or inattention before age 12 in 2+ settings for more than 6 months. - Diagnosis Vanderbilt Assessment Scale parent + teacher forms . Rule out sleep disorders anxiety learning disabilities bipolar. - Treatment Stimulants methylphenidate amphetamines + behavioral therapy. Non-stimulants are second-line. IEP for school. - Complications Appetite suppression from stimulants; comorbid OCD; persists into adulthood in 50% of cases. - Lecturer s Point In children sleep deprivation mimics ADHD by causing hyperactivity. For boards best ADHD treatment = medication + behavioral therapy. 7. Vaccine Schedule Ages 6-10 Slides 23-24 - Ages 4-6 last doses DTaP 5th/final before age 7 IPV 4th/final MMR 2nd Varicella 2nd - Ages 7-10 No routine vaccines - Age 11 Next wave begins with Tdap Meningococcal and HPV 8. Common Pathologies Slides 25-32 Strep Pharyngitis - Peak incidence ages 5-9 ; use Centor criteria to risk-stratify - Centor Criteria 1 pt each Age 3-14 tonsillar exudates tender anterior cervical nodes absence of cough fever greater than 38 degrees C - Rapid strep positive = treat; if negative but high suspicion = send throat culture gold standard - Treatment Penicillin or amoxicillin first-line correlate Strep Pharyngitis Group A Streptococcus - Pathophysiology Pharyngeal/tonsillar infection by GAS S. pyogenes . - Presentation Sore throat tonsillar exudates anterior cervical lymphadenopathy fever absence of cough. - Diagnosis Rapid strep test; throat culture if negative with high suspicion. - Treatment Penicillin or amoxicillin first-line. - Complications Rheumatic fever peritonsillar abscess post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis. - Lecturer s Point Absence of cough is a key Centor criterion distinguishing bacterial from viral pharyngitis. Dental Caries & Dentition - Dental caries can progress to gum disease and abscesses - Tetracycline causes permanent tooth staining in children Lecturer Emphasis - First molars/central incisors erupt at 6-7 years ; delayed eruption may indicate systemic/genetic disease Constipation - Very common in school-age children due to stool withholding when using public restrooms - Bristol Stool Scale Types 1-2 = constipation; 3-4 = normal; 6-7 = diarrhea - Treatment Fiber osmotic juices prune/pear/plum Miralax PEG daily; enema for severe impaction - Associated with UTIs -- stool withholding leads to urinary retention Lecturer Emphasis - DDx Always consider appendicitis -- check McBurney s point psoas/obturator signs rebound guarding correlate Functional Constipation in Children - Pathophysiology Stool withholding causes rectal distension and impaired contractile function. - Presentation Infrequent hard stools abdominal pain palpable stool in LLQ possible encopresis. - Diagnosis Clinical; KUB X-ray can confirm stool burden. - Treatment Fiber osmotic juices daily Miralax. Enema for impaction. - Lecturer s Point Constipation is commonly associated with UTIs because children who withhold stool often withhold urine. 9. Scoliosis Screening Slides 33-35 - Adams Forward Bend Test Assess shoulder/scapular symmetry then have patient bend forward -- look for a rib hump on the convex side - Scoliometer Objectively measures angle of trunk rotation - Onset typically at age 11+ but screening starts earlier in clinic 10. Tanner Staging and Precocious Puberty Slides 36-42 Tanner staging is a standardized system for describing pubertal progression. Girls receive two Tanner stages breast + pubic hair separately . Boys are staged by testicular volume and pubic hair. - Tanner Stage 2 = Start of Puberty - Girls breast bud - Boys testes enlarge to 4 mL or greater - Orchidometer String of calibrated beads to measure testicular volume Lecturer Emphasis Precocious Puberty - Girls Secondary sexual characteristics before age 8 before age 6 = always abnormal - Boys Before age 9 - Isolated premature adrenarche pubic hair only common in overweight girls usually benign - Isolated premature thelarche breast only usually benign - Central vs. Peripheral Central = GnRH-dependent excess gonadotropin ; Peripheral = GnRH-independent tumor or autonomous hormone production correlate Precocious Puberty - Pathophysiology Premature HPG axis activation central or autonomous sex steroid production peripheral . - Presentation Secondary sexual characteristics before age 8 girls or 9 boys . Advanced bone age short adult stature. - Diagnosis Bone age LH/FSH GnRH stimulation test. Brain MRI for central; pelvic/adrenal imaging for peripheral. - Treatment Central GnRH agonists leuprolide . Peripheral treat underlying cause. - Lecturer s Point Isolated premature adrenarche in overweight girls is usually benign requiring reassurance and follow-up. mnemonic Tanner Stage 2 = Start of Puberty In girls the breast bud marks Tanner Stage 2. In boys testes enlarging to 4 mL or greater marks Tanner Stage 2. Remember Two to start Four for the floor. analysis content/L84 HighYield Render.pdf ankingResource primarySource BandB chapter Pediatrics > Well-Child Care > School-Age 6-10 years alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Psychiatry > Pathology > Childhood & Early-Onset Disorders > ADHD resource Bootcamp chapter Psychiatry > Developmental Disorders > ADHD resource Physeo chapter Psychiatry > Psychiatry > Childhood & Early Onset Disorders resource SketchyPharm chapter Autonomic Drugs > Sympathetic > Indirect Sympathomimetics resource Pixorize chapter Pharmacology > Sympathomimetics & Sympatholytics > Clonidine and Guanfacine flashcards front What is the AAP-recommended screen time limit for school-age children ages 6-10 ? back 2 hours per day of recreational screen time. tag Concept front At what age does the AAP recommend a random non-fasting lipid screening? back Between ages 9 and 11. tag Clinical front How is pediatric obesity defined? back BMI at or above the 95th percentile for age and sex. Overweight is the 85th to 95th percentile. tag Concept front What is the formula for mid-parental height in girls? back Paternal height in cm - 13 + Maternal height in cm / 2. For boys add 13 instead of subtracting. tag Concept front What diagnostic test helps distinguish constitutional growth delay from pathologic short stature? back A bone age X-ray. In constitutional delay bone age is younger than chronological age indicating the child will eventually catch up. tag Clinical front What do ACEs stand for and what health outcomes are associated with high ACE scores? back Adverse Childhood Experiences. High scores correlate with obesity diabetes cardiovascular disease depression substance abuse and shortened lifespan. tag Concept front What is the PHQ-2 cutoff score that prompts administration of the PHQ-9? back A PHQ-2 score of 3 or more is considered positive and triggers the full PHQ-9 for deeper depression screening. tag Clinical front What objective tool is used to diagnose ADHD and what is required for diagnosis? back The Vanderbilt Assessment Scale with separate forms completed by parent and teacher. Impairment must be present in 2 or more settings. tag Clinical front What is the best treatment for ADHD according to evidence? back Combination of stimulant medication e.g. methylphenidate amphetamines plus behavioral therapy. tag Clinical front In children sleep deprivation can cause what symptom that mimics ADHD? back Hyperactivity. Unlike adults who become sleepy children with sleep deprivation often become irritable and hyperactive. tag Concept front What are the Centor criteria for strep pharyngitis? back Age 3-14 tonsillar exudates/swelling tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy absence of cough and fever greater than 38 degrees C. Each criterion scores 1 point. tag Clinical front What is the Bristol Stool Scale used for? back It is a visual tool to classify stool consistency from Type 1 hard lumps constipation to Type 7 watery diarrhea . Types 3-4 are considered normal. tag Concept front What physical exam maneuver screens for scoliosis? back The Adams Forward Bend Test. The patient bends forward and the examiner looks for a rib hump on the convex side indicating vertebral rotation. tag Clinical front What marks Tanner Stage 2 the onset of puberty in boys vs. girls? back Girls breast bud formation. Boys testicular enlargement to 4 mL or greater volume. tag Concept front At what age is puberty considered precocious in girls vs. boys? back Girls development of secondary sexual characteristics before age 8. Boys before age 9. tag Clinical pearls title Sleep Deprivation Mimics ADHD in Kids content In children sleep disorders and sleep deprivation can cause hyperactivity and behavioral disturbances that mimic ADHD rather than the expected sleepiness seen in adults. Always rule out sleep issues before diagnosing ADHD. title ADHD Best Treatment content For board questions the best treatment for ADHD is stimulant medication PLUS behavioral therapy multimodal approach not medication alone. title Centor Criteria Key Feature content The absence of cough is a key Centor criterion that helps distinguish bacterial strep pharyngitis from a viral upper respiratory infection. title Constipation and UTIs are Linked content In school-age children functional constipation from stool withholding is commonly associated with UTIs because children who withhold stool often withhold urine as well. title ACEs Have Lifelong Health Impact content A higher ACE score has a dose-dependent correlation with obesity diabetes cardiovascular disease depression and substance abuse. ACEs affect all communities regardless of socioeconomic status. title Bone Age in Growth Evaluation content A delayed bone age suggests constitutional growth delay the child is a late bloomer and will catch up . A normal or advanced bone age points toward familial short stature or pathologic causes. title PHQ-2 as a Gateway Screen content Depression screening with the PHQ-2 begins at age 10. A score of 3 or more triggers the full PHQ-9. If the PHQ-9 reveals suicidal ideation urgent intervention is needed. title Tanner 2 = Puberty Onset content In girls breast bud = Tanner Stage 2 start of puberty . In boys testicular volume reaching 4 mL or greater = Tanner Stage 2. questions id 1 question A 7-year-old boy is brought to his pediatrician for a well-child visit. His parents are concerned because he is the shortest in his class. Both parents are of average height. His growth velocity has been normal but consistently tracking along the 5th percentile. A bone age X-ray shows a skeletal maturity of a 5-year-old. All other labs are within normal limits. What is the most likely diagnosis? options Familial short stature Constitutional growth delay Growth hormone deficiency Celiac disease Hypothyroidism correctAnswer 1 rationale The key finding is a delayed bone age skeletal maturity of a 5-year-old in a 7-year-old with normal growth velocity and normal labs. This is the hallmark of constitutional growth delay where the child is a late bloomer and will eventually catch up to their mid-parental height once their delayed growth spurt occurs. Familial short stature would show a normal bone age. GH deficiency would show decreased growth velocity. id 2 question A parent brings their 8-year-old daughter to the clinic. The teacher has been complaining that the child cannot focus in class is easily distracted and frequently does not complete assignments. At home the parent reports the child has difficulty organizing tasks. The parent fills out a Vanderbilt form. Which additional step is MOST important before confirming a diagnosis of ADHD? options Order a brain MRI Start a trial of methylphenidate Have the teacher complete a separate Vanderbilt assessment form Order genetic testing for ADHD Perform a urine drug screen correctAnswer 2 rationale ADHD diagnosis requires impairment in TWO or more settings documented independently. The Vanderbilt Assessment Scale must be completed by both the parent AND the teacher. A single informant is insufficient for diagnosis. Brain MRI and genetic testing are not part of ADHD workup. A medication trial should not precede formal diagnosis. id 3 question A 9-year-old girl presents with sore throat fever of 38.5 degrees C and bilateral tonsillar exudates. She has tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy and denies any cough or rhinorrhea. Her rapid strep test returns positive. What is the most appropriate next step in management? options Provide supportive care only and follow up in 1 week Send a throat culture before initiating any treatment Prescribe amoxicillin Prescribe azithromycin Start empiric oseltamivir correctAnswer 2 rationale With a positive rapid strep antigen test the diagnosis of GAS pharyngitis is confirmed and treatment should begin immediately. Amoxicillin or penicillin is first-line therapy. A throat culture is only needed if the rapid test is negative but clinical suspicion remains high. Azithromycin is reserved for penicillin-allergic patients. Supportive care alone is inappropriate for confirmed bacterial strep. id 4 question A mother brings her 6-year-old daughter to the pediatrician because she noticed the child has been developing breast tissue. On exam you note a breast bud bilaterally but no pubic hair axillary hair body odor or acne. What is the most appropriate next step? options Reassure the mother and schedule a follow-up visit in 6 months Immediately refer to pediatric endocrinology for GnRH agonist therapy Obtain a bone age X-ray and carefully evaluate with close follow-up Order a brain MRI to rule out a hypothalamic hamartoma Begin leuprolide therapy correctAnswer 2 rationale In a girl between ages 6-8 with isolated breast development premature thelarche and no other signs of puberty the recommended approach is a careful evaluation including a bone age X-ray thorough history and physical and close follow-up. Isolated premature thelarche is often benign. Immediate GnRH agonist therapy or brain MRI would be premature without further evaluation. However since she is at the borderline age 6 more evaluation is warranted rather than simple reassurance. id 5 question An 8-year-old boy is brought to clinic for recurrent abdominal pain. His mother reports he only has a bowel movement every 3-4 days and the stools are hard and pellet-like Bristol Type 1 . He has also had two urinary tract infections in the past year. On exam you palpate a firm mass in the left lower quadrant. What is the most likely unifying diagnosis? options Irritable bowel syndrome Appendicitis Functional constipation with secondary urinary retention Hirschsprung disease Inflammatory bowel disease correctAnswer 2 rationale The combination of infrequent hard stools Bristol Type 1 a palpable fecal mass in the LLQ and recurrent UTIs in a school-age child is classic for functional constipation with secondary urinary retention. Children who withhold stool at school often withhold urine as well leading to UTIs. Hirschsprung disease typically presents in neonates/infants. Appendicitis presents acutely with RLQ pain. IBD would present with diarrhea weight loss and bloody stools. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l86", "title": "Lecture #86: Pharmacology: Antidepressants and Mood Stabilizers", "module": "Pharmacology", "content": "id l86 title Lecture 86 Pharmacology Antidepressants and Mood Stabilizers lecturer M. Pino Ph.D. module Pharmacology session Lecture 86 prev l84 next l87 pdf content/L86 HighYield Render.pdf summary Lecture 86 Pharmacology Antidepressants and Mood Stabilizers Lecturer M. Pino Ph.D. Session Lecture 86 The Amine Hypothesis of Depression Depression is fundamentally driven by a deficiency or dysregulation of monoamine neurotransmitters—specifically serotonin norepinephrine and dopamine. This is classically demonstrated by reserpine an older antihypertensive drug that depleted these neurotransmitters and consequently induced severe depression in patients. Similarly lipophilic beta-blockers and presynaptic alpha-2 agonists like clonidine can lower sympathetic tone and induce depressive symptoms. Conversely any increase in cortisol such as steroid use or deficiency in estrogen/testosterone can precipitate depression as seen in postpartum and menopausal states. Atrophy of the hippocampus and cortex as seen in neurodegenerative diseases like Alzheimer s or Parkinson s also significantly increases the risk of depression. Thus all modern antidepressant therapies focus on enhancing monoaminergic transmission. Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors MAOIs MAOIs like phenelzine block the MAO-A enzyme preventing the breakdown of serotonin norepinephrine and dopamine. Although highly effective for refractory depression they are rarely used today due to severe and potentially fatal drug-food interactions. When patients consume tyramine-rich foods aged cheeses red wine the inhibited MAO-A cannot break tyramine down. This tyramine displaces norepinephrine into the circulation leading to a massive sympathetic surge and a life-threatening hypertensive crisis. correlate Hypertensive Crisis - Pathophysiology Accumulation of tyramine due to MAO-A inhibition displacing norepinephrine into the systemic circulation. - Presentation Severe headache stiff neck photosensitivity tachycardia nausea vomiting and dangerously high blood pressure >200/100 mmHg . - Diagnosis Clinical presentation in a patient taking an MAOI who consumed tyramine-rich foods cheese wine avocado soy sauce or sympathomimetics amphetamines phenylephrine . - Treatment Phentolamine a non-selective alpha-blocker to rapidly reverse the alpha-1 mediated vasoconstriction. - Lecturer s Point You MUST utilize a washout period when switching a patient from an SSRI to an MAOI to avoid serotonin syndrome. Tricyclic Antidepressants TCAs Tricyclic antidepressants imipramine desipramine clomipramine amitriptyline amoxapine exert their antidepressant effect by inhibiting the reuptake of both serotonin and norepinephrine. However they are infamous for their broad non-specific receptor blockade which includes alpha-1 histamine H1 and muscarinic receptors. This leads to marked orthostatic hypotension sedation weight gain and anticholinergic side effects dry mouth tachycardia constipation urinary retention . Overdose is exceptionally dangerous due to sodium channel blockade in the heart. Specific Uses Lecturer Emphasis Imipramine Historically used for childhood enuresis bedwetting due to its strong muscarinic blockade though desmopressin is preferred today. Clomipramine Traditionally the best TCA for Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder OCD . Amitriptyline Used for neuropathic pain and migraine prophylaxis. Amoxapine Blocks D2 receptors; can treat depression with psychotic features but carries a risk of Parkinson-like extrapyramidal symptoms. correlate TCA Overdose - Pathophysiology Severe blockade of cardiac fast sodium channels and potassium channels leading to prolonged depolarization and repolarization. - Presentation The classic 3 Cs Coma Convulsions and Cardiotoxicity arrhythmias widened QRS and prolonged QT intervals . - Treatment Sodium bicarbonate NaHCO3 is used to overcome the sodium channel blockade and stabilize the myocardium. Supportive care for seizures. Selective Serotonin Reuptake Inhibitors SSRIs SSRIs fluoxetine sertraline citalopram escitalopram paroxetine fluvoxamine are the first-line pharmacologic treatment for major depressive disorder anxiety disorders OCD PTSD and eating disorders. They selectively inhibit the serotonin transporter enhancing serotonergic tone without the autonomic instability cardiotoxicity or seizure risks of TCAs. However they carry a black box warning for increased suicidality in adolescents during the initial weeks of treatment primarily because physical energy often returns before mood fully lifts. Key Adverse Effects GI & Neurologic Nausea/vomiting due to 5-HT3 activation and headaches due to cerebral vasodilation. Sexual Dysfunction Extremely common; often due to direct serotonergic inhibition of nitric oxide synthase and suppression of dopamine arousal pathways. Bleeding Risk Inhibition of serotonin uptake into platelets prevents proper aggregation leading to prolonged bleeding times especially if combined with NSAIDs. Weight Gain Often due to 5-HT2C receptor modulation. Discontinuation Syndrome Abruptly stopping short half-life SSRIs causes anxiety dizziness and brain zaps . Fluoxetine has the lowest risk due to its very long half-life. Cardiac Lecturer Emphasis Citalopram can prolong the QT interval; maximum dose in the elderly >65 is strictly 20 mg. highyield Serotonin Syndrome vs. Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome NMS - Serotonin Syndrome Caused by combining serotonergic drugs SSRIs MAOIs TCAs dextromethorphan meperidine . Rapid onset. Features myoclonus hyperreflexia tremors hyperthermia and autonomic instability. Treated with Cyproheptadine a 5-HT2 receptor antagonist and supportive care. - NMS Caused by dopamine blockade antipsychotics . Gradual onset. Classic feature is lead-pipe rigidity. Serotonin-Norepinephrine Reuptake Inhibitors SNRIs SNRIs venlafaxine desvenlafaxine duloxetine inhibit the reuptake of both serotonin and norepinephrine. Unlike TCAs they do not block alpha-1 histamine or muscarinic receptors. While they are highly effective for depression their dual mechanism makes them exceptionally useful for treating co-morbid neuropathic pain such as diabetic neuropathy or fibromyalgia. Atypical Antidepressants This broad category encompasses agents that work through novel non-SSRI mechanisms. They are frequently utilized to circumvent the specific side effects of SSRIs such as sexual dysfunction or weight gain or to target treatment-resistant depression. | Drug | Mechanism | Key Feature | Treatment/Notes | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | Bupropion | NE and DA reuptake inhibitor spares serotonin | Zero sexual dysfunction. Also an nAChR antagonist. | Used for smoking cessation. Lowers seizure threshold; Absolutely contraindicated in Bulimia or seizure disorders. | | Mirtazapine | Alpha-2 antagonist; 5-HT2/5-HT3 antagonist; H1 antagonist | Severe sedation and massive weight gain. | Good for depressed patients with insomnia and anorexia. Less sexual dysfunction. | | Trazodone | SARI Serotonin Antagonist and Reuptake Inhibitor + Alpha-1 & H1 block | Highly sedating used for sleep . Causes Priapism due to Alpha-1 block . | Priapism is a medical emergency requiring ER visit. | | Vilazodone | 5-HT reuptake inhibitor + partial 5-HT1A agonist | Combines SSRI action with buspirone-like anxiolytic action. | Lower risk of weight gain and sexual dysfunction. | | Brexanolone | Positive allosteric modulator of GABA-A | Rapid onset acts in hours . IV administration only. | Specifically targets Postpartum Depression. Requires REMS program monitoring. | | Esketamine | Non-competitive NMDA receptor antagonist blocks glutamate | Rapid massive surge in BDNF and synaptogenesis. | Used for Treatment-Resistant Depression TRD . Must be given intranasally in a clinic due to dissociation and abuse potential. | Bipolar Disorder and Mood Stabilizers Bipolar disorder is characterized by cyclical shifts between depressive lows and manic highs Bipolar I involves full mania; Bipolar II involves hypomania . The goal of mood stabilization is to dampen the excessive release of excitatory neurotransmitters dopamine/glutamate without plunging the patient into depression. Medications used include Lithium various anti-epileptics Valproate Carbamazepine Lamotrigine and atypical antipsychotics. Lithium Lithium is a classic mood stabilizer that acts intracellularly by inhibiting the IP3/DAG Gq second messenger pathway and protein kinase C directly dampening hyperactive neurotransmission. It has an exceptionally narrow therapeutic index toxic at just slightly above therapeutic levels and requires diligent serum monitoring. Drug Interactions Lithium is cleared by the kidneys similarly to sodium. Any drug that alters GFR or proximal sodium reabsorption will cause fatal lithium toxicity. Thiazide Diuretics Deplete sodium causing a massive compensatory reabsorption of sodium and lithium in the proximal tubule. NSAIDs Block afferent arteriole prostaglandins dropping GFR. ACE Inhibitors Block efferent arteriole angiotensin II dropping GFR. Adverse Effects Tremors Nephrogenic Diabetes Insipidus Lithium blocks ADH action in the collecting duct causing immense polyuria and polydipsia. Treated with Amiloride . Hypothyroidism Lithium blocks the proteolysis and release of thyroid hormone from the gland. Patients often require levothyroxine supplementation. Teratogenicity Causes Ebstein anomaly downward displacement of the tricuspid valve if taken during pregnancy. correlate Valproate Valproic Acid - Pathophysiology Broad-spectrum anti-epileptic that blocks Na+ and T-type Ca2+ channels inhibits NMDA and increases GABA synthesis/decreases GABA degradation. - Presentation/Use Highly effective alternative mood stabilizer for patients who cannot tolerate Lithium. - Adverse Effects Weight gain tremor and fatal Hepatotoxicity inhibits beta-oxidation of fatty acids causing mitochondrial failure . Also a potent CYP2D6/CYP3A4 inhibitor. - Teratogenicity Strongly associated with Neural Tube Defects spina bifida due to interference with folate metabolism. correlate Carbamazepine - Pathophysiology Blocks fast sodium channels. Also inhibits NE reuptake. - Presentation/Use Used for Bipolar disorder Trigeminal Neuralgia and tonic-clonic seizures. - Adverse Effects SIADH Syndrome of Inappropriate ADH causing hyponatremia/water intoxication. Aplastic anemia bone marrow suppression . - Lecturer s Point Can trigger a fatal Stevens-Johnson Syndrome SJS particularly in patients of East Asian descent carrying the HLA-B 1502 allele. Potent CYP450 inducer. Lamotrigine and Atypical Antipsychotics Lamotrigine Blocks sodium channels and decreases glutamate. Highly effective for the depressive phase of bipolar. Primary risk is Stevens-Johnson Syndrome SJS necessitating an extremely slow upward titration. Atypical Antipsychotics e.g. Aripiprazole Olanzapine Quetiapine These act by heavily blocking 5-HT2A and dampening D2 receptors in the mesolimbic pathway to blunt acute mania. Aripiprazole is unique as a partial D2 agonist—lowering dopamine where it is too high mesolimbic but boosting it where it is too low mesocortical making it highly versatile for mood. highyield Cytochrome P450 Interactions in Psychiatry Be highly vigilant of CYP interactions - Inhibitors Fluoxetine Paroxetine Bupropion Valproate. They will raise the levels of co-administered drugs. - Inducers Carbamazepine. It will lower the levels of co-administered drugs e.g. oral contraceptives . mnemonic Lithium Side Effects LITHIUM - L Low Thyroid Hypothyroidism - I Insipidus Nephrogenic DI - T Tremor - H Heart Ebstein Anomaly - I Increased White Count Leukocytosis - U Unwanted weight gain - M Mothers shouldn t take it Teratogenic Pharmacology of Antidepressants and Mood Stabilizers Antidepressants MAOIs Mechanism MAO-A inhibition Adverse Effects Tyramine hypertensive crisis Interactions Washout required for SSRIs TCAs Examples Imipramine Amitriptyline Mechanism Block NE/5-HT reuptake plus H1/Alpha-1/M1 Toxicity Coma Convulsions Cardiotoxicity 3 Cs Treatment Sodium Bicarbonate SSRIs Examples Fluoxetine Citalopram Mechanism 5-HT transporter inhibition Side Effects Sexual dysfunction weight gain bleeding risk Serotonin Syndrome Tremor myoclonus autonomic instability; treated with Cyproheptadine SNRIs Examples Venlafaxine Duloxetine Use Depression with neuropathic pain Atypicals Bupropion Spares 5-HT contraindicated in bulimia Mirtazapine Alpha-2 block causes weight gain/sedation Trazodone SARI causes priapism Esketamine NMDA antagonist rapidly boosts BDNF Mood Stabilizers Lithium Mechanism Blocks IP3/DAG Gq Toxicity Risk ACEi NSAIDs Thiazides Side Effects Tremor Hypothyroidism Nephrogenic DI Ebstein anomaly Valproate Mechanism Blocks Na+/Ca2+ channels boosts GABA Side Effects Hepatotoxicity Neural tube defects Weight gain Interactions CYP inhibitor Carbamazepine Mechanism Blocks Na+ channels Fast Side Effects SIADH Aplastic Anemia SJS HLA-B 1502 Interactions CYP inducer Lamotrigine Mechanism Blocks Na+ channels limits glutamate Side Effects Stevens-Johnson Syndrome SJS analysis content/L86 HighYield Render Analysis.md ankingResource primarySource BandB chapter Psychiatry > Pharmacology > Antidepressants alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Psychiatry > Pharmacology resource Pathoma chapter N/A resource SketchyPharm chapter Neuro/Psych > Antidepressants flashcards front What specific dietary compound must patients taking MAOIs avoid to prevent a hypertensive crisis? back Tyramine found in aged cheese red wine . tag Concept front What is the antidote used to treat the massive sympathetic surge and hypertensive crisis caused by a tyramine reaction in patients on MAOIs? back Phentolamine a non-selective alpha-blocker . tag Clinical front What are the classic 3 Cs of Tricyclic Antidepressant TCA overdose? back Coma Convulsions and Cardiotoxicity arrhythmias . tag Clinical front What is the specific life-saving treatment for cardiotoxicity caused by a TCA overdose? back Sodium Bicarbonate NaHCO3 to overcome sodium channel blockade. tag Clinical front Which antidepressant class carries a black box warning for increased suicidality in adolescents during the initial weeks of treatment? back SSRIs. tag Clinical front Which atypical antidepressant lowers the seizure threshold and is strictly contraindicated in patients with bulimia nervosa? back Bupropion. tag Clinical front Which atypical antidepressant known for being highly sedating carries a unique risk of causing priapism? back Trazodone. tag Clinical front What are three classes of medications that can crucially reduce GFR and lead to fatal Lithium toxicity? back Thiazide Diuretics NSAIDs and ACE Inhibitors. tag Clinical front What specific congenital heart defect is associated with maternal use of Lithium during pregnancy? back Ebstein anomaly downward displacement of the tricuspid valve . tag Clinical front Which mood stabilizer is a prominent CYP inducer and carries a significant risk of Stevens-Johnson syndrome especially in patients with an HLA-B 1502 allele? back Carbamazepine. tag Clinical pearls title Serotonin Syndrome Treatment content If a patient taking multiple serotonergic drugs develops hyperthermia myoclonus and autonomic instability the diagnosis is Serotonin Syndrome. Treat with supportive care and Cyproheptadine a 5-HT2 receptor antagonist. title Lithium-Induced Nephrogenic DI content Lithium can block the action of ADH on the collecting ducts leading to Nephrogenic Diabetes Insipidus polyuria and polydipsia . The treatment of choice for lithium-induced DI is the potassium-sparing diuretic Amiloride which blocks the sodium channels through which lithium enters the principal cells. questions id 1 question A 24-year-old female with major depressive disorder presents to the emergency department complaining of an excruciating headache and neck stiffness. Her blood pressure is 220/120 mmHg and her heart rate is 110 bpm. She states she was at a wine and cheese tasting party a few hours ago. Which of the following medications is she most likely taking for her depression? options Fluoxetine Phenelzine Bupropion Amitriptyline Lithium correctAnswer 1 rationale The patient is experiencing a hypertensive crisis provoked by the ingestion of tyramine-rich foods wine cheese . Phenelzine is an MAOI. By blocking MAO-A tyramine cannot be degraded and displaces norepinephrine causing a massive sympathetic surge. id 2 question A 45-year-old male with bipolar disorder is currently stable on a mood stabilizer. He presents to the clinic complaining of increased fatigue feeling cold all the time weight gain and coarse hair. His heart rate is 55 bpm. He also mentions he has to urinate frequently. Given these adverse effects which of the following drugs is he most likely taking? options Valproic Acid Carbamazepine Lithium Lamotrigine Aripiprazole correctAnswer 2 rationale The patient is describing symptoms of hypothyroidism fatigue cold intolerance bradycardia and polyuria likely nephrogenic diabetes insipidus . Both are classic adverse effects of Lithium therapy. Lithium inhibits the proteolysis necessary to release thyroid hormone and blocks ADH action in the kidneys. id 3 question A 19-year-old college student with bulimia nervosa presents to the clinic seeking treatment for depression and smoking cessation. Which of the following atypical antidepressants must be absolutely avoided in this patient? options Mirtazapine Trazodone Vilazodone Bupropion Desvenlafaxine correctAnswer 3 rationale Bupropion is an atypical antidepressant utilized for depression and smoking cessation. However it lowers the seizure threshold. In patients with eating disorders like bulimia electrolyte imbalances such as hypokalemia from vomiting already increase the risk of seizures making bupropion absolutely contraindicated. id 4 question A patient is brought to the ED by EMS after a suicide attempt via intentional overdose. He is unconscious. EKG reveals a notably widened QRS complex and prolonged QT interval. Shortly after arrival the patient begins actively seizing. Which of the following is the most appropriate first-line pharmacologic intervention to address his cardiac toxicity? options Cyproheptadine Phentolamine Sodium Bicarbonate Diazepam Flumazenil correctAnswer 2 rationale The patient presents with the 3 Cs of a Tricyclic Antidepressant TCA overdose Coma Convulsions and Cardiotoxicity widened QRS due to sodium channel blockade . The specific antidote to neutralize the sodium channel blockade and stabilize the myocardium is Sodium Bicarbonate. id 5 question A patient with refractory major depressive disorder is scheduled to receive a novel treatment at a specialized infusion clinic. The treating physician warns that the patient must be monitored for at least two hours post-administration due to the profound risk of dissociation sedation and perceptual disturbances. The medication being administered most likely exerts its primary effect through which mechanism? options Inhibition of the serotonin transporter Positive allosteric modulation of GABA-A receptors Non-competitive antagonism of the NMDA receptor Inhibition of both dopamine and norepinephrine reuptake Alpha-2 adrenergic antagonism correctAnswer 2 rationale The clinical scenario describes the administration of Esketamine which is used for treatment-resistant depression and requires supervised in-clinic dosing due to its dramatic dissociative side effects. Esketamine works as a non-competitive antagonist of the NMDA glutamate receptor initiating a rapid surge in BDNF and synaptogenesis. anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l87", "title": "Lecture #87: Antipsychotics", "module": "Pharmacology", "content": "id l87 title Lecture 87 Antipsychotics lecturer M. Pino Ph.D. module Pharmacology pdf content/L87 HighYield Render.pdf summary Lecture 87 Antipsychotics\\n\\n Introduction to Schizophrenia\\nSchizophrenia usually presents in early adulthood or late adolescence. It is characterized by three main symptom domains positive symptoms negative symptoms and cognitive symptoms. Positive symptoms refer to an exaggeration or distortion of normal function including delusions hallucinations most commonly auditory disorganized speech and disorganized or catatonic behavior. Negative symptoms represent a loss or diminution of normal function such as anhedonia inability to experience pleasure alogia poverty of speech flat affect and avolition lack of motivation . Cognitive symptoms which are often the most debilitating and hardest to treat involve deficits in attention memory and executive function.\\n\\n Dopamine Pathways & The Dopamine Hypothesis\\nThe original dopamine hypothesis posits that schizophrenia is caused by an overactivity of dopaminergic pathways in the brain. There are four key dopaminergic tracts that must be understood to grasp both the pathology of the disease and the effects/side effects of antipsychotic drugs \\n\\n1. Mesolimbic Pathway Projects from the ventral tegmental area VTA to the nucleus accumbens. Hyperactivity here is thought to cause the positive symptoms of schizophrenia.\\n2. Mesocortical Pathway Projects from the VTA to the prefrontal cortex. Hypoactivity here is implicated in the negative and cognitive symptoms. Note the paradox giving a D2 blocker might worsen negative symptoms by further reducing dopamine activity here .\\n3. Nigrostriatal Pathway Projects from the substantia nigra to the striatum. Involved in motor control. Blockade of D2 receptors here by antipsychotics leads to Extrapyramidal Symptoms EPS .\\n4. Tuberoinfundibular Pathway Projects from the hypothalamus to the anterior pituitary. Dopamine normally inhibits prolactin release. D2 blockade here causes hyperprolactinemia galactorrhea gynecomastia amenorrhea sexual dysfunction .\\n\\n First-Generation Antipsychotics Typical \\nFirst-generation or typical antipsychotics are primarily D2 receptor antagonists. Their efficacy in treating positive symptoms correlates strongly with their affinity for D2 receptors. However blocking D2 receptors globally causes significant on-target side effects EPS and hyperprolactinemia . They are broadly divided into high potency and low potency agents.\\n\\n High Potency e.g. Haloperidol Fluphenazine Trifluoperazine These have high affinity for D2 receptors but lower affinity for muscarinic histaminic H1 and alpha-1 receptors. Consequently they are less sedating and cause less hypotension/anticholinergic effects but they have a much higher risk of causing EPS.\\n Low Potency e.g. Chlorpromazine Thioridazine These have lower affinity for D2 meaning higher doses are needed. However they strongly block muscarinic H1 and alpha-1 receptors leading to high sedation orthostatic hypotension and severe anticholinergic effects dry mouth blurred vision constipation urinary retention . They have a relatively lower risk of EPS.\\n\\n Adverse Effects of First-Generation Antipsychotics\\nThe adverse effect profile is extensive and requires careful management.\\n\\n Extrapyramidal Symptoms EPS Occur due to D2 blockade in the nigrostriatal tract.\\n Acute Dystonia Hours to days. Sustained painful muscle contractions e.g. torticollis oculogyric crisis . Treatment Anticholinergics like Benztropine or Diphenhydramine.\\n Akathisia Days to weeks. Subjective feeling of inner restlessness; inability to sit still. Treatment Beta-blockers e.g. Propranolol are first-line. Benzodiazepines may also help.\\n Parkinsonism Weeks to months. Bradykinesia rigidity resting tremor masked facies. Treatment Anticholinergics Benztropine or Amantadine. Avoid levodopa exacerbates psychosis .\\n Tardive Dyskinesia TD Months to years. Irreversible involuntary repetitive movements lip smacking choreoathetoid movements of limbs . Thought to be due to D2 receptor upregulation/supersensitivity from chronic blockade. Prevention is key; switch to an atypical like Clozapine if it develops. VMAT2 inhibitors Valbenazine Deutetrabenazine can be used to treat symptoms.\\n\\n Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome NMS A life-threatening emergency. Characterized by severe lead-pipe muscle rigidity hyperthermia autonomic instability tachycardia labile BP and altered mental status. Labs may show elevated CK and leukocytosis. Treatment Immediate discontinuation of the offending agent supportive care cooling hydration and administration of Dantrolene ryanodine receptor antagonist or Bromocriptine dopamine agonist .\\n\\n Other Adverse Effects \\n Hyperprolactinemia Due to D2 block in the tuberoinfundibular tract.\\n QT Prolongation Can lead to Torsades de Pointes. Notable with Thioridazine Haloperidol and Ziprasidone.\\n Ophthalmologic Chlorpromazine causes Corneal deposits. Thioridazine causes reTinal deposits.\\n Anticholinergic/Alpha-1/H1 Sedation weight gain hypotension dry mouth etc. More common with low potency .\\n\\n Second-Generation Antipsychotics Atypical \\nAtypical antipsychotics were developed to address the limitations of typicals namely EPS and lack of efficacy for negative symptoms . The working mechanism is the Serotonin Hypothesis these drugs are distinct in that they have a higher affinity for blocking Serotonin 5-HT2A receptors than Dopamine D2 receptors.\\n\\nBlocking 5-HT2A on dopaminergic neurons in the nigrostriatal and mesocortical tracts actually increases dopamine release locally. This localized dopamine increase competes with the D2 blockade significantly reducing the risk of EPS in the striatum and potentially improving negative/cognitive symptoms in the cortex. However they bring a new major liability Metabolic Syndrome .\\n\\nAtypicals are associated with significant weight gain dyslipidemia and new-onset type 2 diabetes hyperglycemia . This is partly mediated by 5-HT2C and H1 blockade. Monitoring weight blood pressure fasting lipids and fasting glucose is critical.\\n\\n Specific Second-Generation Agents\\nKey agents in this class have unique profiles \\n Clozapine The first atypical. Unmatched efficacy for treatment-resistant schizophrenia and reducing suicidality. However it requires strict REMS monitoring due to the risk of life-threatening agranulocytosis requires frequent CBC checks especially in the first 6 months . Also causes seizures dose-dependent extreme weight gain high sedation and peculiar nighttime sialorrhea drooling due to complex muscarinic interactions.\\n Olanzapine Similar to Clozapine but without agranulocytosis. It is the worst offender for significant weight gain and metabolic syndrome.\\n Risperidone and its active metabolite Paliperidone Among the atypicals it binds D2 the strongest. Consequently it has the highest tendency among atypicals to cause hyperprolactinemia and EPS especially at higher doses . Paliperidone is notable for its long-acting injectable formulations monthly to every 6 months improving compliance.\\n Quetiapine Known for very strong H1 blockade making it highly sedating. It has very weak transient binding to D2 meaning it has one of the lowest risks for EPS often a choice for patients with Parkinson s disease needing an antipsychotic .\\n Ziprasidone Notable for being relatively weight-neutral but carries a higher risk of QT prolongation and requires ingestion with a 500-calorie meal for absorption.\\n Aripiprazole Unique mechanism—it is a partial agonist at D2 and 5-HT1A and an antagonist at 5-HT2A. By partially agonizing D2 it reduces dopamine signaling where it s too high mesolimbic but maintains some tone where it s too low. It has a favorable metabolic profile but can cause akathisia.\\n Pimavanserin An inverse agonist/antagonist exclusively at 5-HT2A receptors with zero affinity for dopamine muscarinic H1 or alpha receptors. Specifically approved for psychosis in Parkinson s disease as it won t worsen motor symptoms.\\n Lumateperone Modulates glutamate serotonin and dopamine. High affinity for 5-HT2A and postsynaptic D2 antagonism with presynaptic D2 partial agonism.\\n Xanomeline-Trospium The newest class entry. Targets muscarinic receptors M1/M4 agonist rather than D2/5-HT2A directly to rebalance dopamine/glutamate. pearls title First-generation vs Second-generation content First-generation antipsychotics primarily block D2 receptors leading to higher EPS risk; efficacy correlates with D2 affinity. Second-generation atypical antipsychotics block 5-HT2A > D2 reducing EPS risk but increasing the risk of metabolic syndrome weight gain dyslipidemia diabetes . title EPS Timeline content Acute dystonia hours/days Akathisia days/weeks Parkinsonism weeks/months Tardive Dyskinesia months/years . title Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome content Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome NMS presents with fever lead-pipe rigidity altered mental status and autonomic instability. Treat by stopping the drug cooling and giving Dantrolene or Bromocriptine. title Clozapine Profile content Clozapine is for treatment-resistant schizophrenia and requires routine CBC monitoring due to the risk of agranulocytosis. It also severely impacts weight. title Atypical Metabolic Profiles content Olanzapine causes the most weight gain among atypicals; Ziprasidone and Aripiprazole are more weight-neutral. title Risperidone Profile content Risperidone is the atypical most likely to cause hyperprolactinemia and EPS at higher doses . questions question A patient started on Haloperidol two days ago comes to the ER with a severely stiff neck facing upward and is unable to look down. What is the most appropriate treatment? options Propranolol Dantrolene Benztropine Levodopa Diazepam correctAnswer Benztropine explanation The patient is experiencing acute dystonia spasm of neck/eye muscles due to acute D2 blockade. The treatment is an anticholinergic agent like Benztropine or Diphenhydramine. type multiple choice question Which of the following adverse effects is the primary reason why Clozapine is reserved for treatment-resistant schizophrenia? options Tardive dyskinesia Agranulocytosis Neuroleptic malignant syndrome Prolonged QT interval Severe hyperprolactinemia correctAnswer Agranulocytosis explanation Clozapine carries a significant risk of agranulocytosis a dangerous drop in white blood cells . Patients must have regular CBC monitoring. It has a very low risk of EPS and tardive dyskinesia. type multiple choice question A patient on a high-dose typical antipsychotic develops severe muscle rigidity a temperature of 104°F tachycardia and altered mental status. Which of the following mechanisms describes the antidote for this condition? options Dopamine receptor antagonism Muscarinic receptor antagonism Ryanodine receptor antagonism Beta-adrenergic receptor antagonism Serotonin receptor antagonism correctAnswer Ryanodine receptor antagonism explanation The patient has Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome NMS . Dantrolene is used for NMS and it works by antagonizing the ryanodine receptor in skeletal muscle preventing the release of calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum and thus stopping muscle contraction/rigidity. Bromocriptine a dopamine agonist is also a treatment option. type multiple choice question Which of the following antipsychotics acts as a partial agonist at the D2 receptor? options Quetiapine Haloperidol Aripiprazole Olanzapine Risperidone correctAnswer Aripiprazole explanation Aripiprazole acts as a partial agonist at D2 and 5-HT1A receptors and an antagonist at 5-HT2A receptors giving it a unique profile and a very low risk of metabolic syndrome. type multiple choice question A 45-year-old male with schizophrenia is switched from haloperidol to an atypical antipsychotic to better manage his negative symptoms. Six months later his routine labs show significantly elevated fasting glucose and triglycerides and he has gained 25 lbs. Which drug was most likely started? options Ziprasidone Olanzapine Aripiprazole Lurasidone Fluphenazine correctAnswer Olanzapine explanation Olanzapine along with Clozapine carries the highest risk of metabolic syndrome among the second-generation antipsychotics leading to severe weight gain dyslipidemia and hyperglycemia. Ziprasidone and Aripiprazole are generally weight-neutral. type multiple choice ankingResource primarySource In-house lecture is best chapter In-house lecture is best alternatives flashcards front What do positive symptoms of schizophrenia refer to and what pathway is involved? back Exaggeration or distortion of normal function delusions hallucinations ; Mesolimbic pathway. tag Concept front What do negative symptoms of schizophrenia refer to and what pathway is involved? back Loss of normal function anhedonia flat affect ; Mesocortical pathway. tag Concept front Which dopamine pathway is involved in the Extrapyramidal Symptoms EPS caused by typical antipsychotics? back Nigrostriatal pathway. tag Clinical front Which dopamine pathway is responsible for the hyperprolactinemia side effect of antipsychotics? back Tuberoinfundibular pathway. tag Clinical front What is the primary mechanism of action of First-Generation Typical Antipsychotics? back Primary D2 receptor antagonists. tag Pharmacology front What describes the presentation and treatment of Acute Dystonia? back Hours to days onset; sustained muscle contractions; treated with anticholinergics like Benztropine or Diphenhydramine. tag Clinical front What characterizes Tardive Dyskinesia and what is key to its management? back Months to years onset; irreversible involuntary movements; prevention by switching to an atypical antipsychotic is key. tag Clinical front What are the core features and treatment of Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome NMS ? back Severe lead-pipe rigidity hyperthermia autonomic instability; treated by stopping the agent using Dantrolene or Bromocriptine. tag Clinical front What is the unique mechanism of action defining Second-Generation Atypical Antipsychotics? back Higher affinity for blocking Serotonin 5-HT2A receptors than Dopamine D2 receptors. tag Pharmacology front Which atypical antipsychotic carries the highest risk of life-threatening agranulocytosis? back Clozapine. tag Clinical anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l88", "title": "Lecture #88: Basic Theoretical Principles of Psychotherapy", "module": "Psychiatry", "content": "id l88 title Lecture 88 Basic Theoretical Principles of Psychotherapy lecturer A. Raj Psy.D. module Psychiatry pdf content/L88 HighYield Render.pdf summary Lecture 88 Basic Theoretical Principles of Psychotherapy Lecturer A. Raj Psy.D. Session Lecture 88 This lecture delves into the Basic Theoretical Principles of Psychotherapy emphasizing the critical role of understanding human behavior change. It highlights the vast array of psychotherapeutic approaches noting that there are over 400 distinct orientations worldwide. However the core focus is placed on the most evidence-based and commonly utilized modalities in clinical practice. The Dodo Bird Effect is a foundational concept discussed suggesting that despite the myriad of psychotherapeutic orientations they are generally equivalent in their overall efficacy. This is attributed to Common Factors —shared elements across different therapies that contribute significantly to positive outcomes. The most potent among these is the Therapeutic Alliance accounting for ~30% of outcomes which encompasses the bond between therapist and patient their agreement on therapeutic goals and the tasks required to achieve them. Other conceptual factors include extratherapeutic change client variables and expectancy placebo effect . The lecture systematically categorizes major therapeutic orientations 1. Psychoanalytic and Psychodynamic Therapies Rooted in Freud s theories these therapies are insight-oriented focusing on how unconscious processes and early childhood experiences shape current behavior. The goal is to bring the unconscious into consciousness to resolve internal conflicts. - Key Concepts Defense mechanisms e.g. projection denial transference patient projecting feelings onto the therapist and countertransference therapist projecting feelings onto the patient . - Core Techniques Free association dream analysis and uncovering resistance. While traditional psychoanalysis is long-term and intensive modern psychodynamic therapy is often briefer and more focused utilized for conditions like personality disorders anxiety and depression. 2. Behavioral Therapies In stark contrast to exploring the unconscious behavioral therapies focus strictly on observable behaviors and how they are learned. The fundamental premise is that all behavior is learned and can therefore be unlearned or modified through conditioning. - Classical Conditioning Pavlov Learning through association e.g. pairing a neutral stimulus with an unconditioned stimulus to elicit a conditioned response . - Operant Conditioning Skinner Learning through consequences. - Reinforcement Increases a behavior Positive adding a reward; Negative removing an aversive stimulus . - Punishment Decreases a behavior Positive adding an aversive stimulus; Negative removing a rewarding stimulus . - Core Techniques Systematic desensitization gradual exposure to fear-inducing stimuli while practicing relaxation highly effective for phobias exposure and response prevention ERP gold standard for OCD and token economies. 3. Cognitive Therapies and CBT Cognitive therapy posits that our thoughts cognitions are the primary determinants of our emotions and behaviors. Psychological distress stems from faulty or irrational thinking patterns. - Cognitive Behavioral Therapy CBT The most widely researched and practiced form of psychotherapy. It combines cognitive restructuring identifying and challenging cognitive distortions like all-or-nothing thinking or catastrophizing with behavioral activation. - Key Triad Beck The Cognitive Triad of depression involves negative views of oneself the world and the future. - CBT is problem-focused goal-oriented and time-limited serving as a first-line treatment for a vast array of disorders including depression anxiety disorders and PTSD. 4. Humanistic/Experiential Therapies Developed as the third force in psychology reacting against the determinism of psychoanalysis and behaviorism. These therapies emphasize inherent human goodness personal growth and self-actualization. - Person-Centered Therapy Carl Rogers The therapist provides a supportive environment facilitating the client s self-discovery. - Core Conditions for Change Empathy unconditional positive regard non-judgmental acceptance and congruence therapist genuineness . - This approach is often integrated into other modalities to build a strong therapeutic alliance but may be less directive for severe mental illness. 5. Third Wave Behavioral Therapies These newer modalities build upon CBT but incorporate elements of mindfulness acceptance and psychological flexibility rather than solely focusing on changing thoughts. - Dialectical Behavior Therapy DBT Developed specifically for Borderline Personality Disorder and chronic suicidality. It balances acceptance of the patient as they are with the need for change. Core modules include mindfulness distress tolerance emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness. - Acceptance and Commitment Therapy ACT Focuses on accepting negative thoughts and feelings rather than fighting them while committing to behavior change that aligns with personal values. In summary psychotherapy is a highly effective structured intervention for mental health disorders. While diverse in theory the ultimate goal remains facilitating positive behavior change alleviating symptoms and improving patient functioning heavily reliant on the therapeutic alliance. ankingResource primarySource In-house lecture is best chapter In-house lecture is best alternatives flashcards front What effect suggests that despite diverse orientations therapies generally have equivalent efficacy largely due to Common Factors? back The Dodo Bird Effect . tag Concept front According to the Dodo Bird Effect what is the most potent shared element contributing significantly to positive therapeutic outcomes? back The Therapeutic Alliance. tag Concept front What is the primary goal of Psychoanalytic and Psychodynamic Therapies? back To bring the unconscious into consciousness to resolve internal conflicts. tag Concept front What term describes the phenomenon of a patient projecting feelings about formative persons onto the therapist? back Transference. tag Clinical front In Operant Conditioning what does Positive Reinforcement involve? back Adding a rewarding stimulus to increase a behavior. tag Concept front What behavioral therapy technique is the gold standard for treating Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder OCD ? back Exposure and Response Prevention ERP . tag Clinical front What does Cognitive Behavioral Therapy CBT combine to treat disorders like depression and anxiety? back Cognitive restructuring with behavioral activation. tag Clinical front What are the three core conditions for change in Person-Centered Humanistic Therapy according to Carl Rogers? back Empathy unconditional positive regard and congruence. tag Concept front Which Third Wave behavioral therapy was developed specifically for Borderline Personality Disorder and chronic suicidality? back Dialectical Behavior Therapy DBT . tag Clinical front In Operant Conditioning what does Negative Punishment involve? back Removing a rewarding stimulus to decrease a behavior. tag Concept anking match Conditioning usually deals with voluntary responses resources Which type of conditioning usually deals with voluntary responses? \\nOperant conditioning\\n FirstAid 13 Psychiatry 01 Psychology 02 Operant Conditioning match Operant conditioning technique involves addition of a stimulus in efforts to increase behavior resources Which operant conditioning technique involves addition of a stimulus in efforts to increase behavior? \\nPositive reinforcement\\n FirstAid 13 Psychiatry 01 Psychology 02 Operant Conditioning match Operant conditioning technique involves removal of a stimulus in efforts to increase behavior resources Which operant conditioning technique involves removal of a stimulus in efforts to increase behavior? \\nNegative reinforcement\\n FirstAid 13 Psychiatry 01 Psychology 02 Operant Conditioning match Operant conditioning technique involves addition of a stimulus in efforts to decrease behavior resources Which operant conditioning technique involves addition of a stimulus in efforts to decrease behavior? \\nPositive punishment\\n FirstAid 13 Psychiatry 01 Psychology 02 Operant Conditioning match Operant conditioning technique involves removal of a stimulus in efforts to decrease behavior resources Which operant conditioning technique involves removal of a stimulus in efforts to decrease behavior? \\nNegative punishment\\n FirstAid 13 Psychiatry 01 Psychology 02 Operant Conditioning match Discontinuation of reinforcement eventually eliminates a behavior resources Extinction is the idea that discontinuation of reinforcement positive or negative eventually eliminates a behavior\\n FirstAid 13 Psychiatry 01 Psychology 02 Operant Conditioning match Projecting feelings about formative or other important persons onto the physician resources Transference is characterized by the patient projecting feelings about formative or other important persons onto the physician\\n FirstAid 13 Psychiatry 01 Psychology 03 Transference & Countertransference"}, {"id": "l89", "title": "Lecture #89: Neuropsychological Testing", "module": "Psychiatry", "content": "id l89 title Lecture 89 Neuropsychological Testing lecturer L. Hoffman Ph.D. module Psychiatry pdf content/L89 HighYield Render.pdf summary Lecture 89 Neuropsychological Testing Lecturer L. Hoffman Ph.D. Session Lecture 89 This lecture provides an overview of psychological and neuropsychological assessments focusing on diagnostic instruments intelligence testing cognitive screening and personality evaluation. Diagnostic Psychiatric Instruments The SCID Structured Clinical Interview for DSM-5 is a highly formalized clinician-administered tool taking up to two hours to comprehensively diagnose major mental health disorders. A shorter alternative is the M.I.N.I Mini International Neuropsychiatric Interview . Depression and Psychosis Scales - HAM-D Hamilton Rating Scale for Depression Designed to quantify the severity of depression in patients already diagnosed with Major Depressive Disorder taking ~20 minutes. It is clinician-administered and tracks treatment progress. - BDI-2 Beck Depression Inventory A self-reported 21-item questionnaire used widely in both research and clinical settings. - BPRS and PANSS Used specifically for assessing the symptom severity and treatment response in psychotic disorders like schizophrenia. Cognitive Impairment Scales Dementia & MCI - MMSE Mini-Mental State Examination The most common cognitive impairment screen testing orientation registration attention/calculation and recall. - MoCA Montreal Cognitive Assessment Highly sensitive for Mild Cognitive Impairment MCI because it robustly tests executive functioning conceptual thinking and visuospatial skills alongside memory and language. Scores >= 26 are normal. Intelligence and Personality Testing - WAIS Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale The gold standard IQ test computing Full-Scale Verbal and Performance IQ mean = 100 . - Objective vs. Projective Personality Tests - Objective e.g. MMPI-2 Highly structured self-reported true/false items that use validity scales to gauge test-taker truthfulness and uncover psychopathologies. - Projective e.g. Rorschach Inkblot Thematic Apperception Test Less structured; patients project internal conflicts fears and unconscious thoughts onto ambiguous stimuli. Evaluators require highly specialized training to interpret these responses. Neuropsychological Assessment These tests establish the intricate relationship between brain functioning behavior and emotion. The DRS-2 Dementia Rating Scale and RBANS Repeatable Battery for the Assessment of Neuropsychological Status are comprehensive tools commonly used to assess cognitive function thoroughly following head injuries or suspected dementia. ankingResource primarySource In-house lecture is best chapter In-house lecture is best alternatives flashcards front What diagnostic psychiatric instrument is a clinician-administered tool taking up to two hours to comprehensively diagnose DSM-5 disorders? back The SCID Structured Clinical Interview for DSM-5 . tag Concept front What is the HAM-D Hamilton Rating Scale for Depression designed to measure? back To quantify the severity of depression in patients already diagnosed with Major Depressive Disorder. tag Clinical front Which scale is widely used specifically for assessing the symptom severity and treatment response in psychotic disorders like schizophrenia? back BPRS and PANSS. tag Clinical front What is the most common cognitive impairment screen testing orientation registration attention/calculation and recall? back MMSE Mini-Mental State Examination . tag Clinical front Why is the Montreal Cognitive Assessment MoCA highly sensitive for Mild Cognitive Impairment MCI ? back It robustly tests executive functioning conceptual thinking and visuospatial skills alongside memory and language. tag Clinical front What is the typical normal score cutoff for the Montreal Cognitive Assessment MoCA ? back Scores >= 26 are normal. tag Clinical front What is considered the gold standard IQ test that computes Full-Scale Verbal and Performance IQ? back WAIS Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale . tag Concept front Which test is an example of an Objective personality test that uses validity scales to gauge test-taker truthfulness? back Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory MMPI-2 . tag Clinical front What typifies Projective personality tests such as the Rorschach Inkblot or Thematic Apperception Test TAT ? back Patients project internal conflicts and unconscious thoughts onto ambiguous stimuli. tag Concept front When are comprehensive tools like the DRS-2 and RBANS commonly used? back To assess cognitive function thoroughly following head injuries or suspected dementia. tag Clinical anking match Montreal Cognitive Assessment MoCA resources More sensitive than MMSE for detecting Mild Cognitive Impairment MCI match Folstein Mini-Mental State Examination MMSE resources Cognitive screening taking <10 mins to test orientation registration attention recall and language. match Hamilton Rating Scale for Depression HAM-D resources Quantifies severity of depression in patients who already have a formalized MDD diagnosis. match Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory MMPI-2 resources Objective personality test consisting of 567 T/F items containing important built-in validity scales. match Rorschach Inkblot Test and TAT resources Projective tests requiring the patient to project emotions and conflicts onto ambiguous visual stimuli."}, {"id": "l90", "title": "Lecture #90: Personality Disorders", "module": "Psychiatry", "content": "id l90 title Lecture 90 Personality Disorders lecturer L. Jarkon D.O. module Psychiatry pdf content/L90 HighYield Render.pdf summary Lecture 90 Personality Disorders Lecturer L. Jarkon D.O. Session Lecture 90 This lecture provides a comprehensive overview of personality disorders outlining their core definitions diagnostic criteria and the shared hallmarks of each of the three major clusters A B and C . The lecture emphasizes how these pervasive inflexible behavioral patterns can create significant strain on interpersonal relationships and social functioning. Introduction to Personality Disorders Slide 3 According to the DSM-5 a personality disorder is defined as an enduring inflexible and pervasive pattern of inner experience and behavior that deviates markedly from the expectations of the individual s culture. To properly diagnose these disorders a clinician must establish a long-term pattern of functioning extending across various situations. - Diagnostic Criteria & Demographics Slide 4 5 - Must be present by early adulthood. Typically these disorders cannot be diagnosed in individuals under 18 unless the characteristics have persisted for over a year. - Exception Antisocial Personality Disorder cannot be diagnosed until age 18; prior to age 18 it is considered a conduct disorder. - The behavior must be distinguished from temporary traits emerging exclusively during exceptionally stressful periods substance abuse or the onset of other mental health episodes. - Clinicians must consider social cultural and ethnic backgrounds. They must also avoid overgeneralizing based on societal gender stereotypes e.g. assuming males are solely antisocial and females are borderline or histrionic . highyield Personality Disorder Markers A personality disorder requires evaluating a long-term pattern of functioning over time. The behaviors must not occur exclusively during an active episode of another mental health or substance use disorder. Cluster A Personality Disorders The Odd or Eccentric Slide 6 The Cluster A personality disorders are hallmarked by odd and eccentric behavior and are broadly related to psychotic disorders. Crucially these behaviors do not occur exclusively during the course of a frank psychotic disorder e.g. schizophrenia or severe bipolar . - Paranoid Personality Disorder - Key Features Characterized by pervasive distrust and suspiciousness. The motives of others are consistently interpreted as malevolent. - Behaviors These individuals are guarded reluctant to confide for fear of retribution highly controlling pathologically jealous and quick to overreact with anger to perceived insults. - Schizoid Personality Disorder - Key Features Detachment from social relationships by preference . - Behaviors They display a restricted range of emotional expression. They lack friends have little interest in intimacy and are fundamentally indifferent to praise or criticism. - Lecturer Emphasis They are perfectly happy not being in the midst of other people—it is their distinct choice. - Schizotypal Personality Disorder - Key Features Characterized by odd beliefs magical thinking and unusual perceptions. - Behaviors They may be superstitious e.g. believing in a sixth sense or telepathy and have unusual perceptual illusions. They exhibit eccentric appearances and odd vague or overly elaborative speech patterns. Cluster B Personality Disorders The Dramatic Emotional Erratic Slide 10 Cluster B personality disorders are grouped by their dramatic emotional and highly erratic presentations. They are conceptually related to mood disorders substance abuse and somatoform disorders. correlate Antisocial Personality Disorder - Pathophysiology Characterized by an enduring lack of conforming to social norms and a complete disregard for and violation of the rights of others. - Presentation Dishonesty irresponsibility criminal behaviors and a profound lack of remorse. - Diagnosis Cannot be diagnosed until age 18. However onset of conduct disorder before age 15 is a strong clinical indicator. - Lecturer s Point Colloquially these individuals are frequently referred to as psychopaths or sociopaths. correlate Borderline Personality Disorder - Pathophysiology Characterized by severe instability in interpersonal relationships self-image and affects. - Presentation Intense fear of abandonment chronic feelings of emptiness and highly emotional lability. Patients also display impulsivity in at least two potentially damaging areas e.g. excessive spending sexual promiscuity substance abuse reckless driving binge eating . - Complications Recurrent suicidal behavior gestures and self-harm e.g. cutting . Can experience transient paranoia or severe dissociative symptoms. - Lecturer s Point A hallmark defense mechanism is splitting —alternating between idealizing and devaluing an individual. They can be highly manipulative often provoking splits between providers to polarize treatment decisions and undermine the therapeutic process. - Histrionic Personality Disorder - Key Features Characterized by excessive emotionality and excessive attention-seeking behavior. - Behaviors They are often sexually provocative dramatic theatrical and easily influenced. Though they desire connection their relationships are overwhelmingly superficial and they struggle to maintain true intimate relationships. - Narcissistic Personality Disorder - Key Features A grandiose sense of self-importance combined with a distinct lack of empathy. - Behaviors They routinely seek excessive admiration possess a strong sense of entitlement and are preoccupied with fantasies of unlimited success power or ideal love. They are arrogant exploit others and often harbor intense jealousy or believe others are jealous of them . Cluster C Personality Disorders The Anxious and Fearful Cluster C personality disorders are hallmarked by anxiety and fear. While related to anxiety disorders these entrenched personality traits persist independent of active anxiety episodes. correlate Avoidant Personality Disorder - Pathophysiology Pervasive pattern of social inhibition feelings of inadequacy and hypersensitivity to negative evaluation. - Presentation They are oversensitive to rejection or criticism intensely fear being embarrassed or ridiculed and are consequently highly reluctant to take personal risks. - Lecturer s Point Avoidant PD must be distinguished from Schizoid PD. Individuals with avoidant personality disorder want to make friends but are paralyzed by hypersensitivity to rejection. In contrast schizoid individuals have no desire for social connection and are completely indifferent to criticism. - Dependent Personality Disorder Slide 17 - Key Features Submissive and clingy behavior driven by an intense fear of separation. - Behaviors They have severe difficulty making daily decisions without excessive reassurance. They struggle to disagree due to fear of losing support and fear initiating ideas due to a lack of confidence. - Lecturer Emphasis They have an unrealistic fear of being unable to care for themselves. Because of this they are highly vulnerable to ending up in toxic relationships where they are easily manipulated and taken advantage of. mnemonic Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder O-N-O - O Orderly - N Neat and perfectionistic - O Obstinate stubborn and rigid with routines highyield OCPD vs. OCD In Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder OCPD behaviors are strongly ego-syntonic —the patient does not believe they have an issue and feels their rigid routine is the right way. In contrast Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder OCD is ego-dystonic meaning the patient recognizes that their obsessive thoughts and compulsions are irrational and problematic. Other Personality Disorders and Medical Conditions Slide 19 Significant personality changes can also occur due to the direct physiological effects of a medical condition or medication. The observed personality represents a clear deviation from the individual s previously typical pattern and causes significant distress in functioning. - PANDAS Pediatric Autoimmune Neuropsychiatric Disorders Associated with Streptococcal Infection - Mechanism/Presentation Certain streptococcal infections have been associated with a sudden onset or increase in OCD-like symptoms or tics in some children. - Treatment/Notes When treated with appropriate antibiotic intervention the severe OCD symptoms often safely remit. pearls Cluster A vs. B vs. C Hallmarks Cluster A disorders are odd or eccentric Cluster B disorders are dramatic emotional or erratic and Cluster C disorders are anxious and fearful . Schizoid vs. Avoidant Patients with Schizoid Personality Disorder are perfectly content with social isolation indifferent to praise/criticism . Patients with Avoidant Personality Disorder deeply desire relationships but are paralyzed by a fear of rejection. Antisocial Personality Disorder Age Requirement Antisocial personality disorder cannot be diagnosed before age 18. If the criteria are met in a patient under 18 the correct diagnosis is Conduct Disorder. Ego-syntonic vs. Ego-dystonic OCPD is ego-syntonic the patient does not see a problem with their perfectionism and rigidity whereas OCD is ego-dystonic the patient typically knows their compulsions are problematic and unwanted . Splitting in Borderline Personality Disorder A major defense mechanism seen in Borderline Personality Disorder where the patient categorizes people as all good or all bad idealization vs. devaluation often leading to polarization and manipulation among treatment providers. flashcards What defines a personality disorder according to the DSM-5? An enduring inflexible and pervasive pattern of inner experience and behavior that deviates markedly from cultural expectations. Concept What is the age requirement for diagnosing Antisocial Personality Disorder and what is the precursor diagnosis in children? Must be 18 or older. The precursor diagnosis before age 18 is Conduct Disorder usually symptom onset before age 15 . Clinical What are the hallmarks of Cluster A personality disorders? Odd or eccentric behavior Paranoid Schizoid Schizotypal . Concept What is the fundamental difference between Schizoid and Avoidant personality disorders? Schizoid patients prefer isolation and don t want friends indifferent to criticism . Avoidant patients desperately want friends but are terrified of rejection. Clinical Which personality disorder is characterized by splitting unstable relationships emptiness and impulsivity? Borderline Personality Disorder Clinical Which personality disorder involves a grandiose sense of self-importance combined with a distinct lack of empathy? Narcissistic Personality Disorder Clinical What mnemonic describes Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder OCPD ? O-N-O Orderly Neat/perfectionistic Obstinate stubborn/rigid . Glossary What is the difference between OCPD and OCD regarding the patient s insight? OCPD is ego-syntonic they don t think they have a problem . OCD is ego-dystonic they know their thoughts/behaviors are problematic . Concept What condition involves sudden onset OCD symptoms in children following a streptococcal infection? PANDAS Pediatric Autoimmune Neuropsychiatric Disorders Associated with Streptococcal Infection . Clinical What are the hallmarks of Cluster B personality disorders? Dramatic emotional and erratic behavior Antisocial Borderline Histrionic Narcissistic . Concept anking match Cluster A Personality Disorders resources Characterized by odd or eccentric behavior. Includes Paranoid Schizoid and Schizotypal Weird . match Cluster B Personality Disorders resources Characterized by dramatic emotional erratic behavior. Includes Antisocial Borderline Histrionic and Narcissistic Wild . match Cluster C Personality Disorders resources Characterized by anxious or fearful behavior. Includes Avoidant Dependent and Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder Worried . match Antisocial Personality Disorder resources Impulsivity criminality disregard for/violation of rights of others. Conduct disorder is the childhood precursor. match Borderline Personality Disorder resources Unstable mood relationship instability empty feeling splitting defense mechanism. Higher rate of self-mutilation and suicide. match Avoidant Personality Disorder resources Social withdrawal with hypersensitivity to rejection and feelings of inadequacy but desires relationships . match Schizoid Personality Disorder resources Voluntary social withdrawal limited emotional expression and content with social isolation. match Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder OCPD resources Preoccupation with order perfectionism and control. It is ego-syntonic meaning their behavior is consistent with their own beliefs and attitudes. ankingResource BandB Psychiatry > Personality Disorders FirstAid Psychiatry > Personality Disorders Pathoma N/A questions 1 A 30-year-old man presents to the clinic. He describes feeling very uncomfortable around people and says he \\ prefers to be alone.\\ He has no close friends and works a night shift to avoid interacting with coworkers. When the physician compliments his punctual attendance he shows no emotional reaction. Which of the following is the most likely personality disorder? Avoidant Schizoid Schizotypal Paranoid Antisocial 1 Schizoid Personality Disorder is characterized by voluntary social withdrawal and a restricted range of emotional expression. Unlike Avoidant Personality Disorder these individuals prefer isolation and are fundamentally indifferent to praise or criticism. 2 A 24-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department after intentionally cutting her wrists. She explains that her boyfriend of two weeks threatened to leave her and she \\ couldn t bear the emptiness.\\ During the interview she alternates between praising the physician as \\ the only one who understands her\\ and yelling that the physician is \\ completely incompetent.\\ Which of the following defense mechanisms is she exhibiting? Projection Splitting Displacement Reaction formation Sublimation 1 Borderline personality disorder is characterized by unstable relationships impulsivity fear of abandonment chronic emptiness and self-harm. Splitting—alternating rapidly between idealization and devaluation—is a classic defense mechanism commonly seen in this demographic often polarizing treatment providers. 3 A 40-year-old man constantly checks his subordinates work for minor errors often missing major deadlines because he insists the formatting must be \\ perfect.\\ He is incredibly rigid about office rules and refuses to compromise. When asked if this causes stress he replies \\ There is nothing wrong with me; this is the right way to do things.\\ This patient s condition is best described as Obsessive-compulsive disorder OCD Narcissistic personality disorder Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder OCPD Paranoid personality disorder Avoidant personality disorder 2 OCPD is characterized by a pervasive preoccupation with order O neatness/perfectionism N and obstinance/rigidity O . Crucially the disorder is ego-syntonic the patient genuinely believes nothing is wrong with their approach differentiating it entirely from OCD which is ego-dystonic. 4 A 19-year-old male is court-ordered for psychiatric evaluation after being arrested for theft. His history reveals multiple instances of fighting lying and truancy starting at age 12. During the interview he shows no remorse. What is the most appropriate diagnosis? Conduct disorder Oppositional defiant disorder Borderline personality disorder Antisocial personality disorder Narcissistic personality disorder 3 The patient is 19 over 18 displays an enduring pattern of disregarding the rights of others criminality lack of remorse and crucially has a recorded childhood history of conduct disorder symptoms surfacing before age 15. Thus he strictly meets the criteria for Antisocial Personality Disorder. 5 A 28-year-old woman is highly fearful of taking a new promotion at work because she dreads criticism from her new boss. She expresses a deep desire to attend office social events but routinely stays home feeling \\ too inadequate and embarrassed\\ to talk to anyone. Which of the following differentiates her most likely diagnosis from schizoid personality disorder? Presence of magical thinking History of childhood trauma Desire for interpersonal relationships Lack of remorse Ego-syntonic presentation 2 This patient has Avoidant Personality Disorder hypersensitive to rejection feels inadequate but deeply wants friends . Schizoid personality disorder involves social withdrawal by preference with zero desire for close interpersonal relationships."}, {"id": "l91", "title": "Lecture #91: Eating Disorders", "module": "Psychiatry", "content": "id l91 title Lecture 91 Eating Disorders lecturer A. Coladner D.O. module Psychiatry pdf content/L91 HighYield Render.pdf summary Lecture 91 Eating Disorders Lecturer A. Coladner D.O. Session Lecture 91 Eating disorders are psychiatric conditions characterized by severe disturbances in real and perceived body image body weight and eating patterns. Because the psychological component—a distorted perception of oneself—drives the physical illness patients frequently experience severe and sometimes fatal medical complications resulting from malnutrition or purging behaviors. Women between the ages of 12 and 25 are the most frequently affected demographic. Introduction to Eating Disorders Slide 4 A key element of diagnosing eating disorders is accurately assessing body status though Body Mass Index BMI is widely used to determine low body weight it has limitations because it does not account for body fat percentage. Early screening is performed primarily via the 26-item questionnaire known as the Eating Aptitude Test. highyield Body Mass Index BMI is frequently used to determine low body weight in the context of eating disorders but it is not a highly accurate assessment of obesity because it fails to take body fat percentage into account. Lecturer Emphasis Core Eating Disorders Because severe nutritional disturbances affect the entire hormonal and metabolic balance of the body Anorexia Nervosa and Bulimia Nervosa can lead to irreversible and devastating multi-organ damage. correlate Anorexia Nervosa - Pathophysiology Starvation-induced fat and carbohydrate catabolism leading to widespread organ atrophy. - Presentation Low body weight amenorrhea fatigue weakness constipation lanugo fine body hair and delayed puberty. - Diagnosis Requires low body weight an intense fear of weight gain and a distorted body image for at least 3 months. - Treatment Nutritional rehabilitation and psychotherapy. - Risk Factors Typical onset around age 18 and females are 3x more likely to develop it than males. - Complications Fatal arrhythmias cerebral atrophy osteoporosis and stress fractures. The most common causes of death are suicide and tachyarrhythmias. - Lecturer s Point Extreme caution must be taken with Refeeding syndrome a rare but potentially fatal complication caused by rapidly increasing caloric intake; early identification is key to success. correlate Bulimia Nervosa - Pathophysiology Episodes of binge eating consuming very large quantities of food in a small timeframe feeling out of control followed by compensatory purging or non-purging behaviors to prevent weight gain. - Presentation Patients often maintain a normal body weight making it commonly missed. Signs include tachycardia hypotension and erosion of dental enamel from recurrent emesis . - Diagnosis Binge eating and compensatory behaviors vomiting laxatives excessive exercise occurring weekly for at least 3 months. - Treatment Nutritional rehabilitation psychotherapy CBT and pharmacotherapy. - Lecturer s Point The foremost pharmacotherapy is the SSRI fluoxetine. If ineffective or intolerable other SSRIs or TCAs like amitriptyline or nortriptyline can be tried. correlate Binge Eating Disorder - Pathophysiology Recurrent episodes of overeating without the compensatory mechanisms seen in bulimia inevitably leading to obesity-related issues. - Presentation Markedly associated with overweight patients experiencing profound distress and disgust concerning their eating episodes. - Diagnosis Binge eating weekly for 3 months with NO compensatory purging. Must include eating habits like eating rapidly until uncomfortably full or when not hungry. - Treatment Cognitive Behavioral Therapy CBT is the mainstay. - Lecturer s Point It is the most prevalent eating disorder in existence. Other Feeding Disorders Often Presenting in Childhood In pediatric and specific vulnerable populations feeding disorders revolve less around distorted body image and more around the psychological mechanisms of eating the food itself. This can involve restrictive avoidance without body image distortion ARFID or eating non-nutritional items entirely. - Avoidant Restrictive Food Intake Disorder ARFID Slide 30 - Key Features Occurs primarily in children characterized by an unmet need for nutrition leading to severe deficiencies and failure to gain weight. - Features It is distinct from anorexia because body image is not distorted. - Management Supplemental feedings enteral or oral are required to ensure survival and growth. correlate Pica - Pathophysiology Compulsive consumption of non-food non-nutritional substances like chalk dirt crayons . - Diagnosis Must occur for at least 1 month. It is usually not diagnosed before age 2 as this can be a normal exploratory phase. - Risk Factors Associated with autism schizophrenia and pregnancy. - Treatment Principally managed through behavioral therapy; no medications have proven effective. - Lecturer s Point It is a well-known clinical manifestation of iron deficiency anemia. correlate Rumination Disorder - Pathophysiology Repeated regurgitation of food that is not due to an underlying gastrointestinal condition like GERD or pyloric stenosis. - Presentation Often seen in healthy infants 3-12 months old . It occurs effortlessly within 30 minutes of every meal. - Treatment If the patient is old enough it incorporates diaphragmatic breathing and chewing gum after meals. Treatment Modalities Overview Slide 27 Because eating disorders uniquely intertwine cognitive dysregulation and maladaptive coping strategies Cognitive Behavioral Therapy CBT acts as the foundation of recovery across most conditions. It leverages thought exercises or real experiences to facilitate an eventual reduction in symptoms and an improvement in overall life functioning heavily utilizing relaxation techniques and stress management. pearls BMI Limitations BMI is widely used to determine low body weight but fails to account for body fat percentage significantly limiting its accuracy. Refeeding Syndrome A potentially fatal effect of rapidly increasing caloric intake in malnourished anorexia patients leading to massive intracellular electrolyte shifts. Anorexia Mortality The most common causes of death in anorexia nervosa are suicide and fatal tachyarrhythmias. Bulimia Physical Exam Findings Erosion of dental enamel is a classic physical exam finding in bulimia nervosa due to the acid from recurrent induced emesis. First-line Bulimia Drug Fluoxetine an SSRI is unequivocally the first-line pharmacotherapy for bulimia nervosa. Binge Eating Distinction Unlike Bulimia Nervosa Binge Eating Disorder lacks compensatory behaviors no purging or excessive exercise and is the most prevalent eating disorder. Pica and Iron Pica can frequently be a clinical manifestation of underlying iron deficiency anemia. Rumination Therapy Diaphragmatic breathing and chewing gum after meals are surprisingly effective treatments for rumination disorder in older patients. flashcards What is the most widely used screening measure for eating disorders? The Eating Aptitude Test a 26-item questionnaire Concept What potentially fatal complication can arise from rapidly increasing caloric intake in a starving anorexia patient? Refeeding Syndrome Clinical What are the two most common causes of death in Anorexia Nervosa? Suicide and tachyarrhythmia Concept Erosion of dental enamel is a classic physical exam finding for which eating disorder? Bulimia Nervosa Clinical What is the first-line pharmacotherapy for bulimia nervosa? Fluoxetine an SSRI Concept How does Binge Eating Disorder fundamentally differ from Bulimia Nervosa? Binge Eating Disorder lacks the compensatory purging behaviors vomiting exercising laxatives seen in Bulimia. Concept What is the most prevalent eating disorder? Binge Eating Disorder Concept What condition is characterized by the consumption of non-nutritional substances like dirt or chalk? Pica Concept Pica can commonly be a clinical manifestation of what underlying hematologic condition? Iron deficiency anemia Clinical What is the recommended treatment for Rumination Disorder? Diaphragmatic breathing and chewing gum after meals Treatment anking match Anorexia Nervosa resources Excessive dieting exercise or binge eating/purging with extremely low body weight. match Bulimia Nervosa resources Episodes of binge eating with recurrent inappropriate compensatory behaviors occurring weekly for at least 3 months. match Binge Eating Disorder resources Episodes of binge eating without recurrent inappropriate compensatory behaviors occurring weekly for at least 3 months. match Pica resources Craving and eating non-nutritional substances often associated with pregnancy or iron deficiency anemia. match Fluoxetine in Bulimia resources First-line pharmacotherapy for the treatment of Bulimia Nervosa. ankingResource BandB Psychiatry > Pathology > Eating Disorders FirstAid Psychiatry > Pathology > Eating Disorders OME Psychiatry > Eating Disorders Bootcamp Psychiatry > Eating Disorders"}, {"id": "l92", "title": "Lecture #92: Sexual Disorders", "module": "Psychiatry", "content": "id l92 title Lecture 92 Sexual Disorders lecturer A. Loftis D.O. M.S. module Psychiatry pdf content/L92 HighYield Render.pdf summary Lecture 92 Sexual Disorders Lecturer A. Loftis D.O. M.S. Session Lecture 92 This lecture provides an overview of Primary Sexual Dysfunctions and Paraphilic Disorders. Understanding the normal human sexual response cycle—which includes excitement desire plateau orgasm and resolution—is a crucial foundation because sexual dysfunctions typically manifest as a disturbance in one or more of these phases. For any of these conditions a thorough history including sexual history and physical exam are required to rule out other causes such as chronic medical conditions psychosocial stressors medications or other psychiatric disorders. Introduction to Sexual Dysfunctions Slide 7 A primary sexual dysfunction is characterized by a persistent or recurrent reduction or absence of sexual interest arousal or climax. It is not a one-time occurrence; the symptoms must be present on almost all occasions of sexual activity 75-100% of the time and must have persisted for at least six months. Importantly the dysfunction must cause clinically significant distress to the individual and cannot be better explained by another condition. - Key Considerations - Must rule out other causes Chronic diseases e.g. diabetes hypertension substance use alcohol marijuana cocaine psychotropics SSRIs and perimenopause. - The condition must be distressing to the patient or causing relationship strain. Arousal Disorders Arousal disorders manifest as a lack of sexual interest thoughts fantasies or responsiveness to sexual cues. correlate Female Sexual Interest - Arousal Disorder - Pathophysiology Recurrent deficiency or lack of sexual interest/arousal lasting at least 6 months and causing distress. - Presentation Absent or reduced erotic thoughts lack of initiation or unreceptiveness to sexual activity. Often accompanied by reduced physical arousal or pleasure during sexual encounters. - Diagnosis Clinical often supported by the Female Sexual Function Index. Must rule out perimenopause anxiety depression and endocrinological disorders. - Treatment Primarily Cognitive Behavioral Therapy CBT and sex therapy. Medical options include addressing underlying causes topical lubricants/moisturizers and off-label use of testosterone estrogen or bupropion in some cases. - Lecturer s Point Age plays a role; being over the age of 50 significantly increases the likelihood of this disorder. correlate Male Hypoactive Sexual Desire Disorder - Pathophysiology The male equivalent of arousal disorder characterized by a persistent and recurrent lack of sexual thoughts fantasies or desire for sexual activity. - Presentation Similar to female arousal disorder but specific to the male arousal cycle. Must last for 6 months and cause significant distress. - Diagnosis Psychological testing is not required; diagnosis relies heavily on a targeted clinical history. - Treatment Testosterone therapy and Cognitive Behavioral Therapy CBT . - Prognosis Transient cases generally have a good prognosis while chronic presentations are often harder to manage. Orgasmic and Ejaculatory Disorders This category involves disturbances in reaching climax following a normal excitement and arousal phase. - Female Orgasmic Disorder Slide 14 - Key Features Marked delay infrequency or absence of orgasm. Can also present as markedly reduced intensity of orgasmic sensations. - Risk Factors Strongly associated with SSRI/antidepressant use a classic side effect as well as relationship stressors or chronic medical issues. - Treatment Medication substitution if SSRI-induced CBT and sex therapy. Prognosis is generally good especially in long-term committed relationships. correlate Premature Early Ejaculation - Pathophysiology Ejaculation occurring within approximately 1 minute of vaginal penetration or before the individual wishes it leading to significant distress. - Presentation A recurrent pattern occurring 75-100% of the time lasting at least 6 months. Average normal ejaculation time is roughly 3 minutes so it must happen significantly earlier than normal. - Etiology Mainly psychological factors such as performance anxiety panic disorders and relationship stress. It is sometimes a learned behavior from adolescence. - Treatment SSRIs used off-label to purposely delay orgasm due to their common side effect profile and CBT techniques e.g. start-stop method squeeze technique . - Lecturer s Point Because women s arousal response can be delayed compared to men SSRIs act to effectively dampen the excitement just enough to prolong the encounter without causing impotence. correlate Delayed Ejaculation - Pathophysiology Persistent delay or complete absence of ejaculation. - Presentation The normal timeframe to ejaculate is around 25 to 30 minutes from the start of sexual penetration. Distress occurs when men take significantly longer or cannot ejaculate at all despite desire. - Treatment Changing medication as it is highly linked to SSRI use and psychosexual therapy. - Prognosis Better when substance-induced reversible by removing the agent but poorer if entirely psychological or medical in origin. Pain and Penetration Disorders Pain disorders involve pelvic or genital pain during sex or the physical impossibility of penetration due to involuntarily muscle contractions. correlate Genito-Pelvic Pain / Penetration Disorder - Pathophysiology A consolidation of what was formerly known as dyspareunia pelvic pain during intercourse and vaginismus involuntary tensing/tightening of pelvic floor muscles preventing penetration . - Presentation Patients report extreme fear or anxiety of genital pain difficulty having intercourse and recurrent pelvic pain. - Risk Factors Often linked to psychological anxiety PTSD or a history of sexual/physical abuse resulting in a hyperactive response to perineal contact. Must rule out physical etiologies like endometriosis PID or vaginal atrophy. - Treatment CBT Pelvic Floor Physical Therapy PFPT Osteopathic Manipulative Medicine OMM vaginal lubricants/hormone creams and sometimes benzodiazepines for extreme anxiety before penetration attempts. - Lecturer s Point Treating the underlying etiology like a recurrent yeast infection is crucial as chronic pain from an infection can lead to psychological aversion. Erectile Disorders Erectile dysfunction commonly affects men as they age but can present in patients as young as 20. correlate Male Erectile Disorder ED - Pathophysiology Persistent recurrent inability to attain or maintain an adequate erection during sexual activity. - Presentation Heavily associated with embarrassment self-doubt and loss of self-confidence resulting in the avoidance of sexual intercourse. - Etiology/Risk Factors Increases with age. Highly tied to comorbidities hypertension diabetes cardiovascular disease medications antihypertensives and substance use smoking alcohol . - Treatment Modification of lifestyle risk factors Phosphodiesterase type 5 PDE-5 inhibitors e.g. sildenafil and CBT. - Lecturer s Point A thorough evaluation is needed before giving PDE-5 inhibitors because giving them to a patient with certain cardiac histories or concurrent nitrate therapies can be deadly. highyield Priapism Slide 28 A persistent and usually very painful erection lasting more than 4 hours in the absence of sexual excitation or persisting long after ejaculation . It is caused by pathologic engorgement of the corpora cavernosa leaving the glans penis and corpus spongiosum flaccid. Ischemic Priapism Very painful constitutes a urologic emergency and requires immediate aspiration. Often linked to medications Trazodone atypical antipsychotics or sickle cell disease. Progression past 24 hours leads to permanent erectile dysfunction. Non-ischemic Priapism Less painful often related to trauma and is typically managed conservatively with observation. Paraphilic Disorders Slide 33 Paraphilic behaviors denote a preference for sexual fantasies or practices outside forms of typical courtship and genital stimulation. A paraphilic behavior only becomes a Paraphilic Disorder when it meets specific DSM-5 thresholds it causes personal distress or it involves behaviors that are extreme dangerous illegal or rely on another person s psychological distress injury or death. There are no FDA-approved treatments for paraphilias. | Disorder | Mechanism | Key Feature | Treatment/Notes | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | Sexual Sadism Disorder | Gratification from psychological/physical suffering of others | Focus is on giving abuse humiliation or domination. | CBT SSRIs or GnRH agonists to suppress sex drive. | | Pedophilic Disorder | Sexual desire for prepubescent children <13 yo | Patient must be >16 yo or at least 5 years older than the victim. | High recidivism. Highly treatment-resistant therapy/meds . The behavior itself defines the disorder. | pearls Sexual Dysfunction Timeline To diagnose a primary sexual dysfunction the symptoms must be present in 75-100% of encounters persist for at least 6 months and cause clinically significant distress to the patient. SSRI Effects on the Sexual Cycle SSRIs commonly cause delayed ejaculation and anorgasmia. Because of this they are often a leading iatrogenic cause of Orgasmic Disorders but they are also used therapeutically to successfully treat Premature Ejaculation. Priapism Anatomy In priapism only the corpora cavernosa are pathologically engorged with blood. The glans penis and the corpus spongiosum remain distinctively flaccid. Vaginismus to Genito-Pelvic Pain Disorder What was historically termed Dyspareunia pelvic pain and Vaginismus involuntary pelvic floor clenching is now grouped into the single diagnosis Genito-Pelvic Pain / Penetration Disorder. It is best treated with Pelvic Floor PT and CBT. Characteristics of Paraphilic Disorders A quirky sexual interest paraphilia only becomes a Paraphilic Disorder if it causes the individual distress or if acting upon the interest involves illegal acts danger children or non-consenting adults. flashcards What is the primary pharmacological treatment for Premature Ejaculation? SSRIs Selective Serotonin Reuptake Inhibitors leveraging their common side effect of delaying orgasm. Treatment What are the common anatomical findings on physical exam for a patient with priapism? Rigid engorgement of the corpora cavernosa with a flaccid corpus spongiosum and glans penis. Anatomy A patient taking fluoxetine an SSRI complains of difficulty reaching climax during sex. What is the diagnosis? Delayed Ejaculation or Orgasmic Disorder in females which is a common medication-induced side effect of SSRIs. Clinical What two historical diagnoses have been combined into Genito-Pelvic Pain / Penetration Disorder in the DSM-5? Dyspareunia painful intercourse and Vaginismus involuntary pelvic floor muscle spasms . Concept What is the key differentiator between a normal paraphilic behavior and a Paraphilic Disorder ? A disorder is diagnosed if the behavior causes personal distress or involves dangerous illegal acts against non-consenting adults or involves children. Concept Male Erectile Disorder is highly correlated with which two common medical conditions? Hypertension and its treatments and Diabetes Mellitus. Pathology What is the long-term complication of ischemic priapism if it remains untreated for over 24 hours? Irreversible cavernous fibrosis leading to permanent erectile dysfunction. Clinical What therapies are considered first-line for Genito-Pelvic Pain / Penetration Disorder? Cognitive Behavioral Therapy CBT and Pelvic Floor Physical Therapy PFPT . Treatment What is the difference between Sexual Sadism Disorder and Sexual Masochism? Sadism involves gaining sexual gratification from giving or inflicting pain/humiliation to another whereas masochism involves receiving it. Concept What is the minimum age requirement and age gap necessary to diagnose Pedophilic Disorder? The patient must be at least 16 years old and be at least 5 years older than the prepubescent victim who is <13 years old . Criteria anking match Sexual Dysfunction resources General term for problems in the sexual response cycle. Can be caused by aging chronic medical illnesses DM HTN stress and medications. match Erectile Dysfunction resources Inability to maintain an erection. Evaluated using the International Index of Erectile Function. Treated with PDE-5 inhibitors. Must rule out nitrate usage. match Premature Ejaculation resources Ejaculation occurring <1 minute of penetration. Primarily psychological. Treated effectively off-label with SSRIs or CBT start/stop methods . match Priapism resources Erection lasting >4 hours without sexual stimulation. Ischemic subtype is a urologic emergency treated by corporal aspiration. Highly associated with Sickle Cell Disease and Trazodone. match Genito-Pelvic Pain / Penetration Disorder resources Includes historical Vaginismus spasms and Dyspareunia pain . Often associated with prior trauma/abuse. Treated with pelvic floor physical therapy and CBT. ankingResource BandB Psychiatry > Sexual Dysfunctions FirstAid Psychiatry > Pathology > Sexual Dysfunction FirstAid Reproductive > Pathology > Penile Pathology Priapism Bootcamp Psychiatry > Sleep and Psychosomatic Conditions > Erectile Dysfunction questions 1 A 35-year-old woman is evaluated by her gynecologist for pelvic pain during intercourse. She states that she has experienced excruciating pain with any attempt at vaginal penetration for the past eight months. She notes that her pelvic muscles involuntarily \\ clench up\\ whenever penetration is attempted a problem that began after a traumatic assault. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in long-term management? Immediate surgical exploration for endometriosis Prescribing a PDE-5 inhibitor Pelvic floor physical therapy and CBT Oral estrogen replacement Broad-spectrum antibiotics for PID 2 This patient is describing Genito-Pelvic Pain/Penetration Disorder specifically the vaginismus presentation characterized by involuntary pelvic floor tightening . Because her symptoms are heavily linked to a traumatic history Cognitive Behavioral Therapy CBT combined with Pelvic Floor Physical Therapy PFPT to overcome the muscle spasms is the best long-term management approach. 2 A 28-year-old man visits his primary care physician complaining that he consistently ejaculates within 30 to 45 seconds of beginning intercourse. He expresses deep frustration and embarrassment noting this has occurred in almost every encounter for the past two years. Physical examination is unremarkable. Which of the following pharmacologic agents is most commonly used off-label to treat this patient’s condition? Trazodone A selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor SSRI A phosphodiesterase type 5 PDE-5 inhibitor Testosterone replacement A statin 1 The patient has Premature Ejaculation early ejaculation occurring typically < 1 minute . SSRIs are uniquely beneficial for this disorder because delayed ejaculation/anorgasmia is a prominent and well-known side effect of these medications. Utilizing this \\ side effect\\ helps prolong the time to ejaculation. 3 A 60-year-old male with a history of poorly controlled hypertension and type 2 diabetes mentions that he has been unable to maintain an erection during intercourse for the past year. He finds this deeply distressing. He currently takes hydrochlorothiazide and metformin. Which of the following is the most vital step before prescribing a phosphodiesterase type 5 PDE-5 inhibitor? Obtain a careful cardiac history and review of current medications for nitrates Refer him immediately for a pudendal nerve conduction study Order a pelvic ultrasound Start him on high-dose testosterone Discontinue his metformin 0 This patient has Male Erectile Disorder heavily influenced by age diabetes and antihypertensive medications. PDE-5 inhibitors e.g. sildenafil are excellent first-line medications; however they cause systemic vasodilation. If a patient is concurrently taking nitrates for ischemic heart disease combining them with a PDE-5 inhibitor can cause severe fatal hypotension. 4 A 40-year-old man presents to the emergency department strictly complaining of a very painful persistent erection that has lasted for the past 6 hours far beyond any sexual encounter. He has a history of sickle cell trait but no current acute illnesses. Physical examination reveals that his corpora cavernosa are rigidly engorged but the corpus spongiosum and glans are flaccid. What is the most significant long-term consequence if this is not treated within 24 hours? Retrograde ejaculation Testicular atrophy High risk of penile malignancy Permanent erectile dysfunction Transition to a non-ischemic priapism 3 The patient is suffering from Ischemic Priapism an acute urological emergency where stagnant unoxygenated blood is trapped in the corpora cavernosa. If ischemic priapism persists for more than 24 hours without aspiration the prolonged hypoxia leads to irreversible cavernous fibrosis and permanent erectile dysfunction in up to 90% of cases. 5 A 45-year-old male is court-ordered for an evaluation. He admits that he experiences intense sexual arousal uniquely from the act of humiliating bounding and inflicting pain onto non-consenting strangers which led to his recent arrest. He feels no remorse and claims this is \\ just how he is wired.\\ Which of the following represents the most accurate DSM-5 classification for this individual s behavior? A normal variation of human sexuality Schizoid Personality Disorder Sexual Sadism Disorder Genito-Pelvic Penetration Disorder Borderline Personality Disorder 2 A paraphilic behavior transcends into a Paraphilic Disorder when it involves extreme dangerous or illegal behaviors especially relying on the psychological distress injury or lack of consent of another person. Sexual Sadism Disorder is defined by achieving sexual gratification through the physical or psychological suffering of another."}, {"id": "l93", "title": "Lecture #93: Clinical Decision Making of Patient with Acute Psychosis - Assessment of Competency", "module": "Psychiatry", "content": "id l93 title Lecture 93 Clinical Decision Making of Patient with Acute Psychosis - Assessment of Competency lecturer J. Goldberg D.O. module Psychiatry pdf content/L93 HighYield Render.pdf summary Lecture 93 Clinical Decision Making of Patient with Acute Psychosis - Assessment of Competency Lecturer J. Goldberg D.O. Session Lecture 93 This lecture delves into the ethical and legal frameworks governing clinical decision-making specifically focusing on patients with acute psychosis cognitive impairments or severe mental illness. A central theme is the critical distinction between capacity a clinical determination and competency a legal status . As a physician understanding how to assess decision-making capacity and knowing the rights of admitted psychiatric patients are paramount to providing appropriate lawful and ethical care without violating a patient’s constitutional rights. Admitted Patients Rights and Civil Commitment When patients are admitted to an inpatient psychiatric or general medical unit they retain their fundamental rights. This includes the right to quality care the right to object to treatment and the right to be free from unwarranted restraint or seclusion. Civil commitment involuntary admission can occur when a patient s mental illness makes them a clear and present danger to themselves or others. Importantly just because a patient is civilly committed does not automatically mean they lack decision-making capacity. highyield Rivers vs. Katz This landmark New York State Court of Appeals decision 1986 established that involuntary psychiatric patients have a constitutional right to refuse psychiatric medication. The state cannot forcibly medicate a patient without due process a judicial hearing unless there is an emergency situation where the patient poses an imminent danger to themselves or others in which case a one-time STAT dose may be given. Overriding a patient s objection for ongoing treatment requires proving the patient lacks capacity and that the treatment is necessary and the least restrictive option. Capacity vs. Competency A critical distinction must be made between these two terms - Capacity A clinical determination made by any treating physician not necessarily a psychiatrist regarding a patient’s ability to make an informed decision about a specific medical procedure or treatment at a specific point in time. - Competency A legal determination made by a judge in a court of law e.g. competency to stand trial make a will or enter a contract . The Four Domains of Decisional Capacity To determine if a patient has clinical capacity to make a healthcare decision the treating physician must systematically evaluate four key domains. A deficit in any of these areas may indicate a lack of capacity for that specific decision. | Domain | Key Feature | Practical Application | | --- | --- | --- | | Communicating a Choice | The patient can clearly express their preference. | The patient states I do not want dialysis. | | Understanding relevant information | Factual comprehension of what is happening. | The patient knows their kidneys are failing and dialysis is the proposed treatment. | | Appreciating the situation | Applying the facts to their own unique condition. | The patient realizes that refusing treatment will lead to their death not just a hypothetical outcome. | | Reasoning rationally | Manipulating the information rationally. | The patient can weigh the pros and cons logically e.g. Dialysis is painful but I want to live . | correlate Acute Psychosis and Capacity - Pathophysiology Characterized by a severe disruption in reality testing often presenting with delusions hallucinations or disorganized thought processes. - Presentation A patient experiencing acute psychosis e.g. a manic episode of Bipolar Disorder or Schizophrenia may violently refuse life-saving interventions. - Diagnosis Clinical evaluation. - Treatment Address the underlying psychiatric emergency while assessing capacity for concurrent medical needs. - Lecturer s Point All institutionalized psychiatric patients are presumed to have decision-making capacity until proven otherwise. Psychosis does not automatically equate to a lack of capacity for all decisions; capacity is task-specific e.g. a patient may lack capacity to refuse dialysis but retain capacity to consent to a standard blood draw . Common Myths about Capacity - Myth Only psychiatrists can assess capacity. Fact The primary treating physician surgeon internist ER doc is often in the best position to assess capacity for the specific intervention they are proposing. - Myth Decision-making capacity is all or nothing. Fact It fluctuates. A patient may clear up after electrolytes are corrected or they may have capacity for simple decisions but not complex high-risk ones. - Myth Disagreeing with medical advice means you lack capacity. Fact Patients with capacity have the absolute right to refuse treatment even if the physician disagrees with the decision. A capacity evaluation must be objective. pearls Capacity vs Competency Capacity is a clinical determination made by any treating physician regarding specific healthcare decisions. Competency is a legal status determined by a judge. Rivers vs. Katz A landmark NY case establishing that involuntary psychiatric patients maintain their right to refuse medication requiring due process judicial hearing to override except in imminent emergencies. Four Domains of Capacity A patient must be able to 1. Communicate a choice. 2. Understand relevant information. 3. Appreciate the situation and its consequences. 4. Reason manipulate information rationally. Capacity is Decision-Specific Capacity is not all-or-nothing. A patient may lack capacity for a high-risk complex procedure while retaining capacity for simple low-risk interventions like an EKG or blood draw. Psychiatric Diagnosis Does Not Preclude Capacity All institutionalized or psychiatric patients are presumed to have capacity until proven otherwise. A diagnosis of psychosis or being involuntarily committed does not automatically negate decision-making capacity. flashcards What is the difference between capacity and competency? Capacity is a clinical term assessed by physicians for specific medical decisions; competency is a legal term decided exclusively by a judge. Concept According to the Rivers v. Katz ruling under what single circumstance can a patient s refusal of medication be overridden without a judicial hearing? In a true emergency where the patient poses an imminent danger to themselves or others a one-time STAT dose may be administered. Clinical What are the four components evaluated when assessing decision-making capacity? 1. Communicating a choice. 2. Understanding relevant factual information. 3. Appreciating the situation/consequences. 4. Reasoning rationally. Criteria True or False Only a board-certified psychiatrist is qualified to assess a patient s medical decision-making capacity. False. Any treating physician the one recommending the intervention is qualified and often the best person to assess capacity for that specific decision. Concept Does a patient involuntarily committed to an inpatient psychiatric facility automatically lose their right to refuse medical treatment? No. All patients are presumed to retain capacity and the right to refuse treatment unless explicitly proven otherwise through formal capacity assessment and due process. Legal If a patient correctly states that their appendix is infected but refuses surgery because they believe the surgeon is an alien implanting a tracking device which domain of capacity is impaired? Reasoning rationally the ability to manipulate information in a rational/logical manner . Clinical Application anking match Capacity resources Clinical term evaluated by physicians. Requires understanding appreciation reasoning and communicating a choice. Can fluctuate is decision-specific. match Competence resources Legal term determined by a judge/court. Involves broader capabilities like standing trial or managing finances. match Informed Consent & Refusal resources Patients with capacity have the right to refuse any medical treatment even life-saving interventions. Exceptions exist for emergencies imminent danger to self/others . ankingResource BandB Psychiatry > Ethics/Legal > Competency & Civil Commitment FirstAid Psychiatry > Law & Ethics Bootcamp Psychiatry > Ethics questions 1 A 45-year-old man is involuntarily committed to an inpatient psychiatric unit due to an acute manic episode with psychotic features. He is exhibiting grandiosity and severe agitation. The treatment team believes he urgently needs an antipsychotic medication but he adamantly refuses stating it is \\ poison.\\ Under the precedents set by cases like Rivers v. Katz which of the following is the most appropriate next step if the patient is NOT an imminent danger to himself or others? Administer the medication intramuscularly immediately as he is involuntarily committed. Obtain consent from his next of kin and proceed with medication. Seek a judicial hearing to demonstrate he lacks capacity and the medication is necessary. Wait for his mania to subside naturally before attempting treatment. Discharge the patient since he is refusing care. 2 According to Rivers v. Katz involuntary psychiatric patients retain their constitutional right to refuse treatment. Unless there is an emergency where the patient is an imminent danger to themselves or others which the stem explicitly rules out the state must utilize due process typically via a judicial hearing to forcibly medicate the patient. 2 A 68-year-old woman with a history of schizophrenia is admitted to the medical ward with acute appendicitis. The surgical team recommends an appendectomy. She is able to state that her appendix is infected and that surgery will remove it but she believes the surgeons are actually aliens trying to implant a tracking device in her abdomen which is why she is refusing the procedure. Which of the four domains of decisional capacity is this patient primarily lacking? Communicating a choice Understanding relevant information Appreciating the situation and its consequences Reasoning rationally manipulating information rationally Competency to stand trial 3 The patient can communicate her choice refusing surgery and understands the factual information appendix is infected surgery removes it . However her delusion about aliens prevents her from manipulating that information in a rational manner to weigh the tangible pros and cons of the procedure. 3 An attending physician is asked to evaluate a patient for competency before the patient signs a new lease agreement. The patient has a history of major depressive disorder and was recently hospitalized for a suicide attempt. Which of the following statements regarding the evaluation of this patient is most accurate? The attending physician can declare the patient incompetent if they fail a cognitive exam. Competency is a legal determination that can strictly only be decided by a judge. Only a board-certified psychiatrist can determine if the patient has capacity. The patient s recent hospitalization automatically renders them incompetent to sign legal documents. Capacity is an \\ all or nothing\\ phenomenon; if the patient can consent to a blood draw they are competent to sign the lease. 1 Capacity is the clinical term assessed by physicians regarding medical decisions. Competency is a legal standard e.g. competency to sign a contract stand trial and can only be officially determined by the legal system a judge . 4 A 25-year-old male is brought to the emergency department highly intoxicated with alcohol. He has a deep laceration on his arm that requires sutures. He is slurring his words unable to focus and repeatedly yells that he wants to leave immediately without treatment. The physician determines he lacks capacity due to intoxication and restrains him to suture the wound. Two hours later his blood alcohol level drops significantly he is cooperative and he asks about his wound care. Which of the following capacity myths does this scenario highlight? Institutionalized patients lose decision-making capacity. Disagreeing with medical advice means a lack of capacity. Only psychiatrists can assess capacity. Decision-making capacity is \\ all or nothing\\ and static. Patients without relevant information lack capacity. 3 Capacity is not a permanent label. It can fluctuate rapidly due to acute reversible conditions like intoxication delirium or electrolyte imbalances. A patient may lack capacity at one moment but regain it hours later once the acute issue resolves. 5 A patient on an inpatient medical psychiatric unit becomes acutely violent throwing chairs and attempting to strike staff members. He has a known history of severe psychotic disorder and has consistently refused all medication since admission. Based on the rights of admitted patients what is the most appropriate immediate action? File for a judicial hearing and wait for the ruling before taking action. Call the patient s family to get proxy consent for medication. Administer a one-time STAT dose of medication over his objection due to imminent danger. Discharge the patient to a more secure forensic facility. Restrict his visitors and phone privileges until he agrees to take oral medication. 2 While patients have a right to refuse treatment the primary exception in an institutional setting is when their behavior poses an imminent danger and safety threat to themselves or others. In such genuine emergencies a one-time dose of medication can be administered forcibly without a prior judicial hearing."}, {"id": "l94", "title": "Lecture #94: Disaster Psychiatry & PTSD", "module": "Psychiatry", "content": "id l94 title Lecture 94 Disaster Psychiatry & PTSD lecturer J. Goldberg D.O. module Psychiatry session Lecture 94 prev l93 next null pdf content/L94 HighYield Render.pdf summary Lecture 94 Disaster Psychiatry & Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder Lecturer J. Goldberg D.O. Session Lecture 94 Part 1 Disaster Psychiatry Disasters are severe ecological and psychosocial disruptions that greatly exceed the coping capacity of an affected community. Whether natural e.g. hurricanes wildfires or human-caused they leave indelible physical and psychological marks. The initial mental health response in a disaster scenario is pragmatic and built upon three core pillars Protect Direct and Connect PDC . Providers must protect survivors from further harm direct them to necessary life-sustaining resources and connect them with loved ones or support systems. During the immediate aftermath interventions focus on restoring basic needs and safety rather than intensive psychological therapy. Four Domains of Disaster Reaction 1. Cognitive Trouble concentrating questioning beliefs. 2. Emotional Anxiety panic feeling overwhelmed. 3. Behavioral Sleep problems hypervigilance not eating. 4. Physical Headaches GI distress fatigue hypertension. All of these are considered normal reactions to an abnormal event . Special Populations Children face unique risks particularly if separated from parents. Older adults face profound fears of losing independence. First responders endure conflicting roles witnessing immense destruction while managing personal losses making them vulnerable to hero-to-scapegoat community shifts and severe burnout. Psychological First Aid PFA Promotes safety calm connectedness self-efficacy and hope. Do not force survivors to share their stories or offer basic unsolicited platitudes like everything will be OK. correlate Psychological First Aid - Indication Immediate aftermath of a disaster. - Core Principles Protect Direct Connect. Promote safety calm self-efficacy and hope. - Contraindications Do not force patients to recall or share details of the trauma. Do not utilize benzodiazepines as there is no evidence of a protective benefit against future PTSD and they carry significant risk. Part 2 Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder PTSD Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder PTSD is a trauma and stressor-related disorder that develops in a subset of individuals following exposure to an extreme stressor. To be diagnosed with PTSD a patient must meet specific DSM-5 diagnostic criteria. Notably the symptoms must persist for more than one month . If symptoms last less than one month the diagnosis is typically Acute Stress Disorder. Epidemiology & Prognosis While a majority of people experience a traumatic event in their lifetime most do not develop PTSD. Women are more frequently affected than men often due to sexual assault. PTSD is a highly chronic condition; only one-third of patients recover in a year and another third remain symptomatic after 10 years. There is a strong biological consequence to the disease—PTSD patients have higher rates of comorbid hypertension atherosclerotic disease and significantly higher mortality. Criteria A The Stressor Direct exposure witnessing learning about a violent or accidental trauma to a close friend/relative or repeated indirect exposure to aversive details e.g. first responders . Criteria B Intrusion Recurrent intrusive memories traumatic nightmares dissociative flashbacks and marked physiologic reactivity to trauma cues. Criteria C Avoidance Profound avoidance of trauma-related thoughts feelings people or places. Criteria D Cognition and Mood Inability to recall key features persistent negative beliefs distorted blame feeling alienated or diminished interest in activities. Criteria E Arousal and Reactivity Hypervigilance exaggerated startle response irritability angry outbursts and sleep disturbance. correlate Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder PTSD - Pathophysiology Hyperactivity of the amygdala fear center and decreased functional integrity and volume of the hippocampus memory/context and medial prefrontal cortex. - Presentation Must experience symptoms across four domains Intrusion nightmares flashbacks Avoidance Negative Alterations in Cognition/Mood and Hyperarousal. - Diagnosis Clinical utilizing DSM-5 criteria A-E alongside significant social/occupational impairment. Symptoms MUST last >1 month. Assessment can be initiated using the PC-PTSD screening tool in primary care setups. - Treatment First-line psychotherapy is Trauma-Focused CBT Prolonged Exposure or Cognitive Processing Therapy . First-line pharmacotherapy includes SSRIs Paroxetine Sertraline . Prazosin can be utilized off-label to treat trauma-induced nightmares. Emerging and Alternative Therapies While trauma-focused CBT and SSRIs are the gold standard many patients have treatment-resistant PTSD. Alternative complementary medicine like Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction MBSR and yoga are utilized by a substantial portion of patients. Additionally Eye Movement Desensitization and Reprocessing EMDR is widely used to engage both hemispheres of the brain using saccadic eye movements. There is also promising ongoing research into the use of MDMA ketamine and psilocybin-assisted psychotherapy as well as transcranial magnetic stimulation TMS and virtual reality exposure to help integrate the emotional and rational processing of profound trauma. highyield PTSD Subtypes and Timeframes Always verify the timeline of symptom onset and duration. - Acute Stress Disorder Symptoms last from 3 days up to 1 month following the trauma. - PTSD Symptoms persist for >1 month . - PTSD with Delayed Expression Full diagnostic criteria are not met until at least 6 months after the trauma. mnemonic TRAUMA PTSD Symptoms - T Traumatic event Criterion A - R Re-experiencing / Intrusion Criterion B - A Avoidance Criterion C - U Unable to function / Unpleasant mood & cognitions Criterion D - M Month or more of symptoms - A Arousal Criterion E analysis content/L94 HighYield Render Analysis.md ankingResource primarySource BandB chapter Psychiatry > Anxiety Disorders alternatives resource FirstAid chapter Psychiatry > Pathology > Trauma- and stressor-related disorders resource SketchyPharm chapter Psych > Antidepressants SSRIs SNRIs flashcards front What are the three core principles of the initial mental health response during a disaster? back Protect Direct and Connect PDC . tag Clinical front What is a major contraindication when performing Psychological First Aid on a recent trauma survivor? back Do not force the survivor to share their story or recall deeply personal details. tag Clinical front In order to diagnose Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder PTSD how long must symptoms persist? back Greater than 1 month. tag Concept front If a patient presents with symptoms identical to PTSD but the trauma occurred 2 weeks ago what is the appropriate diagnosis? back Acute Stress Disorder. tag Clinical front What neuroanatomical changes are frequently observed on imaging in patients with chronic PTSD? back Decreased volume/integrity of the hippocampus and hyperreactivity of the amygdala. tag Pathology front What are the two first-line SSRIs indicated and FDA-approved for the treatment of PTSD? back Paroxetine and Sertraline. tag Pharmacology front Which medication is frequently used off-label to treat the nightmare and sleep disturbance symptoms associated with PTSD? back Prazosin an alpha-1 blocker . tag Pharmacology front What specific form of Cognitive Behavioral Therapy involves the patient giving written accounts of the trauma to challenge faulty beliefs? back Cognitive Processing Therapy CPT . tag Concept front How is PTSD with Delayed Expression identified? back The full diagnostic criteria for the disorder are not met until at least 6 months after the traumatic event. tag Concept front Why are benzodiazepines generally avoided in the immediate aftermath of a disaster or for acute trauma? back There is no evidence of a protective benefit against developing PTSD and it may interfere with emotional processing or lead to substance abuse. tag Pharmacology pearls title Avoid Benzodiazepines in Acute Trauma content While it is tempting to use anxiolytics in the chaotic aftermath of a disaster benzodiazepines show no protective benefit against the development of PTSD can hinder normal psychological coping mechanisms and present a substantial risk for substance abuse given the stressful context. title Delayed Expression content A patient may experience a traumatic event and appear relatively asymptomatic or mildly symptomatic only to develop full-blown PTSD months or even years later. If the complete diagnosis is achieved 6 months or more after the event it is defined as PTSD with Delayed Expression. title Physical Memory of Trauma content The physiologic reactivity to trauma cues is a core component of PTSD. Research evaluating patients exposed to the Oklahoma City bombing showed increased baseline resting heart rates and exaggerated tachycardia when presented with traumatic reminders highlighting that trauma maintains a physical cardiovascular toll contributing to the comorbid atherosclerotic disease frequently seen in PTSD patients. questions id 1 question A 28-year-old woman is brought to the clinic by her husband three weeks after surviving a devastating house fire. She reports persistent difficulty sleeping frequent terrifying nightmares about the fire and a profound sense of detachment from her family. She actively avoids going anywhere near her old neighborhood and jumps whenever she hears a siren. She has been unable to return to work. Which of the following is the most appropriate diagnosis? options Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder Generalized Anxiety Disorder Specific Phobia Acute Stress Disorder Major Depressive Disorder correctAnswer 3 rationale This patient exhibits the hallmark symptoms of a trauma-related disorder Criteria A Trauma Criteria B Intrusion/Nightmares Criteria C Avoidance Criteria D/E Detachment and Hyperarousal . However because the symptoms have been present for only three weeks the diagnosis is Acute Stress Disorder. PTSD requires symptoms to be present for greater than 1 month. id 2 question You are part of a medical relief team responding to an earthquake that devastated a coastal city 48 hours ago. You are evaluating a 45-year-old man who lost his home and is currently living in a temporary shelter. He is visibly distressed pacing and trembling. When you approach him he begins crying and says he doesn t know what to do. According to the principles of Psychological First Aid which of the following is the most appropriate initial intervention? options Ask him to describe exactly what happened when the earthquake struck to facilitate emotional processing. Administer a low-dose benzodiazepine to prevent the development of Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder. Provide him with water direct him to the local family reunification board and ensure his immediate physical safety. Assure him that everything will go back to normal soon and that he is lucky to have survived. Immediately begin Prolonged Exposure therapy. correctAnswer 2 rationale The core principles of Psychological First Aid in the acute aftermath of a disaster are Protect Direct and Connect. Providing water and reuniting him with family addresses immediate safety and connectedness. It is contraindicated to force survivors to share traumatic details or offer empty platitudes and there is no evidence that benzodiazepines prevent PTSD. id 3 question A 34-year-old combat veteran with a history of Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder presents to establishing care at a new primary care clinic. He is currently stabilized on sertraline and actively participating in trauma-focused Cognitive Behavioral Therapy. However he complains of enduring severe nightmares depicting firefights that cause him to wake up drenched in sweat significantly impacting his sleep quality. Which of the following medications is most frequently utilized off-label to specifically target this symptom? options Lorazepam Prazosin Fluoxetine Zolpidem Mirtazapine correctAnswer 1 rationale Prazosin an alpha-1 adrenergic antagonist is frequently used sequentially off-label to treat the specific symptom of trauma-related nightmares and sleep disturbances in patients with PTSD. While SSRIs sertraline fluoxetine are first-line for the broader disorder prazosin specifically reduces the sympathetic outflow responsible for these intense night terrors. id 4 question To accurately diagnose Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder according to DSM-5 criteria a patient must exhibit symptoms from four distinct clusters following a qualifying traumatic event. Which of the following is an example of an Intrusion Criterion B symptom? options Refusing to watch movies that depict violence. Feeling alienated and detached from close friends and family members. Experiencing a dissociative flashback where the patient feels they are reliving the trauma. An exaggerated startle response and pervasive hypervigilance. Demonstrating reckless or self-destructive behavior. correctAnswer 2 rationale Experiencing a dissociative flashback recurrent distressing memories or traumatic nightmares are classified as Intrusion symptoms Criterion B . Refusing to watch violent movies represents Avoidance Criterion C . Alienation represents Negative Alterations in Cognition and Mood Criterion D . Hypervigilance and reckless behavior represent Arousal and Reactivity Criterion E . id 5 question A functional MRI neuroimaging study is investigating the structural and metabolic brain changes in patients diagnosed with chronic severe Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder compared to healthy controls. Which of the following findings is most consistent with the chronic pathophysiology of this disorder? options Increased volume and functional integrity of the hippocampus. Decreased metabolic activity in the amygdala. Hyperactivity of the amygdala with decreased volume of the hippocampus. Increased cortical thickness in the medial prefrontal cortex. No observable neuroanatomical or metabolic deviations. correctAnswer 2 rationale Chronic PTSD is characterized neurobiologically by a hyperactive amygdala which mediates the exaggerated fear response. Conversely the hippocampus vital for memory consolidation and context and the medial prefrontal cortex responsible for emotional regulation and extinguishing fear tend to show decreased functional integrity and diminished volume. mindMap Disaster Psychiatry & PTSD ├── Disaster Psychiatry │ ├── Phases │ │ ├── Threat/Pre-disaster │ │ ├── Heroic │ │ ├── Honeymoon │ │ ├── Disillusionment │ │ └── Reconstruction │ ├── Reactions │ │ ├── Cognitive │ │ ├── Emotional │ │ ├── Behavioral │ │ └── Physical normal response │ ├── Psychological First Aid │ │ ├── Protect │ │ ├── Direct │ │ └── Connect │ └── Contraindications │ ├── Forced debriefing │ ├── Simple platitudes │ └── Benzodiazepines └── Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder PTSD ├── Diagnosis DSM-5 │ ├── Criteria A Trauma Exposure │ ├── Criteria B Intrusion Nightmares Flashbacks │ ├── Criteria C Avoidance │ ├── Criteria D Negative Cognition/Mood │ ├── Criteria E Hyperarousal │ └── Timeline > 1 Month if <1 month = Acute Stress Disorder ├── Subtypes │ ├── With Dissociative Symptoms │ ├── With Delayed Expression >6 months │ └── Preschool Subtype <6 years ├── Pathophysiology │ ├── Amygdala Hyperactive │ ├── Hippocampus Decreased volume │ └── Prefrontal Cortex Decreased regulation └── Treatment ├── Psychotherapy │ ├── Prolonged Exposure PE │ ├── Cognitive Processing Therapy CPT │ └── EMDR └── Pharmacotherapy ├── First-line SSRIs Paroxetine Sertraline ├── Nightmares Prazosin └── Avoid Benzodiazepines anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best"}, {"id": "l95", "title": "Lecture #95: Special Issues in Psychiatry: Suicidal Patients, Legal Issues and Violent Patients", "module": "Psychiatry", "content": "id l95 title Lecture 95 Special Issues in Psychiatry Suicidal Patients Legal Issues and Violent Patients lecturer J. Goldberg D.O. module Psychiatry pdf content/L95 HighYield Render.pdf summary Lecture 95 Special Issues in Psychiatry Suicidal Patients Legal Issues and Violent Patients Lecturer J. Goldberg D.O. Session Lecture 95 Suicide Assessment and Risk Factors Slide 2 Suicide is a complex phenomenon and a leading cause of death closely tied to psychiatric illness social isolation and access to lethal means like firearms. Understanding the demographic clinical and environmental risk factors is the first crucial step in identifying those at risk. The interplay of feelings of loneliness hopelessness and acute stressors such as illness or loss often precipitates suicidal ideation. - Epidemiology and Statistics - 11th leading cause of death in the US bumped from a higher spot by COVID-19 . - Roughly 132 suicides a day with men dying by suicide almost four times more often than women. - Firearms account for over 50% of all suicide deaths in the US. - Suicide rate increases with age e.g. 85+ and 55+ age groups show higher adjusted rates . - White males account for nearly 70% of suicide deaths. highyield Firearm Safety and Suicide Prevention Because firearms account for over 50% of all suicides and are highly lethal the single best preventative measure clinicians can take is to educate high-risk individuals and their families about safe firearm storage. This includes limiting access and storing bullets separately from the firearm to thwart impulsive attempts. - Key Risk Factors - Demographics White male older age or very young in specific subsets divorced widowed never married or unemployed. - Psychiatric History Over 90% of those who die by suicide have a psychiatric disorder comorbid depression alcoholism psychosis . - Previous Attempt MANDATORY TO KNOW A prior suicide attempt with severe intent and lethal means is the most robust predictor of future suicide. - Recent Psychiatric Hospitalization The first 30 days post-discharge forms a high-risk window for suicide. - Environmental & Medical Social isolation access to guns recent loss severe medical illness loss of functioning and intractable pain. correlate Suicide Risk Factors and Assessment - Pathophysiology Complex interplay of biological psychiatric illness psychological hopelessness impulsivity and social isolation loss factors leading to the desire to end one s life. - Presentation May present with preparations for death updating a will statements of intent concurrent substance abuse or worsening depressive symptoms. - Diagnosis Clinical interview exploring suicidal thoughts plans and access to means. Tools like the Columbia Suicide Severity Rating Scale C-SSRS help standardize screening. - Treatment Psychotherapy securing the environment removing firearms safety planning and pharmacotherapy e.g. Clozapine for schizophrenia Lithium for bipolar disorder carefully monitored SSRIs Ketamine/Esketamine for refractory depression . - Risk Factors Previous attempt recent psychiatric discharge within 30 days white male demographic chronic pain/medical illness family history of suicide active substance abuse. - Lecturer s Point The single best thing that we could do to prevent suicide is to educate about safe firearm storage limit access to high-risk individuals for firearms... mnemonic SAD PERSONS Scale for Suicide Risk SAD PERSONS - S Sex Male - A Age Very young or very old - D Depression Accompanying depression - P Previous attempt - E Ethanol Alcohol abuse - R Rational thinking loss Psychosis - S Social supports lacking - O Organized plan - N No spouse Widowed divorced single - S Sickness Severe medical condition mnemonic NO HOPE Scale for Suicide Risk NO HOPE - N No framework for meaning/living - O Ominous change in clinical condition - H Hostile interpersonal environment - O Out of hospital recently Post-discharge - P Predisposing impulsivity/aggressiveness - E Excuses for dying Hopelessness feeling like a burden Clinical Approach to the Suicidal Patient Slide 15 In addressing a potentially suicidal patient building a therapeutic alliance is paramount before probing deeply into self-harm intentions. Many patients will not volunteer their suicidal thoughts unless they feel understood and unjudged. It is striking that many individuals who complete suicide have been seen by a primary care provider within the preceding month highlighting the critical role of primary care screening. - Interviewing and Screening - Establish rapport and trust first; avoid being judgmental. - Ask directly about thoughts of suicide plans and access to lethal means. - Check for futuristic thinking to gauge protective factors. - Explore precipitants e.g. Why now? What was the recent loss? . - Columbia Suicide Severity Rating Scale C-SSRS - An evidence-based screening tool used across primary care and behavioral health. - Starts with general questions Have you wished you were dead? and escalates to specifics about plans and intent based on answers to initial items. - The Primary Care Opportunity - Lecturer Emphasis Patients who die by suicide are more likely to have seen a primary care physician in the previous month than any other healthcare provider. - This visit represents a critical window to assess needs and intervene. - The Patient Safety Plan - A collaborative written plan detailing warning signs internal coping strategies social contacts for support professionals to call and steps to make the environment safe. - Crucially includes identifying the one thing that is most important to me and worth living for to empower the patient. - Pharmacologic Interventions for Suicidality | Medication | Mechanism/Indication | Key Feature/Notes | | --- | --- | --- | | Clozapine | Atypical Antipsychotic | FDA-approved specifically to reduce the risk of suicide in Schizophrenia. | | Lithium | Mood Stabilizer | Shown to have a five-fold decrease in suicide risk for Bipolar Disorder patients. | | SSRIs | Antidepressants | Carry a black box warning for increased suicide risk in youth but avoidance leads to worse outcomes. Use but monitor for restlessness and side effects. | | Ketamine/Esketamine | NMDA Receptor Antagonist | Esketamine intranasal is FDA-approved for suicidality. Ketamine IV infusions also show rapid anti-suicidal effects outside of underlying antidepressant action. Monitored for dissociation. | Assessing and Managing the Violent Patient Slide 30 Psychiatric professionals frequently interact with individuals who may harbor aggressive thoughts; however assessing whether these will translate into actual violence requires evaluating the imminence probability and nature of the threat. Importantly mental illness alone is not a primary driver of violence—patients are statistically far more likely to be victims than perpetrators. - Dangerousness Assessment - Must evaluate the true probability that the fear of harm will manifest. - Factors in Nature of harm magnitude probability imminence and frequency. - Characteristics of the Violent Patient Often a young male poorly educated lacking a support network with a past history of violence and early victimization. Look for the childhood triad fire setting bedwetting and cruelty to animals. - Psychiatric Conditions Associated with Violence - Schizophrenia Command auditory hallucinations dictating harm. - Mania Sudden violence when limits are set. - Borderline Personality Disorder Violence triggered by perceived abandonment. - Antisocial Personality Disorder Lack of guilt or remorse while hurting others. - Organic/Medical Causes Intoxication steroids roid rage amphetamines/ cocaine sympathomimetic excitement anticholinergic syndrome mad as a hatter and akathisia from antipsychotics extreme restlessness leading to lashing out . highyield Psychiatric Patients and Violence A critical clinical pearl is that people with mental illness are significantly more likely to be victimized by violence and aggression than to be perpetrators themselves. Despite media portrayals mental illness alone does not equate to violence. Confidentiality and the Duty to Protect Slide 35 Confidentiality is the bedrock of psychiatric treatment; without it patients would not disclose their innermost struggles. However the law and medical ethics map out clear boundaries where the safety of the patient or the public overrides this confidentiality. - Exceptions to Confidentiality - Patient request for release. - Mandatory reporting statutes e.g. child abuse . - Subpoenas or court-ordered evaluations. - Patient-initiated litigation involving their mental health. - Emergency situations Under HIPAA if a patient lacks capacity to agree or object and the clinician determines an emergency exists they may disclose personal health information to a contact or authority if it prevents imminent harm. - The Tarasoff Case Duty to Warn/Protect - Stems from a 1976 case where a therapist was told by a patient of his plot to kill an identified student Tatiana Tarasoff but the victim was never warned. - Ruling Established that mental health professionals have a duty to not only warn but also protect potential victims if a patient poses a foreseeable danger. - Lecturer Emphasis The potential victim must be specifically identified . A general global threat does not trigger this duty. Clinician Safety and Interview Stance Slide 45 When approaching a potentially violent patient verbal de-escalation must be paired with physical safety protocols. The goal is to remain non-confrontational while ensuring the clinician can safely exit if the situation deteriorates. - Physical Stance and Environment - Buffer Zone Maintain a space four times larger than usual at least 1.5 arm s lengths . - Positioning Never approach from behind. Do not face them directly face-to-face perceived as confrontational . - Safe and Supportive Stance Blading Stand with the front foot at 12 o clock and the back foot at 10 or 2 o clock. Present the narrowest profile. Keep knees slightly bent and hands above the beltline neutral not folded . - Evasion The Electric Slide Use a sideways sidestep rather than turning and running to retreat while keeping eyes on the patient. - Room Layout Both the clinician and patient should ideally be equidistant from the door so the patient does not feel cornered. Never block the exit. pearls Most Robust Predictor of Suicide A prior suicide attempt with severe intent and lethal means is the single most robust predictor of a future completed suicide. Primary Care Role in Suicide Prevention Patients who die by suicide are significantly more likely to have seen a primary care provider within the preceding month than a mental health professional. Tarasoff Duty to Protect Mental health professionals are legally and ethically obligated to breach confidentiality to warn and protect a specifically identified potential victim of a patient s credible threat of violence. Mental Illness and Violence Individuals with serious mental illness are statistically much more likely to be victims of violence than perpetrators of violence. Pharmacotherapy for Suicidality Clozapine is FDA-approved to reduce suicide risk in schizophrenia. Lithium reduces suicide risk in bipolar disorder. Intranasal Esketamine is FDA-approved for suicidality in refractory depression. flashcards What is the single most robust predictor of future suicide? A previous suicide attempt. Clinical According to the Tarasoff decision under what condition must a therapist breach confidentiality regarding a patient s violent threats? When there is a foreseeable danger to a specifically identified potential victim. Legal What demographic group accounts for nearly 70% of suicide deaths in the United States? White males particularly older white males . Epidemiology Which medication is specifically FDA-approved to reduce the risk of suicide in patients with Schizophrenia? Clozapine. Pharmacology What is the blading stance used by clinicians when interviewing a potentially violent patient? Standing at an angle to the patient presenting a narrow profile with the front foot at 12 o clock back foot at 10 or 2 knees slightly bent and hands above the beltline. Safety Are psychiatric patients more often the perpetrators or the victims of violence? Victims. People with mental illness are significantly more likely to be victimized by violence than to commit it. Concept anking match Suicide Risk Factors resources Previous attempt is the strongest predictor. Other factors include older white male lack of social support access to firearms and severe medical illness. match Tarasoff Duty resources Mental health professionals have a duty to not only warn but also protect specifically identified potential victims of a patient s credible threat of violence. match Violence and Mental Illness resources Mental illness alone is not a primary driver of violence—patients are statistically far more likely to be victims than perpetrators. ankingResource BandB Psychiatry > Psychiatry > Suicide FirstAid Psychiatry > Pathology > Suicide Bootcamp Psychiatry > Legal FirstAid Psychiatry > Law & Ethics questions 1 A 45-year-old male with a history of schizophrenia is brought to the emergency department. His family notes he recently began taking a new medication and has since become extremely restless pacing the halls at home and has become physically aggressive towards his brother when asked to sit down. He states \\ I just can t stop moving my skin is crawling.\\ Which of the following best explains his aggressive behavior? Activation of command auditory hallucinations New-onset mania Akathisia secondary to an antipsychotic Sympathomimetic intoxication Antisocial personality disorder exacerbation 2 Akathisia is a common side effect of antipsychotic medications characterized by extreme subjective and objective motor restlessness. If severe and unrecognized the intense discomfort can cause patients to strike out or become physically aggressive due to the inability to remain still. Options A and B are unlikely to present primarily with the physical sensation of \\ skin crawling\\ and inability to sit still. 2 A 22-year-old college student tells his campus therapist that he has acquired a handgun and intends to shoot his ex-girlfriend naming her explicitly because she broke up with him. He describes his plan in detail. According to the precedent set by the Tarasoff case what is the therapist s legal and ethical obligation? Maintain strict confidentiality to preserve the therapeutic alliance Inform the student s parents so they can intervene Inform campus security and the identified potential victim Arrange for voluntary hospitalization without breaking confidentiality Only contact the police if the student physically brings the weapon to the clinic 2 The Tarasoff case established the \\ Duty to Protect.\\ When a patient poses a foreseeable danger to a specifically identified potential victim mental health professionals must warn the victim and notify law enforcement to protect them overriding normal confidentiality. 3 A 68-year-old man visits his primary care provider complaining of joint pain and insomnia. He has a history of major depressive disorder and reports that since his wife died last month he feels there is \\ no point in going on.\\ Which of the following factors in this patient s history is statistically the most robust predictor of a future completed suicide? His advanced age His recent widower status A prior suicide attempt 10 years ago His visit to a primary care provider A family history of depression 2 While all listed factors age widowed recent loss PCP visit are associated with suicide a previous suicide attempt is widely considered the single most robust and significant predictor of future suicide risk. 4 A first-year psychiatry resident is evaluating a patient in the emergency department who is acutely agitated speaking loudly and making vague threats toward the hospital staff. Which of the following physical positioning strategies is most appropriate for the resident to maintain safety? Stand directly facing the patient while maintaining direct eye contact Stand with hands crossed over the chest to convey authority Stand between the patient and the door to prevent elopement Maintain a buffer zone of at least 1.5 arm s lengths and stand at an angle Approach the patient from behind to avoid confrontation 3 Safe interviewing of an agitated patient involves maintaining a larger physical distance standing at an angle \\ blading\\ rather than face-to-face to appear less confrontational keeping hands visible and uncaged not crossed and ensuring both parties have access to the exit without either feeling trapped. 5 A 30-year-old female with refractory depression and a history of multiple suicide attempts is currently experiencing a severe depressive episode with active suicidal ideation in the hospital. She has failed multiple SSRIs and SNRIs. Which of the following treatments is FDA-approved specifically to target her acute suicidality? Oral Clozapine Intravenous Ketamine Intranasal Esketamine Oral Lithium Electroconvulsive Therapy ECT 2 Esketamine an enantiomer of ketamine delivered intranasally carries a specific FDA indication for the treatment of suicidality in the context of major depressive disorder. Clozapine is FDA-approved for reducing suicide risk in schizophrenia . Lithium is effective but primarily used in bipolar disorder."}, {"id": "l96", "title": "Lecture #96: DPR: Adolescent Wellness", "module": "DPR", "content": "id l96 title Lecture 96 DPR Adolescent Wellness lecturer T. Chan D.O. module DPR pdf content/L96 HighYield Render.pdf summary Lecture 96 DPR Adolescent Wellness Lecturer T. Chan D.O. Session Lecture 96 Adolescent Development and Scope of Practice Slide 6 Adolescence is a massive physical and psychosocial transition period traditionally divided into Early Middle and Late stages spanning ages 10 to 24. A key role of primary care physicians is to guide adolescents and their families through these changes while simultaneously screening for high-risk behaviors and mental health conditions. - Stages of Adolescence - Early 10-13 years Characterized by rapid physical changes puberty onset and concrete thinking. Great concern over normal physical development. - Middle 14-17 years Characterized by a peak in peer involvement intense risk-taking behaviors and feelings of omnipotence. Development of abstract thought. - Late 18-24+ years Solidification of identity transition to adult roles and intimacy in relationships. Physical development is largely complete. highyield The Adolescent Wellness Visit Paradigm Unlike pediatric visits where the parent is the primary informant adolescent visits must transition to treating the adolescent as the primary patient. It is crucial to interview the adolescent alone for a portion of the visit to build trust and gather accurate information regarding sensitive topics like sexuality and substance abuse. Confidentiality and Ethics Slide 12 Establishing confidentiality at the beginning of the interview is essential for building rapport and encouraging honest disclosure of high-risk behaviors. - Parameters of Confidentiality - Clinicians must explicitly state that what is discussed in the room stays in the room except under specific circumstances . - Exceptions to Confidentiality Intent to harm self suicide intent to harm others homicide/violence and suspected abuse physical sexual or severe neglect . - Adolescents in most states can consent to care for contraception STIs pregnancy substance abuse treatment and mental health services without parental notification. correlate Confidentiality and Consent Exceptions - Legal Framework While parents generally consent for minors emancipated minors married military self-supporting and partially emancipated minors seeking sensitive services STIs pregnancy mental health can consent for themselves. - Tarasoff Duty If an adolescent poses a specific threat to an identified individual confidentiality must be breached to warn the victim and authorities. - Reporting Suspicions of child abuse or neglect must be reported to Child Protective Services immediately overriding any confidentiality promises. The HEADS Assessment Slide 20 The HEADS assessment is a structured psychosocial interview tool designed to systematically screen adolescents for common high-risk behaviors and environmental stressors. mnemonic HEADS Assessment H - Home Family dynamics living situation E - Education / Employment School performance bullying jobs A - Activities Hobbies screen time peer group D - Drugs / Drinking Substance use vaping alcohol S - Sexuality / Suicide / Safety Sexual activity mental health seatbelts weapons Note Some versions include Diet and Depression. - Detailed Screening Components - Mental Health Screen for depression using PHQ-2 or PHQ-9. Ask directly about suicidal ideation and self-harm cutting . - Substance Use Use screening tools like CRAFFT or CAGE. Ask about peer usage first Do your friends drink? to decrease defensiveness. - Sexuality Take a non-judgmental sexual history. Discuss gender identity and sexual orientation using open-ended questions. Ensure screening for STIs e.g. Chlamydia and Gonorrhea via urine NAAT . - Safety Discuss seatbelt use helmet use texting while driving and access to firearms in the home. Physical Examination and Pubertal Staging Slide 45 The physical examination in adolescents shifts from developmental milestones to assessing pubertal progression and screening for condition-specific to rapid growth. - Key Physical Findings - Skin Acanthosis nigricans marker of insulin resistance/obesity risk severe cystic acne consider PCOS or hyperandrogenism and self-inflicted linear scars cutting . - Musculoskeletal Screen for scoliosis using the Adam s Forward Bend Test especially during the rapid growth spurt. - Gynecological/Genitourinary A chaperone should ideally be present for sensitive exams. - Tanner Staging Sexual Maturity Rating - Objectively tracks pubertal development based on breast/genital size and pubic hair. - Stage I Pre-pubertal. - Stage II Earliest signs of puberty. Breast buds in individuals with ovaries thelarche ; testicular enlargement in individuals with testes. - Stage III-IV Progressive enlargement and coarsening of pubic hair. - Stage V Adult size and morphology. highyield Precocious vs. Delayed Puberty Precocious puberty is generally defined as secondary sexual characteristics before age 8 in females and age 9 in males. Delayed puberty is the absence of any signs of puberty by age 13 in females and age 14 in males. Early identification and referral to endocrinology are critical to maximize adult height potential and address psychological distress. pearls Exceptions to Confidentiality Confidentiality must be broken in cases of imminent threat to self suicide imminent threat to others homicide/Tarasoff duty or suspected abuse/neglect. HEADS Assessment Tool The HEADS mnemonic Home Education Activities Drugs Sexuality/Suicide is the standard framework for gathering a comprehensive adolescent psychosocial history. Minor Consent for Sensitive Services Minors do not need parental consent to receive care for contraception STIs pregnancy management or substance abuse treatment in almost all states. Tanner Staging Utility Tanner stages objectively measure pubertal progression from Stage I pre-pubertal to Stage V adult maturation and are assessed independently for genitalia and pubic hair. Acanthosis Nigricans The presence of thickened hyperpigmented skin on the neck or axillae in an adolescent is an important clinical marker for insulin resistance obesity risk and Type II Diabetes. flashcards What are the three mandatory exceptions to adolescent patient confidentiality? 1. Imminent threat to self suicide \\\\n2. Imminent threat to others homicide/violence \\\\n3. Suspected abuse or neglect Ethics What does the mnemonic HEADS stand for in the adolescent interview? Home Education/Employment Activities Drugs/Drinking Sexuality/Suicide/Safety. Clinical Skills What physical exam test is used to screen for scoliosis in adolescents? The Adam s forward bend test. Physical Exam At what Tanner stage do breast buds initially appear in females thelarche ? Tanner Stage II. Development Are parents legally required to consent for a 16-year-old to receive STI testing or contraception? No. Minors can legally consent to sensitive services like STI testing contraception and substance abuse treatment without parental notification. Legal anking match Confidentiality Exceptions resources Confidentiality is broken for Threats to self/others Knife/gunshot wounds Reportable diseases and Suspected abuse. match Tanner Stages resources Tanner stages 1-5 rate sexual maturity. Stage II is the onset of puberty breast buds/thelarche pubarche testicular enlargement . ankingResource B&B Behavioral > General > Pediatrics FirstAid Public Health > Ethics > Confidentiality FirstAid Repro > Physiology > Tanner Stages OME Clinical > Pediatrics questions 1 A 15-year-old female presents to the clinic alone requesting a prescription for oral contraceptive pills. She states she does not want her parents to know as they are strictly opposed to premarital sex. What is the most appropriate next step in management? Refuse to prescribe the medication until parental consent is obtained Prescribe the medication and respect her confidentiality Inform her parents but prescribe the medication anyway Refer her to a free clinic where parental consent laws do not apply Require the father of the child to be present 1 In the vast majority of jurisdictions minors can legally consent to reproductive health services including contraception STI testing/treatment and pregnancy care without parental consent or notification. Respecting confidentiality encourages adolescents to seek necessary health care. 2 During a routine sports physical a 14-year-old male is noted to have a spinal curvature. Which of the following physical exam maneuvers is the most appropriate initial screening tool for this condition? Straight leg raise test Trendelenburg test Adam s forward bend test Ober test Phalen s maneuver 2 The Adam s forward bend test is the standard clinical screening test for adolescent idiopathic scoliosis. A scoliometer can be used to measure the angle of trunk rotation during the test. 3 A 16-year-old male confidentially tells you that he has been feeling very depressed lately and has been thinking about taking an overdose of his mother s sleeping pills. He has a plan to do it this weekend when his parents are out of town. What is the appropriate management? Respect his confidentiality and schedule a follow-up appointment next week. Prescribe an SSRI and ask him to promise not to harm himself. Breach confidentiality to inform his parents and arrange for emergency psychiatric evaluation. Call his school counselor to intervene. Discuss the issue with his primary care provider but take no immediate action. 2 Active suicidal ideation with a plan and intent represents an imminent threat to self. This is an explicit exception to medical confidentiality. Protecting the patient s life by notifying parents and securing an emergency evaluation supersedes all privacy concerns. 4 A 12-year-old girl is evaluated during a wellness visit. She has no breast development but has newly developed sparse straight pubic hair along the medial border of the labia. What Tanner stage best describes her pubic hair development? Tanner Stage I Tanner Stage II Tanner Stage III Tanner Stage IV Tanner Stage V 1 Tanner Stage II involves the initial appearance of long downy slightly pigmented pubic hair pubarche primarily along the labia or at the base of the penis. Stage I is pre-pubertal no hair . Stage III involves darker coarser curled hair."}, {"id": "l97", "title": "Lecture #97: OMM Lab 23 Prep - Shoulder Counterstrain", "module": "OMM", "content": "id l97 title Lecture 97 OMM Lab 23 Prep - Shoulder Counterstrain lecturer T. Li D.O. module OMM pdf content/L97 HighYield Render.pdf summary Lecture 97 OMM Lab 23 Prep - Shoulder Counterstrain and Thoracic Review Lecturer T. Li D.O. Session Lecture 97 This prep lecture for OMM Lab 23 outlines counterstrain diagnosis and treatment for the shoulder and provides a review of thoracic foundation material. Counterstrain in the shoulder region requires careful soft tissue palpation to sense tender points and their resolution during treatment. Counterstrain Practice Principles Slide X Counterstrain relies on identifying tenderness and carefully positioning the patient to achieve mechanical ease. - Prediction A critical skill in counterstrain is sensing tissue texture changes during setup to predict exactly how much reduction in tenderness to expect aiming for 0/10. - Workflow Always check for an active tender point before setting up the final treatment position to confirm the diagnosis. - Sequencing Practice efficiently by grouping treatments sharing a similar setup e.g. supine vs. prone . Anterior Counterstrain Points Slide X These points are addressed with the patient in a supine position. - Pectoralis Minor - Located on the myotendinous region near the coracoid process on the upper anterior chest wall. - Subscapularis - Positioned on the anterolateral undersurface of the scapula. - Lecturer s Nuance Best accessed by palpating the posterior axillary fold feeling for the bony lateral edge of the scapula then moving medially. Proceed with light pressure to avoid causing severe pain. - Short Head of the Biceps - Found on the tendon near the coracoid process. - Treatment involves shoulder adduction due to its medial location. - Long Head of the Biceps - Diagnosed in the bicipital groove of the anterior humeral head. The tendon feels relatively thin and smooth. - Treatment incorporates shoulder abduction. Posterior Counterstrain Points Slide X These points are assigned to muscles acting on the posterior aspect of the scapula and shoulder. - Supraspinatus - Located in the middle portion of the muscle belly. - Treated supine with the shoulder abducted and flexed and the forearm directed vertically toward the patient s head much like an unofficially named Statue of Liberty pose with a flexed elbow. - Levator Scapulae - Found on the distal end of the muscle near the superior medial portion of the scapula. - Treated prone with the physician applying traction to the patient s arm. - Mechanism Traction induces a clockwise rotation of the right scapula promoting relaxation and shortening of the levator scapulae to resolve tenderness. - Rhomboid Major and Minor - Located near their attachments along the medial edge of the scapula. - Treated with the patient prone using varying degrees of shoulder extension and adduction. Thoracic and Upper Extremity Review Slide X Students are expected to apply previously learned diagnostic and treatment techniques for the Acromioclavicular AC Sternoclavicular SC and Glenohumeral GH joints as well as the thoracic spine. highyield Levator Scapula Treatment Mechanism Applying traction to the arm in the prone position directly introduces rotation of the scapula to create ease in the levator scapula. highyield Subscapularis Diagnosis Subscapularis point diagnosis accesses the anterior surface of the scapula by identifying the lateral edge from the posterior axillary fold and moving medially. Always communicate with the patient to avoid excessive pressure. ankingResource primarySource BandB chapter Musculoskeletal > Anatomy > Shoulder alternatives flashcards front What is the optimal initial approach step when assigned a counterstrain point treatment? back Start by pressing on the point to determine if it is active tender before assuming diagnosis or setting up the treatment position. tag Concept front Where is the subscapularis counterstrain point palpated? back On the anterolateral undersurface of the scapula accessed from the posterior axillary fold by moving medial to the lateral edge of the scapula. tag Clinical front Which biceps counterstrain point incorporates shoulder adduction as part of its treatment position? back The Short Head of the Biceps due to its medial position. tag Clinical front How is the supraspinatus counterstrain point treated? back With the patient supine shoulder abducted and flexed and the forearm directed vertically over the head the Statue of Liberty pose . tag Clinical front Why is traction applied to the arm when treating the levator scapulae counterstrain point? back Traction introduces primarily a rotation of the scapula which creates shortening and ease in the levator scapulae to relieve tenderness. tag Concept front Where are the rhomboid major and minor counterstrain points typically located? back Near their attachments to the medial edge of the scapula. tag Anatomy anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best pearls Levator Scapula Traction Traction applied to the arm in a prone position for Levator Scapula counterstrain acts to rotate the scapula and shorten the muscle Biceps Short vs Long Head Short Head of the Biceps is treated with adduction due to its medial position; the Long Head is treated with abduction. Subscapularis Point Access Despite its anterior anatomical location the subscapularis point is clinically accessed from the posterior axillary fold by moving medially off the lateral border of the scapula. questions 1 A 24-year-old medical student is evaluated for shoulder pain in OMM lab. The instructor directs you to find the subscapularis counterstrain point. From which of the following areas is the best approach to palpate this point on the anterior undersurface of the scapula? Medial to the lateral edge from the posterior axillary fold Directly inferior to the clavicle in the pectodeltoid groove Superior medial portion of the scapula near the neck Mid-belly of the muscle above the spine of the scapula Medial edge of the scapula near the spinous processes 0 As outlined in the lecture the subscapularis is located on the anterior aspect of the scapula but is best accessed clinically by palpating the posterior axillary fold feeling for the bony lateral edge of the scapula and moving medially into the axilla. 2 While performing counterstrain on a classmate s shoulder you diagnose a tender point on the short head of the biceps. Which of the following is an essential component of the treatment positioning for this point? Prone position Shoulder abduction Shoulder adduction Scapular rotation Direct elbow extension 2 The short head of the biceps is located more medially near the coracoid process making shoulder adduction a key component in its counterstrain treatment. The long head is treated with abduction. 3 A patient is treated with counterstrain for tenderness at the distal end of the levator scapula. With the patient prone traction is applied to the arm. This traction is physiologically intending to do which of the following? Lengthen the levator scapula directly Create counter-irritation to reduce pain signaling Stimulate the Golgi tendon organs Induce rotation of the scapula to shorten the levator scapula Compress the nerve root supplying the overactive muscle 3 Applying traction to the arm of a prone patient rotates the scapula clockwise or counterclockwise on the left simulating the muscle s elevating/rotating action and bringing the origin and insertion closer together to relieve the counterstrain point. 4 A tender point is located near the middle portion of the muscle belly on the posterior aspect of the shoulder. The indicated treatment position is supine with the shoulder abducted and flexed and the forearm pointing vertically. What is this muscle? Pectoralis minor Supraspinatus Rhomboid major Latissimus dorsi Subscapularis 1 The supraspinatus tender point is treated in this \\ Statue of Liberty\\ position supine with the shoulder abducted and flexed . 5 Which of the following is considered a best practice workflow principle in osteopathic counterstrain as highlighted in the lab pre-briefing? Automatically placing patients into textbook positions regardless of symptoms Skipping the initial sequence if tenderness decreases by 50% immediately Always predicting resolution without verifying tenderness with patient Assessing for a reduction of pain specifically using the tenderness scale Assessing active motion of the joint exclusively before treatment 3 The lecture emphasizes diagnosing the point first establishing a tenderness scale predicting the ease and then utilizing that scale with the patient s feedback to fine-tune to a 0/10 level."}, {"id": "l98", "title": "Lecture #98: CNS Viral Infections", "module": "Microbiology", "content": "id l98 title Lecture 98 CNS Viral Infections lecturer T. Slieman Ph.D. module Microbiology pdf content/L98 HighYield Render.pdf summary Lecture 98 CNS Viral Infections Lecturer T. Slieman Ph.D. Session Lecture 98 Central nervous system infections can be caused by various pathogens. Viruses are the most prominent etiology of acute meningitis and encephalitis. While bacterial meningitis has high mortality and is treated with immediate empiric antibiotics viral central nervous system infections are typically supportive. Pathogenesis and Entry Slide 5 Viruses utilize multiple routes to gain access to the privileged central nervous system. The most common pathway is the hematogenous route. - Hematogenous Spread Achieved via inoculation at another site respiratory epithelium animal/bug bites or transplacentally with subsequent spread from blood to CNS through the choroid plexus or CNS endothelial cells. - Direct Inoculation Rarely occurs through trauma or surgery. - Neurotropism Slide 6 Viruses must display tropism for CNS tissue. - Poliovirus targets motor neurons spinal cord/medulla . - Rabies virus enters distal peripheral nerves and travels via retrograde axoplasmic transport. - VZV and HSV establish latency in sensory ganglia and can reactivate to spread along sensory nerves Slide 7 . Acute Viral Meningitis Slide 13 Viruses are the leading cause of aseptic meningitis. The presentation differs notably from bacterial meningitis typically featuring a less severe clinical course. - Presentation Fever headache nuchal rigidity and photophobia. - CSF Findings - Normal or elevated opening pressure. - Increased Lymphocytes lymphocytic pleocytosis . - Normal protein and normal/elevated glucose. - Etiologies Enteroviruses Coxsackievirus are the most common cause. correlate Coxsackievirus Infection - Pathophysiology Enterovirus transmitted via fecal-oral or respiratory route. - Presentation Vesicular diseases Foot and mouth disease Herpangina acute viral meningitis pericarditis. - Diagnosis Nucleic Acid Amplification NAA / PCR. - Treatment Self-limiting supportive care. - Lecturer s Point Most common cause of acute viral meningitis. Viral Encephalitis Slide 18 Encephalitis involves inflammation of the brain parenchyma itself and can be associated with meningitis meningoencephalitis . Sporadic Encephalitis Slide 32 - Herpes Simplex Virus HSV - HSV-1 is the most common viral cause of sporadic viral encephalitis in adults. - Reactivated HSV spreads to the brain infecting meninges and parenchyma. - Patients display bizarre behavior personality changes insomnia and gustatory hallucinations. - Neonatal HSV 70% HSV-2 is acquired during vaginal delivery and can disseminate to CNS. - Treatment requires prompt acyclovir or derivatives . Epidemic Encephalitis Arboviruses Slide 23 Epidemics of encephalitis are primarily driven by vector-borne arboviruses exhibiting seasonal variation peaking in summer/fall . | Virus | Classification | Key Feature | Endemic Region | |---|---|---|---| | Eastern Equine Encephalitis EEE | Alphavirus | High mortality 1/3 clinical cases die | New England states | | Western Equine Encephalitis WEE | Alphavirus | Involves birds and mosquitoes | Western USA and Canada | | St. Louis Encephalitis SLE | Flavivirus | Most common mosquito-transmitted human pathogen in US | Widespread in lower 48 states | correlate Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy PML - Pathophysiology JC Virus human polyomavirus dsDNA enters respiratory tract goes to kidneys infects B cells/monocytes and establishes latency. Reactivates in immunocompromised patients spreading to CNS. - Presentation Subacute demyelinating and necrotic disease leading to paralysis of arms/legs. - Complications Often progresses to death. - Lecturer s Point Distinctive demyelination in immunocompromised state. correlate Rabies - Pathophysiology Rhabdoviridae Bullet-shaped enveloped -ssRNA . Transmitted by bite saliva from infected animal reservoirs raccoons bats . Virus travels via retrograde axoplasmic flow to CNS. - Presentation Classic behavioral changes of rabies universally fatal encephalitis once symptomatic. - Diagnosis Cytoplasmic eosinophilic inclusion bodies Negri bodies in pyramidal cells of hippocampus and Purkinje cells. Antigens via direct immunofluorescence. RT-PCR to confirm. - Treatment Prophylaxis post-exposure; fatal once symptoms appear. highyield CSF Analysis in Meningitis Bacterial meningitis classically presents with increased opening pressure drastically elevated PMNs neutrophils elevated protein and decreased glucose. In contrast viral meningitis presents with elevated lymphocytes normal protein and normal glucose. highyield Negri Bodies Rabies infection frequently causes distinct cytoplasmic eosinophilic inclusion bodies called Negri bodies highly characteristic in neuronal cells. ankingResource primarySource BandB chapter Infectious Disease > Viruses > RNA Viruses alternatives resource SketchyMicro chapter Viruses > RNA resource Pathoma chapter Central Nervous System > Infections resource FirstAid chapter Microbiology > Virology flashcards front What is the most common mechanism or route by which viruses enter the central nervous system? back The hematogenous route often following inoculation at another site respiratory bite and spreading from the blood through the choroid plexus or endothelial cells. tag Concept front What classic CSF analysis findings differentiate viral from bacterial meningitis? back Viral meningitis typically shows increased lymphocytes normal protein and normal/elevated glucose whereas bacterial shows increased PMNs elevated protein and decreased glucose. tag Clinical front What virus is the most common cause of acute viral meningitis? back Enteroviruses specifically Coxsackieviruses . tag Concept front What is the most common viral cause of sporadic viral encephalitis in adults? back Herpes Simplex Virus type 1 HSV-1 . tag Clinical front What symptoms commonly manifest in patients suffering from HSV encephalitis? back Bizarre behavior personality changes insomnia and gustatory hallucinations. tag Clinical front Which arbovirus is the most common mosquito-transmitted human pathogen in the U.S. and a leading cause of epidemic encephalitis? back St. Louis encephalitis SLE virus. tag Clinical front What disease is caused by the reactivation of the JC virus in immunocompromised individuals? back Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy PML . tag Clinical front How does the Rabies virus travel to the Central Nervous System after a bite? back It travels from peripheral nerves to the CNS via retrograde axoplasmic transport. tag Concept front What pathognomonic histological finding is seen in neuronal cells especially Purkinje and pyramidal cells infected with Rabies virus? back Cytoplasmic eosinophilic inclusion bodies known as Negri bodies. tag Clinical front Which patients are at greatest risk for developing PML due to JC Virus? back Immunocompromised patients. tag Clinical anking match Herpes Simplex Virus Encephalitis resources Most common cause of sporadic encephalitis; predilection for temporal lobes causing behavioral changes. match Rabies Virus and Negri Bodies resources Bullet-shaped virus causing fatal encephalitis; Negri bodies found in Purkinje cells of cerebellum and hippocampal neurons. match JC Virus resources Causes PML in HIV/AIDS and other immunocompromised states; demyelinating disease. match Viral Meningitis CSF resources Lymphocytic pleocytosis normal glucose normal/slightly elevated protein. pearls title Viral vs Bacterial Meningitis content Viral meningitis features lymphocytic pleocytosis with normal glucose while bacterial meningitis shows neutrophilic pleocytosis with decreased glucose. title HSV-1 Encephalitis content HSV-1 is the most common cause of sporadic viral encephalitis often presenting with temporal lobe signs like altered behavior and gustatory hallucinations. title Rabies Virus Transport content Rabies virus binds to acetylcholine receptors enters peripheral nerves and travels to the CNS via retrograde axoplasmic transport. title Pathognomonic Negri Bodies content Negri bodies are cytoplasmic eosinophilic inclusions diagnostic of rabies found prominently in the hippocampus and cerebellum. questions id 1 question A 24-year-old male presents to the emergency department with fever severe headache and photophobia. Physical examination reveals nuchal rigidity. Lumbar puncture is performed. Which of the following cerebrospinal fluid CSF findings most strongly suggests a viral rather than a bacterial etiology? options Significantly decreased glucose concentration Markedly elevated protein levels Predominance of polymorphonuclear leukocytes PMNs Lymphocytic pleocytosis with normal glucose Elevated opening pressure with cloudy appearance correctAnswer 3 rationale Viral aseptic meningitis characteristically presents with an increase in lymphocytes lymphocytic pleocytosis normal or slightly elevated protein and normal glucose levels. Bacterial meningitis classically shows highly elevated PMNs high protein and decreased glucose. id 2 question A 55-year-old woman is brought to the clinic by her husband due to bizarre behavior sudden personality changes and complaints of smelling burning rubber gustatory/olfactory hallucinations . She has a history of cold sores. MRI of the brain reveals temporal lobe edema. What is the most likely causative agent? options Coxsackievirus A Herpes Simplex Virus type 1 HSV-1 St. Louis encephalitis virus JC virus Rabies virus correctAnswer 1 rationale HSV-1 is the most common cause of sporadic viral encephalitis in adults. Reactivation and spread to the brain characteristically affect the temporal lobes leading to bizarre behavior personality changes and olfactory/gustatory hallucinations. id 3 question A 35-year-old man with advanced HIV infection presents with progressive weakness and paralysis in his left arm and right leg. MRI shows multiple demyelinating lesions in the white matter without mass effect. A brain biopsy confirms a subacute demyelinating and necrotic disease. Which of the following viruses is most likely responsible for this patient s condition? options Poliovirus Varicella-Zoster Virus VZV JC virus Eastern Equine Encephalitis virus Cytomegalovirus CMV correctAnswer 2 rationale The patient is presenting with Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy PML which is caused by the reactivation of the JC virus a polyomavirus in immunocompromised patients. It leads to a subacute demyelinating disease affecting the central nervous system. id 4 question A 12-year-old boy dies of a rapidly progressing encephalitic illness one month after a bat bite that was not evaluated by a physician. Postmortem examination of the brain reveals specific cytoplasmic eosinophilic inclusion bodies within the pyramidal cells of the hippocampus and Purkinje cells of the cerebellum. How did the infectious agent most likely reach his central nervous system? options Hematogenous spread across the choroid plexus Retrograde axoplasmic transport via peripheral nerves Direct inoculation into the brain parenchyma Anterograde transport through the olfactory tract Infection of the ependymal lining via the ventricles correctAnswer 1 rationale The patient died from Rabies encephalitis resulting from a bat bite. The pathognomonic findings are Negri bodies cytoplasmic eosinophilic inclusions . The Rabies virus travels from the site of the bite peripheral nerves to the CNS via retrograde axoplasmic transport. id 5 question During the late summer an outbreak of acute viral meningitis is noted in a local community. Several children present with fever headache and vesicles in the posterior oropharynx herpangina . Which of the following viral agents is the most likely cause of this outbreak? options Herpes Simplex Virus type 2 Coxsackievirus St. Louis encephalitis virus Epstein-Barr Virus Western Equine Encephalitis virus correctAnswer 1 rationale Enteroviruses particularly Coxsackieviruses are the most common cause of acute viral meningitis. They frequently cause outbreaks in the late summer and fall often presenting with vesicular diseases such as herpangina or hand foot and mouth disease."}, {"id": "l99", "title": "Lecture #99: CNS Bacterial Infections", "module": "Microbiology", "content": "id l99 title Lecture 99 CNS Bacterial Infections lecturer T. Slieman Ph.D. module Microbiology pdf content/L99 HighYield Render.pdf summary Lecture 99 CNS Bacterial Infections Lecturer T. Slieman Ph.D. Session Lecture 99 Infection and inflammation of the central nervous system presents a unique medical challenge. Because the brain and spinal cord are enclosed within the rigid structures of the skull and vertebral column any inflammatory edema leads to increased intracranial pressure. This pressure displaces tissues impedes cerebrospinal fluid and venous flow and ultimately causes the classic neurologic deficits and systemic symptoms associated with meningitis and encephalitis. Pathogenesis and Immune Response Slide 5-11 To protect its delicate tissues the CNS maintains a highly restrictive immune environment effectively keeping most pathogens out but also offering a safe haven for those that manage to break through. While macrophages microglia and Virchow-Robin spaces provide some local defense overall immune presence is minimal until an infection triggers a massive inflammatory cascade. - Routes of Entry - Hematogenous Spread The most common pathway. Pathogens enter the blood via respiratory epithelium bites or across the placenta and cross into the CNS through the choroid plexus or brain endothelial cells. - Direct Inoculation Less common; occurs post-trauma or neurosurgery. - Inflammatory Cascade - Once inside the CNS infection prompts the release of cytokines leading to increased capillary permeability. - Subsequent influx of immune cells and fluid causes significant cerebral edema raising intracranial pressure which underlies the clinical presentation. Acute Bacterial Meningitis Slide 15 Unlike viral meningitis which typically takes a milder course acute bacterial meningitis has a sudden onset rapid progression and high mortality if left untreated. Symptoms may be focal neurologic deficits or global fever systemic signs . - Etiology by Age Group - Newborns Group B Streptococcus S. agalactiae E. coli Listeria monocytogenes . - Children and Adults <60y Streptococcus pneumoniae Neisseria meningitidis Haemophilus influenzae . - Elderly >60y Streptococcus pneumoniae Listeria monocytogenes . - Treatment Strategy - Immediate empiric broad-spectrum antibiotics Ceftriaxone or Cefotaxime are mandatory. - Vancomycin is frequently added due to rising beta-lactam resistance. - Ampicillin is added if Listeria is suspected e.g. in newborns the elderly or immunocompromised . - Dexamethasone is often administered to mitigate inflammation-induced neurologic damage especially in H. influenzae cases. - CSF Profile Highly elevated polymorphonuclear cells neutrophils elevated protein and drastically decreased glucose. Opening pressure is significantly elevated. correlate Streptococcus pneumoniae Meningitis - Pathophysiology Gram-positive lancet-shaped diplococci. Alpha-hemolytic. It possesses a polysaccharide capsule and produces IgA protease and pneumolysin. It colonizes the upper respiratory tract. - Presentation Acute bacterial meningitis often alongside or following otitis media pneumonia or sinusitis. - Treatment Ceftriaxone + Vancomycin due to beta-lactam resistance . - Risk Factors Asplenic patients due to capsule immunocompromised smokers diabetics. - Lecturer s Point S. pneumoniae is the most common cause of bacterial meningitis across most age groups. Vaccination significantly reduces incidence. correlate Neisseria meningitidis Meningococcal Disease - Pathophysiology Gram-negative diplococci. Transmitted via respiratory aerosols. Contains a polysaccharide capsule produces IgA protease and beta-lactamase and sheds outer membrane blebs during replication that worsen inflammation. - Presentation High fever nuchal rigidity AMS and a characteristic petechial rash on the trunk and extremities. - Risk Factors Outbreaks are common in close quarters like college dormitories. Patients with complement deficiencies C5-C9 are at exceedingly high risk. - Treatment Third-generation cephalosporins e.g. Ceftriaxone . - Lecturer s Point It is on the WHO watch list for drug resistance. Vaccine covers A C W Y and there is a separate vaccine for type B. correlate Listeria monocytogenes Infection - Pathophysiology Gram-positive intracellular rod. Capable of growing at refrigerated temperatures and tolerant to high salt/nitrites associated with deli meats and unpasteurized dairy . It uses listeriolysin O to escape phagosomes and hijacks cellular actin to move from cell to cell. - Presentation Severe meningitis in vulnerable populations. - Risk Factors Pregnant women transplacental transmission newborns the elderly and the immunocompromised. - Treatment Ampicillin must be added to the empiric regimen. correlate Group B Streptococcus S. agalactiae - Pathophysiology Gram-positive cocci in chains. Beta-hemolytic and bacitracin resistant. Part of the normal vaginal and gastrointestinal flora. - Presentation Neonatal meningitis pneumonia and sepsis. - Risk Factors Premature rupture of membranes prolonged labor pre-term birth. - Treatment/Prevention Mothers are swabbed prior to delivery. If positive intrapartum Penicillin G is administered every 4 hours to prevent vertical transmission. Chronic Bacterial Meningitis and Brain Abscesses Slide 30 Chronic meningitis presents with an insidious onset over weeks to months. The most frequent bacterial cause is the reactivation of latent Tuberculosis that disseminates to the CNS. - Tuberculosis Meningitis - Pathophysiology Dissemination of Mycobacterium tuberculosis to the brain forming granulomas tuberculomas that cause space-occupying effects and chronic inflammation. - Diagnosis Acid-fast staining using rhodamine-auramine or tuberculostearic acid detection in CSF can be performed but CSF culture for TB is the gold standard can take up to 8 weeks . - Treatment Multi-drug RIPE therapy Rifampin Isoniazid Pyrazinamide Ethambutol due to rising multi-drug resistance. - Brain Abscesses - Characteristics Focal encapsulated infections within brain parenchyma. Often well-separated from surrounding tissue keeping generalized inflammation initially low. - Symptoms Neurological deficits specific to the affected lobe e.g. temporal lobe abscess may cause auditory/visual deficits . - Etiology Staphylococcus aureus Group A/D Streptococcus or mixed flora. Common after trauma dental work or sinus infections. - Treatment Poor antibiotic penetration into the encapsulated abscess means mortality is high. Stereotactic aspiration/drainage is often required alongside prolonged antibiotics. highyield Empiric Meningitis Therapy Always obtain blood and CSF cultures prior to starting antibiotics if possible but NEVER delay treatment if a lumbar puncture cannot be safely performed immediately. The standard empiric therapy for adults is Ceftriaxone + Vancomycin. Ampicillin is added to cover Listeria in neonates patients over 50 and the immunocompromised. highyield CSF Analysis in Bacterial Meningitis Bacterial meningitis classically presents with increased opening pressure drastically elevated PMNs neutrophils elevated protein and decreased glucose. This reliably distinguishes it from viral meningitis which shows elevated lymphocytes normal protein and normal to slightly elevated glucose. ankingResource primarySource BandB chapter Infectious Disease > Bacteria alternatives resource Pathoma chapter Central Nervous System > Infections resource SketchyMicro chapter Bacteria > Gram Positive Cocci resource SketchyMicro chapter Bacteria > Gram Negative Diplococci resource FirstAid chapter Microbiology > Bacteriology flashcards front What are the classic CSF findings in acute bacterial meningitis? back Increased opening pressure increased PMNs neutrophils elevated protein and decreased glucose. tag Clinical front What is the most common cause of bacterial meningitis in newborns? back Group B Streptococcus S. agalactiae followed by E. coli and Listeria. tag Clinical front Which pathogen requires the addition of Ampicillin to empiric meningitis therapy? back Listeria monocytogenes. tag Clinical front What virulence factor of S. pneumoniae and N. meningitidis cleaves mucosal antibodies? back IgA protease. tag Concept front What unique mechanism does Listeria use to spread between host cells? back Actin polymerization hijacking cellular actin to create rocket tails . tag Concept front Which immunodeficiency dramatically increases the risk of Neisseria meningitidis infection? back Complement deficiency specifically late complement components C5-C9 . tag Clinical front What is the gold standard method for diagnosing Tuberculosis meningitis? back CSF culture for TB though it may take up to 8 weeks to grow . tag Clinical front What medication is administered intrapartum to pregnant women colonized with Group B Strep? back Penicillin G. tag Clinical front Why are brain abscesses notoriously difficult to treat with antibiotics alone? back They are well-encapsulated which severely limits antibiotic penetration often necessitating surgical aspiration. tag Concept front What distinct morphological feature of Neisseria meningitidis exacerbates the inflammatory response? back Shedding of outer membrane blebs during replication. tag Concept anking match Streptococcus pneumoniae resources Most common cause of meningitis in adults. Gram positive lancet diplococci. match Neisseria meningitidis resources College student outbreaks. Petechial rash. Gram negative diplococci. MAC complement deficiency. match Listeria monocytogenes resources Pregnant women deli meats unpasteurized dairy. Actin rockets. Intracellular. Ampicillin. match Group B Streptococcus resources Neonatal meningitis. Screen pregnant women at 35-37 weeks. Intrapartum penicillin. match CSF findings in meningitis resources Bacterial High PMNs Low glucose High protein. Viral High lymphocytes normal glucose. pearls title Empiric Therapy By Age content Adults receive Ceftriaxone + Vancomycin. For newborns and adults >50 Ampicillin is added for Listeria coverage. title Listeria s Unique Properties content Listeria grows at cold temperatures refrigeration and uses listeriolysin O and actin polymerization to safely cross the placenta and the blood-brain barrier. title N. meningitidis Rash content A petechial rash in a young adult with signs of meningitis is highly suggestive of meningococcal disease which can rapidly progress to Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome. title Brain Abscess Presentation content Unlike diffuse meningitis abscesses are focal. A temporal lobe abscess may cause localized hearing or vision changes without generalized signs until it enlarges significantly. questions id 1 question A 20-year-old college student presents to the emergency department with a severe headache stiff neck and high fever. Physical examination reveals a petechial rash on his trunk and lower extremities. Cerebrospinal fluid analysis reveals an elevated opening pressure a massive predominance of neutrophils high protein and low glucose. Gram stain shows gram-negative diplococci. Which of the following immunodeficiencies would put a patient at the highest risk for this specific infection? options IgA deficiency T-cell dysfunction Terminal complement C5-C9 deficiency Phagocyte oxidase deficiency Splenectomy correctAnswer 2 rationale The patient is presenting with acute bacterial meningitis caused by Neisseria meningitidis indicated by the college setting petechial rash and gram-negative diplococci. Patients with terminal complement deficiencies C5-C9 are unable to form the membrane attack complex MAC and are at exceptionally high risk for recurrent Neisseria infections. id 2 question A 72-year-old man with a history of hypertension and diabetes is brought to the hospital by his daughter due to worsening confusion fever and a stiff neck over the past 24 hours. A lumbar puncture is performed confirming bacterial meningitis. The physician starts empiric treatment with Ceftriaxone Vancomycin and Ampicillin. The addition of Ampicillin specifically targets which of the following pathogens? options Streptococcus pneumoniae Haemophilus influenzae Neisseria meningitidis Listeria monocytogenes Group B Streptococcus correctAnswer 3 rationale In elderly patients >60 years and the immunocompromised Listeria monocytogenes becomes a significant cause of bacterial meningitis. Standard empiric therapy Ceftriaxone + Vancomycin does not adequately cover Listeria necessitating the addition of Ampicillin. id 3 question A newborn baby delivered vaginally at 38 weeks gestation develops poor feeding irritability and a fever 3 days postpartum. Blood cultures grow gram-positive cocci in chains that exhibit beta-hemolysis and are resistant to bacitracin. Which of the following prophylactic measures could have most likely prevented this infection? options Maternal vaccination during the third trimester Administration of intrapartum intravenous Penicillin G Avoiding unpasteurized dairy and deli meats during pregnancy Prophylactic Ceftriaxone for the infant immediately after birth Cesarean section delivery correctAnswer 1 rationale The newborn is suffering from Group B Streptococcus S. agalactiae meningitis/sepsis. GBS is a gram-positive cocci in chains beta-hemolytic and bacitracin resistant. Transmission is prevented by screening the mother and administering intrapartum intravenous Penicillin G every 4 hours during labor. id 4 question A 45-year-old immigrant with a history of untreated HIV presents with a 4-week history of low-grade fever night sweats and progressively worsening headache. An MRI of the brain reveals basilar meningeal enhancement and several ring-enhancing lesions. Cerebrospinal fluid analysis shows lymphocytic pleocytosis highly elevated protein and very low glucose. Which of the following diagnostic tests is the gold standard to confirm the most likely etiology? options Nucleic acid amplification test NAAT for Herpes Simplex Virus India ink stain of the CSF CSF culture for Mycobacterium tuberculosis Acid-fast stain with rhodamine-auramine Detection of tuberculostearic acid in the CSF correctAnswer 2 rationale The patient s subacute/chronic presentation with basilar meningitis and tuberculomas is highly concerning for Tuberculosis meningitis. While acid-fast stains and tuberculostearic acid detection can provide rapid clues the gold standard for definitive diagnosis is CSF culture for Mycobacterium tuberculosis despite taking up to 8 weeks to grow. id 5 question A 6-year-old boy presents to the pediatrician with fever ear pain and unilateral hearing loss for the past week. Two days later he develops excruciating headaches photophobia and nuchal rigidity. A lumbar puncture reveals cloudy CSF. Gram stain demonstrates gram-positive lancet-shaped diplococci. Which of the following virulence factors allows this organism to evade phagocytosis in the bloodstream? options Listeriolysin O Polysaccharide capsule IgA protease Pneumolysin Outer membrane blebs correctAnswer 1 rationale The patient is infected with Streptococcus pneumoniae classically described as gram-positive lancet-shaped diplococci. It commonly causes meningitis following contiguous spread from otitis media or sinusitis. Its primary virulence factor for evading phagocytosis and surviving in the bloodstream is its prominent polysaccharide capsule."}, {"id": "l100", "title": "Lecture #100: CNS Fungal & Parasitic Infections", "module": "Microbiology", "content": "id l100 title Lecture 100 CNS Fungal & Parasitic Infections lecturer T. Slieman Ph.D. module Microbiology pdf content/L100 HighYield Render.pdf summary Lecture 100 CNS Fungal & Parasitic Infections Lecturer T. Slieman Ph.D. Session Lecture 100 This lecture covers the etiology presentation and treatment of chronic central nervous system CNS infections caused primarily by opportunistic fungi and various parasites. Fungal Chronic Meningitis Fungal CNS infections typically present as a chronic sub-acute meningitis. They predominantly cause disease in immunocompromised individuals e.g. impaired cell-mediated immunity . CSF analysis generally reveals a moderate lymphocytic pleocytosis . - Cryptococcus neoformans The only encapsulated fungal agent visible with India Ink stain or detected via Cryptococcal antigen latex agglutination test . Often causes subacute meningitis or hydrocephalus. Treatment involves Amphotericin B IV + Flucytosine . - Coccidioides C. immitis / C. posadasii Endemic to the Southwestern US. Can present with erythema nodosum hydrocephalus and CSF eosinophilia . Complement fixing antibodies are diagnostic in many cases. Highly infectious in culture. Treatment Amphotericin B IV + Azoles . - Histoplasma capsulatum Endemic to the Ohio River Valley. Linked to caves and bird/bat droppings. Very rarely causes chronic meningitis. Diagnosis may show yeast cells inside macrophages on blood smear. Treatment Amphotericin B IV . - Scedosporium apiospermum Pseudallescheria boydii Found in polluted water and sewage; often associated with near-drowning events. Causes meningitis or brain abscesses. Notably resistant to Amphotericin B instead treated with Miconazole . | Pathogen | Region / Source | Key Presentation / Finding | Treatment | |---|---|---|---| | Cryptococcus neoformans | Opportunistic | Subacute meningitis/hydrocephalus India ink + | Amphotericin B IV + Flucytosine | | Coccidioides C. immitis / posadasii | Southwestern US | Erythema nodosum CSF Eosinophilia | Amphotericin B IV + Azoles | | Histoplasma capsulatum | Ohio River Valley bat/bird guano | Rare CNS findings Macrophages with yeasts | Amphotericin B IV | | Scedosporium apiospermum | Polluted water / Near-drowning | Meningitis / Brain abscess Ampho B resistant | Miconazole | Parasitic Chronic Meningitis Parasitic CNS infections are less commonly identified but can be highly neurotropic. - Taenia solium Pork Tapeworm Causes Neurocysticercosis via ingestion of eggs. Endemic in Mexico and South America. Presents often with seizures and obstructive hydrocephalus. CT scan reveals characteristic calcified cystic lesions . Common CSF finding Eosinophilic pleocytosis . Treatment Albendazole inhibits tubulin polymerization plus steroids and anti-epileptics. - Angiostrongylus cantonensis Rat Lungworm Endemic in Asia/Pacific Islands. Contracted by eating raw snails or shellfish. Very long incubation period up to a year . Presents with severe global headaches and marked peripheral eosinophilia . No specific anthelminthic treatment; usually resolves spontaneously. - Toxoplasma gondii & Others Occasional causes of chronic meningitis symptoms heavily associated with immunocompromised states. | Pathogen | Source | Key Presentation / Finding | Treatment | |---|---|---|---| | Taenia solium | Pork Tapeworm Eggs | Seizures Calcified cysts on CT CSF Eosinophilia | Albendazole | | Angiostrongylus cantonensis | Rat Lungworm Snails/Shellfish | Long incubation Global headache Peripheral eosinophilia | Supportive Resolves spontaneously | Parasitic Encephalitis - Naegleria fowleri Causes Primary Amoebic Meningoencephalitis PAM . A thermophilic protozoan contracted by swimming in warm freshwater enters through the cribriform plate . Almost 100% fatal. Presents with a rapid loss of smell and altered consciousness. CSF shows massive neutrophilic pleocytosis and motile trophozoites on wet mount. - Balamuthia mandrillaris Causes Granulomatous Amoebic Encephalitis GAE . Found in soil; infection typically from cuts or inhalation. Granulomatous lesions on imaging mimic TB. Extremely poor prognosis ~90% mortality . Treatment is a combination of Sulfadiazine Macrolides Rifampin and Fluconazole. - Baylisascaris procyonis Raccoon Roundworm Contracted mainly by children via accidental ingestion of dirt/feces pica . Causes severe eosinophilic pleocytosis and deep white matter MRI abnormalities. Treated with Albendazole. - Gnathostoma species Acquired from undercooked freshwater fish/eels/frogs in Asia. Larvae migrate under the skin to the CNS. Treated with Albendazole or Ivermectin for cutaneous symptoms . | Pathogen | Source / Transmission | Key Presentation / Finding | Treatment | |---|---|---|---| | Naegleria fowleri | Warm freshwater swimming | Loss of smell Rapid PAM Neutrophilic pleocytosis | Almost universally fatal | | Balamuthia mandrillaris | Soil cuts/inhalation | GAE Granulomas mimicking TB | Poor prognosis Combo therapy | | Baylisascaris procyonis | Raccoon feces pica in kids | Eosinophilic pleocytosis Deep white matter lesions | Albendazole | | Gnathostoma species | Undercooked freshwater fish | Larvae migration under skin to CNS | Albendazole / Ivermectin | Parasitic & Fungal Brain Abscesses Parasites and fungi can also produce space-occupying CNS lesions abscesses . Presentation mimics typical brain abscesses headache neuro deficits hemiparesis and seizures. - Entamoeba histolytica Mainly causes amoebic dysentery but can metastasize to the brain. Diagnostic microscopy may show trophozoites with ingested RBCs. Treated with Metronidazole or Tinidazole followed by Paromomycin or Iodoquinol. - Schistosoma japonicum Blood Fluke Enters the body by penetrating intact skin in freshwater. The most pathogenic schistosome species due to abundant egg shedding. Treated with Praziquantel causes spasm and paralysis of the worms by increasing calcium influx . - Paragonimus species Lung Fluke Acquired by eating raw crab or crayfish e.g. in the midwestern US . Symptoms can mimic TB. Treated with Praziquantel. | Pathogen | Source / Transmission | Key Presentation / Finding | Treatment | |---|---|---|---| | Entamoeba histolytica | Fecal-oral dysentery | Brain abscess Trophozoites with ingested RBCs | Metronidazole / Tinidazole | | Schistosoma japonicum | Freshwater intact skin penetration | Blood fluke high egg shedding causing inflammation | Praziquantel | | Paragonimus species | Lung Fluke raw crab/crayfish | Symptoms mimicking TB | Praziquantel | ankingResource primarySource In-house lecture is best chapter In-house lecture is best alternatives flashcards front What encapsulated fungus typically causes a chronic or subacute meningitis and is diagnosed via India Ink or latex agglutination? back Cryptococcus neoformans. tag Clinical front Which fungal pathogen is endemic to the Southwestern US causes CSF eosinophilia and is highly infectious to lab personnel? back Coccidioides C. immitis / C. posadasii . tag Clinical front What is the treatment for chronic meningitis caused by Scedosporium apiospermum Pseudallescheria boydii a fungus often associated with near-drowning events? back Miconazole it is notably resistant to Amphotericin B . tag Treatment front What pork parasite causes Neurocysticercosis leading to seizures and calcified brain cysts on CT? back Taenia solium. tag Concept front What is the mechanism of action of Albendazole commonly used to treat Taenia solium and Baylisascaris infections? back It inhibits tubulin polymerization. tag Pharmacology front Infection by which parasite occurs after swimming in warm freshwater and causes rapidly fatal Primary Amoebic Meningoencephalitis PAM ? back Naegleria fowleri. tag Concept front An immunocompromised patient presents with dysentery and a brain abscess. Trophozoites with engulfed red blood cells are seen. What is the agent? back Entamoeba histolytica. tag Clinical front What trematode can penetrate intact skin in freshwater and occasionally cause brain abscesses? back Schistosoma japonicum. tag Clinical front What drug is used to treat Schistosoma and Paragonimus infections functioning by increasing calcium influx to cause worm paralysis? back Praziquantel. tag Pharmacology front Infection with Paragonimus species lung flukes can mimic TB and cause CNS signs. How is it most commonly acquired in the midwestern US? back By eating raw or undercooked crayfish. tag Clinical anking match Cryptococcus neoformans resources FirstAid 03 Microbiology 03 Mycology 03 Opportunistic Fungal Infections Cryptococcus Neoformans SketchyMicro 02 Fungi 03 Opportunistic Fungal Infections 03 Cryptococcus neoformans match Coccidioides resources FirstAid 03 Microbiology 03 Mycology 01 Systemic Mycoses Coccidioides Immitis SketchyMicro 02 Fungi 01 Systemic Mycoses 03 Coccidioides immitis match Histoplasma capsulatum resources FirstAid 03 Microbiology 03 Mycology 01 Systemic Mycoses Histoplasma Capsulatum SketchyMicro 02 Fungi 01 Systemic Mycoses 01 Histoplasma capsulatum match Taenia solium resources FirstAid 03 Microbiology 04 Parasitology 07 Cestodes Tapeworms Taenia SketchyMicro 04 Parasites 06 Helminths - Trematodes & Cestodes 01 Cestodes match Naegleria fowleri resources FirstAid 03 Microbiology 04 Parasitology 02 Protozoa CNS Infections Naegleria Fowleri SketchyMicro 04 Parasites 02 Protozoa of the CNS 03 Naegleria fowleri pearls title Scedosporium apiospermum Isolation content Also known as Pseudallescheria boydii this fungal pathogen is particularly associated with near-drowning events in polluted waters and is notably resistant to Amphotericin B requiring Miconazole instead. title Taenia solium and Tubulin content T. solium causes Neurocysticercosis recognizable by characteristic calcified cysts on CT. The primary treatment Albendazole works by inhibiting tubulin polymerization preventing worm cell division. title Thermoduric Naegleria fowleri content N. fowleri thrives in warm freshwater during the summer. Once inhaled it causes a nearly 100% fatal meningoencephalitis with severe neutrophilic pleocytosis and a rapid loss of smell. title Skin-Penetrating Schistosoma content Unlike most parasites which must be ingested or require a vector Schistosoma species especially S. japonicum can directly penetrate intact skin exposed to contaminated freshwater. questions id 1 question A 32-year-old immigrant from Mexico presents to the emergency department after experiencing a new-onset generalized tonic-clonic seizure. He has been suffering from recurrent generalized headaches for the past three months. A head CT scan reveals multiple calcified cystic lesions throughout the brain parenchyma. Analysis of his cerebrospinal fluid shows eosinophilic pleocytosis. The drug of choice for treating his underlying infection primarily exerts its effect through which of the following mechanisms? options Binding to DNA and blocking synthesis Inhibiting tubulin polymerization Increasing the influx of calcium causing tetany Creating pores in the fungal cell membrane Inhibiting protein synthesis via the 30S subunit correctAnswer 2 rationale The patient s presentation of seizures headaches and calcified cystic lesions on CT along with his origin in Mexico points to Neurocysticercosis caused by the pork tapeworm Taenia solium. The treatment of choice is Albendazole which functions by inhibiting tubulin polymerization in the parasite. id 2 question A 14-year-old boy is brought to the intensive care unit with profound altered mental status high fever and severe neck stiffness. His parents state that he began experiencing severe global headaches and a loss of smell two days after participating in a wakeboarding competition at a local warm freshwater lake. Despite aggressive empiric antibiotics his condition rapidly deteriorates. A fresh wet mount of his highly purulent neutrophilic cerebrospinal fluid reveals motile trophozoites. Which of the following is the most likely causative organism? options Cryptococcus neoformans Balamuthia mandrillaris Naegleria fowleri Angiostrongylus cantonensis Neisseria meningitidis correctAnswer 3 rationale The patient is suffering from Primary Amoebic Meningoencephalitis PAM caused by Naegleria fowleri. This thermophilic protozoan is contracted by swimming in warm freshwater. It enters via the cribriform plate causing loss of smell and leads to rapidly fatal purulent meningoencephalitis. id 3 question A 45-year-old man who recently survived a near-drowning accident in a flooded sewage-contaminated river develops signs of meningitis and altered mental status a week later. Cultures of his cerebrospinal fluid grow a mold that is identified as Scedosporium apiospermum Pseudallescheria boydii . Which of the following antifungal agents would be most appropriate for his treatment given the organism s typical resistance profile? options Amphotericin B Miconazole Flucytosine Caspofungin Terbinafine correctAnswer 2 rationale Scedosporium apiospermum Pseudallescheria boydii is famously associated with near-drowning events in polluted waters. It is distinctly notable for its inherent resistance to Amphotericin B the standard therapy for most systemic fungal infections. Miconazole has historically been and continues to be evaluated as a preferred alternative. id 4 question A 27-year-old international aid worker returns from an extended mission in rural Southeast Asia. She presents with fever right upper quadrant pain and new-onset focal neurological deficits. Imaging of her brain reveals a space-occupying abscess. During her occupational history she mentions frequently wading through local rivers and ponds. A stool/tissue exam reveals a trematode infection known for high egg shedding and direct penetration of intact skin. Treatment is immediately started. The chosen anthelminthic medication functions by which of the following mechanisms? options Inhibiting tubulin polymerization Inhibiting acetylcholinesterase leading to paralysis Generating toxic free radicals inside the parasite Opening chloride channels leading to hyperpolarization Increasing calcium influx resulting in severe spastic paralysis correctAnswer 5 rationale The patient has a Schistosoma japonicum infection. This blood fluke is unique as it penetrates intact skin during freshwater exposure. It can migrate and metastasize to cause brain abscesses. The drug of choice for Schistosoma and Paragonimus is Praziquantel which works by increasing the influx of calcium causing spastic paralysis of the worms. id 5 question A previously healthy 30-year-old man living in Arizona presents with a persistent cough low-grade fever and painful red nodules on his shins erythema nodosum . Over the following weeks he develops signs of chronic meningitis including a severe headache and confusion. Analysis of his cerebrospinal fluid demonstrates a marked pleocytosis characterized by a high percentage of eosinophils. Which of the following is the most likely etiology of his meningitis? options Taenia solium Histoplasma capsulatum Angiostrongylus cantonensis Coccidioides immitis Cryptococcus neoformans correctAnswer 4 rationale The patient is infected with Coccidioides C. immitis / C. posadasii which is endemic to the Southwestern US like Arizona . Primary infection often causes respiratory symptoms and erythema nodosum. Dissemination can lead to a chronic meningitis uniquely characterized by CSF eosinophilia among the fungal etiologies."}, {"id": "l101", "title": "Lecture #101: Pharmacology Antiherpes Antiviral Agents", "module": "Pharmacology", "content": "id l101 title Lecture 101 Pharmacology Antiherpes Antiviral Agents lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. module Pharmacology pdf content/L101 HighYield Render.pdf summary Lecture 101 Pharmacology Antiherpes Antiviral Agents Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. Session Lecture 101 This lecture covers the pharmacological management of Herpesviridae specifically Herpes Simplex Virus HSV Varicella-Zoster Virus VZV and Cytomegalovirus CMV . Because these are large DNA viruses that establish latency in the host treatment does not cure the infection but suppresses replication. HSV and VZV Agents Slide 6 Drugs targeting HSV and VZV rely heavily on viral and host kinases for activation. They act as guanosine analogs that competitively inhibit viral DNA polymerase and depending on the drug act as chain terminators. - Acyclovir & Valacyclovir - Mechanism Acyclovir is a guanosine analog lacking a 3 -hydroxyl group. It is a prodrug requiring initial monophosphorylation by viral thymidine kinase TK followed by di- and tri-phosphorylation by host kinases. The active triphosphate competitively inhibits viral DNA polymerase and acts as an absolute chain terminator . Valacyclovir is merely the L-valyl ester prodrug of acyclovir with significantly improved oral bioavailability. - Pharmacokinetics Renal excretion of unchanged drug through glomerular filtration and active secretion. Dose adjustments are required in renal impairment. - Resistance Most commonly due to absent or deficient viral thymidine kinase or altered DNA polymerase. - Penciclovir & Famciclovir - Mechanism Penciclovir retains the 3 -hydroxyl group. Like acyclovir it is phosphorylated initially by viral TK and then host kinases. It competitively inhibits DNA polymerase but is not an obligate chain terminator. Famciclovir is its oral prodrug. - Pharmacokinetics Famciclovir has much better systemic absorption than penciclovir which is usually topical . correlate Intravenous Acyclovir Toxicity - Pathophysiology Acyclovir is relatively insoluble in urine. Rapid or high-dose IV administration can cause the drug to crystallize in the renal tubules leading to obstructive nephropathy. - Presentation Acute kidney injury elevated creatinine. - Treatment Aggressive IV saline hydration prior to and during administration and slowing the infusion rate. CMV Specific Agents - Polymerase Inhibitors Slide 15 Cytomegalovirus does not encode thymidine kinase; instead it utilizes a different kinase UL97 for the initial activation step of certain nucleoside analogs. - Ganciclovir & Valganciclovir - Mechanism Guanosine analogs that require activation by CMV UL97 kinase for the first phosphorylation step followed by host kinases. They competitively inhibit CMV DNA polymerase and cause delayed chain termination. Valganciclovir is the oral prodrug with high bioavailability. - Adverse Effects Lecturer Emphasis Significant bone marrow suppression myelosuppression particularly neutropenia and thrombocytopenia. Teratogenic and carcinogenic potential. Must monitor CBC regularly. - Resistance Primarily mutations in the UL97 kinase most common or UL54 DNA polymerase. - Cidofovir - Mechanism A cytosine nucleotide analog. Crucially it is already monophosphorylated and thus bypasses the need for any viral kinase. It relies solely on host kinases for conversion to the active diphosphate form inhibiting viral DNA polymerase. - Use Highly effective against CMV strains resistant to ganciclovir because it does not need UL97 . correlate Cidofovir Nephrotoxicity - Pathophysiology Cidofovir is actively transported into proximal renal tubule cells via organic anion transporters accumulating at highly toxic concentrations. - Presentation Irreversible dose-dependent acute renal failure proteinuria and Fanconi syndrome. Lecturer Emphasis This is a severe life-threatening toxicity. - Prevention/Treatment Must co-administer oral Probenecid blocks the organic anion transporter reducing tubular secretion/accumulation AND provide aggressive pre-hydration with normal saline. - Foscarnet - Mechanism An inorganic pyrophosphate analog. It binds directly to the pyrophosphate-binding site of viral DNA polymerase and HIV reverse transcriptase non-competitively preventing cleavage of pyrophosphate from nucleoside triphosphates. Does NOT require any kinase activation. - Use Alternative for acyclovir-resistant HSV/VZV and ganciclovir-resistant CMV. correlate Foscarnet Metabolic Toxicity - Pathophysiology Foscarnet chelates divalent cations $Ca^ 2+ $ $Mg^ 2+ $ . - Presentation Severe symptomatic hypocalcemia and hypomagnesemia leading to seizures tremors and arrhythmias. Also causes significant nephrotoxicity. - Treatment Extensive pre-hydration saline loading manages the nephrotoxicity. Monitor and replete electrolytes. CMV Specific Agents - Novel Targets Slide 23 Newer agents target alternative steps in the CMV replication cycle circumventing traditional polymerase or kinase resistance. - Letermovir - Mechanism Inhibits the CMV DNA terminase complex by binding to protein pUL56 . This blocks the cleavage and packaging of viral DNA into capsids maturation . - Use Specifically indicated for prophylaxis of CMV in seropositive recipients of allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell transplants. - Interactions Lecturer Emphasis High risk of severe drug interactions due to CYP3A4 inhibition e.g. markedly increases levels of Cyclosporine Tacrolimus Sirolimus used in transplant patients . - Maribavir - Mechanism A direct competitive inhibitor of the CMV UL97 kinase blocking its primary function not just using it for activation . - Pharmacokinetics/Interactions Do NOT combine with ganciclovir. Because maribavir inhibits UL97 it prevents the required activation of ganciclovir rendering ganciclovir ineffective. | Drug Class/Target | Agents | Key Characteristic / Mechanism | Major Toxicity / Note | |---|---|---|---| | HSV Polymerase Inhibitors | Acyclovir Valacyclovir | Needs viral TK; chain terminator | IV crystal nephropathy | | HSV Polymerase Inhibitors | Penciclovir Famciclovir | Needs viral TK; not a chain terminator | Better oral bioavailability Fam | | CMV Polymerase Inhibitors | Ganciclovir Valganciclovir | Needs CMV UL97 kinase | Neutropenia Myelosuppression | | CMV Polymerase Inhibitors | Cidofovir | Nucleotide no viral kinase needed | Irreversible nephropathy needs Probenecid | | CMV Polymerase Inhibitors | Foscarnet | Pyrophosphate analog no kinase needed | Hypocalcemia seizures | | CMV Terminase Inhibitor | Letermovir | Binds pUL56 terminase complex | CYP3A4 interactions Cyclosporine | | CMV UL97 Inhibitor | Maribavir | Inhibits UL97 kinase directly | Antagonizes ganciclovir | highyield Cidofovir and Foscarnet do not require viral kinases for activation. They are the drugs of choice for HSV VZV or CMV strains that have mutated their functional kinases e.g. thymidine kinase or UL97 . Cidofovir requires co-administration with Probenecid to prevent severe nephrotoxicity. highyield Acyclovir must be administered with aggressive IV hydration to prevent crystalluria and subsequent renal failure. Ganciclovir notably causes severe bone marrow suppression neutropenia . ankingResource primarySource BandB chapter Infectious Disease > Viruses > Antiviral Therapy alternatives resource SketchyPharm chapter Antimicrobials > Antivirals resource FirstAid chapter Microbiology > Antimicrobials flashcards front Which antiviral agents require activation by the viral thymidine kinase in HSV/VZV infections? back Acyclovir Valacyclovir Penciclovir and Famciclovir. tag Concept front What is the primary dose-limiting toxicity of intravenous acyclovir? back Crystalline nephropathy acute renal failure which is prevented by aggressive IV hydration. tag Clinical front What is the key structural difference between acyclovir and penciclovir in terms of their mechanism of action? back Acyclovir lacks a 3 -hydroxyl group and is an obligate chain terminator. Penciclovir retains the 3 -hydroxyl and does not obligately terminate the chain. tag Concept front Which viral enzyme is responsible for the initial phosphorylation activation of Ganciclovir in CMV infections? back CMV UL97 kinase. tag Concept front What is the most significant dose-limiting adverse effect of Ganciclovir and Valganciclovir? back Myelosuppression particularly severe neutropenia and thrombocytopenia . tag Clinical front Why is Cidofovir effective against Ganciclovir-resistant CMV or Acyclovir-resistant HSV? back It is a nucleotide analog already monophosphorylated and does not require activation by viral kinases e.g. UL97 or thymidine kinase . tag Pharmacology front What must be co-administered with Cidofovir to prevent irreversible severe nephrotoxicity? back Probenecid to block tubular secretion and aggressive IV normal saline. tag Pharmacology front What antiviral drug is an inorganic pyrophosphate analog that directly inhibits viral DNA polymerase without requiring kinase activation? back Foscarnet. tag Pharmacology front What metabolic abnormalities are classically associated with Foscarnet toxicity? back Severe symptomatic hypocalcemia and hypomagnesemia due to ion chelation leading to seizures. tag Clinical front Why is Maribavir contraindicated for use in combination with Ganciclovir? back Maribavir directly inhibits the CMV UL97 kinase which is the exact enzyme required to activate Ganciclovir rendering Ganciclovir ineffective. tag Pharmacology anking match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best pearls title Probenecid with Cidofovir content Cidofovir s major dose-limiting toxicity is severe proximal tubular damage. By co-administering probenecid the organic anion transporters in the kidney are blocked preventing the accumulation of cidofovir in the tubular cells. title Letermovir Drug Interactions content Letermovir is a crucial prophylactic agent for CMV in transplant patients but it is a strong CYP3A4 inhibitor. This requires careful dose adjustments of concurrent immunosuppressants like cyclosporine and tacrolimus. title Viral vs Host Kinases content Acyclovir and Ganciclovir depend on a single viral kinase for their first phosphorylation giving them high specificity for infected cells. Subsequent phosphorylations rely entirely on host cell kinases. questions id 1 question A 45-year-old man who recently underwent an allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell transplant presents with worsening vision and floaters. He is diagnosed with CMV retinitis. Treatment with an intravenous antiviral is initiated. Two weeks later laboratory results show an absolute neutrophil count ANC of 400 cells/mm³ normal 1500-8000 . Which of the following drugs was most likely administered? options Acyclovir Foscarnet Cidofovir Ganciclovir Letermovir correctAnswer 4 rationale Ganciclovir is a first-line agent for CMV infections such as retinitis. Its most prominent and dose-limiting adverse effect is severe bone marrow suppression manifesting particularly as neutropenia. id 2 question A 32-year-old woman with a history of recurrent genital herpes is admitted to the hospital with severe HSV encephalitis. She is started on high-dose intravenous acyclovir. Three days later her serum creatinine acutely rises from 0.8 mg/dL to 2.9 mg/dL. Which of the following is the most appropriate step to have prevented this complication? options Co-administration of probenecid Aggressive intravenous normal saline hydration Routine monitoring of complete blood counts Supplementing calcium and magnesium Switching to oral valacyclovir correctAnswer 2 rationale Intravenous acyclovir can precipitate in the renal tubules causing crystalline nephropathy and acute kidney injury. This is best prevented by ensuring adequate and aggressive intravenous fluid hydration prior to and during the infusion. id 3 question A patient with advanced HIV develops an acyclovir-resistant HSV infection. The viral isolate is found to lack functional thymidine kinase activity. The physician decides to start an antiviral that does not require intracellular activation by viral or host cellular kinases. Which of the following medications was selected? options Foscarnet Cidofovir Penciclovir Ganciclovir Famciclovir correctAnswer 1 rationale Foscarnet is a pyrophosphate analog that directly binds and inhibits viral DNA polymerase. It does not require any phosphorylation by viral or host kinases for its activity making it effective against thymidine kinase-deficient strains. id 4 question A 55-year-old renal transplant recipient is diagnosed with a highly resistant strain of CMV that contains mutations in the UL97 gene. The medical team decides to treat with cidofovir. To minimize the risk of severe drug-induced toxicity which of the following mechanisms must be pharmacologically blocked during cidofovir administration? options Cytochrome P450 3A4 metabolism Renal tubular organic anion transport Gastrointestinal P-glycoprotein efflux Viral terminase complex Divalent cation chelation correctAnswer 2 rationale Cidofovir causes severe irreversible nephrotoxicity by accumulating in proximal tubule cells. Probenecid is given to block the organic anion transporters in the renal tubules reducing this accumulation. id 5 question An infectious disease specialist is reviewing a new prophylactic regimen for a stem cell transplant patient who is CMV seropositive. The patient is currently taking cyclosporine to prevent graft rejection. The physician prescribes Letermovir to prevent CMV reactivation. Which of the following statements best describes the mechanism of action of Letermovir? options It is a competitive inhibitor of the CMV UL97 kinase. It acts as an obligate chain terminator for viral DNA polymerase. It binds to the pyrophosphate-binding site of viral DNA polymerase. It binds to pUL56 inhibiting the viral DNA terminase complex. It is a cytosine nucleotide analog that blocks viral replication. correctAnswer 4 rationale Letermovir uniquely targets the CMV DNA terminase complex by binding to the pUL56 subunit inhibiting the processing and packaging of viral DNA. It is highly specific for CMV and is primarily used for prophylaxis in transplant recipients."}, {"id": "l102", "title": "Lecture #102: Pharmacology: CNS Infections", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l102 module Neuro l102 Lecture 102 Pharmacology CNS Infections lecturer M. Pino Ph.D. Pharmacology pdf content/L102 HighYield Render.pdf Lecture 102 Pharmacology CNS Infections\\n\\n Lecturer M. Pino Ph.D.\\n Session Lecture 102\\n\\nThis lecture covers pharmacological management of CNS infections bacterial viral fungal meningitis . Empiric treatment requires broad-spectrum bactericidal drugs that cross the blood-brain barrier. Key drug classes mechanisms toxicities resistance patterns and adjunctive glucocorticoids are discussed.\\n\\n Antimicrobial Principles Slide 5 \\n- Bactericidal agents kill bacteria — essential for meningitis where host immunity alone is insufficient.\\n- Bacteriostatic agents inhibit growth relying on host defenses. Not preferred as monotherapy for meningitis.\\n- Broad spectrum gram-positive + gram-negative is vital for empiric therapy before pathogen identification.\\n\\n Cell Wall Inhibitors Beta-Lactams Slide 8 \\nAll share a beta-lactam ring; bactericidal via binding PBPs and blocking transpeptidation peptidoglycan cross-linking .\\n\\n Penicillins & Aminopenicillins \\n- Penicillin G/Dicloxacillin narrow-spectrum gram-positive. Dicloxacillin covers MRSA.\\n- Ampicillin broader spectrum; used for Listeria meningitis elderly pregnant immunosuppressed .\\n- Anti-pseudomonal Piperacillin Ticarcillin Carbenicillin.\\n- Adverse effects anaphylaxis pseudomembranous colitis interstitial nephritis hapten reaction with eosinophilia/rash .\\n- Probenecid blocks renal tubular secretion prolonging half-life.\\n\\n Cephalosporins \\n- Third-generation Ceftriaxone Ceftazidime = mainstay for empiric meningitis. Bactericidal broad-spectrum excellent CSF penetration. Fifth-generation Ceftaroline covers MRSA.\\n- 1-2% cross-reactivity with penicillin allergies older agents with similar side chains . Some with methylthiotetrazole ring cause bleeding vitamin K epoxide reductase inhibition and disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol.\\n\\n Carbapenems & Monobactams \\n- Imipenem always given with Cilastatin renal dehydropeptidase inhibitor to prevent toxic metabolite formation and seizure risk.\\n- Aztreonam Gram-negative aerobes only. Safe in true penicillin anaphylaxis no cross-reactive side chains .\\n\\n Beta-Lactam Resistance Slide 10 \\n- Beta-lactamases Cleave the ring. Countered by suicide inhibitors Clavulanic acid Sulbactam Tazobactam — e.g. Augmentin = Amoxicillin + Clavulanic acid.\\n- Mutated PBPs Primary MRSA mechanism.\\n- Porin down-regulation & efflux pumps Gram-negative resistance.\\n\\n Cell Wall Inhibitors Non-Beta-Lactams Slide 16 \\n\\n Vancomycin \\n- Binds D-alanyl-D-alanine terminus of peptidoglycan precursors blocking cross-linking. Effective against gram-positives.\\n- Use MRSA meningitis with 3rd-gen cephalosporin ; oral for severe C. difficile .\\n- Toxicity ototoxicity nephrotoxicity caution with aminoglycosides/loop diuretics . Rapid IV infusion causes Red Man Syndrome direct mast cell degranulation releasing histamine — NOT IgE-mediated; treat by slowing infusion + diphenhydramine .\\n- Resistance VRE replaces D-alanine with D-lactate → use Linezolid Daptomycin or Ceftaroline.\\n\\n Cycloserine Blocks alanine racemase L-ala → D-ala . Second-line for MDR-TB. Significant CNS toxicity.\\n\\n Protein Synthesis Inhibitors Slide 23 \\n\\n Aminoglycosides Gentamicin Amikacin Tobramycin \\n- Irreversibly bind 30S subunit. Bactericidal. Post-antibiotic effect allows once-daily dosing.\\n- Neuromuscular blockade Decrease presynaptic ACh release — dangerous in myasthenia gravis or with paralytics.\\n- Ototoxicity Free radical damage to inner ear hair cells; irreversible deafness including fetal .\\n- Nephrotoxicity Proximal tubule accumulation → reversible non-oliguric ATN.\\n\\n Chloramphenicol \\n- Binds 50S subunit inhibits peptidyltransferase. Bacteriostatic broad-spectrum. Extremely lipophilic → excellent BBB penetration brain abscesses Rickettsial meningitis/RMSF .\\n- Bone marrow suppression Dose-dependent reversible anemia OR idiosyncratic irreversible aplastic anemia.\\n- Gray Baby Syndrome Neonates lack UDP-glucuronyl transferase → drug accumulates → cyanosis hypotension shock.\\n\\n Tetracyclines Doxycycline Minocycline Demeclocycline \\n- Bind 30S subunit block aminoacyl-tRNA access.\\n- Doxycycline = DOC for Lyme disease and Rickettsia. Demeclocycline inhibits ADH → treats SIADH.\\n- Chelates Ca2+/Fe2+/antacids → impairs own absorption and deposits in developing bone/teeth contraindicated in pregnancy and children <8 .\\n- Expired tetracyclines degrade into toxins causing Fanconi Syndrome proximal tubule damage . Minocycline causes vestibular toxicity.\\n\\n Fluoroquinolones Slide 32 \\nCiprofloxacin Levofloxacin Moxifloxacin \\ -floxacin\\ . Broad-spectrum bactericidal.\\n- Inhibit DNA gyrase topoisomerase II and topoisomerase IV.\\n- Older agents Cipro = gram-negatives; newer respiratory quinolones Levo Moxi = strong gram-positive coverage.\\n- QT prolongation cardiac K+ channel blockade . Tendon rupture upregulate matrix metalloproteinases; risk heightened in elderly concurrent steroids . Contraindicated in pregnancy and children <18.\\n- Chelation by dietary cations; photosensitivity.\\n\\n Rifampin Slide 18 \\n- Inhibits DNA-dependent RNA polymerase .\\n- TB multidrug therapy RIPE 6 months ; prophylaxis for meningococcal contacts .\\n- Strong CYP450 inducer reduces OCP efficacy . Harmless red-orange discoloration of all body fluids.\\n\\n Antiviral Agents Slide 39 \\nMost common viral meningitis Enterovirus supportive care only . HSV-2 requires targeted therapy.\\n\\n- Acyclovir/Valacyclovir Guanosine analogs requiring first phosphorylation by viral thymidine kinase then host kinases → active triphosphate inhibits DNA polymerase. IV acyclovir crystallizes in renal tubules → obstructive nephropathy requires aggressive hydration . Resistance mutated viral TK → use Foscarnet.\\n- Ganciclovir/Valganciclovir 100x more potent against CMV . Dose-limiting toxicity bone marrow suppression neutropenia .\\n- Foscarnet Pyrophosphate analog; directly inhibits viral DNA polymerase without kinase activation salvage for acyclovir-resistant strains . Chelates calcium → hypocalcemia ; renal Mg2+ wasting → arrhythmia risk.\\n- Neuraminidase inhibitors Oseltamivir Zanamivir Block viral neuraminidase preventing influenza virion release.\\n\\n Antifungal Agents Slide 45 \\nFungal meningitis targets immunocompromised patients e.g. Cryptococcus in HIV/AIDS .\\n\\n- Amphotericin B Binds ergosterol in fungal membrane creating pores → ion leak → cell death. Broad-spectrum fungicidal. Toxicity \\ shake and bake\\ infusion reaction premedicate with NSAIDs ; nephrotoxicity binds mammalian cholesterol vasospasms renal arterioles ; K+/Mg2+ wasting → cardiac arrhythmias . Liposomal formulations reduce renal toxicity.\\n- Azoles Fluconazole Itraconazole Voriconazole Block 14-alpha-sterol demethylase lanosterol → ergosterol . Fluconazole has excellent CSF penetration. All inhibit CYP450 → drug interactions. Ketoconazole suppresses androgen synthesis → gynecomastia.\\n- Flucytosine 5-FC Deaminated inside fungal cells to 5-FU → halts pyrimidine synthesis. Often combined with Amphotericin B for synergy. Toxicity bone marrow suppression.\\n\\n Adjunct Glucocorticoid Therapy\\nDexamethasone given with the first antibiotic dose to suppress the inflammatory surge as bacteria lyse. Blocks phospholipase A2 suppressing prostaglandins/leukotrienes. Extended steroid use risks hyperglycemia osteoporosis immunosuppression hypertension.\\n\\n Empirical Therapy Summary\\n\\n| Scenario | Regimen | Rationale |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Empiric | 3rd-gen cephalosporin + Vancomycin | Bactericidal broad-spectrum BBB penetration MRSA coverage |\\n| Immunocompromised/Elderly | Add Ampicillin | Covers Listeria |\\n| Pediatric/Pregnant | Beta-lactams | Avoids bone/teeth toxicity of tetracyclines/fluoroquinolones |\\n| Penicillin Allergy | Moxifloxacin or Aztreonam + Vancomycin | Bypasses beta-lactam cross-reactivity |\\n| Contact Prophylaxis | Rifampin | Meningococcal chemoprophylaxis |\\n\\n highyield\\nEmpiric meningitis treatment 3rd-gen cephalosporin Ceftriaxone + Vancomycin . In elderly pregnant or immunocompromised patients add Ampicillin for Listeria coverage. Adjust to targeted therapy upon culture results.\\n \\n\\n highyield\\nAminoglycosides post-antibiotic effect once-daily dosing but triple threat of ototoxicity irreversible nephrotoxicity ATN and neuromuscular blockade contraindicated with myasthenia gravis or paralytics .\\n \\n\\n mnemonic\\n Protein Synthesis Inhibitors \\n BUY AT 30 CCEL at 50 \\n\\n- AT 30 30S A minoglycosides bactericidal T etracyclines bacteriostatic \\n- CCEL at 50 50S C hloramphenicol C lindamycin E rythromycin Macrolides L inezolid\\n BandB Infectious Disease > Bacteria > Antibiotics SketchyPharm Antimicrobials > Antibiotics SketchyPharm Antimicrobials > Antivirals FirstAid Microbiology > Antimicrobials SketchyPharm Antimicrobials > Antifungals What is the key difference between a bactericidal and bacteriostatic antibiotic? Bactericidal antibiotics kill the bacteria outright while bacteriostatic antibiotics only inhibit their replication forcing reliance on the host immune system. Concept Why is Cilastatin co-administered with Imipenem? Cilastatin inhibits renal dehydropeptidase blocking the drug s degradation in the kidney and avoiding the accumulation of a highly neurotoxic/seizure-inducing metabolite. Pharmacology What unique feature of Aztreonam makes it clinically indispensable? Aztreonam is a monobactam completely devoid of structural cross-reactivity with penicillins; it is safe for patients with a true penicillin anaphylactic allergy. Clinical What is the mechanism of action of Vancomycin? Vancomycin tightly binds to the D-alanyl-D-alanine terminus of peptidoglycan precursors heavily blocking cell wall synthesis in gram-positive bacteria. Pharmacology A patient experiences sudden flushing and pruritus after a rapid IV infusion of Vancomycin. What is the mechanism of this response? Red Man Syndrome. It is caused by direct Mast Cell degranulation releasing histamine; it is not an IgE-mediated allergy. Clinical What is the major danger of co-administering Aminoglycosides alongside neuromuscular blockers? Aminoglycosides heavily decrease acetylcholine release from the pre-synaptic terminal worsening or triggering dangerous neuromuscular blockade and profound weakness. Clinical What is the root pathophysiology causing Gray Baby Syndrome in neonates receiving Chloramphenicol? Neonates lack sufficient UDP-glucuronyl transferase in their premature livers making them unable to conjugate and excrete the drug. Pathophysiology Which antibiotic vividly deposits in newly calcifying bone and developing teeth? Tetracyclines. They strongly chelate divalent/trivalent cations like calcium causing permanent tooth discoloration. Pharmacology What is the classic severe connective tissue complication of treating an athlete with Fluoroquinolones? Tendon rupture e.g. Achilles tendon . Fluoroquinolones significantly upregulate matrix metalloproteinases which destroy cartilage. Clinical Patients placed on Rifampin must be warned of what specific harmless side effect? Red-orange discoloration of all bodily secretions prominently including sweat tears and urine. Clinical What is the required initial activation step for Acyclovir inside an infected cell? Acyclovir is a prodrug that strictly requires its first phosphorylation to be performed by the viral Thymidine Kinase. Concept What is the primary dose-limiting toxicity of Ganciclovir when treating CMV? Profound bone marrow suppression commonly manifesting as extreme neutropenia. Clinical What systemic electrolyte abnormalities does Foscarnet classically induce? It dangerously chelates calcium causing powerful hypocalcemia and aggressive renal wasting of magnesium. Clinical What is the mechanism of action of Amphotericin B? It binds specifically to ergosterol in the fungal membrane violently tearing physical pores that leak out essential cellular ions killing the cell. Pharmacology match In-house lecture is best resources In-house lecture is best Vancomycin Toxicity Mechanism Red Man Syndrome is caused by rapid infusion leading to direct mast cell degranulation of histamine not an IgE-mediated anaphylactic allergy. Premedicate with diphenhydramine and infuse slowly. Empiric Meningitis Regimen A third-generation cephalosporin Ceftriaxone plus Vancomycin is the gold standard for empiric treatment. If the patient is elderly pregnant or immunocompromised Ampicillin must be added to cover Listeria. Acyclovir Crystal Nephropathy Intravenous acyclovir has a propensity to precipitate and crystallize inside the renal tubules. Aggressive pre-and-post-hydration with normal saline is mandatory to physically flush the drug out and stop obstructive kidney injury. Amphotericin Cardiotoxicity While classically nephrotoxic Amphotericin creates membrane pores that leak large amounts of K+ and Mg2+. This massive electrolyte wasting directly triggers severe and deadly cardiac arrhythmias. questions 1 A 68-year-old male is hospitalized for severe pneumonia and is placed on empiric antibiotics. During his stay he develops generalized muscle weakness and difficulty breathing. Review of his medical history indicates he has well-controlled myasthenia gravis. His symptoms rapidly worsened shortly after the administration of an antibiotic designed to target an aerobic gram-negative pathogen. Which of the following is the mechanism of the antibiotic most likely responsible for his sudden deterioration? Inhibition of cell wall transpeptidation Bacteriostatic inhibition of the 50S ribosomal subunit Irreversible binding to the 30S ribosomal subunit Inhibition of bacterial DNA gyrase Inhibition of 14-alpha-sterol demethylase 3 The patient received an aminoglycoside e.g. Gentamicin or Tobramycin to treat a gram-negative infection. Aminoglycosides bind irreversibly to the 30S ribosomal subunit to become bactericidal. However they carry a dangerous side effect of decreasing acetylcholine release from the pre-synaptic nerve terminal exacerbating neuromuscular blockade and precipitating acute crises in patients with myasthenia gravis. 2 A 23-year-old college student presents to the student health clinic reporting that his urine and tears have turned a persistent bright red-orange color. He strongly denies any dysuria flank pain or visual disturbances. Two days ago his roommate was rapidly hospitalized for Neisseria meningitidis. Which of the following most accurately describes the drug this patient was given for prophylaxis? It selectively inhibits viral thymidine kinase It aggressively inhibits the synthesis of ergosterol It inhibits RNA polymerase and strongly induces CYP450 It competitively inhibits dihydrofolate reductase It binds D-alanyl-D-alanine to disrupt cell wall synthesis 3 The patient is taking Rifampin as a prophylactic measure against meningococcal disease spreading in his dorm. Rifampin works by inhibiting bacterial DNA-dependent RNA polymerase. It classically secretes a harmless red-orange coloration in bodily fluids. Furthermore it is a formidable inducer of hepatic CYP450 enzymes. 3 A 35-year-old pregnant woman in her 34th week of gestation presents with nuchal rigidity high fever and photophobia. Lumbar puncture confirms bacterial meningitis. Her history is significant for a recorded life-threatening anaphylactic reaction to amoxicillin. Seeking to prevent structural cartilage toxicity or tooth discoloration in the fetus the physician opts out of fluoroquinolones and tetracyclines. The pathogen is subsequently identified as a strictly aerobic gram-negative rod. Which of the following beta-lactam antibiotics is safest to administer? Cefazolin Piperacillin Ceftriaxone Aztreonam Imipenem-Cilastatin 4 Aztreonam is a monobactam structurally designed solely for gram-negative aerobes. It crucially lacks the standard immunogenic side-chains inherent in penicillins and cephalosporins. It is universally considered safe to give to patients bearing a true documented anaphylactic penicillin allergy. 4 A 55-year-old severely immunocompromised HIV patient is aggressively treated with intravenous medications for confirmed Cytomegalovirus CMV retinitis. Three weeks into continuous therapy routine laboratory testing reveals a critically depressed absolute neutrophil count ANC of 300 cells/mm\\u00b3 Normal 1500-8000 alongside anemia and thrombocytopenia. The treating physician pauses the current regimen replacing it with Foscarnet. What enzyme does the initially prescribed drug utilize to achieve its active form before inhibiting DNA polymerase? Host cellular tyrosine kinase Bacterial DNA gyrase Host hepatic UDP-glucuronyl transferase Viral neuraminidase Viral thymidine kinase / UL97 kinase 5 The patient was experiencing severe bone marrow suppression caused by Ganciclovir. Ganciclovir is highly potent against CMV but requires initial activation. Depending on the herpesvirus strain like CMV it is preferentially phosphorylated by viral-specific enzymes like UL97 kinase or thymidine kinase prior to final activation by host kinases. Since Foscarnet doesn t need kinase activation it was utilized as a substitute. 5 An 81-year-old woman with a history of recurrent cystitis is treated with repeated courses of expired oral antibiotics she kept in her medical cabinet. She presents to the clinic lethargic passing profound volumes of dilute urine and displaying glucosuria alongside hypokalemia despite having a normal serum glucose. Which of the following antimicrobial classes did she most likely consume leading to her current condition? Aminoglycosides Tetracyclines Cephalosporins Macrolides Fluoroquinolones 2 The patient is presenting with Fanconi Syndrome a severe toxic form of proximal renal tubular dysfunction resulting in the mass wasting of glucose amino acids and bicarbonate into the urine. This is a classic adverse complication resulting specifically from the ingestion of outdated or chemically degraded tetracyclines."}, {"id": "l103", "title": "Lecture #103: Pathology of Skeletal Muscle", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l103 module Neuro l103 Lecture 103 Pathology of Skeletal Muscle lecturer W. Zaloga D.O. Pathology pdf content/L103 HighYield Render.pdf Lecture 103 Pathology of Skeletal Muscle\\n\\n Lecturer W. Zaloga D.O.\\n Session Lecture 103\\n\\nThis lecture covers skeletal muscle pathology normal muscle structure inherited disorders muscular dystrophies channelopathies congenital myopathies metabolic and mitochondrial myopathies inflammatory myopathies and autoimmune neuromuscular junction disorders. A key theme is distinguishing neurogenic from myopathic patterns on biopsy.\\n\\n Normal Muscle Structure and the Sarcomere Slide 3 \\nSkeletal muscle fibers are multinucleated cells with peripherally located nuclei beneath the sarcolemma. The sarcomere is the contractile unit A-band myosin dark I-band actin light H-band and Z-disc. Creatine kinase CK at the M-line catalyzes ADP + phosphocreatine to ATP + creatine providing a rapid ATP reserve. When muscle is damaged CK is released into blood serum CK = clinical marker of muscle injury .\\n\\n Myofiber Types Slide 5 \\nType 1 and Type 2 fibers normally interdigitate in a checkerboard pattern on NADH-TR stain.\\n\\n| Feature | Type 1 Slow Twitch | Type 2 Fast Twitch |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Metabolism | Oxidative phosphorylation | Anaerobic glycolysis |\\n| Mitochondria | High | Low |\\n| Fatigue Resistance | High endurance | Low rapid fatigue |\\n| Function | Posture sustained | Sprinting resistance |\\n| NADH-TR Stain | Dark | Lighter with speckling |\\n\\n highyield\\nThe normal checkerboard pattern is lost in neurogenic injury. When denervated fibers are reinnervated by sprouting from adjacent healthy motor neurons large groups of the same fiber type cluster together fiber-type grouping .\\n \\n\\n Myogenic vs. Neurogenic Injury Slide 10 \\n\\n- Myopathic Segmental degeneration/regeneration of individual fibers scattered not grouped . Elevated serum CK. Regenerating fibers show central nucleation and basophilic staining. Chronic fibrosis and fatty infiltration.\\n- Neurogenic Grouped atrophy of angular flattened fibers. Adjacent healthy motor neurons sprout to reinnervate producing fiber-type grouping. Surrounding fibers may hypertrophy.\\n\\n highyield\\nNeurogenic = groups of angular atrophic fibers denervation . Myopathic = random mixture of necrotic regenerating hypertrophied and atrophic fibers with increased central nucleation.\\n \\n\\n Spinal Muscular Atrophy Type 1 Werdnig-Hoffmann Slide 19 \\nAutosomal recessive disorder SMN gene causing progressive motor neuron loss and skeletal muscle denervation. SMA1 presents in infancy with generalized hypotonia floppy infant within 4 months; death by age 2-3 from respiratory failure. Biopsy shows grouped denervation atrophy neurogenic pattern with fiber-type grouping.\\n\\n Muscular Dystrophies Duchenne and Becker Slide 20 \\nBoth are X-linked recessive mutations in the dystrophin gene. Dystrophin connects the actin cytoskeleton to the ECM via a glycoprotein complex; without it contraction damages the sarcolemma.\\n\\n- Duchenne DMD Dystrophin completely absent frameshift/nonsense mutations . Onset ages 2-5. Proximal weakness pelvic girdle first . Pseudohypertrophy of calves replaced by fat/fibrous tissue . Gower s sign climbing up own legs to stand . Elevated CK. Cardiomyopathy mild intellectual impairment. Death from respiratory insufficiency in 20s-30s . Biopsy segmental degeneration fiber size variation progressive fatty/fibrous replacement.\\n- Becker BMD Dystrophin partially functional in-frame deletions . Later onset slower progression near-normal lifespan.\\n\\n Myotonic Dystrophy Slide 25 \\nAutosomal dominant trinucleotide repeat disorder affecting myotonin protein kinase causing chloride channel dysfunction. Chloride channels are essential for relaxation; dysfunction causes myotonia inability to relax after contraction . Hallmark grip myotonia cannot release a sustained grip . Onset 20-40 years. Progressive wasting cardiac arrhythmias elevated CPK.\\n\\n Ion Channel Myopathies Channelopathies Slide 26 \\n- Calcium channel mutations Most common cause of hypokalemic periodic paralysis.\\n- Chloride channel mutations Myotonic dystrophy.\\n- Ryanodine receptor mutations Central core disease malignant hyperthermia.\\n\\n Malignant Hyperthermia Slide 28 \\nAutosomal dominant ryanodine receptor RyR mutation. Triggered by halogenated inhaled anesthetics halothane sevoflurane or succinylcholine. Uncontrolled calcium release from SR causes severe muscle tetany and hyperthermia >106 F . Treatment dantrolene RyR antagonist blocking calcium release .\\n\\n Congenital Myopathies Slide 32 \\n- Central Core Disease RyR mutation; hypotonia at birth; biopsy shows central cores of reduced enzyme activity.\\n- Nemaline Myopathy AD or AR; thin filament protein mutations; floppy baby syndrome; biopsy shows nemaline rods on Gomori trichrome dark spindle-shaped particles beneath sarcolemma .\\n\\n Metabolic Myopathies\\n\\n| Disease | Enzyme Deficiency | Key Features | Trigger |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| CPT2 Deficiency | Carnitine palmitoyltransferase 2 | Toxic fatty acid buildup hypotonia dilated cardiomyopathy | Exercise/fasting |\\n| McArdle Disease Type 5 | Myophosphorylase | Painful cramps myoglobinuria flat lactate curve normal glucose | Exercise |\\n| Pompe Disease Type 2 | Acid alpha-glucosidase | Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy severe hypotonia in infants | Progressive |\\n\\n Mitochondrial Myopathies Slide 39 \\nMitochondrial DNA mutations with maternal inheritance . Impaired ATP generation affects high-demand tissues muscle heart neurons . Features muscle weakness elevated CK cardiac dysrhythmias seizures chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia CPEO ptosis diplopia .\\n\\n MERRF Myoclonic Epilepsy with Ragged Red Fibers Progressive myoclonus generalized seizures limb-girdle weakness. Biopsy hallmark ragged red fibers on Gomori trichrome sub-sarcolemmal aggregates of abnormal mitochondria .\\n\\n Leber s Hereditary Optic Neuropathy Primarily adult males; CPEO and visual loss.\\n\\n Inflammatory Myopathies\\n\\n Dermatomyositis Slide 46 \\nImmune-mediated attack on muscle capillaries complement + CD4+ T cells in perimysial distribution causing ischemic myocyte necrosis. Peak 50-60 female predominance.\\n\\n- Skin Heliotrope rash lilac eyelid discoloration + periorbital edema Gottron s papules knuckles/elbows/knees mechanic s hands.\\n- Symmetric proximal weakness dysphagia possible. 24% have associated malignancy. \\n- Biopsy perifascicular atrophy perimysial CD4+ infiltration complement in capillaries.\\n- Antibodies Anti-MI2 skin Anti-JO1 lung/arthritis . Treatment immunosuppressives.\\n\\n Polymyositis Slide 50 \\nSimilar to dermatomyositis but no skin involvement . CD8+ cytotoxic T-cells directly attack individual fibers in endomysial distribution. Peak 30-60 female predominance. Proximal weakness elevated CK.\\n\\n| Feature | Dermatomyositis | Polymyositis |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Skin | Heliotrope Gottron s | None |\\n| Inflammation | Perimysial CD4+ | Endomysial CD8+ |\\n| Vascular Injury | Yes complement | No |\\n| Biopsy | Perifascicular atrophy | Scattered CD8+ infiltration |\\n| Malignancy | 24% | Lower |\\n\\n Inclusion Body Myositis Slide 53 \\nMost common inflammatory myopathy in patients over 68 . Affects distal muscles first unlike DM/PM can be asymmetric. Biopsy inclusion bodies degraded organelles some CD8+ infiltration vacuolization. cN1A antibody positive in ~50%. Poorly responsive to immunosuppressives.\\n\\n Myasthenia Gravis Slide 56 \\nAutoantibodies against postsynaptic AChR reduce receptor density. Painless fatigable weakness worsening with exertion improving with rest. Diplopia/ptosis ocular muscles first . Thymic abnormalities thymoma 10% thymic hyperplasia 30% young patients . EMG decremental response. Treatment AChE inhibitors also diagnostic plasmapheresis immunosuppressives thymectomy.\\n\\n Lambert-Eaton Syndrome Slide 57 \\nAutoantibodies against presynaptic voltage-gated Ca2+ channels prevent ACh release. Weakness improves with exertion opposite of MG . Autonomic symptoms dry mouth constipation . 50% have associated malignancy small cell lung CA . EMG incremental response.\\n\\n| Feature | Myasthenia Gravis | Lambert-Eaton |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Target | Postsynaptic AChR | Presynaptic Ca2+ channel |\\n| Weakness | Worsens with use | Improves with use |\\n| EMG | Decremental | Incremental |\\n| Associations | Thymoma | 50% malignancy SCLC |\\n\\n mnemonic\\n Myasthenia vs. Lambert-Eaton \\n MY-asthenia = MY muscles get WEAKER with use \\n- MG Antibodies destroy AChR post-synaptic . More use = more fatigue = worse.\\n- Lambert-Eaton Antibodies block Ca2+ channels pre-synaptic . More use = more Ca2+ accumulates = improves.\\n BandB Musculoskeletal > Muscle Disorders Pathoma Musculoskeletal > Skeletal Muscle Pathology FirstAid Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Muscular Dystrophies SketchyPath Musculoskeletal > Neuromuscular Junction Disorders Physeo Pathology > Musculoskeletal > Myopathies SketchyBiochem Metabolism > Carbohydrates > Glycogen Storage Diseases What is the function of creatine kinase CK at the M-line of the sarcomere? CK catalyzes the reversible conversion of ADP + phosphocreatine to ATP + creatine serving as a rapid ATP reserve for the muscle. Concept What is fiber-type grouping and what does it indicate? Fiber-type grouping is the loss of the normal checkerboard pattern of Type 1 and Type 2 fibers replaced by large clusters of the same type. It indicates neurogenic injury with denervation followed by reinnervation via axonal sprouting. Concept What is the inheritance pattern and protein defect in Duchenne muscular dystrophy? X-linked recessive with complete absence of dystrophin a subsarcolemmal protein connecting actin to the extracellular matrix. Clinical What is Gower s sign? A maneuver where a child with proximal muscle weakness climbs up their own legs with their hands to stand from the floor classically seen in Duchenne muscular dystrophy. Clinical What is pseudohypertrophy of the calf muscles? Calf muscles appear enlarged despite being weak because functional muscle tissue is progressively replaced by fat and fibrous tissue seen in DMD. Clinical What is the most common cause of death in Duchenne muscular dystrophy? Respiratory insufficiency typically in the 20s-30s as the diaphragm and respiratory muscles progressively fail. Clinical What ion channel is affected in myotonic dystrophy and what is the hallmark sign? Chloride channels are affected leading to myotonia grip myotonia inability to relax a sustained grip . Clinical What is the treatment for malignant hyperthermia? Dantrolene a ryanodine receptor antagonist that blocks uncontrolled calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Clinical What triggers malignant hyperthermia? Halogenated inhaled anesthetics halothane sevoflurane and succinylcholine in patients with inherited ryanodine receptor mutations. Clinical How does McArdle disease differ from Pompe disease? McArdle Type 5 involves myophosphorylase deficiency with episodic painful cramps myoglobinuria and normal glucose. Pompe Type 2 involves lysosomal acid maltase deficiency with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and severe hypotonia. Clinical What is the diagnostic hallmark of MERRF syndrome on muscle biopsy? Ragged red fibers on modified Gomori trichrome stain representing sub-sarcolemmal accumulations of abnormal mitochondria. Clinical How do you distinguish dermatomyositis from polymyositis on biopsy? Dermatomyositis shows perimysial inflammation with CD4+ cells complement in capillaries and perifascicular atrophy. Polymyositis shows endomysial inflammation with CD8+ cytotoxic T-cells scattered around individual fibers. Clinical How does weakness differ between myasthenia gravis and Lambert-Eaton syndrome? In myasthenia gravis weakness worsens with repeated exertion. In Lambert-Eaton weakness improves with exertion. Clinical What percentage of dermatomyositis patients have an associated malignancy? 24%. Clinical match Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy DMD resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Muscular Dystrophies; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Muscle Disorders; SketchyPath > Musculoskeletal > Muscular Dystrophies match Becker Muscular Dystrophy resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Muscular Dystrophies > Becker; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Muscle Disorders match Myasthenia Gravis resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Myasthenia Gravis; SketchyPath > Musculoskeletal > NMJ Disorders; Physeo > Pathology > Musculoskeletal match Lambert-Eaton Myasthenic Syndrome resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Lambert Eaton; SketchyPath > Musculoskeletal > NMJ Disorders match Dermatomyositis and Polymyositis resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Inflammatory Myopathies; Pathoma > Musculoskeletal > Inflammatory Myopathies; BandB > Musculoskeletal match Malignant Hyperthermia resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Malignant Hyperthermia; SketchyPharm > Anesthetics > Inhaled; BandB > Musculoskeletal match McArdle Disease Type 5 Glycogen Storage Disease resources AnKing > FirstAid > Biochemistry > Metabolism > Glycogen Storage Diseases > Type 5; SketchyBiochem > Metabolism > Carbohydrates > GSD; Pixorize > Biochem > GSD > McArdle match Pompe Disease Type 2 Glycogen Storage Disease resources AnKing > FirstAid > Biochemistry > Metabolism > Glycogen Storage Diseases > Type 2; SketchyBiochem > Metabolism > Carbohydrates > GSD; Physeo > Biochem > Glycogen match Myotonic Dystrophy resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Myotonic Dystrophy; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Muscle Disorders match Mitochondrial Myopathies MERRF resources AnKing > FirstAid > Biochemistry > Metabolism > Mitochondrial Diseases; Pathoma > Musculoskeletal; BandB > Genetics > Mitochondrial Inheritance match Spinal Muscular Atrophy Werdnig-Hoffmann resources AnKing > FirstAid > Neurology > Pathology > Motor Neuron Diseases; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Muscle Disorders Neurogenic vs. Myopathic Biopsy Pattern Neurogenic injury shows grouped angular atrophic fibers denervation with eventual fiber-type grouping. Myopathic injury shows a random mixture of necrotic regenerating hypertrophied and atrophic fibers with increased central nucleation. Duchenne vs. Becker Dystrophin Duchenne = dystrophin completely ABSENT severe early onset death by 20s-30s . Becker = dystrophin partially FUNCTIONAL milder later onset near-normal lifespan . Malignant Hyperthermia Treatment Dantrolene is the specific antidote for malignant hyperthermia. It is a ryanodine receptor antagonist that blocks the uncontrolled calcium efflux causing the hypermetabolic crisis. McArdle Disease Lactate Test In McArdle disease the venous lactate curve is flat during exercise does not rise while ammonia still rises. This is because glycogen cannot be broken down to feed into glycolysis. Dermatomyositis vs. Polymyositis Inflammation Dermatomyositis = perimysial inflammation CD4+ complement with perifascicular atrophy. Polymyositis = endomysial inflammation CD8+ scattered among individual fibers. Myasthenia Gravis vs. Lambert-Eaton MG post-synaptic AChR antibodies weakness worsens with use associated with thymoma. Lambert-Eaton pre-synaptic Ca2+ channel antibodies weakness improves with use 50% have malignancy small cell lung CA . Ragged Red Fibers Ragged red fibers on Gomori trichrome stain are the hallmark of mitochondrial myopathies especially MERRF . They represent sub-sarcolemmal accumulations of abnormal mitochondria. Grip Myotonia Grip myotonia inability to release a sustained grip is the hallmark sign of myotonic dystrophy caused by chloride channel dysfunction from a trinucleotide repeat expansion. questions 1 A 4-year-old boy presents with progressive difficulty walking and frequent falls. Physical examination reveals enlarged calf muscles that feel firm on palpation and proximal muscle weakness. He uses his hands to push off his thighs when rising from the floor. Serum creatine kinase is markedly elevated. Which of the following is the most likely eventual cause of death in this patient? Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy Respiratory insufficiency Rhabdomyolysis-induced renal failure Central nervous system degeneration Malignant hyperthermia 2 This patient presents with Duchenne muscular dystrophy DMD evidenced by early childhood onset of proximal weakness pseudohypertrophy of the calves Gower s sign and markedly elevated CK. The most common cause of death in DMD is respiratory insufficiency in the 20s-30s as the diaphragm and respiratory muscles progressively fail. While cardiomyopathy is also common respiratory failure is the leading cause of death. 2 A 25-year-old male undergoes an appendectomy under general anesthesia. Shortly after induction with a halogenated inhaled anesthetic and administration of succinylcholine for intubation he develops severe muscle rigidity tachycardia tachypnea and a rapidly rising body temperature of 107 degrees F. Which of the following is the most appropriate pharmacological intervention? Acetylcholinesterase inhibitors Intravenous calcium gluconate Dantrolene Propranolol Dexamethasone 3 This patient is experiencing malignant hyperthermia a life-threatening condition caused by an inherited autosomal dominant mutation in the ryanodine receptor. Halogenated anesthetics and succinylcholine trigger uncontrolled calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum causing severe tetany and a hypermetabolic state. Dantrolene is the specific antidote; it is a ryanodine receptor antagonist that blocks calcium release. 3 A 55-year-old woman presents with symmetric proximal muscle weakness a violaceous discoloration of her eyelids with periorbital edema and scaling erythematous papules over her knuckles. Muscle biopsy shows perifascicular atrophy with perimysial inflammatory infiltrate. Which of the following cell types predominates in the inflammatory infiltrate? CD8+ cytotoxic T-cells CD4+ T-helper cells Natural killer cells B lymphocytes Neutrophils 2 This patient presents with dermatomyositis characterized by the heliotrope rash violaceous eyelid discoloration with edema Gottron s papules scaling eruptions over the knuckles and proximal muscle weakness. The perimysial inflammatory infiltrate in dermatomyositis is predominantly composed of CD4+ T-helper cells with complement deposition in capillaries in contrast to polymyositis which features endomysial CD8+ cytotoxic T-cells. 4 A 35-year-old female presents with proximal muscle weakness that has gradually worsened over six months. She reports difficulty washing her hair and climbing stairs. She has no skin findings. Muscle biopsy reveals endomysial lymphocytic inflammation surrounding individual muscle fibers with the inflammatory cells staining positive for CD8. Serum creatine kinase is elevated. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? Dermatomyositis Polymyositis Inclusion body myositis Duchenne muscular dystrophy Myasthenia gravis 2 This patient presents with proximal muscle weakness without skin involvement and the biopsy shows endomysial inflammation with CD8+ T-cells which is the classic histologic pattern of polymyositis. Dermatomyositis would show perimysial inflammation with CD4+ cells and skin findings. Inclusion body myositis presents in patients over 68 with distal muscle weakness. DMD presents in childhood. MG is a neuromuscular junction disorder without biopsy inflammation. 5 A 28-year-old female presents with new onset diplopia and ptosis that worsens throughout the day. Electromyography demonstrates a decremental response with repetitive nerve stimulation. CT of the chest reveals an anterior mediastinal mass. Which of the following best describes the underlying pathophysiology? Autoantibodies against presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels Autoantibodies against postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors Mutation in the ryanodine receptor causing uncontrolled calcium release Immune-mediated endomysial CD8+ T-cell infiltration Trinucleotide repeat expansion affecting chloride channels 2 This patient has myasthenia gravis an autoimmune disorder where autoantibodies destroy postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction. The weakness worsens with exertion diplopia/ptosis worse at end of day EMG shows decremental response and the anterior mediastinal mass is a thymoma which is associated with MG in ~10% of patients. Lambert-Eaton presynaptic Ca2+ channel antibodies would show improving weakness with use."}, {"id": "l104", "title": "Lecture #104: Pathology of Bone and Joints - Part 1", "module": "Unknown", "content": "id l104 module Unknown l104 Lecture 104 Pathology of Bone and Joints - Part 1 lecturer E. Petcu M.D. Pathology pdf content/L104 HighYield Render.pdf Lecture 104 Pathology of Bone and Joints - Part 1\\n\\n Lecturer E. Petcu M.D.\\n Session Lecture 104\\n\\nThis lecture surveys the major pathologic conditions of bones and joints osteoarthritis OA vs. rheumatoid arthritis RA juvenile idiopathic arthritis JIA skeletal dysplasias achondroplasia thanatophoric dysplasia osteogenesis imperfecta osteoporosis osteopetrosis osteomalacia/rickets Paget disease and osteonecrosis.\\n\\n Osteoarthritis Slides 2-7 \\nOA is the most common joint disease a mechanical/degenerative process with secondary not primary inflammation. Progressive wear causes loss of chondrocytes degradation of hyaline cartilage ECM and compensatory chondrocyte clustering that cannot keep pace with destruction. Subchondral bone scleroses eburnation osteophytes form at joint margins subchondral cysts develop and loose cartilage/bone fragments joint mice enter the joint space Slide 4 .\\n\\n- Pain worsens with activity improves with rest.\\n- Asymmetric weight-bearing joints hips knees spine and hands.\\n- Heberden nodes DIP osteophytes and Bouchard nodes PIP osteophytes .\\n- Crepitus; no systemic symptoms; non-inflammatory aspirate; no autoantibodies.\\n- Radiographs joint space narrowing osteophytes subchondral sclerosis/cysts.\\n\\n Rheumatoid Arthritis Slides 8-16 \\nRA is the most common autoimmune arthritis driven by CD4+ T cells reacting to an unknown arthritogenic antigen. About 50% of risk is linked to HLA-DR4. Environmental factors smoking infections promote citrullination of self-proteins creating neoantigens Slide 9 .\\n\\n- Pathogenesis CD4+ T cells release cytokines. IFN-gamma Th1 activates macrophages; IL-17 Th17 recruits neutrophils/monocytes; RANKL stimulates osteoclast bone resorption; TNF/IL-1 drive synovial protease release destroying cartilage. Anti-TNF biologics target this pathway.\\n- Histology Synovium shows germinal centers plasma cells and pannus edematous synovium + inflammatory cells + granulation tissue growing over and eroding cartilage . Progresses to fibrous then bony ankylosis.\\n- Autoantibodies Anti-CCP most specific ~70% of patients and RF IgM against IgG Fc ~80% less specific .\\n- Clinical Symmetric small joints hands/feet DIP spared morning stiffness systemic symptoms fever fatigue weight loss . Hand findings ulnar deviation radial deviation of wrist swan neck/boutonniere deformities Slide 13 . Lab elevated ESR CRP positive RF/anti-CCP decreased complement in joint fluid.\\n- Extra-articular Rheumatoid nodules necrotizing granulomas with fibrinoid necrosis and palisading macrophages; pulmonary nodules = Caplan syndrome vasculitis uveitis/scleritis interstitial lung disease pericarditis anemia of chronic disease Felty syndrome RA + neutropenia + splenomegaly .\\n\\n highyield\\n Osteoarthritis vs. Rheumatoid Arthritis is one of the most tested comparisons in musculoskeletal pathology \\n- OA Mechanical/degenerative asymmetric weight-bearing joints DIP involvement Heberden nodes worse with activity no systemic symptoms non-inflammatory aspirate.\\n- RA Autoimmune CD4+ T cells symmetric small joints of hands/feet DIP spared worse in the morning systemic symptoms anti-CCP/RF positive pannus formation.\\n \\n\\n| Feature | Osteoarthritis | Rheumatoid Arthritis |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Mechanism | Mechanical/degenerative | Autoimmune CD4+ T cells |\\n| Inflammation | Secondary minimal | Primary severe |\\n| Symmetry | Asymmetric | Symmetric |\\n| DIP Joints | Involved Heberden nodes | Spared |\\n| Pain Pattern | Worse with activity | Worse in the morning |\\n| Systemic | None | Fever fatigue weight loss |\\n| Pathology | Osteophytes eburnation joint mice | Pannus erosions ankylosis |\\n| Lab | Non-inflammatory aspirate | Anti-CCP RF elevated ESR/CRP |\\n| Extra-articular | None | Nodules vasculitis Felty syndrome |\\n\\n Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis Slide 16 \\nJIA is a heterogeneous group of disorders developing before age 16 lasting at least 6 weeks with unknown etiology possible HLA/PTPN22 associations . Classic presentation daily spiking fevers salmon-pink macular rash polyarthritis 2+ joints anterior uveitis. No rheumatoid nodules. Labs show leukocytosis thrombocytosis anemia positive ANA elevated ESR/CRP. About 10% develop serious disability. Treatment NSAIDs steroids methotrexate TNF inhibitors.\\n\\n correlate\\nJuvenile Idiopathic Arthritis\\n\\n- Presentation Daily spiking fevers salmon-pink macular rash polyarthritis anterior uveitis; no rheumatoid nodules.\\n- Diagnosis Age <16 arthritis 6+ weeks ANA positive elevated ESR/CRP.\\n- Lecturer s Point Salmon-pink rash with spiking fevers = hallmark of systemic JIA Still disease .\\n \\n\\n Achondroplasia Slides 17-19 \\nThe most common skeletal dysplasia and cause of dwarfism. Autosomal dominant gain-of-function mutation in FGFR3 which normally inhibits endochondral bone growth. The activating mutation exaggerates this inhibition selectively impairing long bone growth while intramembranous ossification skull facial bones clavicle remains normal Slide 18 .\\n\\n- Rhizomelic shortening proximal limbs normal trunk length frontal bossing depressed nasal bridge trident hand narrow foramen magnum lordosis genu varum.\\n- Normal lifespan intelligence reproductive status. 85% sporadic; risk increases with advanced paternal age.\\n\\n Thanatophoric dysplasia is the most common lethal form of dwarfism 1 in 20 000 . Also FGFR3 gain-of-function different mutation . Underdeveloped thoracic cavity causes respiratory insufficiency and death at birth.\\n\\n mnemonic\\n Achondroplasia = FGFR3 Gain-of-Function \\nFGFR3 normally puts the brakes on bone growth. Activating mutation = too much braking = short bones.\\nEndochondral = affected. Intramembranous = normal.\\n \\n\\n Osteogenesis Imperfecta Slides 20-21 \\nOI brittle bone disease is the most common inherited disorder of connective tissue. Mutations in COL1A1/COL1A2 cause defective type 1 collagen synthesis. Bone forms but in insufficient quantity Slide 20 .\\n\\n- Multiple fractures from minimal trauma may mimic child abuse .\\n- Blue sclera decreased collagen makes sclera translucent revealing underlying choroidal veins.\\n- Hearing loss sensorineural + conductive from middle/inner ear bone abnormalities.\\n- Dentinogenesis imperfecta small misshapen blue-yellow teeth dentin deficiency .\\n- Type 1 normal lifespan fractures decrease after puberty. Type 2 fatal in utero/perinatally.\\n\\n correlate\\nOsteogenesis Imperfecta\\n\\n- Pathophysiology AD mutations in COL1A1/COL1A2 cause defective type 1 collagen. Bone forms but too little of it.\\n- Classic Triad Fractures from minimal trauma + blue sclera + dentinogenesis imperfecta.\\n- Lecturer s Point Blue sclera = translucent collagen-deficient sclera revealing choroidal veins NOT a pigmentation change.\\n \\n\\n Osteoporosis Slides 22-26 \\nDecreased bone mass severe enough to increase fracture risk. DEXA T-score <= -2.5 defines osteoporosis. Male-to-female ratio 1 4. Two main forms \\n\\n- Senile Reduced osteoblast proliferative/biosynthetic capacity + decreased physical activity.\\n- Postmenopausal Estrogen loss increases inflammatory cytokines IL-6 TNF IL-1 increases RANKL decreases OPG stimulating osteoclast activity. Resorption dominates. Up to 2% cortical and 9% cancellous bone lost per year. Vertebral bodies most affected.\\n\\nFracture sites vertebral body most common; compression fractures causing height loss/kyphosis femoral neck highest mortality distal radius. Plain X-ray detects only after 30-40% bone loss. DEXA recommended for all women over 65. Treatment calcium/vitamin D weight-bearing exercise bisphosphonates.\\n\\n highyield\\n DEXA Scan T-Score Thresholds \\n- T >= -1.0 Normal\\n- T between -1.0 and -2.5 Osteopenia\\n- T <= -2.5 Osteoporosis\\n\\nMost common fracture sites vertebral body most common femoral neck highest mortality distal radius .\\n \\n\\n Osteopetrosis Slides 27-29 \\nRare genetic diseases with reduced bone resorption due to defective osteoclast function causing diffuse symmetric skeletal sclerosis. Despite the name petra = stone bones are brittle because they consist of unremodeled woven bone not organized lamellar bone Slide 27 .\\n\\n- Classic mutation carbonic anhydrase 2 CA2 preventing acidification of the resorption pit.\\n- Bone fills marrow space causing pancytopenia and extramedullary hematopoiesis hepatosplenomegaly .\\n- Imaging bone-in-bone pattern Erlenmeyer flask deformity flared metaphyses narrow neural foramina cranial nerve palsies deafness .\\n- Severe infantile form AR ; milder adult form AD .\\n- Treatment bone marrow transplant provides functional osteoclast precursors .\\n\\n mnemonic\\n Osteopetrosis vs. Osteoporosis \\n- Osteo petrosis = too MUCH bone but brittle/woven . Osteoclasts DON T WORK. Marrow crowded out = pancytopenia.\\n- Osteo porosis = too LITTLE bone but normal quality . Osteoclasts work TOO WELL. Marrow preserved.\\n \\n\\n Osteomalacia and Rickets Slides 30-31 \\nDefective mineralization from vitamin D deficiency low sunlight dietary deficiency kidney disease . Osteomalacia = adults osteoid not mineralized ; rickets = children growth plates not mineralized . US incidence ~1 in 1 000 due to dairy fortification.\\n\\n- Labs Low vitamin D low calcium high PTH low phosphate high alkaline phosphatase hyperactive osteoblasts compensating .\\n- Osteomalacia Looser zones insufficiency fractures at scapula ribs ulna .\\n- Rickets Epiphyseal widening metaphyseal cupping/fraying genu varum bow legs rachitic rosary beading at costochondral junctions .\\n- Treatment Vitamin D >1 000 IU/day.\\n\\n Paget Disease of Bone Slides 34-40 \\nDisordered bone remodeling with increased but structurally abnormal bone mass. Affects ~1% of US adults over 40. SQSTM1 gene mutations 50% familial 10% sporadic increase NF-kB/osteoclast activity. Possible measles virus contribution.\\n\\n- Three phases 1 Osteolytic bone resorption 2 Mixed resorption + formation; osteoclasts with up to 100 nuclei 3 Osteosclerotic/burnout osteoblast-dominant . All three may coexist.\\n- Histologic hallmark Mosaic jigsaw puzzle pattern of lamellar bone with prominent cement lines.\\n- Often asymptomatic. Monostotic ~50% or polyostotic. Axial skeleton/proximal femur in ~80%.\\n- Skull thickening with hearing loss picture-frame vertebrae anterior bowing of femur/tibia chalk-stick fractures high-output heart failure AV shunts leontiasis ossea sarcomatous transformation <1% .\\n- Lab Elevated alkaline phosphatase; calcium phosphorus PTH all NORMAL.\\n- Treatment Calcitonin bisphosphonates.\\n\\n correlate\\nPaget Disease of Bone\\n\\n- Key Lab Elevated alk phos with NORMAL calcium/phosphorus/PTH distinguishes from hyperparathyroidism and osteomalacia .\\n- Histology Mosaic jigsaw puzzle pattern is pathognomonic.\\n- Complications High-output heart failure secondary OA sarcoma <1% .\\n \\n\\n Osteonecrosis Avascular Necrosis Slides 40-41 \\nIschemic death of bone and marrow. Most common causes fractures and corticosteroid use. Most common site femoral head medial circumflex femoral artery . Bilateral in 80% of cases. Other risk factors alcohol bisphosphonates connective tissue disease sickle cell crisis Gaucher disease decompression sickness.\\n\\n- Medullary infarcts geographic trabecular bone + marrow vs. subchondral infarcts wedge-shaped subchondral plate . Overlying cartilage survives via synovial fluid nutrition.\\n- Histology empty lacunae + necrotic adipocytes. Subchondral collapse leads to secondary OA. BandB Musculoskeletal > Bone Disorders Pathoma Musculoskeletal > Bone Pathology FirstAid Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Bone Disorders SketchyPath Musculoskeletal > Bone Disorders Physeo Pathology > Musculoskeletal > Bone SketchyBiochem Metabolism > Vitamins > Vitamin D What is the key pathologic difference between osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis? OA is a mechanical/degenerative process with secondary minimal inflammation. RA is a primary autoimmune disease driven by CD4+ T cells with severe chronic inflammation pannus formation and cartilage/bone destruction. Concept What are Heberden nodes and Bouchard nodes and which arthritis are they associated with? Heberden nodes are osteophytes at DIP joints OA only . Bouchard nodes are osteophytes at PIP joints seen in both OA and RA . DIP involvement helps distinguish OA from RA which spares the DIP joints. Clinical What is pannus and in which disease is it found? Pannus is a mass of edematous synovium inflammatory cells granulation tissue and fibroblasts that grows over and erodes articular cartilage. It is the hallmark pathologic finding in rheumatoid arthritis. Concept Which autoantibody is the most specific and sensitive serologic marker for rheumatoid arthritis? Anti-citrullinated peptide antibody anti-CCP detected in up to 70% of RA patients. It is more specific than rheumatoid factor RF . Clinical What is the genetic basis of achondroplasia and which type of ossification is affected? Achondroplasia is caused by an autosomal dominant gain-of-function mutation in FGFR3. It affects endochondral ossification long bone growth while intramembranous ossification skull facial bones clavicle is unaffected. Concept What causes the blue sclera in osteogenesis imperfecta? Decreased type 1 collagen content makes the sclera translucent allowing partial visualization of the underlying choroidal veins giving a blue appearance. Clinical What are the DEXA scan T-score thresholds for osteoporosis osteopenia and normal bone? T-score <= -2.5 = osteoporosis. T-score between -1.0 and -2.5 = osteopenia. T-score >= -1.0 = normal bone density. Clinical What mechanism drives postmenopausal osteoporosis? Estrogen deficiency increases inflammatory cytokines IL-6 TNF IL-1 which increase RANKL and decrease OPG stimulating osteoclast recruitment and activity. Resorption dominates over formation. Concept What is the underlying defect in osteopetrosis and why are the bones brittle despite being dense? Defective osteoclast function often due to carbonic anhydrase 2 mutation prevents bone resorption. Bones are dense but brittle because they consist of unremodeled woven bone rather than organized lamellar bone. Concept What is the treatment for osteopetrosis? Bone marrow transplant which provides hematopoietic precursors for functional osteoclasts. Clinical What is the histologic hallmark of Paget disease of bone? A mosaic jigsaw puzzle-like pattern of lamellar bone produced by unusually prominent cement lines joining haphazardly oriented units of lamellar bone. Concept What laboratory finding distinguishes Paget disease from osteomalacia/rickets? In Paget disease alkaline phosphatase is elevated but calcium phosphorus and PTH are NORMAL. In osteomalacia/rickets alkaline phosphatase is elevated AND calcium is low PTH is high phosphate is low and vitamin D is low. Clinical What are the classic clinical features of rickets in children? Epiphyseal widening metaphyseal cupping and fraying genu varum bow legs and rachitic rosary palpable beading at costochondral junctions from unmineralized osteoid deposition . Clinical What are the two most common causes of osteonecrosis avascular necrosis ? Fractures disrupting blood supply and corticosteroid administration. The most common site is the femoral head. Clinical What is Caplan syndrome? The presence of rheumatoid nodules in the lungs seen in patients with rheumatoid arthritis especially coal miners with concurrent pneumoconiosis . Clinical match Osteoarthritis resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Osteoarthritis; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Bone Disorders; Pathoma > Musculoskeletal > Joint Pathology match Rheumatoid Arthritis resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Rheumatoid Arthritis; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Bone Disorders; SketchyPath > Musculoskeletal > Joint Disorders match Achondroplasia resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Achondroplasia; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Bone Disorders; Pathoma > Musculoskeletal match Osteogenesis Imperfecta resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Osteogenesis Imperfecta; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Bone Disorders; SketchyPath > Musculoskeletal > Bone match Osteoporosis resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Osteoporosis; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Bone Disorders; Pathoma > Musculoskeletal > Metabolic Bone Disease match Osteopetrosis resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Osteopetrosis; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Bone Disorders; Pathoma > Musculoskeletal match Rickets and Osteomalacia resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Rickets; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Bone Disorders; SketchyBiochem > Vitamins > Vitamin D match Paget Disease of Bone resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Paget Disease; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Bone Disorders; Pathoma > Musculoskeletal > Paget Disease match Osteonecrosis Avascular Necrosis resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Avascular Necrosis; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Bone Disorders match Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis resources AnKing > FirstAid > Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Juvenile Idiopathic Arthritis; BandB > Musculoskeletal > Bone Disorders OA vs. RA DIP Involvement DIP joints are involved in osteoarthritis Heberden nodes but SPARED in rheumatoid arthritis. The presence or absence of DIP involvement is a high-yield distinguishing feature. Anti-CCP vs. Rheumatoid Factor Anti-CCP is more SPECIFIC than RF for rheumatoid arthritis. RF can be positive in many other conditions SLE Sjogren hepatitis C . Anti-CCP is detected in ~70% and RF in ~80% of RA patients. Achondroplasia Endochondral vs. Intramembranous FGFR3 gain-of-function inhibits endochondral ossification long bones shortened but intramembranous ossification is unaffected skull and facial bones normal . This is why patients have a large head relative to shortened limbs. OI Blue Sclera Mechanism Blue sclera in osteogenesis imperfecta results from decreased type 1 collagen making the sclera translucent revealing underlying choroidal veins. This is NOT a pigmentation change. Osteoporosis DEXA Scores T-score <= -2.5 = osteoporosis. Between -1 and -2.5 = osteopenia. >= -1 = normal. DEXA recommended for all women over 65. Plain X-ray only detects osteoporosis after 30-40% of bone mass is lost. Osteopetrosis Dense but Brittle Despite the name implying stone-like hardness osteopetrotic bones are BRITTLE and fracture-prone because they consist of unremodeled woven bone not organized lamellar bone. Treatment is bone marrow transplant. Paget Disease Lab Values Elevated alkaline phosphatase with NORMAL calcium phosphorus and PTH distinguishes Paget disease from hyperparathyroidism and osteomalacia. The mosaic jigsaw puzzle pattern is the histologic hallmark. AVN Femoral Head and Bilateral The most common site for avascular necrosis is the femoral head medial circumflex femoral artery injury . 80% of cases are bilateral. Most common causes fractures and corticosteroid use. questions 1 A 65-year-old woman presents with pain in her right knee that worsens throughout the day and improves with rest. Physical examination reveals crepitus with range of motion and bony enlargement of the distal interphalangeal joints bilaterally. Radiographs show joint space narrowing subchondral sclerosis and osteophyte formation. Synovial fluid analysis reveals a non-inflammatory aspirate. Which of the following best describes the underlying pathologic process in this patient s condition? Autoimmune destruction of articular cartilage by pannus formation Mechanical wear and degradation of articular cartilage with chondrocyte clustering and subchondral changes Deposition of monosodium urate crystals in the joint space Immune complex deposition in the synovial membrane Infection of the joint space with purulent exudate 2 This patient presents with osteoarthritis OA evidenced by pain that worsens with activity Heberden nodes DIP osteophytes crepitus non-inflammatory aspirate and radiographic findings of joint space narrowing with osteophytes and subchondral sclerosis. OA is a mechanical/degenerative process with progressive cartilage destruction chondrocyte clustering eburnation and osteophyte formation not a primary autoimmune or inflammatory process. 2 A 42-year-old woman presents with symmetric swelling and pain in her metacarpophalangeal and proximal interphalangeal joints that is worst in the morning and improves throughout the day. She also reports fatigue and low-grade fevers. Laboratory studies show a positive anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide anti-CCP antibody and elevated ESR. A biopsy of the synovium would most likely show which of the following? Monosodium urate crystal deposition with neutrophilic infiltration Chondrocyte clusters with subchondral cyst formation Pannus composed of edematous synovium granulation tissue and inflammatory cells growing over articular cartilage Caseous granulomas with Langhans giant cells Fibrinoid necrosis of vessel walls with neutrophilic infiltration 3 This patient has rheumatoid arthritis RA characterized by symmetric small joint involvement morning stiffness systemic symptoms and positive anti-CCP antibody. The hallmark histologic finding in RA is pannus formation a mass of edematous synovium inflammatory cells granulation tissue and fibroblasts that grows over and erodes articular cartilage. Anti-CCP is the most specific serologic marker for RA. 3 A newborn is found to have disproportionately shortened proximal extremities with a normal-length trunk frontal bossing and a depressed nasal bridge. Genetic testing reveals a gain-of-function mutation in the FGFR3 gene. Which of the following types of bone formation is primarily affected in this condition? Intramembranous ossification Endochondral ossification Appositional bone growth Periosteal bone formation Both intramembranous and endochondral ossification equally 2 This infant has achondroplasia the most common skeletal dysplasia and cause of dwarfism. It is caused by an autosomal dominant gain-of-function mutation in FGFR3 that inhibits endochondral ossification long bone growth while intramembranous ossification skull facial bones clavicle is unaffected. This is why patients have rhizomelic limb shortening with a relatively large head and normal trunk. 4 A 70-year-old man is found to have an elevated serum alkaline phosphatase on routine bloodwork. Calcium phosphorus and PTH levels are all within normal limits. A skull radiograph shows thickened calvarium. A bone biopsy demonstrates lamellar bone arranged in a mosaic pattern with prominent cement lines. Which of the following is the most serious potential complication of this patient s condition? Osteomalacia Osteomyelitis High-output heart failure Hypercalcemia with renal stones Pathologic fracture from osteoporosis 3 This patient has Paget disease of bone characterized by elevated alkaline phosphatase with normal calcium phosphorus and PTH skull thickening and the mosaic jigsaw puzzle pattern of lamellar bone on biopsy. The most serious complication is high-output heart failure which results from arteriovenous shunts created in the hypervascular pagetic bone. Other complications include chalk-stick fractures hearing loss and sarcomatous transformation <1% . 5 A 3-year-old child is brought to the emergency department with a femoral shaft fracture after a minor fall. The parents report this is the child s fourth fracture in the past year. Physical examination reveals blue sclera and small misshapen teeth with a blue-yellow discoloration. Which of the following is the most likely underlying defect? Deficiency of osteoclast function due to carbonic anhydrase 2 mutation Gain-of-function mutation in FGFR3 affecting endochondral ossification Defective type 1 collagen synthesis due to COL1A1 or COL1A2 mutations Vitamin D deficiency causing impaired mineralization of osteoid SQSTM1 mutation causing disordered bone remodeling 3 This child has osteogenesis imperfecta brittle bone disease the most common inherited disorder of connective tissue. It is caused by mutations in COL1A1 or COL1A2 type 1 collagen genes leading to defective collagen synthesis. The classic triad includes multiple fractures from minimal trauma blue sclera translucent collagen-deficient sclera revealing choroidal veins and dentinogenesis imperfecta small misshapen blue-yellow teeth . Type 1 OI has a normal lifespan with decreasing fracture frequency after puberty."}, {"id": "l105", "title": "Lecture #105: Pathology of Bone and Joints - Part 2", "module": "Unknown", "content": "id l105 module Unknown l105 Lecture 105 Pathology of Bone and Joints - Part 2 lecturer E. Petcu M.D. Pathology pdf content/L105 HighYield Render.pdf Lecture 105 Pathology of Bone and Joints - Part 2\\n\\n Lecturer E. Petcu M.D. | Session Lecture 105\\n\\nContinuation of L104 . This lecture covers fractures osteomyelitis seronegative spondyloarthropathies Lyme arthritis fibrous dysplasia bone cysts and both benign and malignant bone tumors. The key theme patient age lesion location and radiographic appearance are the strongest diagnostic clues.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Fractures and Fracture Repair\\n\\n Types Simple closed skin intact compound open communicates with skin comminuted multiple fragments displaced ends not aligned stress repetitive loading greenstick incomplete in infants and pathological through weakened bone e.g. tumor .\\n\\n Repair sequence Hematoma fibrin mesh + growth factors PDGF/TGF-beta/FGF activate osteoprogenitor cells --> Soft callus pro-callus provides anchorage but no rigidity --> Hard callus woven bone trabeculae weeks 2-3 --> Endochondral ossification mineralization weight-bearing --> Remodeling restoration of medullary cavity . Inadequate immobilization --> non-union --> cystic degeneration --> pseudoarthrosis false joint lined by synovial-like cells .\\n\\n highyield\\nFracture repair Hematoma --> Soft Callus --> Hard Callus --> Endochondral Ossification --> Remodeling. Non-union leads to pseudoarthrosis.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Osteomyelitis\\n\\nInflammation of bone and marrow. S. aureus causes 80-90% of pyogenic cases. E. coli/Pseudomonas/Klebsiella in GU infections; H. influenzae/Group B Strep in neonates; Salmonella in sickle cell disease . Organisms reach bone hematogenously by contiguous spread or direct implantation.\\n\\n Acute stage Neutrophil recruitment --> bone necrosis within 48 hours --> subperiosteal abscess --> periosteal lifting impairs blood supply. Key terms Dead bone = sequestrum ; reactive new bone shell = involucrum ; draining sinus = cloaca indicates chronicity . Chronic osteomyelitis in 5-25% of cases. X-ray lytic focus surrounded by sclerosis.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Seronegative Spondyloarthropathies\\n\\nDefined by RF-negative enthesopathy not synovitis sacroiliac involvement HLA-B27 association bony ankylosis.\\n\\n Ankylosing spondylitis Bony ankylosis of sacroiliac and vertebral joints. Lower back pain + spinal immobility in 2nd-3rd decades. 90% HLA-B27 positive.\\n\\n Reactive Arthritis Reiter Triad of arthritis + urethritis + conjunctivitis . Triggered by Salmonella/Shigella/Campylobacter GI or Chlamydia trachomatis STI . Acute asymmetric oligoarthritis of lower extremities. RF-negative HLA-B27 positive. 50% recur; active disease beyond 6 months = chronic.\\n\\n Lyme Arthritis Borrelia burgdorferi via Ixodes ticks. Three phases 1 Early localized erythema migrans bull s-eye rash round red expanding non-pruritic ; 2 Early disseminated cranial nerves heart meninges; 3 Late disseminated migratory arthritis in 80% untreated cases large joints 1-2 at a time . Arthritis occurs in less than 10% overall because most are treated early. Treatment doxycycline/amoxicillin/cefuroxime axetil 90%+ cure .\\n\\n---\\n\\n Fibrous Dysplasia\\n\\nBenign condition normal bone components present but fail to mature. Caused by somatic GNAS1 gain-of-function mutations constitutively active Gs protein . Histology curvilinear woven bone trabeculae \\ Chinese letters\\ without osteoblastic rimming.\\n\\n| Form | Features |\\n|---|---|\\n| Monostotic | Adolescence; femur/tibia/ribs/jaw; often asymptomatic |\\n| Polyostotic | Earlier age; craniofacial involvement; crippling deformities; rarely transforms to sarcoma |\\n| McCune-Albright | Polyostotic unilateral + cafe-au-lait spots ipsilateral + precocious puberty + endocrinopathies |\\n\\n---\\n\\n Bone Cysts\\n\\n- Simple unicameral Fluid-filled fibrous-lined first 2 decades near growth plate. Not a neoplasm. May fracture if large.\\n- Aneurysmal ABC Blood-filled multiloculated. Adolescence; femur/tibia/vertebra. X-ray expansive lytic lesion with sclerotic eggshell rim. 70% have USP6 rearrangements on 17p13. Benign but locally aggressive; 10-50% recurrence.\\n\\n Ganglion cysts Small near wrist joints no cell lining. Baker cyst Popliteal synovial cyst associated with RA.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Bone Tumors Overview\\n\\nPrimary bone tumors are rare and outnumbered by metastases. Most occur in the first 3 decades at the distal femur/proximal tibia. Age is the most important diagnostic clue. Painful lesions suggest malignancy.\\n\\n Benign Tumors\\n\\n| Feature | Osteoid Osteoma | Osteoblastoma |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Size | Less than 2 cm | Greater than 2 cm |\\n| Location | Long bones | Spine |\\n| Pain | Nocturnal PGE2 ; relieved by aspirin/NSAIDs | NOT nocturnal; NOT aspirin-responsive |\\n| Treatment | Radiofrequency ablation | Curettage/en bloc excision |\\n\\n highyield\\nOsteoid osteoma less than 2 cm nocturnal pain relieved by aspirin PGE2 . Osteoblastoma greater than 2 cm spine pain NOT relieved by aspirin.\\n \\n\\n Osteochondroma exostosis Most common benign bone tumor. Bony stalk + cartilage cap. Metaphysis near growth plate knee . 85% solitary. Men 3 1.\\n\\n Enchondroma Benign hyaline cartilage tumor in medullary cavity. Ages 20-50. IDH1/IDH2 mutations. Ollier disease unilateral enchondromas 25-30% chondrosarcoma risk and Maffucci syndrome enchondromas + spindle cell hemangiomas glioma risk .\\n\\n Giant cell tumor osteoclastoma Epiphysis near knee. X-ray soap-bubble appearance . Giant cells with 100+ nuclei. 4% metastasize to lungs; 40-60% local recurrence.\\n\\n Malignant Tumors\\n\\n Osteosarcoma Most common primary malignant bone tumor 20% of bone cancers . Bimodal 75% before age 20 growth spurt ; older adults with Paget/radiation. Metaphysis near knee. X-ray sunburst pattern or Codman triangle . Diagnosis requires malignant cells producing osteoid. Genetics RB 75% sporadic TP53 Li-Fraumeni CDKN2A. All patients assumed to have occult metastasis. 5-year survival less than 20% with mets.\\n\\n Ewing sarcoma Second most common pediatric bone sarcoma. 80% under 20. Caucasian predilection. Diaphysis of long bones/pelvis. Mimics infection fever elevated ESR leukocytosis . X-ray onion-skin periosteal reaction . Histology small round blue cells forming Homer Wright rosettes . t 11;22 EWSR1-FLI1 fusion in 90%+. Treatment neoadjuvant chemo + surgery + adjuvant chemo; 75% 5-year survival.\\n\\n Chondrosarcoma Malignant cartilage tumor. Age 40s+ men 2 1. Axial skeleton pelvis/shoulder/ribs . Grade 1 80-90% 5-year survival. Grade 3 43% survival 70% metastasize to lungs.\\n\\n---\\n\\n Metastatic Bone Tumors\\n\\nFar more common than primary tumors. Adults prostate blastic breast kidney lung more than 75% . Kidney/lung/GI/melanoma = lytic . Breast = mixed . Children neuroblastoma Wilms osteosarcoma Ewing rhabdomyosarcoma.\\n\\nTumor cells secrete PTHrP/prostaglandins --> upregulate RANKL --> stimulate osteoclast-driven resorption --> released growth factors TGF-beta IGF-1 feed back to support tumor growth vicious cycle . Treatment bisphosphonates zoledronic acid analgesics radiotherapy.\\n\\n mnemonic\\n Primary Malignant Bone Tumors by Age OEC \\n O steosarcoma - under 20 metaphysis sunburst/Codman\\n E wing - children diaphysis onion-skin t 11;22 \\n C hondrosarcoma - adults 40+ axial skeleton\\n Pathoma Musculoskeletal Pathology > Bone Tumors BandB Musculoskeletal > Bone Disorders SketchyPath Musculoskeletal & Derm > Primary Bone Tumors FirstAid Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Primary Bone Tumors Bootcamp Musculoskeletal > Bone Tumors What is the sequence of fracture repair? Hematoma formation soft callus pro-callus hard callus bony callus with woven bone endochondral ossification and remodeling with restoration of the medullary cavity. Concept What is a pseudoarthrosis? A false joint that forms when persistent fracture non-union leads to cystic degeneration of the malformed callus with the luminal surface becoming lined by synovial-like cells. Concept What organism causes osteomyelitis in patients with sickle cell disease? Salmonella. Sickle cell disease predisposes patients to Salmonella osteomyelitis S. aureus remains most common overall at 80-90% . Clinical What are the sequestrum involucrum and cloaca in osteomyelitis? Sequestrum = dead bone; Involucrum = living shell of reactive new bone surrounding the infected dead bone; Cloaca = draining sinus tract through which dead bone fragments are released indicating chronicity. Concept What is the classic triad of Reactive Arthritis Reiter Syndrome ? Arthritis + non-gonococcal urethritis or cervicitis + conjunctivitis. It is seronegative RF-negative and often HLA-B27 positive. Clinical What is the pathognomonic rash of Lyme disease? Erythema migrans a bull s-eye rash that is round red at least 2 inches across slowly expanding warm to touch and non-pruritic/non-painful. Clinical What gene mutation causes fibrous dysplasia? Somatic gain-of-function mutations in GNAS1 which encodes the stimulatory alpha subunit of Gs protein leading to constitutively active Gs signaling. Concept How does osteoid osteoma pain differ from osteoblastoma pain? Osteoid osteoma causes nocturnal bone pain due to excess PGE2 from proliferating osteoblasts and this pain is relieved by aspirin/NSAIDs. Osteoblastoma pain is NOT nocturnal and NOT relieved by aspirin/NSAIDs. Clinical What is the most common primary malignant tumor of bone and what are its hallmark radiographic findings? Osteosarcoma 20% of all bone cancers . Hallmark X-ray findings include the sunburst pattern and Codman triangle both indicating aggressive periosteal reaction. Clinical What translocation defines Ewing sarcoma and what is its characteristic histologic and radiographic appearance? Balanced t 11;22 creating EWSR1-FLI1 fusion gene. Histology sheets of small round blue cells forming Homer Wright rosettes. X-ray destructive lytic lesion with onion-skin periosteal reaction. Clinical What is the X-ray appearance of a giant cell tumor osteoclastoma ? A soap-bubble appearance in the epiphysis most commonly near the knee distal femur or proximal tibia . Clinical Which cancers most commonly metastasize to bone in adults? Prostate blastic breast kidney and lung carcinomas account for more than 75% of skeletal metastases. Kidney lung GI and melanoma produce lytic lesions; prostate is predominantly blastic. Clinical What are Ollier disease and Maffucci syndrome? Both are non-hereditary disorders characterized by multiple enchondromas. Ollier disease enchondromas predominantly on one side of the body 25-30% risk of chondrosarcoma by age 40 . Maffucci syndrome multiple enchondromas + spindle cell hemangiomas risk of brain gliomas and other malignancies . Clinical match Osteomyelitis resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > Musculoskeletal > search osteomyelitis sequestrum involucrum match Fracture repair and healing resources AnKing Step 1 > BandB > Musculoskeletal > search fracture repair callus match Osteosarcoma Codman triangle sunburst pattern resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma / SketchyPath > Musculoskeletal > search osteosarcoma codman sunburst match Ewing sarcoma onion-skin Homer Wright rosettes t 11;22 resources AnKing Step 1 > SketchyPath > Musculoskeletal > search ewing sarcoma onion skin EWSR1 match Giant cell tumor osteoclastoma soap bubble resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > Musculoskeletal > search giant cell tumor soap bubble epiphysis match Ankylosing spondylitis HLA-B27 resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma / BandB > MSK > search ankylosing spondylitis HLA-B27 match Reactive Arthritis / Reiter Syndrome resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > MSK > search reactive arthritis reiter conjunctivitis urethritis match Lyme disease Borrelia erythema migrans resources AnKing Step 1 > SketchyMicro > Spirochetes > search borrelia lyme erythema migrans match Fibrous dysplasia GNAS1 McCune-Albright resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > MSK > search fibrous dysplasia GNAS1 cafe au lait match Osteoid osteoma vs Osteoblastoma resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > Musculoskeletal > search osteoid osteoma nocturnal aspirin osteoblastoma match Osteochondroma / Enchondroma / Chondrosarcoma resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma / SketchyPath > MSK > search osteochondroma enchondroma chondrosarcoma ollier maffucci match Bone metastases RANKL PTHrP lytic vs blastic resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > MSK > search bone metastasis prostate blastic lytic RANKL match Sickle cell and Salmonella osteomyelitis resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > MSK / Heme > search sickle cell salmonella osteomyelitis Osteoid Osteoma vs. Osteoblastoma Both are benign bone-forming tumors in young males. The key distinction osteoid osteoma is less than 2 cm causes nocturnal pain relieved by aspirin PGE2-mediated and arises in long bones. Osteoblastoma is greater than 2 cm pain is NOT nocturnal and NOT aspirin-responsive and arises in the spine. Sickle Cell + Salmonella Patients with sickle cell disease are uniquely predisposed to Salmonella osteomyelitis. S. aureus remains the most common overall cause of osteomyelitis 80-90% but Salmonella is the classic association with sickle cell. Osteosarcoma Radiology Two hallmark X-ray findings the sunburst pattern stretched-out bone fibers from rapid growth and the Codman triangle triangular periosteal shadow . Both indicate an aggressive bone-forming tumor. This is the most common primary malignant bone tumor peaking in adolescence. Ewing Sarcoma Mimics Infection Ewing sarcoma can present with fever elevated ESR anemia and leukocytosis mimicking osteomyelitis. The onion-skin periosteal reaction on X-ray and small round blue cells with Homer Wright rosettes on histology clinch the diagnosis. Bone Metastases Lytic vs. Blastic Prostate = blastic bone-forming . Kidney lung GI melanoma = lytic bone-destroying . Breast = mixed. Tumor cells do NOT directly resorb bone; they secrete PTHrP and prostaglandins to upregulate RANKL on osteoblasts indirectly driving osteoclast activity. McCune-Albright Syndrome Triad Polyostotic fibrous dysplasia unilateral + cafe-au-lait spots ipsilateral coast of Maine borders + precocious puberty and other endocrinopathies hyperthyroidism GH-secreting pituitary adenomas . Caused by GNAS1 gain-of-function mutation. questions 1 A 14-year-old boy presents with a 3-week history of progressive right knee pain and swelling. X-ray of the distal femur reveals a destructive mass with a mixed lytic-blastic pattern periosteal elevation forming a triangular shadow and a sunburst pattern of radiating bone spicules. Biopsy shows pleomorphic spindle cells producing pink amorphous osteoid. Which of the following genetic mutations is most commonly found in the sporadic form of this neoplasm? EWSR1-FLI1 fusion gene RB gene mutation GNAS1 gain-of-function mutation IDH1 heterozygous mutation TP53 somatic deletion 2 The clinical presentation adolescent distal femur destructive mass with sunburst pattern and Codman triangle malignant osteoid production is classic for osteosarcoma. RB mutations are found in 75% of sporadic osteosarcoma cases. EWSR1-FLI1 is Ewing sarcoma; GNAS1 is fibrous dysplasia; IDH1 is enchondroma/chondrosarcoma. 2 A 10-year-old African American girl with known sickle cell disease presents with fever left tibial tenderness and an elevated ESR. Blood cultures grow a gram-negative motile rod. X-ray of the tibia shows a focal lytic area of bone destruction surrounded by periosteal elevation. Which of the following is the most likely causative organism? Staphylococcus aureus Pseudomonas aeruginosa Group B Streptococcus Salmonella species Escherichia coli 4 While S. aureus is the most common cause of osteomyelitis overall 80-90% patients with sickle cell disease are specifically predisposed to Salmonella osteomyelitis due to functional asplenia and intestinal ischemia. The gram-negative motile rod description fits Salmonella. 3 A 22-year-old man presents with low back pain bilateral sacroiliac joint tenderness and limited spinal mobility. He is rheumatoid factor negative and HLA-B27 positive. His symptoms started gradually and worsened over the past year. X-ray shows bilateral sacroiliac joint fusion. Which of the following best describes the pathological changes underlying this condition? Pannus formation with destruction of articular cartilage in synovial joints Crystal deposition causing neutrophilic inflammation in joint spaces Destruction of articular cartilage and bony ankylosis at ligamentous attachment sites Immune complex deposition causing vasculitis of joint vasculature Granulomatous inflammation of the synovium with non-caseating granulomas 3 This is ankylosing spondylitis a seronegative spondyloarthropathy. It is characterized by pathological changes in ligamentous attachments enthesopathy not synovium with destruction of articular cartilage and bony ankylosis especially of sacroiliac and vertebral apophyseal joints. Pannus formation is rheumatoid arthritis; crystal deposition is gout. 4 A 16-year-old Caucasian boy presents with a 2-month history of a painful enlarging mass on his left femoral diaphysis. He has had intermittent fevers and an elevated ESR. X-ray reveals a permeative lytic lesion with concentric layers of reactive bone deposited in a layered pattern around the cortex. Biopsy shows sheets of uniform small round cells with scant cytoplasm arranged in rosette patterns. What is the most likely cytogenetic finding? Amplification of N-MYC oncogene Deletion of chromosome 13q14 RB gene Translocation t 11;22 producing EWSR1-FLI1 fusion Trisomy of chromosomes 3 7 and 17 Rearrangement of chromosome 17p13 involving USP6 3 The clinical picture Caucasian adolescent boy diaphyseal location fever mimicking infection onion-skin periosteal reaction small round blue cells forming Homer Wright rosettes is classic for Ewing sarcoma. The balanced t 11;22 translocation creating the EWSR1-FLI1 fusion gene is found in more than 90% of cases. 5 A 19-year-old male athlete presents with progressive right leg pain that is worse at night and is completely relieved by taking ibuprofen. X-ray of the tibial cortex reveals a small less than 2 cm radiolucent nidus surrounded by a thick rim of reactive sclerotic bone. Histology shows randomly interconnected trabeculae of woven bone rimmed by osteoblasts within a vascularized fibrous stroma. Which of the following best explains the pathogenesis of his nocturnal pain? Periosteal stretching from an enlarging soft tissue mass Pathological fracture through weakened cortical bone Excessive prostaglandin E2 production by proliferating osteoblasts Neoplastic invasion of periosteal sensory nerve fibers Compression of adjacent neurovascular structures by expanding cyst 3 This is osteoid osteoma a benign bone tumor less than 2 cm classically producing nocturnal bone pain that is relieved by aspirin/NSAIDs. The pain mechanism is excess prostaglandin E2 PGE2 secreted by proliferating osteoblasts within the nidus which sensitizes pain receptors. NSAIDs block PGE2 synthesis explaining pain relief."}, {"id": "l106", "title": "Lecture #106: Pathology of Soft Tissue", "module": "Unknown", "content": "id l106 module Unknown l106 Lecture 106 Pathology of Soft Tissue lecturer W. Zaloga D.O. Pathology pdf content/L106 HighYield Render.pdf Lecture 106 Pathology of Soft Tissue\\n\\n Lecturer W. Zaloga D.O. | Session Lecture 106\\n\\nSoft tissue tumors arise from mesenchymal tissue -- adipose fibrous skeletal muscle smooth muscle and tissues of uncertain origin. These are rare overall but commonly tested because each tumor has characteristic histologic and clinical features that serve as diagnostic anchors. This lecture organizes tumors by cell of origin and moves from benign to malignant for each lineage. For every tumor three diagnostic pillars matter most demographics age/sex location and histologic pattern . Sarcomas are mesenchymal malignancies; they are invasive unencapsulated hemorrhagic/necrotic from outstripping their blood supply and show high nuclear-to-cytoplasmic N C ratios with hyperchromatic nuclei.\\n\\n---\\n\\n General Principles of Soft Tissue Tumors Slides 4-6 \\n\\nSoft tissue tumors may be benign suffix -oma or malignant suffix -sarcoma . Sarcomas are rare constituting less than 1% of all cancers but are disproportionately important on boards. Risk factors discussed include chronic inflammation prior irradiation and pre-existing benign tumors that can undergo malignant transformation.\\n\\n- Benign features mobile painless well-encapsulated mature-appearing cells cured by simple excision\\n- Malignant features fixed painful invasive unencapsulated or pseudo-encapsulated from compression hemorrhagic/necrotic high N C ratio hyperchromatic nuclei abnormal mitoses\\n- Key cell types and their morphology \\n - Fibroblasts spindle-shaped cells with pointy nuclear ends\\n - Smooth muscle spindle-shaped with cigar-shaped nuclei with rounded ends; stain for smooth muscle actin\\n - Skeletal muscle multinucleated long cylindrical cells with peripheral nuclei and cross-striations from actin/myosin filaments\\n\\n highyield\\nBenign tumors are mobile painless encapsulated and cured by excision. Malignant sarcomas are fixed invasive hemorrhagic/necrotic and show high N C ratios.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Adipose Tissue Tumors\\n\\n Lipoma Slides 13-14 \\n\\nThe lipoma is the most common soft tissue tumor in adults. It is a benign accumulation of mature adipocytes located in the subcutaneous tissue of the proximal extremities or trunk typically in middle-aged individuals. Because the cells are mature and the tumor is encapsulated it is mobile painless and cured by simple excision.\\n\\n- Gross yellow soft lobulated well-encapsulated with a thin fibrous capsule\\n- Histology mature adipocytes surrounded by a fibrous capsule\\n- Pseudo-encapsulation vs. true capsule some tumors compress surrounding tissue mimicking a capsule. Lipomas have a true fibrous capsule that contains the tumor\\n\\n Liposarcoma Slides 16-25 \\n\\nThe liposarcoma is the malignant counterpart and the most common sarcoma in adults. It arises deep in the soft tissues of the extremities or retroperitoneum typically in the 50-65 age range. Patients present with pain swelling fatigue and decreased range of motion.\\n\\n| Subtype | Histology | Genetics | Behavior |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Well-differentiated | Mature adipocytes + scattered lipoblasts | 12q13-15 amplification MDM2 p53 inhibitor | Indolent rarely metastasizes |\\n| Myxoid | Abundant basophilic mucopolysaccharide stroma chicken-wire capillaries primitive cells | t 12;16 FUS-DDIT3 fusion gene arrests differentiation | Intermediate aggressiveness |\\n| Pleomorphic | Bizarre nuclei spindle cells high-grade anaplastic cells | Complex karyotype without reproducible abnormalities | Aggressive frequent metastases |\\n\\n- Lipoblast an immature adipocyte with a large dark nucleus that has begun producing lipid droplets in the cytoplasm creating characteristic nuclear scalloping\\n- IHC S100-positive adipocyte marker keratin-negative excludes epithelial desmin-negative excludes muscle \\n- Malignant features not encapsulated hemorrhagic/necrotic from rapid growth outstripping blood supply less fatty tissue than well-differentiated types\\n- Recurrence may recur locally if not adequately excised due to invasive nature\\n\\n highyield\\nLiposarcoma subtypes Well-differentiated indolent MDM2 amplification Myxoid chicken-wire capillaries FUS-DDIT3 fusion Pleomorphic high-grade complex karyotype frequent metastases .\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Fibroblastic Tumors\\n\\n Nodular Fasciitis Slides 26-29 \\n\\nNodular fasciitis is a benign self-limited fibroblastic and myofibroblastic proliferation that can mimic a sarcoma clinically and histologically. It occurs in young adults on the forearm chest or back usually following trauma. Despite rapid growth over weeks to months it is benign and often regresses spontaneously.\\n\\n- Key feature contains a clonal MYH9-USP6 translocation making it technically neoplastic yet it behaves benignly and rarely recurs after excision\\n- Gross not encapsulated but circumscribed remains small usually less than 3 cm \\n- Histology plump immature fibroblasts and myofibroblasts spindle-shaped nuclei with punctate nucleoli frequent normal-appearing mitoses tissue-culture appearance randomly oriented proliferating cells \\n- Zonation hypercellular zones with storiform/fascicular patterns and hypocellular zones with myxoid stroma\\n- Infiltrating lymphocytes are common which historically led to the mistaken belief it was a reactive process\\n\\n correlate\\nNodular Fasciitis\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Clonal neoplastic proliferation of fibroblasts/myofibroblasts with MYH9-USP6 translocation; self-limited despite neoplastic nature\\n- Presentation Rapidly growing firm rubbery tender subcutaneous nodule on forearm/chest/back in young adults often with history of trauma\\n- Diagnosis Histology shows plump spindle cells with tissue-culture appearance and zonation; IHC for smooth muscle actin positive in myofibroblasts\\n- Treatment Excision rarely recurs ; often regresses spontaneously\\n- Lecturer s Point Despite having a true translocation and being clonal it is NOT malignant and does not transform -- a key mimic of sarcoma\\n \\n\\n Superficial Fibromatosis Slides 30-33 \\n\\nSuperficial fibromatosis encompasses benign fibroblastic proliferations in superficial tissues. These tend to resolve spontaneously but may cause local deformity. Males are more commonly affected.\\n\\n| Type | Location | Key Features |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Dupuytren contracture | Palmar fascia | Incidence increases with age; may cause skin dimpling and progressive flexion contracture; one or both hands |\\n| Ledderhose disease | Plantar fascia | Usually in young boys; unilateral; no contractures; can associate with other fibromatoses |\\n| Peyronie disease | Penile shaft dorsolateral | Abnormal curvature and urethral constriction; some resolve spontaneously |\\n\\n- Histology ill-defined fascicles of plump spindle-cell fibroblasts with abundant dense collagen nodular and proliferative\\n- Genetics no well-defined mutations unlike deep fibromatosis though focal beta-catenin accumulation has been found with unclear significance\\n- Palmar and plantar fibromatosis progress in about 50% of cases\\n\\n Deep Fibromatosis Desmoid Tumors Slides 33-36 \\n\\nDeep fibromatosis desmoid tumors are histologically similar to superficial fibromatosis but are locally aggressive. They average 10 cm infiltrate surrounding muscle nerve and fat and frequently recur but never metastasize. They primarily affect women in their teens to 30s.\\n\\n- Location abdominal wall musculoaponeurotic structures shoulder pelvis mesentery\\n- Genetics APC and beta-catenin mutations leading to increased WNT signaling. Most have sporadic beta-catenin mutations\\n- FAP/Gardner syndrome patients with germline APC mutations are predisposed to desmoid tumors\\n- Histology bland fibroblasts in long parallel fascicles with dense collagen no cellular atypia no mitoses -- looks like a scar\\n- Treatment challenging due to infiltrative growth; oncologists often manage these patients despite the lesion being technically benign\\n\\n correlate\\nDeep Fibromatosis Desmoid Tumor \\n\\n- Pathophysiology Fibroblastic proliferation driven by APC/beta-catenin mutations with increased WNT signaling\\n- Presentation Large average 10 cm firm fixed painful mass in abdominal wall or extremities of young women\\n- Diagnosis Gray-white rubbery poorly demarcated mass infiltrating surrounding structures; microscopy shows bland fibroblasts without atypia\\n- Treatment Wide excision; high recurrence rate due to infiltration; may require oncologic management\\n- Risk Factors FAP/Gardner syndrome germline APC mutations \\n- Lecturer s Point Acts like cancer locally aggressive but technically benign -- never metastasizes\\n \\n\\n Fibrosarcoma Slides 37-40 \\n\\nFibrosarcoma is the rare malignant fibroblast tumor. It is a diagnosis of exclusion as many previously classified fibrosarcomas were reclassified when better diagnostic tools became available. Most patients are middle-aged and it occurs in the deep soft tissues of the lower extremity or trunk.\\n\\n- Two types Infantile rarely metastasizes and Adult frequently metastasizes to lung and bone \\n- Gross pseudo-encapsulated fleshy fish-flesh appearance hemorrhagic necrotic white-tan invasive\\n- Histology characteristic herringbone pattern highly cellular increased N C ratio hyperchromatic nuclei mitotic activity\\n- IHC vimentin-positive mesenchymal marker S100-negative excludes adipocyte cytokeratin-negative excludes epithelial \\n- Treatment radical excision + radiation for positive margins; chemotherapy for high-grade tumors\\n- 5-year survival approximately 41%; 10-year survival approximately 29%\\n- Predisposing factors sites of prior injury chronic inflammation irradiated tissue de-differentiation from a well-differentiated liposarcoma\\n\\n highyield\\nFibrosarcoma herringbone pattern on histology diagnosis of exclusion vimentin-positive S100/cytokeratin-negative. 5-year survival approximately 41%.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Skeletal Muscle Tumors\\n\\n Rhabdomyosarcoma Slides 37-40 \\n\\nRhabdomyosarcoma is the most common soft tissue sarcoma of childhood. It shows skeletal muscle differentiation and presents as a rapidly growing painful mass with unexplained weight loss. Treatment requires surgical resection chemotherapy and possibly radiation.\\n\\n| Subtype | Age | Location | Key Features | Prognosis |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Embryonal | Childhood under 20 | GU tract head/neck sinuses | Primitive round cells + rare rhabdomyoblasts | Intermediate |\\n| Sarcoma botryoides | Young children | Vagina bladder nasopharynx | Grape-like polypoid growth cambium layer beneath epithelium; variant of embryonal | Best prognosis |\\n| Alveolar | Older kids/young adults 10-35 | Arms legs trunk | Alveolar pattern resembles lung alveoli discohesive round cells on fibrous septa | Poor; PAX-FOXO1 fusion = worse |\\n| Pleomorphic | Adults 50+ | Extremities | Highly pleomorphic spindle cells high N C ratio | Often fatal |\\n\\n- IHC desmin and myogenin positive skeletal muscle markers S100-negative cytokeratin-negative\\n- Rhabdomyoblast primitive cell showing early skeletal muscle differentiation with developing cross-striations of actin and myosin\\n- Molecular PAX-FOXO1 fusion gene interferes with differentiation and predicts poor survival\\n- Sarcoma botryoides best prognosis; 65% remission with treatment but only 15% four-year survival with metastasis\\n\\n correlate\\nSarcoma Botryoides\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Variant of embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma arising in walls of hollow mucosal-lined structures\\n- Presentation Polypoid grape-like mass protruding from vagina bladder or nasopharynx in young children\\n- Diagnosis Cambium layer dense tumor cell layer beneath epithelium ; desmin/myogenin positive\\n- Treatment Surgery + chemotherapy; most favorable prognosis of all rhabdomyosarcoma subtypes\\n- Lecturer s Point Remember the grape-like cluster gross appearance and that this is the best prognosis among rhabdomyosarcomas\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Tumors of Uncertain Origin\\n\\n Synovial Sarcoma Slides 37-40 \\n\\nDespite its name synovial sarcoma does not originate from synovium. It was historically named for its occurrence near joints but molecular and immunohistochemical studies show it is inconsistent with synovial origin. It occurs in the deep soft tissues adjacent to large joints especially the knee in patients aged 20-40.\\n\\n- Presentation deep-seated pain numbness limited range of motion firm/fixed swelling near joints; often mistaken for arthritis or bursitis for years\\n- Genetics characteristic t X;18 translocation producing proteins that interfere with chromatin gene expression\\n- Histologic types \\n - Monophasic uniform spindle cells with high N C ratio in tightly packed fascicles\\n - Biphasic spindle cells PLUS gland-like structures with cuboidal to columnar epithelioid cells\\n- IHC epithelial components positive for CK7 and other cytokeratins; spindle component variably positive for cytokeratins\\n- Treatment limb-sparing surgery + chemotherapy; surgery + chemo + radiation\\n- 5-year survival 50-60%; 10-year survival 40-50%\\n- Metastasis commonly to lungs; unusually for sarcomas also to regional lymph nodes\\n\\n Undifferentiated Pleomorphic Sarcoma UPS Slides 37-40 \\n\\nUPS is the diagnosis given to high-grade mesenchymal tumors that cannot be classified into any recognized lineage by histomorphology immunohistochemistry or molecular studies. It arises in middle-aged to older adults 50-60+ in the deep soft tissues of the extremity especially the thigh.\\n\\n- Gross can grow up to 20 cm; gray-white fleshy hemorrhagic\\n- Histology sheets of large anaplastic spindle or polygonal cells hyperchromatic bizarre nuclei pleomorphic cells with variable sizes and shapes abnormal mitotic figures\\n- IHC by definition lacks markers of differentiation desmin/myogenin negative S100 negative cytokeratin negative -- no consistent genetic pattern\\n- Treatment surgery + adjuvant chemo and/or radiation\\n- Prognosis generally poor; metastasis occurs in up to 50% of cases\\n\\n---\\n\\n Other Soft Tissue Tumors Briefly Mentioned \\n\\n- Rhabdomyoma benign skeletal muscle tumor; commonly found in the heart; associated with tuberous sclerosis \\n- Leiomyoma benign smooth muscle tumor; most common in the uterus fibroids \\n- Leiomyosarcoma malignant smooth muscle tumor; also commonly found in the uterus; may metastasize\\n\\n mnemonic\\n Soft Tissue Tumor Cell Origins AFSU \\n A dipose Lipoma benign / Liposarcoma malignant \\n F ibroblast Nodular fasciitis / Fibromatosis / Fibrosarcoma\\n S keletal muscle Rhabdomyoma benign / Rhabdomyosarcoma malignant \\n U ncertain Synovial sarcoma t X;18 / UPS diagnosis of exclusion \\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Summary Table\\n\\n| Tumor | Cell of Origin | Benign/Malignant | Key Feature | Hallmark |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Lipoma | Adipose | Benign | Most common soft tissue tumor in adults | Encapsulated mobile painless |\\n| Liposarcoma | Adipose | Malignant | Most common adult sarcoma | Lipoblasts S100+ three subtypes |\\n| Nodular fasciitis | Fibroblast | Benign | Mimics sarcoma | MYH9-USP6 translocation self-limited |\\n| Dupuytren contracture | Fibroblast | Benign | Palmar fibromatosis | Flexion contracture skin dimpling |\\n| Desmoid tumor | Fibroblast | Locally aggressive | Never metastasizes | APC/beta-catenin WNT signaling |\\n| Fibrosarcoma | Fibroblast | Malignant | Diagnosis of exclusion | Herringbone pattern vimentin+ |\\n| Rhabdomyosarcoma | Skeletal muscle | Malignant | Most common childhood sarcoma | Desmin/myogenin+ multiple subtypes |\\n| Synovial sarcoma | Uncertain | Malignant | Misnomer -- NOT from synovium | t X;18 biphasic pattern CK7+ |\\n| UPS | Uncertain | Malignant | Unclassifiable high-grade | By exclusion no lineage markers | Pathoma Musculoskeletal Pathology > Soft Tissue Tumors BandB Musculoskeletal > Soft Tissue Tumors SketchyPath Musculoskeletal & Derm > Soft Tissue Tumors FirstAid Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Soft Tissue Tumors What is the most common soft tissue tumor in adults? Lipoma. It is a benign well-encapsulated tumor of mature adipocytes found in the subcutaneous tissue of the proximal extremities or trunk in middle-aged adults. It is mobile painless and cured by simple excision. Concept What are the three subtypes of liposarcoma? Well-differentiated mature adipocytes + lipoblasts MDM2/12q13-15 amplification indolent Myxoid mucopolysaccharide stroma with chicken-wire capillaries FUS-DDIT3 fusion intermediate and Pleomorphic bizarre nuclei complex karyotype aggressive with frequent metastases . Concept What is a lipoblast? An immature adipocyte with a large dark nucleus that is beginning to produce lipid droplets in the cytoplasm. The lipid droplets create characteristic nuclear scalloping. Lipoblasts are a hallmark of liposarcoma. Concept What is nodular fasciitis and why can it mimic sarcoma? A benign self-limited fibroblastic/myofibroblastic proliferation in young adults. It mimics sarcoma because it grows rapidly weeks to months is highly cellular with frequent mitoses and contains a clonal MYH9-USP6 translocation. Despite this it never becomes malignant and often regresses spontaneously. Clinical What is Dupuytren contracture? A superficial palmar fibromatosis causing progressive flexion contracture of the fingers. It involves irregular thickening of the palmar fascia with nodular fibroblastic tissue and abundant collagen. Incidence increases with age. Clinical What mutations drive deep fibromatosis desmoid tumors ? APC and beta-catenin mutations both leading to increased WNT signaling. Most cases have sporadic beta-catenin mutations. Patients with FAP familial adenomatous polyposis or Gardner syndrome germline APC mutations are predisposed. Concept What is the characteristic histologic pattern of fibrosarcoma? The herringbone pattern -- alternating bundles of spindle cells creating a V-shaped arrangement. Other features include high N C ratio hyperchromatic nuclei and mitotic activity. It is vimentin-positive S100-negative and cytokeratin-negative. Clinical What is the most common soft tissue sarcoma of childhood? Rhabdomyosarcoma. It shows skeletal muscle differentiation and stains positive for desmin and myogenin. The embryonal subtype is most common while sarcoma botryoides has the best prognosis. Concept What is sarcoma botryoides and what is its classic gross appearance? A variant of embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma that develops in the walls of hollow mucosal-lined structures vagina bladder nasopharynx in young children. It grows in a grape-like cluster polypoid fashion and has a cambium layer beneath the epithelium. It has the best prognosis of all rhabdomyosarcoma subtypes. Clinical What translocation defines synovial sarcoma? t X;18 . It produces fusion proteins that interfere with chromatin/gene expression. Synovial sarcoma occurs near large joints in patients aged 20-40 and has monophasic spindle cells only or biphasic spindle cells + glandular epithelial structures patterns. Clinical How is undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma UPS diagnosed? By exclusion. UPS is a high-grade mesenchymal tumor that cannot be classified along any recognized cell lineage. It lacks differentiation markers on IHC desmin/myogenin negative S100 negative cytokeratin negative and has no consistent/reproducible genetic abnormalities. Concept What benign tumor is associated with tuberous sclerosis? Rhabdomyoma -- a benign skeletal muscle tumor most commonly found in the heart. It is classically associated with tuberous sclerosis. Clinical match Lipoma most common soft tissue tumor resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > Musculoskeletal > search lipoma soft tissue benign encapsulated match Liposarcoma subtypes well-differentiated myxoid pleomorphic resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma / BandB > Musculoskeletal > search liposarcoma lipoblast MDM2 myxoid match Nodular fasciitis sarcoma mimic resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > MSK > search nodular fasciitis self-limited fibroblast match Dupuytren contracture palmar fibromatosis resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma / BandB > Musculoskeletal > search dupuytren contracture palmar fibromatosis match Deep fibromatosis / Desmoid tumors APC beta-catenin WNT resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > MSK > search desmoid fibromatosis APC beta-catenin WNT Gardner match Fibrosarcoma herringbone pattern resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > MSK > search fibrosarcoma herringbone vimentin match Rhabdomyosarcoma subtypes embryonal alveolar botryoides pleomorphic resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma / SketchyPath > MSK > search rhabdomyosarcoma botryoides desmin myogenin match Sarcoma botryoides grape-like cambium layer resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > MSK / Repro > search sarcoma botryoides grape cambium vagina match Rhabdomyoma tuberous sclerosis cardiac resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > Cardiac / MSK > search rhabdomyoma tuberous sclerosis cardiac benign match Synovial sarcoma t X;18 biphasic resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > MSK > search synovial sarcoma translocation X 18 biphasic match Leiomyoma and leiomyosarcoma uterine smooth muscle tumors resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > Repro / MSK > search leiomyoma fibroid leiomyosarcoma uterus match Undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma UPS formerly MFH resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > MSK > search undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma UPS MFH match In-house lecture primary resource resources In-house lecture is best. No specific AnKing deck match found for this topic -- supplement with Pathoma Ch. 22 MSK First Aid MSK section and Boards & Beyond MSK Pathology. Lipoma vs. Liposarcoma Lipoma is the most common soft tissue tumor benign encapsulated mature adipocytes subcutaneous . Liposarcoma is the most common adult sarcoma deep invasive contains lipoblasts . The presence of lipoblasts immature adipocytes with nuclear scalloping is a key indicator of liposarcoma. Nodular Fasciitis The Great Sarcoma Mimic Nodular fasciitis is benign despite having a clonal translocation MYH9-USP6 and rapid growth with frequent mitoses. It is self-limited and rarely recurs after excision. Misdiagnosis as sarcoma can lead to inappropriately aggressive treatment. Desmoid Tumors and FAP/Gardner Syndrome Desmoid tumors deep fibromatosis are driven by APC/beta-catenin mutations that increase WNT signaling. Patients with FAP or Gardner syndrome are predisposed. These tumors never metastasize but act locally aggressive infiltrating surrounding structures. They are managed by oncologists despite being technically benign. Fibrosarcoma Herringbone Pattern The herringbone pattern -- alternating V-shaped bundles of spindle cells -- is the histologic hallmark of fibrosarcoma. It is a diagnosis of exclusion requiring IHC and molecular studies to rule out other sarcomas. Many historical fibrosarcomas were reclassified as other tumor types. Rhabdomyosarcoma Subtypes by Age Embryonal/sarcoma botryoides children. Alveolar older children to young adults. Pleomorphic adults 50+. Sarcoma botryoides grape-like cluster in hollow organs has the best prognosis. PAX-FOXO1 fusion alveolar predicts poor survival. Synovial Sarcoma is NOT from Synovium Despite its name synovial sarcoma does not originate from synovial tissue. It has a characteristic t X;18 translocation and can show biphasic histology spindle cells + epithelial glands . It commonly metastasizes to lungs and unusually for sarcomas to regional lymph nodes. questions 1 A 55-year-old man presents with a large deep-seated mass in the retroperitoneum. Biopsy reveals malignant adipose tissue with bizarre hyperchromatic nuclei of various shapes and sizes scattered lipoblasts with nuclear scalloping and areas of necrosis. Which of the following best describes the expected genetic findings in this neoplasm? t 12;16 creating a FUS-DDIT3 fusion gene Amplification of 12q13-15 including MDM2 Complex karyotype without reproducible genetic abnormalities t X;18 creating an SS18-SSX fusion gene Germline APC mutation with loss of heterozygosity 3 The description retroperitoneum malignant adipose bizarre hyperchromatic nuclei of various shapes/sizes lipoblasts necrosis is classic for pleomorphic liposarcoma. Pleomorphic liposarcomas have complex karyotypes without reproducible abnormalities. t 12;16 FUS-DDIT3 = myxoid liposarcoma; 12q13-15 amplification = well-differentiated liposarcoma; t X;18 = synovial sarcoma; APC mutations = desmoid tumors. 2 A 28-year-old woman presents with a rapidly growing firm tender nodule on her forearm that appeared 3 weeks after trauma. Biopsy shows highly cellular tissue with plump spindle cells frequent mitoses all normal-appearing and a tissue-culture-like growth pattern. Despite these alarming features the lesion is most likely benign. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? Fibrosarcoma Nodular fasciitis Deep fibromatosis desmoid tumor Dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans Superficial fibromatosis 2 Nodular fasciitis is a benign self-limited fibroblastic proliferation that classically occurs in young adults on the forearm after trauma. Despite rapid growth high cellularity and frequent mitoses all mitotic figures are normal-appearing. The tissue-culture appearance randomly oriented proliferating cells is characteristic. It contains a MYH9-USP6 translocation but behaves benignly. 3 A 6-year-old girl presents with vaginal bleeding. Examination reveals a polypoid grape-like cluster of tissue protruding from the vagina. Biopsy shows a dense layer of tumor cells beneath the vaginal epithelium cambium layer that stains positive for desmin and myogenin. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? Alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma sarcoma botryoides Synovial sarcoma Leiomyosarcoma Pleomorphic rhabdomyosarcoma 2 The clinical presentation young girl vaginal polypoid grape-like mass with cambium layer and positive desmin/myogenin staining are classic for sarcoma botryoides a variant of embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma. It develops in the walls of hollow mucosal-lined structures in children and has the best prognosis of all rhabdomyosarcoma subtypes. 4 A 35-year-old man presents with a deep-seated painful mass near his knee that has been slowly growing for 2 years. MRI shows a well-defined mass in the popliteal fossa. Biopsy reveals biphasic histology with uniform spindle cells in tightly packed fascicles interspersed with gland-like structures lined by cuboidal to columnar epithelioid cells. The epithelial component stains positive for CK7. Which of the following cytogenetic findings is most likely? t 11;22 producing EWSR1-FLI1 fusion gene t X;18 translocation t 12;16 producing FUS-DDIT3 fusion gene Amplification of 12q13-15 region Complex karyotype with ring chromosomes 2 The location deep soft tissue near the knee patient age 20-40 biphasic histology spindle cells + glandular epithelial structures and CK7 positivity are classic for synovial sarcoma. The t X;18 translocation is the defining cytogenetic finding producing proteins that interfere with chromatin gene expression. Despite its name synovial sarcoma does not originate from synovium. 5 A 25-year-old woman with a history of familial adenomatous polyposis FAP presents with a large firm fixed abdominal wall mass. Biopsy shows bland fibroblasts arranged in long parallel fascicles with dense collagen no cellular atypia and no mitotic activity. The mass infiltrates into the adjacent rectus abdominis muscle. Which of the following pathways is most likely aberrantly activated in this neoplasm? RAS-MAPK signaling pathway JAK-STAT signaling pathway WNT signaling pathway Hedgehog signaling pathway Notch signaling pathway 3 This is a desmoid tumor deep fibromatosis in a patient with FAP. FAP involves germline APC mutations and APC normally degrades beta-catenin. Loss of APC function leads to beta-catenin accumulation and constitutive activation of the WNT signaling pathway which drives fibroblast proliferation. The bland cytology with no atypia or mitoses but locally aggressive/infiltrative behavior is characteristic."}, {"id": "l107", "title": "Lecture #107: DPR - History and Physical Exam of the Hand and Wrist", "module": "DPR", "content": "id l107 module DPR l107 Lecture 107 DPR - History and Physical Exam of the Hand and Wrist lecturer R. Morgan D.O. DPR pdf content/L107 HighYield Render.pdf Lecture 107 History and Physical Exam of the Hand and Wrist\\n\\n Lecturer R. Morgan D.O. | Session Lecture 107\\n\\nThis DPR lecture provides a structured systematic approach to evaluating hand and wrist complaints. The clinical pearl that anchors the entire session the diagnosis is in the history . A thorough history of present illness will guide your differential before you ever lay hands on the patient and the physical exam then confirms or excludes what you are already thinking. The lecture moves through history-taking the four cardinal signs of inflammation inspection palpation landmarks range of motion motor strength grading special tests Tinel s Phalen s Finkelstein Allen neurovascular assessment and osteopathic exam and treatment. A case study of carpal tunnel syndrome ties it all together.\\n\\n---\\n\\n History-Taking for the Hand and Wrist\\n\\nThe first step is always to determine the mechanism of injury . Ask What were you doing when the pain began? The answer may reveal an acute event such as a fall on an outstretched hand FOOSH or an insidious chronic onset from repetitive use such as typing.\\n\\n Key History Elements\\n\\n- Location Have the patient point with one finger. The hand and wrist contain 8 carpal bones multiple phalanges and the distal radius/ulna so precise localization matters enormously\\n- Laterality Is the pain unilateral or bilateral? Did both sides begin simultaneously or sequentially? Symmetric bilateral involvement raises suspicion for systemic arthritis\\n- Pain grading OPPQRSTA Rate severity 0-10 assess progression over time and determine if pain disproportionate to exam raises concern for compartment syndrome \\n- Radiation Does pain stay localized to a bone tendon or muscle or does it travel from the neck or shoulder?\\n- Swelling Is it constant or intermittent? Worse with specific activities such as typing?\\n- Paresthesias Which fingers are involved? Numbness in digits 4-5 suggests ulnar neuropathy possibly originating at the elbow. Distribution determines the nerve root or peripheral nerve involved\\n- Weakness Can they write turn a doorknob do a push-up or type? For elderly patients living alone wrist dysfunction can profoundly affect activities of daily living bathing feeding dressing \\n- Aggravating activities and occupation Typists secretaries drivers and athletes have different risk profiles. Repetitive hobbies sewing painting archery also matter\\n\\n highyield\\nAlways determine the mechanism of injury first. Ask what the patient was doing when pain began. Pain out of proportion to exam suggests compartment syndrome. The distribution of paresthesias identifies the specific nerve root or peripheral nerve.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n The Four Signs of Inflammation\\n\\nOn exam systematically assess each cardinal sign applied to the wrist and hand \\n\\n| Sign | Latin | What to Assess |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Swelling | Tumor | Synovial membrane boggy/doughy joint effusion or soft tissue structures bursa tendons tendon sheaths |\\n| Warmth | Calor | Use the backs of your fingers; compare involved joint with contralateral side. Present in arthritis tendonitis bursitis and osteomyelitis |\\n| Redness | Rubor | Least common sign of inflammation; when present over superficial joints fingers toes knees suspect septic arthritis crystalline arthritis or rheumatoid arthritis |\\n| Pain/Tenderness | Dolor | Localize the specific anatomic structure. Diffuse tenderness + warmth over thickened synovium = arthritis or infection. Focal tenderness = specific bone or muscle injury |\\n\\n highyield\\nRedness rubor over a joint is the least common inflammatory sign but the most specific for septic crystalline or rheumatoid arthritis. Always compare the involved side with the contralateral side.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Inspection\\n\\nBefore palpating visually evaluate both hands simultaneously for \\n\\n- Deformity asymmetry and obvious dislocations\\n- Lacerations bruising and erythema\\n- Muscle atrophy especially thenar atrophy hallmark of chronic carpal tunnel syndrome from median nerve damage \\n- Ganglion cysts may only be visible with the wrist flexed; in neutral/extension the cyst can be hidden between tendons \\n- Nodules such as Heberden s nodes DIP osteoarthritis or Bouchard s nodes PIP osteoarthritis \\n\\n---\\n\\n Palpation Landmarks\\n\\nAlways start as far from the painful area as possible and systematically work toward it. This prevents unnecessary patient discomfort and may reveal additional areas of involvement. Never use your thumb to palpate -- the pulse in your thumb can be confused with the patient s.\\n\\n Key Landmarks\\n\\n| Landmark | Clinical Significance |\\n|---|---|\\n| Anatomical snuffbox scaphoid | Tenderness here after FOOSH = suspect scaphoid fracture most common carpal fracture ; risk of avascular necrosis of the scaphoid due to retrograde blood supply |\\n| CMC joint base of thumb | Common site for osteoarthritis and tendon insertions |\\n| Hook of hamate | Lateral proximal wrist; fracture or dislocation causes tenderness here |\\n| MCP joints dorsal hand | Tenderness suggests synovitis OA or RA; check all MCP joints systematically |\\n\\n correlate\\nScaphoid Fracture\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Most common carpal fracture typically from FOOSH; the scaphoid has a retrograde blood supply enters distally so proximal fractures risk avascular necrosis\\n- Presentation Pain in anatomical snuffbox wrist pain after fall may have negative initial X-ray\\n- Diagnosis Snuffbox tenderness on palpation; if X-ray negative but clinical suspicion high immobilize and re-image in 10-14 days or obtain MRI/CT\\n- Treatment Thumb spica cast for non-displaced fractures; surgical fixation for displaced or proximal pole fractures\\n- Lecturer s Point Pain may begin before you even reach the snuffbox on palpation -- still consider this a positive finding\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Range of Motion\\n\\n Active ROM\\n\\n- Fist and spread Have the patient make a full fist and then spread all fingers fully. Compare bilaterally and simultaneously to detect subtle differences\\n- Radial and ulnar deviation Normal is approximately 20-30 degrees each direction slightly less radially \\n- Wrist flexion and extension Normal is approximately 70 degrees each direction\\n- Always compare both sides simultaneously when possible\\n\\n Passive ROM\\n\\n- Performed by the examiner to determine whether limitation is from pain swelling or structural restriction\\n- Perform on any joint felt to be abnormal during active ROM\\n- Important to identify what is limiting the range -- pain versus mechanical block\\n\\n---\\n\\n Motor Strength Testing\\n\\n Grading Scale 0-5 \\n\\n| Grade | Finding |\\n|---|---|\\n| 5/5 | Full strength against resistance |\\n| 4/5 | Slightly diminished strength but still against gravity and resistance |\\n| 3/5 | Movement against gravity only |\\n| 2/5 | Movement only with gravity eliminated |\\n| 1/5 | Slight involuntary muscle contraction fasciculation/trace |\\n| 0/5 | No movement at all |\\n\\n Grip Strength\\n\\n- Test intrinsic hand muscles by having the patient grasp your two fingers bilaterally and simultaneously\\n- Decreased grip strength = weakness of finger flexors and/or intrinsic hand muscles\\n- Grip weakness + wrist pain = consider De Quervain s tenosynovitis\\n- Test individual finger strength when a specific digit is injured\\n\\n highyield\\nGrip weakness combined with wrist pain is suggestive of De Quervain s tenosynovitis. 3/5 motor strength means movement against gravity only. Always test bilaterally and simultaneously for subtle comparison.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Special Tests\\n\\n Tinel s Sign Carpal Tunnel \\n\\nTap over the median nerve at the volar wrist. A positive test reproduces pain and/or paresthesias in the median nerve distribution thumb index middle and radial half of ring finger . Even pain alone without paresthesias can be considered positive but both together strengthen the finding.\\n\\n Phalen s Test Carpal Tunnel \\n\\nHyperflex both wrists by pressing the dorsa of the hands together. Hold for 30-60 seconds. A positive test reproduces pain and/or paresthesias in the median nerve distribution. This manually compresses the median nerve to reproduce carpal tunnel symptoms.\\n\\n Finkelstein s Test De Quervain s Tenosynovitis \\n\\nHave the patient tuck the thumb into the palm and make a fist then ulnar-deviate the wrist. This stretches the EPB extensor pollicis brevis and APL abductor pollicis longus tendons. Pain along the radial wrist at the first dorsal compartment is a positive result indicating inflammation of the tendon sheaths tenosynovitis .\\n\\n Thumb Abduction Test Carpal Tunnel \\n\\nHave the patient abduct the thumb against resistance. Weakness indicates thenar muscle compromise from median nerve compression. This is especially significant in chronic carpal tunnel syndrome where thenar atrophy has developed.\\n\\n Allen Test Vascular Patency \\n\\nOcclude both radial and ulnar arteries at the wrist. Have the patient open and close the fist several times until the hand blanches. Release one artery at a time and observe for color return within 2-3 seconds. If the hand remains pale there is vascular compromise of that artery. Repeat for the other artery. Also performed before placing an arterial line.\\n\\n correlate\\nCarpal Tunnel Syndrome\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Compression of the median nerve within the carpal tunnel bounded by carpal bones and transverse carpal ligament/flexor retinaculum \\n- Presentation Tingling and numbness in digits 1-3 and radial half of digit 4; pain worse with typing driving or repetitive wrist use; may wake patient at night\\n- Diagnosis Positive Tinel s sign and Phalen s test; thenar atrophy on inspection chronic ; decreased grip strength; decreased sensation in median nerve distribution; EMG/nerve conduction study for confirmation\\n- Treatment Wrist splinting especially at night NSAIDs corticosteroid injection osteopathic manipulation myofascial release of flexor retinaculum and surgical carpal tunnel release for refractory cases\\n- Lecturer s Point Thenar atrophy indicates chronic longstanding median nerve damage and is often irreversible. Pain out of proportion to exam = compartment syndrome not carpal tunnel\\n \\n\\n correlate\\nDe Quervain s Tenosynovitis\\n\\n- Pathophysiology Inflammation of the synovial sheaths surrounding the EPB and APL tendons in the first dorsal compartment of the wrist\\n- Presentation Pain along the radial side of the wrist worsened by thumb use gripping and wrist deviation; grip weakness often present\\n- Diagnosis Positive Finkelstein s test pain with ulnar deviation while thumb is tucked into fist \\n- Treatment Thumb spica splint NSAIDs corticosteroid injection and surgical release for refractory cases\\n- Lecturer s Point This test is very sensitive -- if there is inflammation in those tendon sheaths the patient will have a significantly positive response\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Neurovascular Exam\\n\\n- Pulses Palpate radial artery graded on 4-point scale; 2+/4 = normal bounding . The ulnar artery is often too deep to palpate directly so use the Allen test instead\\n- Capillary refill Compress the nail bed for 2-3 seconds then release. Normal refill is less than 3 seconds. Greater than 3-5 seconds is a positive finding indicating vascular compromise\\n- Sensation Test light touch cotton swab and sharp sensation broken tongue depressor in dermatomal distributions C6 C7 C8 across all five digits bilaterally\\n- After any significant wrist trauma especially FOOSH always assess neurovascular integrity due to risk of vascular compromise or avascular necrosis\\n\\n---\\n\\n Osteopathic Exam and Treatment\\n\\nThe osteopathic component assesses somatic dysfunction of the carpal bones decreased motion of lunate capitate etc. and provides in-office treatment. Key techniques discussed \\n\\n- Myofascial wrist retinaculum release The physician uses specific hand placement over the palmar surface and flexor retinaculum to apply transverse distraction until soft tissue release is achieved\\n- Carpal bone HVLA The physician engages a dorsiflexion or palmar flexion barrier and applies a mobilization with impulse thrust to restore carpal bone motion\\n\\n highyield\\nOMT techniques for the wrist provide immediate pain relief in office. Decreased motion of the lunate and capitate bones is a common finding in carpal tunnel syndrome. The myofascial retinaculum release targets the flexor retinaculum directly.\\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Sample Case Carpal Tunnel Syndrome\\n\\nA 46-year-old female presents with right wrist pain that started 10 days ago and has progressively worsened. She describes a tingling numbness in the middle of her palm 4/10 currently 7/10 at worst 3/10 at best . Typing at work aggravates the pain. She has tried stretches ice heat and ibuprofen 200 mg daily without improvement. She denies radiation.\\n\\n Exam findings No gross abnormalities on inspection; tenderness localized to midline right wrist; full passive and active ROM in unaffected digits; symmetric pulses bilaterally; decreased grip strength right hand; positive Tinel s sign and Phalen s test on the right; osteopathic exam reveals decreased motion of lunate and capitate of the right hand.\\n\\n Diagnosis Carpal tunnel syndrome.\\n\\n mnemonic\\n Wrist Special Tests TPF-A \\n T inel s = tapping the median nerve carpal tunnel \\n P halen s = pressing backs of flexed hands together for 30-60 sec carpal tunnel \\n F inkelstein s = thumb tucked + ulnar deviation De Quervain s \\n A llen = occlude both arteries then release one at a time vascular patency \\n \\n\\n---\\n\\n Summary Table Special Tests\\n\\n| Test | What It Assesses | Positive Finding | Associated Condition |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Tinel s sign | Median nerve irritability | Pain/paresthesias in median nerve distribution with tapping | Carpal tunnel syndrome |\\n| Phalen s test | Median nerve compression | Pain/paresthesias after 30-60 sec of wrist hyperflexion | Carpal tunnel syndrome |\\n| Finkelstein s test | EPB/APL tendon inflammation | Pain along radial wrist with ulnar deviation and thumb tucked | De Quervain s tenosynovitis |\\n| Thumb abduction | Thenar muscle strength | Weakness against resistance | Carpal tunnel chronic |\\n| Allen test | Radial/ulnar artery patency | Hand remains blanched after releasing one artery | Vascular compromise |\\n| Grip strength | Intrinsic hand muscles | Decreased grip bilaterally or unilaterally | Neuropathy arthritis De Quervain s | BandB Musculoskeletal > Upper Extremity Exam SketchyPath Musculoskeletal & Derm > Carpal Tunnel Syndrome FirstAid Musculoskeletal > Upper Extremity > Carpal Tunnel & De Quervain s Pathoma Musculoskeletal > Peripheral Nerve Injuries What is the first and most important question to ask a patient presenting with hand or wrist pain? What were you doing when the pain began? This determines the mechanism of injury acute vs. chronic/insidious onset and guides the remainder of the history and physical exam. Concept What are the four cardinal signs of inflammation and what are their Latin names? Swelling tumor warmth calor redness rubor and pain/tenderness dolor . Rubor is the least common but when present over a superficial joint suggests septic crystalline or rheumatoid arthritis. Concept What does thenar atrophy on inspection suggest? Chronic carpal tunnel syndrome. Prolonged median nerve compression leads to denervation and wasting of the thenar muscles opponens pollicis abductor pollicis brevis flexor pollicis brevis . This is often irreversible. Clinical What is the most common carpal fracture and how is it assessed on exam? The scaphoid fracture assessed by palpating the anatomical snuffbox. Tenderness here after a fall on outstretched hand FOOSH is highly suspicious. Risk of avascular necrosis exists due to the scaphoid s retrograde blood supply. Clinical How is Tinel s sign performed and what does a positive result indicate? Tap over the median nerve at the volar wrist. A positive test reproduces pain and/or paresthesias in the median nerve distribution digits 1-3 and radial half of digit 4 indicating carpal tunnel syndrome. Clinical How is Phalen s test performed and what does a positive result indicate? Hyperflex both wrists by pressing the dorsa of the hands together for 30-60 seconds. If this reproduces pain and/or paresthesias in the median nerve distribution it is positive for carpal tunnel syndrome. Clinical How is Finkelstein s test performed and what does it diagnose? Patient tucks the thumb into the palm makes a fist and the examiner ulnar-deviates the wrist. Pain along the radial wrist first dorsal compartment is positive for De Quervain s tenosynovitis inflammation of EPB and APL tendon sheaths . Clinical What does the Allen test assess and how is it performed? Vascular patency of the radial and ulnar arteries. Occlude both arteries have the patient pump the fist until the hand blanches then release one artery at a time. Normal color returns within 2-3 seconds. Abnormal hand stays blanched vascular compromise of the released artery. Clinical What motor strength grade indicates movement against gravity only? 3/5. The full scale 5/5 = full strength 4/5 = slight weakness 3/5 = against gravity only 2/5 = gravity eliminated 1/5 = trace contraction 0/5 = no movement. Concept What is the normal capillary refill time and what does a prolonged refill suggest? Normal is less than 3 seconds. A refill time of 3-5 seconds or greater is a positive finding suggesting vascular compromise. Assessed by compressing the nail bed for 2-3 seconds and releasing. Clinical Why should you never use your thumb when palpating for a pulse? The thumb has its own arterial pulse princeps pollicis artery which can be mistaken for the patient s pulse leading to a false-positive finding. Concept What combination of grip weakness and wrist pain should make you think of De Quervain s tenosynovitis? Grip weakness accompanied by radial-sided wrist pain especially worsened by thumb use gripping or lifting. Confirm with Finkelstein s test. The EPB and APL tendons in the first dorsal compartment are inflamed. Clinical match Carpal tunnel syndrome median nerve compression Tinel s Phalen s resources AnKing Step 1 > BandB / Pathoma > MSK / Neuro > search carpal tunnel median nerve Phalen Tinel thenar match Scaphoid fracture FOOSH anatomical snuffbox avascular necrosis resources AnKing Step 1 > BandB / FirstAid > MSK > search scaphoid fracture snuffbox avascular necrosis FOOSH match De Quervain s tenosynovitis Finkelstein test EPB APL resources AnKing Step 1 > BandB / FirstAid > MSK > search de quervain tenosynovitis finkelstein EPB APL radial match Allen test radial/ulnar artery patency arterial line resources AnKing Step 1 > BandB > MSK / Cardiovascular > search Allen test radial ulnar artery patency blanch match Motor strength grading scale 0-5 resources AnKing Step 1 > BandB > Neuro > search motor strength grading scale 0 5 gravity match Cardinal signs of inflammation tumor calor rubor dolor resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > Inflammation > search cardinal signs inflammation tumor calor rubor dolor match Median nerve distribution thumb index middle radial ring finger resources AnKing Step 1 > BandB / FirstAid > Neuro / MSK > search median nerve hand distribution thenar match Ulnar nerve digits 4-5 ulnar neuropathy at elbow resources AnKing Step 1 > BandB / FirstAid > Neuro > search ulnar nerve neuropathy digits 4 5 elbow cubital match Ganglion cyst wrist dorsal flexion to visualize resources AnKing Step 1 > BandB > MSK > search ganglion cyst wrist dorsal transillumination match Compartment syndrome pain out of proportion to exam resources AnKing Step 1 > BandB / FirstAid > MSK > search compartment syndrome pain proportion 5 Ps match In-house lecture primary resource resources In-house lecture is best. No specific AnKing deck match found for this DPR topic -- supplement with Boards & Beyond MSK Upper Extremity Exam First Aid MSK section and clinical skills resources for wrist/hand exam techniques. The Diagnosis Is in the History A thorough HPI will give you the likely diagnosis before you ever touch the patient. Every question you ask should have a purpose to include or exclude a diagnosis already on your differential. The physical exam confirms what you already suspect. Scaphoid Fracture The Silent Emergency Scaphoid tenderness in the anatomical snuffbox after FOOSH demands imaging even if initial X-rays are negative. The scaphoid has retrograde blood supply enters distally so proximal fractures risk avascular necrosis if not immobilized promptly. Pain may begin before you even reach the snuffbox -- still a positive finding. Thenar Atrophy = Chronic Carpal Tunnel Visible wasting of the thenar eminence is a late sign of carpal tunnel syndrome indicating longstanding median nerve damage that is often irreversible. This is why early diagnosis with Tinel s and Phalen s testing matters. Finkelstein s Test Is Very Sensitive If there is inflammation in the EPB and APL tendon sheaths the patient will have a strongly positive Finkelstein s test. The combination of grip weakness + radial wrist pain should always prompt this test. Pain Out of Proportion = Compartment Syndrome Carpal tunnel causes low-to-medium grade pain. If pain is disproportionate to exam findings consider compartment syndrome -- a surgical emergency with potential for tissue necrosis. Palpation Technique Matters Never start palpation at the point of maximum tenderness. Always begin as far from the painful area as possible and work toward it. Never use your thumb to palpate its pulse can be mistaken for the patient s . Be systematic -- proximal to distal or distal to proximal but be consistent. questions 1 A 52-year-old female secretary presents with a 3-week history of numbness and tingling in her right thumb index finger and middle finger. The symptoms are worse at night and with typing. On exam she has thenar atrophy of the right hand. Which of the following tests if positive would best support the suspected diagnosis? Finkelstein s test Allen test Phalen s test Adson s test Speed s test 3 The presentation numbness/tingling in median nerve distribution worse at night and with typing thenar atrophy is classic for carpal tunnel syndrome. Phalen s test wrist hyperflexion for 30-60 seconds manually compresses the median nerve and reproduces symptoms. Finkelstein s tests for De Quervain s Allen s tests vascular patency Adson s tests for thoracic outlet syndrome and Speed s tests for biceps tendinopathy. 2 A 25-year-old male falls on an outstretched hand during a basketball game. He presents with wrist pain and tenderness in the anatomical snuffbox. X-ray is negative. What is the most appropriate next step? Reassure the patient and discharge without treatment Immobilize in a thumb spica cast and repeat imaging in 10-14 days Obtain an immediate CT angiogram Begin physical therapy and follow up in 6 weeks Perform an Allen test to assess vascular compromise 2 Snuffbox tenderness after FOOSH highly suggests scaphoid fracture even with a negative initial X-ray fractures may not be visible for 10-14 days . The correct approach is to immobilize in a thumb spica cast treat presumptively and repeat imaging. The scaphoid has retrograde blood supply and missed fractures risk avascular necrosis. MRI can also be obtained for earlier diagnosis. 3 A 30-year-old new mother presents with radial-sided wrist pain that worsens when she lifts her infant. On exam the patient has pain when she tucks her thumb into her palm and the examiner ulnar-deviates her wrist. Which structures are most likely inflamed? Flexor carpi radialis and flexor carpi ulnaris Extensor pollicis brevis and abductor pollicis longus Median nerve and flexor retinaculum Extensor carpi radialis longus and extensor carpi radialis brevis Ulnar nerve and Guyon s canal 2 The positive Finkelstein s test pain with ulnar deviation while thumb is tucked diagnoses De Quervain s tenosynovitis -- inflammation of the EPB extensor pollicis brevis and APL abductor pollicis longus tendon sheaths in the first dorsal compartment. This is common in new mothers from repetitive lifting of infants. 4 During a pre-procedural assessment before placing a radial arterial line a physician occludes both the radial and ulnar arteries has the patient pump the fist and then releases the ulnar artery. The hand remains pale for more than 5 seconds. What does this finding indicate? Normal finding; the ulnar artery is dominant The radial artery is the sole supply to the hand; radial arterial line is safe Inadequate perfusion from the ulnar artery alone; the radial line may not be safe Venous insufficiency of the hand Compartment syndrome is developing 3 The Allen test assesses collateral blood supply to the hand. When the ulnar artery is released and the hand remains pale it means the ulnar artery alone cannot adequately perfuse the hand. This means the hand relies significantly on the radial artery and cannulating it could compromise blood supply. Normal result color returns within 2-3 seconds of releasing either artery. 5 A patient with wrist pain has the following motor strength findings she can move her fingers against gravity but cannot overcome any applied resistance. How would this be graded on the standard motor strength scale? 1/5 2/5 3/5 4/5 5/5 3 3/5 indicates movement against gravity only with no ability to resist applied force. 5/5 = full strength; 4/5 = slight weakness but overcomes resistance; 2/5 = movement with gravity eliminated; 1/5 = trace contraction; 0/5 = no movement."}, {"id": "l108", "title": "Lecture #108: DPR: History and Physical Exam of the Shoulder and Elbow", "module": "OMM", "content": "id l108 module OMM l108 Lecture 108 DPR History and Physical Exam of the Shoulder and Elbow lecturer A. Coladner D.O. DPR pdf content/L108 HighYield Render.pdf Lecture 108 DPR History and Physical Exam of the Shoulder and Elbow Lecturer A. Coladner D.O. | Session Lecture 108 The evaluation of the shoulder and elbow requires a systematic approach moving from a thorough musculoskeletal history to a standardized physical examination sequence Inspection Palpation Range of Motion Active and Passive Strength Testing and Special Testing. Understanding the anatomy—the shoulder as a ball-and-socket joint and the elbow as a hinge joint—is critical for localizing pathology to specific structures such as tendons ligaments or the joint capsule itself. --- History and General Approach A musculoskeletal history should utilize the OPQRST mnemonic while emphasizing specific factors like prior injuries repetitive movements occupational or recreational and the exact localization of pain. - Deep vs. Superficial Deep pain often suggests tears or bone bruises while superficial pain may relate to skin lesions or fatty tumors. - Pain Localization Asking the patient to point with one finger helps distinguish intra-articular from extra-articular pathology. - Radiation Pain persisting for more than three months can radiate significantly e.g. carpal tunnel pain radiating to the shoulder . Practitioners must identify where the pain started to avoid being misled by radiation. - Constitutional Symptoms Fever chills or night sweats suggest an infectious or systemic inflammatory process rather than purely mechanical injury. highyield Radiation of musculoskeletal pain is common; always ask where the pain started . Pointing with one finger to the joint line often indicates intra-articular pathology. --- The Shoulder Physical Exam Inspection and Palpation Inspection must be performed from both the front and back to check for asymmetry muscular atrophy or scars. Bony Landmarks to Palpate - Clavicle and Acromioclavicular AC joint. - Acromion and Spine of the scapula. - Greater Tuberosity/Head of the humerus. - Bicipital Groove Contains the long head of the biceps tendon a frequent site of tenosynovitis. Range of Motion ROM and Strength Shoulder ROM is tested in multiple planes Flexion Extension Abduction Adduction Internal and External rotation. | Movement | Primary Muscles | Clinical Note | |---|---|---| | Flexion | Anterior Deltoid Pectoralis Major | | | Abduction | Supraspinatus first 15° Deltoid after 15° | Essential for Painful Arc testing | | Internal Rotation | Subscapularis Pectoralis Major | Measured by level on T-spine reached by thumb | | External Rotation | Infraspinatus Teres Minor | Tested with elbow at 90° | Manual Muscle Testing MMT Scale - 5/5 Normal strength against full resistance. - 4/5 Movement against gravity with some resistance. - 3/5 Movement against gravity only. - 2/5 Movement with gravity eliminated. - 1/5 Trace flicker of muscle contraction. - 0/5 No contraction paralysis . correlate Scapulohumeral Rhythm Abduction of the shoulder requires both glenohumeral and scapulothoracic movement. The supraspinatus initiates the first 15° of abduction after which the deltoid becomes the primary abductor. Weakness or pain during initiation often points to supraspinatus pathology. --- Shoulder Special Testing Special tests are designed to isolate specific structures or pathologies through provocation. | Test | Target / Finding | Technique | |---|---|---| | Neer Impingement | Shoulder Impingement | Forward pressure on scapula while raising arm; pain = positive | | Hawkins Impingement | Shoulder Impingement | Internal rotation of arm flexed at 90° at elbow/shoulder | | Empty Can Test | Supraspinatus Tendon | Push up against resistance with arms at 30° thumbs down | | Drop Arm Codman | Rotator Cuff Tear | Patient lowers abducted arm slowly; sudden drop = positive | | Apprehension Test | Anterior Instability | Patient feels shoulder will dislocate with external rotation/anterior pressure | | Yergason Test | Biceps Tendon Long Head | Resistance to supination with elbow flexed; pain in bicipital groove | | Crossover Test | AC Joint Pathology | Passive adduction of arm across the chest | highyield Impingement Signs Neer and Hawkins tests are the clinical standards for assessing subacromial impingement. Rotator Cuff Integrity The Empty Can test specifically isolates the supraspinatus while the Drop Arm test highly suggests a full-thickness tear. --- The Elbow Physical Exam The elbow is a stable hinge joint capable of flexion extension pronation and supination. Inspection and Palpation Palpation focuses on the Medial and Lateral Epicondyles and the Olecranon . The Ulnar Nerve is palpated within the ulnar groove medial to the olecranon ; it is superficial and easily irritated. Elbow Special Testing - Tinel s Sign Elbow Tapping over the ulnar nerve at the medial epicondyle. Paresthesia in the ulnar distribution suggests cubital tunnel syndrome. - Cozen’s Test Lateral Epicondylitis Resisted wrist extension with the elbow flexed. Pain at the lateral epicondyle indicates Tennis Elbow. - Medial Epicondylitis Test Resisted wrist flexion. Pain at the medial epicondyle indicates Golfer’s Elbow. correlate Epicondylitis vs. Nerve Entrapment - Lateral Epicondylitis Overuse of wrist extensors attachment site . - Medial Epicondylitis Overuse of wrist flexors attachment site . - Cubital Tunnel Nerve compression ulnar nerve rather than tendonitis; produces neurological symptoms numbness/tingling in 4th/5th digits . --- Summary of Common Diagnoses - Shoulder Rotator cuff pathology tendonitis/tears Glenohumeral instability Biceps tendinopathy Adhesive capsulitis Frozen shoulder . - Elbow Lateral/Medial epicondylitis Cubital tunnel syndrome Olecranon bursitis posterior . mnemonic Elbow Flexor Attachment M edial = M ake a fist Flexors L ateral = L ift the hand Extensors In-house lecture is best Shoulder and Elbow Physical Exam OnlineMedEd OMM > Shoulder What muscle initiates the first 15° of shoulder abduction? The supraspinatus . After 15° the deltoid takes over as the primary abductor. Anatomy A positive Drop Arm Codman test is highly suggestive of what pathology? A rotator cuff tear typically full-thickness . Clinical Which nerve is evaluated by tapping in the ulnar groove at the elbow Tinel s sign ? The ulnar nerve . Clinical What is the clinical name for pain at the lateral epicondyle during resisted wrist extension? Lateral epicondylitis Tennis Elbow . Clinical Describe a grade 3/5 on the Manual Muscle Testing MMT scale. The patient can perform the full range of motion against gravity only but cannot tolerate any added resistance from the examiner. Concept The Neer and Hawkins tests are designed to evaluate for what clinical syndrome? Impingement syndrome of the shoulder. Clinical match Shoulder Physical Exam Neer Hawkins Empty Can resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 01 Anatomy & Physiology 03 Upper Extremity Nerves | AK Step1 v11 Physeo 02 Anatomy 08 MSK 04 Shoulder match Rotator Cuff Muscles SITS resources AnKing Step 1 > search rotator cuff supraspinatus infraspinatus teres minor subscapularis match Lateral and Medial Epicondylitis resources AnKing Step 1 > search lateral epicondylitis medial tennis golfer match Ulnar Nerve Entrapment Cubital Tunnel resources AnKing Step 1 > search ulnar nerve cubital tunnel elbow match In-house lecture primary resource resources In-house lecture is best. Supplement with OnlineMedEd Shoulder and MSK Physical Exam modules. C5 Radiculopathy Mimic Pain in the shoulder area can occasionally be a referred symptom of a C5 radiculopathy; always screen the cervical spine if the shoulder exam is inconclusive. Dislocation Direction Anterior dislocations often occur when the arm is in external rotation while posterior dislocations rarer often via seizures/electrocution usually involve internal rotation . Bicipital Groove Palpation To best palpate the bicipital groove the arm should be slightly externally rotated to bring the groove anteriorly. questions 1 A 45-year-old painter presents with worsening right shoulder pain when working overhead. On examination there is a painful arc of motion between 60° and 120° of abduction. Strength is 5/5 in all planes. Both Neer and Hawkins tests result in sharp pain in the subacromial region. What is the most likely diagnosis? Adhesive Capsulitis Subacromial Impingement Syndrome Full-thickness Supraspinatus Tear Biceps Tendon Rupture Glenohumeral Osteoarthritis 1 Overhead activity painful arc and positive impingement signs Neer/Hawkins with preserved strength 5/5 are classic for impingement syndrome. A full-thickness tear would typically show weakness e.g. positive Empty Can or Drop Arm . 2 During a physical exam a physician asks a patient to hold their arms out at 30° of horizontal adduction and 90° of abduction with their thumbs pointing toward the floor. The patient is asked to resist downward pressure. Weakness and pain are elicited on the left side. Which muscle is primarily being tested? Deltoid Infraspinatus Teres Major Supraspinatus Subscapularis 3 This describes the Empty Can Jobe test which is specifically used to evaluate the integrity and strength of the supraspinatus muscle and tendon. 3 An amateur golfer presents with pain on the inner aspect of his elbow that worsens when he flips his wrist downward while holding a club. On examination there is point tenderness over a bony prominence on the medial side of the humerus. Pain is reproduced by resisting which of the following movements? Wrist Extension Wrist Flexion Elbow Extension Forearm Supination Shoulder External Rotation 1 Medial epicondylitis Golfer s elbow involves the common flexor tendon origin. It is characterized by tenderness at the medial epicondyle and pain reproduced by resisted wrist flexion and pronation. 4 A patient is able to actively flex their elbow against gravity through the full range of motion. However as soon as the examiner applies even minimal resistance the patient s arm gives way. What grade should be assigned to this patient s elbow flexion strength on the MMT scale? Grade 2 Grade 3 Grade 4 Grade 5 Grade 1 1 Grade 3 indicates the ability to move against gravity but not against any added resistance. Grade 4 would require some resistance and Grade 2 is for movement only with gravity eliminated. 5 A high school quarterback is tackled and feels his shoulder pop out. He presents to the ER with his arm held in a slightly abducted and externally rotated position. He is extremely resistant to any internal rotation. Which of the following special tests would most likely be positive in this patient once the acute injury is managed? Empty Can Test Drop Arm Test Apprehension Test Cozen s Test Yergason Test 2 Anterior dislocations are most common and often occur in external rotation. The apprehension test evaluates for glenohumeral instability and susceptibility to recurrent anterior dislocation."}, {"id": "l110", "title": "Lecture #110: Introduction to Orthopedics of the Upper Extremity", "module": "Unknown", "content": "id l110 module Unknown l110 Lecture 110 Introduction to Orthopedics of the Upper Extremity lecturer M. Heller D.O. Presented by N. Chernik Academic Medicine Scholar Orthopedics pdf content/L110 HighYield Render.pdf Lecture 110 Introduction to Orthopedics of the Upper Extremity Lecturer M. Heller D.O. Presented by N. Chernik Academic Medicine Scholar | Session Lecture 110 This lecture provides a systematic survey of orthopedic pathology of the upper extremity moving proximal-to-distal from the shoulder through the hand. Before diving into specific conditions the lecture establishes a framework for evaluating any orthopedic complaint obtain a thorough history focused on the mechanism of injury acute trauma vs. chronic overuse perform a complete musculoskeletal exam with relevant special tests utilize imaging X-ray CT MRI ultrasound for diagnosis and begin with conservative management RICE Rest Ice Compression Elevation before escalating to injections or surgery. A recurring clinical theme is that every fracture or dislocation carries the risk of neurovascular compromise so you must always consider the surrounding anatomy when evaluating these injuries. --- General Orthopedic Concepts Slides 2-4 The approach to any orthopedic concern begins with understanding the mechanism of injury. Is this an acute high-impact trauma that occurred minutes ago or a chronic insidious process that has been worsening over weeks to months? This distinction drives the differential and urgency. A full musculoskeletal exam should follow paired with appropriate imaging. Treatment generally starts conservative with RICE pain control and potentially joint injections escalating to surgical fixation nails pins plates fracture reductions only when necessary. - Complications to Consider with Any Fracture/Dislocation - Avascular necrosis death of bone from loss of blood supply - Nerve damage or entrapment - Compartment syndrome - Osteomyelitis infection - Deep vein thrombosis DVT --- Bone Anatomy and Fracture Types Slides 5-8 Understanding the anatomy of a long bone is essential for describing fracture location and predicting complications. The epiphysis is the end of the bone the metaphysis is the flared region between the epiphysis and the shaft the growth plate physis sits between them and the diaphysis is the long shaft. - Fracture Type Classification | Type | Description | Key Feature | |---|---|---| | Transverse | Fracture line perpendicular to the long axis | Clean horizontal break | | Oblique | Fracture at an angle to the long axis | Diagonal line | | Spiral | Fracture line encircles the shaft | Twisting mechanism | | Comminuted | More than two bone fragments | High-energy injury | | Avulsion | Fragment torn away from main bone mass | Ligament/tendon pull | | Impacted | Fragments driven into each other | Bones wedge together | | Displaced | Fragments separated from each other | Malalignment on imaging | | Compound Open | Bone pierces the skin | Infection risk; surgical emergency | - Pediatric Fractures - Greenstick Fracture Incomplete fracture extending partially through the bone due to bending stress. Fails on the tension side but not the compression side. Slide 7 - Torus Buckle Fracture Cortex buckles on the compression side. Distinguished from greenstick by buckling appearance rather than a fracture line. Slide 7 highyield Greenstick fractures fail on the tension side and are intact on the compression side. Torus fractures buckle on the compression side. Both are seen in pediatric patients due to the plasticity of immature bone. --- Salter-Harris Classification Slide 8 Pediatric growth plate injuries are classified by the Salter-Harris system which predicts the risk of growth arrest. The higher the type the greater the risk of permanent growth disturbance. - Type 1 S = Straight across Fracture runs along the growth plate only. Often occult on X-ray. - Type 2 A = Above Fracture through the growth plate and metaphysis. Most common type. - Type 3 L = Lower/below Fracture through the growth plate and epiphysis. - Type 4 T = Through/Two Fracture through the metaphysis growth plate and epiphysis. Increased risk of growth arrest. - Type 5 R = Rammed/Crush Compression injury to the growth plate. 100% growth arrest rate. Often not visible on initial imaging. mnemonic Salter-Harris Classification SALTR - S Straight across Type 1 - along the growth plate - A Above Type 2 - through metaphysis - L Lower/Below Type 3 - through epiphysis - T Through/Two Type 4 - through all three - R Rammed/Crush Type 5 - compression 100% growth arrest correlate Salter-Harris Type 5 Growth Plate Injury - Pathophysiology Axial compression force crushes the growth plate physis leading to premature closure. - Presentation May present with minimal findings on initial X-ray; diagnosed clinically or retrospectively when growth arrest becomes apparent. - Diagnosis Often a clinical diagnosis; initial X-rays may be normal. MRI can help confirm. - Complications 100% rate of growth arrest leading to limb length discrepancy or angular deformity. - Lecturer s Point Type 5 is the most feared because the growth plate is essentially obliterated and the injury is often invisible on initial imaging. --- Compartment Syndrome Slides 9-10 Skeletal muscles are organized within rigid osseofascial compartments. Compartment syndrome develops when pressure within these non-compliant compartments rises to the point of compromising perfusion. It is a surgical emergency. - Acute Compartment Syndrome - A true medical emergency that can lead to permanent functional loss - Internal causes Hemorrhage or edema e.g. from fractures - External causes Constrictive bandages casts traction - Most common cause Tibial diaphyseal fracture - Other causes Burns animal bites/stings trauma - Chronic Exertional Compartment Syndrome Exercise-induced much less severe self-limiting - Clinical Features 5 Ps - Pain Especially with passive movement most sensitive early finding - Pressure Increased tension of the muscle compartments - Paresthesia Due to nerve ischemia; may become irreversible - Paralysis From nerve and muscle ischemia - Pallor/Pulselessness Late findings indicating vascular compromise - Treatment Fasciotomy surgical decompression correlate Acute Compartment Syndrome - Pathophysiology Increased intracompartmental pressure from hemorrhage or edema compromises perfusion to the muscles and nerves within a rigid osseofascial compartment. - Presentation Pain out of proportion to injury pain with passive stretch most sensitive tense compartment paresthesia progressing to paralysis pallor and pulselessness. - Diagnosis Clinical diagnosis supported by intracompartmental pressure measurement greater than 30 mmHg or within 30 mmHg of diastolic pressure . - Treatment Emergent fasciotomy. - Risk Factors Fractures especially tibial shaft crush injuries burns constrictive casts/bandages. - Complications Volkmann s contracture ischemic contracture of forearm flexors permanent nerve damage limb loss. - Lecturer s Point Pain with passive movement is the earliest and most sensitive clinical finding. Pulselessness is a late sign. highyield Compartment Syndrome 5 Ps Pain with passive stretch - most sensitive Pressure Paresthesia Paralysis Pallor/Pulselessness late . Treatment is emergent fasciotomy . --- Bursitis Slide 11 A bursa is a lubricated fluid-filled sac located between soft tissues that reduces friction near joints. When bursae become irritated or inflamed the result is bursitis. A key distinguishing feature from tendinopathy is that bursitis pain occurs at rest whereas tendinopathy pain is elicited with movement . Lecturer Emphasis - Acute Bursitis Pain and swelling developing over a few days. If onset is rapid hours consider septic bursitis. - Chronic Bursitis Symptoms for weeks to months; may cause functional limitation from joint contractures. - Causes Chronic overuse infection autoimmune disorders - Common Locations Olecranon shoulder subacromial knee prepatellar Achilles --- Shoulder Anatomy and Dislocations Slides 12-17 The shoulder girdle consists of the clavicle and scapula. The sternoclavicular SC joint is the only true articulation connecting the upper extremity to the axial skeleton. The glenohumeral joint is a ball-and-socket joint with the greatest mobility of all joints in the body but this mobility comes at the cost of stability. The labrum dense fibrous tissue lining the glenoid fossa and the rotator cuff muscles serve as stabilizers. - Rotator Cuff Muscles SITS - Supraspinatus Initiates abduction 0-15 degrees ; innervated by suprascapular nerve C5-C6 - Infraspinatus External rotation; innervated by suprascapular nerve C5-C6 - Teres Minor External rotation with adduction; innervated by axillary nerve C5-C6 - Subscapularis Internal rotation; innervated by subscapular nerves C5-C6 - Shoulder Abduction Range of Motion - 0-15 degrees Supraspinatus - 15-90 degrees Deltoid - Greater than 90 degrees Trapezius and serratus anterior Anterior Shoulder Dislocation Slide 14 The most common type of shoulder dislocation. The mechanism involves a force applied when the arm is in abduction external rotation and extension e.g. a blow to the medial elbow in this position . The humeral head dislocates anteriorly traumatizing the joint capsule and labrum. - Associated Lesions - Bankart Lesion Anterior-inferior capsular injury with tear of the glenoid labrum - Hill-Sachs Lesion Compression fracture of the posterolateral humeral head - Both lesions increase the likelihood of recurrent dislocations - Presentation Arm held in slight abduction forearm supported by opposite hand unable to internally rotate dimple under the acromion - Treatment Reduce as quickly as possible immobilize 2-6 weeks then rehab focused on rotator cuff strengthening correlate Anterior Shoulder Dislocation with Bankart and Hill-Sachs Lesions - Pathophysiology Force applied during abduction external rotation and extension drives the humeral head anteriorly tearing the anterior-inferior labrum Bankart and creating a compression fracture on the posterolateral humeral head Hill-Sachs as it impacts the glenoid rim. - Presentation Patient holds arm in slight abduction unable to internally rotate. Dimple visible under the acromion. - Diagnosis AP and lateral X-ray; MRI for labral tear confirmation. - Treatment Emergent reduction immobilization for 2-6 weeks physical therapy focusing on rotator cuff strengthening. Surgery if recurrent. - Complications Recurrent instability especially in young patients axillary nerve injury rotator cuff tears. - Lecturer s Point Both Bankart and Hill-Sachs lesions increase the likelihood of recurrent dislocations. Posterior Shoulder Dislocation Slide 15 Rare and commonly missed on imaging. Associated with seizures electrocution or long-term shoulder instability. - Light Bulb Sign On AP X-ray the internally rotated humerus appears like a light bulb due to widening of the glenohumeral joint space. - Presentation Arm held with hand on the belly; arm cannot be externally rotated. --- Labral Tears Bankart and SLAP Slides 16-17 Bankart Tear A tear of the anterior-inferior labrum typically occurring with anterior dislocations. Symptoms include popping catching grinding and locking of the shoulder. SLAP Tear Superior Labrum Anterior to Posterior A tear of the superior labrum running from front to back. The superior glenoid serves as the attachment point for the long head of the biceps. - Mechanism Acute injury or chronic degradation from repetitive overhead motion throwers swimmers - Presentation Popping catching grinding locking; limited bicep contraction due to long head of biceps attachment on superior labrum - Treatment Unclear ideal treatment; start conservatively --- AC Joint Dislocations and Clavicular Fractures Slides 18-19 AC Joint Dislocation The AC joint is the only articulation between the clavicle and scapula. Injury typically occurs from direct trauma to the shoulder with the arm adducted e.g. a fall onto the point of the shoulder . - Presentation Pain loss of scapular function tenderness over the AC joint palpable step-off deformity - Management Conservative with ice immobilization sling for a few days to six weeks depending on severity Clavicular Fracture Slide 19 The most common fracture in children and a common birth trauma injury. Typically caused by a fall on an outstretched hand FOOSH or direct shoulder trauma. - Most common site Middle third midshaft - Presentation Shoulder drop - the SCM muscle elevates the medial fragment but the lateral fragment drops due to the weight of the arm - Treatment Sling and non-surgical management --- Rotator Cuff Injuries and Tears Slides 20-22 The supraspinatus is the most commonly injured rotator cuff muscle. All four rotator cuff muscles are innervated by C5-C6. The supraspinatus infraspinatus and teres minor attach to the greater tubercle of the humerus while the subscapularis attaches to the lesser tubercle. - Mechanism Acute trauma younger patients or chronic overuse/degradation older patients - Presentation - Anterolateral shoulder pain - Nighttime pain cannot sleep on affected side Lecturer Emphasis - Atrophy of supraspinatus/infraspinatus muscles - Weakness in specific ROM depending on which muscle is torn - Special Tests - Empty Can Jobe Test Arms elevated to shoulder level thumbs pointing down emptying a can . Weakness/pain with downward pressure = supraspinatus tear. - Drop Arm Test Patient fully abducts then slowly lowers the arm. Sudden drop = supraspinatus tear suggests full-thickness tear . - Treatment Conservative management for partial tears; arthroscopic surgery for severe tears in younger patients; conservative approach for elderly correlate Rotator Cuff Tear - Pathophysiology Disruption of the rotator cuff tendon s most commonly the supraspinatus from acute trauma or chronic degenerative overuse. - Presentation Anterolateral shoulder pain worse with overhead activity and at night unable to sleep on affected side . Weakness in abduction initiation supraspinatus external rotation infraspinatus or internal rotation subscapularis . - Diagnosis MRI is the gold standard. Empty Can test and Drop Arm test on physical exam. - Treatment Conservative PT NSAIDs corticosteroid injections for partial tears. Arthroscopic repair for full-thickness tears especially in younger active patients. - Risk Factors Age greater than 40 repetitive overhead activity prior shoulder injury. - Lecturer s Point The supraspinatus is the most commonly injured rotator cuff muscle. Nighttime pain and inability to sleep on the affected side is a classic presentation. --- Shoulder Impingement Slides 23-25 One of the most common causes of shoulder pain. It encompasses pain caused by rotator cuff tendinitis subacromial bursitis and biceps tendinitis. The mechanism involves anatomic narrowing of the subacromial space irritating the tendons within. - Clinical Progression Edema then fibrosis then tendinitis - History Repetitive overhead activity - Special Tests - Hawkins Test Flex the humerus and elbow to 90 degrees then maximally internally rotate the shoulder with overpressure. Pain in the superior shoulder = positive. - Neer Test Stabilize the scapula with downward force then fully flex the humerus overhead with overpressure. Pain in the superior shoulder = positive. - Treatment Mild disease RICE. Chronic pain Cortisone injections. Severe Surgical acromioplasty removal of part of the acromion/bursa . --- Adhesive Capsulitis Frozen Shoulder Slide 26 Fibrosis inflammation and capsular contracture of the shoulder capsule causing pathologic loss of both active and passive range of motion. - Types Primary idiopathic and secondary due to underlying conditions - Demographics Middle-aged patients non-dominant arm - Associations Diabetes Lecturer Emphasis - Presentation Pain on palpation of the anterior and posterior capsule; loss of both active and passive ROM - Treatment Conservative - physical therapy and oral medications correlate Adhesive Capsulitis Frozen Shoulder - Pathophysiology Fibrosis and contracture of the glenohumeral joint capsule leading to progressive loss of both active and passive range of motion. - Presentation Insidious onset of shoulder pain and stiffness. Loss of both active and passive ROM distinguishes it from rotator cuff pathology where passive ROM is preserved . - Diagnosis Clinical diagnosis. Loss of external rotation is typically the earliest and most affected movement. - Treatment Physical therapy NSAIDs corticosteroid injections. Most cases self-resolve over 1-3 years. - Risk Factors Diabetes mellitus thyroid disease prolonged immobilization. - Lecturer s Point Affects middle-aged patients in the non-dominant arm and is commonly associated with diabetes. --- Humeral Fractures and Nerve Injuries Slides 27-31 A critical concept in this lecture is that each level of humeral fracture has a predictable nerve and artery at risk. The mnemonic ARM maps the first letter of each nerve Axillary Radial Median to the fracture location from proximal to distal. | Fracture Site | Nerve at Risk | Artery at Risk | Key Presentation | |---|---|---|---| | Surgical Neck | Axillary nerve | Posterior circumflex humeral artery | Flattened deltoid loss of abduction greater than 15 degrees loss of sensation over lateral deltoid | | Mid-shaft | Radial nerve | Deep brachial artery | Wrist drop loss of elbow/wrist/finger extension loss of sensation over posterior arm/forearm/dorsal hand | | Supracondylar | Median nerve | Brachial artery | Loss of wrist flexion loss of lateral 2 lumbricals opponens pollicis and abductor pollicis brevis function | | Medial Epicondyle | Ulnar nerve | -- | Paresthesia in digits 4-5 decreased grip from loss of 4th/5th digit flexion | mnemonic Humeral Fracture Nerve Injuries ARM - A Axillary nerve Surgical neck fracture - R Radial nerve Mid-shaft fracture - M Median nerve Supracondylar fracture highyield Mid-shaft humeral fracture damages the radial nerve causing wrist drop loss of wrist/finger extension and lost sensation over the posterior arm forearm and dorsal hand. This presentation is similar to Saturday Night Palsy compression of the radial nerve from draping the arm over a hard surface . --- Volkmann s Contracture Slide 32 A devastating complication of supracondylar humeral fracture. Swelling from the fracture can cause compartment syndrome in the forearm compressing the brachial artery and median nerve. This leads to irreversible ischemia of the forearm flexor muscles which shorten and scar over time. - Presentation Fingers and wrists held in permanent flexion; passive extension is nearly impossible. correlate Volkmann s Contracture - Pathophysiology Irreversible ischemic contracture of the forearm flexor compartment due to compartment syndrome most commonly following a supracondylar humeral fracture. The brachial artery is compressed causing ischemia. The median nerve may also be damaged. - Presentation Fingers and wrist held in flexion with passive extension nearly impossible. The forearm is firm and tender. - Treatment Prevention via early fasciotomy for compartment syndrome. Established contracture may require tendon lengthening or release surgery. - Lecturer s Point This is the link between a supracondylar fracture and compartment syndrome - the swelling from the fracture compresses the brachial artery leading to ischemia of the forearm flexors. --- Elbow Pathology Slides 33-39 Elbow Fractures and the Fat Pad Sign Slide 33 The posterior fat pad is normally pressed into the olecranon fossa by the triceps tendon making it invisible on lateral X-ray. When an elbow fracture occurs the resulting joint effusion pushes the fat pad out of the fossa making it visible the sail sign . Lecturer Emphasis - This may be the only visible marker of a fracture especially in pediatric patients Elbow Dislocations Slide 34 - Anterior dislocation Anterior-to-posterior force - Posterior dislocation Fall on an outstretched hand FOOSH - more common - Simple no fracture vs. Complex with associated fractures - Can have associated ligament tears fractures and ulnar nerve injury Olecranon Bursitis Slide 35 Can look similar to an elbow dislocation on inspection but mechanism is key. It is a chronic slowly developing condition from repeated excessive pressure on the elbow. Common in students athletes mechanics and plumbers. - Treatment RICE avoid aggravating activity aspiration if needed corticosteroid injections - If septic bursitis is suspected draw cultures when draining Nursemaid s Elbow Radial Head Subluxation Slide 36 Occurs when a child is pulled rapidly by the hand or wrist displacing the radial head from the immature annular ligament. - Presentation Child holds arm in flexion pronation and adduction - Treatment Reduction with marked supination and flexion audible pop immediate improvement in ROM UCL Tear Tommy John Injury Slide 37 The ulnar collateral ligament attaches the humerus to the ulna. Injury occurs from valgus force at 50-90 degrees of flexion typically during the acceleration phase of throwing pitchers quarterbacks . --- Forearm and Wrist Slides 38-44 Epicondylitis Slides 38-39 | Condition | Location | Mechanism | Nerve | Physical Exam | |---|---|---|---|---| | Lateral Epicondylitis Tennis Elbow | Lateral epicondyle | Overuse of wrist/finger extensors | Radial nerve | Pain with palpation of lateral epicondyle; pain with resisted active wrist extension | | Medial Epicondylitis Golfer s Elbow | Medial epicondyle | Overuse of wrist flexors/forearm pronators | Median nerve | Pain with palpation of medial epicondyle; pain with resisted active wrist flexion | mnemonic Epicondylitis Mnemonic MeGaLaTe - Me = Me dial epicondylitis = Golfer s elbow flexors median nerve - LaTe = La teral epicondylitis = Te nnis elbow extensors radial nerve Distal Radius Fracture Slide 40 One of the most common injuries in orthopedics. Caused by a fall on an outstretched hand FOOSH . Higher risk with osteoporosis in the elderly. Cubital Tunnel Syndrome Slide 41 Entrapment of the ulnar nerve at the elbow within the cubital tunnel 2-3 inch tunnel near the medial epicondyle . The ulnar nerve is compressed by rigid connective tissue. - Presentation Electrical shooting sensation down the ulnar distribution digits 4-5 with tapping over cubital tunnel Tinel s sign at the elbow ; paresthesia in digits 4-5; weakness of intrinsic hand muscles; inability to flex digits 4-5 weak flexor digitorum profundus Scaphoid Fracture Slide 42 The most commonly fractured carpal bone. Caused by a FOOSH. The scaphoid has a retrograde blood supply from a branch of the radial artery blood enters distally and flows proximally . This means a more proximal fracture has a higher risk of avascular necrosis and nonunion. - Presentation Pain and limited wrist ROM; tenderness in the anatomic snuffbox very sensitive test - Key Point X-rays may have low sensitivity for scaphoid fractures. If snuffbox tenderness is present with a negative X-ray further imaging CT/MRI is warranted. Lecturer Emphasis - Treatment Distal fractures low AVN risk Spica cast for up to 20 weeks. Proximal fractures high AVN risk or athletes Consider surgical fixation. correlate Scaphoid Fracture with Avascular Necrosis Risk - Pathophysiology The scaphoid receives its blood supply in a retrograde fashion distal-to-proximal from a branch of the radial artery. Proximal fractures disrupt this supply leading to avascular necrosis. - Presentation Wrist pain after FOOSH with tenderness in the anatomic snuffbox. X-ray may be initially negative. - Diagnosis Clinical suspicion based on snuffbox tenderness. If X-ray is negative obtain MRI or repeat imaging in 10-14 days. - Treatment Thumb spica cast for up to 20 weeks for distal fractures. Surgical fixation for proximal fractures or displaced fractures. - Complications Avascular necrosis nonunion more common in proximal pole fractures . - Lecturer s Point Scaphoid fractures are often missed on your initial X-ray - a negative X-ray with snuffbox tenderness should prompt further imaging. --- Hand Pathology Slides 45-52 Metacarpal Fracture Boxer s Fracture Slide 45 The most common hand injury. Caused by a direct blow to the hand punching . Typically involves the 4th and 5th metacarpals. Deformity at the metacarpal base shortening of the fractured metacarpal but typically no motor deficits. Fight Bite Slide 46 A clenched fist striking a tooth can rupture the MCP joint capsule inoculating it with oral flora. Must be presumed to be a joint-penetrating wound until proven otherwise. - Common pathogens Streptococcus viridans Group A Strep Staphylococcus aureus Eikenella corrodens - Treatment Prophylactic antibiotics; wound exploration Ganglion Cyst Bible Cyst Slide 47 A fluid-filled swelling overlying a joint or tendon sheath most commonly on the dorsal wrist. The key differentiating feature is that it transilluminates light shines through it distinguishing it from solid tumors. - Usually painless; spontaneous resolution is common - Treatment Observation. Aspiration or surgery if symptomatic. Trigger Finger Stenosing Tenosynovitis Slide 48 The finger catches and locks in flexion due to mechanical impingement of the flexor tendon at the A1 pulley of the hand. The tendon gets trapped in flexion and may remain locked. - Risk Factor Diabetes Lecturer Emphasis Mallet Finger Slide 49 Prolonged flexion of the DIP joint due to disruption of the terminal extensor tendon distal to the DIP. Caused by a blow to the distal tip of the digit e.g. catching a ball or an avulsion fracture at the DIP. - Treatment Splinting the DIP in extension Carpal Tunnel Syndrome Slides 50-51 Entrapment of the median nerve in the carpal tunnel between the transverse carpal ligament and the carpal bones . Can also result from a lunate dislocation. - Presentation - Paresthesia pain numbness in the median nerve distribution digits 1-3 and lateral half of digit 4 - Thenar eminence atrophy with spared sensation - the palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve enters the hand external to the carpal tunnel preserving thenar sensation Lecturer Emphasis - Worse at night - Special Tests - Tinel s Sign Percussion over the carpal tunnel causes tingling - Phalen s Maneuver Bilateral wrist flexion causes tingling - Associations Pregnancy rheumatoid arthritis hypothyroidism diabetes acromegaly amyloidosis repetitive wrist use correlate Carpal Tunnel Syndrome - Pathophysiology Compression of the median nerve within the carpal tunnel due to increased pressure edema tenosynovitis anatomic variants . The palmar cutaneous branch travels superficial to the transverse carpal ligament sparing thenar sensation. - Presentation Numbness and tingling in digits 1-3 and lateral 4. Thenar atrophy but spared thenar sensation. Worse at night. Positive Tinel s and Phalen s tests. - Diagnosis Clinical diagnosis confirmed by nerve conduction studies/electromyography. - Treatment Wrist splinting especially at night NSAIDs corticosteroid injection. Surgical carpal tunnel release for refractory cases. - Risk Factors Pregnancy hypothyroidism diabetes rheumatoid arthritis acromegaly amyloidosis repetitive wrist flexion. - Lecturer s Point Thenar atrophy with spared sensation is the hallmark - the palmar cutaneous branch bypasses the carpal tunnel. Guyon s Canal Syndrome Slide 52 Compression of the ulnar nerve at the wrist in Guyon s canal. Can be caused by a hook of hamate fracture. Classically seen in cyclists due to pressure at the wrist. - Presentation Weakness of intrinsic hand muscles decreased grip clawing of digits 4-5 hypothenar atrophy - Key Distinction from Cubital Tunnel In Guyon s canal syndrome extrinsic forearm muscles flexor carpi ulnaris flexor digitorum profundus are normal because the compression is distal to their innervation. In cubital tunnel syndrome these muscles are weak because the compression is proximal. De Quervain s Tenosynovitis Slide 53 Thickening of the abductor pollicis longus APL and extensor pollicis brevis EPB tendons due to excessive friction against the extensor retinaculum. - Commonly seen in New parents holding a child with thumb extended racket sports golfers - Presentation Pain at the radial styloid swelling - Special Test Finkelstein s Test - make a fist around the thumb then ulnar deviate the wrist. Pain = positive. - Treatment Splinting Septic Flexor Tenosynovitis Slide 54 Infection of the synovial sheath surrounding the flexor tendon typically from penetrating trauma. Diagnosed by the Kanavel Signs 4 cardinal signs 1. Flexed posturing of the digit 2. Tenderness to palpation over the tendon sheath 3. Marked pain with passive extension of the digit 4. Fusiform sausage-like swelling of the digit - Treatment IV antibiotics and surgical irrigation/debridement correlate Septic Flexor Tenosynovitis - Pathophysiology Bacterial infection of the flexor tendon sheath usually from a penetrating wound. The closed-space infection can spread rapidly along the tendon sheath. - Presentation Kanavel signs flexed posturing pain with passive extension fusiform swelling and tenderness along the tendon sheath. - Diagnosis Clinical Kanavel signs . Aspiration of the tendon sheath for culture if uncertain. - Treatment IV antibiotics and emergent surgical irrigation and debridement to prevent tendon necrosis. - Complications Tendon necrosis loss of digit function spread of infection to adjacent compartments. - Lecturer s Point The four Kanavel signs are the key to recognizing this surgical emergency. BandB Musculoskeletal > Orthopedics > Upper Extremity Pathoma Musculoskeletal Pathology > Bone Tumors and Fractures FirstAid Musculoskeletal Skin and Connective Tissue > Upper Extremity Injuries SketchyPath Musculoskeletal > Bone and Joint Disorders Physeo Anatomy > MSK > Shoulder and Upper Extremity What fracture classification system is used for pediatric growth plate injuries? The Salter-Harris classification Types 1-5 . Remember SALTR Straight across Above Lower Through/Two Rammed/crush. Type 5 has a 100% growth arrest rate. Concept What are the 5 Ps of compartment syndrome? Pain especially with passive stretch - most sensitive Pressure Paresthesia Paralysis Pallor/Pulselessness late findings . Treatment is emergent fasciotomy. Clinical A mid-shaft humeral fracture damages which nerve and what is the classic presentation? The radial nerve . Classic presentation is wrist drop inability to extend the wrist fingers and elbow with loss of sensation over the posterior arm forearm and dorsal hand. Clinical What two lesions are associated with anterior shoulder dislocation? 1 Bankart lesion - tear of the anterior-inferior glenoid labrum. 2 Hill-Sachs lesion - compression fracture of the posterolateral humeral head. Both increase risk of recurrent dislocation. Clinical What is the light bulb sign on X-ray? It indicates a posterior shoulder dislocation . The humerus internally rotates causing the glenohumeral joint to widen giving the humeral head a light bulb appearance on AP X-ray. Clinical What muscle initiates the first 0-15 degrees of shoulder abduction? The supraspinatus . The deltoid handles 15-90 degrees and the trapezius and serratus anterior handle greater than 90 degrees. Concept What mnemonic maps humeral fracture locations to their at-risk nerves? ARM A xillary nerve surgical neck R adial nerve mid-shaft M edian nerve supracondylar . The medial epicondyle fracture injures the ulnar nerve. Concept What is Volkmann s contracture and what causes it? Irreversible ischemic contracture of the forearm flexor muscles. Caused by compartment syndrome usually from a supracondylar humeral fracture compressing the brachial artery. Fingers and wrist are held in permanent flexion. Clinical Why does a proximal scaphoid fracture have a higher risk of avascular necrosis? The scaphoid has a retrograde blood supply from a branch of the radial artery entering distally and flowing proximally . A proximal fracture disrupts blood flow to the proximal pole causing AVN. Concept What is the key physical exam finding to differentiate a ganglion cyst from a solid tumor? A ganglion cyst transilluminates light passes through the fluid-filled cyst while solid tumors do not. Clinical In carpal tunnel syndrome why is thenar sensation spared despite thenar muscle atrophy? The palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve travels superficial to the transverse carpal ligament external to the carpal tunnel so sensation to the thenar region is preserved even though the motor fibers within the tunnel are compressed. Clinical What are the 4 Kanavel signs of septic flexor tenosynovitis? 1 Flexed posturing of the digit 2 Tenderness along the tendon sheath 3 Pain with passive extension 4 Fusiform sausage-like swelling. Treatment is IV antibiotics and surgical irrigation. Clinical What is nursemaid s elbow and how is it treated? Radial head subluxation in a child caused by sudden traction on the hand/wrist. The immature annular ligament slips over the radial head. Treatment reduction with supination and flexion audible pop immediate improvement . Clinical How do you differentiate cubital tunnel syndrome from Guyon s canal syndrome? Both involve the ulnar nerve. In Guyon s canal wrist compression extrinsic forearm muscles FCU FDP are normal . In cubital tunnel elbow compression these forearm muscles are weak because the lesion is proximal to their innervation. Clinical What is the Finkelstein test and what does it diagnose? Make a fist around the thumb then ulnar deviate the wrist. Pain at the radial styloid = positive for de Quervain s tenosynovitis thickening of the APL and EPB tendons . Clinical match Salter-Harris Fracture Classification resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 02 Pathology 17 Common fractures | search salter harris growth plate match Compartment Syndrome 5 Ps Fasciotomy resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 02 Pathology 17 Common fractures | search compartment syndrome fasciotomy match Rotator Cuff Muscles SITS and Injuries resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 01 Anatomy & Physiology 03 Upper Extremity Nerves | search rotator cuff supraspinatus infraspinatus teres minor subscapularis match Upper Extremity Nerve Injuries ARM Mnemonic resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 01 Anatomy & Physiology 03 Upper Extremity Nerves | search axillary nerve radial nerve wrist drop median nerve match Shoulder Dislocation Bankart Hill-Sachs resources AnKing Step 1 > search anterior shoulder dislocation bankart hill sachs match Carpal Tunnel Syndrome Median Nerve Compression resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 01 Anatomy & Physiology 03 Upper Extremity Nerves | search carpal tunnel median nerve tinel phalen match Scaphoid Fracture and Avascular Necrosis resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 02 Pathology 17 Common fractures | search scaphoid avascular necrosis snuffbox match Volkmann s Contracture Supracondylar Fracture Complication resources AnKing Step 1 > search volkmann contracture supracondylar compartment syndrome match Epicondylitis Tennis Elbow Golfer s Elbow resources AnKing Step 1 > search lateral epicondylitis tennis elbow medial golfer match Nursemaid s Elbow Radial Head Subluxation resources AnKing Step 1 > search nursemaid elbow radial head subluxation annular ligament match De Quervain s Tenosynovitis Finkelstein Test resources AnKing Step 1 > search de quervain tenosynovitis finkelstein test match Septic Flexor Tenosynovitis Kanavel Signs resources AnKing Step 1 > search kanavel signs flexor tenosynovitis septic ARM Mnemonic for Humeral Fracture Nerves Surgical neck = Axillary Mid-shaft = Radial Supracondylar = Median. The first letter of each nerve spells ARM. Medial epicondyle = Ulnar nerve. Scaphoid X-ray Pitfall Scaphoid fractures are frequently missed on initial X-ray. If the patient has anatomic snuffbox tenderness after a FOOSH treat as a fracture thumb spica cast and obtain follow-up imaging CT or MRI even if the initial X-ray is negative. Thenar Sensation in Carpal Tunnel Carpal tunnel syndrome causes thenar muscle atrophy but spared thenar sensation because the palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve travels superficial to the transverse carpal ligament. Bursitis vs. Tendinopathy Bursitis causes pain at rest while tendinopathy causes pain with movement . This distinction helps differentiate two conditions that otherwise present very similarly. Posterior Dislocation Clue A posterior shoulder dislocation is rare and commonly missed. Look for the light bulb sign on AP X-ray and an arm held in internal rotation hand on belly cannot externally rotate . Associate with seizures and electrocution. Guyon s vs. Cubital Tunnel Both involve the ulnar nerve but location matters . Guyon s canal wrist spares the forearm muscles FCU FDP remain normal . Cubital tunnel elbow affects them because the lesion is proximal. Greenstick vs. Torus Fracture Greenstick fractures show a fracture line on the tension side. Torus fractures show buckling on the compression side. Both occur in pediatric patients due to bone plasticity. Adhesive Capsulitis and Diabetes Frozen shoulder adhesive capsulitis is strongly associated with diabetes mellitus . It presents with loss of both active AND passive ROM distinguishing it from rotator cuff pathology where passive ROM is typically preserved. questions 1 A 7-year-old boy falls off a jungle gym onto his outstretched left arm. X-ray reveals a fracture line that extends through the growth plate and into the metaphysis but the epiphysis is intact. According to the Salter-Harris classification what type of fracture does this patient most likely have? Type 1 Type 2 Type 3 Type 4 Type 5 1 A Salter-Harris Type 2 fracture runs through the growth plate AND the metaphysis \\ Above\\ . Type 1 is along the growth plate only Type 3 involves the epiphysis Type 4 involves all three and Type 5 is a compression injury. 2 A 30-year-old male is brought to the ED after a motorcycle accident. He has severe pain in his left leg that worsens with passive toe extension. The anterior compartment is tense and firm. He has paresthesia over the dorsum of his foot. What is the most appropriate next step in management? Apply ice and elevate the limb Administer IV morphine for pain control Obtain an emergent MRI of the leg Perform an emergent fasciotomy Apply a compression bandage 3 This presentation pain with passive stretch tense compartment paresthesia is classic for acute compartment syndrome which is a surgical emergency. The definitive treatment is emergent fasciotomy. Compression or elevation may worsen the condition. 3 A 40-year-old man falls from a ladder onto his outstretched arm. X-ray shows a fracture of the humeral mid-shaft. On exam he is unable to extend his wrist and has decreased sensation over the posterior forearm and dorsal hand. Which nerve is most likely injured? Axillary nerve Musculocutaneous nerve Radial nerve Median nerve Ulnar nerve 2 Mid-shaft humeral fractures classically damage the radial nerve which runs in the spiral radial groove. This causes wrist drop inability to extend the wrist and loss of sensation over the posterior arm/forearm and dorsal hand. The ARM mnemonic Axillary surgical neck Radial mid-shaft Median supracondylar . 4 A 48-year-old accountant presents with numbness and tingling in her first three fingers that is worst at night. On exam she has thenar atrophy but intact sensation over the thenar eminence. Which anatomic structure explains the preserved thenar sensation? The ulnar nerve s dorsal cutaneous branch The palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve The superficial branch of the radial nerve The anterior interosseous nerve The recurrent branch of the median nerve 1 In carpal tunnel syndrome the median nerve is compressed within the carpal tunnel causing thenar atrophy. However the palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve travels superficial to the transverse carpal ligament outside the tunnel so thenar sensation is spared. 5 A 22-year-old college student presents with wrist pain after falling onto her outstretched hand during a volleyball game. X-ray of the wrist is unremarkable. However she has significant tenderness in the anatomic snuffbox. What is the most appropriate next step? Discharge with NSAIDs and reassurance Immobilize in a thumb spica cast and obtain follow-up imaging Perform an emergent MRI of the cervical spine Inject corticosteroids into the wrist joint Refer for immediate surgical fixation 1 Anatomic snuffbox tenderness after a FOOSH is highly suspicious for a scaphoid fracture which is frequently missed on initial X-ray. The standard approach is to immobilize in a thumb spica cast and obtain follow-up imaging CT or repeat X-ray in 10-14 days . The retrograde blood supply of the scaphoid makes AVN a significant concern if the fracture is not properly managed."}, {"id": "l111", "title": "Lecture #111: Orthopedics: Lower Extremity", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l111 module Neuro l111 Lecture 111 Orthopedics Lower Extremity lecturer M. Heller D.O. Orthopedics pdf content/L111 HighYield Render.pdf Lecture 111 Orthopedics Lower Extremity Lecturer M. Heller D.O. | Session Lecture 111 1. Introduction Shattering the Four-Minute Mile Barrier Dr. Heller a sports medicine specialist and NYITCOM alumnus begins this deep dive into lower extremity orthopedics with a historical parallel Roger Bannister . In 1954 Bannister—a medical student—ran the first sub-four-minute mile a milestone once deemed physiologically impossible. Once he shattered that psychological barrier the impossible became routine for top athletes worldwide. For medical students lower extremity orthopedics often represents a similar barrier—a dense complex region of anatomy and trauma. The lesson Persistence and diligence transform the daunting into the attainable. The lower extremity is built for weight-bearing and locomotion requiring a masterclass in stability and mechanical alignment. This session moves from the proximal hip down to the distal ankle covering trauma degeneration and the silent emergencies of the vascular system. --- 2. The Hip Anatomy Stability and Blood Supply The Ball-and-Socket Architecture The hip is a true ball-and-socket joint. Unlike the shallow glenohumeral joint of the shoulder the hip is inherently stable with the femoral head the ball nesting deep within the acetabulum the socket . - Acetabulum & Labrum The acetabulum is part of the pelvic bone. Its surface is lined with articular cartilage for low-friction movement. The labrum is a specialized fibrocartilage rim that deepens the socket providing a secondary layer of stability through intra-articular pressure effectively suctioning the femoral head into place . - Surgical Clock Orthopedists often describe the hip anatomy using a clock system where 12 o clock is superior and 3 o clock is always anterior . correlate The Medial Circumflex Femoral Artery - Anatomy The blood supply to the femoral head is precarious. It is primarily provided by the Medial Circumflex Femoral Artery which gives rise to the retinacular arteries . - The Ring of Fire These retinacular arteries ring the bell of the femoral neck. In intracapsular fractures or dislocations this ring is frequently severed. - Critical Risk Disruption of this supply leads to Avascular Necrosis AVN —the death of bone cells. Because the femoral head is a weight-bearing surface AVN results in collapse severe pain and eventual joint destruction. --- 3. Sports Medicine of the Hip Femoroacetabular Impingement FAI FAI is a mechanical mismatch causing groin pain in young athletes. It occurs when the ball and socket don t fit perfectly leading to friction and labral tears. - Cam Type A bony bump on the femoral head-neck junction. During flexion this bump grinds into the acetabular rim. - Pincer Type The acetabulum has an overhanging edge that pinches the femoral neck and labrum. - Combined Most patients have elements of both. correlate FAI and the FADIR Test - Physical Exam The hallmark for FAI is a positive FADIR Test Flexion ADduction Internal Rotation . This maneuver mechanical stresses the impingement site reproducing the patient s groin pain. - Long-term Outcome Untreated FAI is a leading cause of early-onset osteoarthritis in the hip. Hamstring and Hip Flexor Injuries - Hamstring Avulsion Often occurs at the Ischial Tuberosity the origin . It is a musculotendinous junction injury where the tendon may pull a small fragment of bone with it avulsion . Runners and athletes experiencing sudden forceful deceleration are at risk. Watch for numbness in the sciatic nerve distribution if the hematoma is large. - Hip Pointer An exquisitely painful contusion of the Iliac Crest . Because the bone is superficial direct contact e.g. a football tackle causes significant bruising and swelling of the attached muscles TFL sartorius . --- 4. High-Energy Trauma Hip Dislocations Posterior Dislocation 90% of cases This is the classic Dashboard Injury. In a motor vehicle accident MVA the knee strikes the dashboard driving the femoral head backward out of the acetabulum and into the iliac fossa. highyield Posterior Hip Dislocation Physical Exam The leg is classically 1. Shortened 2. Internally Rotated 3. Adducted and Flexed Contrast with a Hip Fracture which classically presents with External Rotation . - Case Studies - Bo Jackson A legendary multi-sport athlete whose career was cut short not by a full dislocation but by a subluxation . The hip popped out and immediately back in but this minor movement severs the blood supply leading to the AVN that ended his football career. - Pro-Wrestling Example Dr. Heller recounts a wrestler whose back was slammed into another s hip causing a true dislocation. In these cases the hip is locked and cannot be walked off. - Tua Tagovailoa The Dolphins QB suffered a high-energy hip dislocation early in his collegiate career requiring urgent reduction to save the joint. - Nerve Risk The Sciatic Nerve runs directly behind the hip and is the most commonly injured nerve in posterior dislocations. Anterior Dislocation Rare Occurs via a forced external rotation mechanism such as a skier whose skis suddenly splay apart while moving downhill. - Physical Exam The leg is Externally Rotated flexed and abducted. - Neurovascular Risk The Femoral Nerve and Artery are at direct risk in anterior injuries. --- 5. Femur Fractures and Management Classification by Location - Femoral Neck Intracapsular Extremely high risk for AVN due to the ring of fire retinacular artery disruption. - Intertrochanteric Extracapsular Occurs outside the capsule. Lower AVN risk but higher risk of surgical bleeding. - Femoral Shaft Occurs in the mid-thigh. correlate Femoral Shaft Fracture Complications - Blood Loss The thigh can hold a massive amount of blood. A closed femur fracture can lead to 1-2 liters of occult blood loss potentially causing hypovolemic shock. - Management The gold standard is Intramedullary IM Nailing where a titanium rod is placed through the center of the bone. This allows for early weight-bearing and preserves the surrounding soft tissue. --- 6. Osteoarthritis OA The Wear-and-Tear Grind OA is the progressive loss of articular cartilage height. Without this cushion bone grinds on bone. - Clinical Course OA pain is mechanical —it worsens at the end of the day and after weight-bearing activity. - Radiographic Findings Look for joint space narrowing osteophytes bone spurs subchondral sclerosis whitening of the bone and cysts. - Treatment Escalation - Conservative PT weight loss NSAIDs. - Injections Corticosteroids fast relief but can damage cartilage vs. Viscosupplementation hyaluronic acid gel shots that act as synthetic lubricant . - Definitive Total Joint Arthroplasty. --- 7. The Knee Cruciates Collaterals and Menisci Ligamentous Directionality Dr. Heller uses a fingers on the knee technique to remember ligament orientation. If you place your right hand on your right knee crossing your middle finger over your index finger - The Middle Finger represents the ACL Traveling Lateral to Medial . - The Index Finger represents the PCL Traveling Medial to Lateral . ACL The Pop and the Pivot - Mechanism Often a non-contact deceleration or pivoting injury. Odell Beckham Jr. OBJ famously tore his ACL on turf—a classic case of the foot planting while the body continues to rotate. - The Female Risk Female athletes have a 2-8x higher risk due to a wider pelvis increased Q-angle and hormonal effects on ligament laxity. - Diagnosis A sudden pop followed by immediate swelling. Lachman s Test is the gold standard most sensitive . mnemonic O Donoghue’s Unhappy Triad MAM The classic catastrophic knee injury caused by a lateral valgus blow 1. M edial Collateral Ligament MCL 2. A CL 3. M edial Meniscus Note In acute ACL tears the Lateral meniscus is actually more commonly injured first but the Medial is the textbook triad component . PCL and the Sag Sign The PCL is twice as strong as the ACL and usually requires a direct anterior blow e.g. knee hitting a dashboard . - Physical Exam The Posterior Sag Sign —the tibia drops backward when the knee is flexed to 90 degrees. Meniscal Tears Occur during twisting on a weight-bearing knee. - Symptoms Mechanical symptoms are key—locking catching or clicking. - Healing Zones The outer 1/3 Red Zone has a blood supply and can be repaired. The inner 2/3 White Zone is avascular and typically requires debridement meniscectomy because it cannot heal on its own. --- 8. Patellar and Tibial Trauma Patellar Dislocation The patella naturally wants to dislocate laterally . - Mechanism Usually a twist on an extended knee. - The Mahomes Case Patrick Mahomes famously dislocated his patella on national TV. The reduction is often simple straighten the leg and the patella glides back into place. - Medial Patellofemoral Ligament MPFL This is the primary stabilizer that prevents the patella from sliding lateral. It is almost always torn in a dislocation. Open Tibia Fracture The Kevin Ware Injury Dr. Heller cites the horrific injury of Kevin Ware whose tibia bone was seen protruding through the skin on live TV. - High Alert Open fractures are surgical emergencies . The priority is preventing osteomyelitis bone infection through immediate irrigation debridement and stabilization. --- 9. Leg and Ankle Complications and Sprains Deep Vein Thrombosis DVT A silent but high-yield orthopedic complication. mnemonic Virchow’s Triad SHE - S tasis Bed rest long-haul flights >8-9 hours post-op immobilization. - H ypercoagulability Malignancy OCPs pregnancy or genetic factors. - E ndothelial injury Surgery or the initial trauma itself. - Diagnosis Venous Doppler Ultrasound. The key finding is non-compressibility of the vein. If the vein doesn t collapse under probe pressure a clot is present. - Risk DVT can embolize to the lungs Pulmonary Embolism which is potentially fatal. Ankle Sprains The ATFL is King - Inversion Injury Low Sprain Most common. The Anterior Talofibular Ligament ATFL is always the first to go Mnemonic Always Torn First Ligament . - Eversion Injury Result in a Deltoid Ligament tear. Recovery takes twice as long. - High Ankle Sprain Injury to the Syndesmosis AITFL . - Physical Exam The Squeeze Test compressing the tibia and fibula together and the External Rotation Test reproduce syndesmosis pain. highyield Ottawa Ankle Rules Guidelines to avoid unnecessary X-rays - Get an Ankle X-ray ONLY IF Bone tenderness at the posterior edge/tip of the malleoli OR inability to walk 4 steps. - Get a Foot X-ray ONLY IF Bone tenderness at the Base of the 5th Metatarsal or the Navicular bone. --- 10. Compartment Syndrome The Surgical Emergency To conclude Dr. Heller presents a case of a patient post-reperfusion who develops intense burning pain. - Pathophysiology Massive swelling within the rigid fascia increases pressure cutting off capillary flow. - Treatment Immediate emergency fasciotomy . mnemonic The 6 P s of Compartment Syndrome 1. P ain out of proportion to injury or pain with passive stretch— the earliest sign 2. P aresthesia numbness 3. P allor pale skin 4. P aralysis 5. P ulselessness a very late and ominous sign 6. P oikilothermia Coldness What leg position is classic for a posterior hip dislocation? Shortened flexed internally rotated and adducted. Which specific ligament is most commonly injured in a low inversion ankle sprain? Anterior Talofibular Ligament ATFL . What is Virchow s Triad associated with DVT risk? Stasis Endothelial damage and Hypercoagulability. What is the most sensitive physical exam test for an ACL rupture? Lachman s Test. Which nerves are at risk in lateral compartment syndrome of the leg? Superficial Fibular Nerve. What artery is most crucial for femoral head survival in a neck fracture? Medial Circumflex Femoral Artery. What does a positive Thompson Test indicate? Achilles Tendon Rupture lack of plantarflexion on calf squeeze . Which landmark is palpated for the Ottawa Foot Rules? Base of the 5th Metatarsal and the Navicular bone. Boards & Beyond Musculoskeletal > Anatomy > Lower Extremity match Lower extremity fractures hip joint anatomy knee ligamentous injuries source First Aid 2024 page 452-458 source Pathoma 18.3 - Bone Fractures & Osteoarthritis source Sketchy Pathology Lower Extremity Orthopedics Overview source AnKing V12 tags AK Step1 v12 MSK LowerExtremity Fractures questions 1 A 45-year-old male feels a sudden pop in his ankle while playing tennis and feels like he was kicked. He is unable to stand on his toes. Which clinical test would confirm the diagnosis? Lachman s Test Thompson Test McMurray Test FADIR Test Squeeze Test 1 This describes an Achilles Tendon Rupture. The Thompson test squeezing the calf will fail to produce plantarflexion when the tendon is ruptured. 2 A 19-year-old female basketball player lands from a jump and immediately feels a pop followed by rapid swelling. Why is she at higher risk than her male counterparts? Increased pelvic tilt Increased Q-angle Decreased femoral anteversion Increased bone density Decreased ligament laxity 1 Female athletes have a wider pelvis and increased Q-angle creating more valgus stress on the ACL during landing and pivoting. 3 The Dashboard Injury mechanism is most likely to produce which combination of orthopedic injuries? Anterior Hip Dislocation and ACL Tear Femoral Neck Fracture and Meniscal Tear Posterior Hip Dislocation and PCL Tear Patellar Dislocation and MCL Tear Intertrochanteric Fracture and LCL Tear 2 The force striking the knee in a flexed position like hitting a dashboard drives the femur backward relative to the acetabulum Posterior Dislocation and the tibia backward relative to the femur PCL tear . Ottawa Ankle Rules Only X-ray if malleolar tenderness OR inability to take 4 steps. Maisonneuve Fracture A high ankle sprain with a proximal fibula fracture. Always palpate the knee in ankle injuries! Avascular Necrosis AVN A risk in any intracapsular femoral neck fracture due to disruption of the Medial Circumflex artery Ring of Fire . mindmap root Lower Extremity Orthopedics children name Hip children name Anatomy children name Medial Circumflex Art. name Labrum Suction name Dislocation children name Posterior Shortened IR Adducted name Anterior ER Skier name Fractures children name Neck AVN name Shaft Blood loss IM Nail name Knee children name Ligaments children name ACL Pop OBJ Pivot-shift name PCL Sag Sign name Patella children name Mahomes Dislocation Lateral name MPFL Tear name Meniscus children name Catching/Clicking name Red vs White Zone name Ankle / Leg children name DVT children name Virchow s Triad name Doppler Compression name Sprain children name ATFL Always Torn First name Syndesmosis Squeeze Test name Rules children name Ottawa Ankle vs Foot"}, {"id": "l112", "title": "Lecture #112: Orthopedic Concerns of the Spine", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l112 module Neuro l112 Lecture 112 Orthopedic Concerns of the Spine lecturer M. Heller D.O. Orthopedics pdf content/L112 HighYield Render.pdf Lecture 112 Orthopedic Concerns of the Spine Lecturer M. Heller D.O. | Session Lecture 112 Orthopedic concerns of the spine represent one of the most ubiquitous challenges in clinical medicine. Regardless of your future specialty—whether it be pediatrics geriatrics or obstetrics—you will inevitably encounter patients presenting with neck or back pain. This lecture provides a comprehensive framework for the evaluation and management of spinal pathology beginning with a systematic approach to history and physical examination and culminating in the identification of surgical emergencies. The central theme of the lecture is the differentiation between mechanical degenerative and high-risk inflammatory or traumatic conditions. --- The Clinical Framework History and Physical Examination Evaluating spinal pain begins with a granular history. Orthopedic pain is rarely just pain ; its character timing and associated symptoms provide the roadmap to diagnosis. - Pain Characterization OPQRST - Localized Pain Often suggests a focal process such as a tumor or localized infection. - Mechanical Pain Typically worsens with activity and improves with rest; common in fractures or discogenic disease. - Radicular Pain Describes pain that travels along a dermatome often sharp or electric suggesting nerve root impingement e.g. herniated disc or stenosis . - Night Pain A significant red flag. Pain that wakes a patient from sleep or prevents them from lying comfortably can indicate metastatic malignancy. - Red Flags and Systemic Review Always screen for fever and weight loss infection/malignancy neurologic deficits weakness/numbness and trauma history. Understanding the patient s occupation FED TACOS and recreational activities is vital as repetitive stress often drives spinal pathology e.g. gymnasts and spondylolysis . - Physical Exam Techniques - Inspection and Gait Observe the patient walking. Look for compensatory movements or shopping cart signs. - Palpation Check for step-offs spondylolisthesis or midline tenderness compression fractures . - Range of Motion Assess for limitations in flexion extension and rotation. - Specialized Referral If a patient presents with a cervical complaint you MUST examine the shoulders. Conversely if the complaint is lumbar you MUST examine the hips and lower extremities to rule out referred pain. highyield Night pain is a major red flag for tumor/metastatic disease . Mechanical pain that improves with rest suggests vertebral or discogenic issues. If a patient has neck pain always check the shoulders ; back pain requires a hip/lower extremity exam. --- Diagnostic Imaging and Laboratory Evaluation - Plain Radiographs X-ray Usually the first-line imaging study. It is excellent for identifying acute fractures and assessing alignment. - CT Scan Provides superior detail for bony anatomy. Often used for pre-operative planning or post-operative hardware evaluation. - MRI The Gold Standard MRI is the imaging of choice for cervical thoracic and lumbar disease. It provides unparalleled soft-tissue resolution for identifying herniated discs spinal cord lesions like syringomyelia tumors and infections. - Bone Scan Skeletal Scintigraphy A nuclear medicine test using a radioactive tracer to find areas of high metabolic activity. It is invaluable for detecting occult stress fractures metastatic disease or early infection. highyield MRI is the imaging of choice for spinal soft tissue including discs cord lesions and impingement. Bone scans are used to detect high metabolic activity like metastases or infections . --- Cervical Spinal Disorders The cervical spine presents unique anatomical challenges. One key distinction emphasized by Dr. Heller is that cervical nerve roots exit horizontally whereas lumbar roots drop and exit at a lower level. Cervical Sprain and Strain Whiplash Often resulting from acceleration-deceleration injuries whiplash involves soft tissue damage where muscles respond by contracting. - Findings Non-focal non-radiating neck pain trapezius spasm decreased range of motion and frequent association with occipital headaches. - X-ray May show loss of normal cervical lordosis straightening of the neck due to muscle spasm. Cervical Spondylosis This is a chronic age-related wear-and-tear process involving disc degeneration and facet arthropathy. As the disc loses water content it becomes stiffer leading to discogenic pain. - Progression It starts as simple neck pain can progress to Radiculopathy unilateral arm pain/numbness exacerbated by Spurling’s maneuver and eventually to Myelopathy . - Myelopathy Red Flags Bilateral weakness Upper > Lower loss of fine motor skills cannot button a shirt and gait disturbances. This signifies spinal cord compression and requires urgent evaluation. Rheumatoid Cervical Spondylitis This is a critical can t miss condition for board exams. In patients with chronic Rheumatoid Arthritis RA the transverse ligament of the Atlas C1 can become lax or rupture. - Clinical Pearl This leads to C1-C2 subluxation . Dr. Heller warns that aggressive skull manipulation or intubation in these patients can cause fatal spinal cord compression quadriplegia . correlate Rheumatoid Arthritis and C1-C2 Instability - Pathophysiology Chronic inflammation leads to erosion of the transverse ligament of the atlas causing atlantoaxial instability. - Presentation Chronic RA patient now with neck pain or progressive neurologic signs. - Danger High risk of quadriplegia during maneuvers like intubation due to cord compression. - Management Obtain flexion/extension X-rays before any surgical procedure involving neck manipulation. --- Thoracic and Lumbar Disorders The lumbar spine bears the brunt of the body s weight making it a frequent site of mechanical failure. Vertebral Compression Fractures Commonly associated with osteoporosis in elderly patients. They typically present with midline spinal process SP point tenderness usually in the thoracic spine. - Management Treat the underlying osteoporosis with bisphosphonates and manage pain. Lumbar Herniated Nucleus Pulposus HNP Herniation typically occurs posterolaterally because the Posterior Longitudinal Ligament is relatively weak. Dr. Heller identifies L4-L5 and L5-S1 as the most common sites. - Nerve Root Mapping - L4 Root Weakness in knee extension quads decreased patellar reflex. - L5 Root Weakness in great toe extension/dorsiflexion difficulty with heel walking. - S1 Root Weakness in plantarflexion toe walking decreased Achilles reflex. mnemonic AchilleS-1 The Achilles reflex tests the S1 nerve root. Spondylolysis and Spondylolisthesis - Spondylolysis A stress fracture of the pars interarticularis . Classic in young athletes gymnasts football linemen due to repetitive hyperextension. On oblique X-ray look for the Scotty Dog sign a collar on the dog s neck . - Spondylolisthesis The actual slipping of one vertebral body over another usually L4 on L5 . This creates a palpable step-off on physical exam. Ankylosing Spondylitis AS An inflammatory arthritis typically affecting young men in their 20s-30s. It belongs to the seronegative spondyloarthropathies which are characterized by the absence of rheumatoid factor and a strong association with the HLA-B27 gene. - Clinical Profile Persistent morning stiffness that lasts for several hours and crucially improves with activity and exercise. Patients often describe the pain as deep in the gluteal region or lower back. - Extra-articular Findings AS is a systemic disease. It frequently presents with acute anterior uveitis eye pain/redness aortic regurgitation due to aortitis and restricted chest expansion. The latter occurs due to fusion of the costovertebral joints leading to a restrictive lung pattern. - Imaging The hallmark is sacroiliitis often the first sign on X-ray. Progression leads to the development of syndesmophytes—bony bridges between vertebrae—resulting in the classic Bamboo spine appearance. This fusion makes the spine brittle and prone to chalk-stick fractures even with minor trauma. Spinal Stenosis Narrowing of the spinal canal central stenosis or the neural foramina lateral stenosis most commonly due to degenerative changes like facet hypertrophy ligamentum flavum thickening and disc bulging. - The Shopping Cart Sign This is the pathognomonic finding for neurogenic claudication. Patients experience radiating leg pain heaviness or cramping while standing or walking downhill. These symptoms are relieved by leaning forward flexion as this posture increases the cross-sectional area of the spinal canal and reduces compression on the neural elements. - Differential Diagnosis A critical board-style distinction must be made between Neurogenic Claudication Stenosis and Vascular Claudication Peripheral Vascular Disease . - Neurogenic Relieved by sitting or leaning forward; pain can occur with standing alone; pulses are typically normal. - Vascular Relieved simply by stopping activity rest regardless of posture; pain is strictly effort-related; pulses are usually diminished or absent and skin changes may be present. --- Cauda Equina Syndrome The Surgical Emergency The spinal cord ends at the conus medullaris L1 . Below this the canal is filled with the cauda equina—a bundle of peripheral nerve roots L2-S4 . Dr. Heller emphasizes that compression here is a surgical emergency . - Etiology Massive central disc herniation trauma epidural abscess or tumor. - Clinical Presentation - Saddle Anesthesia Numbness in the perineal region. - Bowel/Bladder Dysfunction Urinary retention with overflow incontinence; loss of anal sphincter tone. - Motor Deficits Flaccid paralysis of the lower extremities. - Management Emergent MRI followed by surgical decompression within 48 hours to prevent permanent damage. highyield Cauda Equina Syndrome causes flaccid paralysis peripheral nerve injury while Myelopathy cord injury causes spastic paralysis . Saddle anesthesia and urinary retention make this a surgical emergency. --- Summary and Key Takeaways Spinal pathology requires a keen eye for red flags and a solid grasp of dermatomal anatomy. Start with conservative management NSAIDs PT activity modification for mechanical issues but maintain a high index of suspicion for inflammatory AS RA and emergent Cauda Equina conditions. - Primary Diagnostic Tool MRI. - Emergent Referral Cauda Equina severe motor deficits or suspicion of cord compression. - Board Favorite C1-C2 subluxation in RA patients and HLA-B27/Bamboo spine in AS. BandB Musculoskeletal > Orthopedics > Spine Pathoma Musculoskeletal Pathology > Bone Tumors and Fractures FirstAid Musculoskeletal Skin and Connective Tissue > Spinal Pathology SketchyPath Musculoskeletal > Bone and Joint Disorders Physeo Anatomy > MSK > Spine and Back What is the imaging modality of choice for evaluating herniated discs and spinal cord lesions? MRI is the gold standard for soft tissue resolution in the spine. Diagnostic Which red flag in a back pain history strongly suggests metastatic malignancy? Night pain that wakes the patient from sleep or prevents them from lying comfortably. Clinical In a patient with chronic Rheumatoid Arthritis what potentially fatal spinal complication should you be aware of? C1-C2 subluxation atlantoaxial instability due to erosion of the transverse ligament of the atlas. High Yield What is the difference between Spondylolysis and Spondylolisthesis? Spondylolysis is a stress fracture of the pars interarticularis; Spondylolisthesis is the slipping of one vertebral body over another. Anatomy What is the classic physical exam finding for Spondylolisthesis? A palpable step-off deformity during midline spinal palpation. Physical Exam Which nerve root is likely affected if a patient has a decreased patellar reflex and weak knee extension? The L4 nerve root. Neurology Which nerve root is likely affected if a patient has weakness in great toe extension dorsiflexion ? The L5 nerve root. Neurology What mnemonic correlates the S1 nerve root with its reflex? AchilleS-1 S1 root handles the Achilles reflex. Mnemonic A young man with morning back stiffness that improves with activity likely has what condition? Ankylosing Spondylitis often HLA-B27 positive . Clinical What are the common extra-articular findings in Ankylosing Spondylitis? Uveitis aortic regurgitation and restricted chest expansion. Clinical The Shopping Cart Sign is indicative of which condition? Spinal Stenosis pain is relieved by leaning forward/flexion . Clinical How do you differentiate Neurogenic Claudication stenosis from Peripheral Vascular Disease PVD ? Stenosis is relieved by leaning forward ; PVD is relieved by stopping activity regardless of posture. Clinical Where does the spinal cord end in adults? At the conus medullaris typically level L1 . Anatomy What are the hallmarks of Cauda Equina Syndrome? Saddle anesthesia bowel/bladder incontinence urinary retention and flaccid paralysis . Emergency Is Cauda Equina Syndrome considered a peripheral or central nerve injury? It is a peripheral nerve injury nerve roots below the cord . Concept Dr. Heller s recommendation for Cauda Equina management? Emergent MRI and surgical decompression within 48 hours . Clinical What sign on an oblique X-ray indicates Spondylolysis? The Scotty Dog sign fracture through the neck of the dog . Imaging What is Spurling’s maneuver used to diagnose? Cervical Radiculopathy reproduces arm pain/paresthesia . Physical Exam Define Myelopathy vs. Radiculopathy. Myelopathy is spinal cord compression bilateral signs upper motor neuron ; Radiculopathy is nerve root compression unilateral lower motor neuron . Concept Which population most commonly experiences vertebral compression fractures? Elderly patients with osteoporosis . Clinical What is the imaging modality used to screen for C1-C2 instability in RA patients? Flexion/Extension Radiographs of the cervical spine. Diagnostic Why is intubation risky in a patient with severe Rheumatoid Arthritis? The neck extension required for intubation can cause atlantoaxial subluxation and fatal cord compression. High Yield What blood markers are commonly elevated in spinal infections or tumors? ESR CRP and potentially abnormal SPEP/UPEP for myeloma . Lab Describe the typical presentation of a patient with a lumbar disc herniation at L5-S1. Lateral leg/foot pain weak plantarflexion and diminished Achilles reflex. Clinical What is Whiplash formally known as? Cervical Sprain or Cervical Strain . Clinical match Spinal Stenosis Shopping Cart Sign resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 02 Pathology 24 Spinal stenosis | search spinal stenosis shopping cart match Cauda Equina Syndrome Saddle Anesthesia resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 02 Pathology 23 Cauda equina syndrome | search cauda equina saddle anesthesia match Ankylosing Spondylitis HLA-B27 Bamboo Spine resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 02 Pathology 13 Seronegative Spondyloarthritis Ankylosing Spondylitis | search ankylosing spondylitis bamboo spine match Spondylolysis and Spondylolisthesis resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 02 Pathology 18 Spondylolysis & Spondylolisthesis | search scotty dog spondylolysis match Lumbar Herniated Disc Dermatome Mapping resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 01 Anatomy & Physiology 06 Lower Extremity Nerves | search L4 L5 S1 herniation reflexes match Rheumatoid Arthritis Cervical Involvement resources AnKing Step 1 > search rheumatoid arthritis C1 C2 subluxation questions 1 A 28-year-old male gymnast presents with persistent low back pain that worsens with hyperextension. Oblique X-rays of the lumbar spine reveal a collar on the Scotty dog appearance of the pars interarticularis. What is the most likely diagnosis? Spondylolisthesis Spondylolysis Spinal Stenosis Ankylosing Spondylitis Herniated Nucleus Pulposus 1 Spondylolysis is a stress fracture of the pars interarticularis common in athletes like gymnasts or football linemen who perform repetitive hyperextension. The Scotty dog sign on oblique X-ray where the collar represents the fracture is the classic radiographic finding. 2 A 72-year-old woman with a history of severe Rheumatoid Arthritis RA is being prepared for elective hip surgery. Why is it standard practice to obtain cervical spine X-rays prior to general anesthesia in such patients? To assess for osteoporotic compression fractures To screen for atlantoaxial C1-C2 instability To evaluate for ossification of the posterior longitudinal ligament To rule out cervical spondylotic myelopathy To check for disk herniation that might complicate positioning 1 Chronic RA can erode the transverse ligament of the atlas and the odontoid process leading to atlantoaxial C1-C2 instability. Neck manipulation during intubation can cause catastrophic subluxation and fatal spinal cord compression. 3 A 65-year-old male complains of bilateral leg pain and heaviness that occurs when walking but is relieved by leaning forward on his grocery cart. Physical exam shows normal peripheral pulses and no skin changes. What is the most likely underlying pathophysiology? Atherosclerotic occlusion of the femoral arteries Compression of the spinal cord due to a midline disc herniation Narrowing of the spinal canal leading to nerve root compression Venous insufficiency with valvular incompetence Autoimmune inflammation of the sacroiliac joints 2 This describes neurogenic claudication the shopping cart sign characteristic of Spinal Stenosis. Leaning forward spinal flexion increases the cross-sectional area of the spinal canal relieving the pressure on compressed nerve roots. 4 A patient presents with sudden onset saddle anesthesia urinary retention and bilateral lower extremity weakness after lifting a heavy box. What is the most appropriate immediate next step in management? Strict bed rest and high-dose NSAIDs Urgent physical therapy referral Epidural steroid injection Emergent MRI and neurosurgical consultation Oral muscle relaxants and follow-up in one week 3 These are red flag symptoms for Cauda Equina Syndrome CES . CES is a surgical emergency because delayed decompression can lead to permanent bowel/bladder dysfunction and paralysis. Emergent MRI is required for diagnosis. 5 A 30-year-old man presents with chronic morning back stiffness that significantly improves with swimming. X-ray of the pelvis reveals bilateral sacroiliitis. This condition is most strongly associated with which of the following? HLA-DR4 HLA-B27 HLA-B51 HLA-DR3 HLA-DQ8 1 The presentation young male inflammatory back pain improving with exercise sacroiliitis is diagnostic for Ankylosing Spondylitis. This seronegative spondyloarthropathy is classically associated with HLA-B27 seen in ~90% of cases ."}, {"id": "l113", "title": "Lecture #113: Imaging of the MSK System", "module": "Unknown", "content": "id l113 module Unknown l113 Lecture 113 Imaging of the MSK System lecturer J. Mazzie D.O. Radiology pdf content/L113 HighYield Render.pdf Lecture 113 Imaging of the MSK System Lecturer J. Mazzie D.O. | Session Lecture 113 Radiological evaluation of the musculoskeletal system is not merely about identifying fractures; it is about applying a rigorous systematic approach to visualize anatomy that is often hidden. A single view in radiology is often a no view as the three-dimensional complexity of bones and joints can mask significant pathology on a two-dimensional film. By systematically evaluating alignment joint spaces bone density and soft tissues clinicians can differentiate between benign variants and aggressive malignancies. This lecture establishes the fundamental principles for MSK imaging emphasizing the need for multiple orthogonal views and a high index of suspicion for invisible injuries like stress fractures and occult joint effusions. --- The Systematic Approach to MSK Imaging Slides 4-7 Before attempting to diagnose a specific pathology the radiologist must ensure the study is adequate. This starts with identifying the correct body part and ensuring a minimum of two orthogonal views 90 degrees to each other . The age of the patient is a critical contextual clue; for instance a bone lesion in a child is statistically more likely to be a primary bone tumor whereas in an older adult metastatic disease is the primary concern. - The Four-Step Checklist 1. Check Alignment Trace the relationship between adjacent bones. Slide 8 2. Evaluate Joint Spaces Look for narrowing cartilage loss/arthritis or widening dislocation/dissociation . Slide 10 3. Examine Bone Cortex Trace every millimeter of the white cortical line for breaches. Slide 11 4. Evaluate Soft Tissues Look for effusions or abnormal swelling. Slide 13 --- Joint Alignment and Dislocations Slides 8-9 Alignment refers to the normal anatomical relationship between bones in a joint. Loss of alignment typically indicates either a fracture or a dislocation. In some cases misalignment is chronic such as the hallux valgus bunion deformity seen in the foot which may also feature prominent sesamoid bones—small bones that form within tendons like the patella . - Anterior Glenohumeral Dislocation The humeral head is displaced anteriorly relative to the glenoid Slide 9 . - Lunate Dislocation Often more visible on a lateral view of the wrist; the lunate dislocates volarly towards the palm . correlate Anterior Glenohumeral Dislocation - Pathophysiology Disruption of the glenohumeral joint where the humeral head sits anterior to the glenoid fossa. - Presentation Often seen in trauma; the shoulder appears squared off. - Diagnosis AP and lateral Y-view X-rays are diagnostic. - Lecturer s Point You must have a Y-view or lateral view to appreciate the relationship between the humeral head and the glenoid. --- Bone Cortex and Normal Variants Slide 11-12 Tracing the cortex is a high-yield habit. Each white cortical line must be traced entirely. A breach in this line is often a fracture but the clinician must be careful not to misidentify normal anatomical features as pathology. - Nutrient Foramina These are normal channels in the mid-diaphysis of long bones like the femur and humerus that allow blood vessels to enter. They can look like thin lucent lines but are a normal variant not fractures. Lecturer Emphasis --- Soft Tissues and Joint Effusions Slides 13-14 Soft tissue evaluation often provides the clue to an underlying bone injury. Swelling or an effusion within a joint can indicate pathology even if the fracture itself is subtle or initially invisible. - Lipohemarthrosis This is a pathognomonic finding on a lateral knee X-ray. It presents as a fat-fluid level within the suprapatellar bursa. Because bone marrow contains fat a fracture that extends into the joint allows marrow fat to leak into the synovial fluid. Slide 14 highyield Lipohemarthrosis Fat-Fluid Level A fat-fluid level on a lateral view of a joint commonly the knee indicates an intra-articular fracture like a proximal tibial fracture . Fat floats on top of blood/fluid. --- Principles of Viewing Orthogonal Views Slides 15-22 The lecturer emphasizes the Satisfaction of Search pitfall the tendency to stop looking once one abnormality is found. To avoid this clinicians must follow a strict routine and always obtain at least two views ideally 90 degrees apart. - Orthogonal Power A fracture line may be perfectly parallel to the X-ray beam in one view making it invisible but glaringly obvious in another view Slide 15 . - Comparison Views In pediatric cases or complex anatomy comparing the normal side to the abnormal side is a powerful tool to identify subtle differences Slide 17 . - Don t Get Distracted A large incidental finding like a calcified uterine fibroid should not distract from a subtle femoral neck fracture Slide 19 . --- Pancoast Tumors and Referred Symptoms Slide 20 Radiology requires looking at the whole film. A patient presenting with arm pain or radiculopathy might not have a primary MSK issue in the arm. Instead they may have a large tumor at the apex of the lung. correlate Pancoast Tumor Superior Sulcus Tumor - Pathophysiology A primary lung malignancy arising at the extreme apex that invades the brachial plexus and/or cervical sympathetic chain. - Presentation Can present with radicular arm pain or Horner s Syndrome miosis ptosis anhidrosis . - Lecturer s Point We are responsible for everything on the film; a patient with arm pain may actually have a lung tumor visible on a shoulder or chest film. --- Fracture Descriptions and Pathology Slides 23-28 Fractures are described by their orientation transverse oblique spiral longitudinal and their relationship to the skin open vs. closed . - Open Fractures These are surgical emergencies due to the high risk of infection osteomyelitis . - Pathologic vs. Stress Fractures - Pathologic Fracture Normal stress applied to abnormal bone e.g. bone with a tumor or osteoporosis . - Stress Fracture Abnormal repetitive stress applied to normal bone . correlate Stress Fracture Marsh Fracture - Pathophysiology Repetitive mechanical stress causes micro-fractures in normal bone. - Presentation Insidious onset of localized pain. - Diagnosis Initially X-ray negative. Periosteal reaction new bone formation appears 7-14 days later. MRI is more sensitive early on showing marrow edema. - Lecturer s Point These were first described in soldiers Marching and are common in runners. --- Pediatric Fractures The Greenstick Slide 26 Children s bones are more pliable than adults. They often suffer from incomplete fractures where the bone bends or buckles rather than snapping completely. correlate Greenstick Fracture - Pathophysiology An incomplete fracture occurring in children where the bone is broken on one side and bent on the other. - Presentation Localized pain and deformity in a child. - Lecturer s Point This is an important pediatric fracture pattern to recognize for exams. --- Bone Tumors and Radiographic Aggression Slides 29-37 The appearance of a bone lesion s margins and its effect on the periosteum the outer layer of bone tell a story of how fast the lesion is growing. | Appearance | Description | Growth Rate | Likelihood of Malignancy | |---|---|---|---| | Geographic | Well-defined can trace with a pencil | Slow | Benign e.g. 95% benign | | Sclerotic Rim | Thick white border around lesion | Very Slow | Benign | | Moth-eaten | Multiple small holes ill-defined | Fast | Aggressive e.g. Malignancy or Osteomyelitis | | Permeative | Tiny pin-sized holes widespread | Very Fast | Highly Aggressive | Periosteal Reactions The periosteum reacts to insults by laying down new bone. - Solid Slow growing benign. - Laminated Onion Skin More aggressive. Slide 34 - Speculated Sunburst Very aggressive; indicates a rapidly growing malignancy. - Codman’s Triangle The periosteum is lifted at an angle by a tumor. This is a very aggressive malignant sign. Slide 37 highyield Codman’s Triangle This is the most aggressive form of periosteal reaction. It occurs when a tumor grows so fast it lifts the periosteum off the bone forming a triangular shadow. It is highly indicative of a malignant process. --- Summary of MSK Imaging Pearls - Symmetry is your friend Compare sides. - Air to Metal Densities range from darkest Air to lightest Metal . - Rule of Two At least two orthogonal views are mandatory. - Search Everything Don t stop at the first finding. BandB Musculoskeletal > Radiology > MSK Imaging Basics FirstAid Musculoskeletal Skin and Connective Tissue > Pathology > Bone Tumors Pathoma Musculoskeletal Pathology > Bone Tumors SketchyPath Musculoskeletal > Bone and Joint Disorders What is the Rule of Two in MSK imaging? Always obtain at least two views ideally orthogonal 90 degrees to each other. Concept What does a fat-fluid level on a lateral knee X-ray indicate? Lipohemarthrosis which is pathognomonic for an intra-articular fracture fat leaking from marrow into the joint . Clinical What is the difference between a pathologic and a stress fracture? Pathologic Normal stress on abnormal bone. Stress Abnormal stress on normal bone. Concept What is a Greenstick fracture? An incomplete fracture in a child where one side of the bone breaks and the other side bends. Clinical A Codman s Triangle on X-ray indicates what? An aggressive likely malignant bone tumor causing rapid periosteal lifting. Clinical What is the radiographic term for a well-defined lesion you can trace with a pencil ? Geographic appearance; typically benign. Concept How long does it typically take for a periosteal reaction to appear on X-ray after a stress fracture? 7 to 14 days. Concept What normal variant at the mid-diaphysis of long bones can be mistaken for a fracture? Nutrient foramen. Glossary Which lung tumor can present with radicular arm symptoms due to brachial plexus invasion? Pancoast tumor Superior Sulcus tumor . Clinical What density is the darkest on a standard X-ray? Air gas . Concept match Periosteal reactions Sunburst Codman s Triangle resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 02 Pathology 09 Bone Tumors Osteosarcoma match Greenstick and Torus Fractures resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 02 Pathology 17 Common fractures Pediatric match Scaphoid Fracture Snuffbox Tenderness resources AnKing Step 1 > AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 02 Pathology 17 Common fractures Upper Extremity Satisfaction of Search The tendency for a radiologist to stop looking once they find one abnormality. Always finish your systematic checklist Alignment Joint Spaces Cortex Soft Tissues . Orthogonal Views One view is no view. Fractures parallel to the beam are invisible so you MUST have two views at 90 degrees. Clinical Hint Stress Fractures Early X-rays for stress fractures are usually negative. If pain persists get an MRI sensitive early or repeat X-ray in 2 weeks wait for periosteal reaction . Lipohemarthrosis If you see a fat-fluid level on a knee lateral there s a fracture into the joint even if you can t see the fracture line yet. questions 1 A 10-year-old child presents after a fall onto their arm. X-ray shows the radius is broken on one side but only bent on the other side. What is this fracture called? A. Transverse fracture B. Greenstick fracture C. Torus fracture D. Pathologic fracture E. Spiral fracture 1 A Greenstick fracture is an incomplete pediatric fracture where one side of the cortex breaks and the other side remains intact but bowed. 2 A 24-year-old marathon runner presents with persistent foot pain. Initial X-rays are normal. Two weeks later repeat X-rays show a thin line of new bone formation periosteal reaction along the second metatarsal shaft. What is the most likely diagnosis? A. Osteosarcoma B. Osteomyelitis C. Stress fracture D. Geographic lesion E. Pathologic fracture 2 Stress fractures Marsh fractures are often X-ray negative initially but develop a periosteal reaction after 7-14 days. 3 A lateral knee X-ray on a trauma patient reveals a horizontal line in the suprapatellar bursa with separate densities representing fat and fluid. This finding is highly suggestive of which of the following? A. Septic arthritis B. Patellar tendon rupture C. Intra-articular fracture D. Prepatellar bursitis E. Pancoast tumor 2 Lipohemarthrosis fat-fluid level occurs when marrow fat leaks into the joint space signifying an intra-articular fracture. 4 An aggressive bone lesion exhibits a sunburst appearance with lifting of the periosteum at a triangular angle. This triangular lifting is known as A. Geographic margin B. Onion skinning C. Codman s Triangle D. Nutrient foramen E. March fracture 2 Codman s Triangle is an aggressive periosteal reaction where a rapidly growing tumor lifts the periosteum off the bone. 5 A 65-year-old male with a history of smoking presents with numbness and pain in his right arm. A shoulder X-ray appears mostly normal but a large ill-defined mass is noted at the lung apex. What is the most likely cause of his arm pain? A. Rotator cuff tear B. Glenohumeral dislocation C. Pancoast tumor D. Lunate dislocation E. Greenstick fracture 2 Pancoast tumors at the lung apex can invade the brachial plexus causing referred radicular pain in the upper extremity. mindMap MSK Imaging Fundamentals Systematic Approach - Alignment Relationships between bones - Joint Spaces Narrowing arthritis vs. Widening dislocation - Cortex Tracing the entire cortical line - Soft Tissue Effusions swelling fat-fluid levels Principles of Viewing - Orthogonal Views 2 views at 90 degrees - Satisfaction of Search Fatal error of stopping after one finding - Age Context Pediatrics bone tumors vs. Adults metastasis Fracture Patterns - Greenstick Incomplete pediatric break/bend - Stress Marsh Repetitive stress initially X-ray negative - Pathologic Normal stress on diseased bone - Open Surgical emergency; infection risk Radiographic Aggression - Benign Geographic sclerotic rim slow growth - Aggressive Moth-eaten permeative - Periosteal Reaction - Solid Benign - Onion Skin Aggressive - Sunburst Highly Aggressive - Codman’s Triangle Malignant indicator"}, {"id": "l114", "title": "Introduction to Musculoskeletal Ultrasound", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l114 module Neuro l114 Introduction to Musculoskeletal Ultrasound lecturer J. Mazzie D.O. session Lecture 114 Radiology readingTime 12-15 min highYieldPdf content/L114 HighYield Render.pdf Lecture 114 Introduction to Musculoskeletal Ultrasound Lecturer J. Mazzie D.O. Session Lecture 114 The Evolution and Strategic Utility of MSK Sonography Musculoskeletal MSK sonography has rapidly evolved from a specialized imaging technique into a ubiquitous front-line diagnostic tool. In clinical practice it serves as a bridge between the physical exam and high-end cross-sectional imaging like MRI. The lecturer emphasizes that understanding the fundamental strengths of ultrasound is as important as the physics itself. MSK ultrasound is not merely a cheaper version of MRI; it offers unique diagnostic capabilities—specifically real-time dynamic imaging—that static imaging simply cannot replicate. The Magic of MSK Ultrasound lies in its ability to provide high-detail patient-interactive imaging. It is a modality that allows for immediate clinical correlation. If a patient pointedly says It hurts right here the sonographer can place the probe exactly on that spot and see the anatomy under the patient s area of maximal tenderness. This targeted approach is often more revealing than a broad-field MRI that might miss a focal superficial abnormality. correlate Patient-Interactive Imaging Unlike traditional Radiology where the radiologist often never sees the patient MSK Ultrasound is a contact sport. The examiner is talking to the patient moving their limb and correlating symptoms with images in real-time. This significantly reduces the rate of false positives from incidental findings that aren t actually causing the patient s pain. --- Technical Foundations Transducers and Sound Waves To master MSK ultrasound one must first understand the tools. The transducer is the brain of the operation utilizing the Piezoelectric Effect to convert electrical energy into sound waves and vice versa. Choosing the Right Probe The Golden Rule of ultrasound physics is the inverse relationship between Frequency and Penetration . - High-Frequency Linear Probes These provide superior axial resolution but have poor depth penetration. In MSK these are used for almost everything—shoulders wrists fingers and ankles—where the structures are superficial. - Low-Frequency Curvilinear Probes These trade resolution for depth. They are typically reserved for deep structures like the hip joint or for scanning obese patients where significant soft tissue attenuation occurs. The Grayscale Language To effectively interpret an MSK ultrasound a clinician must speak in grayscale. The brightness of an image or its echogenicity is determined by how much of the sound wave is reflected back to the transducer versus how much is absorbed or transmitted through the tissue. Standard Sonographic Terminology Hyperechoic Bright/White Structures reflecting a high percentage of sound. Examples Bone cortex tendons epineurium. Isoechoic Gray Structures that match the brightness of the surrounding tissue background. Hypoechoic Dark/Gray Structures that absorb/transmit more than they reflect. Example Normal skeletal muscle. Anechoic Black/Void Completely black; indicates simple pure fluid e.g. joint fluid simple cysts . Posterior Acoustic Shadowing Occurs behind very dense structures bone calcifications that block all sound waves leaving a dark void deep to the structure. highyield Lecturer Emphasis The Power of Doppler When a structure is hypoechoic dark it could be a tear or just inflammation. Power Doppler allows us to see neovascularization. If a tendon is dark AND lights up on Doppler it confirms the tissue is acutely inflamed The tendon is mad . --- Normal Anatomic Appearances Mastering the Baseline The lecturer emphasizes that a student must look at thousands of normals to truly recognize the abnormal. Each tissue type in the MSK system has a unique architectural pattern. 1. The Tendon The Braid of Hair Tendons are comprised of highly organized collagen bundles arranged in parallel. This creates a signature fibrillar pattern . - Long-Axis View A healthy tendon looks like a series of thousands of tiny parallel lines often described as a braid of hair or raw silk. - Significance Disruption of these parallel lines indicates tendinosis wear or a tear. - Example The Achilles tendon is the gold standard for visualizing this due to its size and superficial location. 2. Skeletal Muscle The Starry Sky Muscles consist of dark hypoechoic contractile tissue separated by thin bright hyperechoic connective tissue perimysium . - Long-Axis Muscle looks marbled with fine bright streaks. - Short-Axis Transverse Where the Starry Sky Appearance is seen. The hyperechoic fibro-fatty septa look like bright stars against the dark midnight sky of the muscle fibers. 3. Nerves The Honeycomb In the transverse view a nerve looks like a Honeycomb. The holes of the honeycomb are the hypoechoic nerve fascicles and the walls are the bright white epineurium. - Dynamic Check How to tell a nerve from a tendon? Move the joint. Tendons glide like a piston; nerves just jiggle or move slightly side-to-side. 4. Bone and Cartilage - Bone Cortex Always a smooth continuous bright white hyperechoic line. Any step-off or break in this line is a fracture until proven otherwise. - Hyaline Cartilage e.g. covering the femoral head Appears as a uniform hypoechoic dark layer. - Fibrocartilage e.g. Meniscus Labrum Denser than hyaline appearing bright hyperechoic and often triangular. --- The Crucial Technical Challenge Anisotropy important Anisotropy is the most common pitfall for beginner sonographers. It is an artifact that makes a normal tendon look dark hypoechoic mimicking a tear. The Physics of the Fallacy Sound waves reflect most efficiently when they hit a surface at a 90-degree angle . Because tendons are made of highly reflective parallel fibers if the beam hits them at even a slight angle the sound reflects away from the probe. - The Solution The Heel-Toe technique . Angle the probe until it is exactly perpendicular to the tendon. If the dark spot vanishes and becomes bright white it was anisotropy. If it stays dark it s pathology. --- Pathological Findings and Case Correlations Tendinosis vs. Tears - Tendinosis Chronic degeneration. The tendon becomes swollen thickened hypoechoic dark and loses its fibrillar pattern. - Tears Appear as anechoic foci completely black where the fibers have snapped and the gap is filled with fluid or hematoma. Case Study 1 The Pacemaker Dilemma An 18-year-old female injured her shoulder at CrossFit. MRI was contraindicated due to her pacemaker . Ultrasound provided high-resolution imaging that confirmed a supraspinatus tendon tear allowing for surgical planning that MRI could not facilitate. Case Study 2 The Tickle Fracture A physician felt a snap while tickling his child. Ultrasound showed a cortical break in the 5th metacarpal. This was a pathologic fracture occurring through an enchondroma a benign tumor that thinned the bone cortex to eggshell-thinness . Case Study 3 The Popliteal Fullness A 45-year-old with fullness behind the knee. Ultrasound revealed an anechoic collection between the semimembranosus tendon and the medial head of the gastrocnemius . - Diagnosis Baker s Cyst. - The Tail Sign The key to diagnosis is finding the neck connecting the cyst to the joint space. mnemonic The S-M-G of Baker s Cysts S emimembranosus and M edial G astrocnemius are the walls of the Baker s Shop. --- Summary of Clinical Utility MSK Ultrasound is an adjunct tool . It should always be interpreted in the context of the patient s history and prior radiographs. The ultimate superpower of ultrasound is its dynamic testing —the ability to see pathology that only appears when the patient moves. questions 1 A 22-year-old athlete presents with shoulder pain. On ultrasound the supraspinatus tendon appears dark when the probe is angled at 45 degrees but appears bright and fibrillar when the probe is oriented perpendicular to the fibers. What is the most likely explanation for this finding? Acute tendinitis Anisotropy Complete tendon tear Calcific tendinosis Neovascularization 1 Anisotropy is a common ultrasound artifact where tendons appear hypoechoic dark if the sound beam is not perpendicular 90 degrees to the parallel collagen fibers. Correcting the angle to 90 degrees restores the normal hyperechoic fibrillar appearance. A true tear or tendinitis would remain hypoechoic regardless of probe angle. 2 Which of the following sonographic descriptions best fits a normal skeletal muscle in the transverse short axis view? Completely anechoic Fibrillar braid of hair pattern Honeycomb appearance Starry sky appearance Refractive shadowing 3 In the transverse short axis view muscle exhibits a starry sky appearance. The stars are the bright hyperechoic fibro-fatty septa perimysium and the sky is the dark hypoechoic contractile muscle tissue. The braid of hair refers to tendons in long-axis and honeycomb refers to nerves in transverse view. 3 A 55-year-old patient with an implanted metallic heart valve requires imaging of a suspected rotator cuff tear. After negative plain radiographs which imaging modality is the most appropriate next step for assessing the soft tissue architecture? MRI with contrast CT without contrast MSK Ultrasound PET scan Bone scan 2 Ultrasound is the ideal modality for soft tissue evaluation in patients where MRI is contraindicated e.g. pacemakers certain metallic implants . It provides excellent resolution for tendons and ligaments without ionizing radiation or electromagnetic interference issues. 4 A patient presents with fullness in the popliteal fossa. Sonography reveals a Baker s cyst. Between which two anatomic structures is this fluid collection classically situated? Biceps femoris and lateral gastrocnemius Semimembranosus and medial gastrocnemius Quadriceps tendon and patella Popliteus and soleus ACL and PCL 1 A Baker s cyst popliteal cyst typically develops in the bursal space between the semimembranosus tendon and the medial head of the gastrocnemius muscle. Finding the neck of the fluid collection between these two structures is the hallmark diagnostic feature. 5 A physician feels a sudden snap while playing with his child leading to hand pain. Ultrasound shows a focal step-off in the brightest white line of the 5th metacarpal. What is the fundamental sonographic sign of a fracture identified here? Anechoic collection in the joint space Increased echogenicity of the subcutaneous fat Interruption of the hyperechoic bone cortex Honeycomb appearance of the adjacent nerve Starry sky appearance of the tendon 2 The bone cortex is highly reflective and appears as a continuous smooth bright white hyperechoic line on ultrasound. An interruption gap or step-off in this continuous line is the definitive sonographic sign of a fracture. Echogenicity The degree to which a tissue reflects ultrasound waves brightness on the monitor . Glossary Hyperechoic Brighter than surrounding tissue e.g. bone tendons . Glossary Hypoechoic Darker than surrounding tissue e.g. muscle inflamed nerves . Glossary Anechoic Black/void of echoes; represents simple fluid e.g. cysts effusions . Glossary Fibrillar Pattern The sonographic braid of hair appearance characteristic of normal tendons long-axis . Concept Starry Sky Appearance The transverse sonographic appearance of muscle bright septa in dark tissue . Concept Honeycomb Appearance The transverse sonographic appearance of nerves dark fascicles in bright epineurium . Concept Anisotropy Artifact where a tendon appears falsely hypoechoic due to a non-perpendicular probe angle. Clinical Tendinosis on US Swollen hypoechoic tendon with morphology change rounded and neovascularity Power Doppler . Clinical Baker s Cyst Location Fluid collection between the semimembranosus and the medial head of the gastrocnemius. Clinical Pathologic Fracture A fracture occurring through abnormal bone e.g. an enchondroma with minimal trauma. Clinical Dynamic Maneuver Moving a joint during ultrasound to observe tendon subluxation or real-time impingement. Concept Bone Shadowing The dark void deep to the bright white bone cortex due to complete sound reflection. Concept Heel-Toe Technique Angling the probe to ensure it is perpendicular to a tendon to resolve anisotropy artifact. Clinical Power Doppler Ultrasound mode used to detect low-flow neovascularization in inflamed tissues. Glossary BandB Orthopedics > Diagnostic Imaging FirstAid Musculoskeletal > Radiology SketchyPath MSK > Bone & Soft Tissue Physeo Anatomy > Upper Extremity > Shoulder Pathoma MSK > Bone Tumors Bootcamp MSK > Imaging match Musculoskeletal ultrasound for rotator cuff tear resources AnKing Step 2 > MedicalArk > Musculoskeletal ultrasound match Baker s Cyst anatomy resources AnKing Step 1 > Pathoma > Musculoskeletal > Baker s Cyst match Anisotropy artifact resources AnKing Step 1 > Radiology > Artifacts > Anisotropy Dynamic Distinction To distinguish nerves from tendons move the joint; tendons glide like pistons while nerves remain relatively stationary jiggle . Anisotropy Check Always heel-toe the probe to ensure perpendicularity before diagnosing tendinosis; anisotropy resolves with probe adjustment. The Baker s Tail To confirm a Baker s cyst you must find the neck or tail tracking between the semimembranosus and medial gastrocnemius. Reflective Cortex The bone cortex should be a single smooth bright white line; any gap is a fracture until proven otherwise. Pacemaker Safety Ultrasound is the preferred imaging modality for soft tissue in patients with MRI-incompatible devices like pacemakers. mindmap Musculoskeletal Ultrasound Imaging Principles - Advantages Cost-effective Readily Available Real-time/Dynamic No Radiation MRI-Incompatible Safe - Terminology Hyperechoic Bright Bone Tendon Hypoechoic Dark Muscle Inflamed Nerve Anechoic Black Simple Fluid Cysts - Artifacts Anisotropy False hypoechogenicity due to angle. Correct with Heel-Toe probe adjustment. Tissue Signatures - Tendon Fibrillar pattern braid of hair . - Muscle Starry sky view transverse ; hypoechoic with hyperechoic septa. - Nerve Honeycomb appearance; dark fascicles surrounded by bright epineurium. - Bone Highly reflective white line with deep acoustic shadowing. - Cartilage Hyaline Hypoechoic Uniform layer . Fibrocartilage Hyperechoic/Bright Triangular e.g. Meniscus . Key Pathologies - Tendinosis Swollen hypoechoic neovascularity Doppler . - Tendon Tears Anechoic fluid-filled gaps. - Neuropathy Swollen dark nerve; loss of honeycomb pattern. - Baker s Cyst Fluid between semimembranosus and medial gastrocnemius. - Fracture Interruption of the cortical white line."}, {"id": "l115", "title": "Lecture #115: Sports Medicine: Overuse Injuries", "module": "Unknown", "content": "id l115 module Unknown l115 Lecture 115 Sports Medicine Overuse Injuries lecturer H. Zwibel D.O. Orthopedics pdf content/L115 HighYield Render.pdf Lecture 115 Sports Medicine Overuse Injuries Lecturer H. Zwibel D.O. | Session Lecture 115 1. Introduction The Pathophysiology of Overuse Dr. Zwibel introduces a critical framework for understanding sports medicine the distinction between acute trauma and overuse injuries . While acute injuries are sudden overuse injuries result from repetitive microtrauma to bone muscle and tendon tissues. To effectively treat these patients clinicians must move beyond identifying the injury itself and investigate the precipitating risk factors —both extrinsic and intrinsic. Without addressing why the injury occurred the cycle of dysfunction will inevitably recur. --- 2. Risk Factors Why Injuries Happen Extrinsic Factors Environment & Training Extrinsic risk factors are external to the individual and are often the most modifiable. - Training Errors Rapid increases in intensity volume or duration the too much too soon phenomenon . Lack of adequate recovery time is a leading cause of tissue failure. - Technique Improper form during repetitive motions e.g. bowling or pitching places asymmetric stress on tissues. - Environment & Equipment Hard training surfaces e.g. concrete vs. track and worn-out or inappropriate footwear directly impact force transmission. Intrinsic Factors The Individual Intrinsic factors are inherent to the patient’s anatomy and physiology. - Malalignment Anatomical irregularities such as leg length discrepancies flat feet pes planus or high arches pes cavus . - Muscle Imbalances Differences in length and strength between agonist and antagonist muscle groups e.g. tight hamstrings vs. weak quadriceps . - Systemic Issues Genetic endocrine e.g. thyroid dysfunction and metabolic factors that impact tissue healing and bone density. --- 3. Bone Stress Injuries The Continuum of Failure Bone stress injuries represent a spectrum ranging from bone strain to stress reactions and finally stress fractures . Pathophysiology The Modeling Response When bone is subjected to repetitive stress it undergoes a vascular congestion and thrombosis response. This leads to a mismatch where osteoclastic activity bone breakdown outpaces osteoblastic activity bone building . The weakened trabecular bone develops microfractures that can progress to a complete cortical break if the stress continues. highyield The Female Athlete Triad Women are 2 to 10 times more likely to suffer stress injuries than men. This is often driven by the Female Athlete Triad 1. Low Energy Availability Disordered Eating 2. Menstrual Dysfunction Amenorrhea 3. Low Bone Mineral Density Osteoporosis The resulting hypoestrogenic state severely impairs bone health. Clinical Presentation & Diagnosis - Crescendo Pain Pain typically starts as a dull ache and progresses to sharp localized pinpoint pain . In advanced stages pain occurs at rest and even at night. - Imaging Modalities - X-ray Initial test of choice; however it has low sensitivity early on and may not show changes for 4-6 weeks. Look for periosteal reaction or a dreaded black line. - Bone Scan Highly sensitive for metabolic activity but poorly specific for differentiating reaction vs. fracture. - MRI The Gold Standard . Highly sensitive and specific for visualizing bone marrow edema and cortical defects. Treatment The mainstay of treatment is relative rest typically for 6 weeks . While the injured area is protected the patient should engage in cross-training to prevent systemic atrophy. NSAIDs should be avoided as they can hinder bone healing; acetaminophen is the preferred analgesic. --- 4. Muscle Overuse Injuries Adhesions and Pressure Focal Tissue Thickening and Fibrosis Overuse leads to microtrauma and the formation of fascial cross-linkages and adhesions between muscle fibers. On palpation these feel like knots or tight bands. These adhesions reduce the muscle s ability to contract and relax rapidly predisposing the athlete to further injury. - Treatment Stretching strengthening and Osteopathic Manipulative Treatment OMT are effective in breaking down fibrotic tissue. Chronic Exertional Compartment Syndrome CECS Unlike acute compartment syndrome a surgical emergency CECS is a chronic reversible condition. - Mechanism Exercise increases muscle volume; however the surrounding fascia is inelastic and refuses to expand. This raises intercompartmental pressure impairing blood flow and compressing nerves. - Symptoms Pain and pressure that begin with activity and improve immediately with rest . - Diagnosis Measurement of compartmental pressures anterior lateral deep posterior or superficial posterior at rest and post-exercise. - Treatment Conservative management first; fasciotomy is considered only if conservative measures fail. correlate Delayed Onset Muscle Soreness DOMS DOMS peaks 1-2 days after exercise particularly after eccentric movements muscle lengthening under tension . It is caused by muscle fiber damage and cell membrane swelling not lactic acid buildup. --- 5. Tendinopathy The Non-Inflammatory Cascade Modern sports medicine has transitioned from the term tendonitis implying inflammation to tendinopathy as these are primarily degenerative rather than inflammatory processes. Three Stages of Tendinopathy 1. Reactive Tendinopathy A non-inflammatory proliferative response in the cell matrix leading to short-term thickening. 2. Tendon Disrepair The matrix begins to break down. Tenocytes become disorganized and neovascularization ingrowth of small vessels and nerves occurs. 3. Degenerative Tendinopathy Progression to cell death apoptosis and large areas of matrix disorganization with minimal potential for recovery. caution Steroid Injections & Tendon Rupture Corticosteroid injections should not be placed directly into a tendon especially weight-bearing ones like the Achilles . High-dose steroids can weaken the collagen structure leading to catastrophic tendon rupture. --- 6. Clinical Applications Epicondylitis - Lateral Epicondylitis Tennis Elbow Involves the Extensor Carpi Radialis Brevis ECRB . Pain is reproduced with resisted wrist extension. - Medial Epicondylitis Golfer s/Pitcher s Elbow Involves the flexors and pronators Pronator Teres and Flexor Carpi Radialis . Often associated with ulnar nerve irritation. --- 7. Summary & Key Takeaways - Listen to the Body Pain is a feedback mechanism indicating tissue strain. - Diagnostic Flow Start with X-ray but don t rule out stress fractures if initial films are negative; use MRI for confirmation. - Comprehensive Care Correct the underlying biomechanical or systemic dysfunction to prevent recurrence. What are the three components of the Female Athlete Triad? 1. Low energy availability disordered eating 2. Menstrual dysfunction amenorrhea 3. Low bone mineral density osteoporosis . Which imaging modality is considered the gold standard for diagnosing stress fractures? MRI Magnetic Resonance Imaging . Why should NSAIDs generally be avoided in the treatment of stress fractures? NSAIDs have been shown to hinder the bone healing process; acetaminophen is preferred. What is the primary difference between the pain of tendinopathy and chronic compartment syndrome? Tendinopathy pain often improves after warming up but returns later; CECS pain starts with activity and dissipates immediately with rest. What muscle is most commonly involved in lateral epicondylitis? Extensor Carpi Radialis Brevis ECRB . True or False Tendinopathy is primarily an inflammatory condition. False. It is a degenerative and maladaptive process rather than an acute inflammatory one. Which type of muscle contraction is most associated with Delayed Onset Muscle Soreness DOMS ? Eccentric contractions lengthening under tension . In the diagnosis of stress fractures what is the Dreaded Black Line seen on X-ray? A transverse radiolucent line indicating a high-risk cortical stress fracture often seen on the anterior tibia. AnKing Step 1 V12 Musculoskeletal match Stress fractures Female Athlete Triad Epicondylitis Overuse Injuries source Boards & Beyond Musculoskeletal > Anatomy > Upper Extremity Epicondylitis source First Aid 2024 page 448-450 source Pathoma 18.3 - Bone Diseases source AnKing V11 tags AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 12 Musculoskeletal 05 Pathology Sports Medicine questions 1 A 20-year-old female distance runner presents with a 3-week history of worsening right shin pain. Initially the pain only occurred at the end of runs but now it is present during daily walking. Physical exam reveals point tenderness over the distal third of the medial tibia. Initial X-rays are negative. What is the most appropriate next step in management? Return to running with a supportive brace Immediate MRI to confirm diagnosis Recommend relative rest and cross-training Start high-dose Ibuprofen to reduce inflammation Technetium-99 bone scan 2 This case is highly suspicious for a tibial stress injury. Relative rest is the primary treatment even if imaging is initially negative. MRI is a valid diagnostic step but clinical management rest is the priority. NSAIDs should be avoided. 2 A 44-year-old recreational tennis player reports persistent pain in her lateral elbow. The pain is reproduced when she is asked to extend her wrist against resistance while her elbow is extended. What is the most likely histopathological finding in this patient’s affected tendon? Acute neutrophilic infiltration Neovascularization and tenocyte disorganization Amyloid deposition Eosinophilic micro-abscesses Granulomatous inflammation 1 Lateral epicondylitis tennis elbow is a tendinopathy. Histopathology shows a degenerative process characterized by neovascularization tenocyte proliferation and collagen matrix disorganization rather than acute inflammation. 3 An athlete complains of intense forearm tightness and numbness during high-intensity weightlifting. He notes that the symptoms are severe enough to stop his workout but vanish within 5 minutes of halting the exercise. Physical exam at rest is unremarkable. What is the definitive treatment if conservative measures fail? Intramedullary nailing Fasciotomy Corticosteroid injection Surgical tendon transfer Casting for 6 weeks 1 This describes Chronic Exertional Compartment Syndrome CECS . The symptoms are volume-dependent and resolve with rest. While OMT and PT are initial steps fasciotomy is the definitive surgical solution for cases failing conservative care. Pinpoint vs. Diffuse Pain Pinpoint tenderness on bone is highly suggestive of a stress fracture whereas diffuse tenderness along the medial tibial border is more common in Medial Tibial Stress Syndrome Shin Splints . Relative Rest Rest the injury not the athlete. Cross-training swimming cycling maintains cardiovascular fitness while allowing the target tissue to heal. The Ring of Fire In overuse scenarios affecting the hip always maintain a high index of suspicion for Femoral Neck Stress Fractures as displacement can lead to Avascular Necrosis. mindmap root Overuse Injuries children name Bone children name Stress Fracture children name Crescendo Pain name MRI = Gold Standard name Female Triad children name Energy Deficiency name Amenorrhea name Osteoporosis name Muscle children name CECS children name Exertional Pressure name Rest=Resolution name Fasciotomy name DOMS children name Eccentric Loading name 1-2 Day Peak name Tendon children name Tendinopathy children name Degenerative name Neovascularization name Epicondylitis children name Lateral ECRB name Medial Flexors"}, {"id": "l116", "title": "Pediatric Orthopedics", "module": "OMM", "content": "id l116 module OMM l116 Pediatric Orthopedics lecturer M. Scotten M.D. session Lecture 116 Orthopedics readingTime 12-15 min pdf content/L116 HighYield Render.pdf analysis content/L116 HighYield Render Analysis.md Lecture 116 Pediatric Orthopedics Lecturer M. Scotten M.D. Session Lecture 116 The Pediatric Skeleton Distinct from Adults Slide 3 The fundamental axiom of pediatric care is that children are not small adults and this is nowhere more evident than in the skeletal system. A neonate enters the world with approximately 300 bones many of which are largely cartilaginous to allow for the buoyancy and compression required for life in utero and the passage through the birth canal. Over time these structures fuse into the standard 206 bones seen in adulthood. Because of this high cartilage-to-bone ratio pediatric bones possess unique biomechanical properties—they are more pliable more porous and less mineralized than adult bone yet they possess a much thicker periosteum and a remarkable capacity for rapid healing. Unique Biomechanical Properties Slide 5 Pliability and Porosity Children s bones can bend significantly before breaking often leading to incomplete fractures rarely seen in adults. Thick Periosteum The outer covering of the bone is robust in children often keeping displaced fractures contained and providing a massive blood supply for rapid osteoblastic activity. The Physis Growth Plate This is the most critical anatomical distinction. The growth plate is the primary site of longitudinal growth through cellular proliferation and hypertrophy. Clinical Significance Damage to the physis can lead to permanent limb-length discrepancies or angular deformities if the growth is arrested or asymmetrical. --- Pediatric Fracture Patterns Bowing Buckle and Greenstick Slide 10 Because pediatric bones are less dense and more elastic they respond to trauma with patterns that reflect their springy nature. When a child falls on an outstretched hand FOOSH the bone may simply bow without a visible cortical break or it may buckle under the axial load. These fractures often require a high index of clinical suspicion because the radiologic findings can be subtle compared to the dramatic snap seen in adult cortical bone. However the robust blood supply means these injuries often heal in half the time of an equivalent adult injury. Common Patterns Bowing Plastic Fracture The bone bends beyond its limit of elastic recoil but does not break. Torus Buckle Fracture Slide 12 The cortex buckles out under compression. Presentation Common in 5-10 year olds. Management Often just requires splinting; heals in ~2 weeks. Greenstick Fracture Slide 13 Like a young tree branch the bone breaks on one side but remains intact on the other. Management Requires 4-8 weeks of casting due to higher force involved. --- The Salter-Harris Classification Slide 24 Standardized in 1962 the Salter-Harris system is the universal language for describing fractures that involve the growth plate. While these represent a minority of total pediatric fractures they carry the highest risk for long-term morbidity and are a frequent target for board examinations. The system is predicated on where the fracture line travels relative to the physis metaphysis and epiphysis. The higher the number the more likely the joint surface or the regenerative cells of the growth plate are compromised. mnemonic SALTER Classification - S Straight across Type I - Through the physis itself often seen as widening. - A Above Type II - Through the physis and up into the metaphysis. Most Common 75% . - L Lower Type III - Through the physis and down into the epiphysis/joint. Risk of arthritis. - T Through Type IV - Through metaphysis physis and epiphysis. Surgical emergency. - ER ERasure/Crush Type V - Compression of the growth plate. Rare but high-risk for growth arrest. --- Non-Accidental Trauma The Mandatory Reporter s Mandate Slide 31 Orthopedic injuries are frequently the first visible signs of non-accidental trauma NAT or child . As of 2021 over 600 000 children are reported as abused annually in the U.S. with one-third being under the age of two. For a pediatrician a broken bone in a non-ambulatory infant is a massive red flag because breaking infant bone requires significant force. The clinician must maintain a high index of suspicion looking not just at the fracture itself but at the mechanism provided by the caregiver and the presence of other hidden injuries. correlate Non-Accidental Trauma NAT - Pathophysiology Physical force applied intentionally often involving shaking pulling or blunt force. - Presentation Bruises on non-extensor surfaces back torso cigarette burns belt marks or an overly nervous/suspicious parent. - Diagnosis Skeletal survey looking for fractures of different ages CT/MRI for head trauma. - Lecturer s Point Posterior rib fractures are pathognomonic for shaking/squeezing. Coup-contrecoup injuries and retinal hemorrhages define Shaken Baby Syndrome. --- Developmental and Acquired Hip Disorders Slide 42 The pediatric hip is a complex site of development where mechanical instability or vascular compromise can lead to life-altering outcomes. Disorders here are often age-dependent a click in a newborn suggests DDH while a limp in an overweight adolescent points toward SCFE. Understanding the demographics—such as the female predominance in DDH versus the male predominance in LCPD—is essential for rapid narrowing of the differential diagnosis. correlate Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip DDH - Pathophysiology Mechanical instability where the femoral head does not sit tightly in the acetabulum. - Risk Factors Female Firstborn Breech delivery Oligohydramnios. - Diagnosis Barlow dislocating and Ortolani relocating maneuvers; Ultrasound <4-6 months . - Treatment Pavlik harness 6-12 weeks Spica casting. - Lecturer s Point Use the Galeazzi test to check for leg-length discrepancy. correlate Legg-Calve-Perthes Disease LCPD - Pathophysiology Idiopathic avascular necrosis of the femoral head. - Presentation Insidious onset painless or painful limp in children avg age 4-8 . - Diagnosis X-ray showing flattening or fragmentation of the femoral head. - Treatment Weight-bearing limits physical therapy bracing/surgery. - Lecturer s Point 5x more common in males. Presentation includes Trendelenburg gait and loss of internal rotation. correlate Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis SCFE - Pathophysiology The femoral head slips off the neck often compared to ice cream falling off a cone. - Presentation Overweight adolescent in a growth spurt; complains of hip OR knee pain. - Diagnosis Frog-leg lateral X-ray. - Treatment Surgical stabilization with screws. - Lecturer s Point 25% are bilateral. If the patient is a girl think SCFE Skippy over LCPD. --- Foot and Knee Deformities Osgood-Schlatter and Clubfoot Slide 59 Malformations of the lower extremities are among the most common reasons for orthopedic referrals. Osgood-Schlatter disease is the classic growing pain of the active athlete involving the site where the patellar tendon pulls on the tibial tuberosity. In contrast Clubfoot is a congenital deformity that requires immediate intervention following birth to ensure the child can eventually walk with a plantigrade foot. correlate Osgood-Schlatter Disease - Pathophysiology Osteochondritis/apophysitis of the tibial tuberosity from repetitive traction. - Presentation Exquisite point tenderness and swelling on the anterior knee in athletic adolescents. - Management Rest ice NSAIDs. Stopped sport for few weeks . - Lecturer s Point Diagnosis is clinical push on the bump . X-rays are only to rule out tumors/infection if fever/night sweats are present. correlate Clubfoot Talipes Equinovarus - Pathophysiology Congenital deformity involving plantar flexion midfoot adduction and hindfoot inversion. - Management Serial casting Ponseti method started immediately after birth. - Complications Recurrence if parents fail to follow up with physical therapy/stretching. - Lecturer s Point 1 in 1000 births. Must differentiate from Metatarsus Adductus which only requires stretching . --- Bone and Joint Infections The Medical Emergencies Slide 73 Infections of the musculoskeletal system in children are high-stakes scenarios. Bacteria—most commonly Staph aureus —can seed the highly vascular metaphysis of long bones or the synovial space of joints. While Osteomyelitis often presents with localized point tenderness and a grumbling course Septic Arthritis is a true orthopedic emergency that can destroy a joint within hours. The toxic appearance of the child is the primary clinical discriminator used to separate these from the more benign Transient Synovitis. Pathogenesis and Microbiology Routes of Infection Hematogenous spread most common direct inoculation trauma or spread from adjacent tissue. Primary Pathogen Staphylococcus aureus >80% . Special Populations Sickle Cell patients are at unique risk for Salmonella and Pseudomonas . Emerging Pathogens Kingella kingae increasing in incidence . correlate Acute Osteomyelitis - Pathophysiology Hematogenous seeding of the bone metaphysis. - Presentation Limp fever and focal Point Tenderness over the bone. - Diagnosis 2 of Purulence on biopsy + Culture Erythema Positive Imaging . - Treatment 2-6 weeks of IV antibiotics Vanco Clinda or Linezolid to cover MRSA . correlate Septic Arthritis - Pathophysiology Bacterial infection of the joint space. - Presentation Toxic look high fever exquisite pain with any joint movement joint widening on X-ray. - Management Surgical drainage and IV antibiotics. A Medical Emergency . correlate Transient Toxic Synovitis - Pathophysiology Sterile immune-mediated joint inflammation typically following a viral URI or GI illness. - Presentation Ages 3-10; limp but not toxic; normal inflammatory markers WBC CRP ESR . - Management Rest and NSAIDs; usually resolves in 7-10 days. - Lecturer s Point If they don t get better in 24-48 hours reconsider Septic Arthritis. --- Metabolic Bone Disease Rickets Slide 90 Rickets represents a failure of bone mineralization due to a disruption in calcium and phosphate homeostasis. While historically associated with extreme malnutrition it remains relevant today particularly in exclusively breastfed infants without Vitamin D supplementation or those with limited sun exposure. The clinical hallmark is the bowing of weight-bearing bones as the soft unmineralized osteoid fails to support the child s body weight. correlate Rickets - Pathophysiology Vitamin D deficiency nutritional or sun-related leading to low calcium/phosphate and failure to mineralize bone. - Presentation Bowed legs Genu varum Rachitic Rosary costochondral swelling and delayed fontanelle closure. - Diagnosis Low Vitamin D High Alkaline Phosphatase X-ray showing fraying/cupping of metaphyses. - Treatment Vitamin D and Calcium supplementation. --- High Yield Concepts Summary Slide 93 highyield The Physis Priority The epiphyseal growth plate is the single most important structure in pediatric orthopedics. Any injury involving the physis Salter-Harris classification must be managed with extreme care to prevent longitudinal growth arrest. highyield The Toxic Limp When evaluating a child with a limp the first priority is determining if the child is Toxic Septic Arthritis or Non-Toxic Transient Synovitis/LCPD . Fever tachycardia and elevated inflammatory markers CRP/ESR mandate an emergency joint aspiration. questions 1 A 10-month-old infant who is not yet walking is brought to the ED after the mother noticed he was not moving his right arm. An X-ray reveals a transverse humerus fracture. The mother states he rolled off the couch. What is the most appropriate next step in management? Apply a long-arm cast and follow up in 2 weeks Perform a full skeletal survey and notify child protective services Reassure the mother that infant bones are more porous and break easily Test the child for Osteogenesis Imperfecta Check a Vitamin D level to rule out rickets 1 Breaking an infant s bone requires significant force because it is mostly cartilage. A fracture in a non-ambulatory infant especially with an inconsistent mechanism rolled off couch is a massive red flag for non-accidental trauma NAT . Mandatory reporting and a skeletal survey are required. 2 A 13-year-old overweight male presents with a two-week history of dull left knee pain and a limp. Physical exam reveals limited internal rotation of the left hip. What is the most likely diagnosis? Legg-Calve-Perthes Disease Osgood-Schlatter Disease Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis SCFE Septic Arthritis of the knee Transient Synovitis 2 SCFE characteristically presents in overweight adolescents during puberty. It often presents as referred pain to the knee and the classic physical exam finding is loss of internal rotation of the hip. LCPD occurs in younger children avg 4-8 . 3 A 6-year-old male presents with a fracture that extends through the physis and up into the metaphysis of the distal radius. According to the Salter-Harris classification what type of fracture is this? Type I Type II Type III Type IV Type V 1 Salter-Harris Type II involves the Physis and the Metaphysis Above the growth plate . This is the most common type 75% . 4 A 4-year-old girl is brought in for a limp. She had a cold two weeks ago but is otherwise healthy. She is running around the exam room but limps slightly. She is afebrile and her CRP and ESR are within normal limits. What is the best management? Emergency joint aspiration IV Vancomycin for 2 weeks Rest and NSAIDs Surgical stabilization with screws Ponseti serial casting 2 The presentation is classic for Transient Toxic Synovitis a non-toxic child with a limp following a viral illness and normal inflammatory markers. It is treated conservatively with rest and NSAIDs. 5 A newborn is found to have a clunk when the pediatrician abducts the hips and applies anterior pressure to the greater trochanter during the newborn exam. What is the name of this maneuver and the likely diagnosis? Barlow maneuver; Clubfoot Ortolani maneuver; Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip DDH Galeazzi maneuver; SCFE Trendelenburg test; LCPD Ponseti maneuver; Metatarsus adductus 1 The Ortolani maneuver involves relocating a dislocated hip abduction + anterior pressure which produces a clinical clunk. This is the diagnostic exam for DDH in a newborn. Physis The epiphyseal growth plate; the primary site of longitudinal bone growth. Glossary Torus Fracture A buckle fracture of the bone cortex; heals quickly 2 weeks with splinting. Clinical Greenstick Fracture An incomplete fracture where one side of the cortex breaks and the other remains intact. Clinical Salter-Harris Type II Fracture through the physis and metaphysis; the most common type 75% . Concept Pathognomonic NAT signs Posterior rib fractures retinal hemorrhages and coup-contrecoup head injuries. Clinical DDH Risk Factors Female Firstborn Breech delivery Oligohydramnios. Clinical Barlow vs Ortolani Barlow dislocates the hip adduction ; Ortolani relocates it abduction . Clinical SCFE Classic Patient Overweight adolescent with hip/knee pain and loss of internal hip rotation. Concept LCPD Pathophysiology Idiopathic avascular necrosis of the femoral head. Concept Osgood-Schlatter Apophysitis of the tibial tuberosity typically seen in jumping/running athletes. Clinical Clubfoot Treatment Serial casting Ponseti method started immediately after birth. Clinical Septic Arthritis Signs Toxic appearance high fever refusal to move joint joint widening on X-ray. Clinical Rachitic Rosary Knob-like swelling of the costochondral junctions seen in rickets. Clinical Sickle Cell Osteomyelitis Higher risk for Salmonella and Pseudomonas species. Clinical Kingella kingae An emerging pathogen increasingly recognized as a cause of pediatric bone/joint infection. Concept BandB Pediatrics > Orthopedics Pathoma MSK > Bone and Joints FirstAid Musculoskeletal > Pediatric Orthopedics SketchyPath MSK > Bone & Soft Tissue FirstAid Pediatrics > Orthopedics match Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis resources AnKing Step 2 > Pediatrics > Orthopedics > SCFE match Salter-Harris Fracture Classification resources AnKing Step 1 > MSK > Fractures > Salter-Harris match Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip resources AnKing Step 2 > Pediatrics > Orthopedics > DDH match Osgood-Schlatter Disease resources AnKing Step 1 > MSK > Knee > Osgood-Schlatter match Acute Osteomyelitis Pediatrics resources AnKing Step 1 > Microbiology > Staph > Osteomyelitis The Physis Rule Pediatric bones are mostly cartilage; look for widening of the growth plate or step-offs in the periosteum to find subtle fractures. Bilateral SCFE SCFE is bilateral in 25% of cases; always image the contralateral hip even if symptoms are unilateral. Septic Hip Aspiration A high fever and refusal to weight-bear mandate emergency joint aspiration to rule out septic arthritis before joint destruction occurs. NAT Red Flag Any long-bone fracture in a non-walking infant is an automatic red flag for non-accidental trauma until proven otherwise. Clubfoot Urgency Clubfoot treatment serial casting should begin in the first week of life while the neonatal skeleton is most pliable. mindmap Pediatric Orthopedics Pediatric Bone Structure - Cartilage Higher ratio vs adults; buoyant/compressive birth canal . - Physis Growth Plate Site of longitudinal growth proliferation/hypertrophy . - Periosteum Thicker/Stronger; contains displaced fractures; massive blood supply. - Biomechanics Pliable porous less mineralized; rapid healing. Fracture Patterns - Plastic Bowing Bone bends without cortical break. - Torus Buckle Cortical buckling under compression Slide 12 . - Greenstick One side breaks other stays intact Slide 13 . - Salter-Harris Growth Plate Type I S Straight across physis. Type II A Above Physis + Metaphysis - MOST COMMON 75% . Type III L Lower Physis + Epiphysis . Type IV T Through M + P + E . Type V ER ERasure/Crush compression. Non-Accidental Trauma NAT - Epidemiology 600k/year; 1/3 < 2yo; 70% by trusted person. - Signs Bruises torso/back cigarette burns belt marks nervous parent. - X-ray Signs Posterior rib fractures shaking coup-contrecoup head trauma retinal hemorrhage spiral/bucket-handle fractures. Hip Disorders - DDH Females firstborn breech. Barlow Dislocate / Ortolani Relocate . Pavlik harness. - LCPD Avascular necrosis. Males 5x. Insidious limp. X-ray flattening. - SCFE Overweight adolescent. Referred knee pain. Frog-leg X-ray ice cream off cone . Foot & Knee - Osgood-Schlatter Apophysitis of tibial tuberosity. Atheltes. Point tenderness. - Clubfoot Plantar flexion adduction inversion. Serial casting Ponseti . - Metatarsus Adductus Midfoot only; stretching only. Infections - Osteomyelitis Hematogenous; Staph aureus 80% ; metaphysis. IV Abx 2-6 wks . - Septic Arthritis EMERGENCY. Toxic child; refusal to move. Joint widening. - Transient Synovitis Post-viral; non-toxic; normal markers. Rest/NSAIDs. Metabolic - Rickets Vit D deficiency. Bowing Genu varum Rachitic rosary Fraying metaphyses."}, {"id": "l117", "title": "Pharmacology of NSAIDs", "module": "Pharmacology", "content": "id l117 module Pharmacology l117 Pharmacology of NSAIDs lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. session Lecture 117 Pharmacology readingTime 12-15 min pdf content/L117 HighYield Render.pdf analysis content/L117 HighYield Render Analysis.md Lecture 117 Pharmacology of NSAIDs Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. Session Lecture 117 Introduction to the Eicosanoid Pathway Slide 7 Prostanoids are bioactive lipid mediators derived from arachidonic acid that play diverse roles in homeostasis and inflammation. The eicosanoid cascade begins when cell membrane phospholipids are cleaved by phospholipase A2 releasing arachidonic acid which is then processed by cyclooxygenase COX enzymes. Understanding this physiological foundation is essential because NSAIDs exert their therapeutic and toxic effects by blocking these enzymes thus inhibiting the production of prostaglandins thromboxanes and prostacyclins. Arachidonic Acid AA Cascade Source Phospholipids in the cell membrane are converted to AA by Phospholipase A2 . Metabolism AA is metabolized by Cyclooxygenase COX-1 and COX-2 into unstable endoperoxides PGG2 PGH2 . Final Products Specific Isomerases convert endoperoxides into tissue-specific prostanoids PGE2 Involved in fever pain and GI protection. PGI2 Prostacyclin Vasodilator; inhibits platelet aggregation. TXA2 Thromboxane A2 Vasoconstrictor; promotes platelet aggregation Slide 13 . PGF2α Involved in uterine contraction. PGD2 Involved in sleep and mast cell function. Physiological Characteristics Produced in minute quantities by virtually all tissues. Act locally autocrine/paracrine with a very short half-life ~90 seconds . Signal via specific G-protein coupled receptors GPCRs . COX-1 vs. COX-2 Isoenzymes Slide 16 Cyclooxygenase exists in two primary isoforms that differ in their expression patterns and physiological roles. COX-1 is the constitutive housekeeping enzyme responsible for maintaining basic homeostatic functions like gastric mucosal integrity and platelet aggregation. In contrast COX-2 is primarily an inducible enzyme up-regulated during injury or immune response by inflammatory cytokines TNFα IL-1 . However it is vital to recognize that COX-2 is also constitutively expressed in certain critical tissues including the kidney brain and ciliary body which explains why even selective inhibitors carry renal and central nervous system risks. COX-1 Constitutive Housekeeping functions. Present in GI tract cytoprotection Platelets aggregation via TXA2 and Kidney autoregulation . COX-2 Inducible & Constitutive Inducible Up-regulated in inflammatory sites Slide 16 . Constitutive Sites Kidney Medulla and Cortex CNS Ciliary Body of the eye and Female Reproductive tract . Selectivity Some NSAIDs e.g. Celecoxib are designed to target the larger side pocket of the COX-2 catalytic channel to avoid GI toxicity though this selectivity is dose-dependent. highyield The Side Pocket of COX-2 COX-2 selective inhibitors Coxibs are bulky molecules that bind into a distinct side pocket within the COX-2 enzyme that COX-1 lacks. This allows for anti-inflammatory action without disrupting the housekeeping prostaglandins of the GI tract produced by COX-1. However at high doses this selectivity may be lost. NSAID Class Pharmacokinetics Slide 20 NSAIDs are a chemically heterogeneous group of drugs yet they share several key pharmacokinetic properties due to their nature as weak organic acids. Rapid and complete absorption is the norm and their high degree of protein binding—primarily to albumin—is a critical factor in their distribution and potential drug interactions. Their tendency to accumulate at sites of inflammation and in synovial fluid makes them particularly effective for arthritic conditions while their ability to cross the blood-brain barrier facilitates their central analgesic and antipyretic effects. Absorption Rapid and complete; not significantly affected by food. Distribution Highly Protein Bound ~99% Primarily to albumin. Tissue Accumulation Accumulate in synovial fluid Arthritis efficacy and inflammatory sites. CNS Entry Cross the blood-brain barrier to modulate central pain pathways and the hypothalamus fever . Metabolism & Excretion Hepatic Minor substrates of CYP3A4 and CYP2C families; undergo glucuronidation. Enterohepatic Circulation Contributes to prolonged half-lives in some agents. Renal Excretion Metabolites and small amounts of unchanged drug are excreted via the kidneys. Half-Life Variations Short < 6 hrs Ibuprofen Diclofenac Aspirin. Long > 10 hrs Naproxen Meloxicam Celecoxib. Therapeutic Actions and Class Effects Slide 24 The primary therapeutic goals of NSAID therapy are the management of pain fever and inflammation. These effects are mediated through the inhibition of COX-2-derived prostaglandins which sensitize peripheral nociceptors and mediate the hypothalamic set-point for body temperature. While all NSAIDs are roughly equivalent in their analgesic and antipyretic efficacy at therapeutic doses the choice of agent is often dictated by the patient s individual risk profile the desired duration of action and the specific clinical context such as the need for cardiovascular prophylaxis or closure of a patent ductus arteriosus. Analgesia Peripheral Reduces prostaglandin sensitization of nociceptors to mediators like bradykinin. Central Modulates descending pain pathways in the CNS. Antipyretic Blocks PGE2 synthesis in the hypothalamus to reset the thermal set-point. Anti-inflammatory Inhibits recruitment of inflammatory cells and release of mediators. Special Uses Ophthalmic Topical drops for ocular inflammation Slide 24 . Niacin Flushing Aspirin can block PGD2-mediated flushing caused by high-dose Niacin Slide 367 Lecturer Emphasis . Ductus Arteriosus Used to close a Patent Ductus Arteriosus PDA in neonates Indomethacin is classic . correlate Patent Ductus Arteriosus PDA - Pathophysiology Prostaglandins PGE2 normally maintain the patency of the ductus arteriosus in utero to bypass pulmonary circulation. - Presentation If the ductus remains open after birth it can lead to heart failure and pulmonary hypertension. - Treatment NSAIDs e.g. Indomethacin Ibuprofen are used to inhibit PG synthesis and induce closure. - Lecturer s Point Maternal use of NSAIDs in the 3rd trimester can cause premature closure of the PDA in utero leading to fetal hydrops Slide 50 . Adverse Effects The Gastrointestinal System Slide 41 The most frequent site of NSAID-induced injury is the gastrointestinal tract ranging from minor dyspepsia to life-threatening perforation. This toxicity stems primarily from the inhibition of COX-1-derived PGE2 and PGI2 which are vital for maintaining the gastric mucosal barrier. These prostaglandins normally stimulate the secretion of protective mucus and bicarbonate maintain mucosal blood flow and suppress gastric acid production. By removing these defenses NSAIDs leave the mucosa vulnerable to acid-pepsin injury and impair the repair mechanisms required to heal existing erosions. Mechanism Loss of cytoprotective PGE2/PGI2 COX-1 mediated . Spectrum of Injury Dyspepsia gastric/duodenal ulcers GI bleeding and perforation. Increased Risk Factors History of peptic ulcer disease. H. pylori infection Lecturer Emphasis . Concurrent use of Glucocorticoids impair repair Slide 49 . Concurrent use of Anticoagulants increase bleeding risk . Alcohol consumption direct mucosal irritant . correlate Helicobacter pylori Infection - Pathophysiology Gram-negative bacterium that colonizes the gastric mucosa causing chronic inflammation and weakening defenses. - Risk Factors Significantly increases the risk of peptic ulcers when combined with NSAID use. - Lecturer Point Patients with suspected H. pylori or active ulcers should avoid NSAIDs to prevent catastrophic GI bleeding Slide 426 . Adverse Effects Cardiovascular and Hematologic Slide 33 42 The cardiovascular safety of NSAIDs is governed by a delicate balance between Thromboxane A2 TXA2 produced by platelets and Prostacyclin PGI2 produced by the vascular endothelium. While TXA2 promotes platelet aggregation and vasoconstriction PGI2 acts as a counter-regulatory vasodilator and inhibitor of aggregation. Non-selective NSAIDs and COX-2 selective inhibitors can disrupt this balance toward a pro-thrombotic state. Aspirin is the notable exception; at low doses its irreversible inhibition of platelet COX-1 provides unique cardioprotective effects because mature platelets cannot resynthesize the enzyme unlike vascular endothelial cells. Prothrombotic Risk MI and Stroke Mechanism Preferential inhibition of endothelial PGI2 COX-2 over platelet TXA2 COX-1 . nsNSAIDs COX-2 is more sensitive to inhibition than platelet COX-1 in systemic circulation. COX-2 Selective Only block PGI2 leaving TXA2 unopposed. Bleeding Risk Inhibition of platelet TXA2 synthesis reduces platelet plug formation. Contraindication Perioperative CABG surgery US Boxed Warning due to high thrombotic risk Slide 35 . highyield Aspirin The Irreversible Exception Aspirin is the only NSAID that irreversibly acetylates a serine residue in the COX catalytic channel. Because mature platelets lack a nucleus they cannot resynthesize COX-1 and the antiplatelet effect lasts the life of the platelet 8-10 days . In contrast other NSAIDs are reversible competitive inhibitors Slide 33 . Adverse Effects Renal and Electrolytes Slide 38 47 In the healthy individual prostaglandins play a minimal role in renal hemodynamics; however they become vital emergency regulators in states of reduced effective circulating volume. When the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system RAAS is activated PGE2 and PGI2 are produced to maintain afferent arteriolar vasodilation and preserve the glomerular filtration rate GFR . NSAIDs disrupt this autoregulatory mechanism leading to afferent vasoconstriction acute kidney injury AKI and significant sodium and water retention. Mechanism of AKI Normal Auto-regulation PGs dilate the afferent arteriole ; Angiotensin II constricts the efferent arteriole to maintain GFR. NSAID Effect Blocks afferent vasodilation → reduced RBF and GFR Slide 38 . Electrolyte Disturbances Sodium/Water Retention Reduced PG-mediated excretion leads to peripheral edema and worsened Hypertension or Heart Failure. Hyperkalemia PGs normally facilitate potassium excretion; inhibition leads to K+ reabsorption risking cardiac arrhythmias . correlate The Triple Whammy - Mechanism Concurrent use of NSAIDs ACE Inhibitors/ARBs and Diuretics . - Pathophysiology Diuretics/ACEi reduce volume; ACEi/ARBs dilate the efferent arteriole; NSAIDs constricts the afferent arteriole. - Outcome The combined effect severely drops glomerular pressure frequently precipitating Acute Kidney Injury AKI . - Lecturer s Point Avoid this combination in elderly patients or those with heart failure/CKD Slide 47 735 . Special Populations Pregnancy and Lactation Slide 50 NSAID use during pregnancy requires extreme caution due to specific risks to fetal renal and cardiovascular development. While not strongly linked to congenital malformations in the first trimester regular use after 20 weeks gestation can impair fetal renal function leading to a critical reduction in amniotic fluid—a condition known as oligohydramnios. Furthermore use in the third trimester risks the premature closure of the patent ductus arteriosus which is maintained by prostaglandin activity potentially resulting in irreversible fetal pulmonary hypertension and hydrops fetalis. Oligohydramnios Beginning 20 weeks Amniotic fluid is primarily fetal urine. NSAID-induced fetal renal dysfunction → ↓ urine production → oligohydramnios Slide 910 . PDA Closure Beginning 30 weeks Premature closure in utero → pulmonary hypertension and right-sided heart failure Fetal Hydrops . Recommendation Generally avoid all NSAIDs from 20 weeks gestation onward. Low-dose aspirin may be used if the CV benefits outweigh the risks but only under close supervision. Drug-Drug Interactions Slide 46-49 The pharmacologic profile of NSAIDs predisposes them to numerous clinically significant drug interactions. Many of these are pharmacodynamic involving synergistic toxicities with other agents that affect bleeding renal function or mucosal integrity. For instance the use of SSRIs can potentiate NSAID-induced bleeding by depleting platelet serotonin stores which are necessary for aggregation. Additionally NSAIDs can interfere with the renal clearance of drugs with narrow therapeutic windows such as Lithium leading to potentially fatal toxicity levels. Anticoagulants/Antiplatelets Synergistic risk of major bleeding. SSRIs Block serotonin reuptake into platelets → ↓ serotonin release during activation → ↓ aggregation → Increased Bleeding Risk Slide 49 . ACE Inhibitors/ARBs Increased risk of hyperkalemia and AKI. Diuretics Blunted diuretic and antihypertensive efficacy. Lithium NSAIDs increase renal reabsorption of Lithium leading to toxicity Slide 49 . Glucocorticoids Synergistic GI mucosal damage and impaired healing. mnemonic NSAIDs Toxicity G-R-C-H-A - G GI Ulcers/Bleeding - R Renal AKI Na+ retention K+ retention - C Cardiovascular MI Stroke risk - except Aspirin - H Hematologic Bleeding risk - A Asthma Aspirin-induced questions 1 A 72-year-old male with a history of congestive heart failure and chronic kidney disease is prescribed a loop diuretic and an ACE inhibitor. He presents to the clinic with a 3-day history of severe knee pain and admits to taking high doses of over-the-counter ibuprofen. Laboratory tests reveal a significantly elevated serum creatinine. Which of the following mechanisms best explains his acute injury? Efferent arteriolar vasoconstriction Afferent arteriolar vasoconstriction Directly toxic effect on the renal tubular cells Competitive inhibition of the loop diuretic Unopposed action of Prostacyclin in the medulla 1 NSAIDs inhibit the synthesis of PGE2 and PGI2 which are the primary mediators of afferent arteriolar vasodilation in volume-depleted or RAS-activated states like CHF and CKD . This leads to unopposed afferent vasoconstriction reduced glomerular filtration pressure and AKI. ACE inhibitors simultaneously dilate the efferent arteriole further dropping filtration pressure The Triple Whammy . 2 A 32-year-old pregnant woman in her 32nd week of gestation is advised to avoid taking ibuprofen for her back pain. What is the primary fetal risk associated with NSAID use during this specific stage of pregnancy? Neural tube defects Premature labor Premature closure of the ductus arteriosus Spontaneous abortion Fetal macrosomia 2 Prostaglandins are required to keep the ductus arteriosus open in utero. Inhibition of PT synthesis by NSAIDs in the 3rd trimester especially after 30 weeks can cause its premature closure leading to fetal pulmonary hypertension and hydrops fetalis. 3 Why does low-dose aspirin 81 mg provide cardiovascular protection while other non-selective NSAIDs like naproxen may increase the risk of myocardial infarction? Aspirin is selective for COX-2 in the endothelium. Naproxen has a shorter half-life than aspirin. Aspirin irreversibly inhibits platelet COX-1. Naproxen does not reach the portal circulation. Aspirin stimulates the production of Prostacyclin. 2 Aspirin irreversibly acetylates COX-1 in platelets. Since platelets lack nuclei and cannot resynthesize the enzyme the anti-thrombotic effect decreased TXA2 lasts for the life of the platelet 8-10 days . Non-selective NSAIDs are reversible inhibitors and tend to inhibit endothelial PGI2 anti-thrombotic more effectively than platelet TXA2 in systemic circulation potentially shifting the balance toward thrombosis. 4 A patient taking fluoxetine an SSRI for depression is started on an NSAID for osteoarthritis. Which of the following adverse developments should the clinician monitor most closely? Serotonin syndrome Increased risk of gastric perforation Excessive platelet aggregation Petechiae and mucosal bleeding Hypertensive crisis 3 SSRIs inhibit the reuptake of serotonin into platelets. Serotonin is a key mediator required for platelet aggregation. When combined with NSAIDs which inhibit TXA2 there is a synergistic reduction in platelet function and a significantly increased risk of bleeding. 5 Which of the following patients is at the highest risk for developing an NSAID-induced peptic ulcer? A 25-year-old with a history of asthma. A 45-year-old with a high-protein diet. A 60-year-old patient who is also taking prednisone. A 30-year-old using a COX-2 selective inhibitor. A 50-year-old with primary hypertension controlled by an ARB. 2 Glucocorticoids like prednisone do not cause ulcers on their own but significantly increase the risk of GI injury when combined with NSAIDs by impairing the mucosal repair and turnover mechanisms. Advanced age is also an independent risk factor for NSAID-induced GI toxicity. What is the primary source of arachidonic acid in the cell? Cell membrane phospholipids released by Phospholipase A2. Concept Which prostanoid is primarily responsible for promoting platelet aggregation? Thromboxane A2 TXA2 . Concept What is the key mechanism of Aspirin s unique antiplatelet effect? Irreversible acetylation of a serine residue in the COX-1 enzyme. Mechanism Why are COX-2 selective inhibitors associated with an increased CV risk? They inhibit endothelial PGI2 anti-thrombotic without inhibiting platelet TXA2 pro-thrombotic . Mechanism What is the Triple Whammy regarding renal function? NSAIDs constrict afferent + ACEi/ARBs dilate efferent + Diuretics volume depletion . Clinical Define oligohydramnios in the context of maternal NSAID use. Too little amniotic fluid due to fetal renal dysfunction occurring after 20 weeks gestation. Clinical Which GI protection mechanism is lost with COX-1 inhibition? Secretion of mucus and bicarbonate mucosal blood flow and reduction of gastric acid. Concept What is the classic clinical use of Indomethacin in neonates? Closure of a Patent Ductus Arteriosus PDA . Clinical Why should NSAIDs be avoided in patients with hepatic cirrhosis? Increased risk of AKI due to dependence on PG-mediated renal blood flow in cirrhotic states. Clinical What is the effect of NSAID use on serum potassium? Hyperkalemia increased reabsorption which increases the risk of arrhythmias. Clinical BandB Immunology > Pharm > Glucocorticoids & NSAIDs SketchyPharm Blood & Inflammation > Anti-inflammatory > NSAIDs FirstAid Musculoskeletal > Pharm > Arachidonic Acid Pathway Pathoma Hemostasis > Primary Hemostasis > Aspirin match Aspirin mechanism resources Irreversibly inactivates COX via acetylation; impairs platelet aggregation TXA2 decrease . match NSAID renal side effects resources Decreased GFR via afferent arteriole constriction; Na+ and H2O retention. match COX-2 selective inhibitors resources Celecoxib; binds COX-2 side pocket; higher CV risk but lower GI risk. match Aspirin-induced asthma resources Shifting AA metabolism to leukotriene pathway; bronchoconstriction. The 20-Week Rule Avoid NSAIDs after 20 weeks gestation to prevent fetal oligohydramnios. The 30-Week Rule Avoid NSAIDs after 30 weeks gestation to prevent premature closure of the fetal ductus arteriosus. Aspirin & Platelets Aspirin s antiplatelet effect is permanent for the life of the platelet 8-10 days due to irreversible COX-1 inhibition. The AKI Prereq Prostaglandins are essentially emergency vasodilators; they are most vital for kidney function in volume-depleted or heart failure states. SSRI Synergism SSRIs increase NSAID bleeding risk by depleting platelet serotonin stores. mindmap Pharmacology of NSAIDs Prostanoid Pathway - Arachidonic Acid Source Phospholipid cell membrane via Phospholipase A2 - COX Enzymes COX-1 Constitutive/Housekeeping vs. COX-2 Inducible/Inflammatory - Key Products PGE2 GI cytoprotection Fever Pain sensitization TXA2 Platelet aggregation Vasoconstriction PGI2 Anti-aggregation Vasodilation NSAID Class PK - Absorption Rapid/Complete; Weak acids pKa 3-5 - Distribution 99% Albumin bound; Synovial fluid accumulation - Metabolism Hepatic CYPs ; Enterohepatic circulation Adverse Effects - GI Ulcers Perforation Dyspepsia COX-1 loss - CV MI and Stroke risk PGI2/TXA2 imbalance ; BBW for CABG - Renal AKI Afferent constriction ; Hyperkalemia; Hypertensive blunting - Pregnancy Oligohydramnios >20w ; PDA closure >30w Aspirin The Outlier - Mechanism Irreversible acetylation of Serine residue - Dose-Effect Low dose Anti-platelet ; High dose Anti-inflammatory Drug Interactions - The Triple Whammy NSAIDs + ACEi/ARBs + Diuretics - Bleeding Synergism SSRIs Warfarin Anticoagulants - Narrow Window Drugs Lithium reabsorption increases"}, {"id": "l118", "title": "Pharmacology of NSAIDs Part 2", "module": "Pharmacology", "content": "id l118 module Pharmacology l118 Pharmacology of NSAIDs Part 2 lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. session Lecture 118 Pharmacology readingTime 15-18 min pdf content/L118 HighYield Render.pdf analysis content/L118 HighYield Render Analysis.md Lecture 118 Pharmacology of NSAIDs Part 2 Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. Session Lecture 118 Aspirin ASA The Irreversible Inhibitor Slides 29-34 Aspirin acetylsalicylic acid is unique among NSAIDs due to its mechanism of irreversible acetylation of the cyclooxygenase COX enzyme. By covalently modifying a serine residue in the catalytic channel Aspirin permanently disables both COX-1 and COX-2. This effect is most profound in platelets which lack a nucleus and cannot resynthesize new enzymes. Consequently the antiplatelet effect of a single dose of Aspirin persists for the entire 8–10 day lifespan of the platelet. Dose-Dependent Effects Low Dose <325 mg/day Primary use is antiplatelet cardioprotection . It effectively inhibits platelet TXA2 without significantly suppressing endothelial PGI2 throughout the systemic circulation. Intermediate Dose 325–2400 mg/day Used for analgesia and antipyresis. High Dose 2400–4000 mg/day Required for potent anti-inflammatory effects though often limited by toxicity. Cardioprotective Balance Aspirin reduces the risk of myocardial infarction and stroke by shifting the balance towards anti-thrombosis blocking TXA2 while endothelial PGI2 recovers due to enzyme resynthesis in nucleated endothelial cells . Salicylate Toxicity and Management Slides 35-38 Toxicity from Aspirin salicylism presents with a classic progression of symptoms and acid-base disturbances. Early signs include tinnitus ringing in the ears dizziness and hearing loss. Acid-Base Disturbance 1. Stage 1 Respiratory Alkalosis Salicylates directly stimulate the medullary respiratory center causing hyperventilation and a drop in PCO2. 2. Stage 2 Metabolic Acidosis Salicylates uncouple oxidative phosphorylation leading to the accumulation of organic acids lactic acid ketoacids . This result is a High Anion Gap Metabolic Acidosis HAGMA . Clinical Pearl The most common finding in adults is a mixed respiratory alkalosis and metabolic acidosis. Management Ion Trapping Salicylic acid is a weak organic acid pKa 3.5 . By administering Sodium Bicarbonate to alkalinize the urine pH > 7.5 the drug becomes ionized Sal- and trapped in the renal tubules preventing reabsorption and facilitating excretion. highyield Reye Syndrome Reye syndrome is a rare but life-threatening condition characterized by fulminant hepatic failure microvesicular steatosis and encephalopathy . It is strongly associated with Aspirin use in children during a viral illness specifically Influenza or Varicella Chickenpox . For this reason Aspirin is strictly contraindicated in the pediatric population; Acetaminophen or Ibuprofen are the preferred alternatives for fever. Aspirin-Exacerbated Respiratory Disease AERD Slide 47 AERD also known as the Aspirin Triad consists of Asthma Nasal Polyps and Rhinosinusitis . In susceptible individuals blocking the COX pathway shunts arachidonic acid towards the lipoxygenase LOX pathway. This leads to an overproduction of leukotrienes LTC4 LTD4 LTE4 which mediate intense bronchoconstriction and nasal inflammation. Propionic Acid Derivatives Ibuprofen and Naproxen Slides 55-60 These are the most commonly prescribed non-selective NSAIDs nsNSAIDs . They are reversible competitive inhibitors of COX. Ibuprofen Short half-life ~2 hours . Available intravenously for the closure of a Patent Ductus Arteriosus PDA in neonates. Naproxen Notable for a significantly longer half-life ~12–17 hours allowing for convenient twice-daily dosing. Drug Interaction with Aspirin If a patient takes ibuprofen before aspirin the ibuprofen can competitively bind to the COX-1 active site preventing aspirin from irreversibly acetylating it. This eliminates the cardioprotective antiplatelet effect of aspirin. Guidance Patients should take Aspirin at least 30-60 minutes before taking other nsNSAIDs. Acetic Acid Derivatives Indomethacin Ketorolac and Diclofenac Slides 61-70 Indomethacin A very potent NSAID. Uses Acute gouty arthritis closure of PDA and as a tocolytic to stop preterm labor . Adverse Effects High incidence of CNS effects including severe frontal headaches dizziness and confusion. Ketorolac Toradol A potent systemic analgesic with efficacy similar to morphine for acute pain. Limitation Carries an extremely high risk of gastrointestinal bleeding and renal toxicity. Its use is strictly limited to a maximum of 5 days . Diclofenac Shows a slight relative preference for COX-2. Hepatotoxicity Uniquely associated with significant elevations in serum aminotransferases ; monitoring of LFTs is recommended for long-term use. Selective COX-2 Inhibitors Celecoxib Slides 80-85 Celecoxib is designed to target the larger side pocket of the COX-2 enzyme while sparing the narrower COX-1 channel. Benefit Significantly reduced incidence of gastrointestinal ulcers and bleeding compared to nsNSAIDs. Risk Increased risk of cardiovascular thrombotic events MI and stroke . This occurs because it inhibits endothelial PGI2 anti-thrombotic but does NOT inhibit platelet TXA2 pro-thrombotic shifting the physiological balance toward clotting. Contraindication Patients with Sulfonamide allergy as Celecoxib contains a sulfa moiety. Acetaminophen Tylenol Paracetamol Slides 90-100 Acetaminophen is an effective analgesic and antipyretic but lacks significant anti-inflammatory or anti-platelet activity . Metabolism and Toxicity Normally metabolized via glucuronidation and sulfation. A minor portion is converted by CYP2E1 into NAPQI a highly reactive and toxic metabolite. Glutathione normally detoxifies NAPQI. In overdose glutathione is depleted and NAPQI causes central lobular hepatic necrosis . Antidote N-acetylcysteine NAC . It acts by restoring glutathione stores and directly binding to and detoxifying NAPQI. correlate Kawasaki Disease Kawasaki disease is one of the few pediatric exceptions where high-dose Aspirin is used. It is a medium-vessel vasculitis in children that specifically risks the development of coronary artery aneurysms . Aspirin is used here for its both anti-inflammatory and anti-platelet properties to prevent thrombus formation within the aneurysms. questions 1 A 65-year-old male with a history of a recent myocardial infarction is prescribed low-dose aspirin and ibuprofen for osteoarthritis. He is advised to space the timing of these medications. Which of the following is the most appropriate dosing instruction to ensure aspirin maintains its antiplatelet effect? Take ibuprofen 30 minutes before taking aspirin. Take aspirin at least 30-60 minutes before taking ibuprofen. Take both medications simultaneously with food. Discontinue the aspirin as ibuprofen provides equivalent cardioprotection. Take aspirin only once every 8-10 days. 1 Ibuprofen is a reversible inhibitor that competitively blocks the COX-1 binding site. If taken first it prevents aspirin from irreversibly acetylating the enzyme. Aspirin must be taken first at least 30-60 minutes prior to allow it to permanently modify the enzyme before ibuprofen competes for the site. 2 A 24-year-old patient is brought to the emergency department after an intentional aspirin overdose. Arterial blood gas results show a pH of 7.42 a low PCO2 and a low bicarbonate level. The patient also reports persistent ringing in the ears . Which of the following is the most appropriate initial pharmacological treatment to facilitate drug excretion? Ammonium chloride N-acetylcysteine Sodium bicarbonate Activated charcoal only Hemodialysis 2 Salicylic acid is a weak acid pKa 3.5 . Alkalinization of the urine with Sodium Bicarbonate increases the fraction of the drug that is in its ionized form Sal- within the renal tubules. The ionized drug cannot be reabsorbed ion trapping thereby significantly increasing the rate of renal excretion. 3 A 6-year-old boy presents with nausea vomiting and confusion after recovering from a recent flu-like illness. Physical examination reveals hepatomegaly without jaundice. Laboratory studies show elevated AST/ALT and hyperammonemia. A liver biopsy shows microvesicular steatosis. Which of the following medications likely contributed to this presentation? Acetaminophen Ibuprofen Aspirin Indomethacin Celecoxib 2 This case describes Reye syndrome encephalopathy and microvesicular fatty liver which is triggered by using aspirin in children with viral infections like Influenza or Varicella. Acetaminophen and Ibuprofen are the safe alternatives in this population. 4 A 45-year-old patient with a history of severe post-operative pain has been receiving parenteral ketorolac in the hospital for 4 days. Why must the clinician discontinue or switch this medication to another agent tomorrow? Risk of tachyphylaxis and loss of efficacy. Increased risk of serious GI bleeding and renal failure. Potential for serotonin syndrome. Risk of hepatotoxicity serum transaminase elevation . Development of tolerance similar to opioids. 1 Ketorolac is a highly potent analgesic but carries an exceptionally high risk of gastrointestinal bleeding ulceration and acute kidney injury compared to other NSAIDs. Clinical guidelines and the FDA strictly limit its systemic use to a maximum of 5 days. 5 A patient is admitted for an acetaminophen overdose. The mechanism of hepatotoxicity in this patient involves the depletion of which of the following substances? CYP2E1 enzyme Glutathione N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine NAPQI Vitamin K Glucuronic acid 1 In acetaminophen overdose the normal glucuronidation and sulfation pathways are saturated. Increased amounts are metabolized by CYP2E1 to the toxic metabolite NAPQI. NAPQI is normally detoxified by Glutathione; once Glutathione stores are depleted NAPQI causes direct central lobular hepatic necrosis. What is the unique mechanism of Aspirin compared to other NSAIDs? Irreversible acetylation of COX-1 and COX-2. Mechanism Why does the antiplatelet effect of Aspirin last 8-10 days? Platelets lack a nucleus and cannot resynthesize COX-1 after irreversible inhibition. Mechanism What is the primary treatment for salicylate toxicity Salicylism ? Alkalinization of urine with Sodium Bicarbonate Ion Trapping . Clinical What are the components of the Aspirin Triad AERD ? Asthma Nasal Polyps and Aspirin/NSAID sensitivity. Clinical Why is Aspirin contraindicated in children with viral illnesses? Risk of Reye Syndrome fatty liver and encephalopathy . Clinical What is the maximum duration for systemic Ketorolac use? 5 days due to high GI and renal risk . Clinical Which NSAID is specifically used for acute gout and PDA closure? Indomethacin. Clinical Why does Celecoxib increase CV risk but decrease GI risk? Inhibits PGI2 endothelium but not TXA2 platelets favoring thrombosis; spares GI protective COX-1. Mechanism Which medication is used as the antidote for Acetaminophen overdose? N-acetylcysteine restores glutathione . Clinical How should Aspirin be dosed relative to Ibuprofen? Aspirin should be taken at least 30-60 minutes BEFORE ibuprofen. Clinical SketchyPharm Blood & Inflammation > Anti-inflammatory > NSAIDs Physeo Pharm > MSK > Aspirin FirstAid MSK > Pharm > Aspirin match Aspirin ASA resources AK Step1 v11 Physeo 09 Pharm 09 Musculoskeletal 02 Aspirin AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 04 Pharm 03 Aspirin match Acetaminophen resources AK Step1 v11 Physeo 09 Pharm 09 Musculoskeletal 03 Acetaminophen match NSAIDs & Celecoxib resources AK Step1 v11 SketchyPharm 03 Blood & Inflammation 03 Anti-inflammatory 01 NSAIDs AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Musculoskeletal Skin Connective Tissue 04 Pharm 04 Celecoxib The Salicylism Triad Tinnitus Hyperventilation Respiratory Alkalosis and Metabolic Acidosis. Ion Trapping Use bicarbonate to treat Aspirin overdose; an alkaline urine keeps salicylic acid ionized and trapped for excretion. ASA/NSAID Interference Always dose Aspirin FIRST. Reversible NSAIDs can block the binding of irreversible Aspirin if taken beforehand. The 5-Day Rule Limit Ketorolac use to 5 days to prevent severe GI bleeding and renal failure. NAPQI & NAC Acetaminophen toxicity is caused by NAPQI. N-acetylcysteine NAC restores glutathione to detoxify it. mindmap NSAIDs Part 2 Aspirin - Mechanism Irreversible acetylation - Duration Life of platelet 8-10 days - Toxicity Tinnitus acid-base disturbances Salicylism - Contraindications Children Reye Syndrome - Liver/Brain Specific NSAIDs - Propionic Acids Ibuprofen PDA Naproxen Long half-life - Acetic Acids Indomethacin Gout PDA Ketorolac 5-day limit Diclofenac Hepatotoxicity - Enolic Acids Meloxicam once daily Selective COX-2 Celecoxib - Structure Sulfonamide Sulfa allergy - Mechanism Bind side pocket of COX-2 - Pros/Cons Lower GI risk Higher CV risk Acetaminophen - Role No anti-inflammatory/anti-platelet effect - Overdose NAPQI metabolite depletes glutathione - Antidote N-acetylcysteine NAC"}, {"id": "l119", "title": "Innate Immunity Part 1", "module": "Unknown", "content": "id l119 module Unknown l119 Innate Immunity Part 1 lecturer T. Slieman Ph.D. session Lecture 119 Immunology readingTime 15-20 min pdf content/L119 HighYield Render.pdf analysis content/L119 HighYield Render Analysis.md Lecture 119 Innate Immunity Part 1 Lecturer T. Slieman Ph.D. Session Lecture 119 Overview of the Immune System Slide 2-5 The human immune system is broadly divided into two interlinked branches the innate immune response and the adaptive immune response. The innate system serves as the body’s instantaneous non-specific first and second lines of defense. Contrastingly the adaptive system constitutes the highly specific third line of defense that develops over days to weeks generating specialized T-lymphocytes B-lymphocytes and long-lasting memory. The innate system does not have memory; it mounts the exact same response upon repeated exposure to a pathogen using germline-encoded receptors to rapidly hold the fort and prevent disseminated infection until the adaptive system can mount a targeted strike. Core Differences Time Course Innate is immediate minutes to hours ; Adaptive is delayed days to weeks . Specificity Innate recognizes conserved generic molecular patterns PAMPs ; Adaptive recognizes specific antigenic epitopes. Memory Innate possesses no immunological memory; Adaptive confers life-long immunity to re-exposure. Components Innate Physical barriers phagocytes neutrophils macrophages NK cells complement proteins and cytokines. Adaptive T-cells cell-mediated and B-cells/Antibodies humoral . The First Line of Defense Barriers Slide 6-9 Before a pathogen can even trigger a cellular immune response it must bypass the body s mechanical and chemical barricades. These barriers make up the first line of defense constantly preventing the colonization and systemic entry of ubiquitous environmental microbes. The sheer effectiveness of these basic barriers is evident when they fail. Mechanical and Physical Barriers Intact Epithelium The tightly packed squamous cells of the epidermis form a rugged impenetrable armor. Simply breaching this barrier is fatal; burn victims are incredibly susceptible to systemic sepsis specifically because they lose this primary mechanical shield. Mucociliary Escalator In the respiratory tract goblet cells produce sticky mucus that traps inhaled pathogens. Ciliated epithelial cells then consistently sweep this contaminated mucus upward toward the pharynx to be swallowed and destroyed by stomach acid or coughed out. Flushing Mechanisms Tears saliva and urine constantly wash away microbes preventing adherence to the mucosal surfaces. Chemical and Biological Barriers Acidic Microenvironments The low pH of the stomach pH 1-3 skin pH 5.5 and vagina pH 4.5 creates a hostile environment that severely stunts bacterial growth. Microcidal Molecules Lysozyme Found in tears and saliva this enzyme continuously cleaves the peptidoglycan backbone NAG-NAM bonds of bacterial cell walls leading to osmotic lysis. Defensins & Cathelicidins Short cationic antimicrobial peptides that insert themselves into and disrupt microbial membranes. The Microbiome Biological Barrier The trillions of commensal bacteria residing on our skin and within our GI tract outcompete pathogenic invaders for space and nutrients. Furthermore metabolic byproducts from the microbiome modulate the host s immune tone. correlate Microbiome Dysbiosis and Systemic Disease - Pathophysiology Disruption of the normal flora dysbiosis —often through the overuse of broad-spectrum antibiotics or poor diet—can lead to unchecked proliferation of opportunistic pathogens e.g. Clostridioides difficile . - Systemic Implications The lecturer emphasized that the microbiome s influence extends deeply into systemic physiology. An imbalanced microbiome is now heavily implicated not just in local GI diseases but in widespread systemic and neurologic disorders notably playing a role in the pathogenesis of Parkinson s Disease . - Lecturer s Point Always weigh the necessity of antibiotics as collateral damage to the biological barrier can trigger devastating downstream effects. The Second Line of Defense Inflammation Slide 10-15 If a pathogen successfully breaches the first-line barriers e.g. via a laceration or puncture the second line of defense is triggered. The hallmark of the second line of defense is Inflammation a localized tissue response aimed at containing the destructive agent and preparing the tissue for repair. The Cardinal Signs of Inflammation The classic clinical signs of local inflammation driven entirely by vasodilation and increased vascular permeability are memorized by their Latin roots 1. Rubor Redness Caused by arteriolar vasodilation increasing local blood flow. 2. Calor Heat Also caused by increased blood flow and local metabolic activity. 3. Tumor Swelling Edema resulting from fluid and plasma proteins leaking into the interstitial space due to increased microvascular permeability. 4. Dolor Pain Edema placing physical pressure on nerve endings combined with chemical irritation from inflammatory mediators like bradykinin and prostaglandins. 5. Functio Laesa Loss of Function A conscious and reflexive protective mechanism due to pain and swelling. Macrophages as the Sentinels The initial inflammatory cascade is centrally coordinated by tissue-resident Macrophages . These cells are the vigilant generals sitting in the peripheral tissues. Recognition and Alarm Macrophages use specialized receptors to detect signs of damage or microbial invasion. Upon detection they rapidly secrete potent pro-inflammatory cytokines Tumor Necrosis Factor alpha TNF-α Interleukin-1 IL-1 and Interferon-gamma IFN-γ . The Master Switch NF-κB These cytokines act on local host cells by activating NF-κB . NF-κB is a critical transcription factor described as the master switch of the immune system. Once activated NF-κB translocates to the nucleus to rapidly transcribe a massive array of inflammatory genes amplifying the local response. Systemic Effects of Cytokines TNF-α and IL-1 don t just act locally; they enter the bloodstream to cause systemic whole-body effects. Fever IL-1 acts on the hypothalamus as an endogenous pyrogen raising the body s set-point temperature to inhibit bacterial replication. Leukocyte Recruitment TNF-α strongly upregulates adhesion molecules on the vascular endothelium promoting the capture and migration of neutrophils. Hemostasis TNF-α shifts the local endothelium to a pro-thrombotic state intentionally creating micro-clots to wall off the infection and prevent systemic dissemination. Soluble Defense The Complement System Slide 24-29 The innate immune system employs a lethal self-amplifying cascade of approximately 16 circulating plasma proteins known as the Complement System . These proteins continuously circulate in an inactive zymogen state. When triggered they rapidly undergo sequential cleavage and activation to clear pathogenic threats. The system can be initiated via three distinct pathways. The Pathways of Activation Regardless of how it starts all three pathways converge on the creation of the C3 Convertase the most pivotal enzyme in the cascade that cleaves the central C3 protein. 1. The Classical Pathway Traditionally considered part of the adaptive response since it requires antibodies this pathway is triggered when the C1 complex binds to Antibody-Antigen complexes . Specifically it requires either one IgM pentamer or two closely spaced IgG molecules. Therefore IgM is significantly more efficient at activating complement. This pathway uniquely requires Calcium Ca2+ for the C1q-r-s complex assembly. 2. The Lectin Pathway An entirely innate mechanism. It is activated when circulating Mannan-binding lectin MBL binds to repeating mannose or N-acetylglucosamine NAG residues on the surface of fungi bacteria and some viruses. 3. The Alternative Pathway Also entirely innate. This pathway operates in a constant state of low-level spontaneous activation often called tick-over . Normally host cells immediately degrade these active rogue proteins. However if the proteins land on foreign unprotected microbial surfaces e.g. bacterial LPS the pathway rapid amplifies. This pathway specifically requires Magnesium Mg2+ and Factor B . The Four Main Functions of Complement Once the C3 convertase has cleaved C3 into C3a and C3b the cascade executes four essential defensive maneuvers Opsonization C3b and C4b covalently bind to the bacterial surface acting as bright eat me tags for phagocytes. Opsonization is absolutely critical for the clearance of heavily encapsulated bacteria e.g. Streptococcus pneumoniae Neisseria meningitidis which otherwise repel phagocytes. Inflammation Anaphylatoxins The small cleavage fragments C3a and C5a are potent chemoattractants and anaphylatoxins. They recruit neutrophils to the site of infection and trigger mast cells to degranulate releasing histamine and intensely driving up vascular permeability. Lysis The MAC C5b recruits C6 C7 C8 and many molecules of C9 to form the Membrane Attack Complex MAC . This structure inserts a literal pore into the bacterial cell membrane destroying its osmotic integrity and lysing the cell especially critical for fighting Gram-negative bacteria like Neisseria . Immune Complex Clearance Complement binds to circulating antibody-antigen complexes allowing them to be recognized and safely cleared by erythrocytes transporting them to the spleen. correlate Paroxysmal Nocturnal Hemoglobinuria PNH - Pathophysiology The complement alternative pathway is constantly ticking over meaning host cells must actively defend themselves from friendly fire. They do this by expressing GPI-anchored regulatory proteins like DAF Decay-Accelerating Factor / CD55 and MAC-inhibitory protein CD59 on their membranes. PNH is an acquired genetic mutation in the PIGA gene leading to the loss of these protective GPI anchors. - Presentation Without CD55/CD59 shielding the host s red blood cells they are destroyed by the alternative complement pathway MAC lysis . This leads to episodic hemolytic anemia thrombosis and the classic sign of dark hemoglobin-rich urine notably typically seen in the morning upon waking nocturnal hemoglobinuria . - Diagnosis & Treatment Diagnosed via flow cytometry absence of CD55/CD59 . Treated with Eculizumab a terminal complement inhibitor . questions 1 A 45-year-old male burn victim hospitalized for extensive third-degree burns over 40% of his body surface area becomes medically unstable with a high fever and hypotension. Blood cultures grow Pseudomonas aeruginosa. His rapid clinical deterioration is primarily attributed to the loss of which branch of his immunological defense? The cellular adaptive immune response The mechanical component of the first line of defense The soluble complement cascade The phagocytic second line of defense The regulatory action of Natural Killer cells 1 The intact epithelium of the skin serves as an immensely powerful mechanical barrier against environmental pathogens functioning as the crucial first line of defense. Burn victims lose this barrier making them highly susceptible to disseminated life-threatening infections from opportunistic organisms like Pseudomonas. 2 While investigating a patient with recurrent bacterial rhinosinusitis and pneumonia a physician orders functional assays of the patient s immune system. Results indicate a severe defect in the activation of the Master Switch transcription factor responsible for the massive upregulation of inflammatory and immune-related genes following macrophage signaling. Which molecule is defective in this patient? Bradykinin Interleukin-1 IL-1 Interferon-gamma IFN-γ C3 Convertase NF-κB 4 NF-κB Nuclear Factor kappa-light-chain-enhancer of activated B cells is widely recognized as the master switch of the immune system. Upon stimulation by cytokines like TNF-α and IL-1 it translocates to the nucleus to rapidly switch on the transcription of hundreds of genes crucial for the inflammatory response. 3 An immunology researcher is running in vitro experiments to optimize the activation of the Classical Complement Pathway to study its lytic effects on target cells. She prepares a specialized buffer solution for the assay. For the C1q-C1r-C1s complex to assemble and successfully initiate this specific pathway which of the following ions MUST be present in her buffer? Magnesium Mg2+ Calcium Ca2+ Potassium K+ Iron Fe2+ Zinc Zn2+ 1 The Classical Pathway strictly relies on Calcium Ca2+ for the initial structural assembly of the C1 complex holding the C1r and C1s subunits onto the C1q backbone . Conversely the Alternative Pathway requires Magnesium Mg2+ to function. 4 A 30-year-old woman presents to her primary care physician with fatigue and episodes of passing very dark urine predominantly in the mornings. Flow cytometry of her red blood cells reveals an absence of CD55 and CD59 on the cellular surface. This patient s hematologic destruction is directly mediated by an inability to regulate which of the following processes? Macrophage phagocytosis Spontaneous alternative complement pathway activation Antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity ADCC T-cell mediated apoptosis Pathogen opsonization via the lectin pathway 1 The patient has Paroxysmal Nocturnal Hemoglobinuria PNH . RBCs rely on DAF CD55 and MAC-inhibitory protein CD59 to protect themselves from the alternative complement pathway which runs in a constant state of low-level spontaneous activation tick-over . Without these regulators host cells are subjected to unregulated complement-mediated lysis. 5 A massive influx of neutrophils is noted in the purulent sputum of a patient infected with a heavily encapsulated strain of Streptococcus pneumoniae. To effectively engulf and destroy this pathogen the neutrophils rely on the host s complement system to physically tag the thick bacterial capsule. Which complement fragment is primarily responsible for performing this critical function? C5a C3b C5b-9 MAC C3a Factor B 1 C3b along with C4b acts as the primary opsonin in the complement cascade. It covalently binds to the outer surface of pathogens allowing the complement receptors natively present on phagocytes to grab onto the slick protective capsules and initiate phagocytosis. What is the primary function of Lysozyme and where is it found? Found in tears and saliva; it s a microcidal enzyme that cleaves the peptidoglycan cell walls of bacteria. First Line Defense What are the 5 cardinal signs of inflammation in Latin? Rubor Redness Calor Heat Tumor Swelling Dolor Pain and Functio Laesa Loss of Function . Inflammation Which transcription factor acts as the master switch for the inflammatory immune response? NF-κB. Concept Which pro-inflammatory cytokine is a potent endogenous pyrogen that causes fever? Interleukin-1 IL-1 . Cytokines Which immunoglobulin class is most efficient at activating the Classical Complement pathway? IgM due to its large pentameric structure providing multiple binding sites for the C1 complex . Complement What specific ion is required for the assembly of the C1 complex in the Classical pathway? Calcium Ca2+ . Complement Which complement pathway is constantly active in a state of low-level spontaneous activation tick-over ? The Alternative Pathway. Complement What is the primary function of the complement fragments C3a and C5a? Anaphylatoxins They trigger mast cell degranulation and serve as potent chemoattractants to recruit neutrophils. Complement What is the key molecular defect in Paroxysmal Nocturnal Hemoglobinuria PNH ? Loss of GPI anchors resulting in a deficiency of CD55 DAF and CD59 on the surface of red blood cells. Clinical Which portion of the complement system directly punches holes pores into Gram-negative bacterial membranes? The Membrane Attack Complex MAC comprised of C5b-9. Complement B&B Immunology > Innate Immunity > Complement System FirstAid Immunology > Complement SketchyIM Innate Immunity > Complement Pathoma Inflammation and Healing > Inflammation match Complement System Pathways resources AK Step1 v11 B&B 14 Immunology 01 Innate Immunity 01 Complement System AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 12 Immunology 02 Innate Immunity 02 Complement match Paroxysmal Nocturnal Hemoglobinuria PNH resources AK Step1 v11 Pathoma 05 RBC Disorders 03 Normocytic Anemia Extravascular Hemolysis PNH AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 08 Hematology Oncology 04 Pathology 13 Nonhemolytic Normocytic Anemias PNH match Acute Inflammation & Cardinal Signs resources AK Step1 v11 Pathoma 02 Inflammation and Healing 01 Introduction to Inflammation AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 03 Pathology 01 Inflammation 01 Acute Inflammation The Master Switch NF-κB immediately translocates to the nucleus to trigger the vast array of systemic inflammatory genes required for the second line of defense. Opsonization is Essential Un-opsonized encapsulated organisms are inherently invisible or repulsive to phagocytes. C3b tagging is absolute critical for fighting organisms like Strep. pneumo and N. meningitidis. The Constant Tick-Over The Alternative Pathway doesn t need to be consciously triggered ; it is always running in the background. If a rogue C3b lands on host cells DAF/CD55 rapidly deactivates it. If it lands on unprotected pathogen LPS it amplifies instantly. Microbiome as a Defense Mechanism Never underestimate the power of the microbiome. It occupies real estate on mucosal surfaces completely blocking pathogenic adherence through sheer competitive saturation at the first line of defense. mindmap Innate Immunity Part 1 First Line of Defense - Physical Barriers Skin epithelium mucosal membranes mucociliary escalator - Chemical Barriers - Gastric/Vaginal/Skin pH - Lysozyme cleaves peptidoglycan - Defensins & Cathelicidins - Biological Barriers The normal microbiome competes for space/nutrients; linked to systemic health/Parkinson s Second Line of Defense - Cardinal Signs Calor Dolor Rubor Tumor Loss of Function - Macrophages The primary tissue sentinels overseeing inflammation - Cytokines Secrete TNF-alpha IL-1 endogenous pyrogen IFN-gamma - NF-κB The Master Switch; activated by cytokines to upregulate immune genes The Complement System - Pathways of Activation - Classical Relies on Antibody-Antigen complexes IgM > IgG ; requires Ca2+ - Lectin Relies on Mannan-Binding Lectin MBL connecting to microbial sugars - Alternative Spontaneous tick-over activation on foreign surfaces LPS ; requires Mg2+ - Effector Functions - Opsonization C3b/C4b tag encapsulated bacteria - Inflammation C3a/C5a act as anaphylatoxins mast cells chemoattractants - Lysis MAC C5b-9 punches holes in Gram-negative bacteria - Regulation Error PNH - Defect Loss of GPI anchor leading to absence of CD55 and CD59 - Result RBCs destroyed by tick-over alternative pathway Nocturnal Hemoglobinuria"}, {"id": "l120", "title": "Innate Immunity Part 2", "module": "Unknown", "content": "id l120 module Unknown l120 Innate Immunity Part 2 lecturer T. Slieman Ph.D. session Lecture 120 Immunology readingTime 15-20 min pdf content/L120 HighYield Render.pdf analysis content/L120 HighYield Render Analysis.md Lecture 120 Innate Immunity Part 2 Lecturer T. Slieman Ph.D. Session Lecture 120 Pattern Recognition Receptors PRRs Slide 17-21 The innate immune system is able to rapidly identify hostile invaders because it is hard-wired with a set of germline-encoded sensors known as Pattern Recognition Receptors PRRs . Unlike the B and T cell receptors of the adaptive immune system—which undergo complex and time-consuming V D J recombination to recognize infinitely variable specific antigens—PRRs are identical across all individuals of a species. They are evolutionary relics designed to recognize generic highly conserved microbial structures essential for pathogen survival collectively termed Pathogen-Associated Molecular Patterns PAMPs . Examples of PAMPs include bacterial lipopolysaccharide LPS peptidoglycan and viral double-stranded RNA. Toll-Like Receptors TLRs The most extensively studied class of PRRs are the Toll-Like Receptors TLRs . Found abundantly on macrophages dendritic cells and mast cells TLRs serve as the primary alarm system for the innate immune response. Structurally they are transmembrane proteins. Notably they are not only found on the outer cell surface to detect extracellular bacteria but also lining endocytic vesicles to detect intracellular viruses that have been engulfed . Activation Profile Binding of a PAMP to its specific TLR physically activates the phagocyte turning it into a professional Antigen Presenting Cell APC . Crucially TLR activation aggressively drives the transcription of NF-κB the master switch that unleashes a storm of pro-inflammatory cytokines. TLR3 and HSV Different TLRs recognize different threats. TLR3 specifically identifies the double-stranded RNA typically produced during viral replication. A genetic deficiency in TLR3 renders an individual extraordinarily susceptible to aggressive infections by the Herpes Simplex Virus HSV frequently leading to devastating HSV encephalitis. TLR7 and HIV TLR7 recognizes single-stranded viral RNA. Clinical data shows that in patients struggling with HIV a functional limitation or polymorphism in TLR7 is heavily correlated with soaring viral loads an inability to mount an effective initial innate defense and significantly accelerated disease progression from HIV into frank AIDS. The Cytosolic Receptors NLRs and RLHs Slide 40-42 While TLRs patrol the cell surface and endosomal compartments the actual cytoplasm of the cell is guarded by specialized intracellular PRRs. These are essential for detecting viruses that have bypassed the endosomes or intracellular bacteria that have escaped into the cytosol. RLHs Retinoic acid-inducible gene I-like helicases These are the primary cytosolic sensors for viral double-stranded RNA dsRNA . Upon detection RLHs rapidly trigger the cell to produce massive amounts of Type I Interferons IFN-α and IFN-β which shut down local cellular protein synthesis to halt viral replication. NOD-Like Receptors NLRs and The Inflammasome NLRs are cytosolic receptors that primarily recognize bacterial peptidoglycan fragments. When an NLR binds its specific PAMP it physically oligomerizes with other intracellular proteins to form a massive multi-protein complex called the Inflammasome . The inflammasome essentially acts as a molecular executioner; it activates the enzyme Caspase-1 . Active Caspase-1 rapidly cleaves inactive pro-IL-1β into mature highly inflammatory IL-1β and simultaneously triggers a fiery highly inflammatory form of programmed cell death known as pyroptosis . By sacrificing itself through pyroptosis the infected cell deliberately triggers a massive local inflammatory response to warn neighboring cells and attract phagocytes to clean up the liberated intracellular bacteria. correlate NOD Polymorphisms and Chronic Inflammatory Disease - Pathophysiology Because the Inflammasome built by NLRs is so profoundly pro-inflammatory its activity must be tightly regulated. Genetic variants polymorphisms in the genes coding for NOD receptors lead to a trigger-happy innate immune system that constantly inappropriately senses PAMPs—even highly benign ones from the normal gut flora. - Presentation Individuals bearing these NOD polymorphisms suffer from a generalized poorly regulated hyper-inflammatory state. Clinically this manifests as significantly increased incidences of Atopic eczema severe Asthma chronically elevated IgE levels triggering rampant allergies and most notably an extensively documented astronomical increase in the risk of developing Crohn s disease . - Lecturer s Point Paradoxically because their innate sensors are constantly misfiring rather than effectively targeting true threats these individuals are simultaneously more prone to experiencing recurrent unresolving true bacterial infections. Cellular Sentinels Neutrophils vs. Macrophages Slide 43-48 Both Neutrophils and Macrophages are primary amoeboid phagocytes but they operate under vastly different tactical doctrines. The lecturer explicitly contrasted these two cells emphasizing that understanding their unique roles is critical to grasping both acute and chronic infectious physiology. Neutrophils The Shock Troops Neutrophils Polymorphonuclear leukocytes or PMNs are the most abundant white blood cell. They are specifically designated for the acute response . Lifespan Extremely short-lived hours to days . They rapidly enter the tissue engage the enemy and die in mass quantities forming the bulk of clinical pus . Microbicidal Arsenal Neutrophils are inherently highly cytotoxic. Nearly 50% of the contents of their dense intracellular granules consist of defensins —peptides that aggressively punch holes in bacterial membranes. The Oxidative Burst Upon engulfing a pathogen neutrophils furiously consume immense amounts of oxygen to rapidly generate highly toxic superoxide radicals via the NADPH oxidase enzyme complex. This immediate oxidative burst is their primary killing mechanism. Because they are the first responders they do not require complex prior activation commands to begin killing. Macrophages The Generals Macrophages derived from circulating monocytes are the long-term residents of the tissue. They are designated for chronic inflammation and cellular cleanup. Lifespan Long-lived months to years allowing them to persistently hold ground in the tissue. Recruitment Command While inherently less acutely cytotoxic than a fresh neutrophil their primary role is orchestration. Upon detecting a pathogen via their PRRs macrophages secrete powerful chemokines most notably Interleukin-8 IL-8 which serves as a powerful magnet to continuously pull fresh neutrophils out of the bloodstream and into the battlefield. Specific Activation Requirement To reach their full microbicidal angry potential macrophages require a specific two-key activation sequence. They must first detect a PAMP via their PRRs and secondly they must receive the cytokine Interferon-gamma IFN-γ secreted by Th1 cells or NK cells. Once fully activated they become ferocious eaters and highly competent Antigen Presenting Cells APCs . Primary Immunodeficiencies of the Innate System Slide 50-53 Defects in the phagocytic machinery or the complement cascade present with a stark inability to clear common ubiquitous environmental pathogens. Understanding the specific molecular failure dictates the clinical presentation. Phagocyte Disorders Chediak-Higashi Syndrome An autosomal recessive genetic defect leading to a systemic failure in microtubule polymerization. Without functional microtubules cellular trafficking grinds to a halt. Specifically in phagocytes the phagosome containing the engulfed bacteria physically cannot traffic to fuse with the destructive lysosome failure of phagosome-lysosome fusion . The cell continues to accumulate these disconnected organelles leading to the pathognomonic finding on a blood smear massive giant fused granules inside the cytoplasm. Patients suffer from recurrent pyogenic infections partial albinism due to failed melanin trafficking and progressive neurologic abnormalities. Chronic Granulomatous Disease CGD An extremely high-yield disorder caused by a genetic deficiency in the NADPH oxidase enzyme complex located on the phagolysosomal membrane. Without this enzyme neutrophils absolutely cannot perform their signature oxidative burst to generate superoxide radicals. While they can engulf bacteria they cannot quickly destroy them. This defect specifically renders the patient highly susceptible to catalase-positive organisms classically Staphylococcus aureus Pseudomonas Serratia Nocardia and Aspergillus species . Because the neutrophils fail to clear the inciting pathogen the body s only remaining strategy is to constantly wall off the unresolved infections with macrophages forming extensive chronic granulomas everywhere in the body. Leukocyte Adhesion Deficiency LAD-1 and LAD-2 Driven by an absence of the crucial beta-integrin adhesion molecules specifically CD18 on the surface of neutrophils. Without integrins the neutrophils cannot lock onto the vascular endothelium. They can roll along the vessel wall but they cannot firmly attach and undergo diapedesis to squeeze into the infected tissue. Blood tests paradoxically reveal a massive neutrophilia since the cells are all helplessly trapped in the circulation while infected wounds are completely devoid of neutrophils resulting in absent pus formation and severely delayed umbilical cord separation. IFN-γ Receptor Deficiency A severe defect where macrophages lack the receptor to hear the activating call from Th1 cells or NK cells. Because the macrophages can never achieve full activation the patient suffers from rampant severe and often fatal infections from otherwise low-virulence atypical mycobacterial species. highyield Complement System Deficiencies The presentation of a complement deficiency heavily depends on exactly which link in the chain is broken. They do not all present as simple bacterial susceptibilities. 1. Early and Alternative Pathway Defects Defects in MBL or the Alternative pathway the early innate triggers generally result in a broad non-specific increase in susceptibility to early childhood infections. 2. C3 Deficiency C3 is the central convergence point for all three compliment pathways. Losing C3 disables almost the entire system. Because C3b is the primary opsonin these patients present with severe devastating and recurrent pyogenic infections caused predominantly by encapsulated bacteria such as S. pneumoniae . 3. Classical Pathway Deficiencies C1 C2 & C4 Remember that the early classical pathway components uniquely function to clear out spent antibody-antigen immune complexes from the circulation. When these are deficient the immune complexes build up and indiscriminately crash out into the body s capillary beds especially in the kidneys and skin . This manifests as a classic Type III hypersensitivity disease heavily mimicking the symptoms of Systemic Lupus Erythematosus SLE e.g. photosensitive rash arthritis and glomerulonephritis . 4. C1 Inhibitor Deficiency C1-INH normally acts as the brakes on the complement system and the related kallikrein-kinin system. Without these brakes Kallikrein runs unchecked generating massive inappropriate amounts of Bradykinin . This causes a rare but extremely dangerous condition called Hereditary Angioedema . Patients suffer from unpredictable episodic massive swelling edema of the face limbs GI tract and crucially the larynx. Noticeably because this is driven by bradykinin and not histamine this severe swelling is completely completely painless and non-pruritic no hives . questions 1 A 25-year-old female presents to the emergency department with profound rapid-onset swelling of her lips tongue and eyelids. She denies any pain itching or recent exposure to known allergens and physical examination reveals a complete absence of urticaria hives . Her mother reportedly suffered from similar episodes one of which resulted in a near-fatal airway obstruction. Based on the most likely diagnosis which specific physiological regulator is deficient in this patient? C3 Convertase NADPH Oxidase C1 Inhibitor C1-INH Beta-integrins Decay-Accelerating Factor CD55 3 The patient s presentation of massive painless non-pruritic swelling angioedema without hives combined with a strong family history is the classic hallmark of Hereditary Angioedema. This is caused by an inherited deficiency in C1-INH. Without C1-INH the kallikrein pathway operates unregulated leading to a massive overproduction of the potent vasodilator bradykinin which drives the severe plasma leakage and ensuing edema. 2 A 3-year-old boy is brought to the pediatrician for evaluation of recurrent severe cutaneous and deep abscesses. Culture of the latest abscess grows Aspergillus fumigatus. Utilizing a Nitroblue Tetrazolium NBT test the physician determines that the patient s neutrophils are completely incapable of mounting an oxidative burst. Which crucial component of the innate immune response is fundamentally defective in this child? Phagosome-lysosome fusion Firm adhesion to the vascular endothelium via integrins Superoxide radical production via NADPH oxidase Opsonization of encapsulated organisms via C3b Activation of the Inflammasome via NOD-like receptors 3 The patient is suffering from Chronic Granulomatous Disease CGD . CGD is caused by a genetic defect in the NADPH oxidase enzyme complex completely disabling the neutrophil s ability to generate the superoxide radicals required for the rapid oxidative burst killing mechanism. Consequently patients are uniquely susceptible to chronic infections by catalase-positive organisms like Aspergillus and Staphylococcus aureus. 3 A 19-year-old male is admitted to the intensive care unit with a devastating case of Herpes Simplex Virus HSV encephalitis. His medical history is notable for multiple prior unusually severe HSV outbreaks since childhood yet he mounts perfectly normal responses to bacterial and fungal challenges. A targeted genetic panel indicates a defect in a specific class of Pattern Recognition Receptors PRRs . Which of the following receptors is most likely dysfunctional in this patient? Toll-Like Receptor 3 TLR3 Toll-Like Receptor 4 TLR4 NOD-Like Receptor NLR Interferon-gamma Receptor Killer Inhibition Receptor KIR 1 Toll-Like Receptors recognize highly specific microbial molecular patterns. TLR3 is an endosomally located receptor that explicitly senses the double-stranded RNA naturally produced during viral replication. A specific isolated genetic deficiency in TLR3 renders patients uniquely remarkably susceptible to runaway infections with the Herpes Simplex Virus classically presenting as life-threatening HSV encephalitis. 4 A patient is evaluated for chronic severe gastrointestinal inflammation. A detailed family history reveals several relatives suffering from unremitting atopic eczema and profound persistent asthma. Genetic testing on the patient indicates the presence of an activating polymorphism in a specific cytosolic receptor family. This patient is at a significantly elevated risk for developing which of the following gastrointestinal conditions? Celiac Disease Peptic Ulcer Disease Crohn s Disease Familial Adenomatous Polyposis Ulcerative Colitis 3 Polymorphisms in the genes coding for cytosolic NOD-Like Receptors NLRs lead to a hyperactive dysregulated inflammasome response. This persistent inappropriate state of baseline inflammation drives a significantly higher incidence of severe allergic conditions Asthma Eczema and is one of the most firmly established high-yield genetic risk factors for the development of Crohn s disease. 5 A 4-year-old child presents with partial albinism progressive peripheral neuropathy and recurrent debilitating pyogenic skin infections. A peripheral blood smear demonstrates massive giant granules clustered within the cytoplasm of all the patient s neutrophils. The underlying molecular defect in this syndrome primarily prevents which of the following cellular processes? Phagosome-lysosome fusion Neutrophil diapedesis across the endothelium Transcription of NF-κB The oxidative burst mechanism Clearance of immune complexes by the spleen 1 This array of symptoms—partial albinism neuropathy susceptibility to pyogenic infections and the pathognomonic finding of massive giant granules on a blood smear—unmistakably points to Chediak-Higashi Syndrome. The core pathology is a microtubule polymerization defect that physically stalls all intracellular vesicular trafficking. Thus the phagosome containing the engulfed bacterium is physically incapable of trafficking to and fusing with the destructive lysosome. Which specific Toll-Like Receptor TLR detects double-stranded viral RNA and what disease does its deficiency cause? TLR3. Deficiency leads to severe susceptibility to Herpes Simplex Virus HSV encephalitis. Clinical What massive intracellular multi-protein complex is formed by NOD-Like Receptors NLRs to activate Caspase-1? The Inflammasome. Concept Genetic polymorphisms in NOD receptors strongly predispose patients to which specific Inflammatory Bowel Disease? Crohn s Disease. Clinical Which innate cell functions primarily as the shock troops during an acute response and contains granules comprised of 50% defensins? Neutrophils. Cells What specific enzyme complex is genetically deficient in Chronic Granulomatous Disease CGD ? The NADPH Oxidase enzyme complex. Clinical Lack of functional microtubules leading to absent phagosome-lysosome fusion is the hallmark of which disease? Chediak-Higashi Syndrome. Clinical What is the classic presentation of a patient with a Beta-Integrin defect Leukocyte Adhesion Deficiency ? Recurrent infections with absolutely no pus formation delayed umbilical cord separation and a paradoxically massive neutrophilia on blood work. Clinical Deficiencies in the early classical complement pathway C1 C2 C4 result in a failure to clear immune complexes. What is the classic clinical presentation of this? A severe Type III Hypersensitivity reaction resembling Systemic Lupus Erythematosus SLE . Complement Hereditary Angioedema is caused by unregulated bradykinin production resulting from a deficiency in what protein? C1 Inhibitor C1-INH . Clinical Unlike an allergic reaction what two classic clinical features are notably ABSENT during an attack of Hereditary Angioedema? Pain and Urticaria Hives . The swelling is completely painless and non-pruritic. Clinical B&B Immunology > Immunodeficiency > Phagocyte Disorders FirstAid Immunology > Immunodeficiencies Pathoma White Blood Cell Disorders > Leukopenia & Leukocytosis SketchyPath Immunology > Primary Immunodeficiencies Physeo Immunology > Immunodeficiencies match Chronic Granulomatous Disease & LAD resources AK Step1 v11 B&B 14 Immunology 05 Immunodeficiency 03 Phagocyte Disorders AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 12 Immunology 05 Immunodeficiency 06 Phagocyte Disorders match Chediak-Higashi Syndrome resources AK Step1 v11 Pathoma 02 Inflammation and Healing 01 Introduction to Inflammation Chediak-Higashi Syndrome match Toll-Like Receptors & Inflammasome resources AK Step1 v11 B&B 14 Immunology 01 Innate Immunity 02 Cellular Response match Hereditary Angioedema C1-INH Deficiency resources AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 12 Immunology 05 Immunodeficiency 05 Complement Disorders The NLR/Crohn s Connection Always link NOD polymorphisms hyperactive inflammasome with an astronomical risk of developing Crohn s disease Asthma and Atopic eczema. The Pus Contradiction in LAD In Leukocyte Adhesion Deficiency LAD the blood will show an incredibly high neutrophil count because they are trapped in the vasculature. The infected tissue itself will have zero pus because the neutrophils cannot undergo diapedesis to reach the physical battlefield. CGD and Catalase-Positive Bugs Patients with Chronic Granulomatous Disease are distinctly susceptible to catalase-positive organisms Staph aureus Pseudomonas Serratia Nocardia Aspergillus because these specific bugs destroy the tiny amount of self-produced hydrogen peroxide the neutrophil might have otherwise hijacked. The Paradox of Hereditary Angioedema In a patient presenting with terrifying sudden facial swelling the absence of hives urticaria and pain heavily points to Hereditary Angioedema C1-INH deficiency / Bradykinin excess rather than a classic histamine-driven anaphylactic reaction. mindmap Innate Immunity Part 2 Pattern Recognition Receptors PRRs - Toll-Like Receptors TLRs Surface and endosomal sensors that activate NF-κB - TLR3 Detects dsRNA; deficiency leads to HSV Encephalitis - TLR7 Linked to HIV viral load control - Cytosolic Receptors - RLHs Sensors that trigger Type I Interferons to halt viral replication - NOD-Like Receptors NLRs Form the Inflammasome Caspase-1 -> IL-1β & Pyroptosis - Polymorphisms Asthma Eczema massively increased Crohn s Disease risk Neutrophils vs. Macrophages - Neutrophils The Shock Troops - Short-lived acute response - High defensin concentration - Primary weapon The immediate NADPH Oxidase burst - Macrophages The Generals - Long-lived chronic responders - Primary weapon Secreting IL-8 chemokines to summon neutrophils - Require a 2-key activation PAMP + IFN-γ to achieve full microbicidal strength Primary Immunodeficiencies - Phagocyte Disorders - Chediak-Higashi Microtubule defect -> Failed phagosome-lysosome fusion -> Giant granules & Albinism - Chronic Granulomatous Disease CGD NADPH Oxidase defect -> Failed oxidative burst -> Susceptible to catalase-positive organisms S. aureus Aspergillus - LAD Leukocyte Adhesion Deficiency Beta-integrin defect -> Failed diapedesis -> Massive neutrophilia but zero pus - IFN-γ Receptor Deficiency Severe atypical mycobacterial infections - Complement Disorders - C3 Recurrent severe encapsulated bacterial infections - C1 C2 C4 Early Classical Failed clearance of immune complexes -> SLE/Lupus-like Type III hypersensitivity - C1 Inhibitor C1-INH Unregulated Kallikrein/Bradykinin -> Hereditary Angioedema painless swelling without hives"}, {"id": "l121", "title": "DPR: History and Physical Exam of the Spine", "module": "OMM", "content": "id l121 module OMM l121 DPR History and Physical Exam of the Spine lecturer M. Heller D.O. session Lecture 121 DPR readingTime 12-18 min pdf content/L121 HighYield Render.pdf analysis content/L121 HighYield Render Analysis.md Lecture 121 DPR History and Physical Exam of the Spine Lecturer M. Heller D.O. Session Lecture 121 The Subjective History and Subjective Exam Slide 2-15 The foundation of a proper physical examination lies in a highly detailed systematic subjective history. Before palpating the spine or conducting specialized maneuvers the physician must pinpoint whether the pain is mechanical inflammatory radicular or systemic. Distinguishing between acute weekend-warrior injuries and chronicity sets the tone for your diagnostic workup. Chief Complaint Dissection The OPQRST The subjective section is your standard documentation of the patient s complaints what they tell you . Use OPQRST to determine exact pain locations and characteristics. Locating the Pain Joint pain must be categorized as articular inside the joint or extra-articular outside the joint . You must define it as focal/localized suggesting simple acute trauma/injury versus diffuse suggesting systemic arthritis or widespread inflammatory change . Timing & Context Acute vs. Chronic A sudden basketball injury versus unremitting pain for 6 months points toward completely different sets of etiologies. Did the patient start a new activity after 20 years of being sedentary? Overuse injuries are incredibly common in these contexts. Associated Symptoms The presence of systemic symptoms like fevers chills or night sweats heavily implies an infectious or systemic process over simple mechanical back pain. Radicular Pain Inquire directly about pain radiating into the arms cervical or the buttocks/legs lumbar . Assess for associated neurological deficits like paresthesias numbness/tingling or weakness. Red Flag Symptoms Lecturer Emphasis You must always explicitly rule out red flag symptoms that suggest an immediate medical emergency. Cauda Equina Warning Signs Always ask the patient about new-onset bowel or bladder dysfunction incontinence urinary retention . Check for saddle anesthesia numbness on the medial aspect of the upper thighs/inner groin area and progressive lower extremity weakness. If present these suggest compression of the sacral nerve roots and require emergent imaging and decompression. Malignancy or Infection Back pain presenting in an individual younger than 20 or older than 50 with a prior history of cancer long-term steroid use IV drug use or HIV/Immunodeficiency is an immediate red flag. Unexplained Weight Loss or pain that heavily increases at night or with rest is highly suspicious for malignancy or serious infection. The Objective Exam Inspection Palpation and ROM Slide 16-25 Following a deep historical review you enter the objective physical exam. This must systematically proceed from Inspection to Palpation to Active/Passive Range of Motion ROM and finally to Special Testing. 1. Inspection The most commonly skipped step by students. Have the patient undress the relevant area. Tell the patient exactly what you are doing e.g. I am looking at your back to assess muscle symmetry . Look for obvious deformities muscle atrophy localized swelling erythema ecchymosis bruising or skin conditions. Observe the natural spinal curvature cervical lordosis thoracic kyphosis lumbar lordosis . Posture A significant cause of neck pain today is poor digital ergonomics. The head should sit directly above the shoulders; forward head posture where the head translates anteriorly due to phone usage places excessive strain on the cervical musculature. 2. Palpation and ROM Palpation Always palpate using the pads of your fingers or thumbs along the spinous processes searching for point tenderness or step-offs which indicate severe ligamentous injury spondylolisthesis or fracture . Note muscle hypotonicity spasm or somatic dysfunction using an erythema or skin drag test . Active vs Passive ROM Active Patient performs the movement. Tests the muscle tissue and their genuine functional capacity. Resisted Patient performs an isometric contraction against your resistance. Evaluates specifically for muscular weakness or tendinopathy. Cervical Spine Note 50% of flexion/extension originates at the Occipitoatlantal OA joint. 50% of rotation originates at the Atlantoaxial AA joint. The remaining segments handle the other 50% and primary lateral side-bending. Cervical Spine Special Testing and Neurovascular Screen Slide 26-40 If the patient s pain has a radicular component a comprehensive neurological exam Motor Sensory DTRs is mandatory to map the injury to a specific nerve root level. mnemonic Lower Extremity Nerve Root Mapping - L4 L4 Kick the Door Ankle Dorsiflexion medial aspect sensation Patellar reflex . - L5 Nothing for L5 Great toe dorsiflexion dorsal foot sensation no specific reflex . - S1 Achilles Tendon reflection Plantar flexion lateral foot sensation. - C7 Lecturer s Point Giving the bird to a terrible driver in New York requires extending the elbow and flexing the wrist—this is powered by the C7 nerve root triceps reflex . Cervical Impingement Special Tests Lhermitte s Sign Barber s Chair Phenomenon The seated patient actively flexes their head forward. A positive test produces a sharp electric shock sensation radiating down the spine or into the limbs. Highly indicative of cervical spinal stenosis multiple sclerosis MS lesion or significant cord compression. Cervical Axial Compression Test Push directly downward on the patient s head. The added pressure narrows the neuroforamina. If it reproduces radicular arm pain it indicates Cervical Radiculopathy or nerve root impingement. Do NOT apply heavy force if the patient is already in significant pain. Cervical Distraction Test Lifting upwards under the chin and occiput pulling the head up . This widens the neuroforamina. A positive test is indicated by the reduction or relief of radicular pain confirming an underlying impingement. Spurling s Test Extending the neck side-bending and rotating towards the affected side followed by a slight downward compressive force. This maximally closes down the neuroforamen on that specific side. Exacerbating radicular arm symptoms confirms a cervical radiculopathy. highyield NEXUS Criteria vs. Canadian C-Spine Rules Two distinct heavily validated decision aids used to clinically clear an acute trauma patient from requiring a cervical spine CT scan 1. NEXUS Criteria You can safely clear the C-spine no imaging ONLY if the patient matches NONE of the five criteria - No neurological deficit - No spinal midline tenderness - No altered mental status - No severe intoxication - No distracting painful injury e.g. a massive femur fracture drowning out the localized neck pain . 2. Canadian C-Spine Rule Evaluates trauma via risk factors. If High Risk parameters are present Age >65 dangerous mechanism paresthesias imaging is mandated. If only Low Risk factors exist e.g. simple rear-end MVA falling from sitting the patient must demonstrate the ability to actively rotate their neck 45 degrees left and right to be fully cleared. Thoracic and Lumbar Spine Special Testing Slide 45-65 The same algorithmic process of inspection palpation active ROM and neurological screening applies to the lower spine followed by specific provocative tests mapping to classic pathologies. Thoracic tests Adam s Forward Bend Test Primarily screens for scoliosis. The patient bends forward at the waist. You observe the posterior lung fields and rib cage from behind. A unilateral rib hump indicates scoliosis driven by rotational deformation of the vertebrae. Remember The spinous process points towards the concavity of the curve while the vertebral body and ribs rotate towards the convexity causing the visible hump. Adson s Test for Thoracic Outlet Syndrome The examiner abducts extends and externally rotates the patient s arm while palpating their radial pulse. The patient inhale deeply extends and rotates their head towards the tested arm. A positive test is the significant diminishment or loss of the radial pulse implying the scalene muscles are compressing the subclavian artery Thoracic Outlet Syndrome . Scapular Winging Testing Have the patient perform a wall push-up. Protrusion of the medial border indicates primary Serratus Anterior weakness Long Thoracic Nerve injury though the trapezius Spinal Accessory Nerve and rhomboids can also contribute. Lumbar tests Stork Test The patient stands on one leg and physically extends their back backward. The hyperextension forces pressure onto the posterior elements of the spine—specifically the pars interarticularis . Unilateral back pain here strongly hints at Spondylolysis a stress fracture of the pars . Straight Leg Raise SLR With the patient completely completely supine and relaxed passively elevate their straightened leg. Pain radiating past the knee between 30-70 degrees indicates significant stretching and irritation of the sciatic nerve or dural sac impingement . Braggard s Test Lower the leg slightly from the SLR pain point and sharply dorsiflex the foot. Reproducing the pain solidifies the diagnosis of L5 nerve root impingement. Well s Crossed Straight Leg Raise Raising the perfectly healthy uninvolved leg directly produces radiating pain down the opposite affected leg. A highly specific sign of a massive space-occupying disc herniation. Hoover s Test Detects malingering or psychogenic paresis. While asking the patient to lift their paralyzed or injured leg off the table cup your hand under their opposite normal heel. If the patient is genuinely trying to lift the affected leg normal biomechanics dictate they will physically push down hard with their normal heel into your hand to generate leverage. A lack of downward pressure signifies a lack of genuine effort. correlate Camptocormia Bent Spine Syndrome - Pathophysiology Characterized by a severe involuntary forward flexion kyphosis of the thoracolumbar spine that becomes deeply pronounced while standing or walking but completely resolves when the patient lies supine. It is very frequently seen as an axial muscular complication of Parkinson s Disease . - Presentation The patient walks hunched completely forward at nearly a 90-degree angle forcing them to look almost straight down at the ground. Muscle strength is otherwise preserved. - Treatment Physical therapy aggressive OMM tailored to fascial restrictions and hypertonic musculature and optimization of dopaminergic Parkinson s medications. - Lecturer s Point Through properly applied OMM muscle energy myofascial release of the OA and traps a 77-year-old Parkinson s patient presenting with chronic Camptocormia was able to stand completely upright and see the sky for the first time in years. questions 1 A 22-year-old male is brought into the emergency department restrained via backboard and C-collar following a high-speed motorcycle accident. He is agitated screaming in agony about his severely deformed open femur fracture and is actively intoxicated with a blood alcohol concentration of 0.18. According to the NEXUS criteria which of the following perfectly justifies the absolute necessity to order a cervical spine CT for this patient? The mechanism of injury was a high-speed accident over 45mph. His age inherently places him in a high-risk category. He cannot actively rotate his neck 45 degrees left and right. He is heavily intoxicated and suffering from a highly distracting painful injury. The C-collar prevents accurate assessment of his visual fields. 3 To clear a C-spine clinically without imaging via NEXUS criteria the patient MUST be completely sober fully alert free of any midline spinal tenderness free of neurological deficits AND crucially they must not have any major distracting painful injuries like a shattered femur . This patient is intoxicated and has a massive distracting injury inherently disabling the physician s ability to trust his localized pain assessment. Therefore imaging is absolutely mandated. 2 During a routine musculoskeletal physical assessment a patient complains of a chronic pins and needles numbness shooting down their left arm into their thumb. To evaluate for a cervical radiculopathy you instruct the patient to extend their neck slightly laterally side-bend and rotate their head leftward toward the affected side. You then apply a targeted downward axial compression on the top of their head. This maneuver instantly recreates the severe shooting pain. Which specific test did you perform? Spurling s Test Cervical Distraction Test Lhermitte s Sign Adson s Test Adam s Forward Bend Test 0 Spurling s test involves placing the cervical spine in a position extension ipsilateral rotation and side-bending that maximally narrows the intervertebral neuroforamen. Applying an axial downward force further squeezes the space; if a compressed nerve root radiculopathy is present this will powerfully recreate the patient s shooting radicular arm pain. 3 A 77-year-old male with a longstanding 12-year history of Parkinson s Disease presents to the osteopathic manipulative medicine clinic. He shuffles into the room demonstrating a profound abnormal severe forward flexion of his thoracolumbar spine. He is effectively bent in half while walking staring directly at his shoes. However when instructed to lie flat on his back on the examination table for treatment his spine posture almost completely normalizes. What is the most accurate clinical term for this patient s postural presentation? Severe primary structural scoliosis Ankylosing Spondylitis Spondylolysis Camptocormia Thoracic Outlet Syndrome 3 Camptocormia Bent Spine Syndrome is defined by an extreme involuntary forward-flexed kyphotic posture of the trunk that vividly appears when the patient stands or walks but dramatically disappears when they lie down supine. It is a highly recognized axial musculature side-effect heavily associated with advanced Parkinson s disease. 4 A 45-year-old male construction worker is evaluated for severe right-sided lower back pain that relentlessly shoots down his right posterior thigh wrapping past the knee and ending at his lateral right foot. You perform a classic Straight Leg Raise SLR on the patient s right leg. At 40 degrees of elevation the patient yelps as the shooting pain perfectly replicates down the leg. To further confirm the L5/S1 nerve root irritation you lower the leg to 35 degrees where the pain vanishes and then you quickly sharply dorsiflex his right foot. The pain instantly returns. This confirmatory action is strictly known as Well s Crossed-Straight Leg Raise The Stork Test Braggard s Test Hoover s Test Lhermitte s maneuver 2 Braggard s test is the standard confirmatory follow-up to a positive Straight Leg Raise. By dropping the leg a few degrees until the pain subsides and then applying sharp dorsiflexion of the foot the physician brutally stretches the sciatic nerve and its dural attachments without engaging hamstring tightness. Reproducing the pain entirely confirms a true radicular etiology dural/nerve irritation ruling out simple hamstring muscle spasms. 5 A 34-year-old female presents to the clinic complaining of an intense electric shock sensation bounding down her entire spine whenever she aggressively bends her neck forward to look down at her smartphone. Upon neurological review she also reports blurry vision in her left eye and recent episodes of profound clumsiness. This specific exam finding generated by cervical flexion—highly suggestive of conditions spanning from severe spinal stenosis to demyelinating plaques like Multiple Sclerosis—is defined as Adson s sign Positive cervical distraction Lhermitte s Sign Hoover s sign Saddle anesthesia 2 Lhermitte s sign often dubbed the Barber Chair Phenomenon is classically provoked by rapid active anterior flexion of the cervical spine. This stretches the posterior columns of the spinal cord. In the presence of highly sensitive demyelinated cervical plaques characteristic of Multiple Sclerosis or direct structural central cord compression this harmless stretch triggers a terrifying distinct electrical shock vibrating down the spine into the extremities. What test involves extending the neck rotating/side-bending towards the affected side and applying downward axial pressure to check for cervical radiculopathy? Spurling s Test. Clinical Skills When performing the Cervical Distraction Test what response technically constitutes a positive result? The reduction or complete relief of radicular arm pain as lifting the head decompresses the inflamed nerve root . Clinical Skills What is Lhermitte s Sign and what major neurologic disease is it classically associated with? A shooting electric shock sensation traversing down the spine provoked by purely flexing the neck forward. Strongly associated with Multiple Sclerosis MS or cervical stenosis. Clinical Skills What is the NEXUS Criteria primarily used for? A clinical decision aid evaluating 5 parameters to safely rule out a cervical spine injury and clear the patient without requiring a CT scan. Clinical Documentation During Adam s Forward Bend Test you see a unilateral right-sided rib hump. Which way is the vertebral body actually rotating? The vertebral body is pathologically rotating towards the convexity the right causing the ribs on that side to push outward and generate the hump. Biomechanics Adson s Test involves extending the arm while feeling the radial pulse as the patient inhales and turns their head deeply toward the tested side. What is it evaluating for? Thoracic Outlet Syndrome specifically evaluating for subclavian artery compression by hypertonic Scalene muscles . Clinical Skills A patient reporting localized severe unilateral lower back pain upon standing on one leg and hyperextending their spine backward has a positive test for what likely structural defect? The Stork Test evaluates for Spondylolysis a stress fracture of the pars interarticularis . Clinical Skills What is Camptocormia? Bent Spine Syndrome A profound involuntary forward-flexed posture while standing or walking that dramatically normalizes when the patient lies flat. Highly associated with Parkinson s Disease. Clinical In a patient presenting with an acutely herniated lumbar disc doing a Straight Leg Raise SLR on the totally healthy asymptomatic leg produces sharp radiating pain down the broken opposite leg. What test is this? Well s Test Crossed Straight Leg Raise . Clinical Skills How do you perform Braggard s Test after a positive Straight Leg Raise? Lower the leg until the shooting pain stops then rigidly acutely dorsiflex the foot. The pain returning confirms true dural/sciatic irritation rejecting hamstring tightness. Clinical Skills B&B Musculoskeletal > Pathology > Low Back Pain FirstAid Neurology > Clinical SketchyPath Neurology match Spurling / Cervical Radiculopathy resources im amboss medicalark AK Step2 v11 Subjects Neurology 05 Spinal Cord Cervical Spine Radiculopathy match Cauda Equina Syndrome resources AK Step1 v11 B&B 14 Musculoskeletal 03 Pathology 05 Low Back Pain match Straight Leg Raise resources im amboss medicalark AK Step2 v11 Subjects Neurology 05 Spinal Cord Sciatica match Multiple Sclerosis / Lhermitte s Sign resources AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 11 Neurology 03 Pathology 24 Demyelinating Disorders Multiple Sclerosis Presentation Pathophysiology Hoover s Trick for Genuine Effort When evaluating potential malingering a patient who is genuinely trying to lift a paralyzed or severely pained leg off the table via a Straight Leg Raise will completely involuntarily push down hard on the table with their opposite healthy heel in order to lever themselves up. Muscle Spasms Don t Cause Anesthesia A patient with terrible back pain localized strictly to the paraspinal muscles from lifting a heavy box should NOT present with saddle anesthesia bowel or bladder incontinence or dense dermatomal weakness. If they do ignore the muscle strain; they legitimately have a massive red-flag quarter equina compression that needs surgical imaging. The Dural Tug of Braggard s When separating a tight cramped hamstring from a legitimately irritated sciatic nerve in a positive Straight Leg Raise simply lowering the leg and aggressively dorsiflexing the foot Braggard s test perfectly isolates the sciatic nerve dural sheath. If pain fires back down the leg without hip flexion changing it s a nerve root irritation not a hamstring spasm. The NEXUS Mandate When you have an agonizingly painful distraction injury like an open broken femur it completely overrides the brain s ability to accurately process baseline structural neck pain. You absolutely cannot trust the patient s subjective assessment that my neck perfectly doesn t hurt and you MUST legally order C-spine imaging. mindmap History and Physical Exam of the Spine Subjective Evaluation - OPQRST Analysis Differentiating articular acute localized or inflammatory qualities. - Red Flag Symptoms - Cauda Equina Bowel/bladder incontinence saddle anesthesia severe lower motor neuron deficit. Requires an emergency MRI and decompression. - Cancer/Infection Age anomalies <20 or >50 unexplained weight loss IV drug use pain increasing aggressively at night/rest. Objective Assessment - Inspection & Sensation Checking ergonomics assessing proper C7 triceps L4 medial ankle L5 great toe and S1 lateral ankle/Achilles reflexes. - Cervical Clearance Tools - NEXUS Criteria Missing ALL 5 Neuro deficit Tenderness Altered mental status Intoxication Distracting injury allows clearance without imagery. - Canadian C-Spine Age/mechanism heavy. Rotation capacity allows clearance of low-risk factors. Specific Provocative Tests - Cervical Compression - Axial Compression Pushing down recreating pain = Positive Radiculopathy. - Distraction Lifting up reducing pain = Positive decompression Validates radiculopathy or impingement . - Spurling s Side bent rotated to affected side with axial compression. - Lhermitte s Sign Electric shock down spine upon severe forward flexion MS lesion or spinal stenosis . - Thoracic/Lumbar Testing - Adam s Bend Screens for structural rotational scoliosis rib hump on the convexity . - Adson s Inhaling and head turning loses radial pulse Thoracic Outlet Syndrome by the scalenes . - Stork Test Single-leg standing extension highlighting pars interarticularis breakdown Spondylolysis . - Straight Leg Raise Severe radicular pain = Sciatic/dural interference. - Braggard s Dorsiflexing the foot validates a positive SLR. - Well s Positive pain on the un-lifted broken leg = Large central space-occupying lesion. - Hoover s Sign A lack of compensatory downward drive on the non-examined leg exposes poor exertion/malingering. Documented Pathologies - Camptocormia Bent spine syndrome characterized by extreme uncontrolled orthostatic flexion typically masking a Parkinsonian movement disorder treated drastically through structural OMM."}, {"id": "l137", "title": "Test of Immune Function", "module": "Unknown", "content": "id l137 module Unknown l137 Test of Immune Function lecturer S. Punatar D.O. session Lecture 137 Immunology readingTime 10-15 min pdf content/L137 HighYield Render.pdf analysis content/L137 HighYield Render Analysis.md Lecture 137 Test of Immune Function Lecturer S. Punatar D.O. Session Lecture 137 Clinical Foundations Beyond the Laboratory Slide 4 The diagnostic journey of an immunology patient does not begin in the pathology lab with flow cytometry or complex genetic testing. Instead it starts at the bedside with a meticulous history and physical examination. Before ordering expensive assays the clinician must interpret the subtle clues of recurrent infections failure to thrive and familial patterns. Understanding whether an immunodeficiency is congenital iatrogenic or acquired is the first step in classifying the mechanism of disease—be it cellular humoral or a defect in innate immunity. Subjective Clues A history indicative of familial origin or environmental changes is paramount. Red Flags Recurrent infections failure to thrive FTT and fever of unknown origin FUO should trigger immediate concern Slide 4 . Physical Findings Splenomegaly absence of lymph nodes or persistent Candida albicans moniliasis are physical hallmarks of underlying immune defects Slide 4 . The Global Picture Prevalence and Non-Specific Markers Slides 5-9 While the immune system is incredibly specialized its initial response to stress is often generalized. Most immunodeficiencies 55% involve antibody defects while cellular and combined defects are less common but often more severe Slide 5 . When the system is activated by inflammation infection or injury the liver releases acute-phase reactants. These markers act as the smoke indicating a fire somewhere in the body providing a snapshot of the immune status without pinpointing the exact location or cause. highyield Acute Phase Reactants APRs Definition Lab values elevated by at least 25% during inflammatory states. C-Reactive Protein CRP A pattern recognition protein PRP modulated by inflammation. Named after its reaction to the C carbohydrate capsule of Streptococcus Slide 9 . Erythrocyte Sedimentation Rate ESR Measures the viscosity of blood. As fibrinogen breaks down during inflammation blood viscosity increases causing RBCs to sediment faster. Note ESR is age-determinant Slide 8-9 . Cellular Immunity Neutrophils and NK Cells Slides 11-17 The innate immune system serves as the first line of defense utilizing neutrophils for phagocytosis and natural killer NK cells to eliminate intracellular pathogens. Evaluating these cells requires both enumeration counting them and functional assessment checking if they actually work . In conditions like Chronic Granulomatous Disease CGD a neutrophil count might be normal but the cells are functionally blind and unable to kill what they catch Slide 12 . Neutrophil Evaluation Slide 12 Absolute Neutrophil Count ANC Calculated via CBC with differential. Neutropenia Can be congenital Severe Congenital Neutropenia iatrogenic chemotherapy or infectious viral Slide 12 . Functional Testing Dihydrorhodamine DHR Test The preferred test for Chronic Granulomatous Disease. It uses flow cytometry to measure the absorption of a green fluorescent dye Slide 12 . Nitroblue Tetrazolium NBT Test A classic test for NADPH oxidase activity. It turns from yellow to blue if oxidative burst is present; a lack of color change indicates CGD Slide 12 . NK Cell Evaluation Slide 17 Identification NK cells are identified by the markers CD16+ and CD56+ . Clinical Significance NK deficiency syndromes manifest as susceptibility to severe recurrent or atypical herpesvirus infections VZV HSV EBV CMV . Condition Links NK cells are often decreased in SCID but may be elevated in Ataxia-Telangiectasia Slide 17 . The Pillars of Adaptive Immunity T-cells and B-cells Slides 18-22 Complexity increases significantly in the adaptive immune response. T-cells are the regulators and terminators while B-cells are the antibody producers. Defects in these cell lines often lead to combined immunodeficiency pictures. Modern diagnostics allow us to screen for these life-threatening conditions at birth identifying the genetic signals of a missing immune system. T-cell Evaluation Slide 18-21 Enumeration Measured via CD markers CD2+ CD3+ CD4+ CD8+ . CD4+ Decrease Seen in HIV/AIDS DiGeorge Syndrome thymic aplasia and SCID Slide 15 . Functional Testing Lymphocyte Function Mitogen Stimulation Using Phytohemagglutinin PHA . A low stimulatory index indicates decreased T-cell function often seen in chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis Slide 21 . TREC Assay T-cell receptor excision circles TREC are circular DNA fragments found in maturing T-cells. A lack of TRECs is a hallmark finding in neonatal screening for SCID Slide 21 . Lecturer Emphasis B-cell Evaluation Slide 22 Enumeration Measured via CD19+ and CD20+ . X-linked Bruton’s Agammaglobulinemia A male-predominant condition caused by a mutation in the BTK gene leading to a complete failure of B-cell maturation no B-cells found on flow cytometry Slide 22 . Soluble Defense The Complement System Slides 23-25 Beyond cells the immune system uses a network of over 30 soluble proteins known as complement. These proteins act in a cascade to opsonize pathogens and initiate the Membrane Attack Complex MAC . Testing the complement system is essential for patients with recurrent Neisseria infections or unexplained swelling. Complement Assays Slide 24-25 CH50 Hemolytic Complement Assay Screens the Classical Pathway C1-C9 . Decreased levels are seen in SLE C1-C4 and Neisserial bacteremia C5-C9 . AH50 Screens the Alternative Pathway . correlate C1-Esterase Inhibitor Deficiency - Pathophysiology Absence of C1-esterase inhibitor leads to uncontrolled activation of the complement and bradykinin pathways. - Presentation Hereditary Angioedema recurrent swelling without hives or itching . - Diagnosis Low C4 levels consumed in the process Slide 25 . - Lecturer s Point This is a classic test question and a high-yield clinical diagnosis that requires a strong history. Humoral Evaluation Antibodies and SPEP Slides 30-34 When humoral immunity is suspect clinicians quantify individual immunoglobulin classes. Whether it is the common IgA deficiency or the massive overproduction of a single clone in Multiple Myeloma the pattern of Ig levels serves as a diagnostic map. IgG The primary responder to antigen; levels are invalid if the patient received external Ig therapy in the last 6 months Slide 31 . IgA Most common immunodeficiency; presents as recurrent mucosal infections Slide 31 . IgE Highly elevated in Atopic patients and Hyper-IgE Job Syndrome Slide 31 . M-Proteins Identified via Serum Protein Electrophoresis SPEP . Multiple Myeloma Classically shows an IgG or IgA M-protein spike. Waldenstrom Macroglobulinemia Characterized by a spike in IgM Slide 34 . | Item | IgG | IgA | IgM | IgE | | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | | Wiskott-Aldrich | ↓ | ↑ | ↓ | ↑ | | AIDS | ↑ | ↑ | ↑ | ↑ | | Common Variable CVID | ↓ | ↓ | ↓ | - | | Normal Control | Normal | Normal | Normal | Normal | | Bruton s | ↓ | ↓ | ↓ | - | | Ataxia-Telangiectasia | ↓ | ↓ | - | ↓ | Advanced Diagnostic Mapping ANA HLA and IGRA Slides 37-43 The final frontiers of immunology testing involve mapping the body s recognition of self versus non-self. This involves identifying specific autoantibodies in rheumatologic disease and matching HLA types for transplantation or disease association. Autoantibody Profiles Slide 37 ANA General screening for autoimmune disease. Anti-dsDNA / Anti-Sm Highly specific for SLE . Anti-CCP Specific for RA . Anti-SSA/SSB Specific for Sjogren Syndrome . Anti-Scl70 Specific for Scleroderma . HLA Associations Slide 43 HLA-B27 Strongly associated with PAIR Psoriatic arthritis Ankylosing spondylitis IBD-associated arthritis Reactive arthritis . HLA-DR2 Associated with Multiple Sclerosis and SLE. HLA-DR3/DR4 Associated with Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus. IGRA Interferon Gamma Release Assay Slide 29 Used to determine Latent TB LTB infection by measuring the amount of IFN-Gamma released by WBCs when mixed with TB antigens. Unlike the PPD skin test it is qualitative and less susceptible to the BCG vaccine effects. questions 1 A 4-year-old boy presents with recurrent skin abscesses and granulomatous lung infections. His CBC shows a normal white blood cell count with 60% neutrophils. A dihydrorhodamine DHR flow cytometry assay reveals a lack of fluorescent shift. What is the most likely underlying defect? B-cell maturation failure NADPH oxidase deficiency Thymic aplasia C1-esterase inhibitor deficiency Myeloperoxidase deficiency 1 Recurrent infections with catalase-positive organisms skin abscesses lung granulomas and a negative DHR test no shift are hallmarks of Chronic Granulomatous Disease CGD which is caused by a defect in NADPH oxidase Slide 12 . 2 A newborn screening reveals the complete absence of T-cell receptor excision circles TRECs . Physical examination shows the child is otherwise healthy but lacks a visible thymic shadow on chest X-ray. Which of the following is the most definitive diagnosis for this finding? DiGeorge Syndrome Bruton s Agammaglobulinemia Severe Combined Immunodeficiency SCID Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome Common Variable Immunodeficiency 2 While DiGeorge presents with a missing thymus the absence of TRECs on newborn screening is the definitive screening hallmark for SCID Slide 21 . 3 A 28-year-old female with a history of lupus presents with unexplained swelling of her face and lips that developed over the last several hours. She denies any itching or hives and has not started any new medications. Laboratory work reveals a decreased C4 level. What is the most appropriate next test to confirm the likely diagnosis? ANA titer C1-esterase inhibitor level Serum IgE Total hemolytic complement CH50 Anti-dsDNA 1 The presentation of angioedema without pruritus/hives non-allergic and a low C4 level is classic for hereditary angioedema or acquired C1-esterase inhibitor deficiency Slide 25 . 4 A 65-year-old male is evaluated for bone pain and anemia. Serum protein electrophoresis SPEP reveals a significant M-protein spike in the gamma region. Subsequent testing identifies the clone as an overproduction of IgG. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? Waldenstrom Macroglobulinemia Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome Multiple Myeloma X-linked Agammaglobulinemia Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia 2 Multiple myeloma is characterized by an M-protein spike of IgG or IgA on SPEP Slide 34 . Waldenstrom is associated with IgM spikes. 5 Which of the following HLA types is most strongly associated with Ankylosing Spondylitis and other seronegative spondyloarthropathies? HLA-DR2 HLA-DR3 HLA-DR4 HLA-B27 HLA-A3 3 HLA-B27 has a very strong association with the PAIR group of diseases including Ankylosing Spondylitis Slide 43 . What is the preferred diagnostic test for Chronic Granulomatous Disease? Dihydrorhodamine DHR flow cytometry assay. Clinical Which CD markers are used to identify NK cells? CD16+ and CD56+. Glossary What neonatal screening result is characteristic of SCID? Absence of T-cell receptor excision circles TRECs . Concept A low C4 level in the setting of non-pruritic angioedema suggests what deficiency? C1-esterase inhibitor deficiency. Clinical What do the CH50 and AH50 assays measure respectively? CH50 measures the Classical pathway; AH50 measures the Alternative pathway. Concept What gene is mutated in X-linked Bruton s Agammaglobulinemia? BTK gene leads to lack of B-cell maturation . Clinical Which immunoglobulin is predominantly elevated in Waldenstrom Macroglobulinemia? IgM M-protein spike . Clinical What test is used to non-specifically measure the viscosity of blood during inflammation? Erythrocyte Sedimentation Rate ESR . Glossary Which HLA type is associated with Psoriatic and Reactive arthritis? HLA-B27. Concept Anti-CCP antibodies are highly specific for which disease? Rheumatoid Arthritis RA . Clinical What is the mechanism of the Interfeon Gamma Release Assay IGRA ? Measures IFN-Gamma release from T-cells in response to TB antigens. Concept BandB Immunology > Primary Immunodeficiency Pathoma Chapter 2 Inflammation & Chapter 13 M-proteins SketchyPharm Immunosuppressants FirstAid Immunology > Laboratory Tests match Chronic Granulomatous Disease resources AK Step1 v11 B&B 08 Immunology 02 Cellular 03 Phagocytosis match C1-esterase inhibitor deficiency resources AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 08 Immunology 01 Basic Principles 11 Complement C1 esterase inhibitor deficiency match TRECs / SCID resources AK Step1 v11 B&B 08 Immunology 03 Pathology 05 Severe Combined Immunodeficiency match HLA-B27 resources AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 08 Immunology 01 Basic Principles 03 MHC MHC I Associations The C in CRP C-Reactive Protein is named after its ability to react with the C carbohydrate capsule of Streptococcus pneumoniae marking it as one of the body s earliest recognized pattern recognition proteins. ANC Validity and Ig Therapy Quantitative IgG testing is functionally invalid if the patient has received intravenous immunoglobulin IVIG within the past 6 months as the test will measure the donor antibodies rather than the patient s own production. ESR vs. CRP While both are acute-phase reactants ESR is a slower indirect measure of inflammation driven by fibrinogen-induced RBC stacking Rouleaux whereas CRP is a rapid liver-produced pattern recognition protein. mindmap Test of Immune Function Foundations - H&P Importance Familial origin environmental exposure FTT and splenomegaly. - Prevalence Antibody defects 55% are most common. Innate Evaluation - Acute Phase Reactants - CRP Non-specific pattern recognition protein. - ESR Indirect measure of blood viscosity via fibrinogen. - Neutrophils - ANC Calculated from CBC w/diff. - Function DHR Flow Cytometry is preferred; NBT is the classic assay. - NK Cells - Identify CD16+/CD56+. - Deficiency Susceptibility to VZV HSV EBV CMV. Adaptive Evaluation - T-Cells - Enumeration CD3+ CD4+ CD8+. - Function PHA stimulation; TREC assay for neonatal SCID screening. - B-Cells - Enumeration CD19+ CD20+. - Failure Bruton s BTK gene - no B-cell maturation. Soluble Factors - Complement - Assays CH50 Classical AH50 Alternative . - Pathology C1-esterase inhibitor deficiency Hereditary Angioedema -> Low C4. - Antibodies - IgA Common mucosal infection susceptibility. - IgE Atopy and Job Syndrome. - SPEP Identifies M-proteins MM = IgG/IgA; Waldenstrom = IgM . Advanced Tools - IGRA Preferred TB screening IFN-Gamma release . - Autoantibodies Anti-dsDNA SLE Anti-CCP RA ANA Screening . - HLA Typing HLA-B27 Spondyloarthropathies HLA-DR3/4 T1DM ."}, {"id": "l138", "title": "Diagnosis of Immediate Hypersensitivity", "module": "Immunology", "content": "id l138 title Diagnosis of Immediate Hypersensitivity module Immunology lecturer S. Punatar D.O. session Lecture 138 readingTime 12-15 min pdf content/L138 HighYield Render.pdf analysis content/L138 HighYield Render Analysis.md summary Lecture 138 Diagnosis of Immediate Hypersensitivity Lecturer S. Punatar D.O. Session Lecture 138 The Clinical Hook A Peanut and a Drowsy Child Imagine 4-year-old Jack rushed to the ER after eating a chocolate bar Slide 3 . He’s not just itching; he’s pale his blood pressure is dropping and he’s struggling to breathe. This isn t just a reaction ; it s anaphylaxis—the most extreme and life-threatening manifestation of Type I hypersensitivity. Understanding the mechanisms clinical presentations and diagnostic tools for these immediate reactions is the difference between an outpatient follow-up and an emergency room save. Introduction to Hypersensitivity The Gell-Coombs Framework Slide 6 Hypersensitivity is an exaggerated or inappropriate immune response to a typically harmless antigen. To organize these responses we use the Gell-Coombs classification which divides them into four types I through IV based on the underlying immune mechanism. Type I Immediate IgE-mediated. Mast cell/basophil degranulation. e.g. Anaphylaxis Asthma . Type II Antibody-mediated IgG/IgM binding to cell surfaces. e.g. AIHA Myasthenia Gravis . Type III Complex-mediated Soluble immune complexes depositing in tissues. e.g. SLE Serum Sickness . Type IV Delayed T-cell mediated T-helper or T-killer . No antibodies involved. e.g. TB skin test Poison Ivy . highyield The Acid Mnemonic for Hypersensitivity A llergic/Anaphylactic/Atopy Type I C ytotoxic Antibody-mediated Type II I mmune Complex Type III D elayed T-cell mediated Type IV Type I Hypersensitivity The Mechanics of Immediate Slides 8-9 Type I reactions occur within minutes of exposure to an allergen in a previously sensitized individual. The process involves two distinct phases the immediate early phase and the late-phase reaction. The Two Phases of Type I HSR 1. Immediate Phase Minutes Trigger Allergen cross-links pre-formed IgE antibodies already bound to FcεRI receptors on mast cells and basophils. Mediators Primarily Histamine . Effects Vasodilation increased vascular permeability edema and smooth muscle contraction bronchospasm . 2. Late Phase 2-24 Hours Later Mediators Lipid mediators Leukotrienes and cytokines. Key Cell Eosinophils . Effects Sustained inflammation and tissue damage. Note This is why we observe anaphylaxis patients—to watch for a biphasic reaction Slide 21 . The Clinical Spectrum From Atopy to Anaphylaxis Slides 16-20 Atopy is the genetic predisposition to produce IgE against common environmental allergens. It manifests as a triad of conditions Atopic Dermatitis Allergic Rhinitis and Asthma. Anaphylaxis The Systemic Emergency Slide 19 Anaphylaxis is a multi-organ systemic Type I reaction. It is a clinical diagnosis characterized by Skin/Mucosa Hives urticaria angioedema. Respiratory Bronchospasm laryngeal edema stridor . Cardiovascular Hypotension shock tachycardia. GI Vomiting abdominal pain. correlate Anaphylaxis Treatment The Golden Rule IM Epinephrine 1 1000 in the Anterolateral Thigh is the first-line life-saving treatment. Do not wait for antihistamines or steroids to work. If the patient is on a beta-blocker and doesn t respond to Epinephrine the rescue agent is Glucagon Slide 30 . The Atopic March Slide 25 Allergic diseases often follow a predictable chronological sequence from infancy through childhood known as the Atopic March 1. Atopic Dermatitis Eczema Usually the first to appear in infancy Slide 26 . 2. Food Allergy Develops shortly after. 3. Asthma Follows in early childhood. 4. Allergic Rhinitis Typically the final manifestation. Diagnostic Tools Identifying the Offender Slides 31-33 To diagnose immediate hypersensitivity we must prove both sensitization presence of IgE and clinical relevance. 1. In-Vivo Testing Skin Prick Test SPT Method Small amount of allergen is introduced into the epidermis. Positive Result A Wheal ≥ 3mm greater than the negative control Saline Slide 32 . Requirements Patient must stop Antihistamines for 3–7 days prior as they suppress the wheal. Contraindications Extensive skin disease eczema or high risk of anaphylaxis. 2. In-Vitro Testing Serum IgE RAST/ImmunoCAP Method Measures allergen-specific IgE in the blood. Indications Used when skin testing is impossible extensive eczema antihistamine use or dermographism . Advantage No risk of triggering an allergic reaction during the test. Summary Table Gell-Coombs Classification | Type | Name | Mediator | Example | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | I | Immediate | IgE | Anaphylaxis Atopy Asthma | | II | Cytotoxic | IgG/IgM | Rheumatic Fever Goodpasture | | III | Immune Complex | IgG/IgM | SLE Post-strep GN Serum Sickness | | IV | Delayed | T-cells | Contact Dermatitis TB test | Clinical Pearl Summary - Epinephrine IM in the vastus lateralis is the gold standard for anaphylaxis. - Glucagon Rescue for anaphylaxis patients on beta-blockers. - Biphasic Reaction Late-phase reactions hours later are driven by eosinophils and require 4–6hr ED Observation. - Diagnostics Skin Prick ≥3mm or RAST if skin testing impossible . questions id q1 question A 6-year-old girl with a fruit allergy accidentally eats a strawberry and develops hives and lip swelling. Within 10 minutes she becomes lightheaded and her blood pressure is 80/50 mmHg. In addition to calling for help and assessing ABCs what is the most appropriate next step? options Administer oral diphenhydramine Administer intramuscular epinephrine in the deltoid Administer intramuscular epinephrine in the anterolateral thigh Administer intravenous methylprednisolone correctAnswer 2 rationale Epinephrine is the first-line treatment for anaphylaxis. The anterolateral thigh vastus lateralis provides the most rapid and reliable absorption compared to the deltoid or subcutaneous routes. Antihistamines and steroids are not front-line treatments for life-threatening anaphylaxis. clinicalPearl Epinephrine IM in the vastus lateralis is the gold standard for anaphylaxis management. pdfPage 19 id q2 question A 45-year-old man on Propranolol for migraines presents with anaphylaxis after a bee sting. He does not respond to a second dose of intramuscular epinephrine. What medication should be considered next to reverse his symptoms? options Albuterol Glucagon Famotidine Norepinephrine correctAnswer 1 rationale Patients on beta-blockers like propranolol may have blunted responses to epinephrine because their receptors are occupied. Glucagon bypasses the adrenergic receptors and increases cardiac cAMP directly restoring heart rate and contractility in these refractory cases. clinicalPearl Glucagon is the rescue agent for anaphylaxis in patients on beta-blockers. pdfPage 30 id q3 question A patient with severe atopic dermatitis covering 60% of his body requires testing for environmental allergies. He has been taking daily cetirizine for symptom control. What is the most reliable method to identify his specific allergens? options Skin prick testing Patch testing In-vitro IgE testing RAST Intradermal testing correctAnswer 2 rationale Skin-based testing prick patch intradermal requires healthy skin and the cessation of antihistamines which suppress the wheal response . For patients with widespread dermatitis or those unable to stop antihistamines in-vitro serum IgE testing RAST is the safest and most reliable alternative. clinicalPearl RAST is preferred when skin testing is contraindicated by medication or extensive skin disease. pdfPage 33 id q4 question A mother brings in her 12-month-old infant with red crusting papules on the forehead and cheeks. The mother notes the child often scratches the areas at night. What is the most likely progression for this child according to the Atopic March? options Immediate resolution with no further atopies Sequential development of asthma then allergic rhinitis Progression to psoriasis Development of systemic lupus erythematosus correctAnswer 1 rationale The Atopic March describes the typical developmental sequence of allergic diseases Atopic dermatitis in infancy followed by food allergies and later allergic asthma and allergic rhinitis in childhood/adolescence. clinicalPearl The Atopic March typically progresses from Eczema → Food Allergy → Asthma/Rhinitis. pdfPage 25 id q5 question Which of the following describes the mechanism of Type I hypersensitivity late-phase reactions? options Cross-linking of pre-formed IgE on mast cells leading to histamine release Synthesis of leukotrienes and recruitment of eosinophils/neutrophils Antibody-mediated opsonization of cellular antigens T-cell mediated cytokine release and macrophage activation correctAnswer 1 rationale The immediate phase of Type I HSR is driven by pre-formed histamine release. The late phase 2-24 hours later depends on de novo synthesis of lipid mediators like leukotrienes and the infiltration of inflammatory cells particularly eosinophils which cause sustained tissue damage. clinicalPearl Late-phase Type I reactions are sustained by lipid mediators and eosinophil recruitment. pdfPage 8 flashcards front What is the defining characteristic of Type I hypersensitivity? back Mediated by IgE antibodies binding to mast cells and basophils leading to degranulation Slide 8 . tag Concept front Name the two phases of Type I hypersensitivity. back Immediate phase vasodilation leakage and Late phase inflammation Eosinophil recruitment Slide 8 . tag Concept front What are the target cells for Type II hypersensitivity antibodies? back Antigens on cell surfaces or in the extracellular matrix e.g. AIHA Myasthenia Gravis Slide 9 . tag Concept front How does Type III hypersensitivity differ from Type II? back Type III involves soluble immune complexes that circulate and deposit whereas Type II involves antibodies binding to antigens fixed to cells or tissues Slide 11 . tag Concept front What is the gold-standard treatment for anaphylaxis? back Intramuscular Epinephrine in the anterolateral thigh Slide 19 . tag Clinical front Why must patients receiving epinephrine be observed for 4–6 hours? back To monitor for late-phase or biphasic reactions Slide 21 . tag Clinical front What is the threshold wheel diameter for a positive skin prick test? back 3 millimeters Slide 32 . tag Concept front Which atopic disease typically presents first in the Atopic March ? back Atopic Dermatitis Eczema in infancy Slide 25 . tag Clinical front What determines a diagnosis of Allergy versus Atopy ? back Allergy requires both sensitization IgE and clinical symptoms upon exposure; Atopy is just the genetic propensity Slide 36 . tag Concept front What physical exam finding is characterized by a transverse nasal crease? back The Allergic Salute Slide 29 . tag Clinical ankingResource primarySource BandB chapter Immunology > Clinical Immunology alternatives resource Bootcamp chapter Immunology > Hypersensitivities resource SketchyPharm chapter Allergy & Pulmonary resource Pathoma chapter Immunology > Hypersensitivity anking match Hypersensitivity Type I resources AK Step1 v11 B&B 12 Immunology 02 Clinical 01 Hypersensitivity pearls title Epinephrine is First-Line content IM Epinephrine 1 1000 in the anterolateral thigh is the gold standard for anaphylaxis. Do not delay administration for other medications. title Glucagon Rescue content For patients on beta-blockers who do not respond to epinephrine during anaphylaxis Glucagon is the recommended alternative. title Skin Prick Threshold content A positive Skin Prick Test result is a wheal ≥ 3mm larger than the negative saline control. title The Atopic March content The chronological progression of allergic diseases Atopic Dermatitis → Food Allergy → Asthma → Allergic Rhinitis. mindmap Diagnosis of Immediate Hypersensitivity Gell-Coombs Classification - Type I Immediate IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation. - Type II Cytotoxic IgG/IgM binding cell surfaces. - Type III Immune Complex Soluble complexes depositing in tissues. - Type IV Delayed T-cell mediated. Type I Mechanism - Immediate Phase Cross-linking of IgE on mast cells -> Histamine release. - Late Phase Leukotrienes and Eosinophil recruitment 2-24 hours . Clinical Manifestations - Atopy Triad Eczema Allergic Rhinitis Asthma. - Anaphylaxis Multi-organ systemic reaction Shock Bronchospasm GI symptoms . Diagnostic Tools - Skin Prick Test Wheal ≥ 3mm. Must stop antihistamines for 3-7 days. - Serum IgE RAST Preferred when skin testing is impossible e.g. extensive eczema . Management - Epinephrine First-line for anaphylaxis IM anterolateral thigh . - Glucagon Rescue for patients on beta-blockers."}, {"id": "l139", "title": "Allergens and Allergic Rhinitis", "module": "Unknown", "content": "metadata number 139 id l139 title Allergens and Allergic Rhinitis lecturer M. Pino Ph.D. session Lecture 139 pdf content/L139 HighYield Render.pdf analysis content/L139 HighYield Render Analysis.md summary Lecture 139 Allergens and Allergic Rhinitis Lecturer M. Pino Ph.D. Session Lecture 139 Introduction to Histamine and Hypersensitivity Slide 2 Histamine is a central mediator in the human body s response to environmental triggers acting as the primary effector molecule in Type I hypersensitivity reactions. Derived from the decarboxylation of the amino acid histidine histamine is sequestered within the granules of mast cells and basophils waiting for the cross-linking of surface-bound IgE antibodies by an allergen. Once released histamine exerts widespread effects on the vascular system smooth muscles and the central nervous system making it the first responder in the clinical manifestation of allergic rhinitis urticaria and anaphylaxis. Understanding the receptor dynamics is crucial as the physiological response depends entirely on whether histamine binds to H1 or H2 receptors each coupled to distinct G-protein signaling pathways. Synthesis and Storage Produced by decarboxylation of histidine . Primary storage sites Mast cells and Basophils . Slide 3 IgE-Mediated Release Initiated by allergen cross-linking of IgE on cell surfaces. Systemic Effects of Histamine Airway Causes smooth muscle contraction leading to wheezing and difficulty breathing. Blood Vessels Induces vasodilation and increased capillary permeability. Immune System Regulates T cells neutrophils and eosinophils. correlate Type I Hypersensitivity - Pathophysiology IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation upon re-exposure to an allergen. - Presentation Allergic rhinitis asthma urticaria and anaphylaxis. - Diagnosis Clinical history and skin prick testing. - Treatment Antihistamines corticosteroids and epinephrine for severe cases. - Lecturer s Point Note that this is a very short lecture focused on the review of materials already covered in previous modules but essential for boards. Histamine Receptor Physiology Slide 5 The clinical effects of histamine are mediated through four known receptors though H1 and H2 are the primary targets of current pharmacotherapy. The H1 receptor is coupled to the Gq pathway leading to the activation of phospholipase C PLC which subsequently increases inositol trisphosphate IP3 and diacylglycerol DAG to release intracellular calcium. In contrast the H2 receptor utilizes the Gs pathway stimulating adenylyl cyclase to increase cyclic AMP cAMP . This dichotomy explains why H1 blockers are used for allergic reactions whereas H2 blockers like Famotidine are used to suppress gastric acid secretion from parietal cells. H1 Receptor Gq Pathway Mechanism Activation of PLC -> IP3/DAG -> ↑ Ca2+ i . Slide 6 Location/Effects Brain Maintains wakefulness; blocking causes sedation. Blood Vessels Vasodilation -> Hypotension. Nerve Endings Itching and pain. H2 Receptor Gs Pathway Mechanism Activation of Adenylyl Cyclase -> ↑ cAMP . Location/Effects Gastric Parietal Cells Stimulates HCl secretion. Slide 7 Heart Minor stimulatory effects. highyield H1 vs H2 Receptor Coupling - H1 Gq Phospholipase C - H2 Gs Adenylyl Cyclase - Mnemonics 1 is Q 2 is S. Allergic Manifestations and Clinical Signs Slide 8 When histamine is released systemically it produces a characteristic constellation of signs and symptoms. The vasodilation leads to flushing and hypotension while the increased capillary permeability results in edema most notably angioedema of the lips and tongue. In the skin this manifests as urticaria hives and intense pruritus. Crucially the body responds to histamine-induced hypotension via the baroreceptor reflex triggering a sympathetic surge that results in tachycardia. These findings often combined with bronchospasm form the clinical picture of a severe allergic reaction or anaphylaxis. Classic Symptoms Pruritus Itching and Urticaria Hives . Flushing Due to peripheral vasodilation. Hypotension Also driven by vasodilation. Tachycardia Reflexive response to low blood pressure Lecturer Emphasis . Angioedema Severe swelling of the lips tongue and soft tissues. correlate Angioedema - Pathophysiology Swelling of the deep dermis or subcutaneous tissue due to increased capillary permeability. Can be histamine-mediated or bradykinin-mediated. - Presentation Swelling of lips tongue and eyelids; potentially life-threatening if the airway is involved. - Diagnosis Clinical; history of ACE inhibitor use bradykinin-mediated or allergen exposure histamine-mediated . - Treatment H1 blockers steroids and epinephrine. If bradykinin-mediated e.g. from ACE inhibitors switch to an ARB Sartan . - Lecturer s Point Note the connection between ACE inhibitors and bradykinin-induced angioedema which we will visit in depth in the cardiac module. Antihistamines First vs. Second Generation Slide 10 The classification of H1 antagonists into first and second generations is based primarily on their lipophilicity and selectivity. First-generation antihistamines are highly lipophilic allowing them to cross the blood-brain barrier BBB and block H1 receptors in the hypothalamus which leads to significant sedation. Furthermore they are less selective and possess significant anti-muscarinic anti-alpha-adrenergic and anti-serotoninergic properties. Second-generation antihistamines were engineered to be less lipophilic and more selective eliminating the central sedation and the drying anticholinergic side effects found in their predecessors. First-Generation Antihistamines Examples Diphenhydramine Benadryl Doxylamine Promethazine Cyproheptadine Chlorpheniramine . Lipophilicity Crosses BBB -> Sedation . Off-target Effects Anti-muscarinic M3 Block Dry mouth dry eyes urinary retention and constipation. Warning Can worsen BPH and narrow-angle glaucoma. Slide 11 Anti-alpha-1 Orthostatic hypotension notably Promethazine . Anti-serotonin Cyproheptadine. Clinical Uses Allergy motion sickness e.g. Dimenhydrinate and as a sleep aid. Second-Generation Antihistamines Examples Loratadine Claritin Fexofenadine Allegra Cetirizine Zyrtec . Slide 12 Lipophilicity Does NOT cross BBB -> Non-sedating . Selectivity No significant anti-muscarinic or alpha-adrenergic block. Clinical Goal Ideal for daytime use especially for students taking exams or operators of heavy machinery. mnemonic Anticholinergic Side Effects - Dry as a bone Decreased secretions dry mouth/eyes . - Red as a beet Flushing. - Blind as a bat Mydriasis/Cycloplegia Glaucoma risk . - Mad as a hatter Delirium/Confusion. - Full as a flask Urinary retention BPH risk . Specific First-Generation Nuances Slide 13 Several first-generation antihistamines have unique clinical utilities beyond simple allergy relief. Promethazine for instance has strong Dopamine D2 blockade in addition to its alpha-1 and H1 effects making it useful but potentially dangerous for patients with Parkinson’s disease. Cyproheptadine is distinguished by its ability to block serotonin receptors making it the classic antidote for serotonin syndrome. Dimenhydrinate is frequently used for motion sickness due to its action on the vestibular apparatus which contains both H1 and M1 receptors. Promethazine Blocks D2 receptors risk of EPS/tremors . Blocks alpha-1 risk of orthostasis/falling in elderly . Slide 13 Cyproheptadine Blocks Serotonin 5-HT receptors. Use Serotonin Syndrome management; appetite stimulant in cachectic patients. Dimenhydrinate Use Motion sickness vestibular apparatus block . Slide 11 correlate Serotonin Syndrome - Pathophysiology Excess serotonin levels due to drug-drug interactions e.g. SSRIs + Linezolid or MAOIs . - Presentation Hyperthermia autonomic instability tachycardia diarrhea and neuromuscular hyperactivity myoclonus seizures . - Diagnosis Clinical based on history and Hunter criteria. - Treatment Supportive care benzodiazepines and Cyproheptadine . Anaphylaxis and Epinephrine Management Slide 14 In the event of a life-threatening Type I reaction anaphylaxis H1 blockers are insufficient. Epinephrine is the physiological antagonist to histamine and remains the first-line treatment. Unlike Norepinephrine which lacks significant Beta-2 activity Epinephrine stimulates Alpha-1 vasoconstriction to address hypotension Beta-1 increased heart rate and Beta-2 bronchodilation to address bronchospasm . This multi-receptor approach allows Epinephrine to rapidly reverse both the hemodynamic and respiratory collapse associated with anaphylaxis. Alpha-1 Action Counteracts vasodilation/hypotension. Beta-1 Action Counteracts hypotension via chronotropy. Beta-2 Action Triggers bronchodilation by decreasing myosin light chain kinase activity via the Gs/cAMP pathway. Slide 14 Side Effects Palpitations hypertension and CNS excitement anxiety . Adjunctive Pharmacotherapy Asthma and Allergy Slide 15 Beyond antihistamines and epinephrine other classes of drugs manage the downstream or upstream effects of the allergic cascade. Cromolyn and Nedocromil are mast cell stabilizers that prevent degranulation entirely but they must be used preventatively. Once histamine is released these drugs are ineffective. For chronic management of asthma and allergic rhinitis Leukotriene receptor antagonists LTRAs and Glucocorticoids play a pivotal role in suppressing the eicosanoid inflammatory pathway. Mast Cell Stabilizers Drugs Cromolyn Nedocromil . Mechanism Prevents mast cell degranulation by blocking chloride channels. Clinical Note Prophylactic use only; ineffective for acute attacks. Slide 15 Eicosanoid Modifiers LTRAs Montelukast Singulair Zafirlukast . Block leukotriene receptors; particularly useful in asthma and aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease AERD . LOX Inhibitors Zileuton . Blocks 5-lipoxygenase enzyme. Glucocorticoids Mechanism Stimulate Lipocortin to inhibit Phospholipase A2 . Effect Stops the entire arachidonic acid cascade. correlate Aspirin-Exacerbated Respiratory Disease AERD - Pathophysiology Chronic condition consisting of asthma rhinosinusitis with nasal polyps and aspirin sensitivity. Shunting of arachidonic acid to the leukotriene pathway occurs when COX is inhibited. - Presentation Severe bronchospasm and congestion after taking NSAIDs/Aspirin. - Diagnosis Clinical history of the Samter s Triad . - Treatment Avoid NSAIDs; treat with Leukotriene receptor antagonists e.g. Montelukast . Glucocorticoid Mechanics and Adverse Effects Slide 16 Glucocorticoids are potent anti-inflammatory agents that operate via intracellular receptors and gene transcription. Within the cytoplasm the glucocorticoid receptor is bound to heat shock proteins; once the steroid binds these proteins dissociate and the drug-receptor complex dimerizes and enters the nucleus. There it binds to glucocorticoid response elements GRE to modulate protein synthesis—notably Lipocortin which shuts down Phospholipase A2. While extremely effective long-term use is limited by multi-systemic side effects including growth suppression in children fat redistribution Cushingoid features and bone loss. Intracellular Pathway Heat shock proteins -> Dimerization -> Nucleus -> GRE. Slide 16 Systemic Side Effects Growth Suppresses GH secretion problematic in pediatrics . Slide 17 Metabolism Gluconeogenesis hyperglycemia fat redistribution central obesity . Kidney Sodium and water retention hypertension . Bone Increased osteoclast activity and decreased Vitamin D absorption -> Osteoporosis. Rule of Thumb Limit use to 10-14 days for acute episodes to minimize systemic toxicity. Lecturer Emphasis . highyield The Arachidonic Acid Cascade - Glucocorticoids Inhibit Phospholipase A2 via Lipocortin . - Zileuton Inhibits 5-Lipoxygenase. - Montelukast Blocks Leukotriene Receptors. - NSAIDs Inhibit Cyclooxygenase COX . Summary Table Antihistamine Generations | Generation | Lipophilicity | Sedation | Anticholinergic Side Effects | Examples | | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | | First | High | Significant | High M3 Block | Diphenhydramine Promethazine Cyproheptadine | | Second | Low | Minimal | Minimal/Absent | Loratadine Fexofenadine Cetirizine | | Third | Very Low | None | None | Desloratadine Levocetirizine | ankingResource primarySource BandB chapter Pharmacology > Misc > Antihistamines alternatives resource SketchyPharm chapter Respiratory > Obstructive pulmonary disease pharmacology Asthma drugs resource SketchyPharm chapter GI & Endocrine > Thyroid Parathyroid & Adrenal > Glucocorticoids resource FirstAid chapter Immunology > Immunosuppressants > Glucocorticoids resource BandB chapter Pulm > Treatment of COPD & Asthma anking match Antihistamines H1 Blockers resources AnKing Step 1 Zanki Pharmacology H1 Blockers match Glucocorticoids Mechanism resources AnKing Step 1 Zanki Pharmacology Glucocorticoids match Anaphylaxis Management resources AnKing Step 1 Zanki Step Decks Zanki Immunology Anaphylaxis pearls title H1 vs H2 Coupling content H1 = Gq ↑IP3/DAG/Ca2+ ; H2 = Gs ↑cAMP . title First Gen Sides content Crossing BBB Sedation + M3 block Dry mouth urinary retention glaucoma risk . title BPH & Glaucoma content Avoid first-generation antihistamines due to anticholinergic risk. title Cyproheptadine Use content Serotonin syndrome antidote and appetite stimulant cachexia . title Epinephrine vs NE content Epi has Beta-2 bronchodilation NE does not. Epi is first-line for anaphylaxis. title Cromolyn Action content Mast cell stabilizer; purely prophylactic useless in acute attack. title Glucocorticoid Side Effects content Growth suppression weight gain Cushingoid hyperglycemia and osteoporosis. title AERD Samter s Triad content Asthma nasal polyps and aspirin sensitivity. Caused by leukotriene shunting. flashcards front What is the second messenger system for H1 receptors? back Gq -> PLC -> IP3/DAG -> Calcium tag Concept front Why are second-generation antihistamines non-sedating? back They have low lipophilicity and do not cross the blood-brain barrier. tag Concept front Which first-generation antihistamine is used for serotonin syndrome? back Cyproheptadine tag Clinical front What is the primary physiologic reason to use Epinephrine over NE in anaphylaxis? back Beta-2 activity causing bronchodilation. tag Concept front How do glucocorticoids inhibit Phospholipase A2? back By stimulating the synthesis of Lipocortin. tag Concept front What is the classic side effect profile of first-gen H1 blockers in elderly males? back Sedation and urinary retention due to anticholinergic block worsening BPH . tag Clinical front True or False Cromolyn is effective during an acute asthma attack. back False; it is a prophylactic mast cell stabilizer. tag Concept front What happens to arachidonic acid in a patient with AERD after taking aspirin? back Shunting to the leukotriene pathway leading to bronchospasm. tag Clinical front Where are H1 receptors located in the brain and what is their function? back Hypothalamus; maintaining wakefulness. tag Glossary front What are the common skeletal side effects of long-term glucocorticoid use? back Osteoporosis via increased osteoclast activity and decreased Vit D/Calcium absorption . tag Clinical mindMap Histamine & Allergy Histamine Physiology - Origin Histidine decarboxylation - Storage Mast cells Basophils - Receptors H1 Gq Allergy wakefulness vasodilation H2 Gs Gastric acid secretion Parietal cells Antihistamines H1 Antagonists - First Generation Features Lipophilic cross BBB Sedating Anticholinergic Drugs Diphenhydramine Promethazine Cyproheptadine Dimenhydrinate Risks BPH Glaucoma Falling orthostasis - Second Generation Features Polar no BBB cross Non-sedating Selective Drugs Loratadine Fexofenadine Cetirizine Anaphylaxis Management - Primary Drug Epinephrine - Rationale Alpha-1 BP Beta-1 Circulation Beta-2 Bronchodilation Eicosanoid Pathway Modifiers - Glucocorticoids Mechanism Lipocortin -> PLC inhibition Regulation Intracellular receptors/GRE - Mast Cell Stabilizers Cromolyn Nedocromil Prophylaxis - Leukotriene Modifiers LTRAs Montelukast Singulair - Blocks receptors LOX Inhibitor Zileuton - Blocks 5-LOX enzyme"}, {"id": "l140", "title": "Anaphylaxis and Allergic Reactions", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l140 module Neuro l140 Anaphylaxis and Allergic Reactions lecturer J. Maccagnano D.O. session Lecture 140 Immunology readingTime 12-15 min pdf content/L140 HighYield Render.pdf analysis content/L140 HighYield Render Analysis.md Lecture 140 Anaphylaxis and Allergic Reactions Lecturer J. Maccagnano D.O. Session Lecture 140 Introduction to Hypersensitivity Slide 2 Anaphylaxis represents one of the most critical stop and think moments in emergency medicine where rapid recognition and intervention literally define the boundary between life and death. While many allergic reactions are benign and self-limiting the potential for progression to systemic collapse requires a deep understanding of the underlying immunological mechanisms. By mastering the four types of hypersensitivity reactions clinicians can better predict patient trajectories and implement evidence-based treatments that prioritize airway protection and hemodynamic stability. Slide 2 The Four Types of Hypersensitivity - General Framework Slide 8-11 - Hypersensitivity reactions are adverse immunological responses that affect the host rather than protecting it. - Although the focus is on Type 1 Immediate understanding the other three types is essential for boards USMLE/COMLEX and clinical practice. | Type | Name | Mechanism | Key Mediators | Example | | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | | Type 1 | Immediate | IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation | Histamine Leukotrienes | Anaphylaxis Asthma | | Type 2 | Cytotoxic | Antibody IgG/IgM binds to cell-surface antigens | Complement MAC | ABO Transfusion Reaction | | Type 3 | Immune Complex | Freely circulating Ag-AB complexes deposit in vessel walls | C5a Neutrophils | Serum Sickness | | Type 4 | Cell-Mediated | T-cell mediated Delayed | Cytokines Macrophages | Contact Dermatitis | - Type 2 Cytotoxic Reactions Slide 8 - Antigens reside on cell surfaces and attract IgG or IgM antibodies. - This binding activates the complement system leading to the formation of the Membrane Attack Complex MAC which lyses the cell. correlate ABO Incompatibility Transfusion Reaction - Pathophysiology Type 2 HSR where recipient antibodies attack donor red blood cell antigens. - Presentation Fever chills hypotension and potential renal failure. - Diagnosis Clinical suspicion during transfusion; positive Coomb s test. - Treatment Immediate cessation of transfusion; aggressive IV fluid resuscitation. - Lecturer s Point This is a really bad example of cell lysis via the complement system. - Type 3 Immune Complex Reactions Slide 9 - Antigens are freely circulating and form multivalent complexes with antibodies. - These complexes deposit in blood vessel walls where C5a attracts neutrophils that release enzymes destroying the endothelium. - Type 4 Cell-Mediated Delayed Slide 10 - This is the only type that is T-cell mediated rather than antibody-mediated. - Sensitized T-helper cells recognize antigens and release cytokines to recruit more T-cells and macrophages the Pac-Man effect creating a cyclic inflammatory response. Type 1 Hypersensitivity The Allergic Mechanism Slide 12-14 Type 1 reactions occur in two distinct stages an immediate phase driven by preformed mediators and a late-phase reaction driven by newly synthesized inflammatory signals. The logic of the immune system here is over-protection where the body treats harmless substances like peanut butter or pollen as if they were lethal invaders. This sensitized state explains why a patient may have no reaction to their first bee sting but experience full-blown anaphylaxis upon the second exposure. Slide 12 Phasing and Mediators - Immediate Phase Slide 13 - Sensitization Initial exposure creates IgE antibodies that bind to mast cells and basophils. - Activation Upon re-exposure the allergen cross-links preformed IgE on the mast cell surface. - Degranulation Immediate release of preformed histamine tryptase and leukotrienes. highyield Biphasic Reaction A biphasic reaction occurs when anaphylactic symptoms recur within 48 hours typically ~6 hours after the initial event resolved without further exposure to the allergen. This is driven by newly synthesized leukotrienes and the recruitment of eosinophils and neutrophils. Lecturer Emphasis - Late Phase Components Slide 14 - Starts roughly 6 hours after exposure. - Newly synthesized leukotrienes attract inflammatory cells. - High eosinophil counts are a hallmark of this phase and reflect the chronic allergic state seen in many IAM patients. Verbal Hint Clinical Presentation and Symptoms Slide 17 Because mast cells reside in the skin mucous membranes and connective tissues the symptoms of an allergic reaction are multi-systemic. The clinician must perform a rigorous review of systems as a patient might present with only GI symptoms or only respiratory distress depending on the route of exposure respiratory GI or dermal . A high index of suspicion is required to ensure that subtle signs—like a scratchy throat or mild nausea—aren t predictors of imminent airway closure. Slide 17 Systemic Breakdown - ENT/Airway CRITICAL Uvular edema tongue swelling oral pharyngeal edema. Lecturer Emphasis You must look inside their mouth! - Skin 90% of patients present with urticaria hives flushing or pruritus itching . - Respiratory Rhinitis stridor upper airway and wheezing lower airway . - Gastrointestinal Nausea vomiting and diarrhea. - Cardiovascular Chest pain and hypotension leads to anaphylactic shock . - Neurological Headaches and seizures. Diagnosis and Differential Slide 18-20 Accurately diagnosing anaphylaxis is paramount because mild cases are frequently undertreated as simple allergic reactions. Criteria-based diagnosis ensures that life-saving interventions like epinephrine are administered early. The differential diagnosis must be broad including both life-threatening conditions that mimic allergic distress and non-lethal conditions like panic attacks that can present with similar subjective respirations. Slide 18 Anaphylaxis Diagnostic Criteria Diagnosis is confirmed if one of the following three scenarios occurs Slide 19-20 1. Acute Mucocutaneous Involvement hives/swelling PLUS either Respiratory distress wheezing/stridor or Hypotension/End-organ damage. 2. Two or more of the following occurring rapidly after exposure to a known/suspected allergen - Mucocutaneous involvement - Respiratory symptoms - Hypotension - GI symptoms nausea pain 3. Hypotension alone within minutes/hours after exposure to a known allergen. Differential Diagnosis Slide 18 | Life-Threatening | Non-Life-Threatening | | --- | --- | | Angioedema not always allergic | Anxiety / Panic Attack | | Acute Coronary Syndrome ACS | Vasovagal Syncope | | Airway Obstruction / Foreign Body | Dermatitis | | Sepsis / Septic Shock | Gastroenteritis | | Pneumothorax | Chronic Urticaria | | Scombroid Poisoning | Liver Dysfunction | correlate Scombroid Poisoning - Pathophysiology Ingestion of spoiled fish tuna mackerel containing high levels of histamine produced by bacteria. - Presentation Mimics an allergic reaction with flushing headache and hives. - Lecturer s Point Must be in the differential for patients presenting with sudden symptoms after eating fish. Emergency Management and Treatment Slide 22-24 The hierarchy of treatment in anaphylaxis is absolute Airway and Epinephrine always come first. While secondary medications like antihistamines and steroids help with comfort and the prevention of late-phase symptoms they have no proven benefit for the life-threatening components of the reaction. Clinicians must be prepared to escalate care to advanced airway management or IV vasopressors if the initial intramuscular treatment fails. Slide 22 The Gold Standard Epinephrine - Epinephrine Slide 22 - Only evidence-proven medication for life-saving treatment. - Mechanism Increases peripheral vascular resistance raises BP increases cardiac output stabilizes mast cells and reverses bronchoconstriction. - Dose 0.3 - 0.5 mg IM in the thigh large quad muscle ensures better absorption than smaller muscles . - Pediatric Dose 0.01 mg/kg IM. Support and Secondary Agents - Airway Protect the airway! Lecturer Emphasis Intubate early if tongue swelling is progressing . Slide 22 - IV Crystalloids Use for patients who remain hypotensive despite epinephrine. Slide 23 - Albuterol Alleviates bronchospasm wheezing only . Slide 23 - Antihistamines H1 + H2 - Diphenhydramine Benadryl + Famotidine Pepcid . - Synergy Studies show H1 and H2 blockers together help cutaneous symptoms better than H1 alone. Lecturer Emphasis - Corticosteroids May help prevent biphasic reactions; high doses for severe cases but consider risk/benefit in mild cases e.g. hyperglycemia weight gain osteoporosis . Slide 24 30 - Glucagon Required for patients on Beta-blockers who are refractory to epinephrine. Verbal Hint Slide 24 Disposition and Follow-up Slide 26-27 - Low Risk Resolved Observe for a period in the ED. Discharge with an EpiPen prescription and follow-up with an allergist. Slide 26 - High Risk Persistent or Comorbid Admission or prolonged observation is required if the patient has CHF CKD Asthma or is on Beta-blockers. Slide 27 - Severe ICU admission for intubated patients or those requiring IV epinephrine drips. Slide 27 mnemonic Types of Hypersensitivity ACID ACID - A Anaphylactic / Atopic Type 1 - C Cytotoxic Antibody-mediated Type 2 - I Immune Complex Type 3 - D Delayed Cell-mediated Type 4 questions 1 A 24-year-old male is brought to the emergency department after being stung by a bee 15 minutes ago. He is currently experiencing difficulty breathing wheezing and has broad hives over his chest. His blood pressure is 105/70 mmHg. What is the most appropriate next step in management? Administer 50mg Diphenhydramine IV Administer 125mg Methylprednisolone IV Administer 0.3mg Epinephrine IM in the lateral thigh Administer nebulized Albuterol Observe for two hours for signs of shock 2 This patient meets the criteria for anaphylaxis mucocutaneous involvement plus respiratory distress . Epinephrine is the gold standard and only evidence-proven life-saving treatment. It should be given IM in the thigh for optimal absorption. Steroids and antihistamines are secondary and not life-saving in the acute phase. 2 A patient with a known history of severe tree nut allergies presents with hypotension BP 82/50 after eating a salad. He takes Propanolol for migraines. Despite two doses of IM epinephrine he remains hypotensive. What additional medication should be considered? IV Famotidine IV Glucagon IM Diphenhydramine IV Crystalloids Nebulized Albuterol 1 Patients on beta-blockers like Propanolol can be refractory to epinephrine. Glucagon can bypass the blocked beta-receptors to increase cAMP and improve hemodynamics. IV crystalloids should also be given but glucagon specifically addresses the drug-induced resistance. 3 Which of the following best describes the mechanism of a Type 3 Hypersensitivity Reaction? IgE cross-linking on mast cells T-cell recruitment of macrophages via cytokines Antibody binding to cell-surface antigens leading to MAC formation Deposition of Ag-AB complexes in vessel walls attracting neutrophils Delayed reaction starting 48 hours after exposure 3 Type 3 HSR involves freely circulating immune complexes that deposit in tissues/vessels and attract neutrophils via C5a. Option A is Type 1 B is Type 4 and C is Type 2. 4 A 6-year-old female presents with a runny nose and a scattered maculopapular rash after starting a new cold medication. The mother is worried about an allergy. Which finding would most strongly suggest a viral exanthemum rather than a drug allergy? The presence of pruritus A history of fever and malaise before the rash The rash appearing in the late phase 6 hours later The presence of hives urticaria Improvement with antihistamines 1 Not all rashes are allergies. A viral exanthemum is common in children and often preceded by viral prodromal symptoms like fever. Hives urticaria are much more classically associated with Type 1 HSR allergies seen in 90% of cases . 5 In Type 1 Hypersensitivity the late-phase reaction is primarily characterized by the recruitment of which cell type? Basophils Macrophages Eosinophils Plasma cells B-cells 2 The late phase of Type 1 HSR starting ~6 hours post-exposure involves newly synthesized leukotrienes that specifically attract inflammatory cells primarily eosinophils and neutrophils. What is the gold standard life-saving medication for anaphylaxis? Epinephrine 0.3-0.5 mg IM in the thigh Clinical What type of hypersensitivity reaction is an ABO transfusion reaction? Type 2 Cytotoxic Concept What is a biphasic reaction in anaphylaxis? Recurrence of symptoms within 48 hours usually ~6 hours without re-exposure to the allergen. Concept Which cell type is the Pac-Man of the Type 4 HSR? Macrophage Glossary What is the classic triad of Type 1 HSR mediators released upon degranulation? Histamine Tryptase and Leukotrienes Concept What are the three scenarios for diagnosing anaphylaxis? 1. Acute Skin + Resp/CV; 2. 2+ systems after suspected allergen; 3. Hypotension after known allergen. Clinical Why is epinephrine given in the thigh quad muscle? It is a large muscle providing better and faster absorption than smaller muscles. Clinical What medication is given for refractory anaphylaxis in a patient on beta-blockers? Glucagon Clinical What is the common bacteria-produced mediator in Scombroid poisoning? Histamine mimics allergic reaction Concept Which hypersensitivity type is T-cell mediated Delayed ? Type 4 Concept What is the benefit of using H1 and H2 blockers together? They work in synergy to resolve cutaneous symptoms better than H1 blockers alone. Clinical Identify 3 types of high-risk patients for anaphylaxis death. CHF CKD Asthma and those on Beta-blockers. Clinical BandB Immunology > Clinical > Hypersensitivity FirstAid Immunology > Immune Responses > Hypersensitivity Types Bootcamp Immunology > Hypersensitivities Pixorize Immunology > Hypersensitivity Reactions Physeo Immunology > Immunology > Hypersensitivity Reactions match Type 1 Hypersensitivity resources AnKing Step 1 v11 B&B 12 Immunology 02 Clinical 01 Hypersensitivity match Anaphylaxis resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid 02 Immunology 03 Immune Responses 13 Hypersensitivity Types match Scombroid Poisoning resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid 03 Microbiology 06 Systems 15 Food Poisoning match Transfusion Reactions resources AnKing Step 1 v11 B&B 12 Immunology 02 Clinical 03 Transplant & Transfusion Epinephrine Timing Epinephrine is the only evidence-proven medication to save a life in anaphylaxis; do not delay its administration while waiting for antihistamines. Airway Vigilance If a patient s tongue swelling is progressing intubate early to prevent the need for a cricothyroidotomy. H1 + H2 Synergy Using H1 blockers Benadryl and H2 blockers Pepcid together resolves hives and itching more effectively than using either alone. The Thigh Target Always give IM epinephrine in the lateral thigh quadriceps to ensure reliable systemic absorption. Beta-Blocker Refractoriness Patients on beta-blockers may not respond to epinephrine; use glucagon to bypass the inhibited receptors. Biphasic Awareness Always warn patients who had anaphylaxis about the risk of a secondary reaction biphasic within 48 hours. mindMap Hypersensitivity Reactions Type 1 Immediate IgE - Mechanism IgE cross-linking on Mast cells -> Histamine/Leukotrienes - Phases Early Immediate and Late ~6 hours / Biphasic - Clinical Anaphylaxis Asthma Hives Urticaria - Treatment IM Epinephrine 0.3-0.5mg thigh Airway protection IV fluids Glucagon if on Beta-blockers Type 2 Cytotoxic IgG/IgM - Mechanism Antibody binds cell surface Ag -> Complement/MAC -> Lysis - Clinical ABO Transfusion reactions Goodpasture syndrome Type 3 Immune Complex - Mechanism Circulating Ag-AB complexes deposit in vessels -> C5a -> Neutrophils -> Endothelial damage - Clinical Serum Sickness SLE Post-strep glomerulonephritis Type 4 Delayed Cell-mediated - Mechanism T-cells -> Cytokines -> Macrophage activation - Clinical Contact Dermatitis Poison Ivy PPD test MS"}, {"id": "l141", "title": "Immunogenetics, Lymphocyte Activation, Immune Reg. and Tolerance 1", "module": "Microbiology", "content": "id l141 module Microbiology metadata l141 Immunogenetics Lymphocyte Activation Immune Reg. and Tolerance 1 lecturer B. Hickey M.D. session Lecture 141 Immunology readingTime 10-15 min resources pdf content/L141 HighYield Render.pdf analysis content/L141 HighYield Render Analysis.md BandB Immunology > Basic > T-cells FirstAid Immunology > Cellular > Major Histocompatibility Complex I & II Bootcamp Immunology > T-cells > Major Histocompatibility Complex Pixorize Immunology > B & T Cells > MHC I & II Physeo Immunology > Immunology > Major Histocompatibility Complexes match MHC Class I & II resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid 02 Immunology 02 Cellular 02 Major Histocompatibility Complex I & II match T-cell Activation resources AnKing Step 1 v11 B&B 12 Immunology 01 Basic 02 T-cells match Superantigens resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid 03 Microbiology 01 Basic Bacteriology 19 Bacteria with Exotoxins match Toxic Shock Syndrome resources AnKing Step 1 v11 B&B 13 Infectious Disease 01 Basics of Microbiology 05 Virulence match Transplant Rejection resources AnKing Step 1 v11 B&B 12 Immunology 02 Clinical 03 Transplant & Transfusion Lecture 141 Immunogenetics Lymphocyte Activation Immune Reg. and Tolerance 1 Lecturer B. Hickey M.D. Session Lecture 141 Introduction The Rules of Immune Engagement Slide 2-3 The adaptive immune system operates under a set of non-negotiable rules designed to solve a fundamental biological problem how to maintain a potent defense while preventing self-destruction. Unlike the innate immune system which responds to broad patterns of danger the adaptive system—specifically T-cells—requires a high degree of precision. Understanding these rules is not an exercise in memorization but an exploration of the logic that prevents inappropriate or harmful immune activation. If you understand the constraints the details of mechanism will naturally follow. Slide 2 The Conceptual Framework - Non-negotiable Rules These determine what the immune system can see and more importantly what it is incapable of responding to. - Problem-Mechanism Logic Every immunological mechanism solves a specific problem e.g. How do I see inside a cell without destroying it? . - Safety Over Efficiency The system consistently prioritizes controlled activation over rapid indiscriminate responses. T-Cells as Molecular Inspectors Slide 4-5 T-cells serve as the specialized surveillance unit of the immune system primarily focused on detecting threats that originate inside cells—namely viral infections and malignant transformation. This task is fundamentally different from that of antibodies which operate in the extracellular space. T-cells do not bind free antigen; instead they function as inspectors that scan reported protein fragments. Slide 4 The Safety Checkpoint - Context Over Presence If T-cells bound free antigen directly they would likely trigger responses to harmless molecules like food antigens commensal microbes or cellular debris. - Processing Requirement Requiring antigen processing inserts a critical safety checkpoint. Another cell must capture process and present the antigen in a standardized format. - Standardized Reporting Cells report their internal health by displaying fragments of their proteins on the surface. highyield The Inspection Paradigm T-cells don t hunt for antigens in the blood like antibodies do; they interrogate the surface of other cells to verify that the internal protein production is consistent with health. If they see non-self or abnormal self peptides they take action. The MHC Standardized Reporting Platform Slide 6 9 The platform responsible for this reporting is the Major Histocompatibility Complex MHC known in humans as Human Leukocyte Antigens HLA . Rather than acting like receptors MHC molecules serve as standardized display platforms that hold peptides for T-cell inspection. Slide 6 MHC Restriction - The Two-Part Check A T-cell receptor TCR recognizes a specific peptide only when it is bound to a specific MHC molecule. This is called MHC Restriction . Slide 9 - Personalized Logic Your T-cells are educated in the thymus to operate specifically in the context of your own MHC alleles. - Diversity as Survival The MHC locus is highly polymorphic. This variation ensures that a pathogen cannot easily escape immune detection in an entire population even if it evades a specific individual’s display machinery. Slide 8 MHC Class I Intracellular Surveillance Slide 11 17 MHC Class I molecules enable the continuous monitoring of the intracellular environment. They are expressed on all nucleated cells which effectively means every cell with a nucleus is constantly reporting a sample of its internal protein content to the immune system. Slide 11 Mechanism of Class I Display 1. Proteasomal Degradation Routine cellular housekeeping the proteasome breaks down internal proteins self or viral into peptides. 2. TAP Transport Peptides enter the Endoplasmic Reticulum ER via the TAP transporter. 3. Loading and Stability Peptides bind to MHC Class I in the ER. Empty MHC Class I molecules are unstable and are not transported to the surface. 4. Surface Expression The stable peptide-MHC complex moves to the surface for interrogation by CD8+ T-cells . highyield Continuous Reporting Antigen reporting via Class I is non-optional and continuous. Cells cannot hide their internal status. The absence of MHC I expression a common viral/tumor evasion strategy is itself a signal for the immune system e.g. by Natural Killer cells to take notice. Slide 17 MHC Class II Systemic Coordination Slide 18 19 22 While MHC I is for localized targeted cellular decisions kill the infected cell MHC Class II is for systemic coordination . It focuses on the extracellular environment and is restricted to Professional Antigen-Presenting Cells APCs Dendritic cells Macrophages and B-cells. Slide 19 The Logistics of Coordination - Internalization Extracellular antigens are internalized via phagocytosis or endocytosis. - Vesicular Separation These antigens are degraded in specialized vesicles keeping them separated from the intracellular MHC I pathway. - The Invariant Chain and CLIP To prevent accidental loading of intracellular peptides in the ER Class II molecules are protected by an Invariant Chain . As they move toward endosomes the chain is degraded until only a small fragment CLIP remains. Slide 22 - HLA-DM This molecule facilitates the removal of CLIP and the loading of stable extracellular-derived peptides. highyield The Role of CD4+ T-Cells CD4+ cells recognize Class II signals and act as directors rather than executioners. They don t kill the target cell; they release cytokines to coordinate macrophages B-cells and cytotoxic responses. Signal 2 The Logic of Co-stimulation Slide 30 31 32 Recognition of an antigen Signal 1 is necessary but never sufficient for T-cell activation. To prevent accidental triggers from noise or self-peptides the system requires a Second Signal Co-stimulation . Slide 30-31 The Safeguard Mechanism - Signal 1 TCR-MHC/Peptide binding provides specificity . - Signal 2 Co-stimulatory ligand interaction provides context . - Professional Authority Only professional APCs have the machinery to provide both signals. This ensures that the decision to activate a naive T-cell is centralized and regulated. Slide 32 correlate Tolerance and Anergy Slide 33 If a T-cell recognizes an antigen Signal 1 without co-stimulation Signal 2 it enters a state of persistent unresponsiveness called Anergy . This is a major form of Peripheral Tolerance ensuring that T-cells that escape central thymic selection are silenced if they encounter self-antigens in a non-inflammatory context. Clinical Disruptions Superantigens and Transplantation Slide 40 41 46 The rules of antigen presentation and co-stimulation provide safety but they also create system vulnerabilities that pathogens and clinical procedures can exploit. Superantigens The Shortcut Superantigens e.g. TSST-1 from Staph aureus bypass the normal rules of peptide specificity. They bind to the outside of the MHC II molecule and the TCR simultaneously cross-linking them regardless of what peptide is present. Slide 40 - Massive Activation Instead of the typical 0.001% of T-cells being activated 20-30% may fire simultaneously. - Cytokine Storm This results in massive release of IL-2 TNF-alpha and IFN-gamma leading to Toxic Shock Syndrome . Slide 41 Transplantation The Foreign Platform In transplantation the problem is not a shortcut but a complete non-self platform. - Direct Allorecognition Recipient T-cells see the donor’s MHC molecule itself as foreign independent of the peptide it holds. - Indirect Allorecognition Recipient APCs process donor MHC molecules and present them to recipient T-cells as non-self peptides. - Matching Importance This is why HLA matching is critical—to minimize the degree of foreignness perceived by the recipient s immune inspectors. Slide 46 mnemonic MHC Class Rule of 8 8 = n x m - MHC I x CD8 = 8 All nucleated cells intracellular - MHC II x CD4 = 8 APCs only extracellular Summary Table MHC I vs. MHC II Slide 10 41 | Feature | MHC Class I | MHC Class II | | --- | --- | --- | | Expression | All nucleated cells | Professional APCs only | | Pathway | Intracellular / Endogenous | Extracellular / Exogenous | | T-Cell Target | CD8+ Cytotoxic | CD4+ Helper/Director | | Peptide Source | Proteasome -> TAP -> ER | Phagolysosome | | Key Accessory | Beta-2 Microglobulin | Invariant Chain / HLA-DM | | Logic | Precision Killing | Systemic Coordination | | Restriction | MHC I Restricted | MHC II Restricted | questions 1 A research team is studying a rare genetic mutation where the TA P transporter is non-functional. Which of the following immunologic outcomes is most likely expected in these patients? Inability of CD4+ T-cells to coordinate antibody production Loss of MHC Class I expression on the surface of nucleated cells Failure of the Invariant Chain to degrade in endosomes Increased risk of cytokine storm due to superantigen bypass Enhanced peripheral tolerance via T-cell anergy 1 TAP is responsible for transporting peptides from the cytosol into the Endoplasmic Reticulum ER for MHC Class I loading. Since loading is required for MHC Class I stability and surface transport a TAP deficiency results in a failure to display MHC I leading to impaired CD8+ T-cell surveillance. 2 A 19-year-old female presents with rapid onset high fever hypotension and a diffuse sunburn-like rash. She is diagnosed with Toxic Shock Syndrome. Which of the following best describes the molecular mechanism of this condition? Binding of free antigen to naive T-cell receptors without costimulation Indirect allorecognition of toxic peptides by recipient APCs Simultaneous binding of a toxin to the outside of MHC II and the TCR Defective HLA-DM function leading to persistent CLIP binding Selective downregulation of MHC Class I expression in macrophages 2 Toxic Shock Syndrome is caused by superantigens e.g. TSST-1 which cross-link the TCR and MHC II outside the peptide-binding groove. This bypasses the normal requirement for antigen processing and peptide specificity leading to massive non-specific T-cell activation and a cytokine storm. 3 A helper T-cell recognizes a self-peptide displayed on a dendritic cell. However because the tissue is not inflamed the dendritic cell does not express B7 co-stimulatory molecules. What is the most likely fate of this T-cell? Upregulation of IL-2 receptors leading to proliferation Induction of the Pac-Man phagocytic response Progression to targeted cytotoxic killing of the dendritic cell Entry into a state of long-term functional unresponsiveness anergy Re-entry into thymic selection for further education 3 Antigen recognition Signal 1 without co-stimulation Signal 2 leads to anergy. This is a critical mechanism of peripheral tolerance that prevents autoimmunity when T-cells encounter self-antigens in a non-inflammatory context. 4 Which molecule is specifically responsible for preventing the premature loading of intracellular peptides onto MHC Class II during its synthesis in the Endoplasmic Reticulum? Beta-2 microglobulin TAP Transporter Invariant Chain HLA-DM CLIP 2 The Invariant Chain Ii physically blocks the peptide-binding groove of MHC Class II in the ER. It also provides a targeting signal for transport to endosomal compartments. CLIP is the remaining fragment of this chain after partial degradation. 5 What is the primary reason why every nucleated cell expresses MHC Class I while MHC Class II is restricted to a small subset of immune cells? MHC I is less stable and requires universal expression to maintain tissue integrity Every cell is a potential site for viral replication and must be capable of signaling its status to cytotoxic cells Professional APCs must centralized MHC I to prevent systemic autoimmunity MHC II restriction to APCs enables the bypass of superantigen mechanisms Class II expression requires the proteasome which is only found in hematopoietic cells 1 Class I surveillance is universal because any cell with a nucleus can be infected by a virus or undergo malignant transformation. Class II is restricted to APCs because it involves systemic coordination e.g. helper T-cell activation which must be tightly regulated to avoid unnecessary inflammation. What does MHC Restriction mean? A T-cell recognizes a peptide only when presented by a specific usually self MHC molecule. Concept Which cell types express MHC Class I? All nucleated cells. Glossary Which cell types express MHC Class II? Professional APCs Dendritic cells Macrophages B-cells . Glossary What is the Signal 2 required for T-cell activation? Co-stimulation e.g. B7-CD28 interaction . Concept What happens when Signal 1 is received without Signal 2? Anergy long-term functional unresponsiveness . Concept What is the function of the Invariant Chain? Blocks MHC II groove in the ER and targets it to endosomes. Glossary What molecule facilitates removal of CLIP for antigen loading? HLA-DM. Glossary Which T-cells recognize MHC Class I? CD8+ Cytotoxic T-cells . Concept Which T-cells recognize MHC Class II? CD4+ Helper T-cells . Concept How do superantigens cause Toxic Shock Syndrome? By cross-linking MHC II and TCR outside the peptide groove triggering massive non-specific cytokine release. Clinical What is TAP? Transporter Associated with Antigen Processing; moves peptides into the ER for MHC I loading. Glossary Difference between Direct vs Indirect Allorecognition? Direct Recipient TCR recognizes intact donor MHC. Indirect Donor MHC processed and presented as foreign peptide. Concept Inspection Paradigm T-cells don t look for floating antigens; they inspect cellular surfaces to verify healthy protein production. The Rule of 8 MHC I x CD8 = 8; MHC II x CD4 = 8. A simple way to remember restriction pairs. Continuous MHC I Antigen display on MHC I is non-optional; cells must report their internal status or be targeted for lack of presentation. Signal 2 Context Signal 2 provides context—it tells the T-cell that the antigen recognition is happening in a state of danger/inflammation. Superantigen Shortcuts Superantigens reveal a system vulnerability by bypassing regulated processing to force activation. Matching for Match MHC matching in transplants minimizes the perceived foreignness of the donor s cell-surface machinery."}, {"id": "l142", "title": "Immunogenetics, Lymphocyte Activation, Immune Reg. and Tolerance 2", "module": "Unknown", "content": "id l142 module Unknown metadata l142 Immunogenetics Lymphocyte Activation Immune Reg. and Tolerance 2 lecturer B. Hickey M.D. session Lecture 142 Immunology readingTime 12-18 min resources pdf content/L142 HighYield Render.pdf analysis content/L142 HighYield Render Analysis.md BandB Immunology > Basic > T-cells FirstAid Immunology > Cellular > Lymphocyte Activation & Regulation Pathoma Immune System > Transplantation & Tolerance SketchyPath 02 Immune System 01 Transplantation & Tolerance Bootcamp Immunology > T-cells > Selection & Tolerance match T-cell Activation resources AnKing Step 1 v11 B&B 12 Immunology 01 Basic 02 T-cells match Tolerance & Anergy resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid 02 Immunology 02 Cellular 03 Tolerance & Anergy match Regulatory T Cells resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid 02 Immunology 02 Cellular 04 Regulatory T Cells match Transplant Rejection resources AnKing Step 1 v11 B&B 12 Immunology 02 Clinical 03 Transplant & Transfusion match HLA Associations resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid 02 Immunology 01 Principles 05 MHC & HLA Lecture 142 Immunogenetics Lymphocyte Activation Immune Reg. and Tolerance 2 Lecturer B. Hickey M.D. Session Lecture 142 Introduction The Lifecycle of an Immune Response Slide 2-3 The immune system s primary challenge is not just identifying a threat but reacting to it with judicial force—clearing the pathogen while preserving the host s structural integrity. While the previous lecture focused on the initial recognition of antigen this session explores the downstream logic that follows a successful activation decision. From the rare starting clones to the massive effector armies and their eventual necessary dissolution every phase is a calculated trade-off between power and restraint. Understanding these checkpoints is essential for diagnosing and treating immune-mediated diseases where these control loops have failed. Slide 2 The Conceptual Roadmap - Functional Outcomes Activation is only the trigger; the response must then expand differentiate and eventually resolve. Slide 3 - Problem-Logic Anchors Every step solves a logistical problem e.g. How do I amplify a rare specific cell? and offers a safer alternative to uncontrolled activity. - The Central Theme Power requires restraint. Specificity gives the immune system its lethal power but regulation provides the necessary safety to the organism. Clonal Selection and the Quantitative Problem Slide 4-5 Adaptive immunity relies on the principle of clonal selection where only lymphocytes possessing receptors specific to a given antigen are invited to participate in the response. This ensures that the immune system doesn t waste energy on irrelevant targets but it creates a massive logistical bottleneck at the inception of an infection antigen-specific cells are vanishingly rare. If recognition were the only requirement the system would be precise but mathematically incapable of altering the course of a disease. Slide 4 The Necessity of Expansion - Amplification Requirement To have a clinical impact these rare precursors must be amplified into large populations of identical daughter cells. Slide 5 - Clearing the Threat A single lymphocyte cannot coordinate a systemic defense or clear a robust viral load; expansion generates the sufficient force required at the site of infection. - The Cost of Power Because rapid expansion is energetically expensive and carries the risk of mutation or loss of control it is restricted to cells that have cleared multiple safety checkpoints. Clonal Expansion Metabolic Reprogramming Slide 6 12-14 Once the decision to expand is made the lymphocyte must undergo a profound metabolic transformation. Resting lymphocytes are designed for energy efficiency and survival operating in a quiet state. To enter the cell cycle and divide rapidly they must abandon this conservationist posture in favor of an aggressive biosynthetic program. This metabolic shift is not a passive byproduct of activation but a required license to divide that the immune system uses as a regulatory checkpoint. Slide 12 The Metabolic License - Metabolic Shift Activated cells shift towards increased glucose uptake and protein synthesis to support the demands of rapid mitosis. Slide 14 - Biosynthesis Proliferation requires the massive production of lipids nucleic acids and proteins placing extraordinary demands on the cell s energy stores. - Safety Restriction This license to divide is rescinded the moment inflammatory signals wane preventing the system from producing unnecessary effector cells. Signals Driving Expansion Slide 6 15 - TCR-Mediated Signaling Antigen recognition Signal 1 provides the initial go but is insufficient on its own. - Co-stimulation Signal 2 e.g. B7-CD28 provides the context confirming that the antigen was found in a dangerous or inflammatory setting. Slide 6 - Cytokine Reinforcement Cytokines like IL-2 provide Signal 3 which promotes survival and acts as the primary fuel for the expansion engine. Slide 15 Differentiation Assigning Functional Identity Slide 7-8 16-18 As lymphocytes expand they do not remain identical precursors; they must differentiate into specialized working units. This differentiation determines the flavor of the immune response tailoring the output to the specific nature of the pathogen. A viral infection in the cytosol requires a different toolset than a fungal infection in the extracellular space. This functional specialization allows the immune system to deploy its resources with the precision of a surgeon rather than the blunt force of a sledgehammer. Slide 7 Specialized Effector Programs - Effector Commitment The fate of a cell helper vs. cytotoxic is often determined early during the expansion phase not after it is complete. Slide 16 - CD8+ Differentiation Activated CD8+ cells differentiate into cytotoxic effectors CTLs acquiring the intracellular machinery granzymes perforin required to kill infected host cells. Slide 17 - CD4+ Differentiation CD4+ cells differentiate into various helper subsets e.g. Th1 Th2 Th17 each specialized in coordinating a specific branch of immunity. Slide 18 Functional Diversity Slide 8 - Tailored Responses The diversity of effector programs ensures that the immune response is appropriate to the threat e.g. Th1 for intracellular pathogens Th2 for helminths . - Effectiveness vs. Safety Differentiation ensures that the right kind of damage is done to the pathogen while minimizing the wrong kind of damage to the host. Slide 19 Contraction The Resolution of the Response Slide 10 21-23 29-30 No immune response can continue indefinitely. Once the antigen burden is reduced and the threat is controlled maintaining a massive army of short-lived effector cells becomes a liability. These cells are metabolically expensive and if left unrestrained can drive chronic inflammation and permanent tissue scarring. The system therefore utilizes an active contraction phase to restore homeostasis. Slide 10 Mechanics of Contraction - Programmed Cell Death The majority of expanded effector cells undergo apoptosis PCD once the survival signals antigen and cytokines are withdrawn. Slide 22 - Restoring Balance This process restores the baseline state of the immune system clearing the clutter to make room for future responses. - Intentionality Contraction is not a failure of the response; it is a sign of success. Its absence is a hallmark of pathology. Slide 23 correlate Chronic Inflammation Slide 30 - Pathophysiology Failure of the contraction phase leads to persistent effector activity. - Presentation Tissue damage fibrosis and progressive loss of organ function. - Lecturer s Point In chronic inflammatory diseases the therapeutic goal is often to restart the contraction loop or apply the brakes that the system failed to deploy. Immunologic Memory A Deliberate Strategy Slide 9 24-28 While the majority of effector cells are eliminated during contraction a small elite subset is programmed to persist long-term as memory cells. This is not an accidental byproduct of activation but a deliberate evolutionary strategy for long-term preparedness. Memory cells represent the hard-earned wisdom of the immune system allowing for a response that is faster stronger and more efficient upon subsequent encounters with the same pathogen. Slide 9 Features of Memory - Selective Survival Memory cells are metabolically and transcriptionally distinct from effectors optimized for longevity and rapid recall. Slide 24 - Rapid Recall Upon re-exposure memory cells require less co-stimulation than naive cells allowing them to fire much faster. Slide 27 - Homeostatic Maintenance Unlike effectors memory cells survive long after the antigen is gone maintained by cytokines like IL-7 and IL-15 . Slide 28 highyield The Memory Advantage Immune memory allows the system to bypass the quantitative bottleneck clonal selection delay during re-exposure. Because the clones are already expanded and educated the secondary response can clear a pathogen before it causes clinical illness. Self-Tolerance Ensuring Personal Safety Slide 11 31-35 The power of an expanded immune response requires an equally powerful system of tolerance to prevent attacks against self-tissues. Tolerance is established and maintained through a multi-layered approach beginning in the primary lymphoid organs central tolerance and continuing throughout the lifespan of the lymphocyte in the periphery peripheral tolerance . This redundant design reflects the critical importance of preventing autoimmunity in complex multicellular organisms. Slide 32 Central Tolerance The First Filter - T-Cell Selection Thymus Developing T-cells are tested for their ability to recognize self-MHC positive selection and their tendency to react too strongly to self-antigens negative selection . Slide 34 - Negative Selection Clones that bind self-antigens with high affinity are induced to undergo apoptosis pruning the most dangerous clones before they ever leave the thymus. Slide 35 Peripheral Tolerance The Second Filter Slide 36-37 - Dynamic Control Because central selection is incomplete by necessity it can t show every self-antigen peripheral mechanisms provide ongoing surveillance. - Anergy If a lymphocyte recognizes antigen Signal 1 without the context of inflammation Signal 2 it enters a state of functional unresponsiveness. Slide 37 - B7/CD28 vs. CTLA-4 The balance between activating signals CD28 and inhibitory signals CTLA-4 and PD-1 determines whether a peripheral encounter leads to activation or suppression. Regulatory T-Cells Active Immune Restraint Slide 38-41 Tolerance is not merely the absence of a response; it is often maintained through the active suppression of potential self-reactives by Regulatory T-cells Tregs . Tregs serve as the peacekeeping force of the immune system constantly monitoring self-tissues and applying molecular brakes to keep effector cells in check. This active restraint is essential for the lifelong stability of the immune system. Slide 38 Treg Identity and Function - FoxP3 Transcription Factor The development and suppressive function of Tregs are governed by the FoxP3 gene. Slide 39 - Suppression Mechanisms Tregs produce inhibitory cytokines TGF-beta IL-10 and can physically disrupt the interaction between APCs and effector cells. correlate IPEX Syndrome Slide 40 - Pathophysiology Mutation in the FoxP3 gene leading to a complete absence of functional Tregs. - Presentation Severe early-onset multi-organ autoimmunity Enteropathy Polyendocrinopathy X-linked . - Lecturer s Point IPEX is the classic experiment of nature demonstrating that without active Treg suppression the immune system will inevitably destroy its own host. B-Cell Tolerance Specific Layers Slide 42-46 Like T-cells B-cells must also be educated to avoid self-reactivity. While they share many principles of central and peripheral tolerance B-cells possess a unique mechanism called Receptor Editing which allows them to retake the exam if they fail the initial test of self-reactivity. Slide 42 B-Cell Selection - Central B-Cell Tolerance Bone Marrow Strongly self-reactive B-cells are either eliminated or given a chance to rearrange their light chains again—an process called Receptor Editing . Slide 43-44 - Peripheral B-Cell Tolerance B-cells that escape the bone marrow but recognize self-antigen without T-cell help Signal 2 undergo anergy or apoptosis. - The T-Cell Help Checkpoint Most high-affinity antibody responses require T-cell help. If a self-reactive B-cell cannot find a corresponding T-cell helper which has already been pruned by T-cell tolerance the B-cell cannot produce autoantibodies. Slide 46 Transplantation Immunology Slide 47-51 Transplantation represents a unique immunologic challenge where a non-self platform the donor tissue is introduced and expected to persist. In this clinical context the rules of tolerance and recognition work against the host s interests as the immune system accurately identifies the transplant as a foreign threat. Success in transplantation requires the deliberate tricking or suppression of these fundamental immune programs. Slide 47 Allo-recognition Pathways - Direct Allorecognition Recipient T-cells see the intact donor MHC molecules as foreign objects. This accounts for the initial robust rejection response. Slide 48 - Indirect Allorecognition Recipient APCs process the donor MHC and present fragments of it as foreign peptides on the recipient s own MHC. This drives the slower chronic rejection process. Types of Rejection Slide 49 | Type | Timing | Mechanism | Notes | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | Hyperacute | Minutes to Hours | Pre-formed antibodies against donor ABO or HLA | Type II Hypersensitivity; unavoidable if not screened. | | Acute | Days to Months | Cell-mediated CD8+ T-cells and Antibody-mediated | Infiltrate of T-cells and neutrophilic damage. | | Chronic | Months to Years | T-cell mediated Indirect pathway & vascular damage | Fibrosis ischemia and gradual loss of graft function. | correlate Acute Rejection - Pathophysiology Recognition of donor MHC by recipient CD8+ and CD4+ T-cells. - Diagnosis Biopsy showing lymphocytic infiltration of the graft. - Treatment Intensified immunosuppression e.g. high-dose steroids anti-T-cell antibodies . - Lecturer s Point Acute rejection is a normal healthy immune response to foreign tissue; transplantation success is defined by our ability to suppress this normal response. Slide 50 Summary Table Tolerance Checkpoints Slide 54 | Level | Mechanism | Focus | Outcome of Failure | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | Central | Negative Selection | High-affinity self-reactivity in bone marrow/thymus | Immediate systemic autoimmunity | | B-Cell Unique | Receptor Editing | Light chain rearrangement | Decreased self-reactivity without cell loss | | Peripheral | Anergy | Antigen recognition without co-stimulation Signal 2 | Activation by harmless molecules | | Active | Tregs FoxP3 | Active suppression of effectors | Multi-organ autoimmune syndromes IPEX | questions 1 A 3-week-old male infant presents with severe diarrhea a diffuse eczematous rash and elevated blood glucose. Genetic testing reveals a mutation in the forkhead box P3 FoxP3 transcription factor. Which of the following is the most likely underlying immunologic defect in this patient? Failure of light chain rearrangement during B-cell development Loss of active suppression by Regulatory T-cells Inability of CD8+ T-cells to acquire cytotoxic machinery Defective MHC Class I surface expression on nucleated cells Impaired migration of naive T-cells to the thymus 1 This patient has IPEX Syndrome Immune dysregulation Polyendocrinopathy Enteropathy X-linked which is caused by a mutation in FoxP3 . FoxP3 is essential for the development and function of Regulatory T-cells Tregs which actively suppress immune responses. Without Tregs the patient experiences systemic severe autoimmunity. 2 A 45-year-old female undergoes a kidney transplant. Ten minutes after the surgeon releases the vascular clamps the graft becomes blue cold and mottled. Which of the following immunologic mechanisms is responsible for this occurrence? Activation of recipient CD8+ T-cells by donor MHC molecules Processing of donor antigens by recipient dendritic cells Binding of pre-formed host antibodies to the graft endothelium Massive cytokine release from bystander macrophages Delayed-type hypersensitivity reaction involving Th17 cells 2 This is a Hyperacute Rejection which occurs within minutes to hours. It is mediated by pre-formed antibodies Type II Hypersensitivity in the recipient that recognize the donor s ABO or HLA antigens. This leads to immediate complement activation and thrombosis of graft vessels. 3 During a secondary immune response to a viral infection the host clears the pathogen much faster than during the initial exposure. This efficiency is primarily attributed to memory T-cells which differ from naive T-cells in that memory cells Are only capable of acknowledging Signal 1 Antigen Express MHC Class II to communicate with macrophages Have higher metabolic rates while in the resting state Require significantly less co-stimulation for activation Undergo receptor editing if they encounter self-antigen 3 One of the key functional advantages of memory cells is that they have a lower threshold for activation compared to naive cells. Specifically they require less co-stimulation Signal 2 to become activated and proliferate allowing for a more rapid and robust recall response. 4 A T-lymphocyte escapes negative selection in the thymus and enters the periphery. It eventually recognizes its cognate self-antigen displayed on a resting macrophage that does not express B7. The T-cell becomes persistently unresponsive even upon subsequent encounters with the antigen. This state is known as Clonal Selection Receptor Editing Anergy Homeostasis Effector Commitment 2 Anergy is a form of peripheral tolerance that occurs when a T-cell receives Signal 1 Antigen recognition but is denied Signal 2 Co-stimulation . This results in the cell becoming functionally inactive and unresponsive even if encounters the antigen again in an inflammatory context. 5 Which of the following processes occurs uniquely during B-cell development allowing for the salvage of self-reactive clones that would otherwise be eliminated? FoxP3-mediated suppression Metabolic reprogramming Negative selection in the thymus Receptor editing Clonal expansion within the bone marrow 3 Receptor editing is unique to B-cells . During development in the bone marrow if a B-cell receptor binds self-antigen too strongly the cell can \\ edit\\ its receptor by initiating additional light chain gene rearrangements. This provides an alternative to apoptosis that allows potentially useful cells to stay in the pool if they can lose their self-reactivity. What is Clonal Selection ? The concept that only lymphocytes specific to an antigen are activated and proliferate. Concept Why is Clonal Expansion essential? To amplify rare antigen-specific cells into a clinically effective army. Concept What metabolic shift occurs during lymphocyte activation? Switch to increased glucose uptake and biosynthesis to support rapid division. Concept What defines the Signal 3 for expansion? Cytokines e.g. IL-2 which promote survival and mitogenesis. Concept Difference between CD8+ and CD4+ differentiation? CD8+ acquire killing machinery CTLs ; CD4+ specialize into helper subsets Th1 Th2 etc. . Concept What is the primary driver of the Contraction phase? Withdrawal of antigen and survival signals leading to apoptosis of effectors. Concept How are Memory cells maintained long-term? Through homeostatic signals from cytokines like IL-7 and IL-15. Concept Define Central Tolerance . Removal of self-reactive clones during development in thymus or bone marrow. Glossary What is the unique B-cell survival strategy for self-reactivity? Receptor Editing re-arranging light chain genes . Concept What happens when Signal 1 occurs without Signal 2? Anergy functional unresponsiveness . Clinical What is the clinical hallmark of FoxP3 deficiency? IPEX Syndrome severe multi-organ autoimmunity . Clinical What is Direct Allorecognition ? Recipient T-cells recognizing intact donor MHC as foreign. Concept Mechanism of Hyperacute Rejection? Pre-formed antibodies against graft antigens causing vascular thrombosis. Clinical Mechanism of Chronic Rejection? Indirect allorecognition leading to vascular fibrosis and organ ischemia. Clinical Power-Restraint Balance The adaptive immune system is defined by its ability to amplify a tiny signal into a massive response but its survival depends on the multi-layered braking systems Tolerance/Regulation that prevent self-destruction. Metabolic License Rapid proliferation is not an automatic right; cells must earn a metabolic license via Signal 1 + 2 confirmation before they are permitted to consume massive resources for division. Contraction is Victory The death of effector cells at the end of a response is not an error but a sign of successful pathogen clearance and active restoration of organ health. Memory Wisdom Memory is a recall bias that trades naive flexibility for experienced intensity ensuring the second encounter with a threat is definitively settled. FoxP3 Checkpoint Regulatory T-cells Tregs are the proactive enforcement of peace within the body; without their FoxP3-mediated supervision the immune system defaults to auto-destruction IPEX . Tricking the inspector Transplantation success is the process of convincing molecular inspectors T-cells to tolerate a foreign platform donor MHC that their fundamental education says shouldn t be there. mindmap Immune Response Lifecycle Clonal Selection & Expansion - Principle Only specific cells selected Slide 4 - Problem precursors are too rare to clear infection Slide 13 - Mechanism Metabolic shift to high glucose/biosynthesis Slide 14 - Signals 1 TCR 2 Co-stim 3 IL-2/Cytokines Slide 15 Differentiation & Effector Fate - Specialization Short-lived working units Slide 16 - CD8+ CTLs Acquisition of granzymes/perforin Slide 17 - CD4+ Helpers Functional identity Th1 vs Th2 determined early Slide 18 Contraction & Homeostasis - Mechanism Apoptosis of effectors after antigen removal Slide 22 - Importance Prevents chronic injury and saves resources Slide 23 - Failure Leads to chronic inflammatory states Slide 30 Immunologic Memory - Strategy Intentional longevity of early survivors Slide 9 - Recall Lower activation threshold; faster secondary fire Slide 27 - Survival IL-7 and IL-15 maintenance independently of antigen Slide 28 Self-Tolerance Mechanisms Central Tolerance - Site Thymus T and Bone Marrow B Slide 33 - T-Selection Positive recognize self MHC & Negative strong self-binding = kill Slide 34-35 - B-Strategy Receptor Editing - light chain salvage mechanism Slide 43-44 Peripheral Tolerance - Checkpoints Antigen recognition without co-stimulation Slide 37 - Outcome Anergy or functional silencing Slide 37 - T-Cell Help B-cells need Signal 2 from T-cells to produce antibodies Slide 46 Active Suppression Tregs - Defining Gene FoxP3 Slide 39 - Function Produce TGF-beta/IL-10; suppress APCs Slide 38 - Deficiency IPEX Syndrome Slide 40 Transplantation Immunology Allo-recognition - Direct Recipient T-cells bind intact donor MHC Slide 48 - Indirect Recipient APCs present donor MHC fragments Slide 48 Graft Rejection - Hyperacute Pre-formed Abs minutes Slide 49 - Acute CD8/CD4 T-cell infiltration days/months Slide 49 - Chronic Indirect pathway; fibrosis and ischemia months/years Slide 49"}, {"id": "l143", "title": "Pharmacology Immunosuppressants Part 1", "module": "Pharmacology", "content": "id l143 module Pharmacology metadata l143 Pharmacology Immunosuppressants Part 1 lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. session Lecture 143 Pharmacology readingTime 12-18 min resources pdf content/L143 HighYield Render.pdf analysis content/L143 HighYield Render Analysis.md SketchyPharm Immunology & Biochemistry > Immunosuppressives FirstAid Immunology > Pharmacology > Immunosuppressants BandB Immunology > Pharmacology > Immunosuppressive Drugs Pixorize Immunology > Immunosuppressants match Antithymocyte Globulin resources AnKing Step 1 v11 SketchyPharm 06 Immune 02 Immunosuppressants match Alemtuzumab resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid 02 Immunology 04 Immunosuppressants 04 Therapeutic Antibodies Alemtuzumab match Basiliximab resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid 02 Immunology 04 Immunosuppressants 04 Therapeutic Antibodies Basiliximab match Glucocorticoids resources AnKing Step 1 v11 B&B 12 Immunology 03 Pharm 02 Immunosuppressive Drugs match Transplant Rejection resources AnKing Step 1 v11 B&B 12 Immunology 02 Clinical 03 Transplant & Transfusion Lecture 143 Pharmacology Immunosuppressants Part 1 Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. Session Lecture 143 Introduction The Philosophy of Transplantation Slide 6-9 The transition from organ failure to organ recovery represents one of the most significant clinical interventions in modern medicine. However the success of transplantation is fundamentally limited not by surgical technique but by the intrinsic nature of the immune system to recognize and destroy non-self tissue. The pharmacology of immunosuppression is the art of dampening this response without completely disabling the host s ability to fight infection or monitor for malignancy. As Dr. Goldstein emphasizes the goal is to achieve a stable balance—a therapeutic window where the graft is protected and the patient is preserved. Slide 6 Key Conceptual Pillars - The CD4+ Maestro The CD4+ T-cell is the central orchestrator of the immune cascade. It recruits the effector cells CD8+ T-cells macrophages NK cells and B-cells that ultimately execute the damage to the graft. Slide 9 - Allograft Dynamics Most transplants are allografts—tissues from donors who are not genetically identical to the recipient. This mismatch triggers the recognition of foreign HLA class I and II antigens. - Dose-Related Strategy To minimize toxicity clinicians use combinations of drugs that target different points in the immune pathway. This synergy allows for lower individual doses of high-toxicity agents while maintaining effective suppression. Slide 9 highyield The Goal of Suppression Immunosuppressive therapy is divided into two distinct phases Induction the heavy lifting done at the time of transplant to prevent immediate rejection and Maintenance the long-term prophylaxis to prevent chronic failure . The Temporal Nature of Rejection Slide 13-14 Rejection is not a single event but a spectrum of immune reactions occurring at different time intervals. Understanding the timing is critical for selecting the appropriate pharmacological intervention. Slide 13 1. Hyperacute Rejection Minutes to Hours This is a catastrophic event driven by preformed antibodies humoral immunity in the host that recognize antigens on the donor vascular endothelium. - Mechanism Immediate complement activation leading to thrombosis and necrosis. - Pharmacological Note Hyperacute rejection cannot be stopped by immunosuppressant drugs. It must be prevented via HLA matching and ABO compatibility screening. Slide 14 2. Acute Rejection Days to Weeks This is the primary target of induction and early maintenance therapy. It can be cellular T-cell mediated humoral antibody-mediated or both. - Pathology Infiltration of CD8+ T-cells cytotoxic and recruitment of macrophages. Slide 14 - Clinical Pearl A single episode of acute rejection if caught early and treated aggressively with high-dose pulse steroids often results in excellent long-term graft survival. 3. Chronic Rejection Years The slow burn of the immune system. Chronic rejection involves gradual fibrosis and loss of graft function. Its mechanisms are less well understood and it is largely resistant to current intensive immunosuppressive regimens once significant fibrosis has occurred. Slide 14 Induction Therapy The Frontline Biologics Slide 17 23 26 29 Induction therapy is the administration of potent immunosuppressants at the time of the transplant surgery. These agents are designed to stun or deplete the immune system during its period of highest reactivity. Slide 17 Antithymocyte Globulin rATG / Thymoglobulin ATG consists of polyclonal antibodies derived by injecting human thymocytes into animals most commonly rabbits . - Mechanism It binds a wide variety of antigens on the T-cell surface leading to profound T-cell depletion through cytotoxicity and sequestration. Slide 17 - Clinical Use It is the preferred agent for patients at high risk of rejection and is also used to treat steroid-resistant acute rejection. - Adverse Effects - Infusion Reactions Fever chills and muscle pain are common. - Cytokine Release Syndrome CRS Rapid T-cell lysis can release a storm of cytokines. Pre-treatment with steroids and antihistamines is mandatory. Slide 23 Alemtuzumab Anti-CD52 A humanized monoclonal antibody directed against CD52 a glycoprotein expressed on almost all T and B lymphocytes. - Effect Potent and prolonged panlymphocyte depletion T and B cells . - Clinical Use Infrequently used in transplantation but highly effective in Multiple Sclerosis and B-cell CLL. - Toxicity Serious risks of pancytopenia and opportunistic infections. Slide 29 Basiliximab Anti-CD25 A chimeric monoclonal antibody that binds specifically to the IL-2 receptor alpha chain CD25 . - Mechanism Blocks the binding of IL-2 to its receptor preventing T-cell proliferation and cytokine production. - Crucial Distinction Basiliximab is a non-depleting agent . It does not kill T-cells; it just stops them from multiplying. Slide 26 - Clinical Profile Preferred for low-risk patients. It is exceptionally well-tolerated with virtually no cytokine release syndrome. Slide 26 correlate Depleting vs. Non-depleting Agents When choosing an induction agent clinicians must weigh the risk of rejection against the risk of infection. ATG and Alemtuzumab wipe the floor depletion creating a massive open door for infections but providing maximum protection against rejection. Basiliximab closes the door non-depleting providing less aggressive protection but maintaining better host defense. Slide 40 Glucocorticoids The Universal Dampeners Slide 18 33 34 Glucocorticoids GCs remain the backbone of both induction and maintenance regimens. They are the most versatile agents in the transplant toolkit. Slide 18 Double Action Mechanism 1. Gene Regulation GCs bind intracellular receptors and translocate to the nucleus to regulate gene expression suppressing the synthesis of inflammatory cytokines IL-1 IL-2 IL-6 TNF-alpha . 2. Cellular Redistribution They rapidly reduce circulating T-cell populations through redistribution and apoptosis. Slide 11 Therapeutic Applications - Induction High-dose IV Methylprednisolone given with ATG or Basiliximab. - Maintenance/Prophylaxis Oral Prednisone is usually the third component of the standard Triple Therapy CNI + Antimetabolite + GC . Slide 33 - Acute Rejection Treatment Pulse therapy—very high doses of IV Methylprednisolone given over 3-5 days. highyield The Cost of Steroids While indispensable steroids carry a heavy burden of dose-related toxicities. - Acute Impaired wound healing hyperglycemia diabetes and mood changes euphoria/psychosis . - Delayed Cushingoid features buffalo hump moon face osteoporosis and avascular necrosis of the bone. Slide 34 Complications and Long-term Risks Slide 20 39 The long-term success of transplantation is often threatened by the very drugs used to sustain it. Two primary categories of complication dominate the clinical landscape Infection and Malignancy. Slide 20 1. Opportunistic Infections The risk is directly proportional to the intensity and duration of immunosuppression. Bacterial fungal PCP/Pneumocystis viral CMV BK virus and parasitic infections are constant threats. Vaccination MUST be completed prior to transplantation. Slide 20 2. Post-Transplant Lymphoproliferative Disorder PTLD PTLD represents one of the most feared complications of chronic immunosuppression. - The Pathogen Epstein-Barr Virus EBV . - The Logic In healthy individuals EBV remains latent in B-cells kept in check by T-cell surveillance. When T-cells are suppressed especially during induction or with profound lymphopenia EBV can trigger an uncontrolled proliferation of B-cells. - Risk Multipliers The degree of T-cell suppression and the patient s EBV-negative status receiving an EBV-positive organ are major risk factors. Slide 20 correlate The PTLD Clinical Picture PTLD can range from benign polyclonal lymph node enlargement to aggressive monomorphic B-cell lymphoma. Management often involves reducing immunosuppression to allow the immune system to wake up and fight the EBV and using agents like Rituximab anti-CD20 . Summary The Triple Therapy Standard Slide 37-40 For most transplant recipients the maintenance phase is managed using a Triple Therapy regimen. While Part 2 of this lecture series covers the specific maintenance agents in detail the conceptual framework is established here Slide 38 1. Calcineurin Inhibitors e.g. Tacrolimus Inhibit the synthesis of IL-2 Signal 1 pathway . 2. Antimetabolites e.g. Mycophenolate Inhibit the de novo purine synthesis required for T and B-cell proliferation. 3. Glucocorticoids e.g. Prednisone Provide broad anti-inflammatory and cytokine suppression to stabilize the entire system. Slide 40 This multimodal approach allows each drug to be used at a lower dose maximizing the efficacy-to-toxicity ratio and ensuring the longest possible life for the allograft. Slide 37 questions 1 A 45-year-old male receives a kidney transplant. During the procedure the surgeon notices that the graft immediately turns cyanotic and mottled within minutes of reperfusion. Which of the following best describes the management strategy to reverse this process? Administration of high-dose pulse methylprednisolone Addition of Rabbit Antithymocyte Globulin rATG to the regimen Emergent plasmapheresis and Rituximab administration Surgical removal of the graft as the reaction cannot be pharmacologically reversed Immediate switch from Basiliximab to Alemtuzumab induction 3 The scenario describes hyperacute rejection minutes to hours which is caused by preformed antibodies. According to the lecture Slide 14 hyperacute rejection cannot be stopped by immunosuppressant drugs and typically requires graft removal. 2 A transplant recipient is being evaluated for induction therapy. The physician chooses Basiliximab because the patient is at low risk for rejection. What is the fundamental difference between the mechanism of Basiliximab and Antithymocyte Globulin ATG ? Basiliximab causes profound B-cell depletion while ATG targets T-cells Basiliximab is a non-depleting agent that blocks IL-2 signaling whereas ATG causes physical T-cell depletion Basiliximab targets the CD52 glycoprotein while ATG targets MHC Class II ATG is a monoclonal antibody whereas Basiliximab is a polyclonal antiserum Basiliximab is used exclusively for maintenance while ATG is used for induction 1 As highlighted in Slide 17 and 26 Basiliximab is a non-depleting agent that blocks the IL-2 receptor CD25 to prevent proliferation. ATG is a depleting agent that causes a massive drop in circulating T-cell counts through cytotoxicity. 3 Which of the following adverse effects is specifically associated with the rapid T-cell lysis caused by high-dose induction with Antithymocyte Globulin ATG ? Post-transplant diabetes mellitus due to pancreatic toxicity Cytokine Release Syndrome CRS characterized by fever and chills Avascular necrosis of the femoral head Moon face and truncal obesity Cushingoid features Refractory hypertension due to salt retention 1 Rapid infusion of depleting antibodies like ATG can trigger Cytokine Release Syndrome CRS as cytokines are released from lysed or activated immune cells Slide 23 . This is often managed with steroid pretreatment. 4 A patient six months post-liver transplant presents with fever generalized lymphadenopathy and elevated Epstein-Barr Virus EBV titers. Biopsy of a lymph node shows B-cell proliferation. What is the most likely physiological cause of this condition? Excessive use of steroids leading to direct B-cell malignant transformation Failure of T-cell immune surveillance to control latent EBV-infected B-cells Humoral-mediated acute rejection targeting the donor B-cells Selective depletion of NK cells by Basiliximab Development of antibodies against the ATG rabbit serum proteins 1 Post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder PTLD is most commonly an EBV-positive B-cell proliferation that arises because the T-cells which normally suppress latent EBV have been significantly inhibited by immunosuppressive therapy Slide 20 . 5 A transplant physician is managing an episode of established acute T-cell mediated rejection. Which of the following is considered the FIRST-LINE pharmacological intervention? Switching from Mycophenolate to Azathioprine Adding Alumtuzumab to the maintenance regimen High-dose intravenous methylprednisolone pulse therapy followed by an oral taper Immediate plasmapheresis and IVIG Discontinuing all immunosuppression to prevent infection 2 For established acute T-cell rejection the general approach is high-dose pulse methylprednisolone Slide 19 . If this fails or the rejection is severe ATG may be added. What is an Allograft? A tissue graft from a human donor who is not genetically identical to the recipient. Glossary Which cell is the Maestro of acute transplant rejection? The CD4+ T-lymphocyte. Concept How is Hyperacute Rejection prevented? Screening for HLA and ABO compatibility cannot be treated with drugs . Clinical Mechanism of Antithymocyte Globulin rATG ? Polyclonal antibodies that cause profound T-cell depletion via cytotoxicity. Pharmacology Mechanism of Basiliximab? Blocks the IL-2 receptor alpha chain CD25 ; it is a non-depleting agent. Pharmacology Which induction agent often causes Cytokine Release Syndrome? Antithymocyte Globulin ATG . Clinical Target of Alemtuzumab? CD52 expressed on both T and B cells . Pharmacology What virus is primarily implicated in PTLD? Epstein-Barr Virus EBV . Pathology Common triple maintenance therapy components? Calcineurin Inhibitor + Antimetabolite + Glucocorticoid. Pharmacology First-line treatment for acute cellular rejection? High-dose IV Methylprednisolone pulse therapy. Clinical Acute side effects of Glucocorticoid pulse therapy? Hyperglycemia impaired wound healing mood changes peptic ulcers. Clinical Mechanism of Chronic Rejection? Gradual fibrosis and loss of function over years; poorly understood/resistant to therapy. Pathology The CD4+ Maestro The CD4+ T-cell is the target of choice because it coordinates B-cells CD8+ cells and macrophages—the entire rejection orchestra. Depletion vs Inhibition ATG kills the soldiers depletion while Basiliximab denies them food blocks the IL-2 growth signal . The EBV Shadow Always monitor EBV status in heavily immunosuppressed patients; PTLD is a malignancy of suppression . Pulse the Rejection Acute rejection is not a death sentence for the graft; pulse steroids can reverse it in over 90% of cases. Steroid Tapering The move from high-dose IV methylprednisolone pulse to oral prednisone maintenance is a standard transition from treatment to prophylaxis."}, {"id": "l144", "title": "Pharmacology Immunosuppressants Part 2", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l144 module Neuro metadata l144 Pharmacology Immunosuppressants Part 2 lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. session Lecture 144 Pharmacology readingTime 12-18 min resources pdf content/L144 HighYield Render.pdf analysis content/L144 HighYield Render Analysis.md SketchyPharm Immunology & Biochemistry > Immunosuppressives FirstAid Immunology > Pharmacology > Immunosuppressants BandB Immunology > Pharmacology > Immunosuppressive Drugs Pixorize Immunology > Immunosuppressants match Tacrolimus resources AnKing Step 1 v11 SketchyPharm 03 Blood & Inflammation 03 Anti-Inflammatory 03 Immunosuppressants match Cyclosporine resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid 02 Immunology 04 Immunosuppressants 01 Immunosuppressants Cyclosporine match Sirolimus resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pixorize 01 Immunology 09 Immunosuppressants 03 Sirolimus Rapamycin match Mycophenolate Mofetil resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid 02 Immunology 04 Immunosuppressants 01 Immunosuppressants Mycophenolate Mofetil match Azathioprine resources AnKing Step 1 v11 B&B 12 Immunology 03 Pharm 02 Immunosuppressive Drugs match Belatacept resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid 02 Immunology 04 Immunosuppressants 04 Therapeutic Antibodies Lecture 144 Pharmacology Immunosuppressants Part 2 Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. Session Lecture 144 Introduction The Philosophy of Maintenance Slide 5-6 Following the initial induction phase of transplantation the clinical focus shifts toward long-term survival through maintenance therapy. The goal of maintenance immunosuppression is to prevent acute and chronic rejection while minimizing the inevitable toxicities associated with lifelong immune dampening. Dr. Goldstein emphasizes that this is a delicate balancing act requiring the integration of multiple drug classes that target distinct points in the T-cell activation pathway. By using a multimodal approach clinicians can achieve synergistic effects with lower individual doses thereby reducing the risk of life-threatening side effects. Slide 6 Key Objectives - Prophylaxis Primarily focused on preventing acute rejection episodes. - Therapeutic Monitoring Essential due to narrow therapeutic windows and high inter-individual variability. Slide 10 - Regimen Diversity Most protocols involve 2 to 4 drugs typically including a Calcineurin Inhibitor CNI an antimetabolite and a glucocorticoid. Slide 6 --- Calcineurin Inhibitors CNIs The Mainstay Slide 10-14 Calcineurin inhibitors represent the absolute mainstay of maintenance immunotherapy. Without these agents the success rates of modern solid organ transplantation would be significantly diminished. Two primary drugs dominate this class Cyclosporine the first to be discovered and Tacrolimus now the preferred agent in most centers . Slide 11 Mechanism of Action The Three-Way Complex The pathway to IL-2 production depends on the activation of Calcineurin a phosphatase. 1. Binding The drug binds to a specific intracellular protein called an immunophilin . Slide 13 - Cyclosporine binds to Cyclophilin . - Tacrolimus binds to FK-Binding Protein 12 FKBP-12 . 2. Inhibition This drug-immunophilin complex binds to and inhibits Calcineurin . 3. NFAT Blockade When Calcineurin is inhibited it cannot dephosphorylate NFAT Nuclear Factor of Activated T cells . 4. Result Inactive NFAT remains in the cytosol and cannot translocate to the nucleus thereby blocking the transcription of IL-2 and other pro-inflammatory cytokines. Slide 13 Pharmacokinetics and Monitoring - Metabolism Both are substrates of CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein P-gp . Slide 10 - Narrow Window They have narrow therapeutic indexes; blood levels must be monitored TDM to avoid rejection low levels or toxicity high levels . - Variability Significant inter-patient variability in absorption and metabolism necessitates frequent dose adjustments. Slide 10 highyield Tacrolimus Preference Tacrolimus FK506 has largely superseded Cyclosporine because it is associated with decreased acute rejection rates and is generally better tolerated. Importantly unlike Cyclosporine Tacrolimus does not lower mycophenolate levels through drug-drug interactions. Slide 15 Toxicities The CNI Burden Despite their efficacy CNIs carry a heavy burden of systemic adverse effects. | Toxicity | Mechanism / Features | | --- | --- | | Nephrotoxicity | Afferent renal arteriole vasoconstriction; can lead to interstitial fibrosis. Slide 18 | | Hypertension | Renal vasoconstriction and sodium retention. Slide 19 | | Neurotoxicity | Fine tremor common headache and insomnia. | | Hyperglycemia | Risk of New-Onset Diabetes After Transplant NODAT especially with Tacrolimus. | | Hyperkalemia | Reduced aldosterone responsiveness in the renal tubules. Slide 19 | correlate Cyclosporine-Specific Extras While both drugs share nephrotoxicity and neurotoxicity Cyclosporine has two classic slide symptoms - Gingival Hyperplasia Overgrowth of gum tissue. - Hirsutism Excess body hair growth. - Lecturer s Point Tacrolimus is notably not associated with these two side effects making it more aesthetically tolerable for many patients. Slide 16 --- Anti-metabolites Blocking Proliferation Slide 22-31 Anti-metabolites are used as adjuncts to CNIs. They do not target the initial activation signal but rather the cell s ability to divide once activated. Mycophenolate Mofetil MMF is the current gold standard having largely replaced Azathioprine . Slide 22 Mycophenolate Mofetil MMF - Mechanism MMF is a pro-drug for Mycophenolic Acid MPA . It is a selective uncompetitive and reversible inhibitor of Inosine Monophosphate Dehydrogenase IMPDH . Slide 25 - B/T Cell Specificity Mature T and B cells are uniquely dependent on the de novo pathway for purine synthesis. Other cells can use the salvage pathway. By blocking IMPDH MMF selectively starves lymphocytes of guanosine nucleotides preventing DNA synthesis and proliferation. Slide 26 - Toxicities Predominantly GI persistent diarrhea nausea and bone marrow suppression leukopenia anemia . Slide 27 Azathioprine AZA - Mechanism A pro-drug converted to 6-Mercaptopurine 6-MP which is then converted into cytotoxic 6-thioguanine nucleotides that incorporate into DNA causing strand breaks. Slide 30 - Metabolism Metabolized by Xanthine Oxidase XO and TPMT . - Pregnancy Notably Azathioprine has been used more safely in pregnancy compared to MMF which is highly teratogenic. Slide 31 mnemonic The AZA Interaction Don t mix AZA and Allopurinol Allopurinol an XO inhibitor blocks the degradation of 6-MP leading to dangerous accumulation and life-threatening bone marrow suppression . --- Co-stimulation Blockers Belatacept Slide 33-36 Belatacept represents a newer class of biologics designed to replace CNIs in certain patients thereby avoiding the chronic nephrotoxicity associated with Tacrolimus/Cyclosporine. Slide 33 - Mechanism It is a fusion protein CTLA-4 fused to IgG1 . It binds to CD80 and CD86 B7 on the antigen-presenting cell APC preventing them from binding to CD28 on the T-cell. This blocks Signal 2 co-stimulation leading to T-cell anergy or apoptosis. Slide 35 - The EBV Caveat Balatacept is contraindicated in patients who are EBV seronegative . Slide 36 correlate Post-Transplant Lymphoproliferative Disorder PTLD PTLD is a dreaded complication where B-cells proliferate uncontrollably due to EBV infection when T-cell surveillance is suppressed. Patients who have never been exposed to EBV seronegative are at catastrophic risk if they receive an EBV-positive organ while on Belatacept. Slide 36 --- mTOR Inhibitors Targeting Cytokine Signaling Slide 38-42 Sirolimus Rapamycin and Everolimus target the intracellular signaling downstream of the IL-2 receptor. - Mechanism Binds FKBP-12 same as Tacrolimus but instead of calcineurin it inhibits the mTOR mammalian target of rapamycin kinase. This blocks the transition from the G1 to the S phase of the cell cycle. Slide 39 - Clinical Nuance Not recommended in liver or lung transplants due to safety concerns. Slide 40 - Toxicities - Impaired Wound Healing Due to anti-angiogenic effects; should not be given immediately after surgery. Slide 41 - Dyslipidemia Profound elevations in cholesterol and triglycerides. - Myelosuppression High risk of thrombocytopenia. Slide 41 --- Summary The Triple Therapy Standard Slide 43-45 The most common maintenance regimen involves a three-pronged attack to maximize suppression and minimize specific drug toxicities 1. Tacrolimus CNI Blocks IL-2 production. 2. Mycophenolate Mofetil Antimetabolite Blocks lymphocyte proliferation. 3. Prednisone Glucocorticoid Provides broad anti-inflammatory support. | Drug Class | Prototype | Target | Major Toxicity | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | CNI | Tacrolimus | Calcineurin / NFAT | Nephrotoxicity | | Antimetabolite | MMF | IMPDH / Purines | GI / Leukopenia | | mTOR Inhibitor | Sirolimus | mTOR / Cell Cycle | Wound Healing / Lipids | | Co-stimulation | Belatacept | B7 CD80/86 | PTLD in EBV- | questions 1 A 38-year-old male who underwent a kidney transplant 3 months ago presents for a follow-up. He complains of a fine tremor in his hands and persistent insomnia. His serum creatinine is slightly elevated from baseline. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of the drug responsible for these findings? Inhibition of Inosine Monophosphate Dehydrogenase IMPDH Binding to FKBP-12 and inhibition of calcineurin Binding to FKBP-12 and inhibition of mTOR Blockade of the CD25 alpha subunit of the IL-2 receptor Incorporation as a cytotoxic nucleotide into the DNA strand 1 The patient is presenting with classic signs of CNI toxicity neurotoxicity fine tremor insomnia and nephrotoxicity elevated creatinine . Tacrolimus binds to FKBP-12 to inhibit calcineurin. While Sirolimus also binds FKBP-12 it inhibits mTOR and is not associated with the same degree of nephrotoxicity or tremor. Slide 13 18 2 A transplant recipient is switched from Azathioprine to Mycophenolate Mofetil MMF due to persistent gout requiring allopurinol therapy. Which of the following best explains the rationale for this change? MMF has a safer profile in patients with HLA-B 5701 Allopurinol significantly increases the risk of MMF-induced nephrotoxicity Allopurinol inhibits xanthine oxidase leading to toxic levels of 6-mercaptopurine MMF does not require therapeutic drug monitoring unlike azathioprine MMF is a direct antagonist of the xanthine oxidase enzyme 2 Azathioprine is converted to 6-MP which is inactivated by xanthine oxidase. Allopurinol an XO inhibitor blocks this pathway shifting 6-MP toward the toxic nucleotide pathway causing severe myelosuppression. MMF does not use this pathway and is a safer alternative. Slide 30 3 A patient is being considered for Belatacept maintenance therapy to avoid the nephrotoxic effects of CNIs. Which of the following tests must be performed prior to initiating this therapy? Thiopurine Methyltransferase TPMT activity levels CYP3A4 genotype testing Epstein-Barr Virus EBV serology Glucose-6-Phosphate Dehydrogenase G6PD levels P-glycoprotein efflux assay 2 Belatacept is contraindicated in EBV-seronegative patients due to the significantly increased risk of Post-Transplant Lymphoproliferative Disorder PTLD . Slide 36 4 A 55-year-old female is started on Sirolimus following a heart transplant. She is scheduled for a minor surgical revision of her transplant site. Why might the surgeon recommend holding or delaying the Sirolimus around the time of the procedure? Risk of profound intraoperative hypertension Increased risk of wound dehiscence and impaired healing Potential for fatal interaction with general anesthetics Sirolimus induces CYP3A4 reducing the efficacy of pain medications Risk of hyperacute rejection due to mTOR sensitization 1 mTOR inhibitors like Sirolimus have anti-angiogenic properties that interfere with tissue repair leading to wound dehiscence and impaired healing. It is typically avoided in the immediate post-operative period. Slide 41 5 Which of the following side effects is uniquely associated with Cyclosporine but typically absent with Tacrolimus therapy? Acute Nephrotoxicity Hyperglycemia and NODAT Gingival Hyperplasia Fine tremor and neurotoxicity Increased risk of opportunistic infection 2 Gingival hyperplasia and hirsutism are classic side effects of Cyclosporine that are not seen with Tacrolimus. Slide 16 Mechanism of Calcineurin Inhibitors CNIs ? Bind immunophilins Cyclophilin/FKBP-12 complex with Calcineurin inhibit NFAT dephosphorylation block IL-2 transcription. Pharmacology Which CNI is preferred and why? Tacrolimus. Better efficacy less acute rejection better tolerated no effect on Mycophenolate levels. Concept Classic toxicities of both Cyclosporine and Tacrolimus? Nephrotoxicity Hypertension Neurotoxicity tremor Hyperkalemia. Clinical Side effects unique to Cyclosporine? Gingival Hyperplasia and Hirsutism. Clinical Mechanism of Mycophenolate Mofetil MMF ? Reversible inhibitor of IMPDH; blocks de novo purine synthesis in T and B cells. Pharmacology Why is MMF selective for lymphocytes? T and B cells depend almost entirely on the de novo pathway for purines while other cells have salvage pathways. Concept Major drug interaction with Azathioprine? Allopurinol XO inhibitor blocks 6-MP inactivation causing severe myelosuppression. Pharmacology Mechanism of Belatacept? CTLA-4 fusion protein; binds B7 CD80/86 on APC to block CD28 co-stimulation Signal 2 . Pharmacology Hard contraindication for Belatacept? EBV seronegative status due to extreme risk of PTLD . Clinical Mechanism of mTOR inhibitors Sirolimus ? Bind FKBP-12 inhibit mTOR kinase block cell cycle progression from G1 to S phase. Pharmacology Why avoid Sirolimus immediately post-op? Impairs wound healing due to anti-angiogenic effects risk of dehiscence . Clinical Metabolic side effect of mTOR inhibitors? Dyslipidemia Hypercholesterolemia and Hypertriglyceridemia . Clinical The Narrow CNI Window CNIs have extremely narrow therapeutic windows and high variability; Therapeutic Drug Monitoring TDM is non-negotiable. Tacrolimus vs Cyclosporine Tacrolimus is the new king of CNIs—more effective and spares the gums/hair though it carries a higher diabetes risk. Purine Starvation MMF targets the De Novo purine pathway effectively starving the rapidly dividing T and B cells without hurting salvage-capable tissues. EBV Vigilance In EBV-negative patients on heavy suppression like Belatacept the virus can trigger PTLD—a malignancy born from a direct failure of immune surveillance. Wait for Healing mTOR inhibitors like Sirolimus are the anastomosis killers —their anti-angiogenic properties make them dangerous to use until the surgical wound is fully knit."}, {"id": "l145", "title": "Clinical Approach to Immunodeficiencies", "module": "Unknown", "content": "id l145 module Unknown metadata l145 Clinical Approach to Immunodeficiencies lecturer Adam Hurst M.D. session Lecture 145 Immunology readingTime 10-15 min resources pdf content/L145 HighYield Render.pdf analysis content/L145 HighYield Render Analysis.md BandB Immunology > Basic > Immune Deficiency Syndromes Pathoma Inflammation > Primary Immunodeficiency SketchyPath Immunology > Immunodeficiency > B-Cell Disorders & DiGeorge Syndrome FirstAid Immunology > Immune Responses > Immunodeficiencies Physeo Immunology > Immunodeficiencies Pixorize Immunology > Immunodeficiencies match Common Variable Immunodeficiency resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid 02 Immunology 03 Immune Responses 16 Immunodeficiencies B-Cell Disorders Common Variable Immunodeficiency match Selective IgA Deficiency resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid 02 Immunology 03 Immune Responses 16 Immunodeficiencies B-Cell Disorders IgA Deficiency match IL-12 Receptor Deficiency resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid 02 Immunology 03 Immune Responses 16 Immunodeficiencies T-Cell Disorders IL12 Receptor Deficiency match Chediak-Higashi Syndrome resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid 02 Immunology 03 Immune Responses 16 Immunodeficiencies Phagocyte Dysfunction Disorders Chediak-Higashi Syndrome match Chronic Granulomatous Disease resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid 02 Immunology 03 Immune Responses 16 Immunodeficiencies Phagocyte Dysfunction Disorders Chronic Granulomatous Disease Lecture 145 Clinical Approach to Immunodeficiencies Lecturer Adam Hurst M.D. Session Lecture 145 Introduction to Immunodeficiency Slide 4 Story Mode Dr. Hurst introduces the concept of immunodeficiency not just as a failure to fight infections but as a complex dysregulation of the immune defense system. He differentiates between the inherent genetic defects we are born with and the myriad of acquired factors—from medications to viral infections—that can compromise an otherwise healthy system later in life. This distinction is critical because while primary immunodeficiencies are often rare and require specialized genetic insights secondary immunodeficiencies are encountered daily in clinical practice often lurking behind common conditions like diabetes or malnutrition. Definition A defect in the immune system resulting in a weakened or dysregulated immune defense. Primary Immunodeficiency PID Inherent Defect Arises from inborn deviations in the genetic code. Presentation Usually presents in infancy or childhood; rarely in adulthood Slide 4 . Prevalence Problems with B cells are the most common PID accounting for >50% of cases. Secondary Immunodeficiency SID Acquired Cause Defect caused by external factors or underlying non-immune diseases. Clinical Approach and Red Flags Slides 5-14 Story Mode The clinical approach to immunodeficiency is essentially a detective story. Because many primary immunodeficiencies mimic common childhood illnesses Dr. Hurst emphasizes that the secret weapon for a clinician is continuity of care. By observing a patient over time a physician can spot the architectural pattern of recurrent infections that would be missed if the patient saw a different urgent care provider for every earache. The goal is to move from treating the infection of the day to identifying the underlying defect of a lifetime. Suspecting PID The 10 Warning Signs Slide 43 4+ new ear infections within one year. 2+ serious sinus infections within one year. 2+ pneumonias within one year. Recurrent deep skin or organ abscesses. Failure to thrive poor growth/development . Persistent thrush in the mouth or fungal infection on skin after age 1. Need for IV antibiotics to clear infections. Family history of PID. highyield Pathogen Association Clues Specific pathogens point toward specific immune defects B-Cell Disorders Bacterial infections of the sinopulmonary tract encapsulated organisms . T-Cell Disorders Opportunistic infections with viruses fungi and protozoa. Phagocytic Disorders Deep tissue infections with catalase-positive bacteria and fungi. B-Cell Disorders CVID and Selective IgA Deficiency Slides 15-22 Story Mode B-cell disorders represent the lion s share of primary immunodeficiencies. Interestingly these patients often appear fine for the first few months of life because they are living on borrowed time—specifically the maternal IgG that crossed the placenta. Once that protection wears off around 3-4 months the lack of host antibodies leaves mucosal surfaces lungs and gut vulnerable. Dr. Hurst focuses on CVID and Selective IgA deficiency as the two heavy hitters in this category each presenting with unique long-term risks from autoimmune friendly fire to the threat of anaphylaxis during blood transfusions. correlate Common Variable Immunodeficiency CVID - Pathophysiology B cells are present but cannot differentiate into plasma cells leading to severe antibody deficiency. - Presentation Recurrent sinopulmonary infections and GI illness Giardia ; often presents in the 2nd–3rd decade. - Diagnosis Low IgG Low IgA Low IgM; poor vaccine response. - Treatment IVIG Intravenous Immunoglobulin and aggressive antibiotics. - Risk Factors Sporadic AD or AR inheritance. - Complications Autoimmune diseases EBV-associated lymphoma and bronchiectasis. - Lecturer s Point Despite the name common it often requires lifelong monitoring for non-infectious complications like granulomatous lung disease. Selective IgA Deficiency Slide 19 Defect Isolated absence of IgA <5–10 mg/dL . Underlying defect is unknown. Prevalence The most common PID 1 in 500 people . The IVIG Trap Patients are at risk for anaphylaxis to blood products or IVIG because they may have anti-IgA antibodies. highyield Anaphylaxis Risk in IgA Deficiency Because patients with Selective IgA Deficiency can produce IgE against IgA they are at high risk for anaphylactic reactions when receiving blood products or IVIG that contain trace amounts of IgA. Always screen or use IgA-depleted products. T-Cell Disorders IL-12 Receptor and CMC Slides 23-29 Story Mode While B-cell defects often lead to predictable bacterial infections T-cell defects are more opportunistic. Dr. Hurst explains that T cells are the conductors of the immune orchestra; if they fail the entire response collapses. A defect in the IL-12 receptor pathway specifically breaks the communication between macrophages and T cells leaving the body unable to mount a defense against intracellular hiders like Mycobacteria. Meanwhile Chronic Mucocutaneous Candidiasis illustrates the importance of IL-17 in protecting our barriers where a single genetic mutation like AIRE can lead to a triad of autoimmune endocrine failure and stuck fungal infections. correlate Autoimmune Polyendocrine Syndrome Type 1 APS1 / APECED - Pathophysiology Mutation in the AIRE Autoimmune Regulator gene leading to defective negative selection of T cells. - Presentation Classic triad Hypoparathyroidism Adrenal Insufficiency Addison s and Chronic Mucocutaneous Candidiasis. - Diagnosis Genetic testing for AIRE mutations; endocrine workup. - Treatment Systemic antifungals; replacement of deficient hormones. - Complications Ectodermal dystrophy skin/tooth enamel issues malabsorption. - Lecturer s Point Think of this when you see a young patient with persistent thrush AND endocrine failure. Phagocytic Dysfunction Chediak-Higashi and CGD Slides 30-36 40 Story Mode Phagocytic disorders are problems with the foot soldiers of the immune system. In Chediak-Higashi the issue is logistical—cells can t transport their poison lysosomes to the right place leading to giant useless granules and silvery hair. In Chronic Granulomatous Disease CGD the gear is missing entirely—the NADPH oxidase engine is broken preventing the respiratory burst needed to kill the most stubborn bacteria. These patients don t just get infected; they form granulomas as the body tries to wall off what it cannot kill leading to obstructions in the gut or bladder. mnemonic CHEDI-AK Shortcut to remember Chediak-Higashi features C Catalase-positive infections H Hair Silvery E Easy bruising Platelet issues D Defect in LYST gene I Inclusions Giant granules A Albinism K Kill Impaired bactericidal activity correlate Chronic Granulomatous Disease CGD - Pathophysiology Defective NADPH oxidase complex prevents creation of reactive oxygen species ROS in phagocytes. - Presentation Deep tissue abscesses pneumonia and osteomyelitis with catalase-positive organisms. - Diagnosis Dihydrorhodamine DHR 123 flow cytometry Preferred or Nitroblue Tetrazolium NBT test. - Treatment Prophylactic TMP-SMX antifungal Itraconazole and IFN-gamma. HSCT for cure. - Risk Factors X-linked recessive most common . - Complications Granulomatous obstruction of the GI or urinary tract pyloric stenosis bladder obstruction . - Lecturer s Point Serratia marcescens skin infections or osteomyelitis is a classic board clue for CGD. Mind Map Immunodeficiency Classification Primary PID B-Cell Humoral CVID Failure of plasma cell differentiation; low G/A/M. Selective IgA Isolated low IgA; anaphylaxis risk. T-Cell Cellular IL-12 Receptor Low IFN-gamma; Mycobacteria/Salmonella risk. CMC IL-17/Th17 defect; recurrent skin/nail Candida. Phagocytic Chediak-Higashi LYST mutation; giant granules; albinism. CGD NADPH oxidase defect; catalase-positive infections; granulomas. Secondary SID Iatrogenic Steroids immunosuppressants. Pathological HIV diabetes nephrotic syndrome. Diagnostic Testing Neutrophil Function DHR Flow and NBT Blue . Humoral IgG/A/M/E levels and vaccine titers. Molecular Genetic sequencing for LYST AIRE etc. questions 1 A 25-year-old female presents with recurrent sinusitis and three bouts of pneumonia over the last two years. She has a history of rheumatoid arthritis and vitiligo. Laboratory workup reveals significantly low IgG and IgA levels while her B-cell count is within the low-normal range. What is the most likely underlying defect? Failure of B-cells to differentiate into plasma cells Genetic deficiency of the NADPH oxidase complex Impaired trafficking of lysosomal regulator proteins Defective development of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches Absence of serum and secretory IgA solely 0 This patient presents with the classic clinical triad of CVID recurrent sinopulmonary infections autoimmune conditions RA vitiligo and hypogammaglobulinemia low IgG/IgA . The underlying defect in CVID is the failure of phenotypically normal B cells to differentiate into antibody-secreting plasma cells. 2 A 3-year-old boy presents with a history of recurrent perianal abscesses and a previous episode of Serratia marcescens osteomyelitis. Stool antigen reveals no parasites. A Dihydrorhodamine DHR 123 flow cytometry test shows a lack of fluorescence when stimulated. What is the most likely diagnosis? Common Variable Immunodeficiency Chédiak-Higashi Syndrome Chronic Granulomatous Disease IL-12 Receptor Deficiency X-linked Agammaglobulinemia 2 CGD is characterized by recurrent infections with catalase-positive organisms like Serratia and abscess formation. The DHR test measures the respiratory burst; a \\ lack\\ of fluorescence indicates a failure of NADPH oxidase to produce superoxides. 3 A 6-year-old girl is brought to the pediatrician for an evaluation of \\ graying\\ hair and light patches on her skin. Her mother reports she has had multiple skin infections and often bruises easily. A peripheral blood smear is ordered showing giant peroxidase-positive inclusions in the neutrophils. Which of the following is the most appropriate curative treatment? Monthly IVIG infusions Daily interferon-gamma injections Lifelong prophylactic TMP-SMX Hematopoietic stem cell transplant Topical antifungal creams 3 The presentation partial albinism/silvery hair easy bruising giant inclusions is diagnostic for Chédiak-Higashi Syndrome. HSCT is the only curative treatment that can prevent the deadly accelerated phase of the disease. 4 A 4-year-child with a history of recurrent Salmonella infections and several episodes of atypical Mycobacterial lymphadenitis is being evaluated. The provider suspects an IL-12 receptor deficiency. What is the molecular consequence of this defect? Inability of neutrophils to ingest bacteria Failure to produce interferon-gamma IFN-ɣ Excess production of IgE antibodies Arrest of plasma cell maturation Failure of phagolysosome fusion 1 IL-12 is the cytokine that stimulates T cells and NK cells to produce IFN-gamma. If the IL-12 receptor is deficient this signaling cascade is broken leading to low IFN-gamma levels. 5 A healthy 22-year-old male discovers he has a primary immunodeficiency after suffering an anaphylactic reaction during a blood transfusion. He recalls having \\ a few more sinus infections than my friends\\ during childhood. Which of the following lab findings is most likely? IgG < 200 mg/dL IgA < 7 mg/dL Elevated IgE and normal IgA Giant granules in neutrophils Lack of dark blue color on NBT test 1 Selective IgA deficiency is often asymptomatic but carry a risk of anaphylactic reaction to blood products due to host IgE antibodies reacting against the IgA present in the donor blood. What is the most common primary immunodeficiency PID ? Selective IgA Deficiency 1 in 500 . Clinical What is the underlying cellular defect in CVID? B cells fail to differentiate into plasma cells. Concept Which test is the preferred quantitative measure for CGD? Dihydrorhodamine DHR 123 flow cytometry. Concept What gene is mutated in Chédiak-Higashi Syndrome? LYST or CHS1 gene. Concept What is the classic triad of APS-1/APECED? Hypoparathyroidism Adrenal Insufficiency Addison s and Chronic Mucocutaneous Candidiasis. Clinical Why are IgA deficient patients at risk during blood transfusions? Anaphylaxis due to anti-IgA antibodies reacting with IgA in blood products. Clinical What type of organisms cause recurrent infections in CGD? Catalase-positive organisms e.g. Staph aureus Serratia Aspergillus . Clinical What is the hallmark of Chédiak-Higashi on peripheral smear? Giant cytoplasmic granules in leukocytes and platelets. Clinical What is the primary treatment for IL-12 receptor deficiency? Antibiotics and Interferon-gamma IFN-ɣ . Concept At what age can Selective IgA Deficiency be definitively diagnosed? After 4 years of age when IgA levels reach adult levels . Clinical Continuity of Care Continuity is the physician s secret weapon for spotting the architectural pattern of recurrent PIDs. Maternal IgG Shield B-cell disorders rarely present before 3-4 months because maternal IgG provides temporary protection. The IVIG Trap In Selective IgA Deficiency avoid standard IVIG as tracing amounts of IgA can trigger anaphylaxis. Serratia Signal Serratia marcescens infection in a child is a major red flag for Chronic Granulomatous Disease CGD . Silvery Clue Silvery hair or silky sheen in a child with recurrent infections points directly to Chédiak-Higashi Syndrome. AIRE and APS1 Mutations in the AIRE gene cause APS1 characterized by the triad of Hypoparathyroidism Addison s and CMC."}, {"id": "l146", "title": "Pediatric Immunology", "module": "Unknown", "content": "id l146 module Unknown metadata l146 Pediatric Immunology lecturer A. Patel D.O. session Lecture 146 Immunology readingTime 12-15 min resources pdf content/L146 HighYield Render.pdf analysis content/L146 HighYield Render Analysis.md FirstAid Immunology > Immune Responses > Immunodeficiencies BandB Immunology > Basic > Immune Deficiency Syndromes Pathoma Inflammation > Primary Immunodeficiency SketchyPath Immunology > Immunodeficiency > B-Cell Disorders T-Cell Disorders and Combined Physeo Immunology > Immunodeficiencies Pixorize Immunology > Immunodeficiencies match Leukocyte Adhesion Deficiency LAD - CD18 defect delayed cord separation resources AnKing Step 1 v11 AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 02 Immunology 03 Immune Responses 16 Immunodeficiencies Phagocyte Dysfunction Disorders Leukocyte Adhesion Deficiency | AK Step1 v11 B&B 02 Immunology 03 Immunodeficiency Phagocyte Dysfunction match SCID - X-linked IL-2 gamma chain vs ADA deficiency T B NK all absent resources AnKing Step 1 v11 AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 02 Immunology 03 Immune Responses 16 Immunodeficiencies Combined B and T Cell Disorders Severe Combined Immunodeficiency | AK Step1 v11 SketchyPath 06 Immunology 02 Immunodeficiency SCID match DiGeorge Syndrome - 22q11.2 deletion CATCH-22 absent thymic shadow hypocalcemia resources AnKing Step 1 v11 AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 02 Immunology 03 Immune Responses 16 Immunodeficiencies T-Cell Disorders DiGeorge Syndrome | AK Step1 v11 SketchyPath 06 Immunology 02 Immunodeficiency DiGeorge Syndrome match X-linked Agammaglobulinemia XLA - BTK defect absent B cells absent tonsils after 6 months resources AnKing Step 1 v11 AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 02 Immunology 03 Immune Responses 16 Immunodeficiencies B-Cell Disorders X-linked Agammaglobulinemia | AK Step1 v11 SketchyPath 06 Immunology 02 Immunodeficiency Brutons Agammaglobulinemia match Hyper-IgM Syndrome - CD40L defect inability to class switch normal IgM but low IgG/IgA/IgE resources AnKing Step 1 v11 AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 02 Immunology 03 Immune Responses 16 Immunodeficiencies B-Cell Disorders Hyper-IgM Syndrome | AK Step1 v11 B&B 02 Immunology 03 Immunodeficiency B Cell Disorders match Job Syndrome Hyper-IgE - STAT3 mutation autosomal dominant cold abscesses FATED mnemonic resources AnKing Step 1 v11 AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 02 Immunology 03 Immune Responses 16 Immunodeficiencies B-Cell Disorders Hyper-IgE Syndrome | AK Step1 v11 SketchyPath 06 Immunology 02 Immunodeficiency Hyper IgE Syndrome match Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome - WAS gene thrombocytopenia small platelets eczema recurrent infections resources AnKing Step 1 v11 AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 02 Immunology 03 Immune Responses 16 Immunodeficiencies Combined B and T Cell Disorders Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome | AK Step1 v11 SketchyPath 06 Immunology 02 Immunodeficiency Wiskott Aldrich match Ataxia-Telangiectasia - ATM gene cerebellar ataxia elevated AFP lymphoma/leukemia risk resources AnKing Step 1 v11 AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 02 Immunology 03 Immune Responses 16 Immunodeficiencies Combined B and T Cell Disorders Ataxia-Telangiectasia | AK Step1 v11 SketchyPath 06 Immunology 02 Immunodeficiency Ataxia Telangiectasia Lecture 146 Pediatric Immunology Lecturer A. Patel D.O. Session Lecture 146 Introduction Recognizing Primary Immunodeficiencies in Children Slides 1-5 Story Mode Pediatric Primary Immune Deficiency Disorders PIDDs represent a spectrum of over 550 disorders and the central challenge is that they masquerade as common childhood ailments. Infants and preschoolers naturally experience 6-10 viral infections per year. The red flags emerge when infections become too frequent too severe or involve unexpected pathogens. Dr. Patel s approach is diagnostic pattern recognition — each disorder has a unique combination of the offending pathogen type age of onset physical exam finding and laboratory signature that points directly to the underlying immune defect. Key Questions to Ask When Suspecting PIDD Is there a family history or consanguinity? Did the patient fail to clear infections despite IV antibiotics? Are there unusual pathogens — Pneumocystis jirovecii severe thrush non-tuberculous mycobacteria? Did the patient have a severe or fatal reaction to a live attenuated vaccine MMR Rotavirus Varicella ? Is there failure to thrive alongside the recurrent infections? Phagocyte Disorders Leukocyte Adhesion Deficiency LAD Type 1 Slides 6-10 Story Mode LAD Type 1 is a defect in the immune system s ability to get its neutrophils from the bloodstream into infected tissue — the molecular equivalent of emergency responders whose vehicles cannot leave the highway. The cause is a mutation in CD18 beta-2 integrin also called LFA-1 which is required for neutrophils to roll along and adhere to the endothelium during the diapedesis cascade. Without CD18 neutrophils circulate in the blood at extremely high numbers but cannot extravasate to fight infections where they are needed most. Molecular Defect Mutation in CD18 beta-2 integrin / LFA-1 — the adhesion molecule required for neutrophil transmigration out of blood vessels. Result Neutrophils are stuck in the vascular compartment; they cannot enter infected tissues. correlate Leukocyte Adhesion Deficiency LAD Type 1 - Pathophysiology CD18 beta-2 integrin defect prevents neutrophil diapedesis from blood into tissues. - Presentation Delayed umbilical cord separation beyond 30 days vs. normal 2-week separation ; recurrent severe gingivitis skin infections and mucosal ulcers without pus; omphalitis umbilical infection in neonates. - Diagnosis Peripheral blood shows profound neutrophilia >20 000 cells/microliter — neutrophils are trapped in the marginating pool and circulating blood. Flow cytometry confirms absence of CD18 on neutrophil surfaces. - Treatment Bone marrow transplant curative ; prophylactic antibiotics and antifungals as bridging therapy. - Lecturer s Point The absence of pus at infection sites is pathognomonic. Pus requires neutrophils — if they cannot leave the blood there is no pus even in severe infections. highyield LAD Board Clue No Pus + Neutrophilia + Delayed Cord The triad of 1 absent pus at infection sites 2 profound circulating neutrophilia and 3 delayed umbilical cord separation >30 days is the board-tested signature of LAD Type 1. The mechanism CD18 is expressed on the umbilical stump s infiltrating neutrophils that help it separate — without CD18 this process fails. Combined T and B Cell Disorders Severe Combined Immunodeficiency SCID Slides 11-17 Story Mode SCID is the most severe of all primary immunodeficiencies — a complete collapse of both adaptive immune arms. Without functional T cells B cells even if numerically present receive no help and cannot mount antibody responses. The infant lives in an invisible bubble of danger — normal flora environmental fungi and even attenuated vaccine strains become life-threatening. It is one of the few immunologic emergencies where every day without curative treatment hematopoietic stem cell transplant increases mortality. Two Major Genetic Subtypes X-linked SCID Most Common 50% Defective IL-2 receptor gamma chain also called the common gamma chain or gamma-c which is shared by receptors for IL-2 IL-4 IL-7 IL-9 IL-15 and IL-21. Loss of this chain impairs T cell and NK cell development. B cells are present but non-functional no T-cell help . Pattern T-/B+/NK-. Adenosine Deaminase ADA Deficiency Second Most Common AR Deoxyadenosine accumulates and is phosphorylated to dATP which is toxic to ALL lymphocyte lineages — T cells B cells AND NK cells are destroyed. Pattern T-/B-/NK-. correlate SCID The Medical Emergency - Pathophysiology Absent or non-functional T cells lead to failure of both cell-mediated and humoral immunity. B cells when present cannot function without T-cell help. - Presentation Onset in the first few months of life. Failure to thrive chronic watery diarrhea persistent oral thrush and recurrent bacterial viral fungal and protozoal infections including PCP — Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia . - Diagnosis Absent or severely low T-cell receptor excision circles TRECs on newborn screening. Chest X-ray shows absent thymic shadow no thymus = no T-cell maturation . Flow cytometry reveals the specific lymphocyte profile T-/B+/NK- for X-SCID; T-/B-/NK- for ADA . - Treatment Hematopoietic stem cell transplant HSCT is curative; must be performed before significant infection load. ADA deficiency enzyme replacement therapy ERT or gene therapy are also options. - Critical Safety Rule ALL live attenuated vaccines MMR Varicella Rotavirus FluMist are absolutely contraindicated. Vaccine strains of measles varicella-zoster or rotavirus become fatal infections in SCID patients. mnemonic X-SCID vs. ADA-SCID Who s Missing? - X-linked SCID IL-2Rgamma T cells and NK cells are gone; B cells are present but useless. B+ in a broken neighborhood. - ADA Deficiency ALL lymphocytes T B NK are absent — dATP is an equal-opportunity poison. The entire city is empty. Use this to match clinical vignettes absent NK cells in X-SCID absent everything in ADA-SCID. T-Cell Disorders DiGeorge Syndrome Thymic Aplasia Slides 18-22 Story Mode DiGeorge syndrome is one of the most commonly tested primary immunodeficiencies on board exams because it is a classic multi-system disorder tied to a single chromosomal deletion. The 22q11.2 deletion causes failure of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches to develop properly leading to a stereotyped cluster of defects. Because T-cell development requires the thymus which forms from the third pharyngeal pouch these patients lack functional T cells — and without T-cell help their B cells cannot generate robust antibody responses either. The resulting vulnerability to opportunistic infections makes DiGeorge a combined immunodeficiency though the primary defect is T-cell based. Genetic Basis 22q11.2 microdeletion detectable by FISH or chromosomal microarray . Embryologic Origin Failure of the 3rd and 4th pharyngeal pouches to develop which form the thymus T-cell maturation and parathyroid glands calcium regulation . correlate DiGeorge Syndrome CATCH-22 - Pathophysiology 22q11.2 microdeletion causes failure of the 3rd and 4th pharyngeal pouches leading to thymic and parathyroid aplasia. - Mnemonic CATCH-22 - C — Cardiac defects most common Tetralogy of Fallot Truncus arteriosus Ventricular septal defect - A — Abnormal facies micrognathia low-set ears hypertelorism - T — Thymic aplasia absent thymic shadow on CXR; low T cells; opportunistic infections - C — Cleft palate - H — Hypocalcemia absent parathyroids leads to low PTH low calcium neonatal tetany and seizures - Immunology Low CD3+ T cells; B cells are present but have impaired function. Gamma-globulin levels vary — some patients can produce some antibodies others cannot. - Diagnosis FISH for 22q11.2 deletion; absent thymic shadow on CXR; low T-cell count on flow cytometry; low PTH with hypocalcemia. - Lymph Node Histology The paracortex T-cell zone is underdeveloped reflecting the lack of circulating T cells. - Treatment Thymic transplant or HSCT for severe cases; calcium and vitamin D supplementation for hypocalcemia. B-Cell Disorders X-Linked Agammaglobulinemia XLA Slides 23-27 Story Mode XLA illustrates the critical role that a single tyrosine kinase plays in B-cell survival. Without BTK Bruton s Tyrosine Kinase B-cell precursors are arrested at the pro-B stage and cannot differentiate into mature B cells. The consequence on physical exam is striking no mature B cells means no germinal centers no secondary lymphoid follicles and therefore no palpable lymph nodes virtually no tonsils and no splenic white pulp. The infant is protected for the first 6 months by maternal IgG transferred across the placenta but once that protection wanes infections begin. Molecular Defect Mutation in BTK Bruton s Tyrosine Kinase gene on the X chromosome. Affects males. Pathophysiology B-cell precursors cannot mature beyond the pro-B stage and fail to enter the periphery. correlate X-Linked Agammaglobulinemia Bruton s Agammaglobulinemia - Pathophysiology BTK mutation arrests B-cell maturation in the bone marrow. No mature B cells enter circulation. Consequently no antibody production of any class pan-hypogammaglobulinemia . - Timing of Presentation Symptoms begin after 6 months of age once maternal IgG passively transferred transplacentally has waned. - Physical Exam Absent or very small tonsils no lymphoid follicles/germinal centers ; absence of palpable peripheral lymph nodes; absent spleen white pulp. - Infections Recurrent sinopulmonary infections with encapsulated bacteria S. pneumoniae H. influenzae ; Giardia lamblia GI ; Enterovirus encephalitis particularly dangerous — no neutralizing antibody . - Labs Nearly absent CD19/CD20 B cells on flow cytometry; profoundly low IgG IgA IgM and IgE; T-cell count is normal. - Treatment Lifelong IVIG supplementation; aggressive antibiotic prophylaxis. B-Cell Disorders Hyper-IgM Syndrome Slides 28-31 Story Mode Hyper-IgM illustrates the necessity of T-B cell crosstalk in antibody class-switching. The B cell can make IgM on its own as a default response but to switch to IgG IgA or IgE it needs a direct molecular handshake with an activated T helper cell — specifically the CD40L CD40 Ligand on the T cell binding the CD40 receptor on the B cell. Without this interaction B cells remain locked in the IgM-producing default state. The clinical consequence is infections typical of both T-cell dysfunction PCP Cryptosporidium — due to impaired T-cell co-stimulation and B-cell dysfunction neutropenic infections from absent IgG opsonization . Molecular Defect X-linked mutation in CD40L CD40 Ligand / CD154 on T helper cells. Result Without CD40-CD40L ligation B cells cannot receive the signal to undergo class-switch recombination. highyield Hyper-IgM The Failed Handshake - B cells produce IgM normally no T-cell help required for IgM . - Class-switch to IgG/IgA/IgE requires CD40L on T cells to bind CD40 on B cells — this interaction is broken in Hyper-IgM. - Labs Elevated or normal IgM; severely low IgG IgA and IgE. - Unique among B-cell disorders also causes opportunistic T-cell type infections Pneumocystis jirovecii Cryptosporidium parvum because CD40L also activates macrophages. B-Cell Disorders Job Syndrome Hyper-IgE Syndrome Slides 32-36 Story Mode Job syndrome is distinctive for two reasons it is the only primary immunodeficiency that is autosomal dominant among this standard group and its infections are literally cold — skin abscesses without the classic signs of warmth or redness. The mutations in STAT3 prevent normal Th17 cell differentiation which normally produces IL-17 to recruit neutrophils and defend mucosal barriers. Without Th17 staphylococcal skin infections cannot mount a proper inflammatory response and bacteria reproduce in cold painless abscesses. IgE rises to extraordinary levels often >2 000 IU/mL for reasons that are incompletely understood. Molecular Defect Autosomal dominant mutation in STAT3 which impairs Th17 maturation. Result Failure of IL-17-mediated mucosal defense; extremely elevated IgE. mnemonic Job Syndrome Remember FATED - F — Facies coarse lion-like facial features - A — Abscesses Cold staphylococcal skin abscesses. Cold means no inflammatory signs no rubor calor or tumor — neutrophil recruitment is broken. - T — Teeth Retained primary baby teeth that fail to shed normally - E — IgE Profoundly elevated often >2 000 IU/mL - D — Dermatitis and bone fractures Severe eczema; fractures from minor trauma due to skeletal abnormalities Inheritance Autosomal Dominant unique among this group of PIDDs — all others are X-linked or AR . Combined Disorders Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome WAS Slides 37-41 Story Mode WAS is an X-linked disorder of actin cytoskeleton reorganization affecting immune cells platelet function and response to polysaccharide antigens. The WASp Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome protein is essential for signaling the actin cytoskeleton to reorganize when immune cells need to probe their environment form immune synapses and migrate toward infections. Without WASp T cells cannot communicate effectively B cells cannot respond to polysaccharide antigens and platelets are abnormally small and dysfunctional. The clinical triad is the highest-yield takeaway. Molecular Defect X-linked recessive mutation in the WAS gene encoding WASp an actin polymerization regulator . Antibody Pattern Low to normal IgG/IgM but characteristically elevated IgE and IgA — a useful laboratory hint. correlate Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome WAS - Pathophysiology WASp defect impairs actin cytoskeleton reorganization in all hematopoietic cells — immune cells cannot form proper synapses platelet production and function are defective. - Classic Triad WATER - W — Wiskott-Aldrich - A — Thrombocytopenia with small platelets micro-thrombocytes bleeding is the most common cause of death along with autoimmune complications. - T — Eczema - ER — Recurrent Infections encapsulated bacteria due to poor polysaccharide antibody response; also viral fungal - Labs Peripheral smear shows characteristically small platelets. Elevated IgE and IgA; low to normal IgM and IgG. - Cancer Risk Significantly elevated risk of lymphoma EBV-associated B-cell lymphoma is most common . - Treatment HSCT is curative. IVIG antibiotic prophylaxis and platelet transfusions as bridging therapy. Combined Disorders Ataxia-Telangiectasia AT Slides 42-46 Story Mode Ataxia-Telangiectasia is a DNA repair disorder — but because lymphocytes require DNA rearrangement via V D J recombination to generate immunoglobulin and T-cell receptor diversity impaired DNA repair is catastrophically damaging to the immune system. The ATM Ataxia-Telangiectasia Mutated gene product normally phosphorylates and activates proteins in response to DNA double-strand breaks. Without ATM cells cannot pause the cell cycle to repair broken DNA — they accumulate mutations and progress to cancer. The nervous system Purkinje cells the immune system IgA secretion and blood vessels telangiectasias are all affected. Molecular Defect Autosomal recessive mutation in the ATM gene Ataxia-Telangiectasia Mutated kinase responsible for DNA double-strand break repair. correlate Ataxia-Telangiectasia AT - Pathophysiology ATM kinase defect impairs DNA double-strand break repair. Lymphocytes undergo aberrant V D J recombination leading to abnormal immunoglobulin production especially IgA deficiency and chromosomal translocations. Neural tissue especially cerebellar Purkinje cells accumulates DNA damage. - Presentation Triad 1. Cerebellar Ataxia Progressive starting in toddlers unsteady gait difficulty walking . The earliest sign. Caused by cerebellar Purkinje cell degeneration from unrepaired DNA damage. 2. Telangiectasias Spider-like dilated capillaries most visible on the conjunctiva bulbar telangiectasias and face/neck skin. Usually appear around age 3-5. 3. Immunodeficiency Primarily low IgA most common also low IgG subclasses and IgE; decreased T-cell counts. Recurrent sinopulmonary infections. - Hallmark Lab Clue Elevated serum alpha-fetoprotein AFP — a classic board clue. The exact mechanism is not fully understood but AFP normally falls after birth; in AT it remains persistently elevated. - Cancer Risk Among the highest of any PIDD — lymphoma and leukemia risk is dramatically elevated. Chromosomal translocations involving T-cell receptor and immunoglobulin gene loci are common. - Radiation Sensitivity AT patients are exquisitely radiosensitive cannot repair X-ray induced DNA breaks — avoid diagnostic radiation when possible. - Treatment No curative treatment. Supportive care IVIG antibiotics physical/occupational therapy for ataxia. Avoid radiation. highyield Ataxia-Telangiectasia Boards-Relevant Pattern Wobbly toddler with spider veins on the eyes and recurrent sinus infections = AT. The board-clinching lab finding elevated AFP in a child with ataxia and telangiectasias. Radiosensitivity is the reason chemotherapy must be carefully dosed — DNA repair is broken. Mind Map Primary Immunodeficiency Classification By Primary Defect Phagocyte - LAD Type 1 CD18 defect; no pus; neutrophilia; delayed cord separation. T-Cell Pure - DiGeorge Syndrome 22q11.2 deletion; CATCH-22; absent thymus; hypocalcemia. B-Cell Humoral - XLA Bruton s BTK defect; absent B cells; no tonsils; onset >6 months. - Hyper-IgM CD40L defect; IgM high IgG/IgA/IgE low; PCP risk. - Job Syndrome STAT3 mutation; cold abscesses; FATED; only AD disorder. Combined B+T - SCID IL-2Rgamma X-linked T-/B+/NK- or ADA T-/B-/NK- ; no thymus; TRECs absent. - Wiskott-Aldrich WASp defect; WATER triad; small platelets; elevated IgE/IgA. - Ataxia-Telangiectasia ATM defect; ataxia + telangiectasias + elevated AFP; lymphoma risk. Key Diagnostic Clues at a Glance | Finding | Think... | |---|---| | No pus at infection sites | LAD Type 1 | | Delayed cord separation >30 days | LAD Type 1 | | Neutrophilia >20 000 with infections | LAD Type 1 | | Absent thymic shadow on CXR | SCID or DiGeorge | | Low TRECs on newborn screening | SCID | | T-/B-/NK- | ADA-deficient SCID | | T-/B+/NK- | X-linked SCID | | Neonatal tetany / hypocalcemia | DiGeorge absent parathyroids | | Tetralogy of Fallot in infant + T-cell deficiency | DiGeorge | | Absent tonsils and lymph nodes | XLA Bruton s | | Onset of infections at 6 months | XLA maternal IgG waning | | High IgM low everything else | Hyper-IgM | | Cold abscesses + lion facies + retained teeth + high IgE | Job Syndrome STAT3 | | Autosomal Dominant PID | Job Syndrome | | Thrombocytopenia small platelets + eczema + infections | Wiskott-Aldrich | | Elevated IgE + IgA in male infant | Wiskott-Aldrich | | Cerebellar ataxia starting in toddler + conjunctival spider veins | Ataxia-Telangiectasia | | Elevated AFP in a child with immune deficiency | Ataxia-Telangiectasia | questions 1 A 3-week-old male infant presents with failure to thrive and persistent watery diarrhea. Newborn screening performed at birth showed undetectable T-cell receptor excision circles TRECs . A chest X-ray reveals the absence of a thymic shadow. Flow cytometry of peripheral blood confirms an absence of T cells B cells and NK cells. Which of the following is the most likely underlying genetic defect? CD40 Ligand deficiency 22q11.2 microdeletion Bruton Tyrosine Kinase mutation Adenosine Deaminase ADA deficiency IL-2 receptor gamma chain mutation 3 The absence of ALL three lymphocyte lineages T B AND NK cells points to ADA deficiency autosomal recessive SCID . Deoxyadenosine accumulation is toxic to all lymphocytes equally. In contrast X-linked SCID IL-2Rgamma mutation produces T-/B+/NK- as B cells can still develop without the IL-2 gamma chain — they just cannot function without T-cell help. DiGeorge 22q11.2 has T-cell deficiency but B cells are present and BTK mutation XLA only affects B cells. 2 A 6-month-old male is evaluated for recurrent episodes of otitis media and sinusitis that started at age 4 months. Physical examination is notable for the absence of palpable cervical lymph nodes and impressively small tonsils. Flow cytometry shows normal CD3+ T-cell counts but a near-total absence of CD19+ B cells. What is the most likely diagnosis? DiGeorge Syndrome X-linked Agammaglobulinemia Bruton s Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome Common Variable Immunodeficiency Leukocyte Adhesion Deficiency 1 XLA Bruton s Agammaglobulinemia is characterized by a BTK mutation causing arrest of B-cell maturation. CD19+ B cells are nearly absent while T cells are normal. Because there are no mature B cells there are no germinal centers — hence absent tonsils and non-palpable lymph nodes. Symptoms start after 6 months when maternal IgG wanes. DiGeorge would show low T-cells; CVID typically presents in the 2nd-3rd decade; WAS would show platelet abnormalities. 3 A 4-year-old child presents with a progressive unsteady gait since age 18 months. Physical examination reveals dilated spider-like vessels on the bulbar conjunctiva. Laboratory testing shows a significantly elevated serum alpha-fetoprotein AFP and low IgA levels. This patient is at significantly increased risk for which of the following? Neonatal tetany due to hypocalcemia Bleeding due to small dysfunctional platelets Hematologic malignancy Lymphoma or Leukemia Cold staphylococcal abscesses with retained primary teeth Omphalitis with absent pus 2 This patient has Ataxia-Telangiectasia cerebellar ataxia + bulbar telangiectasias + elevated AFP + low IgA. The ATM gene defect causes impaired DNA double-strand break repair — chromosomal translocations accumulate in T-cell and B-cell receptor loci during V D J recombination dramatically increasing the risk of lymphoma and leukemia. AFP elevation is a classic and often board-tested feature. Radiation sensitivity is also important to recognize. 4 A neonate is evaluated because the umbilical cord has not separated by day 32 of life. Examination of the umbilical stump shows erythema but no purulent discharge. A complete blood count reveals a WBC of 38 000/microliter with 88% neutrophils. The remainder of the differential and hemoglobin are normal. What is the underlying molecular defect in this infant? STAT3 mutation NADPH oxidase deficiency CD18 / Beta-2 integrin deficiency Myeloperoxidase deficiency WASp protein mutation 2 This presentation — delayed umbilical cord separation >30 days absent pus despite active infection and profound neutrophilia — is pathognomonic for Leukocyte Adhesion Deficiency LAD Type 1. The CD18 defect prevents neutrophils from performing diapedesis out of blood vessels. They accumulate in the blood neutrophilia but cannot enter tissue no pus . Normal umbilical cord separation requires infiltrating neutrophils expressing CD18 to facilitate the process. 5 A 2-year-old male is brought in for recurrent skin infections. He also has severe eczema and a history of easy bruising. A peripheral blood smear reveals platelets that are significantly smaller than normal. What antibody pattern is most consistent with this diagnosis? Low IgA elevated IgG Elevated IgE and IgA low to normal IgM Elevated IgM severely low IgG and IgA Absent IgA only normal all other isotypes Normal levels of all antibody isotypes 1 This is Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome WAS the classic triad of thrombocytopenia with small platelets eczema and recurrent infections in a male infant. The characteristic antibody pattern is elevated IgE and IgA alongside normal to low IgG and IgM. This pattern is opposite to most immunodeficiencies and is a useful board discriminator. Small platelet size on smear is the hallmark laboratory finding that distinguishes WAS platelet destruction from other causes of thrombocytopenia. What is the molecular defect in LAD Type 1 and what is the pathognomonic clinical finding? CD18 beta-2 integrin defect. Pathognomonic delayed umbilical cord separation >30 days with absent pus despite infection and peripheral neutrophilia. Clinical What distinguishes ADA-deficient SCID from X-linked SCID on flow cytometry? ADA deficiency T-/B-/NK- all lymphocytes absent — dATP toxicity . X-linked SCID IL-2Rgamma T-/B+/NK- B cells present but non-functional . Concept Describe the CATCH-22 mnemonic for DiGeorge Syndrome. Cardiac defects Abnormal facies Thymic aplasia low T cells Cleft palate Hypocalcemia absent parathyroids . Caused by 22q11.2 microdeletion. Clinical Why do XLA patients first present at 6 months of age? Maternal IgG crosses the placenta and protects the infant for the first 6 months. Once maternal antibody levels wane the infant s absent BTK-dependent B cells cannot produce replacement antibodies. Concept What is the mechanism of Hyper-IgM Syndrome and why do patients get PCP? CD40L on T cells defect prevents class-switch from IgM to IgG/IgA/IgE. CD40L is also required to activate macrophages against intracellular pathogens so Pneumocystis jirovecii and Cryptosporidium infections occur. Clinical What is the FATED mnemonic for Job Syndrome? Facies coarse/lion-like Abscesses cold staph abscesses — no warmth or redness Teeth retained primary teeth IgE profoundly elevated Dermatitis/bone fractures. STAT3 mutation; autosomal dominant. Concept What are the three components of the Wiskott-Aldrich triad and what is the distinguishing lab finding? Thrombocytopenia small platelets Eczema Recurrent infections. Distinguishing lab small platelets on peripheral smear and elevated IgE + IgA opposite of most PIDs . Clinical What is the classic triad of Ataxia-Telangiectasia and what is the board-specific lab clue? Cerebellar ataxia progressive starts ~18 months + telangiectasias bulbar conjunctiva and skin + immunodeficiency especially IgA deficiency . Board clue elevated serum AFP. Clinical Which primary immunodeficiency is autosomal dominant? Job Syndrome Hyper-IgE Syndrome — STAT3 mutation. All other classic PIDDs discussed are either X-linked recessive or autosomal recessive. Concept What is the curative treatment for SCID LAD Wiskott-Aldrich and Ataxia-Telangiectasia? SCID LAD and Wiskott-Aldrich Hematopoietic Stem Cell Transplant HSCT . Ataxia-Telangiectasia no curative treatment exists — supportive care only. Clinical No Pus = Think LAD The absence of purulent discharge at any infection site is pathognomonic for LAD. Pus requires neutrophils; if they cannot leave the blood CD18 defect there is no pus — yet the CBC shows striking neutrophilia. Absent Shadow = Absent Thymus Any child with absent thymic shadow on CXR should prompt workup for SCID or DiGeorge. Both lose T-cell maturation; the mechanism genetic vs. developmental differs but the radiographic sign is shared. The 6-Month Rule for XLA XLA patients are asymptomatic for the first 6 months because maternal IgG protects them. When infections begin at exactly 4-8 months always think of antibody deficiency XLA or CVID before other causes. Job s Unique Inheritance Among the primary immunodeficiencies tested as a group Job Syndrome STAT3 mutation is the only one with autosomal dominant inheritance. This fact — combined with cold abscesses and elevated IgE — makes it uniquely identifiable. Elevated AFP = AT on the Boards Ataxia-Telangiectasia is the only immunodeficiency with a characteristically elevated alpha-fetoprotein. When you see a wobbly child with spider veins and high AFP the diagnosis is AT until proven otherwise. Vaccine Safety in Immunodeficiency Live attenuated vaccines MMR Varicella FluMist Rotavirus Yellow Fever are absolutely contraindicated in SCID and severely immunocompromised patients. The vaccine strain can replicate and cause fatal disease. Always screen before vaccinating."}, {"id": "l147", "title": "Public and Global Health: Role of Immunizations", "module": "DPR", "content": "id l147 module DPR metadata l147 Public and Global Health Role of Immunizations lecturer C. Abraham M.D. session Lecture 147 Public Health readingTime 15-20 min resources pdf content/L147 HighYield Render.pdf analysis content/L147 HighYield Render Analysis.md BandB Immunology > Basic > Vaccines and Immunization Physeo Immunology > Immunology > Vaccinations OME Pediatrics > Vaccinations FirstAid Immunology > Immune Responses > Vaccines SketchyMicro Viruses > DNA Viruses > Human Papillomavirus Papillomaviridae CDC Immunization Schedules cdc.gov/vaccines/schedules match HPV vaccine subtypes covered Gardasil resources AnKing Step 1 v11 AK Step1 v11 OME banner Clinical 11 Pediatrics 08 Vaccinations | AK Step1 v11 Physeo 08 Immunology 01 Immunology 19 Vaccinations | AK Step1 v11 SketchyMicro 03 Viruses 03 DNA 08 Human papillomavirus Papillomaviridae match HPV 6/11 low risk vs 16/18/31/33 high risk resources AnKing Step 1 v11 AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 03 Microbiology 05 Virology 08 DNA Viruses HPV | AK Step1 v11 B&B 13 Infectious Disease 06 Viruses 02 DNA Viruses match Hepatitis B vaccine - near total elimination of mother-to-child transmission resources AnKing Step 1 v11 AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 03 Microbiology 05 Virology 09 RNA Viruses | AK Step1 v11 OME banner Clinical 11 Pediatrics 08 Vaccinations match Herd immunity threshold - measles 95% resources AnKing Step 1 v11 AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 01 Behavioral Science 06 Epidemiology & Biostatistics 07 Outbreak Investigation | AK Step1 v11 OME banner Clinical 11 Pediatrics 08 Vaccinations match Live attenuated vs inactivated vs component vaccines resources AnKing Step 1 v11 AK Step1 v11 Physeo 08 Immunology 01 Immunology 19 Vaccinations | AK Step1 v11 B&B 13 Infectious Disease Vaccines match mRNA vaccine mechanism SARS-CoV-2 resources AnKing Step 1 v11 AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 03 Microbiology 05 Virology 08 DNA Viruses | AK Step1 v11 OME banner Clinical 11 Pediatrics 08 Vaccinations match Koilocytes / HPV pathology cervical carcinoma prevention resources AnKing Step 1 v11 AK Step1 v11 SketchyPath 08 Repro & GU 01 Uterine & Vulvovaginal Disorders 02 Cervical Neoplasia | AK Step1 v11 OME banner Clinical 13 Gynecology 02 Cervical Cancer match Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System VAERS resources AnKing Step 1 v11 AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 01 Behavioral Science 06 Epidemiology & Biostatistics 07 Outbreak Investigation | NYIT OMS-2 PPOMII 2026 Public and Global Health match Smallpox eradication - global campaign resources AnKing Step 1 v11 AK Step1 v11 FirstAid 03 Microbiology 05 Virology 08 DNA Viruses | AK Step1 v11 OME banner Clinical 11 Pediatrics 08 Vaccinations match Subunit toxoid and conjugate vaccine types resources AnKing Step 1 v11 AK Step1 v11 Physeo 08 Immunology 01 Immunology 19 Vaccinations | AK Step1 v11 B&B 13 Infectious Disease Vaccines Lecture 147 Public and Global Health Role of Immunizations Lecturer C. Abraham M.D. Session Lecture 147 Introduction Why Vaccines Are a Cornerstone of Public Health Slides 1-4 Story Mode Dr. Abraham frames this lecture with a sweeping historical perspective—from the days of Edward Jenner inoculating children with cowpox in the 18th century to the roll-out of mRNA COVID-19 vaccines in 2021. The core message is that immunizations represent arguably the single greatest public health intervention in human history saving hundreds of millions of lives across generations. Having a working knowledge of how vaccines function how they are categorized and how to communicate their value to patients is not merely an academic exercise it is a fundamental clinical competency. Historical Milestone Edward Jenner introduced the concept of vaccination in 1796 using cowpox to immunize against smallpox — the first deliberate use of a live attenuated pathogen. Eradication of Smallpox The World Health Organization WHO declared smallpox eradicated in 1980 — the only human disease ever eradicated globally through vaccination. This remains the gold standard benchmark for vaccine success. Near-Eradication of Polio Polio is nearly eliminated worldwide but a critical complication arose — the oral polio vaccine OPV which contains a live attenuated virus occasionally mutates back to neurovirulence and can circulate in under-vaccinated communities. Vaccine-derived poliovirus VDPV is now a recognized concern. The injectable inactivated polio vaccine IPV does not carry this risk. highyield Smallpox vs. Polio Eradication Smallpox was fully eradicated because 1 it has no animal reservoir 2 there is no asymptomatic carrier state and 3 the vaccine provides complete immunity. Polio has not been fully eradicated because the oral vaccine can revert and circulate. This is a direct consequence of choosing a live attenuated vaccine type. How Vaccines Work Immunological Mechanisms Slides 5-10 Story Mode Before diving into specific vaccines Dr. Abraham reviews the immunological principles that make vaccines effective. A vaccine works by exposing the immune system to an antigen — or a piece of one — without causing disease so that a memory is established. When the real pathogen arrives later the immune system recognizes it and mounts a much faster and stronger response. The key insight is that different vaccine platforms engage the immune system in different ways which has critical implications for efficacy safety and who can receive them. Primary vs. Secondary Immune Response Primary Response A naive immune system exposed to an antigen for the first time takes 1-2 weeks to mount a peak IgM response. This is slow and may not be fast enough to prevent disease. Secondary Response Anamnestic Upon re-exposure or after vaccination memory B and T cells rapidly generate high-titer IgG. This is fast robust and durable — this is what vaccines exploit. Types of Immunity Conferred Neutralizing Antibodies Block pathogen entry into host cells. T-Cell Mediated Cellular Live attenuated vaccines are particularly effective at generating a strong CD4+ and CD8+ T cell response — critical for pathogens that live intracellularly. Both antibody and T-cell responses are needed for long-lasting protection. highyield The Immunological Basis of Boosters Some vaccines e.g. DTaP Tdap require booster doses because antibody titers wane over time. The booster re-stimulates memory cells restoring protective immunity. This is distinct from vaccines like MMR which may confer lifelong immunity after two doses. Vaccine Platform Types The Classification You Must Know Slides 11-16 Story Mode Dr. Abraham spends considerable time on vaccine platforms because different platforms come with different implications for patient safety. The most clinically important distinction is between live attenuated vaccines which can cause disease in immunocompromised patients and all other forms which cannot . Live vaccines generate the most robust long-lasting immunity but carry constraints around who can receive them. Live Attenuated Vaccines Contain weakened but replicating pathogen. Generate the most robust antibody AND T-cell responses. Confer long-lasting often lifelong immunity with fewer doses. CONTRAINDICATED in pregnant women immunocompromised individuals HIV with low CD4 count on chemotherapy on high-dose steroids and patients with SCID. Examples MMR measles mumps rubella Varicella chickenpox Yellow Fever Oral Polio Vaccine OPV — not used in the US FluMist nasal influenza Rotavirus oral . Inactivated Killed Vaccines Contain inactivated whole pathogen killed by heat or chemical . Cannot replicate; cannot cause disease. Generally require multiple doses and boosters. Examples Injected Influenza vaccine flu shot Inactivated Polio IPV Hepatitis A Rabies. Subunit / Component Vaccines Contain only a specific structural piece of the pathogen protein or polysaccharide antigen . Safest for immunocompromised patients; cannot cause disease. Examples Hepatitis B HBsAg surface antigen HPV virus-like particles Pertussis acellular — aP component of DTaP Pneumococcal PCV13/PCV15/PCV20 conjugate . Toxoid Vaccines Inactivated bacterial toxins — train the immune system to neutralize toxin rather than the organism itself. Examples Tetanus toxoid Diphtheria toxoid both in DTaP/Tdap . Conjugate Vaccines Polysaccharide antigens are chemically linked to a carrier protein to enhance immunogenicity polysaccharides alone are T-independent and do not induce memory in young children . Examples Hib Haemophilus influenzae type b PCV pneumococcal conjugate MenACWY meningococcal . mRNA Vaccines novel platform Lipid nanoparticles deliver mRNA encoding a pathogen antigen e.g. SARS-CoV-2 spike protein . Host cells produce the protein temporarily; immune response is mounted. Does NOT alter host DNA — mRNA never enters the cell nucleus. Rapidly developed and highly effective; new capabilities for future vaccines. Examples Moderna and Pfizer-BioNTech COVID-19 vaccines. correlate Live Attenuated Vaccine Safety - Cannot Administer To Pregnant individuals patients with primary or secondary immunodeficiency patients on immunosuppressive medications high-dose steroids chemotherapy biologics . - Can Administer To generally HIV-positive patients IF CD4 count is above a safe threshold usually >200 cells/microliter and on effective antiretroviral therapy. - Household Contact Rule When someone in the household is immunocompromised healthcare providers must consider whether live vaccine virus can be shed and transmitted. e.g. the oral rotavirus vaccine is shed in stool — a concern if caring for immunocompromised family members. Herd Immunity The Math That Protects Us All Slide 17 Story Mode Dr. Abraham explains herd immunity not just as a concept but as a mathematical reality with real-world stakes. When enough people in a population are immune — through vaccination or prior infection — the pathogen cannot sustain transmission chains and even unvaccinated individuals including those too young too ill or allergic are protected. This is the social contract of vaccination. Definition The protection of a community achieved when enough individuals are immune that transmission cannot sustain itself the chain of infection breaks . Herd Immunity Threshold HIT The percentage of the population that must be immune to prevent epidemic spread. Calculated as HIT = 1 - 1/R0 where R0 is the basic reproductive number. R0 Examples critical numbers Measles R0 = 12-18 extremely contagious ; HIT = 95% Polio R0 approximately 5-7; HIT approximately 80-85% COVID-19 original R0 approximately 2-3; HIT approximately 50-67% Influenza seasonal R0 approximately 1.2-1.4; HIT approximately 25-33% Measles in the US At its peak coverage the US had approximately 98% MMR vaccination rates in most regions — above the herd immunity threshold which is why measles was declared eliminated in 2000. As vaccine hesitancy has led to declining coverage outbreaks have re-emerged. highyield Herd Immunity Threshold = 1 - 1/R0 For measles with an R0 of approximately 15 HIT = 1 - 1/15 = 93-95%. This means you need 95 out of every 100 people to be immune to prevent an epidemic. When coverage falls below 95% measles WILL break out — as seen in multiple recent US outbreaks. Specific Vaccine Impact Data Numbers That Matter Slides 18-28 Story Mode Dr. Abraham presents compelling quantitative data on the impact of key vaccines — numbers that can and should be used in patient conversations. These are real-world triumphs of public health that demonstrate without ambiguity that vaccines work. Measles MMR Vaccine Live attenuated vaccine; 2 doses achieve approximately 97% immunity. Herd immunity threshold 95% highest of any common vaccine-preventable disease . WHO estimates Between 2010 and 2018 the measles vaccine prevented approximately 23 million deaths worldwide — mostly from measles pneumonia. Post-measles immune amnesia Measles infection causes the immune system to forget up to 73% of previously acquired immunity to other pathogens — lasting up to 2 years after infection. Even prior MMR vaccination cannot fully protect against this if the child gets measles. Complication prevented Subacute Sclerosing Panencephalitis SSPE — a fatal progressive brain disease occurring 7-10 years after measles infection. Haemophilus influenzae type b Hib Vaccine Conjugate vaccine — polysaccharide linked to protein carrier required because pure polysaccharide is T-independent and does not work in infants under 2 . Near-total eradication of Hib meningitis in children. Reduction in otitis media from Hib. Herd immunity effect Unvaccinated children also benefit from reduced circulation of Hib. Residual Hib disease now occurs mainly in adults with waning immunity or from non-type-b strains. Streptococcus pneumoniae Pneumococcal Conjugate Vaccine — PCV 45% decrease in hospitalizations; 68% decrease in invasive disease in children; 75% decrease in mortality. Not as dramatic as Hib because of the large number of serotypes >90 known ; vaccines cover only the most common. Newer vaccines cover more serotypes PCV15 PCV20 — reductions are growing. Hepatitis B Vaccine Subunit vaccine HBsAg — surface antigen produced in yeast . Near-total elimination of mother-to-child vertical transmission achieved by the newborn dose given on the day of birth . Without the birth dose approximately 40-50 cases of perinatal hepatitis B/year in the US. Perinatal infection leads to chronic hepatitis B in approximately 90% of neonates vs. only approximately 5-10% in adults which can progress to cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma HCC . The hepatitis B vaccine is effectively an anti-cancer vaccine. Estimated 99% decrease in pediatric hepatitis B prevalence in the US since vaccination began. Projected to save 39 million lives worldwide between 2000 and 2030. Human Papillomavirus HPV Vaccine Gardasil 9 Subunit virus-like particle vaccine. Nonavalent 9-valent Gardasil covers HPV types 6 11 16 18 31 33 45 52 and 58. HPV 6 and 11 Low-risk; responsible for approximately 90% of genital warts condyloma acuminatum and laryngeal papillomatosis. HPV 16 and 18 High-risk; responsible for approximately 70% of cervical cancers. Also implicated in oropharyngeal anal vulvar vaginal and penile cancers. Efficacy 56-71% decrease in HPV prevalence among adolescents and young women since vaccine introduction. Projected impact 80-90% decrease in cervical cancer rates when given before sexual debut recommended age 11-12 years; catch-up through age 26 and in select individuals up to age 45 . Sweden is pursuing cervical cancer eradication using the HPV vaccine as a primary prevention tool. The HPV vaccine does NOT work retroactively — it must be given before exposure to the covered strains for full benefit. SARS-CoV-2 mRNA Vaccines Highly effective against severe disease hospitalization and death from COVID-19. During the Delta variant wave ICUs in the United States were overwhelmingly filled with unvaccinated individuals — the vast majority of deaths occurred in unvaccinated populations. Demonstrated a new proof-of-concept for rapid mRNA vaccine development — a platform with broad future applications for infectious diseases and cancer. Vaccine Schedules What You Must Know for Practice Slide 29 Story Mode Dr. Abraham is practical here he does not expect physicians to memorize entire vaccine schedules the CDC schedule alone runs to multiple pages but does expect them to know where to look and to be able to counsel patients. The key skill is not memorization but navigation — knowing which authoritative sources to consult. You do not need to memorize the entire schedule but you do need to know The CDC Immunization Schedule cdc.gov/vaccines/schedules — updated annually. American Academy of Pediatrics AAP — publishes the Red Book and supports the CDC schedule. Infectious Diseases Society of America IDSA — for complex immunocompromised populations. WHO Immunization Schedule — for international travel/global health. Key clinical scenario When a patient is traveling internationally to a region where a disease is endemic e.g. Yellow Fever for Sub-Saharan Africa/South America Japanese Encephalitis for East Asia the physician must consult the CDC travel vaccine recommendations. Special populations with modified schedules Immunocompromised patients Live vaccines often contraindicated. Asplenic/functionally asplenic patients High priority for pneumococcal Hib and meningococcal vaccines. Pregnant patients Tdap strongly recommended in every pregnancy 27-36 weeks ; flu vaccine recommended; all live vaccines contraindicated. Elderly >65 High-dose or adjuvanted influenza; RSV vaccine; Shingrix recombinant zoster vaccine at age 50+. Vaccine Hesitancy and Communication Your Most Important Clinical Skill Slides 30-33 Story Mode Dr. Abraham pivots to arguably the most clinically impactful section of the lecture — how to actually talk to patients. He emphasizes that scientific consensus on vaccine safety and efficacy is absolute; there is no legitimate medical controversy. However patient behavior is profoundly shaped by fear mistrust social networks and the media ecosystem. The physician s role is not to lecture but to build trust through empathetic two-way conversation. Three distinct patient phenotypes demand different communication strategies. Three Patient Phenotypes 1. Vaccine Acceptors Vaccinate their children believe in vaccines. No special communication needed. 2. Anti-vaxxers Entrenched ideologically motivated by factors unrelated to health. Very difficult to change. Often motivated by secondary gain political identity social validation financial gain from selling alternative products . The evidence does not move them. 3. Vaccine Hesitant The most important group for physicians to engage. These patients genuinely want what is best for their family. They are torn between competing voices and information ecosystems. The physician who builds trust and engages in genuine dialogue has the highest impact here. Why Patients Are Vaccine Hesitant Do not perceive the disease as personally relevant I never get the flu . Misunderstand statistical risk I got the flu shot and then got sick — actually this was an immune response to the inactivated vaccine not the flu. Inactivated flu vaccines contain no live virus and cannot cause influenza . Vaccine dragon — patients feel mildly unwell after vaccination mild fever myalgias and attribute this to getting the flu from the shot. In reality this is a normal immune response indicating the vaccine is working. Lack experience of the pre-vaccine era e.g. at the turn of the 20th century approximately 50% of children died before age 5 — largely from infectious diseases now prevented by vaccines . Mistrust of healthcare institutions due to historical abuses or personal negative experiences. Exposure to self-validating isolationism — echo chambers social media family networks that reinforce anti-vaccine beliefs. Misinformation During COVID-19 only 12 social media entities were responsible for approximately 80% of vaccine misinformation online. Key Communication Strategies Do not lecture or shame — patients shut down when they feel attacked. Ask do not tell — find out what the patient s specific concern is before addressing it. Two-way conversation — acknowledge their concerns as legitimate starting points. Be the trusted source — physicians remain among the most trusted sources of medical information for patients. Use that trust. Science is evolving not waffling — updating recommendations as evidence accumulates is a feature of science not a flaw. Help patients understand this. Debunking Common Vaccine Myths Slide 34 Story Mode Dr. Abraham systematically dismantles the most common vaccine myths that physicians will encounter. These talking points should be in every physician s toolkit. Myth Vaccines don t prevent infection. Fact Vaccines reduce severe disease death complications and transmission. Some vaccines e.g. measles do prevent infection nearly entirely. Others reduce severity enough to prevent hospitalization and death. Myth You have to be infected to build immunity. Fact Vaccines expose the immune system to an antigen or piece of one sufficient to build memory — infection is not required. Myth Inactivated vaccines can give you the disease. Fact Inactivated and component vaccines contain NO replicating pathogen. They cannot cause infectious disease. Post-vaccination symptoms mild fever soreness are immune responses not illness. Myth Natural infection is always better than vaccination. Fact For some diseases vaccine-induced immunity is MORE robust than natural infection e.g. HPV vaccine provides stronger and more sustained immunity than infection . Natural infection also carries the risk of death disability and transmitting disease to others. Myth Vaccines contain dangerous chemicals aluminum thimerosal . Fact No currently US-licensed vaccine contains mercury/thimerosal — the only vaccine that did multi-dose influenza vial is no longer manufactured. Aluminum adjuvants when studied have not been linked to any disease process. Myth mRNA vaccines alter your DNA. Fact mRNA never enters the cell nucleus. It cannot integrate into or alter the host s DNA. Myth Vaccines are developed purely for profit. Fact Most vaccines with the exception of COVID-19 which was pandemic-driven are money-losers for pharmaceutical companies. They are produced largely as a public health obligation. Myth Spreading out the vaccine schedule is safer. Fact Alternate schedules have not been validated by research. Their effectiveness and safety compared to the studied schedule cannot be predicted. The standard schedule is designed to protect children at the most vulnerable ages. The Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System VAERS and Legal Framework Slides 35-36 VAERS Vaccine Adverse Event Reporting System A passive voluntary surveillance system — not a controlled study. Patients caregivers and healthcare providers can report any adverse event after vaccination. Limitation Reports represent correlation not causation. Because reporting is voluntary and incomplete VAERS over-represents severe events and under-represents mild ones. Clinical value VAERS has generated important safety signals that led to identification of previously unknown adverse events e.g. myocarditis risk with COVID-19 mRNA vaccines in young males . The program is under ongoing review and evolution. National Vaccine Injury Compensation Program NVICP Created after vaccine litigation in the 1970s-80s reduced the number of vaccine manufacturers from approximately 20 to only 2 threatening the entire vaccine supply. A no-fault alternative to litigation — removes financial liability from companies paid for by a small tax on each vaccine administered approximately 75 cents per dose . An independent review panel of government officials and healthcare providers evaluates claims and issues settlements. Historically approximately $4 billion paid out from 1988-2019. Result Encouraged more companies to enter the vaccine market significantly expanding the number of available vaccines. Mind Map Vaccine Platform Summary By Mechanism Live Attenuated MMR Varicella Yellow Fever FluMist Rotavirus OPV not used in US Key rule Contraindicated in immunocompromised and pregnant patients. Inactivated IPV Hepatitis A Rabies Flu injected Key rule Safe for all; require more doses. Subunit/Component Hepatitis B HBsAg HPV VLP Pertussis acellular Pneumococcal conjugate PCV Key rule Conjugation required for immunogenicity in infants. Toxoid Diphtheria in DTaP/Tdap Tetanus in DTaP/Tdap Key rule Targets toxin not organism. mRNA COVID-19 Pfizer-BioNTech Moderna Key rule Does not alter DNA; cannot cause COVID-19. By Disease Impact | Vaccine | Impact | |---|---| | Measles MMR | 23 million deaths prevented 2010-2018 ; HIT = 95% | | Hib | Near-eradication of Hib meningitis in children | | Pneumococcus PCV | 68% decrease in invasive disease; 75% decrease in mortality | | Hepatitis B | 99% decrease in pediatric prevalence; 39 million lives saved 2000-2030 | | HPV | 56-71% decrease in HPV prevalence; 80-90% projected decrease in cervical cancer | | COVID-19 mRNA | Dramatic reduction in severe disease and death during pandemic | Herd Immunity Reference Formula HIT = 1 - 1/R0 Measles R0 approximately 15; HIT approximately 95% COVID-19 R0 approximately 2-3; HIT approximately 50-67% questions 1 A 6-week-old infant is brought to a well-child visit. The mother states she is concerned about the MMR vaccine because her older child who has leukemia lives in the household and receives chemotherapy. The pediatrician recommends proceeding with all scheduled vaccines. Regarding the MMR vaccine specifically which of the following statements BEST explains the concern and the appropriate action? MMR is an inactivated vaccine and therefore poses no risk to immunocompromised household contacts MMR is a live attenuated vaccine that can be shed and transmitted to immunocompromised contacts so it should be deferred MMR is a live attenuated vaccine but shedding does not occur so it is safe to administer The MMR vaccine is contraindicated only in the immunocompromised patient themselves not their household contacts The risk to the immunocompromised sibling is higher from natural measles than from vaccine-strain measles so MMR should be given 4 This is a nuanced scenario. The AAP and CDC recommend that household contacts of immunocompromised individuals SHOULD receive MMR because the risk of the immunocompromised person acquiring wild-type measles from an unvaccinated contact far exceeds any theoretical risk from vaccine-strain transmission. MMR vaccine-strain measles is generally not transmissible from vaccinated contacts unlike the oral polio vaccine which IS shed . Therefore vaccinating the infant protects the immunocompromised sibling via herd immunity. 2 In a community where 88% of residents are vaccinated against measles a single imported measles case arrives. What is the most likely outcome and what does this illustrate? An epidemic will occur because the herd immunity threshold of 85% has not been crossed The community is protected because 88% exceeds the minimum vaccination rate for any disease An epidemic will occur because measles requires approximately 95% vaccination coverage to prevent outbreaks The single case will be self-limiting because measles is rarely transmissible in vaccinated communities An outbreak is unlikely because the case will be rapidly identified and quarantined 2 Measles has an extraordinarily high R0 12-18 giving it the highest herd immunity threshold of any common vaccine-preventable disease at approximately 95%. At 88% coverage the community has fallen below this threshold meaning measles CAN sustain transmission chains. Each infected person will on average infect more than one other person among the susceptible 12% leading to an outbreak. This is why even modest declines in MMR coverage have led to measles resurgences in previously protected communities. 3 A 35-year-old woman with newly diagnosed HIV CD4 count 95 cells/microliter and no prior vaccination history presents to establish care. Which of the following vaccines is MOST APPROPRIATE to administer today? MMR measles mumps rubella Varicella chickenpox Yellow Fever Inactivated influenza vaccine injection FluMist intranasal live attenuated influenza 3 This patient has advanced HIV with a CD4 count below the threshold typically 200 cells/microliter at which live attenuated vaccines become unsafe. MMR Varicella Yellow Fever and FluMist are all live attenuated vaccines and are contraindicated in patients with severe immunocompromise. The inactivated influenza injection is a killed/inactivated vaccine that cannot cause disease and is safe to administer to any immunocompromised patient. It is in fact strongly recommended in HIV-positive individuals. 4 A family declines the hepatitis B vaccine for their newborn because the baby is not sexually active and does not inject drugs. What is the MOST compelling argument for administering the hepatitis B vaccine on the day of birth? The vaccine prevents an acute hepatitis B illness that is often fatal in neonates Perinatal hepatitis B transmission leads to chronic infection in approximately 90% of exposed neonates which can progress to cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma Hepatitis B is primarily transmitted by the fecal-oral route and is common in daycare settings Without the birth dose the infant cannot receive subsequent doses of the hepatitis B series The vaccine prevents transmission to household members during diaper changes 1 The fundamental reason for the birth dose of hepatitis B vaccine is to prevent perinatal mother-to-child transmission. Neonates infected with hepatitis B at birth develop chronic infection at a rate of approximately 90% compared to only approximately 5-10% of adults . Chronic hepatitis B carries a lifetime risk of cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma. The birth dose combined with hepatitis B immune globulin HBIG when the mother is HBsAg-positive provides near-complete protection against this potentially fatal outcome. 5 A patient tells you I already had COVID-19 so I don t need the vaccine — natural immunity is better than a vaccine. Based on current evidence what is the MOST accurate response? Natural infection always provides superior lifelong immunity compared to any vaccine This is correct; post-infection immunity is durable and the vaccine adds no benefit For some diseases vaccine-induced immunity can be more robust or durable than natural infection and the vaccine may also boost existing post-infection immunity Natural infection immunity lasts exactly 6 months and then the vaccine is required The vaccine is only effective if administered within 30 days of infection 2 The premise that natural immunity is always better is a myth. For several pathogens vaccine-induced immunity is actually more robust more durable or more broadly protective than natural infection. For HPV the vaccine produces higher and more sustained antibody titers than natural infection. For COVID-19 vaccination including after infection was associated with greater protection against variants. Additionally natural infection carries significant risks of severe disease death and long-term sequelae — risks that vaccines avoid while still building immunity. What is the herd immunity threshold HIT for measles and what is the formula? HIT approximately 95%. Formula HIT = 1 - 1/R0 . Measles R0 approximately 12-18. Clinical Which vaccine platform CANNOT be given to immunocompromised or pregnant patients? Live attenuated vaccines e.g. MMR Varicella Yellow Fever FluMist Rotavirus OPV . Clinical What makes conjugate vaccines superior to pure polysaccharide vaccines in infants? Polysaccharides are T-independent antigens and do not generate immune memory in children under 2. Conjugation to a protein creates a T-dependent response with memory B cells. Concept Why is the hepatitis B birth dose so critical? Perinatal hepatitis B infection leads to chronic infection in ~90% of neonates vs ~5-10% in adults risking cirrhosis and HCC. The birth dose prevents this. Clinical How does an mRNA vaccine differ from a live attenuated vaccine in terms of cellular immunity? mRNA vaccines generate antigen presentation via MHC class I eliciting CD8+ T cell responses like live attenuated without using replicating pathogen. Live vaccines also replicate extending antigen presentation. mRNA vaccines cannot cause disease. Concept What is immune amnesia caused by measles infection? Measles infection can wipe out pre-existing immunity including from prior vaccines by depleting memory B and T cells leaving the patient vulnerable to other infections for up to 2 years. Clinical Which HPV types cause genital warts vs. cervical cancer? HPV 6 and 11 = genital warts low risk . HPV 16 18 31 33 = cervical/oropharyngeal/anal cancer high risk . Gardasil 9 covers all 9 of the most important types. Clinical What is the National Vaccine Injury Compensation Program NVICP ? A no-fault federal program established in 1988 to compensate patients for vaccine injuries without litigation. Funded by a ~$0.75 tax on each vaccine dose. Prevented the collapse of the vaccine manufacturing industry. Concept What is the vaccine dragon phenomenon? Mild fever malaise and soreness after vaccination — a normal immune response. The flu shot CANNOT cause the flu no replicating virus . These symptoms mean the immune system is responding. Clinical Which disease is the ONLY disease ever eradicated globally and why was eradication possible? Smallpox. Eradication was possible because no animal reservoir no asymptomatic carrier state and the vaccine provides complete long-lasting immunity. Concept The Eradication Paradox Smallpox was eradicated because we had a perfect vaccine and no animal reservoir. Polio is not fully eradicated partly because the oral polio vaccine live mutates back to virulence — a risk that did not exist with smallpox s vaccine. HIT Math Is Board Gold Memorize the formula HIT = 1 - 1/R0 . Measles R0=15 needs 95% coverage. Any question about measles outbreaks in a highly vaccinated community likely involves coverage falling below this threshold. The Birth Dose Imperative Neonates infected with hepatitis B at birth develop chronic infection 90% of the time. The birth dose of hepatitis B vaccine is not about sexual activity — it is about preventing carcinoma. Vaccine-Dragon Counseling Tell patients in advance You might feel a little achy or have a mild fever for a day or two — that means the vaccine is working. A day of mild symptoms is far better than a week of influenza. This reduces the perception that the shot gave them the flu. Hesitancy vs. Anti-vax Vaccine-hesitant patients CAN be moved with respectful dialogue. True anti-vaxxers are entrenched and driven by non-health motivations. Do not exhaust clinical energy on the latter at the expense of the former. mRNA Does Not Touch DNA mRNA vaccines work in the cytoplasm only; they never enter the nucleus and cannot integrate into the genome. This distinction is critical for patient counseling."}, {"id": "l149", "title": "Hematology: ABO Groups, Rh Disease, and Transfusion Medicine", "module": "Unknown", "content": "id l149 module Unknown metadata l149 Hematology ABO Groups Rh Disease and Transfusion Medicine lecturer W. Zaloga D.O. session Lecture 149 Hematology readingTime 12-15 min resources Boards and Beyond Hematology > Basic Concepts > Blood Groups Pathoma Hematology > Hemolytic Anemias First Aid Hematology > Pathology > Transfusion Reactions SketchyPath Hematology > Anemias > Hemolytic Anemias Overview match Transfusion Reactions resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid Hematology Oncology Pathology Transfusion Reactions match RhoGAM administration resources AnKing Step 1 v11 SketchyPath 05 Hematology 01 Anemias 05 Hemolytic Anemias Overview match ABO Blood Groups resources AnKing Step 1 v11 B&B 10 Hematology 01 Basic Concepts 02 Blood Groups match Delayed Hemolytic Transfusion Reaction resources AnKing Step 1 v11 B&B 10 Hematology 01 Basic Concepts 02 Blood Groups Delayed Hemolysis match Direct vs Indirect Coombs Test resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 03 RBC Pathology 02 Hemolytic Anemias Coombs Test Lecture 149 Hematology — ABO Groups Rh Disease and Transfusion Medicine Lecturer W. Zaloga D.O. Session Lecture 149 1. Immunologic Basis of Transfusion Medicine Slides 1–8 Transfusion medicine is fundamentally an exercise in applied immunology. When we transfuse blood we are introducing foreign antigens into a recipient whose immune system is designed to destroy anything it doesn t recognize as self. The safety of every transfusion depends on matching the donor s antigens to the recipient s blood type. If we fail the consequences range from mild fevers to fatal intravascular hemolysis. Understanding the two major antigen systems — ABO and Rh — and the antibodies they provoke is the foundation upon which all of transfusion medicine is built Slide 2 . Two Categories of Antibodies Natural Naturally Occurring Formed without prior transfusion injection or pregnancy. Predominantly IgM cold-reacting optimal at room temperature . The ABO system is the critical exception — its IgM antibodies are active at 37°C body temperature and are potent activators of complement leading to rapid intravascular hemolysis Slide 5 . Lecturer Emphasis Immune Acquired Require prior sensitization transfusion pregnancy . Predominantly IgG warm-reacting optimal at 37°C . Cause extravascular hemolysis opsonized RBCs removed by splenic/hepatic macrophages . Most clinically significant Anti-Rh D C c E e Anti-Kell Anti-Kidd Anti-Duffy Slide 6 . IgM vs IgG — The Size Rule RBCs in circulation are held apart by an ionic cloud ~25 nm . IgM is 35 nm tip-to-tip and can span this distance → direct agglutination. IgG is only 14 nm → cannot directly agglutinate RBCs in saline → requires the Coombs AHG reagent to bridge the gap Slide 10 . IgG crosses the placenta; IgM does not. This single fact explains why Rh-HDFN occurs IgG anti-D crosses to attack fetal RBCs but ABO-HDFN is generally milder IgM anti-A/B cannot cross . highyield ABO antibodies are the most dangerous in transfusion medicine. They are naturally occurring IgM that fix complement at body temperature → immediate intravascular hemolysis. An ABO-incompatible transfusion almost always due to clerical error can be fatal within minutes. 2. The ABO Blood Group System Slides 9–15 The ABO system is not a simple protein antigen like Rh. It is built from carbohydrates that are added to a precursor molecule by enzyme-producing genes. The lecturer emphasized that understanding this biochemical pathway is the key to understanding why Type O is universal and why Bombay phenotype exists Slide 9 . Lecturer Emphasis The Biosynthetic Pathway Paragloboside → the base oligosaccharide on the RBC surface. H Gene → codes for fucosyltransferase → adds a terminal fucose to paragloboside → creates H-substance the required foundation for A and B . A Gene → codes for N-acetylgalactosaminyltransferase → adds N-acetylgalactosamine to H-substance → Type A . B Gene → codes for galactosyltransferase → adds galactose to H-substance → Type B . O Gene → amorph no functional enzyme → H-substance remains unmodified → Type O Slide 11 . Antibody Production By 3–6 months of age individuals naturally produce antibodies against the ABO antigens they lack likely due to cross-reactivity with environmental antigens bacteria pollen . Type O individuals produce both anti-A and anti-B including significant IgG which can cross the placenta . Worldwide Frequency O > A > B > AB. correlate Bombay Phenotype Oh - Pathophysiology Homozygous for a non-functional H gene h/h . Without H-substance neither A nor B antigens can be produced regardless of the A or B genes present. - Presentation Phenotypically appears as Type O on forward grouping. However they also produce anti-H antibody in addition to anti-A and anti-B. - Diagnosis Discrepant typing; reagent anti-H fails to agglutinate patient s cells. - Treatment Can ONLY receive blood from another Bombay individual. Transfusing any standard blood type including Type O will cause a fatal hemolytic reaction due to anti-H. - Lecturer s Point Everybody else has that H substance except for this phenotype. They can only get blood from another person with that phenotype. Slide 13 3. Compatibility Testing — The Lab Methods Slides 16–23 Before any transfusion can occur the blood bank must answer two questions What is the patient s blood type? And are there any unexpected antibodies that could destroy donor cells? The lecturer walked through the stepwise approach from forward grouping through the crossmatch emphasizing that each test builds on the last Slide 16 . Lecturer Emphasis | Test | What It Detects | Method | Clinical Use | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | Forward Grouping | RBC antigens patient cells + reagent anti-A/B | Immediate spin room temp | Determines ABO type | | Reverse Grouping | Serum antibodies patient serum + reagent A/B cells | Immediate spin | Confirms forward grouping | | DAT Direct Coombs | IgG or C3 already bound to patient s RBCs in vivo | AHG added to patient s washed RBCs | Is antibody stuck to cells inside the body? HDFN AIHA transfusion reaction workup | | IAT Indirect Coombs | Free antibodies in patient s serum that could bind RBCs | Patient serum + reagent cells → incubate → AHG | Antibody screen crossmatch | | Type & Screen | ABO/Rh + antibody screen | Forward + Reverse + IAT screen | Pre-surgical hold; does NOT remove blood from inventory | | Type & Cross | Screen + major crossmatch donor RBCs + patient serum | Full crossmatch ~1 hour | Reserves specific units for patient; removes from inventory | highyield DAT = What s stuck to the patient s cells? in vivo coating — AIHA HDFN transfusion reaction . IAT = What s floating in the patient s serum? screening for unexpected antibodies before transfusion . 4. The Rh System and Hemolytic Disease of the Fetus & Newborn Slides 24–30 After the ABO system the Rh system is the most important blood group for safe transfusion. While the Rh system contains over 40 antigens D C c E e being the most tested it is the D-antigen that determines Rh-positive or Rh-negative status and is the most immunogenic of all non-ABO antigens. The Rh system is the most frequent target of alloantibodies in transfusion and in pregnancy Slide 24 . Lecturer Emphasis Anti-D is NOT naturally occurring. It requires sensitization transfusion of Rh+ blood to an Rh− individual or fetomaternal hemorrhage . Anti-D is IgG → crosses the placenta → extravascular hemolysis splenic destruction of fetal RBCs . correlate Hemolytic Disease of the Fetus and Newborn HDFN / Erythroblastosis Fetalis - Pathophysiology Rh− mother is sensitized by Rh+ fetal RBCs fetomaternal hemorrhage usually during delivery . 1st pregnancy low-affinity low-titer IgG anti-D formed. 2nd pregnancy with Rh+ fetus high-titer high-affinity IgG anti-D crosses placenta → destroys fetal RBCs → severe fetal anemia. - Presentation In utero fetal anemia → compensatory extramedullary hematopoiesis → Hydrops Fetalis generalized edema pleural effusions . Post-natal massive release of unconjugated bilirubin → Kernicterus deposition of bilirubin in basal ganglia → neurologic damage . - Diagnosis Positive DAT on cord blood; maternal anti-D detected by IAT. - Treatment In utero transfusion for severe cases; exchange transfusion and phototherapy post-delivery. - Risk Factors Rh− mother Rh+ father prior sensitization. - Lecturer s Point If she has another Rh positive fetus then you ll get the second response — high titer high affinity antibodies — which will cross the placenta and cause lysis. Slide 27 mnemonic RhoGAM Timing 28 + 72 - 28 RhoGAM given at 28 weeks gestation antepartum prophylaxis - 72 RhoGAM given within 72 hours of delivery postpartum - Mechanism Passive anti-D IgG clears fetal RBCs from maternal circulation before her immune system can mount an active response. ABO-HDFN can occur in the 1st pregnancy Type O mother + Type A/B baby . Generally milder because maternal anti-A/B are predominantly IgM don t cross placenta well though Type O mothers have significant IgG component. 5. Transfusion Reactions Slides 31–45 Transfusion reactions have a very low overall incidence ~1% but clinicians must be able to rapidly identify the type because the management differs dramatically. The lecturer walked through each reaction systematically from most common FNHTR allergic to the most feared AHTR TA-GVHD Slide 31 . Lecturer Emphasis | Reaction | Mechanism | Key Features | Prevention / Treatment | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | AHTR Acute Hemolytic | ABO incompatibility clerical error ; IgM + complement → intravascular hemolysis | Fever flank pain red/brown urine DIC ↓ haptoglobin ↑ LDH AKI | Stop transfusion; IV fluids + pressors; diuresis for renal protection; return blood to lab | | FNHTR Febrile Non-Hemolytic | Cytokines from stored donor WBCs; most common reaction | Fever chills; no hemolysis | Leukoreduction ; antipyretics | | Allergic | Abs against donor plasma proteins | Urticaria → bronchospasm → anaphylaxis | Antihistamines; epinephrine if severe; washed products for recurrence | | Anaphylaxis | Anti-IgA in IgA-deficient recipients | Immediate urticaria → bronchospasm → hypotensive shock | Washed products remove plasma proteins ; epinephrine | | TACO Circulatory Overload | Volume overload cardiogenic pulmonary edema | Dyspnea crackles elevated CVP/PCWP hypertension | Diuretics furosemide ; slow infusion rate | | TRALI Transfusion-Related Acute Lung Injury | Donor anti-neutrophil Abs → activate recipient s pulmonary neutrophils | Non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema; normal CVP | Supportive; avoid implicated donors multiparous females with plasma-rich products | | DHTR Delayed Hemolytic | Anamnestic response to minor antigens Kidd Kell Duffy Rh ; IgG → extravascular hemolysis | Low Hb increased bilirubin new antibody days later; positive DAT | Extended phenotype matching; supportive | | TA-GVHD | Donor T-cells engraft in immunocompromised host → attack host tissues | Rash liver failure pancytopenia; >90% fatal | Irradiation of blood products inactivates donor T-cells | | Septic | Bacterial contamination highest risk with platelets stored at room temp | Fever rigors hypotension/shock | Stop transfusion; broad-spectrum antibiotics; culture | correlate Transfusion-Associated Circulatory Overload TACO vs TRALI - Pathophysiology TACO = volume overload cardiogenic . TRALI = immune-mediated neutrophil activation in lungs non-cardiogenic . - Presentation Both cause acute dyspnea and pulmonary edema post-transfusion. - Diagnosis The critical differentiator is the CVP/PCWP TACO = elevated volume overload . TRALI = normal capillary leak not volume . - Treatment TACO → diuretics. TRALI → supportive diuretics may worsen! . - Lecturer s Point TACO is cardiogenic — the heart can t handle the volume. TRALI is the donor s antibodies attacking the recipient s lungs. Slide 38 6. Blood Components and Special Processing Slides 46–60 Modern transfusion medicine rarely uses whole blood. Instead a single donation is centrifuged into components each stored under specific conditions and used for specific indications. The lecturer emphasized the practical aspects storage times temperatures and the indications that drive component selection Slide 46 . Lecturer Emphasis Packed RBCs pRBCs Refrigerated; shelf life 35 days citrate or 42 days AdSol . 1 unit raises Hb by 1 g/dL and Hct by 3% . Transfuse when Hb <7 g/dL general or higher if coronary artery disease. Platelets Stored at room temperature with continuous agitation → highest bacterial contamination risk . Transfuse when <10K prophylaxis <50K active bleeding <100K CNS surgery . FFP Fresh Frozen Plasma Contains all coagulation factors. Used for DIC liver disease warfarin reversal plasma exchange. Frozen at −20°C. Cryoprecipitate Cold-insoluble fraction of FFP. Contains Fibrinogen Factor VIII vWF Factor XIII Fibronectin . Used for fibrinogen deficiency uremic bleeding vWD DIC. Universal RBC donor = O negative. Universal plasma donor = AB positive. Special Processing Leukoreduction Filters remove WBCs → prevents FNHTR + CMV transmission + HLA alloimmunization Slide 52 . Lecturer Emphasis Irradiation Gamma or X-ray inactivates donor T-cells → prevents TA-GVHD in immunocompromised recipients infants <6 months hematologic malignancies HLA-matched donors . Washing Isotonic saline removes plasma proteins → for IgA-deficient patients with recurrent allergic/anaphylactic reactions. Citrate Toxicity Citrate chelates Ca²⁺ → hypocalcemia perioral tingling prolonged QT cardiac arrhythmias in massive transfusion neonates or liver disease. MTP Massive Transfusion Protocol 1 1 1 ratio of pRBCs Plasma Platelets. Re-check labs after ~5 units pRBCs. Consider tranexamic acid to prevent clot dissolution Slide 56 . Lecturer Emphasis questions 1 A 32-year-old Rh-negative woman delivers her first Rh-positive baby without complications. She did NOT receive RhoGAM during pregnancy. She is now pregnant with her second child who is also Rh-positive. At 28 weeks she develops high-titer anti-D IgG antibodies. Which of the following best explains the pathophysiology of the anemia developing in her fetus? Maternal IgM anti-D crosses the placenta and fixes complement on fetal RBCs Maternal IgG anti-D crosses the placenta and opsonizes fetal RBCs for splenic destruction Fetal anti-A antibodies attack maternal RBCs entering the fetal circulation Maternal NK cells directly kill fetal RBCs via antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity in the bone marrow The fetus develops autoantibodies against its own D-antigen 2 In Rh-HDFN maternal IgG anti-D formed during sensitization in the first pregnancy crosses the placenta and coats fetal Rh+ RBCs. These opsonized cells are then removed by macrophages in the fetal spleen extravascular hemolysis . IgM does not cross the placenta. This is a classic anamnestic secondary immune response — high titer high affinity IgG. 2 A blood bank technologist performs a Direct Antiglobulin Test DAT on a patient who received a transfusion 3 hours ago and now has a fever and dark urine. The DAT is strongly positive. What does this result indicate? Free antibodies are present in the patient s serum that could bind donor cells The patient has an IgA deficiency causing anaphylaxis IgG and/or C3 are already bound to the patient s or transfused RBCs in vivo The patient s platelets are being destroyed by anti-HPA-1A antibodies The donor blood was contaminated with bacteria 3 The DAT Direct Coombs test detects IgG or C3 already bound to RBCs in the patient s circulation. A positive DAT in the setting of a transfusion reaction confirms that antibodies have coated the transfused cells supporting a diagnosis of hemolytic transfusion reaction either acute or delayed . The IAT by contrast detects free antibodies in serum. 3 A patient with no prior history of transfusion receives his first unit of packed RBCs. Thirty minutes into the infusion he develops high fever flank pain and his urine turns dark red. Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of this reaction? IgG antibodies against Rh antigens causing extravascular hemolysis Donor anti-neutrophil antibodies activating recipient pulmonary neutrophils Cytokines released from stored donor leukocytes Pre-formed IgM anti-ABO antibodies fixing complement and causing intravascular hemolysis Volume overload from rapid infusion 4 The acute onset of fever flank pain and red/dark urine hemoglobinuria in a first-time transfusion recipient points to an Acute Hemolytic Transfusion Reaction AHTR . Since this patient has never been transfused the antibodies are naturally occurring — these are pre-formed IgM anti-A or anti-B antibodies that activate complement at body temperature causing immediate intravascular hemolysis. This is most commonly caused by ABO incompatibility due to clerical error. 4 A patient develops acute respiratory distress 4 hours after receiving a platelet transfusion. Chest X-ray shows bilateral pulmonary infiltrates. The central venous pressure CVP is measured and found to be NORMAL. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? Transfusion-Associated Circulatory Overload TACO Transfusion-Related Acute Lung Injury TRALI Acute Hemolytic Transfusion Reaction Anaphylactic transfusion reaction Bacterial contamination of the platelet unit 2 The key differentiator between TACO and TRALI is the CVP/PCWP. TACO cardiogenic pulmonary edema presents with elevated CVP because the heart is overloaded. TRALI non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema caused by donor anti-neutrophil antibodies activating recipient lung neutrophils presents with a NORMAL CVP. Both cause bilateral infiltrates and respiratory distress but the hemodynamics distinguish them. 5 A severely immunocompromised patient with acute leukemia requires a blood transfusion. The blood bank sends irradiated leukoreduced packed RBCs. What is the specific purpose of irradiating the blood product? To destroy any bacteria that may have contaminated the unit To remove donor plasma proteins that could cause an allergic reaction To inactivate donor T-lymphocytes and prevent transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease To reduce the risk of febrile non-hemolytic transfusion reactions To prevent transmission of cytomegalovirus 3 Irradiation gamma or X-ray inactivates viable donor T-lymphocytes in the blood product. In immunocompetent recipients donor T-cells are rapidly cleared. In immunocompromised patients leukemia bone marrow transplant neonates donor T-cells can engraft and mount an immune attack against the host → TA-GVHD which is >90% fatal. Leukoreduction reduces FNHTR and CMV risk but does NOT reliably prevent TA-GVHD. What is the biosynthetic precursor for all ABO antigens? Paragloboside → H gene adds fucose → H-substance → A/B genes modify further. Concept What type of antibodies are ABO antibodies and why are they dangerous? Naturally occurring IgM that activate complement at 37°C → rapid intravascular hemolysis . Concept What is the Bombay phenotype ? h/h genotype → no H-substance → phenotypes as Type O but has anti-H → can ONLY receive Bombay blood. Clinical What does a positive DAT indicate? IgG or C3 is already bound to the patient s RBCs in vivo e.g. AIHA HDFN transfusion reaction . Concept What does the IAT Indirect Coombs detect? Free antibodies in the patient s serum that could potentially bind to RBCs. Used in antibody screening and crossmatching. Concept When should RhoGAM be given to an Rh-negative mother? At 28 weeks gestation AND within 72 hours postpartum. Clinical What is the mechanism of HDFN ? Rh− mother sensitized by Rh+ fetus → forms IgG anti-D → 2nd pregnancy high-titer IgG crosses placenta → extravascular hemolysis of fetal RBCs. Clinical How do you differentiate TACO from TRALI ? CVP/PCWP TACO = elevated cardiogenic . TRALI = normal non-cardiogenic capillary leak . Clinical Which blood product has the highest risk of bacterial contamination and why? Platelets — stored at room temperature vs. refrigerated RBCs . Clinical What does irradiation of blood products prevent? TA-GVHD — inactivates donor T-lymphocytes that could engraft in immunocompromised hosts. Clinical What does leukoreduction prevent? FNHTR febrile non-hemolytic reactions CMV transmission and HLA alloimmunization . Clinical What is the MTP 1 1 1 ratio ? Massive Transfusion Protocol 1 pRBC 1 Plasma 1 Platelet to prevent dilutional coagulopathy. Clinical The Clerical Error Rule The most common cause of a fatal AHTR is not an immunologic mystery — it s a clerical error . Wrong patient wrong label wrong blood. Always check two patient identifiers. Bombay Trap Bombay phenotype looks like Type O on forward grouping. But give them Type O blood and you kill them — they have anti-H. The only safe blood is from another Bombay donor. IgM = Immediate + Intravascular; IgG = Delayed + Extravascular IgM fixes complement → MAC → intravascular lysis AHTR . IgG opsonizes → splenic removal → extravascular lysis DHTR HDFN . The exception ABO-HDFN Type O mom s IgG anti-A/B . TACO vs TRALI — Check the Pressure Both cause dyspnea + bilateral infiltrates after transfusion. Elevated CVP = TACO volume overload → diuretics . Normal CVP = TRALI immune-mediated → supportive only . Citrate Steals Calcium Citrate is the anticoagulant in blood bags. It chelates Ca²⁺. In massive transfusion neonates or liver failure can t metabolize citrate watch for hypocalcemia — tingling prolonged QT arrhythmias."}, {"id": "l150", "title": "Hematopoiesis", "module": "Unknown", "content": "id l150 module Unknown metadata l150 Hematopoiesis lecturer M.M. Plummer M.D. session Lecture 150 Pathology & Foundations readingTime 12-15 min resources Pathoma Hematology > Red Blood Cell Pathology > Hematopoiesis BandB Hematology > Basic Concepts > Hematopoiesis First Aid Hematology > Physiology > Hematopoiesis Physeo Hematology > Hematopoiesis match Hematopoietic Stem Cell Differentiation resources AnKing Step 1 v11 B&B 10 Hematology 01 Basic Concepts 01 Hematopoiesis match Erythropoietin EPO resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid Hematology Oncology Physiology Erythropoietin match Bone Marrow Cellularity resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 05 RBC Pathology 01 Bone Marrow match Extramedullary Hematopoiesis resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 05 RBC Pathology 01 Bone Marrow Extramedullary match Granulocyte Maturation Sequence resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid Hematology Oncology Physiology WBC Maturation Lecture 150 Hematopoiesis Lecturer M.M. Plummer M.D. Session Lecture 150 1. Where Does Hematopoiesis Occur? Slides 1–6 Hematopoiesis — the formation of all blood cells — does not happen in a single location throughout development. Understanding its migration is critical because when the bone marrow fails in adults hematopoiesis reverts to its fetal sites liver and spleen a process called extramedullary hematopoiesis. Dr. Plummer emphasized that understanding this hierarchy is the foundation for understanding leukemias and marrow failure syndromes later in the block Slide 1 . Lecturer Emphasis Fetal Development — The Migration Pattern Yolk Sac weeks 3–8 First site of hematopoiesis; blood islands form here. Liver 6 weeks – birth Becomes the primary site by the 2nd month; predominantly erythropoiesis . Spleen Minor role during fetal life; contributes to hematopoiesis alongside the liver. Bone Marrow 2nd trimester – lifelong Becomes dominant by the 2nd trimester and is the exclusive site after birth. Bone Marrow in Postnatal Life Infants Nearly all bones contain red active marrow — hematopoietically active highly vascular with sinusoids. Adults Red marrow progressively replaced by yellow fatty marrow . By age 16–18 red marrow is largely restricted to flat bones pelvis/iliac crest sternum vertebrae and proximal femur Slide 5 . Lecturer Emphasis Bone marrow biopsy sites posterior iliac crest most common or sternum. mnemonic Fetal Hematopoiesis Migration Young Liver Synthesizes Blood - Y olk sac weeks 3-8 - L iver 6 wk – birth predominantly erythropoiesis - S pleen minor fetal role - B one marrow 2nd trimester → lifelong 2. Bone Marrow Cellularity and the 100 Minus Age Rule Slides 7–9 When a pathologist examines a bone marrow biopsy they first assess its cellularity — the ratio of hematopoietic cells to fat adipocytes . This is critical because both too many cells hypercellular as in leukemia and too few cells hypocellular/aplastic as in aplastic anemia are pathologic. The lecturer emphasized a simple rule to estimate expected cellularity for age Slide 7 . Lecturer Emphasis Normal Marrow Requirements Must show tri-lineage maturation myeloid WBC erythroid RBC and megakaryocytic platelet lineages all present. Must have appropriate cellularity for age. highyield Bone Marrow Cellularity Formula Expected cellularity % ≈ 100 − age ±10% . A 50-year-old should have ~50% cellularity range 40-60% . Less = hypocellular aplastic anemia post-chemo . More = hypercellular leukemia myeloproliferative disorders . Hypercellular >expected Packed with cells very little fat → leukemia myeloproliferative disorders reactive states. Hypocellular <expected Too much fat too few cells → drugs chloramphenicol benzene radiation. Aplastic Almost no hematopoietic cells; marrow replaced entirely by fat → aplastic anemia post-ablative chemotherapy for bone marrow transplant Slide 9 . 3. The Hematopoietic Hierarchy — From Stem Cell to Mature Cell Slides 10–17 All blood cells arise from a single common ancestor — the pluripotent hematopoietic stem cell HSC . This is the monophyletic theory of hematopoiesis. Dr. Plummer walked through the differentiation tree step-by-step emphasizing that blasts of all lineages look morphologically identical under the microscope — you need flow cytometry CD markers to tell them apart Slide 14 . Lecturer Emphasis Pluripotent HSC CD34+ → Common Myeloid Progenitor CFU-GEMM → 5 paths CFU-GM → myeloblast → neutrophils OR monoblast → monocytes CFU-Eo → myeloblast → eosinophils CFU-B → myeloblast → basophils CFU-Meg → megakaryoblast → megakaryocyte → platelets CFU-E → proerythroblast → erythrocyte Common Lymphoid Progenitor CFU-L → Lymphoblast → T-cells B-cells NK cells Key Teaching Point Blasts of all types look essentially the same under light microscopy. The CD cluster of differentiation surface markers identified by flow cytometry are required to distinguish myeloblasts from lymphoblasts from megakaryoblasts. The one exception megakaryoblasts are identifiable because they are significantly larger Slide 15 . Lecturer Emphasis 4. Growth Factors and Colony-Stimulating Factors Slides 18–20 Differentiation doesn t happen spontaneously — it is driven by cytokines small signaling proteins that push progenitor cells along their respective lineages. The lecturer highlighted two growth factors as absolutely essential to memorize EPO and TPO Slide 18 . Lecturer Emphasis | Growth Factor | Source | Target Lineage | Clinical Relevance | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | EPO Erythropoietin | Peritubular interstitial cells of the renal cortex in response to hypoxia | Erythroid CFU-E → RBCs | CKD → ↓ EPO → anemia. Tx recombinant EPO L153 | | TPO Thrombopoietin | Liver also kidney | Megakaryocytic CFU-Meg → platelets | Stimulates megakaryocyte maturation + platelet production | | G-CSF | Various | Granulocytic neutrophils | Used clinically to boost neutrophil counts e.g. post-chemo | | GM-CSF | Various | Granulocytes + monocytes | Broader stimulation than G-CSF | | M-CSF | Various | Monocytic lineage | Monocyte/macrophage differentiation | | IL-3 | T-cells | Multi-lineage broad | Early-acting; works on multiple progenitors | highyield EPO comes from the kidney renal cortex not the liver. This is why patients with chronic kidney disease develop normocytic anemia — they can t produce adequate EPO. TPO comes from the liver. Remember E Erythro = K i dney; T Thrombo = L iver. 5. Granulocyte Maturation — The Neutrophil Series Slides 21–30 The granulocyte series has the most complex maturation hierarchy and Dr. Plummer spent significant time walking through each stage. The critical concept is that specific granules do not appear until the late myelocyte/early metamyelocyte stage — this is where the cell commits to becoming a neutrophil eosinophil or basophil Slide 25 . Lecturer Emphasis Maturation Sequence Myeloblast → Promyelocyte primary/azurophilic granules → Myelocyte secondary/specific granules begin → Metamyelocyte kidney-bean shaped nucleus; lineage commitment definitive → Band horseshoe/C-shaped nucleus → Segmented Neutrophil 2–4 lobes Key Morphologic Landmarks Promyelocyte Large cell with prominent thick granules primary granules . Relevant to Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia APL/M3 L154 . Myelocyte → Metamyelocyte junction Where lineage commitment occurs neutrophilic vs eosinophilic vs basophilic because specific granules appear. Band form Can be seen in peripheral blood in severe infections → left shift = increased bands + metamyelocytes in blood. Mature PMN/Neutrophil Should have 2–4 lobes . ≥5 lobes = hypersegmentation B12/folate deficiency L152 . correlate Left Shift Bandemia - Pathophysiology In severe infection/stress the bone marrow releases immature granulocytes bands metamyelocytes into the peripheral blood faster than normal. - Presentation Elevated WBC count with increased percentage of band forms on the differential. - Diagnosis >6% bands on peripheral blood smear; may see metamyelocytes and even myelocytes in severe cases. - Lecturer s Point If there s a really high infection you will have neutrophils and if they re really high they will have bands. Slide 27 6. Monocyte Series Slides 31–33 The monocyte lineage is the simplest CFU-GM → Monoblast → Promonocyte → Monocyte blood → Macrophage tissue . Monocytes circulate for only about 16 hours before migrating into tissues where they become macrophages/histiocytes. The lecturer noted that tissue-resident macrophages have specific names depending on location Slide 32 . Lecturer Emphasis Kupffer cells liver Langerhans cells skin Microglia CNS Alveolar macrophages lungs Osteoclasts bone Monocytes are larger than neutrophils have more cytoplasm and lack prominent granules — but they can be confused with bands morphologically Slide 33 . 7. Erythropoiesis — The Red Cell Series Slides 34–38 Red blood cell production is driven primarily by erythropoietin EPO from the kidney. The maturation sequence shows progressive cell shrinkage and increasing hemoglobin content culminating in nuclear extrusion to produce an anucleate reticulocyte Slide 34 . Lecturer Emphasis Maturation Sequence CFU-E → Proerythroblast blast → Basophilic erythroblast blue = lots of RNA/ribosomes → Polychromatophilic erythroblast gray-blue = beginning hemoglobin → Orthochromatophilic erythroblast / Normoblast pink = mostly hemoglobin; very dense nucleus → Nuclear extrusion → Reticulocyte → Mature Erythrocyte Reticulocyte Immature RBC that still contains residual RNA gives it a slightly basophilic tint . Normal 1–2% of circulating RBCs. Elevated reticulocyte count indicates the marrow is responding to blood loss or hemolysis. Low reticulocyte count in anemia = marrow failure / underproduction. Human RBCs are anucleate unlike birds reptiles and fish which retain nuclei . correlate Extramedullary Hematopoiesis EMH - Pathophysiology When the bone marrow cannot meet demand for blood cell production replaced by fibrosis metastatic disease or severe chronic anemia hematopoiesis reverts to its fetal sites — primarily the liver and spleen . - Presentation Hepatosplenomegaly; may see nucleated RBCs normoblasts and tear-drop cells dacrocytes in peripheral blood. - Diagnosis Peripheral smear showing leukoerythroblastic picture immature WBCs + nucleated RBCs ; hepatosplenomegaly on exam. - Risk Factors Myelofibrosis most classic association severe thalassemia widespread bone marrow metastases. - Lecturer s Point What happens is something called extramedullary hematopoiesis. Medullary refers to the bone marrow so it s outside the bone marrow. Slide 11 8. Thrombopoiesis — Platelet Production Slides 39–43 Platelets are anucleate fragments derived from the largest cell in the bone marrow — the megakaryocyte . The maturation sequence is CFU-Meg → Megakaryoblast → Promegakaryocyte → Megakaryocyte → Platelets . The megakaryocyte develops a complex multi-lobulated nucleus and forms platelet demarcation channels in its cytoplasm which then fragment off into individual platelets that enter the sinusoidal blood supply Slide 42 . Lecturer Emphasis Thrombopoietin TPO The critical growth factor driving megakaryocyte maturation. Produced by the liver . Megakaryocytes are easily identifiable on bone marrow biopsy — they are enormous compared to all other cells and have complex multi-lobed nuclei. Platelet lifespan ~ 10 days . Normal platelet count 150 000–400 000/µL . 9. Clinical Correlations — Leukemia Preview Slides 44–52 Dr. Plummer concluded with three clinical cases illustrating why understanding hematopoiesis matters in clinical practice Slide 44 . Lecturer Emphasis correlate Acute Leukemia — Marrow Crowding - Pathophysiology Malignant expansion of a single blast lineage fills the bone marrow crowding out normal hematopoietic elements. Despite a very high WBC count the cells are immature blasts babies that cannot function — they cannot fight infection. - Presentation Pancytopenia triad 1 Anemia fatigue pallor — ↓ RBC production ; 2 Thrombocytopenia bleeding gums petechiae epistaxis — ↓ platelet production ; 3 Infection recurrent fevers — functional neutropenia despite high WBC . Also bone pain marrow expansion hepatosplenomegaly gingival hyperplasia especially AML-M4/M5 lymphadenopathy. - Diagnosis >20% blasts in bone marrow WHO or peripheral blood. Flow cytometry to determine lineage myeloid vs lymphoid . - Lecturer s Point It s like sending babies to war — they cannot do anything. It has to be the mature neutrophil that will fight off an infection. Slide 49 Case 1 63yo RUQ pain WBC 12K Acute cholecystitis → neutrophils elevated bacterial infection → neutrophilia . WBC elevation can represent infection inflammation AND stress — all of the above Slide 44 . Case 2 36yo bleeding gums WBC 194K 44% blasts platelets 12K Acute leukemia → blasts crowd out normal marrow → thrombocytopenia bleeding + functional neutropenia infections despite sky-high WBC. Case 3 5yo girl bone pain epistaxis 30% lymphoblasts Acute lymphoblastic leukemia ALL — most common childhood cancer. Lymphoblasts pack the marrow → bone pain pancytopenia L154 . Case 4 3yo boy hives + wheezing after eating eggs Allergic/atopic disease → eosinophils elevated. questions 1 A 50-year-old man undergoes a bone marrow biopsy as part of a workup for pancytopenia. The pathologist reports that the marrow cellularity is 25%. What is the best interpretation of this finding? Normal cellularity for age Hypercellular marrow concerning for leukemia Hypocellular marrow concerning for aplastic anemia or marrow failure Aplastic marrow consistent with post-chemotherapy ablation The cellularity cannot be interpreted without knowing the patient s gender 3 Expected cellularity = 100 − age ±10% . For a 50-year-old 100 − 50 = 50% range 40-60% . A cellularity of 25% is significantly below the expected range indicating a hypocellular marrow. This is concerning for conditions like aplastic anemia drug-induced marrow suppression or myelodysplastic syndrome. Aplastic marrow essentially no cells would be more extreme. 2 A pathologist examining a bone marrow aspirate sees cells at the myelocyte stage. At this stage the cell has just begun producing specific granules. If the specific granules are eosinophilic bright red-orange at what maturation stage did this cell commit to the eosinophil lineage? Myeloblast Promyelocyte Late myelocyte to early metamyelocyte Band form Proerythroblast 3 Dr. Plummer emphasized that lineage commitment neutrophil vs. eosinophil vs. basophil occurs at the late myelocyte to early metamyelocyte stage when specific secondary granules become visible. Before this point all granulocyte precursors look morphologically identical. The myeloblast and promyelocyte stages are too early — they only have primary azurophilic granules. 3 A patient with myelofibrosis presents with massive hepatosplenomegaly. A peripheral blood smear shows tear-drop cells dacrocytes and nucleated red blood cells. What process best explains these findings? Acute hemolytic anemia with intravascular hemolysis Extramedullary hematopoiesis in the liver and spleen Disseminated intravascular coagulation consuming platelets Pernicious anemia with megaloblastic changes Iron deficiency from chronic blood loss 2 In myelofibrosis the bone marrow is replaced by fibrosis and cannot produce adequate blood cells. Hematopoiesis shifts to its fetal sites — the liver and spleen extramedullary hematopoiesis EMH . This causes hepatosplenomegaly. The peripheral smear shows a leukoerythroblastic picture with tear-drop cells squeezed through fibrotic marrow and nucleated RBCs released from EMH sites before full maturation . 4 A 4-year-old boy presents with bone pain fever petechiae and a WBC count of 85 000/µL with 60% lymphoblasts. His hemoglobin is 6 g/dL and platelet count is 15 000/µL. Despite the extremely high WBC count he has recurrent infections. Which of the following best explains why this child is immunocompromised? The elevated WBC count causes hyperviscosity that impairs neutrophil migration The blast cells consume all available immunoglobulins The blast cells are immature and non-functional; mature neutrophils are crowded out of the marrow The child has developed autoantibodies that destroy his mature lymphocytes The leukemia has directly invaded and destroyed the thymus 3 This is the key concept Dr. Plummer emphasized It s like sending babies to war — they cannot do anything. In acute leukemia the marrow is packed with immature blasts that are non-functional. Despite a sky-high WBC count there are very few mature functional neutrophils available to fight infection. The blasts also crowd out normal erythroid and megakaryocytic lineages causing anemia Hb 6 and thrombocytopenia platelets 15K . 5 A researcher is studying the hematopoietic stem cell HSC and wants to identify it using flow cytometry. Which surface marker is most commonly used to identify pluripotent hematopoietic stem cells? CD3 CD19 CD34 CD20 CD56 3 CD34 is the surface marker that identifies pluripotent hematopoietic stem cells in the bone marrow. It is used clinically to identify and collect stem cells for bone marrow transplantation. CD3 marks T-cells CD19/CD20 mark B-cells and CD56 marks NK cells — these are all lineage-specific markers for mature cells not the undifferentiated HSC. What is the sequence of hematopoietic sites during fetal development? Yolk sac wk 3-8 → Liver/Spleen 6 wk – birth → Bone marrow 2nd trimester → lifelong . Liver phase is predominantly erythropoiesis. Concept What surface marker identifies hematopoietic stem cells ? CD34 — used to identify and collect stem cells for bone marrow transplant. Concept What is the formula for expected bone marrow cellularity? 100 − age ±10% . A 30-year-old should have ~70% cellularity 60-80% . Concept What is extramedullary hematopoiesis and when does it occur? Blood cell production outside the bone marrow liver spleen . Occurs when marrow fails myelofibrosis severe anemia marrow infiltration. Causes hepatosplenomegaly. Clinical What does a hypocellular/aplastic bone marrow look like? Almost entirely fat adipocytes with very few hematopoietic cells. Seen in aplastic anemia post-chemo ablation radiation. Concept What does CFU-GEMM stand for and what does it produce? Colony-Forming Unit — G ranulocyte E rythrocyte M onocyte M egakaryocyte. It is the common myeloid progenitor giving rise to all non-lymphoid blood cells. Concept At what stage does a granulocyte commit to becoming a neutrophil eosinophil or basophil? At the late myelocyte / early metamyelocyte stage — when specific secondary granules first appear. Concept What is the granulocyte maturation sequence? Myeloblast → Promyelocyte primary granules → Myelocyte specific granules begin → Metamyelocyte kidney-bean nucleus → Band horseshoe nucleus → Segmented neutrophil 2-4 lobes . Concept Where is erythropoietin EPO produced? Peritubular interstitial cells of the renal cortex in response to hypoxia . Concept Where is thrombopoietin TPO produced and what does it stimulate? Produced by the liver . Stimulates megakaryocyte maturation → platelet production. Concept What is a reticulocyte ? An immature RBC that still contains residual RNA . Normal 1-2% of circulating RBCs. Elevated = marrow is actively responding to blood loss/hemolysis. Low in anemia = marrow failure. Clinical What is a left shift ? Increased band forms and sometimes metamyelocytes/myelocytes in the peripheral blood indicating the marrow is releasing immature granulocytes in response to severe infection or stress. Clinical Why are patients with acute leukemia immunocompromised despite very high WBC counts? The blasts are immature and non-functional — they cannot fight infection. Meanwhile they crowd out normal mature neutrophils from the marrow. Like sending babies to war. Clinical What is the normal platelet lifespan? ~ 10 days . Concept 100 Minus Your Age Expected marrow cellularity = 100 − age ±10% . A 70-year-old with 30% cellularity is normal. A 20-year-old with 30% cellularity is severely hypocellular. Blasts Are Babies A WBC count of 194 000 sounds impressive but if 44% are blasts the patient is effectively neutropenic . Blasts cannot phagocytose cannot present antigens cannot kill bacteria. Only mature neutrophils fight infection. The Promyelocyte Is the APL Cell Promyelocytes have heavy azurophilic primary granules. When this cell becomes malignant it causes Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia APL / M3 — the leukemia that presents with DIC and is treated with ATRA L154 . EPO = Kidney; TPO = Liver Erythropoietin is made by the renal cortex peritubular cells in response to hypoxia. Thrombopoietin is made by the liver . CKD → ↓ EPO → normocytic anemia. Liver failure → ↓ TPO → thrombocytopenia. Extramedullary = Back to the Womb When the marrow fails fibrosis metastatic disease hematopoiesis goes back to its fetal sites — liver and spleen → hepatosplenomegaly. Classic association myelofibrosis . Look for tear-drop cells dacrocytes on the smear."}, {"id": "l151", "title": "Laboratory Medicine and Anemias Part 1", "module": "Unknown", "content": "id l151 module Unknown metadata l151 Laboratory Medicine and Anemias Part 1 lecturer N. Chernik Academic Medicine Scholar Session 1 session Lecture 151 Hematology readingTime 10-15 min resources pdf Lecture 151 - Laboratory Medicine and Anemias Part 1.pdf video https //example.com/lecture151 BandB Hematology > Anemia > Microcytic Anemias FirstAid Hematology > Pathology > Microcytic Anemia Pathoma Hematology > Anemia > Microcytic Anemias match Iron Deficiency Anemia resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 12 Red Blood Cell Disorders 02 Microcytic Anemias Iron Deficiency match Thalassemia resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 12 Red Blood Cell Disorders 02 Microcytic Anemias Thalassemia match Anemia of Chronic Disease resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 12 Red Blood Cell Disorders 02 Microcytic Anemias Chronic Disease match Lead Poisoning resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid Hematology Oncology Pathology Lead Poisoning match Sideroblastic Anemia resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 12 Red Blood Cell Disorders 02 Microcytic Anemias Sideroblastic Story Mode The Foundation of Hematology Evaluation Imagine a patient walking into your clinic. They look pale their breathing is a bit heavy just from the walk to the exam room and they complain that they just don t have the energy they used to. They ve been craving ice cubes constantly—a strange detail they mention almost as an afterthought. This is the starting point of a clinical detective story. Our job as physicians is not just to say you have anemia but to find out why . Is the bone marrow factory failing? Is there a leak in the plumbing? Or are the red blood cells being sabotaged? In this first part of our exploration we dive into the fundamental lab values that serve as our compass. We then focus our lens on the Microcytic Anemias —the conditions where the red blood cells are small usually because they are missing the raw materials or the assembly instructions to build hemoglobin properly. Slide 3 --- 1. Defining and Classifying Anemia Anemia is essentially a functional diagnosis it is a decrease in the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood. While we often measure this by looking at the Hemoglobin Hb Hematocrit Hct or Red Blood Cell RBC count the ultimate consequence is tissue hypoxia. Slide 11 The Production Cycle To understand anemia we must understand the life of a red blood cell. It begins in the bone marrow stimulated by Erythropoietin EPO produced in the kidneys. These cells live for about 120 days before being retired by the macrophages of the spleen and liver. If the factory is slow or if the cells are being retired early anemia results. Slide 15 Initial Laboratory Assessment When evaluating a CBC we use several key metrics The Rule of Three Hb ≈ RBC x 3; Hct ≈ Hb x 3. If these values don t match look for lab errors or unusual cell shapes. Slide 17 Mean Corpuscular Volume MCV This is our primary branching point. Microcytic <80 fL Small cells. Issue with Hemoglobin production. Normocytic 80–100 fL Normal size. Issue with loss or destruction. Macrocytic >100 fL Large cells. Issue with DNA synthesis. Red Cell Distribution Width RDW This measures the variance in cell size anisocytosis . A high RDW means you have a mix of new small cells and old normal cells—a classic sign of early nutrient deficiency. Slide 18 Reticulocyte Count The Marrow Monitor. A high retic count >3% corrected tells you the marrow is healthy and responding to a peripheral problem bleeding or hemolysis . A low count tells you the marrow itself is failing. Slide 19 --- 2. Microcytic Anemias The Assembly Line Failure If the MCV is low the problem is almost always in the synthesis of Hemoglobin . Hemoglobin is made of two parts Heme and Globin . Heme is further divided into Iron and Protoporphyrin . If any of these three components are missing the cell can t fill up with hemoglobin. To maintain a functional concentration of the little hemoglobin it can make the erythroblast undergoes an extra division resulting in a smaller cell. Slide 31 The TAILS Differential 1. T - Thalassemia Globin defect 2. A - Anemia of Chronic Disease Iron sequestering 3. I - Iron Deficiency Anemia Iron depletion 4. L - Lead Poisoning Heme pathway inhibition 5. S - Sideroblastic Anemia Protoporphyrin defect --- 3. Iron Deficiency Anemia IDA The Global Leader correlate IDA Pathophysiology This is the most common cause of anemia worldwide. It s a supply and demand issue iron loss bleeding or increased demand pregnancy outpaces iron intake. Slide 40 Iron Metabolism The Gatekeepers Iron absorption happens in the duodenum . Ferroportin The doorway for iron to leave the enterocyte and enter the blood. Transferrin The taxi that carries iron in the blood. Ferritin The warehouse that stores iron in the marrow and liver. Hepcidin The lock. Produced by the liver it degrades ferroportin and shuts down iron export. Slide 44 Clinical Presentation Look for Pica eating ice dirt or clay Koilonychia spoon nails and conjunctival pallor . Plummer-Vinson Syndrome A classic triad of IDA esophageal webs causing dysphagia and atrophic glossitis. Slide 52 The Four Stages of Depletion 1. Storage Depleted Ferritin drops TIBC rises. 2. Serum Iron Depleted Serum iron drops % Saturation drops. 3. Early Anemia Normocytic anemia quality over quantity . 4. Late Anemia Microcytic hypochromic anemia. Slide 56 --- 4. Anemia of Chronic Disease ACD correlate ACD Mechanism This is a hoarding problem not a deficiency. In the setting of chronic inflammation IL-6 the liver produces Hepcidin . Hepcidin locks iron inside macrophages so that invading bacteria can t use it for growth. Slide 60 Lab Findings High Ferritin The iron is there it s just locked in the warehouse. Low TIBC The liver stops making transferrin taxis because it doesn t want to move iron around during inflammation. Low Serum Iron Because it s all sequestered. Slide 61 --- 5. Sideroblastic Anemia and Lead Poisoning Sideroblastic Anemia This results from a defect in Protoporphyrin synthesis. If you can t make protoporphyrin the iron has nothing to bind to. It accumulates in the mitochondria surrounding the nucleus creating Ringed Sideroblasts visible with Prussian Blue stain on bone marrow . Slide 66 Causes X-linked ALAS defect Alcohol mitochondrial toxin or Isoniazid leads to Vitamin B6 deficiency a cofactor for ALAS . Slide 68 Lead Poisoning Lead is a multi-step inhibitor. it blocks ALAD and Ferrochelatase . MNEMONIC LEAD L - Lead lines gums and epiphyses E - Encephalopathy / E rythrocyte stippling Basophilic Stippling A - Abdominal colic / A nemia D - Drops Wrist/Foot drop Basophilic Stippling Caused by the inhibition of ribonuclease leading to the precipitation of ribosomal RNA in the RBCs. Slide 74 --- 6. The Thalassemias The Genetic Instructions Thalassemia is an inherited defect in the synthesis of the Globin chains . This provides a protective advantage against malaria. Slide 76 Alpha-Thalassemia Gene Deletions There are 4 alpha genes on chromosome 16. 1 Deletion Silent carrier. 2 Deletions Alpha-Thal minor mild microcytosis . 3 Deletions HbH Disease . Beta chains form tetramers β4 that damage RBCs. 4 Deletions Hb Bart s . Gamma chains form tetramers γ4 . Incompatible with life Hydrops Fetalis . Slide 80 Beta-Thalassemia Gene Mutations There are 2 beta genes on chromosome 11. Minor B/B+ Mild microcytosis. The key diagnostic clue is an increased HbA2 >3.5% on electrophoresis. Slide 85 Major B0/B0 Severe anemia presenting after 6 months. Expansion of Marrow Chipmunk facies and Crew-cut skull. Extramedullary Hematopoiesis Hepatosplenomegaly. Peripheral Smear Target cells and nucleated RBCs. Slide 90 questions 1 A 68-year-old male with a history of chronic knee pain managed with high-dose NSAIDs presents with fatigue. Lab results show Hb 8.2 g/dL MCV 72 fL and a Ferritin level of 8 ng/mL. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in evaluation? Oral iron supplementation Bone marrow biopsy Colonoscopy Hemoglobin electrophoresis B12 and Folate levels 2 The patient has microcytic anemia with low ferritin diagnostic of Iron Deficiency Anemia IDA . In an elderly male the most common cause is occult GI blood loss. Colonoscopy is mandatory to rule out malignancy colon cancer even if NSAID use is a suspected cause. 2 An asymptomatic 25-year-old female of Greek descent has a routine CBC showing Hb 11.5 g/dL and MCV 68 fL. Hemoglobin electrophoresis reveals HbA2 of 5.8% and HbF of 2.1%. What is the most likely diagnosis? Iron Deficiency Anemia Anemia of Chronic Disease Alpha-Thalassemia Trait Beta-Thalassemia Minor Sideroblastic Anemia 3 Beta-Thalassemia Minor is often asymptomatic or mildly anemic with significant microcytosis. The classic diagnostic finding is an elevation of HbA2 >3.5% on electrophoresis. 3 A 45-year-old patient with active Rheumatoid Arthritis presents with a hemoglobin of 10.1 g/dL and an MCV of 79 fL. Ferritin is 300 ng/mL High and TIBC is 150 µg/dL Low . Which protein is the primary mediator of this patient s hematological findings? Erythropoietin Hepcidin Transferrin Ferroportin Haptoglobin 1 The patient has Anemia of Chronic Disease ACD . In inflammatory states IL-6 stimulates the production of Hepcidin. Hepcidin inhibits iron export by degrading ferroportin leading to high ferritin and low serum iron/TIBC. 4 A patient being treated for pulmonary tuberculosis presents with new-onset fatigue and numbness. A peripheral smear shows microcytic hypochromic cells with some basophilic stippling. Bone marrow biopsy with Prussian Blue stain shows ringed sideroblasts. What is the most likely cause? Lead toxicity Direct marrow suppression by Mycobacteria Vitamin B6 deficiency Iron deficiency Alpha-globin gene deletion 2 Isoniazid used for TB causes Vitamin B6 pyridoxine deficiency. B6 is a cofactor for ALA-synthase in the heme pathway. Deficiency leads to Sideroblastic Anemia characterized by ringed sideroblasts. 5 A 5-year-old boy living in a house built in 1920 presents with abdominal pain and irritability. Smear shows basophilic stippling in the red blood cells. Which enzyme s inhibition is directly responsible for this specific smear finding? ALA Dehydratase Ferrochelatase Pyrimidine 5 -nucleotidase Uroporphyrinogen Decarboxylase G6PD 2 Lead poisoning inhibits several enzymes. While inhibition of Ferrochelatase and ALAD causes the anemia the basophilic stippling specifically results from the inhibition of Pyrimidine 5 -nucleotidase which prevents ribosomal RNA degradation. What are the components of the Rule of Three in hematology? RBC x 3 ≈ Hb; Hb x 3 ≈ Hct. Labs What is the role of Hepcidin in iron metabolism? It degrades Ferroportin trapping iron inside enterocytes and macrophages. Physiology Which microcytic anemia presents with increased HbA2 ? Beta-Thalassemia Minor. Diagnosis What is the triad of Plummer-Vinson Syndrome ? Iron Deficiency Anemia Esophageal Webs and Atrophic Glossitis. Clinical What does Basophilic Stippling represent pathologically? Precipitation of ribosomal RNA due to lead poisoning. Histology Which enzyme is the rate-limiting step in heme synthesis? ALAS Aminolevulinic Acid Synthase which requires Vitamin B6. Biochemistry What is the primary site of iron absorption in the GI tract? The Duodenum . Physiology What constitutes Hb Bart s ? Four gamma-chains γ4 ; seen in 4-gene deletion Alpha-Thalassemia. Pathology A high RDW is most characteristic of which anemia? Early nutrient deficiencies like Iron Deficiency Anemia . Labs What is the classic bone marrow finding in Sideroblastic Anemia? Ringed Sideroblasts iron-laden mitochondria . Pathology mindmap node Microcytic Anemias MCV < 80 children node Iron Deficiency children node Cause Bleeding GI/Menses node Labs Low Ferritin High TIBC node Symptoms Pica Spoon Nails node Chronic Disease children node Mediator Hepcidin via IL-6 node Labs High Ferritin Low TIBC node Thalassemia children node Alpha Gene Deletions HbH Hb Bart s node Beta Gene Mutations High HbA2 node Heme Pathway Defects children node Lead Blocks ALAD & Ferrochelatase node Sideroblastic ALAS defect B6 def Elderly + IDA = Colon Cancer Never assume iron deficiency in an older patient is just poor diet. It is a GI bleed until proven otherwise by colonoscopy. The Hepcidin Lock In ACD iron is not missing; it is locked away by hepcidin to prevent bacterial usage. This is why giving iron doesn t help ACD. HbA2 is the Key For asymptomatic Greek/Italian patients with low MCV the HbA2 level is the single most important test to diagnose Beta-Thal Minor. B6 and the Ring Sideroblastic anemia = Ringed Sideroblasts. Always check for Isoniazid use or heavy alcohol intake."}, {"id": "l152", "title": "Laboratory Medicine and Anemias Part 2", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l152 module Neuro metadata l152 Laboratory Medicine and Anemias Part 2 lecturer N. Chernik Academic Medicine Scholar Session 2 session Lecture 152 Hematology readingTime 10-15 min resources pdf Lecture 152 - Laboratory Medicine and Anemias Part 2.pdf video https //example.com/lecture152 BandB Hematology > Anemia > Megaloblastic Anemia FirstAid Hematology > Pathology > Macrocytic Anemia Pathoma Hematology > Anemia > Normocytic Anemias match B12 vs Folate resources AnKing Step 1 v11 B&B 10 Hematology 02 Anemia 03 Megaloblastic Anemia match Hereditary Spherocytosis resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 12 Red Blood Cell Disorders 04 Normocytic Anemias with Predominant Extravascular Hemolysis HS match Sickle Cell Anemia resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 12 Red Blood Cell Disorders 04 Normocytic Anemias with Predominant Extravascular Hemolysis Sickle Cell match G6PD Deficiency resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 12 Red Blood Cell Disorders 05 Normocytic Anemias with Predominant Intravascular Hemolysis G6PD match PNH resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 12 Red Blood Cell Disorders 05 Normocytic Anemias with Predominant Intravascular Hemolysis PNH Story Mode The Red Cell’s Journey Through Chaos In our last session we looked at the small cells—the ones where the factory was starved for iron or the blueprint for globin was missing. But what happens when the factory has plenty of raw materials but the blueprint DNA itself is flawed? Or what happens when the cells are made perfectly but they enter the bloodstream only to find it a killing field ? This is the story of the Macrocytic Anemias —where cells grow too large because they can t divide—and the Normocytic Anemias where the battle for survival happens in the spleen the vessels and the bone marrow factory itself. We ll follow the complex voyage of Vitamin B12 the lock and key mechanism of complement protection and the genetic quirks that turn a standard red blood cell into a rigid fragile sphere or a crescent moon. Slide 1 --- 1. Macrocytic Anemias The DNA Synthesis Dilemma When we see an MCV > 100 fL our first stop is the peripheral smear. We aren t just looking at the red cells; we re looking for the Hypersegmented Neutrophils ≥ 5 lobes . If they are there the diagnosis is Megaloblastic Anemia . The Mechanism of Gigantism Why are the cells big? Because DNA synthesis is impaired. In a normal red cell precursor the nucleus and cytoplasm mature together. But if DNA synthesis is slow the cytoplasm keeps growing while the nucleus waits for the go-ahead to divide. The result is a giant immature-looking cell. Slide 19 The Dynamic Duo Folate B9 and B12 Cobalamin Both are required to convert homocysteine to methionine . Without this conversion you run out of tetrahydrofolate THF for DNA synthesis. Folate B9 Think Foliage green leafy veggies . Absorbed in the jejunum . Stores are tiny only months . Classically seen in the tea and toast diet of elderly patients or in chronic alcoholics. Slide 22 28 Vitamin B12 Cobalamin The complex traveler. It requires Intrinsic Factor IF from the stomach s parietal cells but it doesn t bind to it until it reaches the duodenum. Then the pair travels all the way to the terminal ileum for absorption. Stores are massive years which is why vegans don t get deficiency immediately. Slide 32 45 correlate The Clinical Differentiator Both deficiencies show high homocysteine. However only B12 deficiency shows elevated Methylmalonic Acid MMA . Why? Because B12 is a cofactor for converting methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA. High MMA is neurotoxic and leads to Subacute Combined Degeneration SCD of the spinal cord dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts . If you give folate to a B12-deficient patient you ll fix their anemia but leave their spinal cord to rot. Slide 58 --- 2. Normocytic Anemias Hemolysis vs. Marrow Failure When the MCV is 80–100 fL the question changes. The cells are the right size so the assembly line is fine. The problem is either Production The Factory or Destruction The Battlefield . The Reticulocyte Marrow Monitor We check the Corrected Reticulocyte Count CRC . CRC < 3% The marrow is failing to respond. This is a production problem Aplastic anemia CKD early IDA . CRC > 3% The marrow is healthy and pumping out new cells to replace lost ones. This is a peripheral problem—either bleeding or Hemolysis . Slide 305 --- 3. The Battlefield Extravascular vs. Intravascular Hemolysis Extravascular The Spleen as the Judge In this scenario the red blood cells are weird enough for the splenic macrophages to notice. They try to nibble off the abnormal parts. Slide 436 Hereditary Spherocytosis A defect in the cytoskeleton proteins Ankyrin Spectrin or Band 3 . The cell loses membrane and becomes a sphere. Spheres are rigid and get trapped in the spleen sinusoids. High MCHC and positive Osmotic Fragility are the hallmarks. Slide 497 Sickle Cell Disease A point mutation Glutamate → Valine in the beta chain. Under stress hypoxia acidosis the HbS polymerizes into needles. The Vicious Cycle Sickling → Micro-infarctions → Splenic damage. Eventually the spleen dies Autosplenectomy leaving the patient vulnerable to encapsulated bacteria Strep pneumo H. flu and showing Howell-Jolly bodies on smear. Slide 643 Intravascular Death in the Stream The cells burst right in the blood vessels. This releases hemoglobin directly into the plasma. Slide 436 Paroxysmal Nocturnal Hemoglobinuria PNH An acquired mutation in the PIGA gene. This gene makes the anchor GPI that holds complement-protection proteins CD55/DAF and CD59/MIRL on the cell surface. Without the anchor the cells are defenseless against complement. Sleep mild acidosis triggers complement causing morning dark urine. Slide 578 G6PD Deficiency The Oxidative Shield is missing. NADPH produced by G6PD is needed to keep glutathione reduced. Without it oxidative stress fava beans sulfa drugs infections denatures hemoglobin into Heinz bodies . Macrophages bite these out leaving Bite Cells . Slide 802 --- 4. Bone Marrow Failure The Silent Factory When the CRC is low and the marrow biopsy shows nothing but fat we have Aplastic Anemia . This is pancytopenia—the whole production line has shut down. Slide 13 The Inherited Failures Diamond-Blackfan Anemia A Pure Red Cell Aplasia. The WBCs and platelets are fine but the RBCs are missing. Classically associated with triphalangeal thumbs . Fanconi Anemia An issue with DNA cross-link repair. It leads to pancytopenia and is associated with absent thumbs and short stature. Slide 15 --- Summary Table The MAD Mnemonic for Intrinsic Hemolysis | Category | Disease | Key Marker | Classic Presentation | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | M embrane | Hereditary Spherocytosis | ↑ MCHC Osmotic Fragility | Jaundice Splenomegaly | | M embrane | PNH | CD55/59 Negative | Morning dark urine Thrombosis | | A bnormal Hb | Sickle Cell HbS | Hb Electrophoresis | Dactylitis Pain crises | | D eficient Enzyme | G6PD Deficiency | Heinz bodies / Bite cells | Sulfa drugs Fava beans | questions 1 A 55-year-old chronic alcoholic presents with fatigue and a unsteady gait. MCV is 112 fL. Peripheral smear shows hypersegmented neutrophils. Homocysteine is elevated and Methylmalonic Acid MMA is also significantly elevated. What is the most likely long-term complication if only folic acid is given? Worsening anemia Irreversible spinal cord damage Acute myeloid leukemia Hemolytic crisis Hepatic failure 2 The elevated MMA and neurologic symptoms confirm B12 deficiency. Giving folic acid alone will correct the megaloblastic anemia as it bypasses the folate trap but will not address the biochemical defect causing myelin damage leading to irreversible Subacute Combined Degeneration. 2 A 22-year-old male presents with dark urine in the morning and a new blood clot in his hepatic vein Budd-Chiari syndrome . A flow cytometry test is performed. Which of the following findings would confirm the diagnosis? Presence of HbS on electrophoresis Low levels of CD55 and CD59 on RBCs Positive osmotic fragility test Heinz bodies on crystal violet stain High levels of Hepcidin 2 The patient has the classic presentation of PNH morning hemoglobinuria and venous thrombosis the leading cause of death . PNH is caused by a loss of GPI-anchored proteins specifically CD55 DAF and CD59 MIRL which protect RBCs from complement-mediated lysis. 3 A 10-year-old boy of Mediterranean descent presents with jaundice and a palpable spleen. Labs show a high MCHC and a positive osmotic fragility test. What is the most appropriate definitive treatment to resolve his anemia? Eculizumab Hydroxyurea Splenectomy Monthly B12 injections Iron chelation 3 Hereditary Spherocytosis is an extravascular hemolytic anemia where the spleen destroys the abnormal spherocytes. While the underlying membrane defect remains removing the spleen splenectomy stops the premature destruction of RBCs and resolves the anemia. 4 An 8-month-old infant with sickle cell disease presents with symmetric swelling and extreme pain in the hands and feet. What is the most likely finding on a peripheral smear in this patient? Target cells Howell-Jolly bodies Schistocytes Bite cells Ringed sideroblasts 2 The infant is experiencing dactylitis vaso-occlusive crisis . While sickle cells and target cells may be seen the hallmark of chronic splenic damage which begins early in SCD is the presence of Howell-Jolly bodies nuclear remnants due to functional asplenia. 5 A patient with short stature and absent thumbs is found to have a low count of RBCs WBCs and platelets. Marrow biopsy shows fatty replacement. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? Diamond-Blackfan Anemia Fanconi Anemia Aplastic Anemia Idiopathic PNH B12 Deficiency 2 Fanconi Anemia is a congenital aplastic anemia pancytopenia associated with physical malformations classically absent or hypoplastic thumbs and short stature. Diamond-Blackfan is a pure red cell aplasia and is usually associated with triphalangeal thumbs. What is the key differentiator between B12 and Folate deficiency labs? Methylmalonic Acid MMA ; only high in B12 deficiency. Diagnosis Where is Vitamin B12 absorbed? The Terminal Ileum requires Intrinsic Factor . Physiology What are the two spinal cord tracts damaged in B12 deficiency? Dorsal Columns proprioception/vibration and Lateral Corticospinal motor . Neurology What is the molecular defect in Hereditary Spherocytosis? Defects in Ankyrin Spectrin or Band 3 proteins. Genetics Why does PNH cause dark urine specifically in the morning? Mild Respiratory Acidosis during sleep activates complement. Pathology What is the mutation in Sickle Cell Anemia? Glutamate to Valine at position 6 of the beta-chain. Genetics Heinz bodies represent what? Precipitated denatured Hemoglobin due to oxidative stress. Histology What drug is used to treat PNH? Eculizumab anti-C5 antibody . Pharmacology How do you differentiate Diamond-Blackfan from Fanconi thumbs? DBA = Triphalangeal extra bone ; Fanconi = Absent thumbs. Clinical What is the most common cause of death in PNH? Venous Thrombosis e.g. Budd-Chiari . Clinical mindmap node Anemias Part 2 Macrocytic & Normocytic children node Macrocytic MCV > 100 children node Megaloblastic B12/Folate DNA defect node Non-megaloblastic Alcohol Liver disease node Normocytic MCV 80-100 children node Hemolysis CRC > 3% children node Extravascular Spleen HS Sickle Cell node Intravascular Vessels PNH G6PD node Underproduction CRC < 3% Marrow Aplastic CKD B12 Neuropathy Always check B12 levels before giving Folate. Folic acid will mask the anemia but allow the neuropathy to become permanent. The Spherocyte Clue If you see spherocytes and the Direct Coombs is negative it s Hereditary Spherocytosis. If Coombs is positive it s Autoimmune Hemolytic Anemia. Sickle Cell and Bacteria Sickle cell patients are functionally asplenic by age 5. They MUST be vaccinated against encapsulated organisms S. pneumo N. meningitidis H. flu . PNH and Thrombosis PNH is not just about red urine; it is a pro-thrombotic state. Any young patient with an unusual clot Budd-Chiari should be screened for PNH."}, {"id": "l153", "title": "Pharmacology of Anemias", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l153 module Neuro metadata l153 Pharmacology of Anemias lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. session Lecture 153 Pharmacology readingTime 10-15 min resources BandB Hematology > Anemias > Treatment Sketchy Pharm Hematology & Oncology > Anemias Pathoma Hematology > Anemia > Microcytic Anemia First Aid Hematology > Pharmacology > Hematologic Drugs match Iron absorption Vitamin C resources AnKing Step 1 v11 AK Step1 v11 SketchyPharm 05 Hematology & Oncology 01 Anemias 01 Iron B12 and Folate match Hepcidin mechanism resources AnKing Step 1 v11 B&B 10 Hematology 01 Basic Concepts 03 Iron Absorption match B12 vs Folate storage resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid Hematology Oncology Physiology B12 vs Folate match Chelation Therapy resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid Hematology Oncology Pathology Iron Overload match Erythropoiesis-Stimulating Agents resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid Hematology Oncology Pharmacology Erythropoiesis Stimulating Agents match Hydroxyurea mechanism resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid Hematology Oncology Pharmacology Hydroxyurea Lecture 153 Pharmacology of Anemias Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. Session Lecture 153 Story Mode The Apothecary of the Blood Imagine the human body as a grand construction site where billions of red blood cells are built every single day. For this construction to succeed the foremen erythropoietin must signal the start of work and the raw materials Iron B12 and Folate must be delivered on time and in the right form. When the supply chain fails—whether because the raw materials are missing the gatekeepers are blocking the path or the blueprints are being shredded—the result is Anemia . In this lecture Dr. Goldstein acts as the master logician explaining how we manipulate these supply lines using pharmacology. We explore why a patient’s morning coffee might be the reason their iron pills aren t working why treating a B12 deficiency with Folate is a trap that can lead to permanent paralysis and why the very drugs that save a kidney patient from transfusion might also lead to their demise if pushed too far. This is the story of how we balance the delicate chemistry of the blood. 1. The Iron Gatekeepers Absorption and Regulation Slides 10–25 Iron is a sticky and potentially toxic resource. Because the body has no active way to excrete iron the only way to maintain balance is at the point of entry the duodenum . The Forms of Iron Heme Iron Found in animal tissues. It is the VIP of iron absorbed directly and efficiently via the Heme Carrier Protein 1 HCP1 . Non-Heme Iron Fe³⁺ Found in plants. This is the stubborn form. It is insoluble at a neutral pH and must be converted to the Ferrous Fe²⁺ form by gastric acid and duodenal cytochrome B before it can enter the enterocyte via DMT1 . The Vitamin C Synergy Vitamin C ascorbic acid is a powerful reducing agent. It forces iron into the Fe²⁺ state making it much more soluble and ready for absorption. The Blockers Tannins in tea polyphenols in coffee and calcium in dairy form insoluble complexes with iron literally locking it in the gut and preventing absorption Slide 15 . correlate The Hepcidin Lock In chronic inflammatory states like RA or Lupus the liver produces Hepcidin . Hepcidin acts as the body s internal lock on the iron safe. It binds to and destroys Ferroportin the only exit channel for iron in enterocytes and macrophages. This leads to iron sequestration—the iron is in the body but it s locked away where the bone marrow can t use it. This is the primary mechanism behind Anemia of Chronic Disease . 2. Oral vs. IV Iron Choosing the Route Slides 26–45 Oral Iron Ferrous Sulfate The gold standard for initial therapy. It provides 65mg of elemental iron per 325mg tablet. Clinical Pearl Take it on an empty stomach. Food reduces absorption by up to 50% though many patients struggle with the resulting GI irritation constipation and black stools Slide 35 . IV Iron Iron Dextran and Iron Sucrose Reserved for when the gut is closed malabsorption gastric bypass IBD or when blood loss is so rapid the oral route cannot keep up. The Anaphylaxis Risk High-molecular-weight iron dextran is famous for causing fatal hypersensitivity reactions. While low-molecular-weight versions are safer a test dose is still often required. 3. The DNA Duo Vitamin B12 and Folate Slides 46–70 B12 and Folate are the essential co-factors for DNA synthesis. Without them cells can t divide properly leading to big lazy red cells known as megaloblasts . The Folate Trap This is the most dangerous clinical pitfall in hematology. B12 is required to recycle folate into its active form Tetrahydrofolate . If a patient is B12 deficient folate gets trapped in an inactive form. The Danger If you give a B12-deficient patient high doses of Folate you will force DNA synthesis to resume and the anemia will look like it s getting better on a lab report. However B12 is also required for the Methylmalonyl-CoA pathway which maintains the myelin sheath of nerves. Folate cannot fix this pathway. The Result The anemia improves but the patient s spinal cord continues to degenerate Subacute Combined Degeneration potentially leading to permanent paralysis Slide 55 . mnemonic MMA = M for Myelin and Methylmalonic Acid - Folate Deficiency ↑ Homocysteine Normal MMA No Neuro symptoms. - B12 Deficiency ↑ Homocysteine High MMA Neuro symptoms Myelin damage . 4. ESAs The Black Box of Erythropoietin Slides 71–85 For patients whose kidneys no longer produce natural erythropoietin CKD we use Epoetin alfa . Mechanism Binds directly to the EPO receptor on erythroid progenitors activating the JAK-STAT pathway to prevent apoptosis and promote maturation. The Goal is NOT Normalcy Unlike iron therapy the goal of ESAs is to reach the lowest hemoglobin possible to avoid transfusion usually ~10-11 g/dL . The Black Box If you push hemoglobin above 11-12 g/dL the blood becomes too viscous. This leads to a massive spike in thrombotic events MI Stroke DVT and can actually accelerate tumor growth in cancer patients Slide 78 . 5. Sickle Cell and Hydroxyurea Stressing the Marrow Slides 86–100 Hydroxyurea is the life-changing therapy for Sickle Cell Disease SCD . Mechanism It inhibits Ribonucleotide Reductase RNR which slows down DNA synthesis. This induces a state of stress erythropoiesis. The Result The bone marrow under stress reverts to its fetal blueprints and begins producing Fetal Hemoglobin HbF . The Benefit HbF does not sickle. By increasing the percentage of HbF in the blood we physically interfere with the polymerization of the bad HbS reducing pain crises and acute chest syndrome. Toxicity Because it inhibits DNA synthesis the dose-limiting toxicity is Bone Marrow Suppression neutropenia/thrombocytopenia . It is also a significant teratogen Slide 92 . --- Mindmap The Hematologic Apothecary - Iron - Absorption Duodenum DMT1 requires Fe²⁺ Ferrous . - Enhancers Vitamin C Gastric Acid. - Inhibitors Hepcidin ACD Calcium Tannins. - Toxicity Deferoxamine Chelator . - The DNA Co-Factors - B12 Needs Intrinsic Factor Stomach + Terminal Ileum. - Folate Absorbed in Jejunum. - The Trap Folate masks B12 anemia but neuro damage persists. - Growth Factors ESAs - Use CKD Chemo. - Risk Thrombosis/Stroke if Hb > 11. - Signalling JAK-STAT. - Sickle Cell - Hydroxyurea Inhibits RNR → ↑ HbF. - Crizalizumab P-selectin inhibitor reduces adhesion . --- Pearls for the Boards - Iron & The Empty Stomach Iron supplements are best absorbed when the stomach is acidic and empty. Patients often stop taking them due to green/black stools and constipation—warn them! - Pure Red Cell Aplasia A rare but high-yield complication of ESAs where the body makes antibodies against its own EPO. - Cyanokit Hydroxocobalamin a form of B12 is the antidote for cyanide poisoning . It turns the patient s skin and urine red but saves their life by binding cyanide to form non-toxic cyanocobalamin. - The Stress of Hydroxyurea Remember that Hydroxyurea works by being a mild poison to the marrow forcing it to switch back to fetal hemoglobin production. questions 1 A 62-year-old male with Stage 4 Chronic Kidney Disease is started on Epoetin alfa for management of anemia. Two months later his hemoglobin is found to be 12.8 g/dL. What is the most appropriate next step in management? Increase the dose to reach a normal hemoglobin of 14 g/dL. Discontinue the drug immediately and start IV iron. Reduce or hold the dose to target a hemoglobin of 10-11 g/dL. Switch to Darbepoetin alfa for better control. 3 Target hemoglobin for ESAs in CKD is 10-11 g/dL. Hemoglobin >11 g/dL is associated with a significantly increased risk of stroke and myocardial infarction Black Box Warning . 2 A 28-year-old vegan male presents with significant fatigue and paresthesias in his feet. Labs show an MCV of 115 fL and a normal serum folate. If this patient is mistakenly treated with high-dose folic acid monotherapy what is the most likely outcome? His fatigue and neurological symptoms will both resolve rapidly. His anemia will resolve but his paresthesias will likely worsen or become permanent. The folic acid will have no effect on his labs or symptoms. He will develop acute iron overload. 2 This is the Folate Trap. Folate corrects the megaloblastic anemia by bypassing the B12-dependent step in DNA synthesis but it does nothing for the B12-dependent Methylmalonyl-CoA pathway required for myelin maintenance. 3 Which of the following mechanisms best explains why patients with Rheumatoid Arthritis often develop a normocytic anemia that is resistant to oral iron supplementation? Autoantibodies against the DMT1 transporter. Chronic blood loss from the GI tract due to NSAID use. Cytokine-induced elevation of Hepcidin leading to Ferroportin degradation. Direct inhibition of Erythropoietin receptors by TNF-alpha. 3 Chronic inflammation IL-6 stimulates Hepcidin which causes the degradation of Ferroportin. Iron remains trapped inside macrophages and enterocytes leading to Anemia of Chronic Disease. 4 A 4-year-old child is brought to the ED after accidentally ingesting his mother s prenatal vitamins. He has abdominal pain and bloody vomiting. X-ray shows multiple small radio-opaque densities in the stomach. What is the specific antidote? ATRA Deferoxamine N-acetylcysteine Hydroxocobalamin 2 The child has acute iron poisoning. Deferoxamine is the iron chelator used to bind free iron and allow its excretion in the urine. 5 A patient with Sickle Cell Disease on Hydroxyurea therapy shows an increase in HbF from 2% to 15%. This change is primarily mediated by the inhibition of which enzyme? Dihydrofolate Reductase Thymidylate Synthase Ribonucleotide Reductase Ferrochelatase 3 Hydroxyurea inhibits Ribonucleotide Reductase RNR which reduces the pool of deoxyribonucleotides available for DNA synthesis inducing stress erythropoiesis and switching on gamma-globin HbF production. What is the primary site of iron absorption? The duodenum . Physiology Which hormone regulates iron absorption by degrading ferroportin? Hepcidin increased in chronic inflammation . Mechanism What is the Folate Trap ? Treating B12 deficiency with folate corrects the anemia but allows neurological damage to progress. Clinical What is the Black Box warning for ESAs? Increased risk of thrombosis stroke and MI if hemoglobin is targeted >11 g/dL. Safety How does Hydroxyurea help in Sickle Cell Disease? It inhibits Ribonucleotide Reductase inducing stress erythropoiesis which increases HbF Fetal Hemoglobin . Mechanism What is the antidote for acute iron poisoning? Deferoxamine . Toxicology Which B12-dependent metabolite is elevated in B12 deficiency but NOT folate deficiency? Methylmalonic Acid MMA . Diagnosis Why should iron be taken with Vitamin C? Vitamin C reduces iron to the Ferrous Fe²⁺ state which is more soluble and absorbed better. PK The Hepcidin Lock Think of Hepcidin as the prison guard of iron. When it s high inflammation iron is locked inside the enterocytes and macrophages and cannot reach the blood. B12 vs. Folate Storage B12 stores last for years liver whereas Folate stores only last for months . This is why a sudden dietary change leads to folate deficiency much faster than B12 deficiency. Cyanokit Magic Hydroxocobalamin is a unique form of B12 that acts as a sponge for cyanide. It s the only drug that turns your patient red while saving their life."}, {"id": "l154", "title": "Pathology: Leukemias, Lymphomas, and Plasma Cell Neoplasms", "module": "Unknown", "content": "id l154 module Unknown metadata l154 Pathology Leukemias Lymphomas and Plasma Cell Neoplasms lecturer M. Plummer M.D. session Lecture 154 Pathology readingTime 10-15 min resources Pathoma Hematology > WBC Disorders BandB Hematology > Leukemia & Lymphoma Sketchy Path Hematology > WBC Disorders match Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia t 15;17 resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 13 White Blood Cell Disorders 02 Acute Leukemia match Multiple Myeloma CRAB symptoms resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 13 White Blood Cell Disorders 07 Plasma Cell Disorders match Burkitt Lymphoma t 8;14 starry sky resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 13 White Blood Cell Disorders 06 Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma match Hairy Cell Leukemia resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 13 White Blood Cell Disorders 04 Chronic Leukemia Hairy Cell Lecture 154 Pathology Leukemias Lymphomas and Plasma Cell Neoplasms Lecturer M. Plummer M.D. Session Lecture 154 Story Mode The War of the White Cells Imagine the bone marrow as a high-security military academy training different branches of white blood cells neutrophils B-cells T-cells to defend the body. In this Story Mode we witness what happens when these branches turn rogue. When the rogue cells are babies blasts that refuse to grow up but multiply at an alarming rate we call it an Acute Leukemia . They pack the academy so tightly that the good students RBCs and platelets can t even get in leading to the triad of fatigue bleeding and infection. When the rogue cells are mature veterans who refuse to retire it s a Chronic Leukemia . They accumulate slowly often going unnoticed for years until they clog the spleen or transform into an even more aggressive force. Finally when the rogue forces set up fortified bases in the lymph nodes or tissues rather than the blood we call it Lymphoma . Dr. Plummer guides us through this battlefield identifying each enemy by their uniform CD markers and their secret codes chromosomal translocations . 1. The Blast Crisis Acute Leukemias Slides 1–15 Acute leukemias are defined by a bone marrow containing >20% blasts . These are cells that have lost the ability to differentiate. AML Acute Myeloid Leukemia The Myeloid Rebellion. Typically occurs in older adults. The Hallmark Auer Rods —sharp pink crystals of myeloperoxidase MPO found in the cytoplasm. If you see these it’s AML. APL Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia / M3 A high-yield variant driven by t 15;17 . This translocation disrupts the retinoic acid receptor. These cells are packed with granules that if released trigger DIC . Treatment We treat APL with ATRA Vitamin A which forces the babies to grow up and differentiate neutralizing the threat without killing the cells Slide 8 . ALL Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia The Childhood Coup. The most common cancer in children. The Hallmark TdT+ a marker of DNA polymerase only found in lymphoblasts . B-ALL Often expresses CD10 CALLA . Prognosis depends on translocations t 12;21 has a good prognosis children while t 9;22 —the Philadelphia chromosome—has a poor prognosis when found in ALL usually adults . T-ALL Typically presents as a thymic mass in a teenager The 3 T s Teenager Thymic mass T-cell . correlate Down Syndrome Associations Patients with Down Syndrome have a unique relationship with leukemia. - Before age 5 Higher risk of Acute Megakaryoblastic Leukemia a rare subtype of AML . - After age 5 Higher risk of ALL . 2. The Slow Accumulation Chronic Leukemias Slides 16–30 CLL Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia A proliferation of fragile mature B-cells. The Smear Smudge Cells . The cells are so delicate that the act of making the blood slide crushes them. The Paradox These B-cells are useless. Patients suffer from Hypogammaglobulinemia low antibodies and are at high risk for infections. Richter Transformation CLL can suddenly transform into an aggressive Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma DLBCL marked by a rapidly enlarging lymph node Slide 22 . CML Chronic Myelogenous Leukemia Driven by the Philadelphia Chromosome t 9;22 which creates the BCR-ABL always-on tyrosine kinase. The Findings Massively elevated WBC count with a full house of myeloid cells neutrophils metamyelocytes myelocytes and crucially Increased Basophils . Diagnosis Differentiate from a Leukemoid Reaction using the LAP score . CML is LAP-negative the rogue cells are unprofessional and lack alkaline phosphatase . 3. The Fortified Bases Lymphomas Slides 31–45 Lymphomas are malignant cells that form solid masses in lymphoid tissues. Follicular Lymphoma Driven by t 14;18 which overexpresses Bcl-2 . Bcl-2 seals the mitochondria shut preventing apoptosis. The cells don t grow fast; they just refuse to die. Burkitt Lymphoma Driven by t 8;14 which overexpresses c-myc a growth promoter. The Look Starry Sky histology. The stars are macrophages eating the rapidly dying tumor cells amidst a dark blue sky of malignant B-cells. The Presentation Jaw mass African/Endemic form or abdominal mass Sporadic form . Hodgkin Lymphoma The Reactive Rebellion. The Cell The Reed-Sternberg RS cell . A giant B-cell with Owl-eye nuclei CD15+ CD30+ . The Twist The RS cells only make up ~1% of the tumor. They secrete cytokines that summon a massive background of healthy reactive cells eosinophils lymphocytes that form the bulk of the mass. 4. The Plasma Factories Multiple Myeloma Slides 46–55 Multiple Myeloma is a cancer of Plasma Cells the antibody factories . The M-Spike The rogue plasma cells pump out a single type of antibody creating a sharp spike on electrophoresis. The Damage CRAB C alcium Bone destruction leads to hypercalcemia. R enal Myeloma Kidney due to Bence-Jones proteins light chains clogging the tubules. A nemia Marrow crowding. B one Punched-out lytic lesions on X-ray especially the skull and spine Slide 50 . mnemonic HL vs. NHL - Hodgkin HL Orderly contiguous spread node to node . Reed-Sternberg cells. B symptoms common. - Non-Hodgkin NHL Non-contiguous wild spread. Extranodal involvement stomach brain . questions 1 A 4-year-old boy presents with fever bone pain and bruising. His WBC is 45 000/µL. A bone marrow biopsy reveals 40% blasts that are positive for TdT and CD10. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? Acute Myeloid Leukemia B-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia Chronic Myelogenous Leukemia Burkitt Lymphoma 2 TdT is the hallmark of lymphoblasts and CD10 CALLA confirms B-cell lineage. This is the classic childhood ALL. 2 A 65-year-old male is found to have a WBC of 150 000/µL on routine labs. The differential shows an increase in neutrophils myelocytes and metamyelocytes. Basophils are 8%. A Leukocyte Alkaline Phosphatase LAP score is performed and is very low. What is the driver of this disease? Overexpression of Bcl-2 t 15;17 translocation BCR-ABL fusion protein c-myc overexpression 3 The high WBC with basophilia and low LAP score is diagnostic of CML which is driven by the Philadelphia chromosome t 9;22 and the BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase. 3 A biopsy of a neck mass in a 20-year-old male shows large multi-nucleated cells with prominent owl-eye nucleoli that are CD15 and CD30 positive. What is the most likely characteristic of the clinical spread of this disease? Non-contiguous spread to extranodal sites like the stomach. Contiguous spread from one lymph node group to the next. Early hematogenous spread to the liver and lungs. Exclusive involvement of the bone marrow. 2 The cells are Reed-Sternberg cells diagnostic of Hodgkin Lymphoma. HL typically spreads in a predictable contiguous fashion. 4 A 72-year-old male presents with back pain and fatigue. Labs show a hemoglobin of 9.5 g/dL and a serum calcium of 12.2 mg/dL. An X-ray of the spine shows multiple lytic lesions. What would you expect to see on a peripheral blood smear? Schistocytes Target cells Rouleaux formation Bite cells 3 The patient has the CRAB symptoms of Multiple Myeloma. High levels of monoclonal protein M-spike reduce the repulsive charge between RBCs causing them to stack like coins Rouleaux . 5 A 30-year-old patient with a history of H. pylori infection presents with a low-grade gastric lymphoma. Which of the following is the most likely translocation associated with a related indolent B-cell lymphoma that overexpresses Bcl-2? t 8;14 t 11;14 t 14;18 t 9;22 3 While the patient has a MALToma associated with H. pylori the question asks for the translocation associated with the BCL-2 overexpressing indolent lymphoma which is Follicular Lymphoma t 14;18 . What is the marker for all lymphoblasts ALL ? TdT+ Terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase . Diagnosis What chromosomal translocation is pathognomonic for CML? t 9;22 Philadelphia chromosome creating BCR-ABL. Genetics What are Smudge Cells associated with? CLL Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia . Histology What translocation is found in Follicular Lymphoma? t 14;18 Bcl-2 overexpression . Genetics What is the hallmark cell of Hodgkin Lymphoma? Reed-Sternberg cell CD15+ CD30+ . Histology What does the CRAB acronym stand for in Multiple Myeloma? C alcium high R enal failure A nemia B one lesions. Clinical Which leukemia presents with DIC and is treated with ATRA? Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia APL / M3 . Clinical What is the starry sky appearance in Burkitt Lymphoma? Malignant B-cells sky with tingible body macrophages stars . Histology Bcl-2 = The Anti-Death Shield In Follicular Lymphoma the cells don t divide rapidly. They simply live forever because Bcl-2 prevents the mitochondria from releasing Cytochrome C and triggering apoptosis. CML vs. Leukemoid Both have high WBCs. CML has Low LAP and High Basophils . A leukemoid reaction infection has High LAP and no basophilia. Auer Rods = Myeloid If you see a single Auer rod you can stop looking; it is AML. These are crystallized enzymes that prove the cell is of the myeloid lineage."}, {"id": "l155", "title": "Pathology: Hemorrhage and Thrombosis", "module": "Unknown", "content": "id l155 module Unknown metadata l155 Pathology Hemorrhage and Thrombosis lecturer M. Plummer M.D. session Lecture 155 Pathology readingTime 10-15 min resources Pathoma Hematology > Hemostasis BandB Hematology > Basic Concepts > Coagulation Cascade Sketchy Path Hematology > Coagulation Disorders match TTP Pentad & ADAMTS13 resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 12 Red Blood Cell Disorders 10 Microangiopathic Hemolytic Anemia match Factor V Leiden mutation resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 12 Red Blood Cell Disorders 13 Thrombosis match Hemophilia A Factor VIII resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 12 Red Blood Cell Disorders 12 Secondary Hemostasis Disorders match Vitamin K Deficiency resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid Hematology Oncology Pathology Vitamin K Deficiency Lecture 155 Pathology Hemorrhage and Thrombosis Lecturer M. Plummer M.D. Session Lecture 155 Story Mode The Battle of the Dam Think of a blood vessel as a high-pressure water pipe. When the pipe bursts the body has a two-stage emergency response to prevent a catastrophic flood hemorrhage . In Primary Hemostasis the First Responders —the platelets—rush to the site. They act like sandbags piling on top of each other to plug the hole. This plug is quick but weak. If the sandbags are missing thrombocytopenia or the glue vWF is gone you get small leaks—petechiae and nosebleeds. In Secondary Hemostasis the Construction Crew arrives. They pour liquid concrete fibrinogen and turn it into solid rebar fibrin using a complex machinery called the Coagulation Cascade. This creates a permanent rock-solid seal. If a member of this crew is missing Hemophilia the sandbags will eventually wash away and the pipe will burst again leading to deep floods in the joints and muscles. But there is a dark side. Sometimes the response is too aggressive and the dam grows until it blocks the pipe entirely Thrombosis . Or worse the body uses up all its sandbags and concrete everywhere at once leaving no supplies to fix a real leak elsewhere—this is the paradox of DIC . 1. The Sandbags Primary Hemostasis Slides 1–18 Platelets and Von Willebrand Factor vWF are the stars here. Adhesion Endothelial injury exposes collagen. Platelets bind to it via the vWF glue and the GpIb receptor. Aggregation Platelets stack on each other using Fibrinogen as a bridge and the GpIIb/IIIa receptor. Defects Platelet Problems ITP Immune Thrombocytopenic Purpura The body mistakenly tags its own sandbags platelets for destruction. Anti-GpIIb/IIIa antibodies cause the spleen to eat platelets. Bernard-Soulier Syndrome A defect in GpIb . The sandbags have no glue; they can t stick to the hole. Big Platelets are seen on the smear. Glanzmann Thrombasthenia A defect in GpIIb/IIIa . The sandbags can t stick to each other . correlate TTP and the FAT RN In TTP Thrombotic Thrombocytopenic Purpura the body lacks the scissors ADAMTS13 that cut large vWF strands into manageable pieces. These long strands catch platelets everywhere forming thousands of tiny clots that shred red blood cells Schistocytes . - F ever - A nemia Hemolytic - T hrombocytopenia - R enal failure - N euro symptoms The Rule Never give platelets to a TTP patient—it’s like throwing gasoline on a fire. 2. The Concrete Secondary Hemostasis Slides 19–35 This is the Coagulation Cascade . The Intrinsic Pathway aPTT Triggered by contact with subendothelial collagen. Factors XII XI IX VIII. The Extrinsic Pathway PT Triggered by Tissue Factor . Factor VII. Defects Factor Problems Hemophilia A VIII and B IX X-linked recessive. Patients present with deep tissue bleeding into joints hemarthrosis . vWD Von Willebrand Disease The most common inherited bleeding disorder. It’s a double hit —vWF is needed for platelet adhesion AND it carries Factor VIII. Patients have mucosal bleeding AND a prolonged aPTT. Vitamin K Deficiency You need Vitamin K for factors II VII IX X C and S . Without it your concrete won t set. PT rises first because Factor VII has the shortest half-life. 3. Total System Failure DIC Slides 36–45 DIC Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation is the ultimate hematologic nightmare. Mechanism Something Sepsis Cancer Trauma causes a massive release of Tissue Factor throughout the body. The Paradox Clots form everywhere causing organ failure. But because the body uses up ALL its platelets and factors to make these useless clots the patient also bleeds from every site IV lines gums etc. . The Labs Everything is broken. High PT High aPTT Low Platelets High D-dimer Slide 40 . 4. The Blockage Thrombosis Slides 46–55 Thrombosis is an inappropriate clot that blocks flow. Dr. Plummer emphasizes Virchow’s Triad 1 Endothelial Injury 2 Stasis slow flow and 3 Hypercoagulability. Lines of Zahn These are microscopic rings of a tree in a clot—alternating layers of platelets/fibrin pale and RBCs dark . They prove the clot formed while the heart was still beating Slide 48 . Hereditary Hypercoagulability Factor V Leiden The most common. A mutated Factor V that refuses to be turned off by Protein C. Protein C or S Deficiency Loss of natural anticoagulants. If these patients are given Warfarin they can suffer from Warfarin Skin Necrosis because Protein C the brake disappears before the pro-coagulant factors do. Antithrombin III Deficiency Heparin won t work in these patients because Heparin requires Antithrombin to function Slide 52 . questions 1 A 3-year-old boy presents with a swollen painful knee after a minor fall. He has a history of easy bruising. Labs show a normal PT a normal bleeding time but a significantly prolonged aPTT. What is the most likely diagnosis? Von Willebrand Disease Hemophilia A Immune Thrombocytopenic Purpura Vitamin K Deficiency 2 Deep tissue bleeding hemarthrosis and an isolated prolonged aPTT in a young male is classic for Hemophilia A or B. vWD would likely have a prolonged bleeding time as well. 2 A 34-year-old female presents with confusion and fever. Labs show a platelet count of 12 000/µL a hemoglobin of 7.2 g/dL and a normal PT and aPTT. A peripheral smear shows numerous schistocytes. Which of the following is the most likely underlying mechanism? Autoantibodies against GpIIb/IIIa Deficiency of ADAMTS13 Mutation in the Factor V gene Consumption of clotting factors due to sepsis 2 The patient has the TTP pentad. Normal PT/aPTT with low platelets and schistocytes MAHA points to TTP ADAMTS13 deficiency rather than DIC. 3 A patient with a history of recurrent DVTs is started on Heparin for an acute pulmonary embolism. Despite a high-dose infusion his aPTT remains within the normal range. Which of the following is the most likely cause? Factor V Leiden Antithrombin III Deficiency Protein C Deficiency Lupus Anticoagulant 2 Heparin works by potentiating the action of Antithrombin III. If a patient is deficient in ATIII heparin has no substrate to work on leading to heparin resistance. 4 A microscopic examination of a thrombus removed from a patient s femoral artery reveals alternating pale layers of platelets and fibrin and dark layers of red blood cells. What is the clinical significance of this finding? It indicates the patient has a Protein C deficiency. It proves the thrombus formed before death pre-mortem . It suggests the thrombus is purely composed of red cells stasis clot . It indicates the thrombus has been present for at least 3 weeks. 2 These are the Lines of Zahn which only form when blood is flowing pre-mortem . 5 A patient in the ICU with Gram-negative sepsis suddenly begins bleeding from his surgical wound and his IV sites. Labs show a PT of 28s aPTT of 55s and a fibrinogen level of 80 mg/dL low . Which of the following lab findings would most specifically support a diagnosis of DIC in this patient? Low platelet count Presence of schistocytes Markedly elevated D-dimer Low hemoglobin 3 While all of these can be seen in DIC the elevated D-dimer is the most specific marker for the massive fibrin formation and breakdown characteristic of DIC. What is the receptor for platelet adhesion to vWF? GpIb . Physiology What is the receptor for platelet aggregation bridged by fibrinogen ? GpIIb/IIIa . Physiology What enzyme is deficient in TTP? ADAMTS13 vWF metalloprotease . Diagnosis What are the classic lab findings in DIC? ↑ PT ↑ aPTT ↓ Platelets ↓ Fibrinogen ↑ D-dimer . Labs What is the most common inherited hypercoagulable state? Factor V Leiden . Genetics Which coagulation factor has the shortest half-life? Factor VII why PT rises first in Vit K def . Physiology What do the Lines of Zahn indicate? A pre-mortem thrombus formed in flowing blood . Pathology What is Virchow s Triad? 1. Endothelial injury 2. Stasis/Turbulence 3. Hypercoagulability. Pathology The Gasoline Warning Never give platelets to a patient with TTP. It provides more substrate for the already runaway microthrombi to form potentially worsening the CNS symptoms and renal failure. D-dimer = The Fingerprint of a Clot D-dimer is only produced when cross-linked fibrin is broken down. It is the absolute proof that a clot was recently formed and is being degraded. Heparin Resistance If you are giving a patient massive doses of Heparin and the aPTT isn t moving check their Antithrombin III levels. Without ATIII Heparin is useless."}, {"id": "l156", "title": "Pharmacology of Antineoplastic Agents 1", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l156 module Neuro metadata title Pharmacology of Antineoplastic Agents 1 lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. Session 156 readingTime 10-15 min difficulty Advanced tags Oncology Pharmacology Alkylating Agents Antimetabolites Cytotoxic Chemotherapy resources ankingResource primarySource Sketchy Pharm chapter Hematology & Oncology > Antineoplastic Agents alternatives resource Boards and Beyond chapter Hematology > Pharmacology > Chemotherapy Principles resource First Aid chapter Hematology/Oncology > Pharmacology anking match Tumor Lysis Syndrome resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid Hematology Oncology Oncology Tumor Lysis Syndrome match Antimetabolites resources AnKing Step 1 v11 SketchyPharm 05 Hematology & Oncology 02 Antineoplastics 01 Antimetabolites match Alkylating Agents resources AnKing Step 1 v11 SketchyPharm 05 Hematology & Oncology 02 Antineoplastics 02 Alkylating Agents match Anthracyclines resources AnKing Step 1 v11 SketchyPharm 05 Hematology & Oncology 02 Antineoplastics 03 Antitumor Antibiotics summary Story Mode The Poisoned Blueprint Welcome to the world of cytotoxic chemotherapy where the goal is as simple as it is brutal kill the cancer before it kills the patient. In this first part of our journey through antineoplastic agents we explore the classic hammers—the drugs that don t care about specific mutations but instead target the fundamental mechanics of cell division. These are the poisons that attack the blueprint DNA the tools enzymes and the scaffolding microtubules of life itself. Phase I The Rules of the Game Before we look at the drugs we must understand the battlefield. Cancer is not a single entity; it is a collection of cells that have forgotten how to die. The Cell Cycle & Growth Fraction Cytotoxic drugs typically target rapidly dividing cells. This is why tumors with a high Growth Fraction like lymphomas and leukemias respond better than slow-growing solid tumors like colon or lung cancer . Most of our drugs are either Cell Cycle Specific CCS acting only during a certain phase like the S-phase or M-phase or Cell Cycle Non-Specific CCNS like the alkylating agents that can strike a cell even when it is resting in G0. The Problem of Resistance Tumors are clever. They develop defenses almost immediately 1. Efflux Pumps P-glycoprotein MDR1 acts like a molecular bouncer throwing the drug back out of the cell as soon as it enters. 2. Detoxification Cells increase levels of Glutathione which acts as a chemical sponge to neutralize drugs. 3. Target Modification Enzymes like Topoisomerase mutate so the drug can no longer bind. --- Phase II The Alkylating Agents – The Molecular Napalm Alkylating agents are the old guard of chemotherapy. Their mechanism is simple they add an alkyl group to DNA specifically at the N7 position of Guanine . This causes the DNA strands to cross-link preventing them from being read or replicated. Cyclophosphamide The Trojan Horse Cyclophosphamide is a pro-drug . It is inactive until the liver s CYP450 system activates it. However this activation has a dark side. It produces two main metabolites Phosphoramide Mustard The killer that alkylates the DNA. Acrolein The poison that accumulates in the bladder. The Clinical Pearl Acrolein causes Hemorrhagic Cystitis bloody bladder . To prevent this we give MESNA a drug that stays in the bladder and neutralizes Acrolein before it can burn the lining. --- Phase III The Platinum Metals – The Heavy Hitters Drugs like Cisplatin Carboplatin and Oxaliplatin aren t technically alkylators but they act like them. They enter the cell via copper transporters and undergo aquation swapping chloride for water to become highly reactive. The Platinum Trinity 1. Cisplatin The most potent but most toxic. It causes nephrotoxicity kidney damage and extreme emetogenicity nearly 100% of patients will vomit without aggressive anti-emetics . It also causes Ototoxicity hearing loss . 2. Carboplatin Less reactive than cisplatin making it easier on the kidneys and stomach but it is a marrow killer dose-limiting bone marrow suppression . 3. Oxaliplatin The backbone of colorectal cancer treatment FOLFOX . Its unique quirk is peripheral neuropathy which is triggered or worsened by COLD . A patient might feel like they are swallowing glass if they drink ice water. --- Phase IV The Antimetabolites – The Molecular Imposters If alkylating agents are napalm antimetabolites are saboteurs. They look like the natural building blocks of DNA purines and pyrimidines but contain a glitch. When the cell tries to use them the machinery jams. Methotrexate MTX The Folate Saboteur Methotrexate looks exactly like Folic Acid. It inhibits Dihydrofolate Reductase DHFR stopping the production of active folate. No active folate = no DNA synthesis = cell death. The Trap Once inside the cell MTX is polyglutamated. This adds a charge to the molecule trapping it inside the cell to do more damage. The Rescue We give Leucovorin Folinic Acid after high-dose MTX. Leucovorin provides a bypass of the blocked DHFR enzyme rescuing normal cells from death. 5-Fluorouracil 5-FU Thymine-less Death 5-FU is a pyrimidine analog that inhibits Thymidylate Synthase . It creates Thymine-less death —the cell literally runs out of one of the four letters of the genetic code. The Toxicity Watch out for Hand-Foot Syndrome palmar-plantar erythrodysesthesia where the skin on the palms and soles becomes red peels and blisters. The Interaction Unlike with MTX Leucovorin actually increases the toxicity of 5-FU by helping it bind tighter to its target. Cytarabine Ara-C The S-Phase Specialized Ara-C is an arabinose sugar analog. It is the go-to for Acute Myeloid Leukemia AML . It blocks DNA polymerase and inserts itself into DNA causing chain termination. 6-Mercaptopurine 6-MP The Allopurinol Alert 6-MP is a purine analog. It is metabolized by Xanthine Oxidase . Danger If a patient is taking Allopurinol which blocks Xanthine Oxidase 6-MP levels will skyrocket to toxic levels. You must reduce the 6-MP dose by 75% if giving Allopurinol. --- Phase V The Natural Products – Scaffolding & Scissor Sabotage Microtubule Inhibitors The Cell Scaffolding Saboteurs Vincristine Derived from the periwinkle plant. It blocks microtubule assembly . It is famous for causing peripheral neuropathy and severe constipation autonomic neuropathy . CRITICAL WARNING Never give Vincristine intrathecally. It is 100% fatal if it enters the spinal fluid. Taxanes Paclitaxel Derived from the Yew tree. These block microtubule disassembly . They freeze the cell in mitosis. Topoisomerase Inhibitors The Scissor Saboteurs DNA is like a twisted phone cord. Topoisomerases are the scissors that cut the cord to relieve tension so it can be unzipped. Irinotecan Topo I Inhibitor Used for colon cancer. I run to the can —it causes severe diarrhea . Etoposide Topo II Inhibitor Blocks the resealing of double-strand DNA breaks. Doxorubicin The Red Devil An anti-tumor antibiotic that is bright red. It turns the patient s urine red harmless but scary . The Danger It creates free radicals that specifically attack the heart causing irreversible Dilated Cardiomyopathy . It also causes Radiation Recall —if a patient had radiation months ago the drug can cause a sunburn in the exact spot of the old radiation. --- The Big Picture Table Toxicities to Remember | Drug | Major Signature Toxicity | | --- | --- | | Cyclophosphamide | Hemorrhagic Cystitis Use MESNA | | Cisplatin | Nephrotoxicity & Ototoxicity | | Oxaliplatin | Cold-induced Neuropathy | | Doxorubicin | Cardiotoxicity Red Devil | | Vincristine | Neuropathy & Constipation Never Intrathecal! | | Irinotecan | Diarrhea I run to the can | | 5-FU / 5-Fluorouracil | Hand-Foot Syndrome | | Bleomycin | Pulmonary Fibrosis | In the next session we leave the hammers behind and look at the scalpels —targeted therapies and immunotherapy. questions question Which metabolite of cyclophosphamide is responsible for hemorrhagic cystitis and what agent is used to prevent it? options text Phosphoramide mustard; Leucovorin text Acrolein; MESNA isCorrect true text Glutathione; Allopurinol text Uric acid; Rasburicase explanation Acrolein is the toxic metabolite of cyclophosphamide that irritates the bladder lining. MESNA 2-mercaptoethane sulfonate is a thiol compound that concentrates in the bladder and neutralizes acrolein. question A patient being treated for colorectal cancer with FOLFOX which includes Oxaliplatin reports that their hands feel like they are being pricked by needles whenever they take something out of the freezer. What is the mechanism of this drug s primary action? options text Inhibition of Dihydrofolate Reductase text Inhibition of Microtubule polymerization text DNA cross-linking via aquated intermediates isCorrect true text Topoisomerase II inhibition explanation Oxaliplatin is a platinum compound that causes DNA cross-linking. Its signature side effect is a cold-induced peripheral neuropathy. question Why must the dose of 6-Mercaptopurine be drastically reduced if the patient is also taking Allopurinol? options text Allopurinol increases the renal excretion of 6-MP text Allopurinol inhibits Xanthine Oxidase the primary enzyme for 6-MP inactivation isCorrect true text Allopurinol displaces 6-MP from plasma proteins text Allopurinol induces CYP3A4 which over-activates 6-MP explanation 6-Mercaptopurine 6-MP is inactivated by the enzyme Xanthine Oxidase. Allopurinol is a Xanthine Oxidase inhibitor used to treat hyperuricemia. Combining them leads to dangerously high levels of 6-MP. question Which of the following drugs is a cell-cycle specific agent that acts by blocking the assembly of microtubules? options text Paclitaxel text Vincristine isCorrect true text Etoposide text Cyclophosphamide explanation Vincristine Vinca alkaloid blocks microtubule polymerization assembly . Paclitaxel Taxane blocks disassembly stabilizes them . Etoposide and Cyclophosphamide are CCNS or act in other phases. question What is the purpose of Leucovorin Folinic Acid when administered 24 hours after high-dose Methotrexate? options text To increase the binding of MTX to Dihydrofolate Reductase text To rescue normal cells by providing a source of reduced folate isCorrect true text To prevent hemorrhagic cystitis text To alkalinize the urine and prevent crystal nephropathy explanation Leucovorin is folinic acid a reduced form of folate that does not require DHFR for activation. It rescues normal cells from MTX toxicity by bypassing the blocked enzyme. flashcards front What is the mechanism of Methotrexate ? back Inhibition of Dihydrofolate Reductase DHFR . tag Pharmacology front What prevents Cyclophosphamide induced hemorrhagic cystitis? back Mesna + Hydration. tag Pharmacology front Which drug is used for Leucovorin rescue ? back Folinic Acid Leucovorin . tag Pharmacology front What is the mechanism of Taxanes Paclitaxel ? back Hyper-stabilize microtubules inhibit disassembly . tag Pharmacology front Which drug class cross the BBB for brain tumors? back Nitrosoureas Carmustine Lomustine . tag Pharmacology front What is the major dose-limiting toxicity of Bleomycin ? back Pulmonary Fibrosis. tag Toxicity pearls title The V Rule content Vincristine = Nerves Peripheral neuropathy . Vinblastine = Bone marrow Blasts the marrow . title 5-FU vs. MTX content Both are S-phase. MTX inhibits the recycling of folate DHFR . 5-FU inhibits the synthesis of thymidine Thymidylate Synthase . title Acrolein is the Bladder Villain content If a patient has red urine after Cyclophosphamide the acrolein is eating their bladder wall. Give them Mesna. title Amifostine for the Kidney content Cisplatin is Cis-platin sounds like Piss-platin because it kills the kidneys . Use Amifostine to protect them."}, {"id": "l157", "title": "Pharmacology of Antineoplastic Agents 2", "module": "Pharmacology", "content": "id l157 module Pharmacology metadata title Pharmacology of Antineoplastic Agents 2 lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. Session 157 readingTime 10-15 min difficulty Advanced tags Oncology Pharmacology Targeted Therapy Immunotherapy Monoclonal Antibodies resources ankingResource primarySource Sketchy Pharm chapter Hematology & Oncology > Targeted Therapy alternatives resource Boards and Beyond chapter Hematology > Pharmacology > Targeted Therapies resource First Aid chapter Hematology/Oncology > Pharmacology anking match Imatinib BCR-ABL resources AnKing Step 1 v11 SketchyPharm 05 Hematology & Oncology 02 Antineoplastics 05 Targeted Therapies match Trastuzumab HER2 resources AnKing Step 1 v11 SketchyPharm 05 Hematology & Oncology 02 Antineoplastics 05 Targeted Therapies match Bevacizumab VEGF resources AnKing Step 1 v11 SketchyPharm 05 Hematology & Oncology 02 Antineoplastics 05 Targeted Therapies match Checkpoint Inhibitors resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid Hematology Oncology Pharmacology Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors summary Story Mode The Precision Strike In the previous session we used hammers to smash cancer cells. Today we look at the scalpels —targeted therapies and immunotherapy. These drugs don t just attack any cell that divides; they are designed to hit specific molecular targets the oncogenic drivers that make a cancer cell unique. Phase I The Tyrosine Kinase Inhibitors The -tinibs Tyrosine kinases are the on-off switches for cell growth. In cancer these switches are often jammed in the ON position. Small molecule inhibitors ending in -tinib are small enough to enter the cell and block the ATP-binding site of these kinases cutting the power to the growth engine. Imatinib The First Magic Bullet Imatinib was the drug that changed everything. It targets the BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase created by the Philadelphia Chromosome t 9;22 in Chronic Myeloid Leukemia CML . The Glitch Over time the kinase can mutate. The most famous is the T315I mutation which makes Imatinib useless. In this case we have to use a newer-generation drug like Ponatinib . The Toxicity Watch for Periorbital Edema puffy eyes and skin desquamation. Other TKIs to Know Erlotinib / Gefitinib Target the EGFR Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor . Used in Lung Cancer. Signature side effect Acneiform Rash . Lapatinib Targets both EGFR and HER2 . Used in breast cancer. --- Phase II The Monoclonal Antibodies The -mabs While TKIs work inside the cell MABs work outside . They are massive proteins that bind to receptors or circulating factors blocking them from signaling. Trastuzumab Herceptin The HER2 Specialist Trastuzumab targets the HER2/neu receptor in breast cancer. The ADC Trastuzumab Emtansine T-DM1 is an Antibody-Drug Conjugate. It acts like a guided missile carrying a cytotoxic payload DM1 specifically to HER2-expressing cells. The Danger Trastuzumab is associated with Cardiotoxicity decreased LVEF . Crucial Difference Unlike the Red Devil Doxorubicin Trastuzumab s heart damage is usually reversible and not dose-dependent. Bevacizumab The Angiogenesis Saboteur Tumors need blood to grow. They release VEGF to trigger new blood vessel growth. Bevacizumab binds to VEGF mopping it up before it can reach the vessel walls. The Risks Because it interferes with blood vessels it causes Hypertension Hemorrhage and Impaired Wound Healing . You must stop this drug weeks before any surgery or the surgical wound may literally pull apart Dehiscence . Cetuximab The KRAS Gatekeeper Cetuximab targets the EGFR on colon cancer cells. The KRAS Rule This drug only works if the downstream signaling molecule KRAS is NOT mutated wild-type . If KRAS is mutated the cell keeps growing even if you block the receptor above it. --- Phase III The Multiple Myeloma Arsenal Multiple Myeloma plasma cell cancer has its own unique set of scalpels. 1. The Proteasome Inhibitors Bortezomib Think of the proteasome as the cell s trash compactor. Bortezomib jams the compactor. Misfolded proteins build up clogging the cell until it triggers apoptosis. Clinical Pearl It blocks NF-kappa-B signaling. The Risk It can reactivate Shingles VZV . Patients need antiviral prophylaxis. 2. The IMiDs Lenalidomide Lenalidomide binds to a protein called Cereblon leading to the destruction of transcription factors that the cancer cell needs to survive. WARNING These are descendants of Thalidomide . They are extremely teratogenic causing Phocomelia stunted limbs . --- Phase IV Releasing the Brakes – Immunotherapy Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors Cancer cells often hide from the immune system by engaging brakes on T-cells. Drugs like Pembrolizumab PD-1 inhibitor and Ipilimumab CTLA-4 inhibitor cut these brakes allowing the patient s own T-cells to attack the cancer. The Cost By activating the immune system you can trigger autoimmune attacks on healthy organs. These are called irAEs Immune-Related Adverse Events Colitis diarrhea Pneumonitis shortness of breath Hypophysitis pituitary inflammation CAR T-Cell Therapy The Designer Soldiers In CAR T-cell therapy we harvest a patient s T-cells genetically engineer them to recognize a specific tumor antigen like CD19 on B-cells and put them back in. The Storm When these designer soldiers start killing the tumor they release a massive wave of cytokines. This causes Cytokine Release Syndrome CRS —a dangerous high-fever multi-organ failure state. --- The Big Picture Table Targeted Therapy Pearls | Target | Drug | Key Feature | | --- | --- | --- | | BCR-ABL | Imatinib | CML Magic Bullet ; T315I = Resistance | | HER2 | Trastuzumab | Breast cancer; Reversible Cardiotoxicity | | VEGF | Bevacizumab | Blocks angiogenesis; Wound dehiscence | | CD20 | Rituximab | B-cell lymphoma; Infusion reactions | | PD-1 | Pembrolizumab | Releases the brakes ; Autoimmune side effects | | Proteasome | Bortezomib | Multiple Myeloma; Shingles risk | | Cereblon | Lenalidomide | Multiple Myeloma; Severe Teratogen | Targeted therapy is a balancing act more precision but often entirely new and strange toxicities. questions question Which of the following mutations confers resistance to Imatinib in patients with Chronic Myeloid Leukemia? options text KRAS mutation text BRAF V600E text T315I mutation isCorrect true text HER2 overexpression explanation The T315I mutation in the BCR-ABL kinase domain changes a threonine to an isoleucine physically blocking the binding of imatinib. question A patient on Pembrolizumab a PD-1 inhibitor for metastatic melanoma develops severe diarrhea and abdominal pain. What is the most likely cause of these symptoms? options text Cytokine Release Syndrome text Immune-Related Colitis isCorrect true text Hemorrhagic Cystitis text Direct cytotoxic damage to the gut lining explanation Checkpoint inhibitors release the brakes on the immune system which can lead to autoimmune-like attacks on healthy tissues such as the colon colitis . question Why must Bevacizumab be discontinued several weeks before a patient undergoes elective surgery? options text It increases the risk of anesthetic complications text It inhibits angiogenesis leading to impaired wound healing isCorrect true text It causes severe bone marrow suppression text It induces CYP3A4 which metabolizes surgical drugs too quickly explanation By blocking VEGF Bevacizumab prevents the formation of new blood vessels necessary for wound repair leading to a high risk of wound dehiscence. question Which agent used in the treatment of Multiple Myeloma is a proteasome inhibitor that requires antiviral prophylaxis against shingles? options text Lenalidomide text Daratumumab text Bortezomib isCorrect true text Dexamethasone explanation Bortezomib is a proteasome inhibitor associated with a significantly increased risk of Herpes Zoster shingles reactivation. question A patient with HER2+ breast cancer is receiving Trastuzumab. How does its cardiotoxicity differ from that of Doxorubicin? options text It is dose-dependent and irreversible text It is usually reversible and not cumulative isCorrect true text It only occurs when combined with taxanes text Trastuzumab does not cause cardiotoxicity explanation Trastuzumab-induced cardiotoxicity Type II involves impaired myocyte signaling rather than cell death making it typically reversible upon stopping the drug unlike Doxorubicin Type I . flashcards front What is the target of Rituximab ? back CD20 on B-cells. tag Pharmacology front What is the specific target of Imatinib ? back BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase and c-kit . tag Pharmacology front What is the mechanism of Bevacizumab ? back Monoclonal antibody against VEGF ; inhibits angiogenesis. tag Pharmacology front What is the major toxicity of Checkpoint Inhibitors ? back Immune-Related Adverse Events irAEs ; treated with corticosteroids. tag Toxicity front Which drug binds Cereblon and is a potent teratogen? back Lenalidomide or Thalidomide . tag Pharmacology pearls title Mab vs Nib content -mab = Monoclonal Antibody extracellular . -nib = Small molecule inhibitor intracellular kinase . title The KRAS rule content EGFR inhibitors Cetuximab only work in colorectal cancer if KRAS is wild-type . If KRAS is mutated the engine is running even without the key. title VEGF and Wounds content Bevacizumab stops new vessels. If you cut someone open they won t make new vessels to heal the wound. Stop it weeks before surgery. title Trastuzumab Heart Safety content Monitor LVEF. If the ejection fraction drops stop the drug. Usually the heart recovers."}, {"id": "l158", "title": "Pathology: Hemostasis", "module": "Unknown", "content": "id l158 module Unknown metadata title Pathology Hemostasis lecturer E. Petcu M.D. & K. Bressler Session 158 readingTime 10-15 min difficulty Advanced tags Pathology Hematology Hemostasis Coagulation Cascade Fibrinolysis resources ankingResource primarySource Pathoma chapter Hematology > Hemostasis alternatives resource Boards and Beyond chapter Hematology > Basic Concepts > Coagulation Cascade resource First Aid chapter Hematology/Oncology > Pathology > Coagulation Cascade anking match Platelet Plug Formation Primary Hemostasis resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 12 Red Blood Cell Disorders 11 Primary Hemostasis Disorders match Coagulation Cascade Secondary Hemostasis resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 12 Red Blood Cell Disorders 12 Secondary Hemostasis Disorders match Fibrinolysis & Regulation resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid Hematology Oncology Pathology Coagulation Cascade summary Story Mode The Hemostatic Balance Hemostasis is a physiological masterpiece. It is the body s ability to keep blood in a fluid clot-free state within normal vessels while rapidly inducing a localized solid plug at the site of vascular injury. It is a balancing act between stop the bleed and keep it flowing. If the balance tilts too far toward clotting you get thrombosis infarction ; if it tilts too far toward bleeding you get hemorrhage shock . Phase I The Normal Endothelial Shield Healthy blood vessels are not just passive pipes; they are active inhibitors of clotting. The healthy endothelium maintains a slippery surface using several mechanisms 1. Anti-Platelet Effects Endothelial cells secrete Nitric Oxide NO and Prostacyclin PGI2 . These are potent vasodilators and inhibitors of platelet aggregation. 2. Anticoagulant Effects The surface is coated with Heparin-like molecules which activate Antithrombin III and Thrombomodulin which redirects thrombin to activate Protein C . 3. Fibrinolytic Effects Endothelial cells synthesize tPA Tissue Plasminogen Activator the clot buster that clears small fibrin deposits. --- Phase II Primary Hemostasis – The Platelet Plug The moment a vessel is injured the subendothelial matrix collagen is exposed. This triggers a four-step response to form the initial temporary plug. 1. Transient Vasoconstriction Immediate reflex. The damaged cells release Endothelin causing the vessel to squeeze shut. This slows blood flow giving platelets a chance to grab onto the wall. 2. Platelet Adhesion Platelets don t bind directly to collagen easily. They need a bridge. The Bridge von Willebrand Factor vWF . It comes from plasma platelet alpha-granules and Weibel-Palade bodies of endothelial cells. The Receptor Platelets use the GpIb receptor to hook onto vWF. Clinical Pearl A deficiency in GpIb is Bernard-Soulier Syndrome Big Platelets . 3. Platelet Activation & Secretion Once hooked the platelet changes shape from a smooth disk to a spiky ball and releases its internal stores Alpha Granules Contain big things like vWF Fibrinogen and PF4. Dense Granules Contain small activators— SAC Serotonin ADP Calcium . ADP Binds to the P2Y12 receptor on other platelets calling them to the site. Calcium A vital cofactor for the entire coagulation cascade. 4. Platelet Aggregation ADP binding causes the expression of the GpIIb/IIIa receptor. The Glue Fibrinogen acts as a bridge between GpIIb/IIIa receptors on adjacent platelets. Clinical Pearl A deficiency in GpIIb/IIIa is Glanzmann Thrombasthenia . --- Phase III Secondary Hemostasis – The Coagulation Cascade The platelet plug is weak. It needs a steel mesh of Fibrin to hold it together. This is the goal of the coagulation cascade convert soluble Fibrinogen into insoluble Fibrin strands . 1. The Pathways Intrinsic Internal damage Triggered by Factor XII Hageman Factor hitting exposed collagen. It goes XII → XI → IX → VIII. The Link Factor XIIa also converts prekallikrein to Kallikrein which produces Bradykinin pain/vasodilation . Extrinsic External trauma Triggered by Tissue Factor Factor III . It goes III + VII. This is the fast and major physiological pathway for starting a clot. Common Pathway Both meet at Factor X . Xa + Va + Calcium + Phospholipids = Prothrombinase Complex . This complex converts Prothrombin II to Thrombin IIa . Thrombin then converts Fibrinogen I to Fibrin Ia . 2. The Final Lock Factor XIII Fibrin Stabilizing Factor acts like a molecular stapler cross-linking the fibrin strands to make the clot rock-solid. --- Phase IV The Regulatory Brakes The body must stop the clot from spreading across the entire body. 1. Antithrombin III ATIII A scavenger that neutralizes thrombin and Factors IX X XI XII. Heparin makes ATIII 1000x more powerful. 2. Protein C and S Thrombin binds to Thrombomodulin on healthy cells. This complex activates Protein C. Together with Protein S they snipe the essential cofactors Va and VIIIa shutting down the cascade. --- Phase V Fibrinolysis – The Cleanup Once the vessel is repaired the clot must go. The Buster Plasmin . The Activation tPA converts Plasminogen into Plasmin. The Result Plasmin chews up the fibrin. The Marker The breakdown of cross-linked fibrin releases D-dimers . High D-dimer levels tell you a clot was formed and is now being broken down useful in diagnosing DVT or PE . | Lab Test | Pathway Monitored | Common Drug Target | | --- | --- | --- | | aPTT | Intrinsic XII XI IX VIII | Heparin | | PT / INR | Extrinsic VII | Warfarin | | Bleeding Time | Primary Platelets | Aspirin NSAIDs | Hemostasis is a violent process kept under perfect control by the healthy endothelium. When that control fails life hangs in the balance. questions question Which specific receptor on the platelet membrane is responsible for the initial adhesion to von Willebrand factor vWF ? options text GpIIb/IIIa text GpIb isCorrect true text P2Y12 text COX-1 explanation GpIb is the receptor for vWF-mediated adhesion. GpIIb/IIIa is used for aggregation linking platelets together via fibrinogen . question Factor XII Hageman Factor is unique because its activation provides a bridge between the coagulation cascade and which other inflammatory system? options text The Complement system text The Kinin system Bradykinin isCorrect true text The Renin-Angiotensin system text The Glycogenolysis pathway explanation Activated Factor XII XIIa converts prekallikrein to kallikrein which then produces Bradykinin linking clotting to pain vasodilation and increased vascular permeability. question The Protein C and S system provides essential negative feedback to the coagulation cascade. Which specific factors do they inactivate? options text Factors II and X text Factors VII and IX text Factors Va and VIIIa isCorrect true text Factor XIII explanation Activated Protein C APC with its cofactor Protein S proteolytically inactivates the essential cofactors Va and VIIIa effectively slowing the common and intrinsic pathways. question A high level of D-dimer in the blood is a specific marker for what process? options text Platelet adhesion to collagen text Breakdown of cross-linked fibrin isCorrect true text Activation of the extrinsic pathway text Synthesis of new clotting factors in the liver explanation D-dimers are only produced when plasmin cleaves fibrin that has been cross-linked by Factor XIII. They are not produced during the breakdown of fibrinogen or non-cross-linked fibrin. question Which component of the platelet s Dense Granules is required as a cofactor for multiple steps in the secondary hemostasis coagulation cascade? options text Serotonin text ADP text Calcium Factor IV isCorrect true text von Willebrand Factor explanation Calcium is stored in dense granules and is required for the assembly of the prothrombinase and tenase complexes in the coagulation cascade. flashcards front Where is vWF stored in endothelial cells? back Weibel-Palade bodies . tag Hemostasis front What are the contents of platelet Dense Granules ? Mnemonic back SAC S erotonin A DP C alcium. tag Hemostasis front What is the deficiency in Bernard-Soulier Syndrome ? back GpIb receptor Adhesion defect . tag Pathology front What does Antithrombin III inactivate? back Thrombin IIa IXa Xa XIa XIIa. tag Regulation front What is the role of Factor XIII ? back Cross-linking fibrin strands Transglutaminase . tag Hemostasis pearls title The Gp Mnemonic content I comes before II . Adhesion GpIb comes before Aggregation GpIIb/IIIa . title Heparin Logic back Heparin doesn t kill thrombin directly; it makes your natural bouncer Antithrombin III 1000 times more aggressive. title Warfarin vs. Heparin Labs content PT is for the extrinsic pathway short factor VII so it s affected first by Warfarin . PTT is for the intrinsic pathway Heparin . title Healthy vs. Damaged Endothelium content Healthy = PGI2/NO/Thrombomodulin. Damaged = Endothelin/vWF/Tissue Factor."}, {"id": "l159", "title": "Introduction to Gyn/Ob History", "module": "DPR", "content": "id l159 module DPR metadata title Introduction to Gyn/Ob History lecturer Steven Lewis M.D. Session 159 readingTime 10-15 min difficulty Intermediate tags DPR Clinical Skills Obstetrics Gynecology History Taking resources ankingResource primarySource First Aid chapter Reproductive > Basics alternatives resource Boards and Beyond chapter Reproductive > Obstetric Concepts resource Step-Up to Medicine chapter Gynecology anking match GTPAL / Obstetric History resources AnKing Step 2 v11 B&B 06 ObGyn 02 Obstetrics 01 Prenatal Care match Cervical Cancer Screening Pap Smear resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 16 Female Genital System and Breast 02 Cervix match Menopause Symptoms resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid Reproductive Pathology Menopause summary Story Mode The Life Cycles of Women The Gynecological and Obstetrical Gyn/Ob history is one of the most sensitive and information-dense parts of a clinical interview. It requires not just technical knowledge of menstrual cycles and surgical outcomes but a high degree of empathy and non-judgmental communication. We are documenting the story of a woman s reproductive life from her first period to her final years. This is not just a checklist; it is a map of her hormonal and physical journey. --- 1. The Gynecological Foundation The Rhythms of Life We start with the basics of the menstrual cycle. This isn t just about dates; it s about identifying underlying hormonal health and recognizing patterns of dysfunction. Menarche The age of the first period. Early menarche <12 is a risk factor for breast and endometrial cancer due to increased lifetime estrogen exposure. The LMP Always ask for the first day of the Last Menstrual Period. This is the global Time Zero for calculating pregnancy and investigating cycle irregularities. The Cycle Metrics Frequency How many days from the start of one period to the start of the next? Normal is 21–35 days . Duration How many days does the actual bleeding last? Normal is 2–7 days . Flow The Quantifier Don t settle for heavy or light. Ask How many pads or tampons do you soak through on your heaviest day? Do you have to change them during the night? Do you pass clots? Dysmenorrhea Painful periods. Differentiate between normal cramping and pain that keeps her from work or school which might suggest Endometriosis . Menopause The Quiet Exit Defined clinically as 12 consecutive months of amenorrhea in a woman of appropriate age. CRITICAL RED FLAG Any vaginal bleeding after menopause is Endometrial Cancer until proven otherwise. It requires an urgent endometrial biopsy or ultrasound. --- 2. Preventive Screening The Guardrails of Health I. The Pap Smear Cervical Cancer The Target The Transformation Zone Squamocolumnar junction . This is where the delicate columnar cells of the inner cervix meet the tough squamous cells of the outer cervix. It is where 90% of cervical cancers start. The Screening Rules Starts at age 21 regardless of sexual history. From 21–29 Pap every 3 years. From 30–65 Pap + HPV co-testing every 5 years preferred . The History Always ask about previous abnormal results. If she has had a Colposcopy or LEEP procedure her risk profile changes. II. Mammograms and Breast Health The History Ask about lumps skin dimpling Peau d orange or nipple discharge especially if bloody or spontaneous . Self-Exams While no longer recommended as a primary screening tool by some guidelines if performed they should be done 5–7 days after menses when breast tissue is softest and least congested by estrogen. --- 3. The Sexual History The Poker Face 5 P s When asking about sexual health maintain a neutral professional poker face. Normalize the questions by saying To provide the best care I ask these questions of all my patients. 1. Partners Number and gender of partners. Crucial for assessing STI risk . 2. Practices Types of intercourse vaginal oral . This determines where you should test for STIs e.g. throat or rectal swabs . 3. Protection Barrier methods. Ask how consistently they are used. 4. Past STIs History of infections like Chlamydia or Gonorrhea which can lead to Pelvic Inflammatory Disease and infertility . 5. Prevention of Pregnancy Current contraception. Is she happy with it? Is she taking it correctly? e.g. How many pills do you miss in a month? . --- 4. Obstetrical History The GTPAL Code This is the shorthand clinicians use to summarize a patient s entire pregnancy history. It s a code that tells a story of fertility and loss. G Gravida Total number of times pregnant including the current one! . T Term Deliveries after 37 weeks. P Preterm Deliveries between 20 and 37 weeks. A Abortion Pregnancies ending before 20 weeks includes both spontaneous miscarriages and elective terminations . L Living Number of children currently living. Note on Twins A twin pregnancy counts as G1 but the delivery counts as T1 or P1 and the living children count as L2 . Naegele s Rule The Due Date Math If you know the first day of the LMP you can estimate the Expected Date of Confinement EDC LMP + 7 Days - 3 Months + 1 Year. Example LMP May 10 -> May 17 -> Feb 17 next year . --- 5. The Hidden Red Flags Intimate Partner Violence IPV Screen every patient every time in private. Are you safe in your relationship? Has anyone ever hurt you or made you feel afraid? Urinary Incontinence Many women consider this normal after childbirth but it is treatable. Ask Do you leak urine when you cough or sneeze? Postpartum Depression Always screen mothers at their follow-up visits. The transition to motherhood is a period of extreme psychiatric vulnerability. questions question A patient reports her Last Menstrual Period LMP began on August 15th 2023. Using Naegele s Rule what is her estimated date of delivery EDD ? options text May 15th 2024 text May 22nd 2024 isCorrect true text November 22nd 2024 text May 8th 2024 explanation Naegele s Rule Aug 15 + 7 days = Aug 22. Aug 22 - 3 months = May 22. Add one year = May 22 2024. question A 60-year-old woman who has been postmenopausal for 10 years presents with 2 days of vaginal spotting. She denies pain or trauma. What is the most appropriate next step? options text Reassure her that vaginal atrophy can cause light bleeding text Order an endometrial biopsy isCorrect true text Start her on low-dose estrogen cream text Follow up in 6 months to see if it recurs explanation Postmenopausal bleeding is ENDOMETRIAL CANCER until proven otherwise. You must sample the tissue biopsy or visualize the thickness of the lining ultrasound immediately. question A patient has been pregnant 5 times. She had one miscarriage at 8 weeks one twin delivery at 39 weeks one preterm delivery at 32 weeks and one ectopic pregnancy at 6 weeks. She is currently pregnant again. What is her GTPAL? options text G5 T1 P1 A2 L3 isCorrect true text G5 T2 P1 A2 L3 text G4 T1 P1 A2 L3 text G5 T1 P1 A1 L3 explanation G=5 total . T=1 the twin delivery . P=1 the 32-weeker . A=2 miscarriage + ectopic; both < 20 weeks . L=3 two twins + one preterm child . question Which of the following describes the recommended cervical cancer screening for a 25-year-old female with no prior abnormal paps? options text Pap smear every year text Pap smear every 3 years isCorrect true text Pap smear + HPV co-testing every 5 years text Screening is not necessary until age 30 explanation For women aged 21-29 the recommendation is cytology Pap smear alone every 3 years. Co-testing with HPV is not recommended until age 30. question A woman mentions she has been feeling down since the birth of her baby 4 weeks ago. She admits she is having trouble sleeping even when the baby is asleep and feels like she isn t a good mother. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? options text Postpartum Blues text Postpartum Depression isCorrect true text Postpartum Psychosis text Normal maternal adjustment explanation Postpartum Blues typically resolve within 2 weeks. Symptoms persisting beyond 2 weeks with functional impairment insomnia feelings of inadequacy suggest Postpartum Depression. flashcards front Define Primary Amenorrhea . back Failure of menarche to occur by age 15. tag Gyn front What is the Transformation Zone ? back The squamocolumnar junction of the cervix; most common site for cervical cancer . tag Gyn front What is the significance of bloody nipple discharge ? back Highly suspicious for Intraductal Papilloma or malignancy; requires workup. tag Gyn front Define Gravida . back The total number of pregnancies regardless of outcome. tag Ob front What is the GTPAL for a woman with twins term and one singleton preterm ? back G2 T1 P1 A0 L3 . tag Ob pearls title Estrogen and Cancer content Unopposed estrogen early menarche late menopause obesity is the primary driver of endometrial and breast cancer. Progesterone is the protective counterpart. title The P in Para content Para is the number of pregnancies that reached viability >20 weeks . Twins count as P1 one event but L2 two people . title Post-coital Bleeding content Bleeding after intercourse is a classic red flag for cervical cancer or a cervical polyp. title Ask the Safe Question content Always screen for IPV when the patient is alone. Partners may be the perpetrators and will not allow the patient to speak freely."}, {"id": "l160", "title": "OMM Lab 28: Lower Extremity ME", "module": "OMM", "content": "id l160 module OMM metadata title OMM Lab 28 Lower Extremity ME lecturer P. Kooyman D.O. Session 160 readingTime 10-15 min difficulty Advanced tags OMM Lower Extremity Muscle Energy Hip Knee resources ankingResource primarySource Savarese OMT Review chapter Lower Extremity alternatives resource DiGiovanna chapter An Osteopathic Approach to Diagnosis and Treatment resource Kimberly Manual chapter Lower Extremity ME anking match Lower Extremity Muscle Energy resources AnKing Step 1 v11 OMM 08 Lower Extremity Muscle Energy match Hip Somatic Dysfunction resources AnKing Step 1 v11 OMM 08 Lower Extremity Hip match Fibular Head Dysfunction resources AnKing Step 1 v11 OMM 08 Lower Extremity Knee and Fibula summary Story Mode The Mechanics of the Leg The human lower extremity is a marvel of engineering—a series of interconnected levers pulleys and shock absorbers designed to carry the weight of the world. But when the mechanics fail—when a hip prefers to stay bent or a knee refuses to fully extend—the entire chain is compromised. In this lab we use Muscle Energy ME to reset the system. We aren t just moving bones; we are communicating with the nervous system to recalibrate the tension in the muscles. This lecture is your tactical guide for OMM Lab 28. --- 1. The Rules of the Game Muscle Energy Principles Before touching the patient you must internalize the Neuro-Muscular Reset. Direct Technique We take the patient directly into their Restrictive Barrier . We go where the joint doesn t want to go. Active Technique The patient is the motor. They provide the force. The Isometric Contraction The physician s resistance must perfectly match the patient s effort. There should be zero movement of the limb. The 10-25% Rule This is the most common mistake in the lab. If the patient pushes too hard they engage the global muscle groups and trigger a protective stretch reflex. We want a gentle push—just enough to engage the local Golgi Tendon Organs. The Refractory Period After the contraction you must wait 1–2 seconds the sigh of the muscle before moving to the next barrier. This is the Post-Isometric Relaxation window. --- 2. The Hip The Ball-and-Socket Siege The hip is the powerhouse of the lower extremity. We diagnose and treat it in three planes. I. Flexion vs. Extension The Forward/Backward Battle Hip Flexion Dysfunction The hip likes being flexed The Restriction Extension. Position Prone this is the most effective way to engage the extension barrier . Setup The physician stands on the side of the dysfunction and lifts the thigh toward the ceiling until the extension barrier is met. Instruction Push your knee down into the table into the freedom of flexion . Hip Extension Dysfunction The hip likes being extended The Restriction Flexion. Position Supine . Setup Flex the hip and knee until the barrier is met. You can rest the patient s foot on your shoulder for better control. Instruction Push your leg down toward the table into extension . II. Adduction vs. Abduction The Midline Battle Adduction Dysfunction Likes being in The Restriction Abduction. Setup Move the leg away from the midline to the barrier. Instruction Push your leg back toward the middle. Abduction Dysfunction Likes being out The Restriction Adduction. Setup Move the leg across the midline to the barrier. Instruction Push your leg out away from the middle. III. Internal vs. External Rotation The Twist External Rotation Dysfunction Likes being out The Restriction Internal Rotation. Setup Rotate the hip internally move the foot out while the knee is bent to the barrier. Instruction Push your foot back toward the other leg. --- 3. The Fibular Head The Lateral Gatekeeper Lateral knee pain is often not the knee joint itself but the proximal fibular head . It follows a simple reciprocal rule with the ankle. Anterior Fibular Head Diagnosis Prefers to stay forward. Setup Move the head Posteriorly into the barrier. Ankle Link An anterior fibular head is associated with an Everted/Dorsiflexed ankle. Posterior Fibular Head Diagnosis Prefers to stay back. Setup Move the head Anteriorly into the barrier. Ankle Link A posterior fibular head is associated with an Inverted/Plantarflexed ankle The classic Inversion Ankle Sprain . --- 4. Clinical Pearls Avoiding the Pitfalls Monitor the Innominate When treating the hip keep your monitoring hand on the ASIS or PSIS. If you feel the pelvis tilting you have gone past the joint s barrier and are now stressing the lumbar spine. The Post-Isometric Window If you move to the new barrier too quickly you won t get the full benefit. Count One-Mississippi Two-Mississippi after they relax before moving. Re-assessment The goal is not to fix the leg in one go. Do 3–5 cycles then perform your diagnostic test again. If the motion is still restricted check your diagnosis! questions question A patient presents with lateral knee pain after an ankle sprain. You find that the proximal fibular head resists anterior glide. What is the most likely ankle position associated with this finding? options text Eversion and Dorsiflexion text Inversion and Plantarflexion isCorrect true text Eversion and Plantarflexion text Inversion and Dorsiflexion explanation Resistance to anterior glide means the fibular head is Posterior. A posterior fibular head is classically associated with an inverted and plantarflexed ankle the position of a common ankle sprain . question You are treating a patient for a hip extension somatic dysfunction. Which of the following is the correct setup for a direct muscle energy technique? options text Patient prone extend the hip to the barrier text Patient supine flex the hip to the barrier isCorrect true text Patient supine internally rotate the hip to the barrier text Patient prone abduct the hip to the barrier explanation An extension dysfunction means the hip likes extension and is restricted in flexion. Since ME is a direct technique we take the patient into the restriction Flexion in the supine position. question During the treatment of a hip internal rotation dysfunction the physician moves the patient s foot medially toward the other leg to engage the barrier. The patient is then asked to push their foot in which direction? options text Medially into the barrier text Laterally away from the barrier isCorrect true text Toward the ceiling text Toward the floor explanation Internal rotation dysfunction means the hip is restricted in external rotation. To engage the external rotation barrier the foot must be moved medially. The patient then pushes into the freedom Internal rotation which involves moving the foot laterally. question What is the physiological objective of the 1-2 second pause following the patient s contraction in Muscle Energy? options text To allow the patient to catch their breath text To allow for post-isometric relaxation of the agonist muscle isCorrect true text To trigger the reciprocal inhibition of the antagonist text To increase the heart rate to improve blood flow explanation The brief pause allows the neuromuscular system to enter a refractory period where the hypertonic muscle can be lengthened to a new restrictive barrier. question Which of the following is a contraindication for the use of Muscle Energy in a patient with a hip dysfunction? options text Age over 65 text Severe osteoporosis or acute fracture isCorrect true text Chronic muscle tension text History of joint replacement explanation Because Muscle Energy involves active patient force and joint positioning acute fractures or severe osteoporosis risk of fracture are contraindications for this technique. flashcards front What is the patient instruction for treating a Posterior Fibular Head ? back Move the head Anteriorly to the barrier; have patient push Posteriorly . tag OMM front What position is best for treating Hip Flexion Somatic Dysfunction ? back Prone to engage the extension barrier . tag OMM front What is the 10-25% Rule ? back The patient should only use a gentle amount of force during the isometric contraction. tag OMM front Define Post-Isometric Relaxation . back The refractory period after a muscle contraction where the muscle can be safely lengthened. tag OMM front What ankle motion is associated with an Anterior Fibular Head ? back Dorsiflexion and Eversion . tag OMM pearls title The Foot-to-Fila Rule content To internally rotate the hip move the foot OUT . To externally rotate the hip move the foot IN . title Direct = Into the Wall content Always remember ME is direct. You are pushing the patient against the wall of their restriction. title Reciprocal Inhibition vs PIR content PIR involves the muscle you are treating. Reciprocal inhibition involves contracting the opposite muscle to relax the one you are treating."}, {"id": "l161", "title": "Pathology of Hemorrhage", "module": "Unknown", "content": "id l161 module Unknown metadata title Pathology of Hemorrhage lecturer Maria M. Plummer M.D. Session 161 readingTime 10-15 min difficulty Advanced tags Pathology Hematology Hemorrhage Platelet Disorders DIC Hemophilia resources ankingResource primarySource Pathoma chapter Hematology > Primary Hemostasis Disorders alternatives resource Boards and Beyond chapter Hematology > Coagulation Disorders resource First Aid chapter Hematology/Oncology > Pathology > Bleeding Disorders anking match ITP / TTP / HUS resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 12 Red Blood Cell Disorders 11 Primary Hemostasis Disorders match Hemophilia & vWD resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 12 Red Blood Cell Disorders 12 Secondary Hemostasis Disorders match DIC Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 12 Red Blood Cell Disorders 13 Other Hemostasis Disorders summary Story Mode The Failure of the Iron Guard If hemostasis is the Iron Guard of the blood—a perfectly coordinated defense force—then hemorrhage is the catastrophic collapse of that guard. When the vessel wall fails or the platelets vanish or the coagulation factors are consumed in a suicidal frenzy the body begins to leak. This lecture is the map of the battlefield exploring the specific pathologies that turn a minor scratch into a life-threatening crisis. --- 1. The Clinical Vocabulary of Bleeding As a physician you must read the skin like a book. The size and location of the blood tell you which part of the guard has failed. Petechiae < 3mm Tiny pinpoint hemorrhages. These are the hallmarks of Primary Hemostasis failure—either you don t have enough platelets Thrombocytopenia or they aren t sticking. Purpura 3–10mm Larger often palpable spots. These can indicate vasculitis or more severe platelet destruction. Ecchymosis > 1cm The classic bruise. Large area bleeds usually suggest a Secondary Hemostasis failure—the concrete coagulation factors isn t setting. Hemarthrosis Bleeding into joint spaces. This is the high-yield clinical sign for severe factor deficiencies like Hemophilia . It leads to chronic joint destruction and crippling pain. --- 2. Primary Hemostasis Disorders The Vanishing Bricks When the platelets the bricks are gone or broken patients present with mucosal bleeding nosebleeds epistaxis bleeding gums and heavy periods menorrhagia . I. Immune Thrombocytopenic Purpura ITP The Story The body develops friendly fire against its own platelets. The Mechanism Plasma cells produce IgG autoantibodies often against the GpIIb/IIIa receptor . The Spleen The antibody-coated platelets aren t destroyed in the blood; they are pulled out by macrophages in the Spleen . The Clue You will see increased Megakaryocytes on bone marrow biopsy. The factory is fine; the products are being stolen. II. Thrombotic Thrombocytopenic Purpura TTP The Story A traffic jam of platelets in the small vessels. The Mechanism A deficiency in ADAMTS13 the molecular scissors that normally chops up huge von Willebrand Factor vWF multimers. The Result Long vWF strands catch platelets like a spiderweb forming microthrombi. The Pentad FAT RN F ever A nemia MAHA T hrombocytopenia R enal failure N euro symptoms confusion/seizures . The Smear Passing RBCs get shredded by the micro-clots creating Schistocytes . --- 3. Secondary Hemostasis Disorders The Weak Concrete When the concrete factors is missing the platelet plug eventually washes away leading to deep delayed bleeding. Hemophilia A Factor VIII The most famous. X-linked recessive. von Willebrand Disease vWD The Double Hit. Hit 1 vWF is the glue for platelets. Without it you get a Primary hemostasis defect prolonged bleeding time . Hit 2 vWF carries and stabilizes Factor VIII. Without it VIII is destroyed causing a Secondary hemostasis defect prolonged PTT . Clinical Clue A patient with both nosebleeds AND heavy bruising. --- 4. The Final Nightmare DIC Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation DIC is the ultimate hematologic paradox the patient is clotting and bleeding to death at the same time. The Trigger A massive systemic insult Sepsis Trauma Obstetric emergency triggers widespread clotting. The Consumption Clotting factors and platelets are used up everywhere. When a real leak happens like at an IV site there are no supplies left to fix it. The Labs The Lab of Horrors — Everything is prolonged PT PTT Platelets are low Fibrinogen is low and D-dimer is high . The Red Flag Waterhouse-Friderichsen Syndrome —bilateral adrenal hemorrhage due to DIC during Meningococcemia. --- 5. Qualitative Platelet Defects Bernard-Soulier BS Deficiency of GpIb. Big Suckers —look for Giant Platelets on the smear. Glanzmann Thrombasthenia Deficiency of GpIIb/IIIa. The platelets are there but they can t link together. questions question A 28-year-old female presents with confusion a fever of 101.5°F and a petechial rash. Labs show a platelet count of 15 000/µL and a normal PT and aPTT. A peripheral smear shows numerous schistocytes. What is the most likely underlying deficiency? options text ADAMTS13 isCorrect true text GpIb receptor text Factor VIII text Protein C explanation The patient has the TTP pentad FAT RN . Normal PT/PTT with low platelets and schistocytes MAHA points to a deficiency of ADAMTS13. question Which of the following findings would you expect to see on a bone marrow biopsy of a patient with chronic ITP? options text Complete absence of all cell lines text Increased number of Megakaryocytes isCorrect true text Blast cells > 20% text Ringed sideroblasts explanation In ITP the destruction of platelets is peripheral spleen . The bone marrow responds to the low platelet count by increasing the number of megakaryocytes to try and compensate. question A child with severe Meningococcemia develops sudden-onset hypotension and shock. Autopsy reveals bilateral hemorrhage into the adrenal glands. This specific complication is known as options text Trousseau Syndrome text Richter Transformation text Waterhouse-Friderichsen Syndrome isCorrect true text Plummer-Vinson Syndrome explanation Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome is the classic association for bilateral adrenal hemorrhage due to DIC during N. meningitidis sepsis. question A patient is diagnosed with von Willebrand Disease. Which of the following laboratory profiles would most likely be seen? options text Normal PT prolonged PTT prolonged Bleeding Time isCorrect true text Prolonged PT normal PTT normal Bleeding Time text Normal PT normal PTT prolonged Bleeding Time text Prolonged PT prolonged PTT normal Bleeding Time explanation vWD affects both platelet adhesion prolonged Bleeding Time and stabilizes Factor VIII prolonged PTT . PT the extrinsic pathway remains normal. question What is the primary mechanism of action of the Shiga-like toxin from E. coli O157 H7 that leads to HUS? options text Inhibition of ADAMTS13 text Endothelial injury leading to microthrombi formation isCorrect true text Direct destruction of the bone marrow text Activation of the intrinsic coagulation pathway explanation The toxin causes direct injury to endothelial cells primarily in the kidney which triggers the formation of microthrombi consumption of platelets and shearing of RBCs. flashcards front What size are Petechiae ? back < 3 mm. tag Pathology front What does ADAMTS13 normally do? back Cleaves large vWF multimers . tag Pathology front What is the classic association for Schistocytes ? back MAHA Microangiopathic Hemolytic Anemia - seen in TTP HUS and DIC. tag Pathology front Which receptor is deficient in Bernard-Soulier Syndrome ? back GpIb Adhesion . tag Pathology front What lab marker is the most specific for DIC ? back D-dimer breakdown of cross-linked fibrin . tag Pathology pearls title The Christmas Factor content Hemophilia B is a deficiency of Factor IX Christmas Factor . Hemophilia A is Factor VIII. title Ristocetin Test content Agglutination with Ristocetin is used to test vWF function. It fails in vWD and Bernard-Soulier. title The FAT RN Mnemonic content F ever A nemia T hrombocytopenia R enal failure N euro symptoms. title Primary vs Secondary Hemorrhage content Primary = Skin/Mucosa Platelets . Secondary = Deep/Joints Factors ."}, {"id": "l162", "title": "Pathology of Thrombosis", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l162 module Neuro metadata title Pathology of Thrombosis lecturer Maria M. Plummer M.D. Session 162 readingTime 10-15 min difficulty Advanced tags Pathology Hematology Thrombosis Embolism Virchow s Triad resources ankingResource primarySource Pathoma chapter Hematology > Thrombosis alternatives resource Boards and Beyond chapter Hematology > Basic Concepts > Thrombosis resource First Aid chapter Hematology/Oncology > Pathology > Hypercoagulable States anking match Virchow s Triad resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 12 Red Blood Cell Disorders 14 Thrombosis match Embolism PE Fat Air resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 12 Red Blood Cell Disorders 15 Embolism match Hypercoagulable States resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 12 Red Blood Cell Disorders 14 Thrombosis Hypercoagulable States summary Story Mode The Dangerous Stagnation In a healthy body blood is a fluid of life rushing through vessels with the grace of a mountain stream. But in disease that stream can turn into a stagnant swamp. A Thrombus is a solid mass formed within the vascular system from the constituents of flowing blood. It is the leading cause of death in the developed world manifesting as the heart attacks and strokes that claim millions of lives. To understand why it happens we look to a 19th-century giant Rudolf Virchow and his three ingredients for a clot. --- 1. Virchow s Triad The Recipe for a Disaster Thrombosis doesn t just happen. It requires a perfect storm of at least one and usually more of these three factors. I. Endothelial Injury The Core Insult The smooth anti-thrombotic shield of the vessel wall is the only thing keeping the blood fluid. When it s broken the game is over. The Insult Atherosclerosis is the classic culprit. But don t forget Hypertension the high pressure sandblasts the wall Smoking chemical toxins and Diabetes advanced glycation products . The Result The Iron Guard is down. Subendothelial collagen and Tissue Factor are exposed immediately summoning platelets and the coagulation cascade. II. Alteration in Normal Blood Flow Blood is meant to flow in a Laminar fashion—fast in the middle slow at the edges. Stasis The Sludge Slowing of flow. Common in the deep veins of the legs during a long flight or after surgery. Stasis prevents the dilution of clotting factors and allows them to concentrate into a clot. Turbulence The Whirlpool Chaotic flow often at vessel branch points or over plaques. Turbulence physically injures the endothelium and creates pockets of stasis. III. Hypercoagulability The Thirsty Blood Sometimes the blood itself is primed to clot. Primary Genetic Factor V Leiden The most common. A mutation in the Factor V gene makes it resistant to the brakes Protein C . It is the Factor that won t die. Prothrombin G20210A A mutation that simply causes the body to overproduce prothrombin. Secondary Acquired The Estrogen Link Pregnancy and Oral Contraceptives increase the liver s production of clotting factors and decrease natural anticoagulants. Malignancy Trousseau Syndrome Cancer cells can release procoagulants into the blood causing migratory thrombophlebitis clots that seem to move from one leg to the other . --- 2. Thrombus vs. Postmortem Clot The Autopsy Mystery As a pathologist how do you know if a clot killed the patient or just formed after the heart stopped? Lines of Zahn These are alternating layers of pale platelets/fibrin and dark RBCs material. They are the rings of a tree that only form in flowing blood . If you see them the thrombus was formed while the patient was alive Antemortem . Attachment Real thrombi are firmly glued to the vessel wall. Postmortem clots are loose non-laminated and often have a chicken fat yellow plasma or currant jelly dark RBC appearance. --- 3. The Fate of the Thrombus Once a thrombus forms it follows one of four paths 1. Propagation It grows like a vine eventually blocking the entire vessel. 2. Dissolution The body s natural clot buster Plasmin breaks it down. 3. Organization & Recanalization The body turns the clot into a fibrous scar and drills new tiny channels through it to restore some flow. 4. Embolization The Traveling Killer The most dangerous fate. Part of the clot breaks off and travels until it hits a vessel too small to pass. --- 4. Embolism The Projectile An embolus is a detached mass solid liquid or gas carried by the blood to a site distant from its origin. I. Pulmonary Embolism PE The Origin >95% come from Deep Vein Thromboses DVT in the legs above the knee . The Saddle Embolus A massive clot that straddles the bifurcation of the pulmonary artery causing immediate sudden death. The Silent PE Most PEs are small and silent because the lung has a Dual Blood Supply Pulmonary and Bronchial arteries . II. The Specialized Emboli Fat Embolism Occurs after Long Bone Fractures e.g. femur . Look for the triad Respiratory distress Neuro symptoms and a petechial rash . Air Embolism Gas bubbles in the blood e.g. The Bends in divers or during surgery . Amniotic Fluid Embolism A rare devastating complication of labor. Fetal material enters maternal blood triggering massive DIC and shock. Paradoxical Embolism A venous clot that passes through a hole in the heart ASD/VSD to enter the arterial system e.g. causing a stroke from a leg clot . questions question Which of the following is the most common inherited cause of a hypercoagulable state? options text Protein C deficiency text Antithrombin III deficiency text Factor V Leiden mutation isCorrect true text Prothrombin gene mutation explanation Factor V Leiden is the most frequent genetic risk factor for venous thrombosis. The mutation makes Factor V resistant to inactivation by Activated Protein C. question At autopsy a large clot is found in the iliac vein. Which of the following features would most strongly indicate that this was a postmortem clot rather than an antemortem thrombus? options text Firm attachment to the vessel wall text Presence of Lines of Zahn text A chicken fat appearance with no laminations isCorrect true text Organization and recanalization explanation Postmortem clots are typically loose non-laminated and show a chicken fat yellow supernatant and currant jelly RBC sediment appearance. Antemortem thrombi are attached and show Lines of Zahn. question A 19-year-old male is hospitalized for a femur fracture. Two days later he develops sudden confusion shortness of breath and small red spots on his chest and neck. What is the most likely diagnosis? options text Pulmonary Embolism text Fat Embolism Syndrome isCorrect true text Air Embolism text Amniotic Fluid Embolism explanation The combination of long bone fracture respiratory distress neurological changes and a petechial rash is pathognomonic for Fat Embolism Syndrome. question What is the primary reason why a patient with atrial fibrillation is at an increased risk for systemic thromboembolism? options text Endothelial injury due to rapid heart rate text Stasis of blood in the left atrial appendage isCorrect true text Genetic hypercoagulability text Turbulence across the mitral valve explanation In AFib the atria do not contract effectively leading to stasis of blood especially in the left atrial appendage. This stasis allows for thrombus formation which can then embolize to the brain stroke . question A patient with a history of recurrent DVTs is found to have a stroke. Evaluation reveals a patent foramen ovale PFO . What type of embolism does this represent? options text Systemic Thromboembolism text Paradoxical Embolism isCorrect true text Saddle Embolism text Air Embolism explanation A paradoxical embolism occurs when a venous clot bypasses the lungs by crossing from the right to the left side of the heart e.g. through a PFO and enters the arterial circulation. flashcards front What are the three parts of Virchow s Triad ? back 1. Endothelial Injury. 2. Stasis/Turbulence. 3. Hypercoagulability. tag Pathology front What do Lines of Zahn represent? back Alternating layers of platelets/fibrin and RBCs; indicates an antemortem thrombus. tag Pathology front What is Trousseau Syndrome ? back Migratory thrombophlebitis associated with occult malignancy especially pancreatic . tag Pathology front What is a Saddle Embolus ? back A large PE that straddles the bifurcation of the pulmonary artery; usually fatal. tag Pathology front Where do >95% of Pulmonary Emboli originate? back DVT in the deep veins of the legs above the knee . tag Pathology pearls title The Fate Mnemonic content P ropagate E mbolize D issolve O rganize PEDO . title Why Lungs Don t Infarct content Lungs have a dual blood supply Bronchial and Pulmonary . A PE blocks one but the other usually saves the tissue. title Air Embolism Volume content It typically takes > 100 mL of air to cause a clinically significant air embolism in the heart. title Estrogen and Clots content Oral contraceptives increase Factor VII and X and decrease Antithrombin III tilting the balance toward thrombosis."}, {"id": "l163", "title": "Pediatric Hematology and Oncology", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l163 module Neuro metadata title Pediatric Hematology and Oncology lecturer Thomas Chan D.O. Session 163 readingTime 10-15 min difficulty Advanced tags Pediatrics Hematology Oncology Leukemia Anemia Solid Tumors resources ankingResource primarySource Boards and Beyond chapter Pediatrics > Hematology/Oncology alternatives resource Pathoma chapter Hematology > Leukemias resource First Aid chapter Pediatrics > Pathology anking match Pediatric Anemias TEC / DBA resources AnKing Step 2 v11 B&B 05 Hematology 01 Anemia 07 Pediatric Anemia match Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia ALL resources AnKing Step 1 v11 Pathoma 13 WBC Disorders 02 Acute Leukemia ALL match Neuroblastoma & Wilms Tumor resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid Renal Pathology Wilms Tumor summary Story Mode The Small Factory Under Siege Pediatric hematology and oncology are unique because we are dealing with a factory the bone marrow that is still growing adapting and extremely plastic. While children can develop many of the same diseases as adults the presentation epidemiology and prognosis are often vastly different. This lecture focuses on the Red Flags that tell a pediatrician when a simple tired kid is actually a medical emergency. --- 1. Pediatric Anemias The Stuttering Factory Anemia in children is a common finding often picked up during routine screening ages 1–2 . The Factory Analogy is your best tool Reticulocytes are the new products. If retics are high the factory is working overtime hemolysis or bleeding . If retics are low the factory has shut down. I. The Normal Low Physiologic Nadir The Story A healthy 6–8 week old baby is found to have a hemoglobin of 10 g/dL. The Mechanism At birth babies have a huge amount of Hemoglobin F. Once they start breathing air their oxygen levels rise. This signals the marrow to take a break. Erythropoietin production drops. The Outcome This is a normal healthy transition. It normalizes as the baby starts making adult hemoglobin HbA . No treatment needed . II. The Viral Shutdown Transient Erythroblastopenia of Childhood TEC The Story A 2-year-old looks pale a few weeks after a minor cold. The Labs Normocytic anemia with Low Reticulocytes . The Reality A temporary acquired shutdown of the RBC production line likely triggered by a virus. It resolves on its own within 1–2 months. III. The Genetic Defects Diamond-Blackfan DBA vs. Fanconi Diamond-Blackfan Anemia DBA The Defect A congenital Pure Red Cell Aplasia only RBCs are missing . The Clues Macrocytic anemia + Physical Anomalies specifically Triphalangeal Thumbs short stature and craniofacial defects . Fanconi Anemia The Defect A congenital Bone Marrow Failure. Everything is missing Pancytopenia . The Clues Short stature café-au-lait spots and Absent or Hypoplastic Thumbs . The Risk These children have a high risk of developing AML. --- 2. Sickle Cell Disease High-Stakes Pediatric Crises In children Sickle Cell Disease is not just about pain; it s about sudden life-threatening events. Splenic Sequestration The Crisis The spleen suddenly traps a massive volume of the child s blood. The Signs Sudden massive enlargement of the spleen + Sudden drop in Hb + Hypovolemic Shock . This is a surgical emergency. Aplastic Crisis The Trigger Parvovirus B19 . The Sign Hb drops and Reticulocytes are ZERO . The virus has killed the factory s worker cells. Acute Chest Syndrome The Sign Fever chest pain and a new infiltrate on chest X-ray. It is the leading cause of death in pediatric SCD patients. --- 3. Pediatric Oncology The Big Three I. ALL The King of Childhood Cancer The Presentation Fever bone pain often refusing to walk and lymphadenopathy. The Markers TdT+ DNA polymerase and CD10+ CALLA . The Association Down Syndrome specifically if the child is OVER 5 years old . II. The Abdominal Masses Neuroblastoma vs. Wilms This is a classic board comparison. Neuroblastoma The Origin Adrenal medulla sympathetic chain . The Sign A firm irregular mass that CROSSES the midline . The Syndrome Opsoclonus-Myoclonus Dancing eyes dancing feet . The Labs Elevated VMA and HVA in the urine. Wilms Tumor Nephroblastoma The Sign A smooth firm mass that does NOT cross the midline . The Syndrome Often associated with WAGR Wilms Aniridia Genitourinary anomalies Range of developmental delay . III. The Bone Tumors Osteosarcoma Metaphysis near the joint . Sunburst pattern or Codman triangle on X-ray. Ewing Sarcoma Diaphysis the shaft . Onion-skin appearance. Associated with the t 11;22 translocation. --- 4. The Red Flag Summary If you see these in a child do not delay Night pain pain that wakes a child from sleep . Painless rubbery lymph nodes. Unexplained weight loss. Blueberry Muffin rash Leukemia cutis - common in neonatal AML . questions question A 4-year-old child presents with a large abdominal mass that crosses the midline. Physical exam reveals jerking movements of the eyes and extremities. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? options text Wilms Tumor text Neuroblastoma isCorrect true text Hepatoblastoma text Renal Cell Carcinoma explanation Neuroblastoma arises from the sympathetic chain frequently crosses the midline and is uniquely associated with the paraneoplastic opsoclonus-myoclonus syndrome dancing eyes/feet . question A 7-year-old child with Down Syndrome presents with new-onset fatigue and bruising. Bone marrow biopsy reveals blasts that are TdT+ and CD10+. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? options text Acute Myeloid Leukemia M7 text Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia isCorrect true text Chronic Myelogenous Leukemia text Juvenile Myelomonocytic Leukemia explanation Children with Down Syndrome have an increased risk of ALL after the age of 5. TdT and CD10 are classic markers for B-ALL. question A child with Sickle Cell Disease presents with a sudden drop in hemoglobin from 8 g/dL to 3 g/dL. The reticulocyte count is 0.1%. Which of the following viruses is the most likely cause? options text EBV text CMV text Parvovirus B19 isCorrect true text HIV explanation In SCD an aplastic crisis is marked by a drop in Hb and a very low reticulocyte count marrow shutdown . This is classically caused by Parvovirus B19 which infects erythroid precursors. question A 3-year-old child presents with a smooth painless abdominal mass that does not cross the midline. The child has no irises aniridia . What is the most likely diagnosis? options text Wilms Tumor isCorrect true text Neuroblastoma text Teratoma text Pheochromocytoma explanation The WAGR syndrome Wilms tumor Aniridia Genitourinary anomalies and Range of developmental delay is a classic association for Nephroblastoma Wilms tumor . question Which of the following X-ray findings is most characteristic of Ewing Sarcoma? options text Sunburst pattern in the metaphysis text Onion-skinning of the periosteum isCorrect true text Codman triangle in the epiphysis text Lytic lesion in the skull explanation Ewing Sarcoma presents with a characteristic onion-skin periosteal reaction typically in the diaphysis shaft of long bones. flashcards front What is the Physiologic Nadir ? back A normal drop in Hb occurring around 6-8 weeks of age. tag Pediatrics front Which leukemia is associated with Down Syndrome under age 5 ? back AML M7 . tag Oncology front What is the hallmark of Diamond-Blackfan Anemia ? back Pure red cell aplasia + Triphalangeal Thumbs . tag Hematology front What are the markers for ALL ? back TdT+ and CD10+ . tag Oncology front Which bone tumor is associated with t 11;22 ? back Ewing Sarcoma . tag Oncology pearls title The Cross Rule content Neuroblastoma crosses the midline. Wilms tumor stays on its side. title Reticulocyte Count Logic content If the child is anemic High Retic = Loss/Destruction. Low Retic = Production failure. title Dancing Eyes content Opsoclonus-myoclonus syndrome is a high-yield look-out for occult Neuroblastoma. title Osteo vs Ewing content Osteosarcoma = Sunburst Metaphysis . Ewing = Onion-skin Diaphysis ."}, {"id": "l164", "title": "Pharmacology of Coagulation: Anticoagulants", "module": "Pharmacology", "content": "id l164 module Pharmacology metadata title Pharmacology of Coagulation Anticoagulants lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. Session 164 readingTime 10-15 min difficulty Advanced tags Pharmacology Coagulation Heparin Warfarin DOACs resources ankingResource primarySource Boards and Beyond chapter Hematology > Coagulation Pharmacology alternatives resource SketchyPharm chapter Blood & Inflammation > Anticoagulants resource First Aid chapter Hematology/Oncology > Pharmacology > Anticoagulants anking match Heparin & LMWH resources AnKing Step 1 v11 SketchyPharm 06 Blood and Inflammation 01 Anticoagulants 01 Heparin LMWH Fondaparinux Direct Thrombin Inhibitors match Warfarin resources AnKing Step 1 v11 SketchyPharm 06 Blood and Inflammation 01 Anticoagulants 02 Warfarin match Factor Xa Inhibitors DOACs resources AnKing Step 1 v11 SketchyPharm 06 Blood and Inflammation 01 Anticoagulants 03 Direct Factor Xa Inhibitors summary Story Mode The Art of Thinning Blood The human coagulation system is like a high-stakes waterfall—once it starts it s hard to stop. Anticoagulants are the dams we build to control that flow. Unlike antiplatelet drugs the bricks anticoagulants target the soluble enzymatic factors of the cascade. This lecture is the tactical guide to using these powerful but dangerous tools to prevent strokes and pulmonary emboli while avoiding the nightmare of internal hemorrhage. --- 1. The Indirect Inhibitors The Heparin Family These drugs don t kill the clotting factors directly. They are potentiators —they hire a bodyguard called Antithrombin III ATIII to do the dirty work. I. Unfractionated Heparin UFH The Heavy Chain The Chain Long complex strings of sugars. The Mechanism Heparin binds to ATIII and changes its shape making it 1 000 times more effective at neutralizing Thrombin IIa and Factor Xa . The Catch To neutralize Thrombin the heparin chain must be long enough to wrap around both ATIII and Thrombin at the same time. The Lab Monitored by aPTT Intrinsic pathway . The Reversal Protamine Sulfate . The Danger HIT Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia . IgG antibodies form against Heparin-PF4 complexes causing the platelets to clump together thrombosis and then disappear thrombocytopenia . II. Low Molecular Weight Heparin Enoxaparin The Scalpel The Chain Shortened versions of heparin. The Mechanism Because they are short they can t wrap around Thrombin effectively. They mostly target Factor Xa . The Pros More predictable longer half-life and usually requires zero monitoring. III. Fondaparinux The Sniper The Mechanism A synthetic pentasaccharide that exclusively targets Factor Xa. It is the safest choice if a patient has a history of HIT. --- 2. The Vitamin K Antagonist Warfarin Warfarin is the Old Guard. It doesn t stop the factors already in your blood; it stops the liver from making new ones. The Mechanism It inhibits VKORC1 Vitamin K Epoxide Reductase . Without reduced Vitamin K the liver cannot gamma-carboxylate Factors II VII IX X and Proteins C and S . The Delay It takes 3–5 days to work. Why? Because you have to wait for the factors already in the blood to die off. The Pro-thrombotic Paradox Protein C the brake has the shortest half-life. When you start Warfarin your brakes fail before your engine stops. This creates a temporary hypercoagulable state. The Solution You MUST bridge the patient with Heparin for the first few days. The Reversal Vitamin K slow Fresh Frozen Plasma FFP or PCC fast . --- 3. The New Guard DOACs Direct Oral Anticoagulants These are set it and forget it drugs. They directly bind to their targets without needing ATIII. I. Direct Xa Inhibitors Rivaroxaban Apixaban The Clue They have Xa in their names. The Use AFib and DVT/PE prevention. Reversal Andexanet alfa . II. Direct Thrombin Inhibitors Dabigatran The Mechanism Binds directly to the active site of Thrombin IIa . The Side Effect Significant GI upset dyspepsia . Reversal Idarucizumab Praxbind . --- 4. Clinical Comparison Summary | Feature | Heparin UFH | Warfarin | DOACs | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | Onset | Immediate | Slow Days | Fast Hours | | Monitoring | aPTT | PT/INR | None | | Pregnancy | Safe Large molecule | Teratogen | Not recommended | | Antidote | Protamine | Vit K / FFP | Specific MABs | --- 5. High-Yield Pearls Warfarin Skin Necrosis Happens in patients with underlying Protein C deficiency who are started on Warfarin without a bridge. The skin literally dies as tiny clots form in the microvasculature. The HIT Rule If the platelet count drops by 50% after 5 days of Heparin stop all heparin and switch to a Direct Thrombin Inhibitor like Argatroban . Monitoring LMWH While not routine you monitor Anti-Xa levels in pregnant women or patients with renal failure. questions question Which of the following describes the primary mechanism of Unfractionated Heparin? options text Directly binds to the active site of Thrombin text Binds to Antithrombin III and potentiates its effect on IIa and Xa isCorrect true text Inhibits Vitamin K Epoxide Reductase text Prevents the synthesis of Factor VIII explanation Heparin is an indirect inhibitor; it requires Antithrombin III as a cofactor to neutralize Thrombin IIa and Factor Xa. question A patient with Atrial Fibrillation is started on Warfarin. On day 2 of therapy she develops painful necrotizing skin lesions on her breasts and thighs. What is the most likely cause? options text Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia text Inherited Protein C deficiency isCorrect true text Vitamin K toxicity text Allergy to the dye in the tablet explanation Warfarin-induced skin necrosis occurs when the rapid drop in Protein C an anticoagulant with a short half-life creates a transient hypercoagulable state causing micro-thrombosis in the skin vessels. question Which medication is the preferred anticoagulant for a pregnant woman requiring treatment for a DVT? options text Warfarin text Enoxaparin LMWH isCorrect true text Rivaroxaban text Dabigatran explanation Heparins both UFH and LMWH are large molecules that do not cross the placenta making them safe for the fetus. Warfarin is a known teratogen. question What is the specific reversal agent for the direct thrombin inhibitor Dabigatran? options text Protamine Sulfate text Idarucizumab isCorrect true text Andexanet alfa text Vitamin K explanation Idarucizumab Praxbind is a monoclonal antibody fragment that specifically binds to and reverses the anticoagulant effect of dabigatran. question Which of the following lab tests is used to monitor the effect of a Warfarin dose? options text aPTT text Bleeding Time text PT/INR isCorrect true text Anti-Xa levels explanation Warfarin inhibits the extrinsic pathway Factor VII is affected first which is monitored by the Prothrombin Time PT and standardized as the INR. flashcards front What are the Vitamin K dependent factors? back II VII IX X Protein C and S . tag Pharmacology front What is the antidote for Heparin ? back Protamine Sulfate . tag Pharmacology front Why do we bridge Warfarin with Heparin? back To prevent skin necrosis caused by the rapid drop in Protein C . tag Pharmacology front Which anticoagulant targets ONLY Factor Xa via ATIII? back Fondaparinux . tag Pharmacology front What is the target of Dabigatran ? back Directly inhibits Thrombin IIa . tag Pharmacology pearls title HIT Type II content Stop all heparin. Start Argatroban. Do NOT give platelets it s fuel for the fire . title The Xa in the Name content Riva roxa ban and Api xa ban inhibit Factor Xa . title Renal Failure Warning content LMWH and DOACs are cleared by the kidneys. If the GFR is low use Unfractionated Heparin instead. title Warfarin and Green Veggies content It s about consistency . If you eat more Vitamin K your INR will drop. If you eat less it will rise."}, {"id": "l165", "title": "Pharmacology: Antiplatelets and Fibrinolytics", "module": "Pharmacology", "content": "id l165 module Pharmacology metadata title Pharmacology Antiplatelets and Fibrinolytics lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. Session 165 readingTime 10-15 min difficulty Advanced tags Pharmacology Coagulation Antiplatelets Aspirin tPA Thrombolytics resources ankingResource primarySource Boards and Beyond chapter Hematology > Coagulation Pharmacology alternatives resource SketchyPharm chapter Blood & Inflammation > Antiplatelets / Fibrinolytics resource First Aid chapter Hematology/Oncology > Pharmacology > Antiplatelet Drugs anking match Antiplatelet Agents Aspirin/Clopidogrel resources AnKing Step 1 v11 SketchyPharm 06 Blood and Inflammation 02 Antiplatelet Agents match Fibrinolytics tPA resources AnKing Step 1 v11 SketchyPharm 06 Blood and Inflammation 03 Fibrinolytics match Antidotes & Reversal Agents resources AnKing Step 1 v11 SketchyPharm 06 Blood and Inflammation 01 Anticoagulants 04 Antidotes summary Story Mode The Wall and the Sledgehammer In the previous session we built dams to stop the waterfall Anticoagulants . Today we focus on the Bricks of the clot—the Platelets—and the Sledgehammer we use when the clot has already formed and is actively killing tissue. This lecture is about Antiplatelet agents which prevent the first line of defense from assembling and Fibrinolytics the high-stakes clot busters that melt the final product. --- 1. Antiplatelet Agents Keeping the Bricks Apart Platelets are the bricks that form the initial hemostatic plug. They only aggregate when they receive a signal. Our drugs jam those signals. I. The Irreversible Inactivator Aspirin The Mechanism Irreversibly acetylates COX-1 . This shuts down the production of Thromboxane A2 TXA2 the bat signal that platelets use to call for backup. The Durability Platelets have no nucleus. They can t make more enzymes. One baby aspirin kills the COX-1 for the entire life of the platelet 7–10 days . The Catch NSAIDs like Ibuprofen also block COX-1 but they are reversible . If you take Ibuprofen before your Aspirin it can block the Aspirin from doing its job. II. The ADP Receptor Blockers P2Y12 Inhibitors The Drugs Clopidogrel Plavix Prasugrel Ticagrelor . The Mechanism They block the P2Y12 receptor preventing ADP from activating the platelet. The Pro-drug Rule Clopidogrel must be activated by the liver CYP2C19 . High-Yield Some patients are poor metabolizers of Clopidogrel. It won t work for them. The Exception Ticagrelor is the only one that is reversible and does not require hepatic activation. III. The Final Common Pathway GP IIb/IIIa Inhibitors The Drugs Abciximab Eptifibatide Tirofiban . The Mechanism These block the GpIIb/IIIa receptor—the hook that platelets use to grab onto fibrinogen and link to each other. The Potency These are the strongest antiplatelet agents because they block the final step of aggregation regardless of what started the process. --- 2. Fibrinolytics The Clot Busters The Sledgehammer When a patient is having a massive stroke or MI right now we don t have time to prevent a new clot; we need to destroy the one that s already there. The Drugs Alteplase tPA Tenecteplase Reteplase . The Mechanism These are catalysts. They convert Plasminogen to Plasmin . Plasmin is a set of molecular scissors that cuts through the fibrin mesh the rebar of the clot. The Window Ischemic Stroke You have 4.5 hours from the last seen normal to use these. STEMI Used if a Cath Lab PCI is more than 90–120 minutes away. The Danger These drugs are blind. They will melt a life-saving clot in the brain just as fast as the one in the heart. Absolute Contraindications Any history of intracranial hemorrhage recent head trauma or active internal bleeding. --- 3. Reversal Agents The Undo Button What if your patient on these drugs starts bleeding catastrophically? tPA Overdose Give Aminocaproic Acid or Tranexamic Acid TXA . These are lysine analogs that bind to plasminogen and prevent it from being activated. They dull the scissors. Heparin Reversal with Protamine . Warfarin Reversal with Vitamin K slow or PCC/FFP fast . Dabigatran Reversal with Idarucizumab . --- 4. Antiplatelet/Fibrinolytic Summary Table | Drug Class | Example | Mechanism | Specific Toxicity | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | COX Inhibitor | Aspirin | ↓ TXA2 Irreversible | GI ulcers Tinnitus | | P2Y12 Blocker | Clopidogrel | Blocks ADP signaling | Neutropenia rare | | GpIIb/IIIa Blocker | Abciximab | Blocks Fibrinogen binding | Thrombocytopenia | | Fibrinolytic | Alteplase | Plasminogen → Plasmin | Intracranial Hemorrhage | --- 5. Pearls for the Boards Aspirin Toxicity High doses lead to Tinnitus ringing in the ears and a mixed respiratory alkalosis/metabolic acidosis. Ticagrelor Side Effect Can cause Dyspnea shortness of breath that is not related to heart failure. The Mnemonic A bciximab = A lways B locks C lotting. Bleeding Time All antiplatelet agents increase the Bleeding Time primary hemostasis but they do NOT change the PT or aPTT. questions question Which of the following antiplatelet agents irreversibly inhibits the P2Y12 ADP receptor and requires activation by the liver CYP2C19 ? options text Aspirin text Clopidogrel isCorrect true text Ticagrelor text Abciximab explanation Clopidogrel is an irreversible P2Y12 inhibitor and a pro-drug requiring activation by the CYP2C19 enzyme. question A patient presents with signs of an ischemic stroke that began 3 hours ago. Which medication is the most appropriate first-line therapy to restore perfusion? options text Heparin text Aspirin text Alteplase tPA isCorrect true text Warfarin explanation Alteplase is a fibrinolytic indicated for acute ischemic stroke if administered within the 4.5-hour window. It actively dissolves the existing clot. question What is the mechanism of action of Aminocaproic acid when used as an antidote for tPA overdose? options text It directly inactivates the tPA molecule text It competitively inhibits plasminogen activation isCorrect true text It promotes the synthesis of Factor VII text It neutralizes the negative charge of the clot explanation Aminocaproic acid is a lysine analog that binds to plasminogen and plasmin preventing their binding to fibrin and thus inhibiting fibrinolysis. question How long does the antiplatelet effect of a single dose of Aspirin typically last? options text 4-6 hours text 24 hours text 7-10 days isCorrect true text Until the next dose is taken explanation Aspirin irreversibly acetylates COX-1 in platelets. Since platelets are anucleate and cannot synthesize new proteins the effect lasts for the entire lifespan of the platelet 7-10 days . question Which of the following is an ABSOLUTE contraindication to the use of fibrinolytics in a patient with a STEMI? options text Current menstruation text Pregnancy text Any history of intracranial hemorrhage isCorrect true text Peptic ulcer disease explanation Any history of intracranial hemorrhage is an absolute contraindication for fibrinolytics due to the extreme risk of fatal re-bleeding. Menstruation and pregnancy are relative contraindications. flashcards front What is the mechanism of Aspirin ? back Irreversible inhibition of COX-1 stopping TXA2 synthesis. tag Pharmacology front Which antiplatelet drug blocks GpIIb/IIIa ? back Abciximab Eptifibatide or Tirofiban. tag Pharmacology front What is the clot buster mechanism? back Converts Plasminogen to Plasmin which digests fibrin. tag Pharmacology front What is the antidote for tPA overdose? back Aminocaproic Acid or TXA . tag Pharmacology front Which P2Y12 inhibitor is reversible ? back Ticagrelor . tag Pharmacology pearls title GP IIb/IIIa = The Final Door content Platelets can be activated by many things ADP TXA2 Thrombin but they ALL must use GP IIb/IIIa to aggregate. These drugs are the most potent antiplatelets. title The 4.5 Hour Rule content In stroke time is brain. tPA must be given within 4.5 hours of symptom onset. title Aspirin vs NSAIDs content NSAIDs can block the aspirin binding site on COX-1. If a patient needs both they should take Aspirin at least 2 hours BEFORE the NSAID. title Battening down the hatches content Before giving tPA ensure you have two large-bore IVs and have checked the blood pressure. High BP >185/110 is a major risk factor for bleeding."}, {"id": "l166", "title": "Microbiology: Emerging Blood-Borne Pathogens", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l166 module Neuro metadata title Microbiology Emerging Blood-Borne Pathogens lecturer C. Abraham M.D. Session 166 readingTime 10-15 min difficulty Advanced tags Microbiology Parasitology Bartonella Malaria Zika COVID-19 resources ankingResource primarySource SketchyMicro chapter Parasites alternatives resource First Aid chapter Microbiology > Parasitology resource Boards and Beyond chapter Infectious Disease > Parasites anking match Bartonella henselae resources AnKing Step 1 v11 SketchyMicro 01 Bacteria 03 Gram Negative Rods 05 Bartonella henselae match Plasmodium Malaria resources AnKing Step 1 v11 SketchyMicro 04 Parasites 01 Protozoa 03 Plasmodium species match Zika Virus resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid Microbiology Virology RNA viruses Zika summary Story Mode The Hidden Invaders and Global Frontiers The world of infectious disease is never static. It is a constantly shifting landscape where pathogens jump from animals to humans Zoonosis travel across oceans in the bellies of mosquitoes Vectors and adapt to our very cells. This lecture is a deep dive into the Emerging threats—those that travel through our blood hiding in our erythrocytes or colonizing our endothelium. We will journey from the high-altitude valleys of the Andes to the crowded trenches of World War I and finally to the modern global pandemic. 1. The Bartonella Trilogy A Tale of Three Species The Bartonella genus consists of pleomorphic gram-negative rods that are notoriously slow-growing taking 2–6 weeks in culture and difficult to see on a standard Gram stain. To find them we must use the Warthin-Starry silver stain which reveals them as dark irregular clusters. They are masters of the endovasculature infecting CD34+ progenitor cells and erythrocytes. I. Bartonella henselae The Domestic Threat The Story This is the agent of Cat Scratch Disease . It is transmitted by cat fleas or directly via a scratch or bite. The Clinical Journey 3–5 days after the scratch a small red papule appears at the site. This is followed by painful regional lymphadenopathy usually the axillary or cervical nodes and fever. The Immunocompromised Horror In patients with advanced AIDS B. henselae doesn t just cause a swollen node; it causes Bacillary Angiomatosis . This presents as raised red-purple vascular skin nodules that look nearly identical to Kaposi’s Sarcoma. On biopsy however you’ll see neutrophilic infiltrates Kaposi’s has spindle cells and lymphocytes . It can also cause Peliosis Hepatis where the liver becomes riddled with blood-filled cystic spaces. Treatment Azithromycin or Doxycycline. II. Bartonella quintana The Soldier’s Plague The Story Known as Trench Fever this was the scourge of soldiers in World War I. Today it is primarily seen in settings of poverty overcrowding and homelessness. The Vector Human Body Lice specifically their feces . The Presentation It causes a relapsing fever that typically recurs every five days hence quintana . Patients complain of severe headache back pain and excruciating leg pain shin splints . Treatment Prolonged Doxycycline + Gentamicin. III. Bartonella bacilliformis The Andean Scourge The Story Found only in the high-altitude valleys of the Andes Peru Ecuador Colombia . The Vector The bite of the Sandfly . Two Phases 1. Oroya Fever Acute A catastrophic hemolytic anemia where the bacteria coat up to 80% of erythrocytes. Untreated it has a 90% mortality rate. 2. Verruga Peruana Chronic If the patient survives the acute phase they develop Peruvian Warts —disfiguring blood-filled vascular nodules. --- 2. The Micro-Predators Blood-Borne Parasites I. Malaria The Ancient Killer Malaria is caused by Plasmodium species and remains one of the world s greatest health burdens. The Life Cycle The Anopheles mosquito injects sporozoites -> they travel to the liver Exoerythrocytic phase -> they rupture into the blood Erythrocytic phase causing the classic cyclic fevers. The Species P. falciparum The most deadly. It causes Cerebral Malaria because parasitized RBCs develop knobs that stick to capillary walls sequestration causing organ failure and coma. Fevers are irregular. P. vivax/ovale These form Hypnozoites —dormant stages in the liver that can wake up months or years later. You must treat with Primaquine to prevent relapse check for G6PD deficiency first! . P. malariae Notable for a 72-hour fever cycle quartan . II. Babesia microti The Lyme Look-alike The Vector The Ixodes tick the same one that carries Lyme disease! . The Hallmark On a blood smear look for the Maltese Cross tetrads of parasites . Clinical Like malaria it causes hemolytic anemia and fever. It is particularly dangerous in asplenic patients who cannot clear the infected cells. III. Trypanosomiasis Sleeping Sickness vs. Chagas T. cruzi Chagas Found in the Americas. Transmitted by the Reduviid bug Kissing bug which poops near the bite site. Chronic complications include Megacolon Megaesophagus and Dilated Cardiomyopathy . T. brucei African Sleeping Sickness Transmitted by the Tsetse fly . It progresses from a systemic illness Winterbottom s sign posterior cervical nodes to a CNS invasion that reverses the sleep/wake cycle. --- 3. The Modern Emerging Viruses I. Zika Virus The Neurotropic Threat Family Flaviviridae +ssRNA . Vector Aedes mosquito. The Congenital Crisis While adults get a mild rash and conjunctivitis the real danger is to the fetus. If a mother is infected during the first trimester the virus attacks the developing fetal brain leading to Congenital Zika Syndrome —characterized by severe microcephaly collapsed skull and profound developmental delay. II. SARS-CoV-2 The Global Disrupter Family Coronaviridae Enveloped +ssRNA . Mechanism Uses its Spike protein to bind to ACE2 receptors on human cells. The Cytokine Storm The virus itself causes damage but the body s over-reaction the inflammatory phase is what often leads to ARDS and septic shock. Management Pearl Steroids Dexamethasone are life-saving in the late inflammatory phase but can be harmful if given during the early viral replication phase. --- 4. Key Vector & Pathogen Summary Table | Pathogen | Vector | Geographic Hint | Pathognomonic Finding | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | B. henselae | Cat Flea | Worldwide | Regional Lymphadenopathy | | B. quintana | Body Lice | Urban Poverty | 5-day Relapsing Fever | | B. bacilliformis | Sandfly | Andes Mountains | Hemolytic Anemia / Peruvian Warts | | P. falciparum | Mosquito | Tropics | Banana-shaped Gametocytes | | Babesia | Ixodes Tick | NE United States | Maltese Cross on Smear | | T. cruzi | Reduviid Bug | South/Central America| Romaña s Sign Swollen eye | questions question A 45-year-old homeless man in New York City presents with a fever that seems to occur every 5 days. He also complains of severe pain in his shins. He is found to have body lice. What is the most likely causative organism? options text Bartonella henselae text Bartonella quintana isCorrect true text Borrelia recurrentis text Rickettsia prowazekii explanation Bartonella quintana causes Trench Fever characterized by a 5-day relapsing fever and severe leg/bone pain. It is transmitted by human body lice and is often seen in homeless populations. question Which of the following findings on a liver biopsy would most specifically suggest a diagnosis of Bacillary Angiomatosis rather than Kaposi Sarcoma in an AIDS patient? options text Spindle-shaped cells text Lymphocytic infiltrate text Neutrophilic infiltrate isCorrect true text Extravasated erythrocytes explanation While both present as vascular skin lesions in HIV Bacillary Angiomatosis Bartonella is characterized by a neutrophilic infiltrate whereas Kaposi Sarcoma HHV-8 features a lymphocytic infiltrate and spindle cells. question A traveler returning from a hiking trip in Peru develops a sudden severe hemolytic anemia. Blood smear shows small pleomorphic rods within 75% of his red blood cells. What is the vector for this disease? options text Anopheles mosquito text Sandfly isCorrect true text Tsetse fly text Reduviid bug explanation The description of severe hemolytic anemia in the Andes points to the acute phase of Bartonella bacilliformis Oroya Fever which is transmitted by the Sandfly. question A patient is diagnosed with Malaria caused by Plasmodium vivax. In addition to Chloroquine the physician prescribes Primaquine. What is the primary purpose of adding Primaquine? options text To treat the erythrocytic stage in Chloroquine-resistant areas text To prevent transmission to mosquitoes text To eradicate hypnozoites in the liver isCorrect true text To prevent cerebral malaria explanation P. vivax and P. ovale form dormant hypnozoites in the liver. Standard treatments like Chloroquine only kill the blood stage; Primaquine is required to kill the liver stage and prevent clinical relapse. question An infant is born with severe microcephaly redundant scalp skin and intracranial calcifications. The mother traveled to Brazil during her first trimester. What is the genome of the most likely causative virus? options text Double-stranded DNA text Negative-sense single-stranded RNA text Positive-sense single-stranded RNA isCorrect true text Double-stranded RNA explanation The presentation is classic for Congenital Zika Syndrome. Zika virus is a member of the Flaviviridae family which are enveloped positive-sense single-stranded RNA viruses. flashcards front What is the specialized stain used to see Bartonella ? back The Warthin-Starry Silver stain. tag Microbiology front Which Bartonella species causes Peruvian Warts ? back B. bacilliformis chronic phase . tag Microbiology front What is the hallmark of Babesia on a blood smear? back The Maltese Cross tetrads . tag Microbiology front Define Winterbottom s Sign . back Posterior cervical lymphadenopathy in African Sleeping Sickness . tag Microbiology front Which receptor does SARS-CoV-2 utilize for entry? back The ACE2 receptor. tag Microbiology pearls title Bartonella and CD34 content Bartonella species are unique in their ability to infect CD34+ endothelial progenitor cells leading to the vascular proliferation seen in Bacillary Angiomatosis. title The Ixodes Duo content If a patient has Babesiosis Maltese cross always screen them for Lyme Disease Borrelia as the same tick transmits both. title Primaquine Warning content Never give Primaquine without checking for G6PD deficiency as it can trigger a life-threatening hemolytic crisis. title Zika vs. Others content Unlike many other Flaviviruses Zika can be transmitted sexually and vertically not just by mosquitoes."}, {"id": "l167", "title": "Microbiology: HIV and Retroviruses", "module": "Microbiology", "content": "id l167 module Microbiology metadata title Microbiology HIV and Retroviruses lecturer C. Abraham M.D. Session 167 readingTime 10-15 min difficulty Advanced tags Microbiology Virology HIV AIDS ART Retroviruses resources ankingResource primarySource SketchyMicro chapter RNA Viruses > Retroviruses alternatives resource First Aid chapter Microbiology > Virology > RNA Viruses > HIV resource Boards and Beyond chapter Infectious Disease > HIV anking match HIV Life Cycle resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid Microbiology Virology RNA viruses HIV Replication match HIV Genes gag pol env resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid Microbiology Virology RNA viruses HIV Structure match Antiretroviral Therapy ART resources AnKing Step 1 v11 SketchyPharm 08 Antimicrobials 05 Antivirals 04 HIV ART summary Story Mode The Hijacker in the Genetic Blueprint In the vast kingdom of viruses most are like smash-and-grab burglars they enter the cell steal the machinery to make copies and then destroy the house as they leave. But retroviruses and specifically HIV are different. They are high-level identity thieves. They don’t just use your cellular machinery; they write themselves into your very DNA becoming a permanent part of your genetic blueprint. This lecture explores the origin structure and clinical siege of the Human Immunodeficiency Virus. --- 1. The Shadow of the Past Origin and HTLV Before we dive into HIV we must recognize its cousin HTLV-1 Human T-cell Leukemia Virus . HTLV-1 Endemic to Japan the Caribbean and parts of Africa. It causes a slowly progressive neurologic disease called Tropical Spastic Paraparesis weakness and rigidity in the legs and a devastating malignancy known as Adult T-cell Leukemia/Lymphoma ATLL . Unlike HIV there is no effective antiretroviral therapy to prevent ATLL once infected. The Origin of HIV HIV began as a zoonotic jump. Between 1884 and 1924 in the Belgian Congo the Simian Immunodeficiency Virus SIV from chimpanzees crossed into humans likely through the hunting and processing of bushmeat. It traveled along trade routes to Kinshasa where it began its silent global expansion. --- 2. The Structure of the Enemy HIV is an enveloped positive-sense single-stranded RNA virus. It travels with two copies of its genome and a toolbox of specialized enzymes. The Three Master Genes 1. gag Group-specific Antigen Encodes the structural proteins. The most important is p24 the capsid protein that we detect in early screening. 2. pol Polymerase The machinery. It encodes Reverse Transcriptase Converts viral RNA to DNA notoriously error-prone leading to the virus s ability to mutate and escape the immune system . Integrase The splicer that inserts viral DNA into the host chromosome. Protease The cleaver that cuts polyproteins into functional units during maturation. 3. env Envelope The keys to the kingdom. gp120 The attachment protein. It binds to the CD4 receptor and a co-receptor. gp41 The fusion protein. Once gp120 binds gp41 pulls the viral envelope and cell membrane together to allow entry. --- 3. The Seven-Step Heist The Life Cycle 1. Binding gp120 binds CD4 and a co-receptor. CCR5 Used in early infection M-tropic ; found on macrophages/dendritic cells . CXCR4 Used in late-stage infection T-mropic ; found on T-cells . 2. Fusion gp41 allows the viral core to enter the cytoplasm. 3. Reverse Transcription The viral RNA is turned into DNA. This is where most mutations occur. 4. Integration Viral DNA moves to the nucleus and is spliced into the host genome by Integrase . The virus is now a Provirus . 5. Replication The host cell prints viral RNA and proteins. 6. Assembly New virus particles gather at the cell surface. 7. Budding and Maturation The virus leaves the cell. Protease cleaves the proteins to make the virus infectious. Without maturation the virus is a blank unable to infect new cells. --- 4. The Clinical Journey The Story of Bobby We follow a patient Bobby through the stages of infection I. The Acute Phase The Flu that isn t 2–4 weeks after exposure Bobby develops a fever sore throat and lymphadenopathy. This is the Acute Retroviral Syndrome . The Numbers The viral load is in the millions; the CD4 count drops sharply. Bobby is at his most infectious point during this phase. II. The Latent Phase The Silent War Over the next 8–10 years Bobby feels fine. But in his lymph nodes a war of attrition is occurring. The virus is replicating and the immune system is trying to keep up. The Set Point The viral load stabilizes at a set point. The higher the set point the faster the progression to AIDS. III. The AIDS Crisis When Bobby s CD4 count drops below 200 or if he develops an AIDS-defining illness like PCP or Kaposi’s he is officially diagnosed with AIDS. His immune system is now so depleted that vultures opportunistic infections can strike. --- 5. The Arsenal Antiretroviral Therapy ART We use Triple Therapy to corner the virus. NRTIs Nucleoside Reverse Transcriptase Inhibitors These are fake building blocks. They require phosphorylation by host kinases to become active. Once the virus uses them DNA chain elongation stops. e.g. Zidovudine Tenofovir . NNRTIs These bind directly to Reverse Transcriptase at a different site to shut it down. They do not require phosphorylation. e.g. Efavirenz . Protease Inhibitors -navir These block the cleaver preventing maturation. Integrase Inhibitors -tegravir These block the virus from entering the host s blueprint. Entry Inhibitors Maraviroc These bind to the CCR5 co-receptor. They only work if the virus is CCR5-tropic! --- 6. The Modern Frontiers U=U If a patient has an Undetectable viral load the virus is Untransmittable through . PrEP Pre-Exposure Prophylaxis High-risk individuals can take a daily pill Tenofovir/Emtricitabine to prevent infection. The Future Researchers are experimenting with genetic engineering harvesting a patient s T-cells and using CRISPR to remove the CCR5 gene making the cells invincible to common HIV strains. questions question A patient is started on a medication that binds to the CCR5 receptor on host cells. Before starting this drug which of the following tests must be performed to ensure the drug will be effective? options text Viral load testing text CD4 count text Viral tropism assay isCorrect true text HLA-B 5701 testing explanation Maraviroc is a CCR5 antagonist. Since some HIV strains can switch to using the CXCR4 receptor T-tropic or use both dual-tropic a tropism assay is required to ensure the patient s virus exclusively uses the CCR5 receptor. question Which of the following HIV proteins is responsible for the fusion of the viral envelope with the host cell membrane allowing the viral core to enter the cytoplasm? options text gp120 text gp41 isCorrect true text p24 text Reverse Transcriptase explanation gp120 is for attachment binding to CD4/co-receptor while gp41 is for fusion. A useful mnemonic is 41 is one fusion and 120 is zero attachment/zero-ing in . question An HIV-positive patient is diagnosed with Adult T-cell Leukemia/Lymphoma ATLL . Which of the following viruses is the most likely causative agent for this specific malignancy? options text HIV-1 text HIV-2 text HTLV-1 isCorrect true text EBV explanation HTLV-1 Human T-cell Lymphotropic Virus type 1 is specifically associated with ATLL and Tropical Spastic Paraparesis. question A medical student is accidentally stuck by a needle while treating a patient with a high HIV viral load. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management? options text Wait 2 weeks for a p24 antigen test text Start triple-drug Post-Exposure Prophylaxis PEP immediately isCorrect true text Start Pre-Exposure Prophylaxis PrEP text Reassure the student that the risk is negligible explanation Post-Exposure Prophylaxis PEP should be started as soon as possible ideally within 2 hours no later than 72 hours after a high-risk exposure to prevent the establishment of infection. question Which class of antiretroviral drugs requires intracellular phosphorylation by host cell enzymes to become active inhibitors of viral replication? options text NRTIs isCorrect true text NNRTIs text Protease Inhibitors text Integrase Inhibitors explanation NRTIs Nucleoside Reverse Transcriptase Inhibitors are nucleoside analogs that must be phosphorylated by host cell kinases to become the active triphosphate form that competes with natural nucleotides. flashcards front What is the function of the pol gene? back Encodes the enzymes Reverse Transcriptase Integrase and Protease . tag Microbiology front Which co-receptor is used by HIV in early macrophage-tropic infection? back CCR5 . tag Microbiology front What is the diagnostic definition of AIDS ? back CD4 count < 200 or the presence of an AIDS-defining illness . tag Microbiology front Define the Window Period in HIV testing. back The time between infection and the ability of tests to detect the virus shortened by p24 antigen testing . tag Microbiology front What does Zidovudine AZT specifically inhibit? back Reverse Transcriptase it is an NRTI . tag Microbiology pearls title The HIV Screening Algorithm content 4th Generation Assay p24 Ag + HIV Ab -> If positive Differentiation Assay HIV-1 vs HIV-2 -> If indeterminate HIV RNA Nucleic Acid Test. title HLA-B 5701 and Abacavir content Always screen for the HLA-B 5701 allele before starting Abacavir; its presence carries a high risk of a life-threatening hypersensitivity reaction. title The Delta-32 Mutation content Individuals with a homozygous CCR5-delta 32 mutation are resistant to M-tropic early HIV infection because the virus cannot enter their cells. title Maturation vs. Budding content A virus can bud from a cell without protease but it cannot mature. Without maturation it is non-infectious junk."}, {"id": "l168", "title": "Microbiology: Hemorrhagic Viral Infections", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l168 module Neuro metadata title Microbiology Hemorrhagic Viral Infections lecturer T. Slieman Ph.D. Session 168 readingTime 10-15 min difficulty Advanced tags Microbiology Virology Ebola Dengue Yellow Fever Lassa resources ankingResource primarySource SketchyMicro chapter RNA Viruses alternatives resource First Aid chapter Microbiology > Virology > RNA Viruses resource Boards and Beyond chapter Infectious Disease > Viral Hemorrhagic Fevers anking match Filoviridae Ebola/Marburg resources AnKing Step 1 v11 SketchyMicro 02 RNA Viruses 05 Negative Sense 04 Filovirus match Flaviviridae Dengue/Yellow Fever resources AnKing Step 1 v11 SketchyMicro 02 RNA Viruses 02 Positive Sense 03 Flavivirus match Arenaviridae Lassa resources AnKing Step 1 v11 SketchyMicro 02 RNA Viruses 05 Negative Sense 03 Arenavirus summary Story Mode The Storm of the Internal Bleeding In the world of virology there are common colds and then there are the storms. Hemorrhagic fever viruses are the absolute elite of human pathogens. They don t just target an organ; they dismantle the very integrity of the vascular system. When these viruses strike the body’s own immune response—the massive release of cytokines—becomes a destructive force that causes blood vessels to leak leading to internal bleeding shock and often death. This lecture covers the four main families of these storm-bringers. --- 1. The Filoviridae The Shepherd’s Crook Ebola & Marburg If you were to look under an electron microscope you would see long filamentous viruses that often loop into the shape of a shepherd’s crook. These are the Filoviruses. The Reservoir The African fruit bat is the natural host. Humans are accidental hosts who usually get infected through contact with bat excreta or by handling infected primates. The Pathogenesis Ebola and Marburg target the sentinels of the immune system—monocytes macrophages and dendritic cells. They then hijack these cells to produce a Cytokine Storm . This storm destroys the endothelial lining of our blood vessels. The Result Massive vascular leakage edema DIC Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation and hypovolemic shock. The Fatality Mortality rates range from 50% to a staggering 90% specifically the Zaire strain . Case Note In 2014 the world realized how small the planet had become when a patient traveled from West Africa to Texas introducing Ebola to the United States and infecting several healthcare workers. --- 2. The Flaviviridae The Yellow and the Painful These viruses are primarily Arboviruses meaning they are transmitted by Arthropod-borne vectors—specifically the Aedes mosquito . I. Yellow Fever The Jaundiced Plague The Story Historically famous for decimating populations during the building of the Panama Canal. The Presentation It presents with a classic triad High fever Jaundice the Yellow and Hemorrhage often seen as Black Vomit or hematemesis due to digested blood in the stomach . The Pathology On liver biopsy you will see Councilman Bodies —eosinophilic apoptotic globules of hepatocytes. Prevention A highly effective live-attenuated vaccine exists but remember you cannot give live vaccines to infants < 6 months pregnant women or the immunocompromised. II. Dengue Virus The Breakbone Fever The Presentation Causes excruciating joint and muscle pain making patients feel as if their bones are literally breaking. The Trap ADE There are 4 different serotypes. If you are infected with Serotype 1 you develop life-long immunity to Serotype 1. However if you are later infected with Serotype 2 your old antibodies won t kill it. Instead they bind to the new virus and actually help it enter your macrophages. This is called Antibody-Dependent Enhancement ADE and is why a second Dengue infection is much more likely to result in lethal Dengue Hemorrhagic Fever . --- 3. The Bunyaviridae The Southwest Desert Mystery Unlike the others one of the most important Bunyaviruses in the US is not transmitted by a mosquito. Hantavirus Sin Nombre Virus The Vector The Deer Mouse Peromyscus . The Transmission Inhalation of aerosolized mouse urine or droppings often when cleaning out old cabins or sheds . The Weather Connection Outbreaks often follow a cycle of heavy rain which increases the mouse food supply and population followed by dryness which causes the droppings to turn to dust and aerosolize . The Syndrome Hantavirus Pulmonary Syndrome HPS . It starts with flu-like symptoms but rapidly progresses to severe life-threatening pulmonary edema. Geography Famous in the Four Corners region NM AZ CO UT . --- 4. The Arenaviridae The West African Rat Lassa Fever The Vector The Multi-mammate rat . The Story Found in West Africa Nigeria Sierra Leone . The Complication While 80% of cases are mild Lassa fever has one very specific and common long-term consequence Deafness . Roughly 1/3 of survivors lose their hearing to some degree. The Treatment Ribavirin can be effective if started early. --- 5. Segmented RNA Viruses The BOAR Mnemonic Several of these families have segmented genomes which allows them to swap genetic material Antigenic Shift . B unya 3 segments Small Medium Large O rthomyxo 8 segments - Influenza A rena 2 segments - Lassa R eo 10-12 segments - Rotavirus questions question A liver biopsy from a patient who died of a severe hemorrhagic fever reveals eosinophilic apoptotic bodies within hepatocytes. The patient s clinical course was marked by jaundice and black vomit. Which of the following is the most likely causative agent? options text Ebola Virus text Yellow Fever Virus isCorrect true text Dengue Virus text Lassa Virus explanation Yellow Fever is characterized by jaundice liver failure hemorrhage black vomit and the presence of Councilman bodies apoptotic hepatocytes on biopsy. question A 28-year-old traveler returns from a mission trip to Nigeria. A week later he develops fever and malaise. While he eventually recovers he notices that he can no longer hear out of his left ear. Which of the following viruses is the most likely cause? options text Lassa Virus isCorrect true text Marburg Virus text Rift Valley Fever Virus text Crimean-Congo Hemorrhagic Fever explanation Sensorineural hearing loss is the most common permanent sequela of Lassa fever infection occurring in approximately 1/3 of patients. question Which of the following describes the mechanism behind the increased severity of a second Dengue infection compared to the first? options text Viral reassortment in a segmented genome text Direct destruction of the bone marrow text Antibody-dependent enhancement isCorrect true text Exotoxin production explanation Antibody-dependent enhancement ADE occurs when non-neutralizing antibodies from a previous infection facilitate viral entry into host cells via Fc receptors leading to higher viral loads and a more massive inflammatory response. question A hiker in New Mexico develops sudden-onset respiratory distress 2 weeks after cleaning out a rodent-infested storage shed. He is diagnosed with a viral hemorrhagic fever. Which of the following is the primary reservoir for this virus? options text Fruit bats text Deer mouse isCorrect true text Multi-mammate rat text Aedes mosquito explanation The description of Hantavirus Pulmonary Syndrome HPS in the US Southwest following exposure to rodent droppings points to the Deer Mouse Peromyscus as the reservoir. question A patient with suspected Ebola is in the ICU. Which of the following best describes the initial cellular targets of the virus that lead to the systemic Cytokine Storm ? options text Hepatocytes text Erythrocytes text Macrophages and Dendritic cells isCorrect true text Neurons explanation Filoviruses like Ebola primarily infect monocytes macrophages and dendritic cells. These cells then release a massive amount of pro-inflammatory cytokines leading to systemic vascular leakage and shock. flashcards front What are Councilman bodies ? back Eosinophilic apoptotic hepatocytes seen in Yellow Fever . tag Microbiology front Name the segmented RNA viruses. back BOAR Bunyaviridae Orthomyxoviridae Arenaviridae Reoviridae. tag Microbiology front What is the vector for Dengue ? back Aedes aegypti mosquito. tag Microbiology front Which virus causes Hantavirus Pulmonary Syndrome ? back Sin Nombre Virus Bunyaviridae . tag Microbiology front What is the shape of Ebola under EM? back Long filamentous Shepherd s crook . tag Microbiology pearls title The ADE Trap content Dengue is the classic example of how partial immunity can actually be more dangerous than no immunity at all. title Segmented Math back Bunya has 3 segments; Arena has 2. This is high-yield for board exams. title Ribavirin and Lassa content Ribavirin is the go-to antiviral for Lassa fever but it is not effective for Filoviruses like Ebola. title Yellow Fever Vaccine content It is a live vaccine; always ask about egg allergies as it is grown in chick embryos."}, {"id": "l169", "title": "Microbiology: Opportunistic Infections", "module": "Neuro", "content": "id l169 module Neuro metadata title Microbiology Opportunistic Infections lecturer C. Abraham M.D. Session 169 readingTime 10-15 min difficulty Advanced tags Microbiology AIDS Opportunistic Infections Fungal PML Toxoplasmosis resources ankingResource primarySource SketchyMicro chapter Fungi / Parasites alternatives resource First Aid chapter Microbiology > AIDS-defining illnesses resource Boards and Beyond chapter Infectious Disease > Opportunistic Infections anking match Pneumocystis jirovecii PCP resources AnKing Step 1 v11 SketchyMicro 03 Fungi 05 Opportunistic Fungi 04 Pneumocystis jirovecii match Toxoplasma gondii resources AnKing Step 1 v11 SketchyMicro 04 Parasites 01 Protozoa 02 Toxoplasma gondii match Cryptococcus neoformans resources AnKing Step 1 v11 SketchyMicro 03 Fungi 05 Opportunistic Fungi 02 Cryptococcus neoformans summary Story Mode The Vultures and the Siege In a healthy individual the immune system is an impenetrable fortress. But when the walls are breached—whether by a virus like HIV a surgeon s knife or the scorched earth of chemotherapy—the environment changes. Organisms that were once harmless neighbors Commensals or distant background noise Saprophytes suddenly become vultures circling the vulnerable. This lecture explores the opportunistic siege that occurs when the guard is down. --- 1. The Breach Defining Vulnerability Immune deficiency is not just a low white cell count; it is any state where the body s natural defenses are compromised. Physical Barriers A severe burn or a central venous catheter turns the skin from a shield into a doorway for Staph epidermidis and Pseudomonas . The Microbiome Shift Broad-spectrum antibiotics kill the good bacteria leading to C. difficile overgrowth. In stem-cell transplant patients even a healthy probiotic can lead to Lactobacillus sepsis. The Extremes of Age Neonates have no memory cells; the elderly have a waning system leading to the reactivation of dormant viruses like Shingles Zoster . The Complement Gap Patients with SLE or those taking Eculizumab which inhibits C5 have a specific hole in their armor that makes them magnets for Neisseria infections. --- 2. The Respiratory Siege PCP and the Steroid Paradox Pneumocystis jirovecii PCP The Presentation A subacute dry cough and progressive shortness of breath. X-ray shows diffuse ground-glass opacities. The Diagnosis It cannot be cultured. We must use Methenamine Silver Stain on lung fluid to see the crushed ping-pong ball cysts. The CD4 Count Usually occurs when CD4 < 200 . The Paradox When you kill PCP with high-dose TMP-SMX the dying fungi release a massive wave of inflammation. To prevent the patient from drowning in their own immune response you must add steroids if their oxygen levels are low PaO2 < 70 . --- 3. The Neurological War Toxo JC and Crypto When an AIDS patient presents with a headache or a seizure the MRI is the map of the battlefield. I. Toxoplasma gondii The Ring-Enhancer The Story Reactivation of cysts from undercooked meat or cat feces. MRI Multiple ring-enhancing lesions in the gray matter especially the basal ganglia . Treatment Pyrimethamine and Sulfadiazine. II. PML / JC Virus The Demyelinator The Story A silent virus that 90% of us carry. In severe immunosuppression it attacks the oligodendrocytes. MRI Non-enhancing white matter lesions no ring . Presentation Subacute cognitive decline and focal motor deficits. No specific treatment exists other than restoring the immune system with ART. III. Cryptococcus The High-Pressure Yeast The Story An encapsulated yeast inhaled from bird droppings. It travels to the meninges. The Crisis The thick capsule blocks the drainage of CSF. This causes a massive increase in Intracranial Pressure . The Diagnosis India Ink shows the halos of the capsule or Latex Agglutination for the capsular antigen. Management Induction with Amphotericin B + Flucytosine . You must perform serial lumbar punctures to manually drain the pressure and save the patient s brain and eyes. --- 4. The End-Stage Threats MAC and CMV When the CD4 count drops below 50 the most aggressive opportunists appear. Mycobacterium avium complex MAC Clinical High fevers drenching night sweats and a sky-high Alkaline Phosphatase . Special Case Lady Windermere Syndrome —MAC lung disease seen in thin elderly women who suppress their cough. CMV Cytomegalovirus Retinitis The Pizza Pie retina hemorrhage + white exudates . It can blind a patient in days. GI Deep linear ulcers in the esophagus. --- 5. The IRIS Trap Immune Reconstitution Inflammatory Syndrome IRIS is the most cruel irony in HIV medicine. The Mechanism You start ART -> the CD4 count rises -> the immune system wakes up and sees the massive burden of PCP or Cryptococcus that was already there. It launches a violent attack. The Strategy We must clear the fuel before starting the fire. We typically wait 2–4 weeks after starting treatment for an opportunistic infection before we begin ART. questions question An AIDS patient with a CD4 count of 120 presents with a new-onset seizure. MRI of the brain shows multiple ring-enhancing lesions in the basal ganglia. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? options text Primary CNS Lymphoma text Toxoplasmosis isCorrect true text Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy PML text Cryptococcal Meningitis explanation Multiple ring-enhancing lesions in the gray matter of an HIV patient are highly characteristic of Toxoplasmosis. Primary CNS Lymphoma often presents as a single large ring-enhancing lesion. question A patient is diagnosed with Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia PCP . His arterial blood gas shows a PaO2 of 62 mmHg. In addition to Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole which of the following should be added to his regimen? options text Caspofungin text Prednisone isCorrect true text Fluconazole text Azithromycin explanation In severe PCP defined by a PaO2 < 70 mmHg or an A-a gradient > 35 adjunctive corticosteroids are indicated to reduce the inflammatory response triggered by dying organisms which can worsen gas exchange. question A patient with advanced AIDS CD4 = 25 presents with a severe headache. Lumbar puncture reveals an opening pressure of 30 cm H2O. India ink stain is positive for encapsulated yeasts. What is the most appropriate initial management to prevent permanent vision loss? options text Start oral Fluconazole text Immediate surgical decompression text Serial therapeutic lumbar punctures isCorrect true text Start ganciclovir explanation In Cryptococcal meningitis the yeast capsules block CSF resorption leading to high intracranial pressure. Serial LPs are often necessary to manually lower the pressure and prevent complications like blindness and herniation. question A 40-year-old male with a history of SLE and chronic prednisone use presents with fever and a stiff neck. CSF Gram stain reveals Gram-negative diplococci. Which specific part of the immune system is most likely compromised? options text T-cell mediated immunity text Terminal complement C5-C9 isCorrect true text Neutrophil oxidative burst text IgA production explanation Patients with SLE or those taking C5 inhibitors like Eculizumab are at high risk for Neisseria species infections the Gram-negative diplococci seen here due to an inability to form the Membrane Attack Complex MAC . question Which of the following is the most appropriate reason to delay starting Antiretroviral Therapy ART for 2 weeks in a patient recently diagnosed with Cryptococcal meningitis? options text To avoid cytochrome P450 interactions text To allow the patient to adjust to one medication at a time text To prevent Immune Reconstitution Inflammatory Syndrome IRIS isCorrect true text ART is not effective until the fungal burden is zero explanation Starting ART causes a rapid rise in CD4 cells. If there is a high burden of Cryptococcus the resulting inflammatory response IRIS in the closed space of the skull can be fatal due to cerebral edema. flashcards front What is the threshold for Toxoplasma prophylaxis? back CD4 < 100 and IgG positive. tag Microbiology front What is the threshold for PCP prophylaxis? back CD4 < 200 . tag Microbiology front Which stain is used to see MAC in blood or bone marrow? back Ziehl-Neelsen Modified Acid-Fast . tag Microbiology front What is the pizza pie retina indicative of? back CMV Retinitis . tag Microbiology front Which virus causes PML ? back JC Polyomavirus . tag Microbiology pearls title Methenamine Silver Stain content Used for both PCP Fungi and Bartonella Bacteria . It turns the organisms black/dark brown. title The Steroid PaO2 Rule content Add steroids to PCP treatment if PaO2 < 70 or A-a gradient > 35. title Cryptococcus Titer content The Cryptococcal Antigen CrAg titer in the CSF is a better prognostic marker than the blood titer. title Corynebacterium in Shunts content While Staph is more common Corynebacterium is a classic board-association for VP shunt infections."}, {"id": "l170", "title": "Behavioral Medicine: The Patient Encounter", "module": "DPR", "content": "id l170 module DPR metadata title Behavioral Medicine The Patient Encounter lecturer L. Hoffman Ph.D. Session 170 readingTime 10-15 min difficulty Intermediate tags DPR Behavioral Medicine Mental Status Exam MSE Psychiatry Communication resources ankingResource primarySource First Aid chapter Psychiatry > Psychiatry basics alternatives resource Boards and Beyond chapter Psychiatry > Mental Status Exam anking match Mental Status Exam MSE resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid Psychiatry Mental Status Examination match Mood vs. Affect resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid Psychiatry Mental Status Examination Mood and Affect match Thought Process resources AnKing Step 1 v11 FirstAid Psychiatry Mental Status Examination Thought Process summary Story Mode The Art of the Behavioral Encounter In traditional clinical medicine the encounter is often a quest for a physical sign—a murmur a rash or a mass. But in Behavioral Medicine the encounter is different. It is a journey into the patient s internal experience. Here your ears and your observation are your primary diagnostic tools. This lecture focuses on how to systematically assess a person s mental state while building a therapeutic relationship that can literally save lives. --- 1. The Reality The Mental Health Crisis The statistics of mental health in America are staggering. The Prevalence 1 in 5 adults roughly 50 million Americans experience mental illness annually. Since the COVID-19 pandemic the incidence of anxiety and depression has surged by 30–40%. The Disparity While 21% of all adults are affected that number rises to 44% in the LGBTQ population . The Treatment Gap Over 50% of those with mental illness receive zero treatment . This gap has profound societal impacts 24% of state prisoners and 26% of homeless individuals in shelters suffer from serious persistent mental illness SPMI . The Ultimate Cost Suicide is the 8th leading cause of death overall but it is the second leading cause of death for youth aged 15–25. Ninety percent of those who die by suicide have an underlying mental illness most commonly untreated depression . The Lifeline In 2022 the US launched the 988 national emergency line for mental health crises. --- 2. The Tool The Mental Status Exam MSE The MSE is the Physical Exam of the Mind. It is a structured way of documenting your observations during the interview. I. Appearance & Behavior Grooming Is the patient disheveled or meticulously dressed? Psychomotor Are they agitated pacing wringing hands or retarded moving in slow motion ? Eye Contact Are they avoiding it or is it intense and unblinking? II. Speech Rate & Volume Is it slow and soft depression or loud and pressured the unstoppable machine-gun speech of mania ? III. Mood vs. Affect The Weather Metaphor Mood The Climate The patient s long-term internal state. You ask How have you been feeling lately? They answer I feel 1/10 like a failure. Affect The Weather Your objective observation of their current expression. Flat No emotional resonance. Blunted Very little emotion. Labile Rapid extreme shifts laughing one second crying the next . Congruency Does the Affect match the Mood? e.g. if a patient says they are suicidal while laughing their affect is incongruent . IV. Thought Process & Content Process The How Circumstantial They take a long winding road but eventually answer the question. Tangential They take the winding road and never come back. Flight of Ideas Rapidly shifting from one topic to another with only vague associations. Word Salad Complete lack of logic or association. Content The What Delusions fixed false beliefs Obsessions or Suicidal/Homicidal Ideations. V. Perceptions Hallucinations Seeing or hearing things that aren t there. Illusions Misinterpreting something that is there e.g. seeing a coat on a rack and thinking it s a person . VI. Insight & Judgment Insight Do you know why you re here? A patient who says The government sent me to be poisoned has poor insight. Judgment The ability to reach a logical decision. What would you do if you found a stamped addressed envelope on the sidewalk? --- 3. The Differentials The Medical Mimics Never assume a patient is purely psychiatric until you have ruled out the Medical Mimics. Hypothyroidism Can present as classic severe Major Depression. Hyperthyroidism / Arrhythmia Can mimic an Anxiety Disorder or Panic Attack. UTI / Dehydration In elderly patients these often present as sudden behavioral changes confusion or acting crazy Delirium . B12 Deficiency Can cause psychosis or megaloblastic madness. --- 4. The Encounter Communication & Boundaries Techniques Use Active Listening Reflection repeating back what you heard and Validation It makes sense that you would feel overwhelmed right now . The SOAP Note S Subjective Patient s story + PHQ-9 Depression or GAD-7 Anxiety scores. O Objective The MSE findings and Lab results TSH B12 Urine screen . A Assessment Your synthesis and a specific Suicide Risk Estimate . P Plan Medication Therapy CBT and a Safety Plan . Transference vs. Counter-transference Transference The patient projects feelings from their past onto the doctor. Counter-transference The doctor projects their own feelings onto the patient. Recognizing this is crucial for maintaining professional boundaries. questions question During an interview a patient says I feel like I m the king of the world! However his facial expression remains completely blank and his voice is monotone throughout the encounter. How would you describe his affect? options text Flat and Incongruent isCorrect true text Labile and Congruent text Blunted and Congruent text Restricted and Incongruent explanation A flat affect refers to a complete lack of emotional expression. It is incongruent because it does not match the patient s stated king of the world manic mood. question A 19-year-old college student presents with 3 weeks of severe anxiety heart palpitations and tremors. She has no psychiatric history. Which of the following is the most appropriate first diagnostic step? options text Start a low-dose SSRI text Perform a Mental Status Exam and order a TSH isCorrect true text Refer for Cognitive Behavioral Therapy CBT text Administer a GAD-7 questionnaire and start a Benzodiazepine explanation Medical mimics must be ruled out first. Hyperthyroidism detected by TSH can present with symptoms that look identical to a primary anxiety disorder. question A patient responds to a question about his family by talking about his favorite food then his car then his neighbor s dog and never actually mentions his family. What thought process is this? options text Circumstantial text Tangential isCorrect true text Flight of Ideas text Linear explanation Tangential thought process occurs when a patient drifts away from the question and never returns to the point. Circumstantial thought would eventually come back to the answer. question Which of the following is the leading cause of death for individuals aged 15–25 in the United States? options text Accidental overdose text Suicide isCorrect true text Homicide text Malignancy explanation According to the lecture statistics suicide is the second leading cause of death in this age group trailing only accidental injuries which was the third leading cause in previous years now often grouped as the top tier of preventable death in youth . question A doctor realizes he is feeling unusually angry and defensive whenever a particular patient speaks. This is an example of which clinical phenomenon? options text Transference text Counter-transference isCorrect true text Mirroring text Reaction Formation explanation Counter-transference is the projection of the clinician s own feelings often based on their past experiences onto the patient. flashcards front What is the difference between an Illusion and a Hallucination ? back Illusion = Misinterpreting a real stimulus; Hallucination = Perceiving something with NO stimulus. tag DPR front Define Pressured Speech . back Fast loud unstoppable speech; classic sign of Mania . tag DPR front What part of the MSE is tested by the envelope on the sidewalk question? back Judgment . tag DPR front What is a PHQ-9 used for? back Screening for and monitoring the severity of Depression . tag DPR front What does 988 represent? back The US national emergency line for mental health and suicide prevention. tag DPR pearls title The Safety Assessment content You MUST always document a specific assessment of suicide and homicide risk in a behavioral health note. If you don t document it it didn t happen. title Climate vs. Weather content Mood is the climate long-term ; Affect is the weather current moment . title B12 and Psychosis content Don t forget that vitamin deficiencies B12 Folate can cause Megaloblastic Madness —a state of paranoia and psychosis. title Normalizing the Question content To ask about suicide normalize it Many people feeling this way have thought about hurting themselves. Have you?"}];